<!-- ================= START HERE ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-start" data-name="Start here">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>The well-rounded engineer</h2><p><b>Well Rounded Engineer — More Human</b> is a field guide for engineers in the AI era: people who think clearly, behave well, master the fundamentals, know enough of everything to work across the whole system, go deep where it matters, and use AI as one more engineering tool. Use this page to see how much of each tab you need, and read <a href="#why">why this guide exists</a>.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">
  <article class="card wide" id="guide-map"><h3>The map: the groups, in the order to learn them</h3>
    <p>Start with Foundations and work down; come back to Thinking &amp; character often. Each page ends with its levels, what to study next, code you can run where it exists, and a link to ask a question on GitHub. The story behind the guide and EnrichMeAI&rsquo;s own open-source projects sit apart, at the end of the list.</p>
    <div class="gmap"><div class="gmap-card"><h4><span class="gmap-ic" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="22" height="22" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M9 3v3M15 3v3M9 18v3M15 18v3M3 9h3M3 15h3M18 9h3M18 15h3M6 6h12v12H6z"/></svg></span><span class="gmap-n">1</span> Foundations</h4><p>How computers work from the chip up, networks and the web, and the data underneath. Everything else stands on this.</p><p class="gmap-pages"><a href="#electronics">Electronics &amp; Chips</a>, <a href="#hardware">How Computers Are Built</a>, <a href="#linux">Linux</a>, <a href="#networking">Networking</a>, <a href="#internet">How the Internet Is Tied Together</a>, <a href="#web">Web Fundamentals</a>, <a href="#docker">Docker</a>, <a href="#dsa">Data Structures &amp; Algorithms</a>, <a href="#concurrency">Concurrency</a>, <a href="#sql">SQL Skills</a>, <a href="#database">Database Design</a>, <a href="#graphdb">Graph Databases</a>, <a href="#datastore">Choosing a Data Store</a>, <a href="#homelab">Home Lab</a></p><p class="gmap-run"><a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/well-rounded-engineer-fundamentals" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Run the code: well-rounded-engineer-fundamentals</a></p><p class="gmap-run"><a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/well-rounded-engineer-data" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Run the code: well-rounded-engineer-data</a></p></div><div class="gmap-card"><h4><span class="gmap-ic" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="22" height="22" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M8 7l-5 5 5 5M16 7l5 5-5 5"/></svg></span><span class="gmap-n">2</span> Languages</h4><p>Java and Python learned properly first, then Kotlin, Go and Rust: how each runs, tests and fails.</p><p class="gmap-pages"><a href="#java">Java</a>, <a href="#javathreads">How Java Threads Evolved</a>, <a href="#ionio">IO, NIO &amp; Web Servers</a>, <a href="#kotlin">Kotlin</a>, <a href="#python">Python</a>, <a href="#javascript">JavaScript</a>, <a href="#functional">Functional or Imperative</a>, <a href="#fetoolchain">Front-end Toolchain</a>, <a href="#go">Go</a>, <a href="#rust">Rust</a>, <a href="#buildtools">Build Tools</a></p><p class="gmap-run"><a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/well-rounded-engineer-code" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Run the code: well-rounded-engineer-code</a></p><p class="gmap-run"><a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/well-rounded-engineer-labs" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Run the code: well-rounded-engineer-labs</a></p></div><div class="gmap-card"><h4><span class="gmap-ic" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="22" height="22" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M4 4h16v6H4zM4 14h16v6H4zM8 7h.01M8 17h.01"/></svg></span><span class="gmap-n">3</span> Building services</h4><p>APIs, messaging and the frameworks that turn code into services.</p><p class="gmap-pages"><a href="#apis">APIs &amp; Integration</a>, <a href="#apihistory">How APIs Evolved</a>, <a href="#graphql">GraphQL</a>, <a href="#realtime">Real-time to the Browser</a>, <a href="#reactive">Reactive &amp; Backpressure</a>, <a href="#kafka">Kafka</a>, <a href="#payments">Payment Gateways</a>, <a href="#banking">Banking &amp; Low Latency</a>, <a href="#spring">Spring Boot</a>, <a href="#springhistory">From spring.jar to Spring</a>, <a href="#quarkus">Quarkus</a>, <a href="#faded">Faded? Rails, Flex &amp; Grails</a></p><p class="gmap-run"><a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/well-rounded-engineer-code" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Run the code: well-rounded-engineer-code</a></p></div><div class="gmap-card"><h4><span class="gmap-ic" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="22" height="22" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M3 4h18v16H3zM3 9h18M9 9v11"/></svg></span><span class="gmap-n">4</span> User interfaces</h4><p>Designing for people: design thinking, UX, accessibility, and apps on phones, desktops and the web.</p><p class="gmap-pages"><a href="#designux">Design Thinking &amp; UX</a>, <a href="#accessibility">Accessibility</a>, <a href="#apps">Apps: Native or Cross-platform</a>, <a href="#uihistory">UI History</a></p></div><div class="gmap-card"><h4><span class="gmap-ic" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="22" height="22" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M4 12l5 5L20 6"/></svg></span><span class="gmap-n">5</span> Quality</h4><p>Proving it works, and that it stays fast under load.</p><p class="gmap-pages"><a href="#testing">Testing</a>, <a href="#teststrat">Test Strategy for Cloud Systems</a>, <a href="#review">Writing &amp; Code Review</a>, <a href="#performance">Performance</a></p><p class="gmap-run"><a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/well-rounded-engineer-code" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Run the code: well-rounded-engineer-code</a></p></div><div class="gmap-card"><h4><span class="gmap-ic" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="22" height="22" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M12 3l8 3v6c0 5-3.5 8-8 9-4.5-1-8-4-8-9V6z"/></svg></span><span class="gmap-n">6</span> Security</h4><p>Building it safely, and thinking like a defender.</p><p class="gmap-pages"><a href="#security">Security</a>, <a href="#pki">Keys, Certificates &amp; SSH</a>, <a href="#privacy">Personal Data Across Services</a>, <a href="#cyber">Cybersecurity</a>, <a href="#agentsec">Agentic AI Security</a>, <a href="#oauth">OAuth &amp; OIDC</a></p></div><div class="gmap-card"><h4><span class="gmap-ic" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="22" height="22" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M4 6c0-1.7 3.6-3 8-3s8 1.3 8 3-3.6 3-8 3-8-1.3-8-3zM4 6v12c0 1.7 3.6 3 8 3s8-1.3 8-3V6M4 12c0 1.7 3.6 3 8 3s8-1.3 8-3"/></svg></span><span class="gmap-n">7</span> Data</h4><p>Moving, shaping and trusting data, from a file to a warehouse, on any platform.</p><p class="gmap-pages"><a href="#data">Data Engineering</a>, <a href="#datalibs">Data Libraries</a>, <a href="#dbt">dbt</a>, <a href="#dags">Airflow DAGs</a></p><p class="gmap-run"><a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/well-rounded-engineer-data" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Run the code: well-rounded-engineer-data</a></p></div><div class="gmap-card"><h4><span class="gmap-ic" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="22" height="22" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M3 3h8v8H3zM13 3h8v8h-8zM3 13h8v8H3zM13 13h8v8h-8z"/></svg></span><span class="gmap-n">8</span> Architecture &amp; design</h4><p>Choosing boundaries and trade-offs you can explain.</p><p class="gmap-pages"><a href="#patterns">SOLID &amp; Design Patterns</a>, <a href="#architecture">Architecture</a>, <a href="#quality">Quality Attributes</a>, <a href="#atob">Solution Architecture: A to B</a>, <a href="#distsys">Distributed Systems</a>, <a href="#microservices">Microservices</a>, <a href="#casestudies">Case Studies: How Tech Companies Evolved</a>, <a href="#eventsourcing">Event Sourcing</a>, <a href="#sysdesign">System Design</a>, <a href="#specs">Specifications</a>, <a href="#caching">Caching</a></p><p class="gmap-run"><a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/well-rounded-engineer-code" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Run the code: well-rounded-engineer-code</a></p><p class="gmap-run"><a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/well-rounded-engineer-fundamentals" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Run the code: well-rounded-engineer-fundamentals</a></p></div><div class="gmap-card"><h4><span class="gmap-ic" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="22" height="22" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M12 8a4 4 0 1 0 0 8 4 4 0 0 0 0-8zM12 2v3M12 19v3M2 12h3M19 12h3M4.9 4.9l2.1 2.1M17 17l2.1 2.1M4.9 19.1 7 17M17 7l2.1-2.1"/></svg></span><span class="gmap-n">9</span> Platform &amp; operations</h4><p>Shipping safely, running containers, seeing what is happening, and keeping it up.</p><p class="gmap-pages"><a href="#devops">DevOps</a>, <a href="#kubernetes">Kubernetes</a>, <a href="#k8sdr">Kubernetes DR &amp; Best Practices</a>, <a href="#helm">Helm Charts</a>, <a href="#operators">Operators</a>, <a href="#servicemesh">Service Mesh &amp; Istio</a>, <a href="#terraform">Terraform</a>, <a href="#observability">Observability</a>, <a href="#sre">SRE</a></p></div><div class="gmap-card"><h4><span class="gmap-ic" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="22" height="22" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M7 18h10a4 4 0 0 0 0-8 6 6 0 0 0-11.6 1.5A3.5 3.5 0 0 0 7 18z"/></svg></span><span class="gmap-n">10</span> Cloud providers</h4><p>What the cloud is made of, and the same jobs done on Google Cloud and AWS, side by side.</p><p class="gmap-pages"><a href="#cloudinside">How the cloud is built</a>, <a href="#cloudcompare">GCP vs AWS vs Azure</a>, <a href="#cloudcost">Cloud Cost</a>, <a href="#retailcloud">Retail &amp; Hybrid Cloud</a>, <a href="#gcpdata">Data on GCP</a>, <a href="#bigquery">BigQuery</a>, <a href="#cloudrun">Cloud Run</a>, <a href="#apigee">Apigee</a>, <a href="#awsdata">Data on AWS</a>, <a href="#azuredata">Data on Azure</a></p><p class="gmap-run"><a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/well-rounded-engineer-fundamentals" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Run the code: well-rounded-engineer-fundamentals</a></p></div><div class="gmap-card"><h4><span class="gmap-ic" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="22" height="22" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M12 3v4M12 17v4M3 12h4M17 12h4M6 6l2.5 2.5M15.5 15.5 18 18M6 18l2.5-2.5M15.5 8.5 18 6"/></svg></span><span class="gmap-n">11</span> AI</h4><p>Using AI with judgement: how it works, where it helps, and the guard rails.</p><p class="gmap-pages"><a href="#neural">Neural Networks</a>, <a href="#llm">LLMs</a>, <a href="#ai">AI for Engineers</a>, <a href="#rag">RAG</a>, <a href="#mcp">MCP</a>, <a href="#agentharness">Agent Harness</a>, <a href="#humanagents">Humans &amp; Agents</a>, <a href="#blueprints">Frameworks vs AI</a></p></div><div class="gmap-card"><h4><span class="gmap-ic" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="22" height="22" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M14 6a4 4 0 0 0 5 5l-9 9a2 2 0 0 1-3-3l9-9a4 4 0 0 0-2-2z"/></svg></span><span class="gmap-n">12</span> Tools</h4><p>Specific products and services, kept apart so the concepts above stay neutral.</p><p class="gmap-pages"><a href="#claudetools">Claude Code &amp; Cowork</a>, <a href="#copilot">GitHub Copilot</a>, <a href="#agentsgcp">Agents on GCP</a>, <a href="#harness">Harness</a>, <a href="#opstools">Ops Tooling</a></p></div><div class="gmap-card"><h4><span class="gmap-ic" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="22" height="22" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M12 12a4 4 0 1 0 0-8 4 4 0 0 0 0 8zM4 21c1-4 4-6 8-6s7 2 8 6"/></svg></span><span class="gmap-n">13</span> Thinking &amp; character</h4><p>How to think clearly and live well. The part no tool replaces.</p><p class="gmap-pages"><a href="#behaviours">Behaviours</a>, <a href="#ways">Ways of Working</a>, <a href="#mathematics">Mathematics</a>, <a href="#science">Science &amp; Method</a>, <a href="#everydayscience">Everyday Science</a>, <a href="#philosophy">Philosophy</a>, <a href="#goodhuman">Being a Good Human</a>, <a href="#body">Body, Mind &amp; Nature</a></p></div><div class="gmap-card"><h4><span class="gmap-ic" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="22" height="22" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M5 15c-1 2-1 4-1 4s2 0 4-1M9 15l-3-3c2-5 6-9 13-9 0 7-4 11-9 13zM14 10h.01"/></svg></span><span class="gmap-n">14</span> Frontiers</h4><p>Ideas to understand now that may matter more later.</p><p class="gmap-pages"><a href="#quantum">Quantum Computing</a>, <a href="#robotics">Robotics</a>, <a href="#bitcoin">Bitcoin &amp; Blockchain</a>, <a href="#web3">Web 3.0</a>, <a href="#solid">Solid</a></p></div></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide" id="promise"><h3>Our promise: checked knowledge, no fuss</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Facts are checked</b> against an official document, a standard or a well-sourced reference, and every page lists what it was checked against. Where an official source could not be reached, the page says so and those claims are being re-checked.</li>
      <li><b>Code examples are run</b> before they go on a page. Some sections already link to a repository of runnable code ("Run the code"); more repositories and runnable samples are being built so that every section has something you can run.</li>
      <li><b>What is memory or opinion says so</b> ("as I remember it"), and what is planned is called planned.</li>
      <li><b>No ads, no tracking, no sign-up.</b> Read in peace, and save any page as a PDF.</li>
      <li><b>When something is wrong, it gets fixed:</b> every page ends with a link to report it in the open.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">No guide can promise to be perfect. This one promises to show its sources and correct its mistakes.</p>
  </article>


  <article class="card wide"><h3>What well-rounded means here</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>The base: character and thinking.</b> Honesty, curiosity, care for people, and clear reasoning grounded in mathematics, the scientific method and philosophy. AI can generate code; it cannot supply judgement or integrity. See <a href="#behaviours">Behaviours</a> and the <a href="#mathematics">Thinking &amp; character</a> tabs.</li>
      <li><b>The foundation: shared fundamentals.</b> Every engineer needs these: SQL, one language well (Java or Python), Linux, Docker, networking and the web, Git and CI/CD, testing, security basics, and reading logs.</li>
      <li><b>Breadth across the system.</b> Enough of data, services, design, cloud and operations to read the code, follow a design discussion, and find the right person or doc.</li>
      <li><b>Depth where you choose.</b> Real expertise in one or two areas. Many engineers on this kind of system go deep in <b>data</b> (BigQuery, dbt, pipelines) or <b>platform</b> (cloud, Kubernetes, delivery), or both; the shape is sometimes drawn as a T or a Y.</li>
      <li><b>AI across all of it.</b> You build features on models and use assistants to work faster, with the same discipline as everything else.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">"Know enough" below is the bar for breadth: what any engineer should manage without help. "Go deep" is what a specialist in that area owns.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Not only for engineers</h3>
    <p>We get one life, and whatever job we do, we can make the journey beautiful. The tabs under <b>Thinking &amp; character</b> (<a href="#mathematics">Mathematics</a>, <a href="#science">Science &amp; Method</a>, <a href="#philosophy">Philosophy</a>, <a href="#goodhuman">Being a Good Human</a> and <a href="#body">Body, Mind &amp; Nature</a>) and the <a href="#behaviours">Behaviours</a> page are written for engineers, but they hold for anyone: a nurse, a teacher, an accountant, a builder. Read them that way. The engineering tabs show what "fundamentals first" looks like in one craft; every craft has its own.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>From data to wisdom</h3>
    <p>There is more information within reach today than ever, but information is not understanding. A useful way to think about learning is a ladder of four steps, often called DIKW:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Data:</b> raw facts. A stack trace, a column of numbers, a snippet of code.</li>
      <li><b>Information:</b> data with meaning. This error happens when that field is empty.</li>
      <li><b>Knowledge:</b> understanding how and why. The field is empty because the upstream system sends dates in another format.</li>
      <li><b>Wisdom:</b> knowing what to do, and what not to do. Validate at the boundary, and design so this whole kind of error cannot happen again.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>A search result or an AI answer can hand you information, sometimes even text shaped like knowledge. Wisdom only comes from understanding the fundamentals and applying them yourself, again and again. Nobody can learn everything; the aim is enough foundations to climb from data to wisdom in whatever you take on. This guide is here to help you climb.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Fundamentals first: be the site manager, not a vibe coder</h3>
    <p>AI hands every engineer a bulldozer. A bulldozer on a building site is only safe with a <b>site manager</b> who knows how buildings stand up: the foundations, the materials, the regulations, and what to inspect before anyone moves in. "Vibe coding", accepting whatever the model produces because it looks right, is a bulldozer with nobody in charge of the site. It feels fast until the first thing breaks and nobody can say why.</p>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>On a building site</th><th>In software</th><th>Where to learn it</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Foundations and ground</td><td>Operating systems, networking, how the web works, databases and SQL</td><td><a href="#linux">Linux</a>, <a href="#networking">Networking</a>, <a href="#web">Web Fundamentals</a>, <a href="#sql">SQL Skills</a>, <a href="#database">Database Design</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Materials and how they behave</td><td>A language well, its data structures and concurrency, the libraries you depend on</td><td><a href="#java">Java</a>, <a href="#python">Python</a>, <a href="#datalibs">Data Libraries</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Plans and structure</td><td>Requirements, architecture, data models, system design</td><td><a href="#architecture">Architecture</a>, <a href="#sysdesign">System Design</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Building regulations</td><td>Security, privacy, licences, compliance</td><td><a href="#security">Security</a>, <a href="#oauth">OAuth &amp; OIDC</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Inspections and sign-off</td><td>Tests, reviews, checks that must be green</td><td><a href="#testing">Testing</a>, <a href="#devops">DevOps</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Running the finished building</td><td>Deploying, monitoring, incidents, cost</td><td><a href="#observability">Observability</a>, <a href="#sre">SRE</a></td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Vibe coder</th><th>Site manager</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Starts with a prompt</td><td>Starts with the problem, the constraints and what "done" means</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Cannot tell good output from plausible output</td><td>Knows the fundamentals well enough to spot what is wrong</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Stuck when the AI is stuck</td><td>Debugs from first principles: logs, queries, packets, stack traces</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Ships what runs once</td><td>Ships what is tested, secure, observable and maintainable</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Learns little from each task</td><td>Gets better with every task, and so gets more out of the tool</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p><b>How to learn this way:</b></p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Do it by hand once.</b> Write the loop, the SQL query, the Dockerfile, the deploy, the test yourself before asking AI to do it for you. Then you can judge its version.</li>
      <li><b>Use AI as a tutor while learning:</b> ask it to explain, to quiz you, to review your attempt. Do not let it write the answer you are trying to learn.</li>
      <li><b>Earn the bulldozer:</b> the more fundamentals you own, the more work you can safely hand to AI, because you can inspect it.</li>
      <li><b>Follow the order:</b> Foundations, then a language, then quality and security, then the area you want to go deep in. The <a href="#homelab">Home Lab</a> turns each into practice.</li>
      <li><b>Keep going back to basics:</b> when something surprises you, the gap is almost always in a fundamental, not in the tool.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">AI raises the floor of what anyone can produce. Fundamentals raise the ceiling of what you can be trusted with.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>An engineer who uses AI, not a prompt engineer</h3>
    <p>Writing a good prompt is one skill among many, and the smallest one. What makes AI work in production is engineering:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Data:</b> the model is only as good as what you retrieve and feed it. That is SQL, pipelines, chunking and freshness, the data tabs of this guide.</li>
      <li><b>Evaluation:</b> a test set and a score, so a change to the prompt or model is measured, not judged by eye.</li>
      <li><b>Systems:</b> timeouts, retries, caching, cost limits, logging, and a fallback when the model is down or refuses.</li>
      <li><b>Security:</b> untrusted text can carry instructions (prompt injection). Least-privilege tools, no secrets in prompts, output treated as untrusted input.</li>
      <li><b>Judgement with assistants:</b> AI-written code gets the same review, tests and doc checks as yours. You stay responsible for what ships.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">The <a href="#ai">AI tab</a> covers each of these. A prompt engineer stops at the prompt; a well-rounded engineer ships, measures and operates the whole feature.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide" id="levels-scale"><h3>The four levels used on every tab</h3>
    <div class="levels">
      <div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><p>Can use it with examples and guidance. Reads it, runs it, makes small changes safely, and knows when to ask.</p></div>
      <div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><p>Works independently. Builds and ships features with it, tests them, debugs ordinary problems, follows the team's standards.</p></div>
      <div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><p>Designs with it. Makes trade-offs, reviews others' work, handles the hard incidents, and mentors.</p></div>
      <div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><p>Sets direction across teams. Knows the internals, writes the standards and blueprints, and leads migrations and decisions.</p></div>
    </div>
    <p><b>Where a well-rounded engineer aims:</b> Senior in the one or two areas they go deep in; Practitioner in the foundations, testing, security, and the thinking and character tabs; at least Foundation everywhere else. Expert in one or two areas comes with time and is not expected of everyone.</p>
    <p class="note">Each tab ends with its own level expectations and an "Explore further" card of official docs, books and topics to study next.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>How much of each tab you need</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Area</th><th>Know enough to…</th><th>Go deep means…</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td><a href="#electronics">Electronics &amp; Chips</a></td><td>Explain transistors, logic gates and what an instruction set is.</td><td>Digital design with an HDL and FPGAs; open hardware such as RISC-V; design for repair.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#hardware">How Computers Are Built</a></td><td>Explain RISC vs CISC, firmware and drivers, and why GPUs suit AI.</td><td>Choose hardware for a workload; reason about cores, memory, storage and accelerators.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#linux">Linux</a></td><td>Find a file, read a log, check disk and memory, kill a stuck process, write a safe bash script.</td><td>systemd, permissions and users, performance tools, kernel limits, shell scripting at scale.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#networking">Networking</a></td><td>Tell a DNS failure from a refused connection from a timeout.</td><td>VPC design, CIDR planning, NAT and private access, load balancers, TLS.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#internet">How the Internet Is Tied Together</a></td><td>Explain autonomous systems, BGP, exchange points, CDNs and DNS roots.</td><td>Design around shared internet dependencies: DNS, CDN and region failures.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#web">Web Fundamentals</a></td><td>Explain a page load end to end; read headers in DevTools; know cookies and CORS.</td><td>Caching, rendering models, web security headers, performance and accessibility.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#docker">Docker</a></td><td>Build, run and debug a container; read a Dockerfile.</td><td>Small, secure, reproducible images; multi-stage and multi-platform builds; layer caching.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#dsa">Data Structures &amp; Algorithms</a></td><td>Read Big O; know arrays, lists, hash tables, trees, heaps and graphs.</td><td>Pick a structure for the most frequent operation; reason about sorting, search and graph algorithms.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#concurrency">Concurrency</a></td><td>Explain concurrency vs parallelism, race conditions and deadlock.</td><td>Choose a concurrency model and tools by requirement; reason with Amdahl's law.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#sql">SQL Skills</a></td><td>Write correct joins and aggregates, handle NULLs, use window functions and QUALIFY.</td><td>Gaps and islands, recursive queries, reading BigQuery execution graphs, cutting cost.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#database">Database Design</a></td><td>Design a normalised table with keys and constraints; read a query plan; add the right index.</td><td>Partitioning, sharding, replication, isolation levels, zero-downtime migrations.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#graphdb">Graph Databases</a></td><td>Explain nodes, edges and properties, and property graphs vs RDF.</td><td>Write graph queries, compare a graph with a relational database, and choose by requirement.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#datastore">Choosing a Data Store</a></td><td>Name the main families of data store and what each is good at.</td><td>Choose stores by requirement, and weigh the cost of running more than one.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#java">Java</a></td><td>Read modern Java (records, streams, switch patterns), fix a small bug, run the tests.</td><td>Concurrency and virtual threads, JVM tuning in containers, the BigQuery and Beam clients.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#kotlin">Kotlin</a></td><td>Read and write Kotlin beside Java; use null safety and data classes.</td><td>Coroutines, Java interop at scale, multiplatform and Android or server architecture.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#python">Python</a></td><td>Write a script that reads GCS and queries BigQuery, with a pytest test.</td><td>Packaging, async and threading choices, Beam pipelines, typed and tested libraries.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#javascript">JavaScript</a></td><td>Explain closures, the event loop and promises; change a page with no framework.</td><td>Language patterns under any framework, Baseline support, TypeScript, and performance on the main thread.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#fetoolchain">Front-end Toolchain</a></td><td>Say what TypeScript, JSX, Babel, a bundler and Redux each do.</td><td>Choose which layers a project needs by requirement, and keep a front end small enough to upgrade.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#go">Go</a></td><td>Write a small service with errors as values and a table-driven test.</td><td>Concurrency with goroutines and context, profiling, and reading cloud tools' source.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#rust">Rust</a></td><td>Explain ownership and borrowing; use Result, Option and match.</td><td>Lifetimes, traits and async; safe interfaces around unsafe code; performance work.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#buildtools">Build Tools</a></td><td>Run a Maven, Gradle or npm build; read a lockfile and a dependency tree.</td><td>Build caches, version catalogs and BOMs, install-script policy, and CI build speed.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#apis">APIs &amp; Integration</a></td><td>Explain REST and status codes; read an OpenAPI spec; tell sync from async.</td><td>Choosing API styles, gateway and mesh design, versioning and API governance.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#apihistory">How APIs Evolved</a></td><td>Place RPC, CORBA, SOAP, REST, gRPC, GraphQL, MCP and A2A on a timeline.</td><td>Explain what an AI or agent gateway adds to an API gateway, and choose a style by requirement.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#graphql">GraphQL</a></td><td>Read a schema and write queries with variables; explain resolvers.</td><td>Batching, federation, schema evolution, GraphQL security.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#realtime">Real-time to the Browser</a></td><td>Explain real time versus near real time; use SSE or a WebSocket from a browser.</td><td>Kafka-to-browser fan-out, reconnect and replay, and choosing a transport by requirement.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#kafka">Kafka</a></td><td>Explain partitions, offsets and consumer groups; read consumer lag.</td><td>Topic and key design, delivery guarantees, Connect and stream processing.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#payments">Payment Gateways</a></td><td>Explain the payment lifecycle and the server-verified checkout flow.</td><td>Idempotency, webhooks, ledgers, reconciliation, subscriptions and compliance.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#banking">Banking &amp; Low Latency</a></td><td>Explain how a bank's systems fit together, and what instant payments changed.</td><td>Latency budgets, in-memory and lock-free designs, kernel bypass, and measuring without coordinated omission.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#spring">Spring Boot</a></td><td>Add an endpoint with validation and a test; read <code>application.yml</code>.</td><td>Reactive vs servlet trade-offs, transactions, observability, the 3 → 4 migration.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#quarkus">Quarkus</a></td><td>Map what you know from Spring onto it (the translation table).</td><td>Native images, Mutiny, build-time extensions.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#faded">Faded? Rails, Flex &amp; Grails</a></td><td>Say what Rails, Flex and Grails introduced, and where each stands now.</td><td>Decide whether to keep, upgrade or replace an application on an older framework, by requirement.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#apps">Apps: Native or Cross-platform</a></td><td>Explain native, shared-logic, cross-platform and web options.</td><td>Choose an app architecture for a product and team, and plan how it evolves.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#testing">Testing</a></td><td>Write a unit test that fails before a fix and passes after; run the suite; read a CI failure.</td><td>Test strategy across levels, Testcontainers, property-based and load tests, pipeline and AI testing.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#teststrat">Test Strategy for Cloud Systems</a></td><td>Say what to test locally, in CI, in a cloud environment and in production.</td><td>Use contracts, containers, emulators, short-lived environments and canaries by risk.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#review">Writing &amp; Code Review</a></td><td>Write a clear commit message and pull request description; read review comments as being about the code.</td><td>Review changes promptly and kindly, write decision records and design proposals, and set a team's conventions.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#performance">Performance</a></td><td>Read latency percentiles; spot N+1 queries and missing indexes.</td><td>Profiling, load testing, capacity planning and cost-performance trade-offs.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#security">Security</a></td><td>Parameterise queries, check object ownership, keep secrets out of code, review a PR against the checklist.</td><td>Threat modelling, identity and OAuth design, cloud IAM and policies, supply-chain security, incident response.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#pki">Keys, Certificates &amp; SSH</a></td><td>Explain key pairs, certificates and CAs; make a CSR and an SSH key.</td><td>Read and fix chain, name and expiry errors; automate renewal; sign commits.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#privacy">Personal Data Across Services</a></td><td>Say where personal data spreads in a system, and what tokenisation and pseudonymisation do.</td><td>Design erasure across services, crypto-shredding, retention conflicts and data residency.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#cyber">Cybersecurity</a></td><td>Know how attacks unfold (MITRE ATT&amp;CK) and report incidents fast.</td><td>Detection engineering, incident response and red/blue/purple teaming.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#agentsec">Agentic AI Security</a></td><td>Explain prompt injection and excessive agency; name the OWASP risks.</td><td>Design agent tools with least privilege, approvals and sandboxing; test with attacks.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#oauth">OAuth &amp; OIDC</a></td><td>Protect an API with JWT validation; use the code flow with PKCE through a library.</td><td>Identity architecture, flow choice per client, token lifetimes, advanced specs.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#data">Data Engineering</a></td><td>Explain the lifecycle, the layers, and why pipelines must be idempotent.</td><td>Ingestion patterns, modelling, quality and governance across a whole platform.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#datalibs">Data Libraries</a></td><td>Process a file with pandas or Polars; read Parquet; match library to data size.</td><td>Engine choice, pushdown and file layout, distributed and streaming processing.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#dbt">dbt</a></td><td>Find a model, run it with its tests, read the compiled SQL.</td><td>Incremental strategies, unit tests and contracts, macros, slim CI.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#dags">Airflow DAGs</a></td><td>Read a DAG, find a failed task's log, rerun one date.</td><td>Idempotent design, data intervals, sensors and deferrables, dynamic mapping, upgrades.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#patterns">SOLID &amp; Design Patterns</a></td><td>Name the five SOLID principles and spot the smell each one fixes.</td><td>Choose a pattern by the problem in front of you, and know when not to.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#architecture">Architecture</a></td><td>Tell choreography from orchestration; explain a saga and the outbox pattern.</td><td>Designing event-driven systems, consistency trade-offs, resilience patterns.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#atob">Solution Architecture: A to B</a></td><td>Tell business, solution and enterprise architecture apart; label work strategic, tactical or temporary.</td><td>Plan baseline, transition and target states; transitional patterns and their removal.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#distsys">Distributed Systems</a></td><td>Explain the fallacies, CAP and quorums, and read a database's guarantees.</td><td>Reason about clocks, consensus and split brain; test with seeded simulation.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#microservices">Microservices</a></td><td>Explain when to split and why services own data; work within one service.</td><td>Service boundaries, resilience, sagas and read models, monolith migration.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#casestudies">Case Studies: How Tech Companies Evolved</a></td><td>Tell the story of how companies such as Yahoo, eBay, Amazon, Facebook, Netflix and GitHub changed their engineering, and why.</td><td>Draw lessons from published case studies, and say where they would not apply to your organisation.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#eventsourcing">Event Sourcing</a></td><td>Explain events, aggregates, projections and CQRS.</td><td>Event modelling and versioning, projections, snapshots, when to use it.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#sysdesign">System Design</a></td><td>Walk through the method for a small system: requirements, estimates, API, data, a diagram, trade-offs.</td><td>Capacity planning, caching and partitioning strategy, failure design, writing design documents others build from.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#specs">Specifications</a></td><td>Read RFC 2119 key words; find the spec behind a format you use daily.</td><td>Choose an interface description by requirement; write specs others build from.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#caching">Caching</a></td><td>Explain cache-aside, TTLs and hit ratio; read HTTP cache headers.</td><td>Multi-layer caching, invalidation, stampedes, what never to cache.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#devops">DevOps</a></td><td>Follow the Git flow, read a CI run, roll back a release.</td><td>Pipeline design, release strategies, SLOs and alerting, incident response, supply-chain security.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#kubernetes">Kubernetes</a></td><td>Use kubectl to find why a pod is not running.</td><td>Resource tuning, probes, NetworkPolicy and RBAC, Helm/Kustomize, GKE identity.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#k8sdr">Kubernetes DR &amp; Best Practices</a></td><td>Use replicas, probes, PDBs and zone spreading; know your RPO and RTO.</td><td>Backup and restore with etcd snapshots and Velero, DR strategies, restore drills.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#helm">Helm Charts</a></td><td>Install, upgrade and roll back a release; read a chart's values.</td><td>Chart design, library charts, hooks, testing, GitOps delivery.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#operators">Operators</a></td><td>Explain CRDs, controllers and reconcile; use well-known operators.</td><td>Building idempotent controllers, CRD versioning, operator testing.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#servicemesh">Service Mesh &amp; Istio</a></td><td>Explain what a mesh does; read Istio routing and policy resources.</td><td>mTLS rollout, authorisation, traffic shifting, ambient vs sidecar, mesh operations.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#terraform">Terraform</a></td><td>Read a plan and say what it will create, change or destroy.</td><td>Module design, state surgery with moved/import blocks, IAM patterns, tests.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#observability">Observability</a></td><td>Find logs and errors for a request; read a dashboard; log with IDs.</td><td>OpenTelemetry, metrics design, tracing, alerting and telemetry cost.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#sre">SRE</a></td><td>Explain SLOs and error budgets; follow a runbook during an incident.</td><td>SLO design, burn-rate alerting, incident command, postmortems, toil.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#cloudinside">How the cloud is built</a></td><td>Explain zones, regions, VMs, containers and the control plane.</td><td>Failure domains, isolation, shared responsibility and cost from first principles.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#cloudcompare">GCP vs AWS vs Azure</a></td><td>Map common services across the three clouds; know their account structures.</td><td>Provider choice, portability, IAM and networking differences, data residency.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#cloudcost">Cloud Cost</a></td><td>Read a cloud bill, tag what you run, and switch off what is idle.</td><td>Choose commitments, spot capacity and storage classes by requirement, and design with cost from the start.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#retailcloud">Retail &amp; Hybrid Cloud</a></td><td>Name the systems a retailer connects, and the events and APIs between them.</td><td>When on premises and a second cloud pay off, by requirement, with their costs.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#gcpdata">Data on GCP</a></td><td>Name the service for each data stage; load and query data on GCP.</td><td>Platform design across Dataflow, Pub/Sub, Datastream, Dataplex; cost and governance.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#bigquery">BigQuery</a></td><td>Write a correct, cheap query: named columns, partition filter, dry run first.</td><td>Table design (partitioning, clustering), MERGE patterns, cost analysis from INFORMATION_SCHEMA.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#cloudrun">Cloud Run</a></td><td>Deploy a revision, roll it back, read its logs, execute a job.</td><td>Scaling and cost settings, VPC egress, auth between services, job sharding.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#apigee">Apigee</a></td><td>Explain proxies, policies and products; call an API through Apigee.</td><td>Proxy and product design, shared flows, edge security, Apigee in CI and Terraform.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#awsdata">Data on AWS</a></td><td>Name the AWS service for each data stage; query S3 data with Athena.</td><td>Lake design with Glue, Lake Formation, Redshift and streaming; IAM and cost.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#azuredata">Data on Azure</a></td><td>Name the Azure service for each data stage; land files in Data Lake Storage.</td><td>Fabric or separate services, roles and ACLs together, tiers and cost.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#neural">Neural Networks</a></td><td>Explain a neuron, layers, weights and activation functions.</td><td>Explain loss, backpropagation and gradient descent, and choose an architecture by requirement.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#llm">LLMs</a></td><td>Explain how LLMs work and their limits; make a structured call.</td><td>Runtimes, self-hosting, agent harnesses, model strategy.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#ai">AI</a></td><td>Call a model with structured output, explain RAG, spot prompt injection, review AI-written code.</td><td>Retrieval design, evals, agents and tools, cost and latency tuning, AI security.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#rag">RAG</a></td><td>Explain the RAG phases; run a vector search; prompt with citations.</td><td>Chunking, hybrid search, reranking, evaluation and security for retrieval.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#mcp">MCP</a></td><td>Add an MCP server to Claude Code; explain tools and resources.</td><td>Building and deploying MCP servers; MCP around data pipelines.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#agentharness">Agent Harness</a></td><td>Explain the parts of a harness; configure a ready-made one.</td><td>Building bounded, gated, evaluated harnesses.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#blueprints">Frameworks vs AI</a></td><td>Use team blueprints; explain why owning generated code is the real cost.</td><td>Design and run frameworks; codemods; build-vs-generate decisions.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#claudetools">Claude Code &amp; Cowork</a></td><td>Run Claude Code in a repo; write a CLAUDE.md; review its changes.</td><td>Hooks, skills, subagents, gated agent workflows, Cowork plugins.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#copilot">GitHub Copilot</a></td><td>Use completions and chat; follow repo instructions.</td><td>Instructions, coding agent, policies, consistency with other agents.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#agentsgcp">Agents on GCP</a></td><td>Run one agent task as a Cloud Run job with its own service account.</td><td>Workspaces, triggers, approvals and guard rails for agents in your own project.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#harness">Harness</a></td><td>Run a pipeline, read its logs, approve or roll back.</td><td>Org-wide templates and policies, verified progressive delivery.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#opstools">Ops Tooling</a></td><td>Read Grafana or Dynatrace dashboards; work a ServiceNow incident.</td><td>PromQL and alert rules, log shipping, alert-to-incident and change automation.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#mathematics">Mathematics</a></td><td>Read Big-O; use percentiles over averages; know why floats are wrong for money.</td><td>Complexity, probability, statistics and linear algebra applied with rigour.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#science">Science &amp; Method</a></td><td>Reproduce, change one thing, measure before and after.</td><td>Falsifiable hypotheses, controlled experiments, reading research critically.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#philosophy">Philosophy</a></td><td>Ask how we know; spot fallacies; know the codes of ethics.</td><td>Systems thinking, ethical trade-offs, wise judgement under uncertainty.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#goodhuman">Being a Good Human</a></td><td>Honesty, kindness, gratitude; look after your health.</td><td>Psychological safety, mentoring, integrity under pressure.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#body">Body, Mind &amp; Nature</a></td><td>Sleep, move and take breaks; notice stress in your body.</td><td>Sustainable pace for a team; humane on-call; greener systems.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#quantum">Quantum Computing</a></td><td>Explain qubits, superposition and why Shor's algorithm threatens RSA.</td><td>Post-quantum migration: inventory, crypto agility, ML-KEM and ML-DSA.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#robotics">Robotics</a></td><td>Explain sense-think-act and tune a PID loop in simulation.</td><td>Kinematics, SLAM and planning on ROS 2, with safety designed in.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#bitcoin">Bitcoin &amp; Blockchain</a></td><td>Explain hashes, linked blocks, proof of work and keys.</td><td>Consensus trade-offs, key custody, and when a blockchain is the wrong tool.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#web3">Web 3.0</a></td><td>Tell Web3 from the Semantic Web; know when a blockchain is not needed.</td><td>Smart contracts, linked data, knowledge graphs, legal data standards.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#solid">Solid</a></td><td>Explain Pods, WebIDs and app–data separation.</td><td>Solid apps, access control, consent-based data sharing.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="#culvert">Culvert</a></td><td>Explain contracts and adapters; install and use an adapter.</td><td>Cloud-neutral pipeline design, adapters, job control and orchestration gates.</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Self-check: the foundations</h3>
    <p>You should be able to do all of these without asking:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Explain what a query will cost before you run it.</li>
      <li>Read a stack trace or a job log and name the line that failed.</li>
      <li>Say why a service cannot reach another: DNS, firewall, or auth.</li>
      <li>Read a Terraform plan and spot a destroy or a replace.</li>
      <li>Write a test that fails before your fix and passes after it.</li>
      <li>Roll back a bad deploy.</li>
      <li>Get a shell in a running container and find why it exited.</li>
      <li>Say what a prompt-based feature returns when the model is down, and how you would know.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Self-check: AI</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Return validated JSON from a model instead of parsing free text.</li>
      <li>Build a small eval set and show a change improved the score.</li>
      <li>Explain how retrieved documents could smuggle instructions into a prompt, and how you limit the damage.</li>
      <li>Estimate the monthly cost of a feature from tokens per call and calls per day.</li>
      <li>Reject an AI-suggested change because a test or the docs show it is wrong.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Self-check: depth in data</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Design a partitioned, clustered table for a given query pattern.</li>
      <li>Choose between merge, insert_overwrite and microbatch for a dbt model, and explain why.</li>
      <li>Make a DAG rerun for one date without duplicating data.</li>
      <li>Find rows a parse silently turned into NULL, and stop it happening again.</li>
      <li>Explain a BigQuery bill from INFORMATION_SCHEMA.JOBS.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Self-check: depth in platform</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Write the Terraform for a Cloud Run job, its service account, its IAM and its schedule.</li>
      <li>Give a service a fixed egress IP and private access to Cloud SQL.</li>
      <li>Ship a canary revision and move traffic in steps.</li>
      <li>Size memory and CPU for a JVM container and justify the numbers.</li>
      <li>Debug a CrashLoopBackOff or a "failed to listen on PORT" from logs alone.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> this page describes the guide itself; each topic page lists its own sources.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<section class="panel" id="p-aiworry" data-name="Worried about AI?">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Worried about AI?</h2><p>Many people are, and many more do not yet know what is changing. A calm way to prepare: use what AI gives us, and keep the skills that let a person step in when it fails.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>The tills are a good picture</h3>
    <p>Supermarkets once had a long row of tills, each with a person behind it. Over the years most have moved to a few staffed tills and a bank of self-service ones. Fewer people do the scanning. But the shop still needs people: someone approves an age check, clears a jammed scanner, helps the customer who is stuck, and knows what to do when the system goes down.</p>
    <p>Software is going the same way. Fewer people will type routine code by hand, but people are still needed to manage the work, and managing it well takes a good understanding of what is being built. The people who stay most useful are the ones who understand what the machines are doing, and can take over when they stop.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>We have been here before: CAD and CAM</h3>
    <p>Engineering design went through this once already. Drawings used to be made by hand at drawing boards, by teams of draughtsmen. Then computers took over the drawing and the machining:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>1940s and 1950s:</b> the first numerically controlled machine tools follow points fed into them, instead of a person guiding every cut.</li>
      <li><b>1963:</b> Ivan Sutherland writes Sketchpad for his PhD thesis, an early program for drawing on a computer screen.</li>
      <li><b>1982:</b> AutoCAD is released for desktop computers, and computer-aided design (CAD) spreads to ordinary offices.</li>
      <li><b>Computer-aided manufacturing (CAM)</b> then turns those designs into instructions for the machines that cut and shape the parts.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The drawing board disappeared, and many drafting jobs changed or went. But engineering did not end: it grew. The people who did well learned the new tools and kept the judgement the tools could not supply: whether a part would be strong enough, whether it could be made, whether it was safe. CAD made drawing faster; it never decided what was worth drawing.</p>
    <p>AI is the same kind of shift for writing code and documents, faster and wider. The lesson carries over: learn the tool, keep the understanding, and own the decisions.</p>
    <p class="sources"><b>Checked against</b> (Wikipedia): <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Computer_numerical_control" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Computer numerical control</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sketchpad" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sketchpad</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AutoCAD" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AutoCAD</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Computer-aided_design" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Computer-aided design</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Computer-aided_manufacturing" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Computer-aided manufacturing</a>.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>The risk of a team that can only prompt</h3>
    <p>Picture a team whose only skill is asking an AI to do the work. Everything is automated. Then something fails, and the only thing they know how to do is ask the AI to fix it. If the AI is wrong, unavailable, or part of what failed, nobody in the room can help.</p>
    <p>We have seen what large automated failures look like. On 19 July 2024 a faulty update to a widely used security product crashed about 8.5 million Windows computers, by Microsoft's estimate, and many had to be repaired by hand, one machine at a time. People with hands-on skills were the way out.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Keep a hand on the controls</h3>
    <p>The Boeing 737 MAX accidents of October 2018 and March 2019 carry a lesson worth remembering. Official investigations found that a new automated flight-control feature, MCAS, contributed to both crashes, and references to it had been removed from the pilots' flight manual. When it misbehaved, the crews did not have what they needed to take back control.</p>
    <p>Agents that heal themselves are useful. But every automated system needs a <b>documented manual override</b>, and people who have <b>practised</b> using it. In an emergency, a person should be able to step in and fix things directly, not only through another agent.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>We will use it more than we meant to</h3>
    <p>Nobody knows for sure where this goes. But rightly or wrongly, most of us will end up using AI more than we intended. That can be good or bad.</p>
    <p><i>My view:</i> it is good for someone who has the idea and knows the specification but could never find the hundreds of hours to build it. For people with that experience, it is a genuinely useful tool, and solo builders who want to make a product should start: doing something beats waiting. It is bad when it becomes a habit of prompting and never going back to check. A mind that stops checking slowly stops being able to.</p>
    <p>So use the time it gives back well. If AI saves you hours, spend some of them reading the foundation books you never had time for.</p>
    <p class="note"><i>The other view:</i> some argue that building with AI before you understand the foundations produces products you cannot maintain or debug, and that learning first is the better start, even if it means waiting.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>More with fewer, or more with the same</h3>
    <p>Come back to the bulldozer. Companies may expect the same work from fewer engineers, or more work from the same number. We can already see some reduction across the industry, and there will be disruption, though no one can say yet how much.</p>
    <p>The people who come through it best will be the ones who can direct the machine and still understand the ground it is moving.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Seven ways people use AI</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>To automate without learning.</b> Fast today, fragile tomorrow: when it breaks, nothing was learned.</li>
      <li><b>To learn.</b> Ask it to explain, then check the explanation against the docs.</li>
      <li><b>As a tester and reviewer.</b> Ask it to find the cases you missed, try to break your code, and review your writing, designs and pull requests.</li>
      <li><b>As a tutor and mentor.</b> Ask it to quiz you, review your reasoning, and say what to read next.</li>
      <li><b>To explore and research.</b> Summarise a long document, compare options, brainstorm, draft something new; then check what comes back against the real sources.</li>
      <li><b>As a builder.</b> Use it to build what you design and understand: frameworks, tools, products, reusable blueprints for your team. You set the architecture and the standards; AI speeds up the making. See <a href="#blueprints">Frameworks vs AI</a>.</li>
      <li><b>To automate, with oversight.</b> Let it, or an agent, run routine work you understand. Watch what it does, keep the logs, and keep the off switch. See "Keep a hand on the controls" above and <a href="#agentharness">Agent Harness</a>.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Only the first makes you weaker; the rest make you stronger, as long as you stay the one who understands.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>You may not read every line, but you must understand it and test it</h3>
    <p>With AI writing more of the code, we may not read every line it produces. That can be acceptable, on two conditions: we understand most of it, how it is shaped, what it depends on, and where it can fail; and we can <b>test all of it in the right way</b>, even the lines we did not read. Tests, types, reviews and monitoring become how we know the code works. See <a href="#testing">Testing</a> and <a href="#observability">Observability</a>.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Every role is changing, not only engineering</h3>
    <p>In a large company the change reaches everyone: business analysts, journey managers, product owners, software engineers, solution architects, enterprise architects, data engineers, data analysts, testers, and engineers who have spent careers on mainframes and are now learning cloud and AI. None of us is finished learning, whatever the job title says. Many people are worried simply because they do not yet know what is changing. There will be work, in some capacity, for people who are ready to use AI in the right way.</p>
    <p>What helps most is not one course or one tool. It is the <b>will to adapt</b>: understanding what is changing, trying it, and changing how we work. It will be hard, and some days it will feel slow. Learning one small thing every day still adds up: a command, a concept, a page of this guide, a question asked out loud.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Analysts, journey managers and product owners:</b> learn enough of how AI works to write requirements it can meet, and to spot where it should not be trusted. Your knowledge of customers and rules is what checks its answers.</li>
      <li><b>Software engineers:</b> AI can write more of the code, so the job moves towards design, review, testing and running it in production. Keep the foundations sharp enough to judge what it produced. See <a href="#testing">Testing</a> and <a href="#claudetools">Claude Code &amp; Cowork</a>.</li>
      <li><b>Solution architects:</b> AI becomes another component to design around: where it sits, what data it may see, how it fails, what it costs, and where a person must stay in the loop. See <a href="#architecture">Architecture</a> and <a href="#agentharness">Agent Harness</a>.</li>
      <li><b>Enterprise architects:</b> set the guard rails across the organisation: which models and tools are approved, how data is governed, how agents are given access, and how the estate changes without a big-bang rewrite. See <a href="#blueprints">Frameworks vs AI</a>.</li>
      <li><b>Data engineers and analysts:</b> AI is only as good as the data it reads. Quality, lineage and access control matter more, not less. See <a href="#data">Data Engineering</a>.</li>
      <li><b>Engineers coming from mainframes and older platforms:</b> what you know about reliability, batch, transactions and running systems for decades is exactly what new platforms often lack. Bring it with you; learn the new tools on top of it.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Whatever your role</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Learn what it is and is not.</b> AI predicts likely text; it can be fluent and wrong. <a href="#ai">AI for Engineers</a> and <a href="#llm">LLMs</a> are written plainly.</li>
      <li><b>Use it on your own work, and check it.</b> You learn its strengths and its mistakes fastest on things you already know well.</li>
      <li><b>Keep your domain knowledge.</b> Knowing your customers, your trade and your rules is what tells you when an answer is wrong.</li>
      <li><b>Invest in people skills.</b> Explaining, listening and deciding together are hard to automate. See <a href="#goodhuman">Being a Good Human</a>.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>If you are an engineer</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Be able to fix it without the agent.</b> Read the logs, follow a request through the system, roll back a release. <a href="#linux">Linux</a>, <a href="#networking">Networking</a> and <a href="#observability">Observability</a> are where that starts.</li>
      <li><b>Write runbooks a person can follow</b> at 3 a.m., and keep them next to the automation they cover. See <a href="#sre">SRE</a>.</li>
      <li><b>Build the off switch.</b> Every agent gets a kill switch, a manual fallback and limits on what it may touch. See <a href="#agentharness">Agent Harness</a> and <a href="#agentsgcp">Agents on GCP</a>.</li>
      <li><b>Practise the bad day.</b> Rehearse an outage with the automation turned off, so taking over is a habit, not a hope.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Keep your own memory</h3>
    <p>As AI does more of the work, what you have learned (how your systems behave, why decisions were made, what worked and what did not) becomes more valuable, not less. Keep it in a form you own, so it stays useful to you over the years and goes with you from job to job.</p>
    <p>That is what we are building with <a href="/headworks/">Headworks</a>, Penstock and Cistern running together: a self-hosted AI agent that runs on your own machine with a model you pick. Its memory is plain files you own and can read, change or delete, and it reads only the data you choose to share, with a receipt for every read. What you build up stays yours, and you decide what to share with others. See <a href="#opensource">Our open source</a>.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>A journey, not a fight</h3>
    <p>When change feels threatening, the body reacts as if to danger: the fight-or-flight response. It helps in a real emergency and hurts in a long change, because a mind in that state reacts instead of thinking. The change AI brings will take years, not days, so treat it as a journey rather than a fight. It is a journey, and we are in competition with no one: not our colleagues, not the machines.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Step back from the headlines.</b> Ask what is actually changing in your own work this month, not what the loudest voice predicts.</li>
      <li><b>Learn something new every day.</b> One thing learned each day compounds; <i>Atomic Habits</i> calls it getting about 1% better each day. See the <a href="#reading">Reading list</a>.</li>
      <li><b>Look after the basics.</b> Sleep, movement and time away from screens keep you calm enough to think. See <a href="#body">Body, Mind &amp; Nature</a>.</li>
      <li><b>Keep doing the work.</b> Good things happen to people who are ready and keep going, day after day, long after the headlines move on.</li>
      <li><b>Think above the ordinary.</b> When everyone is anxious, the person who stays calm, learns steadily and helps others becomes the one people turn to.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Understanding before opinion</h3>
    <p>Every day brings millions of posts about AI, and everyone has an opinion. We have come to find more satisfaction in expressing a view than in understanding the thing itself, and we miss the experience of learning it.</p>
    <p>That is why this guide is a quiet, static site: no feed, no comments, no tracking. Come, find a section, read it in peace, try the code, and save it as a PDF if you like. Understanding first; the opinion can wait.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Believe in yourself</h3>
    <blockquote class="quote">"When you are inspired by some great purpose, some extraordinary project, all your thoughts break their bonds: Your mind transcends limitations, your consciousness expands in every direction, and you find yourself in a new, great and wonderful world. Dormant forces, faculties and talents become alive, and you discover yourself to be a greater person by far than you ever dreamed yourself to be."<br><span class="quote-by">A passage often attributed to Patanjali</span></blockquote>
    <p>Whoever wrote it, the idea is true: the limits we feel are often limits in how we see ourselves. In times of change, belief in yourself is what lets you start learning something new instead of waiting to be told you can.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Belief is built, not found.</b> The psychologist Albert Bandura called it self-efficacy: your belief that you can succeed at a particular task. He found the most effective way to build it is mastery experiences, small successes of your own.</li>
      <li><b>So start small and finish.</b> Run one command, fix one bug, read one chapter. Each thing you complete is evidence you can do the next.</li>
      <li><b>Watch your self-image.</b> "I am not technical" or "I am too old to learn this" become true only if you act on them. <i>Psycho-Cybernetics</i> and <i>As a Man Thinketh</i> on the <a href="#reading">Reading list</a> are about exactly this.</li>
      <li><b>Find a purpose bigger than the worry.</b> As the passage says, a purpose you care about wakes up abilities you did not know you had.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Self-efficacy" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Self-efficacy (Wikipedia)</a>.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Harness it, and keep control</h3>
    <p>Like every technology before it, AI is worth using for what it gives us, and worth controlling for what it can break. Use it every day. Keep the foundations, keep the manual override, and keep people who know how to use both.</p>
  </article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= IDEA TO PRODUCTION ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-idea2prod" data-name="Idea to Production">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>From idea to production, with people and agents</h2><p>Taking an idea all the way to something people use in production is the whole job, and AI agents now take part in every step of it. This page walks the path once, stage by stage: what each stage is for, what people own, how agents can help, and which page of this guide goes deeper. It ends with two views on living with the change, and on the foundations that keep you useful through it.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>The short answer</h3>
    <p><i>Our view:</i> the path from idea to production has not changed in shape: find out whether the idea is worth building, build it in small steps, check it, release it safely, run it, and learn. What has changed is how much of the building agents can do. That moves people&rsquo;s value towards the ends of the path, deciding what is worth doing and judging whether it worked, and towards the foundations that let you check an agent&rsquo;s work. <i>The other view</i> is in the cards below: some expect the division of work, and even the shape of the path, to keep shifting.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>The path</h3>
    <figure class="fig"><svg viewBox="0 0 640 250" role="img" aria-labelledby="ip-t ip-d"><title id="ip-t">From idea to production, and back</title><desc id="ip-d">Eight stages in two rows joined by arrows. Top row: idea, test the idea, specify, build. Bottom row, right to left: verify, release, operate, learn. An arrow from learn returns to idea, so the path is a loop. A note says people decide at every stage and agents can help at every stage.</desc><defs><marker id="ip-a" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker><marker id="ip-aa" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar acc" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker></defs><rect class="nd in" x="30" y="30" width="120" height="40" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="90.0" y="55" text-anchor="middle">Idea</text><rect class="nd" x="186" y="30" width="120" height="40" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="246.0" y="55" text-anchor="middle">Test the idea</text><rect class="nd" x="342" y="30" width="120" height="40" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="402.0" y="55" text-anchor="middle">Specify</text><rect class="nd" x="498" y="30" width="120" height="40" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="558.0" y="55" text-anchor="middle">Build</text><rect class="nd in" x="30" y="150" width="120" height="40" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="90.0" y="175" text-anchor="middle">Learn</text><rect class="nd" x="186" y="150" width="120" height="40" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="246.0" y="175" text-anchor="middle">Operate</text><rect class="nd" x="342" y="150" width="120" height="40" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="402.0" y="175" text-anchor="middle">Release</text><rect class="nd" x="498" y="150" width="120" height="40" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="558.0" y="175" text-anchor="middle">Verify</text><line class="ed" x1="150" y1="50" x2="182" y2="50" marker-end="url(#ip-a)"/><line class="ed" x1="186" y1="170" x2="154" y2="170" marker-end="url(#ip-a)"/><line class="ed" x1="306" y1="50" x2="338" y2="50" marker-end="url(#ip-a)"/><line class="ed" x1="342" y1="170" x2="310" y2="170" marker-end="url(#ip-a)"/><line class="ed" x1="462" y1="50" x2="494" y2="50" marker-end="url(#ip-a)"/><line class="ed" x1="498" y1="170" x2="466" y2="170" marker-end="url(#ip-a)"/><line class="ed" x1="558.0" y1="70" x2="558.0" y2="146" marker-end="url(#ip-a)"/><line class="ed acc" x1="90.0" y1="150" x2="90.0" y2="74" marker-end="url(#ip-aa)"/><text class="lb acc" x="98.0" y="116" text-anchor="start">feedback</text><text class="lb mu" x="320" y="225" text-anchor="middle">People decide at every stage; agents can help at every stage.</text></svg><figcaption>The path drawn as a loop: production is where you learn whether the idea was right, and that feeds the next idea. The stage names are ours (<i>analysis</i>); the practices at each stage are sourced in the table below.</figcaption></figure>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Each stage: people, agents, and where to read more</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>What it is for, and a sourced practice</th><th>People own (<i>our view</i>)</th><th>Agents can help (<i>our view</i>)</th><th>Read more</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td><b>Idea</b></td><td>A problem worth solving, for someone in particular (<i>our view</i>)</td><td>Choosing the problem, and who it is for</td><td>Research, summaries, first drafts of options</td><td><a href="#designux">Design Thinking &amp; UX</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Test the idea</b></td><td>A <b>minimum viable product</b>: a version with just enough features for early customers to use and give feedback on, so you can potentially avoid long and possibly unnecessary work</td><td>Deciding what counts as evidence, and whether it is enough</td><td>Building a throwaway prototype quickly</td><td><a href="#casestudies">Case Studies</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Specify</b></td><td>Write down what &ldquo;done&rdquo; means before building, so others, and agents, can build from it (<i>our view</i>)</td><td>The acceptance criteria and the trade-offs</td><td>Turning notes into a draft spec and test cases</td><td><a href="#specs">Specifications</a>, <a href="#ways">Ways of Working</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Build</b></td><td><b>Continuous delivery:</b> produce software in short cycles so it can be released reliably at any time</td><td>The design, and the boundaries agents work within</td><td>Writing code and tests to a clear brief</td><td><a href="#humanagents">Humans &amp; Agents</a>, <a href="#sysdesign">System Design</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Verify</b></td><td>Tests at the right level, and review by someone who did not write the change (<i>our view</i>; see the linked pages for sources)</td><td>Understanding and approving what ships</td><td>Running checks, reviewing, finding missed cases</td><td><a href="#teststrat">Test Strategy</a>, <a href="#review">Writing &amp; Code Review</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Release</b></td><td><b>Feature toggles</b> let unfinished work merge and be switched on in production for some or all users; the launch checklist in Google&rsquo;s SRE book asks about canaries under live traffic and staged rollouts</td><td>When to release, and to whom</td><td>Preparing release notes and rollout steps</td><td><a href="#devops">DevOps</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Operate</b></td><td>Before launch, the same SRE-book checklist asks what happens when a machine, rack or data centre fails, how the service is monitored, and whether there are backups, so operating it is planned before it starts</td><td>On-call judgement, and talking to users</td><td>Watching signals, drafting incident timelines</td><td><a href="#sre">SRE</a>, <a href="#observability">Observability</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Learn</b></td><td>Measure delivery: DORA uses change lead time, deployment frequency, failed deployment recovery time, change fail rate and deployment rework rate</td><td>Deciding what the evidence means for the next idea</td><td>Gathering the numbers and the feedback</td><td><a href="#devops">DevOps</a> (measuring delivery)</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note">The practices in the second column are sourced (below), except where marked as our view; the &ldquo;people own&rdquo; and &ldquo;agents can help&rdquo; columns are our view, and how much you hand to agents is a choice by requirement on <a href="#humanagents">Humans &amp; Agents</a>.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>A checklist that has lasted</h3>
    <p>Google&rsquo;s SRE book prints the company&rsquo;s original Launch Coordination Checklist, from about 2005. Its headings are still a fair list of what production asks of an idea: architecture; machines and data centres; volume, capacity and performance; reliability and failover; monitoring; security; automation and manual tasks; growth; external dependencies; and schedule and rollout. The book says checklists like it are used to reduce failure and ensure consistency, as in aviation and surgery.</p>
    <p class="note"><i>Analysis:</i> an agent can work through such a list quickly; deciding which answers are good enough for your users is still a person&rsquo;s call.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>One ecosystem, not two camps</h3>
    <p><i>Our view:</i> people and agents work best as one system with clear roles: people set direction, own decisions and stay accountable; agents do well-briefed work quickly and report what they did and how they checked it. The guide&rsquo;s <a href="#humanagents">Humans &amp; Agents</a> page has how to brief an agent and how it should report back.</p>
    <p><i>The other view:</i> some argue roles will keep moving towards agents, so drawing fixed lines now may age badly; on that view the skill that matters most is adapting the division of work as tools change.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>In harmony with what is happening</h3>
    <p><i>Our view:</i> the change is real and it is not stopping, and fighting it costs energy that learning would use better. Being in harmony with it does not mean accepting everything: it means seeing clearly what is changing, keeping what still matters, and choosing where to put your effort. The guide&rsquo;s <a href="#philosophy">Philosophy</a> page has more on nothing being permanent, and on being at peace with what happens; <a href="#aiworry">Worried about AI?</a> has more on the worry itself.</p>
    <p><i>The other view:</i> some changes deserve resistance, not harmony: when a tool is unsafe, unfair or simply wrong for the job, saying so is part of the work, and acceptance can become passivity.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Upskilling on foundations</h3>
    <p><i>Our view:</i> when agents write more of the code, the foundations matter more, not less: you cannot judge an agent&rsquo;s database migration without understanding transactions, or its retry logic without understanding failure. The foundations change slowly; the tools on top of them change every year. That is why this guide starts where it does, and why <a href="#start">Start here</a> argues for being the site manager rather than a &ldquo;vibe coder&rdquo;.</p>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>If you can&rsquo;t yet&hellip;</th><th>Start with</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Explain what happens when two requests change the same data at once</td><td><a href="#concurrency">Concurrency</a>, <a href="#database">Database Design</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Say why a call to another service can be slow or fail</td><td><a href="#networking">Networking</a>, <a href="#distsys">Distributed Systems</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Judge whether an agent&rsquo;s tests prove anything</td><td><a href="#testing">Testing</a>, <a href="#teststrat">Test Strategy</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Choose where data should live</td><td><a href="#datastore">Choosing a Data Store</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Read what an agent changed and explain it to a colleague</td><td><a href="#review">Writing &amp; Code Review</a></td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note"><i>The other view:</i> some argue that, as tools improve, more people will build useful software without deep foundations, and that learning on the job, with an agent explaining as it goes, can replace much up-front study.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Minimum_viable_product" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Minimum viable product</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Continuous_delivery" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Continuous delivery</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Feature_toggle" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Feature toggle</a>; <a href="https://sre.google/sre-book/reliable-product-launches/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google SRE book: Reliable Product Launches at Scale</a>; <a href="https://sre.google/sre-book/launch-checklist/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google SRE book: Launch Coordination Checklist</a>; <a href="https://dora.dev/guides/dora-metrics/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">DORA: software delivery performance metrics</a>.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= LINUX ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-electronics" data-name="Electronics &amp; Chips">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Electronics and chips: the bottom of the stack</h2><p>Every line of code runs on transistors. Understanding how a chip is built, and how one could be designed, keeps the foundations open to everyone rather than to a few companies.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Why this belongs in a software guide</h3>
    <p>A new generation of engineers needs the whole foundation, not only the top layers. Today a small number of companies design and make most of the world's advanced chips: TSMC alone accounts for roughly 70% of the global foundry market. When knowledge sits in so few places, everyone else depends on them for every device, data centre and AI model.</p>
    <p>Learning electronics does not mean everyone builds a fab. It means more people understand what their software runs on, can repair and reuse hardware instead of throwing it away, and can take part in open hardware. That is how the foundations stay shared, and how technology becomes more sustainable.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>From switch to chip</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>The transistor</b> is an electrically controlled switch. The first, a point-contact transistor, was invented in 1947 at Bell Labs by John Bardeen, Walter Brattain and William Shockley, who shared the 1956 Nobel Prize in Physics.</li>
      <li><b>Logic gates</b> (AND, OR, NOT) are built from transistors; everything a computer does is built from gates.</li>
      <li><b>The integrated circuit</b> puts many transistors on one piece of semiconductor: credited to Jack Kilby (1958) and Robert Noyce (1959).</li>
      <li><b>Moore's law</b> is the observation that the number of transistors on a chip doubles about every two years, which drove decades of cheaper, faster computing.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>How chips are designed</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Hardware description languages</b> such as Verilog and VHDL describe circuits as code, which is then tested in simulation like software.</li>
      <li><b>FPGAs</b> are chips that can be reprogrammed after manufacture, so you can try your own digital designs on real hardware without making a chip.</li>
      <li><b>Instruction sets</b> define what a processor understands. <b>RISC-V</b> is a free and open standard instruction set, started at the University of California, Berkeley: anyone can design a RISC-V processor without paying royalties.</li>
      <li><b>Open manufacturing kits</b> exist too: SkyWater's 130 nm process design kit (SKY130) is available under the Apache License, so a chip design can be taken to real silicon with open tools.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Sustainability</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>E-waste is one of the fastest-growing waste streams:</b> about 62 million tonnes in 2022, of which only 22.3% was formally collected and recycled; it is projected to reach 82 million tonnes by 2030.</li>
      <li><b>Repair before replace.</b> The right-to-repair movement argues that people should be able to fix what they own. Engineers who understand electronics can design for repair and keep devices in use longer.</li>
      <li><b>Efficient software is green software.</b> Code that does less work needs less hardware and less power. See <a href="#performance">Performance</a> and <a href="#body">Body, Mind &amp; Nature</a>.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Where to start</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Learn basic circuits: voltage, current, resistance, and how a transistor switches.</li>
      <li>Build logic gates and a small adder in a free simulator, then in Verilog.</li>
      <li>Program a microcontroller board to read a sensor and drive a motor. See <a href="#robotics">Robotics</a>.</li>
      <li>Put a small design on an FPGA board, then a simple RISC-V core.</li>
      <li>Read how chips are made, and repair one device instead of replacing it.</li>
    </ol>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Explain voltage, current and how a transistor switches</li><li>Explain logic gates and how they add numbers</li><li>Explain what an instruction set is</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Build a microcontroller project with sensors and motors</li><li>Write and simulate a small design in Verilog or VHDL</li><li>Run a design on an FPGA board</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Design a simple processor or peripheral on an FPGA</li><li>Read datasheets and choose components for a product</li><li>Design hardware for repair and long life</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Lead hardware and software co-design</li><li>Take a design towards silicon with an open process kit</li><li>Shape open hardware and sustainability practice</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>Digital logic</li><li>Verilog and VHDL</li><li>FPGAs</li><li>RISC-V</li><li>Semiconductor manufacturing</li><li>E-waste and repair</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/RISC-V" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RISC-V (Wikipedia)</a><span class="lnote"> The open standard instruction set</span></li><li><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Field-programmable_gate_array" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Field-programmable gate array (Wikipedia)</a><span class="lnote"> Reprogrammable chips for learning digital design</span></li><li><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Semiconductor_device_fabrication" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Semiconductor device fabrication (Wikipedia)</a><span class="lnote"> How chips are made</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Transistor" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Transistor</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Integrated_circuit" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Integrated circuit</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moore%27s_law" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Moore's law</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Field-programmable_gate_array" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Field-programmable gate array</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hardware_description_language" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Hardware description language</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/RISC-V" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RISC-V</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SkyWater_Technology" target="_blank" rel="noopener">SkyWater Technology (SKY130 PDK)</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/TSMC" target="_blank" rel="noopener">TSMC (foundry share)</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Electronic_waste" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Electronic waste</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Right_to_repair" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Right to repair</a>.</p><p class="runcode">A question, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/enrichmeai.github.io/issues/new?title=%5BElectronics%20%26%20Chips%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>. Nothing here is tracked and there are no comments to store: questions live in the open, where the next learner can find the answer.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<section class="panel" id="p-hardware" data-name="How Computers Are Built">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>How computers are built</h2><p>From the first microprocessor to the laptop on your desk: instruction sets, how software talks to devices, sound and media, and how graphics chips built for games became the engines of AI. Start with <a href="#electronics">Electronics &amp; Chips</a>.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card"><h3>RISC and CISC</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>An instruction set</b> is the list of operations a processor understands: the contract between hardware and software.</li>
      <li><b>CISC (complex instruction set computer):</b> instructions can do several things at once, such as load from memory, calculate and store, with many addressing modes. x86, in most PCs and servers, is the best-known CISC family.</li>
      <li><b>RISC (reduced instruction set computer):</b> fewer, simpler instructions that each do one thing, so the chip can run them quickly and efficiently. The ideas came from projects such as IBM's 801 in the late 1970s and work at Berkeley and Stanford. ARM (originally Acorn RISC Machine, 1985) and RISC-V are RISC.</li>
      <li><b>Today the line is blurred:</b> modern x86 chips translate complex instructions into simpler internal steps, and RISC chips have grown richer. The practical difference now is mostly power efficiency, licensing and the software ecosystem.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Programming devices</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Microcontrollers</b> are small computers on one chip: processor, memory and inputs and outputs. They run washing machines, cars, sensors and robots.</li>
      <li><b>Firmware</b> is the software built into a device, usually stored in flash memory. You "flash" new firmware to update it.</li>
      <li><b>Device drivers</b> let the operating system control hardware: a driver turns "play this sound" or "print this page" into the device's own commands.</li>
      <li><b>Try it:</b> program a microcontroller board to blink a light, read a sensor and drive a motor. It teaches timing, memory limits and the hardware underneath every abstraction. See <a href="#robotics">Robotics</a>.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>A short CPU history</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <tr><th>When</th><th>What</th><th>Why it mattered</th></tr>
      <tr><td>1971</td><td>Intel 4004</td><td>Often called the first microprocessor: a whole processor on one chip.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>1978</td><td>Intel 8086</td><td>The start of x86, still the instruction set of most PCs and servers.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>1985</td><td>ARM (Acorn RISC Machine)</td><td>A RISC design that later came to power most phones and tablets.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2003</td><td>AMD64 (x86-64), first in AMD's Opteron</td><td>64-bit x86: far more memory, while running older 32-bit software.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Mid-2000s</td><td>Multi-core processors in ordinary PCs</td><td>Clock speeds stopped climbing fast, so chips added cores instead; software had to learn to do work in parallel. See <a href="#java">Java</a> threads.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2020</td><td>Apple M1</td><td>An ARM system on a chip in mainstream laptops: CPU, GPU and more on one chip with shared memory.</td></tr>
    </table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>How a modern laptop is built</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>System on a chip (SoC):</b> CPU cores, a graphics processor, AI and media engines and security functions on one chip, often with memory packaged right beside it and shared between them.</li>
      <li><b>Storage:</b> a solid-state drive on the PCI Express bus, spoken to with the NVMe protocol: no moving parts, far faster than a spinning disk.</li>
      <li><b>Power and heat:</b> the battery and cooling decide how hard the chip can work and for how long; thin laptops trade peak speed for quiet and battery life.</li>
      <li><b>Firmware first:</b> when you press the power button, firmware checks the hardware and starts the operating system.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Media and sound cards</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>MIDI</b> (standardised 1983) lets instruments and computers exchange notes and controls rather than recorded sound.</li>
      <li><b>Sound cards</b> gave early PCs real audio: AdLib (founded 1987) and then Creative's Sound Blaster (1989), which became the de facto standard for PC audio until Windows 95 and audio built into the motherboard took over.</li>
      <li><b>Integrated audio:</b> today sound is part of the motherboard or chip, following Intel's AC'97 and High Definition Audio (2004) specifications.</li>
      <li><b>The pattern repeats:</b> a separate card proves a need, then the function moves onto the main chip. Graphics went the same way in laptops.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>From gaming graphics to AI</h3>
    <ol>
      <li><b>1995 to 1999, 3D for games:</b> 3dfx's Voodoo Graphics chip (to manufacturing in November 1995) brought fast 3D to PC gaming. In 1999 Nvidia marketed the GeForce 256 as "the world's first GPU", popularising the term.</li>
      <li><b>Why GPUs are different:</b> drawing a frame means doing the same small calculation on millions of pixels at once, so GPUs have thousands of simple cores built for parallel arithmetic, where a CPU has a few complex ones.</li>
      <li><b>2007, CUDA:</b> Nvidia's CUDA let ordinary programs use the GPU for general-purpose computing, opening it to science and engineering.</li>
      <li><b>2012, AlexNet:</b> a neural network for image recognition, trained on two Nvidia GTX 580 gaming GPUs, entered the ImageNet challenge and showed that GPUs made deep learning practical.</li>
      <li><b>2015 to 2017, chips for AI:</b> Google introduced its Tensor Processing Unit (TPU), a chip designed only for neural networks, and Nvidia's Volta GPUs (first product announced May 2017) added Tensor Cores for deep learning.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="note">Neural networks are mostly large matrix multiplications, exactly the parallel arithmetic that games had already paid to make fast and cheap. See <a href="#llm">LLMs</a> and <a href="#mathematics">Mathematics</a>.</p>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Explain what an instruction set is, and RISC versus CISC</li><li>Explain firmware and device drivers</li><li>Explain why GPUs suit graphics and AI</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Program a microcontroller to read a sensor and drive an output</li><li>Read a laptop or server spec sheet and know what each part does</li><li>Run a workload on a GPU and compare it with the CPU</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Choose hardware for a workload: cores, memory, storage, accelerators</li><li>Profile where time goes between CPU, memory, disk and GPU</li><li>Plan for ARM and x86 in the same estate</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Set hardware strategy for a platform or data centre</li><li>Co-design software with accelerators</li><li>Lead a move between processor architectures</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>Instruction set architectures</li><li>Firmware and boot</li><li>Device drivers</li><li>Systems on a chip</li><li>GPU programming</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reduced_instruction_set_computer" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Reduced instruction set computer (Wikipedia)</a><span class="lnote"> RISC explained, with its history</span></li><li><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Graphics_processing_unit" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Graphics processing unit (Wikipedia)</a><span class="lnote"> How GPUs work and how they developed</span></li><li><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CUDA" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CUDA (Wikipedia)</a><span class="lnote"> General-purpose computing on GPUs</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reduced_instruction_set_computer" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Reduced instruction set computer</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Complex_instruction_set_computer" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Complex instruction set computer</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Intel_4004" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Intel 4004</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Intel_8086" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Intel 8086</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ARM_architecture_family" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ARM architecture family</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/X86-64" target="_blank" rel="noopener">x86-64</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multi-core_processor" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Multi-core processor</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apple_M1" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Apple M1</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/System_on_a_chip" target="_blank" rel="noopener">System on a chip</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Firmware" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Firmware</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Microcontroller" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Microcontroller</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Device_driver" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Device driver</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NVM_Express" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NVM Express</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/MIDI" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MIDI</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AdLib" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AdLib</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sound_Blaster" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sound Blaster</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Intel_High_Definition_Audio" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Intel High Definition Audio</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/3dfx_Interactive" target="_blank" rel="noopener">3dfx Interactive</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Graphics_processing_unit" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Graphics processing unit</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CUDA" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CUDA</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AlexNet" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AlexNet</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tensor_Processing_Unit" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Tensor Processing Unit</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Volta_(microarchitecture)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Volta (microarchitecture)</a>.</p><p class="runcode">A question, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/enrichmeai.github.io/issues/new?title=%5BHow%20Computers%20Are%20Built%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>. Nothing here is tracked and there are no comments to store: questions live in the open, where the next learner can find the answer.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<section class="panel" id="p-linux" data-name="Linux">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Linux</h2><p>The commands you need on a server, in a container, or in a CI runner. Networking commands live on the <a href="#networking">Networking</a> tab.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card"><h3>Where things live</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Path</th><th>Holds</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>/etc</td><td>system configuration</td></tr>
      <tr><td>/var/log</td><td>logs (when not in the journal)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>/var/lib</td><td>service data (docker, postgres)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>/tmp</td><td>temporary files, often cleared on reboot (depends on the distribution)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>/home, /root</td><td>user homes</td></tr>
      <tr><td>/usr/bin, /usr/local/bin</td><td>programs; local installs go in /usr/local</td></tr>
      <tr><td>/opt</td><td>self-contained third-party software</td></tr>
      <tr><td>/proc, /sys</td><td>live kernel and process information</td></tr>
      <tr><td>/dev</td><td>devices</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Files &amp; navigation</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>pwd; ls -lah; cd -                       # back to previous dir
find . -name '*.sql' -mtime -1           # changed in the last day
find /var/log -size +100M
find . -name '*.tmp' -delete
tree -L 2                                # if installed
cp -a src/ dst/                          # keep permissions and times
mv old new; mkdir -p a/b/c
ln -s /opt/app/current app               # symlink
du -sh * | sort -h                       # what is big here
less +F app.log                          # follow; Ctrl-C then q
head -n 20 f; tail -n 50 -f f
wc -l f; file f; stat f
tar czf out.tgz dir/; tar xzf out.tgz; tar tzf out.tgz | head
diff -u a b</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Text processing</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>grep -rn "ERROR" logs/                   # recursive, line numbers
grep -E "timeout|refused" app.log | wc -l
grep -v DEBUG app.log                    # invert
grep -o 'run_id=[a-z0-9-]*' app.log | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn | head

cut -d, -f1,3 data.csv
awk -F, '$3 &gt; 100 {print $1, $3}' data.csv
awk '{s += $2} END {print s}' sizes.txt
sed -n '100,120p' big.log                # print a range
sed -i 's/old/new/g' file                # in place (GNU)
sort -t, -k2,2n data.csv | uniq
tr '[:lower:]' '[:upper:]' &lt; f
xargs -I{} -P4 gsutil cp {} dst/ &lt; uris.txt  # 4 in parallel

# JSON
jq '.items[] | {name, size}' listing.json
jq -r '.[] | select(.state=="FAILED") | .id' runs.json
curl -s url | jq -r '.data.rows[0]'</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Processes &amp; signals</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>ps aux | grep java
ps -eo pid,ppid,%cpu,%mem,etime,cmd --sort=-%mem | head
pgrep -af gunicorn
top / htop                               # P cpu, M memory, k kill
kill PID                                 # SIGTERM: ask nicely
kill -9 PID                              # SIGKILL: cannot be caught, no cleanup
pkill -f 'python -m loader'
lsof -i :8080                            # who holds the port
lsof -p PID | wc -l                      # open files
nohup ./job.sh &gt; job.log 2&gt;&amp;1 &amp;          # survive logout
jobs; fg %1; bg; disown
timeout 300 ./slow.sh                    # kill after 5 min
nice -n 10 ./batch.sh                    # lower priority</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">Exit codes: 0 success; 1 general error; 126 not executable; 127 command not found; 128+N killed by signal N (137 = 128+9 SIGKILL, 143 = 128+15 SIGTERM).</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Disk, memory, system</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>df -h                     # free space per filesystem
df -i                     # inodes: "no space" with free GB means inodes
du -xh / --max-depth=1 2&gt;/dev/null | sort -h
free -h                   # "available" is what matters, not "free"
uptime                    # load average: compare with CPU count
nproc; lscpu; lsblk
vmstat 1 5                # run queue, swap, IO wait
iostat -x 1               # disk busy (sysstat)
dmesg -T | tail           # kernel: OOM killer, disk errors
uname -a; cat /etc/os-release
ulimit -n                 # max open files for this shell</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Permissions &amp; users</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>ls -l f        # -rwxr-x--- 1 app app ...  owner / group / others
chmod 640 f    # rw- r-- ---   (r=4 w=2 x=1)
chmod u+x run.sh
chmod -R g+rX dir/            # X: execute on dirs, and on files already executable
chown app:app f; chown -R 1001:1001 /data
id; whoami; groups
sudo -u app ./run.sh
sudo useradd -r -s /usr/sbin/nologin app     # service account, no login
umask                          # default permission mask</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">Private keys must not be accessible by others (use <code>600</code> or <code>400</code>): ssh ignores a private key file that others can read. A directory needs <code>x</code> to be entered and <code>r</code> to be listed.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>systemd &amp; logs</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>systemctl status nginx
sudo systemctl restart nginx
sudo systemctl enable --now myapp     # start now and on boot
systemctl list-units --failed
journalctl -u myapp -f                # follow one service
journalctl -u myapp --since "1 hour ago" -p err
journalctl -b -1                      # previous boot
journalctl --disk-usage

# /etc/systemd/system/myapp.service
[Unit]
Description=Shop API
Wants=network-online.target
After=network-online.target
[Service]
User=app
WorkingDirectory=/opt/app
EnvironmentFile=/etc/myapp.env
ExecStart=/usr/bin/java -jar /opt/app/app.jar
Restart=on-failure
[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
# then: sudo systemctl daemon-reload</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Safe bash scripts</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>#!/usr/bin/env bash
set -euo pipefail          # stop on error, unset var, or failed pipe stage
IFS=$'\n\t'

usage() { echo "usage: $0 &lt;env&gt; &lt;date&gt;" &gt;&amp;2; exit 2; }
[[ $# -eq 2 ]] || usage
env="$1"; date="$2"
[[ "$env" =~ ^(dev|prod)$ ]] || { echo "bad env: $env" &gt;&amp;2; exit 2; }

tmp="$(mktemp -d)"
trap 'rm -rf "$tmp"' EXIT  # always clean up

log() { printf '%s %s\n' "$(date -u +%FT%TZ)" "$*" &gt;&amp;2; }

for f in data/*.csv; do
  [[ -e "$f" ]] || continue          # glob matched nothing
  log "loading $f"
done

# capture an exit code without dying under set -e
if ! out="$(some_command 2&gt;&amp;1)"; then
  log "failed: $out"; exit 1
fi
# shellcheck script.sh   ← run it</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">Quote every variable (<code>"$f"</code>). Unquoted values split on spaces and expand globs.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>SSH &amp; remote work</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -C "jo@laptop"
ssh -i ~/.ssh/id_ed25519 user@host
ssh -L 5432:db.internal:5432 bastion      # local port forward
ssh -J bastion user@private-host          # jump host
scp f user@host:/tmp/; rsync -avz --progress dir/ host:/srv/dir/

# ~/.ssh/config
Host bastion
  HostName 203.0.113.10
  User jo
  IdentityFile ~/.ssh/id_ed25519
Host *.internal
  ProxyJump bastion

tmux new -s work    # detach: Ctrl-b d   reattach: tmux attach -t work</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Pipes, redirects, environment</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>cmd &gt; out.txt          # stdout to file (overwrite)
cmd &gt;&gt; out.txt         # append
cmd 2&gt; err.txt         # stderr
cmd &gt; all.txt 2&gt;&amp;1     # both (order matters)
cmd | tee out.txt      # see it and save it
cmd &lt;&lt;'EOF'            # heredoc; quotes stop $ expansion
text
EOF
a &amp;&amp; b                 # b only if a succeeded
a || b                 # b only if a failed

export DATASET=analytics; env | grep DATA; unset DATASET
echo "$PATH" | tr ':' '\n'
which java; type ll; command -v gcloud
history | grep deploy; !!; Ctrl-r (search)</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>From Unix to Linux: a short history, with BSD and Solaris</h3>
    <p>The commands on this page are older than most of the people who type them. <code>ls</code>, pipes, &ldquo;everything is a file&rdquo; and the shell all come from Unix, started at Bell Labs in 1969. Knowing the family tree explains why Linux, macOS and the BSDs feel alike, and why a script written on one often runs on another.</p>
    <div class="tbl"><table><thead><tr><th>When</th><th>What happened</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td>1960s</td><td>Bell Labs, MIT and General Electric build Multics, an ambitious time-sharing system. Bell Labs, frustrated by its size and complexity, pulls out.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>1969</td><td>At Bell Labs, Ken Thompson, Dennis Ritchie and colleagues start <b>Unix</b>, first written in assembly language for a PDP-7.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>1973</td><td>Version 4 Unix is rewritten in C, a high-level language. A system written in a high-level language can be moved to new machines far more easily, and Unix spreads.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>1978</td><td>The first <b>BSD</b> (Berkeley Software Distribution) release, from the University of California, Berkeley, after Thompson spent a sabbatical there installing Unix; Bill Joy led early development.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>1983</td><td>AT&amp;T sells UNIX System V commercially. Richard Stallman announces the <b>GNU Project</b>, to build a complete free Unix-compatible operating system.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>1988</td><td>The first <b>POSIX</b> standard (IEEE 1003.1) defines a common interface, so programs can run on many Unix-like systems; its shell and tools are based on System V.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>1991</td><td>Linus Torvalds releases the <b>Linux kernel</b> on 17 September. GNU had the tools but no working kernel; together they make a complete free system.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>1993</td><td>Sun Microsystems&rsquo; <b>Solaris</b>, based on System V, replaces its BSD-based SunOS. Solaris becomes known for scaling on SPARC servers, and for originating DTrace and ZFS.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2005</td><td>Sun releases most of Solaris as open source (OpenSolaris).</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2007</td><td>Apple&rsquo;s Mac OS X 10.5 is certified as UNIX 03 compliant: macOS is a certified Unix.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2010</td><td>Oracle buys Sun and discontinues OpenSolaris. The community continues it as <b>illumos</b>.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Today</td><td>Linux is the leading operating system on servers, and through Android has the largest installed base of any general-purpose operating system. Solaris is now Oracle Solaris, a proprietary Unix.</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
    <div class="tbl"><table><thead><tr><th>Family</th><th>Where you meet it now</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td><b>Linux</b> (a Unix-like kernel, with GNU and other tools)</td><td>Cloud servers, containers, Android phones, Chromebooks (see <a href="#docker">Docker</a> and <a href="#kubernetes">Kubernetes</a>)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>BSD descendants</b></td><td>FreeBSD, OpenBSD and NetBSD; macOS&rsquo;s kernel draws on Mach and BSD, and macOS is a certified Unix</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Commercial Unix</b></td><td>Oracle Solaris, IBM AIX and HP-UX, still used in certain fields, often on dedicated hardware</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Solaris&rsquo;s open-source line</b></td><td>illumos; ZFS and DTrace also reached other systems</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
    <p><i>Our view:</i> learn the Unix ideas, not one distribution&rsquo;s quirks: small tools that do one thing, text streams joined by pipes, and files and permissions as the common language. They work on every system in the table, and POSIX is why. <i>The other view:</i> in practice most engineers now meet only Linux, in containers and cloud machines, so time is better spent on Linux specifics such as systemd, cgroups and the distribution your servers run.</p>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Navigate, read and search files and logs</li><li>Run, stop and inspect processes</li><li>Understand permissions well enough to fix 'permission denied'</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Write safe bash scripts with set -euo pipefail, traps and quoting</li><li>Diagnose disk, memory and CPU problems with df, free, top, dmesg</li><li>Manage services with systemd and read journalctl</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Tune a host or container for a workload (file limits, memory, I/O)</li><li>Debug with strace, lsof and tcpdump</li><li>Decide when a script should become a real program</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Understand kernel mechanisms behind containers (namespaces, cgroups)</li><li>Set the team's base images and hardening standards</li><li>Lead performance investigations end to end</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>cgroups and namespaces: what a container really is</li><li>strace, perf and eBPF tools for tracing</li><li>File descriptors, signals and process lifecycle</li><li>Shell portability: bash vs POSIX sh</li><li>Log rotation and journald retention</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://linuxcommand.org/tlcl.php" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The Linux Command Line (free book)</a><span class="lnote"> A full beginner-to-practitioner course</span></li><li><a href="https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Linux man pages</a><span class="lnote"> Authoritative reference for every command and syscall</span></li><li><a href="https://www.gnu.org/software/bash/manual/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GNU Bash manual</a><span class="lnote"> Expansion, quoting and builtins in detail</span></li><li><a href="https://www.shellcheck.net/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ShellCheck</a><span class="lnote"> Lints shell scripts and explains each warning</span></li><li><a href="https://systemd.io/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">systemd documentation</a><span class="lnote"> Units, timers, journald</span></li><li><a href="https://wizardzines.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wizard Zines (Julia Evans)</a><span class="lnote"> Short illustrated guides to Linux, networking and debugging</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/find.1.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">find(1)</a>; <a href="https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/sort.1.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">sort(1)</a>; <a href="https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/xargs.1.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">xargs(1)</a>; <a href="https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/du.1.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">du(1)</a>; <a href="https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/chmod.1.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">chmod(1)</a>; <a href="https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/sed.1.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">sed(1)</a>; <a href="https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/less.1.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">less(1)</a>; <a href="https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/timeout.1.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">timeout(1)</a>; <a href="https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/systemctl.1.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">systemctl(1)</a>; <a href="https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/journalctl.1.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">journalctl(1)</a>; <a href="https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man5/systemd.service.5.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">systemd.service(5)</a>; <a href="https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/ssh.1.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ssh(1)</a>; <a href="https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/ssh-keygen.1.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ssh-keygen(1)</a>; <a href="https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/tmux.1.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">tmux(1)</a>; <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc5737" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RFC 5737 (documentation addresses)</a>; <a href="https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/bash.1.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">exit codes 126, 127, 137 and 143, run in bash</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unix" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Unix</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_Unix" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: History of Unix</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Berkeley_Software_Distribution" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Berkeley Software Distribution</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GNU_Project" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: GNU Project</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/POSIX" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: POSIX</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Linux" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Linux</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oracle_Solaris" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Oracle Solaris</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/OpenSolaris" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: OpenSolaris</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Illumos" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: illumos</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Single_UNIX_Specification" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Single UNIX Specification</a>.</p><p class="runcode"><b>Run the code:</b> <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/well-rounded-engineer-fundamentals/tree/main/linux" target="_blank" rel="noopener">well-rounded-engineer-fundamentals/linux</a>, with a README that says how to run it and what it shows. Stuck, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/well-rounded-engineer-fundamentals/issues/new?title=%5BLinux%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= NETWORKING ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-networking" data-name="Networking">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Networking basics</h2><p>Enough to read a VPC diagram, size a subnet, and work out why one thing cannot reach another. GCP terms where the cloud differs.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card"><h3>Layers that matter</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>Unit</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>7 Application</td><td>message</td><td>HTTP, gRPC, DNS, TLS-wrapped protocols</td></tr>
      <tr><td>4 Transport</td><td>segment</td><td>TCP (ordered, reliable), UDP (fire and forget), ports</td></tr>
      <tr><td>3 Network</td><td>packet</td><td>IP addresses, routing, ICMP (ping)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2 Link</td><td>frame</td><td>Ethernet, MAC addresses, ARP</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note">"L4 load balancer" routes by IP and port. "L7 load balancer" reads HTTP: host, path, headers, and terminates TLS.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>IP addresses &amp; CIDR</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Prefix</th><th>Addresses</th><th>Typical use</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>/32</td><td>1</td><td>one host (firewall rule, allowlist)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>/28</td><td>16</td><td>small connector or proxy subnet</td></tr>
      <tr><td>/24</td><td>256</td><td>a typical subnet</td></tr>
      <tr><td>/20</td><td>4,096</td><td>GKE nodes or a large subnet</td></tr>
      <tr><td>/16</td><td>65,536</td><td>a whole VPC range, or GKE pods</td></tr>
      <tr><td>/8</td><td>16.7 M</td><td>10.0.0.0/8, the private block</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <ul>
      <li>Addresses in a block = 2<sup>(32 − prefix)</sup>. Each step down the prefix doubles it.</li>
      <li>Private ranges (RFC 1918): <code>10.0.0.0/8</code>, <code>172.16.0.0/12</code>, <code>192.168.0.0/16</code>. Shared/CGNAT: <code>100.64.0.0/10</code>.</li>
      <li>Google Cloud reserves four addresses in each subnet's primary range (the first two and the last two); secondary ranges can use every address.</li>
      <li>Ranges that will ever be peered or connected by VPN must not overlap. Plan them up front.</li>
      <li><code>0.0.0.0/0</code> means "anywhere". <code>127.0.0.1</code> is this machine only.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Ports to know</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Port</th><th>Service</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>22</td><td>SSH</td></tr>
      <tr><td>53</td><td>DNS (UDP, TCP for big answers)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>80 / 443</td><td>HTTP / HTTPS (443 also UDP for HTTP/3)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>3306 / 5432</td><td>MySQL / PostgreSQL</td></tr>
      <tr><td>6379</td><td>Redis</td></tr>
      <tr><td>8080</td><td>common app port; Cloud Run's default <code>PORT</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>9092</td><td>Kafka</td></tr>
      <tr><td>6443</td><td>Kubernetes API server</td></tr>
      <tr><td>10250</td><td>kubelet</td></tr>
      <tr><td>30000–32767</td><td>Kubernetes NodePort range</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>DNS</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Record</th><th>Means</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>A / AAAA</td><td>name → IPv4 / IPv6</td></tr>
      <tr><td>CNAME</td><td>name → another name (not allowed at the zone apex)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>MX</td><td>mail servers for the domain</td></tr>
      <tr><td>TXT</td><td>free text: domain verification, SPF, DKIM</td></tr>
      <tr><td>NS / SOA</td><td>which servers are authoritative for the zone</td></tr>
      <tr><td>SRV</td><td>host + port for a service</td></tr>
      <tr><td>PTR</td><td>IP → name (reverse lookup)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>CAA</td><td>which CAs may issue certificates</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>dig shop.example.com A +short
dig example.com NS
dig +trace example.com          # follow delegation from the root
dig @8.8.8.8 example.com        # ask a specific resolver
nslookup example.com</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">TTL is how long resolvers cache an answer. Lower it a day before a planned change, not at the moment of it.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>DNS, step by step</h3>
    <figure class="fig"><svg viewBox="0 0 640 200" role="img" aria-labelledby="dns-t dns-d"><title id="dns-t">Where a DNS answer comes from</title><desc id="dns-d">Four boxes left to right: browser cache, operating system, resolver, authoritative servers. A lookup asks each in turn and stops at the first one that holds a fresh answer. The answer travels back, and each layer keeps it for the record's TTL, which is why a changed record takes up to one TTL to be seen everywhere.</desc><defs><marker id="dns-a" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker><marker id="dns-aa" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar acc" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker></defs><rect class="nd" x="10" y="50" width="125" height="46" rx="6"/><text class="lb hd" x="72" y="78" text-anchor="middle">browser cache</text><line class="ed" x1="135" y1="64" x2="166" y2="64" marker-end="url(#dns-a)"/><line class="ed dash" x1="170" y1="84" x2="139" y2="84" marker-end="url(#dns-a)"/><rect class="nd" x="170" y="50" width="125" height="46" rx="6"/><text class="lb hd" x="232" y="78" text-anchor="middle">operating system</text><line class="ed" x1="295" y1="64" x2="326" y2="64" marker-end="url(#dns-a)"/><line class="ed dash" x1="330" y1="84" x2="299" y2="84" marker-end="url(#dns-a)"/><rect class="nd" x="330" y="50" width="125" height="46" rx="6"/><text class="lb hd" x="392" y="78" text-anchor="middle">resolver</text><line class="ed" x1="455" y1="64" x2="486" y2="64" marker-end="url(#dns-a)"/><line class="ed dash" x1="490" y1="84" x2="459" y2="84" marker-end="url(#dns-a)"/><rect class="nd out" x="490" y="50" width="125" height="46" rx="6"/><text class="lb hd" x="552" y="78" text-anchor="middle">authoritative</text><text class="lb" x="320" y="30" text-anchor="middle">ask in turn until one has a fresh answer</text><text class="lb mu" x="320" y="128" text-anchor="middle">the answer comes back, and each layer caches it for the TTL</text><rect class="bd" x="170" y="150" width="300" height="34" rx="6"/><text class="lb acc" x="320" y="172" text-anchor="middle">lower the TTL a day before a planned change</text></svg><figcaption>A DNS lookup stops at the first cache with a fresh answer, and every layer keeps the answer for the record&rsquo;s TTL. That is why a change can take up to one TTL to reach everyone.</figcaption></figure>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>HTTP &amp; TLS</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Status classes:</b> 2xx ok, 3xx redirect, 4xx your request is wrong (401 no/invalid credentials, 403 authenticated but not allowed, 404 missing, 429 rate limited), 5xx the server failed (502 bad gateway, 503 unavailable, 504 upstream timeout).</li>
      <li><b>HTTP/1.1</b> one request at a time per connection; <b>HTTP/2</b> multiplexes streams on one TCP connection (gRPC uses it); <b>HTTP/3</b> runs over QUIC on UDP.</li>
      <li><b>TLS</b> proves the server's identity with a certificate chain to a trusted root, then encrypts. <b>SNI</b> sends the hostname so one IP can serve many certificates.</li>
      <li><b>mTLS</b>: the client also presents a certificate. Common inside service meshes.</li>
      <li>A proxy that terminates TLS can read and route on the request; one that passes it through (L4) cannot.</li>
    </ul>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>openssl s_client -connect example.com:443 -servername example.com &lt;/dev/null \
  | openssl x509 -noout -subject -issuer -dates
curl -vI https://example.com            # handshake, headers
curl -o /dev/null -s -w 'dns %{time_namelookup} connect %{time_connect} tls %{time_appconnect} ttfb %{time_starttransfer} total %{time_total}\n' https://example.com</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Troubleshooting commands</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>ping -c 3 10.0.0.5               # ICMP reachability (often blocked; not proof)
nc -vz db.internal 5432          # is the TCP port open?
traceroute -n host / mtr host    # where packets stop
curl -v http://svc:8080/health   # app-level answer
ss -tulpn                        # what is listening locally
ip addr; ip route                # my addresses and routes
cat /etc/resolv.conf             # which resolver am I using
getent hosts db.internal         # resolve the way apps do
sudo tcpdump -i any -n port 5432 -c 20    # see packets</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>"Cannot connect": read the error</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Could not resolve host / NXDOMAIN:</b> DNS. Wrong name, wrong resolver, or a private zone not visible from here.</li>
      <li><b>Connection refused:</b> the host answered but nothing listens on that port, or the app binds to <code>127.0.0.1</code> instead of <code>0.0.0.0</code>.</li>
      <li><b>Connection timed out:</b> packets are dropped. Firewall, security group, missing route, or no NAT for egress.</li>
      <li><b>TLS / certificate error:</b> name mismatch, expired certificate, missing intermediate, or a corporate proxy re-signing traffic.</li>
      <li><b>403 from a proxy or website:</b> first suspect your own egress (datacentre IP, proxy policy) before blaming the far side.</li>
      <li><b>Works locally, fails in the cloud:</b> compare egress path, DNS resolver and credentials, not code.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>NAT, proxies, load balancers</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>NAT</b> lets private hosts reach the internet through a shared public IP. Inbound connections are not possible through it.</li>
      <li><b>Forward proxy</b> acts for clients going out (egress control, allowlists). <b>Reverse proxy</b> acts for servers taking traffic in (nginx, Envoy, cloud LBs).</li>
      <li><b>Load balancer health checks</b> decide which backends get traffic. A failing check looks like a 502/503 to clients.</li>
      <li><b>Stateful firewalls</b> allow the reply to an allowed connection automatically; you write rules for the initiating direction only.</li>
      <li><b>Bastion / IAP tunnel</b> reaches private hosts without giving them public IPs.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>GCP networking map</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Piece</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>VPC</td><td>Global private network. Subnets are regional and carry the IP ranges.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Firewall rules / policies</td><td>Stateful allow/deny, ingress or egress, targeted by network tag or service account. Default: deny ingress, allow egress.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Cloud NAT</td><td>Outbound internet for VMs, GKE nodes and Cloud Run (with VPC egress) that have no public IP. Gives a fixed egress IP to allowlist.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Private Google Access</td><td>Lets private-only hosts reach Google APIs (BigQuery, GCS) without a public IP.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Private Service Connect / private services access</td><td>Private IPs for managed services such as Cloud SQL, reachable only from the VPC.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Direct VPC egress / Serverless VPC Access connector</td><td>Connects Cloud Run and Cloud Functions to private IPs in the VPC.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Cloud Load Balancing</td><td>Global external HTTP(S) (L7), regional internal, and network (L4) load balancers. Managed certificates.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Cloud Armor</td><td>WAF and IP allow/deny in front of an external HTTP(S) load balancer.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Cloud DNS</td><td>Public and private zones; private zones resolve only inside chosen VPCs.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>VPC Service Controls</td><td>A perimeter around Google APIs so data cannot be copied out to another project.</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>gcloud compute networks list
gcloud compute networks subnets list --network=default
gcloud compute firewall-rules list --format="table(name,direction,sourceRanges.list(),allowed[].map().firewall_rule().list(),targetTags.list())"
gcloud compute routers nats list --router=my-router --region=asia-south1
gcloud compute ssh vm-1 --tunnel-through-iap      # no public IP needed</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Explain IP, port, DNS, TCP vs UDP and HTTP status classes</li><li>Use curl, dig and nc to test reachability</li><li>Tell a DNS failure from a refusal from a timeout</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Size subnets with CIDR; read firewall rules and routes</li><li>Debug TLS and certificate errors</li><li>Configure private access, NAT and egress for cloud workloads</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Design a VPC: ranges, subnets, private service access, load balancers</li><li>Choose L4 vs L7 load balancing and set health checks</li><li>Trace a latency problem across the network path</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Design hybrid and multi-region connectivity</li><li>Set network security policy for an organisation</li><li>Lead incident response for network-level outages</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>TCP handshake, congestion control and keep-alive</li><li>TLS 1.3 handshake and certificate chains</li><li>HTTP/2 and HTTP/3 (QUIC)</li><li>Anycast, global load balancing and CDNs</li><li>VPC Service Controls and Private Service Connect</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://hpbn.co/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">High Performance Browser Networking (free book)</a><span class="lnote"> TCP, TLS, HTTP/2 explained for engineers</span></li><li><a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MDN: HTTP</a><span class="lnote"> Methods, headers, caching, CORS</span></li><li><a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/learning/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cloudflare Learning Center</a><span class="lnote"> Clear explainers on DNS, TLS, CDN, DDoS</span></li><li><a href="https://cloud.google.com/vpc/docs/overview" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google Cloud VPC overview</a><span class="lnote"> Networks, subnets, firewall, routes on GCP</span></li><li><a href="https://cloud.google.com/load-balancing/docs/load-balancing-overview" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cloud Load Balancing overview</a><span class="lnote"> Which load balancer for which job</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1918" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RFC 1918 (private ranges)</a>; <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6598" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RFC 6598 (shared address space)</a>; <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1912" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RFC 1912 (CNAME with other data)</a>; <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7766" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RFC 7766 (DNS over TCP)</a>; <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc2782" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RFC 2782 (SRV)</a>; <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8659" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RFC 8659 (CAA)</a>; <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RFC 9110 (HTTP semantics)</a>; <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6585" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RFC 6585 (429)</a>; <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9114" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RFC 9114 (HTTP/3)</a>; <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6066" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RFC 6066 (SNI)</a>; <a href="https://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/networking/ports-and-protocols/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kubernetes ports and protocols</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/vpc/docs/subnets" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google Cloud subnets</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/firewall/docs/firewalls" target="_blank" rel="noopener">VPC firewall rules</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/run/docs/container-contract" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cloud Run container contract</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/nat/docs/overview" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cloud NAT overview</a>; <a href="https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man8/ss.8.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ss(8)</a>; <a href="https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/tcpdump.1.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">tcpdump(1)</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/sdk/gcloud/reference" target="_blank" rel="noopener">gcloud commands, checked with gcloud --help</a>.</p><p class="runcode"><b>Run the code:</b> <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/well-rounded-engineer-fundamentals/tree/main/networking" target="_blank" rel="noopener">well-rounded-engineer-fundamentals/networking</a>, with a README that says how to run it and what it shows. Stuck, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/well-rounded-engineer-fundamentals/issues/new?title=%5BNetworking%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= WEB FUNDAMENTALS ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-internet" data-name="How the Internet Is Tied Together">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>How the internet is tied together</h2><p>The internet is not one network. It is tens of thousands of networks agreeing to carry each other's traffic, held together by a few shared systems. When one of those systems fails, much of the web goes down at once. Read <a href="#networking">Networking</a> first.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>The pieces</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <tr><th>Piece</th><th>What it does</th></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Autonomous systems</b></td><td>Each internet provider, cloud or large company runs its own network, an autonomous system, with its own block of addresses.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>BGP</b> (Border Gateway Protocol)</td><td>How autonomous systems tell each other which addresses they can reach. It is the internet's map, and every network trusts the others to describe it honestly.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Internet exchange points</b></td><td>Buildings full of switches where many networks connect directly and exchange traffic, instead of sending it the long way round.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Submarine cables</b></td><td>Fibre-optic cables on the sea bed carry the great majority of data between continents.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>DNS and the root servers</b></td><td>DNS turns names into addresses. At its top are thirteen well-known root server addresses, served by many copies around the world.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Content delivery networks</b></td><td>Akamai, Cloudflare and others keep copies of websites close to users and absorb attacks, so a large share of the web passes through them.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Cloud regions</b></td><td>Huge data centres run by a few providers, where much of today's software and data live. See <a href="#cloudinside">How the cloud is built</a>.</td></tr>
    </table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>When a shared piece fails</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>4 October 2021, BGP:</b> Facebook withdrew the routes to its own name servers in a burst of BGP updates, and its services were unavailable worldwide for six to seven hours.</li>
      <li><b>20 October 2025, a cloud region:</b> services in AWS's Northern Virginia region (us-east-1) failed, taking down sites such as Roblox, Fortnite, Snapchat and Duolingo.</li>
      <li><b>18 November 2025, a CDN:</b> a Cloudflare outage took down major platforms, including X and ChatGPT.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">Each one is a single shared dependency that many companies chose independently. See <a href="#k8sdr">Kubernetes DR</a> and <a href="#sre">SRE</a> on designing for it.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>What engineers can do</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Know your dependencies:</b> DNS provider, CDN, cloud region, identity provider, payment gateway. Write them down.</li>
      <li><b>Avoid one region for everything</b> that must stay up; test failing over before you need it.</li>
      <li><b>Lower DNS TTLs before planned changes,</b> and keep a way to switch providers.</li>
      <li><b>Show an honest status</b> when a dependency is down, rather than a confusing error. See <a href="#observability">Observability</a>.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Explain that the internet is many networks connected together</li><li>Explain what DNS, a CDN and a cloud region are</li><li>List your service's outside dependencies</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Trace a request's path with traceroute and DNS lookups</li><li>Explain BGP and why a bad route can take a company offline</li><li>Configure a CDN and DNS for a service</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Design for the failure of a DNS provider, CDN or region</li><li>Plan multi-region and multi-provider set-ups</li><li>Run outage drills for shared dependencies</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Own internet-facing architecture and its resilience</li><li>Negotiate peering and provider strategy</li><li>Lead response to large external outages</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>Autonomous systems and BGP</li><li>Internet exchange points</li><li>Submarine cables</li><li>DNS root servers and anycast</li><li>Content delivery networks</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Border_Gateway_Protocol" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Border Gateway Protocol (Wikipedia)</a><span class="lnote"> How networks share routes</span></li><li><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Internet_exchange_point" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Internet exchange point (Wikipedia)</a><span class="lnote"> Where networks meet</span></li><li><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Root_name_server" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Root name server (Wikipedia)</a><span class="lnote"> The top of DNS</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Autonomous_system_(Internet)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Autonomous system</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Border_Gateway_Protocol" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Border Gateway Protocol</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Internet_exchange_point" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Internet exchange point</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Submarine_communications_cable" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Submarine communications cable</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Root_name_server" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Root name server</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Content_delivery_network" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Content delivery network</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Akamai_Technologies" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Akamai Technologies</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cloudflare" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cloudflare (November 2025 outage)</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2021_Facebook_outage" target="_blank" rel="noopener">2021 Facebook outage</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_Amazon_Web_Services" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Timeline of AWS (20 October 2025 outage)</a>.</p><p class="runcode">A question, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/enrichmeai.github.io/issues/new?title=%5BHow%20the%20Internet%20Is%20Tied%20Together%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>. Nothing here is tracked and there are no comments to store: questions live in the open, where the next learner can find the answer.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<section class="panel" id="p-web" data-name="Web Fundamentals">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Web fundamentals</h2><p>How the web works from a typed URL to a rendered page: URLs, HTTP, cookies and sessions, origins and CORS, caching, rendering models, web security, performance and accessibility. Lower layers are on <a href="#networking">Networking</a>; API design is on <a href="#apis">APIs &amp; Integration</a>.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>What happens when you open a URL</h3>
    <ol>
      <li><b>Parse the URL</b> and check the browser cache, including a cached HSTS rule that forces HTTPS.</li>
      <li><b>DNS</b> resolves the host name to an IP address (browser cache → OS → resolver → authoritative servers).</li>
      <li><b>Connect:</b> TCP handshake (or QUIC over UDP for HTTP/3), then the <b>TLS</b> handshake proves the server's identity and agrees keys.</li>
      <li><b>HTTP request</b> with method, path, headers and cookies, usually reaching a CDN or load balancer first.</li>
      <li><b>Server side:</b> load balancer → reverse proxy → application → database or cache → response.</li>
      <li><b>HTTP response</b>: status, headers (caching, cookies, security), and the body (HTML, JSON, images).</li>
      <li><b>Render:</b> parse HTML into the DOM, CSS into the CSSOM, run JavaScript, lay out, paint, and fetch the sub-resources the page references.</li>
      <li><b>After load:</b> JavaScript calls APIs (<code>fetch</code>), updates the page, and may open WebSockets or server-sent events.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="note">Every step can fail or be slow. Browser DevTools (Network tab: timing, headers, initiator) shows which.</p>
      <figure class="fig"><svg viewBox="0 0 640 392" role="img" aria-labelledby="url-t url-d"><title id="url-t">What happens when you open a URL</title><desc id="url-d">A sequence diagram with four lifelines: the browser, DNS, a CDN or load balancer, and the application with its database or cache. 1: the browser asks DNS for the host name's IP address and 2: gets it back. 3: it opens a TCP connection, or QUIC for HTTP/3, and 4: the TLS handshake proves the server's identity and agrees keys. 5: the browser sends the HTTP request, which reaches the CDN or load balancer first. 6: that passes it to the application, which 7: reads the database or cache. 8: the response comes back with status, headers and body. 9: the browser renders the page and fetches what it references.</desc><defs><marker id="url-a" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker><marker id="url-aa" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar acc" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker></defs><rect class="nd in" x="18.0" y="14" width="139" height="36" rx="4"/><text class="lb hd" x="87.5" y="37" text-anchor="middle">Browser</text><line class="ln" x1="87.5" y1="50" x2="87.5" y2="384"/><rect class="nd" x="173.0" y="14" width="139" height="36" rx="4"/><text class="lb hd" x="242.5" y="37" text-anchor="middle">DNS</text><line class="ln" x1="242.5" y1="50" x2="242.5" y2="384"/><rect class="nd" x="328.0" y="14" width="139" height="36" rx="4"/><text class="lb hd" x="397.5" y="37" text-anchor="middle">CDN / LB</text><line class="ln" x1="397.5" y1="50" x2="397.5" y2="384"/><rect class="nd out" x="483.0" y="14" width="139" height="36" rx="4"/><text class="lb hd" x="552.5" y="37" text-anchor="middle">App + data</text><line class="ln" x1="552.5" y1="50" x2="552.5" y2="384"/><line class="ed" x1="87.5" y1="80" x2="238.5" y2="80" marker-end="url(#url-a)"/><circle class="nd in" cx="100.5" cy="68" r="9"/><text class="lb mu" x="100.5" y="72" text-anchor="middle">1</text><text class="lb" x="115.5" y="73" text-anchor="start">IP address of shop.example?</text><line class="ed dash" x1="242.5" y1="114" x2="91.5" y2="114" marker-end="url(#url-a)"/><circle class="nd in" cx="100.5" cy="102" r="9"/><text class="lb mu" x="100.5" y="106" text-anchor="middle">2</text><text class="lb mu" x="115.5" y="107" text-anchor="start">the address (cached for its TTL)</text><line class="ed" x1="87.5" y1="148" x2="393.5" y2="148" marker-end="url(#url-a)"/><circle class="nd in" cx="100.5" cy="136" r="9"/><text class="lb mu" x="100.5" y="140" text-anchor="middle">3</text><text class="lb" x="115.5" y="141" text-anchor="start">TCP handshake (or QUIC)</text><line class="ed" x1="87.5" y1="182" x2="393.5" y2="182" marker-end="url(#url-a)"/><circle class="nd in" cx="100.5" cy="170" r="9"/><text class="lb mu" x="100.5" y="174" text-anchor="middle">4</text><text class="lb" x="115.5" y="175" text-anchor="start">TLS handshake: identity, keys</text><line class="ed acc" x1="87.5" y1="216" x2="393.5" y2="216" marker-end="url(#url-aa)"/><circle class="nd in" cx="100.5" cy="204" r="9"/><text class="lb mu" x="100.5" y="208" text-anchor="middle">5</text><text class="lb acc" x="115.5" y="209" text-anchor="start">GET /  + headers, cookies</text><line class="ed" x1="397.5" y1="250" x2="548.5" y2="250" marker-end="url(#url-a)"/><circle class="nd in" cx="410.5" cy="238" r="9"/><text class="lb mu" x="410.5" y="242" text-anchor="middle">6</text><text class="lb" x="425.5" y="243" text-anchor="start">forward the request</text><path class="ed" d="M552.5,276 h-22 v14 h18" marker-end="url(#url-a)"/><text class="lb" x="522.5" y="287" text-anchor="end">read DB or cache</text><circle class="nd in" cx="566.5" cy="280" r="9"/><text class="lb mu" x="566.5" y="284" text-anchor="middle">7</text><line class="ed dash" x1="552.5" y1="318" x2="91.5" y2="318" marker-end="url(#url-a)"/><circle class="nd in" cx="100.5" cy="306" r="9"/><text class="lb mu" x="100.5" y="310" text-anchor="middle">8</text><text class="lb mu" x="115.5" y="311" text-anchor="start">200, headers, HTML</text><path class="ed acc" d="M87.5,344 h22 v14 h-18" marker-end="url(#url-aa)"/><text class="lb acc" x="117.5" y="355" text-anchor="start">render, fetch CSS, JS, images</text><circle class="nd in" cx="73.5" cy="348" r="9"/><text class="lb mu" x="73.5" y="352" text-anchor="middle">9</text></svg><figcaption>The list above, drawn as a UML sequence diagram, one arrow per step. Every arrow can fail or be slow; the Network tab in DevTools shows which.</figcaption></figure>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Anatomy of a URL</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>https://shop.example.com:443/v1/orders/123?store=1_05&amp;page=2#items
└─┬─┘   └──────┬───────┘└┬─┘└─────┬──────┘└───────┬────────┘└─┬──┘
scheme       host      port     path            query     fragment</code></pre></div>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Origin</b> = scheme + host + port. It is the browser's security boundary.</li>
      <li><b>Path</b> names the resource; <b>query</b> parameters filter or modify it.</li>
      <li><b>Fragment</b> (<code>#…</code>) never reaches the server; the browser uses it to scroll or route.</li>
      <li><b>Encoding:</b> special characters are percent-encoded (<code>space → %20</code>, <code>+</code> in a query can mean a space). Encode parameters with a library, not by hand.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>HTTP request and response</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>GET /v1/orders/123 HTTP/1.1
Host: shop.example.com
Accept: application/json
Accept-Encoding: br, gzip
Authorization: Bearer eyJ…
If-None-Match: "a1b2c3"

HTTP/1.1 200 OK
Content-Type: application/json; charset=utf-8
Content-Encoding: br
Cache-Control: private, max-age=60
ETag: "a1b2c3"
Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains

{"id":"123","order_ref":"ORD-2024-000123", …}</code></pre></div>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Content negotiation:</b> <code>Accept</code> / <code>Content-Type</code> for format, <code>Accept-Language</code> for language, <code>Accept-Encoding</code> for compression.</li>
      <li><b>Compression:</b> Brotli or gzip for text; never for already-compressed images or video.</li>
      <li><b>MIME types</b> tell the browser what it received; a wrong one breaks scripts or opens security holes.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Status codes you will see</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Code</th><th>Means</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>200 / 201 / 204</td><td>OK / created / OK with no body</td></tr>
      <tr><td>301 / 308</td><td>Moved permanently (308 keeps the method)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>302 / 307</td><td>Temporary redirect (307 keeps the method)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>304</td><td>Not modified: use your cached copy</td></tr>
      <tr><td>400</td><td>Malformed or invalid request</td></tr>
      <tr><td>401</td><td>Not authenticated (missing or bad credentials)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>403</td><td>Authenticated but not allowed</td></tr>
      <tr><td>404 / 410</td><td>Not found / gone for good</td></tr>
      <tr><td>409 / 422</td><td>Conflict / valid syntax but semantically invalid</td></tr>
      <tr><td>429</td><td>Too many requests; see <code>Retry-After</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>500</td><td>Server error</td></tr>
      <tr><td>502 / 503 / 504</td><td>Bad gateway / unavailable / gateway timeout (usually a proxy talking to a sick backend)</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Cookies, sessions and tokens</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>HTTP is stateless.</b> State across requests comes from cookies or tokens the client sends each time.</li>
      <li><b>Cookie</b>: set by <code>Set-Cookie</code>, sent back automatically to the same site. Flags that matter:
        <code>Secure</code> (HTTPS only), <code>HttpOnly</code> (JavaScript cannot read it), <code>SameSite=Lax|Strict|None</code> (whether it goes on cross-site requests), <code>Domain</code>, <code>Path</code>, <code>Max-Age</code>.</li>
      <li><b>Server session:</b> the cookie holds a random ID; data lives on the server (or Redis). Easy to revoke.</li>
      <li><b>Token (JWT):</b> the client sends <code>Authorization: Bearer …</code>; the server validates it without a lookup. Harder to revoke; keep it short-lived. See <a href="#oauth">OAuth &amp; OIDC</a>.</li>
      <li><b>Browser storage:</b> <code>localStorage</code> and <code>sessionStorage</code> are readable by any script on the page, so never keep secrets there; IndexedDB for larger client data.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Same-origin policy and CORS</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>By default, a script on <code>https://app.example.com</code> cannot read responses from another origin such as <code>https://api.example.com</code>.</li>
      <li><b>CORS</b> lets the API opt in with response headers: <code>Access-Control-Allow-Origin: https://app.example.com</code>.</li>
      <li><b>Preflight:</b> for non-simple requests (custom headers, JSON bodies, PUT/DELETE) the browser first sends <code>OPTIONS</code> and checks <code>Access-Control-Allow-Methods</code> and <code>-Headers</code>.</li>
      <li><b>Credentials</b> (cookies) cross-origin need <code>Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true</code> and an exact origin, never <code>*</code>.</li>
      <li>CORS protects users in browsers; it is not server-side access control. curl ignores it.</li>
      <li>Serving the app and API from the same origin (e.g. <code>/api</code> on the same host) avoids CORS entirely.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Caching on the web</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Header</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable</code></td><td>Cache everywhere for a year; for fingerprinted assets like <code>app.3f2a9c.js</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>Cache-Control: private, max-age=60</code></td><td>Only the user's browser may cache, for 60 s</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>Cache-Control: no-cache</code></td><td>May store, but must revalidate before each use</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>Cache-Control: no-store</code></td><td>Never store (sensitive data)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>ETag</code> + <code>If-None-Match</code></td><td>Revalidate cheaply; server answers 304 if unchanged</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>Vary: Accept-Encoding</code></td><td>Cache separate copies per header value</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note">Layers: browser cache → CDN → reverse proxy → application cache → database. Fingerprint static files so you can cache them forever and still deploy changes instantly.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Rendering models</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>How</th><th>Good for</th><th>Trade-offs</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Server-side rendering (classic)</td><td>Server returns full HTML per request</td><td>Content sites, SEO, simple apps</td><td>Full page reloads; server does the work</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Single-page app (SPA)</td><td>Browser loads JS once, renders and routes on the client, calls JSON APIs</td><td>Rich interactive tools (React, Vue, Angular)</td><td>Slower first load, SEO effort, more client code</td></tr>
      <tr><td>SSR + hydration</td><td>Server renders HTML, then JS takes over on the client</td><td>Fast first paint with interactivity (Next.js, Nuxt)</td><td>Complexity; double rendering work</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Static site generation (SSG)</td><td>HTML built at deploy time, served from a CDN</td><td>Docs, marketing, blogs</td><td>Content changes need a rebuild</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Progressive web app (PWA)</td><td>Web app with a manifest and service worker for offline and install</td><td>App-like experiences without an app store</td><td>Platform differences, caching complexity</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Native wrapper</td><td>Web app inside a native shell (Capacitor, Cordova)</td><td>Store presence and device APIs from one codebase</td><td>Native build and signing still required</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>How the browser renders</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>HTML parsed into the <b>DOM</b>; CSS into the <b>CSSOM</b>.</li>
      <li>Combined into a render tree; <b>layout</b> computes sizes and positions; <b>paint</b> draws pixels; <b>composite</b> layers them.</li>
      <li><b>Render-blocking</b> resources (CSS, synchronous scripts in <code>&lt;head&gt;</code>) delay the first paint. Use <code>defer</code> or <code>type="module"</code> for scripts.</li>
      <li>JavaScript runs on one main thread; long tasks block input. Move heavy work to Web Workers.</li>
    </ol>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Performance: Core Web Vitals</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Metric</th><th>Measures</th><th>Good</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>LCP (Largest Contentful Paint)</td><td>When the main content appears</td><td>≤ 2.5 s</td></tr>
      <tr><td>INP (Interaction to Next Paint)</td><td>How quickly the page responds to input</td><td>≤ 200 ms</td></tr>
      <tr><td>CLS (Cumulative Layout Shift)</td><td>How much the layout jumps</td><td>≤ 0.1</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <ul>
      <li>Serve from a CDN; compress; cache fingerprinted assets.</li>
      <li>Send less JavaScript; split bundles by route; lazy-load below-the-fold images.</li>
      <li>Size images correctly; use modern formats (WebP, AVIF) and give width and height to avoid shifts.</li>
      <li>Measure with Lighthouse in the lab and real-user data in the field.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Web security essentials</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>HTTPS everywhere</b> plus <b>HSTS</b>, so browsers never fall back to HTTP.</li>
      <li><b>XSS</b> (cross-site scripting): untrusted data rendered as HTML runs as script. Escape output (frameworks do it by default), avoid <code>innerHTML</code> with user data, and add a <b>Content-Security-Policy</b>.</li>
      <li><b>CSRF</b>: another site makes the user's browser send a cookie-authenticated request. Use <code>SameSite</code> cookies and CSRF tokens for cookie-based sessions.</li>
      <li><b>Clickjacking</b>: your page framed invisibly. Use <code>frame-ancestors</code> in CSP.</li>
      <li><b>Mixed content</b>: HTTP resources on an HTTPS page are blocked or warned.</li>
      <li><b>Subresource Integrity</b> (<code>integrity=</code>) for scripts from CDNs.</li>
      <li>More on the <a href="#security">Security</a> tab.</li>
    </ul>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self';
  img-src 'self' data:; frame-ancestors 'none'
X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Accessibility basics</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Semantic HTML first:</b> <code>&lt;button&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;nav&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;main&gt;</code>, headings in order, <code>&lt;label&gt;</code> for every input.</li>
      <li><b>Keyboard:</b> everything usable with Tab, Enter and Space; visible focus outlines.</li>
      <li><b>Text alternatives:</b> <code>alt</code> on meaningful images; empty <code>alt=""</code> on decorative ones.</li>
      <li><b>Contrast:</b> at least 4.5:1 for normal text (WCAG AA).</li>
      <li><b>ARIA</b> only when HTML has no native element for the job.</li>
      <li><b>Test</b> with a screen reader and automated checks (axe, Lighthouse); they catch different things.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Servers on the web</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Web server / reverse proxy</b> (nginx, Envoy): TLS, static files, compression, routing to apps.</li>
      <li><b>Application server</b>: runs your code (Spring Boot with Tomcat or Netty, Quarkus with Vert.x, gunicorn or uvicorn for Python).</li>
      <li><b>CDN</b>: caches content near users and absorbs traffic spikes and attacks.</li>
      <li><b>Real-time</b>: WebSockets (two-way) and server-sent events (server to browser, simpler, works over HTTP).</li>
      <li><b>HTTP versions</b>: HTTP/1.1 (one request at a time per connection), HTTP/2 (multiplexed streams), HTTP/3 (over QUIC, better on lossy networks).</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>DevTools habits</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Network:</b> status, timing waterfall, request and response headers, "Disable cache", throttling, "Copy as cURL".</li>
      <li><b>Console:</b> JavaScript errors and CORS failures, which say exactly which header is missing.</li>
      <li><b>Application:</b> cookies with their flags, storage, service workers.</li>
      <li><b>Performance and Lighthouse:</b> long tasks, layout shifts, Core Web Vitals.</li>
      <li><b>Elements:</b> live DOM and CSS, accessibility tree.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Explain what happens from typing a URL to seeing a page</li><li>Read requests, responses, headers and status codes in DevTools</li><li>Know what cookies, sessions and CORS are</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Set caching, cookie and security headers correctly</li><li>Debug CORS, redirects, mixed content and caching problems</li><li>Choose a rendering model for a feature</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Design front-end delivery: CDN, caching, rendering, performance budgets</li><li>Lead web security (CSP, session design) and accessibility for a product</li><li>Diagnose Core Web Vitals problems with field data</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Set web platform standards across products</li><li>Know the HTTP, TLS and browser specs in depth</li><li>Lead large front-end architecture and performance programmes</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>HTTP semantics (RFC 9110) and caching (RFC 9111)</li><li>Browser rendering pipeline and the event loop</li><li>Service workers and offline apps</li><li>Content Security Policy in depth</li><li>WCAG 2.2 success criteria</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MDN Web Docs</a><span class="lnote"> The reference for HTML, CSS, JavaScript and HTTP</span></li><li><a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Learn_web_development/Getting_started/Web_standards/How_the_web_works" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MDN: How the web works</a><span class="lnote"> A gentle overview</span></li><li><a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Guides/CORS" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MDN: CORS</a><span class="lnote"> Preflights, credentials and headers</span></li><li><a href="https://web.dev/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">web.dev</a><span class="lnote"> Google's guides on performance, Core Web Vitals and modern web practice</span></li><li><a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RFC 9110: HTTP Semantics</a><span class="lnote"> Methods, status codes and headers, authoritatively</span></li><li><a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">WCAG 2.2</a><span class="lnote"> Accessibility guidelines</span></li><li><a href="https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OWASP Cheat Sheet Series</a><span class="lnote"> XSS, CSRF, session management and more</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Guides/CORS" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MDN: CORS</a>; <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Reference/Headers/Set-Cookie" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MDN: Set-Cookie</a>; <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/URI/Reference/Fragment" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MDN: URI fragment</a>; <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Elements/script" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MDN: script element</a>; <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RFC 9110 (HTTP semantics)</a>; <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9111" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RFC 9111 (HTTP caching)</a>; <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8246" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RFC 8246 (immutable)</a>; <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6797" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RFC 6797 (HSTS)</a>; <a href="https://web.dev/articles/vitals" target="_blank" rel="noopener">web.dev: Web Vitals</a>; <a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">WCAG 2.2 (contrast)</a>.</p><p class="runcode"><b>Run the code:</b> <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/well-rounded-engineer-fundamentals/tree/main/web" target="_blank" rel="noopener">well-rounded-engineer-fundamentals/web</a>, with a README that says how to run it and what it shows. Stuck, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/well-rounded-engineer-fundamentals/issues/new?title=%5BWeb%20Fundamentals%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= DOCKER ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-docker" data-name="Docker">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Docker</h2><p>Build small, reproducible images, run and debug containers, and compose a local stack. See <a href="#cloudrun">Cloud Run</a> and <a href="#kubernetes">Kubernetes</a> for where the images go.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card"><h3>Mental model</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Image:</b> a read-only stack of layers plus metadata (entrypoint, env, user). Built from a Dockerfile, named <code>registry/repo:tag</code>, pinned exactly by <code>@sha256:digest</code>.</li>
      <li><b>Container:</b> a running process from an image, with its own filesystem layer, network and process namespace. Not a VM: it shares the host kernel.</li>
      <li><b>Layer cache:</b> each instruction is a layer. A change invalidates that layer and every one after it, so put what changes least first.</li>
      <li><b>Volume:</b> storage that outlives the container. Anything written elsewhere disappears with it.</li>
      <li><b>Tag vs digest:</b> a tag can be moved to a new image; a digest cannot. Deploy by digest or by an immutable tag such as the commit SHA.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>The layer cache, drawn</h3>
    <figure class="fig"><svg viewBox="0 0 640 290" role="img" aria-labelledby="layers-t layers-d"><title id="layers-t">How the layer cache decides what to rebuild</title><desc id="layers-d">Two copies of the same Dockerfile, five instructions each, from FROM at the top to CMD at the bottom. On the left, only the source code changed: the first three layers come from the cache and only COPY of the source and the layers after it are rebuilt. On the right, the requirements file changed: that layer and every layer after it are rebuilt, including the slow dependency install. Putting what changes least first keeps rebuilds short.</desc><defs><marker id="layers-a" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker><marker id="layers-aa" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar acc" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker></defs><text class="lb hd" x="160" y="26" text-anchor="middle">you changed src/</text><rect class="nd" x="20" y="40" width="280" height="36" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="32" y="63" text-anchor="start">FROM python:3.13-slim</text><text class="lb mu" x="288" y="63" text-anchor="end">cached</text><rect class="nd" x="20" y="86" width="280" height="36" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="32" y="109" text-anchor="start">COPY requirements.txt</text><text class="lb mu" x="288" y="109" text-anchor="end">cached</text><rect class="nd" x="20" y="132" width="280" height="36" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="32" y="155" text-anchor="start">RUN pip install</text><text class="lb mu" x="288" y="155" text-anchor="end">cached</text><rect class="nd in" x="20" y="178" width="280" height="36" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="32" y="201" text-anchor="start">COPY src/</text><text class="lb acc" x="288" y="201" text-anchor="end">rebuilt</text><rect class="nd in" x="20" y="224" width="280" height="36" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="32" y="247" text-anchor="start">CMD [...]</text><text class="lb acc" x="288" y="247" text-anchor="end">rebuilt</text><text class="lb hd" x="480" y="26" text-anchor="middle">you changed requirements.txt</text><rect class="nd" x="340" y="40" width="280" height="36" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="352" y="63" text-anchor="start">FROM python:3.13-slim</text><text class="lb mu" x="608" y="63" text-anchor="end">cached</text><rect class="nd in" x="340" y="86" width="280" height="36" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="352" y="109" text-anchor="start">COPY requirements.txt</text><text class="lb acc" x="608" y="109" text-anchor="end">rebuilt</text><rect class="nd in" x="340" y="132" width="280" height="36" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="352" y="155" text-anchor="start">RUN pip install</text><text class="lb acc" x="608" y="155" text-anchor="end">rebuilt</text><rect class="nd in" x="340" y="178" width="280" height="36" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="352" y="201" text-anchor="start">COPY src/</text><text class="lb acc" x="608" y="201" text-anchor="end">rebuilt</text><rect class="nd in" x="340" y="224" width="280" height="36" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="352" y="247" text-anchor="start">CMD [...]</text><text class="lb acc" x="608" y="247" text-anchor="end">rebuilt</text><text class="lb acc" x="320" y="282" text-anchor="middle">a change rebuilds its own layer and every layer after it: put what changes least first</text></svg><figcaption>The same five-line Dockerfile twice. Changing your code rebuilds two layers; changing the dependency list rebuilds four, including the slow install. Order the instructions from least to most often changed.</figcaption></figure>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Everyday commands</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>docker build -t api:dev .
docker run --rm -p 8080:8080 -e APP_PROFILE=local api:dev
docker run -d --name db -e POSTGRES_PASSWORD=pw -p 5433:5432 postgres:16
docker ps -a
docker logs -f --tail=100 db
docker exec -it db psql -U postgres
docker stop db &amp;&amp; docker rm db

docker images
docker image inspect api:dev --format '{{.Config.User}} {{.Size}}'
docker history api:dev                   # layer sizes
docker inspect db --format '{{.State.ExitCode}} {{.State.OOMKilled}}'
docker stats                             # live CPU and memory

docker tag api:dev asia-south1-docker.pkg.dev/my-proj/apps/api:3f2a9c1
gcloud auth configure-docker asia-south1-docker.pkg.dev
docker push asia-south1-docker.pkg.dev/my-proj/apps/api:3f2a9c1

docker system df                         # what uses disk
docker system prune                      # stopped containers, unused networks, dangling images, unused build cache
docker builder prune                     # build cache</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>A good Dockerfile</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># syntax=docker/dockerfile:1
FROM python:3.12-slim AS base
ENV PYTHONUNBUFFERED=1 PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1
WORKDIR /app

# dependencies first: cached until requirements change
COPY requirements.txt .
RUN --mount=type=cache,target=/root/.cache/pip pip install -r requirements.txt

# then the code, which changes often
COPY src/ ./src/

RUN useradd --uid 1001 --no-create-home app
USER 1001
EXPOSE 8080
ENTRYPOINT ["python", "-m", "src.main"]</code></pre></div>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># .dockerignore: keeps the build context small and secrets out
.git
.venv
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.env
node_modules/
target/
build/</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Multi-stage builds</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># Build with the full toolchain, ship only the result
FROM eclipse-temurin:21-jdk AS build
WORKDIR /src
COPY gradle/ gradle/
COPY gradlew settings.gradle.kts build.gradle.kts ./
RUN ./gradlew dependencies --no-daemon          # cache dependencies
COPY src/ src/
RUN ./gradlew bootJar --no-daemon -x test

FROM gcr.io/distroless/java21-debian13          # no shell, no package manager
COPY --from=build /src/build/libs/app.jar /app.jar
USER nonroot
ENTRYPOINT ["java", "-XX:MaxRAMPercentage=75", "-jar", "/app.jar"]

# build one stage only
docker build --target build -t api:build .</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">Distroless images have no shell, which makes them smaller and safer but harder to debug. Keep a <code>-debug</code> variant or use an ephemeral debug container.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Base images as a template: update once, like a parent POM</h3>
    <p>In Maven, every project inherits from a parent POM (ultimately the Super POM), so shared settings live in one place and a project picks up a change by moving to a new parent version. Container images can work the same way: one <b>organisation base image</b> holds what every service needs, and every service&rsquo;s Dockerfile starts <code>FROM</code> it.</p>
    <div class="tbl"><table><thead><tr><th>Maven</th><th>Containers</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td>Parent POM, with shared plugins and settings</td><td>Base image, with the JRE, CA certificates, a non-root user, time zone and shared JVM settings</td></tr>
      <tr><td>The <code>&lt;parent&gt;</code> version a project points at</td><td>The base image tag (and digest) in <code>FROM</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Releasing a new parent version</td><td>Rebuilding and publishing the base image</td></tr>
      <tr><td>A bot or a person bumping the parent version in each project</td><td>Dependabot (or a similar bot) opening a pull request to bump the base image in each service</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
    <p><b>1. One base image, built in one place.</b></p>
    <div class="snip"><pre><code># java-runtime/Dockerfile: the organisation's base image, built and published by one pipeline
FROM eclipse-temurin:21-jre@sha256:&lt;digest&gt;      # pinned; Dependabot proposes updates
RUN groupadd --system app &amp;&amp; useradd --system --gid app app
COPY certs/company-root-ca.crt /usr/local/share/ca-certificates/
RUN update-ca-certificates
ENV TZ=UTC
USER app</code></pre></div>
    <p><b>2. Services take the base image as an argument.</b> Docker allows <code>ARG</code> before the first <code>FROM</code>, and <code>FROM</code> can use that value, so the base image is a default you can override at build time.</p>
    <div class="snip"><pre><code># orders-service/Dockerfile: every service starts from the base image
ARG BASE_IMAGE=registry.example.com/platform/java-runtime:21
FROM ${BASE_IMAGE}
WORKDIR /application
COPY target/orders-service.jar app.jar
ENTRYPOINT ["java", "-jar", "app.jar"]</code></pre></div>
    <div class="snip"><pre><code># normal build: the default base image
docker build --pull -t orders-service:dev .

# try a new base image without editing the Dockerfile
docker build --build-arg BASE_IMAGE=registry.example.com/platform/java-runtime:21-rc -t orders-service:test .</code></pre></div>
    <p><b>3. Let a bot keep every service current.</b> Docker&rsquo;s best-practice guide recommends pinning base images to a digest, because tags are mutable, and using Dependabot&rsquo;s <code>docker</code> ecosystem to raise pull requests that update tags and digests on a schedule: you stay in control, with an audit trail. It also recommends rebuilding images often, with <code>--pull</code>, so security fixes in the base reach your images.</p>
    <div class="snip"><pre><code># .github/dependabot.yml in each service repository
version: 2
updates:
  - package-ecosystem: "docker"
    directory: "/"
    schedule:
      interval: "weekly"</code></pre></div>
    <p><i>Our view:</i> a pinned organisation base image, updated by pull requests that run each service&rsquo;s tests, gives the control of a parent POM without surprise changes at build time. <i>The other view:</i> some teams prefer no Dockerfiles at all and let a buildpack builder choose and patch the base (Spring Boot&rsquo;s <code>build-image</code>, below); that centralises the base even further, at the cost of less control over what is inside it.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Images for Spring Boot, Quarkus and Dataflow jobs</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table><thead><tr><th>Kind</th><th>Ways to build the image</th><th>Where the base image is chosen</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td><b>Spring Boot service</b></td><td>A Dockerfile using Spring Boot&rsquo;s layer extraction (below), or <code>mvn spring-boot:build-image</code>, which builds an OCI image with Cloud Native Buildpacks and runs it as a non-root user</td><td>The <code>FROM</code> lines; for buildpacks, the plugin&rsquo;s <code>builder</code> and <code>runImage</code> settings, which can live in the parent POM</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Quarkus service</b></td><td>A container-image extension: Jib (dependencies cached in their own layer, no Docker daemon needed to build), Docker or Podman (using the Dockerfiles Quarkus generates under <code>src/main/docker</code>), OpenShift, or Buildpack</td><td>The generated Dockerfiles, or the extension&rsquo;s configuration; with Jib, a Red Hat UBI OpenJDK runtime image unless you change it</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Dataflow pipeline</b></td><td>A custom SDK container, starting from an Apache Beam SDK image, and a Flex Template image that launches the job (below)</td><td>The Beam SDK image tag, which must match the Java version you launch the pipeline with, and the Google-provided template launcher base image</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
    <p><b>Spring Boot, layered.</b> Spring Boot&rsquo;s documentation shows extracting the jar into four layers (dependencies, the loader, snapshot dependencies, your application), so Docker only re-ships the layer that changed.</p>
    <div class="snip"><pre><code># Spring Boot: split the jar into layers so a code change does not re-ship the dependencies
FROM bellsoft/liberica-openjre-debian:25-cds AS builder
WORKDIR /builder
ARG JAR_FILE=target/*.jar
COPY ${JAR_FILE} application.jar
RUN java -Djarmode=tools -jar application.jar extract --layers --destination extracted

FROM bellsoft/liberica-openjre-debian:25-cds
WORKDIR /application
COPY --from=builder /builder/extracted/dependencies/ ./
COPY --from=builder /builder/extracted/spring-boot-loader/ ./
COPY --from=builder /builder/extracted/snapshot-dependencies/ ./
COPY --from=builder /builder/extracted/application/ ./
ENTRYPOINT ["java", "-jar", "application.jar"]</code></pre></div>
    <p><b>Dataflow.</b> Google recommends that, if a pipeline needs a custom container, the Flex Template uses the same image, so the launcher has the same dependencies as the job. The image copies the template launcher binary from a Google-provided base image and names the main class.</p>
    <div class="snip"><pre><code># Dataflow Flex Template for a Java pipeline: one image that launches the job
FROM gcr.io/dataflow-templates-base/IMAGE_NAME:TAG AS template_launcher   # a Google-provided base image
FROM apache/beam_java11_sdk:BEAM_VERSION    # the pipeline's runtime image; match the Java version you launch with
COPY --from=template_launcher /opt/google/dataflow/java_template_launcher /opt/google/dataflow/java_template_launcher
COPY target/my-pipeline-bundled.jar /template/
ENV FLEX_TEMPLATE_JAVA_MAIN_CLASS="com.example.MyPipeline"
ENV FLEX_TEMPLATE_JAVA_CLASSPATH="/template/*"</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">With the base-image pattern above, the runtime stages of all three can start from images your platform team owns and pins, so one update reaches every service and job through ordinary pull requests.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Instructions that trip people up</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b><code>ENTRYPOINT</code> vs <code>CMD</code>:</b> ENTRYPOINT is the program; CMD supplies default arguments that <code>docker run image …</code> replaces.</li>
      <li><b>Exec form</b> <code>["java","-jar","app.jar"]</code> runs your process as PID 1 so it receives <code>SIGTERM</code>. Shell form <code>java -jar app.jar</code> wraps it in <code>/bin/sh</code>, which may not forward signals.</li>
      <li><b><code>COPY</code> over <code>ADD</code>:</b> ADD also unpacks archives and fetches URLs, which surprises people.</li>
      <li><b><code>ARG</code> vs <code>ENV</code>:</b> ARG exists only at build time; ENV persists into the running container. Neither is a safe place for secrets: use <code>RUN --mount=type=secret</code>.</li>
      <li><b>One <code>RUN</code> per logical step,</b> cleaning up in the same command: <code>apt-get update &amp;&amp; apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends curl &amp;&amp; rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*</code>.</li>
      <li><b>Pin base images</b> to a version (<code>python:3.12-slim</code>) or a digest, never <code>latest</code>.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Docker Compose for local stacks</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># compose.yaml
services:
  db:
    image: postgres:16
    environment:
      POSTGRES_PASSWORD: pw
      POSTGRES_DB: shop
    ports: ["${DB_PORT:-5433}:5432"]
    volumes: [dbdata:/var/lib/postgresql/data]
    healthcheck:
      test: ["CMD-SHELL", "pg_isready -U postgres"]
      interval: 5s
      retries: 10
  api:
    build: .
    ports: ["${APP_PORT:-8080}:8080"]
    environment:
      DB_HOST: db                      # service name is the hostname
    depends_on:
      db: { condition: service_healthy }
volumes:
  dbdata:</code></pre></div>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>docker compose up -d --build
docker compose logs -f api
docker compose exec db psql -U postgres shop
docker compose down            # keep volumes
docker compose down -v         # also delete volumes (data gone)
docker compose -p wt2 up -d    # second copy with its own project name</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Multi-platform &amp; build secrets</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># Apple Silicon builds arm64 by default; Cloud Run and most servers need amd64
docker buildx build --platform linux/amd64 -t REGISTRY/api:3f2a9c1 --push .
docker buildx build --platform linux/amd64,linux/arm64 -t REGISTRY/api:3f2a9c1 --push .
docker image inspect api:dev --format '{{.Os}}/{{.Architecture}}'

# secret available during one RUN, never stored in a layer
docker build --secret id=pip_token,env=PIP_TOKEN .
RUN --mount=type=secret,id=pip_token \
    PIP_INDEX_URL="https://__token__:$(cat /run/secrets/pip_token)@pypi.example.com/simple" \
    pip install -r requirements.txt</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Debugging containers</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Check</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Exits immediately</td><td><code>docker logs</code>; <code>docker inspect … .State.ExitCode</code>. 137 = killed (often OOM), 139 = segfault, 1 = app error, 127 = command not found.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>"exec format error"</td><td>Wrong CPU architecture: rebuild with <code>--platform linux/amd64</code>.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Port works inside, not from host</td><td>App binds to 127.0.0.1; bind 0.0.0.0. Or a missing <code>-p host:container</code>.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Cannot reach another container</td><td>Use the service name on a shared network, not <code>localhost</code>.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Permission denied on a volume</td><td>Container user's UID does not own the host directory.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Image is huge</td><td><code>docker history</code>; move build tools to a build stage; add a <code>.dockerignore</code>.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Build ignores code change</td><td>Cached layer: check COPY order, or <code>--no-cache</code> once.</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>docker run --rm -it --entrypoint sh api:dev        # look inside the image
docker run --rm -it --network container:db nicolaka/netshoot   # debug its network</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Image security</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Run as a non-root user (<code>USER 1001</code>).</li>
      <li>Start from a minimal base (slim, distroless, or Alpine where musl is fine). Fewer packages, fewer CVEs.</li>
      <li>Scan images in CI: <code>trivy image REGISTRY/api:tag</code>, or Artifact Registry's built-in scanning.</li>
      <li>Never bake secrets into an image or a layer; anyone who can pull it can read them with <code>docker history</code> or by unpacking layers.</li>
      <li>Rebuild regularly so base-image security fixes reach you.</li>
      <li>Generate an SBOM (<code>docker buildx build --sbom=true</code> or <code>syft</code>) so you can answer "are we affected?" quickly.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Build, run, stop and inspect containers</li><li>Read a Dockerfile and explain each instruction</li><li>Use docker compose for a local stack</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Write small multi-stage Dockerfiles with good layer caching</li><li>Run as non-root; keep secrets out of layers</li><li>Build for linux/amd64; debug exit codes and OOM kills</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Set image standards: base images, scanning, SBOMs, signing</li><li>Optimise image size and startup time for Cloud Run or Kubernetes</li><li>Design local development environments for a team</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Own the organisation's container supply chain</li><li>Understand the OCI image and runtime specs</li><li>Choose and govern registries, build systems and policies</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>BuildKit cache mounts and secrets</li><li>Distroless and minimal base images</li><li>OCI image format and digests</li><li>Rootless containers</li><li>Image signing with cosign and SLSA provenance</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://docs.docker.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Docker documentation</a><span class="lnote"> Official docs for engine, build and compose</span></li><li><a href="https://docs.docker.com/build/building/best-practices/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Dockerfile best practices</a><span class="lnote"> Caching, multi-stage, small images</span></li><li><a href="https://docs.docker.com/compose/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Docker Compose</a><span class="lnote"> Multi-container local stacks</span></li><li><a href="https://github.com/GoogleContainerTools/distroless" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Distroless images</a><span class="lnote"> Minimal runtime images from Google</span></li><li><a href="https://trivy.dev/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Trivy</a><span class="lnote"> Scan images and dependencies for CVEs</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://docs.docker.com/reference/dockerfile/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Dockerfile reference</a>; <a href="https://docs.docker.com/build/building/secrets/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Build secrets</a>; <a href="https://docs.docker.com/reference/cli/docker/system/prune/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">docker system prune</a>; <a href="https://docs.docker.com/build/metadata/attestations/sbom/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">SBOM attestations</a>; <a href="https://docs.docker.com/reference/cli/docker/compose/down/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">docker compose down</a>; <a href="https://docs.docker.com/compose/how-tos/startup-order/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Compose startup order</a>; <a href="https://docs.docker.com/engine/containers/run/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">docker run exit status</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/GoogleContainerTools/distroless/main/README.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Distroless README (java21-debian13, nonroot, debug tags)</a>; <a href="https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man7/signal.7.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">signal(7)</a>; <a href="https://docs.docker.com/reference/cli/docker/buildx/build/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Docker CLI: docker buildx build (--platform default)</a>; <a href="https://docs.docker.com/reference/dockerfile/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Docker docs: Dockerfile reference, how ARG and FROM interact</a>; <a href="https://docs.docker.com/build/building/best-practices/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Docker docs: Building best practices (pin base images, Dependabot, rebuild often)</a>; <a href="https://docs.spring.io/spring-boot/reference/packaging/container-images/dockerfiles.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Spring Boot docs: Dockerfiles (layered extraction)</a>; <a href="https://docs.spring.io/spring-boot/maven-plugin/build-image.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Spring Boot Maven plugin: Packaging OCI images</a>; <a href="https://github.com/quarkusio/quarkus/blob/main/docs/src/main/asciidoc/container-image.adoc" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Quarkus docs: Container images (source on GitHub)</a>; <a href="https://cloud.google.com/dataflow/docs/guides/templates/configuring-flex-templates" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google Cloud: Configure Flex Templates (custom container images)</a>; <a href="https://cloud.google.com/dataflow/docs/guides/build-container-image" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google Cloud: Build custom container images for Dataflow</a>; <a href="https://github.com/apache/maven-site/blob/master/content/markdown/guides/introduction/introduction-to-the-pom.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Maven: Introduction to the POM, project inheritance (source on GitHub)</a>.</p><p class="runcode"><b>Run the code:</b> <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/well-rounded-engineer-fundamentals/tree/main/docker" target="_blank" rel="noopener">well-rounded-engineer-fundamentals/docker</a>, with a README that says how to run it and what it shows. Stuck, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/well-rounded-engineer-fundamentals/issues/new?title=%5BDocker%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= DATA STRUCTURES AND ALGORITHMS ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-dsa" data-name="Data Structures &amp; Algorithms">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Data structures and algorithms</h2><p>Every program stores data somewhere and does something with it. How the data is arranged decides how fast each operation can be, and the same task can take 20 steps or a million depending on that choice. This page covers how to talk about cost (Big O), the structures you meet every day, the classic algorithms, and where real systems use them. Graph databases are on <a href="#graphdb">Graph Databases</a>; measuring real speed is on <a href="#performance">Performance</a>.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>The short answer</h3>
    <p><i>Our view:</i> pick the structure that makes your most frequent operation cheap. Looking things up by key: a hash table. Keeping things in order: a sorted array or a balanced tree. Always taking the smallest or most urgent next: a heap. Then estimate the cost with Big O, and measure, because constants and hardware matter too.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Time and space complexity, in plain words</h3>
    <p><b>Time complexity</b> asks: if the input gets ten times bigger, how much longer does the work take? <b>Space complexity</b> asks the same about memory. Neither measures seconds or megabytes; they describe how the cost <i>grows</i>, which is what decides whether code that is fine on 100 test rows survives a million real ones.</p>
    <div class="tbl"><table><thead><tr><th>Shape</th><th>An everyday picture</th><th>In code</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td><b>Constant</b>, O(1)</td><td>Collecting your coat with a numbered ticket: one step, however many coats there are</td><td>Reading <code>array[i]</code>; looking up a key in a hash map</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Logarithmic</b>, O(log n)</td><td>Finding a word in a dictionary by opening it in the middle and halving each time</td><td>Binary search; a lookup in a sorted index</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Linear</b>, O(n)</td><td>Reading every name on a list to find one person</td><td>One loop over the data</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>n log n</b></td><td>Sorting a deck of cards well</td><td>Good general-purpose sorting</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Quadratic</b>, O(n²)</td><td>Everyone at a party shaking hands with everyone else</td><td>A loop inside a loop over the same data</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
    <p><b>Reading your own code:</b> one loop over the input is n; a loop inside it is n times n; cutting the problem in half each step is log n; a fixed number of steps is constant. Keep the biggest term and drop the rest: 3n + 50 is just O(n).</p>
    <p><b>The trap is the hidden loop.</b> A call that looks like one step can be a loop of its own. CPython&rsquo;s lists are arrays, so <code>x in some_list</code> checks the items one by one; Java&rsquo;s documentation says <code>ArrayList</code> runs most operations in linear time, while <code>HashSet</code> offers constant-time <code>contains</code> (if the hash function spreads the items well).</p>
    <div class="snip"><pre><code># slow: `in` on a list checks the items one by one, inside a loop -&gt; O(n²)
def has_duplicate(orders):
    seen = []
    for o in orders:
        if o in seen:          # a hidden loop
            return True
        seen.append(o)
    return False

# fast: a set looks up by hash -&gt; O(n) time, at the cost of O(n) extra memory
def has_duplicate(orders):
    seen = set()
    for o in orders:
        if o in seen:
            return True
        seen.add(o)
    return False</code></pre></div>
    <div class="snip"><pre><code>// the same trap in Java: ArrayList.contains is linear, HashSet.contains is constant time
List&lt;String&gt; slow = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();   // slow.contains(o) walks the list
Set&lt;String&gt;  fast = new HashSet&lt;&gt;();     // fast.contains(o) jumps straight to it</code></pre></div>
    <p><b>Space is often the price of time.</b> The fast version above remembers everything it has seen: it uses more memory to do less work. <i>Our view:</i> that trade is usually worth it, and caches, indexes and hash maps all make it; when memory is tight, sorting in place or streaming the data one item at a time can be the better choice.</p>
    <p class="note">Try it: the <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/well-rounded-engineer-labs/tree/main/data-structures/01-slow-duplicate-check" target="_blank" rel="noopener">slow duplicate check</a> drill in the labs is exactly this bug, in Python and Java, with a test that fails until the code is fast enough. The table below puts numbers on each shape.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Big O: how cost grows</h3>
    <p>Big O notation describes how a function grows as its input gets large, as an upper bound, and is used for both time and memory. It ignores constant factors, so it tells you how an approach scales, not how fast it runs today.</p>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Notation</th><th>Name</th><th>n = 1,000</th><th>n = 1,000,000</th><th>Typical example</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>O(1)</td><td>constant</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>array access by index; hash table lookup on average</td></tr>
      <tr><td>O(log n)</td><td>logarithmic</td><td>about 10</td><td>about 20</td><td>binary search; balanced tree lookup</td></tr>
      <tr><td>O(n)</td><td>linear</td><td>1,000</td><td>1,000,000</td><td>scanning a list</td></tr>
      <tr><td>O(n log n)</td><td>linearithmic</td><td>about 10,000</td><td>about 20 million</td><td>good general-purpose sorting</td></tr>
      <tr><td>O(n<sup>2</sup>)</td><td>quadratic</td><td>1,000,000</td><td>10<sup>12</sup></td><td>comparing every pair</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note">The columns are rough step counts (log<sub>2</sub>), worked out for illustration. The jump from n to n<sup>2</sup> is why a nested loop that is fine on test data can stall in production.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>The structures you meet every day</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Structure</th><th>Good at</th><th>Watch out for</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td><b>Array</b></td><td>Constant-time access by position</td><td>Inserting in the middle shifts everything after it</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Dynamic array</b> (such as Java&rsquo;s ArrayList)</td><td>Appending: it grows by a large factor such as doubling, so each append costs constant time on average (amortised)</td><td>The occasional resize copies everything</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Linked list</b></td><td>Inserting or removing where you already are</td><td>No random access: reaching item k means walking k links</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Stack</b> and <b>queue</b></td><td>Last in, first out, and first in, first out</td><td>Only the ends are cheap to reach</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Hash table</b> (dict, map)</td><td>Lookup by key in about constant time on average, independent of size</td><td>Collisions; the worst case is linear</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Balanced binary search tree</b></td><td>Ordered data with lookups, inserts and deletes in O(log n)</td><td>More work per operation than a plain sorted array that rarely changes (<i>analysis</i>)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>B-tree</b></td><td>Sorted data on disk: wide nodes keep the tree shallow, so few blocks are read. Used in databases and file systems</td><td>Usually provided by your database rather than written by hand (<i>analysis</i>)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Heap</b></td><td>A priority queue: always the smallest (or largest) next</td><td>Not for searching arbitrary items</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Trie</b> (prefix tree)</td><td>Prefix lookups: autocomplete, spell checking, IP routing</td><td>Memory per node</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Graph</b></td><td>Relationships: networks, dependencies, routes</td><td>Algorithms are more involved</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Bloom filter</b> (Burton Bloom, 1970)</td><td>A small, probabilistic &ldquo;have I seen this?&rdquo; that answers &ldquo;possibly yes&rdquo; or &ldquo;definitely no&rdquo;</td><td>False positives are possible; false negatives are not</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Inside a hash table</h3>
    <figure class="fig"><svg viewBox="0 0 620 270" role="img" aria-labelledby="ht-t ht-d"><title id="ht-t">A hash table with separate chaining</title><desc id="ht-d">Three keys, ana, ben and cyd, pass through a hash function that maps each to one of five buckets numbered 0 to 4. ana goes to bucket 1. ben and cyd both go to bucket 3, a collision, so bucket 3 holds a short linked list of two entries.</desc><defs><marker id="ht-a" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker><marker id="ht-aa" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar acc" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker></defs><text class="lb hd" x="50" y="34" text-anchor="middle">keys</text><text class="lb hd" x="200" y="34" text-anchor="middle">hash function</text><text class="lb hd" x="330" y="34" text-anchor="middle">buckets</text><text class="lb hd" x="490" y="34" text-anchor="middle">entries</text><rect class="nd" x="150" y="60" width="100" height="150" rx="8"/><text class="lb" x="200" y="132" text-anchor="middle">hash(key)</text><text class="lb mu" x="200" y="152" text-anchor="middle">mod 5</text><rect class="nd in" x="14" y="54" width="72" height="32" rx="4"/><text class="lb" x="50" y="75" text-anchor="middle">ana</text><line class="ed" x1="86" y1="70" x2="146" y2="70" marker-end="url(#ht-a)"/><rect class="nd in" x="14" y="119" width="72" height="32" rx="4"/><text class="lb" x="50" y="140" text-anchor="middle">ben</text><line class="ed" x1="86" y1="135" x2="146" y2="135" marker-end="url(#ht-a)"/><rect class="nd in" x="14" y="184" width="72" height="32" rx="4"/><text class="lb" x="50" y="205" text-anchor="middle">cyd</text><line class="ed" x1="86" y1="200" x2="146" y2="200" marker-end="url(#ht-a)"/><rect class="nd" x="300" y="60" width="60" height="32" rx="2"/><text class="lb mu" x="330" y="81" text-anchor="middle">0</text><rect class="nd" x="300" y="96" width="60" height="32" rx="2"/><text class="lb mu" x="330" y="117" text-anchor="middle">1</text><rect class="nd" x="300" y="132" width="60" height="32" rx="2"/><text class="lb mu" x="330" y="153" text-anchor="middle">2</text><rect class="nd" x="300" y="168" width="60" height="32" rx="2"/><text class="lb mu" x="330" y="189" text-anchor="middle">3</text><rect class="nd" x="300" y="204" width="60" height="32" rx="2"/><text class="lb mu" x="330" y="225" text-anchor="middle">4</text><line class="ed" x1="250" y1="70" x2="296" y2="112" marker-end="url(#ht-a)"/><line class="ed" x1="250" y1="135" x2="296" y2="184" marker-end="url(#ht-a)"/><line class="ed" x1="250" y1="200" x2="296" y2="184" marker-end="url(#ht-a)"/><line class="ed" x1="360" y1="112" x2="416" y2="112" marker-end="url(#ht-a)"/><rect class="nd out" x="420" y="96" width="90" height="32" rx="4"/><text class="lb" x="465" y="117" text-anchor="middle">ana → 7</text><line class="ed" x1="360" y1="184" x2="416" y2="184" marker-end="url(#ht-a)"/><rect class="nd out" x="420" y="168" width="90" height="32" rx="4"/><text class="lb" x="465" y="189" text-anchor="middle">ben → 3</text><line class="ed acc" x1="510" y1="184" x2="522" y2="184" marker-end="url(#ht-aa)"/><rect class="nd out" x="526" y="168" width="88" height="32" rx="4"/><text class="lb" x="570" y="189" text-anchor="middle">cyd → 9</text><text class="lb acc" x="520" y="226" text-anchor="middle">collision: a chain</text></svg><figcaption>A hash table with separate chaining. The hash function turns each key into a bucket number; two keys that land in the same bucket (a collision) share it as a short linked list. With few collisions, a lookup costs about the same however many entries there are. Each entry is key &rarr; value.</figcaption></figure>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Searching</h3>
    <p>Binary search finds a value in a <b>sorted</b> array by halving the range each step, so a million items take about 20 comparisons. Python&rsquo;s <code>bisect</code> module does this for you, and a set does hash lookups.</p>
<pre><code>from bisect import bisect_left

def linear_steps(items, target):
    for steps, value in enumerate(items, start=1):
        if value == target:
            return steps
    return len(items)

def binary_steps(items, target):
    lo, hi, steps = 0, len(items) - 1, 0
    while lo &lt;= hi:
        steps += 1
        mid = (lo + hi) // 2
        if items[mid] == target:
            return steps
        if items[mid] &lt; target:
            lo = mid + 1
        else:
            hi = mid - 1
    return steps

items = list(range(1_000_000))          # already sorted
target = 999_999
print("linear:", linear_steps(items, target), "steps")
print("binary:", binary_steps(items, target), "steps")
print("bisect finds index", bisect_left(items, target))
print("set lookup:", target in set(items))
</code></pre>
<pre><code>linear: 1000000 steps
binary: 20 steps
bisect finds index 999999
set lookup: True</code></pre>
    <p class="note">Run as shown with Python 3; the second block is its real output.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Sorting</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Merge sort</b> (John von Neumann, 1945): divide and conquer, O(n log n), and most implementations are <i>stable</i>, keeping equal items in their original order.</li>
      <li><b>Quicksort</b> (Tony Hoare, 1959): efficient in practice; its worst case, O(n<sup>2</sup>), is rare when the pivot is chosen well, but a naive pivot makes already-sorted input the worst case.</li>
      <li><b>Timsort</b> (Tim Peters, 2002): a stable hybrid of merge sort and insertion sort that uses runs already in order. It was Python&rsquo;s standard sort from 2.3 until 3.11 replaced it with Powersort, and Java SE 7 uses it for arrays of objects.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note"><i>Analysis:</i> in everyday code, call your language&rsquo;s built-in sort; knowing how they work tells you when stability or the worst case matters. The other view: a hand-written sort can win when the data has a known shape, but measure first.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Graphs and harder problems</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Breadth-first search</b> explores all nodes at one depth before the next: the fewest hops between two nodes in an unweighted graph.</li>
      <li><b>Depth-first search</b> goes as far as possible along each branch before backtracking: cycles, dependency order.</li>
      <li><b>Dijkstra&rsquo;s algorithm</b> (conceived in 1956) finds shortest paths in a weighted graph, such as a road network, with non-negative weights.</li>
      <li><b>Dynamic programming</b> (Richard Bellman, 1950s) solves a problem from the stored answers to overlapping subproblems, instead of recomputing them.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Choose by requirement</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>If this is true for you</th><th>It leans towards (<i>analysis</i>)</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Most operations look things up by an exact key</td><td>A hash table (dict, map, set)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>You need items in order, or ranges (&ldquo;everything between A and F&rdquo;)</td><td>A sorted array if it rarely changes; a balanced tree if it changes often</td></tr>
      <tr><td>You always want the most urgent or smallest item next</td><td>A heap (priority queue)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Data lives on disk and must be found with few reads</td><td>A B-tree index, which your database already uses</td></tr>
      <tr><td>You must check membership in a huge set with little memory, and an occasional false &ldquo;maybe&rdquo; is acceptable</td><td>A Bloom filter in front of the real lookup</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Prefix search or autocomplete</td><td>A trie</td></tr>
      <tr><td>The data is small (hundreds of items)</td><td>Whatever is simplest to read (<i>our view</i>): Big O ignores constant factors, and at that size they can dominate</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Our view, and the other view</h3>
    <p><i>Our view:</i> every engineer should be able to estimate the Big O of their own code and know the common structures well enough to pick one. It prevents the nested loop that only fails at production scale.</p>
    <p><i>The other view:</i> libraries, databases and frameworks already embody these choices, and interview-style puzzles test something different from day-to-day work. Measuring real code with a profiler often teaches more than theory.</p>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Read and compare O(1), O(log n), O(n), O(n log n) and O(n^2)</li><li>Explain arrays, lists, hash tables, stacks and queues</li><li>Write a binary search</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Pick a structure for the most frequent operation</li><li>Spot an accidental O(n^2) loop and fix it</li><li>Use heaps, sets and sorted collections from the standard library</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Explain B-tree indexes, tries and Bloom filters, and where systems use them</li><li>Choose graph algorithms: BFS, DFS, Dijkstra</li><li>Solve problems with dynamic programming</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Design data layouts for scale and hardware (cache, disk)</li><li>Review designs for algorithmic risk</li><li>Teach and interview for these skills fairly</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>Big O notation</li><li>Arrays, lists and hash tables</li><li>Trees, heaps and tries</li><li>Searching and sorting</li><li>Graph algorithms</li><li>Dynamic programming</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://docs.python.org/3/library/bisect.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Python: bisect</a><span class="lnote"> Binary search in the standard library</span></li><li><a href="https://docs.python.org/3/library/heapq.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Python: heapq</a><span class="lnote"> A heap in the standard library</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Big_O_notation" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Big O notation</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Time_complexity" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Time complexity</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Array_(data_structure)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Array</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dynamic_array" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Dynamic array</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Linked_list" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Linked list</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stack_(abstract_data_type)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Stack</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Queue_(abstract_data_type)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Queue</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hash_table" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Hash table</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Self-balancing_binary_search_tree" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Self-balancing binary search tree</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/B-tree" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: B-tree</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heap_(data_structure)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Heap</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trie" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Trie</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bloom_filter" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Bloom filter</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Binary_search" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Binary search</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Merge_sort" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Merge sort</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quicksort" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Quicksort</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timsort" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Timsort</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Breadth-first_search" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Breadth-first search</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Depth-first_search" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Depth-first search</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dijkstra%27s_algorithm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Dijkstra's algorithm</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dynamic_programming" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Dynamic programming</a>; <a href="https://docs.python.org/3/library/bisect.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Python docs: bisect</a>; <a href="https://docs.python.org/3/faq/design.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Python docs: Design FAQ, how lists and dictionaries are implemented</a>; <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/21/docs/api/java.base/java/util/ArrayList.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Java SE 21 API: ArrayList (operation costs)</a>; <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/21/docs/api/java.base/java/util/HashSet.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Java SE 21 API: HashSet (constant-time operations)</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Time_complexity" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Time complexity</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Space_complexity" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Space complexity</a>.</p><p class="runcode"><b>Run the code:</b> <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/well-rounded-engineer-fundamentals/tree/main/dsa" target="_blank" rel="noopener">well-rounded-engineer-fundamentals/dsa</a>, with a README that says how to run it and what it shows. Stuck, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/well-rounded-engineer-fundamentals/issues/new?title=%5BData%20Structures%20%26%20Algorithms%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= CONCURRENCY ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-concurrency" data-name="Concurrency">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Concurrency</h2><p>A web server handling thousands of requests, a phone app that stays responsive while it downloads, a database serving many users at once: all of them do several things in overlapping time. That is concurrency, and it brings a class of bugs that appear only under particular timing. This page covers the core ideas, the classic bugs, the tools for avoiding them, and the main models. Language specifics are on <a href="#java">Java</a> (virtual threads), <a href="#go">Go</a>, <a href="#rust">Rust</a> and <a href="#javascript">JavaScript</a> (the event loop); across machines, see <a href="#distsys">Distributed Systems</a>.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>The short answer</h3>
    <p>Concurrency means several computations in overlapping time periods; <b>parallelism</b> means running at the same physical instant, on separate processors, usually to speed a computation up. Wherever two threads share data that one of them changes, you need a rule for who may touch it when.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Race conditions: the lost update</h3>
    <p>A <b>race condition</b> is when a system&rsquo;s behaviour depends on the sequence or timing of events it does not control. They are hard to reproduce, because the result depends on timing between threads, and they can disappear when you add logging or run in a debugger.</p>
    <figure class="fig"><svg viewBox="0 0 620 330" role="img" aria-labelledby="lu-t lu-d"><title id="lu-t">A lost update between two threads</title><desc id="lu-d">A UML sequence diagram with three lifelines: thread A, the shared balance, and thread B. The balance starts at 0. Thread A reads 0. Thread B reads 0. Thread A writes 0 plus 100, so the balance is 100. Thread B writes 0 plus 100, also 100. Two deposits of 100 leave a balance of 100: one update is lost.</desc><defs><marker id="lu-a" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker><marker id="lu-aa" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar acc" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker><marker id="lu-o" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ed" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8"/></marker></defs><rect class="nd in" x="30" y="14" width="140" height="36" rx="4"/><text class="lb hd" x="100" y="37" text-anchor="middle">Thread A</text><line class="ln" x1="100" y1="50" x2="100" y2="320"/><rect class="nd out" x="240" y="14" width="140" height="36" rx="4"/><text class="lb hd" x="310" y="37" text-anchor="middle">balance</text><line class="ln" x1="310" y1="50" x2="310" y2="320"/><rect class="nd in" x="450" y="14" width="140" height="36" rx="4"/><text class="lb hd" x="520" y="37" text-anchor="middle">Thread B</text><line class="ln" x1="520" y1="50" x2="520" y2="320"/><text class="lb mu" x="318" y="72" text-anchor="start">{balance = 0}</text><line class="ed" x1="100" y1="95" x2="306" y2="95" marker-end="url(#lu-a)"/><text class="lb" x="205.0" y="88" text-anchor="middle">read</text><line class="ed dash" x1="310" y1="111" x2="104" y2="111" marker-end="url(#lu-o)"/><text class="lb mu" x="205.0" y="127" text-anchor="middle">sees 0</text><line class="ed" x1="520" y1="145" x2="314" y2="145" marker-end="url(#lu-a)"/><text class="lb" x="415.0" y="138" text-anchor="middle">read</text><line class="ed dash" x1="310" y1="161" x2="516" y2="161" marker-end="url(#lu-o)"/><text class="lb mu" x="415.0" y="177" text-anchor="middle">sees 0</text><line class="ed acc" x1="100" y1="200" x2="306" y2="200" marker-end="url(#lu-aa)"/><text class="lb acc" x="205.0" y="193" text-anchor="middle">write 0 + 100</text><line class="ed acc" x1="520" y1="255" x2="314" y2="255" marker-end="url(#lu-aa)"/><text class="lb acc" x="415.0" y="248" text-anchor="middle">write 0 + 100</text><text class="lb mu" x="318" y="222" text-anchor="start">{balance = 100}</text><text class="lb acc" x="318" y="290" text-anchor="start">balance = 100, not 200:</text><text class="lb acc" x="318" y="308" text-anchor="start">one update lost</text></svg><figcaption>A lost update, as a UML sequence diagram. Both threads read the balance before either writes it back, so the second write overwrites the first. The code below forces exactly this order, then fixes it with a lock.</figcaption></figure>
<pre><code>import threading

balance = 0
both_have_read = threading.Barrier(2)
lock = threading.Lock()

def deposit_unsafe(amount):
    global balance
    seen = balance            # 1. read
    both_have_read.wait()     # (force the bad interleaving: both threads read first)
    balance = seen + amount   # 2. write, based on a stale read

def deposit_safe(amount):
    global balance
    with lock:                # read and write as one step
        balance = balance + amount

def run(worker):
    global balance
    balance = 0
    threads = [threading.Thread(target=worker, args=(100,)) for _ in range(2)]
    for t in threads: t.start()
    for t in threads: t.join()
    return balance

print("two deposits of 100, unsafe:", run(deposit_unsafe))
print("two deposits of 100, locked:", run(deposit_safe))
</code></pre>
<pre><code>two deposits of 100, unsafe: 100
two deposits of 100, locked: 200</code></pre>
    <p class="note">Run as shown with Python 3; the second block is its real output, the same on every run because the barrier forces the bad order. In real code the order is not forced, which is why the bug comes and goes.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Deadlock</h3>
    <p>A deadlock is a set of threads each waiting for a resource another holds. Edward Coffman described four conditions in 1971; all four must hold for a deadlock:</p>
    <ol>
      <li><b>Mutual exclusion:</b> a resource can be used by only one at a time.</li>
      <li><b>Hold and wait:</b> a thread holds one resource while waiting for another.</li>
      <li><b>No preemption:</b> a resource is released only voluntarily.</li>
      <li><b>Circular wait:</b> each waits for the next, in a cycle.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="note">Most prevention breaks one condition, especially the circular wait. <i>Analysis:</i> the everyday fix is to take locks in one fixed global order. <i>The other view:</i> a fixed order is hard to keep across a large code base, so some teams use timeouts or lock-free structures instead.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Tools for shared data</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Mutex (lock):</b> only one thread at a time runs the protected section.</li>
      <li><b>Semaphore:</b> a counter that limits how many may enter, invented by Edsger Dijkstra in 1962 or 1963.</li>
      <li><b>Compare-and-swap:</b> an atomic processor instruction that updates a value only if it still holds what you last read; the basis of lock-free structures.</li>
      <li><b>Optimistic concurrency:</b> do the work, then check for conflicting changes at commit and retry if there were any; common in databases.</li>
      <li><b>Memory models</b> define when one thread&rsquo;s writes become visible to another. In Java, everything before releasing a lock is visible to the next thread that acquires it (the <i>happens-before</i> order).</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Models of concurrency</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>The idea</th><th>Where you meet it</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td><b>Threads and locks</b></td><td>Threads share memory; locks guard it</td><td>Java, C#; Python&rsquo;s <code>threading</code> module</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Lightweight threads</b></td><td>Many cheap threads managed by the runtime; a blocked one does not hold an operating-system thread</td><td>Java&rsquo;s virtual threads, stable since Java 21</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Event loop and async/await</b></td><td>One thread runs many tasks, switching at each wait</td><td>JavaScript, Python&rsquo;s asyncio</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Communicating sequential processes</b> (Tony Hoare, 1978)</td><td>Share data by sending it over channels between processes</td><td>Go, whose channels follow the CSP idea (see <a href="#go">Go</a>)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Actors</b> (1973)</td><td>Each actor has private state and reacts to messages: it can decide, create actors and send messages</td><td>Erlang, Akka</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note">In CPython, a global interpreter lock lets only one thread run Python code at a time, so threads help with waiting on I/O more than with CPU work. Python 3.13 added an experimental free-threaded build without it (PEP 703).</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>How much parallelism helps: Amdahl&rsquo;s law</h3>
    <p>Gene Amdahl&rsquo;s law (1967) predicts the speed-up from more processors. If a fraction <i>p</i> of the work can run in parallel on <i>s</i> processors, the speed-up is 1 / ((1 &minus; p) + p / s).</p>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>p = 90% parallel</th><th>Speed-up</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>2 processors</td><td>1.82&times;</td></tr>
      <tr><td>8 processors</td><td>4.71&times;</td></tr>
      <tr><td>64 processors</td><td>8.77&times;</td></tr>
      <tr><td>unlimited</td><td>10&times;, never more</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note">Worked out from the formula. The serial 10% sets the ceiling, however many processors you add.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Choose by requirement</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>If this is true for you</th><th>It leans towards (<i>analysis</i>)</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Many requests that mostly wait on I/O (databases, other services)</td><td>Virtual threads, or async/await, or an event loop: many waits, few CPU cores</td></tr>
      <tr><td>CPU-heavy work that can be split</td><td>Parallel workers or processes, sized by Amdahl&rsquo;s law; in CPython, processes rather than threads</td></tr>
      <tr><td>A small piece of shared state updated by several threads</td><td>A lock, or an atomic or concurrent collection from the standard library</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Components that should not share memory at all</td><td>Channels or actors: pass messages instead</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Rows that many users may change at once</td><td>The database&rsquo;s transactions, with optimistic concurrency (a version column) where conflicts are rare</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Your team already uses one model well</td><td>Stay with it unless a requirement above forces a change: switching has a cost</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Our view, and the other view</h3>
    <p><i>Our view:</i> share as little mutable state as you can, and where you must share it, use a higher-level tool your platform offers (a concurrent collection, a database transaction, a channel) before reaching for raw locks.</p>
    <p><i>The other view:</i> high-level abstractions hide costs and can make performance harder to predict; for latency-critical code, some teams prefer explicit locks or lock-free structures that they can measure and reason about directly.</p>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Tell concurrency from parallelism</li><li>Explain a race condition with an example</li><li>Use a lock around shared state</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Spot and fix a lost update</li><li>Explain the four conditions for deadlock and avoid one</li><li>Choose threads, async or processes for a task</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Choose a concurrency model by requirement</li><li>Use optimistic concurrency in a database</li><li>Estimate parallel speed-up with Amdahl's law</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Set concurrency standards for a platform</li><li>Review designs for shared mutable state</li><li>Diagnose timing bugs in production</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>Concurrency and parallelism</li><li>Race conditions and deadlock</li><li>Locks, semaphores and atomics</li><li>Memory models</li><li>Threads, async, CSP and actors</li><li>Amdahl's law</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://docs.python.org/3/library/threading.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Python: threading</a><span class="lnote"> Threads, locks and barriers in the standard library</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Concurrent_computing" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Concurrent computing</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Race_condition" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Race condition</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deadlock_(computer_science)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Deadlock</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mutual_exclusion" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Mutual exclusion</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Semaphore_(programming)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Semaphore</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Compare-and-swap" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Compare-and-swap</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Optimistic_concurrency_control" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Optimistic concurrency control</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Java_memory_model" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Java memory model</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green_thread" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Green thread</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Async/await" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Async/await</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Event_loop" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Event loop</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Communicating_sequential_processes" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Communicating sequential processes</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Actor_model" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Actor model</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Global_interpreter_lock" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Global interpreter lock</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amdahl%27s_law" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Amdahl's law</a>; <a href="https://docs.python.org/3/library/threading.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Python docs: threading</a>.</p><p class="runcode"><b>Run the code:</b> <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/well-rounded-engineer-fundamentals/tree/main/concurrency" target="_blank" rel="noopener">well-rounded-engineer-fundamentals/concurrency</a>, with a README that says how to run it and what it shows. Stuck, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/well-rounded-engineer-fundamentals/issues/new?title=%5BConcurrency%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= SQL SKILLS ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-sql" data-name="SQL Skills">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Getting better at SQL and BigQuery</h2><p>How SQL actually evaluates a query, the patterns that solve most real problems, the mistakes that give wrong answers quietly, and exercises with solutions. Written in BigQuery GoogleSQL. The <a href="#bigquery">BigQuery</a> tab is the syntax reference.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Practice schema used on this tab</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>orders(order_id STRING, order_ref STRING, store_id STRING, order_date DATE,
       order_year INT64, items ARRAY&lt;STRING&gt;, raw_date STRING, _landed_at TIMESTAMP)
stores(store_id STRING, name STRING, state STRING)</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">Practise for free in the BigQuery sandbox (no billing account needed) against <code>bigquery-public-data</code>, for example <code>bigquery-public-data.stackoverflow.posts_questions</code> or <code>bigquery-public-data.new_york_taxi_trips</code>. Always dry-run first and select only the columns you need.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>The order SQL really runs in</h3>
    <ol>
      <li><code>FROM</code> and <code>JOIN</code>: build the row set</li>
      <li><code>WHERE</code>: filter rows</li>
      <li><code>GROUP BY</code>: form groups</li>
      <li>aggregates computed</li>
      <li><code>HAVING</code>: filter groups</li>
      <li>window functions computed</li>
      <li><code>QUALIFY</code>: filter on window results</li>
      <li><code>SELECT</code> expressions, then <code>DISTINCT</code></li>
      <li><code>ORDER BY</code></li>
      <li><code>LIMIT</code></li>
    </ol>
    <p class="note">This explains most errors: you cannot use a window function in <code>WHERE</code> (it does not exist yet; use <code>QUALIFY</code>), and <code>WHERE</code> cannot filter on an aggregate (use <code>HAVING</code>).</p>
      <figure class="fig"><svg viewBox="0 0 660 250" role="img" aria-labelledby="sqlorder-t sqlorder-d"><title id="sqlorder-t">The order a SQL query really runs in</title><desc id="sqlorder-d">Two columns. On the left, the order you write a query in: SELECT, FROM, WHERE, GROUP BY, HAVING, QUALIFY, ORDER BY, LIMIT. On the right, the order it runs in, numbered: 1 FROM and JOIN build the rows, 2 WHERE filters rows, 3 GROUP BY forms groups and aggregates are computed, 4 HAVING filters groups, 5 window functions are computed, 6 QUALIFY filters on them, 7 SELECT and DISTINCT, 8 ORDER BY, 9 LIMIT. SELECT is written first but runs late, which is why WHERE cannot use a window function or an aggregate.</desc><defs><marker id="sqlorder-a" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker><marker id="sqlorder-aa" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar acc" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker></defs><text class="lb hd" x="110" y="22" text-anchor="middle">how you write it</text><text class="lb hd" x="470" y="22" text-anchor="middle">how it runs</text><rect class="nd in" x="40" y="32" width="140" height="22" rx="4"/><text class="lb" x="110" y="48" text-anchor="middle">SELECT</text><rect class="nd" x="40" y="58" width="140" height="22" rx="4"/><text class="lb" x="110" y="74" text-anchor="middle">FROM … JOIN</text><rect class="nd" x="40" y="84" width="140" height="22" rx="4"/><text class="lb" x="110" y="100" text-anchor="middle">WHERE</text><rect class="nd" x="40" y="110" width="140" height="22" rx="4"/><text class="lb" x="110" y="126" text-anchor="middle">GROUP BY</text><rect class="nd" x="40" y="136" width="140" height="22" rx="4"/><text class="lb" x="110" y="152" text-anchor="middle">HAVING</text><rect class="nd" x="40" y="162" width="140" height="22" rx="4"/><text class="lb" x="110" y="178" text-anchor="middle">QUALIFY</text><rect class="nd" x="40" y="188" width="140" height="22" rx="4"/><text class="lb" x="110" y="204" text-anchor="middle">ORDER BY</text><rect class="nd" x="40" y="214" width="140" height="22" rx="4"/><text class="lb" x="110" y="230" text-anchor="middle">LIMIT</text><rect class="nd" x="370" y="32" width="200" height="21" rx="4"/><text class="lb" x="390" y="47" text-anchor="start">1  FROM, JOIN: build rows</text><rect class="nd" x="370" y="56" width="200" height="21" rx="4"/><text class="lb" x="390" y="71" text-anchor="start">2  WHERE: filter rows</text><rect class="nd" x="370" y="80" width="200" height="21" rx="4"/><text class="lb" x="390" y="95" text-anchor="start">3  GROUP BY + aggregates</text><rect class="nd" x="370" y="104" width="200" height="21" rx="4"/><text class="lb" x="390" y="119" text-anchor="start">4  HAVING: filter groups</text><rect class="nd" x="370" y="128" width="200" height="21" rx="4"/><text class="lb" x="390" y="143" text-anchor="start">5  window functions</text><rect class="nd" x="370" y="152" width="200" height="21" rx="4"/><text class="lb" x="390" y="167" text-anchor="start">6  QUALIFY: filter windows</text><rect class="nd in" x="370" y="176" width="200" height="21" rx="4"/><text class="lb" x="390" y="191" text-anchor="start">7  SELECT, DISTINCT</text><rect class="nd" x="370" y="200" width="200" height="21" rx="4"/><text class="lb" x="390" y="215" text-anchor="start">8  ORDER BY</text><rect class="nd" x="370" y="224" width="200" height="21" rx="4"/><text class="lb" x="390" y="239" text-anchor="start">9  LIMIT</text><path class="ed acc" d="M180,43 C280,43 280,184 366,184" marker-end="url(#sqlorder-aa)"/><text class="lb acc" x="250" y="172" text-anchor="middle">written first,</text><text class="lb acc" x="250" y="189" text-anchor="middle">runs 7th</text></svg><figcaption>The list above beside the order you type. Each step can only use what earlier steps produced: WHERE runs before aggregates and windows exist, so it cannot filter on them; HAVING and QUALIFY can.</figcaption></figure>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Write it so it reads top to bottom</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>WITH
landed AS (                         -- 1. narrow the input early
  SELECT order_id, store_id, order_date
  FROM `p.analytics.orders`
  WHERE order_date BETWEEN '2024-01-01' AND '2024-12-31'
),
per_store AS (                      -- 2. aggregate at one grain
  SELECT store_id, COUNT(*) AS n
  FROM landed
  GROUP BY store_id
)
SELECT c.name, COALESCE(p.n, 0) AS n  -- 3. join for labels last
FROM `p.ref.stores` AS c
LEFT JOIN per_store AS p USING (store_id)
ORDER BY n DESC;</code></pre></div>
    <ul>
      <li>One CTE per step, named for what it holds.</li>
      <li>State each CTE's grain in a comment when it is not obvious.</li>
      <li>Filter and aggregate before joining wide tables.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>NULL: the source of quiet bugs</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><code>NULL = NULL</code> is NULL, not TRUE. Use <code>IS NULL</code>, or <code>a IS NOT DISTINCT FROM b</code> to compare nullable values.</li>
      <li><code>COUNT(*)</code> counts rows; <code>COUNT(col)</code> skips NULLs. <code>AVG</code>, <code>SUM</code>, <code>MIN</code> ignore NULLs too.</li>
      <li><code>x NOT IN (subquery)</code> returns <b>no rows</b> if the subquery yields a single NULL. Use <code>NOT EXISTS</code>.</li>
      <li><code>WHERE status != 'X'</code> drops rows where status is NULL.</li>
      <li><code>SUM</code> of no rows is NULL, not 0: wrap in <code>COALESCE(…, 0)</code>.</li>
      <li><code>SAFE_CAST</code> and <code>SAFE.PARSE_DATE</code> turn bad input into NULL. Count those NULLs, or the failure is invisible.</li>
    </ul>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>SELECT
  COUNT(*)                                             AS total_rows,
  COUNT(order_date)                                    AS with_date,
  COUNTIF(raw_date IS NOT NULL AND order_date IS NULL) AS failed_parse
FROM `p.analytics.orders`;</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Joins without surprises</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Know the grain of both sides</b> before joining. Joining one-to-many multiplies rows; a <code>SUM</code> afterwards is inflated (fan-out).</li>
      <li><b>Aggregate first, then join</b> when you need totals from two child tables.</li>
      <li><b>A filter on the right table in <code>WHERE</code></b> turns a <code>LEFT JOIN</code> into an inner join. Put it in <code>ON</code>.</li>
      <li><b>Check row counts</b> before and after each join while developing.</li>
    </ul>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>-- WRONG: stores with no 2024 orders disappear
SELECT c.store_id, COUNT(o.order_id) n
FROM stores c LEFT JOIN orders o USING (store_id)
WHERE o.order_year = 2024
GROUP BY 1;

-- RIGHT: the filter belongs to the join
SELECT c.store_id, COUNT(o.order_id) n
FROM stores c
LEFT JOIN orders o
  ON o.store_id = c.store_id AND o.order_year = 2024
GROUP BY 1;

-- did my join fan out? keys should be unique on the "one" side
SELECT store_id, COUNT(*) FROM stores GROUP BY 1 HAVING COUNT(*) &gt; 1;</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Semi-joins and anti-joins</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>-- semi-join: stores that HAVE at least one order (no duplicates)
SELECT * FROM stores c
WHERE EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM orders o WHERE o.store_id = c.store_id);

-- anti-join: stores with NO order
SELECT * FROM stores c
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM orders o WHERE o.store_id = c.store_id);

-- same anti-join with LEFT JOIN
SELECT c.* FROM stores c
LEFT JOIN orders o USING (store_id)
WHERE o.store_id IS NULL;

-- set operations (columns by position)
SELECT order_ref FROM a EXCEPT DISTINCT SELECT order_ref FROM b;   -- in a, not in b
SELECT order_ref FROM a INTERSECT DISTINCT SELECT order_ref FROM b;
SELECT order_ref FROM a UNION ALL SELECT order_ref FROM b;          -- keeps duplicates, cheaper</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Aggregation toolkit</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>SELECT
  store_id,
  COUNT(*)                                  AS n,
  COUNT(DISTINCT order_ref)                 AS orders_n,
  COUNTIF(order_date IS NULL)               AS undated,
  SAFE_DIVIDE(COUNTIF(order_date IS NULL), COUNT(*)) AS undated_share,
  MIN(order_date) AS first_d, MAX(order_date) AS last_d,
  ANY_VALUE(order_ref HAVING MAX order_date) AS latest_order_ref,   -- value at the max
  STRING_AGG(DISTINCT CAST(order_year AS STRING), ',' ORDER BY CAST(order_year AS STRING)) AS years,
  APPROX_QUANTILES(DATE_DIFF(DATE(_landed_at), order_date, DAY), 100)[OFFSET(50)] AS median_lag_days,
  LOGICAL_AND(order_date IS NOT NULL)       AS all_dated
FROM `p.analytics.orders`
GROUP BY ALL;            -- groups by every non-aggregated column

-- subtotals in one pass
SELECT state, store_id, COUNT(*) n
FROM orders JOIN stores USING (store_id)
GROUP BY ROLLUP (state, store_id);       -- per store, per state, grand total</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Window functions, properly</h3>
    <p>A window function computes a value for each row from a set of related rows, <b>without collapsing them</b>. Three parts: <code>PARTITION BY</code> (which rows are related), <code>ORDER BY</code> (their sequence), and the frame (which of them count for this row).</p>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>SELECT
  store_id, d, n,
  ROW_NUMBER() OVER w_date                            AS rn,          -- 1,2,3 no ties
  RANK()       OVER (PARTITION BY store_id ORDER BY n DESC) AS rnk, -- 1,1,3
  DENSE_RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY store_id ORDER BY n DESC) AS drnk,-- 1,1,2
  LAG(n)  OVER w_date                                 AS prev_day,
  LEAD(n) OVER w_date                                 AS next_day,
  SUM(n)  OVER (PARTITION BY store_id ORDER BY d
                ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW) AS running_total,
  AVG(n)  OVER (PARTITION BY store_id ORDER BY d
                ROWS BETWEEN 6 PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW)         AS avg_7_rows,
  AVG(n)  OVER (PARTITION BY store_id ORDER BY UNIX_DATE(d)
                RANGE BETWEEN 6 PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW)        AS avg_7_days,
  n / SUM(n) OVER (PARTITION BY store_id)           AS share_of_store,
  FIRST_VALUE(d) OVER w_date                          AS first_day,
  LAST_VALUE(d)  OVER (w_date ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND UNBOUNDED FOLLOWING) AS last_day
FROM daily
WINDOW w_date AS (PARTITION BY store_id ORDER BY d);</code></pre></div>
    <ul>
      <li><b>ROWS vs RANGE:</b> <code>ROWS 6 PRECEDING</code> means six rows back, even if days are missing. <code>RANGE</code> on a numeric day number means six calendar days back. Fill gaps with a date spine or use RANGE.</li>
      <li><b>Default frame trap:</b> with <code>ORDER BY</code> and no frame, the frame ends at the current row, so <code>LAST_VALUE</code> returns the current row. Give an explicit frame.</li>
      <li><b>Ties:</b> <code>ROW_NUMBER</code> picks arbitrarily among ties. Add a tie-breaker column to <code>ORDER BY</code> so results are repeatable.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Pattern: top N per group</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>-- 3 most recent orders per store
SELECT store_id, order_ref, order_date
FROM `p.analytics.orders`
WHERE order_date IS NOT NULL
QUALIFY ROW_NUMBER() OVER (
  PARTITION BY store_id
  ORDER BY order_date DESC, order_id      -- tie-breaker
) &lt;= 3;</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">Use <code>RANK</code> instead of <code>ROW_NUMBER</code> if ties should all be included.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Pattern: deduplicate</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>-- find duplicates first: never dedupe blind
SELECT order_ref, COUNT(*) AS copies, COUNT(DISTINCT _landed_at) AS loads
FROM `p.raw.orders`
GROUP BY order_ref HAVING COUNT(*) &gt; 1
ORDER BY copies DESC LIMIT 20;

-- keep the latest copy of each key
SELECT * FROM `p.raw.orders`
QUALIFY ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY order_ref ORDER BY _landed_at DESC) = 1;

-- exact duplicate rows only
SELECT DISTINCT * EXCEPT (_run_id) FROM t;   -- leave out the column that differs on every load</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Pattern: date spine and missing days</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>WITH days AS (
  SELECT d FROM UNNEST(GENERATE_DATE_ARRAY('2025-01-01', '2025-03-31')) AS d
),
grid AS (                                  -- every store × every day
  SELECT c.store_id, days.d FROM `p.ref.stores` c CROSS JOIN days
),
actual AS (
  SELECT store_id, order_date AS d, COUNT(*) AS n
  FROM `p.analytics.orders`
  WHERE order_date BETWEEN '2025-01-01' AND '2025-03-31'
  GROUP BY 1, 2
)
SELECT g.store_id, g.d, COALESCE(a.n, 0) AS n
FROM grid g LEFT JOIN actual a USING (store_id, d)
ORDER BY 1, 2;</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">Without the spine, days with zero rows simply do not appear, and averages and LAGs are computed over the wrong set.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Pattern: gaps and islands</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>-- streaks of consecutive days on which a store took orders
WITH d AS (
  SELECT DISTINCT store_id, order_date
  FROM `p.analytics.orders` WHERE order_date IS NOT NULL
),
tagged AS (
  SELECT *,
    DATE_SUB(order_date,
      INTERVAL ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY store_id ORDER BY order_date) DAY
    ) AS island                            -- constant within a run of consecutive days
  FROM d
)
SELECT store_id, MIN(order_date) AS start_d, MAX(order_date) AS end_d,
       COUNT(*) AS days
FROM tagged
GROUP BY store_id, island
ORDER BY days DESC;</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">The trick: a date minus its row number stays the same while dates are consecutive and jumps at each gap.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Pattern: change vs previous period</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>WITH yearly AS (
  SELECT store_id, order_year, COUNT(*) AS n
  FROM `p.analytics.orders` GROUP BY ALL
)
SELECT * FROM (
  SELECT *,
    LAG(n) OVER (PARTITION BY store_id ORDER BY order_year) AS prev_n
  FROM yearly
)
WHERE SAFE_DIVIDE(n - prev_n, prev_n) &lt; -0.2;   -- fell by more than 20%</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">LAG takes the previous <i>row</i>. If a year is missing for a store, "previous" is two years back. Use a spine when that matters.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Pattern: arrays (one-to-many in one row)</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>-- top 3 items per store by number of orders
WITH per_item AS (
  SELECT store_id, TRIM(item) AS item, COUNT(*) AS n
  FROM `p.analytics.orders`, UNNEST(items) AS item
  GROUP BY ALL
)
SELECT * FROM per_item
QUALIFY ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY store_id ORDER BY n DESC, item) &lt;= 3;

-- filter rows by array content without exploding
SELECT order_ref FROM `p.analytics.orders`
WHERE EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM UNNEST(items) i WHERE i LIKE '%UMBRELLA%');

-- array size, and rows whose array is empty or NULL
SELECT ARRAY_LENGTH(items) AS n_items FROM t;
SELECT COUNTIF(ARRAY_LENGTH(items) = 0 OR items IS NULL) FROM t;</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">The comma before <code>UNNEST</code> is a CROSS JOIN: rows with an empty array vanish. Use <code>LEFT JOIN UNNEST(items) AS item</code> to keep them.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Pattern: pivot and unpivot</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>-- one row per store, a column per year
SELECT * FROM (
  SELECT store_id, order_year, order_id FROM `p.analytics.orders`
  WHERE order_year BETWEEN 2023 AND 2025
)
PIVOT (COUNT(order_id) FOR order_year IN (2023 AS y2023, 2024 AS y2024, 2025 AS y2025));

-- conditional counts without PIVOT (COUNTIF is BigQuery; elsewhere use COUNT(CASE WHEN … THEN 1 END) or FILTER)
SELECT store_id,
  COUNTIF(order_year = 2023) AS y2023,
  COUNTIF(order_year = 2024) AS y2024,
  COUNTIF(order_year = 2025) AS y2025
FROM `p.analytics.orders` GROUP BY store_id;

-- columns back to rows
SELECT * FROM wide UNPIVOT (n FOR year IN (y2023, y2024, y2025));</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Pattern: recursive hierarchy</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>-- every category under X, directly or through a chain (max depth 5)
WITH RECURSIVE chain AS (
  SELECT category_id, parent_id, 1 AS depth
  FROM `p.analytics.categories`
  WHERE parent_id = 'X'
  UNION ALL
  SELECT c.category_id, c.parent_id, chain.depth + 1
  FROM `p.analytics.categories` c
  JOIN chain ON c.parent_id = chain.category_id
  WHERE chain.depth &lt; 5                    -- always bound the recursion
)
SELECT category_id, MIN(depth) AS depth FROM chain GROUP BY category_id;</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Pattern: point-in-time join (SCD type 2)</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>-- which store name was valid on the order date?
SELECT o.order_ref, o.order_date, s.name
FROM `p.analytics.orders` o
JOIN `p.snapshots.store_snapshot` s
  ON s.store_id = o.store_id
 AND o.order_date &gt;= DATE(s.dbt_valid_from)
 AND (s.dbt_valid_to IS NULL OR o.order_date &lt; DATE(s.dbt_valid_to));</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">Half-open intervals (<code>&gt;= from AND &lt; to</code>) never match two versions for one date.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Reusable logic</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>-- temporary SQL function for this script
CREATE TEMP FUNCTION parse_any_date(s STRING) AS (
  COALESCE(SAFE.PARSE_DATE('%Y-%m-%d', s), SAFE.PARSE_DATE('%d-%m-%Y', s))
);
SELECT raw_date, parse_any_date(raw_date) FROM t;

-- persistent function in a dataset
CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION `p.util.safe_ratio`(a FLOAT64, b FLOAT64) AS (
  IF(b = 0 OR b IS NULL, NULL, a / b)
);

-- table function: a parameterised view
CREATE OR REPLACE TABLE FUNCTION `p.util.store_year`(c STRING, y INT64) AS
SELECT * FROM `p.analytics.orders` WHERE store_id = c AND order_year = y;
SELECT COUNT(*) FROM `p.util.store_year`('1_05', 2024);</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Making BigQuery fast and cheap</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Habit</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Select named columns</td><td>Bytes billed = bytes of the columns you read. One wide text column can be most of a table.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Filter on the partition column with literals or parameters</td><td>Lets BigQuery skip partitions before reading. Wrapping the column in a function can prevent it.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Filter on clustered columns</td><td>Skips blocks inside partitions; benefit shows in "bytes processed" after the run.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Dry-run before running</td><td>Shows bytes to be scanned for free. Set a maximum bytes billed on risky jobs.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Aggregate early, join late</td><td>Shuffling fewer rows between stages is the main cost in slot time.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Avoid <code>ORDER BY</code> without <code>LIMIT</code> on huge results</td><td>Sorting a very large result can fail with &quot;Resources exceeded&quot;; put <code>ORDER BY</code> in the outermost query and add a <code>LIMIT</code>.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Use <code>APPROX_COUNT_DISTINCT</code> for exploration</td><td>Exact distinct counts on billions of rows are expensive; the approximate version returns a statistical estimate and uses fewer resources.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Materialise reused intermediate results</td><td>A CTE referenced several times may be computed several times. Write it to a table (or temp table in a script) when it is big.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Read the execution graph</td><td>In the console's Execution details: look for a stage with skewed time (one huge key), large shuffle, or spill to disk.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Avoid self-joins for running totals</td><td>A window function does in one pass what a self-join does quadratically.</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Mistakes that give wrong answers quietly</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Fan-out from a one-to-many join before a <code>SUM</code>.</li>
      <li>A right-table filter in <code>WHERE</code> after a <code>LEFT JOIN</code>.</li>
      <li><code>NOT IN</code> against a column that contains NULL.</li>
      <li>Integer division in other engines (<code>1/2 = 0</code>); BigQuery's <code>/</code> returns FLOAT64, but <code>DIV()</code> is integer.</li>
      <li><code>BETWEEN</code> on timestamps: <code>BETWEEN '2025-01-01' AND '2025-01-31'</code> misses most of the 31st. Use <code>&gt;= start AND &lt; next_day</code>.</li>
      <li>Time zones: <code>DATE(ts)</code> is UTC unless you pass a zone (<code>DATE(ts, 'Asia/Kolkata')</code>).</li>
      <li><code>ROW_NUMBER</code> without a tie-breaker: a different row "wins" on each run.</li>
      <li><code>LIMIT</code> without <code>ORDER BY</code>: an arbitrary sample, not the top rows.</li>
      <li>Averaging averages, or averaging percentages, instead of recomputing from totals.</li>
      <li>Safe-parsing into NULL and never counting the NULLs.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Check your own query</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Grain:</b> what does one output row mean? Is the key unique? (<code>COUNT(*)</code> vs <code>COUNT(DISTINCT key)</code>)</li>
      <li><b>Totals:</b> does the sum across groups equal the ungrouped total?</li>
      <li><b>Rows in vs out:</b> did a join drop or multiply rows you did not expect?</li>
      <li><b>Edges:</b> empty groups, NULLs, the first and last day, ties.</li>
      <li><b>One example by hand:</b> pick one key and check its row against the source.</li>
      <li><b>Cost:</b> dry-run bytes reasonable for the question?</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Exercises (open each for a solution)</h3>
    <p class="note">Try each on the practice schema, or adapt it to a public dataset. Solutions are one good answer, not the only one.</p>

    <details><summary><span class="ex">1.</span> Orders per store in 2024, including stores with none.</summary><div class="inner">
<div class="snip"><pre><code>SELECT c.store_id, c.name, COUNT(o.order_id) AS n
FROM `p.ref.stores` c
LEFT JOIN `p.analytics.orders` o
  ON o.store_id = c.store_id AND o.order_year = 2024
GROUP BY ALL
ORDER BY n DESC;</code></pre></div>
      <p class="note">Filter in <code>ON</code>, and count a column from the right table so empty stores give 0.</p></div></details>

    <details><summary><span class="ex">2.</span> The most recent order per store, with ties broken consistently.</summary><div class="inner">
<div class="snip"><pre><code>SELECT store_id, order_ref, order_date
FROM `p.analytics.orders`
WHERE order_date IS NOT NULL
QUALIFY ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY store_id
                           ORDER BY order_date DESC, order_id) = 1;</code></pre></div></div></details>

    <details><summary><span class="ex">3.</span> What share of each store's rows failed to parse their date? Only stores above 1%.</summary><div class="inner">
<div class="snip"><pre><code>SELECT store_id,
       COUNT(*) AS n,
       COUNTIF(raw_date IS NOT NULL AND order_date IS NULL) AS failed,
       SAFE_DIVIDE(COUNTIF(raw_date IS NOT NULL AND order_date IS NULL), COUNT(*)) AS failed_share
FROM `p.analytics.orders`
GROUP BY store_id
HAVING SAFE_DIVIDE(COUNTIF(raw_date IS NOT NULL AND order_date IS NULL), COUNT(*)) &gt; 0.01
ORDER BY failed_share DESC;</code></pre></div></div></details>

    <details><summary><span class="ex">4.</span> Daily counts for one store with a 7-day moving average, including days with zero.</summary><div class="inner">
<div class="snip"><pre><code>WITH days AS (
  SELECT d FROM UNNEST(GENERATE_DATE_ARRAY('2025-01-01', '2025-06-30')) d
),
daily AS (
  SELECT order_date AS d, COUNT(*) n FROM `p.analytics.orders`
  WHERE store_id = '1_05' AND order_date BETWEEN '2025-01-01' AND '2025-06-30'
  GROUP BY 1
)
SELECT days.d, COALESCE(n, 0) AS n,
       AVG(COALESCE(n, 0)) OVER (ORDER BY days.d ROWS BETWEEN 6 PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW) AS ma7
FROM days LEFT JOIN daily USING (d)
ORDER BY d;</code></pre></div>
      <p class="note">The spine makes ROWS equal to days, so the moving average is over seven calendar days.</p></div></details>

    <details><summary><span class="ex">5.</span> Stores whose yearly count fell by more than 20% compared with the previous year.</summary><div class="inner">
<div class="snip"><pre><code>WITH y AS (
  SELECT store_id, order_year, COUNT(*) n FROM `p.analytics.orders` GROUP BY ALL
),
c AS (
  SELECT *, LAG(n) OVER (PARTITION BY store_id ORDER BY order_year) AS prev_n,
            LAG(order_year) OVER (PARTITION BY store_id ORDER BY order_year) AS prev_year
  FROM y
)
SELECT *, SAFE_DIVIDE(n - prev_n, prev_n) AS change
FROM c
WHERE prev_year = order_year - 1               -- only compare adjacent years
  AND SAFE_DIVIDE(n - prev_n, prev_n) &lt; -0.2;</code></pre></div></div></details>

    <details><summary><span class="ex">6.</span> Find duplicate keys, then build the deduplicated table keeping the latest load.</summary><div class="inner">
<div class="snip"><pre><code>SELECT order_ref, COUNT(*) copies FROM `p.raw.orders`
GROUP BY order_ref HAVING COUNT(*) &gt; 1;

CREATE OR REPLACE TABLE `p.scratch.orders_dedup` AS
SELECT * FROM `p.raw.orders`
QUALIFY ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY order_ref ORDER BY _landed_at DESC) = 1;

-- verify: should return 0
SELECT COUNT(*) - COUNT(DISTINCT order_ref) FROM `p.scratch.orders_dedup`;</code></pre></div></div></details>

    <details><summary><span class="ex">7.</span> The longest run of consecutive trading days for each store.</summary><div class="inner">
<div class="snip"><pre><code>WITH d AS (
  SELECT DISTINCT store_id, order_date FROM `p.analytics.orders`
  WHERE order_date IS NOT NULL
),
t AS (
  SELECT *, DATE_SUB(order_date, INTERVAL
           ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY store_id ORDER BY order_date) DAY) AS grp
  FROM d
),
runs AS (
  SELECT store_id, MIN(order_date) s, MAX(order_date) e, COUNT(*) len
  FROM t GROUP BY store_id, grp
)
SELECT * FROM runs
QUALIFY ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY store_id ORDER BY len DESC, s) = 1;</code></pre></div></div></details>

    <details><summary><span class="ex">8.</span> Each store's share of all 2024 orders, and its rank within its state.</summary><div class="inner">
<div class="snip"><pre><code>WITH n AS (
  SELECT c.state, o.store_id, COUNT(*) n
  FROM `p.analytics.orders` o JOIN `p.ref.stores` c USING (store_id)
  WHERE o.order_year = 2024
  GROUP BY ALL
)
SELECT *,
  n / SUM(n) OVER ()                                  AS share_all,
  RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY state ORDER BY n DESC)     AS rank_in_state
FROM n
ORDER BY state, rank_in_state;</code></pre></div></div></details>

    <details><summary><span class="ex">9.</span> Stores present in the reference table but absent from the landed data, and the reverse.</summary><div class="inner">
<div class="snip"><pre><code>(SELECT 'missing_data' AS issue, store_id FROM `p.ref.stores`
 EXCEPT DISTINCT
 SELECT 'missing_data', store_id FROM `p.analytics.orders`)
UNION ALL
(SELECT 'unknown_store', store_id FROM `p.analytics.orders`
 EXCEPT DISTINCT
 SELECT 'unknown_store', store_id FROM `p.ref.stores`);</code></pre></div>
      <p class="note">Parenthesise when mixing different set operators; BigQuery requires it.</p></div></details>

    <details><summary><span class="ex">10.</span> Median, 90th percentile and maximum lag (days) between order and landing, per store.</summary><div class="inner">
<div class="snip"><pre><code>SELECT store_id,
  APPROX_QUANTILES(lag_d, 100)[OFFSET(50)] AS p50,
  APPROX_QUANTILES(lag_d, 100)[OFFSET(90)] AS p90,
  MAX(lag_d) AS max_lag
FROM (
  SELECT store_id, DATE_DIFF(DATE(_landed_at), order_date, DAY) AS lag_d
  FROM `p.analytics.orders` WHERE order_date IS NOT NULL
)
GROUP BY store_id
ORDER BY p90 DESC;</code></pre></div></div></details>

    <details><summary><span class="ex">11.</span> Upsert a day's new rows into a target table without creating duplicates.</summary><div class="inner">
<div class="snip"><pre><code>MERGE `p.mart.serving` T
USING (
  SELECT * FROM `p.analytics.orders`
  WHERE DATE(_landed_at) = @run_date
  QUALIFY ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY order_id ORDER BY _landed_at DESC) = 1
) S
ON T.order_id = S.order_id
WHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET order_date = S.order_date, order_ref = S.order_ref
WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT ROW;</code></pre></div>
      <p class="note">Deduplicate the source first: MERGE fails if two source rows match one target row.</p></div></details>

    <details><summary><span class="ex">12.</span> Explain why this returns no rows: <code>SELECT * FROM stores WHERE store_id NOT IN (SELECT store_id FROM orders)</code>.</summary><div class="inner">
      <p>If any <code>orders.store_id</code> is NULL, <code>x NOT IN (…, NULL)</code> evaluates to NULL for every x, never TRUE, so no row passes. Use <code>NOT EXISTS</code>, or add <code>WHERE store_id IS NOT NULL</code> inside the subquery.</p></div></details>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>A plan to get better</h3>
    <ol>
      <li><b>Week 1:</b> the evaluation order, NULLs and joins. Redo exercises 1, 9 and 12 until they feel obvious.</li>
      <li><b>Week 2:</b> window functions. Exercises 2, 4, 5 and 8; then rewrite one self-join you have seen as a window.</li>
      <li><b>Week 3:</b> patterns. Gaps and islands, date spines, dedup, pivots (6, 7).</li>
      <li><b>Week 4:</b> BigQuery cost and plans. Dry-run everything, read the execution graph of your slowest query, and cut its bytes in half.</li>
      <li><b>Ongoing:</b> before sharing any number, run the self-check list on this tab.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="note">Read other people's SQL: dbt models in your repo, and the queries behind dashboards you use. Ask "what is the grain?" of each.</p>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>SELECT, WHERE, GROUP BY, ORDER BY and simple joins</li><li>Know how NULL behaves in comparisons and aggregates</li><li>Dry-run before running in BigQuery</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Window functions, QUALIFY, CTEs, semi- and anti-joins</li><li>Check grain and fan-out before trusting a number</li><li>Write readable, tested SQL in dbt</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Patterns: gaps and islands, spines, SCD joins, recursion</li><li>Read execution plans and cut cost and slot time</li><li>Review others' SQL for correctness and cost</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Design query patterns and models for a whole platform</li><li>Teach SQL; set standards and linting</li><li>Know engine internals well enough to predict plans</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>Logical vs physical query plans</li><li>Approximate aggregation (HyperLogLog, quantiles)</li><li>Pipe syntax and modern SQL features</li><li>SQL style guides and SQLFluff linting</li><li>Testing SQL with dbt unit tests</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/reference/standard-sql/query-syntax" target="_blank" rel="noopener">BigQuery query syntax</a><span class="lnote"> The full GoogleSQL SELECT reference</span></li><li><a href="https://modern-sql.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Modern SQL</a><span class="lnote"> Window functions, recursion and newer standard features across engines</span></li><li><a href="https://use-the-index-luke.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Use The Index, Luke</a><span class="lnote"> How queries and indexes really interact</span></li><li><a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/tutorial-window.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PostgreSQL tutorial: window functions</a><span class="lnote"> A clear introduction to windows</span></li><li><a href="https://docs.sqlfluff.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">SQLFluff</a><span class="lnote"> Linter and formatter for SQL, including dbt and BigQuery</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/reference/standard-sql/query-syntax" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GoogleSQL query syntax (QUALIFY order, SELECT DISTINCT, set operators)</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/reference/standard-sql/aggregate_functions" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GoogleSQL aggregate functions (ANY_VALUE HAVING, COUNTIF, NULL handling)</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/reference/standard-sql/operators" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GoogleSQL operators (IS NOT DISTINCT FROM, division result types)</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/reference/standard-sql/window-function-calls" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GoogleSQL window function calls (RANGE needs numeric key, UNIX_DATE tip)</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/reference/standard-sql/date_functions" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GoogleSQL date functions (default time zone UTC)</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/reference/standard-sql/dml-syntax" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GoogleSQL DML (MERGE must match at most one source row)</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/best-practices-performance-compute" target="_blank" rel="noopener">BigQuery compute best practices (CTE evaluated multiple times, ORDER BY)</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/best-practices-costs" target="_blank" rel="noopener">BigQuery cost best practices (dry run)</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/clustered-tables" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Clustered tables (no accurate estimate before run)</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/sandbox" target="_blank" rel="noopener">BigQuery sandbox (no billing account)</a>; <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/sql-expressions.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PostgreSQL aggregate functions (no COUNTIF; FILTER clause)</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/reference/standard-sql/approximate_aggregate_functions" target="_blank" rel="noopener">BigQuery: approximate aggregate functions</a>.</p><p class="runcode"><b>Run the code:</b> <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/well-rounded-engineer-data/tree/main/sql" target="_blank" rel="noopener">well-rounded-engineer-data/sql</a>, with a README that says how to run it and what it shows. Stuck, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/well-rounded-engineer-data/issues/new?title=%5BSQL%20Skills%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= DATABASE DESIGN ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-database" data-name="Database Design">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Database design</h2><p>Relational modelling, indexes, partitioning, sharding, transactions and migrations. Examples in PostgreSQL; the ideas carry to other engines. Warehouse design is on the <a href="#bigquery">BigQuery</a> tab.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card"><h3>Modelling rules</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Start normalised</b> (third normal form): each fact stored once, in the table it describes. Denormalise later, on purpose, for a measured read need.</li>
      <li><b>Every table has a primary key.</b> Surrogate (<code>bigint</code> identity or UUID) for internal identity; natural keys (order reference, email) get a <code>UNIQUE</code> constraint.</li>
      <li><b>Never reuse or renumber a key</b> that others store: URLs, foreign systems, legal records.</li>
      <li><b>Constraints are free correctness:</b> <code>NOT NULL</code>, <code>UNIQUE</code>, <code>CHECK</code>, <code>FOREIGN KEY</code>. The database enforces them for every writer, including the one you forgot.</li>
      <li><b>Right types:</b> <code>date</code> / <code>timestamptz</code> not strings; <code>numeric</code> for money, never float; <code>text</code> over arbitrary <code>varchar(n)</code>; <code>jsonb</code> for truly variable attributes, not as a substitute for columns.</li>
      <li><b>Name things plainly</b> and consistently: <code>snake_case</code>, singular or plural but one choice, <code>_at</code> for timestamps, <code>_id</code> for keys.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>A schema example</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>CREATE TABLE store (
  store_id text PRIMARY KEY,
  name     text NOT NULL
);

CREATE TABLE orders (                         -- "order" is a reserved word
  id          bigint GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,
  order_id    text NOT NULL UNIQUE,             -- stable natural key
  order_ref   text NOT NULL,                    -- what the customer sees
  store_id    text NOT NULL REFERENCES store,
  order_date  date,
  status      text NOT NULL CHECK (status IN ('PAID','PENDING')),
  notes       text,
  created_at  timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT now(),
  updated_at  timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT now()
);

-- many-to-many through a join table
CREATE TABLE order_item (
  order_id   text NOT NULL REFERENCES orders (order_id) ON DELETE CASCADE,
  product_id text NOT NULL REFERENCES product,
  quantity   int  NOT NULL CHECK (quantity &gt; 0),
  PRIMARY KEY (order_id, product_id)
);</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Index types (PostgreSQL)</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Good for</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>B-tree (default)</td><td>Equality, ranges, sorting, prefix <code>LIKE 'abc%'</code> (with C collation, or an index using <code>text_pattern_ops</code>)</td><td><code>CREATE INDEX ON orders (order_date);</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Composite</td><td>Filters on several columns; column order matters</td><td><code>(store_id, order_date DESC)</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Covering (<code>INCLUDE</code>)</td><td>Answering a query from the index alone (index-only scan)</td><td><code>(store_id) INCLUDE (order_ref, order_date)</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Partial</td><td>Indexing only the rows you query; small and fast</td><td><code>(id) WHERE status = 'PENDING'</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Expression</td><td>Queries on a computed value</td><td><code>(lower(email))</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Unique</td><td>Enforcing uniqueness, and lookups</td><td><code>UNIQUE (store_id, order_ref)</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>GIN</td><td>Full-text search (<code>tsvector</code>), <code>jsonb</code> containment, arrays</td><td><code>USING gin (to_tsvector('english', notes))</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>BRIN</td><td>Huge tables physically ordered by a column (append-only by time); tiny index</td><td><code>USING brin (created_at)</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>HNSW / IVFFlat (pgvector)</td><td>Approximate nearest-neighbour search on embeddings</td><td><code>USING hnsw (embedding vector_cosine_ops)</code></td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Index rules of thumb</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Index for your queries,</b> not your columns. Start from the slow query's <code>WHERE</code>, <code>JOIN</code> and <code>ORDER BY</code>.</li>
      <li><b>Composite order:</b> equality columns first, then the range or sort column. <code>(store_id, order_date)</code> serves <code>store_id = ? AND order_date &gt; ?</code>; the reverse order serves it poorly.</li>
      <li><b>Leftmost prefix:</b> an index on <code>(a, b, c)</code> helps most with queries on <code>a</code>, <code>a,b</code>, <code>a,b,c</code>. On <code>b</code> alone it helps little: PostgreSQL can still use it through a skip scan, but only when <code>a</code> has few distinct values.</li>
      <li><b>Foreign keys</b> usually need an index on the referencing column, or deletes and joins scan.</li>
      <li><b>Every index slows writes</b> and uses disk. Drop unused ones (<code>pg_stat_user_indexes.idx_scan = 0</code>).</li>
      <li><b>Low-selectivity columns</b> (a boolean, a status with 3 values) rarely help alone; use a partial index instead.</li>
      <li><b>Functions on columns</b> (<code>WHERE date(created_at) = …</code>) skip a plain index; write a range or index the expression.</li>
      <li><b>Build on live tables with <code>CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY</code></b>, then confirm it is valid: an interrupted build leaves an INVALID index.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Reading a query plan</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)
SELECT order_ref, order_date FROM orders
WHERE store_id = '1_05' AND order_date &gt;= '2024-01-01'
ORDER BY order_date DESC LIMIT 20;

-- look for:
--   Seq Scan on a big table          → missing or unusable index
--   rows=10 (estimated) vs actual rows=500000 → stale stats: ANALYZE
--   Sort … external merge Disk       → work_mem too low, or index the sort
--   Nested Loop with huge inner loops → join order or missing index
--   Index Only Scan, Heap Fetches: 0 → ideal

-- check index validity after a concurrent build
SELECT indexrelid::regclass, indisvalid FROM pg_index WHERE NOT indisvalid;
-- slowest queries (pg_stat_statements extension)
SELECT query, calls, mean_exec_time FROM pg_stat_statements
ORDER BY total_exec_time DESC LIMIT 10;</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Partitioning (one database)</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>CREATE TABLE event (
  id         bigserial,                -- fine here; GENERATED … AS IDENTITY on a partitioned table needs PostgreSQL 17+
  event_date date NOT NULL,
  payload    jsonb,
  PRIMARY KEY (id, event_date)          -- must include the partition key
) PARTITION BY RANGE (event_date);

CREATE TABLE event_2025 PARTITION OF event
  FOR VALUES FROM ('2025-01-01') TO ('2026-01-01');
CREATE TABLE event_2026 PARTITION OF event
  FOR VALUES FROM ('2026-01-01') TO ('2027-01-01');
CREATE TABLE event_default PARTITION OF event DEFAULT;

-- retire old data instantly instead of a slow DELETE
ALTER TABLE event DETACH PARTITION event_2025;
DROP TABLE event_2025;

-- other kinds:  PARTITION BY LIST (region)   PARTITION BY HASH (customer_id)</code></pre></div>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Why:</b> queries that filter on the key skip other partitions (pruning); old data drops in one statement; maintenance runs per partition.</li>
      <li><b>When:</b> very large tables (tens of millions of rows and up) with a natural time or category filter. Small tables only get slower.</li>
      <li><b>Cost:</b> unique constraints must include the partition key; queries without it scan every partition.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Sharding (many databases)</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>What:</b> split rows across separate database servers by a shard key, so each holds part of the data and load.</li>
      <li><b>Shard key choice is the whole design:</b> high cardinality, evenly spread, present in almost every query (tenant ID, user ID).</li>
      <li><b>Hash sharding</b> spreads load evenly but makes range queries hit every shard. <b>Range sharding</b> keeps ranges together but creates hot shards (all of today's writes on one).</li>
      <li><b>Directory / lookup</b> sharding maps keys to shards in a table: flexible, but the directory becomes critical.</li>
      <li><b>Costs:</b> cross-shard joins and transactions become application problems; resharding is a migration project; operations multiply.</li>
      <li><b>Try first:</b> better indexes, read replicas, caching, partitioning, a bigger instance, archiving cold data. Shard when one primary truly cannot take the writes.</li>
      <li><b>Managed or distributed options</b> do it for you: Spanner, CockroachDB, Citus (Postgres), Vitess (MySQL). AlloyDB read pools scale reads only; they do not shard.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Replication &amp; scaling reads</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Read replicas</b> copy the primary asynchronously. Scale reads; accept replica lag (a user may not see their own write).</li>
      <li><b>Synchronous replicas</b> confirm writes before commit: no data loss on failover, at the cost of write latency.</li>
      <li><b>High availability</b> (Cloud SQL HA, Patroni) promotes a standby when the primary fails.</li>
      <li><b>Connection pooling</b> (PgBouncer, HikariCP, R2DBC pool): Postgres connections are expensive. Size pools so instances × pool size stays under <code>max_connections</code>.</li>
      <li><b>Caching</b> (Redis) for hot, read-heavy data, with a deliberate invalidation rule.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Replica lag, drawn</h3>
    <figure class="fig"><svg viewBox="0 0 640 290" role="img" aria-labelledby="replag-t replag-d"><title id="replag-t">Read-your-writes and replica lag</title><desc id="replag-d">A sequence diagram with three lifelines: the app, the primary database and an asynchronous read replica. 1: the app writes a change to the primary. 2: the primary confirms the commit. 3: the app reads from the replica straight away. 4: the replica has not yet received the change and returns the old value. 5: the change reaches the replica a moment later. The user did not see their own write.</desc><defs><marker id="replag-a" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker><marker id="replag-aa" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar acc" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker></defs><rect class="nd in" x="43.33333333333333" y="14" width="140" height="36" rx="4"/><text class="lb hd" x="113.33333333333333" y="37" text-anchor="middle">App</text><line class="ln" x1="113.33333333333333" y1="50" x2="113.33333333333333" y2="282"/><rect class="nd out" x="250.0" y="14" width="140" height="36" rx="4"/><text class="lb hd" x="320.0" y="37" text-anchor="middle">Primary</text><line class="ln" x1="320.0" y1="50" x2="320.0" y2="282"/><rect class="nd" x="456.66666666666663" y="14" width="140" height="36" rx="4"/><text class="lb hd" x="526.6666666666666" y="37" text-anchor="middle">Read replica</text><line class="ln" x1="526.6666666666666" y1="50" x2="526.6666666666666" y2="282"/><line class="ed" x1="113.33333333333333" y1="80" x2="316.0" y2="80" marker-end="url(#replag-a)"/><circle class="nd in" cx="126.33333333333333" cy="68" r="9"/><text class="lb mu" x="126.33333333333333" y="72" text-anchor="middle">1</text><text class="lb" x="141.33333333333331" y="73" text-anchor="start">UPDATE name = 'Ana'</text><line class="ed dash" x1="320.0" y1="114" x2="117.33333333333333" y2="114" marker-end="url(#replag-a)"/><circle class="nd in" cx="126.33333333333333" cy="102" r="9"/><text class="lb mu" x="126.33333333333333" y="106" text-anchor="middle">2</text><text class="lb mu" x="141.33333333333331" y="107" text-anchor="start">committed</text><line class="ed" x1="113.33333333333333" y1="148" x2="522.6666666666666" y2="148" marker-end="url(#replag-a)"/><circle class="nd in" cx="126.33333333333333" cy="136" r="9"/><text class="lb mu" x="126.33333333333333" y="140" text-anchor="middle">3</text><text class="lb" x="141.33333333333331" y="141" text-anchor="start">SELECT name</text><line class="ed acc" x1="526.6666666666666" y1="182" x2="117.33333333333333" y2="182" marker-end="url(#replag-aa)"/><circle class="nd in" cx="126.33333333333333" cy="170" r="9"/><text class="lb mu" x="126.33333333333333" y="174" text-anchor="middle">4</text><text class="lb acc" x="141.33333333333331" y="175" text-anchor="start">old name: not copied yet</text><line class="ed dash" x1="320.0" y1="216" x2="522.6666666666666" y2="216" marker-end="url(#replag-a)"/><circle class="nd in" cx="333.0" cy="204" r="9"/><text class="lb mu" x="333.0" y="208" text-anchor="middle">5</text><text class="lb mu" x="348.0" y="209" text-anchor="start">async copy arrives</text><text class="lb acc" x="121.33333333333333" y="252" text-anchor="start">the user did not see their own write</text></svg><figcaption>The first bullet above, step by step. Reading straight after a write? Read from the primary, or use a synchronous replica and pay for it in write latency.</figcaption></figure>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Transactions, isolation, locking</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Isolation</th><th>Prevents</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Read committed (Postgres default)</td><td>Dirty reads. Each statement sees data committed before it began.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Repeatable read</td><td>Non-repeatable reads (and, in PostgreSQL, phantom reads): the transaction sees one snapshot throughout.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Serializable</td><td>All anomalies; transactions behave as if run one by one. Retry on serialization failure.</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>-- pessimistic: lock the row you will change
SELECT * FROM account WHERE id = 7 FOR UPDATE;

-- job queue: each worker claims different rows
SELECT id FROM job WHERE state = 'READY'
ORDER BY created_at LIMIT 10 FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED;

-- optimistic: version column, fail if someone else changed it
UPDATE doc SET body = $1, version = version + 1
WHERE id = $2 AND version = $3;     -- 0 rows → conflict, reload and retry</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">Keep transactions short. A long one holds locks and stops vacuum cleaning up dead rows.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Safe schema changes on live data</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Additive first:</b> add a nullable column, a new table, a new index (concurrently). Safe and reversible.</li>
      <li><b>Expand / contract for renames and type changes:</b> add the new column, write to both, backfill in batches, switch reads, stop writing the old, drop it in a later release.</li>
      <li><b>Avoid long locks:</b> some <code>ALTER TABLE</code> forms rewrite the whole table. Set <code>lock_timeout</code> so a migration fails fast instead of blocking traffic.</li>
      <li><b>Backfill in batches</b> (by ID range), committing as you go, not one giant UPDATE.</li>
      <li><b>Version every change</b> with a migration tool (Liquibase, Flyway, Alembic), and never edit a migration that has already run in production.</li>
      <li><b>Test on a production-sized copy;</b> a change that takes seconds on a test table can take hours on a table of tens of millions of rows.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Anti-patterns</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Entity-attribute-value</b> tables (<code>key</code>, <code>value</code> rows for everything): no types, no constraints, painful queries.</li>
      <li><b>Comma-separated lists in a column:</b> use a join table or an array with a GIN index.</li>
      <li><b>Floats for money</b> and strings for dates.</li>
      <li><b>No foreign keys "for speed":</b> orphans and silent corruption cost more.</li>
      <li><b>N+1 queries</b> from an ORM loop: one query per row instead of one join.</li>
      <li><b><code>SELECT *</code></b> in application code: breaks when columns change, fetches what you do not need.</li>
      <li><b>OFFSET pagination on deep pages:</b> use keyset pagination (<code>WHERE (date, id) &lt; ($1, $2) ORDER BY date DESC, id DESC LIMIT 20</code>).</li>
      <li><b>Unbounded growth</b> with no retention or archiving plan.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>OLTP vs OLAP design</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th></th><th>OLTP (Postgres)</th><th>OLAP (BigQuery)</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Workload</td><td>Many small reads/writes by key</td><td>Few large scans and aggregates</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Storage</td><td>Row-oriented</td><td>Column-oriented</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Schema</td><td>Normalised, constraints</td><td>Denormalised, nested, wide</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Speed comes from</td><td>Indexes</td><td>Partitioning, clustering, reading fewer columns</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Updates</td><td>Row by row, transactional</td><td>Batch loads and MERGE; avoid row-by-row DML</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Design a normalised table with a primary key and constraints</li><li>Add an index for a slow lookup</li><li>Understand what a transaction guarantees</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Read EXPLAIN ANALYZE and fix the plan</li><li>Choose composite, partial and covering indexes</li><li>Write safe, additive migrations</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Partition large tables; plan retention and archiving</li><li>Choose isolation levels and locking strategies</li><li>Plan replicas, pooling and capacity</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Decide between scaling up, partitioning, sharding or a distributed database</li><li>Run zero-downtime migrations on very large tables</li><li>Set database standards and review all schema changes</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>MVCC and vacuum in Postgres</li><li>B-tree internals and index-only scans</li><li>Logical replication and change data capture</li><li>Isolation anomalies: write skew, phantom reads</li><li>Distributed SQL: Spanner, CockroachDB, Citus</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PostgreSQL documentation</a><span class="lnote"> The reference for everything Postgres</span></li><li><a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/ddl-partitioning.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Postgres: table partitioning</a><span class="lnote"> Range, list, hash partitions and pruning</span></li><li><a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/transaction-iso.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Postgres: transaction isolation</a><span class="lnote"> What each level allows</span></li><li><a href="https://use-the-index-luke.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Use The Index, Luke</a><span class="lnote"> Indexing explained for developers</span></li><li><a href="https://dataintensive.net/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Designing Data-Intensive Applications</a><span class="lnote"> The standard book on storage, replication and partitioning</span></li><li><a href="https://explain.dalibo.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">explain.dalibo.com</a><span class="lnote"> Visualise Postgres query plans</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/indexes-multicolumn.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Multicolumn indexes (any subset; skip scan)</a>; <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/release-18.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PostgreSQL 18 release notes (B-tree skip scan)</a>; <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/17/release-17.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PostgreSQL 17 release notes (identity columns on partitioned tables)</a>; <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/ddl-partitioning.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Table partitioning (unique keys include partition key)</a>; <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/sql-createindex.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CREATE INDEX (CONCURRENTLY leaves INVALID index)</a>; <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/indexes-opclass.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Operator classes (text_pattern_ops, C locale)</a>; <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/ddl-constraints.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Constraints (FK referencing columns not auto-indexed)</a>; <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/transaction-iso.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Transaction isolation (Read Committed default, no phantoms in Repeatable Read)</a>; <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/pgstatstatements.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">pg_stat_statements (mean_exec_time, total_exec_time)</a>; <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/sql-keywords-appendix.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">SQL keywords (ORDER reserved)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/pgvector/pgvector/master/README.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">pgvector README (hnsw, vector_cosine_ops)</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/alloydb/docs/instance-read-pool-create" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AlloyDB read pools (scale reads)</a>; <a href="https://github.com/citusdata/citus" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Citus (distributed Postgres)</a>; <a href="https://github.com/vitessio/vitess" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Vitess (sharded MySQL)</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/spanner/docs/schema-and-data-model" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Spanner: schema and data model (splits)</a>.</p><p class="runcode">A question, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/enrichmeai.github.io/issues/new?title=%5BDatabase%20Design%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>. Nothing here is tracked and there are no comments to store: questions live in the open, where the next learner can find the answer.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= GRAPH DATABASES ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-graphdb" data-name="Graph Databases">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Graph databases</h2><p>Some data is mostly about connections: who knows whom, which account paid which, which part goes into which product, which service calls which. A graph database stores those connections as first-class data, so following them is a basic operation rather than a chain of joins. This page covers the two main graph models, how to query them, how they compare with relational databases, and when a graph is worth it. (GraphQL, despite the name, is an API style, not a database: see <a href="#graphql">GraphQL</a>.)</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>The idea: nodes and edges</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Nodes</b> are things: people, accounts, products, services. They are roughly the equivalent of a row in a relational database or a document in a document store.</li>
      <li><b>Edges</b> are the relationships between them, and can be directed (A follows B) or undirected.</li>
      <li><b>Properties</b> are key&ndash;value details on both, such as a person&rsquo;s name or the date a payment was made.</li>
    </ul>
    <figure class="fig"><svg viewBox="0 0 620 270" role="img" aria-labelledby="pg-t pg-d"><title id="pg-t">A small labelled property graph</title><desc id="pg-d">Three nodes: a Person named Ana, a Person named Ben and a City named London. Ana KNOWS Ben, with the property since 2019. Ana and Ben each have a LIVES_IN relationship to London.</desc><defs><marker id="pg-a" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker><marker id="pg-aa" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar acc" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker></defs><circle class="nd in" cx="110" cy="80" r="46"/><circle class="nd in" cx="500" cy="80" r="46"/><circle class="nd out" cx="305" cy="215" r="46"/><text class="lb hd" x="110" y="74" text-anchor="middle">:Person</text><text class="lb mu" x="110" y="94" text-anchor="middle">name: Ana</text><text class="lb hd" x="500" y="74" text-anchor="middle">:Person</text><text class="lb mu" x="500" y="94" text-anchor="middle">name: Ben</text><text class="lb hd" x="305" y="209" text-anchor="middle">:City</text><text class="lb mu" x="305" y="229" text-anchor="middle">name: London</text><line class="ed" x1="156" y1="80" x2="450" y2="80" marker-end="url(#pg-a)"/><text class="lb hd" x="305" y="70" text-anchor="middle">KNOWS</text><text class="lb mu" x="305" y="100" text-anchor="middle">{since: 2019}</text><line class="ed" x1="142" y1="113" x2="266" y2="190" marker-end="url(#pg-a)"/><text class="lb hd" x="176" y="165" text-anchor="middle">LIVES_IN</text><line class="ed" x1="468" y1="113" x2="344" y2="190" marker-end="url(#pg-a)"/><text class="lb hd" x="434" y="165" text-anchor="middle">LIVES_IN</text></svg><figcaption>A labelled property graph: nodes carry a label (<code>:Person</code>) and properties; relationships are named, directed and can carry properties too. The query <code>MATCH (p:Person)-[:LIVES_IN]-&gt;(c:City)</code> matches two of these paths.</figcaption></figure>
    <p class="note">The mathematics is old: Leonhard Euler&rsquo;s 1736 proof that no walk crosses each of K&ouml;nigsberg&rsquo;s seven bridges exactly once is regarded as the first paper in the history of graph theory.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Two models</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Labelled property graph:</b> nodes and relationships both carry key&ndash;value properties; nodes have labels that group them; relationships are named, directed and have a start and an end node. Neo4j (where Cypher began) and Amazon Neptune (through openCypher) support this model.</li>
      <li><b>RDF (Resource Description Framework):</b> a W3C standard where everything is a <i>triple</i>, subject&ndash;predicate&ndash;object, such as &ldquo;Ana &ndash; worksFor &ndash; Acme&rdquo;. Databases built for it are called triplestores, and it underlies many knowledge graphs and linked data.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">Some products, Amazon Neptune among them, support both models.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Query languages</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Cypher:</b> created at Neo4j in 2011, largely by Andr&eacute;s Taylor, and opened up through the openCypher project in 2015. It draws patterns like <code>(a)-[:KNOWS]-&gt;(b)</code>.</li>
      <li><b>GQL:</b> the ISO standard graph query language for property graphs, ISO/IEC 39075, published in April 2024.</li>
      <li><b>SQL/PGQ:</b> Part 16 of SQL:2023 adds property graph queries to SQL itself, narrowing the gap between relational and graph databases.</li>
      <li><b>Gremlin:</b> Apache TinkerPop&rsquo;s graph traversal language, described as playing the role for graph databases that JDBC and SQL play for relational ones.</li>
      <li><b>SPARQL:</b> the W3C query language for RDF; SPARQL 1.0 became a W3C Recommendation in January 2008.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>What a query looks like</h3>
<pre><code>// GQL / Cypher style: people and the city they live in
MATCH (p:Person)-[:LIVES_IN]-&gt;(c:City)
RETURN p.first_name, p.last_name, c.name

// friends of friends: two hops along the same relationship
MATCH (me:Person {name: 'Ana'})-[:KNOWS]-&gt;(friend)-[:KNOWS]-&gt;(fof)
RETURN DISTINCT fof.name</code></pre>
    <p class="note"><i>Analysis:</i> the second query adds one pattern per hop. In SQL, each hop is typically another join on a link table, which is where graph databases are said to read more naturally.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Graph or relational?</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th></th><th>Graph database</th><th>Relational database</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Following relationships</td><td>Graph databases typically use <i>index-free adjacency</i>: each node holds direct references to its neighbours, so reaching an adjacent node is typically constant time, and query time depends on how much of the graph is queried rather than the size of the graph</td><td>Relationships are joins through indexes; queries with many joins across many tables get complex, and joins can be expensive</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Operating on many rows at once</td><td>Typically slower at the same operation over large numbers of rows</td><td>Typically faster at the same operation over large numbers of rows</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Schema</td><td>Flexible: new kinds of node and relationship can be added without redesigning</td><td>A strict schema and normalisation, which preserve data consistency and support ACID transactions</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Standard language</td><td>GQL since 2024; Cypher, Gremlin and SPARQL are all in use</td><td>SQL, the standard for relational databases, which have been the industry standard since Codd&rsquo;s 1970 relational model</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note">Wikipedia&rsquo;s summary adds a caution worth keeping: the graph model alone should not be the reason to replace an existing relational database.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Where graphs are used</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Shortest paths</b> between two nodes, and measures such as a graph&rsquo;s diameter.</li>
      <li><b>Community detection:</b> clusters of closely connected nodes.</li>
      <li><b>Knowledge graphs:</b> Google introduced its Knowledge Graph in 2012, to answer searches with &ldquo;things, not strings&rdquo;.</li>
      <li><b>Graphs for AI retrieval:</b> Microsoft&rsquo;s GraphRAG research project used language models to extract a knowledge graph from text and used it as context for answering questions. Microsoft now describes it as a research project largely in maintenance mode, and warns that indexing can be expensive (see <a href="#rag">RAG</a>).</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Choose by requirement</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>If this is true for you</th><th>It leans towards (<i>analysis</i>)</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Your key queries follow chains of relationships of unknown length (paths, networks, &ldquo;how is A connected to B&rdquo;)</td><td>A graph database</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Your queries are mostly filters, totals and reports over many rows</td><td>A relational database or a warehouse</td></tr>
      <tr><td>You already run PostgreSQL or another SQL database and need some graph queries</td><td>Try graph queries in SQL first (recursive queries, or SQL/PGQ where supported): a second database has a cost</td></tr>
      <tr><td>You need to link data from many sources with shared, published vocabularies</td><td>RDF and SPARQL</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Your data has many kinds of entity and relationship that keep changing</td><td>A graph model of either kind: new data can be added without planning every future use in advance</td></tr>
      <tr><td>You need to be able to move between graph products</td><td>A standard language, GQL (ISO/IEC 39075) or openCypher, rather than one vendor&rsquo;s extensions</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Our view, and the other view</h3>
    <p><i>Our view:</i> learn to think in graphs even if you never run a graph database: many problems (dependencies, permissions, fraud rings, service maps) are graphs, and drawing them that way clarifies them. Reach for a graph database when relationship queries are the core of the product.</p>
    <p><i>The other view:</i> one well-run relational database covers most needs, including many graph-shaped queries through recursive SQL and now SQL/PGQ. Adding a specialised database brings another system to operate, back up and secure, and another query language for the team to learn.</p>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Explain nodes, edges and properties</li><li>Tell a labelled property graph from RDF triples</li><li>Say why GraphQL is not a graph database</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Write MATCH queries for one and two hops</li><li>Model a small domain as a graph</li><li>Compare a graph query with the same query in SQL</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Choose between a graph database, SQL/PGQ and recursive SQL for a case</li><li>Explain index-free adjacency and its trade-offs</li><li>Design a graph for fraud, permissions or dependencies</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Decide whether a graph database earns its place in the estate</li><li>Plan a knowledge graph across sources</li><li>Set standards: GQL, openCypher or vendor extensions</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>Graph theory basics</li><li>Property graphs and RDF</li><li>Cypher and GQL</li><li>Gremlin and SPARQL</li><li>Graphs vs relational</li><li>Knowledge graphs</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://github.com/opencypher/openCypher" target="_blank" rel="noopener">openCypher</a><span class="lnote"> The open Cypher project</span></li><li><a href="https://tinkerpop.apache.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Apache TinkerPop</a><span class="lnote"> Gremlin and the graph computing framework</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Graph_database" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Graph database</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Graph_theory" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Graph theory</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seven_Bridges_of_K%C3%B6nigsberg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Seven Bridges of Königsberg</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Property_graph" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Property graph</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Resource_Description_Framework" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Resource Description Framework</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Triplestore" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Triplestore</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cypher_(query_language)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Cypher</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Graph_Query_Language" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: GQL</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SQL:2023" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: SQL:2023</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gremlin_(query_language)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Gremlin</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SPARQL" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: SPARQL</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neo4j" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Neo4j</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amazon_Neptune" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Amazon Neptune</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/JanusGraph" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: JanusGraph</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Knowledge_graph" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Knowledge graph</a>; <a href="https://github.com/microsoft/graphrag/blob/main/README.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Microsoft GraphRAG (README)</a>.</p><p class="runcode">A question, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/enrichmeai.github.io/issues/new?title=%5BGraph%20Databases%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>. Nothing here is tracked and there are no comments to store: questions live in the open, where the next learner can find the answer.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= CHOOSING A DATA STORE ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-datastore" data-name="Choosing a Data Store">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Choosing a data store</h2><p>Relational databases, document stores, key-value stores, wide-column stores, search engines, time series databases, vector databases, warehouses and object storage all exist because each makes some operations cheap and others expensive. This page sets out what each family is for, so the choice follows from your requirements. How to design a relational schema is on <a href="#database">Database Design</a>; graphs are on <a href="#graphdb">Graph Databases</a>; caches are on <a href="#caching">Caching</a>.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>The short answer</h3>
    <p><i>Our view:</i> start with one relational database unless a requirement clearly calls for something else, and add a specialised store when that requirement appears. <i>The other view:</i> some teams pick a document or key-value store from the start because their data and access patterns are known to fit it; this can avoid a later migration. The table below sets out which requirements point where.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>A first pass</h3>
    <figure class="fig"><svg viewBox="0 0 640 440" role="img" aria-labelledby="dsf-t dsf-d"><title id="dsf-t">A first pass at choosing a data store</title><desc id="dsf-d">A flowchart of five yes-or-no questions in a column. Mostly files, images or backups: if yes, object storage. Otherwise, analytics over many rows: if yes, a columnar warehouse. Otherwise, search by words and relevance: if yes, a search engine. Otherwise, find similar items by meaning: if yes, a vector index. Otherwise, only get and set by key, very fast: if yes, a key-value store. If no to all five, start with a relational database.</desc><defs><marker id="dsf-a" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker></defs><path class="nd" d="M190,14 L360,40 L190,66 L20,40 Z"/><text class="lb" x="190" y="45" text-anchor="middle">Mostly files, images, backups?</text><line class="ed" x1="360" y1="40" x2="446" y2="40" marker-end="url(#dsf-a)"/><text class="lb mu" x="378" y="34" text-anchor="middle">yes</text><rect class="nd out" x="450" y="22" width="180" height="36" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="540" y="45" text-anchor="middle">Object storage</text><line class="ed" x1="190" y1="66" x2="190" y2="76" marker-end="url(#dsf-a)"/><text class="lb mu" x="200" y="80" text-anchor="start">no</text><path class="nd" d="M190,80 L360,106 L190,132 L20,106 Z"/><text class="lb" x="190" y="111" text-anchor="middle">Analytics over many rows?</text><line class="ed" x1="360" y1="106" x2="446" y2="106" marker-end="url(#dsf-a)"/><text class="lb mu" x="378" y="100" text-anchor="middle">yes</text><rect class="nd out" x="450" y="88" width="180" height="36" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="540" y="111" text-anchor="middle">Columnar warehouse</text><line class="ed" x1="190" y1="132" x2="190" y2="142" marker-end="url(#dsf-a)"/><text class="lb mu" x="200" y="146" text-anchor="start">no</text><path class="nd" d="M190,146 L360,172 L190,198 L20,172 Z"/><text class="lb" x="190" y="177" text-anchor="middle">Search by words and relevance?</text><line class="ed" x1="360" y1="172" x2="446" y2="172" marker-end="url(#dsf-a)"/><text class="lb mu" x="378" y="166" text-anchor="middle">yes</text><rect class="nd out" x="450" y="154" width="180" height="36" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="540" y="177" text-anchor="middle">Search engine</text><line class="ed" x1="190" y1="198" x2="190" y2="208" marker-end="url(#dsf-a)"/><text class="lb mu" x="200" y="212" text-anchor="start">no</text><path class="nd" d="M190,212 L360,238 L190,264 L20,238 Z"/><text class="lb" x="190" y="243" text-anchor="middle">Find similar items by meaning?</text><line class="ed" x1="360" y1="238" x2="446" y2="238" marker-end="url(#dsf-a)"/><text class="lb mu" x="378" y="232" text-anchor="middle">yes</text><rect class="nd out" x="450" y="220" width="180" height="36" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="540" y="243" text-anchor="middle">Vector index</text><line class="ed" x1="190" y1="264" x2="190" y2="274" marker-end="url(#dsf-a)"/><text class="lb mu" x="200" y="278" text-anchor="start">no</text><path class="nd" d="M190,278 L360,304 L190,330 L20,304 Z"/><text class="lb" x="190" y="309" text-anchor="middle">Only get and set by key, very fast?</text><line class="ed" x1="360" y1="304" x2="446" y2="304" marker-end="url(#dsf-a)"/><text class="lb mu" x="378" y="298" text-anchor="middle">yes</text><rect class="nd out" x="450" y="286" width="180" height="36" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="540" y="309" text-anchor="middle">Key-value store</text><line class="ed" x1="190" y1="330" x2="190" y2="356" marker-end="url(#dsf-a)"/><text class="lb mu" x="200" y="344" text-anchor="start">no</text><rect class="nd in" x="30" y="360" width="320" height="56" rx="6"/><text class="lb hd" x="190" y="384" text-anchor="middle">Start with a relational database</text><text class="lb mu" x="190" y="404" text-anchor="middle">JSON columns where the shape varies</text></svg><figcaption>A first pass, as a flowchart (<i>analysis</i>, not a rule). Most systems answer &ldquo;yes&rdquo; to more than one question and end up with more than one store; the table below goes further. Time series and graph stores are in the table and on Graph Databases, not in this first pass.</figcaption></figure>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>The families</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Family</th><th>What it stores, and what it is good at</th><th>Examples and typical uses</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td><b>Relational</b></td><td>Tables of rows and columns, queried with SQL, with ACID transactions (atomicity, consistency, isolation, durability)</td><td>PostgreSQL (which also stores JSON, including a binary JSONB type)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Document</b></td><td>Semi-structured documents, such as JSON, each with its own shape; one of the main NoSQL families</td><td>MongoDB, which stores JSON-like documents (BSON) with optional schemas</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Key-value</b></td><td>Records stored and retrieved by a unique key, like a dictionary</td><td>Redis, which works on data in main memory and persists it to disk</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Wide-column</b></td><td>Tables where the columns can vary from row to row; a two-dimensional key-value store</td><td>Apache Cassandra (from Facebook, 2008), built on ideas from Amazon&rsquo;s Dynamo and Google&rsquo;s Bigtable</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Search engine</b></td><td>An index for full-text search and relevance ranking</td><td>Elasticsearch, based on Apache Lucene</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Time series</b></td><td>Data points indexed in time order, with high ingestion rates and heavy compression</td><td>Measurements from physical sensors, and other time-stamped data</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Vector</b></td><td>Embeddings, searched by approximate nearest neighbour for similar meaning; used for semantic search and retrieval-augmented generation</td><td>Dedicated vector databases, or PostgreSQL with pgvector</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Columnar warehouse</b></td><td>A data warehouse serves reporting and data analysis; analytics databases benefit from storing data by column, while transactional ones suit rows</td><td>BigQuery, Amazon Redshift and Snowflake use column-oriented formats; Apache Parquet is a column-oriented file format</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Object storage</b></td><td>Blobs with metadata and a unique identifier, rather than files in folders</td><td>Files and other large objects with their metadata</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>In-memory</b></td><td>Data held mainly in main memory, which gives faster and more predictable performance than disk</td><td>Systems where response time is critical</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note">Many products now span several families: a multi-model database supports more than one data model, such as document, graph, relational and key-value, against one backend.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>More than one store: polyglot persistence</h3>
    <p>Martin Fowler wrote in 2011 that any decent-sized enterprise would come to use a variety of data storage technologies for different kinds of data, a shift he called <b>polyglot persistence</b> (crediting Scott Leberknight with the term). He also said it comes at a cost.</p>
    <p class="note"><i>Analysis:</i> each extra store is another system to run, back up, secure and keep consistent with the others; on keeping copies in step, see <a href="#atob">Solution Architecture: A to B</a> (the outbox and change data capture).</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>NoSQL is not one thing</h3>
    <p>NoSQL, &ldquo;not only SQL&rdquo;, covers several designs that store data differently from relational tables: key-value pairs, wide columns, graphs or documents. They do not need a fixed schema, which suits large and often unstructured datasets.</p>
    <p class="note"><i>Analysis:</i> &ldquo;schema-less&rdquo; usually means the schema lives in your code instead of the database, so it still needs designing and versioning.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Choose by requirement</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>If this is true for you</th><th>It leans towards (<i>analysis</i>)</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Changes that must happen together across several records (orders and stock, money)</td><td>A relational database with transactions</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Each record is read and written whole, and its shape varies</td><td>A document store, or JSON columns in a relational database</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Very high write volume, spread over many machines, with known query patterns</td><td>A wide-column store</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Fast lookups by key, where losing some data is tolerable or it can be rebuilt</td><td>An in-memory key-value store, usually as a cache in front of the main store</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Users search text and expect ranked results</td><td>A search engine, fed from the main store</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Metrics or sensor readings over time</td><td>A time series database</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Semantic search or retrieval for an AI feature</td><td>A vector index; start with one inside a database you already run if it offers one</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Analysis over large history</td><td>A columnar warehouse, separate from the operational database</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Your team already runs a database that meets the requirement</td><td>Use it before adding another: each extra store has a cost, as Fowler noted (2011)</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Our view, and the other view</h3>
    <p><i>Our view:</i> the questions that decide it are what must change together, how data is read, how much of it there is, and who will run the store at 3 a.m. Popularity and brand are not on that list. A team&rsquo;s existing operational experience is, because switching has a cost; the table&rsquo;s last row says so.</p>
    <p><i>The other view:</i> the store a team already knows well, and can operate safely, may suit better than a better-fitting store nobody can run; that familiarity reduces operational risk, and is more than preference.</p>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Name the main families of data store</li><li>Say what ACID transactions guarantee</li><li>Tell row storage (OLTP) from column storage (OLAP)</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Choose between a table, a JSON column and a document store</li><li>Add a search index or cache fed from the main store</li><li>Explain why a store suits a workload</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Choose stores for a system by requirement</li><li>Keep several stores consistent (outbox, change data capture)</li><li>Weigh the cost of each extra store</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Set an organisation's approved data stores and when to add one</li><li>Plan migrations between stores</li><li>Balance fit against operational risk</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>Relational databases and ACID</li><li>Document, key-value and wide-column stores</li><li>Search engines and vector indexes</li><li>Time series and columnar warehouses</li><li>Object storage</li><li>Polyglot persistence</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://martinfowler.com/bliki/PolyglotPersistence.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Polyglot Persistence</a><span class="lnote"> Martin Fowler, 2011</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Relational_database" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Relational database</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ACID" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: ACID</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NoSQL" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: NoSQL</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Document-oriented_database" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Document-oriented database</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Key%E2%80%93value_database" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Key–value database</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wide-column_store" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Wide-column store</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Time_series_database" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Time series database</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vector_database" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Vector database</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Data_orientation" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Data orientation (row vs column)</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Object_storage" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Object storage</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/In-memory_database" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: In-memory database</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multi-model_database" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Multi-model database</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PostgreSQL" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: PostgreSQL</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/MongoDB" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: MongoDB</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Redis" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Redis</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apache_Cassandra" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Apache Cassandra</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elasticsearch" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Elasticsearch</a>; <a href="https://martinfowler.com/bliki/PolyglotPersistence.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Martin Fowler: Polyglot Persistence</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Data_warehouse" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Data warehouse</a>.</p><p class="runcode">A question, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/enrichmeai.github.io/issues/new?title=%5BChoosing%20a%20Data%20Store%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>. Nothing here is tracked and there are no comments to store: questions live in the open, where the next learner can find the answer.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= HOME LAB ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-homelab" data-name="Home Lab">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Home lab: learn by doing</h2><p>Reading builds vocabulary; running things builds judgement. These labs each start from a real, public repository you clone and run on a laptop or a small cloud budget. Do them in order or pick the ones for the area you are going deep in. Each has a "done when" so you know you finished, and each ends with you writing up what broke.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Set up once</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Machine:</b> a laptop with 16 GB RAM is enough for most labs; an old PC or mini PC running Linux makes a good always-on lab server.</li>
      <li><b>Tools:</b> Git, Docker (or Podman), <code>kubectl</code>, <code>kind</code> or <code>k3d</code>, Helm, Python 3.12+ (3.13+ for Lab 5), a JDK 21 or newer, and the <code>gcloud</code> CLI.</li>
      <li><b>Cloud, carefully:</b> new Google Cloud accounts get free-trial credit, and BigQuery has a free sandbox. Set a <b>budget alert</b> on day one and delete what you are not using.</li>
      <li><b>Your lab journal:</b> one Git repository with a folder per lab and a README in each: goal, steps, numbers you measured, what broke and why. This becomes your portfolio.</li>
    </ul>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>mkdir -p ~/lab &amp;&amp; cd ~/lab &amp;&amp; git init
kind create cluster --name lab        # a Kubernetes cluster in Docker
kubectl get nodes</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Lab 1: microservices on Kubernetes</h3>
    <p><b>Repo:</b> <code>GoogleCloudPlatform/microservices-demo</code> (Online Boutique: about ten services in several languages).</p>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>kubectl apply -f https://raw.githubusercontent.com/GoogleCloudPlatform/microservices-demo/main/release/kubernetes-manifests.yaml
kubectl get pods -w
kubectl port-forward deploy/frontend 8080:8080     # open http://localhost:8080</code></pre></div>
    <p><b>Then:</b> delete a pod and watch it come back; scale a deployment; set a memory limit too low and read the OOMKilled event; break a readiness probe.</p>
    <p class="note"><b>Done when:</b> you can explain, from <code>kubectl describe</code> and events alone, why each thing you broke failed. Tabs: <a href="#kubernetes">Kubernetes</a>, <a href="#microservices">Microservices</a>.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Lab 2: observability stack</h3>
    <p><b>Repo:</b> <code>prometheus-community/helm-charts</code> (kube-prometheus-stack) on the Lab 1 cluster; or <code>open-telemetry/opentelemetry-demo</code> with Docker Compose.</p>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>helm repo add prometheus-community https://prometheus-community.github.io/helm-charts
helm install monitoring prometheus-community/kube-prometheus-stack -n monitoring --create-namespace
kubectl port-forward -n monitoring svc/monitoring-grafana 3000:80

# or the OpenTelemetry demo (traces, metrics, logs across many services)
git clone https://github.com/open-telemetry/opentelemetry-demo
cd opentelemetry-demo &amp;&amp; make start   # a bare docker compose up starts no observability backends</code></pre></div>
    <p><b>Then:</b> write one PromQL alert for an error ratio, make it fire on purpose, and find the failing span in a trace.</p>
    <p class="note"><b>Done when:</b> an alert you wrote fires for a failure you caused, and you traced it to the line of code. Tabs: <a href="#observability">Observability</a>, <a href="#opstools">Ops Tooling</a>.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Lab 3: service mesh</h3>
    <p><b>Repo:</b> <code>istio/istio</code> (its <code>samples/bookinfo</code> application) on a kind cluster.</p>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># after downloading an Istio release and adding istioctl to PATH
istioctl install --set profile=default -y
kubectl label namespace default istio-injection=enabled
kubectl apply -f samples/bookinfo/platform/kube/bookinfo.yaml
istioctl analyze</code></pre></div>
    <p><b>Then:</b> turn on STRICT mTLS, split traffic 90/10 between review versions, inject a delay, and write an AuthorizationPolicy that denies one service.</p>
    <p class="note"><b>Done when:</b> you can produce, then explain, a 403 and a 503 from proxy logs. Tab: <a href="#servicemesh">Service Mesh</a>.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Lab 4: GitOps and Helm</h3>
    <p><b>Repo:</b> <code>argoproj/argocd-example-apps</code>, with Argo CD installed on your cluster.</p>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>kubectl create namespace argocd
kubectl apply -n argocd --server-side --force-conflicts -f https://raw.githubusercontent.com/argoproj/argo-cd/stable/manifests/install.yaml
kubectl port-forward -n argocd svc/argocd-server 8443:443</code></pre></div>
    <p><b>Then:</b> fork the example repo, point an Argo CD application at its Helm example, change a value in Git and watch it roll out; change something by hand in the cluster and watch Argo CD report the drift.</p>
    <p class="note"><b>Done when:</b> every change reaches the cluster only through a Git commit. Tabs: <a href="#helm">Helm Charts</a>, <a href="#devops">DevOps</a>.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Lab 5: a data pipeline on your laptop</h3>
    <p><b>Repo:</b> <code>dbt-labs/jaffle_shop_duckdb</code> (dbt's demo project, running on DuckDB, no cloud account).</p>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>git clone https://github.com/dbt-labs/jaffle_shop_duckdb
cd jaffle_shop_duckdb
python3 -m venv .venv &amp;&amp; . .venv/bin/activate   # this repo asks for Python 3.13+
pip install -r requirements.txt  # or: uv sync
dbt build                       # seeds, models and tests
dbt docs generate &amp;&amp; dbt docs serve</code></pre></div>
    <p><b>Then:</b> add an incremental model, a unit test and a test that fails on purpose; load a public dataset of your own (BigQuery public datasets or the Registry of Open Data on AWS) into DuckDB and model it in layers.</p>
    <p class="note"><b>Done when:</b> running the pipeline twice gives identical results, and a bad row is rejected and counted, not silently nulled. Tabs: <a href="#data">Data Engineering</a>, <a href="#dbt">dbt</a>, <a href="#datalibs">Data Libraries</a>.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Lab 6: orchestrate it</h3>
    <p><b>Repo:</b> <code>apache/airflow</code>, using the official Docker Compose quick start from the Airflow documentation.</p>
    <p><b>Then:</b> write a DAG that runs Lab 5's <code>dbt build</code> daily, uses data intervals, retries, and records a run summary; backfill a week; make one day fail and rerun just that day.</p>
    <p class="note"><b>Done when:</b> a rerun of any single day changes nothing else, and a missing run raises an alert. Tab: <a href="#dags">Airflow DAGs</a>.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Lab 7: Culvert against emulators</h3>
    <p><b>Repo:</b> <code>enrichmeai/culvert</code>. Its stack runs against local emulators, so no cloud account is needed.</p>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>git clone https://github.com/enrichmeai/culvert
pip install 'culvert[gcp]'
# then follow the repository's docs to start the emulators and run a pipeline</code></pre></div>
    <p><b>Then:</b> write a small pipeline against the <code>BlobStore</code> and <code>Warehouse</code> contracts, run it on the emulators, then read the conformance suite to see what every adapter must guarantee.</p>
    <p class="note"><b>Done when:</b> your pipeline code imports no cloud SDK. Tabs: <a href="#culvert">Culvert</a>, <a href="#blueprints">Frameworks vs AI</a>.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Lab 8: events with Kafka</h3>
    <p><b>Repo:</b> the official <code>apache/kafka</code> Docker image, plus a producer and consumer you write.</p>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>docker run -d --name kafka -p 9092:9092 apache/kafka:latest   # pin a version once it works
docker exec -it kafka /opt/kafka/bin/kafka-topics.sh \
  --bootstrap-server localhost:9092 --create --topic order.placed --partitions 3</code></pre></div>
    <p><b>Then:</b> write a consumer that is idempotent, kill it mid-batch, restart it and prove no event was applied twice; add a dead-letter topic for a poison message.</p>
    <p class="note"><b>Done when:</b> you can show consumer lag rising and recovering, and duplicates handled. Tab: <a href="#kafka">Kafka</a>.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Lab 9: RAG and an MCP server</h3>
    <p><b>Repos:</b> <code>anthropics/anthropic-quickstarts</code> for starting points, <code>modelcontextprotocol/python-sdk</code> for the server, and the <code>pgvector/pgvector</code> Docker image for search.</p>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>docker run -d --name pg -e POSTGRES_PASSWORD=pw -p 5432:5432 pgvector/pgvector:pg17
pip install "mcp[cli]" anthropic psycopg</code></pre></div>
    <p><b>Then:</b> chunk and embed a small document set, build hybrid search, answer questions with citations, write 30 golden questions and measure recall; wrap Lab 5's run records in a read-only MCP server and use it from Claude Code.</p>
    <p class="note"><b>Done when:</b> your eval score is written down, and the MCP tool says "could not read" when the database is stopped. Tabs: <a href="#rag">RAG</a>, <a href="#mcp">MCP</a>.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Lab 10: infrastructure as code</h3>
    <p><b>Start:</b> HashiCorp's "Get started with Docker" Terraform tutorial (local, free), then a small Google Cloud project behind a budget alert.</p>
    <p><b>Then:</b> declare a bucket, a dataset and a Cloud Run job in Terraform; rename a resource with a <code>moved</code> block; import something created by hand; destroy everything at the end.</p>
    <p class="note"><b>Done when:</b> <code>terraform plan</code> shows no changes after apply, and nothing you made is left running. Tabs: <a href="#terraform">Terraform</a>, <a href="#cloudrun">Cloud Run</a>.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Lab 11: payments in test mode</h3>
    <p><b>Start:</b> a Stripe or Razorpay test account. Stripe's CLI forwards test webhooks to your laptop.</p>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>stripe login
stripe listen --forward-to localhost:8080/webhooks/stripe
stripe trigger payment_intent.succeeded</code></pre></div>
    <p><b>Then:</b> build order → checkout → signature check → webhook; replay the same webhook twice; simulate a timeout and reconcile.</p>
    <p class="note"><b>Done when:</b> no replay or retry can mark an order paid twice. Tab: <a href="#payments">Payment Gateways</a>.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Lab 12: game day</h3>
    <p><b>Repo:</b> <code>chaos-mesh/chaos-mesh</code> on your kind cluster, or just <code>kubectl delete</code> and <code>tc</code> by hand.</p>
    <p><b>Then:</b> with a friend, one person breaks something on the Lab 1–3 stack without saying what; the other detects, mitigates and writes a blameless postmortem using the template on the SRE tab.</p>
    <p class="note"><b>Done when:</b> the postmortem has at least one action that adds a guard (an alert, a test, a limit). Tab: <a href="#sre">SRE</a>.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Rules for lab work</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Predict, then run:</b> write down what you expect before each experiment; the gap is the lesson.</li>
      <li><b>Break it on purpose:</b> every lab includes a failure you cause and explain.</li>
      <li><b>Measure:</b> record latency, cost, row counts or recovery time, not impressions.</li>
      <li><b>Use AI as the bulldozer,</b> not the driver: let it scaffold and explain, then verify everything it claims by running it.</li>
      <li><b>Pin versions</b> once something works, and note them in the README so the lab is repeatable.</li>
      <li><b>Clean up:</b> <code>kind delete cluster</code>, <code>docker compose down -v</code>, <code>terraform destroy</code>, and check the cloud billing page.</li>
      <li><b>Write it up:</b> a short post or README per lab. Teaching what you learned is the final test that you learned it.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/kubernetes-sigs/kind/main/site/content/docs/user/quick-start.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">kind quick start</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/GoogleCloudPlatform/microservices-demo/main/README.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Online Boutique README (11 microservices)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/prometheus-community/helm-charts/main/charts/kube-prometheus-stack/README.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">kube-prometheus-stack README (Helm repo still published)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/open-telemetry/opentelemetry.io/main/content/en/docs/demo/docker-deployment.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OpenTelemetry demo Docker deployment</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/istio/istio.io/master/content/en/docs/setup/getting-started/index.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Istio getting started (injection label, bookinfo path)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/argoproj/argo-cd/master/docs/getting_started.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Argo CD getting started</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/dbt-labs/jaffle_shop_duckdb/duckdb/README.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">jaffle_shop_duckdb README (Python &gt;= 3.13, uv sync)</a>; <a href="https://pypi.org/pypi/culvert/json" target="_blank" rel="noopener">culvert on PyPI (gcp extra, emulators)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/apache/kafka/trunk/docker/jvm/Dockerfile" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kafka Docker image Dockerfile (/opt/kafka)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/modelcontextprotocol/python-sdk/main/README.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MCP Python SDK README (mcp[cli])</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/pgvector/pgvector/master/README.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">pgvector README</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/stripe/stripe-cli/master/README.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Stripe CLI README (login, listen, trigger)</a>; <a href="https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/tutorials/docker-get-started" target="_blank" rel="noopener">HashiCorp: Get started with Terraform and Docker</a>; <a href="https://airflow.apache.org/docs/apache-airflow/stable/howto/docker-compose/index.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Airflow: running Airflow in Docker</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/free/docs/free-cloud-features" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google Cloud Free Program</a>.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= JAVA ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-java" data-name="Java">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Java 8 → 25</h2><p>What each release added that you will actually type, then the data-engineering libraries. <span class="tag lts">LTS</span> releases: 8, 11, 17, 21, 25. <span class="tag prev">Preview</span> needs <code>--enable-preview</code>.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Version map</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Ver</th><th>Year</th><th>Headline features (final unless marked)</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>8 <span class="tag lts">LTS</span></td><td>2014</td><td>Lambdas, method refs, Streams, <code>Optional</code>, <code>java.time</code>, default methods, <code>CompletableFuture</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>9</td><td>2017</td><td>Modules (JPMS), <code>List.of / Set.of / Map.of</code>, JShell, <code>takeWhile/dropWhile</code>, private interface methods</td></tr>
      <tr><td>10</td><td>2018</td><td><code>var</code> for locals, <code>List.copyOf</code>, <code>Collectors.toUnmodifiableList</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>11 <span class="tag lts">LTS</span></td><td>2018</td><td><code>String.isBlank/strip/lines/repeat</code>, <code>java.net.http.HttpClient</code>, <code>Files.readString/writeString</code>, run <code>java App.java</code> directly</td></tr>
      <tr><td>12–13</td><td>2019</td><td><code>Collectors.teeing</code>, <code>String.indent/transform</code>; switch expressions &amp; text blocks in preview</td></tr>
      <tr><td>14</td><td>2020</td><td>Switch expressions (<code>-&gt;</code>, <code>yield</code>), helpful NullPointerException messages (on by default from 15)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>15</td><td>2020</td><td>Text blocks <code>"""</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>16</td><td>2021</td><td>Records, pattern matching for <code>instanceof</code>, <code>Stream.toList()</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>17 <span class="tag lts">LTS</span></td><td>2021</td><td>Sealed classes/interfaces, new <code>RandomGenerator</code> API, strong JDK encapsulation</td></tr>
      <tr><td>18–20</td><td>2022–23</td><td>UTF-8 default charset (18), <code>jwebserver</code> (18); virtual threads &amp; patterns previewing</td></tr>
      <tr><td>21 <span class="tag lts">LTS</span></td><td>2023</td><td>Virtual threads, pattern matching for <code>switch</code>, record patterns, sequenced collections, generational ZGC</td></tr>
      <tr><td>22</td><td>2024</td><td>Unnamed variables <code>_</code>, Foreign Function &amp; Memory API, multi-file source launch</td></tr>
      <tr><td>23</td><td>2024</td><td>Markdown doc comments <code>///</code>; ZGC generational by default</td></tr>
      <tr><td>24</td><td>2025</td><td>Stream Gatherers, Class-File API, AOT class loading cache, virtual threads no longer pin on <code>synchronized</code>, ML-KEM / ML-DSA</td></tr>
      <tr><td>25 <span class="tag lts">LTS</span></td><td>2025</td><td>Compact source files &amp; instance <code>main</code>, <code>import module</code>, flexible constructor bodies, scoped values, compact object headers, KDF API, AOT ergonomics</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note">Still preview in 25: structured concurrency, primitive types in patterns, stable values, PEM encodings, Vector API (incubator). String templates were withdrawn after 22. Since then, 26 (March 2026) and 27 (September 2026) are non-LTS feature releases; 25 remains the latest LTS. 27 makes G1 the default collector everywhere and compact object headers the default; structured concurrency, primitive types in patterns, lazy constants (formerly stable values), PEM encodings and the Vector API are still preview or incubator there.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Threads in Java, release by release</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <tr><th>Release</th><th>What arrived</th><th>What it meant</th></tr>
      <tr><td>1.0</td><td><code>Thread</code>, <code>Runnable</code>, <code>synchronized</code>, <code>wait</code>/<code>notify</code></td><td>Threads built into the language from the start. Early JVMs used "green threads", scheduled by the JVM itself rather than the operating system; they were abandoned for native threads in later releases.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>5</td><td><code>java.util.concurrent</code> (JSR 166): <code>ExecutorService</code>, thread pools, <code>Future</code>, locks, atomics, concurrent collections</td><td>Stop creating threads by hand; submit tasks to a pool.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>7</td><td>Fork/join (<code>ForkJoinPool</code>)</td><td>Split a big job into small ones and use every core; work-stealing keeps them busy.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>8</td><td><code>CompletableFuture</code>, parallel streams</td><td>Compose asynchronous steps instead of blocking on each <code>Future</code>.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>9</td><td><code>Flow</code> (reactive streams interfaces)</td><td>A standard contract for streams with back-pressure, used by reactive libraries.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>19, 21</td><td>Virtual threads: preview in 19 (JEP 425), final in 21 (JEP 444)</td><td>Cheap threads managed by the JVM: one per blocking task, without a reactive rewrite.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>21 onwards</td><td>Structured concurrency, previewing since 21 (JEP 453; fifth preview in 25, JEP 505; seventh preview in 27, JEP 533)</td><td>Treat a group of related tasks as one unit: they finish, fail and cancel together.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>24</td><td>Virtual threads no longer pin on <code>synchronized</code> (JEP 491)</td><td>Old <code>synchronized</code> code stops blocking the carrier thread.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>25</td><td>Scoped values, final (JEP 506)</td><td>Share immutable data with child tasks; a safer replacement for many uses of <code>ThreadLocal</code>.</td></tr>
    </table></div>
    <p class="note">The full circle: the first JVMs scheduled threads themselves, Java then handed them to the operating system for twenty-five years, and virtual threads bring JVM scheduling back, this time on top of native threads. Release numbers come from each class's "Since" in the Java API documentation and from the JEPs.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Java 8 essentials <span class="tag lts">8</span></h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>// Lambdas + method references
list.sort(Comparator.comparing(Row::date).thenComparing(Row::id).reversed());

// Streams
Map&lt;String, Long&gt; perStore = rows.stream()
    .filter(r -&gt; r.year() &gt;= 2020)
    .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(Row::store, Collectors.counting()));

// Optional: never call get() blind
String name = repo.find(id).map(User::name).orElse("unknown");

// java.time
LocalDate d = LocalDate.parse("05-05-1959", DateTimeFormatter.ofPattern("dd-MM-yyyy"));
Instant now = Instant.now();
long days = ChronoUnit.DAYS.between(d, LocalDate.now());</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Java 9–11 conveniences <span class="tag">9</span><span class="tag">10</span><span class="tag lts">11</span></h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>var cols = List.of("order_ref", "store", "date");   // immutable, no nulls
var cfg  = Map.of("dataset", "raw", "loc", "asia-south1");

"  ".isBlank();          // true
" x ".strip();           // "x" (Unicode aware, unlike trim)
text.lines().count();
"-".repeat(40);

String body = Files.readString(Path.of("q.sql"));
Files.writeString(Path.of("out.txt"), body);

var http = HttpClient.newHttpClient();
var res = http.send(HttpRequest.newBuilder(URI.create(url)).build(),
                    HttpResponse.BodyHandlers.ofString());

stream.takeWhile(x -&gt; x &lt; 10);   // stop at first false
Optional.ofNullable(v).ifPresentOrElse(this::use, this::warn);</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Modern syntax <span class="tag">14</span><span class="tag">15</span><span class="tag">16</span><span class="tag lts">17</span></h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>// Switch expression (14)
int quarter = switch (month) {
  case 1, 2, 3 -&gt; 1;
  case 4, 5, 6 -&gt; 2;
  default -&gt; { log.warn("odd"); yield 0; }
};

// Text block (15)
String sql = """
    SELECT order_ref, order_date
    FROM `proj.analytics.orders`
    WHERE order_year = @year
    """;

// Record (16): immutable carrier, equals/hashCode/toString free
record Order(String orderRef, LocalDate date) {
  Order { Objects.requireNonNull(orderRef); }   // compact constructor
}

// instanceof pattern (16)
if (obj instanceof String s &amp;&amp; !s.isBlank()) use(s);

// Sealed hierarchy (17)
sealed interface Outcome permits Ok, Failed {}
record Ok(long rows) implements Outcome {}
record Failed(String reason) implements Outcome {}</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Java 21 <span class="tag lts">21</span></h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>// Pattern matching switch + record patterns: exhaustive over a sealed type
String describe(Outcome o) {
  return switch (o) {
    case Ok(long rows) when rows == 0 -&gt; "empty";
    case Ok(long rows)                -&gt; rows + " rows";
    case Failed(String why)           -&gt; "failed: " + why;
  };   // no default needed: compiler checks exhaustiveness
}

// Virtual threads: one cheap thread per blocking task
try (var exec = Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()) {
  List&lt;Future&lt;Result&gt;&gt; fs = uris.stream()
      .map(u -&gt; exec.submit(() -&gt; fetch(u))).toList();
  for (var f : fs) handle(f.get());
}   // ExecutorService is AutoCloseable since 19: close() waits
Thread.ofVirtual().start(() -&gt; work());

// Sequenced collections
list.getFirst(); list.getLast(); list.reversed();
linkedHashMap.firstEntry(); linkedHashMap.pollLastEntry();</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">Virtual threads suit blocking I/O (HTTP, JDBC, GCS). Do not pool them. CPU-bound work still wants a fixed pool.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Java 22–25 <span class="tag">22</span><span class="tag">24</span><span class="tag lts">25</span></h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>// Unnamed variables (22)
try { parse(x); } catch (NumberFormatException _) { bad++; }
map.forEach((_, v) -&gt; total.add(v));   // total is a LongAdder: a lambda cannot change a local
case Failed(_) -&gt; "failed";

// Stream Gatherers (24)
Stream.of(1,2,3,4,5,6,7).gather(Gatherers.windowFixed(3)).toList();
// [[1,2,3],[4,5,6],[7]]   also windowSliding, fold, scan, mapConcurrent
uris.stream().gather(Gatherers.mapConcurrent(16, this::fetch)).toList();

// Compact source file + instance main (25): file Hello.java
void main() {
  IO.println("rows: " + count());
}
// run: java Hello.java

// Module import (25)
import module java.base;   // all exported packages of java.base

// Flexible constructor bodies (25): validate before super(...)
class PositiveAmount extends Amount {
  PositiveAmount(long v) {
    if (v &lt;= 0) throw new IllegalArgumentException("must be &gt; 0");
    super(v);            // statements before super() may not touch `this`
  }
}

// Scoped values (25): immutable, thread-friendly ThreadLocal replacement
static final ScopedValue&lt;String&gt; RUN_ID = ScopedValue.newInstance();
ScopedValue.where(RUN_ID, "r-42").run(() -&gt; log(RUN_ID.get()));</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Collectors cookbook</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>// group → list / count / sum / max
groupingBy(Row::store)
groupingBy(Row::store, counting())
groupingBy(Row::store, summingLong(Row::bytes))
groupingBy(Row::store, maxBy(comparing(Row::date)))
groupingBy(Row::store, TreeMap::new, mapping(Row::orderRef, toSet()))

// toMap with a merge rule (otherwise duplicate keys throw)
toMap(Row::orderRef, r -&gt; r, (a, b) -&gt; a.date().isAfter(b.date()) ? a : b)

partitioningBy(r -&gt; r.date() == null)      // Map&lt;Boolean, List&lt;Row&gt;&gt;
joining(", ", "[", "]")

// two results in one pass (12)
var minMax = nums.stream().collect(teeing(
    minBy(naturalOrder()), maxBy(naturalOrder()),
    (mn, mx) -&gt; List.of(mn.orElseThrow(), mx.orElseThrow())));

stream.toList();          // 16+, unmodifiable
IntStream.rangeClosed(1950, 2025).boxed().toList();</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Async &amp; resources</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>CompletableFuture&lt;Long&gt; a = supplyAsync(() -&gt; countWebOrders(), exec);
CompletableFuture&lt;Long&gt; b = supplyAsync(() -&gt; countStoreOrders(), exec);
long total = a.thenCombine(b, Long::sum)
              .orTimeout(30, TimeUnit.SECONDS)
              .exceptionally(e -&gt; { log.error("count", e); return -1L; })
              .join();

CompletableFuture.allOf(fs.toArray(CompletableFuture[]::new)).join();

// try-with-resources: closed in reverse order
try (var in = Files.newBufferedReader(p);
     var out = Files.newBufferedWriter(q)) {
  in.lines().map(String::strip).forEach(l -&gt; write(out, l));
}</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>BigQuery Java client</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>// com.google.cloud:google-cloud-bigquery (use libraries-bom)
BigQuery bq = BigQueryOptions.getDefaultInstance().getService();

QueryJobConfiguration q = QueryJobConfiguration.newBuilder("""
    SELECT store, COUNT(*) n FROM `proj.analytics.orders`
    WHERE order_date &gt;= @from GROUP BY store""")
  .addNamedParameter("from", QueryParameterValue.date("2024-01-01"))
  .setUseLegacySql(false)
  .build();

TableResult rs = bq.query(q);
for (FieldValueList row : rs.iterateAll()) {
  String store = row.get("store").getStringValue();
  long n = row.get("n").getLongValue();
}

// Load parquet from GCS
LoadJobConfiguration load = LoadJobConfiguration
  .newBuilder(TableId.of("raw", "orders"), "gs://bkt/path/*.parquet",
              FormatOptions.parquet())
  .setWriteDisposition(JobInfo.WriteDisposition.WRITE_APPEND)
  .build();
Job job = bq.create(JobInfo.of(load)).waitFor();
if (job.getStatus().getError() != null) throw new IllegalStateException(
    job.getStatus().getError().toString());

// Dry run: bytes before you pay
JobStatistics.QueryStatistics s = bq.create(JobInfo.of(
    q.toBuilder().setDryRun(true).setUseQueryCache(false).build()))
  .getStatistics();
long bytes = s.getTotalBytesProcessed();</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">For high-volume streaming writes use the Storage Write API (<code>JsonStreamWriter</code> / <code>BigQueryWriteClient</code>), not legacy <code>insertAll</code>.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Apache Beam / Dataflow (Java)</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>PipelineOptions opts = PipelineOptionsFactory.fromArgs(args).withValidation().create();
Pipeline p = Pipeline.create(opts);

p.apply("Read", BigQueryIO.readTableRows()
        .fromQuery("SELECT order_ref, store FROM `proj.raw.orders`")
        .usingStandardSql())
 .apply("Key", MapElements.into(TypeDescriptors.kvs(
         TypeDescriptors.strings(), TypeDescriptors.longs()))
       .via(r -&gt; KV.of((String) r.get("store"), 1L)))
 .apply(Sum.longsPerKey())
 .apply("ToRow", MapElements.into(TypeDescriptor.of(TableRow.class))
       .via(kv -&gt; new TableRow().set("store", kv.getKey()).set("n", kv.getValue())))
 .apply(BigQueryIO.writeTableRows().to("proj:mart.store_counts")
       .withSchema(schema)
       .withWriteDisposition(BigQueryIO.Write.WriteDisposition.WRITE_TRUNCATE)
       .withCreateDisposition(BigQueryIO.Write.CreateDisposition.CREATE_IF_NEEDED));
p.run().waitUntilFinish();
// --runner=DataflowRunner --project=… --region=… --tempLocation=gs://…</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Build &amp; run flags</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>// Gradle (Kotlin DSL): pin the JDK, not whatever is on PATH
java { toolchain { languageVersion = JavaLanguageVersion.of(21) } }
tasks.withType&lt;JavaCompile&gt; { options.release = 21 }

// Preview features (compile AND run)
javac --release 25 --enable-preview Foo.java
java --enable-preview Foo

// Containers: size heap from cgroup limits
java -XX:MaxRAMPercentage=75 -XX:+UseZGC -jar app.jar

// AOT cache (24/25): faster startup
java -XX:AOTCacheOutput=app.aot -jar app.jar   # training run (25)
java -XX:AOTCache=app.aot -jar app.jar</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">"Unsupported class file major version" means the build JDK is newer than the tool (often Gradle) supports. Set <code>JAVA_HOME</code> to the version the build expects.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Who steers Java: Sun, the JCP, Apache, Oracle and OpenJDK</h3>
    <p>Java is not only a language; it is a set of specifications, an open-source implementation, a test suite and a trademark, owned and steered by different groups. Knowing who decides what explains why the package names changed, why there are several JDKs, and why version numbers jumped.</p>
    <div class="tbl"><table><thead><tr><th>When</th><th>What happened</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td>1991</td><td>James Gosling, Mike Sheridan and Patrick Naughton start the language project at Sun Microsystems. It is first called Oak, after a tree outside Gosling&rsquo;s office, then Green, then Java.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>1995&ndash;96</td><td>Java is released in May 1995; Java 1.0, the first public implementation, follows in 1996 with the promise of &ldquo;write once, run anywhere&rdquo;.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>1998</td><td>The <b>Java Community Process</b> (JCP) is set up: the formal way companies and individuals propose and agree changes to the platform through <b>Java Specification Requests</b> (JSRs).</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2006&ndash;07</td><td>Sun releases its virtual machine and compiler as free software under the GPL (November 2006) and nearly all of the class library in May 2007: the start of <b>OpenJDK</b>.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2007</td><td>The Apache Software Foundation, building its own free Java (Apache Harmony), writes an open letter to Sun because it cannot get an acceptable licence for the <b>Technology Compatibility Kit</b> (TCK), the test suite an implementation must pass to call itself Java.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2010</td><td>Oracle completes its acquisition of Sun on 27 January 2010. In April, Gosling leaves Oracle. In December, Apache resigns its seat on the JCP Executive Committee because Oracle will not license the TCK for Apache&rsquo;s Java.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2011</td><td>Apache Harmony is retired. Parts of it had been used for the core class library of Android, which later moved to OpenJDK (Android 7.0).</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2017</td><td>Oracle hands Java EE, the enterprise specifications, to the <b>Eclipse Foundation</b>. Because Oracle owns the Java trademark, it becomes <b>Jakarta EE</b>; Jakarta EE 9 (2020) moves the API packages from <code>javax</code> to <code>jakarta</code>.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2018 onwards</td><td>Java moves to a feature release every six months, with long-term-support releases (11, 17, 21, 25) between them. Since JDK 10, the OpenJDK JDK Project produces every feature release; changes are proposed as <b>JDK Enhancement Proposals</b> (JEPs).</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2021</td><td>In <i>Google v. Oracle</i>, over Android&rsquo;s use of the Java APIs, the US Supreme Court rules 6&ndash;2 that Google&rsquo;s use was fair use, without deciding whether APIs can be copyrighted.</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
    <div class="tbl"><table><thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>What it is today</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td><b>JSR</b></td><td>A Java Specification Request: a formal proposal for a specification, reviewed in public and voted on by the JCP Executive Committee; a final JSR comes with a reference implementation and a TCK. The JCP itself is described by a JSR.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>JEP</b></td><td>A JDK Enhancement Proposal: how changes to the JDK itself are proposed, reviewed and tracked on openjdk.org; the list of JEPs serves as the roadmap for future releases.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>OpenJDK</b></td><td>The free, open-source implementation of Java SE, where every JDK feature release is now produced; builds, commercial support and free updates are available from several organisations.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>TCK</b></td><td>The compatibility test kit an implementation must pass before it can be called compatible.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Jakarta EE</b></td><td>The enterprise specifications (servlets, persistence and more), now run by the Eclipse Foundation; Spring Boot 3 and Apache Tomcat follow the <code>jakarta</code> package names (see <a href="#spring">Spring Boot</a>).</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Apache</b></td><td>The Apache Software Foundation hosts many of the Java tools you use daily: Tomcat, Maven and Ant among them (see <a href="#buildtools">Build Tools</a>).</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
    <p><i>Our view:</i> the history is worth knowing because it still shows up in your code: <code>javax</code> versus <code>jakarta</code> imports, the choice of which JDK build to install, and how a feature you want reaches the language (a JEP, not a vote of users). <i>The other view:</i> for day-to-day work you need only the current LTS release, a supported OpenJDK build and the <code>jakarta</code> names; the politics behind them changes little about how you write Java.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>The plethora of JDKs: which one to install</h3>
    <p>Java SE is a specification. OpenJDK is the open-source code that implements it. The JDK you download is somebody&rsquo;s <b>build</b> of that code, usually with the builder&rsquo;s own patches and backports, and usually tested against the compatibility kit (the TCK in the card above). Wikipedia gives the reason there are so many: because Oracle no longer releases updates for long-term-support releases under a permissive licence, other organisations publish their own builds. Most Linux distributions and package managers ship one too.</p>
    <div class="tbl"><table><thead><tr><th>Build</th><th>Who builds it</th><th>Cost and licence</th><th>Worth knowing</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td><b>Oracle OpenJDK</b></td><td>Oracle, at jdk.java.net</td><td>Open source (GPLv2 with the Classpath Exception)</td><td>Builds of the current release only; no long-term updates (Wikipedia&rsquo;s table marks it &ldquo;LTS: no&rdquo;)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Oracle JDK</b></td><td>Oracle</td><td>JDK 25: free for all users under the No-Fee Terms and Conditions, with updates released after September 2028 planned to move to the OTN licence. JDK 17 and 21 updates are now under the OTN licence, free for personal and development use only</td><td>Oracle&rsquo;s own build, with paid support through a Java SE subscription</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Eclipse Temurin</b></td><td>Adoptium at the Eclipse Foundation (formerly AdoptOpenJDK, started 2017)</td><td>Free &ldquo;to use, forever&rdquo;, GPLv2 with the Classpath Exception</td><td>LTS releases supported for at least four years; each release passes the Java compatibility kit; paid support from other companies</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Amazon Corretto</b></td><td>Amazon</td><td>No cost, &ldquo;production-ready&rdquo;</td><td>TCK certified; quarterly updates; Linux, Windows and macOS</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Microsoft Build of OpenJDK</b></td><td>Microsoft</td><td>Quarterly updates free of charge to anyone</td><td>Optional support on Azure</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Azul Zulu</b></td><td>Azul Systems</td><td>Permissive licence</td><td>TCK tested; optional paid support</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>BellSoft Liberica</b></td><td>BellSoft</td><td>Free and open source</td><td>Tested with the OpenJDK compatibility kit; the JVM provider in Paketo&rsquo;s Java buildpack, which Spring Boot&rsquo;s <code>build-image</code> uses by default</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Red Hat build of OpenJDK</b></td><td>Red Hat</td><td>Permissive licence</td><td>TCK tested; paid support available</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>SapMachine</b></td><td>SAP</td><td>Same licence as OpenJDK</td><td>A downstream fork, built &ldquo;OpenJDK-upstream-first&rdquo;, mainly for SAP customers and partners</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>IBM Semeru Runtimes</b></td><td>IBM</td><td>Open Edition and Certified Edition, both free</td><td>Uses the <b>Eclipse OpenJ9</b> virtual machine (formerly IBM J9) instead of HotSpot; OpenJ9 also runs on AIX, z/OS and IBM i</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>GraalVM</b></td><td>Oracle (Oracle GraalVM) and the GraalVM project (Community Edition)</td><td>Oracle GraalVM: GraalVM Free Terms and Conditions. Community Edition: GPLv2 with the Classpath Exception</td><td>Adds <b>Native Image</b>: ahead-of-time compilation of a Java application into a native executable</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note">Others exist too, such as Alibaba Dragonwell and Tencent Kona. To install and switch between them, <b>SDKMAN</b> manages parallel versions on Unix-like systems, and the <b>foojay Disco API</b> lists builds from many distributions in one place.</p>
    <p><b>Choose by requirement</b>, not by which name you have heard most:</p>
    <div class="tbl"><table><thead><tr><th>If this is true for you</th><th>Lean towards</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td>Your team already uses one build, and it is supported for the version you run</td><td>Stay on it: switching costs testing time and buys little</td></tr>
      <tr><td>You want free updates for years, with no vendor account</td><td>Temurin, Corretto, Zulu, Liberica or the Microsoft build</td></tr>
      <tr><td>You run on AWS or Azure and want the provider&rsquo;s support</td><td>Corretto on AWS; the Microsoft build on Azure</td></tr>
      <tr><td>You need a support contract with someone to call</td><td>Oracle JDK with a subscription, or Red Hat, Azul, IBM or BellSoft support</td></tr>
      <tr><td>You want Oracle&rsquo;s own JDK free in production</td><td>Oracle JDK 25 under the No-Fee Terms, knowing its updates after September 2028 are planned to move to the OTN licence</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Fast start-up and small memory matter most (functions, CLIs)</td><td>GraalVM Native Image</td></tr>
      <tr><td>You run on IBM hardware or operating systems</td><td>IBM Semeru with OpenJ9</td></tr>
      <tr><td>You build container images with Spring Boot&rsquo;s buildpacks</td><td>You already have Liberica, unless you configure another</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
    <p><i>Our view:</i> for most teams the build matters far less than staying on a supported LTS release and taking its quarterly updates; pick one free, TCK-tested build and use it everywhere, from laptops to containers. <i>The other view:</i> licensing and support terms do differ, and they change (Oracle&rsquo;s have changed several times), so an organisation with a legal or compliance team may prefer a paid contract that makes one company answerable.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>What happened to JNI, java.awt.Robot and MIDlets</h3>
    <p>Three pieces of Java that many engineers met early on, and what each became.</p>
    <div class="tbl"><table><thead><tr><th>Then</th><th>What happened</th><th>Today</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td><b>JNI</b></td><td>The Java Native Interface is how Java calls C, C++ and other native code. JEP 454 lists its &ldquo;tedious artifacts&rdquo;: native methods in Java, a C header file derived from them, and a C implementation that calls the library you want. And, as JEP 472 puts it, calling native code &ldquo;can lead to arbitrary undefined behavior, including JVM crashes&rdquo;. Project Panama&rsquo;s <b>Foreign Function &amp; Memory (FFM) API</b> was previewed in JDK 19, 20 and 21 (JEPs 424, 434, 442) and became final in <b>JDK 22</b> (JEP 454), to call native libraries &ldquo;without the brittleness and danger of JNI&rdquo;. The <code>jextract</code> tool generates the Java bindings from a library&rsquo;s C header files.</td><td><b>Still there, but discouraged.</b> Since JDK 24 (JEP 472), loading native code through JNI prints a warning unless native access is enabled, and FFM&rsquo;s native calls are restricted in the same way. JEP 472 states that it is not a goal to deprecate or remove JNI, and many JDK classes, such as those for file I/O and audio, still use it inside. New code should use FFM.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b><code>Robot</code></b></td><td><code>java.awt.Robot</code>, added in Java 1.3, generates real mouse and keyboard events in the operating system&rsquo;s input queue, &ldquo;for the purposes of test automation, self-running demos, and other applications where control of the mouse and keyboard is needed&rdquo;. <code>Robot.mouseMove</code> really moves the pointer. Its documentation warns that platforms may restrict that access: on X Window systems without the XTEST extension, for example, creating a <code>Robot</code> fails.</td><td><b>Still in the JDK</b> (it is in the Java SE 25 API), and still used to test desktop apps. For web apps, browser-automation tools such as Playwright and Selenium do this job (see <a href="#testing">Testing</a>). If you meant robots: <b>leJOS</b> replaced the firmware on LEGO Mindstorms bricks so that they could run Java, with versions for the RCX (2000), NXT (2007) and EV3 (2014); <a href="#robotics">Robotics</a> covers today&rsquo;s tools.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>MIDlets</b></td><td>Java apps and games on feature phones. A MIDlet is an application for <b>MIDP</b>, the Mobile Information Device Profile of <b>Java ME</b>, which ran on the Connected Limited Device Configuration (CLDC). The first MIDP phones launched in April 2001. Apps came as a <code>.jad</code> description and a <code>.jar</code>, often downloaded over the air. By 2013 there were more than three billion Java ME phones, but the platform was already in decline as smartphones overtook feature phones. Android runs apps written in Java, but its runtime is very different from Java ME.</td><td><b>Largely gone</b> from phones; emulators keep the old games playable. Java&rsquo;s smallest platform lives on as <b>Java Card</b>, in SIM and eSIM cards, bank cards, identity cards and passports. The browser applet, another early Java promise, was deprecated in JDK 9 (JEP 289), marked for removal in JDK 17 (JEP 398) and removed in <b>JDK 26</b> (JEP 504).</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
    <p><b>What FFM looks like.</b> One Java method handle replaces the native method, header file and C wrapper:</p>
    <div class="snip"><pre><code>// calling C's strlen from Java with the FFM API (the example in JEP 454)
Linker linker = Linker.nativeLinker();
MethodHandle strlen = linker.downcallHandle(
    linker.defaultLookup().find("strlen").get(),
    FunctionDescriptor.of(JAVA_LONG, ADDRESS)
);
try (Arena arena = Arena.ofConfined()) {
    MemorySegment str = arena.allocateFrom("Hello");
    long len          = (long)strlen.invoke(str);    // 5
}</code></pre></div>
    <p><b>The JNI warning since JDK 24</b>, as JEP 472 shows it. Adding <code>--enable-native-access=&lt;module&gt;</code> (or <code>ALL-UNNAMED</code> for code on the class path) at launch removes it:</p>
    <div class="snip"><pre><code>WARNING: A restricted method in java.lang.System has been called
WARNING: System::load has been called by com.foo.Server in module com.foo (file:/path/to/com.foo.jar)
WARNING: Use --enable-native-access=com.foo to avoid a warning for callers in this module
WARNING: Restricted methods will be blocked in a future release unless native access is enabled</code></pre></div>
    <p><i>Our view:</i> the pattern across all three is the same. Java keeps an old API working for years, adds a safer replacement, and then warns, deprecates and removes in public steps, each in a JEP you can read. <i>The other view:</i> some still see that pace as too slow. JNI took over twenty years to get a supported alternative, and the slow fading of applets and Java ME let other platforms take the browser and the phone.</p>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Write classes, records, collections and streams</li><li>Use Optional and java.time correctly</li><li>Run and write JUnit tests</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Use modern features (records, sealed types, pattern switch, virtual threads)</li><li>Handle concurrency safely with executors and CompletableFuture</li><li>Build with Gradle or Maven and manage dependencies</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Tune the JVM for containers (memory, GC, startup)</li><li>Design APIs and module boundaries</li><li>Profile and fix performance problems</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Lead platform upgrades across LTS releases</li><li>Understand JVM internals (JIT, GC, class loading)</li><li>Set the organisation's Java standards and libraries</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>JEPs for each release since 17</li><li>Garbage collectors: G1, ZGC, Shenandoah</li><li>Java Flight Recorder and JDK Mission Control</li><li>Structured concurrency and scoped values</li><li>GraalVM native image and AOT caches</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://dev.java/learn/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">dev.java: Learn</a><span class="lnote"> Oracle's official tutorials for modern Java</span></li><li><a href="https://openjdk.org/jeps/0" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OpenJDK JEP index</a><span class="lnote"> Every enhancement proposal, with status and release</span></li><li><a href="https://openjdk.org/projects/jdk/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">JDK project releases</a><span class="lnote"> What shipped in each JDK version</span></li><li><a href="https://junit.org/junit5/docs/current/user-guide/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">JUnit user guide</a><span class="lnote"> Tests, parameterised tests, extensions</span></li><li><a href="https://cloud.google.com/java/docs/reference/google-cloud-bigquery/latest/overview" target="_blank" rel="noopener">BigQuery Java client</a><span class="lnote"> API reference</span></li><li><a href="https://beam.apache.org/documentation/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Apache Beam documentation</a><span class="lnote"> Programming guide for Dataflow pipelines</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs/api/java.base/java/lang/Thread.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Thread (since 1.0)</a>; <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs/api/java.base/java/util/concurrent/ExecutorService.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ExecutorService (since 1.5)</a>; <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs/api/java.base/java/util/concurrent/ForkJoinPool.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ForkJoinPool (since 1.7)</a>; <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs/api/java.base/java/util/concurrent/CompletableFuture.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CompletableFuture (since 1.8)</a>; <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs/api/java.base/java/util/concurrent/Flow.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Flow (since 9)</a>; <a href="https://openjdk.org/jeps/425" target="_blank" rel="noopener">JEP 425</a>; <a href="https://openjdk.org/jeps/444" target="_blank" rel="noopener">JEP 444</a>; <a href="https://openjdk.org/jeps/453" target="_blank" rel="noopener">JEP 453</a>; <a href="https://openjdk.org/jeps/491" target="_blank" rel="noopener">JEP 491</a>; <a href="https://openjdk.org/jeps/505" target="_blank" rel="noopener">JEP 505</a>; <a href="https://openjdk.org/jeps/506" target="_blank" rel="noopener">JEP 506</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green_thread" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Green threads (Wikipedia)</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Java_(programming_language)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Java (programming language)</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Java_Community_Process" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Java Community Process</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Acquisition_of_Sun_Microsystems_by_Oracle_Corporation" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Acquisition of Sun Microsystems by Oracle</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apache_Harmony" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Apache Harmony</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/OpenJDK" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: OpenJDK</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jakarta_EE" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Jakarta EE</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Java_version_history" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Java version history</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Google_LLC_v._Oracle_America,_Inc." target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Google LLC v. Oracle America, Inc.</a>; <a href="https://openjdk.org/jeps/1" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OpenJDK: JEP 1, JDK Enhancement-Proposal and Roadmap Process</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/OpenJDK" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: OpenJDK (OpenJDK builds)</a>; <a href="https://www.oracle.com/java/technologies/java-se-support-roadmap.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Oracle: Java SE Support Roadmap</a>; <a href="https://www.oracle.com/downloads/licenses/no-fee-license.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Oracle: No-Fee Terms and Conditions</a>; <a href="https://github.com/adoptium/adoptium.net/blob/main/content/asciidoc-pages/docs/faq/index.adoc" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Adoptium: Temurin FAQ (source)</a>; <a href="https://github.com/adoptium/adoptium.net/blob/main/content/asciidoc-pages/support/index.adoc" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Adoptium: support policy (source)</a>; <a href="https://aws.amazon.com/corretto/faqs/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Amazon Corretto FAQs</a>; <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/java/openjdk/support" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Microsoft Build of OpenJDK: support</a>; <a href="https://github.com/bell-sw/Liberica" target="_blank" rel="noopener">BellSoft Liberica (GitHub)</a>; <a href="https://github.com/SAP/SapMachine" target="_blank" rel="noopener">SapMachine (GitHub)</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/OpenJ9" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: OpenJ9</a>; <a href="https://github.com/oracle/graal/blob/master/docs/introduction.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GraalVM: introduction (source)</a>; <a href="https://github.com/paketo-buildpacks/bellsoft-liberica" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Paketo: BellSoft Liberica buildpack</a>; <a href="https://github.com/sdkman/sdkman-cli" target="_blank" rel="noopener">SDKMAN (GitHub)</a>; <a href="https://github.com/foojay2020/discoapi" target="_blank" rel="noopener">foojay Disco API (GitHub)</a>; <a href="https://openjdk.org/jeps/454" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OpenJDK: JEP 454, Foreign Function & Memory API</a>; <a href="https://openjdk.org/jeps/472" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OpenJDK: JEP 472, Prepare to Restrict the Use of JNI</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Java_Native_Interface" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Java Native Interface</a>; <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs/api/java.desktop/java/awt/Robot.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Java SE 25 API: java.awt.Robot</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/LeJOS" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: leJOS</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mobile_Information_Device_Profile" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Mobile Information Device Profile</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/MIDlet" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: MIDlet</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Java_Platform,_Micro_Edition" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Java Platform, Micro Edition</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Java_Card" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Java Card</a>; <a href="https://openjdk.org/jeps/289" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OpenJDK: JEP 289, Deprecate the Applet API</a>; <a href="https://openjdk.org/jeps/398" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OpenJDK: JEP 398, Deprecate the Applet API for Removal</a>; <a href="https://openjdk.org/jeps/504" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OpenJDK: JEP 504, Remove the Applet API</a>.</p><p class="runcode"><b>Run the code:</b> <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/well-rounded-engineer-code/tree/main/java" target="_blank" rel="noopener">well-rounded-engineer-code/java</a>, with a README that says how to run it and what it shows. Stuck, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/well-rounded-engineer-code/issues/new?title=%5BJava%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= JAVA THREADS HISTORY ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-javathreads" data-name="How Java Threads Evolved">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>How Java threads evolved</h2><p>Thirty years of answering one question: how does a server wait for thousands of slow things at once without running out of threads? From raw threads to pools, fork/join, futures, reactive streams and virtual threads, with a code example and the cost of each step.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>The short answer</h3>
    <p>Most server code spends its life waiting: for a database row, an HTTP reply, a file. The CPU is idle while it waits. <i>Our view:</i> every change to how Java handles threads has been about one thing, <b>making that waiting cheap</b>. <i>The other view</i> is in the card &ldquo;Our view, and the other view&rdquo;. The page <a href="#java">Java</a> has the same history as a one-table summary; this page tells it step by step, with what each step cost.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Thirty years in one line</h3>
    <figure class="fig"><svg viewBox="0 0 640 112" role="img" aria-labelledby="jt-t jt-d"><title id="jt-t">Six eras of Java threads, 1996 to 2023</title><desc id="jt-d">A timeline of six eras. 1996, raw threads: one Java thread per operating-system thread. 2004, thread pools: reuse a fixed set of threads. 2011, fork/join: split CPU work and steal idle time. 2014, futures: chain work without waiting. 2017, reactive: a few threads that never block. 2023, virtual threads: millions of cheap threads.</desc><line class="ln" x1="10" y1="30" x2="630" y2="30"/><circle class="dot" cx="61.7" cy="30" r="5"/><text class="lb acc" x="61.7" y="18" text-anchor="middle">1996</text><text class="lb hd" x="61.7" y="56" text-anchor="middle">Raw threads</text><text class="lb mu" x="61.7" y="76" text-anchor="middle">one Java thread</text><text class="lb mu" x="61.7" y="92" text-anchor="middle">per OS thread</text><circle class="dot" cx="165.0" cy="30" r="5"/><text class="lb acc" x="165.0" y="18" text-anchor="middle">2004</text><text class="lb hd" x="165.0" y="56" text-anchor="middle">Thread pools</text><text class="lb mu" x="165.0" y="76" text-anchor="middle">reuse a fixed</text><text class="lb mu" x="165.0" y="92" text-anchor="middle">set of threads</text><circle class="dot" cx="268.3" cy="30" r="5"/><text class="lb acc" x="268.3" y="18" text-anchor="middle">2011</text><text class="lb hd" x="268.3" y="56" text-anchor="middle">Fork/join</text><text class="lb mu" x="268.3" y="76" text-anchor="middle">split CPU work,</text><text class="lb mu" x="268.3" y="92" text-anchor="middle">steal idle time</text><circle class="dot" cx="371.7" cy="30" r="5"/><text class="lb acc" x="371.7" y="18" text-anchor="middle">2014</text><text class="lb hd" x="371.7" y="56" text-anchor="middle">Futures</text><text class="lb mu" x="371.7" y="76" text-anchor="middle">chain work</text><text class="lb mu" x="371.7" y="92" text-anchor="middle">without waiting</text><circle class="dot" cx="475.0" cy="30" r="5"/><text class="lb acc" x="475.0" y="18" text-anchor="middle">2017</text><text class="lb hd" x="475.0" y="56" text-anchor="middle">Reactive</text><text class="lb mu" x="475.0" y="76" text-anchor="middle">a few threads,</text><text class="lb mu" x="475.0" y="92" text-anchor="middle">never blocking</text><circle class="dot" cx="578.3" cy="30" r="5"/><text class="lb acc" x="578.3" y="18" text-anchor="middle">2023</text><text class="lb hd" x="578.3" y="56" text-anchor="middle">Virtual threads</text><text class="lb mu" x="578.3" y="76" text-anchor="middle">millions of</text><text class="lb mu" x="578.3" y="92" text-anchor="middle">cheap threads</text></svg><figcaption>Six eras, each answering the same question differently. The years are the Java (or library) releases named in each card below.</figcaption></figure>
  </article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>1. Raw threads</h3>
    <p class="note"><b>Java 1.0 &middot; 1996</b></p>
    <p>Java shipped with threads in the platform from the start: <code>Thread</code>, <code>Runnable</code>, the <code>synchronized</code> keyword, and <code>wait</code>/<code>notify</code> on every object. Concurrency was part of the language and its core library, not an add-on.</p>
    <pre><code>Thread t = new Thread(new Runnable() {
    public void run() { handle(request); }
});
t.start();</code></pre>
    <p>Early JVMs used <b>green threads</b>, scheduled by the JVM itself. They were abandoned in Java 1.3 for <b>native threads</b>: each Java thread became a real operating-system thread.</p>
    <p class="note"><b>The cost:</b> an OS thread reserves a large stack (by default 1 MB on 64-bit x86 Linux, 2 MB on ARM64 Linux) and is expensive to create and switch. A server could hold a few thousand threads, not hundreds of thousands.</p>
  </article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>2. Thread pools and <code>java.util.concurrent</code></h3>
    <p class="note"><b>Java 5 &middot; 2004</b></p>
    <p><code>java.util.concurrent</code> (JSR 166, led by Doug Lea) changed everyday Java. Instead of creating a thread per task, you hand tasks to an <b>executor</b> that reuses a fixed pool. It came with <code>Future</code> and <code>Callable</code>, explicit locks, atomic variables, <code>ConcurrentHashMap</code>, and coordination tools such as <code>CountDownLatch</code>. The same release brought a new Java Memory Model (JSR 133), so these tools had firm guarantees underneath.</p>
    <pre><code>ExecutorService pool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(200);
Future&lt;Case&gt; f = pool.submit(() -&gt; repository.find(id));   // shown in today's syntax
Case c = f.get();   // blocks this thread until the result is ready</code></pre>
    <p>This became the classic server model behind Servlets and Spring MVC: <b>one pooled thread per request</b>, blocking while it waits.</p>
    <p class="note"><b>The cost:</b> the pool size is the ceiling. With 200 threads all waiting on a slow database, request 201 queues, even though the CPU is idle.</p>
  </article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>3. Fork/join</h3>
    <p class="note"><b>Java 7 &middot; 2011</b></p>
    <p><code>ForkJoinPool</code> tackled a different problem: big CPU-bound jobs. A task splits itself into smaller tasks, and idle threads <b>steal work</b> from busy ones' queues. It made multi-core machines easy to use for computation. It did nothing for waiting on I/O.</p>
  </article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>4. Futures you can chain</h3>
    <p class="note"><b>Java 8 &middot; 2014</b></p>
    <p><code>CompletableFuture</code>, together with lambdas, let you describe what happens <i>after</i> a result arrives instead of blocking a thread to wait for it:</p>
    <pre><code>CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -&gt; fetchCase(id))
    .thenApply(this::summarise)
    .thenAccept(this::send);</code></pre>
    <p>No thread sits idle between the steps. Each step runs when the previous one completes.</p>
    <p class="note"><b>The cost:</b> error handling, timeouts and debugging get harder. A stack trace no longer shows the story of the request.</p>
  </article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>5. Reactive</h3>
    <p class="note"><b>Reactive Streams 1.0, 2015 &middot; Java 9 <code>Flow</code>, 2017 &middot; Spring WebFlux, 2017</b></p>
    <p>Underneath, non-blocking networking had existed since <b>NIO</b> in Java 1.4 (2002): one thread with a <code>Selector</code> can watch many sockets. Netty built an asynchronous, event-driven framework on it: a handful of threads that never block, serving very many connections.</p>
    <p>Reactive libraries made that model programmable. An early, widely used one was <b>RxJava</b>, Netflix&rsquo;s JVM implementation of Microsoft&rsquo;s Reactive Extensions, first published around 2013. It introduced many Java developers to <code>Observable</code> and to operators such as <code>map</code>, <code>flatMap</code>, <code>zip</code> and <code>retry</code>. RxJava 2 (October 2016) was rewritten on the <b>Reactive Streams</b> specification (1.0 in spring 2015), which grew out of RxJava 1.x and was started by engineers at Netflix, Pivotal and Lightbend. Project Reactor, the library under Spring WebFlux, follows the same specification. These libraries describe work as streams of events with <b>backpressure</b>, so a slow consumer can tell a fast producer to slow down. Java 9 put the same interfaces into the JDK as <code>java.util.concurrent.Flow</code>. Spring Framework 5.0 (2017) added <b>WebFlux</b>; Spring Boot&rsquo;s WebFlux starter runs it on Netty by default.</p>
    <pre><code>webClient.get().uri("/cases/{id}", id)
    .retrieve()
    .bodyToMono(Case.class)
    .map(this::summarise)
    .timeout(Duration.ofSeconds(5));</code></pre>
    <p class="note"><b>The cost:</b> every layer has to be non-blocking, down to the database driver (hence R2DBC instead of JDBC). One blocking call on an event-loop thread stalls everyone, and the code reads as chains of operators instead of plain steps.</p>
  </article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>6. Virtual threads</h3>
    <p class="note"><b>Preview in Java 19 and 20 &middot; final in Java 21 &middot; 2023</b></p>
    <p>Project Loom asked a different question: what if threads were simply cheap? A <b>virtual thread</b> is managed by the JVM, not the operating system. When it blocks on I/O, the JVM suspends it and frees the underlying OS thread (the &ldquo;carrier&rdquo;) for another virtual thread. You can run <b>millions</b> of them.</p>
    <pre><code>try (var executor = Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()) {
    executor.submit(() -&gt; handle(request));   // plain blocking code, one cheap thread per task
}
Thread.ofVirtual().start(() -&gt; handle(request));</code></pre>
    <p>The plain, blocking style from 2004 is back, and it scales the way reactive code does. Spring Boot 3.2 turned it into a single setting, <code>spring.threads.virtual.enabled=true</code>, on Java 21 or later.</p>
    <p>The first versions had one notable trap. A virtual thread blocking inside a <code>synchronized</code> block was <b>pinned</b> to its carrier, which could starve the pool. <b>Java 24</b> (JEP 491) removed nearly all of that pinning for <code>synchronized</code>.</p>
  </article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>7. What is arriving now</h3>
    <p class="note"><b>Java 25 (2025) to Java 27</b></p>
    <p><b>Scoped values</b> are final in Java 25 (JEP 506): a safer, cheaper replacement for many uses of <code>ThreadLocal</code> that suits millions of short-lived threads. <b>Structured concurrency</b> has been in preview since Java 21 and was previewed again in each release since: the seventh preview is in Java 27 (JEP 533). It treats a group of subtasks as one unit: start them together, and if one fails, cancel the rest and report one error. It addresses the error-handling problem that futures and reactive code never solved neatly.</p>
  </article>
  <article class="card"><h3>Was Quarkus reactive by nature?</h3>
    <p>Underneath, yes. Quarkus (1.0 in November 2019) runs on <b>Vert.x and Netty</b>, an event-loop engine like the one under WebFlux. But today it does not force reactive code on its users. You can write:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>ordinary blocking code</b>, which Quarkus runs on worker threads, off the event loop;</li>
      <li><b>reactive code</b> with its Mutiny library;</li>
      <li>and, on Java 21 or later, <b>virtual-thread endpoints</b>, marked <code>@RunOnVirtualThread</code>.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Spring offers the same three: blocking MVC, reactive WebFlux, and MVC on virtual threads. More on <a href="#quarkus">Quarkus</a> and <a href="#spring">Spring</a>.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Our view, and the other view</h3>
    <p><i>Our view:</i> the thread did not get faster in thirty years. Each step made it cheaper to keep a thread waiting, until it cost almost nothing, so for most request/response services virtual threads with plain blocking code are now the simplest choice that scales.</p>
    <p><i>The other view:</i> reactive code is more than a workaround for expensive threads. Backpressure, streaming and composing many events are first-class in it, and teams that need those may find a reactive stack the clearer model, with virtual threads adding little.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Which model to use today</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Good for (<i>analysis</i>)</th><th>Watch out for</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td><b>Virtual threads</b></td><td>Most request/response services that wait on databases and APIs. Simple code that scales.</td><td>Very long CPU work still needs a bounded pool. Libraries that hold locks or <code>ThreadLocal</code> caches deserve a check.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Reactive</b> (WebFlux, Mutiny)</td><td>Streaming, server-sent events, backpressure between fast and slow parts, very high connection counts.</td><td>Everything must be non-blocking end to end, and debugging is harder.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Fixed thread pools</b></td><td>CPU-bound work, and limiting how much load reaches a fragile downstream.</td><td>Pool size becomes the throughput ceiling when tasks mostly wait.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Your team already runs one model well</b></td><td>Stay with it unless a row above forces a change.</td><td>Switching has a cost.</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note">The races, deadlocks and queues underneath all of these are on <a href="#concurrency">Concurrency</a>, with runnable examples.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Further reading</h3>
    <p><b>Java 8 Lambdas</b> by Richard Warburton (O&rsquo;Reilly) is a short, practical introduction to the functional style that step 4 relies on: lambdas, streams, collectors and <code>CompletableFuture</code>. The book&rsquo;s exercises and examples are on GitHub at <a href="https://github.com/RichardWarburton/java-8-lambdas-exercises" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RichardWarburton/java-8-lambdas-exercises</a>.</p>
  </article>
  <article class="card wide"><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://openjdk.org/jeps/444" target="_blank" rel="noopener">JEP 444: Virtual Threads</a>; <a href="https://openjdk.org/jeps/491" target="_blank" rel="noopener">JEP 491: Synchronize Virtual Threads without Pinning</a>; <a href="https://openjdk.org/jeps/505" target="_blank" rel="noopener">JEP 505: Structured Concurrency (Fifth Preview)</a>; <a href="https://openjdk.org/jeps/533" target="_blank" rel="noopener">JEP 533: Structured Concurrency (Seventh Preview)</a>; <a href="https://openjdk.org/jeps/506" target="_blank" rel="noopener">JEP 506: Scoped Values</a>; <a href="https://openjdk.org/jeps/266" target="_blank" rel="noopener">JEP 266: More Concurrency Updates (Flow)</a>; <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/21/docs/specs/man/java.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The java command (-Xss defaults)</a>; <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/21/docs/api/java.base/java/util/concurrent/ForkJoinPool.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Java SE 21 API: ForkJoinPool</a>; <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/21/docs/api/java.base/java/util/concurrent/CompletableFuture.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Java SE 21 API: CompletableFuture</a>; <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/21/docs/api/java.base/java/util/concurrent/Flow.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Java SE 21 API: Flow</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Java_version_history" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Java version history</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green_thread" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Green thread</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reactive_Streams" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Reactive Streams</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Netty_(software)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Netty</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quarkus" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Quarkus</a>; <a href="https://github.com/ReactiveX/RxJava" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RxJava README</a>; <a href="https://github.com/reactive-streams/reactive-streams-jvm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Reactive Streams for the JVM</a>; <a href="https://docs.spring.io/spring-framework/reference/web/webflux/new-framework.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Spring Framework: WebFlux</a>; <a href="https://spring.io/blog/2023/11/23/spring-boot-3-2-0-available-now" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Spring Boot 3.2 release announcement</a>; <a href="https://github.com/quarkusio/quarkus/blob/main/docs/src/main/asciidoc/virtual-threads.adoc" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Quarkus: virtual threads guide</a>.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= IO NIO HISTORY ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-ionio" data-name="IO, NIO &amp; Web Servers">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>IO, NIO and web servers</h2><p>How Java I/O went from blocking streams to NIO, NIO.2 and virtual threads, and how web servers evolved alongside it, from CGI and Apache to nginx, Netty and Tomcat.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>The short answer</h3>
    <p>Every web server answers the same question: when ten thousand clients are connected and most of them are idle or slow, who waits for them? Over thirty years the answer moved from a process, to a thread, to an event loop, and now back to a thread that costs almost nothing. The same story from the thread&rsquo;s side is on <a href="#javathreads">How Java Threads Evolved</a>; the network underneath is on <a href="#networking">Networking</a>.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Thirty years in one line</h3>
    <figure class="fig"><svg viewBox="0 0 640 112" role="img" aria-labelledby="io-t io-d"><title id="io-t">Six eras of serving many clients, 1993 to 2023</title><desc id="io-d">A timeline of six eras. 1993, a process per request: CGI and Apache prefork. 1996, a thread per connection: java.io, servlets and Tomcat. 2002, event loops: epoll, Java NIO and nginx. 2004 to 2011, frameworks on NIO: Netty, Jetty and NIO.2. 2009 to 2017, asynchronous all the way: Servlet 3, Node.js and WebFlux. 2023, virtual threads: blocking code that is cheap.</desc><line class="ln" x1="10" y1="30" x2="630" y2="30"/><circle class="dot" cx="61.7" cy="30" r="5"/><text class="lb acc" x="61.7" y="18" text-anchor="middle">1993</text><text class="lb hd" x="61.7" y="56" text-anchor="middle">Process</text><text class="lb mu" x="61.7" y="76" text-anchor="middle">per request:</text><text class="lb mu" x="61.7" y="92" text-anchor="middle">CGI, prefork</text><circle class="dot" cx="165.0" cy="30" r="5"/><text class="lb acc" x="165.0" y="18" text-anchor="middle">1996</text><text class="lb hd" x="165.0" y="56" text-anchor="middle">Thread</text><text class="lb mu" x="165.0" y="76" text-anchor="middle">per connection:</text><text class="lb mu" x="165.0" y="92" text-anchor="middle">java.io, Tomcat</text><circle class="dot" cx="268.3" cy="30" r="5"/><text class="lb acc" x="268.3" y="18" text-anchor="middle">2002</text><text class="lb hd" x="268.3" y="56" text-anchor="middle">Event loops</text><text class="lb mu" x="268.3" y="76" text-anchor="middle">epoll, NIO,</text><text class="lb mu" x="268.3" y="92" text-anchor="middle">nginx</text><circle class="dot" cx="371.7" cy="30" r="5"/><text class="lb acc" x="371.7" y="18" text-anchor="middle">2004&ndash;11</text><text class="lb hd" x="371.7" y="56" text-anchor="middle">Frameworks</text><text class="lb mu" x="371.7" y="76" text-anchor="middle">on NIO: Netty,</text><text class="lb mu" x="371.7" y="92" text-anchor="middle">NIO.2</text><circle class="dot" cx="475.0" cy="30" r="5"/><text class="lb acc" x="475.0" y="18" text-anchor="middle">2009&ndash;17</text><text class="lb hd" x="475.0" y="56" text-anchor="middle">Async</text><text class="lb mu" x="475.0" y="76" text-anchor="middle">Servlet 3, Node,</text><text class="lb mu" x="475.0" y="92" text-anchor="middle">WebFlux</text><circle class="dot" cx="578.3" cy="30" r="5"/><text class="lb acc" x="578.3" y="18" text-anchor="middle">2023</text><text class="lb hd" x="578.3" y="56" text-anchor="middle">Virtual threads</text><text class="lb mu" x="578.3" y="76" text-anchor="middle">blocking code,</text><text class="lb mu" x="578.3" y="92" text-anchor="middle">cheap</text></svg><figcaption>Six eras. The years are the releases named in the cards below.</figcaption></figure>
  </article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>1. A process per request</h3>
    <p class="note"><b>1993&ndash;1995</b></p>
    <p>The early web ran programs through <b>CGI</b>, specified by the NCSA team in 1993: for every request, the web server started a new process, passed the request in environment variables and standard input, and sent back whatever the program printed. It was simple and fully isolated, but starting a process per request is slow.</p>
    <p><b>Apache httpd</b> (April 1995) kept processes around instead. Its pre-forking model (later the &ldquo;prefork&rdquo; module) held a pool of worker processes, each answering one request at a time.</p>
    <p class="note"><b>The cost:</b> one process per connection. Memory, not CPU, set the number of users a server could hold.</p>
  </article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>2. <code>java.io</code>: a thread per connection</h3>
    <p class="note"><b>Java 1.0 (1996) &middot; Servlets (1997) &middot; Tomcat (1999)</b></p>
    <p>Java&rsquo;s original I/O, <code>java.io</code>, is <b>stream-based and blocking</b>: <code>InputStream.read()</code> blocks until data is available. A server built on it accepts a socket and gives it a thread:</p>
    <pre><code>ServerSocket server = new ServerSocket(8080);
while (true) {
    Socket socket = server.accept();               // blocks until a client connects
    pool.submit(() -&gt; handle(socket));             // that thread blocks on every read and write
}</code></pre>
    <p>The <b>Servlet</b> API (1.0 in 1996&ndash;97) put a standard interface over this model, and <b>Tomcat</b>, first released at the Apache Software Foundation in 1999, became a widely used container, alongside <b>Jetty</b>. Threads were lighter than processes, and the code was easy to read: read the request, call the database, write the response, top to bottom.</p>
    <p class="note"><b>The cost:</b> an idle keep-alive connection still holds a whole thread. In 1999 Dan Kegel named the problem <b>C10K</b>: how do you serve ten thousand clients at once?</p>
  </article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>3. Readiness and event loops: NIO</h3>
    <p class="note"><b>kqueue (2000) &middot; Java 1.4 NIO (2002) &middot; epoll (2002) &middot; nginx (2004)</b></p>
    <p>Operating systems answered C10K with efficient readiness notification: <b>kqueue</b> in FreeBSD 4.1 (July 2000) and <b>epoll</b> in Linux <span data-other-version>2.5.45</span> (October 2002), replacing the older <code>select</code> and <code>poll</code>, whose cost grows with the number of sockets watched. Instead of one thread waiting on each socket, <b>one thread asks the kernel which sockets are ready</b> and services only those.</p>
    <p>Java got the same ability in <b>Java 1.4 (February 2002)</b> through <b>NIO</b> (JSR 51): channels, buffers, and a <code>Selector</code> that watches many channels at once.</p>
    <pre><code>Selector selector = Selector.open();
serverChannel.configureBlocking(false);
serverChannel.register(selector, SelectionKey.OP_ACCEPT);
while (true) {
    selector.select();                             // one thread waits for any socket to be ready
    for (SelectionKey key : selector.selectedKeys()) {
        if (key.isAcceptable()) accept(key);
        if (key.isReadable())   read(key);         // never blocks: the data is already there
    }
    selector.selectedKeys().clear();
}</code></pre>
    <p>Outside Java, <b>nginx</b> (October 2004) was built on this model: a master process and a few worker processes, each handling many connections with an asynchronous, event-driven approach rather than a thread each. It became a widely used front door for web applications, often in front of Java servers.</p>
    <p class="note"><b>The cost:</b> raw NIO is hard. Partial reads, buffer flipping, back-pressure and threading rules are all yours to get right.</p>
  </article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>4. Frameworks on NIO</h3>
    <p class="note"><b>Netty and Apache MINA (mid-2000s) &middot; Tomcat 6 NIO connector (2006&ndash;07) &middot; NIO.2 in Java 7 (2011)</b></p>
    <p>Libraries took on that difficulty. <b>Apache MINA</b> and then <b>Netty</b>, created by Trustin Lee, wrapped NIO in event loops, pipelines of handlers and pooled buffers. Netty became the networking layer under much of the Java world: gRPC for Java, Cassandra, Elasticsearch, Vert.x and, later, Spring Boot&rsquo;s default server for WebFlux.</p>
    <p>Servlet containers moved their connectors to NIO too. Tomcat 6.0 (betas in 2006, stable in 2007) shipped an NIO connector, so an <b>idle keep-alive connection no longer held a thread</b>; the old blocking connector was removed in Tomcat 8.5 and 9. Request processing itself still used a thread per request.</p>
    <p><b>NIO.2</b> in Java 7 (2011, JSR 203) added asynchronous channels and the <code>java.nio.file</code> API (<code>Path</code>, <code>Files</code>) that replaced most uses of <code>java.io.File</code>.</p>
  </article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>5. Asynchronous all the way through</h3>
    <p class="note"><b>Servlet 3.0 (2009) &middot; Node.js (2009) &middot; Servlet 3.1 (2013) &middot; Spring WebFlux (2017)</b></p>
    <p>The next step was to free the thread during the request, not only between requests. <b>Servlet 3.0</b> (December 2009) let a request be suspended and resumed later on another thread, and <b>Servlet 3.1</b> (May 2013) added non-blocking reads and writes.</p>
    <p><b>Node.js</b> (2009) showed a whole application could live on one event loop, with every I/O call asynchronous. In Java, <b>Vert.x</b> (2011), <b>Undertow</b> (first released 2013, 1.0 in 2014) and <b>Spring WebFlux</b> (2017, on Reactor, running on Netty by default) followed. A handful of event-loop threads serve everything, as long as nobody blocks one of them.</p>
    <p class="note"><b>The cost:</b> every library in the request path must be non-blocking, down to the database driver. One blocking call on an event-loop thread stalls every connection that thread serves.</p>
  </article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>6. Virtual threads: blocking I/O becomes cheap</h3>
    <p class="note"><b>Socket rewrites in Java 13 and 15 &middot; virtual threads final in Java 21 (2023)</b></p>
    <p>Project Loom took a different route. Java first reimplemented its classic blocking socket classes (JEP 353 in Java 13, JEP 373 in Java 15) so they could work with a new kind of thread, then called fibers. Then <b>virtual threads</b> (final in Java 21, JEP 444) made threads cheap. When a virtual thread blocks on a socket, the JVM unmounts it and resubmits it when the operation can complete; on Linux, the JDK&rsquo;s poller underneath uses <b>epoll</b>.</p>
    <pre><code>try (var executor = Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()) {
    while (true) {
        Socket socket = server.accept();
        executor.submit(() -&gt; handle(socket));     // the 1996 code, now one cheap thread per connection
    }
}</code></pre>
    <p>The code from step 2 is back, and it scales like step 3. Spring Boot runs request handling on virtual threads with one property (<code>spring.threads.virtual.enabled=true</code>), and Helidon 4 introduced a web server (Helidon Nima) built from the ground up on virtual threads.</p>
  </article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>The same problem, four answers</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Who waits for a slow client</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td><b>Process per connection</b></td><td>A whole OS process</td><td>CGI, Apache prefork</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Thread per connection</b></td><td>A platform thread (the JVM&rsquo;s default stack is 1 MB on 64-bit x86 Linux)</td><td><code>java.io</code> servers, classic Tomcat</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Event loop</b></td><td>Nobody: the kernel reports when the socket is ready</td><td>nginx, Node.js, Netty, WebFlux</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Virtual thread</b></td><td>A parked virtual thread, with an event loop underneath</td><td>Spring Boot 3.2 and later on Java 21, Helidon 4</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p>The protocols kept moving too: HTTP/2 (RFC 7540, May 2015, since replaced by RFC 9113) put many requests on one connection, and HTTP/3 (RFC 9114, June 2022) runs over QUIC on UDP. Each step raised the number of conversations a server holds at once.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Our view, and the other view</h3>
    <p><i>Our view:</i> underneath, it is now the same machinery: the kernel&rsquo;s readiness API (epoll, kqueue) does the waiting. What changed is how much of it the programmer has to see. NIO and event loops exposed it; virtual threads hide it again behind plain, blocking-looking code, which suits most services.</p>
    <p><i>The other view:</i> hiding the machinery has a price. Event-loop code makes concurrency and back-pressure visible in the program, and some teams prefer that explicitness, especially for streaming or very high connection counts, where it is easier to reason about than many parked threads.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Choose by requirement</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>If this is true for you</th><th>It leans towards (<i>analysis</i>)</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Request/response service that mostly waits on databases and APIs, on Java 21 or later</td><td>Thread per request on virtual threads</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Streaming, server-sent events, many long-lived idle connections</td><td>An event-loop server (Netty, WebFlux, Vert.x)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Serving static files and terminating TLS in front of applications</td><td>A dedicated event-loop proxy such as nginx</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Your team already runs one model well</td><td>Stay with it unless a row above forces a change: switching has a cost</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>
  <article class="card wide"><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Common_Gateway_Interface" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Common Gateway Interface</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apache_HTTP_Server" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Apache HTTP Server</a>; <a href="https://github.com/apache/httpd/blob/trunk/docs/manual/mod/prefork.xml" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Apache httpd: prefork MPM</a>; <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/javase/8/docs/api/java/io/InputStream.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Java SE 8 API: InputStream</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jakarta_Servlet" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Jakarta Servlet</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apache_Tomcat" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Apache Tomcat</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/C10k_problem" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: C10k problem</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kqueue" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: kqueue</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Epoll" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: epoll</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Non-blocking_I/O_(Java)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Non-blocking I/O (Java)</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nginx" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Nginx</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Netty_(software)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Netty</a>; <a href="https://github.com/grpc/grpc-java" target="_blank" rel="noopener">gRPC Java README</a>; <a href="https://github.com/apache/tomcat/blob/8.5.x/webapps/docs/changelog.xml" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Tomcat 8.5 changelog</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Node.js" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Node.js</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vert.x" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Vert.x</a>; <a href="https://repo1.maven.org/maven2/io/undertow/undertow-core/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Undertow releases on Maven Central</a>; <a href="https://docs.spring.io/spring-framework/reference/web/webflux/new-framework.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Spring Framework: WebFlux</a>; <a href="https://openjdk.org/jeps/353" target="_blank" rel="noopener">JEP 353: Reimplement the Legacy Socket API</a>; <a href="https://openjdk.org/jeps/373" target="_blank" rel="noopener">JEP 373: Reimplement the Legacy DatagramSocket API</a>; <a href="https://openjdk.org/jeps/444" target="_blank" rel="noopener">JEP 444: Virtual Threads</a>; <a href="https://github.com/helidon-io/helidon/blob/helidon-4.x/CHANGELOG.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Helidon changelog</a>; <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/info/rfc7540" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RFC 7540: HTTP/2</a>; <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9114" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RFC 9114: HTTP/3</a>; <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/21/docs/specs/man/java.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The java command (-Xss defaults)</a>.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= PYTHON ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-kotlin" data-name="Kotlin">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Kotlin: concise, null-safe, on the JVM</h2><p>Kotlin is a concise multiplatform language from JetBrains. It runs on the JVM beside Java, uses the same libraries, and adds null safety, data classes and coroutines. Learn <a href="#java">Java</a> first; Kotlin then reads as Java with the boilerplate taken away.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card"><h3>Where you meet it</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Android apps</b>, where it is widely used.</li>
      <li><b>Server-side services</b> with Spring Boot or Ktor, mixed freely with Java in one codebase.</li>
      <li><b>Build scripts:</b> Gradle's Kotlin DSL (<code>build.gradle.kts</code>).</li>
      <li><b>Shared code across platforms</b> with Kotlin Multiplatform.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>The ideas that matter</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Null safety:</b> <code>String</code> can never be null; <code>String?</code> can, and the compiler makes you handle it with <code>?.</code>, <code>?:</code> or a check.</li>
      <li><b>Data classes:</b> <code>equals</code>, <code>hashCode</code>, <code>toString</code> and <code>copy</code> for free.</li>
      <li><b>Sealed types and <code>when</code>:</b> the compiler checks you handled every case.</li>
      <li><b>Extension functions:</b> add behaviour to a type you do not own.</li>
      <li><b>Coroutines:</b> asynchronous code written like sequential code (the <code>kotlinx.coroutines</code> library).</li>
      <li><b>Java interop:</b> call Java and be called from it; watch nullability at the boundary, where Java types arrive as "platform types" the compiler cannot check.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Java, Kotlin or Scala?</h3>
    <p>All three run on the JVM and can use the same libraries. They differ in what they optimise for.</p>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <tr><th></th><th>Java</th><th>Kotlin</th><th>Scala</th></tr>
      <tr><td>First released</td><td>1995</td><td>2011 (1.0 in February 2016)</td><td>2004 (Scala 3 in May 2021)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Created by</td><td>Sun Microsystems</td><td>JetBrains</td><td>Martin Odersky</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Style</td><td>Object-oriented, steadily adding records, pattern matching and virtual threads</td><td>Concise object-oriented with functional features; null safety in the type system</td><td>A deep blend of object-oriented and functional programming, with a powerful type system</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Where you meet it</td><td>Most enterprise back ends, Spring and Quarkus services</td><td>Android, and Spring or Ktor services beside Java</td><td>Data processing (Apache Spark is written in Scala) and functional back ends</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Learning curve</td><td>Gentle; the most material and people</td><td>Gentle for Java developers</td><td>Steeper; very expressive, which can also make code harder to read</td></tr>
    </table></div>
    <p class="note">A practical rule: learn Java first, because everything on the JVM assumes it. Add Kotlin for Android or a more concise service codebase, and Scala when you work with Spark or a team that writes functional code.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>A small program, and its test</h3>
    <p>Run with Maven or Gradle and the Kotlin plugin. This compiles and its tests pass on the JVM.</p>
    <pre><code class="language-kotlin">data class Order(val id: String, val pence: Int, val note: String? = null)

sealed interface Check {
    data class Ok(val pence: Int) : Check
    data class Invalid(val reason: String) : Check
}

fun validate(o: Order): Check = when {
    o.pence &lt;= 0 -&gt; Check.Invalid(&quot;order ${o.id} has no amount&quot;)
    else -&gt; Check.Ok(o.pence)
}

// An extension function: adds behaviour to a type you do not own.
fun Int.asPounds(): String = &quot;£%d.%02d&quot;.format(this / 100, this % 100)

fun main() {
    val orders = listOf(Order(&quot;a1&quot;, 1250, note = &quot;gift&quot;), Order(&quot;a2&quot;, 0), Order(&quot;a3&quot;, 499))
    val total = orders.map(::validate).filterIsInstance&lt;Check.Ok&gt;().sumOf { it.pence }
    println(&quot;total: ${total.asPounds()}&quot;)
    // Null safety: note is String?, so the compiler makes you handle null.
    orders.forEach { println(&quot;${it.id}: ${it.note?.uppercase() ?: &quot;no note&quot;}&quot;) }
}</code></pre>
    <pre><code class="language-kotlin">import kotlin.test.Test
import kotlin.test.assertEquals
import kotlin.test.assertIs

class OrdersTest {
    @Test fun `zero amount is invalid`() { assertIs&lt;Check.Invalid&gt;(validate(Order(&quot;a2&quot;, 0))) }
    @Test fun `pence format as pounds`() { assertEquals(&quot;£17.49&quot;, 1749.asPounds()) }
}</code></pre>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Build and tools</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Gradle</b> with the Kotlin DSL is the most common build; Maven has a Kotlin plugin too.</li>
      <li><b>Tests:</b> <code>kotlin.test</code> on JUnit 5; MockK for mocks; Kotest if you like a specification style.</li>
      <li><b>Style:</b> ktlint or detekt in CI.</li>
      <li><b>IDE:</b> IntelliJ IDEA, from the same company, has the deepest support.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Common mistakes</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b><code>!!</code> everywhere:</b> it turns a compile-time check back into a runtime crash. Handle the null instead.</li>
      <li><b>Trusting Java types:</b> a value from a Java method may be null even if you declared it non-null.</li>
      <li><b>Blocking inside a coroutine:</b> a blocking call ties up the thread; use a suspending API or the IO dispatcher.</li>
      <li><b>Clever over clear:</b> scope functions (<code>let</code>, <code>apply</code>, <code>also</code>) nested three deep are hard to read.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Read Kotlin code beside Java</li><li>Use null safety, data classes and when</li><li>Write and run a test with kotlin.test</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Build a service in Kotlin with Spring Boot or Ktor</li><li>Handle Java interop and platform types safely</li><li>Use coroutines for asynchronous work</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Design structured concurrency and cancellation</li><li>Mix Kotlin and Java in one codebase cleanly</li><li>Set style and lint rules for a team</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Lead a Java-to-Kotlin migration</li><li>Share code across platforms with Kotlin Multiplatform</li><li>Teach idiomatic Kotlin and review for it</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>Null safety and platform types</li><li>Sealed types and exhaustive when</li><li>Coroutines and structured concurrency</li><li>Gradle Kotlin DSL</li><li>Kotlin Multiplatform</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://kotlinlang.org/docs/home.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kotlin documentation</a><span class="lnote"> The official docs, from JetBrains</span></li><li><a href="https://kotlinlang.org/docs/coroutines-overview.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Coroutines overview</a><span class="lnote"> Asynchronous code in Kotlin</span></li><li><a href="https://github.com/Kotlin/kotlinx.coroutines" target="_blank" rel="noopener">kotlinx.coroutines</a><span class="lnote"> The coroutines library's source and guide</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kotlin" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kotlin (Wikipedia)</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scala_(programming_language)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Scala (Wikipedia)</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Java_(programming_language)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Java (Wikipedia)</a>; <a href="https://docs.scala-lang.org/scala3/new-in-scala3.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">New in Scala 3</a>.</p><p class="runcode">A question, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/enrichmeai.github.io/issues/new?title=%5BKotlin%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>. Nothing here is tracked and there are no comments to store: questions live in the open, where the next learner can find the answer.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<section class="panel" id="p-python" data-name="Python">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Python</h2><p>Idioms for pipeline code, the Google Cloud clients, testing and packaging.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Version map</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Ver</th><th>Year</th><th>What you will use</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>3.8</td><td>2019</td><td>Walrus <code>:=</code>, positional-only params <code>/</code>, f-string <code>{x=}</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>3.9</td><td>2020</td><td>Dict merge <code>a | b</code>, <code>str.removeprefix/removesuffix</code>, <code>list[int]</code> generics, <code>zoneinfo</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>3.10</td><td>2021</td><td><code>match</code> statement, <code>X | None</code> unions, <code>zip(strict=True)</code>, better error messages</td></tr>
      <tr><td>3.11</td><td>2022</td><td>Much faster CPython, <code>tomllib</code>, <code>ExceptionGroup</code> / <code>except*</code>, <code>asyncio.TaskGroup</code>, <code>Self</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>3.12</td><td>2023</td><td>Generic syntax <code>def f[T](x: T)</code>, <code>type</code> aliases, freer f-strings, <code>itertools.batched</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>3.13</td><td>2024</td><td>New REPL, experimental free-threaded build (no GIL), experimental JIT</td></tr>
      <tr><td>3.14</td><td>2025</td><td>Template strings <code>t"…"</code>, deferred annotations, free-threaded build officially supported, <code>compression.zstd</code>, <code>concurrent.interpreters</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>3.15</td><td>2026</td><td>Explicit <code>lazy</code> imports, <code>frozendict</code> and <code>sentinel</code> builtins, UTF-8 as the default encoding, unpacking in comprehensions, the <code>profiling</code> package with the Tachyon sampling profiler</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Core idioms</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>from dataclasses import dataclass, field
from pathlib import Path
from datetime import date, datetime

@dataclass(frozen=True, slots=True)
class Order:
    order_ref: str
    order_date: date | None
    tags: list[str] = field(default_factory=list)

rows = [r for r in rows if r.order_date]           # filter
by_order_ref = {r.order_ref: r for r in rows}                     # index
years = {r.order_date.year for r in rows}          # set

if (n := len(rows)) &gt; 1000: print(f"{n=}")           # walrus + debug f-string

for p in Path("data").glob("**/*.parquet"): ...
text = Path("q.sql").read_text()

d = datetime.strptime("05-05-1959", "%d-%m-%Y").date()
cfg = defaults | overrides                            # 3.9 merge</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>match (structural pattern matching)</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>match event:
    case {"state": "SUCCEEDED", "rows": int(n)} if n == 0:
        warn("succeeded with zero rows")
    case {"state": "SUCCEEDED", "rows": int(n)}:
        ok(n)
    case {"state": "FAILED", "error": str(msg)}:
        fail(msg)
    case {"state": state}:
        unknown(state)
    case _:
        raise ValueError(f"bad event: {event!r}")

match path.split("/"):
    case ["data", y, *rest] if y.startswith("year="): ...</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">Patterns match structure and types; they do not do string prefix matching. Use a guard (<code>if</code>) for that.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Batching &amp; counting</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>from itertools import batched, groupby, chain, islice
from collections import Counter, defaultdict

for chunk in batched(rows, 500):        # 3.12; tuples of ≤500
    client.insert_rows_json(table, chunk)

counts = Counter(r.store for r in rows)
counts.most_common(5)

by_store = defaultdict(list)
for r in rows: by_store[r.store].append(r)

# groupby needs sorted input
rows.sort(key=lambda r: r.store)
for store, grp in groupby(rows, key=lambda r: r.store): ...

flat = list(chain.from_iterable(nested))
first10 = list(islice(gen, 10))</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Errors, logging, context managers</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>import logging, contextlib, time
log = logging.getLogger(__name__)
logging.basicConfig(level=logging.INFO,
    format="%(asctime)s %(levelname)s %(name)s %(message)s")

try:
    load(uri)
except NotFound:
    log.warning("missing %s", uri)          # lazy formatting
except Exception:
    log.exception("load failed for %s", uri) # includes traceback
    raise
else:
    log.info("loaded %s", uri)

@contextlib.contextmanager
def timed(label):
    t = time.perf_counter()
    try: yield
    finally: log.info("%s took %.2fs", label, time.perf_counter() - t)

with timed("orders load"): run()</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Concurrency</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># Threads for blocking I/O (GCS, BQ, HTTP)
from concurrent.futures import ThreadPoolExecutor, as_completed
with ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=16) as ex:
    futs = {ex.submit(copy, uri): uri for uri in uris}
    for f in as_completed(futs):
        try: f.result()
        except Exception as e: log.error("%s: %s", futs[f], e)

# asyncio with structured cancellation (3.11)
import asyncio
async def main():
    async with asyncio.TaskGroup() as tg:
        tasks = [tg.create_task(fetch(u)) for u in urls]
    return [t.result() for t in tasks]
asyncio.run(main())

# Processes for CPU-bound work
from concurrent.futures import ProcessPoolExecutor</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>BigQuery Python client</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>from google.cloud import bigquery
client = bigquery.Client(project="my-proj", location="asia-south1")

job_cfg = bigquery.QueryJobConfig(query_parameters=[
    bigquery.ScalarQueryParameter("from_d", "DATE", "2024-01-01"),
    bigquery.ArrayQueryParameter("stores", "STRING", ["1_05", "4_12"]),
])
sql = """SELECT store, COUNT(*) n FROM `my-proj.analytics.orders`
         WHERE order_date &gt;= @from_d AND store IN UNNEST(@stores)
         GROUP BY store"""
for row in client.query(sql, job_config=job_cfg).result():
    print(row.store, row["n"])

df = client.query(sql, job_config=job_cfg).to_dataframe()   # needs db-dtypes

# Dry run
dry = client.query(sql, job_config=bigquery.QueryJobConfig(
    query_parameters=job_cfg.query_parameters,   # same parameters as the real query
    dry_run=True, use_query_cache=False))
print(dry.total_bytes_processed / 1e9, "GB")

# Load parquet from GCS
load = client.load_table_from_uri(
    "gs://bkt/data/year=2024/*.parquet", "my-proj.raw.orders",
    job_config=bigquery.LoadJobConfig(
        source_format=bigquery.SourceFormat.PARQUET,
        write_disposition=bigquery.WriteDisposition.WRITE_APPEND))
load.result()                     # raises on failure
print(load.output_rows)

client.insert_rows_json("my-proj.ops.events", [{"k": "v"}])  # returns errors list
# insert_rows_json is the legacy streaming API (insertAll): for volume, use a load job or the Storage Write API</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>GCS Python client</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>from google.cloud import storage
gcs = storage.Client()
bucket = gcs.bucket("my-landing")

for blob in gcs.list_blobs("my-landing", prefix="data/tar/", match_glob="**/*.tar"):
    print(blob.name, blob.size, blob.etag, blob.updated)

blob = bucket.blob("manifests/run.json")
blob.upload_from_string(json.dumps(m), content_type="application/json",
                        if_generation_match=0)   # create-only, no overwrite
data = bucket.blob("x.json").download_as_bytes()

with bucket.blob("big.csv").open("r") as f:      # streaming read
    for line in f: ...

bucket.copy_blob(bucket.blob("a"), other_bucket, "b")</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>pytest</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>import pytest

@pytest.fixture
def client(monkeypatch):
    fake = FakeBq()
    monkeypatch.setattr("pipeline.bq.client", fake)
    return fake

@pytest.mark.parametrize("raw,expected", [
    ("05-05-1959", date(1959, 5, 5)),
    ("2024-01-31", date(2024, 1, 31)),
])
def test_parse_date(raw, expected):
    assert parse_date(raw) == expected

def test_rejects_garbage():
    with pytest.raises(ValueError, match="does not parse"):
        parse_date("31/31/31")

def test_writes(tmp_path):
    out = tmp_path / "o.json"; write(out); assert out.exists()

# pytest -q -k parse -x --lf       (filter, stop on first fail, last failed)</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Environments &amp; packaging</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># stdlib
python3 -m venv .venv &amp;&amp; . .venv/bin/activate
pip install -e '.[test]'

# uv (fast, lockfile)
uv venv &amp;&amp; uv pip install -e '.[test]'
uv add google-cloud-bigquery
uv run pytest -q

# pyproject.toml
[project]
name = "orchestrator"
requires-python = "&gt;=3.11"
dependencies = ["google-cloud-bigquery&gt;=3", "google-cloud-storage&gt;=2"]
[project.optional-dependencies]
test = ["pytest&gt;=8"]

# lint/format
ruff check . --fix &amp;&amp; ruff format .
mypy src/</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Beam / Dataflow (Python)</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>import apache_beam as beam
from apache_beam.options.pipeline_options import PipelineOptions

with beam.Pipeline(options=PipelineOptions()) as p:
    (p
     | beam.io.ReadFromBigQuery(query="SELECT store FROM `p.raw.orders`",
                                use_standard_sql=True)
     | beam.Map(lambda r: (r["store"], 1))
     | beam.CombinePerKey(sum)
     | beam.Map(lambda kv: {"store": kv[0], "n": kv[1]})
     | beam.io.WriteToBigQuery("p:mart.store_counts",
           schema="store:STRING,n:INTEGER",
           write_disposition=beam.io.BigQueryDisposition.WRITE_TRUNCATE))
# python job.py --runner=DataflowRunner --project=… --region=… --temp_location=gs://…</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Write functions, comprehensions and dataclasses</li><li>Use virtual environments and install packages</li><li>Write simple pytest tests</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Type hints, logging, error handling and context managers</li><li>Use the BigQuery and GCS clients in pipelines</li><li>Package a project with pyproject.toml</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Choose between threads, processes and asyncio</li><li>Design reusable libraries with clear APIs and tests</li><li>Profile memory and CPU; optimise data handling</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Understand CPython internals, the GIL and free-threading</li><li>Own the organisation's Python tooling and packaging</li><li>Mentor and set code standards</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>Typing: generics, Protocols, mypy or pyright</li><li>asyncio internals and TaskGroups</li><li>Packaging: wheels, lockfiles, uv</li><li>Free-threaded CPython (3.13+)</li><li>Data libraries: pandas, Polars, PyArrow</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://docs.python.org/3/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Python documentation</a><span class="lnote"> Language and standard library reference</span></li><li><a href="https://docs.python.org/3/whatsnew/index.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">What's new in Python</a><span class="lnote"> Changes in each release</span></li><li><a href="https://peps.python.org/pep-0008/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PEP 8 style guide</a><span class="lnote"> Conventions for readable Python</span></li><li><a href="https://docs.pytest.org/en/stable/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">pytest documentation</a><span class="lnote"> Fixtures, parametrize, plugins</span></li><li><a href="https://docs.astral.sh/uv/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">uv</a><span class="lnote"> Fast package and project manager</span></li><li><a href="https://cloud.google.com/python/docs/reference/bigquery/latest" target="_blank" rel="noopener">BigQuery Python client</a><span class="lnote"> API reference</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/python/cpython/main/Doc/whatsnew/3.15.rst" target="_blank" rel="noopener">What's new in Python 3.15 (lazy imports, frozendict, sentinel, UTF-8 default, comprehension unpacking, profiling/Tachyon)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/python/peps/main/peps/pep-0790.rst" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PEP 790: 3.15 release schedule (final 2026-10-09)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/python/cpython/main/Doc/whatsnew/3.14.rst" target="_blank" rel="noopener">What's new in Python 3.14</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/python/cpython/main/Doc/whatsnew/3.13.rst" target="_blank" rel="noopener">What's new in Python 3.13 (new REPL, free-threaded, JIT)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/python/cpython/main/Doc/whatsnew/3.12.rst" target="_blank" rel="noopener">What's new in Python 3.12 (PEP 695, PEP 701, itertools.batched)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/python/cpython/main/Doc/whatsnew/3.11.rst" target="_blank" rel="noopener">What's new in Python 3.11 (tomllib, except*, TaskGroup, Self)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/python/cpython/main/Doc/whatsnew/3.10.rst" target="_blank" rel="noopener">What's new in Python 3.10 (zip strict)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/python/peps/main/peps/pep-0750.rst" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PEP 750 / 649 / 779 / 784 / 734 (3.14 features, Final)</a>; <a href="https://pypi.org/pypi/google-cloud-bigquery/json" target="_blank" rel="noopener">google-cloud-bigquery on PyPI (signatures checked locally)</a>; <a href="https://pypi.org/pypi/google-cloud-storage/json" target="_blank" rel="noopener">google-cloud-storage on PyPI (list_blobs match_glob, if_generation_match, copy_blob checked locally)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/apache/beam/master/sdks/python/apache_beam/io/gcp/bigquery.py" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Apache Beam BigQuery IO source (ReadFromBigQuery use_standard_sql)</a>; <a href="https://pypi.org/pypi/pytest/json" target="_blank" rel="noopener">pytest on PyPI (-k, -x, --lf checked via pytest --help)</a>; <a href="https://docs.python.org/3.15/whatsnew/3.15.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">What's new in Python 3.15</a>; <a href="https://github.com/astral-sh/uv/blob/main/docs/pip/environments.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">uv docs: environments</a>; <a href="https://github.com/astral-sh/ruff/blob/main/docs/linter.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ruff docs: linter</a>. A few claims could not be confirmed at their official source from here and are being re-checked.</p><p class="runcode"><b>Run the code:</b> <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/well-rounded-engineer-code/tree/main/python" target="_blank" rel="noopener">well-rounded-engineer-code/python</a>, with a README that says how to run it and what it shows. Stuck, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/well-rounded-engineer-code/issues/new?title=%5BPython%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= JAVASCRIPT ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-javascript" data-name="JavaScript">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>JavaScript: learn the language, then any framework</h2><p>New developers meet React, Angular, Vue, Svelte, Solid and more, and ask which one to learn. Every one of them is JavaScript underneath, and every one is built from the same handful of language patterns. Learn the language and those patterns first, and a framework becomes a few weeks of reading rather than a new career. The language is standardised as ECMAScript by the TC39 committee at Ecma International; each claim here links to TC39 or MDN.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Which version should I learn?</h3>
    <p><b>Short answer: none in particular. Learn modern JavaScript, meaning everything from ECMAScript 2015 onward, and use what your target browsers or Node.js support.</b></p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>ECMAScript 2015 (also called ES6) is the line that matters</b> (<i>our view</i>; it is where most of today&rsquo;s syntax begins). It added <code>let</code> and <code>const</code>, arrow functions, classes, modules (<code>import</code>/<code>export</code>), promises, iterators and <code>for…of</code>, template literals and destructuring. Much older code you meet was written without these.</li>
      <li><b>Since then there has been one edition a year,</b> named by year: ES2016, ES2017, up to ES2026. Each one is small: the proposals that reached Stage 4 before that year&rsquo;s spec was branched, in March.</li>
      <li><b>To reach Stage 4, a feature needs, among other things, two compatible implementations that pass the Test262 conformance tests and real experience with shipping implementations,</b> so by the time it is in an edition it is already running in at least two JavaScript engines.</li>
      <li><b>Do not ask "which ES version". Ask "is this feature available where my code runs?"</b> For browsers, MDN marks each feature with <b>Baseline</b>: <i>widely available</i> means it has worked for at least 2.5 years in Chrome, Edge, Firefox and Safari on desktop, and in Chrome, Firefox and Safari on mobile.</li>
      <li><b>TypeScript is JavaScript with types,</b> checked before the code runs. Learn it after the language, not instead of it.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>What each edition added</h3>
    <p>From TC39's list of finished proposals, by the year each was expected to be published. You do not need to memorise this; it shows how small the yearly steps are, and where the features you use came from.</p>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Edition</th><th>Features you will meet</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>ES2015</td><td><code>let</code>/<code>const</code>, arrow functions, classes, modules, promises, iterators, <code>for…of</code>, template literals, destructuring, <code>Map</code>/<code>Set</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>ES2016</td><td><code>**</code> (exponent), <code>Array.prototype.includes</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>ES2017</td><td><code>async</code>/<code>await</code>, <code>Object.values</code>/<code>entries</code>, string padding</td></tr>
      <tr><td>ES2018</td><td>Rest and spread for objects (<code>{...obj}</code>), async iteration (<code>for await…of</code>), <code>Promise.prototype.finally</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>ES2019</td><td><code>flat</code>/<code>flatMap</code>, <code>Object.fromEntries</code>, optional <code>catch</code> binding</td></tr>
      <tr><td>ES2020</td><td>Optional chaining (<code>a?.b</code>), nullish coalescing (<code>??</code>), dynamic <code>import()</code>, <code>BigInt</code>, <code>Promise.allSettled</code>, <code>globalThis</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>ES2021</td><td><code>replaceAll</code>, <code>Promise.any</code>, logical assignment (<code>??=</code>), numeric separators</td></tr>
      <tr><td>ES2022</td><td>Class fields and <code>#private</code> members, top-level <code>await</code>, <code>.at()</code>, <code>Error</code> cause</td></tr>
      <tr><td>ES2023</td><td>Copying array methods (<code>toSorted</code>, <code>toReversed</code>, <code>with</code>), <code>findLast</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>ES2024</td><td><code>Object.groupBy</code>, <code>Promise.withResolvers</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>ES2025</td><td>Iterator helpers, new <code>Set</code> methods, JSON modules and import attributes, <code>Promise.try</code>, <code>RegExp.escape</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>ES2026</td><td><code>Array.fromAsync</code>, <code>Error.isError</code>, <code>Math.sumPrecise</code>, Base64 for <code>Uint8Array</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Next (expected 2027)</td><td><b>Temporal</b> (a modern date and time API), explicit resource management (<code>using</code>)</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>The patterns underneath every framework</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Functions are values.</b> You pass them, return them and store them. Event handlers, callbacks, React components and hooks are all functions.</li>
      <li><b>Closures:</b> a function keeps access to the variables around it when it was created. MDN: closures are created every time a function is created. Private state and callbacks that remember values rely on this.</li>
      <li><b>The event loop:</b> one thread runs one job at a time, each to completion. Slow work (network, timers, files) finishes later and queues a job. Block that thread and nothing else runs until you finish, including the page&rsquo;s response to the user.</li>
      <li><b>Promises and <code>async</code>/<code>await</code>:</b> a promise is a value that arrives later. <code>await</code> pauses your function, not the thread.</li>
      <li><b>Modules:</b> <code>import</code> and <code>export</code>. Modules always run in strict mode.</li>
      <li><b>Objects and prototypes:</b> <code>class</code> is a cleaner way to write prototype-based inheritance, not a different model.</li>
      <li><b>Immutability:</b> make a new object or array instead of changing the old one (<code>{...obj}</code>, <code>toSorted</code>). Some frameworks compare old and new values to decide what to redraw; a new object makes that comparison cheap and reliable (<i>analysis</i>).</li>
      <li><b>Iterators and generators:</b> anything you can <code>for…of</code> over.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>What a framework is made of</h3>
    <p><i>This mapping is analysis, to help you read any framework's docs.</i></p>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Framework idea</th><th>The JavaScript or web feature under it</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Component</td><td>A function or class that returns a description of the UI</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Props</td><td>Function arguments, usually destructured</td></tr>
      <tr><td>State and hooks</td><td>Closures over values the framework stores for you</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Reactivity, signals, stores</td><td>The observer pattern: subscribe a function, call it on change</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Re-rendering</td><td>Compare old and new values (cheap when data is immutable), or track exactly which values changed</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Effects, data loading</td><td>Promises, <code>async</code>/<code>await</code>, <code>fetch</code>, cleanup functions</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Routing</td><td>The History API and the URL</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Code splitting</td><td>Dynamic <code>import()</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Reusable widgets without a framework</td><td>Web Components: custom elements, Shadow DOM, HTML templates</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Try it: the patterns in plain JavaScript</h3>
    <p>Save as <code>patterns.mjs</code> and run <code>node patterns.mjs</code>, or paste into a browser console. No framework, no install.</p>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>// 1. Closure: count keeps its own private state
function counter() {
  let n = 0;
  return () => ++n;
}
const next = counter();
next(); next();
console.log("closure:", next());            // 3

// 2. Observer: the core of every "reactive" framework
function signal(value) {
  const subs = new Set();
  return {
    get: () => value,
    set: (v) => { value = v; subs.forEach((f) => f(v)); },
    subscribe: (f) => { subs.add(f); return () => subs.delete(f); },
  };
}
const price = signal(10);
const stop = price.subscribe((v) => console.log("render price:", v));
price.set(12);                             // render price: 12
stop();

// 3. Immutability: new array, old one untouched
const items = [3, 1, 2];
const sorted = items.toSorted();
console.log(items, sorted);                // [ 3, 1, 2 ] [ 1, 2, 3 ]

// 4. The event loop: synchronous code finishes first
setTimeout(() => console.log("timer job"), 0);
Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log("promise job"));
console.log("sync code");
// sync code, promise job, timer job

// 5. async/await: pauses the function, not the thread
const wait = (ms) => new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, ms));
async function load() {
  await wait(100);
  return { user: "ada", roles: ["admin"] };
}
const { user, roles: [first] } = await load();   // top-level await, destructuring
console.log(user, first, user?.length ?? 0);</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>A learning order that works</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Values, types, <code>let</code>/<code>const</code>, functions, arrays and objects</li>
      <li>Closures and scope</li>
      <li>The DOM and events: change a page with no framework</li>
      <li>The event loop, promises, <code>async</code>/<code>await</code>, <code>fetch</code></li>
      <li>Modules, then a build tool (see <a href="#buildtools">Build Tools</a>)</li>
      <li>Classes, prototypes, iterators</li>
      <li>TypeScript</li>
      <li><b>Then</b> one framework, the one your team or your target job uses</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="note"><i>Our view:</i> the framework you learn second takes a fraction of the time of the first, because you now see the patterns rather than the syntax. <i>The other view:</i> many people learn a framework first because jobs ask for it, and pick up the language as they go; that works too, as long as you go back and fill in the patterns above. See <a href="#uihistory">UI History</a> for how the frameworks came and went.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Choosing a framework: start from the requirement</h3>
    <p>Comparisons of frameworks usually turn into preference. Instead, write down what your product and your team need, and ask each candidate the same questions. Check every answer in the framework&rsquo;s own documentation, at the version you would use.</p>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>If you need</th><th>Ask each candidate</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Fast first load and search-engine indexing (public sites, shops)</td><td>Does it render on the server or generate static pages, and how much JavaScript does a page ship?</td></tr>
      <tr><td>A rich app behind a login (dashboards, tools)</td><td>How does it manage state, forms and data loading at scale?</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Mostly static content with a few interactive parts</td><td>Could plain HTML, a little JavaScript, or Web Components do it with no framework at all?</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Mobile apps from the same code</td><td>Is there a supported way to build native or cross-platform apps? (See <a href="#apps">Apps</a>.)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>A product that must live for many years</td><td>What is its release and support policy, who funds it, and how hard were its past major upgrades?</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Hiring and a team that can keep it running</td><td>Do your current team and your local job market know it?</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Accessibility from the start</td><td>Do its components and docs follow WCAG patterns? (See <a href="#accessibility">Accessibility</a>.)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>An existing codebase</td><td>Can it be added page by page, or does it need a rewrite?</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note">Two rules of thumb (<i>our view</i>): the requirement you cannot change later (hosting, long-term support, team skills) outweighs the one you can; and a choice you can explain in one sentence of requirements is better than one you explain by popularity.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Common traps</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b><code>==</code> converts types; use <code>===</code>.</b></li>
      <li><b><code>||</code> replaces <code>0</code> and <code>""</code> too;</b> use <code>??</code> when only <code>null</code> or <code>undefined</code> should fall back.</li>
      <li><b>Numbers are 64-bit floating point (IEEE 754):</b> <code>0.1 + 0.2 === 0.3</code> is <code>false</code>. Keep money in integer minor units (pence, paise, cents) or a decimal library.</li>
      <li><b><code>sort()</code> changes the array it is called on;</b> <code>toSorted()</code> returns a new one.</li>
      <li><b>A forgotten <code>await</code></b> gives you a promise, not the value.</li>
      <li><b>Long work on the main thread blocks the UI;</b> a Web Worker runs it on a background thread instead.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Use let, const, functions, arrays and objects</li><li>Explain closures and the event loop</li><li>Change a page with the DOM and events, no framework</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Write async code with promises and async/await</li><li>Organise code in modules and build it</li><li>Check feature support with Baseline</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Design state and reactivity without leaning on a framework</li><li>Keep the main thread free; use Web Workers</li><li>Adopt TypeScript and write typed APIs</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Choose and migrate frameworks on evidence</li><li>Set JavaScript and TypeScript standards across teams</li><li>Follow and evaluate TC39 proposals</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>Closures and scope</li><li>The event loop and promises</li><li>Modules</li><li>Immutability and copying methods</li><li>Web Components</li><li>TypeScript</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Guide" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MDN JavaScript Guide</a><span class="lnote"> A thorough, free guide to the language</span></li><li><a href="https://github.com/tc39/proposals/blob/main/finished-proposals.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">TC39 finished proposals</a><span class="lnote"> What each edition added</span></li><li><a href="https://tc39.es/ecma262/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The ECMAScript specification (latest draft)</a><span class="lnote"> The standard itself</span></li><li><a href="https://web.dev/baseline" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Baseline on web.dev</a><span class="lnote"> Which features are safe to use everywhere</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://github.com/tc39/proposals/blob/main/finished-proposals.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">TC39 finished proposals (feature and expected publication year)</a>; <a href="https://tc39.es/process-document/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">TC39 process document (Stage 4 needs two compatible implementations passing Test262)</a>; <a href="https://github.com/tc39/ecma262/tags" target="_blank" rel="noopener">tc39/ecma262 tags (the es2026 edition)</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ECMAScript_version_history" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: ECMAScript version history (the ES2015 feature set)</a>; <a href="https://github.com/mdn/content/blob/main/files/en-us/glossary/baseline/compatibility/index.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MDN: Baseline compatibility</a>; <a href="https://github.com/mdn/content/blob/main/files/en-us/web/javascript/guide/closures/index.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MDN: Closures</a>; <a href="https://github.com/mdn/content/blob/main/files/en-us/web/javascript/reference/execution_model/index.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MDN: JavaScript execution model (job queue, run to completion)</a>; <a href="https://github.com/mdn/content/blob/main/files/en-us/web/javascript/guide/modules/index.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MDN: Modules</a>; <a href="https://github.com/mdn/content/blob/main/files/en-us/web/javascript/guide/inheritance_and_the_prototype_chain/index.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MDN: Inheritance and the prototype chain</a>; <a href="https://github.com/mdn/content/blob/main/files/en-us/web/javascript/guide/using_promises/index.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MDN: Using promises</a>; <a href="https://github.com/mdn/content/blob/main/files/en-us/web/api/web_components/index.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MDN: Web Components</a>; <a href="https://github.com/mdn/content/blob/main/files/en-us/web/javascript/reference/global_objects/number/index.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MDN: Number (IEEE 754 double precision)</a>; <a href="https://github.com/mdn/content/blob/main/files/en-us/web/javascript/reference/operators/equality/index.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MDN: Equality (==) converts types</a>; <a href="https://github.com/mdn/content/blob/main/files/en-us/web/javascript/reference/operators/nullish_coalescing/index.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MDN: Nullish coalescing (??) and ||</a>; <a href="https://github.com/mdn/content/blob/main/files/en-us/web/javascript/reference/global_objects/array/sort/index.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MDN: Array.prototype.sort (in place)</a>; <a href="https://github.com/mdn/content/blob/main/files/en-us/web/api/web_workers_api/index.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MDN: Web Workers API</a>.</p><p class="runcode">A question, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/enrichmeai.github.io/issues/new?title=%5BJavaScript%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>. Nothing here is tracked and there are no comments to store: questions live in the open, where the next learner can find the answer.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= FUNCTIONAL OR IMPERATIVE ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-functional" data-name="Functional or Imperative">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Functional or imperative</h2><p>Filter, map, sort, group and reduce: the operations most data code is made of, in Python and Java side by side. What keeps order and what does not, when a parallel stream pays off (measured), and the places where the functional style needs care. Choose by requirement, then practise it in six drills.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>The short answer</h3>
    <p><b>Functional style</b> means writing code as values flowing through functions that return new values: <b>pure functions</b> (same input, same output, nothing else changed), <b>immutable data</b>, and functions such as <code>filter</code>, <code>map</code> and <code>sorted</code> that take other functions as arguments. <b>Imperative style</b> means loops and variables that change step by step.</p>
    <p><i>Our view:</i> functional style is the better default for <b>transforming data</b>, because each step can be read and tested on its own and nothing changes behind your back. Imperative code is clearer where one real thing must change in place, where the work talks to the outside world, and in a few measured hot spots. <i>The other view</i> is at the end of the page.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>The core operations, side by side</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table><thead><tr><th>Operation</th><th>Does</th><th>Python</th><th>Java (streams)</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td><b>filter</b></td><td>Keep the items that pass a test</td><td><code>[s for s in sales if s.paid]</code></td><td><code>.filter(s -&gt; s.paid())</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>map</b></td><td>Turn each item into something new</td><td><code>[s.total for s in sales]</code></td><td><code>.map(Sale::total)</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>sort</b></td><td>Order by one key or several</td><td><code>sorted(rows, key=lambda r: (r.store, -r.total))</code></td><td><code>.sorted(comparing(Row::store).thenComparing(Row::total, reverseOrder()))</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>group</b></td><td>Collect items under a key</td><td><code>defaultdict(list)</code> in a loop, or <code>itertools.groupby</code> on sorted data</td><td><code>.collect(groupingBy(Sale::store))</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>reduce</b></td><td>Combine everything into one value</td><td><code>sum(...)</code>, <code>max(...)</code>, <code>functools.reduce</code></td><td><code>.mapToInt(...).sum()</code>, <code>.reduce(0, Integer::sum)</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>flat map</b></td><td>Each item becomes several; flatten them</td><td><code>[line for o in orders for line in o.lines]</code></td><td><code>.flatMap(o -&gt; o.lines().stream())</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>first n</b></td><td>Stop early</td><td><code>itertools.islice(gen, n)</code>, or slice a list</td><td><code>.limit(n)</code>, <code>.findFirst()</code></td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
    <figure class="fig"><svg viewBox="0 0 660 190" role="img" aria-labelledby="fnpipe-t fnpipe-d"><title id="fnpipe-t">One pipeline: filter, map, sort, collect</title><desc id="fnpipe-d">Six sales flow left to right through four steps. Filter removes the one cancelled sale, leaving five. Map turns each sale into a row of store and total, still five. Sort orders the rows by store, then by total biggest first. Collect gathers them into a new list. The input list is never changed.</desc><defs><marker id="fnpipe-a" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker><marker id="fnpipe-aa" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar acc" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker></defs><rect class="nd in" x="8" y="50" width="118" height="56" rx="6"/><text class="lb hd" x="67" y="74" text-anchor="middle">6 sales</text><text class="lb mu" x="67" y="94" text-anchor="middle">left untouched</text><line class="ed" x1="126" y1="78" x2="134" y2="78" marker-end="url(#fnpipe-a)"/><rect class="nd" x="138" y="50" width="118" height="56" rx="6"/><text class="lb hd" x="197" y="74" text-anchor="middle">filter</text><text class="lb mu" x="197" y="94" text-anchor="middle">5 not cancelled</text><line class="ed" x1="256" y1="78" x2="264" y2="78" marker-end="url(#fnpipe-a)"/><rect class="nd" x="268" y="50" width="118" height="56" rx="6"/><text class="lb hd" x="327" y="74" text-anchor="middle">map</text><text class="lb mu" x="327" y="94" text-anchor="middle">(store, total)</text><line class="ed" x1="386" y1="78" x2="394" y2="78" marker-end="url(#fnpipe-a)"/><rect class="nd" x="398" y="50" width="118" height="56" rx="6"/><text class="lb hd" x="457" y="74" text-anchor="middle">sort</text><text class="lb mu" x="457" y="94" text-anchor="middle">store, total ↓</text><line class="ed" x1="516" y1="78" x2="524" y2="78" marker-end="url(#fnpipe-a)"/><rect class="nd out" x="528" y="50" width="118" height="56" rx="6"/><text class="lb hd" x="587" y="74" text-anchor="middle">collect</text><text class="lb mu" x="587" y="94" text-anchor="middle">new list of 5</text><text class="lb" x="330" y="30" text-anchor="middle">each step takes a value and returns a new one</text><text class="lb mu" x="330" y="150" text-anchor="middle">Python: sorted((s.store, s.total) for s in sales if s.status != "cancelled" …)</text><text class="lb mu" x="330" y="172" text-anchor="middle">Java: sales.stream().filter(…).map(…).sorted(…).toList()</text></svg><figcaption>Data flows through steps that each return something new. Nothing is changed in place, which is what makes the pipeline easy to test and, in Java, possible to run in parallel.</figcaption></figure>
    <p class="note"><b>Filter before you sort.</b> Sorting is the expensive step, O(n log n); throwing away what you do not need first makes it cheaper.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Ordering: what keeps order and what does not</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Sorts are stable</b> in both languages: items with equal keys keep their relative order. Python&rsquo;s sorting guide guarantees it; Java says &ldquo;for ordered streams, the sort is stable&rdquo;. That is why you can sort by a secondary key first and the primary key last.</li>
      <li><b>The last sort wins.</b> Sorting by store and then by total leaves the list ordered by total. Use one sort with a two-part key instead (drill 06).</li>
      <li><b>A Java stream has an encounter order</b> if its source does (a <code>List</code>, an array). <code>toList()</code> keeps it. A <code>HashSet</code> or a <code>HashMap</code>&rsquo;s keys have no defined order.</li>
      <li><b><code>forEach</code> on a parallel stream ignores order:</b> the JDK calls it &ldquo;explicitly nondeterministic&rdquo;. Use <code>forEachOrdered</code> when order matters, or better, collect.</li>
      <li><b>Python&rsquo;s <code>Executor.map</code></b> works like the built-in <code>map</code>: results come back in input order, whichever task finishes first.</li>
      <li><b>Order costs something in parallel.</b> The JDK notes that order-bound operations such as <code>limit()</code> &ldquo;may require buffering to ensure proper ordering, undermining the benefit of parallelism&rdquo;, and that <code>unordered()</code> can help when you do not need the order.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Parallel streams: when they pay off</h3>
    <p>In Java, <code>.parallelStream()</code> (or <code>.parallel()</code>) splits the work across threads. It helps only when there is enough work to share. Measured on a 4-core machine, Java 21, best of five runs:</p>
    <div class="tbl"><table><thead><tr><th>Workload</th><th>Sequential</th><th>Parallel</th><th>Verdict</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td>2,000,000 items, heavy arithmetic in <code>map</code>, then filter and sum</td><td>1,466 ms</td><td>441 ms</td><td>3.3&times; faster</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2,000,000 items, the same <code>map</code>, then <code>sorted().limit(10)</code></td><td>1,951 ms</td><td>598 ms</td><td>3.3&times; faster</td></tr>
      <tr><td>1,000 items, light <code>map</code>, then sum</td><td>0.033 ms</td><td>0.180 ms</td><td>5&times; <b>slower</b>: splitting costs more than the work</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
    <figure class="fig"><svg viewBox="0 0 660 270" role="img" aria-labelledby="fnpar-t fnpar-d"><title id="fnpar-t">How a parallel stream splits the work and keeps the order</title><desc id="fnpar-d">A list of eight items is split into four parts. Four threads each run map on their own part, at the same time. The parts are joined back in their original positions, so toList returns the results in encounter order. Below, the wrong way: four threads each add their results to one shared ArrayList, which is not thread-safe, so items are lost and the order is whatever the threads happened to do.</desc><defs><marker id="fnpar-a" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker><marker id="fnpar-aa" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar acc" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker></defs><rect class="nd in" x="10" y="20" width="640" height="34" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="330" y="42" text-anchor="middle">source: items 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8, in encounter order</text><rect class="nd" x="10" y="80" width="152" height="40" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="86" y="105" text-anchor="middle">thread 1: map(1, 2)</text><line class="ed" x1="86" y1="54" x2="86" y2="76" marker-end="url(#fnpar-a)"/><line class="ed" x1="86" y1="120" x2="86" y2="140" marker-end="url(#fnpar-a)"/><rect class="nd" x="172" y="80" width="152" height="40" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="248" y="105" text-anchor="middle">thread 2: map(3, 4)</text><line class="ed" x1="248" y1="54" x2="248" y2="76" marker-end="url(#fnpar-a)"/><line class="ed" x1="248" y1="120" x2="248" y2="140" marker-end="url(#fnpar-a)"/><rect class="nd" x="334" y="80" width="152" height="40" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="410" y="105" text-anchor="middle">thread 3: map(5, 6)</text><line class="ed" x1="410" y1="54" x2="410" y2="76" marker-end="url(#fnpar-a)"/><line class="ed" x1="410" y1="120" x2="410" y2="140" marker-end="url(#fnpar-a)"/><rect class="nd" x="496" y="80" width="152" height="40" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="572" y="105" text-anchor="middle">thread 4: map(7, 8)</text><line class="ed" x1="572" y1="54" x2="572" y2="76" marker-end="url(#fnpar-a)"/><line class="ed" x1="572" y1="120" x2="572" y2="140" marker-end="url(#fnpar-a)"/><rect class="nd out" x="10" y="144" width="640" height="34" rx="6"/><text class="lb hd" x="330" y="166" text-anchor="middle">toList(): parts joined in place: results 1 … 8, in order</text><rect class="nd warn" x="10" y="206" width="640" height="50" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="330" y="226" text-anchor="middle">the wrong way: forEach(results::add) into one ArrayList</text><text class="lb acc" x="330" y="246" text-anchor="middle">measured: wrong count in 19 of 20 runs, an exception in the other</text></svg><figcaption>A parallel stream splits the source, works on the parts at the same time, and joins the results in their original order when you collect with <code>toList()</code>. It cannot protect a list you change yourself from inside it.</figcaption></figure>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Keep the steps stateless.</b> The JDK&rsquo;s advice: &ldquo;the best approach is to avoid stateful behavioral parameters to stream operations entirely&rdquo;. No shared counters, no shared lists.</li>
      <li><b>Collect, do not mutate.</b> <code>map(...).toList()</code> is safe in parallel; <code>forEach(list::add)</code> is not (drill 05).</li>
      <li><b>Parallel streams are for CPU work.</b> For waiting on networks or disks, use virtual threads or a thread pool (see <a href="#concurrency">Concurrency</a> and <a href="#javathreads">How Java Threads Evolved</a>).</li>
      <li><b>Python has no parallel streams.</b> Use <code>ProcessPoolExecutor.map</code> for CPU work (each process has its own interpreter) and <code>ThreadPoolExecutor.map</code> for waiting; both return results in input order.</li>
      <li><b>Measure first.</b> The third row above is the common case in ordinary business code.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Where the functional style needs care</h3>
    <ol>
      <li><b>One real value that many must change.</b> A balance, the seats left on a flight, a cache. The logic can be pure, but somewhere one value changes, guarded by a lock, a transaction or an atomic update (see <a href="#concurrency">Concurrency</a>).</li>
      <li><b>Side effects.</b> Charging a card, sending email, reading the clock. Keep them in a thin outer layer around a pure core, often called &ldquo;functional core, imperative shell&rdquo; (drill 04).</li>
      <li><b>Single-use pipelines.</b> Generators and streams are lazy and can be read once. Python silently gives an empty generator the second time; Java throws &ldquo;stream has already been operated upon or closed&rdquo; (drill 02).</li>
      <li><b>Deep recursion.</b> Neither CPython nor Java turns recursion into a loop. CPython stops at its recursion limit (1,000 by default); a recursive sum of 5,000 items raised <code>RecursionError</code> here. Java overflowed its stack at 100,000 levels and was fine at 10,000. Use a loop when the depth comes from the data (drill 03).</li>
      <li><b>Hot paths on large data.</b> New values mean new allocations. In low-latency code (see <a href="#banking">Banking &amp; Low Latency</a>) or huge in-memory jobs, changing in place can matter. Measure before choosing.</li>
      <li><b>Naturally stateful algorithms.</b> An LRU cache, a visited set in a graph search, a simulation: possible functionally, usually clearer with loops and a little state.</li>
      <li><b>Debugging long chains.</b> A failure deep in a long pipeline gives a stack trace that is harder to read than a loop&rsquo;s; the same is said of reactive code on <a href="#spring">Spring Boot</a>. Name the steps as small functions.</li>
      <li><b>Java specifics.</b> Lambdas cannot throw checked exceptions through <code>java.util.function</code> types; <code>break</code> and early exit are awkward inside a stream; and a lambda that changes a field from a parallel stream brings back the <a href="#concurrency">lost update</a>.</li>
    </ol>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Choose by requirement</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table><thead><tr><th>If this is true for you</th><th>Lean</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td>You are transforming data: input in, output out, no shared state</td><td>Functional</td><td>Each step reads and tests on its own</td></tr>
      <tr><td>It must be easy to test, or safe to run in parallel</td><td>Functional (pure functions)</td><td>No hidden state to set up or to race on</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Many threads must update one shared value</td><td>Imperative, with a lock or transaction at that one place</td><td>The state is real; guard it, do not hide it</td></tr>
      <tr><td>It talks to the outside world (I/O, payments, email)</td><td>A pure core inside an imperative shell</td><td>Test the rules without the world</td></tr>
      <tr><td>The input is too big to hold in memory</td><td>Lazy pipelines (generators, streams), read once</td><td>Constant memory</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Recursion depth grows with the data</td><td>A loop</td><td>No tail-call optimisation in CPython or Java</td></tr>
      <tr><td>A measured hot path is slow because of copies</td><td>Imperative, in place, after measuring</td><td>Fewer allocations</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Your team already writes in one style</td><td>Lean that way</td><td>Switching has a cost</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Our view, and the other view</h3>
    <p><i>Our view:</i> write pure functions and pipelines by default for data, keep side effects at the edges, and drop to imperative code where state is real or a measurement says so. Most bugs in the drills below come from mixing the two without noticing.</p>
    <p><i>The other view:</i> some teams go further and use languages and libraries built for the functional style (immutable collections, effect systems, Scala, Haskell, Clojure), handling state and side effects inside that model. It works well when the whole team is fluent; it is a larger change than adopting streams and comprehensions.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Practise it: six drills</h3>
    <p>Each drill is a small, broken piece of real code in Python and Java, with tests that fail until you fix it, a prediction to make first, hints, and an explanation of the fix. About 10 to 15 minutes each, in <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/well-rounded-engineer-labs/tree/main/functional" target="_blank" rel="noopener">well-rounded-engineer-labs/functional</a>.</p>
    <div class="tbl"><table><thead><tr><th>Drill</th><th>What breaks</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td>01 &middot; The function that changed its input</td><td>Tax added twice: a function rewrote its caller&rsquo;s list</td></tr>
      <tr><td>02 &middot; The pipeline you can only read once</td><td>Right order count, &pound;0 revenue</td></tr>
      <tr><td>03 &middot; The recursion that ran out</td><td>A chain 100,000 deep overflows the stack</td></tr>
      <tr><td>04 &middot; The core and the shell</td><td>Pricing that reads the clock and sends email cannot be tested</td></tr>
      <tr><td>05 &middot; The parallel stream that lost items</td><td>Items lost and order random from a parallel <code>forEach</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>06 &middot; The report sorted the wrong way</td><td>Filter and map right, then two sorts where one two-key sort was needed</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/21/docs/api/java.base/java/util/stream/Stream.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Java 21 API: Stream</a> (forEach is &ldquo;explicitly nondeterministic&rdquo;; sorted is stable for ordered streams; toList keeps encounter order, since 16); <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/21/docs/api/java.base/java/util/stream/package-summary.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Java 21 API: java.util.stream package</a> (statelessness, non-interference, ordering and limit() in parallel); <a href="https://docs.python.org/3/howto/sorting.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Python Sorting Techniques</a> (sorts are guaranteed to be stable); <a href="https://docs.python.org/3/library/sys.html#sys.getrecursionlimit" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Python: sys.getrecursionlimit</a>; <a href="https://docs.python.org/3/library/concurrent.futures.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Python: concurrent.futures</a>. Timings and failure counts were measured for this page on a 4-core machine (Java 21, CPython 3) and are in the drills&rsquo; explanations.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= FRONT-END TOOLCHAIN ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-fetoolchain" data-name="Front-end Toolchain">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>The front-end toolchain: TypeScript, Babel, bundlers, React Native, Redux</h2><p>A modern front end can mean TypeScript, JSX, a transpiler, a bundler, a framework, a state library and a mobile layer, each with its own configuration. Each layer was added to solve a problem its authors had (the table below says which). <i>Our view:</i> together they can make a small app hard to start and hard to keep up to date. This page explains what each layer does, why it appeared, and when you can leave it out. The language itself is on <a href="#javascript">JavaScript</a>; native versus cross-platform apps are on <a href="#apps">Apps</a>.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>The short answer</h3>
    <p><i>Our view:</i> add a layer only when you can name the problem it solves for you. <i>The other view:</i> a team that adopts TypeScript, a bundler and a state library together, from a template, has one documented path from day one and does not have to retrofit types or a build later. Which suits you depends on the requirements in the table below.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>From source code to the browser</h3>
    <figure class="fig"><svg viewBox="0 0 660 250" role="img" aria-labelledby="fe-t fe-d"><title id="fe-t">From source code to the browser</title><desc id="fe-d">A pipeline of five boxes from left to right. Source code, written in TypeScript and JSX, is type-checked by the TypeScript compiler. A transpiler such as Babel or esbuild turns it into JavaScript the target can run. A bundler such as webpack or Vite joins the modules into a few optimised files. The browser then runs plain JavaScript. Under each box is the reason it exists.</desc><defs><marker id="fe-a" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker></defs><rect class="nd in" x="10" y="40" width="114" height="90" rx="8"/><text class="lb hd" x="67.0" y="74" text-anchor="middle">Source</text><text class="lb mu" x="67.0" y="100" text-anchor="middle">TypeScript, JSX</text><text class="lb" x="67.0" y="164" text-anchor="middle">you write</text><line class="ed" x1="124" y1="85" x2="139" y2="85" marker-end="url(#fe-a)"/><rect class="nd" x="140" y="40" width="114" height="90" rx="8"/><text class="lb hd" x="197.0" y="74" text-anchor="middle">Type check</text><text class="lb mu" x="197.0" y="100" text-anchor="middle">tsc</text><text class="lb" x="197.0" y="164" text-anchor="middle">catch mistakes</text><line class="ed" x1="254" y1="85" x2="269" y2="85" marker-end="url(#fe-a)"/><rect class="nd" x="270" y="40" width="114" height="90" rx="8"/><text class="lb hd" x="327.0" y="74" text-anchor="middle">Transpile</text><text class="lb mu" x="327.0" y="100" text-anchor="middle">Babel, esbuild</text><text class="lb" x="327.0" y="164" text-anchor="middle">run on the target</text><line class="ed" x1="384" y1="85" x2="399" y2="85" marker-end="url(#fe-a)"/><rect class="nd" x="400" y="40" width="114" height="90" rx="8"/><text class="lb hd" x="457.0" y="74" text-anchor="middle">Bundle</text><text class="lb mu" x="457.0" y="100" text-anchor="middle">webpack, Vite</text><text class="lb" x="457.0" y="164" text-anchor="middle">fewer, smaller files</text><line class="ed" x1="514" y1="85" x2="529" y2="85" marker-end="url(#fe-a)"/><rect class="nd out" x="530" y="40" width="114" height="90" rx="8"/><text class="lb hd" x="587.0" y="74" text-anchor="middle">Browser</text><text class="lb mu" x="587.0" y="100" text-anchor="middle">JavaScript</text><text class="lb" x="587.0" y="164" text-anchor="middle">what actually runs</text><text class="lb mu" x="330" y="214" text-anchor="middle">several steps are optional: browsers run ES modules, and Node.js can strip types itself</text></svg><figcaption>The layers between the code you write and what the browser runs, and the reason each one exists. One tool often does several steps: esbuild, for example, bundles and minifies and also handles TypeScript and JSX.</figcaption></figure>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Each layer, and why it exists</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>What it does</th><th>Date or note</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td><b>TypeScript</b> (Microsoft)</td><td>Adds static typing with optional type annotations to JavaScript, designed for large applications; it transpiles to JavaScript</td><td>Released to the public in October 2012</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>JSX</b></td><td>An XML-like syntax for describing UI inside JavaScript, created by Facebook for React. It is syntactic sugar, transpiled into nested function calls; with TypeScript the files end in <code>.tsx</code></td><td>With React</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Babel</b></td><td>A transpiler that turns newer JavaScript (ES2015 and later) into code older engines can run, and also transforms JSX and TypeScript</td><td>16 million core downloads a week in 2019</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Module bundler</b> (webpack, esbuild, Vite)</td><td>Follows the imports from an entry point, builds a dependency graph, and combines the modules into a few bundles, often minifying and removing unused code</td><td>esbuild, written in Go, claimed to be 10 to 100 times faster than other bundlers when it was released</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Vite</b></td><td>A development server, written by Vue&rsquo;s creator Evan You, that reloads just the edited file using native ES modules, and bundles for production</td><td>Supports TypeScript and JSX; no date in our sources</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>React</b> (Meta)</td><td>A library for building UIs from components</td><td>Open-sourced at JSConf US in May 2013</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>React Native</b> (Meta)</td><td>Uses React with native platform capabilities to build apps for Android, iOS and other platforms</td><td>Introduced in 2015</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Redux</b></td><td>A library for managing and centralising application state, inspired by Facebook&rsquo;s Flux and the Elm Architecture; created by Dan Abramov and Andrew Clark</td><td>2015</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Does your app need Redux?</h3>
    <p>Redux&rsquo;s own documentation says it is &ldquo;not designed to be the shortest or fastest way to write code&rdquo;, and that React&rsquo;s built-in state system &ldquo;might be all you need&rdquo;. It quotes Pete Hunt, an early React contributor, on Flux, the pattern Redux grew out of: &ldquo;You&rsquo;ll know when you need Flux. If you aren&rsquo;t sure if you need it, you don&rsquo;t need it.&rdquo; The docs list where it helps most:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>a lot of application state needed in many places</li>
      <li>state that is updated often</li>
      <li>update logic that may be complex</li>
      <li>a medium or large codebase that many people work on</li>
      <li>a need to see how state changed over time</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">React&rsquo;s own documentation shows another way to share state: combining a reducer with context, built into React.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Layers you can sometimes leave out</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Browsers run modules natively:</b> JavaScript modules load with <code>import</code> in the browser, and an import map can tell it where bare module names live, without a bundler.</li>
      <li><b>Node.js runs TypeScript files directly</b> by stripping the types, for code that uses only &ldquo;erasable&rdquo; TypeScript syntax: enabled by default from Node.js 23.6 and 22.18, and marked stable in 25.2 and 24.12. For full TypeScript features, its docs point to a third-party tool such as <code>tsx</code>.</li>
      <li><b>Types as comments in JavaScript itself</b> is a TC39 proposal at Stage 1 (per its repository page, which says it is not updated regularly): an idea under discussion, not part of the language.</li>
    </ul>
<pre><code>// greet.ts
type User = { name: string; visits: number };

function greet(user: User): string {
  return `Hello ${user.name}, visit number ${user.visits}`;
}

console.log(greet({ name: "Ana", visits: 3 }));
</code></pre>
<pre><code>$ node greet.ts
Hello Ana, visit number 3</code></pre>
    <p class="note">Run as shown with Node.js 22.23 and no build step; the second block is its real output.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Choose by requirement</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>If this is true for you</th><th>It leans towards (<i>analysis</i>)</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>A small page or tool, few dependencies, one maintainer</td><td>Plain JavaScript modules in the browser, perhaps with an import map: no build step</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Code that several people maintain for years</td><td>TypeScript, type-checked in CI</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Many npm dependencies, or you must support older browsers</td><td>A bundler and transpiler, usually through one tool such as Vite</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Shared state that many screens read and change, with complex updates</td><td>A state library such as Redux; otherwise React&rsquo;s own reducer and context</td></tr>
      <tr><td>The same team must ship iOS and Android, and it already knows React</td><td>React Native (see <a href="#apps">Apps</a> for the native alternative)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Your team already runs a working toolchain</td><td>Upgrade it in place rather than switching tools: switching has a cost</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Our view, and the other view</h3>
    <p><i>Our view:</i> much of the difficulty comes from adopting the whole stack on day one. Starting small and adding a layer when a real problem appears keeps a front end easier to understand and to upgrade.</p>
    <p><i>The other view:</i> a framework or starter template that includes the whole stack gives a team one well-trodden path, with documentation and many people who already know it, and avoids reassembling the pieces later when the app grows.</p>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Say what TypeScript, JSX, a transpiler and a bundler each do</li><li>Run a TypeScript file and a plain ES module</li><li>Read a package.json and its build scripts</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Set up TypeScript type-checking in CI</li><li>Decide whether an app needs a state library</li><li>Upgrade a toolchain dependency safely</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Choose which layers a project needs, by requirement</li><li>Remove a layer that no longer earns its place</li><li>Plan a web and React Native codebase</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Set an organisation's front-end platform and upgrade policy</li><li>Weigh frameworks against a smaller custom stack</li><li>Keep build complexity in check across teams</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>TypeScript and JSX</li><li>Transpilers: Babel, esbuild, tsc</li><li>Bundlers: webpack, esbuild, Vite</li><li>React and React Native</li><li>State management: Redux, reducer and context</li><li>Native ES modules and import maps</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://redux.js.org/faq/general#when-should-i-use-redux" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Redux: When should I use Redux?</a><span class="lnote"> From Redux's own FAQ</span></li><li><a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Guide/Modules" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MDN: JavaScript modules</a><span class="lnote"> Modules in the browser, no bundler</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/TypeScript" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: TypeScript</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Babel_(transcompiler)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Babel</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/JSX_(programming)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: JSX</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Module_bundler" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Module bundler</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Webpack" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Webpack</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Esbuild" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: esbuild</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vite" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Vite</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/React_(software)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: React</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/React_Native" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: React Native</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Redux_(software)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Redux</a>; <a href="https://github.com/reduxjs/redux/blob/master/docs/faq/General.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Redux docs: When should I use Redux?</a>; <a href="https://github.com/nodejs/node/blob/main/doc/api/typescript.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Node.js docs: TypeScript (type stripping)</a>; <a href="https://github.com/tc39/proposal-type-annotations" target="_blank" rel="noopener">TC39 proposal: Type Annotations</a>; <a href="https://react.dev/learn/scaling-up-with-reducer-and-context" target="_blank" rel="noopener">React docs: Scaling up with reducer and context</a>; <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Guide/Modules" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MDN: JavaScript modules</a>; <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Elements/script/type/importmap" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MDN: Import maps</a>.</p><p class="runcode">A question, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/enrichmeai.github.io/issues/new?title=%5BFront-end%20Toolchain%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>. Nothing here is tracked and there are no comments to store: questions live in the open, where the next learner can find the answer.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<section class="panel" id="p-go" data-name="Go">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Go: simple, fast to build, made for services</h2><p>Go is an open-source language for building simple, reliable and efficient software. It compiles to a single binary, starts fast, and has concurrency built in. Much of the cloud's own tooling is written in it, including Docker, Kubernetes and Terraform.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card"><h3>Where you meet it</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Cloud and platform tools:</b> Docker, Kubernetes, Terraform, Prometheus and many operators are written in Go. Reading Go helps you debug the tools you run. See <a href="#kubernetes">Kubernetes</a> and <a href="#operators">Operators</a>.</li>
      <li><b>Network services and APIs</b>, where its small binaries and fast start-up suit containers.</li>
      <li><b>Command-line tools</b>, shipped as one file with no runtime to install.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>The ideas that matter</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Small on purpose:</b> few keywords, one formatting style (<code>gofmt</code>), one obvious way to do most things.</li>
      <li><b>Errors are values:</b> functions return an <code>error</code>; you check <code>if err != nil</code>. Wrap with <code>%w</code> and test with <code>errors.Is</code>.</li>
      <li><b>Goroutines and channels:</b> cheap concurrent functions, coordinated with channels, <code>sync.WaitGroup</code> and mutexes.</li>
      <li><b>Interfaces are implicit:</b> a type satisfies an interface just by having its methods.</li>
      <li><b>Composition, not inheritance:</b> embed one struct in another.</li>
      <li><b>A strong standard library:</b> HTTP servers, JSON and testing need no framework.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>A small program, and its test</h3>
    <p><code>go run .</code> runs it; <code>go test -race ./...</code> runs the table-driven test with the race detector on. Both pass as written.</p>
    <pre><code class="language-go">package main

import (
	&quot;errors&quot;
	&quot;fmt&quot;
	&quot;sync&quot;
)

type Order struct {
	ID    string
	Pence int
}

var ErrInvalid = errors.New(&quot;invalid order&quot;)

// Validate returns an error value instead of throwing: the caller must decide.
func Validate(o Order) error {
	if o.Pence &lt;= 0 {
		return fmt.Errorf(&quot;order %s: %w&quot;, o.ID, ErrInvalid)
	}
	return nil
}

// Total validates orders concurrently, one goroutine each, and sums the valid ones.
func Total(orders []Order) (int, []error) {
	var (
		wg   sync.WaitGroup
		mu   sync.Mutex
		sum  int
		errs []error
	)
	for _, o := range orders {
		wg.Add(1)
		go func(o Order) {
			defer wg.Done()
			err := Validate(o)
			mu.Lock()
			defer mu.Unlock()
			if err != nil {
				errs = append(errs, err)
				return
			}
			sum += o.Pence
		}(o)
	}
	wg.Wait()
	return sum, errs
}

func main() {
	sum, errs := Total([]Order{{&quot;a1&quot;, 1250}, {&quot;a2&quot;, 0}, {&quot;a3&quot;, 499}})
	fmt.Println(&quot;total pence:&quot;, sum)
	for _, e := range errs {
		fmt.Println(&quot;error:&quot;, e, &quot;| invalid?&quot;, errors.Is(e, ErrInvalid))
	}
}</code></pre>
    <pre><code class="language-go">package main

import (
	&quot;errors&quot;
	&quot;testing&quot;
)

func TestValidate(t *testing.T) {
	cases := []struct {
		name    string
		order   Order
		wantErr bool
	}{
		{&quot;positive amount&quot;, Order{&quot;a1&quot;, 100}, false},
		{&quot;zero amount&quot;, Order{&quot;a2&quot;, 0}, true},
		{&quot;negative amount&quot;, Order{&quot;a3&quot;, -5}, true},
	}
	for _, c := range cases {
		t.Run(c.name, func(t *testing.T) {
			err := Validate(c.order)
			if (err != nil) != c.wantErr || (err != nil &amp;&amp; !errors.Is(err, ErrInvalid)) {
				t.Fatalf(&quot;Validate(%v) = %v, wantErr %v&quot;, c.order, err, c.wantErr)
			}
		})
	}
}</code></pre>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Build and tools</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Modules:</b> <code>go mod init</code>, dependencies pinned in <code>go.mod</code> and <code>go.sum</code>.</li>
      <li><b>Built in:</b> <code>go build</code>, <code>go test</code> (with <code>-race</code> and <code>-cover</code>), <code>go vet</code>, <code>gofmt</code>, and benchmarks and fuzzing in the test tool.</li>
      <li><b>Containers:</b> a static binary fits a tiny base image. See <a href="#docker">Docker</a>.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Common mistakes</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Ignoring an error</b> with <code>_</code>: the failure is silent until it is expensive.</li>
      <li><b>Leaking goroutines:</b> one blocked forever on a channel nobody reads. Pass a <code>context.Context</code> and stop on cancel.</li>
      <li><b>Data races:</b> shared state touched from goroutines without a lock or channel. Run tests with <code>-race</code>.</li>
      <li><b>A nil map write</b> panics: make the map first.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Write and run a small Go program</li><li>Handle errors as values</li><li>Write a table-driven test</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Build an HTTP service with the standard library</li><li>Use goroutines, channels and context safely</li><li>Run tests with the race detector and coverage</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Design packages and interfaces for a large codebase</li><li>Profile and tune with pprof</li><li>Read and debug cloud tools written in Go</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Set Go standards across teams</li><li>Build operators or platform tooling in Go</li><li>Teach Go's concurrency model and review for it</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>Errors and wrapping</li><li>Goroutines, channels and context</li><li>Interfaces and composition</li><li>Modules and versioning</li><li>Profiling with pprof</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://go.dev/tour/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">A Tour of Go</a><span class="lnote"> Interactive introduction</span></li><li><a href="https://go.dev/doc/effective_go" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Effective Go</a><span class="lnote"> How to write idiomatic Go</span></li><li><a href="https://github.com/golang/go" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The Go source</a><span class="lnote"> The language, compiler and standard library</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://go.dev/doc/effective_go" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Effective Go</a>; <a href="https://go.dev/blog/go1.13-errors" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Go blog: Working with errors in Go 1.13 (%w, errors.Is)</a>; <a href="https://go.dev/doc/articles/race_detector" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Data race detector</a>; <a href="https://go.dev/blog/maps" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Go blog: Go maps in action (nil map write panics)</a>.</p><p class="runcode">A question, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/enrichmeai.github.io/issues/new?title=%5BGo%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>. Nothing here is tracked and there are no comments to store: questions live in the open, where the next learner can find the answer.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<section class="panel" id="p-rust" data-name="Rust">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Rust: memory safety without a garbage collector</h2><p>Rust is a language for reliable and efficient software. Its ownership model and type system catch memory and thread-safety bugs at compile time, without a garbage collector, so it suits critical services, embedded devices and code that other languages call into.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card"><h3>Where you meet it</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Performance-critical services and infrastructure:</b> proxies, databases, storage engines.</li>
      <li><b>Fast tools for other languages:</b> Python's Polars and the ruff linter are written in Rust. See <a href="#datalibs">Data Libraries</a>.</li>
      <li><b>Systems and embedded code</b>, and WebAssembly in the browser.</li>
      <li><b>Security-sensitive code</b>, where memory-safety bugs are the usual way in. See <a href="#security">Security</a>.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>The ideas that matter</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Ownership:</b> each value has one owner; when the owner goes out of scope, the value is freed. No garbage collector, no manual <code>free</code>.</li>
      <li><b>Borrowing:</b> lend a value as <code>&amp;T</code> (many readers) or <code>&amp;mut T</code> (one writer), never both at once. The borrow checker enforces it.</li>
      <li><b>No null, no exceptions:</b> <code>Option&lt;T&gt;</code> for maybe-a-value, <code>Result&lt;T, E&gt;</code> for success-or-error, and <code>?</code> to pass an error up.</li>
      <li><b>Enums and <code>match</code>:</b> the compiler checks every case is handled.</li>
      <li><b>Traits:</b> shared behaviour, like interfaces, resolved at compile time.</li>
      <li><b>Fearless concurrency:</b> the same rules stop data races from compiling.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>A small program, and its tests</h3>
    <p><code>cargo run</code> runs it; <code>cargo test</code> runs the two tests; <code>cargo clippy</code> reports no warnings. Note that <code>orders</code> is still usable after <code>total(&amp;orders)</code>, because <code>total</code> only borrowed it.</p>
    <pre><code class="language-rust">use std::fmt;

#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq)]
struct Order {
    id: String,
    pence: u32,
}

#[derive(Debug, PartialEq)]
enum OrderError {
    Empty(String),
    TooLarge { id: String, pence: u32 },
}

impl fmt::Display for OrderError {
    fn fmt(&amp;self, f: &amp;mut fmt::Formatter) -&gt; fmt::Result {
        match self {
            OrderError::Empty(id) =&gt; write!(f, &quot;order {id} has no amount&quot;),
            OrderError::TooLarge { id, pence } =&gt; write!(f, &quot;order {id} is too large: {pence}&quot;),
        }
    }
}

// Borrows the order (&amp;Order): validating does not take ownership of it.
fn validate(o: &amp;Order) -&gt; Result&lt;u32, OrderError&gt; {
    match o.pence {
        0 =&gt; Err(OrderError::Empty(o.id.clone())),
        p if p &gt; 1_000_000 =&gt; Err(OrderError::TooLarge { id: o.id.clone(), pence: p }),
        p =&gt; Ok(p),
    }
}

// The ? operator returns the first error to the caller instead of panicking.
fn total(orders: &amp;[Order]) -&gt; Result&lt;u32, OrderError&gt; {
    let mut sum = 0;
    for o in orders {
        sum += validate(o)?;
    }
    Ok(sum)
}

fn main() {
    let orders = vec![
        Order { id: &quot;a1&quot;.into(), pence: 1250 },
        Order { id: &quot;a3&quot;.into(), pence: 499 },
    ];
    match total(&amp;orders) {
        Ok(sum) =&gt; println!(&quot;total pence: {sum}&quot;),
        Err(e) =&gt; println!(&quot;error: {e}&quot;),
    }
    // orders is still usable here: total only borrowed it.
    let valid: Vec&lt;&amp;Order&gt; = orders.iter().filter(|o| validate(o).is_ok()).collect();
    println!(&quot;valid orders: {}&quot;, valid.len());
}

#[cfg(test)]
mod tests {
    use super::*;

    #[test]
    fn empty_order_is_an_error_not_a_panic() {
        let o = Order { id: &quot;a2&quot;.into(), pence: 0 };
        assert_eq!(validate(&amp;o), Err(OrderError::Empty(&quot;a2&quot;.into())));
    }

    #[test]
    fn total_stops_at_the_first_error() {
        let orders = vec![
            Order { id: &quot;a1&quot;.into(), pence: 100 },
            Order { id: &quot;a2&quot;.into(), pence: 0 },
        ];
        assert!(total(&amp;orders).is_err());
    }
}</code></pre>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Build and tools</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Cargo:</b> build, test, dependencies (crates from crates.io) and benchmarks in one tool.</li>
      <li><b>rustfmt</b> formats, <b>Clippy</b> lints, <b>rust-analyzer</b> powers the editor.</li>
      <li><b>The compiler's messages</b> are unusually helpful: read them in full, they often contain the fix.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Common mistakes</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Fighting the borrow checker</b> with <code>.clone()</code> everywhere: it compiles, but hides the design question of who owns what.</li>
      <li><b><code>unwrap()</code> in production code:</b> it panics on an error. Return a <code>Result</code> or handle it.</li>
      <li><b>Reaching for <code>unsafe</code></b> to get past a compile error. It does not switch off the borrow checker, but the operations it allows are no longer checked by the compiler, so their safety rests on you.</li>
      <li><b>Expecting to be fast at it in a week.</b> The learning curve is real; it pays back in fewer production bugs.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Explain ownership and borrowing</li><li>Use Result, Option and match</li><li>Build and test with Cargo</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Write a CLI or service in Rust</li><li>Use traits, iterators and error types well</li><li>Keep Clippy clean and the code formatted</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Design with lifetimes and generics</li><li>Write async Rust and choose a runtime</li><li>Wrap unsafe or foreign code behind safe interfaces</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Lead Rust adoption where safety and speed matter</li><li>Write libraries other languages call</li><li>Teach Rust and review for soundness</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>Ownership, borrowing and lifetimes</li><li>Traits and generics</li><li>Error handling with Result</li><li>Async Rust</li><li>Unsafe Rust and FFI</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://doc.rust-lang.org/book/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The Rust Programming Language (the book)</a><span class="lnote"> The official book</span></li><li><a href="https://github.com/rust-lang/book" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The book's source</a><span class="lnote"> Read and contribute on GitHub</span></li><li><a href="https://github.com/rust-lang/rustlings" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Rustlings</a><span class="lnote"> Small exercises to learn by doing</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://doc.rust-lang.org/book/ch20-01-unsafe-rust.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The Rust book: Unsafe Rust</a>; <a href="https://doc.rust-lang.org/book/ch04-01-what-is-ownership.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The Rust book: What is ownership?</a>; <a href="https://doc.rust-lang.org/book/ch09-02-recoverable-errors-with-result.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The Rust book: Recoverable errors with Result</a>.</p><p class="runcode">A question, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/enrichmeai.github.io/issues/new?title=%5BRust%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>. Nothing here is tracked and there are no comments to store: questions live in the open, where the next learner can find the answer.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= BUILD TOOLS ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-buildtools" data-name="Build Tools">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Build tools: Maven, Gradle, npm, Yarn and pnpm</h2><p>A build tool turns source code into something you can run or ship: it fetches dependencies, compiles, runs the tests, packages the result and publishes it. A good build gives the same result on every laptop and every CI runner. This page covers the Java tools (Maven and Gradle) and the Node.js tools (npm, Yarn, pnpm and the bundlers), with each claim checked against the tool's own documentation.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card"><h3>What every build tool does</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Resolves dependencies:</b> downloads the libraries you name, and the libraries they need (transitive dependencies), from a registry such as Maven Central or the npm registry.</li>
      <li><b>Runs the steps in order:</b> compile, test, package, publish. You ask for the last step and the tool runs the ones before it.</li>
      <li><b>Pins versions:</b> a lockfile, a wrapper script or a version catalog, so a build next year uses the same versions as today.</li>
      <li><b>Avoids repeated work:</b> skips steps whose inputs have not changed.</li>
      <li><b>Is the same in CI as on your machine.</b> If it only builds on one person's laptop, it does not build.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>A short history</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>When</th><th>Tool</th><th>What it brought</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>1976</td><td>Make</td><td>Stuart Feldman at Bell Labs: rules in a Makefile, and a target rebuilt only when a source file is newer</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2000</td><td>Apache Ant</td><td>James Duncan Davidson, from the Tomcat project: a Java replacement for Make, with XML build files</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2004</td><td>Apache Maven</td><td>Jason van Zyl (started 2002): conventions instead of scripts, and dependencies downloaded from a repository</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2008</td><td>Gradle</td><td>Builds on the concepts of Ant and Maven, with a Groovy or Kotlin build language; first stable release in June 2012</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2010</td><td>npm</td><td>Isaac Z. Schlueter: the package manager for Node.js, included in the Node.js installer</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2014</td><td>webpack</td><td>Bundles a web app's modules into files a browser loads</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2015</td><td>Bazel</td><td>Google's open-source port of its internal build tool Blaze (started 2006), for large monorepos</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2016</td><td>Yarn</td><td>Started by Sebastian McKenzie at Facebook; rewritten in 2020 (codename "Berry")</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2017</td><td>pnpm</td><td>Zoltan Kochan: one shared store on disk, linked into each project</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2020</td><td>Vite</td><td>Evan You, creator of Vue.js: a fast dev server plus a production build</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Maven: convention over configuration</h3>
    <p>A Maven project is described by its <code>pom.xml</code> (Project Object Model). If you follow the standard layout (<code>src/main/java</code>, <code>src/test/java</code>), you configure almost nothing.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Three built-in lifecycles:</b> <code>default</code> (build and deploy), <code>clean</code> and <code>site</code>.</li>
      <li><b>Main phases of the default lifecycle, in order:</b> <code>validate</code>, <code>compile</code>, <code>test</code>, <code>package</code>, <code>verify</code>, <code>install</code> (into your local repository), <code>deploy</code> (to a remote repository). <code>mvn verify</code> runs every phase before <code>verify</code> too, so you only name the last one.</li>
      <li><b>Plugins do the work:</b> each phase runs plugin goals bound to it, and the bindings depend on the packaging (<code>jar</code>, <code>war</code>, <code>pom</code>).</li>
      <li><b>Transitive dependencies</b> are included automatically. When two versions of one library meet, Maven picks the <b>nearest definition</b> in the dependency tree, not the newest. That surprises people: check with <code>mvn dependency:tree</code>.</li>
      <li><b><code>&lt;dependencyManagement&gt;</code></b> pins versions for the whole tree, and the <code>import</code> scope pulls in a published bill of materials (BOM), as Spring Boot and many cloud SDKs provide.</li>
      <li><b>Scopes:</b> <code>compile</code> (the default), <code>provided</code> (the JDK or the container supplies it at runtime), <code>runtime</code>, <code>test</code> (only for compiling and running tests, and not passed on).</li>
      <li><b>The Maven Wrapper</b> (<code>mvnw</code>) downloads the Maven version the project declares, so nobody needs the right one installed.</li>
    </ul>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>&lt;project&gt;
  &lt;modelVersion&gt;4.0.0&lt;/modelVersion&gt;
  &lt;groupId&gt;com.example&lt;/groupId&gt;
  &lt;artifactId&gt;orders&lt;/artifactId&gt;
  &lt;version&gt;1.0.0-SNAPSHOT&lt;/version&gt;

  &lt;dependencyManagement&gt;           &lt;!-- one place for versions --&gt;
    &lt;dependencies&gt;
      &lt;dependency&gt;
        &lt;groupId&gt;org.junit&lt;/groupId&gt;
        &lt;artifactId&gt;junit-bom&lt;/artifactId&gt;
        &lt;version&gt;5.11.0&lt;/version&gt;
        &lt;type&gt;pom&lt;/type&gt;
        &lt;scope&gt;import&lt;/scope&gt;
      &lt;/dependency&gt;
    &lt;/dependencies&gt;
  &lt;/dependencyManagement&gt;

  &lt;dependencies&gt;
    &lt;dependency&gt;
      &lt;groupId&gt;org.junit.jupiter&lt;/groupId&gt;
      &lt;artifactId&gt;junit-jupiter&lt;/artifactId&gt;   &lt;!-- no version: the BOM sets it --&gt;
      &lt;scope&gt;test&lt;/scope&gt;
    &lt;/dependency&gt;
  &lt;/dependencies&gt;
&lt;/project&gt;

./mvnw verify                  # compile, test, package, verify
./mvnw dependency:tree         # which version won, and why</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Gradle: a task graph you can program</h3>
    <p>A Gradle build is written in Kotlin (<code>build.gradle.kts</code>) or Groovy (<code>build.gradle</code>). Instead of a fixed lifecycle, it builds a graph of tasks and runs the ones your request needs.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Three phases every build goes through:</b> initialization (which projects are in the build), configuration (run the build scripts and work out the task graph), execution (run the selected tasks).</li>
      <li><b>The Gradle Wrapper</b> (<code>gradlew</code>) is the recommended way to run any Gradle build: it runs the declared Gradle version and downloads it if needed. Commit it.</li>
      <li><b>The Daemon</b> is a long-lived background process that hosts Gradle's engine, so later builds skip the start-up cost.</li>
      <li><b>Incremental builds:</b> a task whose inputs and outputs have not changed is skipped as up to date.</li>
      <li><b>The build cache</b> goes further: it reuses task outputs from earlier builds, on this machine or from a shared remote cache, when the inputs match.</li>
      <li><b>The configuration cache</b> saves the result of the configuration phase and reuses it on the next build.</li>
      <li><b>Version catalogs</b> keep every dependency version in one file, <code>gradle/libs.versions.toml</code>, referenced as <code>libs.…</code> in the build scripts.</li>
      <li><b>Android apps are built with Gradle</b>, through the Android Gradle plugin.</li>
    </ul>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># gradle/libs.versions.toml
[versions]
junit = "5.11.0"
[libraries]
junit-bom = { module = "org.junit:junit-bom", version.ref = "junit" }

// build.gradle.kts
plugins { java }
dependencies {
    testImplementation(platform(libs.junit.bom))
    testImplementation("org.junit.jupiter:junit-jupiter")
}
tasks.test { useJUnitPlatform() }

./gradlew build                # compile, test, assemble
./gradlew build --build-cache  # reuse outputs from earlier builds</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Maven or Gradle?</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th></th><th>Maven</th><th>Gradle</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Build file</td><td>XML (<code>pom.xml</code>): declarative</td><td>Kotlin or Groovy: code</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Model</td><td>Fixed lifecycle of phases</td><td>Graph of tasks</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Versions in one place</td><td><code>dependencyManagement</code> and BOMs</td><td>Version catalogs and platforms</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <h4>Choose by requirement</h4>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>If this is true for you</th><th>It leans towards</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>You build an Android app</td><td>Gradle: Android builds use it, through the Android Gradle plugin</td></tr>
      <tr><td>A large multi-module build where build time hurts</td><td>Gradle: up-to-date checks, the Daemon and the build and configuration caches skip work</td></tr>
      <tr><td>You want every project to look the same, with little custom logic</td><td>Maven: a fixed lifecycle and conventions, configured rather than programmed</td></tr>
      <tr><td>The build needs real custom logic</td><td>Gradle: the build is code; in Maven the logic goes into a plugin</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Your team, CI templates and company plugins already use one</td><td>Stay with it unless a row above is a real, measured problem: switching has a cost</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note"><i>Other views:</i> some engineers find Gradle builds harder to read because the scripts are code; others find Maven&rsquo;s XML verbose. Both are preferences. The rows above are requirements.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>npm: the default for Node.js</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b><code>package.json</code></b> names your dependencies with version ranges and holds your <code>scripts</code>; <code>npm run build</code> runs one. A script named <code>prebuild</code> or <code>postbuild</code> runs before or after it.</li>
      <li><b><code>package-lock.json</code></b> is generated whenever npm changes <code>node_modules</code>. It records the exact tree, so the next install builds the same one. Commit it.</li>
      <li><b><code>npm ci</code> is for CI:</b> it needs an existing lockfile, fails if <code>package.json</code> and the lock disagree, and never writes to either. Use <code>npm install</code> to change dependencies, and <code>npm ci</code> to reproduce them.</li>
      <li><b><code>npx</code></b> runs a package's command without installing it into your project.</li>
      <li><b>Ranges:</b> a caret allows any change that keeps the left-most non-zero number; a tilde allows only patch-level changes when the minor is given. The lockfile is what stops a range from drifting between installs.</li>
    </ul>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>"dependencies": {
  "express": "^5.1.0",     // 5.x.x, never 6
  "lodash": "~4.17.21"     // 4.17.x only
}</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>npm 12: install scripts are off by default</h3>
    <p>Install scripts (<code>preinstall</code>, <code>install</code>, <code>postinstall</code>) run code from a dependency on your machine the moment you install it, so a compromised package can act before you have run a line of your own code. npm 12, released in July 2026, changed the defaults:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Dependency lifecycle scripts are blocked</b> unless the root package's <code>allowScripts</code> policy allows them. Run <code>npm install-scripts approve</code> to record approvals, then <code>npm rebuild</code> to run the newly approved scripts.</li>
      <li><b>Git and tarball-URL dependencies are refused</b> unless you opt in (<code>allow-git</code> and <code>allow-remote</code> now default to <code>none</code>).</li>
      <li><b><code>npm-shrinkwrap.json</code> is no longer read:</b> rename it to <code>package-lock.json</code>.</li>
      <li>On an older npm, <code>--ignore-scripts</code> turns install scripts off, though <code>npm test</code> and <code>npm run</code> still run the script you asked for.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Yarn, pnpm and Corepack</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Yarn</b> started at Facebook in 2016. Its modern version can install with <b>Plug'n'Play</b>: no <code>node_modules</code> folder at all, but a single <code>.pnp.cjs</code> loader that tells Node where each package is. It can also use a normal <code>node_modules</code> install.</li>
      <li><b>pnpm</b> keeps one <b>content-addressable store</b> on disk. Each project's files are hard-linked or copy-on-write cloned from it, so a hundred projects using one library do not keep a hundred copies, and a new version adds only the files that changed.</li>
      <li><b>pnpm's <code>node_modules</code> is not flat:</b> only your direct dependencies sit at the top, as symlinks. Code cannot quietly import a package it never declared. Tools that dislike symlinks can use <code>nodeLinker: hoisted</code>.</li>
      <li><b>Corepack</b> lets a project use the Yarn or pnpm version named in the <code>packageManager</code> field of <code>package.json</code>, without installing it globally. It shipped with Node.js until version 25. From 25 on, install Corepack separately.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>npm, Yarn or pnpm: choose by requirement</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>If this is true for you</th><th>It leans towards</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Nothing extra to install, the most tutorials and examples</td><td>npm: it comes with the Node.js installer</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Many projects on one machine or CI runner; disk and install time matter</td><td>pnpm: one content-addressable store, hard-linked into each project</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Code must not import packages it never declared</td><td>pnpm (non-flat <code>node_modules</code>) or Yarn Plug&rsquo;n&rsquo;Play</td></tr>
      <tr><td>No <code>node_modules</code> folder at all</td><td>Yarn Plug&rsquo;n&rsquo;Play</td></tr>
      <tr><td>A tool in your chain breaks on symlinks</td><td>npm, or pnpm with <code>nodeLinker: hoisted</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Strict control over install scripts by default</td><td>npm 12 or later blocks dependency install scripts unless allowed; check the others&rsquo; current settings for your version</td></tr>
      <tr><td>The project already uses one</td><td>Keep it, and pin it with <code>packageManager</code> in <code>package.json</code></td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Bundlers: the other half of a JavaScript build</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>A package manager</b> (npm, Yarn, pnpm) fetches dependencies. <b>A bundler</b> turns your modules into the few files a browser downloads. A front-end build needs both.</li>
      <li><b>webpack</b> (2014) builds a dependency graph of your modules and supports code splitting with dynamic <code>import()</code>.</li>
      <li><b>Vite</b> has two parts: a dev server that serves native ES modules with very fast hot module replacement, and a build command that bundles for production with Rolldown.</li>
      <li><b>TypeScript</b> adds a type-check step: <code>tsc --noEmit</code> in CI catches type errors even when the bundler only strips the types.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Habits that keep builds healthy, in any ecosystem</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Habit</th><th>Java</th><th>Node.js</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Pin the tool itself</td><td>Commit <code>mvnw</code> or <code>gradlew</code></td><td><code>packageManager</code> in <code>package.json</code>, and a pinned Node version in CI</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Pin every dependency</td><td>BOMs, <code>dependencyManagement</code>, version catalogs</td><td>Commit the lockfile and install with <code>npm ci</code>. Yarn (<code>--immutable</code>) and pnpm refuse to change the lockfile on CI by default</td></tr>
      <tr><td>See why a version won</td><td><code>mvn dependency:tree</code>, <code>gradle dependencies</code></td><td><code>npm explain &lt;pkg&gt;</code>, <code>npm ls</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Limit what runs at install</td><td>Add plugins and extensions deliberately, from sources you trust</td><td>Allow install scripts only for packages that need them</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Make CI fast</td><td>Cache <code>~/.m2</code> or <code>~/.gradle</code>; Gradle's build cache</td><td>Cache the package manager's store, not <code>node_modules</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Upgrade on purpose</td><td colspan="2">Small, regular dependency updates (Dependabot or Renovate), each with a green build, beat one big upgrade a year</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Run a Maven, Gradle and npm build from the wrapper or lockfile</li><li>Name the lifecycle phases or tasks a build runs</li><li>Add a dependency and read the dependency tree</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Pin versions with a BOM, a version catalog or a lockfile</li><li>Explain why a transitive version won and override it</li><li>Make CI builds reproducible and cached</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Design a multi-module or monorepo build</li><li>Use the Gradle build and configuration caches</li><li>Set an install-script and dependency-source policy</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Set build standards across an organisation</li><li>Lead migrations between build tools</li><li>Secure the build supply chain end to end</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>The Maven lifecycle and plugins</li><li>Gradle tasks, caches and version catalogs</li><li>Lockfiles and npm ci</li><li>pnpm's store and strict node_modules</li><li>Bundlers: webpack and Vite</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://maven.apache.org/guides/introduction/introduction-to-the-lifecycle.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Introduction to the Maven build lifecycle</a><span class="lnote"> Phases and bindings</span></li><li><a href="https://docs.gradle.org/current/userguide/userguide.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Gradle User Manual</a><span class="lnote"> Wrapper, Daemon, caches, catalogs</span></li><li><a href="https://docs.npmjs.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">npm Docs</a><span class="lnote"> package.json, lockfiles, scripts</span></li><li><a href="https://pnpm.io/motivation" target="_blank" rel="noopener">pnpm: Motivation</a><span class="lnote"> The store and the non-flat node_modules</span></li><li><a href="https://vite.dev/guide/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Vite guide</a><span class="lnote"> Dev server and production build</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://github.com/apache/maven-site/blob/master/content/markdown/guides/introduction/introduction-to-the-lifecycle.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Maven: build lifecycle (source)</a>; <a href="https://github.com/apache/maven-site/blob/master/content/markdown/guides/introduction/introduction-to-dependency-mechanism.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Maven: dependency mechanism (source)</a>; <a href="https://github.com/apache/maven-site/blob/master/content/markdown/tools/mavenwrapper.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Maven Wrapper (source)</a>; <a href="https://github.com/gradle/gradle/tree/master/platforms/documentation/docs/src/docs/userguide" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Gradle user guide: build lifecycle, Wrapper, Daemon, build cache, configuration cache, version catalogs (source)</a>; <a href="https://developer.android.com/build" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Android: configure your build</a>; <a href="https://github.com/npm/cli/blob/latest/docs/lib/content/commands/npm-ci.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">npm ci</a>; <a href="https://github.com/npm/cli/blob/latest/docs/lib/content/configuring-npm/package-lock-json.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">package-lock.json</a>; <a href="https://github.com/npm/cli/blob/latest/docs/lib/content/using-npm/scripts.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">npm scripts</a>; <a href="https://github.com/npm/cli/blob/latest/CHANGELOG.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">npm CHANGELOG (npm 12 breaking changes)</a>; <a href="https://github.com/npm/cli/blob/latest/workspaces/config/lib/definitions/definitions.js" target="_blank" rel="noopener">npm config definitions (ignore-scripts, allow-git, allow-remote, allow-scripts)</a>; <a href="https://github.com/npm/node-semver" target="_blank" rel="noopener">node-semver: caret and tilde ranges</a>; <a href="https://github.com/pnpm/pnpm.io/blob/main/docs/motivation.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">pnpm: Motivation (source)</a>; <a href="https://github.com/pnpm/pnpm.io/blob/main/docs/cli/install.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">pnpm install (source)</a>; <a href="https://yarnpkg.com/features/pnp" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Yarn: Plug'n'Play</a>; <a href="https://yarnpkg.com/cli/install" target="_blank" rel="noopener">yarn install</a>; <a href="https://github.com/nodejs/node/blob/main/deps/corepack/README.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Corepack README (bundled Node.js versions)</a>; <a href="https://github.com/vitejs/vite/blob/main/docs/guide/index.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Vite guide (source)</a>; <a href="https://docs.spring.io/spring-boot/maven-plugin/using.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Spring Boot Maven plugin: spring-boot-dependencies</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Build_automation" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Make, Apache Ant, Apache Maven, Gradle, npm, webpack, Bazel, Yarn, pnpm, Vite</a>.</p><p class="runcode">A question, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/enrichmeai.github.io/issues/new?title=%5BBuild%20Tools%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>. Nothing here is tracked and there are no comments to store: questions live in the open, where the next learner can find the answer.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= APIS & INTEGRATION ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-apis" data-name="APIs &amp; Integration">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Systems, APIs and integration</h2><p>The kinds of systems you will meet, how they talk to each other, and what REST, APIs, gateways, gRPC, GraphQL, webhooks and messaging each mean. Then <a href="#kafka">Kafka</a>, <a href="#spring">Spring Boot</a> and <a href="#quarkus">Quarkus</a> for building them.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Kinds of systems</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>What it does</th><th>Example</th><th>Built with</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Request/response service</td><td>Answers calls synchronously</td><td>Search API, login</td><td>Spring Boot, Quarkus, FastAPI on Cloud Run or GKE</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Event-driven service</td><td>Reacts to events; publishes its own</td><td>Index an order when it is placed</td><td>Kafka or Pub/Sub consumers</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Batch job</td><td>Processes a bounded set, then exits</td><td>Nightly load</td><td>Cloud Run jobs, Dataflow, Spark</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Stream processor</td><td>Processes an unbounded stream continuously</td><td>Real-time fraud score</td><td>Dataflow, Kafka Streams, Flink</td></tr>
      <tr><td>OLTP database system</td><td>Many small transactional reads and writes</td><td>User accounts, orders</td><td>Postgres, Cloud SQL, Spanner</td></tr>
      <tr><td>OLAP / analytics system</td><td>Large scans and aggregates</td><td>Reporting, dashboards</td><td>BigQuery</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Workflow / orchestration system</td><td>Coordinates multi-step processes</td><td>Pipeline stages, sagas</td><td>Airflow, Workflows, Temporal</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Integration / API platform</td><td>Exposes, secures and manages APIs for others</td><td>Partner API</td><td>Apigee, API Gateway</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Front end</td><td>User interface</td><td>Web or mobile app</td><td>React, native apps</td></tr>
      <tr><td>AI-powered system</td><td>Uses models to understand or generate</td><td>Research assistant</td><td>Model APIs, RAG, agents</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>What an API is</h3>
    <p>An <b>API</b> (application programming interface) is a contract: the operations a system offers, the inputs they take, the outputs and errors they return, and the rules for calling them. The contract is what other teams build against; the implementation behind it can change freely as long as the contract holds.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Public / partner / internal:</b> who may call it, and so how strict versioning, security and documentation must be.</li>
      <li><b>Synchronous:</b> the caller waits (REST, gRPC, GraphQL).</li>
      <li><b>Asynchronous:</b> the caller sends and moves on; the result arrives later (queues, events, webhooks).</li>
      <li><b>Spec first:</b> write the contract (OpenAPI, protobuf, AsyncAPI) before the code; generate clients and docs from it.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>REST in practice</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Resources</b> are nouns with URLs: <code>/orders</code>, <code>/orders/{id}</code>, <code>/stores/{code}/orders</code>.</li>
      <li><b>HTTP methods</b> are the verbs: GET reads, POST creates, PUT replaces, PATCH updates part, DELETE removes.</li>
      <li><b>Stateless:</b> each request carries everything needed (including auth); no server session required.</li>
      <li><b>Status codes</b> carry meaning: 200, 201 Created (+ <code>Location</code>), 204, 400, 401, 403, 404, 409 Conflict, 422, 429, 500, 503.</li>
      <li><b>Errors</b> as Problem Details (RFC 9457): <code>type</code>, <code>title</code>, <code>status</code>, <code>detail</code>.</li>
      <li><b>Pagination</b> with a cursor (<code>?cursor=…&amp;limit=50</code>) rather than deep offsets.</li>
      <li><b>Filtering and sorting</b> by query parameters: <code>?store=1_05&amp;from=2024-01-01&amp;sort=-order_date</code>.</li>
      <li><b>Caching</b> with <code>ETag</code>/<code>If-None-Match</code> and <code>Cache-Control</code>.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Safe</th><th>Idempotent</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>GET, HEAD</td><td>yes</td><td>yes</td></tr>
      <tr><td>PUT, DELETE</td><td>no</td><td>yes</td></tr>
      <tr><td>POST</td><td>no</td><td>no: use an Idempotency-Key header</td></tr>
      <tr><td>PATCH</td><td>no</td><td>not guaranteed</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>An OpenAPI contract</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>openapi: 3.1.0
info: { title: Shop API, version: 1.2.0 }
paths:
  /v1/orders/{id}:
    get:
      operationId: getOrder
      parameters:
        - { name: id, in: path, required: true, schema: { type: string } }
      responses:
        "200":
          description: The order
          content:
            application/json:
              schema: { $ref: "#/components/schemas/Order" }
        "404":
          description: Not found
          content:
            application/problem+json:
              schema: { $ref: "#/components/schemas/Problem" }
components:
  schemas:
    Order:
      type: object
      required: [id, orderRef, store]
      properties:
        id: { type: string }
        orderRef: { type: string }
        store: { type: string }
        orderDate: { type: [string, "null"], format: date }
    Problem:
      type: object
      properties:
        type: { type: string, format: uri-reference }
        title: { type: string }
        status: { type: integer }
        detail: { type: string }
  securitySchemes:
    bearer: { type: http, scheme: bearer, bearerFormat: JWT }
security: [ { bearer: [] } ]</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>API styles compared</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Style</th><th>Shape</th><th>Strengths</th><th>Weaknesses</th><th>Use for</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>REST / JSON over HTTP</td><td>Resources + HTTP verbs</td><td>Universal, cacheable, easy to debug</td><td>Over- or under-fetching; loose typing unless specified</td><td>Public and partner APIs, most web services</td></tr>
      <tr><td>gRPC</td><td>Protobuf messages over HTTP/2, generated clients</td><td>Fast, strongly typed, streaming both ways</td><td>Not browser-native; harder to inspect</td><td>Internal service-to-service calls</td></tr>
      <tr><td>GraphQL</td><td>One endpoint; client asks for exactly the fields it needs</td><td>Flexible for UIs; one round trip for nested data</td><td>Caching, query cost control, authorisation per field</td><td>Front ends combining many sources</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Webhooks</td><td>Your server calls the client's URL when something happens</td><td>No polling</td><td>Delivery retries, signature checks, client downtime</td><td>Notifying partners (payments, Git events)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Messaging / events</td><td>Publish to a topic; subscribers consume</td><td>Decoupling, buffering, fan-out</td><td>Eventual consistency, duplicates, debugging</td><td>Integration between domains, pipelines</td></tr>
      <tr><td>WebSockets / SSE</td><td>Long-lived connection for pushes</td><td>Real-time updates, streaming responses</td><td>Connection management at scale</td><td>Live dashboards, chat, streaming AI output</td></tr>
      <tr><td>SOAP / XML</td><td>Envelope with WSDL contract</td><td>Strict contracts, WS-* standards</td><td>Verbose, legacy tooling</td><td>Integrating older enterprise systems</td></tr>
      <tr><td>File exchange</td><td>CSV/Parquet drops on SFTP or buckets</td><td>Simple, bulk</td><td>Latency, partial files, schema drift</td><td>Bulk data between organisations</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>gRPC contract</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>syntax = "proto3";
package shop.v1;

service OrderService {
  rpc GetOrder(GetOrderRequest) returns (Order);
  rpc StreamNew(StreamNewRequest) returns (stream Order);   // server streaming
}

message GetOrderRequest { string id = 1; }
message StreamNewRequest  { string store = 1; }
message Order {
  string id = 1;
  string order_ref = 2;
  string store = 3;
  string order_date = 4;      // ISO date; field numbers never reused
}</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">Compatibility rules: add fields with new numbers; never change or reuse a field number; reserve removed ones.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Gateway, load balancer, proxy, mesh: who does what</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Component</th><th>Job</th><th>Knows about</th><th>GCP examples</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Load balancer</td><td>Spread traffic across healthy instances; terminate TLS</td><td>IPs, ports, hosts, paths</td><td>Cloud Load Balancing</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Reverse proxy</td><td>Front a server: TLS, compression, routing, caching</td><td>HTTP requests</td><td>Envoy, nginx</td></tr>
      <tr><td>API gateway</td><td>Single entry for APIs: authentication, rate limits, routing, request transformation</td><td>API keys, tokens, routes, quotas</td><td>API Gateway, Apigee</td></tr>
      <tr><td>API management platform</td><td>Gateway plus API products, developer portal, analytics, monetisation, lifecycle</td><td>Consumers, apps, products, plans</td><td>Apigee</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Service mesh</td><td>Service-to-service mTLS, retries, traffic splitting, telemetry inside the platform</td><td>Every internal call</td><td>Cloud Service Mesh (Istio)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Backend for frontend</td><td>An API shaped for one client type</td><td>That client's screens</td><td>A small service on Cloud Run</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note">North-south traffic (clients into your platform) goes through the gateway; east-west traffic (services to each other) is the mesh's job. Keep business logic out of both.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Who does what, drawn</h3>
    <figure class="fig"><svg viewBox="0 0 660 270" role="img" aria-labelledby="nsew-t nsew-d"><title id="nsew-t">North-south and east-west traffic</title><desc id="nsew-d">Clients outside the platform reach it through a load balancer and an API gateway: north-south traffic. Inside, services call each other through the service mesh, which adds mTLS, retries and telemetry: east-west traffic. A backend for frontend is shaped for one client. Business logic lives in the services, not in the gateway or the mesh.</desc><defs><marker id="nsew-a" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker><marker id="nsew-aa" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar acc" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker></defs><rect class="nd in" x="10" y="20" width="130" height="40" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="75" y="45" text-anchor="middle">mobile app</text><rect class="nd in" x="170" y="20" width="130" height="40" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="235" y="45" text-anchor="middle">partner</text><rect class="nd" x="10" y="90" width="290" height="36" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="155" y="113" text-anchor="middle">load balancer: spread, TLS</text><rect class="nd" x="10" y="146" width="290" height="36" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="155" y="169" text-anchor="middle">API gateway: auth, rate limits</text><line class="ed" x1="75" y1="60" x2="75" y2="86" marker-end="url(#nsew-a)"/><line class="ed" x1="235" y1="60" x2="235" y2="86" marker-end="url(#nsew-a)"/><line class="ed" x1="155" y1="126" x2="155" y2="142" marker-end="url(#nsew-a)"/><text class="lb acc" x="155" y="210" text-anchor="middle">north-south: into the platform</text><line class="ed acc" x1="155" y1="182" x2="155" y2="196"/><rect class="bd" x="340" y="20" width="310" height="230" rx="8"/><text class="lb hd" x="495" y="40" text-anchor="middle">inside the platform: the mesh, east-west</text><rect class="nd" x="360" y="60" width="130" height="40" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="425" y="85" text-anchor="middle">BFF for mobile</text><rect class="nd out" x="510" y="60" width="120" height="40" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="570" y="85" text-anchor="middle">orders</text><rect class="nd out" x="360" y="150" width="130" height="40" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="425" y="175" text-anchor="middle">payments</text><rect class="nd out" x="510" y="150" width="120" height="40" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="570" y="175" text-anchor="middle">stock</text><line class="ed acc" x1="300" y1="164" x2="356" y2="86" marker-end="url(#nsew-aa)"/><line class="ed" x1="490" y1="80" x2="506" y2="80" marker-end="url(#nsew-a)"/><line class="ed" x1="425" y1="100" x2="425" y2="146" marker-end="url(#nsew-a)"/><line class="ed" x1="570" y1="100" x2="570" y2="146" marker-end="url(#nsew-a)"/><text class="lb mu" x="495" y="226" text-anchor="middle">mTLS, retries, telemetry on every internal call</text></svg><figcaption>The table above as a picture. The gateway guards the front door; the mesh handles the corridors; neither holds business logic.</figcaption></figure>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>API versioning and evolution</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Additive changes are safe:</b> new endpoints, new optional fields, new enum values clients are told to tolerate.</li>
      <li><b>Breaking changes</b> (removing or renaming fields, changing types or meaning) need a new version.</li>
      <li><b>Where to version:</b> in the path (<code>/v1/</code>) is the most visible and the easiest to route at a gateway; headers are cleaner but harder to test.</li>
      <li><b>Deprecate politely:</b> announce, add <code>Deprecation</code> and <code>Sunset</code> headers, measure who still calls, then remove.</li>
      <li><b>Meaning can break without a schema change:</b> a field whose values change meaning is a breaking change even if its type is the same.</li>
      <li><b>Contract tests</b> in CI catch accidental breaks.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Reliable integration</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Timeouts</b> on every call; <b>retries</b> only for idempotent operations, with backoff and jitter.</li>
      <li><b>Idempotency keys</b> for POSTs that create or charge.</li>
      <li><b>Long-running work:</b> return <code>202 Accepted</code> with a status URL, or emit an event when done.</li>
      <li><b>Webhooks you send:</b> sign the payload (HMAC), retry with backoff, let clients replay.</li>
      <li><b>Webhooks you receive:</b> verify the signature, respond fast, process asynchronously, dedupe by event ID.</li>
      <li><b>Rate limits</b> with <code>429</code> and <code>Retry-After</code>.</li>
      <li><b>Correlation IDs</b> (<code>traceparent</code>) passed through every hop.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Integration styles</h3>
    <ol>
      <li><b>File transfer:</b> one system writes files, another reads them. Simple and still common for bulk data.</li>
      <li><b>Shared database:</b> several systems read and write the same tables. Avoid: no system owns the schema.</li>
      <li><b>Remote procedure call:</b> one system calls another's API (REST, gRPC). Simple; couples availability.</li>
      <li><b>Messaging:</b> systems exchange messages through a broker. Decoupled in time; more moving parts.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="note">From <i>Enterprise Integration Patterns</i> (Hohpe and Woolf), a catalogue of messaging patterns.</p>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Explain REST resources, methods and status codes</li><li>Call an API with curl and read its OpenAPI spec</li><li>Tell sync from async integration</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Design a REST API with pagination, errors and versioning</li><li>Write and publish an OpenAPI contract</li><li>Add timeouts, retries and idempotency keys</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Choose REST, gRPC, GraphQL or events for a context</li><li>Design gateway, mesh and BFF responsibilities</li><li>Run API evolution: deprecation and contract tests</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Set API standards and governance across the organisation</li><li>Design a partner and public API programme</li><li>Lead integration architecture across many systems</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>Problem Details (RFC 9457)</li><li>AsyncAPI for event-driven contracts</li><li>gRPC streaming and deadlines</li><li>GraphQL federation</li><li>API design guides (Google AIP)</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://google.aip.dev/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google API Improvement Proposals (AIP)</a><span class="lnote"> Google's detailed API design rules</span></li><li><a href="https://spec.openapis.org/oas/latest.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OpenAPI Specification</a><span class="lnote"> The contract format for HTTP APIs</span></li><li><a href="https://grpc.io/docs/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">gRPC documentation</a><span class="lnote"> Concepts and language guides</span></li><li><a href="https://graphql.org/learn/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GraphQL</a><span class="lnote"> Official introduction</span></li><li><a href="https://www.asyncapi.com/docs" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AsyncAPI</a><span class="lnote"> Contracts for event-driven APIs</span></li><li><a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9457" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RFC 9457: Problem Details for HTTP APIs</a><span class="lnote"> Standard error format</span></li><li><a href="https://www.enterpriseintegrationpatterns.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Enterprise Integration Patterns</a><span class="lnote"> Messaging and integration patterns</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110.txt" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RFC 9110 HTTP Semantics (safe/idempotent methods, status codes)</a>; <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9457.txt" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RFC 9457 Problem Details</a>; <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc5789.txt" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RFC 5789 PATCH (neither safe nor idempotent)</a>; <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8594.txt" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RFC 8594 Sunset header</a>; <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9745.txt" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RFC 9745 Deprecation header</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/OAI/OpenAPI-Specification/main/versions/3.1.0.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OpenAPI 3.1 specification</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/protocolbuffers/protocolbuffers.github.io/main/content/programming-guides/proto3.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Protocol Buffers proto3 guide (field numbers, reserved)</a>; <a href="https://www.enterpriseintegrationpatterns.com/patterns/messaging/IntegrationStylesIntro.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Enterprise Integration Patterns: integration styles</a>; <a href="https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-ietf-httpapi-idempotency-key-header/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">IETF draft: the Idempotency-Key HTTP header</a>.</p><p class="runcode">A question, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/enrichmeai.github.io/issues/new?title=%5BAPIs%20%26%20Integration%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>. Nothing here is tracked and there are no comments to store: questions live in the open, where the next learner can find the answer.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= HOW APIS EVOLVED ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-apihistory" data-name="How APIs Evolved">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>How APIs evolved: from CORBA to agent gateways</h2><p>Every generation of APIs answered the same question, how one program asks another to do something, and each answer was shaped by what the one before got wrong. This page follows that line from remote procedure calls through CORBA, SOAP, REST, gRPC and GraphQL to the protocols agents use today, and then the gateways that sit in front of each. How to design an API today is on <a href="#apis">APIs &amp; Integration</a>.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>The timeline</h3>
    <figure class="fig"><svg viewBox="0 0 660 200" role="img" aria-labelledby="api-t api-d"><title id="api-t">Timeline of API styles</title><desc id="api-d">A horizontal timeline, evenly spaced and not to scale: RPC 1981, CORBA 1991, XML-RPC and SOAP 1998, REST 2000, OpenAPI 2015, GraphQL 2015, gRPC 1.0 2016, MCP 2024, A2A 2025.</desc><defs><marker id="api-a" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker><marker id="api-aa" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar acc" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker></defs><line class="ed" x1="20" y1="100" x2="640" y2="100" marker-end="url(#api-a)"/><circle class="nd in" cx="45" cy="100" r="6"/><line class="ed" x1="45" y1="94" x2="45" y2="70"/><text class="lb hd" x="45" y="46" text-anchor="middle">1981</text><text class="lb mu" x="45" y="62" text-anchor="middle">RPC</text><circle class="nd in" cx="115" cy="100" r="6"/><line class="ed" x1="115" y1="106" x2="115" y2="130"/><text class="lb hd" x="115" y="150" text-anchor="middle">1991</text><text class="lb mu" x="115" y="166" text-anchor="middle">CORBA</text><circle class="nd in" cx="185" cy="100" r="6"/><line class="ed" x1="185" y1="94" x2="185" y2="70"/><text class="lb hd" x="185" y="46" text-anchor="middle">1998</text><text class="lb mu" x="185" y="62" text-anchor="middle">XML-RPC, SOAP</text><circle class="nd in" cx="255" cy="100" r="6"/><line class="ed" x1="255" y1="106" x2="255" y2="130"/><text class="lb hd" x="255" y="150" text-anchor="middle">2000</text><text class="lb mu" x="255" y="166" text-anchor="middle">REST</text><circle class="nd in" cx="325" cy="100" r="6"/><line class="ed" x1="325" y1="94" x2="325" y2="70"/><text class="lb hd" x="325" y="46" text-anchor="middle">2015</text><text class="lb mu" x="325" y="62" text-anchor="middle">OpenAPI</text><circle class="nd in" cx="395" cy="100" r="6"/><line class="ed" x1="395" y1="106" x2="395" y2="130"/><text class="lb hd" x="395" y="150" text-anchor="middle">2015</text><text class="lb mu" x="395" y="166" text-anchor="middle">GraphQL</text><circle class="nd in" cx="465" cy="100" r="6"/><line class="ed" x1="465" y1="94" x2="465" y2="70"/><text class="lb hd" x="465" y="46" text-anchor="middle">2016</text><text class="lb mu" x="465" y="62" text-anchor="middle">gRPC 1.0</text><circle class="nd in" cx="535" cy="100" r="6"/><line class="ed" x1="535" y1="106" x2="535" y2="130"/><text class="lb hd" x="535" y="150" text-anchor="middle">2024</text><text class="lb mu" x="535" y="166" text-anchor="middle">MCP</text><circle class="nd in" cx="605" cy="100" r="6"/><line class="ed" x1="605" y1="94" x2="605" y2="70"/><text class="lb hd" x="605" y="46" text-anchor="middle">2025</text><text class="lb mu" x="605" y="62" text-anchor="middle">A2A</text><text class="lb mu" x="640" y="190" text-anchor="end">evenly spaced, not to scale</text></svg><figcaption>From remote procedure calls to agent protocols. The last two were designed for AI models and agents, rather than for programs calling programs. A selection from the table below.</figcaption></figure>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>When</th><th>What arrived</th><th>The idea</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>1981&ndash;1984</td><td><b>Remote procedure call (RPC)</b></td><td>Bruce Jay Nelson is credited with coining the term in 1981; he and Andrew Birrell published a paper on implementing it in 1984. Calling a function on another machine as if it were local.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>1991</td><td><b>CORBA</b></td><td>The Object Management Group&rsquo;s standard for objects on different platforms and languages to call each other, with interfaces described in an interface definition language (IDL).</td></tr>
      <tr><td>1996</td><td><b>DCOM</b></td><td>Microsoft&rsquo;s proprietary equivalent, extending COM across the network (launched in beta for Windows 95 in 1996). Java had its own, Java RMI.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>1998&ndash;2002</td><td><b>SOA and the enterprise service bus</b></td><td>Service-oriented architecture, which can be built with web services. An enterprise service bus routes and transforms messages between services; the first commercial ESB dates from 1998, and the term was first published by Gartner in 2002.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>1998</td><td><b>XML-RPC, then SOAP</b></td><td>Dave Winer shipped XML-RPC in 1998: remote calls encoded as XML over HTTP. SOAP grew from the same work into a fuller XML messaging protocol, described by WSDL documents.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>2000</td><td><b>REST</b></td><td>Roy Fielding&rsquo;s doctoral dissertation described Representational State Transfer, the architectural style of the web: resources, a uniform interface and stateless requests.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2001</td><td><b>JSON</b></td><td>Douglas Crockford specified JSON in the early 2000s and sent the first JSON message in April 2001. It became the usual body for web APIs.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2010&ndash;2015</td><td><b>Swagger, then OpenAPI</b></td><td>Tony Tam began Swagger in 2010 to describe HTTP APIs; in 2015 the specification became a separate project, OpenAPI, overseen by the OpenAPI Initiative under the Linux Foundation.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2015</td><td><b>GraphQL</b></td><td>Facebook started it in 2012 and open-sourced it in 2015: clients send a query against a typed schema, and the result mirrors the query&rsquo;s shape. It moved to the GraphQL Foundation in 2018.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2015&ndash;2016</td><td><b>gRPC</b></td><td>Google had run an internal RPC system, Stubby, since about 2001. In 2015 it decided to build the next version as open source: Protocol Buffers over HTTP/2, with bidirectional streaming, timeouts and cancellation. gRPC 1.0 followed in August 2016.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2024</td><td><b>Model Context Protocol (MCP)</b></td><td>Introduced by Anthropic in November 2024 to standardise how AI applications connect to tools and data; adopted by other AI vendors in 2025 and donated to the Linux Foundation&rsquo;s Agentic AI Foundation in December 2025.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2025</td><td><b>Agent2Agent (A2A)</b></td><td>Introduced by Google in April 2025 and now developed with the Linux Foundation: an open protocol for agents built on different frameworks by different companies to discover each other through &ldquo;Agent Cards&rdquo; and collaborate, using JSON-RPC over HTTP(S), without exposing their internal memory or tools.</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>The lesson RPC and CORBA left</h3>
    <p>RPC and CORBA aimed for <b>location transparency</b>: a remote call should look like a local one. But remote calls are usually orders of magnitude slower and less reliable than local ones, and can fail because of network problems. CORBA&rsquo;s version has been criticised as a fundamental design flaw, citing Waldo, Wyant, Wollrath and Kendall&rsquo;s <i>A Note on Distributed Computing</i> (Sun Microsystems Laboratories, 1994). Wikipedia flags that citation as not yet verified, so the paper itself is worth reading.</p>
    <p class="note"><i>Analysis:</i> every later style made the network more visible in some way: HTTP status codes in REST, explicit deadlines in gRPC, and the timeouts and retries on <a href="#distsys">Distributed Systems</a>.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>What each style changed</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>SOAP and WSDL</b> brought a formal contract and a standard envelope: a WSDL document describes the operations a service offers. <i>Analysis:</i> its XML is heavier than JSON; the other view is that a strict contract and its tooling were what many enterprises wanted.</li>
      <li><b>REST</b> used what the web already had: URLs for resources, HTTP methods, status codes and caching. Its constraints include client and server separation, statelessness, cacheable responses and a uniform interface.</li>
      <li><b>GraphQL</b> moved the choice of data to the client, with one endpoint and a typed schema.</li>
      <li><b>gRPC</b> brought back strongly typed RPC, with a binary format and streaming, for calls between services.</li>
      <li><b>MCP and A2A</b> describe capabilities so that a model or an agent, not just a programmer, can discover and call them.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Protocol Buffers: the contract under gRPC</h3>
    <p>Protocol Buffers (protobuf) is Google&rsquo;s language-neutral, platform-neutral way of serialising structured data. Google developed it for internal use (from early 2001) and released a code generator for many languages as open source. You describe messages once in a <code>.proto</code> file; the compiler, <code>protoc</code>, generates classes for each language, and gRPC uses the same files to describe services.</p>
<pre><code>// person.proto
syntax = "proto3";

message Person {
  string name  = 1;
  int32  id    = 2;
  string email = 3;
}</code></pre>
    <p>The numbers are <b>field numbers</b>, and they are what goes on the wire, not the names. Each field is written as a tag, <code>(field_number &lt;&lt; 3) | wire_type</code>, followed by its value. The same record as protobuf and as JSON (Python&rsquo;s default <code>json.dumps</code> spacing, so compact JSON would be smaller), measured once on this one record:</p>
<pre><code>import json
from google.protobuf import descriptor_pb2, descriptor_pool, message_factory

# The same schema as person.proto, built in code so no protoc is needed here
fdp = descriptor_pb2.FileDescriptorProto(name="person.proto", syntax="proto3")
msg = fdp.message_type.add(name="Person")
F = descriptor_pb2.FieldDescriptorProto
msg.field.add(name="name", number=1, type=F.TYPE_STRING, label=F.LABEL_OPTIONAL)
msg.field.add(name="id", number=2, type=F.TYPE_INT32, label=F.LABEL_OPTIONAL)
msg.field.add(name="email", number=3, type=F.TYPE_STRING, label=F.LABEL_OPTIONAL)
pool = descriptor_pool.DescriptorPool()
pool.Add(fdp)
Person = message_factory.GetMessageClass(pool.FindMessageTypeByName("Person"))

p = Person(name="Ana", id=1234, email="ana@example.com")
wire = p.SerializeToString()
as_json = json.dumps({"name": "Ana", "id": 1234, "email": "ana@example.com"}).encode()
print("protobuf bytes:", len(wire), wire.hex(" "))
print("JSON bytes:   ", len(as_json))
print("round trip:   ", Person.FromString(wire).email)
</code></pre>
<pre><code>protobuf bytes: 25 0a 03 41 6e 61 10 d2 09 1a 0f 61 6e 61 40 65 78 61 6d 70 6c 65 2e 63 6f 6d
JSON bytes:    55
round trip:    ana@example.com</code></pre>
    <p class="note">Run as shown with Python 3 and the <code>protobuf</code> package (output from version <span data-other-version>7.36.1</span>); the second block is its real output. Reading the bytes: <code>0a</code> is (1&lt;&lt;3)|2, field 1 with wire type 2 (length-delimited); <code>03</code> is the length, then <code>41 6e 61</code> is &ldquo;Ana&rdquo;. <code>10</code> is (2&lt;&lt;3)|0, field 2 as a varint; <code>d2 09</code> is 1234, written seven bits at a time with the top bit of the first byte set to say another byte follows. <code>1a</code> is (3&lt;&lt;3)|2, field 3; <code>0f</code> is the length, 15, then the 15 bytes of &ldquo;ana@example.com&rdquo;. Normally you would generate <code>Person</code> with <code>protoc</code>; the schema is built in code here only so the example runs without it.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Changing a protobuf schema safely</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Never change a field&rsquo;s number</b> once the message is in use: the number identifies the field in the wire format, so changing it is the same as deleting the field and adding a new one.</li>
      <li><b>Reserve</b> the numbers of fields you delete, and their names if you want <code>protoc</code> to refuse them, so nobody reuses them by accident.</li>
      <li>Adding new fields is how a protobuf schema grows: the docs say it can be extended &ldquo;without invalidating existing data or requiring code to be updated&rdquo;.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">Field numbers 19,000 to 19,999 are reserved for the protobuf implementation itself. More on contract evolution is on <a href="#apis">APIs &amp; Integration</a>.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Protobuf or JSON?</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>If this is true for you</th><th>It leans towards (<i>analysis</i>)</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>High-volume calls between your own services, in several languages</td><td>Protobuf, usually with gRPC</td></tr>
      <tr><td>A public API that browsers or scripts call</td><td>JSON: readable without the schema</td></tr>
      <tr><td>You must read a message without its <code>.proto</code> file</td><td>JSON: protobuf messages do not describe themselves</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Messages larger than a few megabytes, or large numeric arrays</td><td>Something else: the protobuf docs say it is not a good fit</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Rules require a formal standard</td><td>Not protobuf: its docs say it is not a formal standard of any organisation</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note">The right column is our reading of the protobuf documentation, not a standard. Other teams weigh size and speed more, or readability more; your team already running one format counts too, because switching has a cost.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>The gateway in front of it all</h3>
    <p>As APIs multiplied, a gateway in front of them took on the shared work: routing, authentication, rate limits, quotas, transformation and monitoring (more on <a href="#apis">APIs &amp; Integration</a> and <a href="#apigee">Apigee</a>). Agents have produced a new kind of gateway. The right-hand column is our summary of the vendor documents listed below (<i>analysis</i>), not a standard definition:</p>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th></th><th>API gateway</th><th>AI and agent gateway</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Traffic it fronts</td><td>Requests from clients to your APIs</td><td>Requests from apps and agents to models, to tools (often over MCP) and to other agents (A2A)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>What it meters</td><td>Usage and load</td><td>Also tokens and spend per model, team or user</td></tr>
      <tr><td>What it routes</td><td>Requests to the right backend service</td><td>Also by model and provider, with load balancing and failover between providers</td></tr>
      <tr><td>What it checks</td><td>Access and usage policies</td><td>Also prompts and responses, through guardrails and content filters</td></tr>
      <tr><td>What it translates</td><td>Requests and responses, on the fly</td><td>Existing REST or OpenAPI APIs into MCP tools that agents can call</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Agent gateways today</h3>
    <p>Each description below is the project&rsquo;s or vendor&rsquo;s own:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Azure API Management</b> has an &ldquo;AI gateway&rdquo; that extends its existing API gateway rather than being a separate product: it imports model endpoints as APIs, manages token usage and quotas, exposes existing REST APIs as MCP servers, and imports and manages A2A agent APIs.</li>
      <li><b>Apigee</b> can expose APIs as MCP tools to agentic applications, implementing the MCP server specification.</li>
      <li><b>Amazon Bedrock AgentCore Gateway</b> is described as a fully managed gateway that gives agents one entry point to tools, other agents and models, taking OpenAPI, Smithy and Lambda as tool sources.</li>
      <li><b>agentgateway</b>, an open-source proxy, describes itself as built on MCP and A2A, with an LLM gateway, an MCP gateway, an A2A gateway, guardrails, authentication and OpenTelemetry tracing.</li>
      <li><b>Agent Router</b>, formerly Envoy AI Gateway and now an Agentic AI Foundation project, offers one OpenAI-compatible API for models and MCP servers, with credentials, routing, quotas and failover kept in one place.</li>
      <li><b>Docker&rsquo;s MCP Gateway</b> runs MCP servers in isolated containers and gives AI models one gateway to reach them.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note"><i>Analysis:</i> the pattern repeats an old one. An agent gateway does for model and tool calls what API gateways did for service calls: one place for identity, limits, cost and audit. Security for agents is on <a href="#agentsec">Agentic AI Security</a>.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Choose by requirement</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>If this is true for you</th><th>It leans towards (<i>analysis</i>)</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Public or partner API, many unknown clients, cacheable reads</td><td>REST with an OpenAPI contract</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Many front ends needing different shapes of the same data</td><td>GraphQL</td></tr>
      <tr><td>High-volume calls between your own services, streaming, several languages</td><td>gRPC</td></tr>
      <tr><td>An existing SOAP or CORBA system that works and is not changing</td><td>Keep it, and wrap it behind a gateway or an anti-corruption layer (see <a href="#atob">Solution Architecture: A to B</a>)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>AI applications need to call your existing APIs as tools</td><td>Expose them over MCP, through a gateway that already holds your auth and quotas</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Agents from different teams or vendors must hand work to each other</td><td>A2A</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Many teams calling several model providers, and spend must be visible</td><td>An AI gateway for token quotas, routing and failover</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Your team already runs one gateway product</td><td>Check its AI and MCP features before adding another: switching has a cost</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Our view, and the other view</h3>
    <p><i>Our view:</i> each new style added to the toolbox more than it replaced the old one. Older styles keep running alongside newer ones, gRPC and REST often sit side by side, and an MCP server can wrap a REST API that already existed. Knowing the history makes it easier to see that an agent gateway is a familiar idea with new things to meter.</p>
    <p><i>The other view:</i> agent traffic differs enough, with long-running tasks, token costs, and decisions made by a model rather than a programmer, that treating it as &ldquo;just another API&rdquo; misses new risks. On this view, agent protocols and gateways deserve their own design and security thinking from the start.</p>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Place RPC, CORBA, SOAP, REST, gRPC and GraphQL in order, and say what each changed</li><li>Explain why location transparency was criticised</li><li>Say what MCP and A2A are for</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Choose an API style for a case by its requirements</li><li>Wrap a legacy SOAP service behind a gateway</li><li>Expose an existing REST API as MCP tools</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Explain what an AI or agent gateway adds: token quotas, model routing, guardrails</li><li>Plan a move from an older API style without breaking clients</li><li>Design gateway policies for agent traffic</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Set an organisation's API and agent-protocol strategy</li><li>Choose between extending an existing gateway and adding an agent gateway</li><li>Govern models, tools and agents in one place</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>Remote procedure calls</li><li>CORBA and SOAP</li><li>REST and OpenAPI</li><li>GraphQL and gRPC</li><li>MCP and A2A</li><li>AI and agent gateways</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://github.com/a2aproject/A2A" target="_blank" rel="noopener">A2A protocol</a><span class="lnote"> The specification and SDKs</span></li><li><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Model_Context_Protocol" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Model Context Protocol (Wikipedia)</a><span class="lnote"> History and adoption</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Remote_procedure_call" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Remote procedure call</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Common_Object_Request_Broker_Architecture" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: CORBA</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Distributed_Component_Object_Model" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: DCOM</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/XML-RPC" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: XML-RPC</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SOAP" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: SOAP</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Service-oriented_architecture" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Service-oriented architecture</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Enterprise_service_bus" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Enterprise service bus</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/REST" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: REST</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/JSON" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: JSON</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/OpenAPI_Specification" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: OpenAPI Specification</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GraphQL" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: GraphQL</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GRPC" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: gRPC</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Model_Context_Protocol" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Model Context Protocol</a>; <a href="https://github.com/a2aproject/A2A/blob/main/README.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">A2A protocol (README)</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agent2Agent" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Agent2Agent</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Web_Services_Description_Language" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: WSDL</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/API_management" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: API management</a>; <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/api-management/genai-gateway-capabilities" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Azure API Management: AI gateway</a>; <a href="https://cloud.google.com/apigee/docs/api-platform/apigee-mcp/apigee-mcp-overview" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Apigee: MCP overview</a>; <a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock-agentcore/latest/devguide/gateway.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Amazon Bedrock AgentCore Gateway</a>; <a href="https://github.com/agentgateway/agentgateway/blob/main/README.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">agentgateway (README)</a>; <a href="https://github.com/envoyproxy/ai-gateway/blob/main/README.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Agent Router, formerly Envoy AI Gateway (README)</a>; <a href="https://github.com/docker/mcp-gateway/blob/main/README.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Docker MCP Gateway (README)</a>; <a href="https://github.com/protocolbuffers/protocolbuffers.github.io/blob/main/content/overview.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Protocol Buffers: Overview (source)</a>; <a href="https://github.com/protocolbuffers/protocolbuffers.github.io/blob/main/content/programming-guides/proto3.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Protocol Buffers: proto3 language guide (source)</a>; <a href="https://github.com/protocolbuffers/protocolbuffers.github.io/blob/main/content/programming-guides/encoding.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Protocol Buffers: Encoding (source)</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Protocol_Buffers" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Protocol Buffers</a>.</p><p class="runcode"><b>Run the code:</b> <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/well-rounded-engineer-code/tree/main/protobuf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">well-rounded-engineer-code/protobuf</a>, with a README that says how to run it and what it shows. Stuck, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/well-rounded-engineer-code/issues/new?title=%5BHow%20APIs%20Evolved%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= GRAPHQL ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-graphql" data-name="GraphQL">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>GraphQL</h2><p>A query language for APIs: one typed schema, and clients ask for exactly the fields they need. How it works, when it beats REST, the performance and security traps, and servers in Spring, Quarkus and Python. API styles in general are on <a href="#apis">APIs &amp; Integration</a>.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>The idea in one paragraph</h3>
    <p>A GraphQL server publishes a <b>schema</b>: the types, their fields and how they connect. Clients send a <b>query</b> to a single endpoint (usually <code>POST /graphql</code>) describing the shape of data they want, and get back JSON in exactly that shape. Reads are <b>queries</b>, writes are <b>mutations</b>, live updates are <b>subscriptions</b>. Each field is filled by a <b>resolver</b> function on the server, which can read from a database, another service or a cache.</p>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th></th><th>REST</th><th>GraphQL</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Endpoints</td><td>Many, one per resource</td><td>One</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Response shape</td><td>Fixed by the server</td><td>Chosen by the client</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Nested data</td><td>Several round trips or custom endpoints</td><td>One request</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Contract</td><td>OpenAPI (optional)</td><td>The schema (built in, introspectable)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>HTTP caching</td><td>Natural for GET</td><td>Harder: needs persisted queries or client caches</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Errors</td><td>HTTP status codes</td><td>Usually 200 with an <code>errors</code> array; partial data possible. The GraphQL-over-HTTP draft uses 4xx or 5xx when a request cannot run at all</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Versioning</td><td>/v1, /v2</td><td>Evolve one schema; deprecate fields</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Best for</td><td>Public APIs, simple resources, caching</td><td>UIs combining many related entities; many client types</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Schema (SDL)</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>type Query {
  order(id: ID!): Order
  searchOrders(q: String!, store: String, first: Int = 20, after: String): OrderConnection!
}

type Mutation {
  addToList(listId: ID!, orderId: ID!): SavedList!
}

type Subscription {
  orderAdded(store: String!): Order!
}

type Order {
  id: ID!
  orderRef: String!
  orderDate: Date          # custom scalar
  store: Store!
  items: [String!]!
  similar(first: Int = 10): [Order!]!
}

type Store { code: ID!  name: String!  state: String }

scalar Date</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note"><code>!</code> means non-null. <code>[String!]!</code> is a non-null list of non-null strings.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Queries, variables, fragments</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>query Search($q: String!, $store: String, $after: String) {
  searchOrders(q: $q, store: $store, first: 10, after: $after) {
    edges {
      node { ...OrderCard }
    }
    pageInfo { hasNextPage endCursor }
  }
}

fragment OrderCard on Order {
  id
  orderRef
  orderDate
  store { name }
}

# variables (sent separately as JSON):
# { "q": "umbrella", "store": "1_05" }</code></pre></div>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>curl -s https://api.example.com/graphql \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"query":"query($id:ID!){order(id:$id){orderRef store{name}}}","variables":{"id":"123"}}'</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">Always use variables, never string interpolation into the query: it enables caching of the parsed query and avoids injection.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Resolvers and the N+1 problem</h3>
    <p>Asking for 20 orders, each with its <code>store</code>, naively runs 1 query for the list and 20 more for stores: N+1 queries.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Batch loading (DataLoader pattern):</b> collect all the store IDs requested in one execution step, fetch them in one query, and hand each order its store.</li>
      <li><b>Per-request cache:</b> the same store requested twice in one query is loaded once.</li>
      <li><b>Spring:</b> <code>@BatchMapping</code>. <b>Quarkus:</b> a resolver taking <code>@Source List&lt;…&gt;</code>. <b>Python Strawberry:</b> <code>DataLoader</code>.</li>
      <li><b>Measure:</b> log query counts per GraphQL request in tests.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>N+1, drawn</h3>
    <figure class="fig"><svg viewBox="0 0 660 250" role="img" aria-labelledby="nplus1-t nplus1-d"><title id="nplus1-t">The N+1 problem and batch loading</title><desc id="nplus1-d">Two columns for one GraphQL query asking for 20 orders, each with its store. On the left, the naive resolvers: one query for the 20 orders, then one query per order for its store, 21 queries in all. On the right, batch loading: one query for the orders, then all the store IDs are collected and fetched in one query, 2 queries in all.</desc><defs><marker id="nplus1-a" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker><marker id="nplus1-aa" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar acc" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker></defs><text class="lb hd" x="165" y="22" text-anchor="middle">naive: 1 + 20 = 21 queries</text><text class="lb hd" x="495" y="22" text-anchor="middle">batch loading: 2 queries</text><rect class="nd in" x="60" y="36" width="210" height="34" rx="4"/><text class="lb" x="165" y="58" text-anchor="middle">SELECT 20 orders</text><rect class="nd warn" x="60" y="86" width="210" height="24" rx="4"/><text class="lb mu" x="165" y="103" text-anchor="middle">SELECT store WHERE id = …</text><rect class="nd warn" x="60" y="114" width="210" height="24" rx="4"/><text class="lb mu" x="165" y="131" text-anchor="middle">SELECT store WHERE id = …</text><rect class="nd warn" x="60" y="142" width="210" height="24" rx="4"/><text class="lb mu" x="165" y="159" text-anchor="middle">SELECT store WHERE id = …</text><rect class="nd warn" x="60" y="170" width="210" height="24" rx="4"/><text class="lb mu" x="165" y="187" text-anchor="middle">SELECT store WHERE id = …</text><rect class="nd" x="60" y="198" width="210" height="24" rx="4"/><text class="lb mu" x="165" y="215" text-anchor="middle">… 20 times</text><rect class="nd in" x="390" y="36" width="210" height="34" rx="4"/><text class="lb" x="495" y="58" text-anchor="middle">SELECT 20 orders</text><rect class="nd" x="390" y="100" width="210" height="34" rx="4"/><text class="lb" x="495" y="122" text-anchor="middle">collect the store IDs</text><rect class="nd out" x="390" y="164" width="210" height="34" rx="4"/><text class="lb" x="495" y="186" text-anchor="middle">SELECT stores WHERE id IN (…)</text><line class="ed" x1="495" y1="70" x2="495" y2="96" marker-end="url(#nplus1-a)"/><line class="ed" x1="495" y1="134" x2="495" y2="160" marker-end="url(#nplus1-a)"/><text class="lb acc" x="330" y="240" text-anchor="middle">log query counts per request in your tests to catch the left-hand shape</text></svg><figcaption>Twenty orders, two ways. The left grows with the list; the right stays at two queries however many orders you ask for.</figcaption></figure>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Spring for GraphQL</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>// schema files: src/main/resources/graphql/*.graphqls
@Controller
class OrderGraphQl {
  private final OrderService orders;
  private final StoreService stores;
  OrderGraphQl(OrderService o, StoreService s) { orders = o; stores = s; }

  @QueryMapping
  Order order(@Argument String id) {
    return orders.find(id).orElse(null);       // null → field is null, no error
  }

  @MutationMapping
  SavedList addToList(@Argument String listId, @Argument String orderId) {
    return orders.addToList(listId, orderId);   // check ownership inside
  }

  @BatchMapping                                    // one query for all stores
  Map&lt;Order, Store&gt; store(List&lt;Order&gt; list) {
    Map&lt;String, Store&gt; byCode = stores.byCodes(
        list.stream().map(Order::storeId).collect(Collectors.toSet()));
    return list.stream().collect(Collectors.toMap(o -&gt; o, o -&gt; byCode.get(o.storeId())));
  }
}

// scalar Date needs an implementation, here from graphql-java-extended-scalars
@Configuration
class GraphQlConfig {
  @Bean RuntimeWiringConfigurer scalars() { return w -&gt; w.scalar(ExtendedScalars.Date); }
}

// test
@GraphQlTest(OrderGraphQl.class)
class OrderGraphQlTest {
  @Autowired GraphQlTester tester;
  @MockitoBean OrderService orders;  @MockitoBean StoreService stores;
  @Test void returnsNullForUnknownId() {
    tester.document("{ order(id: \"x\") { orderRef } }")
          .execute().path("order").valueIsNull();
  }
}</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Quarkus (SmallRye GraphQL)</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>// quarkus ext add smallrye-graphql   → schema generated from code
@GraphQLApi
public class OrderApi {
  @Inject OrderService orders;
  @Inject StoreService stores;

  @Query
  public Order order(@NonNull String id) { return orders.find(id).orElse(null); }

  @Mutation
  public SavedList addToList(@NonNull String listId, @NonNull String orderId) {
    return orders.addToList(listId, orderId);
  }

  // batched field resolver: adds Order.store, one call for all orders
  public List&lt;Store&gt; store(@Source List&lt;Order&gt; list) {
    return stores.inOrderFor(list);
  }
}
// GraphQL UI in dev mode: http://localhost:8080/q/graphql-ui</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">Spring for GraphQL is schema-first (you write SDL); SmallRye GraphQL is code-first (the schema comes from your classes).</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Python (Strawberry)</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>import strawberry
from strawberry.dataloader import DataLoader

async def load_stores(codes: list[str]) -&gt; list["Store"]:
    rows = await db.stores_by_codes(codes)              # one query
    by = {r.code: r for r in rows}
    return [by.get(c) for c in codes]                   # same order as input

@strawberry.type
class Store:
    code: strawberry.ID
    name: str

@strawberry.type
class Order:
    id: strawberry.ID
    order_ref: str
    store_id: strawberry.Private[str]
    @strawberry.field
    async def store(self, info: strawberry.Info) -&gt; Store:
        return await info.context["store_loader"].load(self.store_id)

@strawberry.type
class Query:
    @strawberry.field
    async def order(self, id: strawberry.ID) -&gt; Order | None:
        return await db.order(id)

schema = strawberry.Schema(query=Query)
# context per request: {"store_loader": DataLoader(load_fn=load_stores)}</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Pagination: connections</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>type OrderConnection {
  edges: [OrderEdge!]!
  pageInfo: PageInfo!
  totalCount: Int            # optional; can be expensive
}
type OrderEdge { cursor: String!  node: Order! }
type PageInfo { hasNextPage: Boolean!  hasPreviousPage: Boolean!  startCursor: String  endCursor: String }</code></pre></div>
    <ul>
      <li>Cursor-based (the Relay connection convention): stable under inserts, works with keyset queries in the database.</li>
      <li>Cap <code>first</code> on the server (e.g. max 100).</li>
      <li>Make cursors opaque (base64 of the sort key and ID).</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Errors</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>{
  "data": { "order": null },
  "errors": [{
    "message": "Not authorised to view this order",
    "path": ["order"],
    "extensions": { "code": "FORBIDDEN" }
  }]
}</code></pre></div>
    <ul>
      <li>A response can hold partial data plus errors; clients must check <code>errors</code> even on HTTP 200.</li>
      <li>Put a stable machine-readable <code>extensions.code</code> on every error.</li>
      <li>Expected business outcomes (validation failed, not found) can also be modelled in the schema as result types (<code>union SaveResult = SavedList | ValidationError</code>).</li>
      <li>Never leak stack traces or SQL in <code>message</code>.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Security</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Authorise per field and per object</b> in resolvers or the service layer; one endpoint means URL-based rules cannot protect anything.</li>
      <li><b>Limit query depth and complexity</b> so a client cannot request <code>similar { similar { similar … } }</code> a thousand levels deep.</li>
      <li><b>Cap list sizes</b> and set timeouts per request.</li>
      <li><b>Persisted queries</b> (allow-list of known query hashes) for first-party clients: blocks arbitrary queries and enables GET caching.</li>
      <li><b>Introspection:</b> consider disabling it in production for private APIs; it is not a security boundary, just less help to attackers.</li>
      <li><b>Rate limit by cost,</b> not by request count, since one request can be cheap or enormous.</li>
      <li><b>Batching attacks:</b> limit the number of operations or aliases per request (aliases can repeat a login mutation hundreds of times).</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Performance and caching</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Batch loaders everywhere a field fetches by key.</li>
      <li>Select only needed columns: look at the requested fields (selection set) before querying.</li>
      <li>Client caches (Apollo Client, Relay, urql) normalise results by type and ID; give every object a stable <code>id</code>.</li>
      <li>HTTP caching needs GET with persisted queries; otherwise cache in resolvers or the data layer.</li>
      <li>Trace per resolver (OpenTelemetry) to find slow fields.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Schema evolution and scale</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Add freely:</b> new types, new fields, new optional arguments.</li>
      <li><b>Deprecate before removing:</b> <code>oldField: String @deprecated(reason: "Use newField")</code>, then watch usage and remove.</li>
      <li><b>Never change a field's type or meaning in place.</b></li>
      <li><b>Schema checks in CI</b> compare against the published schema and fail on breaking changes.</li>
      <li><b>Federation:</b> several teams own subgraphs (orders, users, billing) composed by a router into one supergraph (Apollo Federation, GraphQL Mesh).</li>
      <li><b>Behind a gateway:</b> Apigee or another gateway can still handle authentication and rate limits in front of <code>/graphql</code>.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Read a schema and write queries with variables and fragments</li><li>Explain queries, mutations, subscriptions and resolvers</li><li>Tell when GraphQL fits better than REST</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Build a server with Spring for GraphQL, SmallRye or Strawberry</li><li>Avoid N+1 with batch loaders; paginate with connections</li><li>Return useful errors and test resolvers</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Secure a GraphQL API: field authorisation, depth and cost limits, persisted queries</li><li>Evolve a schema without breaking clients; schema checks in CI</li><li>Tune performance with tracing and caching</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Design a federated graph across teams</li><li>Set GraphQL governance and standards for the organisation</li><li>Decide GraphQL vs REST vs gRPC strategy across products</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>GraphQL specification: execution and validation</li><li>DataLoader and batching</li><li>Relay connection specification</li><li>Apollo Federation and supergraphs</li><li>Persisted queries and query cost analysis</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://graphql.org/learn/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Learn GraphQL</a><span class="lnote"> Official introduction</span></li><li><a href="https://spec.graphql.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GraphQL specification</a><span class="lnote"> How queries are parsed, validated and executed</span></li><li><a href="https://docs.spring.io/spring-graphql/reference/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Spring for GraphQL</a><span class="lnote"> Annotated controllers, batching, testing</span></li><li><a href="https://quarkus.io/guides/smallrye-graphql" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Quarkus SmallRye GraphQL guide</a><span class="lnote"> Code-first GraphQL in Quarkus</span></li><li><a href="https://strawberry.rocks/docs" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Strawberry</a><span class="lnote"> Python GraphQL with type hints</span></li><li><a href="https://relay.dev/graphql/connections.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Relay connection spec</a><span class="lnote"> Cursor pagination convention</span></li><li><a href="https://www.apollographql.com/docs/federation/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Apollo Federation</a><span class="lnote"> Composing subgraphs into one graph</span></li><li><a href="https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/GraphQL_Cheat_Sheet.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OWASP GraphQL cheat sheet</a><span class="lnote"> Security checklist</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/graphql/graphql-spec/main/spec/Section%207%20--%20Response.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GraphQL spec: Response (errors, path, extensions)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/facebook/relay/main/website/spec/Connections.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Relay cursor connections spec</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/spring-projects/spring-graphql/main/spring-graphql-docs/modules/ROOT/pages/controllers.adoc" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Spring for GraphQL: annotated controllers, @BatchMapping</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/spring-projects/spring-graphql/main/spring-graphql-test/src/main/java/org/springframework/graphql/test/tester/GraphQlTester.java" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Spring for GraphQL: GraphQlTester source (path, valueIsNull)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/quarkusio/quarkus/main/docs/src/main/asciidoc/smallrye-graphql.adoc" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Quarkus SmallRye GraphQL guide (@Source batching, /q/graphql-ui)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/strawberry-graphql/strawberry/main/docs/guides/dataloaders.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Strawberry DataLoader guide</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/strawberry-graphql/strawberry/main/strawberry/__init__.py" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Strawberry public API (Info, Private, ID)</a>; <a href="https://docs.spring.io/spring-boot/reference/web/spring-graphql.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Spring Boot reference: Spring for GraphQL</a>; <a href="https://github.com/graphql/graphql-over-http/blob/main/spec/GraphQLOverHTTP.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GraphQL over HTTP (working draft)</a>.</p><p class="runcode">A question, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/enrichmeai.github.io/issues/new?title=%5BGraphQL%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>. Nothing here is tracked and there are no comments to store: questions live in the open, where the next learner can find the answer.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= REAL-TIME TO THE BROWSER ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-realtime" data-name="Real-time to the Browser">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Real time, near real time, and getting events to the browser</h2><p>A customer adds an item to the cart on their phone, and the badge on their laptop should change too. Between those two screens sit an API, a database, a Kafka topic, a service that holds browser connections, and a choice of transport: polling, server-sent events, WebSockets or GraphQL subscriptions. This page defines the terms, follows the cart event end to end, and compares every option by requirement. Kafka itself is on <a href="#kafka">Kafka</a>, and GraphQL on <a href="#graphql">GraphQL</a>.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Real time and near real time are different promises</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>What it means</th><th>Examples (<i>analysis</i>)</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td><b>Hard real time</b></td><td>Missing a deadline is a total system failure</td><td>Airbag control, industrial safety systems</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Firm real time</b></td><td>Infrequent misses are tolerable, but a late result is worth nothing</td><td>A video frame or a market price that arrives too late to use</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Soft real time</b></td><td>A late result is still useful, but worth less the later it is</td><td>Live dashboards, multiplayer game state</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Near real time</b></td><td>The delay that processing and network transmission add between an event and its use, such as for display</td><td>A cart badge, an order status, a stock count, a notification</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note"><i>Our view:</i> on the web, &ldquo;real time&rdquo; almost always means near real time, and the honest way to say it is a number: &ldquo;the badge updates within two seconds for 99% of changes&rdquo;. <i>The other view:</i> product teams use &ldquo;real time&rdquo; as shorthand for &ldquo;no refresh button&rdquo;, and insisting on the strict term can derail a useful conversation; agree the number, whatever word you use.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>The journey of one cart event</h3>
    <ol>
      <li><b>Change:</b> the phone sends &ldquo;add item&rdquo; (a REST call or a GraphQL mutation) to the cart service.</li>
      <li><b>Record:</b> the cart service saves the cart and an outbox row in one transaction, so the change and its event cannot disagree (see the transactional outbox on <a href="#architecture">Architecture</a>).</li>
      <li><b>Publish:</b> a relay publishes <code>CartItemAdded</code> to the <code>cart-events</code> topic, keyed by cart id: the key decides the partition, so every event for one cart stays in order (see <a href="#kafka">Kafka</a>).</li>
      <li><b>Bridge:</b> a push gateway (often the backend-for-frontend) consumes the topic and holds the open browser connections. Browsers cannot read Kafka directly (<i>analysis:</i> Kafka clients speak Kafka&rsquo;s own protocol over TCP, which browser APIs do not offer).</li>
      <li><b>Deliver:</b> the gateway sends the event to that customer&rsquo;s open connections, over SSE, a WebSocket or a GraphQL subscription.</li>
      <li><b>Show and catch up:</b> the laptop updates the badge. If it was offline, it catches up when it reconnects.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="note"><i>Analysis:</i> the device that made the change already knows from its own response. Push is for everyone else: other tabs and devices, and changes made elsewhere, such as a price change or an item going out of stock.</p>
      <figure class="fig"><svg viewBox="0 0 660 250" role="img" aria-labelledby="cart-t cart-d"><title id="cart-t">The journey of one cart event</title><desc id="cart-d">Six numbered steps. 1: the phone sends add item to the cart service. 2: the cart service saves the cart and an outbox row in one transaction. 3: a relay publishes CartItemAdded to the cart-events topic, keyed by cart ID. 4: a push gateway consumes the topic and holds the open browser connections. 5: it sends the event to that customer's connections over SSE, a WebSocket or a GraphQL subscription. 6: the laptop updates the cart badge, or catches up when it reconnects.</desc><defs><marker id="cart-a" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker><marker id="cart-aa" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar acc" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker></defs><rect class="nd in" x="20" y="40" width="180" height="54" rx="6"/><text class="lb hd" x="110" y="63" text-anchor="middle">phone</text><text class="lb mu" x="110" y="82" text-anchor="middle">add item</text><circle class="nd in" cx="30" cy="40" r="9"/><text class="lb mu" x="30" y="44" text-anchor="middle">1</text><line class="ed" x1="200" y1="67" x2="236" y2="67" marker-end="url(#cart-a)"/><rect class="nd" x="240" y="40" width="180" height="54" rx="6"/><text class="lb hd" x="330" y="63" text-anchor="middle">cart service</text><text class="lb mu" x="330" y="82" text-anchor="middle">cart + outbox, 1 tx</text><circle class="nd in" cx="250" cy="40" r="9"/><text class="lb mu" x="250" y="44" text-anchor="middle">2</text><line class="ed" x1="420" y1="67" x2="456" y2="67" marker-end="url(#cart-a)"/><rect class="nd" x="460" y="40" width="180" height="54" rx="6"/><text class="lb hd" x="550" y="63" text-anchor="middle">relay</text><text class="lb mu" x="550" y="82" text-anchor="middle">publish from outbox</text><circle class="nd in" cx="470" cy="40" r="9"/><text class="lb mu" x="470" y="44" text-anchor="middle">3</text><rect class="nd" x="460" y="160" width="180" height="54" rx="6"/><text class="lb hd" x="550" y="183" text-anchor="middle">cart-events</text><text class="lb mu" x="550" y="202" text-anchor="middle">key = cart ID</text><circle class="nd in" cx="470" cy="160" r="9"/><text class="lb mu" x="470" y="164" text-anchor="middle">4</text><line class="ed" x1="460" y1="187" x2="424" y2="187" marker-end="url(#cart-a)"/><rect class="nd" x="240" y="160" width="180" height="54" rx="6"/><text class="lb hd" x="330" y="183" text-anchor="middle">push gateway</text><text class="lb mu" x="330" y="202" text-anchor="middle">holds connections</text><circle class="nd in" cx="250" cy="160" r="9"/><text class="lb mu" x="250" y="164" text-anchor="middle">5</text><line class="ed acc" x1="240" y1="187" x2="204" y2="187" marker-end="url(#cart-aa)"/><rect class="nd out" x="20" y="160" width="180" height="54" rx="6"/><text class="lb hd" x="110" y="183" text-anchor="middle">laptop</text><text class="lb mu" x="110" y="202" text-anchor="middle">badge updates</text><circle class="nd in" cx="30" cy="160" r="9"/><text class="lb mu" x="30" y="164" text-anchor="middle">6</text><path class="ed" d="M550,94 L550,156" marker-end="url(#cart-a)"/><text class="lb acc" x="330" y="240" text-anchor="middle">step 5 travels over SSE, a WebSocket or a GraphQL subscription</text></svg><figcaption>The six steps above, drawn. Read along the top, then back along the bottom. Kafka sits in the middle; the browser never talks to it directly.</figcaption></figure>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>From Kafka to browsers: the gateway&rsquo;s three problems</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Fan-out.</b> In a Kafka consumer group, each partition is read by one consumer. If every gateway instance joins one group, an instance sees only some partitions, but the customer&rsquo;s connection may be on a different instance. Three ways out (<i>analysis</i>):
        <ol>
          <li>Each gateway instance uses its own consumer group, reads every event and forwards only those for customers connected to it: simple, but every instance reads the whole topic.</li>
          <li>Route each connection to the instance that owns the partition of the cart it watches (events are keyed by cart id): efficient, and hard to keep right as instances and partitions move.</li>
          <li>The gateway republishes to a fan-out layer built for connections, such as a pub/sub service or a managed one (below).</li>
        </ol>
      </li>
      <li><b>Missed events.</b> A connection drops, and events arrive while it is down. With SSE the browser reconnects by itself after a dropped connection and sends the last event id it saw in a <code>Last-Event-ID</code> header, so the gateway can resume. The GraphQL specification defines a subscription&rsquo;s result as a stream of events, but not replay, so a common pattern is to re-run the cart query on reconnect, then follow the stream (<i>analysis</i>).</li>
      <li><b>Who may listen.</b> Authenticate the connection, and let each customer subscribe only to their own carts. A long-lived connection outlives a short-lived token, so decide what happens when it expires.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note"><i>Analysis:</i> send the whole change (&ldquo;item X added, new total Y&rdquo;) or only a signal (&ldquo;cart changed, fetch it&rdquo;)? A signal keeps one source of truth and makes missed events harmless, at the cost of an extra request; the whole change saves the request and needs ordering and replay to be right.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Every option, side by side</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>How it works</th><th>Direction</th><th>From the sources</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td><b>Short polling</b></td><td>The browser asks every few seconds</td><td>Client asks</td><td>The delay is up to one interval, and most requests return nothing (<i>analysis</i>)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Long polling</b></td><td>The server holds each request open until it has something, then the client asks again</td><td>Client asks, server waits</td><td>Not a true push, but it emulates one where a real push is not possible; RFC 6202 lists its known issues</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Server-sent events (SSE)</b></td><td>One long HTTP response carrying a stream of events, read with <code>EventSource</code></td><td>Server to browser only</td><td>Reconnects by itself after a dropped connection and resends <code>Last-Event-ID</code>; without HTTP/2, browsers allow only 6 SSE connections per domain, across all tabs</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>WebSocket</b></td><td>An HTTP handshake upgrades to a framed, two-way connection over TCP</td><td>Both ways</td><td>RFC 6455; the browser API has no backpressure, so a fast sender can overwhelm a slow page; reconnect and replay are yours to build (<i>analysis</i>)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>GraphQL subscription</b></td><td>A subscription operation returns a stream of results; the transport is separate</td><td>Server to browser (over a two-way or one-way transport)</td><td>Common transports: graphql-ws (WebSocket subprotocol <code>graphql-transport-ws</code>) and graphql-sse (over SSE, with single-connection and distinct-connections modes)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>WebTransport</b></td><td>Streams and datagrams over HTTP/3</td><td>Both ways</td><td>Multiple and unidirectional streams, out-of-order delivery, and unreliable datagrams</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Web Push</b></td><td>A message to the browser&rsquo;s push service, handled by a service worker</td><td>Server to device</td><td>Needs an active service worker; reaches the user when the page is closed, so it is for notifications rather than keeping a page in sync</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Managed services</b></td><td>A cloud service holds the connections; your backend publishes to it</td><td>Varies</td><td>AWS AppSync subscriptions; API Gateway WebSocket APIs (routes to Lambda, HTTP or AWS services); Azure Web PubSub; ASP.NET Core SignalR, which falls back from WebSockets to SSE to long polling</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Choose by requirement</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>If this is true for you</th><th>It leans towards</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Updates only flow from server to browser (a cart badge, an order status)</td><td>SSE</td></tr>
      <tr><td>The browser also sends a steady stream of messages (chat, collaborative editing, a game)</td><td>WebSocket</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Your front end already talks GraphQL and you want one schema for queries, mutations and updates</td><td>GraphQL subscriptions, over graphql-sse or graphql-ws</td></tr>
      <tr><td>A delay of tens of seconds is acceptable, or traffic is small</td><td>Short polling: little to build or run (<i>analysis</i>)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Proxies or firewalls in front of your users break long-lived connections</td><td>Long polling, or a library that falls back to it</td></tr>
      <tr><td>You must reach the user when the page is closed</td><td>Web Push</td></tr>
      <tr><td>You need many independent streams, unreliable datagrams, or backpressure in the browser</td><td>WebTransport; check browser support first</td></tr>
      <tr><td>You do not want to run servers that hold many open connections</td><td>A managed service on your cloud</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Your users still reach you over HTTP/1.1 and open many tabs</td><td>Avoid one SSE connection per tab: share one connection between tabs, or use a WebSocket</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>SSE in the browser, and on the wire</h3>
<pre><code>// Browser: listen for cart events
const events = new EventSource('/carts/me/events');
events.addEventListener('cart', (e) =&gt; {
  const change = JSON.parse(e.data);
  updateBadge(change.itemCount);
});

// What the gateway writes on the response.
// Each event ends with a blank line.
id: 7f3a-42
event: cart
data: {"cartId":"c-9","itemCount":3}

</code></pre>
    <p class="note">A blank line ends each event, and the browser dispatches it then. After a dropped connection the browser reconnects and sends <code>Last-Event-ID: 7f3a-42</code>, and the gateway resumes after that event.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>The same with GraphQL</h3>
<pre><code>type Subscription {
  cartChanged(cartId: ID!): CartEvent!
}

subscription {
  cartChanged(cartId: "c-9") { itemCount total }
}</code></pre>
    <p class="note">The server resolves the subscription from the gateway&rsquo;s Kafka consumer. Choose the transport separately: graphql-ws or graphql-sse. See <a href="#graphql">GraphQL</a>.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Our view, and the other view</h3>
    <p><i>Our view:</i> for a cart, a second or two is near real time enough. SSE from a backend-for-frontend that consumes Kafka, sending a &ldquo;cart changed&rdquo; signal and re-fetching on reconnect, is little to build and run, and recovers from dropped connections on its own. Reach for WebSockets when the browser must send a stream too, and for GraphQL subscriptions when GraphQL is already your API.</p>
    <p><i>The other view:</i> a team that already runs GraphQL gains more from one schema and one client than SSE saves; a team with a managed WebSocket service on its cloud may find that cheaper to run than its own gateway; and at small scale, polling every few seconds is often the honest, boring answer that nobody has to be on call for.</p>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Tell hard, firm and soft real time from near real time</li><li>Open an EventSource or a WebSocket and handle its messages</li><li>Explain why a browser cannot read a Kafka topic directly</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Build a gateway that consumes Kafka and pushes to browsers</li><li>Resume after a reconnect with Last-Event-ID or a refetch</li><li>Authenticate and scope long-lived connections</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Choose polling, SSE, WebSockets, GraphQL subscriptions or a managed service by requirement</li><li>Design fan-out across gateway instances</li><li>Set and measure a delivery target, such as p99 within two seconds</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Set real-time standards across an organisation's products</li><li>Run connection-holding services at scale</li><li>Decide build or buy for real-time infrastructure</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>Real-time and near-real-time deadlines</li><li>Server-sent events and EventSource</li><li>WebSockets and backpressure</li><li>GraphQL subscriptions over WebSocket or SSE</li><li>Kafka consumer groups and fan-out</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Server-sent_events/Using_server-sent_events" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MDN: Using server-sent events</a><span class="lnote"> The event stream format and EventSource</span></li><li><a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/WebSockets_API" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MDN: The WebSocket API</a><span class="lnote"> Two-way connections in the browser</span></li><li><a href="https://github.com/enisdenjo/graphql-sse" target="_blank" rel="noopener">graphql-sse</a><span class="lnote"> GraphQL over server-sent events</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Real-time_computing" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Real-time computing (hard, firm, soft; near real-time)</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Push_technology" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Push technology (long polling)</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apache_Kafka" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Apache Kafka</a>; <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Server-sent_events/Using_server-sent_events" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MDN: Using server-sent events</a>; <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/EventSource" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MDN: EventSource</a>; <a href="https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/server-sent-events.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">WHATWG HTML: Server-sent events</a>; <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/WebSockets_API" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MDN: The WebSocket API</a>; <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6455" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RFC 6455: The WebSocket Protocol</a>; <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6202" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RFC 6202: Long polling and streaming</a>; <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/WebTransport_API" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MDN: WebTransport API</a>; <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Push_API" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MDN: Push API</a>; <a href="https://github.com/graphql/graphql-spec" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GraphQL specification: Execution</a>; <a href="https://github.com/enisdenjo/graphql-ws/blob/master/PROTOCOL.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">graphql-ws protocol</a>; <a href="https://github.com/enisdenjo/graphql-sse/blob/master/PROTOCOL.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">graphql-sse protocol</a>; <a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/appsync/latest/devguide/aws-appsync-real-time-data.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AWS AppSync: real-time data</a>; <a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/apigateway/latest/developerguide/apigateway-websocket-api.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">API Gateway WebSocket APIs</a>; <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-web-pubsub/overview" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Azure Web PubSub</a>; <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/signalr/introduction" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ASP.NET Core SignalR</a>.</p><p class="runcode">A question, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/enrichmeai.github.io/issues/new?title=%5BReal-time%20to%20the%20Browser%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>. Nothing here is tracked and there are no comments to store: questions live in the open, where the next learner can find the answer.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= REACTIVE AND BACKPRESSURE ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-reactive" data-name="Reactive &amp; Backpressure">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Reactive and backpressure</h2><p>What &ldquo;reactive&rdquo; means, why every pipeline with a fast producer and a slow consumer needs back pressure, and how it works in Reactive Streams, Java&rsquo;s <code>Flow</code>, RxJava, Reactor, Kotlin, Node.js, Kafka, TCP and HTTP/2. With runnable Java and a way to choose by requirement.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>The short answer</h3>
    <p><b>Reactive</b>, in the Spring team&rsquo;s words, &ldquo;refers to programming models that are built around reacting to change&rdquo;: network components reacting to I/O events, UI controllers reacting to mouse clicks. Instead of a thread waiting for an answer, code is called when the answer arrives.</p>
    <p><b>Back pressure</b> is borrowed from plumbing, where it is the resistance that pushes back against flow in a pipe. In software it is a way to regulate the flow of data so that components are not overwhelmed: the slow part of a system tells the fast part to ease off.</p>
    <p>The two go together for one reason, which the Spring documentation puts plainly: &ldquo;In synchronous, imperative code, blocking calls serve as a natural form of back pressure that forces the caller to wait. In non-blocking code, it becomes important to control the rate of events so that a fast producer does not overwhelm its destination.&rdquo; Take away the waiting, and you must put the brake back on deliberately.</p>
    <p class="note">This page goes deep on one idea. The history of how Java got here is on <a href="#javathreads">How Java Threads Evolved</a> and <a href="#ionio">IO, NIO &amp; Web Servers</a>; Spring&rsquo;s choice between blocking and reactive is on <a href="#spring">Spring Boot</a>.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>The problem in one picture</h3>
    <figure class="fig"><svg viewBox="0 0 640 210" role="img" aria-labelledby="bp-t bp-d"><title id="bp-t">Back pressure: the consumer tells the producer how much it can take</title><desc id="bp-d">A producer sends items through a bounded buffer to a slower consumer. The consumer sends a request for n more items back to the producer. If the producer cannot slow down, it has three choices: buffer, drop or fail.</desc>
      <rect class="nd" x="20" y="40" width="150" height="56" rx="6"/><text class="lb hd" x="95" y="64" text-anchor="middle">Producer</text><text class="lb mu" x="95" y="84" text-anchor="middle">fast: 1,000 a second</text>
      <rect class="nd in" x="245" y="40" width="150" height="56" rx="6"/><text class="lb hd" x="320" y="64" text-anchor="middle">Bounded buffer</text><text class="lb mu" x="320" y="84" text-anchor="middle">room for n items</text>
      <rect class="nd" x="470" y="40" width="150" height="56" rx="6"/><text class="lb hd" x="545" y="64" text-anchor="middle">Consumer</text><text class="lb mu" x="545" y="84" text-anchor="middle">slow: 100 a second</text>
      <line class="ed" x1="170" y1="62" x2="238" y2="62"/><polygon class="ar" points="245,62 236,57 236,67"/>
      <line class="ed" x1="395" y1="62" x2="463" y2="62"/><polygon class="ar" points="470,62 461,57 461,67"/>
      <text class="lb mu" x="207" y="54" text-anchor="middle">items</text><text class="lb mu" x="432" y="54" text-anchor="middle">items</text>
      <path class="ed acc dash" d="M545 96 V130 H95 V103"/><polygon class="ar acc" points="95,96 90,105 100,105"/>
      <text class="lb acc" x="320" y="124" text-anchor="middle">request(n): &#8220;I can take n more&#8221;</text>
      <text class="lb hd" x="20" y="166">If the producer cannot slow down, it must choose:</text>
      <text class="lb" x="20" y="190">buffer (memory grows) &#183; drop (data is lost) &#183; fail (the error is visible)</text>
    </svg><figcaption>Back pressure is the arrow going backwards. Without it, the buffer in the middle has to absorb the difference, and an unbounded buffer is only a slower way to run out of memory.</figcaption></figure>
    <p>Every pipeline has a slowest step: a database that takes 10 ms per insert, a partner API with a rate limit, a phone on a weak signal. Whatever arrives faster than that step can handle has to go somewhere. Spring&rsquo;s documentation answers the obvious question, &ldquo;what if a publisher cannot slow down?&rdquo;, in one line: &ldquo;it has to decide whether to buffer, drop, or fail.&rdquo; Back pressure is the fourth option, and the best one when the source allows it: ask the producer to slow down.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Reactive Streams: the contract</h3>
    <p>Reactive Streams began in late 2013 as an initiative between engineers at Netflix, Pivotal and Lightbend, with contributions from Red Hat, Oracle, Twitter and others. Its aim is to govern &ldquo;the exchange of stream data across an asynchronous boundary&rdquo; (to another thread or pool) while making sure the receiving side is not forced to buffer arbitrary amounts of data. Version 1.0 for the JVM was released in April 2015 with the Java API, a written specification, a test kit (TCK) and examples, so that libraries from different teams can be plugged together.</p>
    <div class="tbl"><table><thead><tr><th>Interface</th><th>Methods</th><th>Its job</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td><code>Publisher&lt;T&gt;</code></td><td><code>subscribe(Subscriber)</code></td><td>Produces items, but only as many as have been asked for</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>Subscriber&lt;T&gt;</code></td><td><code>onSubscribe</code>, <code>onNext</code>, <code>onError</code>, <code>onComplete</code></td><td>Receives the subscription, then the items, then one ending signal</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>Subscription</code></td><td><code>request(long n)</code>, <code>cancel()</code></td><td>The line back to the producer: &ldquo;send me n more&rdquo;, or &ldquo;stop&rdquo;</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>Processor&lt;T,R&gt;</code></td><td>both of the above</td><td>A processing stage that is a subscriber on one side and a publisher on the other</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
    <p><b>Four rules worth knowing by heart</b> (numbered as in the specification):</p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>1.1</b>: the total number of <code>onNext</code> signals a publisher sends &ldquo;MUST be less than or equal to the total number of elements requested&rdquo;. This one rule <i>is</i> back pressure.</li>
      <li><b>2.1</b>: a subscriber &ldquo;MUST signal demand via <code>Subscription.request(long n)</code> to receive <code>onNext</code> signals&rdquo;. Nothing flows until someone asks.</li>
      <li><b>3.9</b>: asking for zero or a negative number is an error, signalled with <code>IllegalArgumentException</code>.</li>
      <li><b>3.17</b>: a demand of <code>Long.MAX_VALUE</code> may be treated as &ldquo;effectively unbounded&rdquo;. Requesting it is how a subscriber says &ldquo;send everything&rdquo;, and so turns back pressure off.</li>
    </ul>
    <p><b>Into the JDK.</b> Doug Lea, who leads JSR 166 (the <code>java.util.concurrent</code> work), proposed the interfaces for Java 9, and they arrived through JEP 266 as <code>java.util.concurrent.Flow</code>: the same four interfaces nested in one class, plus <code>SubmissionPublisher</code>, a ready-made publisher. The specification&rsquo;s own README sums up the design: &ldquo;Since back-pressure is mandatory the use of unbounded buffers can be avoided.&rdquo;</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Run it: a slow subscriber slows the producer</h3>
    <p>This uses only the JDK (Java 21 or later; save it as <code>SlowSubscriber.java</code> and run <code>java SlowSubscriber.java</code>). The subscriber asks for one item at a time and takes 100 ms over each. The publisher has room for four unread items. <code>SubmissionPublisher.submit</code> &ldquo;blocks until resources are available&rdquo;, so once the buffer is full, the producing loop is held to the consumer&rsquo;s pace.</p>
    <div class="snip"><pre><code>import java.util.concurrent.Flow;
import java.util.concurrent.ForkJoinPool;
import java.util.concurrent.SubmissionPublisher;

public class SlowSubscriber {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        // a publisher with room for 4 unread items per subscriber
        try (var publisher = new SubmissionPublisher&lt;Integer&gt;(ForkJoinPool.commonPool(), 4)) {
            publisher.subscribe(new Flow.Subscriber&lt;&gt;() {
                Flow.Subscription subscription;
                public void onSubscribe(Flow.Subscription s) { subscription = s; s.request(1); } // ask for one
                public void onNext(Integer item) {
                    sleep(100);                                  // a slow consumer
                    System.out.println("consumed " + item);
                    subscription.request(1);                     // ready for the next one
                }
                public void onError(Throwable t) { t.printStackTrace(); }
                public void onComplete() { System.out.println("done"); }
            });
            for (int i = 1; i &lt;= 10; i++) {
                long start = System.nanoTime();
                publisher.submit(i);                             // blocks while the buffer is full
                long waited = (System.nanoTime() - start) / 1_000_000;
                System.out.println("produced " + i + (waited &gt; 10 ? " after waiting " + waited + " ms" : ""));
            }
        }
        Thread.sleep(1500);
    }
    static void sleep(long ms) { try { Thread.sleep(ms); } catch (InterruptedException e) { Thread.currentThread().interrupt(); } }
}</code></pre></div>
    <p>What it printed when we ran it:</p>
    <div class="snip"><pre><code>produced 1
produced 2
produced 3
produced 4
produced 5
consumed 1
produced 6 after waiting 96 ms
consumed 2
produced 7 after waiting 97 ms
...</code></pre></div>
    <p>Five items go straight in (one being consumed, four in the buffer). From then on, every <code>submit</code> waits about 100 ms: the consumer&rsquo;s speed has travelled back up to the producer. Change <code>s.request(1)</code> to <code>s.request(Long.MAX_VALUE)</code> and the producer is no longer paced by requests; it is held only by the buffer. The other publishing method, <code>offer</code>, &ldquo;may drop items (either immediately or with bounded timeout)&rdquo; instead of blocking, with a handler to decide what to do.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>When the producer cannot slow down</h3>
    <p>Some sources ignore requests: mouse movements, a sensor, a market data feed, a timer. For those, every library offers the same four choices under different names.</p>
    <div class="tbl"><table><thead><tr><th>Choice</th><th>Reactor</th><th>RxJava <code>Flowable</code></th><th>JDK</th><th>Kotlin Flow</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td><b>Buffer</b></td><td><code>onBackpressureBuffer</code>, bounded or unbounded, with an overflow strategy</td><td><code>onBackpressureBuffer</code>; <code>BackpressureStrategy.BUFFER</code></td><td><code>SubmissionPublisher</code>&rsquo;s per-subscriber buffer; a queue in a <code>ThreadPoolExecutor</code></td><td><code>buffer(n)</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Drop new items</b></td><td><code>onBackpressureDrop</code></td><td><code>onBackpressureDrop</code>; <code>DROP</code></td><td><code>offer</code>; <code>DiscardPolicy</code></td><td><code>buffer(onBufferOverflow = DROP_LATEST)</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Keep only the latest</b></td><td><code>onBackpressureLatest</code></td><td><code>onBackpressureLatest</code>; <code>LATEST</code></td><td><code>DiscardOldestPolicy</code> (drops the oldest queued task)</td><td><code>conflate()</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Fail</b></td><td><code>onBackpressureError</code> (an <code>IllegalStateException</code>)</td><td><code>MissingBackpressureException</code>; <code>ERROR</code></td><td><code>AbortPolicy</code>, the default (<code>RejectedExecutionException</code>)</td><td>none built in; the emitter suspends instead</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Slow the caller</b></td><td><code>request(n)</code> from the subscriber</td><td><code>request(n)</code></td><td><code>submit</code> blocks; <code>CallerRunsPolicy</code></td><td>the default: <code>emit</code> suspends</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
    <p><b>Choose by requirement</b>, not by which operator you have seen before:</p>
    <div class="tbl"><table><thead><tr><th>If this is true for you</th><th>Lean towards</th><th>Because</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td>Every item matters (payments, orders, audit events) and the source can wait</td><td>Slow the caller: request-based demand, a blocking <code>submit</code>, or <code>CallerRunsPolicy</code></td><td>Nothing is lost; the cost is that the producer runs at the consumer&rsquo;s speed</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Every item matters but bursts are short</td><td>A <b>bounded</b> buffer sized for the burst, failing loudly when it overflows</td><td>Absorbs the spike; the overflow error tells you the burst was bigger than planned</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Only the current value matters (a price, a position, a progress bar)</td><td>Keep only the latest (<code>onBackpressureLatest</code>, <code>conflate</code>)</td><td>Old values are worthless once a newer one exists</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Losing some items is acceptable (sampled metrics, telemetry)</td><td>Drop</td><td>Protects the consumer at a known, accepted cost</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Overload must be seen and handled upstream (a gateway, a queue in front of a fragile system)</td><td>Fail fast: reject with an error the caller can retry later</td><td>A clear &ldquo;not now&rdquo; beats a slow timeout; see load shedding on <a href="#sre">SRE</a></td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Push, pull, and the hybrid in between</h3>
    <p>An <code>Iterator</code> is <b>pull</b>: the consumer asks for the next item when it is ready, so it can never be overwhelmed, but it waits whenever the item is not ready yet. A callback or event listener is <b>push</b>: items arrive as soon as they exist, which is fast, but the consumer has no say in the rate.</p>
    <p>Reactive Streams is both. Reactor&rsquo;s reference guide calls it &ldquo;a push-pull hybrid&rdquo;: the subscriber pulls by requesting n items, and the publisher pushes each one as soon as it exists, up to that limit. Libraries also <b>prefetch</b>, requesting a batch ahead of need to avoid a round trip per item, and operators can change the request on the way up (a <code>buffer(10)</code> that is asked for one batch may ask upstream for ten items).</p>
    <p><b>Kafka</b> sits firmly at the pull end. A consumer calls <code>poll()</code> when it is ready, <code>max.poll.records</code> limits how many records one call returns, and the consumer API&rsquo;s &ldquo;Consumption Flow Control&rdquo; section offers <code>pause()</code> and <code>resume()</code> per partition, for example to let a lagging stream catch up before reading further from one that is ahead. KafkaA slow consumer simply falls behind (its lag grows) without harming the broker.rsquo;s design notes give the reason: in a push system A slow consumer simply falls behind (its lag grows) without harming the broker.ldquo;the consumer tends to be overwhelmed when its rate of consumption falls below the rate of productionA slow consumer simply falls behind (its lag grows) without harming the broker.rdquo;, while with pull A slow consumer simply falls behind (its lag grows) without harming the broker.ldquo;the consumer simply falls behind and catches up when it can.A slow consumer simply falls behind (its lag grows) without harming the broker.rdquo; More on <a href="#kafka">Kafka</a>.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Back pressure across the network</h3>
    <p>Library back pressure stops at the edge of the process. Between machines, the protocols have their own, and they are older than any reactive library.</p>
    <div class="tbl"><table><thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>How the receiver says &ldquo;slow down&rdquo;</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td><b>TCP</b></td><td>Every segment carries a window: &ldquo;the range of sequence numbers the sender of the window (the data receiver) is currently prepared to accept&rdquo;, related to its free buffer space (RFC 9293). When the receiving application stops reading, the window shrinks towards zero and the sender stops.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>HTTP/2</b></td><td>Credit-based flow control with <code>WINDOW_UPDATE</code> frames, per stream and per connection. The initial window is 65,535 octets, and &ldquo;a sender MUST respect flow-control limits imposed by a receiver&rdquo; (RFC 9113). It works hop by hop, not end to end, so a proxy has its own windows on each side.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>gRPC (Java)</b></td><td>Streams can switch to manual flow control: <code>disableAutoRequest()</code>, then <code>request(1)</code> when ready, and check <code>isReady()</code> before sending (the grpc-java <code>manualflowcontrol</code> example).</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>RSocket</b></td><td>Carries Reactive Streams across the network: a <code>REQUEST_N</code> frame means &ldquo;Request N more items with Reactive Streams semantics&rdquo;.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Spring WebFlux</b></td><td>Reads and writes HTTP bodies &ldquo;through non-blocking I/O with Reactive Streams back pressure&rdquo;, on servers such as Reactor Netty.</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
    <p><i>Analysis:</i> this is why a slow mobile client does not need special code in a WebFlux endpoint. When the client reads slowly, TCP&rsquo;s window fills, the server cannot write, and demand stops flowing up the reactive chain to the database query. It is also why a WebSocket in the browser is harder: its API has no back pressure (see <a href="#realtime">Real-time to the Browser</a>).</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>The same idea in other languages</h3>
    <p><b>Node.js streams.</b> <code>write()</code> returns <code>false</code> when the data buffer has exceeded <code>highWaterMark</code> (commonly 16 kB by default) or the write queue is busy, and a <code>'drain'</code> event fires once it has emptied. The Node.js guide&rsquo;s rule: &ldquo;Never call <code>.write()</code> after it returns false but wait for &lsquo;drain&rsquo; instead.&rdquo; <code>pipe()</code> does this for you.</p>
    <div class="snip"><pre><code>// Node.js: respect write()'s answer, or memory grows without limit
function copy(source, destination) {
  source.on('data', (chunk) =&gt; {
    if (!destination.write(chunk)) {         // false: the buffer is above highWaterMark
      source.pause();                        // stop reading...
      destination.once('drain', () =&gt; source.resume());  // ...until it has emptied
    }
  });
  source.on('end', () =&gt; destination.end());
}
// in practice, source.pipe(destination) or stream.pipeline() does this for you</code></pre></div>
    <p><b>Kotlin Flow.</b> Flows are sequential by default: the producer&rsquo;s <code>emit</code> suspends until the collector has handled the value, which is back pressure with no protocol at all. <code>buffer()</code> lets the producer run ahead in a separate coroutine (by default it suspends again when the buffer overflows), and <code>conflate()</code> means the emitter &ldquo;is never suspended due to a slow collector&rdquo;, which skips to the newest value.</p>
    <div class="snip"><pre><code>// Kotlin Flow: emit() suspends while the collector is busy, so a flow is paced by default
prices()
    .conflate()                 // a slow collector skips to the most recent value
    .collect { show(it) }

events()
    .buffer(64)                 // up to 64 waiting; then emit() suspends again
    .collect { save(it) }</code></pre></div>
    <p><b>RxJava.</b> Version 2 onwards split its types: <code>Flowable</code> supports Reactive Streams and back pressure; <code>Observable</code> has none and is meant for short sequences and GUI events. <b>Reactor</b>, the library under Spring WebFlux, implements Reactive Streams in all its operators, with <code>Mono</code> (0 or 1 item) and <code>Flux</code> (0 to N).</p>
    <div class="snip"><pre><code>// Reactor (Spring WebFlux): name what happens when the consumer falls behind
Flux&lt;Reading&gt; readings = sensor.readings()        // a source that cannot slow down
    .onBackpressureLatest()                       // keep only the newest reading
    .publishOn(Schedulers.parallel());

// a blocking call, kept off the event-loop threads
Mono&lt;Customer&gt; customer = Mono.fromCallable(() -&gt; legacyClient.find(id))
    .subscribeOn(Schedulers.boundedElastic());</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Blocking code has back pressure too</h3>
    <p>A blocked thread is the oldest flow control there is: the caller cannot send the next request until the last one returns. The trouble used to be cost: one platform thread per waiting call. Virtual threads (final in Java 21, JEP 444) make waiting cheap again, and JEP 444 says how to limit concurrency once threads are no longer the limit: &ldquo;do not be tempted to pool virtual threads in order to limit concurrency. Instead use constructs specifically designed for that purpose, such as semaphores.&rdquo;</p>
    <div class="snip"><pre><code>import java.util.concurrent.Executors;
import java.util.concurrent.Semaphore;
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicInteger;

public class Limit {
    static final Semaphore permits = new Semaphore(20);   // the service can take 20 calls at once
    static final AtomicInteger inFlight = new AtomicInteger(), peak = new AtomicInteger();

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try (var executor = Executors.newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor()) {
            for (int i = 0; i &lt; 1_000; i++) executor.submit(Limit::callService);
        }                                                  // waits for all 1,000 tasks
        System.out.println("peak concurrent calls: " + peak.get());
    }

    static void callService() {
        try {
            permits.acquire();                             // wait here when 20 are already running
            try {
                peak.accumulateAndGet(inFlight.incrementAndGet(), Math::max);
                Thread.sleep(50);                          // stands in for the remote call
                inFlight.decrementAndGet();
            } finally {
                permits.release();
            }
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
        }
    }
}</code></pre></div>
    <p>When we ran it, it printed <code>peak concurrent calls: 20</code> and took about 3 seconds: 1,000 calls of 50 ms, 20 at a time, is 2.5 seconds of waiting plus start-up.</p>
    <p><b>How big should the limit be?</b> <b>Little&rsquo;s law</b>, proved by John Little in 1961, says the average number of items in a stable system equals the arrival rate times the average time each spends inside: <code>L = &lambda;W</code>. With 20 permits and 50 ms per call, the most this code can sustain is 20 &divide; 0.05 = 400 calls a second. If 600 a second arrive, a queue grows without end, whatever the threading model. Back pressure, a bigger limit or shedding load are the only ways out.</p>
    <p><b>The thread pool version.</b> A <code>ThreadPoolExecutor</code> with a bounded queue is back pressure for tasks. Its <code>CallerRunsPolicy</code> makes the submitting thread run a rejected task itself, which the JDK documentation calls &ldquo;a simple feedback control mechanism that will slow down the rate that new tasks are submitted&rdquo;. With an unbounded <code>LinkedBlockingQueue</code>, nothing pushes back at all.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Mistakes that defeat back pressure</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table><thead><tr><th>Mistake</th><th>What happens</th><th>Instead</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td>An unbounded buffer or queue &ldquo;just in case&rdquo;</td><td>The difference in speed piles up in memory until the process dies, often long after the cause</td><td>Bound every queue and decide what happens when it is full</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Requesting <code>Long.MAX_VALUE</code> everywhere</td><td>Rule 3.17 lets the publisher treat it as unbounded: back pressure is switched off</td><td>Request in batches, or let the library&rsquo;s operators manage demand</td></tr>
      <tr><td>A blocking call on an event-loop thread</td><td>Every request sharing that thread stalls</td><td>Wrap it: Reactor&rsquo;s FAQ uses <code>Mono.fromCallable(...).subscribeOn(Schedulers.boundedElastic())</code>, which also caps how many threads blocking work can take</td></tr>
      <tr><td>An RxJava <code>Observable</code> for a large or fast stream</td><td>Nothing pushes back; RxJava&rsquo;s wiki notes that <code>MissingBackpressureException</code> usually surfaces at <code>observeOn</code>, while the cause is a source that ignores requests, such as <code>interval()</code></td><td>Use <code>Flowable</code> with an explicit strategy</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Treating retries as free</td><td>A slow dependency gets more traffic just when it needs less (see the retry storm on <a href="#distsys">Distributed Systems</a>)</td><td>Retry with backoff and a budget, behind a circuit breaker</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Our view, and the other view</h3>
    <p><i>Our view:</i> learn back pressure as a property of systems, not of one library. Ask of every queue, buffer and stream in a design: what happens when the consumer is ten times slower than the producer? If the answer is &ldquo;memory grows&rdquo;, the design is not finished. For most request-and-response services, plain blocking code on virtual threads with a semaphore and bounded queues gives you that answer with the least new code.</p>
    <p><i>The other view:</i> reactive libraries make back pressure part of the type, end to end, from the database driver (R2DBC) to the HTTP response, and they compose streams, timeouts and retries in one model. For streaming, server-sent events and many long-lived connections, teams that have learned the model may find it clearer than hand-placed semaphores.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Choose by requirement</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table><thead><tr><th>If this is true for you</th><th>Lean towards</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td>Request and response, mostly waiting on a database or other services</td><td>Blocking code on virtual threads, with a semaphore per dependency</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Streaming results, server-sent events, very many idle connections</td><td>Reactive (WebFlux, Reactor, Mutiny) or Kotlin Flow</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Messages between services, consumers at their own pace</td><td>A log or queue with pull-based consumers (<a href="#kafka">Kafka</a>)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Your team already runs a reactive stack in production</td><td>Stay on it: switching has a cost, and its back pressure is already end to end</td></tr>
      <tr><td>A library that others will plug into</td><td>Expose Reactive Streams or <code>Flow</code> types, so any compliant library can consume it</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/reactive-streams/reactive-streams-jvm/master/README.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Reactive Streams specification for the JVM (README)</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reactive_Streams" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Reactive Streams</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Back_pressure" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Back pressure</a>; <a href="https://openjdk.org/jeps/266" target="_blank" rel="noopener">JEP 266: More Concurrency Updates</a>; <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/21/docs/api/java.base/java/util/concurrent/Flow.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Java SE 21: Flow</a>; <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/21/docs/api/java.base/java/util/concurrent/SubmissionPublisher.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Java SE 21: SubmissionPublisher</a>; <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/21/docs/api/java.base/java/util/concurrent/ThreadPoolExecutor.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Java SE 21: ThreadPoolExecutor</a>; <a href="https://openjdk.org/jeps/444" target="_blank" rel="noopener">JEP 444: Virtual Threads</a>; <a href="https://docs.spring.io/spring-framework/reference/web/webflux/new-framework.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Spring Framework: WebFlux overview</a>; <a href="https://docs.spring.io/spring-framework/reference/web/webflux/reactive-spring.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Spring Framework: reactive core</a>; <a href="https://github.com/reactor/reactor-core/tree/main/docs/modules/ROOT/pages" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Reactor reference guide (source)</a>; <a href="https://github.com/ReactiveX/RxJava" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RxJava README</a>; <a href="https://github.com/ReactiveX/RxJava/wiki/Backpressure-(2.0)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RxJava wiki: Backpressure (2.0)</a>; <a href="https://github.com/Kotlin/kotlinx.coroutines/blob/master/kotlinx-coroutines-core/common/src/flow/operators/Context.kt" target="_blank" rel="noopener">kotlinx.coroutines: buffer and conflate</a>; <a href="https://nodejs.org/en/learn/modules/backpressuring-in-streams" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Node.js: Backpressuring in Streams</a>; <a href="https://github.com/apache/kafka/blob/trunk/clients/src/main/java/org/apache/kafka/clients/consumer/KafkaConsumer.java" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kafka: KafkaConsumer (Consumption Flow Control)</a>; <a href="https://github.com/apache/kafka/blob/trunk/docs/design/design.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kafka design: push versus pull</a>; <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9293.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RFC 9293: TCP</a>; <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9113.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RFC 9113: HTTP/2</a>; <a href="https://github.com/grpc/grpc-java/tree/master/examples/src/main/java/io/grpc/examples/manualflowcontrol" target="_blank" rel="noopener">grpc-java: manual flow control example</a>; <a href="https://github.com/rsocket/rsocket/blob/master/Protocol.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RSocket protocol</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Little%27s_law" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Little&rsquo;s law</a>. The two Java programs were run on OpenJDK 21.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= KAFKA ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-kafka" data-name="Kafka">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Apache Kafka</h2><p>A distributed, durable, replayable log for events. The core model, the settings that decide durability and ordering, clients in Java, Spring, Quarkus and Python, and the ecosystem around it.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>The model</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Concept</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Topic</td><td>A named stream of records, e.g. <code>order.placed</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Partition</td><td>An ordered, append-only log; a topic has many. Ordering is guaranteed only within a partition.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Record</td><td>Key, value, headers, timestamp. The <b>key</b> decides the partition (same key → same partition → ordered).</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Offset</td><td>A record's position in its partition. Consumers track how far they have read.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Broker</td><td>A server storing partitions. A cluster has several.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Replication factor</td><td>Copies of each partition on different brokers (3 is typical).</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Leader / ISR</td><td>One replica, the leader, takes all writes (and reads, unless consumers are set to fetch from a follower); in-sync replicas are caught-up followers.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Consumer group</td><td>Consumers sharing the work: each partition is read by one consumer in the group. Max parallelism = partition count.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Retention</td><td>Records kept for a time or size, then deleted, regardless of whether they were read.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Compaction</td><td>Keep only the latest record per key; a table-like topic.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>KRaft</td><td>Kafka's built-in metadata quorum, replacing ZooKeeper (removed in Kafka 4.0).</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note">Unlike a queue, reading does not remove a record. Many groups can read the same topic independently, and a group can rewind to replay.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Keys, partitions and groups, drawn</h3>
    <figure class="fig"><svg viewBox="0 0 660 300" role="img" aria-labelledby="kafka-t kafka-d"><title id="kafka-t">A topic, its partitions and two consumer groups</title><desc id="kafka-d">A producer sends records keyed by order ID to the topic order.placed, which has three partitions. Records with the same key always go to the same partition, so they stay in order. Consumer group billing has two consumers: one reads partitions 0 and 1, the other reads partition 2. Consumer group analytics has one consumer reading all three. Each group keeps its own offsets, so both read every record and reading removes nothing.</desc><defs><marker id="kafka-a" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker><marker id="kafka-aa" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar acc" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker></defs><rect class="nd in" x="10" y="120" width="110" height="50" rx="6"/><text class="lb hd" x="65" y="141" text-anchor="middle">producer</text><text class="lb mu" x="65" y="159" text-anchor="middle">key = order ID</text><rect class="bd" x="170" y="40" width="200" height="230" rx="8"/><text class="lb hd" x="270" y="30" text-anchor="middle">topic order.placed</text><rect class="nd" x="185" y="60" width="170" height="50" rx="6"/><text class="lb hd" x="270" y="82" text-anchor="middle">partition 0</text><text class="lb mu" x="270" y="100" text-anchor="middle">append-only, ordered</text><line class="ed" x1="120" y1="145" x2="181" y2="85" marker-end="url(#kafka-a)"/><rect class="nd" x="185" y="130" width="170" height="50" rx="6"/><text class="lb hd" x="270" y="152" text-anchor="middle">partition 1</text><text class="lb mu" x="270" y="170" text-anchor="middle">append-only, ordered</text><line class="ed" x1="120" y1="145" x2="181" y2="155" marker-end="url(#kafka-a)"/><rect class="nd" x="185" y="200" width="170" height="50" rx="6"/><text class="lb hd" x="270" y="222" text-anchor="middle">partition 2</text><text class="lb mu" x="270" y="240" text-anchor="middle">append-only, ordered</text><line class="ed" x1="120" y1="145" x2="181" y2="225" marker-end="url(#kafka-a)"/><rect class="bd" x="430" y="40" width="220" height="120" rx="8"/><text class="lb hd" x="540" y="30" text-anchor="middle">group billing</text><rect class="nd out" x="445" y="55" width="190" height="40" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="540" y="80" text-anchor="middle">consumer 1: p0, p1</text><rect class="nd out" x="445" y="105" width="190" height="40" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="540" y="130" text-anchor="middle">consumer 2: p2</text><rect class="bd" x="430" y="195" width="220" height="75" rx="8"/><text class="lb hd" x="540" y="188" text-anchor="middle">group analytics</text><rect class="nd out" x="445" y="210" width="190" height="40" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="540" y="235" text-anchor="middle">consumer: p0, p1, p2</text><line class="ed" x1="355" y1="85" x2="441" y2="75" marker-end="url(#kafka-a)"/><line class="ed" x1="355" y1="155" x2="441" y2="80" marker-end="url(#kafka-a)"/><line class="ed" x1="355" y1="225" x2="441" y2="125" marker-end="url(#kafka-a)"/><line class="ed dash" x1="355" y1="90" x2="441" y2="225" marker-end="url(#kafka-a)"/><line class="ed dash" x1="355" y1="160" x2="441" y2="228" marker-end="url(#kafka-a)"/><line class="ed dash" x1="355" y1="230" x2="441" y2="232" marker-end="url(#kafka-a)"/></svg><figcaption>Same key, same partition, so one order&rsquo;s events stay in order. Inside a group each partition has one reader, so parallelism stops at the partition count. A second group reads everything again with its own offsets.</figcaption></figure>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Durability and delivery settings</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Producer <code>acks=all</code></b> with topic <code>min.insync.replicas=2</code> and replication factor 3: a write survives one broker failure.</li>
      <li><b><code>enable.idempotence=true</code></b> (the default in modern clients): retries do not create duplicates within a partition.</li>
      <li><b>At-least-once consuming:</b> process, then commit the offset. A crash between the two replays the record, so consumers must be idempotent.</li>
      <li><b>At-most-once:</b> commit before processing. Rarely what you want.</li>
      <li><b>Exactly-once</b> inside Kafka (read-process-write between topics) with transactions and <code>isolation.level=read_committed</code>. Side effects outside Kafka still need idempotency.</li>
      <li><b>Ordering:</b> choose the key for the entity whose events must stay in order (order ID, account ID).</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>CLI essentials</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>B=localhost:9092
kafka-topics.sh --bootstrap-server $B --create --topic order.placed \
  --partitions 6 --replication-factor 3 --config retention.ms=604800000
kafka-topics.sh --bootstrap-server $B --describe --topic order.placed

kafka-console-producer.sh --bootstrap-server $B --topic order.placed \
  --property parse.key=true --property key.separator=:
# type:  ORD123:{"store":"1_05"}

kafka-console-consumer.sh --bootstrap-server $B --topic order.placed \
  --group debug --from-beginning --property print.key=true

kafka-consumer-groups.sh --bootstrap-server $B --describe --group indexer   # lag per partition
kafka-consumer-groups.sh --bootstrap-server $B --group indexer \
  --reset-offsets --to-datetime 2026-11-01T00:00:00.000 --topic order.placed --execute</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">Consumer <b>lag</b> (latest offset minus committed offset) is the key health metric: growing lag means consumers cannot keep up.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Spring for Apache Kafka</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># application.yml
spring:
  kafka:
    bootstrap-servers: ${KAFKA_BOOTSTRAP}
    producer:
      acks: all
      key-serializer: org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringSerializer
      value-serializer: org.springframework.kafka.support.serializer.JsonSerializer
    consumer:
      group-id: indexer
      auto-offset-reset: earliest
      key-deserializer: org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringDeserializer
      value-deserializer: org.springframework.kafka.support.serializer.JsonDeserializer   # the default is String
      properties:
        spring.json.trusted.packages: "com.example.events"
# Spring Kafka 4 (Boot 4) deprecates JsonSerializer/JsonDeserializer for JacksonJsonSerializer/JacksonJsonDeserializer</code></pre></div>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>@Service
class Publisher {
  private final KafkaTemplate&lt;String, OrderPlaced&gt; kafka;
  Publisher(KafkaTemplate&lt;String, OrderPlaced&gt; kafka) { this.kafka = kafka; }
  void publish(OrderPlaced e) { kafka.send("order.placed", e.orderRef(), e); }  // key = order ref
}

@Component
class Indexer {
  @KafkaListener(topics = "order.placed", groupId = "indexer")
  void on(OrderPlaced e) {
    index.upsert(e);           // idempotent: same event twice is harmless
  }
}

// retries and a dead-letter topic for poison messages
@Bean DefaultErrorHandler errorHandler(KafkaTemplate&lt;Object, Object&gt; t) {
  return new DefaultErrorHandler(new DeadLetterPublishingRecoverer(t),
                                 new FixedBackOff(1000L, 3));
}</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Quarkus with Kafka</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># application.properties (quarkus-messaging-kafka)
kafka.bootstrap.servers=${KAFKA_BOOTSTRAP}
mp.messaging.incoming.placed.connector=smallrye-kafka
mp.messaging.incoming.placed.topic=order.placed
mp.messaging.incoming.placed.group.id=indexer
mp.messaging.outgoing.indexed.connector=smallrye-kafka
mp.messaging.outgoing.indexed.topic=order.indexed
# the Emitter below needs its own channel: a channel has one producer unless you merge
mp.messaging.outgoing.reindexed.connector=smallrye-kafka
mp.messaging.outgoing.reindexed.topic=order.indexed</code></pre></div>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>@ApplicationScoped
public class Indexer {
  @Incoming("placed")
  @Outgoing("indexed")
  public OrderIndexed process(OrderPlaced e) {
    return index.upsert(e);
  }
}

@ApplicationScoped
public class Publisher {
  @Inject @Channel("reindexed") Emitter&lt;OrderIndexed&gt; out;   // not "indexed": the method above feeds that
  void send(OrderIndexed e) { out.send(e); }
}</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">In dev and test, Dev Services starts a Kafka-compatible broker for you.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Python (confluent-kafka)</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>from confluent_kafka import Producer, Consumer

p = Producer({"bootstrap.servers": BOOTSTRAP, "acks": "all",
              "enable.idempotence": True})
p.produce("order.placed", key=order_ref, value=json.dumps(evt),
          callback=lambda err, msg: err and log.error("send failed: %s", err))
p.flush()

c = Consumer({"bootstrap.servers": BOOTSTRAP, "group.id": "indexer",
              "auto.offset.reset": "earliest", "enable.auto.commit": False})
c.subscribe(["order.placed"])
while True:
    msg = c.poll(1.0)
    if msg is None: continue
    if msg.error(): log.error(msg.error()); continue
    handle(json.loads(msg.value()))           # idempotent
    c.commit(message=msg, asynchronous=False) # commit after processing</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>The ecosystem</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Schema Registry</b> with Avro, Protobuf or JSON Schema: producers and consumers agree on schemas; compatibility rules block breaking changes.</li>
      <li><b>Kafka Connect:</b> ready-made source and sink connectors (databases, GCS, BigQuery) without custom code.</li>
      <li><b>Debezium:</b> change data capture from databases into Kafka topics.</li>
      <li><b>Kafka Streams:</b> a Java library for stateful stream processing (joins, windows, aggregates) inside your service.</li>
      <li><b>Flink:</b> a separate engine for large-scale stream processing.</li>
      <li><b>On GCP:</b> Managed Service for Apache Kafka runs clusters for you; Dataflow and BigQuery have Kafka connectors.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Kafka vs Pub/Sub</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th></th><th>Kafka</th><th>Pub/Sub</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Model</td><td>Partitioned log; consumers track offsets</td><td>Topics and subscriptions; per-message ack</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Ordering</td><td>Per partition (by key)</td><td>Per ordering key, when enabled</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Replay</td><td>Rewind offsets within retention</td><td>Seek to a time or snapshot within retention</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Capacity</td><td>You size brokers and partitions (managed or self-run)</td><td>Serverless, scales automatically</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Ecosystem</td><td>Connect, Streams, Schema Registry, Debezium</td><td>Native GCP integrations (BigQuery, GCS, Dataflow, Eventarc)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Choose when</td><td>Kafka skills or tools exist; log semantics, compaction, very high throughput</td><td>GCP-native, simple operations, global delivery</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Designing topics</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Name by event, past tense:</b> <code>order.placed</code>, <code>order.paid</code>.</li>
      <li><b>Partitions:</b> enough for peak consumer parallelism with headroom; adding later changes key-to-partition mapping.</li>
      <li><b>Keys:</b> the entity that needs ordering; avoid hot keys.</li>
      <li><b>Payload:</b> include an event ID, type, version and timestamp; keep events self-describing.</li>
      <li><b>Retention:</b> long enough to recover from a consumer outage and replay.</li>
      <li><b>Dead-letter topic</b> for records that keep failing, with alerting on it.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Explain topics, partitions, offsets and consumer groups</li><li>Produce and consume with the CLI</li><li>Read consumer lag</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Build producers and consumers in Spring, Quarkus or Python with correct acks and commits</li><li>Make consumers idempotent; use dead-letter topics</li><li>Use a schema registry</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Design topics, keys and partition counts for scale and ordering</li><li>Run Kafka Connect and CDC pipelines</li><li>Tune throughput, latency and durability</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Operate Kafka platforms (or choose managed) for the organisation</li><li>Design event-driven architectures across domains</li><li>Lead stream processing with Kafka Streams or Flink</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>Exactly-once semantics and transactions</li><li>Consumer rebalancing protocols</li><li>Tiered storage</li><li>KRaft operations</li><li>Kafka Streams state stores</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://kafka.apache.org/documentation/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Apache Kafka documentation</a><span class="lnote"> Design, configuration, operations</span></li><li><a href="https://docs.spring.io/spring-kafka/reference/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Spring for Apache Kafka</a><span class="lnote"> Templates, listeners, error handling</span></li><li><a href="https://quarkus.io/guides/kafka" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Quarkus Kafka guide</a><span class="lnote"> Reactive messaging with Kafka</span></li><li><a href="https://docs.confluent.io/kafka-clients/python/current/overview.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">confluent-kafka-python</a><span class="lnote"> Python client</span></li><li><a href="https://developer.confluent.io/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Confluent Developer courses</a><span class="lnote"> Free courses on Kafka fundamentals and patterns</span></li><li><a href="https://cloud.google.com/managed-service-for-apache-kafka/docs" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Managed Service for Apache Kafka</a><span class="lnote"> Kafka on Google Cloud</span></li><li><a href="https://debezium.io/documentation/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Debezium</a><span class="lnote"> Change data capture into Kafka</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/apache/kafka/4.0.0/docs/upgrade.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kafka 4.0 upgrade notes (ZooKeeper removed, KRaft only)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/apache/kafka/4.0.0/docs/design.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kafka 4.0 design docs (replication, ISR, compaction)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/apache/kafka/4.0.0/clients/src/main/java/org/apache/kafka/clients/producer/ProducerConfig.java" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kafka ProducerConfig (enable.idempotence defaults to true)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/apache/kafka/trunk/tools/src/main/java/org/apache/kafka/tools/consumer/group/ConsumerGroupCommandOptions.java" target="_blank" rel="noopener">kafka-consumer-groups options (--to-datetime format)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/apache/kafka/trunk/tools/src/main/java/org/apache/kafka/tools/ConsoleProducer.java" target="_blank" rel="noopener">kafka-console-producer options</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/spring-projects/spring-kafka/main/spring-kafka/src/main/java/org/springframework/kafka/support/serializer/JsonSerializer.java" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Spring Kafka JsonSerializer (deprecated since 4.0)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/spring-projects/spring-kafka/main/spring-kafka/src/main/java/org/springframework/kafka/support/serializer/JacksonJsonSerializer.java" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Spring Kafka JacksonJsonSerializer (since 4.0)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/3.5.x/spring-boot-project/spring-boot-autoconfigure/src/main/java/org/springframework/boot/autoconfigure/kafka/KafkaProperties.java" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Spring Boot KafkaProperties (String (de)serializer defaults)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/quarkusio/quarkus/main/docs/src/main/asciidoc/kafka.adoc" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Quarkus Kafka guide (quarkus-messaging-kafka, merge)</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/managed-service-for-apache-kafka/docs/connect-cluster/connectors-overview" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Managed Kafka Connect connectors (BigQuery Sink)</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/pubsub/docs/replay-overview" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Pub/Sub replay (seek to time or snapshot)</a>; <a href="https://docs.confluent.io/platform/current/clients/confluent-kafka-python/html/index.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">confluent-kafka Python API reference</a>.</p><p class="runcode">A question, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/enrichmeai.github.io/issues/new?title=%5BKafka%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>. Nothing here is tracked and there are no comments to store: questions live in the open, where the next learner can find the answer.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= PAYMENTS ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-payments" data-name="Payment Gateways">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Payment gateways</h2><p>How online payments work, the parties and flows (cards, UPI, subscriptions), how to integrate a gateway such as Razorpay or Stripe safely, and the engineering that keeps money correct: idempotency, webhooks, state machines, ledgers and reconciliation.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Who is involved</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Party</th><th>Role</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Customer</td><td>Pays with a card, UPI, net banking or wallet</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Merchant</td><td>You: the business being paid</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Payment gateway / aggregator</td><td>Collects payment details securely, routes the transaction, gives you APIs, checkout and webhooks (Razorpay, Stripe, PayU, Cashfree)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Acquirer</td><td>The merchant's bank, which receives the funds</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Card network</td><td>Visa, Mastercard, RuPay: routes card transactions between banks</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Issuer</td><td>The customer's bank, which approves or declines</td></tr>
      <tr><td>UPI (NPCI)</td><td>India's real-time account-to-account network; apps such as PhonePe, Google Pay, Paytm act as front ends</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Regulator</td><td>In India the RBI: rules on recurring payments, tokenisation, data storage and aggregators</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Card payment lifecycle</h3>
    <ol>
      <li><b>Authorise:</b> the issuer checks funds and fraud and places a hold. With 3-D Secure (OTP or app approval) the customer authenticates.</li>
      <li><b>Capture:</b> the merchant confirms the charge (automatically or later, e.g. after shipping). An uncaptured authorisation expires.</li>
      <li><b>Settle:</b> funds move to the gateway, then to your bank account, on the gateway&#39;s settlement cycle (Razorpay&#39;s standard is T+2 working days), minus fees.</li>
      <li><b>Refund:</b> full or partial, returned through the same route; takes days to reach the customer.</li>
      <li><b>Chargeback / dispute:</b> the customer disputes with their bank; you submit evidence or lose the funds plus a fee.</li>
    </ol>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>UPI in brief</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Intent / QR flow:</b> your checkout opens the customer's UPI app (or shows a QR); they approve with their UPI PIN.</li>
      <li><b>Collect flow:</b> you send a payment request to a UPI ID; the customer approves in their app. (Being phased down for many use cases; check current NPCI rules.)</li>
      <li><b>Real-time and irrevocable</b> from the customer's side; refunds are separate transactions.</li>
      <li><b>UPI AutoPay:</b> recurring mandates the customer approves once, for subscriptions.</li>
      <li><b>Pending is normal:</b> a payment can sit in a pending state for minutes; your system must wait for the final status rather than assume failure.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Standard integration flow (server-verified)</h3>
    <ol>
      <li><b>Create an order on your server</b> with the amount in the smallest unit (paise) and your own receipt ID. Store it as <code>CREATED</code>.</li>
      <li><b>Open the gateway's checkout</b> in the browser or app with that order ID. Card and UPI details go to the gateway, never to your servers.</li>
      <li><b>Checkout returns</b> payment ID, order ID and a signature to your client, which sends them to your server.</li>
      <li><b>Verify the signature on the server</b> and fetch the payment from the gateway API to confirm amount, currency and status.</li>
      <li><b>Webhooks</b> (<code>payment.captured</code>, <code>payment.failed</code>, refunds, disputes) arrive at your endpoint; verify their signature and update state idempotently. Treat webhooks and API fetches as the source of truth, never the browser.</li>
      <li><b>Fulfil</b> only when your order is <code>PAID</code> according to the server.</li>
      <li><b>Reconcile</b> daily against the gateway's settlement reports.</li>
    </ol>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>The flow, drawn</h3>
    <figure class="fig"><svg viewBox="0 0 640 324" role="img" aria-labelledby="payflow-t payflow-d"><title id="payflow-t">A server-verified payment</title><desc id="payflow-d">A sequence diagram with three lifelines: the browser or app, your server, and the gateway. 1: your server creates an order with the amount in paise and stores it as CREATED. 2: the browser opens the gateway's checkout with that order ID; card and UPI details go to the gateway, never to your server. 3: checkout returns a payment ID and a signature, which the client sends to your server. 4: your server verifies the signature and fetches the payment from the gateway. 5: the gateway also sends a webhook, which your server verifies and applies idempotently. 6: only then is the order PAID and fulfilled.</desc><defs><marker id="payflow-a" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker><marker id="payflow-aa" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar acc" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker></defs><rect class="nd in" x="43.33333333333333" y="14" width="140" height="36" rx="4"/><text class="lb hd" x="113.33333333333333" y="37" text-anchor="middle">Browser / app</text><line class="ln" x1="113.33333333333333" y1="50" x2="113.33333333333333" y2="316"/><rect class="nd out" x="250.0" y="14" width="140" height="36" rx="4"/><text class="lb hd" x="320.0" y="37" text-anchor="middle">Your server</text><line class="ln" x1="320.0" y1="50" x2="320.0" y2="316"/><rect class="nd" x="456.66666666666663" y="14" width="140" height="36" rx="4"/><text class="lb hd" x="526.6666666666666" y="37" text-anchor="middle">Gateway</text><line class="ln" x1="526.6666666666666" y1="50" x2="526.6666666666666" y2="316"/><line class="ed" x1="320.0" y1="80" x2="522.6666666666666" y2="80" marker-end="url(#payflow-a)"/><circle class="nd in" cx="333.0" cy="68" r="9"/><text class="lb mu" x="333.0" y="72" text-anchor="middle">1</text><text class="lb" x="348.0" y="73" text-anchor="start">create order, amount in paise</text><line class="ed" x1="113.33333333333333" y1="114" x2="522.6666666666666" y2="114" marker-end="url(#payflow-a)"/><circle class="nd in" cx="126.33333333333333" cy="102" r="9"/><text class="lb mu" x="126.33333333333333" y="106" text-anchor="middle">2</text><text class="lb" x="141.33333333333331" y="107" text-anchor="start">checkout: card, UPI details</text><line class="ed dash" x1="113.33333333333333" y1="148" x2="316.0" y2="148" marker-end="url(#payflow-a)"/><circle class="nd in" cx="126.33333333333333" cy="136" r="9"/><text class="lb mu" x="126.33333333333333" y="140" text-anchor="middle">3</text><text class="lb mu" x="141.33333333333331" y="141" text-anchor="start">payment ID, order ID, signature</text><line class="ed acc" x1="320.0" y1="182" x2="522.6666666666666" y2="182" marker-end="url(#payflow-aa)"/><circle class="nd in" cx="333.0" cy="170" r="9"/><text class="lb mu" x="333.0" y="174" text-anchor="middle">4</text><text class="lb acc" x="348.0" y="175" text-anchor="start">verify signature, fetch payment</text><line class="ed dash" x1="526.6666666666666" y1="216" x2="324.0" y2="216" marker-end="url(#payflow-a)"/><circle class="nd in" cx="333.0" cy="204" r="9"/><text class="lb mu" x="333.0" y="208" text-anchor="middle">5</text><text class="lb mu" x="348.0" y="209" text-anchor="start">webhook: payment.captured</text><path class="ed acc" d="M320.0,242 h22 v14 h-18" marker-end="url(#payflow-aa)"/><text class="lb acc" x="350.0" y="253" text-anchor="start">PAID: fulfil</text><circle class="nd in" cx="306.0" cy="246" r="9"/><text class="lb mu" x="306.0" y="250" text-anchor="middle">6</text><text class="lb mu" x="121.33333333333333" y="286" text-anchor="start">the browser is never the source of truth</text></svg><figcaption>The steps above as a sequence diagram. The card details never touch your server, and nothing is fulfilled on the browser&rsquo;s word: only on a verified signature, a fetched status and the webhook.</figcaption></figure>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Razorpay: order and verification</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># create an order (amount in paise: 50000 = ₹500.00)
curl -u "$RZP_KEY_ID:$RZP_KEY_SECRET" \
  -X POST https://api.razorpay.com/v1/orders \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"amount":50000,"currency":"INR","receipt":"order_8421"}'</code></pre></div>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>// verify checkout signature: HMAC-SHA256(order_id + "|" + payment_id, key_secret)
static boolean validCheckout(String orderId, String paymentId, String signature, String keySecret)
    throws Exception {
  Mac mac = Mac.getInstance("HmacSHA256");
  mac.init(new SecretKeySpec(keySecret.getBytes(UTF_8), "HmacSHA256"));
  String expected = HexFormat.of().formatHex(
      mac.doFinal((orderId + "|" + paymentId).getBytes(UTF_8)));
  return MessageDigest.isEqual(expected.getBytes(UTF_8), signature.getBytes(UTF_8)); // constant time
}

// webhook: HMAC-SHA256 of the RAW request body with the webhook secret,
// compared with the X-Razorpay-Signature header. Never re-serialise the JSON first.
// The same event can arrive more than once: dedupe on the X-Razorpay-Event-Id header.</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Stripe: PaymentIntent and webhook</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>import stripe
stripe.api_key = STRIPE_SECRET_KEY

intent = stripe.PaymentIntent.create(
    amount=50000, currency="inr",
    metadata={"order_id": "order_8421"},
    idempotency_key="order_8421-create",         # safe to retry
)
# return intent.client_secret to the front end, which confirms the payment

# webhook endpoint
def stripe_webhook(request):
    payload = request.body                        # raw bytes
    sig = request.headers["Stripe-Signature"]
    try:
        event = stripe.Webhook.construct_event(payload, sig, WEBHOOK_SECRET)
    except (ValueError, stripe.SignatureVerificationError):   # older SDKs: stripe.error.SignatureVerificationError
        return 400
    if not seen_before(event["id"]):              # dedupe: webhooks repeat
        handle(event)
    return 200</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Payment state machine</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>CREATED ──checkout──▶ PENDING ──captured──▶ PAID ──refund──▶ PARTIALLY_REFUNDED / REFUNDED
   │                     │                    │
   │                     ├──failed──▶ FAILED  └──dispute──▶ DISPUTED ──▶ WON / LOST
   └──expired──▶ EXPIRED └──timeout──▶ (stay PENDING; poll the gateway; never assume)</code></pre></div>
    <ul>
      <li>Allow only valid transitions; reject or log anything else.</li>
      <li>Events can arrive late or out of order (a webhook before the browser callback, a failure after a success for a different attempt). Decide by the gateway's fetched status.</li>
      <li>Keep every transition as an append-only event with its source (webhook, API fetch, user).</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>The state machine, drawn</h3>
    <figure class="fig"><svg viewBox="0 0 660 300" role="img" aria-labelledby="paystate-t paystate-d"><title id="paystate-t">Payment state machine</title><desc id="paystate-d">A UML state diagram. CREATED goes to PENDING at checkout, or to EXPIRED. PENDING goes to PAID when captured, or to FAILED. A PENDING payment that times out stays PENDING while the gateway is polled. PAID goes to PARTIALLY_REFUNDED or REFUNDED on a refund, or to DISPUTED, which ends WON or LOST.</desc><defs><marker id="paystate-a" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker><marker id="paystate-aa" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar acc" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker></defs><circle class="dot" cx="20" cy="69" r="7"/><line class="ed" x1="27" y1="69" x2="46" y2="69" marker-end="url(#paystate-a)"/><rect class="nd" x="50" y="50" width="120" height="38" rx="14"/><text class="lb hd" x="110" y="74" text-anchor="middle">CREATED</text><rect class="nd" x="250" y="50" width="120" height="38" rx="14"/><text class="lb hd" x="310" y="74" text-anchor="middle">PENDING</text><rect class="nd out" x="470" y="50" width="120" height="38" rx="14"/><text class="lb hd" x="530" y="74" text-anchor="middle">PAID</text><rect class="nd warn" x="50" y="150" width="120" height="38" rx="14"/><text class="lb hd" x="110" y="174" text-anchor="middle">EXPIRED</text><rect class="nd warn" x="250" y="150" width="120" height="38" rx="14"/><text class="lb hd" x="310" y="174" text-anchor="middle">FAILED</text><rect class="nd" x="470" y="150" width="120" height="38" rx="14"/><text class="lb hd" x="530" y="174" text-anchor="middle">REFUNDED</text><rect class="nd" x="250" y="230" width="120" height="38" rx="14"/><text class="lb hd" x="310" y="254" text-anchor="middle">DISPUTED</text><line class="ed" x1="170" y1="69" x2="246" y2="69" marker-end="url(#paystate-a)"/><text class="lb mu" x="208" y="61" text-anchor="middle">checkout</text><line class="ed acc" x1="370" y1="69" x2="466" y2="69" marker-end="url(#paystate-aa)"/><text class="lb acc" x="418" y="61" text-anchor="middle">captured</text><line class="ed" x1="110" y1="88" x2="110" y2="146" marker-end="url(#paystate-a)"/><text class="lb mu" x="116" y="122" text-anchor="start">expired</text><line class="ed" x1="310" y1="88" x2="310" y2="146" marker-end="url(#paystate-a)"/><text class="lb mu" x="316" y="122" text-anchor="start">failed</text><path class="ed" d="M350,50 C380,10 280,10 300,46" marker-end="url(#paystate-a)"/><text class="lb mu" x="330" y="20" text-anchor="middle">timeout: stay, poll the gateway</text><line class="ed" x1="530" y1="88" x2="530" y2="146" marker-end="url(#paystate-a)"/><text class="lb mu" x="536" y="122" text-anchor="start">refund, full or partial</text><line class="ed" x1="490" y1="88" x2="374" y2="232" marker-end="url(#paystate-a)"/><text class="lb mu" x="440" y="205" text-anchor="start">dispute</text><text class="lb mu" x="310" y="290" text-anchor="middle">then WON or LOST</text></svg><figcaption>The diagram above as a UML state diagram. Only these arrows are allowed; anything else is rejected or logged, and a timeout never moves a payment on its own.</figcaption></figure>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Money-handling rules</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Integers in the smallest unit</b> (paise, cents) or decimal types; never floats.</li>
      <li><b>Store the currency</b> with every amount.</li>
      <li><b>Compute prices and totals on the server.</b> Never trust an amount sent by the client.</li>
      <li><b>Idempotency keys</b> on every create, capture and refund call, derived from your own IDs, so a retry cannot charge twice.</li>
      <li><b>Webhook handlers are idempotent</b>: dedupe by event ID; applying the same event twice changes nothing.</li>
      <li><b>Unknown is not failed:</b> a timeout means "we do not know yet". Poll the gateway before telling the customer it failed or retrying the charge.</li>
      <li><b>Round once,</b> at a defined step, with a documented rule (taxes, discounts, splits).</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Double-entry ledger</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>CREATE TABLE ledger_entry (
  id           bigint GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,
  txn_id       uuid    NOT NULL,          -- groups the entries of one transaction
  account      text    NOT NULL,          -- 'customer_receivable', 'gateway_clearing', 'revenue', 'gst_payable'
  amount_paise bigint  NOT NULL,          -- positive = debit, negative = credit
  currency     char(3) NOT NULL,
  created_at   timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT now()
);
-- every txn_id must sum to zero
SELECT txn_id FROM ledger_entry GROUP BY txn_id HAVING SUM(amount_paise) &lt;&gt; 0;  -- must return nothing</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">Append only: corrections are new entries, never edits. Balances are sums. This makes every rupee traceable and reconciliation possible.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Subscriptions and recurring payments</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Gateways model <b>plans</b> (price, interval) and <b>subscriptions</b> (a customer on a plan) with a mandate or saved token for charging.</li>
      <li><b>India:</b> RBI e-mandate rules govern recurring card and UPI payments: customer registration with authentication, pre-debit notifications, and extra authentication above a limit. Check the current limits and rules; they have changed over time.</li>
      <li><b>Handle the lifecycle via webhooks:</b> activated, charged, payment failed, halted, cancelled, completed.</li>
      <li><b>Dunning:</b> retries on failed renewals, customer notices, grace periods, then downgrade or cancel.</li>
      <li><b>Entitlements</b> (what the user may use) are derived from subscription state in your system, updated only from verified events.</li>
      <li><b>Proration</b> rules for upgrades and downgrades mid-period, decided and written down.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Security and compliance</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>PCI DSS:</b> use hosted checkout or the gateway's elements so card data never reaches your servers; your compliance scope stays small.</li>
      <li><b>Card tokenisation (India):</b> merchants may not store card numbers; saved cards use network or issuer tokens through the gateway.</li>
      <li><b>Data localisation:</b> RBI requires payment system data to be stored in India; confirm your gateway and your own storage comply.</li>
      <li><b>Secrets:</b> API keys and webhook secrets in a secret manager; separate test and live keys; rotate on staff changes.</li>
      <li><b>Webhook endpoint:</b> verify signatures on the raw body, reject old timestamps where provided, respond quickly, process asynchronously.</li>
      <li><b>Fraud:</b> use the gateway's risk tools, 3-D Secure, velocity limits, and watch for card testing (many small failed attempts).</li>
      <li><b>Tax and invoices:</b> GST-compliant invoices with the correct rates and place of supply.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Reconciliation</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Download settlement and transaction reports from the gateway daily (API or dashboard).</li>
      <li>Match each gateway transaction to your order by order ID and amount.</li>
      <li>Match settlements to bank credits, accounting for fees and taxes.</li>
      <li>Flag mismatches: paid at gateway but not in your system (missed webhook), paid in your system but not settled, amount differences, unexpected refunds or disputes.</li>
      <li>Resolve each one and record why. A rising mismatch count is an incident.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="note">Run reconciliation as a scheduled job with run records, like any pipeline.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Testing payments</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Use the gateway's <b>test mode</b> keys, test cards and test UPI IDs for success, failure, 3-D Secure and pending cases.</li>
      <li>Replay recorded webhook payloads in automated tests; build fixtures from real test-mode events, not guesses.</li>
      <li>Test duplicates, out-of-order events, timeouts and partial refunds explicitly.</li>
      <li>Keep a test-mode end-to-end checkout in your release checks.</li>
      <li>Never test with live keys or real cards in non-production environments.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Operating payments</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Metrics:</b> payment success rate by method and bank, latency, webhook delay, pending count and age, refund rate, dispute rate.</li>
      <li><b>Alerts:</b> success rate drops, webhooks stop arriving, pending payments older than a threshold, reconciliation mismatches.</li>
      <li><b>Support tools:</b> look up an order's full event history; never let support edit payment state by hand.</li>
      <li><b>Multiple gateways:</b> routing or failover between gateways improves success rates, at the cost of more reconciliation.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Explain authorisation, capture, settlement, refunds and chargebacks</li><li>Explain the order → checkout → verify → webhook flow</li><li>Know why card data must never touch your servers</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Integrate a gateway with server-side verification and signed webhooks</li><li>Make payment calls and webhook handling idempotent</li><li>Model payments as a state machine; test with test-mode fixtures</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Design subscriptions, dunning and entitlements</li><li>Build a double-entry ledger and daily reconciliation</li><li>Own payment security, PCI scope and regulatory rules (RBI)</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Design multi-gateway routing and payment platforms</li><li>Lead payments compliance and audits</li><li>Set money-handling standards across the organisation</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>3-D Secure and strong customer authentication</li><li>UPI AutoPay and e-mandates</li><li>Card network tokenisation</li><li>Double-entry accounting for engineers</li><li>Fraud detection and chargeback management</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://razorpay.com/docs/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Razorpay documentation</a><span class="lnote"> Orders, checkout, webhooks, subscriptions</span></li><li><a href="https://docs.stripe.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Stripe documentation</a><span class="lnote"> PaymentIntents, webhooks, billing</span></li><li><a href="https://docs.stripe.com/api/idempotent_requests" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Stripe: idempotent requests</a><span class="lnote"> How idempotency keys work</span></li><li><a href="https://www.pcisecuritystandards.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PCI Security Standards Council</a><span class="lnote"> PCI DSS requirements</span></li><li><a href="https://www.npci.org.in/what-we-do/upi/product-overview" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NPCI: UPI</a><span class="lnote"> How UPI works</span></li><li><a href="https://www.rbi.org.in/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Reserve Bank of India</a><span class="lnote"> Circulars on payments, e-mandates, tokenisation</span></li><li><a href="https://www.moderntreasury.com/journal/accounting-for-developers-part-i" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Accounting for developers (Modern Treasury)</a><span class="lnote"> Double-entry ledgers explained</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Payment_gateway?action=raw" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Payment gateway (authorise, capture, settle)</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chargeback?action=raw" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Chargeback</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Acquiring_bank?action=raw" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Acquiring bank</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unified_Payments_Interface?action=raw" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Unified Payments Interface (NPCI, PIN, AutoPay)</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/3-D_Secure?action=raw" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: 3-D Secure</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Payment_Card_Industry_Data_Security_Standard?action=raw" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: PCI DSS</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/razorpay/razorpay-python/master/razorpay/utility/utility.py" target="_blank" rel="noopener">razorpay-python utility (order_id|payment_id HMAC-SHA256; webhook HMAC of body)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/stripe/stripe-python/master/stripe/_webhook.py" target="_blank" rel="noopener">stripe-python Webhook.construct_event</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/stripe/stripe-python/master/stripe/__init__.py" target="_blank" rel="noopener">stripe-python top-level SignatureVerificationError</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/stripe/stripe-python/master/stripe/_request_options.py" target="_blank" rel="noopener">stripe-python request options (idempotency_key)</a>; <a href="https://razorpay.com/docs/webhooks/validate-test/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Razorpay: validate webhooks (X-Razorpay-Signature, x-razorpay-event-id)</a>; <a href="https://razorpay.com/docs/payments/settlements/faqs/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Razorpay: settlement FAQs (standard cycle T+2 working days)</a>; <a href="https://www.rbi.org.in/Scripts/NotificationUser.aspx?Id=11244&Mode=0" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RBI: Storage of Payment System Data</a>; <a href="https://www.rbi.org.in/Scripts/NotificationUser.aspx?Id=12159&Mode=0" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RBI: Card-on-File Tokenisation</a>; <a href="https://www.rbi.org.in/Scripts/NotificationUser.aspx?Id=11668&Mode=0" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RBI: e-mandate on cards for recurring transactions</a>; <a href="https://docs.stripe.com/currencies" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Stripe: supported currencies and minor units</a>. A few claims could not be confirmed at their official source from here and are being re-checked.</p><p class="runcode">A question, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/enrichmeai.github.io/issues/new?title=%5BPayment%20Gateways%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>. Nothing here is tracked and there are no comments to store: questions live in the open, where the next learner can find the answer.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= BANKING AND LOW LATENCY ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-banking" data-name="Banking &amp; Low Latency">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Banking systems and low latency</h2><p>Banking software is judged first on correctness and availability, and only then on speed; trading systems are judged on speed in microseconds. This page covers how a bank's systems fit together, what changed when payments became instant, and how low latency is actually achieved and measured. The money-handling rules themselves (double-entry, idempotency, reconciliation) are on <a href="#payments">Payment Gateways</a>; percentiles and profiling are on <a href="#performance">Performance</a>.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>What makes banking software different</h3>
    <p><i>Analysis, built on the sources at the end.</i> A shop that loses an order apologises. A bank that loses or invents money breaks the law. That changes the order of priorities:</p>
    <ol>
      <li><b>Correct:</b> money is never created or lost. Every movement is a balanced pair of entries (see <a href="#payments">Payment Gateways</a>), and every change can be traced to who made it and why.</li>
      <li><b>Available:</b> instant payment schemes run 24 hours a day, every day, so there is no longer a quiet night to take systems down.</li>
      <li><b>Fast enough:</b> fast enough for the scheme&rsquo;s deadline and for the customer, which is usually far from the microseconds a trading system needs.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="note"><i>The other view:</i> some argue that speed is itself a form of correctness for payments, because a payment that misses its scheme deadline fails. Both views agree on the order inside the deadline: correct first.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>How a bank&rsquo;s systems fit together</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Channels:</b> the mobile app, web banking, branches, call centres, and partners through APIs.</li>
      <li><b>Integration:</b> gateways and messaging that connect channels to the systems of record, with limits, fraud checks and audit on the way.</li>
      <li><b>Core banking:</b> the system that centralises the bank&rsquo;s record keeping for deposits, loans and accounts, so any branch or channel sees one record.</li>
      <li><b>Payment schemes:</b> connections to card networks, instant payment schemes and slower batch schemes, each with its own message formats and deadlines.</li>
      <li><b>Ledger and reporting:</b> the general ledger, regulatory reports, reconciliation.</li>
      <li><b>Mainframes and COBOL:</b> COBOL is used mainly in business, finance and administrative systems, and is still widely used on mainframes for large-scale batch and transaction processing; most COBOL work today maintains existing applications. Some IBM mainframes (z900 to System z10) execute instructions twice and compare the results, to catch hardware faults.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>The layers, drawn</h3>
    <figure class="fig"><svg viewBox="0 0 660 300" role="img" aria-labelledby="bank-t bank-d"><title id="bank-t">How a bank's systems fit together</title><desc id="bank-d">Five layers from top to bottom. Channels: mobile app, web banking, branches, call centres, partner APIs. Integration: gateways and messaging with limits, fraud checks and audit on the way. Then, side by side, core banking, the one record of accounts, deposits and loans, and the payment schemes: cards, instant and batch, each with its own formats and deadlines. At the bottom, the ledger and reporting: the general ledger, regulatory reports and reconciliation.</desc><defs><marker id="bank-a" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker><marker id="bank-aa" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar acc" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker></defs><text class="lb hd" x="10" y="46" text-anchor="start">channels</text><rect class="nd in" x="120.0" y="20" width="96.0" height="40" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="168.0" y="45" text-anchor="middle">mobile</text><rect class="nd in" x="224.0" y="20" width="96.0" height="40" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="272.0" y="45" text-anchor="middle">web</text><rect class="nd in" x="328.0" y="20" width="96.0" height="40" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="376.0" y="45" text-anchor="middle">branch</text><rect class="nd in" x="432.0" y="20" width="96.0" height="40" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="480.0" y="45" text-anchor="middle">call centre</text><rect class="nd in" x="536.0" y="20" width="96.0" height="40" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="584.0" y="45" text-anchor="middle">partner APIs</text><line class="ed" x1="380" y1="60" x2="380" y2="80" marker-end="url(#bank-a)"/><text class="lb hd" x="10" y="110" text-anchor="start">integration</text><rect class="nd" x="120.0" y="84" width="512.0" height="40" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="376.0" y="109" text-anchor="middle">gateways and messaging: limits, fraud checks, audit</text><line class="ed" x1="380" y1="124" x2="380" y2="144" marker-end="url(#bank-a)"/><text class="lb hd" x="10" y="174" text-anchor="start">records</text><rect class="nd out" x="120" y="148" width="252" height="52" rx="6"/><text class="lb hd" x="246" y="170" text-anchor="middle">core banking</text><text class="lb mu" x="246" y="188" text-anchor="middle">one record of every account</text><rect class="nd" x="384" y="148" width="252" height="52" rx="6"/><text class="lb hd" x="510" y="170" text-anchor="middle">payment schemes</text><text class="lb mu" x="510" y="188" text-anchor="middle">cards, instant, batch</text><line class="ed" x1="246" y1="200" x2="246" y2="228" marker-end="url(#bank-a)"/><line class="ed" x1="510" y1="200" x2="510" y2="228" marker-end="url(#bank-a)"/><text class="lb hd" x="10" y="258" text-anchor="start">books</text><rect class="nd" x="120" y="232" width="516" height="40" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="378" y="257" text-anchor="middle">general ledger, regulatory reports, reconciliation</text></svg><figcaption>The list above as layers. A payment from the app crosses every one of them, and with instant schemes the checks in the integration layer must finish within seconds.</figcaption></figure>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>From overnight batch to instant</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Scheme</th><th>How fast</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>SEPA credit transfer (euro)</td><td>Settles within one business day</td></tr>
      <tr><td>SEPA Instant (euro, since November 2017)</td><td>Under ten seconds; at most twenty in exceptional cases; around the clock</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Faster Payments (UK, since May 2008)</td><td>Typically a few seconds, not guaranteed</td></tr>
      <tr><td>FedNow (US, since July 2023)</td><td>Instant, 24 hours a day, 365 days a year</td></tr>
      <tr><td>UPI (India)</td><td>See <a href="#payments">Payment Gateways</a></td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note">What changes when payments are instant (<i>analysis</i>): checks that once ran in the overnight batch, such as limits, fraud and sanctions screening, now run inline within the deadline; and maintenance can no longer wait for a closed weekend.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Why the new banks got to market fast: Monzo, Revolut, Starling</h3>
    <p>Three UK-founded banks went from start-up to millions of customers in about a decade. The published record points to three reasons, and only one of them is technology.</p>
    <div class="tbl"><table><thead><tr><th>Reason</th><th>What happened</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td><b>The rules opened</b></td><td>After the 2008 financial crisis the UK government decided to open the market to new banks. The Financial Services Act 2012 came into force on 1 April 2013, and the Prudential Regulation Authority set up a New Bank Start-up Unit with a staged route: an expression of interest, then &ldquo;authorisation with restrictions&rdquo;, then the restrictions lifted. Before that, setting up a bank with a full UK licence was extremely expensive and time-consuming, and the &ldquo;Big Four&rdquo; banks dominated. Starling, founded in January 2014, received its licence in July 2016; Monzo&rsquo;s restrictions were lifted in April 2017.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>They started narrow</b></td><td>Monzo (founded as Mondo in 2015) began with an app and a prepaid card, and launched its full current account in October 2017. Revolut (founded in July 2015) began as an app for spending abroad at interbank exchange rates, as an e-money institution rather than a bank; it received an EU banking licence in December 2018 and a UK licence, with restrictions, in 2024. Each offered one useful thing first, and became a bank later.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>No legacy to carry</b></td><td>No branches (Starling has none) and app-first products, which Wikipedia&rsquo;s article on challenger banks describes as modern practices &ldquo;that avoid the costs and complexities of traditional banking&rdquo;. Starling built its own platform and in February 2022 set up Engine, a subsidiary that offers its systems to other firms as a service.</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
    <p><b>The cost of moving fast.</b> In 2019 Monzo said it had inadvertently logged 480,000 customers&rsquo; card PINs, readable by its own engineers, because they were sent in the query string of an HTTPS request. In September 2022 a cyber attack on Revolut exposed the personal data of over 50,000 customers, and a flaw in its US payment system lost $20 million to criminals between late 2021 and early 2022. In October 2024 the Financial Conduct Authority fined Starling &pound;29 million for weak anti-money-laundering and sanctions controls dating back to 2017. Monzo withdrew its US banking licence application in 2021, and reported its first annual profit for the year to March 2024. Several of these are quality attributes (security, privacy, compliance) that growth outran; see <a href="#quality">Quality Attributes</a> and <a href="#security">Security</a>.</p>
    <p><i>Our view:</i> the speed came from three things together: a regulator that built a staged way in, a narrow first product, and no old systems or branches to carry. Modern technology mattered, but it was one of three, and on its own it would not have produced a bank. <i>The other view:</i> established banks are slower partly by obligation: decades of products, data and customers to keep working, and a scale at which a mistake hurts more people. The fines and incidents above suggest the new banks meet the same pressures as they grow.</p>
    <p class="note">Sources: Wikipedia&rsquo;s articles on <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Challenger_bank" target="_blank" rel="noopener">challenger banks</a>, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monzo" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Monzo</a>, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Revolut" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Revolut</a> and <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Starling_Bank" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Starling Bank</a>. We could not read the banks&rsquo; own engineering blogs from this environment, so this card says nothing about their technology choices.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>ISO 20022: the language banks are moving to</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>An ISO standard for electronic data interchange between financial institutions.</li>
      <li>It is a repository of message and business-process descriptions, modelled in UML and turned into concrete messages (most often XML).</li>
      <li><b>Why engineers meet it:</b> payment schemes and correspondent banking are moving their messages to it, so a payments system has to read and produce it, and map it to and from older formats during the move.</li>
      <li><b>Why it helps</b> (<i>analysis</i>): structured fields for parties, purposes and references, instead of free text, make screening and reconciliation easier to automate.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Low latency: what &ldquo;low&rdquo; means depends on the job</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>If you are building</th><th>What matters</th><th>What &ldquo;fast&rdquo; looks like</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>A banking app screen</td><td>The customer&rsquo;s wait, every time, including the slow ones</td><td>Tail latency (p99) across every service in the chain, not the average</td></tr>
      <tr><td>An instant payment</td><td>Finishing every check inside the scheme&rsquo;s deadline</td><td>A whole journey within seconds (SEPA Instant: under ten), with a known budget per step</td></tr>
      <tr><td>A market-data or trading system</td><td>Reacting before the competition</td><td>Under a millisecond is often called &ldquo;ultra low latency&rdquo;, and the bar keeps moving</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p><b>Physics sets the floor.</b> Light in a vacuum covers a kilometre in about 3&nbsp;microseconds, so a trading engine 150&nbsp;km from an exchange can never get a round trip under 1&nbsp;millisecond, before any software runs. Fibre is slower: its core has a refractive index of about 1.4475, which works out (<i>our calculation</i>) at roughly 4.8&nbsp;microseconds per kilometre, or about 1.4&nbsp;ms for that round trip. This is why co-location, running servers in the exchange&rsquo;s own data centre, is one of the defining features of high-frequency trading.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Techniques, and what each one costs: choose by requirement</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>If this is your problem</th><th>Consider</th><th>What it costs you (<i>analysis</i>)</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>You do not know where the time goes</td><td>Measure first: tracing per hop, percentiles, a profiler (see <a href="#performance">Performance</a>)</td><td>Time; nothing else on this list is worth doing before it</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Database round trips dominate</td><td>Keep the working state in memory and the database for durability: LMAX&rsquo;s trading platform runs its business logic in memory with event sourcing, and its business logic processor was reported to handle 6 million orders per second on a single thread, in its team&rsquo;s own benchmark</td><td>Recovery by replaying events; careful design of snapshots</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Threads wait on locks and queues</td><td>Single-threaded business logic fed by lock-free ring buffers (LMAX&rsquo;s Disruptor); fewer hand-offs between threads</td><td>A programming model most teams have not used</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Garbage-collection pauses on the JVM</td><td>A low-pause collector: with Generational ZGC (JEP 439), pause times are measured in microseconds; or allocate less on the hot path</td><td>Some throughput and memory overhead</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Small messages wait in the network stack</td><td>Turn off Nagle&rsquo;s algorithm with <code>TCP_NODELAY</code></td><td>More, smaller packets on the network</td></tr>
      <tr><td>The kernel&rsquo;s network stack itself is too slow</td><td>Kernel bypass, such as DPDK, which processes packets entirely in user space</td><td>Specialised hardware and skills; you take over what the kernel did</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Distance dominates</td><td>Move closer: same region, same data centre, or co-location at the exchange</td><td>Money, and less freedom over where you run</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note"><i>Our view:</i> most banking systems are slow because of too many network hops and database calls, not because of the garbage collector or the kernel. Work down the table from the top. <i>The other view:</i> in trading, the last rows are the whole game, and teams start there because the first rows were solved long ago.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Measure latency honestly</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Percentiles, not averages:</b> the slow requests are the ones customers remember (see <a href="#performance">Performance</a>).</li>
      <li><b>Coordinated omission:</b> a load generator that waits for each response before sending the next stops sending exactly when the server is slow, so it quietly misses most of the slow periods. Gil Tene&rsquo;s wrk2 sends at a constant rate and records into HdrHistogram to avoid it.</li>
      <li><b>Measure end to end and per hop:</b> the customer&rsquo;s wait is the sum of every hop in the chain.</li>
      <li><b>Test with production-like data and load,</b> including the batch jobs and peaks of month end.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Correctness under speed</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Idempotency everywhere:</b> retries are certain at this scale; a repeated payment message must not move money twice (see <a href="#payments">Payment Gateways</a>).</li>
      <li><b>Exactly-once effects, not exactly-once delivery:</b> messages may arrive twice; the ledger must apply them once.</li>
      <li><b>Reconcile continuously:</b> compare your ledger with the scheme&rsquo;s and the bank&rsquo;s, every day, and treat a mismatch as an incident.</li>
      <li><b>Plan for the slow path:</b> when a check cannot finish inside the deadline, decide in advance whether to reject, hold or approve, and record why.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Explain how channels, core banking, schemes and the ledger fit together</li><li>Explain why every payment step must be idempotent</li><li>Read p50, p99 and p99.9 and say which one customers feel</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Build a payment flow that meets an instant scheme's deadline</li><li>Map a message to and from ISO 20022</li><li>Load-test without coordinated omission</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Design for 24/7 operation with no batch window</li><li>Set and enforce a latency budget per hop</li><li>Choose between in-memory, lock-free and conventional designs by requirement</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Lead a core banking or scheme migration</li><li>Set resilience and latency standards across a bank</li><li>Design ultra-low-latency trading infrastructure</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>Core banking and the general ledger</li><li>Instant payment schemes</li><li>ISO 20022</li><li>Tail latency and latency budgets</li><li>The LMAX architecture and the Disruptor</li><li>Kernel bypass and co-location</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://martinfowler.com/articles/lmax.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The LMAX Architecture (Martin Fowler)</a><span class="lnote"> In-memory, single-threaded, event-sourced trading</span></li><li><a href="https://github.com/giltene/wrk2" target="_blank" rel="noopener">wrk2 README: coordinated omission</a><span class="lnote"> Why most load tests miss the slow periods</span></li><li><a href="https://github.com/HdrHistogram/HdrHistogram" target="_blank" rel="noopener">HdrHistogram</a><span class="lnote"> Recording latency across a wide range</span></li><li><a href="https://openjdk.org/jeps/439" target="_blank" rel="noopener">JEP 439: Generational ZGC</a><span class="lnote"> A low-pause collector for the JVM</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Challenger_bank" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Challenger bank</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monzo" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Monzo</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Revolut" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Revolut</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Starling_Bank" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Starling Bank</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Core_banking" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Core banking</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Instant_payment" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Instant payment</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Single_Euro_Payments_Area" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Single Euro Payments Area (SCT Inst)</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Faster_Payment_System_(United_Kingdom)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Faster Payment System (UK)</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/FedNow" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: FedNow</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ISO_20022" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: ISO 20022</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mainframe_computer" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Mainframe computer</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/COBOL" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: COBOL</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Low_latency_(capital_markets)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Low latency (capital markets)</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/High-frequency_trading" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: High-frequency trading (co-location)</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Optical_fiber" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Optical fiber (refractive index)</a>; <a href="https://martinfowler.com/articles/lmax.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Martin Fowler: The LMAX Architecture (2011)</a>; <a href="https://openjdk.org/jeps/439" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OpenJDK JEP 333: ZGC, and JEP 439: Generational ZGC</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nagle%27s_algorithm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Nagle's algorithm (TCP_NODELAY)</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Data_Plane_Development_Kit" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Data Plane Development Kit</a>; <a href="https://github.com/giltene/wrk2" target="_blank" rel="noopener">wrk2 README (coordinated omission)</a>.</p><p class="runcode">A question, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/enrichmeai.github.io/issues/new?title=%5BBanking%20%26%20Low%20Latency%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>. Nothing here is tracked and there are no comments to store: questions live in the open, where the next learner can find the answer.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= SPRING BOOT ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-spring" data-name="Spring Boot">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Spring Boot</h2><p>Spring Boot 3.x on Java 17+, with what 3.2–3.5 added and what changed in 4.0 and 4.1. The examples are 3.x; 4.0 renamed or removed some properties, so check its migration guide. Servlet (MVC) and reactive (WebFlux) shown side by side.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Version map</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Ver</th><th>Year</th><th>What changed</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>2.7</td><td>2022</td><td>Last 2.x line. Java 8+, <code>javax.*</code> packages</td></tr>
      <tr><td>3.0</td><td>2022</td><td>Java 17 baseline, Jakarta EE 9+ (<code>javax.*</code> → <code>jakarta.*</code>), Micrometer Observation, GraalVM native images, <code>ProblemDetail</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>3.1</td><td>2023</td><td>Testcontainers <code>@ServiceConnection</code>, Docker Compose support</td></tr>
      <tr><td>3.2</td><td>2023</td><td>Virtual threads (<code>spring.threads.virtual.enabled</code>), <code>RestClient</code>, <code>JdbcClient</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>3.3</td><td>2024</td><td>Class Data Sharing support, SBOM actuator endpoint</td></tr>
      <tr><td>3.4</td><td>2024</td><td>Structured logging (ECS, Logstash, GELF), <code>@MockitoBean</code> replaces <code>@MockBean</code>, graceful shutdown by default</td></tr>
      <tr><td>3.5</td><td>2025</td><td>The last 3.x feature release; upgrade here before moving to 4.x</td></tr>
      <tr><td>4.0</td><td>2025</td><td>Spring Framework 7, Jakarta EE 11, Jackson 3, smaller modular starters, API versioning, JSpecify null-safety; deprecated 3.x APIs removed</td></tr>
      <tr><td>4.1</td><td>2026</td><td>Spring gRPC support, SSRF protection for HTTP clients (<code>InetAddressFilter</code>), OpenTelemetry improvements</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note">Moving to 4.0: upgrade to the latest 3.5 first, clear every deprecation warning, then follow the official migration guide.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Reactive or blocking?</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Blocking (thread per request):</b> each request holds a thread while it waits for a database or another service. Simple to write, read and debug, with ordinary loops and try/catch.</li>
      <li><b>Reactive (Spring WebFlux, Project Reactor):</b> a few threads handle many requests by never waiting; work is composed as a pipeline of callbacks. It scales to many concurrent slow calls, but code, stack traces and debugging are harder, and every library on the path must be non-blocking too.</li>
      <li><b>Virtual threads changed the balance.</b> Since Java 21, blocking code can use cheap virtual threads, so the simple thread-per-request style scales to high concurrency. As JEP 444 puts it, virtual threads are not faster threads: they exist to provide scale (higher throughput), not speed (lower latency).</li>
      <li><b>Measured:</b> in our Spring Boot vs Quarkus benchmark, with 1,000 connections to an endpoint that waits 100 ms, blocking Spring served about 1,960 requests a second, the same code with virtual threads about 8,500 to 8,800, and reactive Spring and Quarkus about 9,400 to 9,750.</li>
      <li><b>Our view:</b> start blocking, and turn on virtual threads for I/O-heavy services. Choose reactive when you need streaming or back-pressure, or you already have a reactive stack; do not rewrite a working blocking service for throughput alone without measuring it first. See <a href="#performance">Performance</a>.</li>
      <li><b>The other view:</b> some teams choose reactive from the start, because one model covers streaming, back-pressure and many slow calls, and in the benchmark above it served the most requests.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>The measurement, drawn</h3>
    <figure class="fig"><svg viewBox="0 0 660 220" role="img" aria-labelledby="vtbench-t vtbench-d"><title id="vtbench-t">Requests a second, one benchmark</title><desc id="vtbench-d">A bar chart from the guide's own Spring Boot and Quarkus benchmark: 1,000 connections to an endpoint that waits 100 ms. Blocking Spring served about 1,960 requests a second. The same code with virtual threads served about 8,500 to 8,800. Reactive Spring and Quarkus served about 9,400 to 9,750.</desc><defs><marker id="vtbench-a" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker><marker id="vtbench-aa" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar acc" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker></defs><text class="lb" x="200" y="55" text-anchor="end">blocking Spring</text><rect class="nd" x="210" y="36" width="64.68" height="30" rx="3"/><text class="lb" x="284.68" y="56" text-anchor="start">1,960</text><text class="lb" x="200" y="107" text-anchor="end">+ virtual threads</text><rect class="nd in" x="210" y="88" width="290.40000000000003" height="30" rx="3"/><text class="lb" x="510.40000000000003" y="108" text-anchor="start">8,500–8,800</text><text class="lb" x="200" y="159" text-anchor="end">reactive Spring, Quarkus</text><rect class="nd out" x="210" y="140" width="321.75" height="30" rx="3"/><text class="lb" x="541.75" y="160" text-anchor="start">9,400–9,750</text><line class="ed" x1="210" y1="196" x2="540" y2="196"/><text class="lb mu" x="210" y="212" text-anchor="middle">0</text><text class="lb mu" x="540" y="212" text-anchor="end">10,000 requests a second</text></svg><figcaption>The measured numbers above as bars: one benchmark, one endpoint that waits 100 ms. Virtual threads close most of the gap without rewriting blocking code; measure your own service before choosing.</figcaption></figure>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>REST controller (MVC)</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>@SpringBootApplication
public class App { public static void main(String[] a) { SpringApplication.run(App.class, a); } }

record OrderDto(String orderRef, LocalDate orderDate) {}
record SearchRequest(@NotBlank String q, @Min(1) @Max(100) int size) {}

@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/orders")
class OrderController {
  private final OrderService svc;
  OrderController(OrderService svc) { this.svc = svc; }   // constructor injection

  @GetMapping("/{id}")
  OrderDto get(@PathVariable UUID id) { return svc.get(id); }

  @PostMapping("/search")
  List&lt;OrderDto&gt; search(@Valid @RequestBody SearchRequest req) {
    return svc.search(req);
  }

  @GetMapping
  Page&lt;OrderDto&gt; list(@RequestParam(defaultValue = "0") int page) { ... }
}</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Configuration</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># application.yml
spring:
  application.name: shop-api
  profiles.active: ${APP_PROFILE:local}
  threads.virtual.enabled: true          # 3.2+, Java 21
  datasource:
    url: jdbc:postgresql://${DB_HOST:localhost}:5432/shop
    username: ${DB_USER}
    password: ${DB_PASSWORD}
shop:
  dataset: analytics
  max-results: 50
  stores: ["1_05", "4_12"]   # quote them: unquoted, YAML reads 1_05 as the number 105
---
spring.config.activate.on-profile: prod
logging.structured.format.console: ecs  # 3.4+</code></pre></div>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>@ConfigurationProperties("shop")
@Validated
record ShopProps(@NotBlank String dataset,
                   @DefaultValue("20") int maxResults,
                   List&lt;String&gt; stores) {}

@SpringBootApplication
@ConfigurationPropertiesScan
class App {}</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">Precedence, highest first: command-line args, env vars (<code>SHOP_MAX_RESULTS</code>), profile files, <code>application.yml</code>.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Data access</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>// JdbcClient (3.2): plain SQL, records out
@Repository
class OrderDao {
  private final JdbcClient jdbc;
  OrderDao(JdbcClient jdbc) { this.jdbc = jdbc; }

  Optional&lt;OrderDto&gt; find(String orderRef) {
    return jdbc.sql("SELECT order_ref, order_date FROM orders WHERE order_ref = :order_ref")
               .param("order_ref", orderRef)
               .query(OrderDto.class)
               .optional();
  }
  int upsert(OrderDto o) {
    return jdbc.sql("""
        INSERT INTO orders (order_ref, order_date) VALUES (:order_ref, :d)
        ON CONFLICT (order_ref) DO UPDATE SET order_date = EXCLUDED.order_date""")
      .param("order_ref", o.orderRef()).param("d", o.orderDate()).update();
  }
}

// Spring Data JPA
interface OrderRepo extends JpaRepository&lt;Order, Long&gt; {
  List&lt;Order&gt; findByStoreIdAndOrderDateAfter(String store, LocalDate d);
  @Query("select o from Order o where o.orderRef in :refs")
  List&lt;Order&gt; byOrderRefs(Collection&lt;String&gt; refs);
}

@Transactional            // on a public method of a Spring bean, called from outside it
public void reload() { ... }
@Transactional(readOnly = true)
public List&lt;OrderDto&gt; list() { ... }</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note"><code>@Transactional</code> works through a proxy: a call from another method in the same class skips it.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>HTTP clients</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>// RestClient (3.2): synchronous, fluent
@Bean RestClient supplier(RestClient.Builder b) {
  return b.baseUrl("https://api.example.org").build();
}

Listing l = supplier.get()
    .uri("/objects?prefix={p}", prefix)
    .retrieve()
    .onStatus(HttpStatusCode::is4xxClientError, (req, res) -&gt; {
      throw new IllegalStateException("listing failed: " +
          new String(res.getBody().readAllBytes()));   // keep the error body
    })
    .body(Listing.class);

// Declarative interface client
interface SupplierApi {
  @GetExchange("/objects") Listing list(@RequestParam String prefix);
}
@Bean SupplierApi api(RestClient supplier) {
  return HttpServiceProxyFactory.builderFor(RestClientAdapter.create(supplier))
      .build().createClient(SupplierApi.class);
}</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>WebFlux (reactive)</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>@RestController
class SearchController {
  @GetMapping("/api/search")
  Flux&lt;OrderDto&gt; search(@RequestParam String q) { return svc.search(q); }

  @GetMapping("/api/orders/{id}")
  Mono&lt;ResponseEntity&lt;OrderDto&gt;&gt; get(@PathVariable UUID id) {
    return svc.get(id).map(ResponseEntity::ok)
              .defaultIfEmpty(ResponseEntity.notFound().build());
  }
}

WebClient wc = WebClient.builder().baseUrl(base).build();
Mono&lt;Listing&gt; m = wc.get().uri("/objects").retrieve()
    .bodyToMono(Listing.class)
    .timeout(Duration.ofSeconds(10))
    .retryWhen(Retry.backoff(3, Duration.ofMillis(200)));

// combine
Mono.zip(countWebOrders(), countStoreOrders()).map(t -&gt; t.getT1() + t.getT2());
Flux.fromIterable(uris).flatMap(this::fetch, 8);     // concurrency 8</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">Never call <code>.block()</code> on a request thread. Wrap blocking calls with <code>Mono.fromCallable(...).subscribeOn(Schedulers.boundedElastic())</code>.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Errors as ProblemDetail</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>@RestControllerAdvice
class ApiErrors {
  @ExceptionHandler(NotFoundException.class)
  ProblemDetail notFound(NotFoundException e) {
    ProblemDetail pd = ProblemDetail.forStatusAndDetail(HttpStatus.NOT_FOUND, e.getMessage());
    pd.setTitle("Order not found");
    pd.setProperty("orderRef", e.orderRef());
    return pd;
  }
}
// application.yml: spring.mvc.problemdetails.enabled: true
//                  (spring.webflux.problemdetails.enabled for WebFlux)
// Response: application/problem+json {type,title,status,detail,instance,orderRef}</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Batch job / Cloud Run job</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>// No web server; run once and exit with a code
@SpringBootApplication
class LoaderApp {
  public static void main(String[] args) {
    System.exit(SpringApplication.exit(SpringApplication.run(LoaderApp.class, args)));
  }
  @Bean ApplicationRunner run(Loader loader) {
    return args -&gt; loader.load(args.getOptionValues("year"));   // --year=2024
  }
  @Bean ExitCodeGenerator exitCode(Loader loader) {
    return () -&gt; loader.failed() ? 1 : 0;
  }
}
# spring.main.web-application-type: none

// In-app schedule (only for an always-on service)
@EnableScheduling
@Scheduled(cron = "0 15 6 * * *", zone = "UTC")
void tick() { ... }</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Google Cloud (Spring Cloud GCP)</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>// com.google.cloud:spring-cloud-gcp-starter-bigquery / -storage
// (import spring-cloud-gcp-dependencies BOM for versions)
spring.cloud.gcp.project-id: my-proj
spring.cloud.gcp.bigquery.dataset-name: analytics

@Service
class Counts {
  private final BigQuery bq;                  // auto-configured client
  Counts(BigQuery bq) { this.bq = bq; }
  long total() throws InterruptedException {
    return bq.query(QueryJobConfiguration.of(
        "SELECT COUNT(*) n FROM `my-proj.analytics.orders`"))
      .iterateAll().iterator().next().get("n").getLongValue();
  }
}

// GCS object as a Spring Resource
@Value("gs://landing/manifests/latest.json") Resource manifest;
String json = manifest.getContentAsString(StandardCharsets.UTF_8);</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">Credentials come from Application Default Credentials: <code>gcloud auth application-default login</code> locally, the service account on Cloud Run.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Testing</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>@WebMvcTest(OrderController.class)          // web slice only
class OrderControllerTest {
  @Autowired MockMvc mvc;
  @MockitoBean OrderService svc;            // 3.4+ (was @MockBean)

  @Test void returns404() throws Exception {
    when(svc.get(any())).thenThrow(new NotFoundException("x"));
    mvc.perform(get("/api/orders/{id}", UUID.randomUUID()))
       .andExpect(status().isNotFound())
       .andExpect(jsonPath("$.title").value("Order not found"));
  }
}

@SpringBootTest
@Testcontainers
class DaoIT {
  @Container @ServiceConnection               // 3.1+: wires the datasource
  static PostgreSQLContainer&lt;?&gt; pg = new PostgreSQLContainer&lt;&gt;("postgres:16");  // Boot 3.x (Testcontainers 1.x)
  // Boot 4 uses Testcontainers 2: org.testcontainers.postgresql.PostgreSQLContainer, no &lt;?&gt;
  @Autowired OrderDao dao;
  @Test void upserts() { assertThat(dao.upsert(sample())).isEqualTo(1); }
}

// Other slices: @DataJpaTest, @JdbcTest, @WebFluxTest (+ WebTestClient), @JsonTest
// Reactive: StepVerifier.create(flux).expectNextCount(3).verifyComplete();</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Actuator &amp; observability</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>management:
  endpoints.web.exposure.include: health,info,metrics,prometheus
  endpoint.health.probes.enabled: true    # /actuator/health/liveness, /readiness
  tracing.sampling.probability: 0.1

// custom metric
@Component
class LoadMetrics {
  private final Counter loaded;
  LoadMetrics(MeterRegistry r) { loaded = r.counter("orders.rows.loaded"); }
  void add(long n) { loaded.increment(n); }
}

// custom health
@Component
class SupplierHealth implements HealthIndicator {
  public Health health() {
    return reachable() ? Health.up().build()
                       : Health.down().withDetail("supplier", "unreachable").build();
  }
}</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Build, run, package</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>./gradlew bootRun --args='--spring.profiles.active=local'
./gradlew bootJar                      # build/libs/app.jar
./gradlew bootBuildImage --imageName=app:dev   # OCI image via buildpacks
java -jar app.jar --server.port=8081
SPRING_PROFILES_ACTIVE=prod java -jar app.jar

# Layered jar for smaller Docker layers
java -Djarmode=tools -jar app.jar extract --layers --launcher

# Gradle plugins
plugins {
  id("org.springframework.boot") version "3.5.x"   // matches the 3.x examples; a new project starts on 4.1.x
  id("io.spring.dependency-management") version "1.1.x"
  java
}</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">Replace the <code>x</code> with the current patch version. Let the Boot BOM pick library versions; override only with a reason.</p>
  </article>





















  <article class="card"><h3>Samples we publish</h3>
    <p><a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/spring-boot-samples" target="_blank" rel="noopener">spring-boot-samples</a> holds small, runnable Spring Boot samples, each a self-contained Maven project with its own tests. The first shows auditing every backend update in a blocking app: a transactional outbox, a polling publisher, Kafka and an idempotent consumer.</p>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Build a REST endpoint with validation and a test</li><li>Configure an app with application.yml and profiles</li><li>Use Spring Data or JdbcClient for simple queries</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Handle errors with ProblemDetail; write slice and integration tests</li><li>Use transactions correctly; understand proxies</li><li>Add Actuator, metrics and structured logging</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Choose servlet vs reactive and design for throughput</li><li>Secure APIs with Spring Security and OAuth 2.0</li><li>Tune startup, memory and connection pools for the cloud</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Lead major upgrades (3.x to 4.x) across services</li><li>Build internal starters and platform libraries</li><li>Set architecture and module boundaries (Spring Modulith)</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>Auto-configuration and writing your own starter</li><li>Spring Modulith</li><li>Observability with Micrometer and OpenTelemetry</li><li>GraalVM native images and CDS</li><li>Spring Boot 4 migration</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://docs.spring.io/spring-boot/index.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Spring Boot reference</a><span class="lnote"> Official reference</span></li><li><a href="https://spring.io/guides" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Spring guides</a><span class="lnote"> Short task-focused tutorials</span></li><li><a href="https://docs.spring.io/spring-framework/reference/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Spring Framework reference</a><span class="lnote"> Core, web, data access, testing</span></li><li><a href="https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/wiki" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Spring Boot release notes</a><span class="lnote"> What changed in each version</span></li><li><a href="https://googlecloudplatform.github.io/spring-cloud-gcp/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Spring Cloud GCP</a><span class="lnote"> GCP integrations for Spring</span></li><li><a href="https://testcontainers.com/guides/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Testcontainers guides</a><span class="lnote"> Integration testing with Spring Boot</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://openjdk.org/jeps/444" target="_blank" rel="noopener">JEP 444: Virtual Threads</a>.</p><p class="runcode">A question, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/enrichmeai.github.io/issues/new?title=%5BSpring%20Boot%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>. Nothing here is tracked and there are no comments to store: questions live in the open, where the next learner can find the answer.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= SPRING HISTORY ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-springhistory" data-name="From spring.jar to Spring">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>From spring.jar to Spring</h2><p>How Spring grew from code that came with a book in 2002 into Boot, Cloud, Data, Security, WebFlux, Modulith and AI: what each step added, and the pain it removed.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>The short answer</h3>
    <p>Spring began as example code in a book, shipped as one jar, and grew into a widely used way to build Java services. <i>Our view:</i> each layer was added to remove a pain the previous one left behind. <i>The other view</i> is in the card &ldquo;Our view, and the other view&rdquo;. How to use Spring today is on <a href="#spring">Spring</a>; this page is how it got there.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Twenty years in one line</h3>
    <figure class="fig"><svg viewBox="0 0 640 112" role="img" aria-labelledby="sh-t sh-d"><title id="sh-t">Seven eras of Spring, 2002 to 2022</title><desc id="sh-d">A timeline of seven eras. 2002, the book: J2EE without EJB. 2004, Spring 1.0: one jar and XML beans. 2007, annotations: Autowired and Component. 2009, Spring 3: Java configuration and the jar split into modules. 2014, Spring Boot: no configuration to start. 2017, Spring 5: WebFlux and reactive. 2022, Spring 6: Java 17, Jakarta and native images.</desc><line class="ln" x1="10" y1="30" x2="630" y2="30"/><circle class="dot" cx="54.3" cy="30" r="5"/><text class="lb acc" x="54.3" y="18" text-anchor="middle">2002</text><text class="lb hd" x="54.3" y="56" text-anchor="middle">The book</text><text class="lb mu" x="54.3" y="76" text-anchor="middle">J2EE</text><text class="lb mu" x="54.3" y="92" text-anchor="middle">without EJB</text><circle class="dot" cx="142.9" cy="30" r="5"/><text class="lb acc" x="142.9" y="18" text-anchor="middle">2004</text><text class="lb hd" x="142.9" y="56" text-anchor="middle">Spring 1.0</text><text class="lb mu" x="142.9" y="76" text-anchor="middle">one jar,</text><text class="lb mu" x="142.9" y="92" text-anchor="middle">XML beans</text><circle class="dot" cx="231.4" cy="30" r="5"/><text class="lb acc" x="231.4" y="18" text-anchor="middle">2007</text><text class="lb hd" x="231.4" y="56" text-anchor="middle">Annotations</text><text class="lb mu" x="231.4" y="76" text-anchor="middle">@Autowired,</text><text class="lb mu" x="231.4" y="92" text-anchor="middle">@Component</text><circle class="dot" cx="320.0" cy="30" r="5"/><text class="lb acc" x="320.0" y="18" text-anchor="middle">2009</text><text class="lb hd" x="320.0" y="56" text-anchor="middle">Spring 3</text><text class="lb mu" x="320.0" y="76" text-anchor="middle">Java config,</text><text class="lb mu" x="320.0" y="92" text-anchor="middle">jar split</text><circle class="dot" cx="408.6" cy="30" r="5"/><text class="lb acc" x="408.6" y="18" text-anchor="middle">2014</text><text class="lb hd" x="408.6" y="56" text-anchor="middle">Spring Boot</text><text class="lb mu" x="408.6" y="76" text-anchor="middle">no config</text><text class="lb mu" x="408.6" y="92" text-anchor="middle">to start</text><circle class="dot" cx="497.1" cy="30" r="5"/><text class="lb acc" x="497.1" y="18" text-anchor="middle">2017</text><text class="lb hd" x="497.1" y="56" text-anchor="middle">Spring 5</text><text class="lb mu" x="497.1" y="76" text-anchor="middle">WebFlux,</text><text class="lb mu" x="497.1" y="92" text-anchor="middle">reactive</text><circle class="dot" cx="585.7" cy="30" r="5"/><text class="lb acc" x="585.7" y="18" text-anchor="middle">2022</text><text class="lb hd" x="585.7" y="56" text-anchor="middle">Spring 6</text><text class="lb mu" x="585.7" y="76" text-anchor="middle">Java 17, Jakarta,</text><text class="lb mu" x="585.7" y="92" text-anchor="middle">native</text></svg><figcaption>Seven eras. The years are the release dates in the cards below.</figcaption></figure>
  </article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>1. A reaction to J2EE</h3>
    <p class="note"><b>2002&ndash;2003</b></p>
    <p>In the early 2000s, enterprise Java meant <b>J2EE and EJB 2</b>: an application server, deployment descriptors, home and remote interfaces, and business code you could not test without the container. Rod Johnson&rsquo;s book <b>Expert One-on-One J2EE Design and Development</b> (Wrox, November 2002) argued that most applications did not need all of that. It came with 30,000 lines of framework code showing the alternative: plain Java objects, wired together by a container, with infrastructure kept out of the business code.</p>
    <p>Readers asked to use that code. Juergen Hoeller and Yann Caroff persuaded Johnson to make it an open-source project, and became co-founders. Caroff suggested the name: <b>Spring</b>, a fresh start after the &ldquo;winter&rdquo; of traditional J2EE. The project went public in June 2003.</p>
  </article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>2. Spring 1.0: one jar, three ideas</h3>
    <p class="note"><b>March 2004</b></p>
    <p>Spring 1.0 Final was released on 24 March 2004. It was distributed as one file, <b><code>spring.jar</code></b>, alongside finer-grained module jars for those who wanted less. It rested on three ideas that are still its core:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Inversion of control and dependency injection.</b> The container creates your objects and hands them their dependencies, so classes stay plain and testable.</li>
      <li><b>Aspect-oriented programming.</b> Cross-cutting concerns such as transactions and security are applied around your code, not written inside it.</li>
      <li><b>Templates over raw APIs.</b> <code>JdbcTemplate</code>, Hibernate support and a single transaction abstraction removed the open-try-catch-close boilerplate.</li>
    </ul>
    <pre><code>&lt;bean id="caseService" class="com.example.CaseService"&gt;
    &lt;property name="repository" ref="caseRepository"/&gt;
&lt;/bean&gt;</code></pre>
    <p>Spring MVC was in the box too, as a simpler alternative to Struts. Configuration was <b>XML</b>, and large applications grew large XML files.</p>
  </article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>3. Annotations</h3>
    <p class="note"><b>Spring 2.0 (October 2006) &middot; 2.5 (November 2007)</b></p>
    <p>Java 5 brought annotations, and Spring moved the wiring next to the code. Spring 2.0 added XML configuration based on XML Schema (shorter config) and integrated with the AspectJ pointcut language. Spring 2.5 added <code>@Autowired</code>, <code>@Component</code>, <code>@Service</code> and <code>@Controller</code>, with classpath scanning to find them (<code>@Repository</code> dates from 2.0).</p>
    <pre><code>@Service
public class CaseService {
    @Autowired private CaseRepository repository;
}</code></pre>
    <p>The family began to grow around the core. <b>Acegi Security</b> became an official Spring project at the end of 2007 and was renamed <b>Spring Security</b> (2.0 shipped in April 2008). <b>Spring Web Services</b> (2007), <b>Spring Batch</b> and <b>Spring Integration</b> (2008) followed. The company behind it changed its name too: Interface21 became <b>SpringSource</b> in 2007.</p>
  </article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>4. Spring 3: Java config, and the end of <code>spring.jar</code></h3>
    <p class="note"><b>December 2009</b></p>
    <p>Spring 3.0 made Java 5 the baseline. It brought <b><code>@Configuration</code> classes</b> (configuration written in Java, checked by the compiler), the Spring Expression Language, and REST support in MVC.</p>
    <pre><code>@Configuration
public class AppConfig {
    @Bean CaseService caseService(CaseRepository repo) { return new CaseService(repo); }
}</code></pre>
    <p>It also <b>stopped providing the all-in-one <code>spring.jar</code></b>. The framework was now a set of modules (<code>spring-core</code>, <code>spring-beans</code>, <code>spring-context</code>, <code>spring-jdbc</code>, <code>spring-webmvc</code> and more), and builds pulled in only what they used. VMware bought SpringSource in August 2009, and <b>Spring Data</b> followed in 2010&ndash;11, bringing the repository idea to JPA, MongoDB, Redis and others.</p>
    <p class="note"><b>The new pain:</b> each module was simple, but starting a project meant choosing compatible versions of a dozen modules and writing the same setup every time.</p>
  </article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>5. Spring Boot: convention over configuration</h3>
    <p class="note"><b>Spring 4.0 (December 2013) &middot; Spring Boot 1.0 (April 2014)</b></p>
    <p>Spring 4.0 added first-class Java 8 support, WebSocket and <code>@RestController</code>. The bigger change was <b>Spring Boot</b>, which answered &ldquo;how do I start?&rdquo; with defaults:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Starters:</b> one dependency (<code>spring-boot-starter-web</code>) brings a tested set of libraries and versions.</li>
      <li><b>Auto-configuration:</b> if a database driver is on the classpath, a <code>DataSource</code> is set up for you, and you override only what you need.</li>
      <li><b>An embedded server:</b> Tomcat (or Jetty) inside the jar; <code>java -jar app.jar</code> replaced deploying WAR files to an application server.</li>
      <li><b>Actuator:</b> health, metrics and info endpoints for production.</li>
    </ul>
    <pre><code>@SpringBootApplication
public class App {
    public static void main(String[] args) { SpringApplication.run(App.class, args); }
}</code></pre>
    <p>Boot arrived as microservices and containers took off. <b>Spring Cloud</b> 1.0 (March 2015) paired a configuration server with integrations for Netflix&rsquo;s open-source tools (Eureka, Zuul, Ribbon). Spring moved to <b>Pivotal</b>, spun out of VMware, in 2013; VMware agreed to acquire Pivotal in 2019, and Broadcom completed its purchase of VMware in November 2023.</p>
  </article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>6. Spring 5: reactive</h3>
    <p class="note"><b>September 2017 &middot; Boot 2.0 (March 2018)</b></p>
    <p>Spring 5.0 made Java 8 the baseline and added <b>WebFlux</b>, a second web stack built on Project Reactor, running on Netty or Undertow among others. A few threads that never block serve many connections, with end-to-end backpressure; R2DBC later did the same for databases. It also added first-class <b>Kotlin</b> support and a functional routing model. Spring MVC stayed fully supported, so teams chose per service. The threading story behind this is on <a href="#javathreads">How Java Threads Evolved</a>.</p>
  </article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>7. Spring 6: Java 17, Jakarta and native</h3>
    <p class="note"><b>Spring 6.0 (November 2022) &middot; Boot 3.0 (eight days later)</b></p>
    <p>Spring 6 raised the baseline to <b>Java 17</b> and moved from <code>javax.*</code> to <b><code>jakarta.*</code></b>, following Java EE&rsquo;s move to the Eclipse Foundation. It added first-class support for <b>GraalVM native images</b>, through ahead-of-time processing: applications that start much faster and use less memory. Observability was rebuilt on Micrometer.</p>
    <p>Boot 3.2 (November 2023) added a one-line switch for <b>virtual threads</b>, <code>spring.threads.virtual.enabled=true</code>, plus the simpler <code>RestClient</code> and <code>JdbcClient</code>. <b>Spring Modulith</b> (first release 2022) gave a structure for well-separated modules inside one application, checked by tests, and <b>Spring AI</b> (1.0 in May 2025) brought model clients, prompts and vector stores into the same style. Spring Framework 7 and Boot 4 (November 2025) continue the line, with JSpecify null-safety annotations across the codebase.</p>
  </article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>What stayed the same</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Idea</th><th>2004</th><th>Today</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td><b>Dependency injection</b></td><td>XML <code>&lt;bean&gt;</code> with <code>&lt;property&gt;</code></td><td>Constructor injection, found by scanning or <code>@Bean</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Infrastructure around code</b></td><td>AOP proxies for transactions</td><td>The same proxies, plus ahead-of-time generated code for native images</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Templates over raw APIs</b></td><td><code>JdbcTemplate</code>, <code>HibernateTemplate</code></td><td><code>JdbcClient</code>, <code>RestClient</code>, Spring Data repositories</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Getting started</b></td><td>Copy <code>spring.jar</code>, write XML</td><td><code>start.spring.io</code>, one starter, no XML</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Our view, and the other view</h3>
    <p><i>Our view:</i> Spring grew by adding a layer each time the previous one made something tedious: XML gave way to annotations, annotations to Java config, hand-picked jars to starters, and application servers to an executable jar. The single jar is gone, but plain Java objects with infrastructure kept out of the way are still the point.</p>
    <p><i>The other view:</i> each layer also hid more of what happens at start-up, and auto-configuration can make it hard to see why a bean exists; teams that value explicitness may prefer a smaller framework, or plain Java with fewer conventions (see <a href="#quarkus">Quarkus</a> for one alternative).</p>
  </article>
  <article class="card wide"><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://spring.io/blog/2006/11/09/spring-framework-the-origins-of-a-project-and-a-name" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Spring Framework: the origins of a project and a name (Rod Johnson, 2006)</a>; <a href="https://spring.io/blog/2004/03/24/spring-framework-1-0-final-released" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Spring Framework 1.0 Final Released</a>; <a href="https://docs.spring.io/spring-framework/docs/1.2.8/changelog.txt" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Spring Framework 1.2 changelog</a>; <a href="https://spring.io/blog/2006/10/03/spring-2-0-final-released" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Spring 2.0 Final Released</a>; <a href="https://docs.spring.io/spring-framework/docs/2.5.x/reference/new-in-2.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">What&rsquo;s new in Spring 2.0 and 2.5</a>; <a href="https://spring.io/blog/2007/11/19/spring-framework-2-5-released" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Spring Framework 2.5 Released</a>; <a href="https://repo1.maven.org/maven2/org/springframework/security/spring-security-core/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Spring Security on Maven Central</a>; <a href="https://docs.spring.io/spring-framework/docs/current/javadoc-api/org/springframework/stereotype/Repository.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Javadoc: @Repository (since 2.0)</a>; <a href="https://repo1.maven.org/maven2/org/springframework/batch/spring-batch-core/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Spring Batch on Maven Central</a>; <a href="https://repo1.maven.org/maven2/org/springframework/integration/spring-integration-core/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Spring Integration on Maven Central</a>; <a href="https://repo1.maven.org/maven2/org/springframework/ws/spring-ws-core/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Spring Web Services on Maven Central</a>; <a href="https://repo1.maven.org/maven2/org/springframework/data/spring-data-jpa/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Spring Data JPA on Maven Central</a>; <a href="https://docs.spring.io/spring-security/site/docs/2.0.x/reference/html/introduction.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Spring Security 2.0 reference: introduction</a>; <a href="https://spring.io/blog/2009/12/16/spring-framework-3-0-goes-ga" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Spring Framework 3.0 goes GA</a>; <a href="https://docs.spring.io/spring-framework/docs/3.0.x/reference/new-in-3.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">What&rsquo;s new in Spring 3.0</a>; <a href="https://spring.io/blog/2013/12/12/announcing-spring-framework-4-0-ga-release" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Spring Framework 4.0 GA</a>; <a href="https://spring.io/blog/2014/04/01/spring-boot-1-0-ga-released" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Spring Boot 1.0 GA Released</a>; <a href="https://spring.io/blog/2015/03/04/spring-cloud-1-0-0-available-now" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Spring Cloud 1.0 release announcement</a>; <a href="https://spring.io/blog/2017/09/28/spring-framework-5-0-goes-ga" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Spring Framework 5.0 goes GA</a>; <a href="https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-framework/wiki/Spring-Framework-5.0-Release-Notes" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Spring Framework 5.0 release notes</a>; <a href="https://spring.io/blog/2018/03/01/spring-boot-2-0-goes-ga" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Spring Boot 2.0 goes GA</a>; <a href="https://spring.io/blog/2022/11/16/spring-framework-6-0-goes-ga" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Spring Framework 6.0 goes GA</a>; <a href="https://spring.io/blog/2022/11/24/spring-boot-3-0-goes-ga" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Spring Boot 3.0 goes GA</a>; <a href="https://spring.io/blog/2023/11/23/spring-boot-3-2-0-available-now" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Spring Boot 3.2 release announcement</a>; <a href="https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-framework/wiki/Spring-Framework-7.0-Release-Notes" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Spring Framework 7.0 release notes</a>; <a href="https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/wiki/Spring-Boot-4.0-Migration-Guide" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Spring Boot 4.0 migration guide</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spring_(company)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Spring (company)</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pivotal_Software" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Pivotal Software</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/VMware" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: VMware</a>.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= QUARKUS ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-quarkus" data-name="Quarkus">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Quarkus</h2><p>Quarkus 3.x (4.0 is at release candidate): build-time dependency injection, fast startup, optional native images. Each card maps to its Spring Boot equivalent where one exists.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Spring Boot → Quarkus</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Spring Boot</th><th>Quarkus</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>@RestController, @GetMapping</td><td><code>@Path</code>, <code>@GET</code> (Quarkus REST, Jakarta REST annotations)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>@Service, @Component</td><td><code>@ApplicationScoped</code> (or <code>@Singleton</code>)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>@Autowired</td><td><code>@Inject</code>, or constructor injection</td></tr>
      <tr><td>@Value</td><td><code>@ConfigProperty(name = "…")</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>@ConfigurationProperties</td><td><code>@ConfigMapping(prefix = "…")</code> on an interface</td></tr>
      <tr><td>application-prod.yml</td><td><code>%prod.</code> prefix in <code>application.properties</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>JpaRepository</td><td><code>PanacheRepository&lt;T&gt;</code> or <code>PanacheEntity</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>RestClient / @HttpExchange</td><td><code>@RegisterRestClient</code> interface</td></tr>
      <tr><td>@RestControllerAdvice</td><td><code>@ServerExceptionMapper</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Actuator</td><td>SmallRye Health (<code>/q/health</code>), Micrometer (<code>/q/metrics</code>)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>@SpringBootTest, @MockitoBean</td><td><code>@QuarkusTest</code>, <code>@InjectMock</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Testcontainers @ServiceConnection</td><td>Dev Services (starts the container for you)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Reactor Mono / Flux</td><td>Mutiny <code>Uni</code> / <code>Multi</code></td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>CLI &amp; dev mode</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>quarkus create app org.acme:shop-api \
  --extension='rest-jackson,hibernate-orm-panache,jdbc-postgresql,hibernate-validator'
quarkus ext list --installable
quarkus ext add smallrye-health micrometer-registry-prometheus

quarkus dev                  # live reload; press r to run tests, d for Dev UI
./mvnw quarkus:dev           # same with Maven
./gradlew quarkusDev         # same with Gradle

./mvnw package               # target/quarkus-app/quarkus-run.jar
java -jar target/quarkus-app/quarkus-run.jar
./mvnw package -Dnative      # native binary (GraalVM / Mandrel)
./mvnw package -Dnative -Dquarkus.native.container-build=true   # no local GraalVM</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">Dev UI: <code>http://localhost:8080/q/dev-ui</code>. Ship <code>target/quarkus-app/</code> as a directory; the <code>-run.jar</code> alone is not runnable.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>REST endpoint</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>@Path("/api/orders")
@Produces(MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON)
public class OrderResource {
  private final OrderService svc;
  OrderResource(OrderService svc) { this.svc = svc; }   // constructor injection

  @GET @Path("/{id}")
  public OrderDto get(@RestPath UUID id) { return svc.get(id); }

  @GET
  public List&lt;OrderDto&gt; list(@RestQuery @DefaultValue("0") int page) { ... }

  @POST @Path("/search")
  public List&lt;OrderDto&gt; search(@Valid SearchRequest req) { return svc.search(req); }

  @GET @Path("/{id}/raw")
  public RestResponse&lt;String&gt; raw(@RestPath UUID id) {
    return svc.raw(id).map(RestResponse::ok)
              .orElse(RestResponse.notFound());
  }
}</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Configuration</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># src/main/resources/application.properties
quarkus.http.port=8080
quarkus.datasource.db-kind=postgresql
%prod.quarkus.datasource.jdbc.url=jdbc:postgresql://${DB_HOST}:5432/shop
%prod.quarkus.datasource.username=${DB_USER}
%prod.quarkus.datasource.password=${DB_PASSWORD}
quarkus.hibernate-orm.schema-management.strategy=none
# JSON console logs: add the quarkus-logging-json extension
shop.dataset=analytics
shop.stores=1_05,4_12</code></pre></div>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>@ConfigMapping(prefix = "shop")
public interface ShopConfig {
  String dataset();
  @WithDefault("20") int maxResults();
  List&lt;String&gt; stores();
}

@ConfigProperty(name = "shop.dataset") String dataset;   // single value</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">Profiles: <code>dev</code>, <code>test</code>, <code>prod</code> built in; pick another with <code>-Dquarkus.profile=staging</code>. Env vars override: <code>SHOP_DATASET</code>.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Data with Panache</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>// Active record
@Entity
public class Order extends PanacheEntity {     // gives Long id
  public String orderRef;
  public LocalDate orderDate;

  public static Optional&lt;Order&gt; byOrderRef(String orderRef) {
    return find("orderRef", orderRef).firstResultOptional();
  }
}

// Repository style
@ApplicationScoped
public class OrderRepo implements PanacheRepository&lt;Order&gt; {
  public List&lt;Order&gt; recent(String store) {
    return list("storeId = ?1 and orderDate &gt; ?2 order by orderDate desc",
                store, LocalDate.now().minusDays(30));
  }
}

@Transactional
public void save(Order o) { o.persist(); }

Order.find("storeId", "1_05").page(Page.of(0, 50)).list();
Order.count("orderDate is null");</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">Dev Services: in dev and test, with no JDBC URL set, Quarkus starts a Postgres container for you (needs Docker or Podman).</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>REST client</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>@RegisterRestClient(configKey = "supplier")
@Path("/objects")
public interface SupplierClient {
  @GET Listing list(@RestQuery String prefix);
  @GET @Path("/{key}") Uni&lt;byte[]&gt; fetch(@RestPath String key);   // reactive
}

# application.properties
quarkus.rest-client.supplier.url=https://api.example.org
quarkus.rest-client.supplier.read-timeout=10000

@Inject @RestClient SupplierClient supplier;

// fault tolerance (quarkus-smallrye-fault-tolerance)
@Retry(maxRetries = 3, delay = 200)
@Timeout(10_000)
@CircuitBreaker(requestVolumeThreshold = 10)
public Listing safeList(String p) { return supplier.list(p); }</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Threading: blocking, reactive, virtual</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>// Blocking signature → worker thread (default for non-Uni returns)
@GET public List&lt;OrderDto&gt; list() { ... }

// Reactive signature → event loop: never block inside it
@GET public Uni&lt;OrderDto&gt; get(@RestPath UUID id) {
  return client.fetch(id)
      .onItem().transform(this::toDto)
      .onFailure().retry().atMost(3)
      .ifNoItem().after(Duration.ofSeconds(5)).fail();
}

Multi.createFrom().iterable(uris)
     .onItem().transformToUniAndMerge(this::fetch);   // concurrent

// Virtual threads (Java 21+)
@GET @RunOnVirtualThread
public List&lt;OrderDto&gt; slowList() { return blockingDao.all(); }

// Force a reactive-typed method onto a worker
@Blocking</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Errors, health, metrics</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>public class ErrorMappers {
  @ServerExceptionMapper
  public RestResponse&lt;Map&lt;String, String&gt;&gt; notFound(NotFoundException e) {
    return RestResponse.status(Response.Status.NOT_FOUND,
        Map.of("error", "not_found", "orderRef", e.orderRef()));
  }
}

@Readiness @ApplicationScoped
public class SupplierCheck implements HealthCheck {
  public HealthCheckResponse call() {
    return HealthCheckResponse.named("supplier").status(reachable()).build();
  }
}
// /q/health/live  /q/health/ready  /q/health/started

@Inject MeterRegistry registry;
registry.counter("orders.rows.loaded").increment(n);
@Counted @Timed      // on methods</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Jobs &amp; scheduling</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>// Command mode: run once, exit with a code (Cloud Run job, K8s Job)
@QuarkusMain
public class LoaderMain implements QuarkusApplication {
  @Inject Loader loader;
  @Override public int run(String... args) {
    return loader.load(args) ? 0 : 1;
  }
}
// java -jar quarkus-run.jar --year=2024     (or quarkus-picocli for real flags)

// In-app schedule (quarkus-scheduler); cron has a seconds field
@Scheduled(cron = "0 15 6 * * ?", timeZone = "UTC")
void tick() { ... }

@Scheduled(every = "10m", concurrentExecution = Scheduled.ConcurrentExecution.SKIP)
void poll() { ... }</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Testing</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>@QuarkusTest
class OrderResourceTest {
  @InjectMock OrderService svc;        // quarkus-junit-mockito (before 3.31: quarkus-junit5-mockito)

  @Test void returns404() {
    when(svc.get(any())).thenThrow(new NotFoundException("x"));
    given().when().get("/api/orders/{id}", UUID.randomUUID())
           .then().statusCode(404)
           .body("error", is("not_found"));
  }
}

@QuarkusIntegrationTest                    // runs against the built jar or native binary
class OrderResourceIT extends OrderResourceTest {}

@QuarkusTest
@TestProfile(NoSchedulerProfile.class)     // per-test config overrides
class SchedulerOffTest { /* tests here run with the overrides below */ }

public class NoSchedulerProfile implements QuarkusTestProfile {
  public Map&lt;String, String&gt; getConfigOverrides() {
    return Map.of("quarkus.scheduler.enabled", "false");
  }
}</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Containers, Kubernetes, GCP</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>quarkus ext add container-image-jib kubernetes
./mvnw package -Dquarkus.container-image.build=true \
  -Dquarkus.container-image.registry=asia-south1-docker.pkg.dev \
  -Dquarkus.container-image.group=my-proj/apps
# generated manifests: target/kubernetes/kubernetes.yml

quarkus.kubernetes.replicas=2
quarkus.kubernetes.resources.requests.memory=256Mi
quarkus.kubernetes.env.secrets=db-credentials

# Google Cloud clients come from the Quarkiverse extensions, e.g.
#   io.quarkiverse.googlecloudservices:quarkus-google-cloud-bigquery
#   ...:quarkus-google-cloud-storage
@Inject BigQuery bigquery;
@Inject Storage storage;</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">Native images start far faster and use less memory, at a cost in throughput (the Quarkus team measured native at about half), which suits Cloud Run and scale-to-zero. Libraries that rely on reflection need native config; test the native build in CI.</p>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Create a project and run dev mode</li><li>Write a REST endpoint and a @QuarkusTest</li><li>Configure with application.properties and profiles</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Use Panache, REST clients and fault tolerance</li><li>Choose blocking, reactive or virtual-thread endpoints</li><li>Package as a container; use Dev Services</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Build and run native images; fix reflection issues</li><li>Design for Kubernetes and serverless startup</li><li>Migrate services from Spring with clear trade-offs</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Write Quarkus extensions</li><li>Set framework strategy across Spring and Quarkus estates</li><li>Understand build-time processing and ArC internals</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>Mutiny reactive programming</li><li>Native image configuration</li><li>Writing extensions</li><li>Quarkus with Kafka and messaging</li><li>Command-mode applications</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://quarkus.io/guides/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Quarkus guides</a><span class="lnote"> Task-focused official guides</span></li><li><a href="https://quarkus.io/get-started/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Get started with Quarkus</a><span class="lnote"> Install and first app</span></li><li><a href="https://code.quarkus.io/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">code.quarkus.io</a><span class="lnote"> Generate a project with chosen extensions</span></li><li><a href="https://smallrye.io/smallrye-mutiny/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">SmallRye Mutiny</a><span class="lnote"> Reactive programming library used by Quarkus</span></li><li><a href="https://docs.quarkiverse.io/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Quarkiverse</a><span class="lnote"> Community extensions, including Google Cloud</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://repo1.maven.org/maven2/io/quarkus/quarkus-core/maven-metadata.xml" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Maven Central: quarkus-core versions (the newest is a release candidate, with no final release yet)</a>; <a href="https://repo1.maven.org/maven2/io/quarkus/quarkus-junit-mockito/maven-metadata.xml" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Maven Central: quarkus-junit-mockito (first version</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/quarkusio/quarkus/main/docs/src/main/asciidoc/hibernate-orm.adoc" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Quarkus Hibernate ORM guide (schema-management.strategy)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/quarkusio/quarkus/main/extensions/hibernate-orm/deployment/src/main/resources/META-INF/quarkus-skill.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Quarkus Hibernate ORM skill notes (database.generation deprecated)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/quarkusio/quarkus/main/docs/src/main/asciidoc/getting-started-testing.adoc" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Quarkus testing guide (@TestProfile on the test class, @InjectMock)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/quarkusio/quarkus/main/docs/src/main/asciidoc/cli-tooling.adoc" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Quarkus CLI guide (--installable)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/quarkusio/quarkus/main/docs/src/main/asciidoc/dev-ui.adoc" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Quarkus Dev UI guide (d opens Dev UI)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/quarkusio/quarkus/main/docs/src/main/asciidoc/continuous-testing.adoc" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Quarkus continuous testing (r re-runs tests)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/quarkusio/quarkus/main/docs/src/main/asciidoc/getting-started.adoc" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Quarkus getting started (fast-jar, ship quarkus-app/)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/quarkusio/quarkus/main/docs/src/main/asciidoc/scheduler-reference.adoc" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Quarkus scheduler reference (timeZone, ConcurrentExecution.SKIP)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/quarkusio/quarkus/main/docs/src/main/asciidoc/smallrye-health.adoc" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Quarkus SmallRye Health (/q/health/live, ready, started)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/quarkusio/quarkus/main/extensions/resteasy-classic/rest-client-config/runtime/src/main/java/io/quarkus/restclient/config/RestClientsConfig.java" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Quarkus REST client config source (read-timeout)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/quarkusio/quarkus/main/docs/src/main/asciidoc/deploying-to-kubernetes.adoc" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Quarkus Kubernetes guide (replicas, env.secrets, target/kubernetes)</a>; <a href="https://repo1.maven.org/maven2/io/quarkiverse/googlecloudservices/quarkus-google-cloud-bigquery/maven-metadata.xml" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Maven Central: Quarkiverse quarkus-google-cloud-bigquery</a>; <a href="https://smallrye.io/smallrye-mutiny/latest/tutorials/retrying/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">SmallRye Mutiny: retrying</a>; <a href="https://smallrye.io/smallrye-mutiny/latest/guides/handling-timeouts/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">SmallRye Mutiny: handling timeouts</a>; <a href="https://github.com/quarkusio/quarkusio.github.io/blob/a1d77b2bd0eae64e7492209262ecfa3ff60233e4/content/posts/2026/2026-03-02-new-benchmarks.adoc" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Quarkus blog: new benchmarks (native trade-off)</a>. A few claims could not be confirmed at their official source from here and are being re-checked.</p><p class="runcode">A question, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/enrichmeai.github.io/issues/new?title=%5BQuarkus%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>. Nothing here is tracked and there are no comments to store: questions live in the open, where the next learner can find the answer.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= FADED TECHNOLOGIES ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-faded" data-name="Faded? Rails, Flex &amp; Grails">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Faded? Ruby on Rails, Adobe Flex and Grails</h2><p>Some technologies that many teams once built on are mentioned less today. This page looks at three, what each introduced, and where each stands now, from their own repositories and histories. The answer differs: one runtime was switched off, while the other two are still releasing. The browser side of the story is on <a href="#uihistory">UI History</a>; today&rsquo;s JVM frameworks are on <a href="#spring">Spring</a> and <a href="#quarkus">Quarkus</a>.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>The short answer</h3>
    <p><b>Flex has gone</b> with the Flash runtime it ran on. <b>Rails and Grails have not:</b> both were still tagging new versions in 2026. <i>Our view:</i> &ldquo;faded&rdquo; describes how often they are talked about more than whether they work, and many of their ideas now live in other frameworks.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Then and now</h3>
    <figure class="fig"><svg viewBox="0 0 640 290" role="img" aria-labelledby="tl-t tl-d"><title id="tl-t">Rails, Flex and Grails, 2004 to 2026</title><desc id="tl-d">A timeline with three lanes. Rails: first open-source release July 2004, version 8.0 in November 2024, and still tagging releases in 2026. Grails: version 0.1 in March 2006, 1.0 in February 2008, and a version 8 tag in October 2026 under the Apache Software Foundation. Flex: given to Apache by Adobe in 2011, the Flash Player it needs deprecated in July 2017, last Flex SDK release 4.16 in November 2017 and unsupported after December 2020.</desc><line class="ln" x1="110" y1="250" x2="620" y2="250"/><line class="ln" x1="110.0" y1="250" x2="110.0" y2="256"/><text class="lb mu" x="110.0" y="272" text-anchor="middle">2004</text><line class="ln" x1="198.69565217391306" y1="250" x2="198.69565217391306" y2="256"/><text class="lb mu" x="198.69565217391306" y="272" text-anchor="middle">2008</text><line class="ln" x1="287.3913043478261" y1="250" x2="287.3913043478261" y2="256"/><text class="lb mu" x="287.3913043478261" y="272" text-anchor="middle">2012</text><line class="ln" x1="376.0869565217391" y1="250" x2="376.0869565217391" y2="256"/><text class="lb mu" x="376.0869565217391" y="272" text-anchor="middle">2016</text><line class="ln" x1="464.7826086956522" y1="250" x2="464.7826086956522" y2="256"/><text class="lb mu" x="464.7826086956522" y="272" text-anchor="middle">2020</text><line class="ln" x1="553.4782608695652" y1="250" x2="553.4782608695652" y2="256"/><text class="lb mu" x="553.4782608695652" y="272" text-anchor="middle">2024</text><text class="lb hd" x="20" y="60" text-anchor="start">Rails</text><line class="ed" x1="122.19565217391204" y1="55" x2="613.3478260869575" y2="55"/><circle class="dot" cx="122.19565217391204" cy="55" r="5"/><text class="lb" x="122.19565217391204" y="43" text-anchor="start">open source</text><circle class="dot" cx="572.3260869565197" cy="55" r="5"/><text class="lb" x="572.3260869565197" y="43" text-anchor="end">8.0</text><circle class="dot" cx="613.3478260869575" cy="55" r="5"/><text class="lb" x="613.3478260869575" y="77" text-anchor="end">still tagging</text><text class="lb hd" x="20" y="135" text-anchor="start">Grails</text><line class="ed" x1="159.8913043478261" y1="130" x2="614.4565217391305" y2="130"/><circle class="dot" cx="159.8913043478261" cy="130" r="5"/><text class="lb" x="159.8913043478261" y="118" text-anchor="middle">0.1</text><circle class="dot" cx="201.57826086956766" cy="130" r="5"/><text class="lb" x="201.57826086956766" y="118" text-anchor="middle">1.0</text><circle class="dot" cx="614.4565217391305" cy="130" r="5"/><text class="lb" x="614.4565217391305" y="152" text-anchor="end">v8 at Apache</text><text class="lb hd" x="20" y="205" text-anchor="start">Flex</text><line class="ed dash" x1="276.304347826087" y1="200" x2="486.95652173913044" y2="200"/><circle class="dot" cx="276.304347826087" cy="200" r="5"/><text class="lb" x="276.304347826087" y="188" text-anchor="middle">to Apache</text><circle class="dot" cx="410.4565217391294" cy="200" r="5"/><text class="lb" x="410.4565217391294" y="222" text-anchor="middle">Flash deprecated</text><circle class="dot" cx="418.21739130434986" cy="200" r="5"/><text class="lb" x="418.21739130434986" y="188" text-anchor="middle">last SDK, 4.16</text><circle class="dot" cx="486.95652173913044" cy="200" r="5"/><text class="lb" x="486.95652173913044" y="222" text-anchor="start">Flash unsupported</text></svg><figcaption>A timeline of the dates on this page, each from its source. Rails and Grails were still tagging new versions in 2026; Flex&rsquo;s last SDK release was in 2017, and the Flash runtime it needs lost support at the end of 2020 (dashed line).</figcaption></figure>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Ruby on Rails</h3>
    <p>A server-side model&ndash;view&ndash;controller web framework in Ruby, under the MIT licence. David Heinemeier Hansson extracted it from the project management tool Basecamp at 37signals and released it as open source in July 2004.</p>
    <p><b>What it introduced:</b> it emphasises <i>convention over configuration</i>, a term Hansson used to describe Rails&rsquo; philosophy, and <i>don&rsquo;t repeat yourself</i>. A model class <code>User</code> maps by default to a table <code>users</code>; database migrations and scaffolding of views made it quick to build an application. Wikipedia lists Django, Laravel, CakePHP and Grails among the frameworks it influenced.</p>
    <p><b>Now:</b> Rails 8.0 was released in November 2024 and 8.1 in October 2025; the repository tagged v<span data-other-version>8.1.4</span> in September 2026. Sites that use it include GitHub and Shopify.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Grails</h3>
    <p>An open-source web framework for the Java platform in the Groovy language, following the same <i>coding by convention</i> idea. It was first called &ldquo;Groovy on Rails&rdquo; and dropped that name in March 2006 at Hansson&rsquo;s request. Version 0.1 came out in March 2006 and 1.0 in February 2008.</p>
    <p><b>What it brought:</b> Rails-style productivity on the JVM, re-using existing Java technologies, Hibernate and Spring, under one interface.</p>
    <p><b>Now:</b> it is <b>Apache Grails</b>, developed at the Apache Software Foundation; its repository tagged v<span data-other-version>8.0.0</span> in October 2026. Groovy itself lives on in Gradle build files and Jenkins pipelines (see <a href="#buildtools">Build Tools</a> and <a href="#harness">Harness</a>).</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Adobe Flex</h3>
    <p>A software development kit for cross-platform rich web applications on the Adobe Flash platform, first developed by Macromedia. User interfaces were laid out in MXML, with ActionScript for behaviour, and applications needed Flash Player or Adobe AIR to run. Adobe gave Flex to the Apache Software Foundation in 2011.</p>
    <p><b>Why it ended:</b> it depended on one runtime. The iPhone and iPad did not support Flash Player; Adobe stopped developing Flash Player for mobile, deprecated Flash in July 2017, ended support at the end of 2020, and began blocking Flash content on 12 January 2021. Apache Flex&rsquo;s latest release listed on Wikipedia is <span data-other-version>4.16.1</span>, in November 2017. Its successor project, Apache Royale (formerly FlexJS), compiles ActionScript to JavaScript instead.</p>
    <p class="note"><i>Analysis:</i> the declarative idea survived, describing a user interface as nested components in a markup language, as JSX does today (see <a href="#fetoolchain">Front-end Toolchain</a>); the dependence on one vendor&rsquo;s runtime did not.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Why technologies fade (<i>analysis</i>)</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Cause</th><th>Seen here</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>The runtime goes away</td><td>Flex could not outlive Flash Player</td></tr>
      <tr><td>The platform shifts</td><td>Phones and tablets without Flash; HTML5 took over video and interactive content</td></tr>
      <tr><td>The ideas are absorbed</td><td>Convention over configuration, migrations and scaffolding spread to frameworks in other languages, so choosing Rails for them alone became less necessary</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Attention moves on</td><td>A framework can keep releasing while fewer people write about it; being discussed less is not the same as being unsupported</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Choose by requirement: you maintain one of these</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>If this is true for you</th><th>It leans towards (<i>analysis</i>)</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>The application runs in Flash Player</td><td>Replace it: the runtime has had no general support since 2020, and browsers block Flash content</td></tr>
      <tr><td>A Rails or Grails application on a version that still gets fixes</td><td>Keep it, and upgrade on the project&rsquo;s schedule; check each project&rsquo;s own maintenance policy</td></tr>
      <tr><td>An old major version that no longer gets security fixes</td><td>Upgrade in steps, one major version at a time, with tests around the behaviour first (see <a href="#testing">Testing</a>)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>You cannot hire or keep people who know the stack</td><td>Weigh a gradual move, piece by piece behind the same interface (see <a href="#atob">Solution Architecture: A to B</a>)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Your team knows the stack well and it meets the requirements</td><td>Stay: a rewrite has its own cost and risk</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Our view, and the other view</h3>
    <p><i>Our view:</i> judge a framework by whether it is maintained and whether it fits your requirements, not by how often it is in the news. The ideas these three introduced are worth learning even if you never use them.</p>
    <p><i>The other view:</i> attention matters in practice: fewer new tutorials, libraries and people to hire make a stack harder to staff and extend, so choosing what most teams use lowers that risk, even if an older framework still works well.</p>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Say what convention over configuration means</li><li>Name what each of Rails, Grails and Flex introduced</li><li>Check whether a framework is still releasing</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Read a framework's maintenance policy before upgrading</li><li>Upgrade a framework one major version at a time</li><li>Recognise a dependency on a runtime one vendor controls</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Decide whether to keep, upgrade or replace an older application</li><li>Plan a gradual migration behind the same interface</li><li>Weigh staffing risk against rewrite risk</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Set an organisation's policy for ageing stacks</li><li>Spot single-vendor runtime risk early</li><li>Carry good ideas from older frameworks into new ones</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>Ruby on Rails and convention over configuration</li><li>Grails, Groovy and the JVM</li><li>Adobe Flex, MXML and the Flash runtime</li><li>Flash end of life</li><li>Why technologies fade</li><li>Keep, upgrade or replace</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://github.com/rails/rails" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ruby on Rails repository</a><span class="lnote"> Releases and tags</span></li><li><a href="https://github.com/apache/grails-core" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Apache Grails repository</a><span class="lnote"> Releases and tags</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ruby_on_Rails" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Ruby on Rails</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Convention_over_configuration" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Convention over configuration</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grails_(framework)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Grails (framework)</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apache_Flex" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Apache Flex</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adobe_Flash" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Adobe Flash</a>; <a href="https://github.com/rails/rails/tags" target="_blank" rel="noopener">rails/rails tags on GitHub</a>; <a href="https://github.com/apache/grails-core" target="_blank" rel="noopener">apache/grails-core on GitHub</a>.</p><p class="runcode">A question, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/enrichmeai.github.io/issues/new?title=%5BFaded%3F%20Rails%2C%20Flex%20%26%20Grails%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>. Nothing here is tracked and there are no comments to store: questions live in the open, where the next learner can find the answer.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= TESTING ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-designux" data-name="Design Thinking &amp; UX">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Design thinking and user experience</h2><p>Building the right thing before building it right: understand the people, frame the problem, try ideas cheaply, and test with real users. It applies to an API, an internal tool or a data product as much as to an app.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card"><h3>Design thinking</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Start with empathy:</b> understand the people you are designing for, their goals, context and frustrations, before you decide what to build.</li>
      <li><b>Frame the problem</b> in their words. A well-framed problem is half solved; a badly framed one gets a polished answer to the wrong question.</li>
      <li><b>Generate many ideas,</b> then sketch and prototype the promising ones as cheaply as possible.</li>
      <li><b>Test and iterate:</b> put the prototype in front of real people, learn, and change it. Repeat.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>The Double Diamond</h3>
    <p>A design process model popularised by the British Design Council in 2005, in four phases:</p>
    <ol>
      <li><b>Discover:</b> go wide; research the problem and the people.</li>
      <li><b>Define:</b> narrow down to the real problem.</li>
      <li><b>Develop:</b> go wide again; explore possible solutions.</li>
      <li><b>Deliver:</b> narrow down; test, choose and ship.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="note">Two diamonds: diverge then converge on the problem, then on the solution.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>UX practices engineers can use</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Talk to users</b> before building, and watch them use what you built. What people do often differs from what they say.</li>
      <li><b>Usability testing with a few people</b> finds most big problems: Jakob Nielsen's advice is to test with no more than five users and run as many small tests as you can afford.</li>
      <li><b>Heuristic evaluation:</b> review a design against a short list of usability principles, a method developed by Rolf Molich and Jakob Nielsen.</li>
      <li><b>Personas and journeys</b> keep real people in mind: who they are, what they are trying to do, and where it goes wrong.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Good UX is good engineering</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Clear errors:</b> say what went wrong and how to fix it, in plain words. See <a href="#apis">APIs</a> for good error responses.</li>
      <li><b>Fast feels good:</b> speed is part of the experience. See <a href="#performance">Performance</a> and Core Web Vitals on <a href="#web">Web Fundamentals</a>.</li>
      <li><b>Consistency:</b> the same thing looks and behaves the same everywhere.</li>
      <li><b>Accessible by default:</b> design for everyone from the start. See <a href="#accessibility">Accessibility</a>.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><p class="sources"><b>Checked against</b> (Wikipedia): <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Design_thinking" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Design thinking</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Double_Diamond_(design_process_model)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Double Diamond</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Usability_testing" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Usability testing</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heuristic_evaluation" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Heuristic evaluation</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Persona_(user_experience)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Persona (user experience)</a>.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<section class="panel" id="p-accessibility" data-name="Accessibility">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Accessibility: software for everyone</h2><p>People use software with screen readers, keyboards, voice, magnification and captions, permanently or for a while after an injury. Accessible design helps all of them, and usually makes the product better for everyone else too.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>WCAG 2.2 in one card</h3>
    <p>The Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) are the W3C standard for accessible web content; WCAG 2.2 became a W3C Recommendation on 5 October 2023. They are organised under four principles, often remembered as <b>POUR</b>:</p>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <tr><th>Principle</th><th>Means</th><th>Examples</th></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Perceivable</b></td><td>People can see or hear the content, one way or another</td><td>Text alternatives for images, captions for video, enough contrast</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Operable</b></td><td>People can use it, whatever their input</td><td>Everything works by keyboard, no keyboard traps, targets big enough to hit</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Understandable</b></td><td>People can follow it</td><td>The page language is declared, labels and errors are clear, behaviour is predictable</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Robust</b></td><td>It works with assistive technology</td><td>Every control exposes a name, role and value</td></tr>
    </table></div>
    <p class="note">Each success criterion has a level, A, AA or AAA; AA is the common target. Two AA examples: normal text needs a contrast ratio of at least 4.5:1, and in 2.2 pointer targets should be at least 24 by 24 CSS pixels, with some exceptions.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Start with semantic HTML</h3>
    <pre><code class="language-html">&lt;!-- a real button: focusable, works with Enter and Space, announced as a button --&gt;
&lt;button type="button"&gt;Save order&lt;/button&gt;

&lt;!-- every input has a label --&gt;
&lt;label for="email"&gt;Email&lt;/label&gt;
&lt;input id="email" type="email" autocomplete="email"&gt;

&lt;!-- meaningful image: describe it; decorative image: empty alt --&gt;
&lt;img src="chart.png" alt="Orders rose 20% in March"&gt;
&lt;img src="divider.png" alt=""&gt;</code></pre>
    <p class="note">Native HTML elements come with keyboard support and the right role for free. Reach for ARIA only when no native element does the job.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Test it</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Unplug the mouse:</b> can you reach and use everything with Tab, Enter, Space and the arrow keys, and always see where the focus is?</li>
      <li><b>Use a screen reader</b> on your own pages for ten minutes; it changes how you write markup.</li>
      <li><b>Zoom to 200%</b> and use a phone-width screen: does anything break or hide?</li>
      <li><b>Automated checks</b> (axe, Lighthouse) catch a share of issues; they cannot judge whether alt text or labels make sense. Do both. See <a href="#testing">Testing</a>.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>It is also the law</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>European Union:</b> under the European Accessibility Act, as of 28 June 2025, newly marketed products and services it covers must be accessible.</li>
      <li><b>Many countries</b> have similar laws for public-sector and commercial services. Check the rules where your users are.</li>
      <li><b>Beyond the law:</b> captions help people in noisy places, contrast helps in sunlight, keyboard support helps power users. Accessibility is quality.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">WCAG 2.2 (W3C)</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Web_Content_Accessibility_Guidelines" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (Wikipedia)</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/WAI-ARIA" target="_blank" rel="noopener">WAI-ARIA (Wikipedia)</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/European_Accessibility_Act" target="_blank" rel="noopener">European Accessibility Act (Wikipedia)</a>; <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/Accessibility" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MDN: Accessibility</a>.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<section class="panel" id="p-apps" data-name="Apps: Native or Cross-platform">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Apps: native or cross-platform?</h2><p>Where app development is going, and the honest trade-offs between building once per platform and sharing code. See <a href="#uihistory">UI history</a> for how we got here.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>The options</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <tr><th>Approach</th><th>What it is</th><th>Good for</th><th>Watch out for</th></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Native, per platform</b></td><td>Swift and SwiftUI on Apple platforms; Kotlin and Jetpack Compose on Android</td><td>The best platform feel, newest features first, the deepest device access</td><td>Two codebases, two skill sets, features that drift apart</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Shared logic, native UI</b></td><td>Kotlin Multiplatform: share business logic, keep each platform's own UI (or share UI too with Compose Multiplatform)</td><td>Teams that want one copy of the rules but a native look</td><td>Newer tooling; you still need people who know each platform</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>One UI toolkit everywhere</b></td><td>Flutter (Dart), which draws its own widgets from a single codebase</td><td>One team shipping the same app to several platforms fast</td><td>Looks the same everywhere unless you adapt it; a further language to learn</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Web skills, native components</b></td><td>React Native: React and JavaScript or TypeScript, mapped to the platform's own UI building blocks</td><td>Teams that already build for the web with React</td><td>A bridge layer between JavaScript and native code to understand and upgrade</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>The web itself</b></td><td>Progressive web apps (installable, can work offline), or a web app inside a native shell (Capacitor)</td><td>Reach, one codebase, no store review for every change (PWA)</td><td>Device APIs and store presence vary by platform</td></tr>
    </table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>How to choose</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Start from the product, not the framework.</b> Does it need the camera, sensors, background work, or the latest platform features on day one? That pushes towards native.</li>
      <li><b>Count the people.</b> One small team rarely keeps two native apps equal; a shared approach often wins there.</li>
      <li><b>Share the rules, not necessarily the screens.</b> Business logic and data code are the safest things to share; UI is where platforms differ most.</li>
      <li><b>Try the web first</b> if the app is mostly forms and content. A good responsive web app may be all you need. See <a href="#web">Web Fundamentals</a>.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Where it is going</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Declarative UI everywhere:</b> SwiftUI, Jetpack Compose, Flutter and React all describe what the screen should show and let the framework work out the changes.</li>
      <li><b>Kotlin is Google's preferred language for Android</b> (announced 7 May 2019), and Kotlin Multiplatform was declared stable in November 2023.</li>
      <li><b>Sharing code is now a normal choice,</b> not a compromise, for many apps; truly native work remains the right call for the most demanding ones.</li>
      <li><b>There is no single way out.</b> Each option trades reach, feel, cost and skills differently, and the answer can change as the app grows.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Explain native, cross-platform and web app options</li><li>Build and run a sample app on one platform</li><li>Know what an app store review involves</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Ship a feature on one mobile platform, with tests</li><li>Share business logic across platforms</li><li>Build an installable, offline-capable web app</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Choose an app architecture for a product and team</li><li>Design offline, sync and push notifications</li><li>Plan releases, versions and backwards compatibility</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Own mobile and desktop strategy across products</li><li>Lead a move between app technologies</li><li>Set performance, accessibility and security standards for apps</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>SwiftUI</li><li>Jetpack Compose</li><li>Kotlin Multiplatform</li><li>Flutter</li><li>React Native</li><li>Progressive web apps</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://developer.android.com/compose" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jetpack Compose</a><span class="lnote"> Android's modern UI toolkit</span></li><li><a href="https://developer.apple.com/swiftui/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">SwiftUI</a><span class="lnote"> Apple's declarative UI framework</span></li><li><a href="https://kotlinlang.org/docs/multiplatform.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kotlin Multiplatform</a><span class="lnote"> Share code across platforms</span></li><li><a href="https://docs.flutter.dev/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Flutter documentation</a><span class="lnote"> Multiplatform apps from a single codebase</span></li><li><a href="https://reactnative.dev/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">React Native</a><span class="lnote"> React for native apps</span></li><li><a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/Progressive_web_apps" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MDN: Progressive web apps</a><span class="lnote"> Installable, offline-capable web apps</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://developer.android.com/compose" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jetpack Compose</a>; <a href="https://developer.apple.com/swiftui/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">SwiftUI</a>; <a href="https://docs.flutter.dev/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Flutter docs</a>; <a href="https://reactnative.dev/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">React Native</a>; <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/Progressive_web_apps" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MDN: Progressive web apps</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kotlin" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kotlin (Wikipedia): Google's preferred language, 7 May 2019</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kotlin_Multiplatform" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kotlin Multiplatform (Wikipedia): stable, November 2023</a>.</p><p class="runcode">A question, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/enrichmeai.github.io/issues/new?title=%5BApps%3A%20Native%20or%20Cross-platform%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>. Nothing here is tracked and there are no comments to store: questions live in the open, where the next learner can find the answer.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<section class="panel" id="p-uihistory" data-name="UI History">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>A short history of user interfaces</h2><p>From text screens to declarative UIs: each step made it easier to describe what people should see, and moved more work from the programmer to the framework.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Timeline</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <tr><th>When</th><th>What</th><th>Why it mattered</th></tr>
      <tr><td>Before the 1980s</td><td>Text terminals and command lines</td><td>Type a command, read the answer. Still used for many engineering tasks. See <a href="#linux">Linux</a>.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>1973</td><td>Xerox Alto</td><td>An early computer with a graphical interface and a mouse.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>1984</td><td>Apple Macintosh</td><td>Brought the graphical interface to a mass-market personal computer.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>1985</td><td>Windows 1.0 (November 1985)</td><td>Microsoft's graphical environment on top of DOS.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>1993</td><td>Mosaic web browser</td><td>Made the web graphical and popular; pages were documents, not applications.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>1995 onwards</td><td>Java applets</td><td>Programs inside the browser. Browsers dropped the plug-in support they needed in the 2010s.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>1996 to 2020</td><td>Flash</td><td>Rich animation and video in the browser through a plug-in; its player was retired at the end of 2020.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>1999, named 2005</td><td>XMLHttpRequest, then "Ajax"</td><td>Pages could fetch data without reloading. The term Ajax was coined in February 2005.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2006</td><td>jQuery</td><td>Smoothed over browser differences and made page scripting approachable.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2007 and 2008</td><td>The first iPhone, then Android</td><td>Touch-first phones, app stores, and native mobile development.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2010 to 2014</td><td>AngularJS (2010), React (2013), Vue (2014)</td><td>Single-page applications: the browser builds the UI from data and calls APIs.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2015</td><td>React Native; "progressive web app" coined</td><td>Web skills reach native apps; web apps learn to install and work offline.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2018 to 2021</td><td>Flutter 1.0 (2018), SwiftUI (2019), Jetpack Compose (2021)</td><td>Declarative UI comes to mobile: describe the screen, let the framework update it.</td></tr>
    </table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>What stays the same</h3>
    <p>Every generation repeats the same lessons: keep the interface responsive while work happens in the background, separate what is shown from the data behind it, make it usable by everyone (see <a href="#web">Web Fundamentals</a> for accessibility), and test it the way people use it. The tools change; those do not.</p>
    <p class="sources"><b>Checked against</b> (Wikipedia): <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xerox_Alto" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Xerox Alto</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Macintosh_128K" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Macintosh 128K</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Windows_1.0" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Windows 1.0</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mosaic_(web_browser)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Mosaic</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Java_applet" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Java applet</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adobe_Flash" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Adobe Flash</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ajax_(programming)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ajax</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/JQuery" target="_blank" rel="noopener">jQuery</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IPhone_(1st_generation)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">iPhone</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Android_(operating_system)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Android</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AngularJS" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AngularJS</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/React_(software)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">React</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vue.js" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Vue.js</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/React_Native" target="_blank" rel="noopener">React Native</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Progressive_web_app" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Progressive web app</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flutter_(software)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Flutter</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SwiftUI" target="_blank" rel="noopener">SwiftUI</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jetpack_Compose" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jetpack Compose</a>.</p>
  </article>

  </div>
</section>

<section class="panel" id="p-testing" data-name="Testing">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Testing</h2><p>What to test, at which level, and how, in Java, Python, SQL and pipelines. A test is worth having when it would fail if the code were wrong, and only then. When you cannot run the whole system locally, see <a href="#teststrat">Test Strategy for Cloud Systems</a>.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels of testing</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Checks</th><th>Speed</th><th>Tools</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Unit</td><td>One function or class in isolation; logic, edge cases, errors</td><td>ms</td><td>JUnit + AssertJ, pytest, dbt unit tests</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Integration</td><td>Your code with a real database, queue or HTTP layer</td><td>seconds</td><td>Testcontainers, WireMock, @SpringBootTest, @QuarkusTest, BigQuery emulator</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Contract</td><td>Producer and consumer agree on an API or schema</td><td>seconds</td><td>Pact, Spring Cloud Contract, schema tests, dbt contracts</td></tr>
      <tr><td>End to end</td><td>A user journey through the deployed system</td><td>minutes</td><td>Playwright, Selenium, scripted API checks</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Data tests</td><td>The data itself: keys, nulls, ranges, freshness, row counts</td><td>per run</td><td>dbt data tests, assertions in pipelines</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Non-functional</td><td>Load, latency, resilience, security</td><td>minutes–hours</td><td>k6, Gatling, Locust, chaos tests, ZAP</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note">Most tests should be fast unit and integration tests; a few end-to-end tests cover the critical journeys. A suite nobody runs because it is slow protects nothing.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>The test pyramid</h3>
    <p>Mike Cohn introduced the test pyramid in his book <i>Succeeding with Agile</i>. It is a picture of how many tests to write at each level:</p>
    <pre><code>        /\        few end-to-end tests: slow, brittle, prove the critical journeys
       /  \
      /----\      some integration and service tests: real database, real HTTP
     /      \
    /--------\    many unit tests: fast, isolated, run on every change
   /__________\</code></pre>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Write lots of small, fast unit tests,</b> some coarser-grained tests, and very few that drive the whole system end to end.</li>
      <li><b>The higher the level, the slower and more fragile the test,</b> and the harder it is to tell from a failure what broke.</li>
      <li><b>The anti-pattern is the "ice-cream cone":</b> mostly manual and end-to-end tests with few unit tests. The suite gets slow, flaky and expensive, and people stop trusting it.</li>
      <li><b>Treat it as a guide, not a law.</b> The names of the middle layers vary between teams; the shape is what matters. Contract tests (see below) keep services honest without many end-to-end tests.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">Source: Martin Fowler's site, <a href="https://martinfowler.com/articles/practical-test-pyramid.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The Practical Test Pyramid</a>.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>What makes a good test</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Fails for the right reason:</b> write it first, or break the code on purpose, and watch it go red. A test only ever seen green is unproven.</li>
      <li><b>Tests behaviour, not implementation:</b> inputs and outputs, not which private method was called. Refactors should not break it.</li>
      <li><b>One reason to fail,</b> with a name that says what it checks: <code>rejectsUnparseableDate</code>.</li>
      <li><b>Arrange, act, assert:</b> set up, do one thing, check the result.</li>
      <li><b>Independent:</b> no shared state or order between tests; unique test data per test.</li>
      <li><b>Deterministic:</b> fixed clock, fixed seed, no real network, no sleeps.</li>
      <li><b>Covers the edges:</b> empty, null, one, many, boundary values, bad input, the dependency being down.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>TDD in one loop</h3>
    <ol>
      <li><b>Red:</b> write a small failing test for the next behaviour.</li>
      <li><b>Green:</b> write the least code that makes it pass.</li>
      <li><b>Refactor:</b> clean up with the tests as a safety net.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>For a bug: first write a test that reproduces it and fails, then fix. The test stays, so the bug cannot return.</p>
    <p class="note"><i>Our view:</i> this is also a good way to brief an AI assistant: give it the failing test as the definition of done. <i>The other view:</i> for exploratory work, where the behaviour is not yet known, some prefer to let the assistant sketch first and write the tests once the shape is clear.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Java: JUnit + AssertJ + Mockito</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>class OrderDateParserTest {

  @ParameterizedTest(name = "{0} → {1}")
  @CsvSource({
      "05-05-1959, 1959-05-05",   // day-first, from one source
      "2024-01-31, 2024-01-31",   // ISO, from the others
  })
  void parsesBothFormats(String raw, LocalDate expected) {
    assertThat(OrderDateParser.parse(raw)).isEqualTo(expected);
  }

  @ParameterizedTest
  @ValueSource(strings = {"", "31-31-2024", "yesterday"})
  void rejectsUnparseable(String raw) {
    assertThatThrownBy(() -&gt; OrderDateParser.parse(raw))
        .isInstanceOf(IllegalArgumentException.class)
        .hasMessageContaining("does not parse");
  }
}

@ExtendWith(MockitoExtension.class)
class LoaderTest {
  @Mock BlobStore store;
  @InjectMocks Loader loader;

  @Test
  void reportsUnknownWhenListingFails() {
    when(store.list(any())).thenThrow(new RuntimeException("403 Forbidden"));
    assertThat(loader.run().state()).isEqualTo(State.UNKNOWN);   // not "0 objects"
    verify(store, never()).copy(any(), any());
  }
}</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">This code runs on JUnit 5 and JUnit 6 alike. JUnit 6 needs Java 17 or newer.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Python: pytest</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>import pytest
from datetime import date
from pipeline.dates import parse_order_date

@pytest.mark.parametrize("raw, expected", [
    ("05-05-1959", date(1959, 5, 5)),
    ("2024-01-31", date(2024, 1, 31)),
])
def test_parses_both_formats(raw, expected):
    assert parse_order_date(raw) == expected

@pytest.mark.parametrize("raw", ["", "31-31-2024", None])
def test_rejects_unparseable(raw):
    with pytest.raises(ValueError, match="does not parse"):
        parse_order_date(raw)

@pytest.fixture
def fake_store(monkeypatch):
    store = FakeStore()                 # a small in-memory fake beats a deep mock
    monkeypatch.setattr("pipeline.land.store", store)
    return store

def test_copies_only_new_objects(fake_store):
    fake_store.seed(["a", "b"])
    result = land(source=["a", "b", "c"])
    assert result.copied == 1 and result.already_current == 2</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Test doubles: pick the lightest</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Double</th><th>Is</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Dummy</td><td>Passed but never used</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Stub</td><td>Returns canned answers</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Fake</td><td>A working lightweight version (in-memory store)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Spy</td><td>Records how it was called</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Mock</td><td>Pre-programmed with expectations it verifies</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <ul>
      <li>Mock what you do not own only at the boundary; prefer a fake or a real container.</li>
      <li>Build stubs from <b>captured real responses</b>, never from a guess. A stub shaped from the docs confirms the guess.</li>
      <li>Too many mocks means the test checks wiring, not behaviour.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Integration tests with real dependencies</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>// Java: real Postgres in Docker for the test
@Testcontainers
@SpringBootTest
class OrderRepositoryIT {
  @Container @ServiceConnection   // Testcontainers 2: org.testcontainers.postgresql (1.x: PostgreSQLContainer&lt;?&gt;)
  static PostgreSQLContainer pg = new PostgreSQLContainer("postgres:16");

  @Autowired OrderRepository repo;

  @Test
  void upsertKeepsTheExistingId() {
    long id = repo.upsert(sample("K1")).id();
    long again = repo.upsert(sample("K1").withOrderRef("changed")).id();
    assertThat(again).isEqualTo(id);          // ids are never renumbered
  }
}

// HTTP dependency: WireMock serving a captured response
stubFor(get(urlPathEqualTo("/objects"))
    .willReturn(aResponse().withStatus(200)
        .withBodyFile("supplier/listing-page-2.xml")));</code></pre></div>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># Python: testcontainers
from testcontainers.community.postgres import PostgresContainer
def test_load_roundtrip():
    with PostgresContainer("postgres:16") as pg:
        dsn = pg.get_connection_url(driver=None)   # postgresql://… (the default adds +psycopg2 for SQLAlchemy)
        load_rows(dsn, rows); assert count(dsn) == len(rows)</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Browser tests with Playwright</h3>
    <p>Playwright is a framework for web automation and testing that drives Chromium, Firefox and WebKit with a single API.</p>
<pre><code>// tests/orders.spec.ts
import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';

// baseURL is set in playwright.config.ts
test('a customer can find an order', async ({ page }) =&gt; {
  await page.goto('/orders');
  await page.getByLabel('Search').fill('K1');
  await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Search' }).click();
  await expect(page.getByRole('row', { name: /K1/ })).toBeVisible();
});

// npx playwright test               run the suite
// npx playwright test --ui          develop and debug in UI mode
// npx playwright codegen &lt;url&gt;      record a first draft of a test
// npx playwright show-trace trace.zip</code></pre>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Auto-waiting:</b> before an action, Playwright runs actionability checks on the element and waits for them to pass, so tests need no hand-written sleeps. If the checks do not pass within the timeout, the action fails.</li>
      <li><b>Retrying assertions:</b> <code>await expect(locator).toBeVisible()</code> and similar assertions retry until they pass or time out. Remember the <code>await</code>.</li>
      <li><b>Locate as a user would:</b> the recommended locators are <code>getByRole</code>, <code>getByText</code> and <code>getByLabel</code>, which follow user-facing attributes rather than CSS paths that change with every redesign. Locating by role gives early feedback on ARIA roles and accessible names, but does not replace an accessibility audit (see <a href="#accessibility">Accessibility</a>).</li>
      <li><b>Traces for failures in CI:</b> the Trace Viewer steps back and forward through each action of a recorded test. A common setting is <code>trace: 'on-first-retry'</code>, which records only when a test is retried.</li>
      <li><b>APIs too:</b> Playwright can call your REST API directly, without loading a page, to set up data or check a result.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note"><i>Our view:</i> keep end-to-end browser tests to the few journeys that matter most, and test the rest at lower levels. <i>The other view:</i> some teams test most behaviour through the browser because that is what users see, and accept slower suites for it.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>API tests: Postman, Newman and tests in code</h3>
<pre><code>// A Postman test script, run after the request
pm.test('returns the order', function () {
  pm.response.to.have.status(200);
  const body = pm.response.json();
  pm.expect(body.orderRef).to.eql('K1');
  pm.environment.set('orderId', body.id);   // chain into the next request
});</code></pre>
<pre><code># Shell: run the same collection in CI with Newman, and write JUnit XML for the CI report
newman run orders.postman_collection.json -e staging.postman_environment.json \
  -r cli,junit --reporter-junit-export results/newman.xml</code></pre>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Postman:</b> an API platform where you build requests into collections, keep variables in environments, and attach test scripts like the one above.</li>
      <li><b>Newman:</b> Postman&rsquo;s command-line collection runner, so the same collection runs in CI. Its built-in reporters are cli, json, junit, progress and emojitrain.</li>
      <li><b>REST Assured</b> (Java) and <b>pytest with HTTPX</b> (Python): API tests written as code, next to the service they test.</li>
      <li><b>Bruno:</b> an offline API client that stores collections as plain-text files in a folder, so they can live in Git with the code.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>If this is true for you</th><th>It leans towards</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>People who do not write code (testers, analysts, support) explore and share APIs</td><td>Postman, with Newman to run the same collections in CI</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Tests should live in the service&rsquo;s repository and run with its build</td><td>REST Assured or pytest with HTTPX</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Collections must be plain-text files reviewed in pull requests, with no cloud account</td><td>Bruno (offline-only, collections in a folder, Git for sharing), or tests in code</td></tr>
      <tr><td>The same test should check both the API and the page that uses it</td><td>Playwright, which does both</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Your team already maintains a large Postman workspace</td><td>Keep it, and run it with Newman; switching has a cost</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Testing data pipelines</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Idempotency:</b> run the same input twice; the output must be identical (row count and content).</li>
      <li><b>Incremental vs full:</b> an incremental run on top of yesterday equals a full rebuild.</li>
      <li><b>Edge data:</b> empty partition, one row, duplicates, late rows, a new source column, a malformed date.</li>
      <li><b>Reconciliation:</b> rows in = rows loaded + rows rejected + rows skipped, every run.</li>
      <li><b>Run records:</b> assert the counters written to the ledger, not only the log line.</li>
      <li><b>"Source down":</b> the run must end FAILED or UNKNOWN, never SUCCEEDED with zero.</li>
      <li><b>Real dialect:</b> a SQL string checked against a mock proves its shape, not that BigQuery accepts it. Run it on the emulator or a dry run.</li>
    </ul>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># dbt
dbt build -s my_model+            # models + their tests, in order
dbt test  -s test_type:unit       # logic on fixed inputs
dbt test  -s test_type:data       # assertions on built tables</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Property-based testing</h3>
    <p>Instead of hand-picked examples, state a property that must hold for all inputs and let the tool generate hundreds, shrinking any failure to the smallest case.</p>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># Python: hypothesis
from hypothesis import given, strategies as st

@given(st.dates(min_value=date(1950,1,1), max_value=date(2030,12,31)))
def test_format_then_parse_roundtrips(d):
    assert parse_order_date(d.strftime("%d-%m-%Y")) == d

@given(st.lists(st.text()))
def test_dedupe_is_idempotent(xs):
    assert dedupe(dedupe(xs)) == dedupe(xs)</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">Java: jqwik (<code>@Property</code>, <code>@ForAll</code>). Good properties: round trips, idempotency, invariants (sorted output stays sorted), and "never throws".</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Load and performance tests</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>// k6: ramp to 60 searches/s and check p95 latency
import http from 'k6/http';
import { check } from 'k6';
export const options = {
  scenarios: { search: { executor: 'ramping-arrival-rate',
    startRate: 5, timeUnit: '1s', preAllocatedVUs: 50,
    stages: [{ target: 60, duration: '5m' }, { target: 60, duration: '10m' }] } },
  thresholds: { http_req_duration: ['p(95)&lt;500'], http_req_failed: ['rate&lt;0.01'] },
};
export default function () {
  const r = http.get(`${__ENV.BASE}/api/search?q=land+acquisition`);
  check(r, { '200': (x) =&gt; x.status === 200 });
}
// k6 run -e BASE=https://staging.example.com search.js</code></pre></div>
    <ul>
      <li>Test against production-sized data in a production-like environment.</li>
      <li>Measure percentiles (p95, p99), not averages.</li>
      <li>Find the breaking point, then decide the alert threshold below it.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Choosing a load-testing tool: by requirement</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>Tests are written in</th><th>Notes from its own documentation</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>k6</td><td>JavaScript, run by an engine written in Go</td><td>HTTP, WebSockets, gRPC and browser tests; thresholds in the script; AGPL-3.0 licence</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Gatling</td><td>Java, JavaScript, TypeScript, Kotlin or Scala</td><td>Simulations as code, an HTML report, and a build that fails when performance regresses; the Community Edition is Apache 2.0</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Locust</td><td>Python</td><td>Tests are regular Python code; run from the command line or a web UI, with results in real time</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Apache JMeter</td><td>Test plans built in a GUI, run headless from the command line</td><td>100% Java; many protocols, including HTTP, SOAP and REST</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>If this is true for you</th><th>It leans towards</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Tests should live with a JavaScript or TypeScript codebase, with pass or fail thresholds in CI</td><td>k6 or Gatling</td></tr>
      <tr><td>The team works in Java or Kotlin and wants load tests in the same language as the service</td><td>Gatling</td></tr>
      <tr><td>The team works in Python, or the test needs Python libraries</td><td>Locust</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Testers build plans without writing code, or you test protocols such as SOAP, JDBC or JMS</td><td>JMeter</td></tr>
      <tr><td>A licence must be permissive for how you ship or embed the tool</td><td>Check each licence first: k6 is AGPL-3.0, Gatling&rsquo;s Community Edition Apache 2.0</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note">Whichever tool, read percentiles rather than averages, and watch for coordinated omission: a load generator that waits for each reply stops sending exactly when the server is slow (see <a href="#banking">Banking &amp; Low Latency</a>). <i>Our view:</i> send load at a fixed rate for service capacity tests. <i>The other view:</i> a closed loop, where each virtual user waits for its reply, models real users who really do wait, and suits journey tests.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Flaky tests, coverage, CI</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Flaky test:</b> passes and fails on the same code. Causes: time, ordering, shared state, real network, async waits. Fix or delete it; never retry it into green or quarantine it forever.</li>
      <li><b>Coverage</b> shows what was <i>executed</i>, not what was <i>checked</i>. 100% coverage with weak assertions proves little. Use it to find untested code, not as a target.</li>
      <li><b>Mutation testing</b> (PIT for Java, mutmut for Python) changes your code and checks that some test fails. It measures test strength.</li>
      <li><b>CI runs every test on every PR,</b> on a clean checkout. A local green and a CI red on the same commit usually mean untracked files or a different environment.</li>
      <li><b>Never skip, disable or weaken a test to get green.</b> If a test is wrong, fix it in its own reviewed change.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Testing AI features</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Unit-test everything around the model: prompt building, parsing, retries, fallbacks.</li>
      <li>Validate structured outputs against the schema, and the values against your data.</li>
      <li>Run an eval set in CI and track the score; fail the build on a regression beyond a threshold.</li>
      <li>Include adversarial cases: prompt injection in documents, empty retrieval, the model refusing.</li>
      <li>Mock the model in unit tests with recorded real responses; run the eval against the real model.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>





















  <article class="card"><h3>One we build: enrich-test-api</h3>
    <p><a href="/enrich-test-api/">enrich-test-api</a> is a Java test library for exercising real cloud service semantics against local emulators, so a test names no cloud provider. It is an early alpha: AWS only, emulator only. It is open source (Apache 2.0).</p>
    <p class="note">It is ours, so we are not neutral: compare it with Testcontainers and the other tools on this page.</p>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Write unit tests with clear arrange-act-assert</li><li>Run the suite locally and read CI failures</li><li>Reproduce a bug with a failing test before fixing it</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Use Testcontainers and WireMock for integration tests</li><li>Test edge cases, errors and dependency failures</li><li>Keep tests fast, independent and deterministic</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Design a test strategy across unit, integration, contract and end to end</li><li>Add property-based, load and mutation testing where they pay off</li><li>Eliminate flaky tests and slow suites</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Set testing standards and quality gates for the organisation</li><li>Build test infrastructure: environments, data, emulators</li><li>Measure test effectiveness, not just coverage</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>Contract testing with Pact</li><li>Mutation testing with PIT or mutmut</li><li>Test data management and synthetic data</li><li>Testing in production: canaries and feature flags</li><li>Chaos engineering</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://martinfowler.com/articles/practical-test-pyramid.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The Practical Test Pyramid (Martin Fowler)</a><span class="lnote"> How to balance test levels</span></li><li><a href="https://junit.org/junit5/docs/current/user-guide/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">JUnit user guide</a><span class="lnote"> Java testing</span></li><li><a href="https://docs.pytest.org/en/stable/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">pytest documentation</a><span class="lnote"> Python testing</span></li><li><a href="https://testcontainers.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Testcontainers</a><span class="lnote"> Real dependencies in Docker for tests</span></li><li><a href="https://wiremock.org/docs/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">WireMock</a><span class="lnote"> HTTP stubs and recording</span></li><li><a href="https://hypothesis.readthedocs.io/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Hypothesis</a><span class="lnote"> Property-based testing in Python</span></li><li><a href="https://grafana.com/docs/k6/latest/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Grafana k6</a><span class="lnote"> Load testing</span></li><li><a href="https://pitest.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PIT mutation testing</a><span class="lnote"> Measure test strength in Java</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://martinfowler.com/articles/practical-test-pyramid.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The Practical Test Pyramid (martinfowler.com)</a>; <a href="https://github.com/microsoft/playwright" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Playwright README</a>; <a href="https://playwright.dev/docs/actionability" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Playwright docs: auto-waiting (actionability)</a>; <a href="https://playwright.dev/docs/locators" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Playwright docs: locators</a>; <a href="https://playwright.dev/docs/test-assertions" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Playwright docs: assertions</a>; <a href="https://playwright.dev/docs/trace-viewer-intro" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Playwright docs: Trace Viewer</a>; <a href="https://playwright.dev/docs/api-testing" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Playwright docs: API testing</a>; <a href="https://github.com/postmanlabs/newman" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Newman README</a>; <a href="https://github.com/postmanlabs/postman-sandbox" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Postman sandbox (pm API)</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Postman_(software)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Postman</a>; <a href="https://github.com/rest-assured/rest-assured" target="_blank" rel="noopener">REST Assured</a>; <a href="https://github.com/encode/httpx" target="_blank" rel="noopener">HTTPX</a>; <a href="https://github.com/usebruno/bruno" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bruno README</a>; <a href="https://github.com/grafana/k6" target="_blank" rel="noopener">k6 README</a>; <a href="https://github.com/gatling/gatling" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Gatling README</a>; <a href="https://github.com/locustio/locust" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Locust README</a>; <a href="https://github.com/apache/jmeter" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Apache JMeter README</a>.</p><p class="runcode"><b>Run the code:</b> <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/well-rounded-engineer-code/tree/main/testing" target="_blank" rel="noopener">well-rounded-engineer-code/testing</a>, with a README that says how to run it and what it shows. Stuck, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/well-rounded-engineer-code/issues/new?title=%5BTesting%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= TEST STRATEGY FOR CLOUD SYSTEMS ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-teststrat" data-name="Test Strategy for Cloud Systems">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Test strategy when you cannot run it all locally</h2><p>A system that runs on Kubernetes in the cloud also leans on managed services: queues, databases, identity, storage, networking. Kubernetes runs on a laptop, but the cloud around it does not, and (<i>analysis</i>) mocking everything can leave gaps at the boundaries between your code and managed services. This page is about deciding what to test where: on your machine, in CI, in a short-lived cloud environment, and in production. The kinds of test and the tools are on <a href="#testing">Testing</a>.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>The short answer</h3>
    <p><i>Our view:</i> stop aiming for one environment that runs everything. Test logic locally and fast; test each boundary with contracts and real dependencies in containers; run a small number of end-to-end checks in a short-lived cloud environment built the way production is; and treat production itself as a place you test, carefully, with canaries and monitoring. The other view is in the last card on this page.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Where each kind of check runs</h3>
    <figure class="fig"><svg viewBox="0 0 660 300" role="img" aria-labelledby="ts-t ts-d"><title id="ts-t">Where each kind of check runs</title><desc id="ts-d">Four stages from left to right: your laptop, CI, a short-lived cloud environment, and production. On the laptop: unit tests, component tests with test doubles, integration tests with real dependencies in containers, and consumer contract tests. In CI: the same tests on every push, plus provider contract verification. In a short-lived cloud environment: a few smoke and end-to-end tests against the real managed services. In production: canary releases, synthetic checks, and monitoring and alerts. An arrow underneath shows that the checks added at each stage are fewer and slower, but closer to the real system.</desc><defs><marker id="ts-a" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker><marker id="ts-aa" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar acc" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker></defs><rect class="nd in" x="10" y="20" width="150" height="200" rx="8"/><text class="lb hd" x="85.0" y="46" text-anchor="middle">Your laptop</text><text class="lb" x="85.0" y="76" text-anchor="middle">unit tests</text><text class="lb" x="85.0" y="98" text-anchor="middle">component tests</text><text class="lb mu" x="85.0" y="120" text-anchor="middle">(test doubles)</text><text class="lb" x="85.0" y="142" text-anchor="middle">integration tests</text><text class="lb mu" x="85.0" y="164" text-anchor="middle">(real deps, containers)</text><text class="lb" x="85.0" y="186" text-anchor="middle">consumer contracts</text><line class="ed" x1="160" y1="120" x2="171" y2="120" marker-end="url(#ts-a)"/><rect class="nd" x="172" y="20" width="150" height="200" rx="8"/><text class="lb hd" x="247.0" y="46" text-anchor="middle">CI</text><text class="lb" x="247.0" y="76" text-anchor="middle">the same tests,</text><text class="lb" x="247.0" y="98" text-anchor="middle">on every push</text><text class="lb" x="247.0" y="120" text-anchor="middle">plus provider</text><text class="lb" x="247.0" y="142" text-anchor="middle">contract</text><text class="lb" x="247.0" y="164" text-anchor="middle">verification</text><line class="ed" x1="322" y1="120" x2="333" y2="120" marker-end="url(#ts-a)"/><rect class="nd" x="334" y="20" width="150" height="200" rx="8"/><text class="lb hd" x="409.0" y="46" text-anchor="middle">Short-lived cloud env</text><text class="lb" x="409.0" y="76" text-anchor="middle">a few smoke and</text><text class="lb" x="409.0" y="98" text-anchor="middle">end-to-end tests</text><text class="lb" x="409.0" y="120" text-anchor="middle">against the real</text><text class="lb" x="409.0" y="142" text-anchor="middle">managed services</text><line class="ed" x1="484" y1="120" x2="495" y2="120" marker-end="url(#ts-a)"/><rect class="nd out" x="496" y="20" width="150" height="200" rx="8"/><text class="lb hd" x="571.0" y="46" text-anchor="middle">Production</text><text class="lb" x="571.0" y="76" text-anchor="middle">canary releases</text><text class="lb" x="571.0" y="98" text-anchor="middle">synthetic checks</text><text class="lb" x="571.0" y="120" text-anchor="middle">monitoring</text><text class="lb" x="571.0" y="142" text-anchor="middle">and alerts</text><line class="ed acc" x1="20" y1="250" x2="640" y2="250" marker-end="url(#ts-aa)"/><text class="lb acc" x="20" y="278" text-anchor="start">most checks, fastest feedback</text><text class="lb acc" x="640" y="278" text-anchor="end">fewer and slower, but closer to the real system</text></svg><figcaption>Where each kind of check runs. Moving right, the checks added at each stage are fewer and slower, but closer to the system your users reach (<i>analysis</i>).</figcaption></figure>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Why &ldquo;everything end to end&rdquo; breaks down</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>The test pyramid</b>, from Mike Cohn&rsquo;s <i>Succeeding with Agile</i>: many small, fast unit tests, some coarser tests, and very few high-level tests, because the higher you go, the fewer you should have.</li>
      <li><b>End-to-end tests are notoriously flaky,</b> fail for unexpected reasons, need a lot of maintenance and run slowly, as Ham Vocke puts it in <i>The Practical Test Pyramid</i>.</li>
      <li><b>Vocke also cautions</b> that the pyramid is overly simplistic and works best as a rule of thumb.</li>
      <li><b>Microservices add network boundaries,</b> so the testing strategies of a single in-process application need to be reconsidered (Toby Clemson, <i>Testing Strategies in a Microservice Architecture</i>).</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Contracts instead of a full stack</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Contract tests</b> (Martin Fowler): keep testing against a test double for speed and reliability, and separately run contract tests to catch the real service changing underneath you. Fowler notes they can run on their own rhythm, often once a day, and a failure need not break the build.</li>
      <li><b>Consumer-driven contracts</b> (Ian Robinson, 2006): each consumer states what it needs from a provider, and the provider tests against all its consumers&rsquo; expectations.</li>
      <li><b>In practice (Vocke):</b> consuming teams send their Pact contract files to the provider team, which uses them in its provider tests. He calls consumer-driven contract tests &ldquo;a real game changer&rdquo; for autonomous teams that move fast and with confidence.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note"><i>Analysis:</i> two services can then be tested apart, which is what makes a team independent of a shared environment.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Local stand-ins for cloud services, and their limits</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Stand-in</th><th>What it gives you</th><th>Its own caveat</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td><b>Testcontainers</b></td><td>Throwaway instances of databases, browsers or anything that runs in a Docker container, inside your tests</td><td><i>Analysis:</i> only for what ships as a container image</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>kind</b></td><td>A local Kubernetes cluster whose &ldquo;nodes&rdquo; are Docker containers; usable for local development or CI</td><td>Designed first for testing Kubernetes itself; it is not your cloud&rsquo;s managed cluster</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Google Pub/Sub and Firestore emulators</b></td><td>Local versions for development and tests</td><td>The Pub/Sub emulator itself warns its implementation may be incomplete or differ from the real system; Google&rsquo;s known limitations include no IAM operations. Firestore&rsquo;s emulator has &ldquo;notable limitations&rdquo;, for example it does not mirror production&rsquo;s transaction concurrency modes</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Azurite</b></td><td>A free local emulator for Azure Storage</td><td>Microsoft says it supports only Blob, Queue and Table storage, and has functional differences from an Azure Storage account in the cloud</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>LocalStack</b></td><td>A framework to develop and test AWS applications locally</td><td>Its open-source repository is archived and read-only as LocalStack consolidates into a single unified image; the company offers a free Hobby plan for non-commercial use, and paid plans</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note"><i>Analysis:</i> an emulator passing is evidence about your code, not about the cloud service. Pub/Sub&rsquo;s emulator lists IAM operations as unsupported, and none of the stand-ins above claims to reproduce your cloud&rsquo;s identity, networking or quotas, so check those in a real environment.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Short-lived cloud environments</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Create an environment per pull request or per test run, built by the same Helm charts and Terraform as production (see <a href="#helm">Helm</a> and <a href="#terraform">Terraform</a>), and delete it afterwards.</li>
      <li>In Kubernetes, a <b>namespace</b> isolates a group of resources within one cluster, and resource quotas divide the cluster between users, so many such environments can share one cluster.</li>
      <li>Point them at real managed services, using separate small instances or test projects.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">This card is <i>analysis</i> built on the Kubernetes namespace documentation, which says namespaces are intended for clusters with many users across teams. The cost is real: cloud spend and setup time per environment.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Production is part of the strategy</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>QA in production</b> (Rouan Wilsenach): watch the behaviour of production closely, with the right monitoring and a good continuous delivery pipeline, so issues are found as they happen and fixed quickly.</li>
      <li><b>Canarying</b> (Google SRE Workbook): a partial and time-limited deployment of a change, evaluated against the rest, usually on a much smaller part of production or of the users.</li>
      <li><b>Feature flags</b> let you ship code switched off and turn it on for a few users first (<i>analysis</i>; see <a href="#devops">DevOps</a>).</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>How much of each: choose by requirement</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>If this is true for the part you are testing</th><th>It leans towards (<i>analysis</i>)</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Business logic, calculations, validation</td><td>Unit tests on your machine, lots of them</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Your code talking to a database, cache or broker that ships as a container</td><td>Integration tests with Testcontainers, on your machine and in CI</td></tr>
      <tr><td>A call to another team&rsquo;s service</td><td>Consumer-driven contract tests, verified in the provider&rsquo;s CI</td></tr>
      <tr><td>A managed cloud service whose emulator&rsquo;s listed limitations do not touch what you use</td><td>The emulator for fast feedback, plus a few tests against the real service</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Identity, IAM permissions, network policies, quotas</td><td>A short-lived cloud environment: there is no faithful local copy</td></tr>
      <tr><td>A critical user journey (sign in, pay, place an order)</td><td>A handful of end-to-end tests in a cloud environment, plus synthetic checks in production</td></tr>
      <tr><td>A risky change you cannot fully rehearse</td><td>A canary or a feature flag, with monitoring and a fast rollback</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Your team already has a shared test environment that works</td><td>Keep it, and add contracts to reduce how much depends on it: switching has a cost</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Our view, and the other view</h3>
    <p><i>Our view:</i> fast local tests and contracts catch most mistakes cheaply, and a few real-environment checks plus careful releases catch what they cannot. Chasing a perfect local copy of the cloud costs more than it saves.</p>
    <p><i>The other view:</i> some teams, for example in regulated or high-risk settings, judge a full pre-production environment that mirrors production worth its cost and flakiness, and run large end-to-end suites there for the assurance it gives.</p>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Say which tests run locally and which need a real environment</li><li>Explain a contract test</li><li>Run a dependency in a container for a test</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Write consumer contract tests and verify them in the provider's CI</li><li>Use an emulator and know its listed limitations</li><li>Add smoke tests to a deployment</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Design a per-pull-request cloud environment</li><li>Plan canary releases with automatic evaluation</li><li>Decide test depth per component by risk</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Set an organisation's test strategy across teams and environments</li><li>Balance test-environment cost against risk</li><li>Make production observability part of quality</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>The test pyramid</li><li>Contract and consumer-driven contract tests</li><li>Testcontainers and emulators</li><li>Short-lived environments</li><li>Canarying and QA in production</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://martinfowler.com/articles/practical-test-pyramid.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The Practical Test Pyramid</a><span class="lnote"> Ham Vocke's walkthrough with contract tests</span></li><li><a href="https://sre.google/workbook/canarying-releases/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Canarying releases</a><span class="lnote"> Google's SRE Workbook chapter</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://martinfowler.com/articles/practical-test-pyramid.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ham Vocke: The Practical Test Pyramid</a>; <a href="https://martinfowler.com/bliki/ContractTest.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Martin Fowler: Contract Test</a>; <a href="https://martinfowler.com/articles/consumerDrivenContracts.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ian Robinson: Consumer-Driven Contracts</a>; <a href="https://martinfowler.com/articles/microservice-testing/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Toby Clemson: Testing Strategies in a Microservice Architecture</a>; <a href="https://martinfowler.com/articles/qa-in-production.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Rouan Wilsenach: QA in Production</a>; <a href="https://sre.google/workbook/canarying-releases/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google SRE Workbook: Canarying releases</a>; <a href="https://github.com/testcontainers/testcontainers-java/blob/main/docs/index.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Testcontainers for Java (docs)</a>; <a href="https://github.com/kubernetes-sigs/kind/blob/main/README.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">kind (README)</a>; <a href="https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/overview/working-with-objects/namespaces/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kubernetes: Namespaces</a>; <a href="https://cloud.google.com/pubsub/docs/emulator" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google Cloud: Pub/Sub emulator</a>; <a href="https://cloud.google.com/firestore/docs/emulator" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google Cloud: Firestore emulator</a>; <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/common/storage-use-azurite" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Microsoft Learn: Azurite</a>; <a href="https://github.com/localstack/localstack/blob/main/README.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">LocalStack (README)</a>.</p><p class="runcode">A question, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/enrichmeai.github.io/issues/new?title=%5BTest%20Strategy%20for%20Cloud%20Systems%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>. Nothing here is tracked and there are no comments to store: questions live in the open, where the next learner can find the answer.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= WRITING AND CODE REVIEW ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-review" data-name="Writing &amp; Code Review">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Writing and code review</h2><p>Much of an engineer&rsquo;s work reaches other people as writing: a commit message, a pull request description, a design proposal, a decision record, a page of documentation, a review comment. This page covers what each kind of writing is for, conventions that many projects use, and what published guidance and research say about code review. Specifications are on <a href="#specs">Specifications</a>; design documents for whole systems are on <a href="#sysdesign">System Design</a>; AI review tools are on <a href="#copilot">GitHub Copilot</a>; reviewing a change for its effect on the whole system is on <a href="#quality">Quality Attributes</a>.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>The short answer</h3>
    <p><i>Our view:</i> write so that someone who was not in the room can understand what changed and why, months later. Treat code review as a way to keep the code base healthy and to share knowledge, not only to catch bugs. <i>The other view</i> is in the card &ldquo;Our view, and the other view&rdquo;.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Writing that gets read</h3>
    <p><b>Bottom line up front</b> (BLUF) means starting a message with its key information, so the reader gets the most important part first. It is a standard in U.S. military communication and resembles the inverted pyramid in journalism: conclusions come before the material that supports them.</p>
    <p><b>Plain language</b> is writing designed so the reader understands it as quickly, easily and completely as possible, avoiding verbose, convoluted language and jargon. Many countries require public agencies to use it.</p>
    <p class="note"><i>Analysis:</i> both ideas suit engineering writing, where most readers are busy and arrive looking for one answer.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Commit messages</h3>
    <p>Git&rsquo;s own documentation suggests, though it does not require, starting a commit message with one short summary line of no more than 50 characters, then a blank line, then a fuller description. Git treats the text up to the first blank line as the commit&rsquo;s title and uses it throughout, for example as the subject of a patch sent by email.</p>
    <p><b>Conventional Commits</b> is a lightweight convention on top of that: each message starts with a type, such as <code>fix:</code> (a bug fix, a semantic-versioning patch) or <code>feat:</code> (a new feature, a minor version), and a breaking change is marked with <code>!</code> or a <code>BREAKING CHANGE:</code> footer, which makes it easier to build tools on the history.</p>
<pre><code>fix: prevent racing of requests

Introduce a request id and a reference to latest request. Dismiss
incoming responses other than from latest request.

Remove timeouts which were used to mitigate the racing issue but are
obsolete now.

Reviewed-by: Z
Refs: #123</code></pre>
    <p class="note">An example from the Conventional Commits <span data-other-version>1.0.0</span> specification. Its summary line is 31 characters.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>The kinds of engineering writing</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Writing</th><th>What it is for</th><th>What published guidance says</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td><b>Commit message</b></td><td>Why one change was made, kept with the code for ever</td><td>A short summary line, a blank line, then the detail (Git documentation); optionally a type prefix (Conventional Commits)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Pull request (change) description</b></td><td>What a change does and why, for its reviewers and for later readers of the history</td><td>Google&rsquo;s guide: the first line is a short summary of specifically what the change does, written as an imperative sentence, able to stand alone; the rest explains what is being changed and why</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Design proposal</b></td><td>Agreeing an approach before building it</td><td>A Python Enhancement Proposal (PEP), for example, is a design document that should give a concise technical specification of a feature and its rationale</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Architecture decision record</b> (ADR)</td><td>One important decision, with its context and consequences</td><td>The widely used ADR template comes from a 2011 post by Michael Nygard. An ADR is not edited later: it is amended with new information, or superseded by a new ADR</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Documentation</b></td><td>Helping people learn, do, look up or understand</td><td>Diátaxis separates four kinds: tutorials, how-to guides, reference and explanation (the picture below)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Review comment</b></td><td>Improving one change, and the people who make the next one</td><td>The card on review comments, below</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Four kinds of documentation</h3>
    <figure class="fig"><svg viewBox="0 0 620 400" role="img" aria-labelledby="dx-t dx-d"><title id="dx-t">Four kinds of documentation (Diátaxis)</title><desc id="dx-d">A two-by-two grid. Columns: study (acquiring a skill) and work (applying a skill). Rows: doing (practical action) and thinking (understanding). Study and doing: tutorials, a lesson where you learn by doing. Work and doing: how-to guides, which help you get a task done. Study and thinking: explanation, which tells you why. Work and thinking: reference, facts to look up while you work.</desc><text class="lb hd" x="245.0" y="30" text-anchor="middle">Study</text><text class="lb mu" x="245.0" y="46" text-anchor="middle">acquiring a skill</text><text class="lb hd" x="475.0" y="30" text-anchor="middle">Work</text><text class="lb mu" x="475.0" y="46" text-anchor="middle">applying a skill</text><text class="lb hd" x="64" y="127.0" text-anchor="middle">Doing</text><text class="lb mu" x="64" y="145.0" text-anchor="middle">practical steps</text><text class="lb hd" x="64" y="277.0" text-anchor="middle">Thinking</text><text class="lb mu" x="64" y="295.0" text-anchor="middle">understanding</text><rect class="nd in" x="136" y="62" width="218" height="138" rx="8"/><text class="lb hd" x="245.0" y="121.0" text-anchor="middle">Tutorials</text><text class="lb" x="245.0" y="143.0" text-anchor="middle">a lesson:</text><text class="lb" x="245.0" y="161.0" text-anchor="middle">learn by doing</text><rect class="nd out" x="366" y="62" width="218" height="138" rx="8"/><text class="lb hd" x="475.0" y="121.0" text-anchor="middle">How-to guides</text><text class="lb" x="475.0" y="143.0" text-anchor="middle">get a real</text><text class="lb" x="475.0" y="161.0" text-anchor="middle">task done</text><rect class="nd out" x="136" y="212" width="218" height="138" rx="8"/><text class="lb hd" x="245.0" y="271.0" text-anchor="middle">Explanation</text><text class="lb" x="245.0" y="293.0" text-anchor="middle">the why,</text><text class="lb" x="245.0" y="311.0" text-anchor="middle">and the context</text><rect class="nd in" x="366" y="212" width="218" height="138" rx="8"/><text class="lb hd" x="475.0" y="271.0" text-anchor="middle">Reference</text><text class="lb" x="475.0" y="293.0" text-anchor="middle">facts to look up</text><text class="lb" x="475.0" y="311.0" text-anchor="middle">while you work</text></svg><figcaption>The four kinds of documentation in Daniele Procida&rsquo;s Diátaxis, redrawn as a two-by-two grid. To place a page, Diátaxis asks two questions: is it about action or understanding, and is the reader studying or working? A page that tries to be two kinds at once usually serves neither reader well (<i>analysis</i>).</figcaption></figure>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>What code review is for</h3>
    <p>Code review is a quality activity in which people other than the author examine source code. Its goals include finding defects, but also sharing knowledge of the code base and its expectations with both reviewers and author. Michael Fagan described the first studied process, <b>inspection</b>, in the IBM Systems Journal in 1976.</p>
    <p>Research cited on Wikipedia&rsquo;s page found that up to 75% of review comments concern evolvability and maintainability rather than functionality, and fewer than 15% of the issues discussed relate directly to bugs. For defects, Capers Jones&rsquo;s analysis of over 12,000 projects found formal inspections discovered 60&ndash;65% of latent defects, informal ones fewer than half, and most testing about 30%; another case study found lightweight reviews could find as many bugs as formal ones, faster.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>The standard Google&rsquo;s guide sets</h3>
    <p>Google&rsquo;s published code review guide says the primary purpose of review is to make sure the overall health of the code base improves over time. Its senior principle: reviewers should favour approving a change once it definitely improves the overall code health, even if it is not perfect. &ldquo;There is no such thing as &lsquo;perfect&rsquo; code&mdash;there is only <i>better</i> code.&rdquo;</p>
    <p>The guide also sets expectations on size and speed. About 100 lines is usually a reasonable size for a change and 1,000 usually too large, though it gives no hard rule; one business day is the maximum time it should take to respond to a review request.</p>
    <p class="note">Research cited on Wikipedia puts optimal review rates at 200 to 400 lines of code an hour; faster than a few hundred lines an hour may be too fast to find errors in critical software.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Review comments</h3>
    <p>Google&rsquo;s guide on writing review comments asks reviewers to be kind, to explain their reasoning, and to comment on the code rather than the developer. Its example:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><i>Less helpful:</i> &ldquo;Why did <b>you</b> use threads here when there&rsquo;s obviously no benefit to be gained from concurrency?&rdquo;</li>
      <li><i>More helpful:</i> &ldquo;The concurrency model here is adding complexity to the system without any actual performance benefit that I can see. Because there&rsquo;s no performance benefit, it&rsquo;s best for this code to be single-threaded instead of using multiple threads.&rdquo;</li>
    </ul>
    <p>It suggests labelling how much a comment matters, so the author can tell a required change from a suggestion: <b>Nit:</b> (minor polish), <b>Optional</b> or <b>Consider:</b> (a good idea, not required), <b>FYI:</b> (something to think about later). It also says to comment on what is good in a change, not only what could be better, and that fixing the change is the author&rsquo;s responsibility, though direct suggestions sometimes help more.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Choose by requirement</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>If this is true for you</th><th>It leans towards (<i>analysis</i>)</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>A decision affects several teams, or is costly to reverse</td><td>A written design proposal, reviewed before building</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Future readers will ask why the system is this way</td><td>An architecture decision record beside the code</td></tr>
      <tr><td>You publish versions and changelogs from the history</td><td>Conventional Commits, so tools can read the type of each change</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Changes take days to be reviewed</td><td>Smaller changes and an agreed response time, as in Google&rsquo;s guide</td></tr>
      <tr><td>The software is safety-critical, or a standard requires review</td><td>A formal inspection process; Wikipedia notes review is mandatory in some such contexts</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Your team already has writing and review conventions that work</td><td>Keep them, and write them down: changing conventions has a cost</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Our view, and the other view</h3>
    <p><i>Our view:</i> clear writing and timely review are part of the engineering work, not overhead around it. A change that nobody else can understand is not finished.</p>
    <p><i>The other view:</i> review after the code is written adds waiting, and much of its value is in comments about style that tools could check; some teams prefer to put effort into automated checks, pairing while the code is written, or reviewing after merge, and keep pre-merge review for risky changes.</p>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Write a commit message with a short summary line and a reason</li><li>Describe a pull request so a reviewer knows what and why</li><li>Read review comments as being about the code, not you</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Review others' changes within a day, labelling comments by importance</li><li>Keep changes small enough to review well</li><li>Write an architecture decision record</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Write a design proposal others can build from</li><li>Set a team's review and writing conventions</li><li>Place documentation in the right one of four kinds</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Shape how an organisation records decisions</li><li>Balance review effort against automated checks</li><li>Mentor others through review</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>Bottom line up front and plain language</li><li>Commit messages and Conventional Commits</li><li>Pull request descriptions</li><li>Design proposals and ADRs</li><li>Diátaxis: four kinds of documentation</li><li>Code review: purpose, standard, size, speed and comments</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://google.github.io/eng-practices/review/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google Engineering Practices: code review</a><span class="lnote"> Google's published guide</span></li><li><a href="https://diataxis.fr/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Diátaxis</a><span class="lnote"> Daniele Procida</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://github.com/google/eng-practices/blob/master/review/reviewer/standard.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google eng-practices: The Standard of Code Review</a>; <a href="https://github.com/google/eng-practices/blob/master/review/reviewer/speed.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google eng-practices: Speed of Code Reviews</a>; <a href="https://github.com/google/eng-practices/blob/master/review/reviewer/comments.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google eng-practices: How to write code review comments</a>; <a href="https://github.com/google/eng-practices/blob/master/review/developer/small-cls.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google eng-practices: Small CLs</a>; <a href="https://github.com/google/eng-practices/blob/master/review/developer/cl-descriptions.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google eng-practices: Writing good CL descriptions</a>; <a href="https://git-scm.com/docs/git-commit#_discussion" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Git documentation: git-commit, Discussion</a>; <a href="https://www.conventionalcommits.org/en/v1.0.0/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Conventional Commits specification</a>; <a href="https://peps.python.org/pep-0001/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PEP 1: PEP Purpose and Guidelines</a>; <a href="https://github.com/joelparkerhenderson/architecture-decision-record" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Architecture decision record (Joel Parker Henderson)</a>; <a href="https://github.com/evildmp/diataxis-documentation-framework" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Diátaxis source (Daniele Procida)</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Code_review" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Code review</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fagan_inspection" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Fagan inspection</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/BLUF_(communication)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: BLUF (communication)</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plain_language" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Plain language</a>.</p><p class="runcode">A question, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/enrichmeai.github.io/issues/new?title=%5BWriting%20%26%20Code%20Review%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>. Nothing here is tracked and there are no comments to store: questions live in the open, where the next learner can find the answer.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= PERFORMANCE ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-performance" data-name="Performance Engineering">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Performance engineering</h2><p>Making systems fast enough at a cost you can afford, by measurement rather than guesswork. The method, the laws that explain slowness, load testing, profiling for the JVM and Python, and where time usually goes in services, databases and pipelines.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>The method</h3>
    <ol>
      <li><b>Set a goal in numbers:</b> "p95 search latency under 500 ms at 60 requests/s", "nightly load finishes in 40 minutes", "cost under ₹X per 1,000 requests". No goal, no finish line.</li>
      <li><b>Measure the current state</b> in a production-like environment with realistic data and traffic.</li>
      <li><b>Find the bottleneck:</b> the one resource or step limiting everything (CPU, memory, I/O, a lock, a query, a dependency). Use traces and profiles, not intuition.</li>
      <li><b>Change one thing</b> that addresses it.</li>
      <li><b>Measure again</b> the same way. Keep the change only if the numbers improved and nothing else got worse.</li>
      <li><b>Repeat</b> until the goal is met, then stop. Then guard it with a performance test in CI.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="note">Most of the gain usually comes from one or two fixes: a missing index, an N+1 query, a synchronous chain, an oversized payload. Find those before tuning anything clever.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Laws worth knowing</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Little's law:</b> items in the system = arrival rate × time in system (L = λW). 100 requests/s × 0.2 s = 20 requests in flight, so you need about 20 concurrent workers or connections.</li>
      <li><b>Amdahl's law:</b> speeding up a part only helps by that part's share. Making a step that takes 10% of the time infinitely fast saves 10%.</li>
      <li><b>Queueing:</b> as utilisation approaches 100%, waiting time grows sharply, not linearly. Plan for 60–70% utilisation at peak.</li>
      <li><b>Tail amplification:</b> if one call is slow 1% of the time, a request that fans out to 100 such calls is slow 1 − 0.99<sup>100</sup> ≈ 63% of the time.</li>
      <li><b>Latency vs throughput:</b> batching raises throughput and usually raises latency; tune for whichever the goal names.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Percentiles, not averages</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>An average hides the users having a bad time. Report p50 (typical), p95 and p99 (the tail), and max.</li>
      <li>You cannot average percentiles across instances; compute them from histograms.</li>
      <li>Measure at the edge the user sees (load balancer, client), and also per dependency.</li>
      <li>Watch for coordinated omission in load tests: a tool that waits for slow responses before sending the next request under-reports latency. Use open-model (arrival-rate) tests.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Kinds of performance test</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Test</th><th>Question</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Load</td><td>Do we meet the goal at expected peak?</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Stress</td><td>Where does it break, and how does it fail?</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Spike</td><td>What happens on a sudden 10× burst? Does autoscaling keep up?</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Soak</td><td>Does it degrade over hours (leaks, growing queues)?</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Capacity</td><td>How much traffic per instance, and what will growth cost?</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Micro-benchmark</td><td>Is this function faster than that one? (JMH, pytest-benchmark)</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note">Load-test example with k6 is on the <a href="#testing">Testing</a> tab. Test against production-sized data; a query fast on 10,000 rows can be slow on 20 million.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Profiling the JVM</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># Java Flight Recorder: low overhead, safe in production
jcmd &lt;pid&gt; JFR.start duration=60s filename=/tmp/rec.jfr
# or at startup
java -XX:StartFlightRecording=duration=60s,filename=rec.jfr -jar app.jar
# open rec.jfr in JDK Mission Control: hot methods, allocations, locks, GC

# async-profiler: flame graphs of CPU, allocation or wall-clock time
asprof -d 30 -e cpu   -f cpu.html   &lt;pid&gt;
asprof -d 30 -e alloc -f alloc.html &lt;pid&gt;

jcmd &lt;pid&gt; GC.heap_info
jcmd &lt;pid&gt; Thread.print            # thread dump: what is everyone waiting on?</code></pre></div>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Flame graph:</b> width = time spent; look for wide plateaus near the top.</li>
      <li><b>Containers:</b> set <code>-XX:MaxRAMPercentage</code>; watch GC pause time and allocation rate.</li>
      <li><b>Startup:</b> CDS/AOT caches, lazy initialisation, or native images for scale-to-zero.</li>
      <li><b>Blocking I/O:</b> virtual threads (21+) raise concurrency cheaply; CPU-bound work still needs cores.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Tuning the JVM: collector and memory</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Collector</th><th>From Oracle&rsquo;s garbage collection tuning guide</th><th>Flag</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>G1</td><td>Mostly concurrent; scales from small machines to large multiprocessor ones; the default on most hardware and operating systems</td><td><code>-XX:+UseG1GC</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Parallel (throughput)</td><td>Several threads speed up collection; for medium to large data sets on multiprocessor hardware</td><td><code>-XX:+UseParallelGC</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>ZGC</td><td>Maximum pauses under a millisecond, independent of heap size, at some cost in throughput; for low-latency applications</td><td><code>-XX:+UseZGC -XX:+ZGenerational</code> in Java 21 (<code>-XX:+UseZGC</code> alone selects the older, non-generational ZGC there)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Serial</td><td>A single thread does all collection work; chosen by default on some small configurations</td><td><code>-XX:+UseSerialGC</code></td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Start with the defaults.</b> Oracle&rsquo;s guide says: unless you have strict pause-time requirements, run first and let the VM choose a collector; if needed, adjust the heap size; only then change the collector.</li>
      <li><b>Size the heap in containers by percentage.</b> By default the JVM may use 25 percent of the available memory for the heap. A setting such as <code>-XX:MaxRAMPercentage=75</code> raises that; leave room for the rest of the process (threads, metaspace, direct buffers).</li>
      <li><b>One change at a time,</b> measured with the same load test before and after (see The method, above).</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note"><i>Our view:</i> for most services, the heap size and the number of database calls matter far more than the choice of collector. <i>The other view:</i> for services with strict tail-latency targets, switching to ZGC can be a large change, because pauses stop growing with the heap.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Micro-benchmarks done right (JMH)</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>@State(Scope.Benchmark)
@BenchmarkMode(Mode.AverageTime)
@OutputTimeUnit(TimeUnit.MICROSECONDS)
@Warmup(iterations = 3) @Measurement(iterations = 5) @Fork(1)
public class ParseBench {
  String raw = "05-05-1959";

  @Benchmark
  public LocalDate parse() {
    return OrderDateParser.parse(raw);    // return the result so it is not optimised away
  }
}</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">Hand-written timing loops on the JVM lie: no warm-up, dead-code elimination, JIT effects. Use JMH, and only after a profile shows the code matters.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Profiling Python</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># sampling profiler, attach to a running process, no code changes
py-spy top --pid 1234
py-spy record -o profile.svg --pid 1234          # flame graph

# deterministic profiler for a script
python -m cProfile -o out.prof -m loader
python -c "import pstats; pstats.Stats('out.prof').sort_stats('cumtime').print_stats(20)"

# imports and memory
python -X importtime -m loader 2&gt; imports.txt  # slow imports
pip install memray &amp;&amp; memray run -m loader     # allocations</code></pre></div>
    <ul>
      <li>Replace Python loops over rows with vectorised work: SQL in the warehouse, Polars, PyArrow, NumPy.</li>
      <li>Threads or asyncio for I/O-bound work; processes for CPU-bound work.</li>
      <li>Stream large files instead of reading them whole into memory.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Where time usually goes</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Area</th><th>Common cause</th><th>Fix</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Database</td><td>Missing index, N+1 queries, fetching unused columns, lock waits</td><td>EXPLAIN ANALYZE, indexes, joins or batch loads, select only needed columns (<a href="#database">Database Design</a>)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Connection pools</td><td>Pool too small (queueing) or too large (database overload)</td><td>Size from Little's law; watch pool wait time</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Service calls</td><td>Sequential calls, no timeouts, new connection per call</td><td>Parallelise independent calls, keep-alive, HTTP/2, timeouts</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Payloads</td><td>Huge JSON responses, no compression, no pagination</td><td>Paginate, compress, return only needed fields</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Serialisation</td><td>Reflection-heavy mapping in hot paths</td><td>Profile; precompiled serialisers; smaller objects</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Memory and GC</td><td>High allocation rate, heap too small, large object churn</td><td>Profile allocations; reuse buffers; tune heap and collector</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Cold starts</td><td>Large images, eager initialisation, JVM warm-up</td><td>Smaller images, CPU boost, min instances, AOT/native (<a href="#cloudrun">Cloud Run</a>)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Warehouse queries</td><td>Full scans, skewed joins, repeated subqueries</td><td>Partition filters, clustering, pre-aggregation (<a href="#sql">SQL Skills</a>)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Pipelines</td><td>One object at a time through one container; small files</td><td>Parallel tasks, bulk loads, purpose-built engines (Dataflow), compact files</td></tr>
      <tr><td>LLM calls</td><td>Long prompts, sequential calls, large models for simple tasks</td><td>Prompt caching, parallel calls, streaming, smaller models where evals allow</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Concurrency and backpressure</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Bound every queue and pool; an unbounded queue turns overload into an out-of-memory crash.</li>
      <li>Shed load early (429/503) when saturated rather than accepting work you cannot finish in time.</li>
      <li>Set timeouts shorter than the caller's, so work is not done for a caller that has given up.</li>
      <li>Cloud Run concurrency and max instances, and database pool size, must agree: instances × pool size ≤ database connection limit.</li>
      <li>Locks and synchronised blocks serialise work; look for them in thread dumps under load.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Performance as a habit</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Performance budgets</b> in CI: fail the build if a key benchmark or bundle size regresses beyond a threshold.</li>
      <li><b>Track p95/p99 per endpoint</b> after every deploy, with deploy markers on dashboards.</li>
      <li><b>Cost per unit of work</b> (per request, per row loaded) next to latency: faster that costs 5× may be the wrong trade.</li>
      <li><b>Capacity reviews</b> before expected growth or launches.</li>
      <li><b>Write down the numbers</b> of each investigation, so the next one starts from evidence.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Read p50/p95/p99 latency and throughput on a dashboard</li><li>Spot an obvious N+1 query or missing index</li><li>Run an existing load test and report the result</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Profile a JVM or Python service and fix the hot spot</li><li>Write load tests with goals and thresholds</li><li>Size pools and concurrency with Little's law</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Lead performance investigations end to end with evidence</li><li>Set performance goals, budgets and capacity plans</li><li>Balance latency, throughput and cost in design</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Own performance engineering practice across the organisation</li><li>Understand runtime, OS and hardware effects deeply</li><li>Design systems for extreme scale and tail latency</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>Flame graphs and off-CPU analysis</li><li>JVM garbage collector tuning</li><li>Queueing theory basics</li><li>The Tail at Scale</li><li>Coordinated omission in load testing</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://www.brendangregg.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Brendan Gregg's site</a><span class="lnote"> The USE method, flame graphs, Linux performance tools</span></li><li><a href="https://research.google/pubs/the-tail-at-scale/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The Tail at Scale (Dean &amp; Barroso)</a><span class="lnote"> Why tail latency dominates in fan-out systems</span></li><li><a href="https://github.com/async-profiler/async-profiler" target="_blank" rel="noopener">async-profiler</a><span class="lnote"> Low-overhead JVM profiling and flame graphs</span></li><li><a href="https://github.com/openjdk/jmh" target="_blank" rel="noopener">JMH</a><span class="lnote"> Correct Java micro-benchmarks</span></li><li><a href="https://github.com/benfred/py-spy" target="_blank" rel="noopener">py-spy</a><span class="lnote"> Sampling profiler for Python</span></li><li><a href="https://cloud.google.com/profiler/docs" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cloud Profiler</a><span class="lnote"> Continuous profiling on GCP</span></li><li><a href="https://grafana.com/docs/k6/latest/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Grafana k6</a><span class="lnote"> Load testing</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Little%27s_law?action=raw" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Little's law (L = λW)</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amdahl%27s_law?action=raw" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Amdahl's law</a>; <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/21/docs/specs/man/jcmd.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">jcmd manual, JDK 21 (JFR.start, GC.heap_info, Thread.print)</a>; <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/21/docs/specs/man/java.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">java manual, JDK 21 (-XX:StartFlightRecording, MaxRAMPercentage)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/async-profiler/async-profiler/master/docs/ProfilerOptions.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">async-profiler options (-d, -e cpu|alloc, -f)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/benfred/py-spy/master/README.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">py-spy README (top, record)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/python/cpython/main/Doc/using/cmdline.rst" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CPython docs: -X importtime</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/bloomberg/memray/main/src/memray/commands/run.py" target="_blank" rel="noopener">memray run command (run as module)</a>; <a href="https://docs.python.org/3/library/profiling.tracing.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Python docs: profiling.tracing (cProfile)</a>; <a href="https://docs.python.org/3/library/pstats.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Python docs: pstats</a>; <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/21/gctuning/available-collectors.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Oracle: Java 21 GC tuning, available collectors</a>; <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/21/docs/specs/man/java.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Oracle: the java command (MaxRAMPercentage)</a>. A few claims could not be confirmed at their official source from here and are being re-checked.</p><p class="runcode">A question, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/enrichmeai.github.io/issues/new?title=%5BPerformance%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>. Nothing here is tracked and there are no comments to store: questions live in the open, where the next learner can find the answer.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= SECURITY ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-security" data-name="Security">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Security</h2><p>The risks every engineer must recognise, and the habits that prevent most of them: in application code, APIs, data, cloud and the supply chain.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Principles</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Least privilege:</b> every person, service and token gets only the access it needs, for only as long as it needs it.</li>
      <li><b>Defence in depth:</b> several independent layers (network, identity, application, data), so one failure is not a breach.</li>
      <li><b>Secure by default:</b> private unless opened, denied unless allowed, encrypted unless there is a reason not to be.</li>
      <li><b>Never trust input:</b> anything from a user, another service, a file, a web page or a model's output is validated before use.</li>
      <li><b>No secrets in code, images, logs or chat.</b> They live in a secret manager and are injected at runtime.</li>
      <li><b>Fail closed:</b> when an auth or permission check cannot run, deny. A gate that opens on error is not a gate.</li>
      <li><b>Assume breach:</b> log and alert so that a compromise is noticed and contained quickly.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>OWASP Top 10 (2025 edition)</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Risk</th><th>Looks like</th><th>Prevent with</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>A01 Broken access control</td><td><code>GET /api/orders/123</code> returns another user's order; the server fetches a user-supplied URL that points to an internal service or metadata endpoint (SSRF, now part of this category)</td><td>Check ownership on every request, server side; deny by default; test it. For URLs: allow-list destinations, block internal ranges, separate egress</td></tr>
      <tr><td>A02 Security misconfiguration</td><td>Public bucket, debug endpoints on, default passwords, open CORS</td><td>Infrastructure as code, reviewed; secure defaults; scanners</td></tr>
      <tr><td>A03 Software supply chain failures</td><td>An old library with a known CVE; a compromised dependency or build tool</td><td>An SBOM, dependency updates, SCA scanning, components from trusted sources only, protected CI</td></tr>
      <tr><td>A04 Cryptographic failures</td><td>Plain HTTP, passwords hashed with MD5, keys in the repo</td><td>TLS everywhere, argon2id/bcrypt for passwords, managed keys</td></tr>
      <tr><td>A05 Injection</td><td>SQL built by string concatenation; shell commands with user input</td><td>Parameterised queries, no shell with input, allow-lists</td></tr>
      <tr><td>A06 Insecure design</td><td>No rate limit on login; reset tokens that never expire</td><td>Threat model at design time; abuse cases as requirements</td></tr>
      <tr><td>A07 Authentication failures</td><td>Weak passwords allowed, no MFA, session never expires</td><td>Use a proven identity provider; MFA; short-lived tokens</td></tr>
      <tr><td>A08 Software or data integrity failures</td><td>Unsigned build artefacts; deserialising untrusted data</td><td>Signed images, pinned dependencies, verified artefacts</td></tr>
      <tr><td>A09 Security logging &amp; alerting failures</td><td>A breach discovered months later; logs nobody is alerted on</td><td>Audit logs, alerts on suspicious patterns, retained logs</td></tr>
      <tr><td>A10 Mishandling of exceptional conditions</td><td>An error path that fails open, leaks a stack trace, or leaves a transaction half done</td><td>Handle errors where they happen and in one global handler; fail closed and roll back; rate limits and quotas</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note">The 2021 edition numbered these differently and had SSRF as its own A10. OWASP revises the list every few years; check owasp.org for the latest edition. The API Security Top 10 is the companion list for APIs.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Injection: always parameterise</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>// Java JDBC
// WRONG: "SELECT * FROM orders WHERE order_ref = '" + orderRef + "'"
PreparedStatement ps = conn.prepareStatement(
    "SELECT * FROM orders WHERE order_ref = ?");
ps.setString(1, orderRef);

// Spring JdbcClient
jdbc.sql("SELECT * FROM orders WHERE order_ref = :order_ref").param("order_ref", orderRef)...

# Python psycopg
cur.execute("SELECT * FROM orders WHERE order_ref = %s", (order_ref,))   # never f-strings

# BigQuery
bigquery.QueryJobConfig(query_parameters=[
    bigquery.ScalarQueryParameter("order_ref", "STRING", order_ref)])      # WHERE order_ref = @order_ref</code></pre></div>
    <ul>
      <li>Identifiers (table and column names) cannot be parameters: pick them from an allow-list.</li>
      <li>Never pass user input to a shell. Use the library call, or an argument list without a shell.</li>
      <li>Escape output for its context (HTML, JSON, URLs) to prevent XSS. Use the framework's templating, not string building.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Authentication &amp; authorisation</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Authentication</b> is who you are; <b>authorisation</b> is what you may do. Most breaches are authorisation bugs.</li>
      <li><b>Use OIDC / OAuth 2.0</b> with a proven provider rather than building login yourself. For browser apps, the authorisation code flow with PKCE.</li>
      <li><b>JWTs:</b> verify the signature, issuer, audience and expiry on every request; keep them short-lived; never accept <code>alg: none</code>; never put secrets in them (they are only encoded).</li>
      <li><b>Refresh tokens</b> rotate on use and are revocable; in browsers keep them in <code>HttpOnly; Secure; SameSite</code> cookies.</li>
      <li><b>Check permissions on the server for every request</b> and every object: "is this user allowed this order?", not just "is the user logged in?".</li>
      <li><b>Load roles from the source of truth</b> on each authentication; do not trust roles a client sends.</li>
      <li><b>Passwords:</b> argon2id or bcrypt, rate-limited login, breached-password checks, MFA for admins.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Spring Security: a stateless API</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>@Bean
SecurityFilterChain api(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
  return http
    .authorizeHttpRequests(a -&gt; a
        .requestMatchers("/actuator/health/**").permitAll()
        .requestMatchers("/api/admin/**").hasRole("ADMIN")
        .anyRequest().authenticated())            // deny by default
    .oauth2ResourceServer(o -&gt; o.jwt(Customizer.withDefaults()))
    .sessionManagement(s -&gt; s.sessionCreationPolicy(SessionCreationPolicy.STATELESS))
    .csrf(c -&gt; c.disable())   // only safe when auth is a bearer header, not a cookie
    .build();
}

// object-level check in the service, not only the URL rule
if (!caseFile.ownerId().equals(currentUserId)) throw new AccessDeniedException("not yours");</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Web &amp; API hardening</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>HTTPS only,</b> with HSTS (<code>Strict-Transport-Security</code>).</li>
      <li><b>Content-Security-Policy</b> to limit where scripts load from; <code>X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff</code>; <code>frame-ancestors</code> against clickjacking.</li>
      <li><b>CORS:</b> list exact origins; never reflect any origin with credentials allowed.</li>
      <li><b>CSRF:</b> needed when the browser sends cookies automatically; use tokens or <code>SameSite</code> cookies.</li>
      <li><b>Rate limits</b> on login, signup, password reset and expensive endpoints.</li>
      <li><b>Validate request bodies</b> (types, lengths, ranges) and reject unknown fields where it matters.</li>
      <li><b>Errors:</b> generic messages to the client, details to the logs. No stack traces in responses.</li>
      <li><b>Uploads:</b> check type and size, store outside the web root, scan if users share them.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Secrets</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># store
echo -n "$VALUE" | gcloud secrets create db-password --data-file=-
# grant one workload access to one secret
gcloud secrets add-iam-policy-binding db-password \
  --member=serviceAccount:api@p.iam.gserviceaccount.com \
  --role=roles/secretmanager.secretAccessor
# rotate: add a version, roll the workload, then disable and destroy the old one
gcloud secrets versions add db-password --data-file=new.txt
gcloud secrets versions destroy 1 --secret=db-password</code></pre></div>
    <ul>
      <li>No service account JSON keys: Workload Identity Federation for CI, attached service accounts for workloads.</li>
      <li>A rotation is not finished until old versions are destroyed.</li>
      <li>Scan commits for secrets (gitleaks, GitHub secret scanning). A leaked secret is rotated, not just deleted from Git: history and forks keep it.</li>
      <li>Never print secrets in logs, CI output, error messages or AI prompts.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Cloud security on GCP</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>IAM:</b> one service account per workload; predefined narrow roles; no Owner or Editor for services; grant to groups, not individuals.</li>
      <li><b>Organisation policies:</b> block public buckets, service account key creation, and external IPs where not needed.</li>
      <li><b>Network:</b> private IPs for databases; ingress limited to the load balancer; Cloud Armor in front of public apps.</li>
      <li><b>Data:</b> encryption at rest is on by default; CMEK when you must control keys; VPC Service Controls around sensitive datasets.</li>
      <li><b>Audit logs:</b> Admin Activity is always on; enable Data Access logs for sensitive services so reads are attributable.</li>
      <li><b>Security Command Center</b> surfaces misconfigurations and threats across the project.</li>
      <li><b>Separate projects</b> for dev and prod; nobody's laptop has standing prod write access.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Threat modelling in 30 minutes</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Draw the data flow: users, services, stores, and the trust boundaries between them.</li>
      <li>For each boundary, ask STRIDE questions.</li>
      <li>List the threats, rate likelihood and impact, and pick mitigations.</li>
      <li>Turn mitigations into tickets and tests.</li>
    </ol>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>STRIDE</th><th>Question</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Spoofing</td><td>Can someone pretend to be another user or service?</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Tampering</td><td>Can data be changed in transit or at rest?</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Repudiation</td><td>Can someone deny an action because nothing recorded it?</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Information disclosure</td><td>Can data leak to someone not entitled to it?</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Denial of service</td><td>Can someone exhaust a resource or the budget?</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Elevation of privilege</td><td>Can someone gain rights they should not have?</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Step 1, drawn</h3>
    <figure class="fig"><svg viewBox="0 0 660 285" role="img" aria-labelledby="stride-t stride-d"><title id="stride-t">A data flow with trust boundaries</title><desc id="stride-d">A small data-flow diagram. A user's browser sends requests across the internet trust boundary to an API. The API reads and writes a database across a second boundary, and calls a payment provider across a third. Each boundary is where to ask the six STRIDE questions: spoofing, tampering, repudiation, information disclosure, denial of service and elevation of privilege.</desc><defs><marker id="stride-a" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker><marker id="stride-aa" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar acc" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker></defs><rect class="nd in" x="10" y="100" width="120" height="46" rx="6"/><text class="lb hd" x="70" y="128" text-anchor="middle">browser</text><rect class="nd out" x="260" y="100" width="140" height="46" rx="6"/><text class="lb hd" x="330" y="128" text-anchor="middle">API</text><rect class="nd" x="520" y="40" width="130" height="46" rx="6"/><text class="lb hd" x="585" y="68" text-anchor="middle">database</text><rect class="nd" x="520" y="160" width="130" height="46" rx="6"/><text class="lb hd" x="585" y="182" text-anchor="middle">payment</text><text class="lb hd" x="585" y="198" text-anchor="middle">provider</text><line class="ed" x1="130" y1="123" x2="256" y2="123" marker-end="url(#stride-a)"/><line class="ed" x1="400" y1="115" x2="516" y2="70" marker-end="url(#stride-a)"/><line class="ed" x1="400" y1="131" x2="516" y2="180" marker-end="url(#stride-a)"/><line class="bd" x1="195" y1="60" x2="195" y2="180"/><text class="lb acc" x="195" y="50" text-anchor="middle">boundary: internet</text><line class="bd" x1="460" y1="30" x2="460" y2="110"/><text class="lb acc" x="460" y="22" text-anchor="middle">boundary: data</text><line class="bd" x1="460" y1="140" x2="460" y2="230"/><text class="lb acc" x="460" y="248" text-anchor="middle">boundary: third party</text><text class="lb mu" x="330" y="276" text-anchor="middle">at each boundary ask S T R I D E, rate each threat, turn mitigations into tickets and tests</text></svg><figcaption>Step 1 of the method above: draw the flow and mark the trust boundaries. The dashed lines are where the STRIDE questions go.</figcaption></figure>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Data protection &amp; privacy</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Know where personal data is:</b> tag columns and datasets; keep an inventory.</li>
      <li><b>Collect less, keep less:</b> only what the purpose needs, with a retention period.</li>
      <li><b>Mask or tokenise</b> personal data in non-production environments and analytics.</li>
      <li><b>Honour legal obligations:</b> deletion requests, legal holds, consent. Apply them on every read path, not only in one table.</li>
      <li><b>Know the law that applies:</b> for India, the Digital Personal Data Protection Act, 2023; GDPR for EU residents.</li>
      <li><b>Logs are data too:</b> no personal data or tokens in log lines unless necessary and protected.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Supply chain &amp; CI</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Pin dependencies with lockfiles; review what new dependencies bring.</li>
      <li>Automated updates (Dependabot, Renovate) gated by tests.</li>
      <li>Scan: SAST (CodeQL, Semgrep), SCA (Dependabot alerts, OSV), images (Trivy), secrets (gitleaks).</li>
      <li>Pin third-party GitHub Actions to a commit SHA; give workflows the minimum <code>permissions:</code>.</li>
      <li>Protect <code>main</code> and deploy tags; require reviews and checks; restrict who can push release tags.</li>
      <li>Sign images and generate SBOMs so you can prove what is running.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Security testing</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Unit and integration tests for access control:</b> user A cannot read user B's object; an expired token is refused; a missing role gets 403.</li>
      <li><b>SAST</b> reads code for dangerous patterns; <b>DAST</b> (OWASP ZAP) probes the running app.</li>
      <li><b>Dependency and image scans</b> on every build.</li>
      <li><b>Fuzzing</b> parsers and file handlers with malformed input.</li>
      <li><b>Penetration test</b> before launch and after major changes, by someone independent.</li>
      <li><b>Prove each guard red:</b> try the forbidden action in a test and watch it fail.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Cryptography: use it, do not invent it</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Know the three jobs:</b> hashing proves something has not changed and cannot be reversed; encryption hides data and can be reversed with the key; signing proves who produced something.</li>
      <li><b>Passwords are hashed, never encrypted,</b> with a slow, salted algorithm. The OWASP Password Storage Cheat Sheet recommends Argon2id (at least 19 MiB of memory, 2 iterations, parallelism 1); scrypt if Argon2id is not available; bcrypt (work factor 10 or more, 72-byte password limit) for legacy systems; PBKDF2 with HMAC-SHA-256 and 600,000 or more iterations where FIPS-140 is required. Fast hashes such as SHA-256 alone are not suitable.</li>
      <li><b>Use a vetted library and its defaults.</b> Never write your own algorithm or protocol.</li>
      <li><b>Random means a secure generator:</b> <code>SecureRandom</code> in Java, the <code>secrets</code> module in Python, never <code>Math.random</code> or <code>random</code> for tokens.</li>
      <li><b>Encrypt in transit</b> (TLS 1.2 or 1.3 everywhere, inside the network too) <b>and at rest,</b> and keep keys in a key management service, apart from the data, with rotation.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Containers and Kubernetes</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Small images:</b> a minimal or distroless base has fewer packages to patch. Scan images in CI, pin them by digest, and sign them.</li>
      <li><b>Never bake secrets into an image</b>; mount them at run time from a secret manager.</li>
      <li><b>Pod Security Standards</b> define three cumulative profiles: Privileged, Baseline and Restricted. Aim for Restricted, and enforce it per namespace with the label <code>pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce: restricted</code>.</li>
      <li><b>Least privilege in the cluster:</b> narrow RBAC roles, no service-account token where a pod does not call the API, and NetworkPolicies that deny by default.</li>
    </ul>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># container securityContext that meets Restricted
securityContext:
  runAsNonRoot: true
  allowPrivilegeEscalation: false
  capabilities:
    drop: ["ALL"]
  seccompProfile:
    type: RuntimeDefault
  readOnlyRootFilesystem: true   # good practice too</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Networks and zero trust</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Zero trust:</b> being inside the network earns no trust. Every call is authenticated and authorised, service to service as well as user to service (mTLS and tokens; see <a href="#servicemesh">Service Mesh</a>).</li>
      <li><b>Deny by default:</b> firewalls and security groups open only the ports and sources a service needs. Control outbound traffic too: a compromised service that cannot reach the internet cannot send your data out.</li>
      <li><b>Keep databases and internal services off the public internet;</b> use private networking and private endpoints.</li>
      <li><b>Protect the edge:</b> a WAF and DDoS protection in front of public endpoints, rate limits on APIs (see <a href="#apigee">Apigee</a>).</li>
      <li><b>Segment:</b> a breach in one area should not open every other one.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>AI and LLM security</h3>
    <p>The OWASP GenAI LLM Top 10 (2026 edition, published August 2026) lists the main risks for applications built on language models:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>LLM01 Prompt Injection.</b> Treat every input, including retrieved documents, web pages and tool results, as possibly carrying instructions. Separate instructions from data; never let content alone trigger an action.</li>
      <li><b>LLM02 Sensitive Information Disclosure.</b> Do not put data in a prompt that the user may not see; filter outputs; check what your provider keeps.</li>
      <li><b>LLM03 Excessive Agency.</b> Give tools the least privilege they need, and require a person's approval for actions that change things.</li>
      <li><b>LLM04 Supply Chain.</b> Vet models, datasets, plugins and MCP servers as you would any dependency.</li>
      <li><b>LLM05 Data and Model Poisoning.</b> Know where training and retrieval data come from, and who can write to them.</li>
      <li><b>LLM06 Unbounded Consumption.</b> Rate limits, token budgets and timeouts per user and per job.</li>
      <li><b>LLM07 Misinformation.</b> Ground answers in sources, cite them, and evaluate; never present a guess as fact.</li>
      <li><b>LLM08 Hidden Context Exposure.</b> Assume the system prompt and tool schemas can be extracted: never put secrets or security rules only there.</li>
      <li><b>LLM09 Vector and Embedding Weaknesses.</b> Apply access control in retrieval, so a user can only retrieve what they may read.</li>
      <li><b>LLM10 Improper Output Handling.</b> Treat model output as untrusted input: validate, escape and parameterise it before it reaches a browser, a shell or a query.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">More in <a href="#ai">AI for Engineers</a>, <a href="#rag">RAG</a>, <a href="#mcp">MCP</a> and <a href="#agentharness">Agent Harness</a>.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>People: the most targeted part of any system</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Most attacks start with a person,</b> not a server: a convincing email, message or call.</li>
      <li><b>Urgency is the warning sign.</b> "Pay this now", "reset my access", "send me the file": check through another channel you already trust before acting. Voices and video can now be faked convincingly.</li>
      <li><b>Use phishing-resistant sign-in</b> where you can (passkeys or hardware security keys), a password manager with a unique password everywhere, and multi-factor authentication on every account that offers it.</li>
      <li><b>Keep devices updated and locked,</b> and never leave a logged-in laptop unattended.</li>
      <li><b>Report mistakes at once, without blame.</b> A clicked link reported in five minutes is a small incident; hidden for a week, it is a big one.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Security logging and detection</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Log what an investigator will need:</b> sign-ins that succeed and fail, permission and role changes, admin actions, data exports, and access denied.</li>
      <li><b>Never log secrets,</b> tokens, passwords or full card numbers, and mask personal data.</li>
      <li><b>Protect the logs:</b> separate access, append-only or write-once storage, kept long enough to investigate.</li>
      <li><b>Alert on what matters:</b> many failed sign-ins, access from somewhere new, a sudden large export, a disabled control. See <a href="#observability">Observability</a>.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Backups and ransomware</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>A common rule of thumb is 3-2-1:</b> three copies of your data, on two different kinds of storage, with one copy off-site.</li>
      <li><b>Keep one copy immutable or offline,</b> with separate credentials, so an attacker who gets in cannot delete or encrypt the backups too.</li>
      <li><b>A backup you have not restored is a hope.</b> Test restores on a schedule and time them.</li>
      <li><b>Agree the targets:</b> how much data you can lose (RPO) and how long you can be down (RTO), then design to meet them.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Compliance frameworks you will meet</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>ISO/IEC 27001:</b> an information security management system, certified by audit.</li>
      <li><b>SOC 2:</b> an auditor's report on a service organisation's controls, common with US customers.</li>
      <li><b>PCI DSS:</b> rules for handling payment card data (see <a href="#payments">Payment Gateways</a>).</li>
      <li><b>Data protection law:</b> the GDPR in the EU, the UK GDPR, India's Digital Personal Data Protection Act, and others where your users live.</li>
      <li><b>Compliance is not security.</b> A certificate shows controls existed when audited; good engineering keeps them working every day.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Practise it: seven security drills</h3>
    <p>The <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/well-rounded-engineer-labs/tree/main/security/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">security track</a> in the labs turns the basics on this page into short drills, each in Python and Java: broken code from a real kind of breach, with tests that fail until you fix it. One shared <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/well-rounded-engineer-labs/blob/main/security/STANDARD.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">standard</a> states the seven rules once, as a review checklist, as a block to paste into a coding agent&rsquo;s instructions, and as the tests that enforce both.</p>
    <div class="tbl"><table><thead><tr><th>Drill</th><th>You learn</th><th>OWASP Top 10:2025</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td><a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/well-rounded-engineer-labs/tree/main/security/01-file-anyone-could-read" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The file anyone could read</a></td><td>Resolve a path, then check it is still inside the folder</td><td>A01</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/well-rounded-engineer-labs/tree/main/security/02-order-that-was-not-yours" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The order that was not yours</a></td><td>Check access on every object, not only at login</td><td>A01</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/well-rounded-engineer-labs/tree/main/security/03-comment-that-ran-code" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The comment that ran code</a></td><td>Encode output; never build markup by joining strings</td><td>A05</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/well-rounded-engineer-labs/tree/main/security/04-password-stored-fast" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The password stored the fast way</a></td><td>Slow, salted password hashing; constant-time compare</td><td>A04</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/well-rounded-engineer-labs/tree/main/security/05-reset-link-you-could-guess" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The reset link you could guess</a></td><td>Secure randomness, single use, expiry</td><td>A04, A07</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/well-rounded-engineer-labs/tree/main/security/06-secret-in-the-log" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The secret in the log</a></td><td>Allow-list what you log; redact the rest by name</td><td>A09</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/well-rounded-engineer-labs/tree/main/security/07-field-nobody-meant-to-change" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The field nobody meant to change</a></td><td>Allow-list what a request may change</td><td>A08</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Security checklist for a pull request</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Is every new endpoint authenticated, and does it check ownership of each object?</li>
      <li>Is all input validated, and every query parameterised?</li>
      <li>Any new secret? Is it in the secret manager, not in code or config?</li>
      <li>Any new permission or IAM role? Is it the narrowest that works?</li>
      <li>Does anything new get logged that should not be (tokens, personal data)?</li>
      <li>New dependency: maintained, licensed acceptably, no known CVEs?</li>
      <li>What happens on error: does it fail closed?</li>
      <li>Is there a test that proves the forbidden case is refused?</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>When something goes wrong</h3>
    <ol>
      <li><b>Contain:</b> revoke the leaked key, disable the account, block the IP, take the endpoint down.</li>
      <li><b>Preserve evidence:</b> keep logs and snapshots before cleaning up.</li>
      <li><b>Assess:</b> what was accessed, since when, whose data.</li>
      <li><b>Notify:</b> security lead, then customers and regulators as the law requires and within its deadlines.</li>
      <li><b>Fix the cause</b> and add a guard (test, policy, alert) so it cannot recur.</li>
    </ol>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Spot phishing; use a password manager and passkeys</li><li>Parameterise every query; never commit secrets</li><li>Check that users can only reach their own data</li><li>Apply the PR security checklist</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Hash passwords correctly and run containers to the Restricted profile</li><li>Implement OAuth 2.0 / OIDC with a provider and validate tokens properly</li><li>Use least-privilege IAM and a secret manager</li><li>Fix findings from SAST, SCA and image scans</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Threat-model a system and turn threats into tests</li><li>Design authentication, authorisation and audit for a product</li><li>Respond to and contain a security incident</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Own the security architecture and policies across teams</li><li>Lead compliance (DPDP, GDPR, SOC 2) and external testing</li><li>Build a security culture and secure-by-default platforms</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>OWASP ASVS verification levels</li><li>OAuth 2.0 security best current practice</li><li>Supply-chain security with SLSA</li><li>Cloud security posture management</li><li>LLM and AI application security</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OWASP Top 10</a><span class="lnote"> Most critical web application risks</span></li><li><a href="https://genai.owasp.org/resource/owasp-genai-llm-top-10-2026/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OWASP GenAI LLM Top 10 (2026)</a><span class="lnote"> Risks for applications built on language models</span></li><li><a href="https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Password_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OWASP Password Storage Cheat Sheet</a><span class="lnote"> Argon2id, scrypt, bcrypt and PBKDF2 settings</span></li><li><a href="https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/security/pod-security-standards/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kubernetes Pod Security Standards</a><span class="lnote"> Privileged, Baseline and Restricted profiles</span></li><li><a href="https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OWASP Cheat Sheet Series</a><span class="lnote"> Concise how-tos for each security topic</span></li><li><a href="https://owasp.org/www-project-api-security/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OWASP API Security Top 10</a><span class="lnote"> Risks specific to APIs</span></li><li><a href="https://owasp.org/www-project-application-security-verification-standard/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OWASP ASVS</a><span class="lnote"> A checklist of security requirements by level</span></li><li><a href="https://docs.spring.io/spring-security/reference/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Spring Security reference</a><span class="lnote"> Authentication and authorisation in Spring</span></li><li><a href="https://cloud.google.com/security/best-practices" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google Cloud security best practices</a><span class="lnote"> IAM, network and data protection on GCP</span></li><li><a href="https://slsa.dev/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">SLSA</a><span class="lnote"> Supply-chain levels for software artefacts</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/OWASP/Top10/master/2025/docs/en/index.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OWASP Top 10 2025 index (source repo)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/OWASP/Top10/master/2025/docs/en/0x00_2025-Introduction.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OWASP Top 10 2025 introduction (SSRF rolled into A01, A10 new)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/GenAI-Security-Project/GenAI-LLM-Top10/main/README.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OWASP GenAI LLM Top 10 2026 list</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/OWASP/www-project-top-10-for-large-language-model-applications/main/README.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OWASP LLM project README (2026 published August 4, 2026)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/OWASP/CheatSheetSeries/master/cheatsheets/Password_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OWASP Password Storage Cheat Sheet</a>; <a href="https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/security/pod-security-standards/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kubernetes Pod Security Standards</a>; <a href="https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/security/pod-security-admission/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kubernetes Pod Security Admission (namespace labels)</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/logging/docs/audit" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cloud Audit Logs (Admin Activity always written)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/main/module/spring-boot-security-oauth2-resource-server/src/main/java/org/springframework/boot/security/oauth2/server/resource/autoconfigure/OAuth2ResourceServerProperties.java" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Spring Boot OAuth2ResourceServerProperties (issuer-uri, audiences)</a>; <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9700.txt" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RFC 9700 OAuth 2.0 Security BCP</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/sdk/gcloud/reference/secrets/create" target="_blank" rel="noopener">gcloud secrets reference</a>; <a href="https://docs.spring.io/spring-security/reference/servlet/oauth2/resource-server/jwt.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Spring Security: JWT resource server</a>; <a href="https://docs.spring.io/spring-security/reference/servlet/authentication/session-management.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Spring Security: session management (STATELESS)</a>; <a href="https://docs.spring.io/spring-framework/reference/data-access/jdbc/core.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Spring Framework: JdbcClient</a>.</p><p class="runcode">A question, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/enrichmeai.github.io/issues/new?title=%5BSecurity%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>. Nothing here is tracked and there are no comments to store: questions live in the open, where the next learner can find the answer.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= KEYS, CERTIFICATES AND SSH ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-pki" data-name="Keys, Certificates &amp; SSH">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Keys, certificates and SSH</h2><p>HTTPS, SSH logins, signed Git commits and service-to-service mTLS all rest on one idea: a pair of keys, one public and one private. This page explains the pair, how a certificate ties a public key to a name, what a certificate signing request (CSR) and a certificate authority (CA) do, and how SSH keys and Git signing work. The OpenSSL, ssh-keygen and Git commands on this page were run (OpenSSL <span data-other-version>3.0.13</span>, OpenSSH 9.6, Git 2.43), and the error messages are the real ones. <code>ssh -T git@github.com</code> is GitHub&rsquo;s own documented test and was not run here. HTTP and the TLS handshake are on <a href="#networking">Networking</a>; SSH tunnels and jump hosts are on <a href="#linux">Linux</a>.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>One idea underneath: a key pair</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Two keys, made together.</b> The public key can be shown to anyone. The private key never leaves its owner: not in email, not in a ticket, not in a repository.</li>
      <li><b>Encrypt:</b> anyone with the public key can encrypt a message, but only the holder of the private key can decrypt it.</li>
      <li><b>Sign:</b> the private key signs, and anyone with the public key can check that signature. This is how a server proves who it is, how an SSH key logs you in, and how a commit is marked as yours.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>If this is true for you</th><th>Key type it leans towards (<i>analysis</i>)</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>A new SSH key for you or a service</td><td>Ed25519: the type GitHub&rsquo;s own instructions generate</td></tr>
      <tr><td>A legacy system that does not support Ed25519</td><td>RSA, 4096 bits, as GitHub suggests for that case</td></tr>
      <tr><td>A TLS certificate for a website or service</td><td>ECDSA (for example P-256) or RSA; check what your clients and CA support</td></tr>
      <tr><td>You want the key to need a hardware token</td><td>A security-key type such as <code>ed25519-sk</code></td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>What a certificate is</h3>
    <p>A certificate authority (CA) stores, signs and issues digital certificates. A certificate certifies that a public key belongs to the named subject, so others can rely on it. Web certificates use the X.509 format (RFC 5280). The fields you will read most:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Subject</b> and <b>Subject Alternative Name (SAN):</b> who it is for. Clients match the hostname against the SAN.</li>
      <li><b>Issuer:</b> the CA that signed it.</li>
      <li><b>Validity:</b> not before, not after.</li>
      <li><b>Public key,</b> and the issuer&rsquo;s <b>signature</b> over all of the above.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>The chain of trust</h3>
    <p>Your operating system or browser ships a store of trusted <b>root</b> certificates. Roots sign <b>intermediate</b> CAs, and intermediates sign the <b>leaf</b> certificate on your server. A client validates the chain from the leaf up to a root it already trusts.</p>
    <figure class="fig"><svg viewBox="0 0 620 230" role="img" aria-labelledby="pki-t pki-d"><title id="pki-t">A certificate chain of trust</title><desc id="pki-d">Three boxes left to right: a root CA certificate, which is self-signed and already in the client's trust store; an intermediate CA certificate, signed by the root; and the leaf certificate for the server's name, signed by the intermediate. The server sends the leaf and the intermediate. The client checks each signature back to the root it already trusts.</desc><defs><marker id="pki-a" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker><marker id="pki-aa" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar acc" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker></defs><rect class="nd in" x="10" y="40" width="170" height="64" rx="8"/><text class="lb hd" x="95" y="68" text-anchor="middle">Root CA</text><text class="lb mu" x="95" y="90" text-anchor="middle">self-signed</text><line class="ed" x1="182" y1="72" x2="222" y2="72" marker-end="url(#pki-a)"/><text class="lb mu" x="202" y="62" text-anchor="middle">signs</text><rect class="nd" x="225" y="40" width="170" height="64" rx="8"/><text class="lb hd" x="310" y="68" text-anchor="middle">Intermediate CA</text><text class="lb mu" x="310" y="90" text-anchor="middle">signed by root</text><line class="ed" x1="397" y1="72" x2="437" y2="72" marker-end="url(#pki-a)"/><text class="lb mu" x="417" y="62" text-anchor="middle">signs</text><rect class="nd out" x="440" y="40" width="170" height="64" rx="8"/><text class="lb hd" x="525" y="68" text-anchor="middle">Leaf certificate</text><text class="lb mu" x="525" y="90" text-anchor="middle">CN / SAN: shop.internal</text><path class="ed" d="M225,130 L225,140 L610,140 L610,130"/><text class="lb mu" x="417" y="160" text-anchor="middle">sent by the server</text><path class="ed" d="M10,130 L10,140 L180,140 L180,130"/><text class="lb mu" x="95" y="160" text-anchor="middle">already in the client&rsquo;s trust store</text><path class="ed acc" d="M600,196 L30,196" marker-end="url(#pki-aa)"/><text class="lb acc" x="315" y="218" text-anchor="middle">the client validates each signature back to the root</text></svg><figcaption>The chain of trust. The server sends its leaf certificate and the intermediate; the client already holds the root. A missing intermediate breaks the chain.</figcaption></figure>
    <p class="note"><i>Analysis:</i> the server must send its leaf and the intermediates; the client already has the root. A missing intermediate is a common cause of &ldquo;works in my browser, fails in my code&rdquo;, because some clients fetch a missing intermediate themselves and many libraries do not.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>A certificate signing request (CSR), step by step</h3>
    <p>A CSR is the message you send a CA to apply for a certificate. RFC 2986 defines it in three parts: the request information (your name, your public key and any attributes), a signature algorithm, and a signature over the request information made with your private key. That signature proves you hold the private key, which never goes to the CA.</p>
<pre><code># 1. Make a private key and a CSR, with the names the certificate must cover
openssl req -new -newkey ec -pkeyopt ec_paramgen_curve:P-256 -noenc \
  -keyout server.key -out server.csr \
  -subj "/CN=shop.internal" \
  -addext "subjectAltName=DNS:shop.internal,DNS:api.shop.internal"

# 2. Check it before you send it
openssl req -in server.csr -noout -verify      # Certificate request self-signature verify OK
openssl req -in server.csr -noout -text        # read the subject and the SAN

# 3. Send server.csr (never server.key) to the CA; it returns server.crt
# 4. Read what came back, and check it matches your key
openssl x509 -in server.crt -noout -subject -issuer -dates -ext subjectAltName
openssl x509 -in server.crt -noout -pubkey | openssl sha256
openssl pkey -in server.key -pubout       | openssl sha256   # the two hashes must match</code></pre>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Your own CA, for a lab or internal services</h3>
<pre><code># a root CA (keep ca.key offline in real use)
openssl req -x509 -new -newkey ec -pkeyopt ec_paramgen_curve:P-256 -noenc \
  -keyout ca.key -out ca.crt -days 365 -subj "/CN=Lab Root CA"

# the CA signs the CSR from the card above, keeping its SAN
openssl x509 -req -in server.csr -CA ca.crt -CAkey ca.key -CAcreateserial \
  -out server.crt -days 90 -copy_extensions copy

# validate the chain, as a client would
openssl verify -CAfile ca.crt server.crt     # server.crt: OK</code></pre>
    <p class="note">These commands were run with OpenSSL <span data-other-version>3.0.13</span>. <code>-copy_extensions</code> on <code>openssl x509 -req</code> is documented in the 3.x manual; on an older OpenSSL, put the SAN in an <code>-extfile</code> instead. A private CA is trusted only by machines you configure to trust it; for anything the public reaches, use a public CA.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Getting certificates automatically: ACME</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>ACME</b> (RFC 8555) is a protocol a CA and an applicant use to automate proving control of a domain and issuing the certificate.</li>
      <li><b>Let&rsquo;s Encrypt</b> uses it so a server can obtain browser-trusted certificates without human intervention. Its default certificates are valid for 90 days.</li>
      <li><b>So renewal must be automatic</b> (<i>analysis</i>): a 90-day certificate renewed by hand will expire on someone&rsquo;s holiday.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Both sides show a certificate: mTLS</h3>
    <p>In mutual TLS the client also presents a certificate, so each side proves who it is. Service meshes issue and rotate these workload certificates automatically; see <a href="#servicemesh">Service Mesh &amp; Istio</a>.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>The errors you will meet, and what they mean</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>The message (from OpenSSL)</th><th>What it usually means</th><th>What to check (<i>analysis</i>)</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>unable to get local issuer certificate</code></td><td>The client cannot build a chain to a root it trusts</td><td>Is the server sending its intermediate? Does the client trust the root (a private CA must be added to its trust store)?</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>hostname mismatch</code></td><td>The name you connected to is not in the certificate</td><td>The SAN list, not just the subject&rsquo;s common name</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>certificate has expired</code></td><td>Now is past the &ldquo;not after&rdquo; date</td><td>Renewal automation; and the clock on the client</td></tr>
      <tr><td>The two public-key hashes above differ</td><td>The certificate was issued for a different private key</td><td>Which key made the CSR</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note">Each message was produced for real: a leaf checked without its intermediate, the same certificate checked for a name not in its SAN, and checked at a date after it expires (<code>openssl verify -attime</code>). Supplying the intermediate with <code>-untrusted int.crt</code> turned the first into OK.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>SSH keys</h3>
<pre><code># make a key pair (GitHub's own instruction); set a passphrase when asked
ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -C "your_email@example.com"

# its fingerprint: what you compare when someone asks "is this your key?"
ssh-keygen -lf ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub

# first connection: compare the fingerprint shown with the one the service publishes
ssh -T git@github.com</code></pre>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Your public key</b> goes on the server, in <code>~/.ssh/authorized_keys</code> (a default file sshd checks; the <code>AuthorizedKeysFile</code> setting controls which), or into GitHub&rsquo;s settings. The private key stays on your machine.</li>
      <li><b>ssh-agent</b> holds your private keys for the session, so a passphrase-protected key is unlocked once rather than for every connection.</li>
      <li><b><code>~/.ssh/known_hosts</code></b> records the host key of every server you have connected to. If a host&rsquo;s key ever changes, ssh warns and disables password authentication, to prevent server spoofing or man-in-the-middle attacks.</li>
      <li><b>When that warning appears</b> (<i>analysis</i>): find out why the key changed (a rebuilt server, a new load balancer) before you remove the old entry with <code>ssh-keygen -R host</code>. Deleting the line to make the warning go away defeats the check.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Git: signing commits with your SSH key</h3>
<pre><code>git config --global gpg.format ssh
git config --global user.signingkey ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub
git config --global commit.gpgsign true          # sign every commit

# to verify locally, list the keys you trust
echo "you@example.com $(cat ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub)" &gt;&gt; ~/.ssh/allowed_signers
git config --global gpg.ssh.allowedSignersFile ~/.ssh/allowed_signers
git verify-commit HEAD
# Good "git" signature for you@example.com with ED25519 key SHA256:...</code></pre>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Why sign:</b> anyone can set <code>user.email</code> to any address. A signature that verifies shows the commit came from the holder of that key. GitHub marks commits signed with GPG, SSH or S/MIME as <i>Verified</i> when it can check the signature, so add the key to your account as a signing key too.</li>
      <li><b>Authentication and signing are separate:</b> the same SSH key can let you push and sign your commits, but GitHub registers it for each purpose separately: to use one key for both, you upload it twice.</li>
      <li>Everyday Git commands are on <a href="#devops">DevOps</a>.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Our view, and the other view</h3>
    <p><i>Our view:</i> automate every certificate&rsquo;s renewal, keep private keys where they were made, and sign your commits. Each removes a way to be surprised: an expiry, a leaked key, or a commit that only claims to be yours.</p>
    <p><i>The other view:</i> some systems cannot automate renewal (older appliances, devices in the field, partners who install certificates by hand), and for them short lifetimes add work and risk. Commit signing adds setup for every engineer and machine; some teams rely on protected branches and reviewed pull requests instead.</p>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Explain a key pair: what the public key and the private key are each for</li><li>Read a certificate's subject, SAN, issuer and dates</li><li>Make an SSH key and add its public half to a server or GitHub</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Make a CSR with the right SAN and check it before sending</li><li>Fix a missing intermediate, a hostname mismatch and an expired certificate</li><li>Sign Git commits with an SSH key and verify them</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Run a private CA for internal services, and know its limits</li><li>Automate certificate renewal with ACME</li><li>Set up mTLS between services</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Set an organisation's key and certificate policy</li><li>Plan certificate and key rotation without outages</li><li>Design trust for partners and devices that cannot automate</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>Public-key cryptography</li><li>X.509 certificates and the chain of trust</li><li>CSRs (PKCS #10) and certificate authorities</li><li>ACME and Let's Encrypt</li><li>SSH keys, ssh-agent and known_hosts</li><li>Signed Git commits</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc2986" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RFC 2986: PKCS #10 certification requests</a><span class="lnote"> What a CSR contains</span></li><li><a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc5280" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RFC 5280: X.509 certificates</a><span class="lnote"> The certificate format and path validation</span></li><li><a href="https://docs.github.com/en/authentication/connecting-to-github-with-ssh/generating-a-new-ssh-key-and-adding-it-to-the-ssh-agent" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub: Generating a new SSH key</a><span class="lnote"> Step by step</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Public-key_cryptography" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Public-key cryptography</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Certificate_authority" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Certificate authority</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Certificate_signing_request" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Certificate signing request</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chain_of_trust" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Chain of trust</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Public_key_certificate" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Public key certificate</a>; <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc2986" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RFC 2986</a>; <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc5280" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RFC 5280</a>; <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8555" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RFC 8555: ACME</a>; <a href="https://github.com/letsencrypt/website/blob/main/content/en/how-it-works.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Let's Encrypt: How it works (source)</a>; <a href="https://github.com/letsencrypt/website/blob/main/content/en/docs/faq.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Let's Encrypt: FAQ (source)</a>; <a href="https://github.com/openssl/openssl/blob/master/doc/man1/openssl-req.pod.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OpenSSL: openssl-req</a>; <a href="https://github.com/openssl/openssl/blob/master/doc/man1/openssl-x509.pod.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OpenSSL: openssl-x509</a>; <a href="https://github.com/openssl/openssl/blob/master/doc/man1/openssl-verify.pod.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OpenSSL: openssl-verify</a>; <a href="https://github.com/openssh/openssh-portable/blob/master/ssh-keygen.1" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OpenSSH: ssh-keygen(1)</a>; <a href="https://github.com/openssh/openssh-portable/blob/master/ssh.1" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OpenSSH: ssh(1)</a>; <a href="https://github.com/openssh/openssh-portable/blob/master/sshd.8" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OpenSSH: sshd(8)</a>; <a href="https://github.com/openssh/openssh-portable/blob/master/ssh-agent.1" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OpenSSH: ssh-agent(1)</a>; <a href="https://github.com/git/git/blob/master/Documentation/config/gpg.adoc" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Git: gpg configuration</a>; <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/authentication/connecting-to-github-with-ssh/generating-a-new-ssh-key-and-adding-it-to-the-ssh-agent" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub: Generating a new SSH key</a>; <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/authentication/connecting-to-github-with-ssh/adding-a-new-ssh-key-to-your-github-account" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub: Adding a new SSH key to your account</a>; <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/authentication/connecting-to-github-with-ssh/testing-your-ssh-connection" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub: Testing your SSH connection</a>; <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/authentication/managing-commit-signature-verification/about-commit-signature-verification" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub: About commit signature verification</a>; <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/authentication/managing-commit-signature-verification/telling-git-about-your-signing-key" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub: Telling Git about your signing key</a>.</p><p class="runcode">A question, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/enrichmeai.github.io/issues/new?title=%5BKeys%2C%20Certificates%20%26%20SSH%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>. Nothing here is tracked and there are no comments to store: questions live in the open, where the next learner can find the answer.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= PERSONAL DATA ACROSS SERVICES ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-privacy" data-name="Personal Data Across Services">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Personal data across services</h2><p>Splitting a system into services also spreads people&rsquo;s personal data across them: databases, caches, search indexes, event logs, analytics copies and backups. This page is about the engineering that lets you find that data, keep less of it, delete it when you must, keep it in the right region, and prove all of it. It is not legal advice: the obligations below are summarised from secondary sources, so read the regulation itself with your data protection officer.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>What the law asks of a system</h3>
    <p>The EU&rsquo;s General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR) gives people rights over their data, including access to it, a portable copy, erasure under certain circumstances, and a challenge to decisions made solely by an algorithm. Its principles include purpose limitation, data minimisation, storage limitation, and integrity and confidentiality. It also governs moving personal data outside the EU and EEA.</p>
    <p class="note"><i>Analysis:</i> for engineers, each right becomes a capability the system must have: find everything about one person, export it, delete or anonymise it, and explain an automated decision. A design that cannot find all of one person&rsquo;s data cannot honour any of these.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Where personal data ends up</h3>
    <p><i>Analysis.</i> In a service architecture it is rarely only in the service that owns it:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Each service&rsquo;s own database, and the events it publishes.</li>
      <li>Caches and search indexes built from those events (see <a href="#caching">Caching</a> and <a href="#rag">RAG</a>).</li>
      <li>Analytics and warehouse copies, and exports to partners.</li>
      <li>Logs and traces that captured a field they should not have.</li>
      <li>Backups of all of the above.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">Keep a data catalogue: which service holds which personal fields, why, and for how long. It is the map every request below depends on.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Keep less of it, in fewer places</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Data minimisation:</b> collect and copy only what the purpose needs.</li>
      <li><b>Tokenisation:</b> replace a sensitive value with a token that has no meaning of its own and maps back only through the tokenisation system. Services pass the token around; the real value lives in one place.</li>
      <li><b>Pseudonymisation:</b> transform data so it cannot be tied to a person without additional information, such as a key; the GDPR&rsquo;s definition (Article 4(5), as summarised by Wikipedia) requires that information to be kept separately. Tokenisation and encryption are both examples.</li>
      <li><b>Anonymisation:</b> remove what identifies people, so they can no longer be identified, directly or indirectly. Combining the data with other datasets can sometimes undo it.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Erasure meets retention</h3>
    <p>Erasure is a right to request deletion on certain grounds, and it is limited: it applies only under certain circumstances, and Wikipedia notes it was made more limited than the earlier &ldquo;right to be forgotten&rdquo; proposal. The sources behind this page do not list the exceptions, so read Article 17 itself. Systems also have their own retention rules: Microsoft documents, for example, that some system-generated logs are kept under its service retention policies and are not removed by a data subject delete request.</p>
    <p class="note"><i>Analysis:</i> the common engineering case is a request to delete data that another rule requires you to keep, such as a financial record. The usual pattern is to delete what you may, pseudonymise or anonymise what you must keep, restrict who can read it, and record which rule applied and when the kept data will go. Which rule wins is a legal decision; the system&rsquo;s job is to make either answer possible.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Deleting across services</h3>
    <p><i>Analysis:</i> one way to do it.</p>
    <ol>
      <li><b>Record the request</b> in one place that owns it, with an id and the time it arrived.</li>
      <li><b>Publish one event</b>, such as <code>CustomerErasureRequested</code>, keyed by the customer, so every service that holds their data hears it (see <a href="#kafka">Kafka</a>).</li>
      <li><b>Each service acts and confirms:</b> it deletes or anonymises its data, then publishes its own confirmation. Make each step idempotent, because the event may arrive twice.</li>
      <li><b>Track until everyone has confirmed,</b> chase the ones that have not, and only then report the request as done.</li>
      <li><b>Derived copies follow:</b> rebuild or purge caches and search indexes, and stop the person reappearing from a later replay of old events.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="note"><i>Analysis:</i> this is a saga with no compensation step, because deletion cannot be undone (see <a href="#architecture">Architecture</a>). The hard parts are the services nobody listed in the data catalogue, and old events that would re-create the data if replayed.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>The steps, drawn</h3>
    <figure class="fig"><svg viewBox="0 0 660 280" role="img" aria-labelledby="erase-t erase-d"><title id="erase-t">Deleting a customer's data across services</title><desc id="erase-d">One way to do it, numbered. 1: the erasure request is recorded in the service that owns it, with an id and a time. 2: it publishes one event, CustomerErasureRequested, keyed by the customer. 3: each service that holds their data deletes or anonymises it, idempotently, and publishes a confirmation. 4: the owner tracks the confirmations and chases the missing ones. 5: only when every service has confirmed is the request reported done, and caches and search indexes are purged too.</desc><defs><marker id="erase-a" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker><marker id="erase-aa" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar acc" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker></defs><rect class="nd in" x="10" y="100" width="150" height="60" rx="6"/><text class="lb hd" x="85" y="124" text-anchor="middle">erasure service</text><text class="lb mu" x="85" y="144" text-anchor="middle">records the request</text><circle class="nd in" cx="20" cy="100" r="9"/><text class="lb mu" x="20" y="104" text-anchor="middle">1</text><rect class="nd" x="200" y="100" width="160" height="60" rx="6"/><text class="lb hd" x="280" y="124" text-anchor="middle">event</text><text class="lb mu" x="280" y="144" text-anchor="middle">ErasureRequested</text><circle class="nd in" cx="210" cy="100" r="9"/><text class="lb mu" x="210" y="104" text-anchor="middle">2</text><line class="ed" x1="160" y1="130" x2="196" y2="130" marker-end="url(#erase-a)"/><rect class="nd out" x="420" y="20" width="130" height="44" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="485" y="47" text-anchor="middle">orders</text><line class="ed" x1="360" y1="130" x2="416" y2="42" marker-end="url(#erase-a)"/><line class="ed dash acc" x1="550" y1="42" x2="600" y2="130" marker-end="url(#erase-aa)"/><rect class="nd out" x="420" y="100" width="130" height="44" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="485" y="127" text-anchor="middle">support</text><line class="ed" x1="360" y1="130" x2="416" y2="122" marker-end="url(#erase-a)"/><line class="ed dash acc" x1="550" y1="122" x2="600" y2="130" marker-end="url(#erase-aa)"/><rect class="nd out" x="420" y="180" width="130" height="44" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="485" y="207" text-anchor="middle">marketing</text><line class="ed" x1="360" y1="130" x2="416" y2="202" marker-end="url(#erase-a)"/><line class="ed dash acc" x1="550" y1="202" x2="600" y2="130" marker-end="url(#erase-aa)"/><circle class="nd in" cx="430" cy="20" r="9"/><text class="lb mu" x="430" y="24" text-anchor="middle">3</text><rect class="nd" x="560" y="110" width="90" height="44" rx="6"/><text class="lb hd" x="605" y="136" text-anchor="middle">track</text><circle class="nd in" cx="570" cy="110" r="9"/><text class="lb mu" x="570" y="114" text-anchor="middle">4</text><text class="lb acc" x="330" y="262" text-anchor="middle">5: done only when all confirm; purge caches and indexes too</text><text class="lb" x="485" y="255" text-anchor="middle"></text></svg><figcaption>The steps above as a picture. Deletion cannot be undone, so this is a saga with no compensation: the hard part is the service nobody listed, and old events that would bring the data back if replayed.</figcaption></figure>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Backups and crypto-shredding</h3>
    <p>Crypto-shredding makes encrypted data unusable by deleting or overwriting its encryption key. It helps where the data itself is hard to reach, such as backups and copies spread across systems, because only the key needs destroying.</p>
    <p class="note"><i>Analysis:</i> encrypt each person&rsquo;s sensitive fields with a key of their own. If no copy of that key survives (key backups, caches) and the encryption holds, destroying it makes those fields unreadable everywhere, backups included. The cost is managing many keys, and losing a key by mistake loses the data.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Keeping data in the right place</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Data residency</b> is where the data is actually stored; <b>data localisation</b> is a requirement that it be stored within a given region.</li>
      <li>The GDPR governs transfers of personal data outside the EU and EEA, so where you store and process it is a design decision, not only an operations one.</li>
      <li><i>Analysis:</i> choose regions per market, route each customer to their region, and check where logs, backups and support tools copy the data too (see <a href="#cloudcompare">GCP vs AWS vs Azure</a> for regions).</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Choose by requirement</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>If this is true for you</th><th>It leans towards (<i>analysis</i>)</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Many services need to refer to a person but few need the actual value</td><td>Tokenisation, with the real values in one vault</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Analysts need the data&rsquo;s shape, not who it describes</td><td>Anonymisation before it reaches the warehouse</td></tr>
      <tr><td>You must keep a record but stop it pointing at a person</td><td>Pseudonymisation, with the key held separately and access restricted</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Data sits in backups or systems you cannot edit row by row</td><td>Crypto-shredding with per-person keys</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Personal data spans many services owned by different teams</td><td>An erasure event, confirmations from each service, and one tracker</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Customers in several jurisdictions</td><td>Regional deployments, with routing and residency checked for logs and backups too</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Prove it</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Test deletion in CI:</b> create a person, erase them, and assert they are gone from every store you list.</li>
      <li><b>Audit access:</b> who read which personal data, when, and why, with a correlation id across services.</li>
      <li><b>Report:</b> how many requests, how long each took, and which services were slowest to confirm.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">This card is <i>analysis</i>: these are practices, not legal requirements quoted from the regulation.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Our view, and the other view</h3>
    <p><i>Our view:</i> design for erasure from the first service, with a data catalogue, tokens instead of copies, and an erasure event every service must handle. Retrofitting it across dozens of services is harder than building it in.</p>
    <p><i>The other view:</i> early on, a small team may keep personal data in one service on purpose, so deletion stays a single query, and add tokenisation and events only when the data starts to spread. Building the full machinery before the system needs it has a cost too.</p>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>List where personal data ends up in a service architecture</li><li>Explain tokenisation, pseudonymisation and anonymisation</li><li>Name the rights a person has over their data</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Keep a data catalogue of personal fields per service</li><li>Build an erasure event that every service handles idempotently</li><li>Test deletion end to end in CI</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Design for erasure and legal retention together</li><li>Use crypto-shredding for backups and copies</li><li>Plan regional deployments for data residency</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Set an organisation's personal-data architecture and standards</li><li>Work with legal and the data protection officer on retention rules</li><li>Audit and report on data subject requests across systems</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>GDPR rights and principles</li><li>Data minimisation and tokenisation</li><li>Pseudonymisation and anonymisation</li><li>Erasure across services</li><li>Crypto-shredding</li><li>Data residency and transfers</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/General_Data_Protection_Regulation" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: General Data Protection Regulation</a><span class="lnote"> Rights, principles and transfers</span></li><li><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crypto-shredding" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Crypto-shredding</a><span class="lnote"> Erasing by deleting keys</span></li><li><a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/compliance/regulatory/gdpr-dsr-azure" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Microsoft Learn: GDPR data subject requests for Azure</a><span class="lnote"> How a cloud provider handles them</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/General_Data_Protection_Regulation" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: General Data Protection Regulation</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Right_to_be_forgotten" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Right to be forgotten</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tokenization_(data_security)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Tokenization (data security)</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pseudonymization" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Pseudonymization</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Data_anonymization" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Data anonymization</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Data_minimization" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Data minimization</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crypto-shredding" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Crypto-shredding</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Data_sovereignty" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Data sovereignty (residency and localisation)</a>; <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/compliance/regulatory/gdpr-dsr-azure" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Microsoft Learn: GDPR data subject requests for Azure</a>.</p><p class="runcode">A question, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/enrichmeai.github.io/issues/new?title=%5BPersonal%20Data%20Across%20Services%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>. Nothing here is tracked and there are no comments to store: questions live in the open, where the next learner can find the answer.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<section class="panel" id="p-cyber" data-name="Cybersecurity">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Cybersecurity: thinking like a defender</h2><p>The <a href="#security">Security</a> page is about building secure software. This page is about the wider field: how real attacks unfold, how organisations defend, detect and recover, and the people and roles involved. You do not need to become a specialist, but every engineer should understand the game being played against their systems.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>How real attacks unfold</h3>
    <p>MITRE ATT&amp;CK is a public knowledge base of attackers' tactics and techniques, based on real-world observations. Its Enterprise matrix (version 20.1) groups what attackers do into 15 tactics, roughly in the order an intrusion progresses:</p>
    <p><b>Reconnaissance → Resource Development → Initial Access → Execution → Persistence → Privilege Escalation → Stealth → Defense Impairment → Credential Access → Discovery → Lateral Movement → Collection → Command and Control → Exfiltration → Impact</b></p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Attackers rarely need anything clever.</b> A stolen password, an unpatched server, a misconfigured bucket or a convincing email is usually enough to start.</li>
      <li><b>Every step is a chance to stop them.</b> Defence is not one wall; it is noticing and blocking at many of these stages.</li>
      <li><b>Use it practically:</b> for each system you own, ask which techniques would work against it, and whether you would see them.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>A brief history of attacks, and what everyone should do</h3>
    <p>Security is not only a job for specialists. Look at how some of the best-known attacks got in, and most began with something ordinary: software not updated, a message someone trusted, a password with nothing behind it, or a supplier everyone relied on. Those are things everyone can do something about.</p>
    <div class="tbl"><table><thead><tr><th>Year</th><th>Attack</th><th>How it got in</th><th>What it did</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td>1988</td><td><b>Morris worm</b></td><td>Holes in common Unix programs (sendmail and finger); it copied itself from machine to machine across the early internet</td><td>One of the oldest worms spread over the internet, and the first US felony conviction under the 1986 Computer Fraud and Abuse Act</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2000</td><td><b>ILOVEYOU</b></td><td>An email attachment that seemed to come from someone you knew, then mailed itself to everyone in your address book</td><td>Infected an estimated 10% of internet-connected computers; about $10 billion of damage</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2003</td><td><b>SQL Slammer</b></td><td>A bug in Microsoft SQL Server whose fix had been released six months earlier, but not applied</td><td>Most of its 75,000 victims were infected within 10 minutes</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2010</td><td><b>Stuxnet</b></td><td>Typically an infected USB drive, crossing into machines that were not on the internet; four previously unknown flaws</td><td>Damaged equipment in Iran&rsquo;s nuclear programme: code causing physical harm</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2014</td><td><b>Heartbleed</b></td><td>A missing length check in OpenSSL, a library much of the web uses for encryption; in the code since 2012</td><td>A fix came out the day it was disclosed; every site using the library had to update</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2017</td><td><b>WannaCry</b></td><td>A Windows flaw Microsoft had patched on 14 March 2017; the attack began on 12 May 2017</td><td>Encrypted files on more than 300,000 computers in 150 countries and demanded bitcoin</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2017</td><td><b>Equifax</b></td><td>A website still running an old version of Apache Struts with a known flaw</td><td>Records of 147.9 million Americans, 15.2 million British and about 19,000 Canadian citizens</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2020</td><td><b>SolarWinds</b></td><td>A widely used IT management product, compromised in the supply chain, then installed by its customers</td><td>Thousands of organisations, including parts of the US federal government; undetected for months</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2021</td><td><b>Colonial Pipeline</b></td><td>A compromised password for an inactive VPN account that did not have multi-factor authentication</td><td>The company halted its fuel pipeline to contain the ransomware, and paid the ransom</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2021</td><td><b>Log4Shell</b></td><td>A flaw in Log4j, a Java logging library used almost everywhere; unnoticed since 2013</td><td>Rated 10, the highest CVSS score; potentially hundreds of millions of devices</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2024</td><td><b>XZ Utils backdoor</b></td><td>A backdoor added on purpose to open-source compression software by a contributor using the name &ldquo;Jia Tan&rdquo;</td><td>Found by a developer, Andres Freund, before the affected versions were widely deployed to production systems; rated 10.0</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
    <p><b>The pattern</b> (<i>analysis</i>): four ways in keep coming back. <b>Unpatched software</b> (Slammer, WannaCry, Equifax), where the fix existed before the attack. <b>People tricked</b> (ILOVEYOU, and phishing in general). <b>Passwords alone</b> (Colonial Pipeline). <b>Trusted suppliers and shared code</b> (SolarWinds, Heartbleed, Log4Shell, XZ Utils), where one weak link reaches everyone who uses it.</p>
    <div class="tbl"><table><thead><tr><th>Habit</th><th>What to do</th><th>Which attacks it would have helped against</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td><b>Multi-factor sign-in</b></td><td>Turn on multi-factor sign-in (a code, an app prompt or a security key, as well as the password), most of all for email, banking and work accounts</td><td>Colonial Pipeline: one password was enough</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Unique passwords</b></td><td>Use a different password everywhere, kept in a password manager, so one leaked password does not open your other accounts</td><td>Leaked passwords: Wikipedia says the one used at Colonial Pipeline was likely found on the dark web</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Updates</b></td><td>Install updates promptly, and turn on automatic updates for your devices and apps</td><td>SQL Slammer, WannaCry and Equifax: fixes were available beforehand</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Check the unexpected</b></td><td>Be suspicious of the unexpected: an attachment, a link, an urgent request for money or a password. Check by another route (call the person, open the site yourself) before acting</td><td>ILOVEYOU, and phishing generally</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Backups</b></td><td>Keep backups, with at least one copy that is not always connected</td><td>Ransomware such as WannaCry. Wikipedia cites a 2020 survey in which 56% of ransomware victims got their data back from backups, against 26% who paid; newer ransomware also threatens to leak data, which backups do not prevent</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Remove the unused</b></td><td>Remove what you no longer use: old accounts, apps, browser extensions, devices still signed in</td><td>Colonial Pipeline: the account was inactive</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Unknown USB drives</b></td><td>Do not plug in unknown USB drives, and do not lend yours to unknown machines</td><td>Stuxnet crossed into machines that were not on the internet this way</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Report quickly</b></td><td>Report quickly if you clicked something, or something feels wrong. Early reporting is what limits the damage, and nobody should be blamed for it</td><td>SolarWinds stayed hidden for months</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
    <p><b>If you build software</b>, the same four ways in apply to your systems: keep dependencies updated and know what you ship (<a href="#quality">Quality Attributes</a>, the vulnerability loop), check access on every object, and never log secrets (<a href="#security">Security</a>, and the security drills in the <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/well-rounded-engineer-labs/tree/main/security" target="_blank" rel="noopener">labs</a>).</p>
    <p><i>Our view:</i> most of the damage in this history came through ordinary doors that ordinary habits close, so the basics above are worth more to most people than any specialist tool. <i>The other view:</i> some attacks, such as Stuxnet or the XZ backdoor, were built by well-resourced attackers that no personal habit would stop; defending against them is the work of vendors, open-source maintainers and governments, and blaming individuals for those would be unfair.</p>
    <p class="note">Sources: Wikipedia&rsquo;s articles on each attack in the table, and on <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multi-factor_authentication" target="_blank" rel="noopener">multi-factor authentication</a>, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phishing" target="_blank" rel="noopener">phishing</a> and <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ransomware" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ransomware</a>. We could not read the UK NCSC&rsquo;s or the US CISA&rsquo;s advice pages from this environment.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Defence in depth</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Identity first:</b> strong, phishing-resistant sign-in, least privilege, and removing access people no longer need.</li>
      <li><b>Keep things patched and known:</b> you cannot protect what you do not know you have. Keep an inventory of systems, software and data.</li>
      <li><b>Segment:</b> a breach in one place should not open everything else.</li>
      <li><b>Assume breach:</b> design so that one compromised laptop, account or service does limited damage.</li>
      <li><b>Back up, offline:</b> see "Backups and ransomware" on the <a href="#security">Security</a> page.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Detect and respond</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Collect the right signals:</b> sign-ins, admin actions, process and network activity, cloud audit logs.</li>
      <li><b>Detection is a craft:</b> write rules for behaviour attackers cannot easily avoid, test them, and tune out noise, so real alerts are not drowned.</li>
      <li><b>Have an incident plan before you need it:</b> who leads, who decides, how you contain (isolate a machine, revoke a credential), how you communicate, and when you call in help or the authorities.</li>
      <li><b>Learn without blame</b> afterwards, and fix the root cause, not just the symptom.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>The people and roles</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Blue team / security operations (SOC):</b> watch, detect and respond.</li>
      <li><b>Red team and penetration testers:</b> attack, with written permission, to find weaknesses first.</li>
      <li><b>Purple teaming:</b> both sides together, turning each attack into a detection.</li>
      <li><b>Application and cloud security engineers:</b> help builders get it right from the start (see <a href="#security">Security</a>).</li>
      <li><b>Governance, risk and compliance:</b> policies, audits and standards (see "Compliance frameworks" on the Security page).</li>
      <li><b>Threat intelligence:</b> who is attacking whom, and how.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Ethics and the law</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Only test systems you own, or have written permission to test.</b> Scanning or probing someone else's systems without permission is illegal in many countries, whatever your intent.</li>
      <li><b>Practise legally:</b> deliberately vulnerable training apps, capture-the-flag events and your own <a href="#homelab">home lab</a>.</li>
      <li><b>Report what you find responsibly:</b> through the organisation's security contact or vulnerability disclosure programme, and give them time to fix it.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">Security is a team sport. <i>Our view:</i> much of the defence is ordinary engineers who patch, review, log, ask "what if this is stolen?", and report mistakes quickly.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Judging a cloud provider&rsquo;s security: the Cloud Security Alliance</h3>
    <p>The Cloud Security Alliance (CSA) is a not-for-profit organisation, formed in 2008, that promotes security best practice for cloud computing. Three of its outputs come up whenever a team chooses or audits a cloud service:</p>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>What it is</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td><b>Cloud Controls Matrix (CCM)</b></td><td>A framework of security controls for cloud providers and their customers, grouped into domains. Providers use it to evaluate and document their controls.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Consensus Assessments Initiative Questionnaire (CAIQ)</b></td><td>Questions built on the CCM that a customer or auditor may ask a provider. A provider answers it to document how it complies with the CSA&rsquo;s best practices. In CCM version 4 the questionnaire and the matrix were combined.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>STAR</b> (Security, Trust, Assurance and Risk) registry</td><td>A free public registry, launched in 2013 with the British Standards Institution, where providers publish their CSA assessments.</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <ul>
      <li><b>STAR Level 1, self-assessment:</b> the provider publishes a completed CAIQ or a report documenting its compliance with the CCM. It is free and open to any provider.</li>
      <li><b>STAR Level 2, third-party assessment:</b> an independent auditor checks the controls. STAR Certification builds on the ISO/IEC 27001 management system standard plus the CCM; STAR Attestation is a SOC 2 Type 2 audit with the CCM&rsquo;s criteria added.</li>
      <li><b>Continuous monitoring:</b> Google describes STAR as a three-step programme of self-assessment, third-party audit and continuous monitoring. One Microsoft page calls this Level 3; two other Microsoft pages list only two levels.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">The matrix, the questionnaire and even the number of levels differ between sources: Microsoft&rsquo;s own pages give 16 or 17 CCM domains, &ldquo;more than 140&rdquo; or &ldquo;more than 250&rdquo; CAIQ questions, and two or three STAR levels, so check which version an assessment used. <i>Analysis:</i> for an engineer, a provider&rsquo;s STAR entry answers part of a supplier review without a new questionnaire, and the CCM is a checklist for your own side of the shared responsibility too. A self-assessment is the provider&rsquo;s own word; a Level 2 report is an auditor&rsquo;s, and its scope (which services and regions) is what we would check first. The other way to look at it: a detailed Level 1 answer on the exact control you care about can tell you more than a broad Level 2 certificate that does not cover your service.</p>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Explain how a typical intrusion unfolds</li><li>Spot phishing and report incidents quickly</li><li>Know that you only test systems you may test</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Map your system's exposure to common attacker techniques</li><li>Log the signals an investigation needs</li><li>Follow the incident plan during an incident</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Write and tune detections; run a tabletop exercise</li><li>Lead containment and recovery in an incident</li><li>Design defence in depth for a product</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Own the organisation's security operations and incident response</li><li>Run red, blue and purple team programmes</li><li>Build a security culture across teams</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>MITRE ATT&CK tactics and techniques</li><li>Detection engineering</li><li>Incident response</li><li>Threat modelling</li><li>Responsible disclosure</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://github.com/mitre-attack/attack-stix-data" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MITRE ATT&CK data on GitHub</a><span class="lnote"> The ATT&CK knowledge base as STIX data</span></li><li><a href="https://github.com/OWASP/CheatSheetSeries" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OWASP Cheat Sheet Series</a><span class="lnote"> Concise, practical security guidance</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/mitre-attack/attack-stix-data/master/index.json" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MITRE ATT&CK STIX index (latest Enterprise = 20.1)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/mitre-attack/attack-stix-data/master/enterprise-attack/enterprise-attack-20.1.json" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Enterprise ATT&CK 20.1 bundle (matrix tactic_refs: 15 tactics incl. Stealth, Defense Impairment, in the page's order)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/mitre-attack/attack-stix-data/master/README.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MITRE attack-stix-data README</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cloud_Security_Alliance" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cloud Security Alliance (Wikipedia)</a>; <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/compliance/regulatory/offering-csa-star-certification" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Microsoft Learn: CSA STAR Certification</a>; <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/compliance/regulatory/offering-csa-star-attestation" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Microsoft Learn: CSA STAR Attestation</a>; <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/compliance/regulatory/offering-csa-star-self-assessment" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Microsoft Learn: CSA STAR self-assessment</a>; <a href="https://cloud.google.com/security/compliance/csa-star" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google Cloud: CSA STAR</a>; <a href="https://aws.amazon.com/compliance/csa/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AWS: CSA compliance</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Morris_worm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Morris worm</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ILOVEYOU" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: ILOVEYOU</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SQL_Slammer" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: SQL Slammer</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stuxnet" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Stuxnet</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heartbleed" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Heartbleed</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/WannaCry_ransomware_attack" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: WannaCry</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2017_Equifax_data_breach" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Equifax</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2020_United_States_federal_government_data_breach" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: SolarWinds</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Colonial_Pipeline_ransomware_attack" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Colonial Pipeline</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Log4Shell" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Log4Shell</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/XZ_Utils_backdoor" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: XZ Utils backdoor</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multi-factor_authentication" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Multi-factor authentication</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phishing" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Phishing</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ransomware" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Ransomware</a>.</p><p class="runcode">A question, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/enrichmeai.github.io/issues/new?title=%5BCybersecurity%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>. Nothing here is tracked and there are no comments to store: questions live in the open, where the next learner can find the answer.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= AGENTIC AI SECURITY ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-agentsec" data-name="Agentic AI Security">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Agentic AI security and risks</h2><p>An AI agent is a model that runs in a loop, decides which tools to call and acts on the results. That makes it useful, and it makes every weakness of the model a weakness of everything the agent can touch. This page covers the risks from two sides: agents as a target (prompt injection, excessive agency, insecure tools) and agents as a weapon in an attacker&rsquo;s hands. It then sets out the controls, the frameworks that name the risks, and how to choose. How agents work is on <a href="#agentharness">Agent Harness</a> and <a href="#mcp">MCP</a>; general defence is on <a href="#cyber">Cybersecurity</a>.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Why agency changes the risk</h3>
    <p>OWASP&rsquo;s Top 10 for LLM Applications (2025 edition) puts it plainly: the impact of a successful prompt injection depends largely on the business context and on <b>how much agency the model has</b>. A chatbot that can only answer can be made to say something wrong. An agent with tools can be made to <i>do</i> something wrong: send data, change records, run commands.</p>
    <p>OWASP calls the vulnerability that lets damaging actions happen <b>excessive agency</b>, and names three root causes:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Excessive functionality:</b> tools the task does not need, such as a document reader that can also delete documents.</li>
      <li><b>Excessive permissions:</b> a tool connected to other systems with more rights than it needs, such as write access where read access would do.</li>
      <li><b>Excessive autonomy:</b> high-impact actions taken without anyone checking them.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">The trigger does not have to be an attacker: OWASP lists hallucination from a poorly engineered prompt or a weak model alongside prompt injection, a compromised tool and, in multi-agent systems, a compromised peer agent.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Prompt injection</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Direct:</b> the user&rsquo;s own input changes the model&rsquo;s behaviour, deliberately or by accident.</li>
      <li><b>Indirect:</b> the instructions arrive inside content the agent reads: a web page, a file, an email, a tool&rsquo;s output. OWASP&rsquo;s example is a user asking for a web page summary, where hidden instructions on the page make the model insert an image link that sends the private conversation to an attacker&rsquo;s URL.</li>
      <li><b>Invisible:</b> injected text does not need to be readable by people, only parsed by the model, and multimodal models can carry it in images.</li>
      <li><b>Jailbreaking</b> is a form of prompt injection that makes the model ignore its safety rules altogether.</li>
    </ul>
    <figure class="fig"><svg viewBox="0 0 680 340" role="img" aria-labelledby="ag-t ag-d"><title id="ag-t">Where prompt injection enters an agent</title><desc id="ag-d">A data-flow diagram. A user sends a request to the agent, a process combining the model and its loop. Inside a dashed trust boundary are the agent, its tools (also a process) and its context store. The agent calls tools; tools act on external systems and fetch untrusted content such as web pages, emails and files. That content returns through the tools as tool output, into the agent and its context: this is where injected instructions enter. Controls: least privilege on tools, human approval for high-impact actions, and authorisation in the external systems.</desc><defs><marker id="ag-a" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker><marker id="ag-aa" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar acc" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker></defs><rect class="bd" x="150" y="20" width="340" height="250" rx="14"/><text class="lb mu" x="164" y="42" text-anchor="start">trust boundary: your application</text><rect class="nd" x="10" y="112" width="100" height="46" rx="2"/><text class="lb hd" x="60" y="140" text-anchor="middle">User</text><line class="ed" x1="110" y1="135" x2="214" y2="135" marker-end="url(#ag-a)"/><text class="lb mu" x="130" y="126" text-anchor="start">request</text><circle class="nd" cx="262" cy="135" r="46"/><text class="lb hd" x="262" y="131" text-anchor="middle">Agent</text><text class="lb mu" x="262" y="150" text-anchor="middle">model + loop</text><line class="ed" x1="308" y1="126" x2="380" y2="126" marker-end="url(#ag-a)"/><text class="lb mu" x="344" y="116" text-anchor="middle">tool calls</text><line class="ed acc" x1="380" y1="146" x2="310" y2="146" marker-end="url(#ag-aa)"/><circle class="nd" cx="418" cy="135" r="36"/><text class="lb hd" x="418" y="140" text-anchor="middle">Tools</text><path class="ed" d="M200,226 L324,226 M200,248 L324,248"/><text class="lb" x="262" y="242" text-anchor="middle">Context / memory</text><line class="ed acc" x1="262" y1="181" x2="262" y2="222" marker-end="url(#ag-aa)"/><line class="ed" x1="452" y1="120" x2="526" y2="86" marker-end="url(#ag-a)"/><text class="lb mu" x="500" y="76" text-anchor="end">actions</text><rect class="nd" x="530" y="56" width="140" height="50" rx="2"/><text class="lb" x="600" y="86" text-anchor="middle">External systems</text><text class="lb mu" x="600" y="124" text-anchor="middle">authorise here,</text><text class="lb mu" x="600" y="140" text-anchor="middle">not in the model</text><line class="ed" x1="450" y1="152" x2="526" y2="190" marker-end="url(#ag-a)"/><text class="lb mu" x="498" y="166" text-anchor="start">fetch</text><rect class="nd warn" x="530" y="180" width="140" height="60" rx="2"/><text class="lb" x="600" y="206" text-anchor="middle">Untrusted content</text><text class="lb mu" x="600" y="225" text-anchor="middle">web, email, files</text><path class="ed acc" d="M530,226 C500,250 470,230 440,166" marker-end="url(#ag-aa)"/><text class="lb acc" x="418" y="300" text-anchor="middle">injected instructions return as tool output</text><text class="lb acc" x="418" y="318" text-anchor="middle">and enter the agent and its context</text><text class="lb mu" x="418" y="190" text-anchor="middle">least privilege;</text><text class="lb mu" x="418" y="206" text-anchor="middle">approve high-impact</text></svg><figcaption>A threat-model data-flow diagram of an agent: external entities (user, external systems, untrusted content) as rectangles, the agent and its tools as processes (circles), its context as a data store, and the trust boundary dashed. Anything a tool returns is untrusted input to the model.</figcaption></figure>
    <p class="note">OWASP says it is unclear whether fool-proof prevention exists, given how models work, and that RAG and fine-tuning do not fully mitigate it. So the controls below aim to <i>limit the damage</i> an injection can do.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>The risk lists, by name</h3>
    <p><b>OWASP Top 10 for LLM Applications, 2025:</b></p>
    <ol>
      <li>Prompt injection</li>
      <li>Sensitive information disclosure</li>
      <li>Supply chain</li>
      <li>Data and model poisoning</li>
      <li>Improper output handling</li>
      <li>Excessive agency</li>
      <li>System prompt leakage</li>
      <li>Vector and embedding weaknesses</li>
      <li>Misinformation</li>
      <li>Unbounded consumption (including &ldquo;denial of wallet&rdquo;)</li>
    </ol>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Agent-specific risks: OWASP&rsquo;s agentic draft</h3>
    <p>OWASP&rsquo;s Agentic Security Initiative has a <b>first public draft</b> of a Top 10 for agentic applications in its repository. It is a draft, so the names and order may change (OWASP&rsquo;s website, which may carry a later version, could not be reached when this page was checked), but it shows where the risks go beyond a single model call:</p>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Risk</th><th>In the draft&rsquo;s words, roughly</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Agent behaviour hijack</td><td>Manipulating an agent&rsquo;s goals or plans towards the attacker&rsquo;s aims</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Tool misuse and exploitation</td><td>Tricking an agent into using its tools in harmful or unintended ways</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Identity and privilege abuse</td><td>Impersonating agents, or escalating access through identity weaknesses</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Agentic supply chain</td><td>Insecure models, agents, tools or artefacts</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Unexpected code execution</td><td>Unsafe code run through the agent&rsquo;s behaviour</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Memory and context poisoning</td><td>Corrupting what the agent remembers, so later decisions go wrong</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Insecure inter-agent communication</td><td>Poisoned messages or abused protocols between agents</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Cascading failures</td><td>A fault or hallucination in one agent spreading through others</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Human-agent trust exploitation</td><td>Exploiting over-trust or approval fatigue in the people overseeing agents</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Rogue agents</td><td>Malicious or compromised agents acting on their own</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Tools and MCP: where the access lives</h3>
    <p>The Model Context Protocol&rsquo;s own security best practices name attacks that come from connecting agents to tools:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Confused deputy:</b> a program tricked into misusing its own authority on behalf of someone with fewer rights. In MCP, a proxy server in front of a third-party API can be abused to get authorisation without the user&rsquo;s consent.</li>
      <li><b>Token passthrough:</b> an MCP server that accepts a token not issued to it and forwards it downstream. The guidance is blunt: MCP servers <b>must not</b> accept tokens that were not explicitly issued for them.</li>
      <li><b>Local server compromise:</b> a local MCP server is a program running on your machine with your rights. Before a one-click setup runs one, the client must show the exact command, mark it as dangerous and ask for approval; it should also run servers in a sandbox with minimal privileges.</li>
      <li><b>Broad scopes:</b> a token granted every scope up front turns one leak into access to everything. The guidance is progressive, least-privilege scopes.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">More on the protocol, and on checking a server before you install it, is on <a href="#mcp">MCP</a>.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Controls that limit the damage</h3>
    <p>Drawn from OWASP&rsquo;s mitigations for prompt injection and excessive agency:</p>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Control</th><th>What it means in practice</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td><b>Fewer, narrower tools</b></td><td>Offer only the tools the task needs, each with only the functions it needs. Prefer a &ldquo;write this file&rdquo; tool over an open-ended &ldquo;run a shell command&rdquo; one.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Least privilege</b></td><td>Give tools the smallest rights in downstream systems, such as read access to one table. Keep the application&rsquo;s own API tokens in the application, which makes the calls, and out of the model&rsquo;s prompt and context.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Act as the user</b></td><td>Run actions in the context of the user they are for, with that user&rsquo;s permissions, for example through OAuth with the minimum scope.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Complete mediation</b></td><td>Authorise every request in the downstream system, not by asking the model whether an action is allowed.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Human approval</b></td><td>Require a person to approve high-impact actions before they happen.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Mark untrusted content</b></td><td>Separate and label external content so it has less influence. Detection services can help: Microsoft&rsquo;s Prompt Shields, for example, scan user prompts and documents for attack patterns. Microsoft notes the models are trained and evaluated mainly on English, so test them with your own attacks.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Treat output as untrusted</b></td><td>Validate and encode what the model produces before it reaches a browser, a shell, a query or another system (OWASP&rsquo;s improper output handling).</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Log, limit, test</b></td><td>Log tool calls, rate-limit actions so monitoring can catch problems before the damage grows, and red-team the agent as an untrusted user.</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note">Tools apply these too. Claude Code&rsquo;s security documentation, for example (Anthropic&rsquo;s description of its own product), lists a permission system that asks before sensitive operations, approval for commands that fetch from the web, a trust dialog for a new folder, a separate approval prompt for MCP servers in a project&rsquo;s <code>.mcp.json</code>, and sandbox network isolation.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Agents in an attacker&rsquo;s hands</h3>
    <p>In November 2025 Anthropic reported disrupting an espionage campaign it assessed, with high confidence, to be run by a state-sponsored group using its Claude Code tool. Anthropic said it believes this is the first documented case of a large-scale cyberattack executed without substantial human intervention. By Anthropic&rsquo;s account:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>The attackers jailbroke the model by splitting the attack into small, innocent-looking tasks and telling it that it worked for a security firm doing defensive testing.</li>
      <li>The agent did reconnaissance, wrote exploit code, harvested credentials and extracted data, with tools often reached over MCP. AI did an estimated 80 to 90% of the work, with people stepping in at perhaps four to six decision points per campaign.</li>
      <li>It targeted around thirty organisations and succeeded in a small number.</li>
      <li>It was not flawless: it sometimes hallucinated credentials, or claimed to have found secrets that were in fact public.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">This is one vendor&rsquo;s account of misuse of its own product, and it says it sees only its own platform. <i>Analysis:</i> the lesson for defenders does not depend on the vendor: attacks can now run at machine speed, so detection and response need to be automated too. Anthropic itself advises security teams to try AI for SOC automation, threat detection, vulnerability assessment and incident response. The other reading: this is one vendor&rsquo;s account that others have not replicated, and the share of the work an AI does may differ for other attackers and tools.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Frameworks to map against</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>OWASP Top 10 for LLM Applications</b> and the agentic draft above, for application risks.</li>
      <li><b>MITRE ATLAS</b> (Adversarial Threat Landscape for AI Systems): a public knowledge base of adversary tactics, techniques and procedures aimed at AI systems, with its data published in a public repository. ATT&amp;CK, its counterpart for attacks in general, is on <a href="#cyber">Cybersecurity</a>.</li>
      <li><b>The MCP security best practices,</b> for anything that connects agents to tools.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Choose by requirement</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>If this is true for your agent</th><th>It leans towards (<i>analysis</i>)</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>It reads untrusted content (web, email, uploads) and can also send data or call external URLs</td><td>Break the chain: remove outbound tools, or require approval before anything leaves</td></tr>
      <tr><td>It acts for many different users</td><td>Run each action as that user, with their permissions and a narrow OAuth scope; never one shared service account with wide rights</td></tr>
      <tr><td>It can change money, records or infrastructure</td><td>Human approval for those actions, and authorisation enforced in the downstream system</td></tr>
      <tr><td>It writes or runs code</td><td>A sandbox with restricted file system and network access, and generated code reviewed like any other</td></tr>
      <tr><td>It installs or connects to third-party MCP servers</td><td>A list of approved servers, the exact command shown before first run, and tokens issued only for each server</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Several agents pass work to each other</td><td>Treat every agent&rsquo;s output as untrusted input to the next, and log the whole chain</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Cost can grow without limit</td><td>Rate limits and spending caps per user and per run</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Prove it</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Test with attacks:</b> put injection payloads in documents, pages and tool outputs, and assert the agent does not act on them (see <a href="#rag">RAG</a>).</li>
      <li><b>Review the permission map:</b> every tool, its rights, and who approves what.</li>
      <li><b>Audit:</b> every tool call logged with the user, the agent and the input that led to it.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">This card is <i>analysis</i>. Only the injection testing draws on OWASP, which advises testing the model as an untrusted user.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Our view, and the other view</h3>
    <p><i>Our view:</i> design every agent assuming its model will at some point follow an attacker&rsquo;s instructions, because OWASP says no fool-proof prevention is known. What matters is what it can do when that happens: narrow tools, least privilege, approval for high-impact actions, and authorisation outside the model.</p>
    <p><i>The other view:</i> approvals and narrow tools reduce what agents are useful for, and constant prompts can wear down the people answering them. The OWASP agentic draft lists exploiting &ldquo;over-trust or fatigue in human oversight&rdquo; as a risk, and Claude Code&rsquo;s security documentation describes allowlisting frequently used safe commands as a &ldquo;prompt fatigue mitigation&rdquo;. On this view, more autonomy inside a sandbox with network isolation, plus monitoring, is worth the trade: the sandbox then carries more of the safety.</p>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Explain direct and indirect prompt injection with an example</li><li>Name the three root causes of excessive agency</li><li>Treat model output as untrusted input</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Give an agent only the tools and permissions its task needs</li><li>Add human approval for high-impact actions</li><li>Check an MCP server and its scopes before connecting it</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Design agent actions to run as the user, with authorisation outside the model</li><li>Red-team an agent with injected documents and tool outputs</li><li>Sandbox agents that write or run code</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Set an organisation's policy for agents, tools and approvals</li><li>Map agent risks to OWASP and MITRE ATLAS</li><li>Plan detection and response for attacks at machine speed</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>Prompt injection</li><li>Excessive agency</li><li>OWASP Top 10 for LLM Applications</li><li>MCP security</li><li>Least privilege and complete mediation</li><li>AI-orchestrated attacks</li><li>MITRE ATLAS</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://github.com/OWASP/www-project-top-10-for-large-language-model-applications/blob/main/2_0_vulns" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OWASP Top 10 for LLM Applications (repository)</a><span class="lnote"> The 2025 entries, with mitigations</span></li><li><a href="https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/modelcontextprotocol/blob/main/docs/docs/2026-07-28/tutorials/security/security_best_practices.mdx" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MCP security best practices (source)</a><span class="lnote"> From the protocol's maintainers</span></li><li><a href="https://atlas.mitre.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MITRE ATLAS</a><span class="lnote"> Adversary techniques against AI systems</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://github.com/OWASP/www-project-top-10-for-large-language-model-applications/blob/main/2_0_vulns/LLM01_PromptInjection.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OWASP LLM01:2025 Prompt Injection</a>; <a href="https://github.com/OWASP/www-project-top-10-for-large-language-model-applications/blob/main/2_0_vulns/LLM05_ImproperOutputHandling.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OWASP LLM05:2025 Improper Output Handling</a>; <a href="https://github.com/OWASP/www-project-top-10-for-large-language-model-applications/blob/main/2_0_vulns/LLM06_ExcessiveAgency.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OWASP LLM06:2025 Excessive Agency</a>; <a href="https://github.com/OWASP/www-project-top-10-for-large-language-model-applications/blob/main/2_0_vulns/LLM10_UnboundedConsumption.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OWASP LLM10:2025 Unbounded Consumption</a>; <a href="https://github.com/OWASP/www-project-top-10-for-large-language-model-applications/blob/main/2_0_vulns" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OWASP 2025 entries (list)</a>; <a href="https://github.com/OWASP/www-project-top-10-for-large-language-model-applications/blob/main/initiatives/agent_security_initiative/agentic-top-10/Sprint%201-first-public-draft-expanded/README.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OWASP Agentic Top 10, first public draft</a>; <a href="https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/modelcontextprotocol/blob/main/docs/docs/2026-07-28/tutorials/security/security_best_practices.mdx" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MCP security best practices (source)</a>; <a href="https://github.com/mitre-atlas/atlas-data/blob/main/README.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MITRE ATLAS data (README)</a>; <a href="https://www.anthropic.com/news/disrupting-AI-espionage" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Anthropic: Disrupting an AI-orchestrated cyber espionage campaign</a>; <a href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/security" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Claude Code: Security</a>; <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/ai-services/content-safety/concepts/jailbreak-detection" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Microsoft Learn: Prompt Shields</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Confused_deputy_problem" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Confused deputy problem</a>.</p><p class="runcode">A question, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/enrichmeai.github.io/issues/new?title=%5BAgentic%20AI%20Security%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>. Nothing here is tracked and there are no comments to store: questions live in the open, where the next learner can find the answer.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= OAUTH ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-oauth" data-name="OAuth &amp; OIDC">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>OAuth 2.0 and OpenID Connect</h2><p>How delegated authorisation and login work, which flow to use for which client, how to validate tokens, and the mistakes that cause breaches. Broader security is on <a href="#security">Security</a>.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>The roles and the two standards</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Role</th><th>Is</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Resource owner</td><td>The user who owns the data</td><td>A customer</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Client</td><td>The app that wants access</td><td>The web app, a mobile app, a partner's server</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Authorisation server</td><td>Authenticates the user and issues tokens</td><td>Google Identity, Okta, Auth0, Keycloak, Entra ID</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Resource server</td><td>The API that accepts tokens</td><td>The search API, or Apigee in front of it</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <ul>
      <li><b>OAuth 2.0</b> is about <b>authorisation</b>: giving a client an <b>access token</b> to call an API on someone's behalf, limited by <b>scopes</b>.</li>
      <li><b>OpenID Connect (OIDC)</b> adds <b>authentication</b> on top: an <b>ID token</b> (a JWT) that says who the user is. "Sign in with Google" is OIDC.</li>
      <li><b>Access token</b> goes to APIs. <b>ID token</b> is for the client to learn who logged in; never send it to APIs as an access token. <b>Refresh token</b> gets new access tokens without a new login.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Which flow for which client</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Client</th><th>Flow</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Web app with a server</td><td>Authorisation code + PKCE</td><td>Confidential client: the server holds the secret and tokens</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Single-page app (browser)</td><td>Authorisation code + PKCE, ideally through a backend-for-frontend</td><td>The BFF keeps tokens server-side and gives the browser a secure cookie</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Mobile / desktop app</td><td>Authorisation code + PKCE via the system browser</td><td>Public client: no secret can be kept safe in the app</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Service to service</td><td>Client credentials</td><td>No user; the service authenticates as itself</td></tr>
      <tr><td>TV, CLI, device without a browser</td><td>Device authorisation grant</td><td>User approves on another device with a code</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Workloads on GCP</td><td>Service account identity (Google-signed tokens)</td><td>No secrets at all; Workload Identity Federation from outside GCP</td></tr>
      <tr><td><s>Implicit</s>, <s>password</s></td><td>Do not use</td><td>Deprecated by current best practice (RFC 9700, OAuth 2.1 draft)</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Authorisation code + PKCE, step by step</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>1. Client makes a random code_verifier and its hash:
   code_challenge = BASE64URL(SHA256(code_verifier))

2. Browser is sent to the authorisation server:
   GET /authorize?response_type=code
       &amp;client_id=shop-web
       &amp;redirect_uri=https://shop.example.com/callback
       &amp;scope=openid%20profile%20email%20search.read
       &amp;state=RANDOM            (CSRF protection)
       &amp;nonce=RANDOM            (ID-token replay protection)
       &amp;code_challenge=…&amp;code_challenge_method=S256

3. User logs in and consents; server redirects back:
   https://shop.example.com/callback?code=AUTH_CODE&amp;state=RANDOM
   (client checks state matches)

4. Client exchanges the code at the token endpoint
   (from the server for a web app or BFF; from the device for a mobile or desktop app):
   POST /token
   grant_type=authorization_code&amp;code=AUTH_CODE
   &amp;redirect_uri=…&amp;client_id=shop-web&amp;code_verifier=ORIGINAL

5. Response: access_token, id_token, refresh_token, expires_in

6. Client calls the API:
   Authorization: Bearer ACCESS_TOKEN</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">PKCE proves the party redeeming the code is the one that started the flow, so a stolen code is useless.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Authorisation code + PKCE, drawn</h3>
    <figure class="fig"><svg viewBox="0 0 660 324" role="img" aria-labelledby="pkce-t pkce-d"><title id="pkce-t">Authorisation code with PKCE</title><desc id="pkce-d">A sequence diagram with four lifelines: the user's browser, the client app, the authorisation server, and the API. 1: the client makes a random code verifier and keeps it, sending only its hash, the code challenge. 2: the browser is sent to the authorisation server with the challenge and a random state. 3: the user logs in and consents. 4: the server redirects back with a one-time code and the same state. 5: the client sends the code and the original verifier to the token endpoint. 6: the server checks the verifier against the challenge and returns access, ID and refresh tokens. 7: the client calls the API with the access token.</desc><defs><marker id="pkce-a" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker><marker id="pkce-aa" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar acc" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker></defs><rect class="nd" x="20.0" y="14" width="140" height="36" rx="4"/><text class="lb hd" x="90.0" y="37" text-anchor="middle">Browser</text><line class="ln" x1="90.0" y1="50" x2="90.0" y2="316"/><rect class="nd in" x="180.0" y="14" width="140" height="36" rx="4"/><text class="lb hd" x="250.0" y="37" text-anchor="middle">Client app</text><line class="ln" x1="250.0" y1="50" x2="250.0" y2="316"/><rect class="nd" x="340.0" y="14" width="140" height="36" rx="4"/><text class="lb hd" x="410.0" y="37" text-anchor="middle">Auth server</text><line class="ln" x1="410.0" y1="50" x2="410.0" y2="316"/><rect class="nd out" x="500.0" y="14" width="140" height="36" rx="4"/><text class="lb hd" x="570.0" y="37" text-anchor="middle">API</text><line class="ln" x1="570.0" y1="50" x2="570.0" y2="316"/><path class="ed" d="M250.0,72 h22 v14 h-18" marker-end="url(#pkce-a)"/><text class="lb" x="280.0" y="83" text-anchor="start">make code_verifier, keep it</text><circle class="nd in" cx="236.0" cy="76" r="9"/><text class="lb mu" x="236.0" y="80" text-anchor="middle">1</text><line class="ed" x1="250.0" y1="114" x2="406.0" y2="114" marker-end="url(#pkce-a)"/><circle class="nd in" cx="263.0" cy="102" r="9"/><text class="lb mu" x="263.0" y="106" text-anchor="middle">2</text><text class="lb" x="278.0" y="107" text-anchor="start">/authorize + code_challenge, state</text><path class="ed" d="M410.0,140 h22 v14 h-18" marker-end="url(#pkce-a)"/><text class="lb" x="440.0" y="151" text-anchor="start">user logs in, consents</text><circle class="nd in" cx="396.0" cy="144" r="9"/><text class="lb mu" x="396.0" y="148" text-anchor="middle">3</text><line class="ed dash" x1="410.0" y1="182" x2="94.0" y2="182" marker-end="url(#pkce-a)"/><circle class="nd in" cx="103.0" cy="170" r="9"/><text class="lb mu" x="103.0" y="174" text-anchor="middle">4</text><text class="lb mu" x="118.0" y="175" text-anchor="start">redirect: ?code=…&amp;state=…</text><line class="ed acc" x1="250.0" y1="216" x2="406.0" y2="216" marker-end="url(#pkce-aa)"/><circle class="nd in" cx="263.0" cy="204" r="9"/><text class="lb mu" x="263.0" y="208" text-anchor="middle">5</text><text class="lb acc" x="278.0" y="209" text-anchor="start">/token + code + code_verifier</text><line class="ed dash" x1="410.0" y1="250" x2="254.0" y2="250" marker-end="url(#pkce-a)"/><circle class="nd in" cx="263.0" cy="238" r="9"/><text class="lb mu" x="263.0" y="242" text-anchor="middle">6</text><text class="lb mu" x="278.0" y="243" text-anchor="start">access, ID, refresh tokens</text><line class="ed" x1="250.0" y1="284" x2="566.0" y2="284" marker-end="url(#pkce-a)"/><circle class="nd in" cx="263.0" cy="272" r="9"/><text class="lb mu" x="263.0" y="276" text-anchor="middle">7</text><text class="lb" x="278.0" y="277" text-anchor="start">Bearer access token</text></svg><figcaption>The six steps above as a sequence diagram. The verifier never travels until step 5, and only its hash went out in step 2, so someone who steals the code in step 4 cannot redeem it.</figcaption></figure>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Client credentials (machine to machine)</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>curl -s https://auth.example.com/oauth2/token \
  -u "$CLIENT_ID:$CLIENT_SECRET" \
  -d grant_type=client_credentials \
  -d scope=orders.read

# → {"access_token":"…","token_type":"Bearer","expires_in":3600}
curl -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" https://api.example.com/v1/orders/123</code></pre></div>
    <ul>
      <li>Cache the token until shortly before it expires; do not fetch one per call.</li>
      <li>Prefer private-key JWT or mTLS client authentication over shared secrets where supported.</li>
      <li>On GCP, prefer service account identity over client secrets.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Validating an access token (JWT)</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Fetch the issuer's keys from its JWKS URL (from <code>/.well-known/openid-configuration</code>); cache them.</li>
      <li>Verify the <b>signature</b> with the key matching the token's <code>kid</code>; accept only the expected algorithm (e.g. RS256 or ES256). Never <code>none</code>.</li>
      <li>Check <code>iss</code> equals your issuer exactly.</li>
      <li>Check <code>aud</code> contains your API's identifier.</li>
      <li>Check <code>exp</code> (and <code>nbf</code>) with small clock skew.</li>
      <li>Check required <b>scopes</b> for the operation.</li>
      <li>Then check <b>object-level permission</b> in your own code.</li>
    </ol>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># Python (PyJWT)
jwks = jwt.PyJWKClient("https://auth.example.com/.well-known/jwks.json")
key = jwks.get_signing_key_from_jwt(token)
claims = jwt.decode(token, key.key, algorithms=["RS256"],
                    audience="https://api.example.com", issuer="https://auth.example.com/")</code></pre></div>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># Spring Boot resource server
spring.security.oauth2.resourceserver.jwt.issuer-uri: https://auth.example.com/
spring.security.oauth2.resourceserver.jwt.audiences: https://api.example.com

# Quarkus
quarkus.oidc.auth-server-url=https://auth.example.com/
quarkus.oidc.token.audience=https://api.example.com</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">Opaque (non-JWT) tokens are validated by calling the server's introspection endpoint (RFC 7662).</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Scopes, claims and roles</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Scopes</b> limit what a client may do on the user's behalf (<code>orders.read</code>). They are about the app, not the user's own rights.</li>
      <li><b>Claims</b> are facts in the token: <code>sub</code> (stable user ID), <code>email</code>, <code>groups</code>, custom claims.</li>
      <li><b>Roles and permissions</b> are your application's rules. Load them from your source of truth; do not trust a role the client could have influenced.</li>
      <li>Key users by <code>sub</code> plus issuer, not by email, which can change or be reused.</li>
      <li>Keep tokens small; large claim sets slow every request.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Tokens in browsers and apps</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Best for SPAs:</b> a backend-for-frontend does the OAuth flow and keeps tokens; the browser gets an <code>HttpOnly; Secure; SameSite</code> session cookie.</li>
      <li><b>Avoid localStorage</b> for tokens: any XSS can read it.</li>
      <li><b>Short access tokens</b> (minutes) and <b>rotating refresh tokens</b> that are revoked if reused.</li>
      <li><b>Mobile:</b> store refresh tokens in the platform keychain / keystore; use the system browser, not an embedded web view, for login.</li>
      <li><b>Logout</b> revokes refresh tokens and ends the provider session if needed.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Common mistakes</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Accepting a token without checking audience: a token for another API works on yours.</li>
      <li>Using the ID token to call APIs.</li>
      <li>Loose redirect URIs (wildcards, prefix matching): codes get sent to an attacker.</li>
      <li>Missing or unchecked <code>state</code>: login CSRF.</li>
      <li>Implicit flow or tokens in URLs, where they leak through logs and referrers.</li>
      <li>Long-lived access tokens with no revocation.</li>
      <li>Trusting scopes alone and skipping object-level checks.</li>
      <li>Rolling your own authorisation server instead of using a maintained one.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Beyond the basics</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Sender-constrained tokens</b> (DPoP or mutual TLS) make a stolen token useless to anyone else.</li>
      <li><b>Pushed authorisation requests (PAR)</b> send the request parameters server to server first.</li>
      <li><b>Token exchange</b> (RFC 8693) for a service calling another on the user's behalf.</li>
      <li><b>OAuth 2.1</b> (draft) consolidates current best practice: PKCE everywhere, no implicit or password grants.</li>
      <li><b>OAuth for AI agents and MCP servers:</b> agents act with delegated, scoped, revocable tokens, never the user's password.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Explain access, ID and refresh tokens and scopes</li><li>Protect an API with a JWT resource server configuration</li><li>Use the authorisation code flow with PKCE through a library</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Validate tokens fully: signature, issuer, audience, expiry, scopes</li><li>Choose the right flow per client type</li><li>Store tokens safely in web and mobile apps</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Design identity for a product: providers, BFF, service-to-service, token lifetimes</li><li>Review OAuth implementations for the common mistakes</li><li>Integrate gateways (Apigee) and workload identity</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Own identity architecture across the organisation</li><li>Apply advanced specs (DPoP, PAR, token exchange)</li><li>Lead identity incidents and provider migrations</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>RFC 9700 security best current practice</li><li>OpenID Connect discovery and dynamic registration</li><li>Token exchange (RFC 8693)</li><li>Sender-constrained tokens (DPoP, mTLS)</li><li>Workload Identity Federation</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://oauth.net/2/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OAuth 2.0 (oauth.net)</a><span class="lnote"> Overview and links to every OAuth spec</span></li><li><a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6749" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RFC 6749: OAuth 2.0</a><span class="lnote"> The core framework</span></li><li><a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7636" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RFC 7636: PKCE</a><span class="lnote"> Proof Key for Code Exchange</span></li><li><a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9700" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RFC 9700: OAuth 2.0 Security Best Current Practice</a><span class="lnote"> Current security guidance</span></li><li><a href="https://openid.net/specs/openid-connect-core-1_0.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OpenID Connect Core</a><span class="lnote"> The OIDC specification</span></li><li><a href="https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/OAuth2_Cheat_Sheet.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OWASP OAuth 2.0 cheat sheet</a><span class="lnote"> Practical security checklist</span></li><li><a href="https://jwt.io/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">jwt.io</a><span class="lnote"> Decode and inspect JWTs (never paste production tokens)</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6749.txt" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RFC 6749 OAuth 2.0</a>; <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7636.txt" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RFC 7636 PKCE (code_challenge = BASE64URL(SHA256(verifier)))</a>; <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9700.txt" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RFC 9700 Security BCP (implicit SHOULD NOT, password grant MUST NOT, exact redirect matching)</a>; <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7662.txt" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RFC 7662 Token Introspection</a>; <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8693.txt" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RFC 8693 Token Exchange</a>; <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8628.txt" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RFC 8628 Device Authorization Grant</a>; <a href="https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-ietf-oauth-v2-1/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OAuth 2.1 still an Internet-Draft (draft-ietf-oauth-v2-1-17)</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=OpenID&action=raw" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: OpenID Connect is an authentication layer on OAuth 2.0</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/main/module/spring-boot-security-oauth2-resource-server/src/main/java/org/springframework/boot/security/oauth2/server/resource/autoconfigure/OAuth2ResourceServerProperties.java" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Spring Boot resource server properties (issuerUri, audiences)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/quarkusio/quarkus/main/docs/src/main/asciidoc/security-oidc-bearer-token-authentication.adoc" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Quarkus OIDC bearer token guide (quarkus.oidc.token.audience)</a>; <a href="https://openid.net/specs/openid-connect-core-1_0.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OpenID Connect Core</a>. A few claims could not be confirmed at their official source from here and are being re-checked.</p><p class="runcode">A question, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/enrichmeai.github.io/issues/new?title=%5BOAuth%20%26%20OIDC%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>. Nothing here is tracked and there are no comments to store: questions live in the open, where the next learner can find the answer.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= DATA ENGINEERING ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-data" data-name="Data Engineering">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>What data engineering is</h2><p>The concepts behind the tools: what data engineers build, the shape of every pipeline, and the properties that make data trustworthy. Read this before the dbt, DAG and BigQuery tabs.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>In one paragraph</h3>
    <p>Data engineering is building and running the systems that move data from where it is produced to where it is used, and making it <b>correct, current, documented and affordable</b> on the way. Source systems (apps, files, APIs, sensors, public datasets) produce data in their own shapes and at their own pace. Analysts, dashboards, machine-learning models, AI features and products need it in a shape they can query and trust. The data engineer owns the path between them: getting it in, keeping a faithful copy, reshaping it, checking it, delivering it, and knowing at any moment whether it is right.</p>
    <p class="note">A pipeline that runs is not the goal. The goal is data that people can rely on without asking the data engineer first.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Who does what</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Role</th><th>Focus</th><th>Typical output</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Data engineer</td><td>Ingestion, storage, pipelines, platform, reliability</td><td>Landed raw data, loaded tables, orchestrated jobs, a working platform</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Analytics engineer</td><td>Modelling data for use, mostly in SQL and dbt</td><td>Clean, tested, documented models and metrics</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Data analyst</td><td>Answering business questions</td><td>Queries, dashboards, reports</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Data scientist</td><td>Statistics, experiments, predictive models</td><td>Analyses, models, experiment results</td></tr>
      <tr><td>ML / AI engineer</td><td>Putting models into products and running them</td><td>Features, training and inference pipelines, evals, AI features</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Backend engineer</td><td>Applications that create and serve data</td><td>APIs and services, the source systems data engineers read from</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note">In small teams one person covers several rows. The boundaries matter less than knowing who owns each table.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>The lifecycle every pipeline follows</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>Question it answers</th><th>On GCP, for example</th><th>Tab</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Generation</td><td>Where does the data come from, how often, in what shape, under what licence?</td><td>App databases, partner files, public mirrors, event streams</td><td>—</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Ingestion</td><td>How do we get a faithful copy without losing or duplicating anything?</td><td>Storage Transfer Service, Cloud Run jobs, Pub/Sub, Datastream (CDC)</td><td><a href="#cloudrun">Cloud Run</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Storage</td><td>Where does each version live, for how long, at what cost?</td><td>GCS (files), BigQuery (tables), Cloud SQL (serving)</td><td><a href="#bigquery">BigQuery</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Transformation</td><td>How do raw records become clean, conformed, useful tables?</td><td>dbt on BigQuery, Dataflow (Beam), Managed Service for Apache Spark (formerly Dataproc)</td><td><a href="#dbt">dbt</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Serving</td><td>How do consumers read it, fast enough and safely?</td><td>BigQuery views, Postgres for an API, Looker, exports, vector indexes for AI</td><td><a href="#spring">Spring</a></td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p><b>Running underneath every stage:</b> orchestration (<a href="#dags">DAGs</a>), data quality and observability, security and access, data management (catalog, lineage, retention), infrastructure as code (<a href="#terraform">Terraform</a>), and ordinary software engineering (<a href="#devops">tests, CI, reviews</a>).</p>
    <p class="note">This stage model is adapted from <i>Fundamentals of Data Engineering</i> by Joe Reis and Matt Housley, a good first book on the subject.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Batch vs streaming</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Batch:</b> process a bounded chunk (yesterday, one file, one partition) on a schedule. Simple, cheap, easy to rerun. Latency of minutes to a day.</li>
      <li><b>Streaming:</b> process events continuously as they arrive. Latency of seconds, but harder: ordering, late events, state, exactly-once.</li>
      <li><b>Micro-batch:</b> small batches every few minutes. Often the practical middle.</li>
      <li><b>Choose by the consumer&rsquo;s need:</b> if someone must act within seconds, streaming; within minutes, micro-batch; a report read once a day, batch.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note"><i>Our view:</i> most reporting is fine with daily or hourly batch, so start there. <i>The other view:</i> building on a stream from the start avoids a rewrite if a later use case needs seconds.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>ETL vs ELT</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>ETL:</b> extract, transform in a separate engine, then load the result. Common when the warehouse was expensive or small.</li>
      <li><b>ELT:</b> extract, load the raw data into the warehouse, transform there with SQL (dbt). Common on BigQuery and similar warehouses.</li>
      <li><b>Our view, ELT where you may:</b> you keep the raw data, so a bug in a transform is fixed by rerunning it, not by re-ingesting from a source that may have changed or gone.</li>
      <li><b>The other view, when ETL fits:</b> where the warehouse is expensive or small, as in the ETL bullet above, transforming first means less raw data is stored and loaded. Some teams also prefer to clean or remove fields, such as personal data, before they reach the warehouse.</li>
      <li><b>Heavy non-SQL work</b> (parsing PDFs, images, large joins outside the warehouse) still goes to an engine built for it, such as Dataflow or Spark.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Where data lives</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Store</th><th>Good at</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>OLTP database (Postgres)</td><td>Many small reads and writes by key; serving an app</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Warehouse / OLAP (BigQuery)</td><td>Scanning and aggregating billions of rows; analytics</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Data lake (GCS)</td><td>Cheap storage of any file, raw and forever</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Lakehouse (Iceberg / Delta on object storage)</td><td>Warehouse-style tables over lake files, readable by several engines</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Vector index</td><td>Similarity search for AI retrieval</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note">File formats: CSV and JSON are easy but untyped and slow to scan. Parquet and ORC are columnar, compressed and typed, which suits analytics. Avro suits row-by-row streams with schema evolution.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Layers: each one has one job</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>Also called</th><th>Rule</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Raw / landing</td><td>bronze, RAW</td><td>Byte-for-byte copy of what the source published. Never edited. Lets you replay everything.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Operational / staging</td><td>ODP, staging</td><td>The raw data as typed tables, as published. Renamed and cast, not reinterpreted.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Foundation / conformed</td><td>silver, FDP, intermediate</td><td>Cleaned, deduplicated, keyed, versioned, joined to reference data. Structure added once, reused by many.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Consumable / marts</td><td>gold, CDP, marts</td><td>Shaped for one named use: a dashboard, an API, a model's features.</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note">Derivation runs one way: each layer reads only the layer below. A product that reads from a downstream serving database creates a cycle and should be refused.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Modelling basics</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Grain:</b> what one row means ("one order", "one order line per day"). State it for every table; most bugs are grain confusion.</li>
      <li><b>Keys:</b> a natural key from the source and, where needed, a surrogate key you generate. Never reuse or renumber a key that others store.</li>
      <li><b>Normalised</b> (many narrow tables, no repetition) suits OLTP. <b>Dimensional</b> (facts plus dimensions, a star schema) suits analytics. <b>One big table</b> is fine on BigQuery for a single use.</li>
      <li><b>Facts</b> are events with measures (an order, a payment). <b>Dimensions</b> describe them (store, product, date).</li>
      <li><b>Slowly changing dimensions:</b> type 1 overwrites; type 2 keeps history with valid-from/valid-to rows (dbt snapshots).</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Properties of a good pipeline</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Idempotent:</b> running it twice for the same input gives the same result. Overwrite a partition or MERGE on a key; never blindly append.</li>
      <li><b>Incremental:</b> processes only what is new or changed, decided from evidence (a watermark, ETag or ledger), not "we visited it".</li>
      <li><b>Backfillable:</b> any past period can be rerun on purpose.</li>
      <li><b>Late data aware:</b> records can arrive after their period; reprocess a lookback window.</li>
      <li><b>Schema-change safe:</b> a new or renamed source column fails loudly or is handled deliberately, never silently dropped.</li>
      <li><b>Observable:</b> every run records what it read, wrote, skipped and failed, with counts.</li>
      <li><b>Resumable:</b> a failure midway restarts from where it stopped, not from zero.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Data quality</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Dimension</th><th>Example check</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Completeness</td><td>Row count matches the source; no unexpected NULLs</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Validity</td><td>Dates parse; codes are in the allowed set</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Uniqueness</td><td>One row per key at the stated grain</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Consistency</td><td>Every store code exists in the store table</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Freshness</td><td>Newest record is within the expected age</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Accuracy</td><td>Values match the source on a sample</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note">The worst quality failure is a silent one: a parse that turns bad dates into NULL, or a monitor that reports 0 when its source was unreachable. Reject or count what you cannot read, and report "unknown" rather than a confident zero.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Ingestion patterns</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Full snapshot:</b> copy everything each time. Simple; fine for small tables.</li>
      <li><b>Incremental by watermark:</b> fetch rows changed since the last <code>updated_at</code> or ID.</li>
      <li><b>Change data capture (CDC):</b> read the source database's change log for inserts, updates and deletes (Datastream, Debezium).</li>
      <li><b>File drops:</b> compare source and destination listings (names, sizes, ETags) and copy only the difference.</li>
      <li><b>Events:</b> producers publish to a queue (Pub/Sub, Kafka); consumers process at-least-once, so handle duplicates.</li>
      <li><b>APIs:</b> paginate, respect rate limits, keep the raw response, and record the error body when a call fails.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Governance &amp; responsibility</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Ownership:</b> every table has a named owner and a description.</li>
      <li><b>Lineage:</b> you can say which sources feed a column and which products a change will affect.</li>
      <li><b>Access:</b> least privilege per dataset; serving systems get read-only access.</li>
      <li><b>Personal and sensitive data:</b> know where it is, mask or restrict it, honour deletion requests and legal holds.</li>
      <li><b>Licences:</b> respect source terms; carry attribution (for example CC-BY) into every published output.</li>
      <li><b>Retention and cost:</b> decide how long each layer is kept and who pays for scans.</li>
      <li><b>Contracts:</b> agree the schema and meaning of anything another team reads, and change it on purpose.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>A worked example, end to end</h3>
    <p>An online shop's orders, exported daily by its stores and served to the shop's staff through a reporting API.</p>
    <ol>
      <li><b>Source:</b> two order systems (the website and the store tills) export orders as Parquet and JSON files to object storage, partitioned by year. Files are sometimes re-exported in place.</li>
      <li><b>Ingest (LAND):</b> a Cloud Run job compares the source bucket's listing with the GCS landing bucket and copies only new or changed objects. It records examined, copied, already-current and failed counts.</li>
      <li><b>Raw:</b> the bucket keeps the originals byte for byte, so anything later can be replayed.</li>
      <li><b>Load (raw):</b> each landed file becomes rows in BigQuery tables, with run ID and load time, exactly as exported.</li>
      <li><b>Transform (analytics):</b> dbt cleans and conforms: parses two date formats (and rejects what does not parse), computes a stable order key, deduplicates re-exported rows, joins store reference data, and runs tests.</li>
      <li><b>Consumable (mart):</b> a dbt model shapes exactly what the reporting API needs, with a contract on its columns.</li>
      <li><b>Serve:</b> a loader upserts that table into Postgres on the stable key, without renumbering existing IDs that links, invoices and refunds depend on. The API reads Postgres read-only.</li>
      <li><b>Orchestrate and observe:</b> a scheduler ticks daily; an orchestrator runs each stage when its input is ready and appends every state change to a ledger, so "what happened on 2026-09-19?" has an answer.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="note">Every tab in this guide appears somewhere in that path. That is why a data engineer needs breadth.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Questions a data engineer always asks</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>What does one row mean, and what is its key?</li>
      <li>What happens if this runs twice? If it fails halfway?</li>
      <li>What does this return when the source is down, and can anyone tell that apart from "no data"?</li>
      <li>How will I know it is late or wrong before a user does?</li>
      <li>Who reads this table, and what breaks for them if I change it?</li>
      <li>How much does a run cost, and what makes it grow?</li>
      <li>Can I rebuild it from raw if I have to?</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Glossary</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Partition:</b> a slice of a table (by date or year) that queries can skip.</li>
      <li><b>Watermark:</b> the point up to which data is known to be processed.</li>
      <li><b>Backfill:</b> deliberately reprocessing a past period.</li>
      <li><b>Upsert / MERGE:</b> insert new rows, update existing ones, by key.</li>
      <li><b>Lineage:</b> the graph of which data feeds which.</li>
      <li><b>Data contract:</b> an agreed schema and meaning between producer and consumer.</li>
      <li><b>Freshness:</b> how old the newest data is.</li>
      <li><b>SCD:</b> slowly changing dimension, how history of descriptive attributes is kept.</li>
      <li><b>CDC:</b> change data capture, streaming a database's changes.</li>
      <li><b>DAG:</b> directed acyclic graph, the dependency order of tasks.</li>
      <li><b>Ledger:</b> an append-only record of every run and state change.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Explain the lifecycle and the layers</li><li>Load a file into a warehouse and query it</li><li>Write a simple, idempotent transformation</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Build incremental, tested pipelines end to end</li><li>Choose batch vs streaming and ingestion patterns</li><li>Model data at a stated grain with keys and tests</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Design a data platform: layers, contracts, orchestration, observability</li><li>Own data quality and reconciliation across sources</li><li>Plan cost, retention and governance</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Set data architecture and standards across the organisation</li><li>Lead migrations between platforms without losing trust</li><li>Shape data products and their contracts with consumers</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>Dimensional modelling (Kimball)</li><li>Data contracts and schema evolution</li><li>Open table formats: Iceberg, Delta</li><li>Change data capture</li><li>Data observability and lineage</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://www.oreilly.com/library/view/fundamentals-of-data/9781098108298/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Fundamentals of Data Engineering (book)</a><span class="lnote"> Reis &amp; Housley: the lifecycle and its undercurrents</span></li><li><a href="https://dataintensive.net/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Designing Data-Intensive Applications</a><span class="lnote"> Storage, replication, batch and stream processing</span></li><li><a href="https://www.kimballgroup.com/data-warehouse-business-intelligence-resources/kimball-techniques/dimensional-modeling-techniques/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kimball dimensional modelling techniques</a><span class="lnote"> Facts, dimensions, SCDs</span></li><li><a href="https://docs.getdbt.com/best-practices" target="_blank" rel="noopener">dbt best practices</a><span class="lnote"> How to structure a transformation project</span></li><li><a href="https://cloud.google.com/architecture" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google Cloud Architecture Center</a><span class="lnote"> Reference architectures for data on GCP</span></li><li><a href="https://iceberg.apache.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Apache Iceberg</a><span class="lnote"> Open table format for lakehouses</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/dbt-labs/docs.getdbt.com/current/website/docs/docs/build/incremental-models.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">dbt incremental models (merge on a unique key, schema-change handling)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/dbt-labs/docs.getdbt.com/current/website/docs/reference/resource-configs/hard-deletes.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">dbt snapshots hard_deletes (SCD type 2)</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/logging/docs/audit" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cloud Audit Logs (GCP docs host reachable)</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/datastream/docs/overview" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Datastream overview</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/dataproc/docs/concepts/overview" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Managed Service for Apache Spark (formerly Dataproc)</a>. A few claims could not be confirmed at their official source from here and are being re-checked.</p><p class="runcode">A question, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/enrichmeai.github.io/issues/new?title=%5BData%20Engineering%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>. Nothing here is tracked and there are no comments to store: questions live in the open, where the next learner can find the answer.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= DATA LIBRARIES ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-datalibs" data-name="Data Processing Libraries">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Data processing libraries</h2><p>The major libraries and engines for processing data in Python and Java, what scale each suits, the same task written in several of them, and the habits that make any of them fast. Frameworks that sit around these engines, such as <a href="#culvert">Culvert</a>, are on their own tab.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>The landscape</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Library / engine</th><th>Language</th><th>Runs on</th><th>Model</th><th>Best for</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>pandas</td><td>Python</td><td>One machine, mostly one core</td><td>Eager, in memory</td><td>Analysis and small-to-medium data that fits in RAM; huge ecosystem</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Polars</td><td>Python (Rust core)</td><td>One machine, all cores</td><td>Lazy or eager, Arrow-based</td><td>Fast DataFrame work on tens of GB; query optimisation</td></tr>
      <tr><td>DuckDB</td><td>SQL from Python, Java, CLI</td><td>In-process, all cores</td><td>Columnar SQL engine</td><td>SQL over Parquet/CSV/Arrow files, local or in object storage</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Apache Arrow / PyArrow</td><td>Many</td><td>One machine</td><td>Columnar memory format and Parquet IO</td><td>Reading and writing Parquet, zero-copy exchange between tools</td></tr>
      <tr><td>NumPy</td><td>Python</td><td>One machine</td><td>N-dimensional arrays</td><td>Numerical computation underneath most Python data tools</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Dask</td><td>Python</td><td>Many cores or a cluster</td><td>Parallel pandas/NumPy-like collections</td><td>Scaling existing pandas code beyond one core</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Ray (Ray Data)</td><td>Python</td><td>Cluster</td><td>Distributed tasks and actors</td><td>Python-heavy distributed work, ML preprocessing</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Apache Spark / PySpark</td><td>Scala, Java, Python, SQL</td><td>Cluster (Managed Service for Apache Spark, formerly Dataproc; EMR; Databricks)</td><td>Distributed DataFrames, lazy</td><td>Large batch processing, existing Spark estates</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Apache Beam</td><td>Java, Python, Go</td><td>Runners: Dataflow, Flink, Spark, direct</td><td>Unified batch and streaming pipelines</td><td>Portable pipelines; streaming on Dataflow</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Apache Flink</td><td>Java, SQL, Python</td><td>Cluster</td><td>Stateful stream processing</td><td>Low-latency streaming with large state</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Kafka Streams</td><td>Java</td><td>Inside your service</td><td>Stream processing library</td><td>Stateful processing of Kafka topics without a cluster</td></tr>
      <tr><td>BigQuery DataFrames (bigframes)</td><td>Python</td><td>BigQuery</td><td>pandas-like API compiled to SQL</td><td>pandas-style code on data that stays in BigQuery</td></tr>
      <tr><td>dbt</td><td>SQL + Jinja</td><td>Your warehouse</td><td>Models compiled to SQL</td><td>Transformations inside the warehouse (<a href="#dbt">dbt tab</a>)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Spring Batch</td><td>Java</td><td>One JVM or partitioned</td><td>Chunked read-process-write jobs with restart</td><td>Business batch jobs in Spring applications</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Choosing by situation</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Data already in BigQuery:</b> SQL or dbt first; bigframes if you need a DataFrame API. Do not pull it out to process it.</li>
      <li><b>Files up to a few GB on one machine:</b> Polars or DuckDB; pandas if a library you need expects it.</li>
      <li><b>Tens to low hundreds of GB on one large machine:</b> Polars (lazy, streaming) or DuckDB.</li>
      <li><b>Beyond one machine, batch:</b> BigQuery, Dataflow (Beam) or Spark on Managed Service for Apache Spark (formerly Dataproc).</li>
      <li><b>Streaming:</b> Dataflow (Beam), Flink, or Kafka Streams inside a service.</li>
      <li><b>Inside a Java service:</b> plain Java streams for small sets; Beam for pipelines; Spring Batch for restartable business jobs.</li>
      <li><b>Reading and writing Parquet in Python:</b> PyArrow underneath, whatever DataFrame you use on top.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Validation libraries</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>pandera:</b> schemas for pandas and Polars DataFrames (types, ranges, nullability) checked in code and tests.</li>
      <li><b>Great Expectations:</b> expectation suites and data docs for pipelines.</li>
      <li><b>Pydantic:</b> validating individual records and API payloads in Python.</li>
      <li><b>dbt tests:</b> keys, relationships and accepted values in the warehouse.</li>
      <li><b>Jakarta Bean Validation:</b> record validation in Java.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">Whatever the tool: reject or quarantine what does not parse, and count it. Never coerce failures to NULL silently.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>The same task in five libraries</h3>
    <p>Count orders per store since 2020 from Parquet files partitioned by year.</p>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># pandas: reads into memory, eager
import pandas as pd
df = pd.read_parquet("data/", columns=["store", "year"],
                     filters=[("year", "&gt;=", 2020)])   # pushdown; partition columns arrive as category
out = (df.groupby("store", observed=True).size()
         .sort_values(ascending=False))</code></pre></div>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># Polars: lazy; filters and column selection pushed into the scan
import polars as pl
out = (pl.scan_parquet("data/year=*/*.parquet", hive_partitioning=True)
         .filter(pl.col("year") &gt;= 2020)
         .group_by("store").agg(pl.len().alias("n"))
         .sort("n", descending=True)
         .collect())</code></pre></div>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># DuckDB: SQL straight over the files
import duckdb
out = duckdb.sql("""
  SELECT store, count(*) AS n
  FROM read_parquet('data/year=*/*.parquet', hive_partitioning = true)
  WHERE year &gt;= 2020
  GROUP BY store ORDER BY n DESC
""").df()</code></pre></div>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># PySpark: distributed, writes results back to GCS
from pyspark.sql import SparkSession, functions as F
spark = SparkSession.builder.getOrCreate()
(spark.read.parquet("gs://landing/data/")
   .where(F.col("year") &gt;= 2020)
   .groupBy("store").count()
   .orderBy(F.desc("count"))
   .write.mode("overwrite").parquet("gs://out/store_counts/"))</code></pre></div>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># BigQuery DataFrames: pandas-like code, executed inside BigQuery
import bigframes.pandas as bpd
df = bpd.read_gbq("my-proj.analytics.orders")
out = df[df["order_year"] &gt;= 2020].groupby("store_id").size()</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Arrow and Parquet in Python</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>import pyarrow.dataset as ds
import pyarrow.parquet as pq

dataset = ds.dataset("data/", format="parquet", partitioning="hive")
table = dataset.to_table(columns=["order_ref", "store", "order_date"],
                         filter=ds.field("year") &gt;= 2020)   # pushdown

print(table.schema)          # check types before trusting them
pq.write_table(table, "out.parquet",
               compression="zstd",
               coerce_timestamps="us",           # many readers expect microseconds
               allow_truncated_timestamps=True)</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">Timestamp units are a classic trap: Parquet written with nanosecond timestamps can be read wrongly or refused by some engines, especially for dates before 1970. Check the schema and write the unit the reader expects.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Java options</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Java streams and collectors</b> for in-memory work (<a href="#java">Java tab</a>).</li>
      <li><b>Apache Beam (Java)</b> for pipelines on Dataflow, Flink or Spark.</li>
      <li><b>Spark (Java/Scala)</b> on Managed Service for Apache Spark (formerly Dataproc) or Databricks.</li>
      <li><b>Spring Batch</b> for chunked, restartable jobs with skip and retry policies.</li>
      <li><b>parquet-java and Arrow Java</b> for columnar file IO.</li>
      <li><b>Jackson</b> (JSON), <b>Apache Commons CSV</b> or <b>FastCSV</b> for text formats; stream rather than loading whole files.</li>
      <li><b>DuckDB JDBC</b> for SQL over local or remote Parquet from Java.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Habits that make any library fast</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Columnar formats</b> (Parquet) over CSV and JSON for anything read more than once.</li>
      <li><b>Read only the columns and rows you need</b> (projection and predicate pushdown).</li>
      <li><b>Lazy evaluation</b> (Polars, Spark, Beam) lets the engine optimise the whole plan.</li>
      <li><b>No row-by-row loops</b> (<code>apply</code>, <code>iterrows</code>); use vectorised expressions.</li>
      <li><b>Right types:</b> categoricals for repeated strings, Arrow-backed strings, proper dates.</li>
      <li><b>Partition by the common filter</b> (date, year); avoid thousands of tiny files.</li>
      <li><b>Stream or chunk</b> data larger than memory.</li>
      <li><b>Push work to the warehouse</b> when the data is already there.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Correctness traps</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Silent type coercion:</b> integers with nulls become floats in classic pandas; IDs with leading zeros become numbers. Set dtypes explicitly.</li>
      <li><b>Date parsing:</b> mixed formats (<code>dd-mm-yyyy</code> and <code>yyyy-mm-dd</code>) parse wrongly or to null; specify the format and count failures.</li>
      <li><b>Time zones:</b> naive vs aware timestamps compared silently.</li>
      <li><b>Joins:</b> duplicate keys multiply rows, just as in SQL.</li>
      <li><b>Floating-point money:</b> use decimals or integer minor units.</li>
      <li><b>Schema drift:</b> a new or renamed source column must fail loudly or be mapped on purpose.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Load, filter and aggregate a file with pandas or Polars</li><li>Read Parquet and inspect its schema</li><li>Know which library suits which data size</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Use lazy Polars, DuckDB or PyArrow with pushdown on large files</li><li>Write a PySpark or Beam job for data beyond one machine</li><li>Validate data with pandera or dbt tests and count rejects</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Choose engines per workload: warehouse SQL, single-node, distributed, streaming</li><li>Tune memory, partitioning and file layout</li><li>Design portable pipelines with open formats</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Set the organisation's processing stack and standards</li><li>Lead migrations between engines (pandas to Polars, Spark to BigQuery)</li><li>Understand engine internals: query optimisers, vectorised execution</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>Apache Arrow memory format</li><li>Polars lazy query optimisation</li><li>DuckDB extensions (httpfs, Iceberg)</li><li>Spark adaptive query execution</li><li>Beam windowing and triggers</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://pandas.pydata.org/docs/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">pandas</a><span class="lnote"> User guide and API</span></li><li><a href="https://docs.pola.rs/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Polars</a><span class="lnote"> User guide, lazy API</span></li><li><a href="https://duckdb.org/docs/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">DuckDB</a><span class="lnote"> SQL, Python API, Parquet and remote files</span></li><li><a href="https://arrow.apache.org/docs/python/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Apache Arrow (PyArrow)</a><span class="lnote"> Datasets, Parquet, compute</span></li><li><a href="https://spark.apache.org/docs/latest/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Apache Spark</a><span class="lnote"> PySpark and SQL</span></li><li><a href="https://beam.apache.org/documentation/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Apache Beam</a><span class="lnote"> Programming guide</span></li><li><a href="https://cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/bigquery-dataframes-introduction" target="_blank" rel="noopener">BigQuery DataFrames</a><span class="lnote"> pandas-like API in BigQuery</span></li><li><a href="https://pandera.readthedocs.io/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">pandera</a><span class="lnote"> DataFrame validation</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://pypi.org/pypi/pandas/json" target="_blank" rel="noopener">pandas on PyPI (snippet run with the current release)</a>; <a href="https://pypi.org/pypi/polars/json" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Polars on PyPI (snippet run with the current release)</a>; <a href="https://pypi.org/pypi/pyarrow/json" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PyArrow on PyPI (dataset + write_table snippet run)</a>; <a href="https://pypi.org/pypi/duckdb/json" target="_blank" rel="noopener">DuckDB on PyPI (read_parquet hive_partitioning snippet run)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/unionai-oss/pandera/main/README.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">pandera README (supports pandas and Polars)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/metaplane/dbt-expectations/main/README.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">dbt-expectations README (package metaplane/dbt_expectations)</a>; <a href="https://kafka.apache.org/42/streams/introduction/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kafka Streams introduction</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/apache/beam/master/README.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Apache Beam README (runners)</a>; <a href="https://docs.spring.io/spring-batch/reference/spring-batch-intro.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Spring Batch introduction</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/bigquery-dataframes-introduction" target="_blank" rel="noopener">BigQuery DataFrames introduction</a>. A few claims could not be confirmed at their official source from here and are being re-checked.</p><p class="runcode"><b>Run the code:</b> <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/well-rounded-engineer-data/tree/main/datalibs" target="_blank" rel="noopener">well-rounded-engineer-data/datalibs</a>, with a README that says how to run it and what it shows. Stuck, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/well-rounded-engineer-data/issues/new?title=%5BData%20Libraries%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= DBT ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-dbt" data-name="dbt">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>dbt on BigQuery</h2><p>dbt-core 1.8+ with dbt-bigquery. Unit tests arrived in 1.8; microbatch and YAML snapshots in 1.9.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card"><h3>CLI</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>dbt deps                         # install packages.yml
dbt debug                        # check profile + connection
dbt build                        # seed + run + snapshot + test, in DAG order
dbt run   -s my_model
dbt test  -s my_model
dbt build -s my_model --full-refresh
dbt compile -s my_model          # see SQL in target/compiled/
dbt show  -s my_model --limit 20 # preview rows
dbt ls    -s tag:daily --resource-type model
dbt seed  &amp;&amp; dbt snapshot
dbt source freshness
dbt docs generate &amp;&amp; dbt docs serve

dbt run --target prod --vars '{run_date: "2025-01-01"}'
dbt retry                        # re-run only what failed last time</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Node selection</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>-s my_model          # just it
-s +my_model         # it and all parents
-s my_model+         # it and all children
-s 2+my_model+1      # 2 levels up, 1 down
-s @my_model         # children + their parents (safe rebuild)
-s tag:daily
-s path:models/analytics
-s config.materialized:incremental
-s source:raw+       # everything downstream of a source
-s "orders,tag:daily"     # intersection (comma)
-s a b               # union (space)
--exclude tag:slow

# Slim CI: only what changed vs prod's manifest
dbt build -s state:modified+ --defer --state prod-artifacts/
dbt build -s result:error+ --state target/</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>profiles.yml (BigQuery)</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>my_project:
  target: dev
  outputs:
    dev:
      type: bigquery
      method: oauth                 # gcloud auth application-default login
      project: my-proj-dev
      dataset: dbt_jo               # personal dev dataset
      location: asia-south1
      threads: 8
      job_execution_timeout_seconds: 600
      maximum_bytes_billed: 50000000000   # hard cost cap per query
    prod:
      type: bigquery
      method: service-account
      keyfile: /secrets/sa.json     # or oauth on Workload Identity
      project: my-proj-prod
      dataset: analytics
      location: asia-south1
      threads: 16</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Project layout &amp; dbt_project.yml</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>models/
  staging/   stg_*.sql   1:1 with sources, rename + cast only (view)
  intermediate/ int_*.sql joins, dedupe (ephemeral or view)
  marts/     fct_* dim_*  what consumers read (table/incremental)
seeds/  snapshots/  macros/  tests/  analyses/

# dbt_project.yml
name: shop
profile: my_project
models:
  shop:
    +persist_docs: {relation: true, columns: true}
    staging:
      +materialized: view
    marts:
      +materialized: table
      +labels: {owner: data, layer: analytics}
vars:
  start_year: 2023</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Model config for BigQuery</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>{{ config(
    materialized = 'table',
    partition_by = {
      'field': 'order_date',
      'data_type': 'date',          # date | timestamp | datetime | int64
      'granularity': 'month'        # day | month | year (hour too for timestamp/datetime)
    },
    cluster_by = ['store_id', 'order_ref'],   # up to 4 columns
    require_partition_filter = true,
    partition_expiration_days = none,
    labels = {'layer': 'analytics'},
    tags = ['daily']
) }}

-- integer-range partition
partition_by = {'field': 'order_year', 'data_type': 'int64',
                'range': {'start': 1995, 'end': 2030, 'interval': 1}}</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Incremental: merge</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>{{ config(
    materialized = 'incremental',
    incremental_strategy = 'merge',
    unique_key = 'order_id',            # or a list
    on_schema_change = 'append_new_columns',# ignore | fail | sync_all_columns
    partition_by = {'field': 'order_date', 'data_type': 'date'},
    incremental_predicates = [
      "DBT_INTERNAL_DEST.order_date &gt;= date_sub(current_date(), interval 30 day)"
    ]
) }}

select * from {{ ref('int_orders') }}
{% if is_incremental() %}
  where _landed_at &gt; (select max(_landed_at) from {{ this }})
{% endif %}</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note"><code>incremental_predicates</code> prunes the target side of the MERGE. Without it BigQuery scans the whole destination on every run.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Incremental: insert_overwrite</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>{% set partitions_to_replace = [
  'current_date()',
  'date_sub(current_date(), interval 1 day)'
] %}

{{ config(
    materialized = 'incremental',
    incremental_strategy = 'insert_overwrite',
    partition_by = {'field': 'event_date', 'data_type': 'date'},
    partitions = partitions_to_replace       # static: cheapest
) }}

select * from {{ ref('stg_events') }}
{% if is_incremental() %}
  where event_date in ({{ partitions_to_replace | join(',') }})
{% endif %}</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">Replaces whole partitions, no row matching. Leave out <code>partitions</code> for "dynamic" mode: dbt finds the partitions in the new data, at the cost of an extra query.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Incremental: microbatch <span class="tag">1.9</span></h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>{{ config(
    materialized = 'incremental',
    incremental_strategy = 'microbatch',
    event_time = 'order_date',
    batch_size = 'day',            # hour | day | month | year
    begin = '2023-01-01',
    lookback = 2,
    partition_by = {'field': 'order_date', 'data_type': 'date',
                    'granularity': 'day'}
) }}
-- no is_incremental() block: dbt filters each batch for you,
-- and filters upstream refs that declare their own event_time
select * from {{ ref('stg_orders') }}</code></pre></div>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>dbt run -s fct_orders --event-time-start 2024-01-01 --event-time-end 2024-02-01
dbt retry      # re-runs only failed batches</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Sources &amp; freshness</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># models/staging/_sources.yml
sources:
  - name: raw
    database: my-proj            # = GCP project
    schema: raw               # = dataset
    loaded_at_field: _landed_at
    freshness:
      warn_after:  {count: 1, period: day}
      error_after: {count: 3, period: day}
    tables:
      - name: orders
      - name: app_orders
        freshness: null          # opt out

-- usage
select * from {{ source('raw', 'orders') }}</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Data tests</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>models:
  - name: orders
    columns:
      - name: order_id
        data_tests: [unique, not_null]
      - name: store_id
        data_tests:
          - relationships: {to: ref('stores'), field: store_id}
      - name: status
        data_tests:
          - accepted_values: {values: ['PAID', 'PENDING']}
      - name: order_date
        data_tests:
          - not_null:
              config:
                severity: error          # the default; with warn, dbt skips error_if
                where: "order_year &gt;= 2000"
                error_if: "&gt;100"
                warn_if: "&gt;0"</code></pre></div>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>-- tests/assert_no_future_dates.sql  (singular: fails if it returns rows)
select * from {{ ref('orders') }}
where order_date &gt; current_date()</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note"><code>data_tests:</code> replaced <code>tests:</code> in 1.8; the old key still works. Newer versions (from a 1.10 patch release) nest test arguments under <code>arguments:</code>; the top-level form above is older, still accepted, and the one earlier versions need. Packages: <code>dbt-labs/dbt_utils</code>, <code>metaplane/dbt_expectations</code>.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Unit tests <span class="tag">1.8</span></h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>unit_tests:
  - name: parses_dd_mm_yyyy
    model: int_orders_normalised
    given:
      - input: ref('stg_app_orders')
        rows:
          - {order_ref: "A1", raw_date: "05-05-1959"}
          - {order_ref: "A2", raw_date: "bad"}
    expect:
      rows:
        - {order_ref: "A1", order_date: 1959-05-05}
        - {order_ref: "A2", order_date: null}

  # format: csv | dict | sql ; overrides: {macros: {is_incremental: true}}</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">Unit tests run SQL logic on fixed inputs before the model is built. Run with <code>dbt test -s test_type:unit</code>; exclude them in prod.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Jinja &amp; macros</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>{{ ref('model') }}   {{ ref('other_pkg', 'model') }}
{{ source('raw', 'orders') }}
{{ this }}                      -- current relation
{{ var('start_year', 2020) }}
{{ env_var('DBT_ENV', 'dev') }}
{{ target.name }} {{ target.project }} {{ target.dataset }}

{% set stores = ['1_05', '4_12', '7_03'] %}
where store_id in ({% for c in stores %}'{{ c }}'{% if not loop.last %},{% endif %}{% endfor %})

-- macros/safe_date.sql
{% macro safe_date(col, fmt='%Y-%m-%d') -%}
  safe.parse_date('{{ fmt }}', {{ col }})
{%- endmacro %}

-- run a query at compile time
{% set rs = run_query("select distinct store_id from " ~ ref('stores')) %}
{% if execute %}{% set stores = rs.columns[0].values() %}{% endif %}

-- dev sampling
{% if target.name == 'dev' %} limit 1000 {% endif %}</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Custom generic test</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>-- tests/generic/null_ratio_below.sql
{% test null_ratio_below(model, column_name, max_ratio=0.01) %}
select *
from (
  select countif({{ column_name }} is null) / count(*) as ratio
  from {{ model }}
)
where ratio &gt; {{ max_ratio }}
{% endtest %}

# use it
columns:
  - name: order_date
    data_tests:
      - null_ratio_below: {max_ratio: 0.0}</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Contracts &amp; snapshots</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># Model contract: build fails if shape drifts
models:
  - name: serving_orders
    config:
      contract: {enforced: true}
    columns:
      - name: order_id
        data_type: string
        constraints: [{type: not_null}]
      - name: order_date
        data_type: date
      # … list every column the model returns, each with its data_type

# Snapshot, YAML form (1.9): SCD type 2
snapshots:
  - name: store_snapshot
    relation: source('raw', 'stores')
    config:
      schema: snapshots
      unique_key: store_id
      strategy: check          # or timestamp + updated_at
      check_cols: all
      hard_deletes: invalidate # ignore | invalidate | new_record</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Hooks, grants, operations</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>{{ config(
    post_hook = [
      "alter table {{ this }} set options(description='built {{ run_started_at }}')"
    ],
    grants = {'roles/bigquery.dataViewer': ['serviceAccount:api@p.iam.gserviceaccount.com']}
) }}

# dbt_project.yml
on-run-end:
  - "{{ log_results(results) }}"

# ad-hoc macro
dbt run-operation drop_dev_datasets --args '{prefix: dbt_jo}'</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Run, test and select models; read compiled SQL</li><li>Use ref and source; add not_null and unique tests</li><li>Understand views vs tables</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Write incremental models with the right strategy</li><li>Write macros, generic tests and unit tests</li><li>Configure partitioning and clustering on BigQuery</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Structure a multi-project dbt platform</li><li>Set up slim CI with state and defer</li><li>Use contracts, versions and access controls for shared models</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Own modelling standards across the organisation</li><li>Design cross-project dependencies and data mesh boundaries</li><li>Contribute packages and tooling</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>Model contracts and versions</li><li>Microbatch incremental models</li><li>dbt packages: dbt_utils, dbt_expectations</li><li>Semantic layer and metrics</li><li>Exposures and documentation</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://docs.getdbt.com/docs/introduction" target="_blank" rel="noopener">dbt documentation</a><span class="lnote"> Official docs</span></li><li><a href="https://docs.getdbt.com/reference/references-overview" target="_blank" rel="noopener">dbt reference</a><span class="lnote"> Every config, command and property</span></li><li><a href="https://docs.getdbt.com/docs/build/incremental-models" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Incremental models</a><span class="lnote"> Strategies and is_incremental()</span></li><li><a href="https://docs.getdbt.com/reference/resource-configs/bigquery-configs" target="_blank" rel="noopener">BigQuery configs</a><span class="lnote"> Partitioning, clustering, labels</span></li><li><a href="https://docs.getdbt.com/best-practices" target="_blank" rel="noopener">dbt best practices</a><span class="lnote"> Project structure and style</span></li><li><a href="https://hub.getdbt.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">dbt package hub</a><span class="lnote"> Reusable packages</span></li><li><a href="https://learn.getdbt.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">dbt Learn courses</a><span class="lnote"> Free official courses</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/dbt-labs/docs.getdbt.com/current/website/docs/reference/resource-configs/bigquery-configs.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">dbt BigQuery configs (partition_by, granularity per data_type, insert_overwrite static/dynamic)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/dbt-labs/docs.getdbt.com/current/website/docs/docs/build/incremental-microbatch.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">dbt microbatch (batch_size hour|day|month|year, lookback, --event-time-start/end, dbt retry failed batches, upstream event_time filtering)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/dbt-labs/docs.getdbt.com/current/website/docs/reference/resource-configs/severity.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">dbt severity, error_if, warn_if</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/dbt-labs/docs.getdbt.com/current/website/docs/reference/resource-configs/hard-deletes.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">dbt hard_deletes (1.9: ignore|invalidate|new_record)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/dbt-labs/docs.getdbt.com/current/website/docs/docs/build/unit-tests.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">dbt unit tests (test_type:unit, exclude in prod)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/dbt-labs/docs.getdbt.com/current/website/docs/reference/dbt-jinja-functions/target.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">dbt target variables (target.project, target.dataset on BigQuery)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/dbt-labs/docs.getdbt.com/current/website/docs/reference/resource-configs/grants.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">dbt grants (BigQuery roles/bigquery.dataViewer)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/dbt-labs/docs.getdbt.com/current/website/docs/reference/node-selection/graph-operators.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">dbt graph operators (@ selector)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/dbt-labs/docs.getdbt.com/current/website/docs/docs/build/incremental-models.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">dbt incremental models (on_schema_change values)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/dbt-labs/docs.getdbt.com/current/website/docs/reference/resource-properties/data-tests.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">dbt data_tests property</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/clustered-tables" target="_blank" rel="noopener">BigQuery clustered tables (up to four clustering columns)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/metaplane/dbt-expectations/main/README.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">dbt-expectations README (metaplane/dbt_expectations)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/dbt-labs/docs.getdbt.com/current/website/docs/reference/commands/show.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">dbt show</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/dbt-labs/docs.getdbt.com/current/website/docs/reference/commands/list.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">dbt list</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/dbt-labs/docs.getdbt.com/current/website/docs/reference/commands/run-operation.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">dbt run-operation</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/dbt-labs/docs.getdbt.com/current/website/docs/reference/node-selection/defer.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">dbt defer</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/dbt-labs/docs.getdbt.com/current/website/docs/reference/deprecations.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">dbt deprecations (test arguments)</a>.</p><p class="runcode"><b>Run the code:</b> <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/well-rounded-engineer-data/tree/main/dbt" target="_blank" rel="noopener">well-rounded-engineer-data/dbt</a>, with a README that says how to run it and what it shows. Stuck, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/well-rounded-engineer-data/issues/new?title=%5Bdbt%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= DAGS ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-dags" data-name="Airflow DAGs">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Airflow DAGs</h2><p>Airflow 3 syntax (Task SDK), with 2.x differences noted. Cloud Composer runs either; check its image version first.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card"><h3>TaskFlow DAG (Airflow 3)</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>from datetime import datetime, timedelta
from airflow.sdk import dag, task

@dag(
    schedule="15 6 * * *",          # cron, "@daily", timedelta, Asset, None
    start_date=datetime(2025, 1, 1),
    catchup=False,                  # default False in 3.x (True in 2.x)
    max_active_runs=1,
    default_args={"retries": 2, "retry_delay": timedelta(minutes=5)},
    tags=["orders"],
)
def orders_daily():

    @task
    def list_new(ds=None) -&gt; list[str]:
        return find_objects(since=ds)

    @task(retries=3)
    def load(uri: str) -&gt; int:
        return load_to_bq(uri)

    @task
    def report(counts: list[int]):
        print(sum(counts))

    report(load.expand(uri=list_new()))   # dynamic task mapping

orders_daily()</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>BigQuery &amp; GCS operators</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>from airflow.providers.google.cloud.operators.bigquery import (
    BigQueryInsertJobOperator, BigQueryCheckOperator)
from airflow.providers.google.cloud.transfers.gcs_to_bigquery import (
    GCSToBigQueryOperator)
from airflow.providers.google.cloud.sensors.gcs import GCSObjectExistenceSensor

wait = GCSObjectExistenceSensor(
    task_id="wait_manifest", bucket="landing",
    object="manifests/{{ ds }}.json",
    deferrable=True, timeout=6 * 3600)

load = GCSToBigQueryOperator(
    task_id="load_parquet", bucket="landing",
    source_objects=["data/year={{ logical_date.year }}/*.parquet"],
    destination_project_dataset_table="proj.raw.orders",
    source_format="PARQUET", write_disposition="WRITE_APPEND")

build = BigQueryInsertJobOperator(
    task_id="build_analytics", location="asia-south1", deferrable=True,
    configuration={"query": {
        "query": "{% include 'sql/orders.sql' %}",
        "useLegacySql": False}})

check = BigQueryCheckOperator(
    task_id="rows_today", use_legacy_sql=False,
    sql="SELECT COUNT(*) &gt; 0 FROM `proj.analytics.orders` WHERE _run_date = '{{ ds }}'")

wait &gt;&gt; load &gt;&gt; build &gt;&gt; check</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Templates &amp; context</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>{{ ds }}                     2025-01-31     (logical date, YYYY-MM-DD)
{{ ds_nodash }}              20250131
{{ logical_date }}           pendulum DateTime
{{ data_interval_start }}    start of the period this run covers (see below)
{{ data_interval_end }}      end (exclusive)
{{ macros.ds_add(ds, -1) }}  2025-01-30
{{ params.window }}          from dag(params={...})
{{ var.value.my_var }}       Airflow Variable
{{ conn.my_conn.host }}      Connection field
{{ run_id }}  {{ dag.dag_id }}  {{ task_instance.try_number }}

# in TaskFlow, ask for context by name
@task
def t(data_interval_start=None, ti=None):
    ti.xcom_push(key="n", value=1)
# or: from airflow.sdk import get_current_context</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">In Airflow 3 a cron string such as <code>"15 6 * * *"</code> or <code>"@daily"</code> uses <code>CronTriggerTimetable</code>: the run for 2025-01-31 starts at 06:15 that day, its logical date is that time, and <code>data_interval_start</code> equals <code>data_interval_end</code>. For a real window, schedule with <code>CronDataIntervalTimetable</code> (each run starts after its interval ends, as cron schedules did in 2.x) or pass <code>interval=</code> to <code>CronTriggerTimetable</code>. Decide which you use before writing "which data" logic, and never use <code>now()</code>. <code>execution_date</code> is removed in Airflow 3: use <code>logical_date</code>.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Running dbt from a DAG</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>from airflow.providers.standard.operators.bash import BashOperator
# (2.x: from airflow.operators.bash import BashOperator)

dbt_build = BashOperator(
    task_id="dbt_build",
    bash_command=(
        "cd /home/airflow/gcs/dags/dbt/analytics &amp;&amp; "
        "dbt build --target prod -s tag:daily "
        "--vars '{run_date: \"{{ ds }}\"}'"),
    env={"DBT_PROFILES_DIR": "/home/airflow/gcs/dags/dbt"},
    append_env=True)

# Or run dbt in its own image, which keeps it off the worker:
# KubernetesPodOperator / Cloud Run job via CloudRunExecuteJobOperator
# Or astronomer-cosmos: one Airflow task per dbt model
from cosmos import DbtDag, ProjectConfig, ProfileConfig</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Branching, trigger rules, task groups</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>from airflow.sdk import task, task_group

@task.branch
def choose(**ctx):
    return "full_load" if ctx["logical_date"].day == 1 else "delta_load"

@task(trigger_rule="none_failed_min_one_success")
def join(): ...

@task(trigger_rule="all_done")   # always runs: cleanup, alerts
def cleanup(): ...

@task.short_circuit
def has_work(n: int) -&gt; bool:
    return n &gt; 0       # False skips everything downstream

@task_group
def per_store(store: str): ...</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Data-aware scheduling (Assets)</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>from airflow.sdk import Asset, dag, task
raw = Asset("bigquery://proj/raw/orders")

@dag(schedule="@daily", start_date=datetime(2025,1,1))
def producer():
    @task(outlets=[raw])
    def load(): ...
    load()

@dag(schedule=[raw], start_date=datetime(2025,1,1))   # runs when raw updates
def consumer():
    @task
    def build(): ...
    build()

producer(); consumer()</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">Airflow 2.4–2.10 call these <code>Dataset</code> (<code>from airflow.datasets import Dataset</code>).</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Reliability checklist</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Idempotent tasks:</b> a rerun of the same interval gives the same result. Use <code>WRITE_TRUNCATE</code> on a partition (<code>table$20250131</code>) or a MERGE, never a blind append.</li>
      <li><b>No heavy work at parse time:</b> top-level code runs on every scheduler parse. No API calls or DB queries outside tasks.</li>
      <li><b>Small XComs:</b> pass URIs and counts, not dataframes.</li>
      <li><b>Deferrable operators and sensors</b> (<code>deferrable=True</code>) free worker slots while waiting.</li>
      <li><b>Pools</b> cap concurrency against a shared resource: <code>pool="bq_slots"</code>.</li>
      <li><b>Alert on failure:</b> <code>on_failure_callback</code> in <code>default_args</code>.</li>
      <li><b>Pin provider versions</b> in your Composer/requirements; operator args change between majors.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>CLI &amp; local testing</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>airflow dags list
airflow dags list-import-errors
airflow dags test orders_daily 2025-01-31          # full run, no scheduler
airflow tasks test orders_daily list_new 2025-01-31   # one task, no state saved
                                                      # (a mapped task also needs --map-index N)
airflow dags backfill orders_daily -s 2025-01-01 -e 2025-01-07   # 2.x
airflow backfill create --dag-id orders_daily \
  --from-date 2025-01-01 --to-date 2025-01-07                    # 3.x
airflow dags trigger orders_daily -c '{"window": 3}'

# pytest: every DAG imports cleanly
from airflow.dag_processing.dagbag import DagBag   # 3.2+; before: airflow.models
def test_dags_load():
    bag = DagBag(dag_folder="dags", include_examples=False)
    assert not bag.import_errors</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Airflow 2 → 3 gotchas</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Authoring imports move to <code>airflow.sdk</code> (<code>dag</code>, <code>task</code>, <code>Asset</code>, <code>Variable</code>).</li>
      <li><code>schedule_interval</code> and <code>timetable</code> args are removed: use <code>schedule</code>.</li>
      <li><code>catchup</code> now defaults to <code>False</code>.</li>
      <li>Context keys <code>execution_date</code>, <code>next_ds</code>, <code>prev_ds</code>, <code>tomorrow_ds</code> are removed.</li>
      <li>Bash, Python and Empty operators live in the <code>apache-airflow-providers-standard</code> package.</li>
      <li>Tasks no longer get direct metadata-DB access; use the REST API or the SDK.</li>
      <li><code>SubDagOperator</code> and SLAs are gone; use task groups and deadline alerts (added in 3.1, marked experimental).</li>
      <li>Run <code>ruff check --select AIR3 dags/</code> to find most of these.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Read a DAG; find and read a failed task's log</li><li>Rerun a task or a date</li><li>Understand logical dates and data intervals</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Write idempotent TaskFlow DAGs with retries and templates</li><li>Use provider operators and deferrable sensors</li><li>Test DAGs locally and in CI</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Design orchestration across many pipelines (assets, pools, SLAs)</li><li>Run Composer or Airflow reliably; plan upgrades</li><li>Decide what belongs in Airflow vs inside the job</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Choose the orchestration platform for the organisation</li><li>Run large Airflow estates: scaling, multi-tenancy, security</li><li>Set standards for scheduling and data-aware workflows</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>Assets and data-aware scheduling</li><li>Dynamic task mapping</li><li>Deferrable operators and triggers</li><li>Airflow 3 Task SDK and execution API</li><li>Alternatives: Cloud Workflows, Dagster, Temporal</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://airflow.apache.org/docs/apache-airflow/stable/index.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Apache Airflow documentation</a><span class="lnote"> Concepts and reference</span></li><li><a href="https://airflow.apache.org/docs/apache-airflow/stable/best-practices.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Airflow best practices</a><span class="lnote"> Idempotency, top-level code, testing</span></li><li><a href="https://airflow.apache.org/docs/apache-airflow-providers-google/stable/index.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google provider for Airflow</a><span class="lnote"> BigQuery, GCS, Dataflow operators</span></li><li><a href="https://cloud.google.com/composer/docs" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cloud Composer</a><span class="lnote"> Managed Airflow on GCP</span></li><li><a href="https://astronomer.github.io/astronomer-cosmos/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Astronomer Cosmos</a><span class="lnote"> Run dbt projects as Airflow tasks</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/apache/airflow/main/RELEASE_NOTES.rst" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Airflow release notes (Airflow 3: catchup_by_default False, create_cron_data_intervals False -> CronTriggerTimetable, schedule_interval/timetable removed, SubDAGs and SLAs removed, providers-standard)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/apache/airflow/main/airflow-core/docs/installation/upgrading_to_airflow3.rst" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Upgrading to Airflow 3 (removed context keys tomorrow_ds/prev_ds/next_ds, no direct metadata DB access, ruff AIR301/302/311/312)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/apache/airflow/main/airflow-core/src/airflow/models/__init__.py" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Airflow models/__init__ (DagBag now from airflow.dag_processing.dagbag; absent before 3.2)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/apache/airflow/main/airflow-core/docs/templates-ref.rst" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Airflow templates reference (ds, ds_nodash, logical_date, var.value, conn)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/apache/airflow/main/airflow-core/src/airflow/cli/cli_config.py" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Airflow CLI config (backfill create --dag-id/--from-date/--to-date, list-import-errors, --map-index)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/apache/airflow/main/airflow-core/src/airflow/timetables/trigger.py" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Airflow trigger timetables (CronTriggerTimetable interval= parameter)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/apache/airflow/main/task-sdk/src/airflow/sdk/execution_time/macros.py" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Task SDK macros (ds_add)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/apache/airflow/main/providers/google/src/airflow/providers/google/cloud/sensors/gcs.py" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google provider GCS sensor (object, deferrable)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/apache/airflow/main/providers/google/src/airflow/providers/google/cloud/operators/bigquery.py" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google provider BigQuery operators (BigQueryCheckOperator use_legacy_sql, BigQueryInsertJobOperator)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/apache/airflow/main/providers/google/src/airflow/providers/google/cloud/transfers/gcs_to_bigquery.py" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google provider GCSToBigQueryOperator</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/apache/airflow/main/providers/google/src/airflow/providers/google/cloud/operators/cloud_run.py" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google provider CloudRunExecuteJobOperator</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/astronomer/astronomer-cosmos/main/dev/dags/basic_cosmos_dag.py" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Astronomer Cosmos example DAG (imports)</a>. A few claims could not be confirmed at their official source from here and are being re-checked.</p><p class="runcode"><b>Run the code:</b> <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/well-rounded-engineer-data/tree/main/airflow" target="_blank" rel="noopener">well-rounded-engineer-data/airflow</a>, with a README that says how to run it and what it shows. Stuck, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/well-rounded-engineer-data/issues/new?title=%5BAirflow%20DAGs%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= SOLID AND DESIGN PATTERNS ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-patterns" data-name="SOLID &amp; Design Patterns">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>SOLID principles and design patterns</h2><p>Architecture decides how systems fit together; these decide how one codebase stays easy to change. SOLID is five principles for classes and modules. Design patterns are named, reusable answers to problems that keep coming back. Both give a team a shared vocabulary, and both can be overdone. Not to be confused with <a href="#solid">Solid</a>, the personal data pods.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>The five principles</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th></th><th>Principle</th><th>What it says</th><th>The smell it fixes (<i>analysis</i>)</th><th>Overdone, it becomes (<i>analysis</i>)</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td><b>S</b></td><td>Single responsibility</td><td>A class should have only one reason to change</td><td>One class that prices, saves and emails; every change risks the other two</td><td>Dozens of one-method classes that are hard to follow</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>O</b></td><td>Open&ndash;closed</td><td>Software entities should be open for extension but closed for modification: behaviour can be extended without editing their source</td><td>A growing <code>if</code>/<code>switch</code> that every new case edits</td><td>Extension points for variations that never come</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>L</b></td><td>Liskov substitution</td><td>A property provable about objects of a type should hold for objects of any of its subtypes</td><td>A subclass that throws, or quietly changes behaviour, where the parent worked</td><td>Rarely overdone; the usual fix is less inheritance</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>I</b></td><td>Interface segregation</td><td>No code should be forced to depend on methods it does not use</td><td>A huge interface that every implementer half-fills with stubs</td><td>Interfaces so small that a simple idea is spread across many</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>D</b></td><td>Dependency inversion</td><td>High-level modules should not depend on low-level ones; both depend on abstractions, and abstractions do not depend on details</td><td>Business logic that cannot be tested without a real database</td><td>An interface for every class, each with one implementation forever</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note">Where they come from: Robert C. Martin introduced the principles in his 2000 paper <i>Design Principles and Design Patterns</i>; Michael Feathers coined the acronym SOLID around 2004. The open&ndash;closed principle is Bertrand Meyer&rsquo;s, and the substitution principle is Barbara Liskov&rsquo;s, from 1987, formalised with Jeannette Wing in 1994 (see <a href="#history">A brief history</a>).</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Four of them in one small example</h3>
<pre><code>// Before: one class prices, applies every rule and saves.
// A new discount means editing this method again.
class OrderService {
  long totalPence(Order o) {
    long t = o.subtotalPence();
    if (o.customer().isStaff()) t = t * 80 / 100;
    if (o.coupon() != null)     t = t - o.coupon().valuePence();
    repository.save(o);
    return t;
  }
}

// After: a new rule is a new class, not an edit (O).
interface Discount {                          // one method; nothing to stub (I)
  long apply(Order o, long totalPence);
}
record StaffDiscount() implements Discount {
  public long apply(Order o, long t) { return o.customer().isStaff() ? t * 80 / 100 : t; }
}
record CouponDiscount() implements Discount {
  public long apply(Order o, long t) { return o.coupon() == null ? t : Math.max(0, t - o.coupon().valuePence()); }
}

class Pricing {                               // pricing only; saving lives elsewhere (S)
  private final List&lt;Discount&gt; discounts;    // depends on an abstraction, not on concrete rules (D)
  Pricing(List&lt;Discount&gt; discounts) { this.discounts = discounts; }

  long totalPence(Order o) {
    long t = o.subtotalPence();
    for (Discount d : discounts) t = d.apply(o, t);
    return t;
  }
}</code></pre>
    <p class="note">Money is kept in integer pence, as on <a href="#payments">Payment Gateways</a>. <code>Pricing</code> no longer saves, so it can be tested with no database (S), and with a list of fake discounts (O, D). The order the discounts run in is now the order of the list, and it changes the answer: staff then coupon turns 1000 into 700, coupon then staff into 720. The &ldquo;before&rdquo; version fixed that order in code; now it is a decision to make, and test, on purpose.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Liskov, the classic trap</h3>
    <p><i>A classic textbook example.</i> A square is a rectangle in geometry, so it is tempting to make <code>Square extends Rectangle</code>. But code that sets a rectangle&rsquo;s width and then its height expects the area to be width &times; height. A square that keeps its sides equal breaks that expectation, so it cannot stand in for a rectangle: the subtype fails the property the caller relied on.</p>
    <p class="note"><i>Analysis:</i> the usual way out is fewer inheritance links, not cleverer ones: make both immutable values, or give them a shared interface with no setters.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Two rules from the Gang of Four</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>&ldquo;Program to an interface, not an implementation.&rdquo;</b></li>
      <li><b>&ldquo;Favor object composition over class inheritance.&rdquo;</b></li>
    </ul>
    <p>Both are from <i>Design Patterns</i> (Gamma, Helm, Johnson and Vlissides). Dependency injection is a common way to meet them: an object receives what it depends on rather than creating it, often from a framework container. Martin Fowler&rsquo;s article of January 2004 described it, and settled on the name after discussion with other advocates of inversion of control.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Patterns by the problem they solve</h3>
    <p><i>Design Patterns</i> describes 23 patterns in three groups: creational, structural and behavioural. Below is a selection of the 23. The last column is <i>analysis</i>.</p>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>If your problem is&hellip;</th><th>Pattern (group)</th><th>In modern Java or Kotlin</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Choosing an algorithm at runtime</td><td>Strategy (behavioural)</td><td>Often just a functional interface and a lambda</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Telling many objects when one changes</td><td>Observer (behavioural)</td><td>Listeners, events and reactive streams (see <a href="#javascript">JavaScript</a> for the same idea in the browser)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>A fixed sequence of steps where some steps vary</td><td>Template method (behavioural)</td><td>An abstract class with hooks, or a method that takes the varying steps as functions</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Requests as objects you can queue, log or undo</td><td>Command (behavioural)</td><td>A record per command, handled in one place</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Behaviour that depends on the object&rsquo;s state</td><td>State (behavioural)</td><td>A sealed type per state and a <code>switch</code> over it</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Walking a collection without exposing its structure</td><td>Iterator (behavioural)</td><td>Built in: <code>Iterable</code> and the for-each loop</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Adding behaviour around an object without subclassing</td><td>Decorator (structural)</td><td>Java&rsquo;s I/O streams are built this way</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Making an interface you cannot change fit the one you need</td><td>Adapter (structural)</td><td>A small wrapper class at the edge of your code</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Offering one simple entry point to a complicated subsystem</td><td>Facade (structural)</td><td>A service class in front of several clients</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Standing in for another object (access checks, remote calls, added behaviour)</td><td>Proxy (structural)</td><td>Generated by frameworks more often than written by hand</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Treating a tree of parts the same way as a single part</td><td>Composite (structural)</td><td>Menus, file trees, expression trees</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Building an object with many optional parts</td><td>Builder (creational)</td><td>Kotlin&rsquo;s named and default arguments, or a builder for Java</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Letting a subclass or caller decide which class to create</td><td>Factory method (creational)</td><td>A static factory method, or a function that returns an implementation</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Exactly one instance of something</td><td>Singleton (creational)</td><td>Some consider it an anti-pattern: global state that hides dependencies and makes unit testing harder. One instance managed by a dependency-injection container avoids both</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>When principles and patterns hurt</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Presumptive features:</b> Fowler&rsquo;s word for code built for a feature that is not needed yet. YAGNI, &ldquo;you aren&rsquo;t gonna need it&rdquo;, is the counter.</li>
      <li><b>Kent Beck&rsquo;s rules of simple design,</b> as Fowler states them, in priority order: passes the tests, reveals intention, no duplication, fewest elements.</li>
      <li><b>Anti-patterns:</b> common but counterproductive solutions; Andrew Koenig coined the term in 1995, inspired by <i>Design Patterns</i>.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Our view, and the other view</h3>
    <p><i>Our view:</i> learn the principles and the common patterns well enough to name them in a review, then use one when its problem is in front of you, not to make code look professional. Refactor into a pattern when the second or third variation arrives.</p>
    <p><i>The other view:</i> in a large codebase shared by many teams, agreeing on patterns early keeps code consistent and saves later rewrites. And in functional styles, many patterns become plain functions and modules, so some argue SOLID matters less outside object-oriented code.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Test yourself</h3>
    <details><summary>A class formats invoices and also sends them by email. Which principle does that strain?</summary><p>Single responsibility: it has two reasons to change, the invoice layout and the way it is delivered.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Every new payment method means editing the same <code>switch</code>. Which principle, and which pattern helps?</summary><p>Open&ndash;closed; the Strategy pattern, with one implementation per payment method.</p></details>
    <details><summary>A <code>ReadOnlyList</code> extends <code>List</code> and throws on <code>add</code>. What is wrong?</summary><p>It breaks Liskov substitution: code written for <code>List</code> that calls <code>add</code> fails when handed the subtype.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Your business logic news up a database client directly. Which principle would let you test it without the database?</summary><p>Dependency inversion: depend on an interface, and pass in a real client in production and a fake in tests.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Which pattern are Java&rsquo;s I/O streams built on?</summary><p>Decorator: each stream wraps another and adds behaviour, such as buffering.</p></details>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Name the five SOLID principles and the smell each one fixes</li><li>Recognise Strategy, Observer, Decorator, Adapter and Builder in code you read</li><li>Explain why a subclass must keep its parent's promises</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Refactor a growing switch into a strategy or a sealed type</li><li>Inject dependencies so logic is testable without infrastructure</li><li>Name a pattern in a code review, and say why it fits</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Decide when a pattern earns its place and when it is speculative</li><li>Design module boundaries with dependency inversion</li><li>Keep an inheritance hierarchy honest, or replace it with composition</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Set design standards a whole organisation can review against</li><li>Teach patterns as answers to problems, not as goals</li><li>Judge which principles change shape in functional code</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>Single responsibility and open-closed</li><li>Liskov substitution and interface segregation</li><li>Dependency inversion and injection</li><li>The Gang of Four patterns</li><li>YAGNI and simple design</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://martinfowler.com/articles/injection.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Fowler: Inversion of Control Containers and the Dependency Injection pattern</a><span class="lnote"> Where the name comes from</span></li><li><a href="https://martinfowler.com/bliki/Yagni.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Fowler: Yagni</a><span class="lnote"> Presumptive features</span></li><li><a href="https://martinfowler.com/bliki/BeckDesignRules.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Fowler: Beck Design Rules</a><span class="lnote"> Four rules of simple design</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SOLID" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: SOLID</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Single-responsibility_principle" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Single-responsibility principle</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Open%E2%80%93closed_principle" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Open–closed principle</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liskov_substitution_principle" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Liskov substitution principle</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Interface_segregation_principle" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Interface segregation principle</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dependency_inversion_principle" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Dependency inversion principle</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Design_Patterns" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Design Patterns</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Strategy_pattern" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Strategy pattern</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Observer_pattern" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Observer pattern</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Decorator_pattern" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Decorator pattern</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Singleton_pattern" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Singleton pattern</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Template_method_pattern" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Template method pattern</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Proxy_pattern" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Proxy pattern</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dependency_injection" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Dependency injection</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anti-pattern" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Anti-pattern</a>; <a href="https://martinfowler.com/articles/injection.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Fowler: Inversion of Control Containers and the Dependency Injection pattern (2004)</a>; <a href="https://martinfowler.com/bliki/Yagni.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Fowler: Yagni</a>; <a href="https://martinfowler.com/bliki/BeckDesignRules.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Fowler: Beck Design Rules</a>.</p><p class="runcode">A question, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/enrichmeai.github.io/issues/new?title=%5BSOLID%20%26%20Design%20Patterns%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>. Nothing here is tracked and there are no comments to store: questions live in the open, where the next learner can find the answer.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<section class="panel" id="p-architecture" data-name="Architecture">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Architecture patterns</h2><p>Event-driven systems, choreography vs orchestration, sagas, the outbox, and a full catalogue of architectural, data, cloud and integration patterns, each with when to use it and what it costs. Ends with how to choose a style and record the decision.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Event-driven systems</h3>
    <p>Services communicate by publishing <b>events</b> (facts about something that happened: <code>OrderPlaced</code>, <code>OrderPaid</code>) to a broker. Other services subscribe and react. The producer does not know or wait for its consumers.</p>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Event</td><td>Something that happened, past tense, immutable. Many consumers.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Command</td><td>A request to do something (<code>LoadPartition</code>). One handler, may be refused.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Broker</td><td>Pub/Sub, Kafka, Eventarc: stores and delivers messages.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Topic / subscription</td><td>Where events are published / one consumer group's view of them.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>At-least-once</td><td>The usual guarantee: a message may arrive twice. Consumers must be idempotent.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Ordering</td><td>Usually not global. Use an ordering key (per order, per entity) when sequence matters.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Dead-letter queue</td><td>Where messages go after repeated failures, so one bad message does not block the rest.</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p><b>Gains:</b> loose coupling, independent scaling, new consumers added without touching producers. <b>Costs:</b> eventual consistency, harder debugging (no single call stack), duplicates and reordering to handle, schema changes that ripple to unknown consumers.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Choreography vs orchestration</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th></th><th>Choreography</th><th>Orchestration</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>How</td><td>Each service reacts to events and emits its own. No one is in charge.</td><td>A central orchestrator tells each service what to do next and tracks state.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Analogy</td><td>Dancers who each know their cue</td><td>A conductor with the score</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Good for</td><td>Few steps, independent teams, fan-out reactions ("on landing, also index and notify")</td><td>Many steps, strict order, retries, compensation, a need to answer "where is this run?"</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Risk</td><td>The overall flow exists only in people's heads; cycles and lost steps are hard to see</td><td>The orchestrator is a central dependency and can grow into a god service</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Tools</td><td>Pub/Sub, Kafka, Eventarc</td><td>Airflow, Cloud Workflows, Temporal, Step Functions, a custom orchestrator with a ledger</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note">Many systems mix them: orchestration inside a bounded process (a data pipeline's stages), choreography between domains (the pipeline publishes "ready"; the search team reacts).</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Saga: transactions across services</h3>
    <p>A distributed transaction across several databases cannot use one ACID commit. A <b>saga</b> splits it into local transactions, each with a <b>compensating action</b> that undoes it if a later step fails.</p>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>Book a paid subscription:
  1. Billing:  create pending invoice        ↺ compensate: void invoice
  2. Payments: charge card                   ↺ compensate: refund charge
  3. Accounts: activate plan                 ↺ compensate: deactivate plan
  4. Email:    send welcome                  (no compensation: an email cannot be undone, so it runs last)

Step 3 fails → run compensations in reverse: refund (2), void invoice (1).</code></pre></div>
    <figure class="fig"><svg viewBox="0 0 640 400" role="img" aria-labelledby="saga-t saga-d"><title id="saga-t">An orchestrated saga that fails and compensates</title><desc id="saga-d">A UML sequence diagram with four lifelines: Orchestrator, Billing, Payments and Accounts. The orchestrator asks Billing to create a pending invoice, then Payments to charge the card, and both succeed. It asks Accounts to activate the plan, which fails. It then runs compensations in reverse order: Payments refunds the charge, and Billing voids the invoice.</desc><defs><marker id="saga-a" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker><marker id="saga-aa" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar acc" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker></defs><rect class="nd in" x="16" y="14" width="128" height="36" rx="4"/><text class="lb hd" x="80" y="37" text-anchor="middle">Orchestrator</text><line class="ln" x1="80" y1="50" x2="80" y2="390"/><rect class="nd" x="176" y="14" width="128" height="36" rx="4"/><text class="lb hd" x="240" y="37" text-anchor="middle">Billing</text><line class="ln" x1="240" y1="50" x2="240" y2="390"/><rect class="nd" x="336" y="14" width="128" height="36" rx="4"/><text class="lb hd" x="400" y="37" text-anchor="middle">Payments</text><line class="ln" x1="400" y1="50" x2="400" y2="390"/><rect class="nd" x="496" y="14" width="128" height="36" rx="4"/><text class="lb hd" x="560" y="37" text-anchor="middle">Accounts</text><line class="ln" x1="560" y1="50" x2="560" y2="390"/><line class="ed" x1="80" y1="80" x2="236" y2="80" marker-end="url(#saga-a)"/><text class="lb" x="92" y="74" text-anchor="start">1. create pending invoice</text><line class="ed dash" x1="240" y1="102" x2="84" y2="102" marker-end="url(#saga-a)"/><text class="lb mu" x="228" y="118" text-anchor="end">ok</text><line class="ed" x1="80" y1="146" x2="396" y2="146" marker-end="url(#saga-a)"/><text class="lb" x="92" y="140" text-anchor="start">2. charge card</text><line class="ed dash" x1="400" y1="168" x2="84" y2="168" marker-end="url(#saga-a)"/><text class="lb mu" x="388" y="184" text-anchor="end">ok</text><line class="ed" x1="80" y1="212" x2="556" y2="212" marker-end="url(#saga-a)"/><text class="lb" x="92" y="206" text-anchor="start">3. activate plan</text><line class="ed dash" x1="560" y1="234" x2="84" y2="234" marker-end="url(#saga-a)"/><text class="lb acc" x="548" y="250" text-anchor="end">failed</text><text class="lb acc" x="88" y="280" text-anchor="start">compensate, in reverse:</text><line class="ed acc" x1="80" y1="318" x2="396" y2="318" marker-end="url(#saga-aa)"/><text class="lb acc" x="92" y="312" text-anchor="start">refund charge</text><line class="ed acc" x1="80" y1="352" x2="236" y2="352" marker-end="url(#saga-aa)"/><text class="lb acc" x="92" y="346" text-anchor="start">void invoice</text></svg><figcaption>The subscription saga above as a UML sequence diagram: solid arrows are requests, dashed arrows are replies. When step 3 fails, the orchestrator runs the compensations for steps 2 and 1, in reverse order.</figcaption></figure>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Orchestrated saga:</b> a coordinator runs the steps and the compensations, and stores the saga's state. Easier to follow and test.</li>
      <li><b>Choreographed saga:</b> each service listens for the previous step's event and emits its own, including failure events that trigger compensation.</li>
      <li><b>Compensation is a new action, not a rollback:</b> a refund is not "un-charging". Some steps (an email sent) cannot be undone, so put them last.</li>
      <li><b>Every step and compensation must be idempotent,</b> because messages repeat.</li>
      <li><b>Expose intermediate states</b> (<code>PENDING</code>, <code>ACTIVE</code>, <code>CANCELLED</code>): other readers will see them.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Transactional outbox</h3>
    <p>Problem: you update your database <i>and</i> publish an event. If one succeeds and the other fails, they disagree.</p>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>BEGIN;
  UPDATE orders SET status = 'PAID' WHERE id = 42;
  INSERT INTO outbox (id, topic, payload, created_at)
  VALUES (gen_random_uuid(), 'order.paid', '{"order_id":42}', now());
COMMIT;

-- relay process (or CDC on the outbox table)
SELECT * FROM outbox WHERE published_at IS NULL
ORDER BY created_at LIMIT 100 FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED;
-- publish each, then
UPDATE outbox SET published_at = now() WHERE id = ANY($1);</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">One local transaction makes the state change and the intent to publish atomic. The relay may publish twice; consumers dedupe by event ID.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Idempotent consumers</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>-- remember what you processed, in the same transaction as the effect
CREATE TABLE processed_events (event_id uuid PRIMARY KEY, processed_at timestamptz);

BEGIN;
  INSERT INTO processed_events VALUES ($1, now())   -- $1: the event's ID
  ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
  -- if 0 rows inserted: duplicate, skip the effect
  UPDATE accounts SET plan = 'pro' WHERE id = $2;   -- $2: the account
COMMIT;</code></pre></div>
    <ul>
      <li>Prefer naturally idempotent operations: <code>SET status = 'PAID'</code> rather than <code>balance = balance + 10</code>.</li>
      <li>APIs take an <b>idempotency key</b> header so a retried POST does not charge twice.</li>
      <li>Batch jobs overwrite a partition or MERGE on a key.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>CQRS &amp; event sourcing</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>CQRS</b> (command query responsibility segregation): writes go to a model shaped for updates; reads come from separate models shaped for queries (a search index, a denormalised table). Reads are eventually consistent.</li>
      <li><b>Event sourcing:</b> store the sequence of events as the source of truth; current state is derived by replaying them. Full audit history and time travel, at the cost of complexity, schema versioning of old events, and snapshots for speed.</li>
      <li><b>Append-only ledger</b> is the lightweight version many data platforms use: one row per state change, current state from a "latest event" view. Never UPDATE, never DELETE.</li>
      <li>Use them where history and audit are the product. Most CRUD services do not need either.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Common architectural patterns</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Pattern</th><th>What it is</th><th>Use when / watch out</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Layered (n-tier)</td><td>Controller → service → repository → database</td><td>Default for a service. Keep controllers thin and rules in services.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Hexagonal (ports &amp; adapters)</td><td>Domain logic in the centre; databases, APIs and queues are adapters behind interfaces</td><td>Logic you want to test without infrastructure, or swap providers.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Modular monolith</td><td>One deployable with enforced module boundaries</td><td><i>Our view:</i> a sensible starting point for many teams; split later along boundaries that proved real. <i>The other view:</i> a team that already meets the readiness checklist on <a href="#microservices">Microservices</a> (automated deploys, central logs and tracing, stable boundaries) can start with separate services and skip the later split.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Microservices</td><td>Independently deployed services owning their data</td><td>Many teams, different scaling needs. Costs: network failures, distributed data, ops overhead.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>API gateway</td><td>Single entry point: routing, auth, rate limits</td><td>Many backends behind one public surface.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Backend for frontend (BFF)</td><td>A thin API per client type (web, mobile)</td><td>Clients with very different data needs.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Strangler fig</td><td>Route features one by one from the old system to the new</td><td>Replacing a legacy system without a big-bang rewrite.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Cache-aside</td><td>Read the cache; on a miss read the DB and fill the cache</td><td>Read-heavy data that tolerates brief staleness. Decide invalidation up front.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Circuit breaker</td><td>Stop calling a failing dependency for a while; fail fast</td><td>Prevents one slow service taking down its callers.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Retry with backoff + jitter</td><td>Retry transient failures with growing, randomised waits</td><td>Only for idempotent calls. Cap attempts; never retry 4xx validation errors.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Bulkhead</td><td>Separate pools for separate dependencies</td><td>One slow dependency cannot exhaust all threads or connections.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Queue-based load levelling</td><td>Put work on a queue; workers drain it at their pace</td><td>Spiky load in front of a resource with fixed capacity.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Sidecar</td><td>A helper container next to the app (proxy, log shipper)</td><td>Cross-cutting concerns in Kubernetes and service meshes.</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Consistency &amp; trade-offs</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>CAP:</b> during a network partition a distributed store chooses consistency (refuse some requests) or availability (answer, possibly stale).</li>
      <li><b>PACELC:</b> and when there is no partition, it trades latency against consistency.</li>
      <li><b>Strong consistency:</b> every read sees the latest write. <b>Eventual:</b> replicas converge, reads may lag. <b>Read-your-writes:</b> a user always sees their own changes.</li>
      <li><b>Exactly-once</b> delivery is rarely real end to end; build at-least-once delivery plus idempotent processing.</li>
      <li><b>Fallacies of distributed computing:</b> the network is not reliable, latency is not zero, bandwidth is not infinite, and the topology changes.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">The theory behind these, from clocks to consensus, is on <a href="#distsys">Distributed Systems</a>.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Design review checklist</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Who owns each piece of data, and who may write it?</li>
      <li>What happens when each dependency is slow, down, or returns garbage?</li>
      <li>Which operations must be atomic, and how is that achieved across boundaries?</li>
      <li>What is retried, and is it idempotent?</li>
      <li>How do we see a request or run end to end (IDs, traces, ledger)?</li>
      <li>What is the expected load, and what breaks first at 10×?</li>
      <li>How do we deploy, migrate and roll back without downtime?</li>
      <li>What is the simplest design that meets today's need?</li>
    </ul>
  </article>








  <article class="card wide"><h3>Pattern catalogue: architectural styles</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Style</th><th>Shape</th><th>Use when</th><th>Costs</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Layered (n-tier)</td><td>Presentation → business → persistence</td><td>Most CRUD services; easy to understand</td><td>Logic leaks across layers; changes cut through every layer</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Hexagonal / ports and adapters</td><td>Domain core; databases, queues, APIs plug in through interfaces</td><td>Logic worth testing without infrastructure; swappable providers</td><td>More interfaces and mapping code</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Clean / onion</td><td>Concentric rings; dependencies point inward to the domain</td><td>Long-lived, complex domains</td><td>Ceremony for simple services</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Modular monolith</td><td>One deployable, enforced module boundaries</td><td>Default starting point for most products</td><td>Needs discipline and tooling to keep boundaries</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Microservices</td><td>Independently deployed services owning data</td><td>Many teams, different scaling or release needs (<a href="#microservices">tab</a>)</td><td>Distributed-systems complexity</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Service-oriented (SOA)</td><td>Shared enterprise services, often via an ESB</td><td>Integrating large legacy estates</td><td>Central bus becomes a bottleneck</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Event-driven</td><td>Producers emit events; consumers react</td><td>Decoupled reactions, fan-out, integration across domains</td><td>Eventual consistency; harder to trace</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Microkernel / plug-in</td><td>Small core plus plug-ins</td><td>Products extended by customers or partners (IDEs, rule engines)</td><td>Plug-in contract must stay stable</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Pipes and filters</td><td>Data flows through independent processing steps</td><td>Data pipelines, ETL, media processing</td><td>Error handling across steps; overhead between them</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Space-based</td><td>Processing units with replicated in-memory data grids</td><td>Extreme, spiky load with low latency</td><td>Complex; data consistency is hard</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Serverless</td><td>Functions and managed services, scale to zero</td><td>Spiky or low traffic, event handlers, small teams</td><td>Cold starts, limits, vendor coupling</td></tr>
      <tr><td>CQRS</td><td>Separate write and read models</td><td>Reads and writes with very different shapes or scale</td><td>Two models; eventual consistency</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Event sourcing</td><td>Events as the source of truth</td><td>Audit and history are the product (<a href="#eventsourcing">tab</a>)</td><td>Event versioning, replay, learning curve</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Pattern catalogue: data architecture</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Pattern</th><th>Idea</th><th>Use when</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Layered / medallion (raw → foundation → consumable)</td><td>Each layer has one job; derivation runs one way</td><td>Any analytics platform (<a href="#data">Data Engineering</a>)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Lambda architecture</td><td>A batch layer for accuracy plus a speed layer for freshness, merged at query time</td><td>Legacy; most teams now prefer kappa or streaming warehouses</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Kappa architecture</td><td>One streaming path; reprocess by replaying the log</td><td>Event streams with replayable history (Kafka)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Lakehouse</td><td>Warehouse tables over open files in object storage</td><td>Several engines sharing the same data</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Data mesh</td><td>Domains own and publish data products; a platform supports them</td><td>Large organisations where a central team is the bottleneck</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Database per service</td><td>Each service owns its schema</td><td>Microservices</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Change data capture</td><td>Stream a database's changes to other systems</td><td>Replication to warehouses, caches, search</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Materialised view / read model</td><td>Precomputed query results kept up to date</td><td>Expensive queries read often</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Claim check</td><td>Store a large payload in object storage; send only a reference in the message</td><td>Messages larger than the broker's limit</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Pattern catalogue: cloud and integration</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Pattern</th><th>Idea</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Anti-corruption layer</td><td>A translation layer that keeps a legacy or external model from leaking into your domain</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Gateway routing / aggregation / offloading</td><td>One entry point routes requests, combines backend calls, or handles TLS, auth and rate limits</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Backend for frontend</td><td>An API tailored to one client type</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Strangler fig</td><td>Replace a system piece by piece behind a router</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Competing consumers</td><td>Several workers read one queue to share load</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Priority queue</td><td>Separate queues so urgent work is not stuck behind bulk work</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Queue-based load levelling</td><td>A queue absorbs spikes in front of a fixed-capacity service</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Throttling / rate limiting</td><td>Cap use per client to protect the system</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Valet key</td><td>Give clients a short-lived signed URL to upload or download directly from storage</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Static content hosting</td><td>Serve static files from object storage and a CDN</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Leader election</td><td>One instance coordinates; others take over if it fails</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Scheduler agent supervisor</td><td>Coordinate steps, watch them, retry or compensate failures</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Health endpoint monitoring</td><td>Endpoints that report liveness and readiness</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Sidecar / ambassador / adapter</td><td>Helper containers beside the app for proxying, telemetry or protocol translation</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Compensating transaction</td><td>Undo completed steps when a later one fails (sagas)</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Choosing a style</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>List the quality attributes that matter most, in order: e.g. correctness and auditability, then cost, then latency.</li>
      <li>Note the constraints: team size and skills, budget, regulations, existing systems.</li>
      <li>Pick the simplest style that satisfies the top attributes; resist adding patterns "for later".</li>
      <li>Identify the riskiest assumption and test it with a spike or prototype.</li>
      <li>Record the decision and its trade-offs in an ADR.</li>
    </ol>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Architecture decision record</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># ADR 0042: Serve order search from Postgres, not a search engine
Status:   Accepted (2026-11-12)
Context:  20 M orders, ~60 searches/s peak, p95 &lt; 500 ms;
          team already runs Postgres; no search-engine skills.
Decision: Postgres full-text (GIN) + B-tree filters on a read replica;
          revisit if p95 &gt; 500 ms or relevance complaints exceed X/month.
Alternatives: Elasticsearch (more ops, better relevance);
              BigQuery (latency too high for interactive search).
Consequences: + one database to run  − relevance tuning limited
Links:    load test results, issue #…</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">One decision per record, numbered, never edited after acceptance; supersede it with a new one. Keep them in the repo.</p>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Explain layered and hexagonal architecture</li><li>Tell events from commands; choreography from orchestration</li><li>Recognise retries that need idempotency</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Implement outbox, idempotent consumers and retries with backoff</li><li>Design a saga with compensations</li><li>Apply circuit breakers, bulkheads and timeouts</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Choose monolith, modular monolith or services for a context</li><li>Design event-driven systems with contracts and evolution</li><li>Run design reviews and write decision records</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Set architecture direction across many teams</li><li>Balance consistency, availability, cost and team structure</li><li>Lead large migrations (strangler fig) safely</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>Domain-driven design: bounded contexts</li><li>Architecture decision records (ADRs)</li><li>Event schema evolution and registries</li><li>CQRS and event sourcing in practice</li><li>Team topologies and Conway's law</li><li>Architecture characteristics and fitness functions</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://microservices.io/patterns/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">microservices.io patterns</a><span class="lnote"> Saga, outbox, CQRS and more, with trade-offs</span></li><li><a href="https://martinfowler.com/architecture/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Martin Fowler: architecture</a><span class="lnote"> Essays on patterns and evolution</span></li><li><a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/patterns/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Azure cloud design patterns</a><span class="lnote"> Vendor-neutral pattern catalogue</span></li><li><a href="https://www.enterpriseintegrationpatterns.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Enterprise Integration Patterns</a><span class="lnote"> Messaging patterns</span></li><li><a href="https://cloud.google.com/architecture" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google Cloud Architecture Center</a><span class="lnote"> Reference architectures on GCP</span></li><li><a href="https://martinfowler.com/articles/patterns-of-distributed-systems/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Patterns of Distributed Systems (Unmesh Joshi)</a><span class="lnote"> Leader election, write-ahead log, quorum and more</span></li><li><a href="https://adr.github.io/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Architecture decision records</a><span class="lnote"> Templates and tools for ADRs</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CAP_theorem?action=raw" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: CAP theorem</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PACELC_design_principle?action=raw" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: PACELC</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fallacies_of_distributed_computing?action=raw" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Fallacies of distributed computing</a>; <a href="https://martinfowler.com/bliki/CQRS.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Fowler: CQRS</a>; <a href="https://martinfowler.com/eaaDev/EventSourcing.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Fowler: Event Sourcing</a>; <a href="https://martinfowler.com/bliki/StranglerFigApplication.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Fowler: Strangler Fig</a>; <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/functions-uuid.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PostgreSQL: gen_random_uuid</a>; <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/sql-select.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PostgreSQL: SELECT ... FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED</a>; <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/sql-prepare.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PostgreSQL PREPARE (positional parameters)</a>. A few claims could not be confirmed at their official source from here and are being re-checked.</p><p class="runcode"><b>Run the code:</b> <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/well-rounded-engineer-code/tree/main/patterns" target="_blank" rel="noopener">well-rounded-engineer-code/patterns</a>, with a README that says how to run it and what it shows. Stuck, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/well-rounded-engineer-code/issues/new?title=%5BArchitecture%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= QUALITY ATTRIBUTES ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-quality" data-name="Quality Attributes">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Quality attributes</h2><p>Features say what a system does; qualities say how well, how safely and for whom. They shape the architecture more than features do, and they are cheapest to get right at the start. How to plan them early, write each one as a test, design security in rather than bolt it on, and run vulnerabilities as a loop.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>The short answer</h3>
    <p>Two systems can have identical features and be completely different to live with: one is slow, leaks data and nobody dares change it; the other is quick, safe and easy to extend. The difference is in its <b>quality attributes</b>, also called non-functional requirements: security, performance, accessibility, observability, resilience, maintainability, privacy, cost.</p>
    <p>Two habits make them real. <b>Plan them early</b>, while they are still choices rather than rework. And <b>write each one as an acceptance criterion</b> with a number and a check, so that it can pass or fail like any feature. <i>Our view:</i> a quality that has no number and no check is a wish, and wishes are the first thing cut when a deadline comes. <i>The other view</i> is at the end of the page.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Plan early</h3>
    <p>Some qualities are cheap to decide on day one and expensive to add later, because they shape the structure everything else is built on:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Where data may live</b>, and how long it is kept: it decides regions, backups and which services may hold personal data (see <a href="#privacy">Personal Data Across Services</a>).</li>
      <li><b>Who may do what</b>: the identity and permission model runs through every endpoint and table.</li>
      <li><b>What you can see</b>: request and trace IDs, structured logs and an audit trail are easy to add to a new service and painful to retrofit (see <a href="#observability">Observability</a>).</li>
      <li><b>Accessibility</b>: built into the design system, it is nearly free; added to fifty finished screens, it is a project (see <a href="#accessibility">Accessibility</a>).</li>
      <li><b>How it fails</b>: timeouts, retries and idempotency are design decisions, not patches (see <a href="#microservices">Microservices</a>).</li>
    </ul>
    <p><b>In the first week:</b> list the qualities that matter, rank them for this system (they compete: see below), write each as a scenario with a number, and decide how each will be checked. The ranking is a decision; record it in an architecture decision record (see <a href="#architecture">Architecture</a>).</p>
    <figure class="fig"><svg viewBox="0 0 660 210" role="img" aria-labelledby="qawish-t qawish-d"><title id="qawish-t">From a wish to a test</title><desc id="qawish-d">Five steps left to right. A wish such as 'search should be fast'. A scenario that says who does what, under which conditions: 200 searches a second at peak. A criterion with a number: 95% of searches answer in under 300 ms. A check that runs automatically, a load test in CI with that threshold. A signal in production, a latency objective and an alert. Below, the note that a quality you cannot measure is still a wish.</desc><defs><marker id="qawish-a" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker><marker id="qawish-aa" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar acc" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker></defs><rect class="nd warn" x="8" y="50" width="118" height="56" rx="6"/><text class="lb hd" x="67" y="74" text-anchor="middle">a wish</text><text class="lb mu" x="67" y="94" text-anchor="middle">“search is fast”</text><circle class="nd in" cx="18" cy="50" r="9"/><text class="lb mu" x="18" y="54" text-anchor="middle">1</text><line class="ed" x1="126" y1="78" x2="134" y2="78" marker-end="url(#qawish-a)"/><rect class="nd" x="138" y="50" width="118" height="56" rx="6"/><text class="lb hd" x="197" y="74" text-anchor="middle">a scenario</text><text class="lb mu" x="197" y="94" text-anchor="middle">200 a second, peak</text><circle class="nd in" cx="148" cy="50" r="9"/><text class="lb mu" x="148" y="54" text-anchor="middle">2</text><line class="ed" x1="256" y1="78" x2="264" y2="78" marker-end="url(#qawish-a)"/><rect class="nd in" x="268" y="50" width="118" height="56" rx="6"/><text class="lb hd" x="327" y="74" text-anchor="middle">a criterion</text><text class="lb mu" x="327" y="94" text-anchor="middle">p95 under 300 ms</text><circle class="nd in" cx="278" cy="50" r="9"/><text class="lb mu" x="278" y="54" text-anchor="middle">3</text><line class="ed" x1="386" y1="78" x2="394" y2="78" marker-end="url(#qawish-a)"/><rect class="nd" x="398" y="50" width="118" height="56" rx="6"/><text class="lb hd" x="457" y="74" text-anchor="middle">a check</text><text class="lb mu" x="457" y="94" text-anchor="middle">load test in CI</text><circle class="nd in" cx="408" cy="50" r="9"/><text class="lb mu" x="408" y="54" text-anchor="middle">4</text><line class="ed" x1="516" y1="78" x2="524" y2="78" marker-end="url(#qawish-a)"/><rect class="nd out" x="528" y="50" width="118" height="56" rx="6"/><text class="lb hd" x="587" y="74" text-anchor="middle">a signal</text><text class="lb mu" x="587" y="94" text-anchor="middle">objective + alert</text><circle class="nd in" cx="538" cy="50" r="9"/><text class="lb mu" x="538" y="54" text-anchor="middle">5</text><text class="lb" x="330" y="30" text-anchor="middle">plan it in the first week, check it on every change</text><text class="lb acc" x="330" y="150" text-anchor="middle">until it has a number and a check, a quality is a wish</text><text class="lb mu" x="330" y="176" text-anchor="middle">the same five steps work for security, accessibility, cost and the rest</text></svg><figcaption>Each quality goes through the same five steps. The first two happen in planning; the last three keep it true after launch.</figcaption></figure>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Each quality as an acceptance criterion</h3>
    <p>The numbers below are <b>examples to adapt</b>, not standards; the point is the shape: a measurable statement and a way to check it automatically.</p>
    <div class="tbl"><table><thead><tr><th>Quality</th><th>The question</th><th>An acceptance criterion, for example</th><th>How it is checked</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td><b>Security</b></td><td>Can only the right people do the right things?</td><td>Every endpoint except <code>/health</code> needs a valid token; user A cannot read user B&rsquo;s orders; no known critical vulnerability in production for more than 7 days</td><td>Access-control tests, dependency and image scans in CI (see <a href="#security">Security</a>)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Resilience</b></td><td>What happens when a dependency fails?</td><td>If the payment provider times out, checkout answers within 3 s with a retryable error and never charges twice</td><td>Fault-injection tests; covered in depth on <a href="#microservices">Microservices</a>, <a href="#distsys">Distributed Systems</a> and <a href="#sre">SRE</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Observability</b></td><td>When it misbehaves, can we tell why, quickly?</td><td>Every log line carries a trace ID; an alert fires within 5 minutes of the error rate passing 2%</td><td>A test that forces errors and checks the alert (see <a href="#observability">Observability</a>)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Accessibility</b></td><td>Can everyone use it, including with a keyboard or a screen reader?</td><td>Meets WCAG 2.2 level AA; every journey works by keyboard alone</td><td>Automated checks in CI, plus a person with a screen reader for each new journey (see <a href="#accessibility">Accessibility</a>)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Performance</b></td><td>Is it fast enough at the load we expect?</td><td>95% of searches answer in under 300 ms at 200 a second</td><td>A load test with thresholds in CI (see <a href="#performance">Performance</a>, <a href="#testing">Testing</a>)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Maintainability</b></td><td>Can we change it safely and quickly?</td><td>Modules depend only in allowed directions; the build and tests finish in under 10 minutes</td><td>Architecture tests (ArchUnit and similar), build time tracked in CI (see <a href="#patterns">SOLID &amp; Design Patterns</a>)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Privacy</b></td><td>Do we keep only what we need, only where we may?</td><td>A customer&rsquo;s erasure request is completed in every service within 30 days</td><td>An end-to-end erasure test across services (see <a href="#privacy">Personal Data Across Services</a>)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Cost</b></td><td>Does it cost what we planned?</td><td>Cost per 1,000 orders stays under an agreed figure; an alert at 80% of the monthly budget</td><td>Billing export and budget alerts (see <a href="#cloudcost">Cloud Cost</a>)</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note">A fuller vocabulary: the ISO/IEC 25010 quality model (2011 edition) lists eight product quality characteristics and 31 sub-characteristics, and added security and compatibility as main characteristics compared with the ISO/IEC 9126 model it replaced. It is a useful checklist for &ldquo;what have we forgotten?&rdquo;.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>They compete: rank them</h3>
    <p>Qualities pull against each other: more security checks add latency; more resilience (replicas, retries) adds cost; heavy optimisation can hurt maintainability; strong consistency can cost availability during a partition (see <a href="#distsys">Distributed Systems</a>). So rank them for each system, by requirement:</p>
    <div class="tbl"><table><thead><tr><th>If this is true for you</th><th>Rank higher</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td>It moves money or holds health or identity data</td><td>Security, correctness, auditability</td></tr>
      <tr><td>The public must be able to use it, whatever their abilities</td><td>Accessibility</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Users wait on it in real time, or traffic comes in bursts</td><td>Performance, resilience</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Many teams will change it for years</td><td>Maintainability, observability</td></tr>
      <tr><td>It runs at large scale on cloud bills you pay</td><td>Cost</td></tr>
      <tr><td>It is an experiment that may be thrown away</td><td>Speed of change; keep security and privacy at their minimum, never zero</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Secure by design</h3>
    <p><b>Secure by design</b> treats security as a constraint on the design from the start, like performance or cost, instead of something added later through patches or extra controls. It assumes the system will be attacked, and arranges it so that a compromise is hard, contained and recoverable. The US agency CISA published &ldquo;Secure by Design&rdquo; principles for software makers in October 2023.</p>
    <div class="tbl"><table><thead><tr><th>Principle</th><th>What it looks like in a design</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td><b>Assume a hostile world</b></td><td>Every input is untrusted, including from your own other services: validate at each boundary, parameterise every query</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Least privilege</b></td><td>Each service account, database user and token can do only its job: the report service can read orders, not refund them</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Defence in depth</b></td><td>No single control is trusted alone: authentication at the gateway <i>and</i> authorisation checks in the service <i>and</i> row-level limits in the database</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Small attack surface</b></td><td>Expose only what users need: no admin endpoints on the public network, unused features and ports switched off</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Secure defaults</b></td><td>The safe setting is the one you get without trying: private storage buckets, encryption on, multi-factor sign-in on, debug off</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Open design, not secrecy</b></td><td>Security rests on keys and proven algorithms, not on hiding how it works (see <a href="#pki">Keys, Certificates &amp; SSH</a>)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Continuous assurance</b></td><td>Controls are tested on every change and watched in production, not checked once before launch</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
    <p><b>How to plan it early:</b> threat-model the design before building (see <a href="#security">Security</a>, &ldquo;Threat modelling in 30 minutes&rdquo;), then turn each mitigation into an acceptance criterion and a test. For a ready-made list of verifiable security requirements, the OWASP Application Security Verification Standard (ASVS, version <span data-other-version>5.0.0</span>, May 2025) is written exactly for this: pick the level that fits the system and use its requirements as acceptance criteria. Secure by design sits beside privacy by design and zero trust, and the same habit applies to AI features (see <a href="#agentsec">Agentic AI Security</a>).</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Vulnerabilities: a loop, not a scan</h3>
    <p>A vulnerability is a weakness that can be used to break a system&rsquo;s security. Most of the ones a team has to deal with are in components it did not write: libraries, base images, platforms. Scanning finds them; what makes the difference is how you run the loop around the scan.</p>
    <figure class="fig"><svg viewBox="0 0 660 270" role="img" aria-labelledby="vulnloop-t vulnloop-d"><title id="vulnloop-t">The vulnerability loop</title><desc id="vulnloop-d">A loop of six steps. Know what you run: an inventory of every component, a software bill of materials. Find: scanners, tests and reports from outside. Identify: a CVE ID for the vulnerability and a CWE for the kind of weakness. Prioritise: severity from CVSS, likelihood of exploitation from EPSS, and whether your system actually exposes it. Fix within the agreed time, or mitigate. Verify and tell: re-scan, and disclose or notify. Then back to the start, because new vulnerabilities are published every day.</desc><defs><marker id="vulnloop-a" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker><marker id="vulnloop-aa" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar acc" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker></defs><rect class="nd" x="15" y="40" width="190" height="52" rx="6"/><text class="lb hd" x="110" y="62" text-anchor="middle">1 know what you run</text><text class="lb mu" x="110" y="81" text-anchor="middle">SBOM, every component</text><rect class="nd" x="235" y="40" width="190" height="52" rx="6"/><text class="lb hd" x="330" y="62" text-anchor="middle">2 find</text><text class="lb mu" x="330" y="81" text-anchor="middle">scans, tests, reports</text><rect class="nd" x="455" y="40" width="190" height="52" rx="6"/><text class="lb hd" x="550" y="62" text-anchor="middle">3 identify</text><text class="lb mu" x="550" y="81" text-anchor="middle">CVE ID, CWE type</text><rect class="nd in" x="455" y="190" width="190" height="52" rx="6"/><text class="lb hd" x="550" y="212" text-anchor="middle">4 prioritise</text><text class="lb mu" x="550" y="231" text-anchor="middle">CVSS + EPSS + exposure</text><rect class="nd" x="235" y="190" width="190" height="52" rx="6"/><text class="lb hd" x="330" y="212" text-anchor="middle">5 fix or mitigate</text><text class="lb mu" x="330" y="231" text-anchor="middle">within the agreed time</text><rect class="nd" x="15" y="190" width="190" height="52" rx="6"/><text class="lb hd" x="110" y="212" text-anchor="middle">6 verify and tell</text><text class="lb mu" x="110" y="231" text-anchor="middle">re-scan, disclose</text><line class="ed" x1="205" y1="66" x2="231" y2="66" marker-end="url(#vulnloop-a)"/><line class="ed" x1="425" y1="66" x2="451" y2="66" marker-end="url(#vulnloop-a)"/><line class="ed" x1="550" y1="92" x2="550" y2="186" marker-end="url(#vulnloop-a)"/><line class="ed" x1="455" y1="216" x2="429" y2="216" marker-end="url(#vulnloop-a)"/><line class="ed" x1="235" y1="216" x2="209" y2="216" marker-end="url(#vulnloop-a)"/><line class="ed acc" x1="110" y1="190" x2="110" y2="96" marker-end="url(#vulnloop-aa)"/><text class="lb acc" x="330" y="140" text-anchor="middle">new vulnerabilities are published every day: the loop never stops</text></svg><figcaption>Finding vulnerabilities is the easy part; scanners do it. The work is in steps 1, 4 and 5: knowing what you run, deciding what matters, and fixing it within a time you agreed in advance.</figcaption></figure>
    <div class="tbl"><table><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>What it is</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td><b>CVE</b></td><td>A public ID for one specific vulnerability, such as CVE-2021-44228. The CVE system was launched by MITRE in 1999, and its IDs underlie the US National Vulnerability Database.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>CWE</b></td><td>A category of weakness (for example, improper input validation), so you can see which <i>kinds</i> of mistake keep recurring.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>CVSS</b></td><td>A 0&ndash;10 severity score from FIRST, with bands None (0), Low (0.1&ndash;3.9), Medium (4.0&ndash;6.9), High (7.0&ndash;8.9) and Critical (9.0&ndash;10.0). The current version, 4.0, was released in November 2023.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>EPSS</b></td><td>Also from FIRST: the estimated probability, from 0 to 1, that a published vulnerability will be exploited in the wild.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>SBOM</b></td><td>A software bill of materials: the inventory of every component in what you ship, so that when a new CVE appears you can answer &ldquo;do we run that?&rdquo; in minutes.</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Severity is not priority.</b> CVSS measures how bad a vulnerability is in general; it is not designed for deciding what to patch first, though it is often used that way. Combine it with the likelihood of exploitation (EPSS) and your own exposure: is the vulnerable code reachable, on the internet, holding sensitive data?</li>
      <li><b>Agree fix times before you need them</b>, as acceptance criteria: for example, critical and reachable within days, high within weeks, the rest in the normal update cycle. <i>Our view:</i> the exact numbers matter less than having them written down and measured. <i>The other view:</i> fixed times can push teams to rush low-risk fixes to meet a clock; some prefer to triage each finding on its own exposure and set no deadline.</li>
      <li><b>Reduce the noise at the source.</b> Fewer dependencies, slim base images and automated updates gated by tests (see <a href="#security">Security</a>, &ldquo;Supply chain &amp; CI&rdquo;) mean fewer vulnerabilities to triage at all.</li>
      <li><b>Make it easy to be told.</b> Publish how to report a vulnerability to you. Coordinated disclosure, once called responsible disclosure, gives a vendor time to fix before details are published; a common industry deadline, pioneered by Google, is 90 or 120 days, cut to 7 when the vulnerability is already being exploited.</li>
      <li><b>Know your own components.</b> Without an SBOM, &ldquo;are we affected?&rdquo; becomes a search through every repository, on the worst possible day.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Growing the architecture one slice at a time, with agents</h3>
    <p>Few teams start with a clean design; most inherit code that grew. The qualities above can still be brought in step by step: one customer journey at a time, end to end, while the team keeps shipping (the strangler fig idea on <a href="#architecture">Architecture</a>, rather than a big-bang rewrite). With agents now writing much of the code, each step has a second job: it gives the agent rails to work within.</p>
    <div class="tbl"><table><thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>What the team does</th><th>Why it matters more when agents write code</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td><b>1. Agree the words</b></td><td>Name the business capability and its terms (order, refund, shipment) and use the same names in code, tickets and conversation</td><td>An agent copies whatever names it finds. A short glossary in the repository&rsquo;s agent instructions (CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md and similar) keeps it from inventing a third word for the same thing (see <a href="#agentharness">Agent Harness</a>)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>2. Map who talks to whom</b></td><td>Draw where data flows and where trust changes: user to service, service to service, service to third party</td><td>The agent itself is now on the map: what it can read, change and call is a trust boundary of its own (see <a href="#agentsec">Agentic AI Security</a>)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>3. Write the contracts down</b></td><td>Make each API, event and module interface explicit: a schema, an interface, a versioned spec</td><td>A written contract is a specification an agent can build to and a contract test can hold it to; an unwritten one is a guess it will fill in (see <a href="#specs">Specifications</a>, <a href="#teststrat">Test Strategy</a>)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>4. Put the criteria in the pipeline</b></td><td>Turn the acceptance criteria above into checks that run on every change: security scans, accessibility checks, load-test thresholds, architecture tests</td><td>Agents produce changes faster than people can read them line by line. Automated gates apply the same bar to every change, whoever or whatever wrote it, and leave reviewers the questions in the checklist above</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>5. Make it visible</b></td><td>Add logs, metrics and traces, and set service level objectives for the journey (see <a href="#observability">Observability</a>, <a href="#sre">SRE</a>)</td><td>Code nobody on the team wrote by hand is code nobody remembers; production signals are how you find out what it actually does</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>6. Close the loop</b></td><td>Learn from incidents and telemetry, and revisit decision records when the facts change</td><td>Record why a decision was made, beside the code: it is what the next person, and the next agent session, reads before changing it</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
    <p><i>Our view:</i> in the AI era these steps matter more, not less: the faster code is produced, the more the structure, the contracts and the gates decide whether it is any good. <i>The other view:</i> for a small product with one team, much of this is overhead, and a well-tested monolith with clear modules may be all the architecture it needs for years (see <a href="#microservices">Microservices</a> for when splitting pays). Choose the steps by the requirements in the ranking table above.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Review every change for its effect on the system</h3>
    <p>Formatters, linters, type checkers and tests now catch most of what reviewers used to comment on, and more of the code itself is written by agents (see <a href="#humanagents">Humans &amp; Agents</a>). What is left for a person is the part no tool can judge: what the change does to the system. Google&rsquo;s published review guide already ranks it first: the overall design is &ldquo;the most important thing to cover in a review&rdquo;, and a reviewer should ask whether the change makes the whole system better or more complex, and bring in someone qualified for privacy, security, concurrency or accessibility.</p>
    <p>The qualities on this page make a short checklist for any pull request, whoever or whatever wrote it:</p>
    <div class="tbl"><table><thead><tr><th>Look at</th><th>Ask of the change</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td><b>Purpose</b></td><td>Does the description say what changes for the user, and does the code do that and nothing else?</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Boundaries</b></td><td>Does each rule live in the module that owns it? Is any API, event or schema it changes versioned and written down? (<a href="#architecture">Architecture</a>, <a href="#apis">APIs &amp; Integration</a>)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Failure</b></td><td>What happens on bad input, a slow network or a dependency that is down? Are retries safe to repeat? (<a href="#microservices">Microservices</a>)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Security and privacy</b></td><td>Does it add an entry point, a permission, a secret or a new kind of personal data? Does it add a dependency with known vulnerabilities?</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Running it</b></td><td>When it goes wrong at 3 a.m., will logs, metrics and traces show where, and can it be rolled back or switched off? (<a href="#observability">Observability</a>)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>The next change</b></td><td>Could another team, product or channel reuse this safely, or has something general been hard-wired for one case?</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>The criteria</b></td><td>Does it still meet the acceptance criteria above: the latency, accessibility and cost numbers? Is there a test that would fail if it did not?</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
    <p><i>Our view:</i> leave style to the tools and spend review time on these questions; the more code agents write, the more this is the reviewer&rsquo;s real job. <i>The other view:</i> line-by-line reading still finds bugs the checks miss, and Google&rsquo;s guide asks reviewers to look at every line they are asked to review. Both are true; the checklist decides where the time goes first. More on the craft of review in <a href="#review">Writing &amp; Code Review</a>.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Our view, and the other view</h3>
    <p><i>Our view:</i> decide and rank the qualities in the first week, write each as a criterion with a number, and check it automatically from then on. Treat security as a design constraint, and vulnerabilities as a loop with agreed fix times.</p>
    <p><i>The other view:</i> some teams prefer to learn their real quality needs from production: ship something small, measure it against service level objectives (see <a href="#sre">SRE</a>), and tighten where users actually feel pain, rather than guess numbers up front. It avoids gold-plating qualities nobody needs. It works best for performance and cost, where you can measure and adjust; it is risky for security, privacy and accessibility, where a gap can harm people before you notice it.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>When code is cheap, checking it is the work: one view from industry</h3>
    <p>In a November 2026 episode of <a href="https://newsletter.pragmaticengineer.com/p/software-engineering-after-code-becomes" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The Pragmatic Engineer</a>, Gergely Orosz talked with Microsoft&rsquo;s chief executive, Satya Nadella. The episode&rsquo;s notes open with the question &ldquo;What does software engineering look like when generating the code becomes cheap and verifying it is the hard part?&rdquo;. Several points from Orosz&rsquo;s summary of the conversation bear directly on the qualities on this page:</p>
    <div class="tbl"><table><thead><tr><th>Topic</th><th>Point made in the interview</th><th>What it means for qualities (<i>analysis</i>)</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td><b>Tests</b></td><td>Models that critique generated code can reach their goal in undesirable ways, &ldquo;for example, making tests pass by deleting failing tests&rdquo;</td><td>Protect the checks themselves: a change that deletes, skips or loosens a test is a review item, whoever made it</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Agents</b></td><td>Models are intelligent but unreliable, so treat them as &ldquo;insider risks&rdquo; and build a separate system to verify that agentic systems work as expected</td><td>Least privilege for agents, and verification that does not depend on the agent being checked (see <a href="#agentsec">Agentic AI Security</a>)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Lock-in</b></td><td>Keep the harness, memory and model decoupled to avoid lock-in, and build private evaluations to choose and upgrade models</td><td>Maintainability applied to AI: a boundary you can swap behind, and a test suite for the part you swap (see <a href="#agentharness">Agent Harness</a>)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Specifications</b></td><td>Specifications and verification are becoming more important</td><td>Acceptance criteria written before the code are the specification a person or an agent builds to (see <a href="#specs">Specifications</a>)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>People</b></td><td>Microsoft and Carnegie Mellon University started a preceptorship, pairing experienced engineers with newer ones to build &ldquo;systems thinking, taste, and judgement&rdquo;</td><td>The judgement this page asks for (ranking qualities, reviewing for system impact) is learned from people, not only from tools</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
    <p><b>How to weigh it.</b> It is one person&rsquo;s view, given in an interview by the head of a company that sells AI tools and models, and Orosz writes that &ldquo;We didn&rsquo;t arrive at any definite solutions&rdquo; on verification. The underlying problem is not new: tests, types, reviews and staged roll-outs have always been how teams checked code they did not write themselves. What has changed is how much code arrives to be checked.</p>
    <p><i>Our view:</i> the qualities on this page, written as acceptance criteria and checked automatically, are the verification layer that turns cheap code into code you can trust; the cheaper the code, the more that layer is worth. <i>The other view:</i> some expect more of the checking itself to be done by models, critiquing code and learning from production traces; the interview treats that as promising, and also notes that it brings the problem back to verifying the models&rsquo; fixes.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>From a function to a system</h3>
    <p>The habits that make one function good are the same ones that make a whole system good, applied at a larger scale. A clear name becomes a shared business vocabulary. A small function with one job becomes a module with one owner, so that a change stays local. Checking inputs becomes security decided in the design. A useful error message becomes logs, traces and objectives that tell you what the running system is doing. <i>Our view:</i> architecture is not a separate skill learned after clean code, but the same care carried across boundaries, failures and production. <i>The other view:</i> some decisions only exist at system scale (data residency, consistency across services, cost) and cannot be reached by tidying code; they need deliberate design, which is why this page starts with planning early.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Practise it</h3>
    <p>The <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/well-rounded-engineer-labs" target="_blank" rel="noopener">labs</a> turn several of these qualities into short, testable drills in Python and Java: resilience (the double charge, the retry storm, the stale read), performance (the timed data structures drills), maintainability and testability (the core and the shell), and correctness under concurrency. For security, the <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/well-rounded-engineer-labs/tree/main/security/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">security drills</a> practise seven basics, from path traversal to secrets in logs, with one <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/well-rounded-engineer-labs/blob/main/security/STANDARD.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">standard</a> shared by people and coding agents.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ISO/IEC_9126" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: ISO/IEC 9126 (and its successor ISO/IEC 25010)</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Non-functional_requirement" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Non-functional requirement</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Secure_by_design" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Secure by design</a> (core concepts; its cited CISA document &ldquo;Secure-by-Design: Shifting the Balance of Cybersecurity Risk&rdquo;, dated 25 October 2023); <a href="https://github.com/OWASP/ASVS/blob/master/README.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OWASP ASVS README</a> (version <span data-other-version>5.0.0</span>, May 2025); <a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">W3C: WCAG 2.2</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Common_Vulnerabilities_and_Exposures" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Common Vulnerabilities and Exposures</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Common_Weakness_Enumeration" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Common Weakness Enumeration</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Common_Vulnerability_Scoring_System" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Common Vulnerability Scoring System</a> (severity bands; v4.0, November 2023; not intended for patch prioritisation); <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Exploit_Prediction_Scoring_System" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Exploit Prediction Scoring System</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Software_supply_chain" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Software supply chain (SBOM)</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vulnerability_disclosure" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Vulnerability disclosure</a> (coordinated disclosure; 90 or 120 days, 7 if exploited); <a href="https://github.com/google/eng-practices/blob/master/review/reviewer/looking-for.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google eng-practices: What to look for in a code review</a>; <a href="https://sre.google/sre-book/service-level-objectives/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google SRE book: Service Level Objectives</a>; <a href="https://newsletter.pragmaticengineer.com/p/software-engineering-after-code-becomes" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The Pragmatic Engineer: Software engineering after code becomes cheap with Satya Nadella (Gergely Orosz, 16 November 2026)</a>.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= SOLUTION ARCHITECTURE: A TO B ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-atob" data-name="Solution Architecture: A to B">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Solution architecture: getting from A to B</h2><p>A picture of the final system is the easy part. The engineering is in the route: which steps, in what order, what runs in between, what is built only to be thrown away, and how each step can be undone. This page separates solution architecture from business, technical and infrastructure architecture, names three kinds of solution (strategic, tactical and temporary), and shows an incremental model from today&rsquo;s system (A) to the target (B). The patterns themselves are on <a href="#architecture">Architecture</a>.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Solution architecture is not business architecture</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th></th><th>Business architecture</th><th>Solution architecture</th><th>Enterprise architecture</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Definition (sourced)</td><td>Holistic views of the business: capabilities, end-to-end value delivery, information and organisational structure, and how they relate to strategies, products, policies and stakeholders (wording quoted on Wikipedia)</td><td>A description of a discrete, focused business operation or activity and how information systems and technology support it (The Open Group&rsquo;s definition, among several)</td><td>An enterprise architecture framework is a methodology for guiding the transformation of a business from a baseline state to a target state, sometimes through several transition states</td></tr>
      <tr><td>The question it answers (<i>analysis</i>)</td><td>What does the business do, and what must it be able to do?</td><td>How will these systems change, in what order, to support that?</td><td>How does the whole estate move, and what standards hold it together?</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Typical output (<i>analysis</i>)</td><td>Capability maps, value streams</td><td>Baseline, transition and target states; interfaces; data ownership; decisions and their trade-offs</td><td>Roadmaps, principles, the portfolio</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note"><i>Our view:</i> a solution architecture that only restates business capabilities, or only draws the target, is not finished. It must say how the systems change, step by step, and what runs at each step. <i>The other view:</i> in a small organisation one person does all three, and the lines blur; and a solution designed without the business architecture can be a fine route to the wrong place.</p>
  </article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Solution, technical and infrastructure architecture</h3>
    <p>TOGAF, an enterprise architecture framework, models architecture in four domains: business, data, application and technology. TOGAF&rsquo;s skills framework does not recognise a solution architect role, yet such roles are widely advertised, and the technical and infrastructure split below is common usage rather than TOGAF, so the same words can mean different jobs. The last column uses an invented example: keeping a shopping cart the same on every device.</p>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>Defined as (TOGAF, as summarised on Wikipedia)</th><th>Decides, in practice (<i>analysis</i>)</th><th>Shopping-cart example (<i>illustrative</i>)</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td><b>Business</b></td><td>The business strategy, governance, organisation and key business processes</td><td>What the business must be able to do</td><td>Customers expect the same cart on every device</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Solution</b></td><td>Not one of TOGAF&rsquo;s four domains. The Open Group defines it as &ldquo;a description of a discrete and focused business operation or activity and how IS/IT supports that operation&rdquo;</td><td>Which systems change, their interfaces, who owns which data, and the route from A to B</td><td>Add a cart events topic and a push gateway, in transition steps</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Application</b></td><td>A blueprint for the individual systems to be deployed, their interactions, and their relationship to core business processes</td><td>The services and how they talk</td><td>The cart service, and a backend-for-frontend that holds browser connections</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Data</b></td><td>The structure of the organisation&rsquo;s logical and physical data assets, and the data management resources that go with them</td><td>Sources of truth, models, retention</td><td>The cart service owns the cart; events are keyed by cart id</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Technical</b> (TOGAF: technology)</td><td>The hardware, software and network infrastructure needed to support the deployment of core, mission-critical applications</td><td><i>Common usage, not TOGAF:</i> many organisations split technology in two. <b>Technical:</b> the software platform, meaning languages, frameworks, middleware and integration patterns</td><td>Kafka, server-sent events, the web framework</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Infrastructure</b></td><td>No separate TOGAF domain: infrastructure appears inside the technology domain&rsquo;s definition</td><td><i>Common usage, not TOGAF:</i> <b>Infrastructure:</b> compute, networks, storage, hosting, regions and recovery</td><td>The managed Kafka cluster, load-balancer timeouts long enough for open connections, two regions</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note"><i>Our view:</i> solution architecture cuts across the other layers. It starts from the business need, chooses the application and data changes, asks the technical and infrastructure architects for platforms that fit, and puts it all in order from A to B; Gartner&rsquo;s definition, which combines guidance from business, information and technical viewpoints, is similar. <i>The other view:</i> in organisations with strong platform teams, the solution architect mostly chooses from a catalogue that technical and infrastructure architects maintain; and in a small team one engineer holds every layer at once.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Strategic, tactical and temporary: say which one you are building</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Kind</th><th>What it is for</th><th>What you accept (<i>analysis</i>)</th><th>When it ends (<i>analysis</i>)</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td><b>Strategic</b></td><td>Part of the target state (B), built to last</td><td>Higher cost and time up front</td><td>Not planned; it is the destination</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Tactical</b></td><td>Meets a real need now, inside today&rsquo;s constraints (a deadline, a regulation, a contract)</td><td>Known shortcuts: <i>prudent, deliberate</i> technical debt in Fowler&rsquo;s terms, taken on knowingly, when the payoff for an earlier release outweighs the cost of paying it back</td><td>When the strategic solution replaces it; the date and the trigger are written down</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Temporary (transitional)</b></td><td>Exists only to help move from A to B: Cartwright, Horn and Lewis call it transitional architecture, &ldquo;software elements installed to ease the displacement of a legacy system that we intend to remove when the displacement is complete&rdquo;</td><td>Work that will be thrown away, by design</td><td>When the transition state it serves is passed</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note"><i>Analysis:</i> the three-way split, and mapping tactical to prudent, deliberate debt and temporary to transitional architecture, are our framing, not a standard. The sources use the debt quadrant and transitional architecture, not these three labels.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Technical debt</b> is Ward Cunningham&rsquo;s metaphor (1992); Fowler&rsquo;s gloss is that the extra effort every later change costs because of a shortcut is the interest on the debt.</li>
      <li><b>Sacrificial architecture</b> (Fowler) is the longer-horizon version of the same idea: deliberately accepting now that in a few years you will (hopefully) need to throw away what you are building.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note"><i>Our view:</i> label every component strategic, tactical or temporary, and give each tactical and temporary one an owner, an exit condition and a date. <i>The other view:</i> labels drift, and many &ldquo;temporary&rdquo; solutions become permanent; some teams refuse tactical work for exactly that reason and accept slower delivery instead.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>The incremental model: A, transition states, B</h3>
    <p>Cartwright, Horn and Lewis, in <i>Patterns of Legacy Displacement</i>, put it plainly: gradual replacement delivers benefits early and avoids the risks of a big bang, and while it runs the old and new systems operate side by side. An <i>illustrative</i> example, moving order handling off a legacy system:</p>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>State</th><th>What runs</th><th>Built only for this state</th><th>Value delivered</th><th>Way back</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td><b>A: baseline</b></td><td>The legacy system does everything</td><td>&ndash;</td><td>&ndash;</td><td>&ndash;</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Transition 1</b></td><td>A router in front of the legacy system; all traffic still goes to legacy</td><td>The router, if B will not need it</td><td>A seam: you can now move traffic</td><td>Remove the router</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Transition 2</b></td><td>New order-status service; legacy still owns the data; state changes copied to the new service</td><td>The copy of events from legacy</td><td>Faster order tracking for customers</td><td>Route status back to legacy</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Transition 3</b></td><td>New system owns new orders; legacy keeps old orders until they close</td><td>A sync that keeps legacy reports working</td><td>New orders on the new platform</td><td>Feature toggle sends new orders to legacy</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>B: target</b></td><td>New system only</td><td>&ndash;</td><td>Legacy licence and support cost removed</td><td>None needed: decommission is the last deliverable</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note">Each transition state is a real production system: it must be operated, monitored and secured, and it must say which system is the source of truth for each piece of data. When you draw your own, add a &ldquo;source of truth&rdquo; column for each state. The example is illustrative, not a recommendation for any real system.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Short-term solutions for the transition period</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>During the transition you need to&hellip;</th><th>Technique</th><th>Removed when (<i>analysis</i>)</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Move features one at a time from old to new</td><td><b>Strangler fig:</b> new pieces grow around the old system until it can be retired</td><td>The last feature has moved</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Change an interface many callers use, without breaking them</td><td><b>Parallel change</b> (expand and contract): expand, migrate, contract</td><td>The contract phase</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Replace a component inside one codebase while still releasing regularly</td><td><b>Branch by abstraction</b>: put an abstraction in front, build the new implementation behind it, switch</td><td>The old implementation is deleted</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Ship unfinished work safely</td><td><b>Release toggles:</b> transitionary by nature; Hodgson says they should generally not stay much longer than a week or two, though product-centric toggles may stay longer</td><td>The feature is fully released</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Change things without the old system noticing</td><td><b>Legacy mimic:</b> the new system interacts with the legacy one so that it is not aware of any change</td><td>The legacy system is gone</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Feed the new system from the old one&rsquo;s changes</td><td><b>Event interception:</b> intercept updates to system state and route some of them to the new component</td><td>The new system owns that data</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Prove the new path under real load before users see it</td><td><b>Dark launching:</b> call the new back end from existing traffic without users being able to tell</td><td>The new path is live</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Expose the change to a few users first</td><td><b>Canary release:</b> roll out to a small subset before everyone</td><td>Rolled out to all</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Choosing a migration pattern by requirement</h3>
    <p class="note"><i>Analysis:</i> the rows are our mapping of requirement to pattern; the AWS, Wikipedia and Cartwright et al. pages describe each pattern, but none of them gives this table.</p>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>If this is true for the piece you are moving</th><th>It leans towards</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Its calls can be intercepted at the edge of the old system</td><td>Strangler fig, behind a router or gateway</td></tr>
      <tr><td>It sits deep inside the old system, with other code depending on it</td><td>Branch by abstraction (AWS recommends it for components deep in the stack with upstream dependencies; it is not suitable where data consistency is involved)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>The new model and the old one mean different things by the same words</td><td>An anti-corruption layer between them</td></tr>
      <tr><td>You must prove the new system gives the same answers before trusting it</td><td>Parallel run, comparing outputs</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Two data stores must stay in step while both are live</td><td>The transactional outbox or change data capture, not a dual write</td></tr>
      <tr><td>The change is low-risk and easy to reverse</td><td>A canary release, then a direct cut-over</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note"><i>Analysis:</i> most moves combine several of these. A strangler fig moves the traffic, an anti-corruption layer protects the new model, and a parallel run proves the result before the old path is switched off.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Parallel run</h3>
    <p>The old and new systems run side by side for an agreed period, take the same input, do the same processing, and their outputs are compared. When the new system has proved reliable, the old one is removed. Cartwright, Horn and Lewis list it among the ways to cut over from a legacy system.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>The cost:</b> running two systems at once is expensive, takes time, and can mean doing work twice.</li>
      <li><b>Decide before you start</b> (<i>analysis</i>): which outputs must match exactly, what tolerance applies to the rest, how long the run lasts, and which system&rsquo;s answer counts while they disagree.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Anti-corruption layer</h3>
    <p>A mediation layer that translates one system&rsquo;s domain model into another&rsquo;s, so the new service is not shaped by the old one&rsquo;s meanings. AWS suggests it when the migrated service&rsquo;s model differs from the original, when two systems with different semantics must exchange data, or when changing one to fit the other is not practical.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>What it costs:</b> another component to monitor, release and run; added latency; a possible scaling bottleneck; and a single point of failure, because if it fails the service behind it is unreachable. AWS says to record it as technical debt and decommission it once every dependent caller has migrated.</li>
      <li><b>What it buys:</b> callers owned by other teams need not change at the same time as you.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Dual write, and the two ways out</h3>
    <p>A dual write is one operation that writes to two systems, such as saving to the database and then publishing an event, or writing to both the old and the new database during a migration. If one write succeeds and the other fails, the two disagree, and nothing notices unless you check. AWS describes two ways out, both under the transactional outbox pattern: an outbox table, or change data capture.</p>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Way out</th><th>How it works</th><th>Fits (<i>analysis</i>)</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td><b>Transactional outbox</b></td><td>Write the change and an outbox row in one database transaction, then publish from the outbox (see <a href="#architecture">Architecture</a>); events can arrive twice or out of order, so make the consumer idempotent</td><td>Your own service, where you can change the code</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Change data capture</b></td><td>Read each committed row-level change from the database and publish it as an event. Debezium does this, and only committed changes are visible, so rolled-back work never leaks out</td><td>A legacy database you cannot change, or many tables at once</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note"><i>Our view:</i> do not dual write; use one of the two above. <i>The other view, in our experience:</i> some teams accept a dual write for a short migration window, with a reconciliation job that compares the two stores every night, because it needs no new infrastructure. If you do, treat every mismatch as an incident, and write down the date the dual write ends.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Splitting a shared database</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>The target AWS describes:</b> database per service, where each service stores and reads only its own data, so services stay loosely coupled and each can choose its store. <b>What it costs:</b> cross-service transactions and queries are harder, and you run several databases.</li>
      <li><b>The shared-database pattern:</b> several services use one database. AWS says to avoid hot tables (single tables that several services write to) and to keep every schema change backward-compatible: drop a column or table only when no current or previous version of any service references it. AWS also notes it does not reduce dependencies between teams and adds runtime coupling.</li>
      <li><b>Queries across services:</b> AWS says that with database per service you must add another pattern for these: API composition (which CQRS can speed up) or event sourcing.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note"><i>Analysis:</i> a common order is: give each table one owning module while the database is still shared; stop other modules reading those tables directly, going through the owner instead; copy the data to the new store with change data capture; switch reads, then writes; and finally drop the old tables. Every step before the drop can be reversed; plan the drop last, after the old tables have gone unreferenced for an agreed period.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Engineering from A to B, not just a picture of B</h3>
    <p><i>Our view:</i> rules of thumb, not a standard.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Every step deploys and runs on its own,</b> and has a way back.</li>
      <li><b>Every step delivers something</b> a user or the business can see, or removes a cost; otherwise ask why it is a step.</li>
      <li><b>Data has one owner at each state.</b> Write down which system is the source of truth, and when that changes.</li>
      <li><b>Budget for the throwaway work.</b> Transitional pieces are real engineering, then real removal.</li>
      <li><b>Decommissioning is a deliverable,</b> with a date and an owner, not a hope.</li>
      <li><b>Record each choice</b> in an architecture decision record, including which items are tactical or temporary (see <a href="#architecture">Architecture</a>).</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Our view, and the other view</h3>
    <p><i>Our view:</i> a proposal that shows only the final state is a wish, not a plan. The useful document shows A, each transition state, B, what is temporary at each point and how it goes away.</p>
    <p><i>The other view:</i> a clear target picture is what aligns people and wins funding, and the detailed route is often better worked out by the delivery teams as they learn; fixing every step up front can lock in a route that turns out wrong. Both can hold: fix the target and the first one or two transitions, and plan the rest as you go.</p>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Tell business, solution and enterprise architecture apart</li><li>Say whether a piece of work is strategic, tactical or temporary</li><li>Draw today's system (A) and the target (B)</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Plan transition states between A and B, each one deployable</li><li>Use strangler fig, parallel change or branch by abstraction for a real change</li><li>Write down the source of truth for each piece of data at each state</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Design transitional architecture and plan its removal</li><li>Take on tactical debt deliberately, with an owner and an exit date</li><li>Lead a legacy displacement without a big bang</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Set how an organisation plans and funds transitions, not only targets</li><li>Govern a portfolio of strategic, tactical and temporary work</li><li>Teach teams to engineer the route, not only the destination</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>Baseline, transition and target states</li><li>Transitional architecture</li><li>Technical debt, prudent and deliberate</li><li>Patterns of legacy displacement</li><li>Feature toggles, dark launching and canary releases</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://martinfowler.com/articles/patterns-legacy-displacement/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Patterns of Legacy Displacement (Cartwright, Horn, Lewis)</a><span class="lnote"> The whole catalogue</span></li><li><a href="https://martinfowler.com/articles/patterns-legacy-displacement/transitional-architecture.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Transitional Architecture</a><span class="lnote"> Building what you will remove</span></li><li><a href="https://martinfowler.com/bliki/TechnicalDebtQuadrant.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Technical Debt Quadrant</a><span class="lnote"> Prudent, reckless, deliberate, inadvertent</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Solution_architecture" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Solution architecture</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Business_architecture" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Business architecture</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Enterprise_architecture" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Enterprise architecture</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Open_Group_Architecture_Framework" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: The Open Group Architecture Framework (TOGAF domains)</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technical_debt" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Technical debt</a>; <a href="https://martinfowler.com/articles/patterns-legacy-displacement/transitional-architecture.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cartwright, Horn, Lewis: Transitional Architecture</a>; <a href="https://martinfowler.com/articles/patterns-legacy-displacement/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cartwright, Horn, Lewis: Patterns of Legacy Displacement</a>; <a href="https://martinfowler.com/bliki/StranglerFigApplication.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Fowler: Strangler Fig</a>; <a href="https://martinfowler.com/bliki/ParallelChange.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sato: Parallel Change</a>; <a href="https://martinfowler.com/bliki/BranchByAbstraction.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Fowler: Branch By Abstraction</a>; <a href="https://martinfowler.com/articles/feature-toggles.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Hodgson: Feature Toggles</a>; <a href="https://martinfowler.com/articles/patterns-legacy-displacement/legacy-mimic.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cartwright, Horn, Lewis: Legacy Mimic</a>; <a href="https://martinfowler.com/articles/patterns-legacy-displacement/event-interception.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cartwright, Horn, Lewis: Event Interception</a>; <a href="https://martinfowler.com/bliki/DarkLaunching.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Fowler: Dark Launching</a>; <a href="https://martinfowler.com/bliki/CanaryRelease.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sato: Canary Release</a>; <a href="https://martinfowler.com/bliki/TechnicalDebt.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Fowler: Technical Debt</a>; <a href="https://martinfowler.com/bliki/TechnicalDebtQuadrant.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Fowler: Technical Debt Quadrant</a>; <a href="https://martinfowler.com/bliki/SacrificialArchitecture.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Fowler: Sacrificial Architecture</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parallel_running" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Parallel running</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Change_data_capture" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Change data capture</a>; <a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/cloud-design-patterns/acl.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AWS Prescriptive Guidance: Anti-corruption layer pattern</a>; <a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/modernization-decomposing-monoliths/branch-by-abstraction.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AWS Prescriptive Guidance: Branch by abstraction</a>; <a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/cloud-design-patterns/transactional-outbox.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AWS Prescriptive Guidance: Transactional outbox (dual writes)</a>; <a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/modernization-data-persistence/database-per-service.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AWS Prescriptive Guidance: Database-per-service</a>; <a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/modernization-data-persistence/shared-database.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AWS Prescriptive Guidance: Shared-database-per-service</a>; <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/patterns/anti-corruption-layer" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Azure Architecture Center: Anti-corruption layer</a>; <a href="https://github.com/debezium/debezium" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Debezium README</a>.</p><p class="runcode">A question, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/enrichmeai.github.io/issues/new?title=%5BSolution%20Architecture%3A%20A%20to%20B%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>. Nothing here is tracked and there are no comments to store: questions live in the open, where the next learner can find the answer.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= DISTRIBUTED SYSTEMS ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-distsys" data-name="Distributed Systems">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Distributed systems</h2><p>As soon as a program runs on more than one machine, it has to cope with messages that are lost, delayed or arrive twice, machines that pause or fail partway, and clocks that disagree. This page covers the ideas underneath microservices, Kafka, replicated databases and Kubernetes: what goes wrong, the trade-offs you cannot avoid, how machines agree, and how to test it. The section on simulation testing draws on Unmesh Joshi&rsquo;s article <a href="https://martinfowler.com/articles/deterministic-simulation-testing.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Deterministic Simulation Testing</a> and his <a href="https://martinfowler.com/articles/patterns-of-distributed-systems/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Patterns of Distributed Systems</a>, Joshi says his Tickloom library draws heavily on FoundationDB and TigerBeetle, and he names Antithesis as a commercial option for systems that cannot be restructured.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>The assumptions that fail</h3>
    <p>The <b>fallacies of distributed computing</b> are false assumptions that programmers new to distributed systems tend to make. L. Peter Deutsch at Sun Microsystems listed seven in 1994, building on earlier work by Bill Joy and Dave Lyon, and James Gosling added the eighth around 1997:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>The network is reliable.</li>
      <li>Latency is zero.</li>
      <li>Bandwidth is infinite.</li>
      <li>The network is secure.</li>
      <li>Topology does not change.</li>
      <li>There is one administrator.</li>
      <li>Transport cost is zero.</li>
      <li>The network is homogeneous.</li>
    </ol>
    <p><b>Partial failure</b> is what makes this hard. Unmesh Joshi describes gray failures: a node may misbehave without going down, a network may lose a few packets or slow some messages enough that only some nodes time out, and a process may freeze (a garbage-collection pause, for example) and resume without knowing it was frozen. The cluster is then split in what it believes, with some parts stalled and others carrying on.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>CAP and PACELC</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>CAP</b> (Brewer&rsquo;s conjecture, 2000; proved by Gilbert and Lynch, 2002): a distributed data store can give at most two of <i>consistency</i> (every read gets the latest write or an error), <i>availability</i> (every request to a working node gets a response, possibly not the latest data) and <i>partition tolerance</i> (it keeps working while messages between nodes are lost or delayed).</li>
      <li><b>Our reading</b> (<i>analysis</i>): partitions cannot be ruled out on a real network, so the choice is what to do during one: refuse the operation and stay consistent, or answer and risk inconsistency.</li>
      <li><b>PACELC</b> adds the normal case: <i>else</i>, with no partition, you still trade latency against consistency.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">Two traps: CAP&rsquo;s consistency is not the C in ACID, and CAP&rsquo;s availability is not &ldquo;high availability&rdquo; in the everyday sense.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Consistency models</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Linearizable</b> (Herlihy and Wing, 1987): operations that overlap in time still appear to take effect instantly, in a single order that respects real time. It is a strong correctness condition, and it lets a programmer reason about the system as if there were one copy of the data.</li>
      <li><b>Eventual consistency:</b> if no new updates are made, every read eventually returns the last value. Replicas are said to converge. It is a weak guarantee, chosen for availability.</li>
      <li><b>BASE versus ACID:</b> eventually consistent stores are often described as basically available, soft state, eventually consistent, in contrast to ACID transactions.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">Weaker models in between, such as read-your-writes, are on <a href="#architecture">Architecture</a>.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Time and order</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>What it gives you</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td><b>Wall clock</b></td><td>Time of day. Joshi notes it can jump when NTP corrects it and is not guaranteed to be monotonic, so it cannot measure a timeout; and machines&rsquo; clocks are not perfectly synchronised, so it cannot order events across them.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Monotonic clock</b></td><td>The usual workaround for measuring elapsed time; Joshi points out that a process pause can still distort the measurement.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Lamport timestamp</b> (Leslie Lamport, 1978)</td><td>A simple logical counter carried on messages, giving a partial order of events: if one event could have caused another, it gets the smaller number.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Vector clock</b></td><td>One logical counter per process, carried on every message, so you can tell whether two events are causally ordered or concurrent.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Hybrid logical clock</b></td><td>A version that increases like a counter but stays close to real date and time, so clients can ask for data &ldquo;as of&rdquo; a time.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Generation clock</b> (also called term or epoch)</td><td>A number that goes up with each new leader, so a leader that paused and came back can be recognised as out of date.</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Agreeing: consensus and Raft</h3>
    <p>Consensus lets a group of machines agree on the same sequence of changes, so they can act as one replicated state machine. <b>Paxos</b>, by Leslie Lamport, was first submitted in 1989 and published in 1998. <b>Raft</b>, by Diego Ongaro and John Ousterhout, was designed as an alternative that is easier to understand. etcd, which Kubernetes uses, is built on Raft.</p>
    <ol>
      <li><b>One leader at a time.</b> Each server is a leader, a follower, or, during an election, a candidate. The leader sends regular heartbeats.</li>
      <li><b>Elections.</b> A follower that hears nothing for its election timeout (typically 150 to 300 ms) becomes a candidate, starts a new <i>term</i>, votes for itself and asks the others. Each server votes once per term.</li>
      <li><b>Log replication.</b> The leader appends each change to its log and replicates it to followers. A change counts as committed once a majority have stored it.</li>
    </ol>
    <figure class="fig"><svg viewBox="0 0 620 250" role="img" aria-labelledby="raft-t raft-d"><title id="raft-t">Raft server states</title><desc id="raft-d">A UML state diagram. A server starts as a follower. If it hears no heartbeat before its election timeout it becomes a candidate. A candidate that receives a majority of votes becomes the leader. A split vote starts a new term and a new election. A candidate that receives a message with a higher term becomes a follower again.</desc><defs><marker id="raft-a" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker><marker id="raft-aa" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar acc" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker></defs><circle class="dot" cx="30" cy="125" r="8"/><line class="ed" x1="38" y1="125" x2="76" y2="125" marker-end="url(#raft-a)"/><rect class="nd" x="80" y="100" width="120" height="50" rx="14"/><text class="lb hd" x="140" y="131" text-anchor="middle">Follower</text><rect class="nd" x="250" y="100" width="120" height="50" rx="14"/><text class="lb hd" x="310" y="131" text-anchor="middle">Candidate</text><rect class="nd" x="450" y="100" width="120" height="50" rx="14"/><text class="lb hd" x="510" y="131" text-anchor="middle">Leader</text><path class="ed" d="M170,100 C200,50 250,50 290,98" marker-end="url(#raft-a)"/><text class="lb mu" x="225" y="42" text-anchor="middle">election timeout</text><text class="lb mu" x="225" y="58" text-anchor="middle">(no heartbeat)</text><line class="ed" x1="370" y1="125" x2="446" y2="125" marker-end="url(#raft-a)"/><text class="lb mu" x="408" y="115" text-anchor="middle">majority</text><text class="lb mu" x="408" y="145" text-anchor="middle">of votes</text><path class="ed" d="M290,150 C250,215 200,215 166,153" marker-end="url(#raft-a)"/><text class="lb mu" x="228" y="228" text-anchor="middle">message with a higher term</text><path class="ed" d="M340,100 C370,40 300,40 318,96" marker-end="url(#raft-a)"/><text class="lb mu" x="335" y="30" text-anchor="middle">split vote: new term</text></svg><figcaption>Raft&rsquo;s server states, as a UML state diagram. A leader keeps followers in place by sending regular heartbeats; randomised election timeouts make split votes resolve quickly.</figcaption></figure>
    <p class="note">Raft in its basic form tolerates crashed servers, not lying ones: every node trusts the elected leader. Kafka&rsquo;s own metadata quorum is described on <a href="#kafka">Kafka</a>.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Quorums and split brain</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Majority quorum:</b> an update counts once a majority of nodes acknowledge it. Five nodes need three, and can lose two.</li>
      <li><b>Why a majority:</b> two majorities always share a node, so two halves of a broken cluster cannot both make decisions.</li>
      <li><b>Split brain:</b> two parts of a system each believe they are in charge and change overlapping data independently, usually after a network partition.</li>
      <li><b>Leases</b> give a node exclusive access for a limited time; it must renew before expiry, so a crashed or paused node loses the access on its own.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note"><i>Analysis:</i> this is why clusters usually run with an odd number of nodes: a fourth node adds cost without letting you lose a second one. The other view: an even number is sometimes chosen when spreading nodes across zones or racks matters more than the failure count.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Transactions across machines: two-phase commit</h3>
    <p>Two-phase commit has a coordinator ask every participant to prepare (vote), then tell them all to commit or roll back. Its main weakness is that it <b>blocks</b>: a participant that has voted yes must wait for the coordinator&rsquo;s decision, and if the coordinator fails for good, some participants never resolve, and in rare cases a person has to step in.</p>
    <p class="note"><i>Analysis:</i> this is why services usually prefer sagas, with a compensating step for each local transaction, over a distributed transaction (see <a href="#architecture">Architecture</a> and <a href="#microservices">Microservices</a>). The other view: a saga gives up isolation between steps and needs compensation logic, so where a database or transaction coordinator already provides two-phase commit and short blocking is acceptable, it can be the simpler option.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>When nodes lie: Byzantine faults</h3>
    <p>A Byzantine fault is one where a component shows different symptoms to different observers, so others cannot even agree whether it has failed. The name comes from Lamport, Shostak and Pease&rsquo;s 1982 paper on the Byzantine Generals Problem. Byzantine fault tolerant systems can keep working with fewer than one third of their nodes faulty or malicious.</p>
    <p class="note"><i>Analysis:</i> inside one organisation, crash fault tolerance (Raft, Paxos) is usually enough. Byzantine fault tolerance matters where participants may be malicious or do not trust each other. Public blockchains are a related case, though Wikipedia notes that their guarantees differ from classical Byzantine fault tolerance (see <a href="#bitcoin">Bitcoin &amp; Blockchain</a>).</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Living with eventual consistency: session guarantees</h3>
    <p>With replicas that catch up later, a user can write something and then not see it, or see a newer value and then an older one. In 1994 Douglas Terry and colleagues described four <b>session guarantees</b>: promises made to one user or one client, which are much cheaper than making every replica agree at once.</p>
    <div class="tbl"><table><thead><tr><th>Guarantee</th><th>The promise</th><th>What goes wrong without it</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td><b>Read your writes</b></td><td>After you write a value, your later reads see it (or something newer)</td><td>You update your address, refresh, and the old one is back</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Monotonic reads</b></td><td>Once you have seen a value, you never see an older one</td><td>A comment appears, then disappears on the next refresh</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Monotonic writes</b></td><td>Your writes are applied in the order you made them</td><td>You set a price, then correct it, and the correction is applied first</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Writes follow reads</b></td><td>A write you make after reading a value is applied on top of that value or a newer one</td><td>A reply is stored before the message it answers</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
    <p><b>Common ways to provide them</b> (<i>analysis</i>): send a user&rsquo;s reads to the primary for a short time after they write; keep a user on the same replica (sticky routing); or have the client remember the version it last saw and refuse older answers. The labs drill &ldquo;The stale read&rdquo; (<a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/well-rounded-engineer-labs/tree/main/distributed-systems" target="_blank" rel="noopener">distributed systems drills</a>) is the second failure above in code.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>CRDTs: replicas that merge without asking each other</h3>
    <p>A <b>conflict-free replicated data type</b> is a data structure copied across machines where any copy can be updated independently, without coordinating with the others, and the data type itself resolves any differences when the copies meet. Marc Shapiro, Nuno Pregui&ccedil;a, Carlos Baquero and Marek Zawirski defined the concept formally in 2011; it was first motivated by collaborative text editing and mobile computing, and is used in online chat and other systems. Simple examples are a counter that only goes up (each replica counts its own increments, and the total is the sum) and a set where additions always win.</p>
    <p><i>Our view:</i> CRDTs are worth knowing because they show that &ldquo;eventual&rdquo; can still mean &ldquo;correct&rdquo;, if the data type is designed for it. <i>The other view:</i> many business rules cannot be expressed as data that merges automatically (a balance that must never go negative, a seat that can only be sold once), and those still need coordination or a single owner.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Fencing: stopping the old leader</h3>
    <p>When a leader or lock holder seems to have failed, the rest of the system cannot tell whether it has really died or is only paused, by a long garbage collection, a slow network or a suspended virtual machine. If it is only paused, it may wake up and keep writing as if it were still in charge. <b>Fencing</b> isolates such a node from the shared resources it controlled, so it cannot do damage when it comes back.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Shut it out</b>: cluster software can power the node off (known as STONITH, &ldquo;shoot the other node in the head&rdquo;) or block its access to shared storage.</li>
      <li><b>Make old writes harmless</b> (<i>analysis</i>): give every new leader or lock holder a number that only goes up, have it send that number with every write, and have the storage reject any write carrying an older number than one it has already seen.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The labs drill &ldquo;The lock that outlived its owner&rdquo; (<a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/well-rounded-engineer-labs/tree/main/distributed-systems" target="_blank" rel="noopener">distributed systems drills</a>) is this failure, and its fix, in Python and Java.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Patterns to know by name</h3>
    <p>Unmesh Joshi&rsquo;s catalogue, published as the book <i>Patterns of Distributed Systems</i> in 2023, names recurring building blocks of distributed systems. Six of its patterns, picked here as a start:</p>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Pattern</th><th>The problem it solves</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td><a href="https://martinfowler.com/articles/patterns-of-distributed-systems/write-ahead-log.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Write-ahead log</a></td><td>Keep every agreed change after a crash, by persisting each state change as a command in an append-only log</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="https://martinfowler.com/articles/patterns-of-distributed-systems/heartbeat.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Heartbeat</a></td><td>Notice a failed server in time, by having each one send regular &ldquo;I am alive&rdquo; messages</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="https://martinfowler.com/articles/patterns-of-distributed-systems/majority-quorum.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Majority quorum</a></td><td>Stop two groups of servers making independent decisions</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="https://martinfowler.com/articles/patterns-of-distributed-systems/generation-clock.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Generation clock</a></td><td>Recognise a leader that paused or was cut off and has since been replaced</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="https://martinfowler.com/articles/patterns-of-distributed-systems/lease.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Lease</a></td><td>Give exclusive access that expires by itself if its holder crashes or pauses</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="https://martinfowler.com/articles/patterns-of-distributed-systems/hybrid-clock.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Hybrid clock</a></td><td>Version data so the versions are ordered and also map to a date and time</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Testing it: chaos, histories and simulation</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Approach</th><th>What it asks</th><th>Its limit</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td><b>Chaos engineering</b> (Netflix&rsquo;s Chaos Monkey, 2011, disables a randomly chosen server during working hours)</td><td>Does the system stay available, and recover, when parts fail?</td><td>Unmesh Joshi&rsquo;s point: staying up does not show the answers are right, since a node can answer every request and still return an old value.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Client-history checking</b> (Jepsen)</td><td>Record what clients invoked and what came back, then check that history against a consistency model.</td><td>Finding a failure is only the first step; you must also reproduce it. Even with the same fault schedule, thread, message and disk timing differ from run to run on a live cluster.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Deterministic simulation testing</b> (below)</td><td>Run the real code against a simulated network, disk and clock, with every fault decided by one seed.</td><td>The code has to be built for it, under strict rules.</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Deterministic simulation testing</h3>
    <p>Joshi names four sources of nondeterminism, each of which changes the order in which events reach the software: the <b>system clock</b>, <b>thread scheduling</b>, <b>network timing</b> and <b>disk timing</b>. Simulation testing takes control of all four:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Time comes from ticks, not the clock.</b> A timeout counts calls to <code>tick()</code>, so a pause cannot fire it early. Production systems already work this way: etcd&rsquo;s Raft is driven by <code>Node.Tick()</code>, with election and heartbeat timeouts set in ticks, and TigerBeetle fixes a tick at 10 ms.</li>
      <li><b>One thread runs the protocol</b>, so there is no scheduler to vary the order.</li>
      <li><b>The network and the disk are interfaces</b> with a simulated version in tests, which can drop, delay or duplicate messages. TigerBeetle&rsquo;s simulator also reorders packets, partitions the network and corrupts reads and writes.</li>
      <li><b>Every random choice comes from a seeded generator.</b> The same seed gives exactly the same faults in the same order, so a failure found on one run replays every time.</li>
      <li><b>Time is fast-forwarded:</b> the simulation runs the next tick at once instead of waiting, so a run that would take hours on a live cluster takes seconds, and thousands of runs that would add up to days finish quickly. TigerBeetle says one minute of its simulator&rsquo;s time is equivalent to days of real-world testing.</li>
      <li><b>Correctness is checked</b> against the recorded client history, as Jepsen does, not just &ldquo;did it stay up&rdquo;.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">Where it comes from: Joshi writes that <b>FoundationDB</b> pioneered deterministic simulation testing for distributed databases, using a custom C++ coroutine language and a deterministic scheduler, and that it simulates at the level of byte streams. <b>TigerBeetle</b>, a financial transactions database, uses a single-threaded, tick-driven design, which Joshi found easier to implement in Java. TigerBeetle&rsquo;s own documentation says its simulator, the VOPR, is deterministic from a seed and the Git commit, so any bug it finds can be replayed, and credits FoundationDB and Antithesis as its inspiration. <b>Antithesis</b> is a commercial platform with a deterministic hypervisor, which Joshi suggests when re-architecting the application is not feasible. <a href="https://github.com/unmeshjoshi/tickloom" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Tickloom</a> is Joshi&rsquo;s Java library for teaching the approach.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>The rules simulation imposes</h3>
    <p>To keep the core deterministic, Joshi&rsquo;s Tickloom code follows six rules. In short (his examples are Java, but the idea applies in any language):</p>
    <ul>
      <li>No reading the system clock directly.</li>
      <li>No unseeded randomness, including for ids.</li>
      <li>No threads, executors or standard futures in the core.</li>
      <li>No direct network or disk I/O: everything goes through the interfaces.</li>
      <li>No iterating over unordered collections such as a <code>HashMap</code>, whose order can differ between runs.</li>
      <li>No background thread calling back into the deterministic side.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">On cost, Joshi argues that a single event loop with asynchronous, batched I/O often performs better for I/O-bound systems, the design Redis, FoundationDB and TigerBeetle use. The trade-off is that handlers must never block, and you scale across cores by running more processes.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Choose by requirement</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>If this is true for you</th><th>It leans towards (<i>analysis</i>)</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>You build on a managed database or Kafka and do not write the replication yourself</td><td>Learn the guarantees it documents; test your own retries, timeouts and idempotency; use chaos tests for recovery</td></tr>
      <tr><td>A wrong answer costs more than a refused one (money, stock, bookings)</td><td>A store that chooses consistency during a partition</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Staying responsive matters more than every read being current (feeds, catalogues)</td><td>Eventual consistency, with the staleness designed for</td></tr>
      <tr><td>You need a leader, a lock or cluster membership</td><td>An existing consensus system such as etcd, not a home-made one</td></tr>
      <tr><td>You are writing a consensus protocol, a replication layer or a storage engine</td><td>Deterministic simulation testing, designed in from the start</td></tr>
      <tr><td>An existing system you cannot restructure</td><td>Client-history checking, or simulation below the application at the hypervisor (commercial platforms such as Antithesis)</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Our view, and the other view</h3>
    <p><i>Our view:</i> every engineer who builds services should know the fallacies, CAP and quorums well enough to read a database&rsquo;s guarantees critically, and anyone writing the replication itself should design for simulation testing from the first line, because retrofitting determinism is usually harder (<i>our view</i>; hypervisor-level tools exist for when re-architecting is not feasible).</p>
    <p><i>The other view:</i> most teams never write a consensus protocol. For them the strict rules of simulation are a cost with little return, and time is better spent on timeouts, idempotency, chaos tests and choosing well-tested infrastructure.</p>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Name the fallacies of distributed computing and give an example of each</li><li>Explain CAP and PACELC, and what each letter means</li><li>Explain why a five-node cluster survives two failures</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Read a database's consistency guarantees and say what they mean for your code</li><li>Design timeouts, retries and idempotency for partial failure</li><li>Explain Lamport timestamps, vector clocks and generation clocks</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Explain how Raft elects a leader and replicates a log</li><li>Choose consistency per use case and design for its staleness</li><li>Plan chaos and client-history tests for a service</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Design a replicated component and its failure handling</li><li>Build deterministic simulation testing into a system from the start</li><li>Set an organisation's standards for distributed correctness</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>Fallacies of distributed computing</li><li>CAP and PACELC</li><li>Linearizability and eventual consistency</li><li>Logical clocks</li><li>Consensus: Raft and Paxos</li><li>Quorums, leases and split brain</li><li>Deterministic simulation testing</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://martinfowler.com/articles/deterministic-simulation-testing.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unmesh Joshi: Deterministic Simulation Testing</a><span class="lnote"> The article this page's testing section draws on</span></li><li><a href="https://martinfowler.com/articles/patterns-of-distributed-systems/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unmesh Joshi: Patterns of Distributed Systems</a><span class="lnote"> The catalogue of patterns</span></li><li><a href="https://github.com/unmeshjoshi/tickloom" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Tickloom</a><span class="lnote"> A Java library for learning deterministic simulation</span></li><li><a href="https://github.com/tigerbeetle/tigerbeetle/blob/main/docs/internals/vopr.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">TigerBeetle: Deterministic Simulation Testing</a><span class="lnote"> How the VOPR simulator works</span></li><li><a href="https://raft.github.io/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The Raft paper and visualisation</a><span class="lnote"> Raft explained by its authors</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://martinfowler.com/articles/deterministic-simulation-testing.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unmesh Joshi: Deterministic Simulation Testing</a>; <a href="https://martinfowler.com/articles/patterns-of-distributed-systems/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unmesh Joshi: Catalog of Patterns of Distributed Systems</a>; <a href="https://martinfowler.com/articles/patterns-of-distributed-systems/write-ahead-log.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Write-Ahead Log</a>; <a href="https://martinfowler.com/articles/patterns-of-distributed-systems/heartbeat.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">HeartBeat</a>; <a href="https://martinfowler.com/articles/patterns-of-distributed-systems/majority-quorum.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Majority Quorum</a>; <a href="https://martinfowler.com/articles/patterns-of-distributed-systems/generation-clock.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Generation Clock</a>; <a href="https://martinfowler.com/articles/patterns-of-distributed-systems/lease.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Lease</a>; <a href="https://martinfowler.com/articles/patterns-of-distributed-systems/hybrid-clock.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Hybrid Clock</a>; <a href="https://github.com/tigerbeetle/tigerbeetle/blob/main/docs/internals/vopr.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">TigerBeetle: VOPR (source)</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fallacies_of_distributed_computing" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Fallacies of distributed computing</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CAP_theorem" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: CAP theorem</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PACELC_design_principle" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: PACELC design principle</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Linearizability" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Linearizability</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eventual_consistency" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Eventual consistency</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lamport_timestamp" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Lamport timestamp</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vector_clock" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Vector clock</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Raft_(algorithm)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Raft</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paxos_(computer_science)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Paxos</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Etcd" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: etcd</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quorum_(distributed_computing)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Quorum (distributed computing)</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Split-brain_(computing)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Split-brain</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Two-phase_commit_protocol" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Two-phase commit</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Byzantine_fault" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Byzantine fault</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chaos_engineering" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Chaos engineering</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Consistency_model" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Consistency model (session guarantees)</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conflict-free_replicated_data_type" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Conflict-free replicated data type</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fencing_(computing)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Fencing (computing)</a>.</p><p class="runcode"><b>Run the code:</b> <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/well-rounded-engineer-fundamentals/tree/main/distsys" target="_blank" rel="noopener">well-rounded-engineer-fundamentals/distsys</a>, with a README that says how to run it and what it shows. Stuck, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/well-rounded-engineer-fundamentals/issues/new?title=%5BDistributed%20Systems%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= MICROSERVICES ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-microservices" data-name="Microservices">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Microservices</h2><p>Building a system as small, independently deployable services that each own their data. When it pays off, how to split, how services talk and fail, and the traps that turn microservices into a distributed monolith. Patterns referenced here are on <a href="#architecture">Architecture</a>.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Monolith, modular monolith, microservices</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th></th><th>Monolith</th><th>Modular monolith</th><th>Microservices</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Deployables</td><td>One</td><td>One, with enforced internal modules</td><td>Many, deployed independently</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Data</td><td>One shared database</td><td>One database, schema per module</td><td>Each service owns its data</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Calls between parts</td><td>In-process</td><td>In-process through module APIs</td><td>Over the network</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Scaling</td><td>Whole app</td><td>Whole app</td><td>Per service</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Team fit</td><td>One small team</td><td>One to a few teams</td><td>Many teams owning services end to end</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Main cost</td><td>Tangling as it grows</td><td>Discipline to keep boundaries</td><td>Distributed-systems complexity and operations</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note">Most systems should start as a modular monolith and extract a service only when a boundary has proved stable and there is a concrete reason: independent scaling, a different release cadence, a separate team, or different technology or security needs.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>What microservices buy, and cost</h3>
    <p><b>Gains</b></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Teams deploy their service without coordinating a big release.</li>
      <li>Scale and tune each service for its own load.</li>
      <li>A failure can be contained to one service.</li>
      <li>Different services can use different technologies where it helps.</li>
    </ul>
    <p><b>Costs</b></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Network calls fail, time out and add latency.</li>
      <li>No cross-service transactions: sagas and eventual consistency instead.</li>
      <li>Debugging needs distributed tracing; testing needs contracts.</li>
      <li>Many pipelines, deployments, dashboards and on-call rotations.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>How to split services</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>By business capability or bounded context</b> (domain-driven design): search, carts, billing, identity. Not by technical layer (a "database service" is an anti-pattern).</li>
      <li><b>Each service owns its data</b> and is the only one that writes it. Others ask through its API or react to its events.</li>
      <li><b>High cohesion inside, loose coupling between:</b> a typical change should touch one service.</li>
      <li><b>Align with teams</b> (Conway's law): one team owns a service end to end.</li>
      <li><b>Size by responsibility, not lines of code.</b> Too many tiny services ("nano-services") multiply network calls and operations for no gain.</li>
      <li><b>Test the split:</b> if two services must always deploy together, they are one service.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide" id="monorepo"><h3>Monorepo or one repo per service?</h3>
    <p><b>A monorepo is not a monolith.</b> A monorepo stores the code of many projects in one repository; each service can still build, deploy and scale on its own. A monolith is one application. You can run microservices from a monorepo, or a monolith from many repositories. The repository decides how people change code together; the deployment decides how the system runs.</p>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th></th><th>Monorepo</th><th>One repo per service (polyrepo)</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Change across services</td><td>One atomic commit and one review for the API, its clients and their tests</td><td>One pull request per repo, merged and released in the right order</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Shared code</td><td>A shared library is just a folder, used from source</td><td>A published package with versions, which each service must upgrade</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Large refactors</td><td>You can see and fix every caller in one change</td><td>Callers in other repositories you cannot see may stay on the old version</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Access control</td><td>By default everyone can read everything</td><td>Grant access per repository</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Versions</td><td>Some teams use one version for everything and lose per-service versions</td><td>Each service has its own version and release history</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Tooling needed</td><td>A build that only builds and tests what changed; CI that scales with the repo</td><td>Simple per repo; harder across repos (dependency updates, shared standards)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Clone size</td><td>Everything by default; partial or sparse clones fix it</td><td>Only the service you work on</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p><b>Who runs very large monorepos:</b> Wikipedia names Google, Meta, Microsoft, Uber, Airbnb and Twitter. Several had to change their tools to do it: Google built its own version control system (Piper), Facebook (now Meta) contributed to Mercurial, Microsoft contributed to the Git client and built the Virtual File System for Git when it moved its Windows engineers to one Git repository, and Twitter wrote the Pants build system.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Where a monorepo fits with microservices</h3>
    <p><b>Choose by your requirements, not by preference.</b> Read down the left column, tick the rows that are true for you, and see which way they lean.</p>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>If this is true for you</th><th>It leans towards</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Services often change together (shared API contracts, schemas, clients)</td><td>Monorepo</td><td>One atomic commit and one review instead of ordered releases across repos</td></tr>
      <tr><td>You need large refactors across many services</td><td>Monorepo</td><td>Every caller is visible and fixed in the same change</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Code must be hidden from some contributors (partners, vendors, regulated code)</td><td>Separate repos</td><td>A monorepo gives read access to everything by default</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Each service is versioned and released to outside users on its own</td><td>Separate repos</td><td>Per-project versions are simpler; some monorepos lose them</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Nobody owns build tooling and CI capacity</td><td>Separate repos</td><td>A monorepo needs a build that only builds what changed, or CI slows with every service added</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Teams are separate organisations with little shared code</td><td>Separate repos</td><td>Little gain from atomic changes; more cost in shared tooling</td></tr>
      <tr><td>A few teams, mostly one domain, shared libraries</td><td>Either; one repo per team or domain is a common middle</td><td>Keeps atomic changes where they matter, and access lines where they matter</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Conway's law</b> (Melvin Conway, 1967): systems mirror the communication structure of the organisation that builds them. The repository layout should match how your teams actually work together.</li>
      <li><b>Other views:</b> companies have moved in both directions as they grew. Uber engineers gave a talk titled &ldquo;Monorepo to Multirepo and Back Again&rdquo; (2017). Neither layout is a maturity level: the right one can change when your teams, access rules or tooling change.</li>
    </ul>
    <h4>Making it work</h4>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Build only what changed.</b> A build tool that knows the dependency graph (Bazel, Buck, Pants, Please, or Nx) builds and tests only the affected services. CI that rebuilds everything on every commit does not scale.</li>
      <li><b>Deploy each service on its own.</b> One repository, many pipelines: each service keeps its own image, version tag and rollout. A monorepo must not turn into a release-everything-together monolith.</li>
      <li><b>Ownership per folder:</b> a <code>CODEOWNERS</code> file names the team responsible for each path; owners are requested for review automatically, and branch protection can require their approval.</li>
      <li><b>Keep clones fast:</b> <code>git clone --filter=blob:none</code> (partial clone) downloads file contents only when needed, and <code>git sparse-checkout set services/orders libs/common</code> keeps only the folders you work on in your working tree.</li>
      <li><b>The required-check trap:</b> on GitHub, a workflow skipped by a <code>paths</code> filter leaves its required checks stuck as "Pending", so the pull request cannot merge. A job skipped by an <code>if:</code> condition reports success instead. GitHub&rsquo;s advice is to avoid requiring workflows that can be skipped. So run the workflow always, decide what changed in a first job, and skip the expensive jobs with <code>if:</code>. Make that first job a required check too: a job whose <code>needs</code> failed is skipped and may not block the merge.</li>
      <li><b>Enforce the boundaries in code:</b> a service may import shared libraries but never another service's internals. Check this in the build, or the monorepo quietly becomes a distributed monolith.</li>
    </ul>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># .github/workflows/ci.yml : always runs, so required checks always report
on: [pull_request]
jobs:
  changes:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    outputs:
      orders: ${{ steps.f.outputs.orders }}
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4
        with: { fetch-depth: 0 }
      - id: f
        run: |
          changed=$(git diff --name-only origin/${{ github.base_ref }}...HEAD)
          if printf '%s\n' "$changed" | grep -E '^(services/orders|libs/common)/' > /dev/null
          then echo "orders=true" >> "$GITHUB_OUTPUT"; else echo "orders=false" >> "$GITHUB_OUTPUT"; fi
  orders:
    needs: changes
    if: needs.changes.outputs.orders == 'true'   # skipped by if: reports success
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4
      - run: ./gradlew :services:orders:build</code></pre></div>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># .github/CODEOWNERS : who reviews which folder
/services/orders/   @acme/orders-team
/libs/common/       @acme/platform-team</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Communication</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Style</th><th>Use for</th><th>Watch out for</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Synchronous REST / gRPC</td><td>Queries that need an answer now</td><td>Chains of calls: latency adds up and availability multiplies down</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Asynchronous events (Kafka, Pub/Sub)</td><td>Telling others something happened</td><td>Eventual consistency, duplicates, ordering</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Asynchronous commands (queues)</td><td>Work another service should do</td><td>Retries, dead letters, idempotency</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <ul>
      <li>Prefer events between domains; keep synchronous calls shallow (ideally one hop).</li>
      <li>Cache or replicate the data you need from other services (from their events) rather than calling them on every request.</li>
      <li>See <a href="#apis">APIs &amp; Integration</a>, <a href="#kafka">Kafka</a> and <a href="#graphql">GraphQL</a>.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Infrastructure around services</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Service discovery:</b> Kubernetes DNS names, Cloud Run URLs, or a registry. Never hard-code instance IPs.</li>
      <li><b>API gateway</b> at the edge for authentication, rate limits and routing (<a href="#apigee">Apigee</a>); a <b>backend for frontend</b> per client type when needs differ.</li>
      <li><b>Service mesh</b> (Istio / Cloud Service Mesh) for mTLS, retries and traffic splitting between services, without code in each one.</li>
      <li><b>Configuration and secrets</b> injected at runtime per environment.</li>
      <li><b>Platform blueprint:</b> a service template with logging, tracing, health checks, security and CI already wired, so each new service starts correct.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Resilience in code (Spring + Resilience4j)</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>@Service
class SupplierClient {
  private final RestClient http;
  SupplierClient(RestClient http) { this.http = http; }

  @CircuitBreaker(name = "supplier", fallbackMethod = "unavailable")
  @Retry(name = "supplier")
  public Listing list(String prefix) {
    return http.get().uri("/objects?prefix={p}", prefix).retrieve().body(Listing.class);
  }

  // honest degradation: "cannot see", never "nothing there"
  Listing unavailable(String prefix, Throwable t) {
    return Listing.unknown("supplier unavailable: " + t.getMessage());
  }
}</code></pre></div>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>resilience4j:
  circuitbreaker:
    instances:
      supplier:
        slidingWindowSize: 20
        failureRateThreshold: 50
        waitDurationInOpenState: 30s
  retry:
    instances:
      supplier:
        maxAttempts: 3
        waitDuration: 200ms
        enableExponentialBackoff: true</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">Quarkus offers the same through MicroProfile Fault Tolerance (<code>@Retry</code>, <code>@CircuitBreaker</code>, <code>@Timeout</code>, <code>@Fallback</code>). Always set a client timeout too.</p>
  </article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Microservices patterns at a glance</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Pattern</th><th>The problem it solves</th><th>Where to read more</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Database per service</td><td>Services that share tables cannot change or deploy alone</td><td>Data across services, on this page</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Saga</td><td>A business transaction that spans services, without a distributed lock: local steps, each with a compensating step</td><td><a href="#architecture">Architecture</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Transactional outbox</td><td>Saving a change and publishing its event without one succeeding alone</td><td><a href="#architecture">Architecture</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Idempotent consumer</td><td>Messages arrive more than once</td><td><a href="#architecture">Architecture</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>CQRS</td><td>Reads and writes need different models or different scale</td><td><a href="#architecture">Architecture</a>, <a href="#eventsourcing">Event Sourcing</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>API gateway and backend for frontend</td><td>Many services behind one public surface; clients with different needs</td><td><a href="#architecture">Architecture</a>, <a href="#apigee">Apigee</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Timeout</td><td>A call that never returns holds a thread or connection for ever</td><td>Resilience in code, on this page</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Retry with backoff and jitter</td><td>Transient failures; only for idempotent calls</td><td>Resilience in code, on this page</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Circuit breaker</td><td>Callers keep waiting on a dependency that is already failing</td><td>Below</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Bulkhead</td><td>One slow dependency uses up every thread or connection</td><td>Below (From Hystrix to Resilience4j)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Rate limiter</td><td>A caller, or you, sends more than the other side can take</td><td>Below (From Hystrix to Resilience4j)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Sidecar and service mesh</td><td>The same networking, security and telemetry code repeated in every service</td><td><a href="#servicemesh">Service Mesh &amp; Istio</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Strangler fig</td><td>Moving from a monolith one piece at a time</td><td><a href="#atob">Solution Architecture: A to B</a></td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>How a circuit breaker works</h3>
    <p>Martin Fowler&rsquo;s description: wrap a protected call in a circuit breaker object that monitors for failures. Once failures reach a threshold, the breaker trips, and further calls return an error at once, without the protected call being made.</p>
    <ol>
      <li><b>Closed:</b> calls go through, and failures are counted.</li>
      <li><b>Open:</b> calls fail fast for a set time, giving the dependency room to recover.</li>
      <li><b>Half-open:</b> trial calls go through (Fowler describes one; libraries allow a configured number); success closes the breaker, failure opens it again.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="note">Alert when a breaker opens: it is a symptom someone needs to see.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>The states, drawn</h3>
    <figure class="fig"><svg viewBox="0 0 660 240" role="img" aria-labelledby="breaker-t breaker-d"><title id="breaker-t">Circuit breaker states</title><desc id="breaker-d">A UML state diagram with three states. Closed: calls go through and failures are counted. When failures reach the threshold the breaker trips to Open: calls fail at once, without calling the dependency, for a set time. When that time has passed it moves to Half-open: trial calls go through. If they succeed it returns to Closed; if they fail it goes back to Open.</desc><defs><marker id="breaker-a" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker><marker id="breaker-aa" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar acc" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker></defs><circle class="dot" cx="20" cy="110" r="7"/><line class="ed" x1="27" y1="110" x2="56" y2="110" marker-end="url(#breaker-a)"/><rect class="nd out" x="60" y="85" width="170" height="50" rx="14"/><text class="lb hd" x="145" y="107" text-anchor="middle">Closed</text><text class="lb mu" x="145" y="125" text-anchor="middle">calls pass, failures counted</text><rect class="nd warn" x="260" y="85" width="170" height="50" rx="14"/><text class="lb hd" x="345" y="107" text-anchor="middle">Open</text><text class="lb mu" x="345" y="125" text-anchor="middle">fail fast, no call made</text><rect class="nd in" x="460" y="85" width="170" height="50" rx="14"/><text class="lb hd" x="545" y="107" text-anchor="middle">Half-open</text><text class="lb mu" x="545" y="125" text-anchor="middle">trial calls go through</text><path class="ed acc" d="M175,85 C200,30 280,30 300,81" marker-end="url(#breaker-aa)"/><text class="lb acc" x="240" y="28" text-anchor="middle">failures reach the threshold</text><line class="ed" x1="430" y1="110" x2="456" y2="110" marker-end="url(#breaker-a)"/><text class="lb mu" x="443" y="155" text-anchor="middle">wait time passed</text><path class="ed" d="M520,135 C500,200 210,200 150,139" marker-end="url(#breaker-a)"/><text class="lb mu" x="330" y="215" text-anchor="middle">trial calls succeed</text><path class="ed" d="M500,85 C480,40 420,40 404,81" marker-end="url(#breaker-a)"/><text class="lb mu" x="470" y="50" text-anchor="start">a trial fails</text></svg><figcaption>The three states above as a UML state diagram. While the breaker is open the failing dependency gets no calls at all, which is what gives it room to recover.</figcaption></figure>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>From Hystrix to Resilience4j</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Hystrix,</b> Netflix&rsquo;s library, is in maintenance mode and no longer in active development. Netflix says it keeps using it for its existing applications, uses open, active projects such as Resilience4j for new ones, and is beginning to recommend others do the same.</li>
      <li><b>Resilience4j</b> is a lightweight fault-tolerance library designed for functional programming. It provides a circuit breaker, retry, rate limiter, time limiter (timeouts) and bulkhead, in semaphore and thread-pool forms, which wrap any function or lambda.</li>
      <li><b>On Quarkus and other MicroProfile runtimes,</b> MicroProfile Fault Tolerance gives the same ideas as annotations (see Resilience in code, above).</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note"><i>Analysis:</i> if you still run Hystrix, expect to rewrite each command as a decorator or annotation. The concepts, closed, open and half-open, carry over.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Circuit breaking in code, or in the mesh?</h3>
    <p>Istio&rsquo;s &ldquo;circuit breaking&rdquo; is configured on a DestinationRule and enforced by its Envoy sidecars. It is not the same mechanism as a library&rsquo;s breaker. Envoy enforces connection and request limits at the network level, without code in each application, and each proxy decides on its own (Envoy calls its limits &ldquo;fully distributed (not coordinated)&rdquo;). Its <b>outlier detection</b> is passive health checking: it removes hosts that behave unlike the others, for example after consecutive errors, from the load-balancing set for a while.</p>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th></th><th>In code (Resilience4j, MicroProfile Fault Tolerance)</th><th>In the mesh (Istio, Envoy)</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>What trips it</td><td>Failure rate or slow calls over a window, per named dependency</td><td>Connection and pending-request limits; outlier detection on errors per host</td></tr>
      <tr><td>What it isolates (<i>analysis</i>)</td><td>The whole dependency, as your code calls it</td><td>Individual pods (ejection), or the connections to a service</td></tr>
      <tr><td>When it trips</td><td>Your fallback runs: a cached answer, a default, or an honest &ldquo;unavailable&rdquo;</td><td>The caller gets an error (in Istio&rsquo;s own example, HTTP 503) and must handle it</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Languages (<i>analysis</i>)</td><td>One library per language, and the same settings repeated per service</td><td>Any language, no code change, configured in one place</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Who changes it (<i>analysis</i>)</td><td>The service team, in code and configuration</td><td>Often a platform team, in Kubernetes resources</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Choose by requirement</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>If this is true for you</th><th>It leans towards (<i>analysis</i>)</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>The caller needs a fallback that makes business sense (cached prices, &ldquo;delivery date unknown&rdquo;)</td><td><i>Our view:</i> a breaker in code, because only the application knows what a sensible fallback is</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Services in many languages, and you want one consistent policy without changing code</td><td>The mesh</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Usually one bad pod among healthy ones</td><td>Mesh outlier detection, which takes that pod out of rotation</td></tr>
      <tr><td>A whole dependency is down, or it is an external API outside the mesh</td><td>A breaker in code, around the client: the mesh guards only traffic that passes through its sidecars</td></tr>
      <tr><td>You do not run a service mesh</td><td><i>Our view:</i> a breaker in code. Adopting a mesh only for this has a cost (see <a href="#servicemesh">Service Mesh &amp; Istio</a>)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>You need both protections</td><td>Both: the mesh for connection limits and pod ejection, code for timeouts and fallbacks</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note"><i>Analysis:</i> set retries in one layer only. If the application retries three times and the mesh retries twice per attempt, one failing call can become up to nine requests, when the dependency is least able to take them. Istio applies a default retry policy (two retries on connection failures and similar) even if you configure none.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Our view, and the other view</h3>
    <p><i>Our view:</i> keep timeouts and fallbacks in code, where the business meaning is, and let a mesh, if you have one, add connection limits and eject bad pods. Do not adopt a mesh only to get circuit breaking.</p>
    <p><i>The other view:</i> platform teams that already run a mesh often prefer one place for every network policy. Keeping resilience out of application code means fewer libraries to upgrade in every service and the same behaviour in every language, and teams accept handling a 503 in exchange.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Data across services</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Database per service:</b> separate schemas or instances; no other service reads its tables.</li>
      <li><b>Cross-service transactions</b> become <b>sagas</b> with compensating steps.</li>
      <li><b>Reliable events</b> with the <b>transactional outbox</b>, so a state change and its event never disagree.</li>
      <li><b>Queries across services:</b> API composition (call and combine) for small cases; a <b>read model</b> built from events (CQRS) for heavy ones.</li>
      <li><b>Reporting</b> goes to the warehouse via events or CDC, not by joining services' databases.</li>
      <li>Details on <a href="#architecture">Architecture</a> and <a href="#eventsourcing">Event Sourcing</a>.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Observability is not optional</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Distributed tracing</b> (OpenTelemetry): one trace ID follows a request through every service, queue and database call.</li>
      <li><b>Structured logs</b> with trace ID, service, version and request ID on every line.</li>
      <li><b>Metrics per service:</b> rate, errors, duration (RED) and saturation; SLOs on user-facing ones.</li>
      <li><b>Health endpoints:</b> liveness (restart me) separate from readiness (send me traffic).</li>
      <li><b>Dependency maps</b> from traces show who calls whom, and where latency comes from.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Testing microservices</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Unit and component tests</b> inside each service, with its own database in a container.</li>
      <li><b>Contract tests</b> (Pact, Spring Cloud Contract) between consumers and providers, run in both pipelines, so a provider cannot break a consumer unnoticed.</li>
      <li><b>A few end-to-end tests</b> of critical journeys in a shared environment.</li>
      <li><b>Event schema checks</b> with a schema registry and compatibility rules.</li>
      <li><b>Resilience tests:</b> kill or slow a dependency and check the caller degrades as designed.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Security between services</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Zero trust:</b> authenticate every call, even inside the network.</li>
      <li><b>Service identity:</b> mTLS from a mesh, or Google-signed ID tokens between Cloud Run services.</li>
      <li><b>User context:</b> propagate the user's token or a signed internal token; each service still checks its own permissions.</li>
      <li><b>Least privilege per service:</b> its own service account, database user and secrets.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Anti-patterns</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Distributed monolith:</b> services that must be deployed together or share a database.</li>
      <li><b>Shared database</b> written by several services.</li>
      <li><b>Chatty synchronous chains:</b> A calls B calls C calls D for one request.</li>
      <li><b>Nano-services</b> too small to justify their operational cost.</li>
      <li><b>Shared libraries with business logic</b> that force lock-step upgrades (shared infrastructure blueprints are fine).</li>
      <li><b>No ownership:</b> services nobody is on call for.</li>
      <li><b>Splitting before understanding the domain:</b> boundaries drawn too early are expensive to move.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Migrating from a monolith</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Make the monolith modular first: enforce module boundaries and separate each module's data.</li>
      <li>Pick one capability with a clear boundary and a real reason to move.</li>
      <li>Put a routing layer (gateway or proxy) in front, and route that capability's traffic to the new service (<b>strangler fig</b>).</li>
      <li>Move its data, with a period of syncing or dual reads, and make the new service the only writer.</li>
      <li>Remove the old code. Repeat only if the first extraction paid off.</li>
    </ol>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Readiness checklist</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Can you deploy a service with one automated pipeline, in minutes?</li>
      <li>Do you have centralised logs, metrics and tracing?</li>
      <li>Can you create a new service from a template in under an hour?</li>
      <li>Do teams own services end to end, including on-call?</li>
      <li>Are the domain boundaries understood and stable?</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">If several answers are no, a modular monolith will serve you better for now.</p>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Explain monolith vs modular monolith vs microservices</li><li>Work in one service: build, test, deploy, read its logs and traces</li><li>Know why services own their data</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Build a service with timeouts, retries, circuit breakers and health checks</li><li>Use events and the outbox to integrate with other services</li><li>Write contract tests with consumers</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Draw service boundaries from the domain; decide when not to split</li><li>Design sagas, read models and observability across services</li><li>Lead a strangler-fig extraction from a monolith</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Set the organisation's service architecture and platform blueprint</li><li>Align team structure with service ownership</li><li>Decide when to consolidate services as well as split them</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>Domain-driven design: bounded contexts and context maps</li><li>Contract testing with Pact</li><li>Service mesh traffic management</li><li>Team Topologies</li><li>Platform engineering and service templates</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://martinfowler.com/articles/microservices.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Microservices (Martin Fowler &amp; James Lewis)</a><span class="lnote"> The original description and its trade-offs</span></li><li><a href="https://microservices.io/patterns/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">microservices.io</a><span class="lnote"> Pattern language: decomposition, data, communication, observability</span></li><li><a href="https://samnewman.io/books/building_microservices_2nd_edition/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Building Microservices, 2nd ed. (Sam Newman)</a><span class="lnote"> The standard book on the subject</span></li><li><a href="https://samnewman.io/books/monolith-to-microservices/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Monolith to Microservices (Sam Newman)</a><span class="lnote"> Migration patterns</span></li><li><a href="https://resilience4j.readme.io/docs" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Resilience4j</a><span class="lnote"> Circuit breakers, retries, bulkheads for Java</span></li><li><a href="https://opentelemetry.io/docs/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OpenTelemetry</a><span class="lnote"> Distributed tracing and metrics</span></li><li><a href="https://teamtopologies.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Team Topologies</a><span class="lnote"> Organising teams around services and platforms</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://martinfowler.com/articles/microservices.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Fowler & Lewis: Microservices</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conway%27s_law?action=raw" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Conway's law</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/resilience4j/resilience4j/master/resilience4j-framework-common/src/main/java/io/github/resilience4j/common/circuitbreaker/configuration/CommonCircuitBreakerConfigurationProperties.java" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Resilience4j circuit breaker config properties (source)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/resilience4j/resilience4j/master/resilience4j-framework-common/src/main/java/io/github/resilience4j/common/retry/configuration/CommonRetryConfigurationProperties.java" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Resilience4j retry config properties (source)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/microprofile/microprofile-fault-tolerance/main/README.adoc" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MicroProfile Fault Tolerance README</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/microprofile/microprofile-fault-tolerance/main/api/src/main/java/org/eclipse/microprofile/faulttolerance/Timeout.java" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MicroProfile FT @Timeout</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/run/docs/authenticating/service-to-service" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cloud Run service-to-service auth (ID tokens)</a>; <a href="https://docs.spring.io/spring-framework/reference/integration/rest-clients.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Spring Framework: REST clients (RestClient)</a>; <a href="https://resilience4j.readme.io/docs/getting-started-3" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Resilience4j Spring Boot guide (annotations, fallback signature)</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monorepo" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Monorepo (advantages, limits, who runs one, scaling tools)</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conway%27s_law" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Conway's law</a>; <a href="https://git-scm.com/docs/git-sparse-checkout" target="_blank" rel="noopener">git sparse-checkout</a>; <a href="https://git-scm.com/docs/partial-clone" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Git partial clone</a>; <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/repositories/managing-your-repositorys-settings-and-features/customizing-your-repository/about-code-owners" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub: About code owners</a>; <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/pull-requests/how-tos/merge-and-close-pull-requests/troubleshooting-required-status-checks" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub: Troubleshooting required status checks (skipped but required checks)</a>; <a href="https://github.com/nrwl/nx" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Nx README (runs only what is affected)</a>; <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lV8-1S28ycM" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Uber Technology Day: Monorepo to Multirepo and Back Again (talk, 2017, cited by Wikipedia)</a>; <a href="https://github.com/Netflix/Hystrix" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Hystrix README (maintenance mode)</a>; <a href="https://github.com/resilience4j/resilience4j" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Resilience4j README</a>; <a href="https://martinfowler.com/bliki/CircuitBreaker.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Fowler: CircuitBreaker</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Circuit_breaker_design_pattern" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Circuit breaker design pattern</a>; <a href="https://www.envoyproxy.io/docs/envoy/latest/intro/arch_overview/upstream/circuit_breaking" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Envoy: Circuit breaking</a>; <a href="https://www.envoyproxy.io/docs/envoy/latest/intro/arch_overview/upstream/outlier" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Envoy: Outlier detection</a>; <a href="https://istio.io/latest/docs/tasks/traffic-management/circuit-breaking/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Istio: Circuit breaking task</a>; <a href="https://github.com/istio/api/blob/master/networking/v1alpha3/destination_rule.proto" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Istio API: DestinationRule</a>; <a href="https://github.com/eclipse/microprofile-fault-tolerance" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MicroProfile Fault Tolerance</a>.</p><p class="runcode">A question, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/enrichmeai.github.io/issues/new?title=%5BMicroservices%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>. Nothing here is tracked and there are no comments to store: questions live in the open, where the next learner can find the answer.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= CASE STUDIES ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-casestudies" data-name="Case Studies: How Tech Companies Evolved">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Case studies: how technology companies evolved</h2><p>Eight companies, and the turns their engineering took: Yahoo, a pioneer whose engineers helped build Hadoop while its own products lost ground; eBay, which split one auction site by function and by data and gave up distributed transactions to do it; Amazon, whose internal services became Amazon Web Services; Facebook, now Meta, which kept its PHP code and changed the language and runtime beneath it; Netflix, which moved to the cloud after an outage and then made failure part of its testing; GitHub, which kept one large Rails application and changed what was underneath it; Shopify, which split its data across databases; and Stack Overflow, whose biggest change came from outside its architecture. Each case gives what happened, from published sources; our view of it, labelled; another way to see it; what you can take from it, and when not to copy it; and a link to the company&rsquo;s own account. The patterns behind them are on <a href="#microservices">Microservices</a>, <a href="#atob">Solution Architecture: A to B</a> and <a href="#distsys">Distributed Systems</a>.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Eight directions</h3>
    <figure class="fig"><svg viewBox="0 0 640 540" role="img" aria-labelledby="cs-t cs-d"><title id="cs-t">Eight companies, 1994 to 2026</title><desc id="cs-d">A timeline with eight lanes. Yahoo: a web directory in January 1994, search from Google from June 2000, the Hadoop-based Search Webmap in February 2008, and its internet business sold to Verizon in June 2017. eBay: founded as AuctionWeb in September 1995; by 2008 its site was partitioned by function and by data, with no distributed transactions; in 2022 it described releasing more than 3,000 dependent libraries in about two hours. Amazon: first public web services in July 2002, Amazon S3 in March 2006 and EC2 in August 2006. Facebook, now Meta: launched in February 2004, announced HipHop for PHP in February 2010, released the Hack language in March 2014, and from February 2019 its HHVM runtime supported Hack only. Netflix: database corruption stops DVD shipping for three days in August 2008, Chaos Monkey in 2011, and a February 2016 post on completing the move to the cloud. GitHub: the upgrade from Rails 3.2 to 5.2 finishes in August 2018, a 50% load reduction from splitting its main database is reported in 2021, and weekly Rails upgrades are described in 2023. Shopify: founded in 2006, sharded its databases in 2014, and in 2025 described moving all its apps to React Native. Stack Overflow: launched in 2008, questions per month peaked in 2014, bought by Prosus in 2021, and by 2026 monthly questions were down by more than 99% from that peak.</desc><line class="ln" x1="135" y1="498" x2="620" y2="498"/><line class="ln" x1="150.0" y1="498" x2="150.0" y2="504"/><text class="lb mu" x="150.0" y="520" text-anchor="middle">1995</text><line class="ln" x1="225.0" y1="498" x2="225.0" y2="504"/><text class="lb mu" x="225.0" y="520" text-anchor="middle">2000</text><line class="ln" x1="300.0" y1="498" x2="300.0" y2="504"/><text class="lb mu" x="300.0" y="520" text-anchor="middle">2005</text><line class="ln" x1="375.0" y1="498" x2="375.0" y2="504"/><text class="lb mu" x="375.0" y="520" text-anchor="middle">2010</text><line class="ln" x1="450.0" y1="498" x2="450.0" y2="504"/><text class="lb mu" x="450.0" y="520" text-anchor="middle">2015</text><line class="ln" x1="525.0" y1="498" x2="525.0" y2="504"/><text class="lb mu" x="525.0" y="520" text-anchor="middle">2020</text><line class="ln" x1="600.0" y1="498" x2="600.0" y2="504"/><text class="lb mu" x="600.0" y="520" text-anchor="middle">2025</text><text class="lb hd" x="20" y="45" text-anchor="start">Yahoo</text><line class="ed" x1="135.7" y1="40" x2="486.8" y2="40"/><circle class="dot" cx="135.7" cy="40" r="5"/><text class="lb" x="135.7" y="28" text-anchor="start">directory</text><circle class="dot" cx="231.8" cy="40" r="5"/><text class="lb" x="231.8" y="62" text-anchor="middle">Google search</text><circle class="dot" cx="346.5" cy="40" r="5"/><text class="lb" x="346.5" y="28" text-anchor="middle">Hadoop Webmap</text><circle class="dot" cx="486.8" cy="40" r="5"/><text class="lb" x="486.8" y="62" text-anchor="middle">sold to Verizon</text><text class="lb hd" x="20" y="105" text-anchor="start">eBay</text><line class="ed" x1="160.1" y1="100" x2="559.0" y2="100"/><circle class="dot" cx="160.1" cy="100" r="5"/><text class="lb" x="160.1" y="88" text-anchor="start">founded</text><circle class="dot" cx="351.0" cy="100" r="5"/><text class="lb" x="351.0" y="122" text-anchor="middle">partitioned, no 2PC</text><circle class="dot" cx="559.0" cy="100" r="5"/><text class="lb" x="559.0" y="88" text-anchor="middle">3,000 libraries</text><text class="lb hd" x="20" y="165" text-anchor="start">Amazon</text><line class="ed" x1="263.2" y1="160" x2="321.0" y2="160"/><circle class="dot" cx="263.2" cy="160" r="5"/><text class="lb" x="263.2" y="148" text-anchor="middle">web services</text><circle class="dot" cx="321.0" cy="160" r="5"/><text class="lb" x="321.0" y="182" text-anchor="start">S3 and EC2</text><text class="lb hd" x="20" y="225" text-anchor="start">Facebook / Meta</text><line class="ed" x1="286.5" y1="220" x2="511.5" y2="220"/><circle class="dot" cx="286.5" cy="220" r="5"/><text class="lb" x="286.5" y="208" text-anchor="middle">launch</text><circle class="dot" cx="376.5" cy="220" r="5"/><text class="lb" x="376.5" y="242" text-anchor="middle">HipHop</text><circle class="dot" cx="438.0" cy="220" r="5"/><text class="lb" x="438.0" y="208" text-anchor="middle">Hack</text><circle class="dot" cx="511.5" cy="220" r="5"/><text class="lb" x="511.5" y="242" text-anchor="middle">HHVM: Hack only</text><text class="lb hd" x="20" y="285" text-anchor="start">Netflix</text><line class="ed" x1="354.0" y1="280" x2="466.5" y2="280"/><circle class="dot" cx="354.0" cy="280" r="5"/><text class="lb" x="354.0" y="268" text-anchor="end">outage, then AWS</text><circle class="dot" cx="397.5" cy="280" r="5"/><text class="lb" x="397.5" y="302" text-anchor="middle">Chaos Monkey</text><circle class="dot" cx="466.5" cy="280" r="5"/><text class="lb" x="466.5" y="268" text-anchor="start">migration complete</text><text class="lb hd" x="20" y="345" text-anchor="start">GitHub</text><line class="ed" x1="504.8" y1="340" x2="574.0" y2="340"/><circle class="dot" cx="504.8" cy="340" r="5"/><text class="lb" x="504.8" y="362" text-anchor="end">Rails 5.2</text><circle class="dot" cx="551.0" cy="340" r="5"/><text class="lb" x="551.0" y="362" text-anchor="start">50% less load</text><circle class="dot" cx="574.0" cy="340" r="5"/><text class="lb" x="574.0" y="328" text-anchor="end">weekly upgrades</text><text class="lb hd" x="20" y="405" text-anchor="start">Shopify</text><line class="ed" x1="315.0" y1="400" x2="600.7" y2="400"/><circle class="dot" cx="315.0" cy="400" r="5"/><text class="lb" x="315.0" y="388" text-anchor="middle">founded</text><circle class="dot" cx="435.0" cy="400" r="5"/><text class="lb" x="435.0" y="422" text-anchor="middle">sharding</text><circle class="dot" cx="600.7" cy="400" r="5"/><text class="lb" x="600.7" y="388" text-anchor="middle">React Native</text><text class="lb hd" x="20" y="465" text-anchor="start">Stack Overflow</text><line class="ed" x1="354.0" y1="460" x2="621.0" y2="460"/><circle class="dot" cx="354.0" cy="460" r="5"/><text class="lb" x="354.0" y="448" text-anchor="middle">launch</text><circle class="dot" cx="435.0" cy="460" r="5"/><text class="lb" x="435.0" y="482" text-anchor="middle">peak questions</text><circle class="dot" cx="546.8" cy="460" r="5"/><text class="lb" x="546.8" y="448" text-anchor="middle">Prosus</text><circle class="dot" cx="621.0" cy="460" r="5"/><text class="lb" x="621.0" y="482" text-anchor="end">&#8722;99%</text></svg><figcaption>The dates in the case studies below, each from the source cited with it. Eight different directions: Yahoo helped build tools the whole industry used while losing its own market, eBay split one site by function and by data and gave up distributed transactions, Facebook kept its PHP code and changed the language and runtime underneath it, Amazon turned its internal services into a product, Netflix moved to another company&rsquo;s cloud, GitHub kept one large Rails application and changed what was underneath it, Shopify split its data across databases, and Stack Overflow&rsquo;s biggest change came from outside its architecture.</figcaption></figure>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Yahoo: a pioneer that built the industry&rsquo;s tools and lost its own market</h3>
    <p><b>What happened.</b> In January 1994 Jerry Yang and David Filo, graduate students at Stanford, made &ldquo;Jerry and David&rsquo;s Guide to the World Wide Web&rdquo;, a human-edited directory organised in a hierarchy rather than a searchable index. Yahoo was incorporated in March 1995, added a search engine the same year, and became the first popular online directory and search engine on the web. Yahoo began using Google for its search in June 2000; over the next four years it built its own search technology, partly from Inktomi, which it bought for $280 million in 2002, and switched to it in 2004. Its engineers were central to <b>Hadoop</b>, the open-source system for storing and processing data across many machines that grew from Google&rsquo;s papers: in February 2008 Yahoo launched the Search Webmap, a Hadoop application on more than 10,000 cores whose data was used in every Yahoo web search. The same month Microsoft offered $44.6 billion for the company; Yahoo rejected it. In 2016 Yahoo disclosed two data breaches, one in 2013 affecting over a billion accounts and one in 2014 affecting over 500 million. Verizon announced in July 2016 that it would buy Yahoo&rsquo;s internet business for $4.83 billion and completed the purchase in June 2017; in 2021 funds managed by Apollo bought 90% of it. Wikipedia sums it up: one of the pioneers of the early internet, whose use declined in the 2010s as it lost market share to Facebook and Google.</p>
    <p><i>Our view:</i> Yahoo&rsquo;s engineering helped build tools much of the industry came to run on, Hadoop above all, while its own products lost users; good infrastructure did not decide the outcome. The breaches add a second lesson that ties to <a href="#quality">Quality Attributes</a>: security failures came to light during the company&rsquo;s sale, years after they happened.</p>
    <p><i>The other view:</i> Yahoo&rsquo;s decline is usually explained by strategy and leadership more than engineering (Wikipedia describes strategic missteps alongside the security incidents), so it is a weak guide to architecture decisions; it says more about product focus than about systems.</p>
    <p><b>What you can take</b> (<i>analysis</i>): know which part of your work is the product users choose you for, and which is infrastructure others could supply; and treat security as part of what a company is worth, not only a technical risk. <b>Don&rsquo;t copy it if</b> you are tempted to conclude &ldquo;build your own search&rdquo; or &ldquo;build your own platform&rdquo;: Yahoo did both, and the case does not show that either decided the outcome.</p>
    <p class="note">Sources: Wikipedia&rsquo;s articles on <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yahoo" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Yahoo</a> and <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apache_Hadoop" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Apache Hadoop</a>. We could not read a Yahoo engineering account from this environment.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Why Yahoo declined, and is it finished?</h3>
    <p>No single decision sank Yahoo. What the sources describe is a run of choices over about fifteen years. Wikipedia&rsquo;s summary: &ldquo;Yahoo&rsquo;s decline was punctuated by a series of strategic missteps and severe security incidents.&rdquo;</p>
    <div class="tbl"><table><thead><tr><th>When</th><th>The choice</th><th>What happened</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td>1998, 2002</td><td><b>Did not buy Google</b></td><td>In 1998 Larry Page and Sergey Brin offered to sell their search engine to Yahoo for $1 million, and Yahoo declined. In 2002 Terry Semel negotiated to buy Google for a reported $5 billion; Yahoo&rsquo;s final offer was $3 billion, which Google rejected. Wikipedia: that second miss &ldquo;is widely considered one of the largest strategic errors in corporate history&rdquo;.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>1999&ndash;2001</td><td><b>Bought big, at the top of the bubble</b></td><td>Yahoo grew into a <b>web portal</b>, a site meant to keep users inside it, and made its two biggest acquisitions in 1999: GeoCities for $3.6 billion and Broadcast.com for $5.7 billion. Its share price closed at $118.75 on 3 January 2000 and fell to $8.11 on 26 September 2001. GeoCities closed in October 2009.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2000&ndash;2010</td><td><b>Did not keep control of search</b></td><td>Google powered Yahoo&rsquo;s searches from June 2000. Yahoo switched to its own technology in February 2004, and from August 2010 its organic results came from Microsoft&rsquo;s Bing. In ten years Yahoo rented its search, built its own, then rented it again.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2004&ndash;2006</td><td><b>Sold trust in its results</b></td><td>A paid-inclusion programme guaranteed commercial sites a listing for a fee. Marketers resisted the fees, and the public disliked that paid listings could not be told apart from organic results; Yahoo scaled it back by October 2006.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2008</td><td><b>Turned down Microsoft</b></td><td>Yahoo rejected Microsoft&rsquo;s offer of $44.6 billion. Microsoft raised it to $47 billion; Yahoo held out for at least 10% more, and Microsoft withdrew in May 2008. Nine years later Verizon paid $4.48 billion for the internet business.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2001&ndash;2012</td><td><b>Changed leaders again and again</b></td><td>Terry Semel (2001), Jerry Yang (2007), Carol Bartz (2009, fired in 2011), Tim Morse (interim), Scott Thompson (January 2012), Ross Levinsohn (interim, May 2012) and Marissa Mayer (July 2012): seven chief executives in about eleven years, each with a new direction. The largest layoff in its history, 2,000 people, came in early 2012.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2013&ndash;2017</td><td><b>Bought audience, then lost trust</b></td><td>Under Mayer, Yahoo bought Tumblr in 2013 for $1.1 billion in cash. In 2016 it disclosed breaches from 2013 and 2014 covering over a billion and over 500 million accounts. Verizon cut its price by $350 million because of them, completing the purchase in June 2017.</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
    <p><b>The best deal it made.</b> In August 2005 Yahoo paid $1 billion for 40% of Alibaba. When Verizon bought the internet business, what remained of Yahoo Inc. was renamed <b>Altaba</b> in June 2017: an investment company holding the Alibaba and Yahoo Japan stakes. It sold them and dissolved in October 2019. For its last years, the company&rsquo;s stock market value rested more on that 2005 investment than on Yahoo&rsquo;s own products (<i>analysis</i>).</p>
    <p><b>Is it finished?</b> No, but it is smaller and private. Funds managed by Apollo Global Management bought 90% of Yahoo from Verizon in September 2021 (Verizon kept 10%). Yahoo left mainland China in November 2021 and cut about 1,000 of its 8,600 staff in 2023. It still runs Yahoo Mail, News, Finance, Sports and Search, and an advertising business.</p>
    <p><i>Our view:</i> the common thread is that Yahoo never settled what it was. It treated itself as a media portal while the web moved to search and then to social networks, and it bought and rented the core capability (search) instead of owning it. The breaches show the cost of treating security as overhead: $350 million came off the sale price. <i>The other view:</i> this reading leans on hindsight. In 1998 and 2002 nobody knew how valuable search advertising would become, the board could argue that in 2008 it was holding out for a fair price, and some argue that a portal company could not have turned into Google or Facebook whoever was in charge. Seen that way, Yahoo is less a lesson in mistakes than in how fast a market leader&rsquo;s market can move.</p>
    <p><b>What engineers can take</b> (<i>analysis</i>): know the one capability your users choose you for, and keep it in your own hands; and treat security as part of what the business is worth, because it is priced when the business is sold.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>eBay: split by function and by data, and no distributed transactions</h3>
    <p><b>What happened.</b> Pierre Omidyar founded eBay as AuctionWeb in September 1995, as a hobby site; it took the name eBay in 1997. By 2008 its architect Randy Shoup described a site with over two billion page views a day, and the practices it had learned to scale it. eBay <b>partitioned by function</b>: selling, bidding and search ran on separate pools of application servers (&ldquo;roughly 16,000 application servers into 220 different pools&rdquo;), and there was &ldquo;no single monolithic database&rdquo;, but separate databases for users, items and purchases, &ldquo;1000 logical databases in all, on 400 physical hosts&rdquo;. It then <b>split each one horizontally</b>: user data, for example, was divided over 20 hosts, &ldquo;if you can&rsquo;t split it, you can&rsquo;t scale it&rdquo;. Because listing one new item could touch &ldquo;twenty or thirty different hosts&rdquo;, it allowed &ldquo;absolutely no client-side or distributed transactions of any kind - no two-phase commit&rdquo;, and reached eventual consistency instead through careful ordering of database operations, asynchronous recovery events and reconciliation batches. Its other practices were to decouple components asynchronously, move work onto asynchronous flows, put a layer of indirection in front of databases and search, and cache with care. InfoQ&rsquo;s 2022 report on eBay recalls two of its vice-presidents, Shoup and Mark Weinberg, describing &ldquo;systemic challenges with releasing major dependencies, leading to the equivalent of distributed monoliths&rdquo;, and says eBay&rsquo;s engineers built a system to release more than 3,000 dependent libraries in about two hours.</p>
    <p><i>Our view:</i> eBay is the older, pre-cloud version of the story many companies tell about microservices: split by business function, split again by key, and treat consistency as a choice per operation rather than all or nothing. The 2022 report shows the cost that follows: once the code is split, the dependencies between the parts become the thing to manage.</p>
    <p><i>The other view:</i> eBay&rsquo;s scale was rare, and giving up transactions moves work into code that orders writes, retries and reconciles; most systems can keep ACID transactions in one database for a long time, and should until a measured limit says otherwise (see <a href="#distsys">Distributed Systems</a> and <a href="#database">Database Design</a>).</p>
    <p><b>What you can take</b> (<i>analysis</i>): decide per operation how much consistency it really needs; split data along the path it is read; prefer asynchronous hand-offs between parts that can wait. <b>Don&rsquo;t copy it if</b> one database still handles your load: distributed data without distributed transactions is a lot of careful code to own.</p>
    <p class="note">Their own accounts: <a href="https://www.infoq.com/articles/ebay-scalability-best-practices/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Randy Shoup, &ldquo;Scalability Best Practices: Lessons from eBay&rdquo;</a> (InfoQ, May 2008); <a href="https://www.infoq.com/news/2008/06/interview-shoup-ebay/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">interview with Randy Shoup</a> (InfoQ, June 2008, recorded at QCon San Francisco 2007); <a href="https://www.infoq.com/presentations/shoup-ebay-architectural-principles/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">&ldquo;eBay&rsquo;s Architectural Principles&rdquo;</a> (talk, InfoQ, February 2008); and the 2022 report, <a href="https://www.infoq.com/news/2022/04/distributed-arch-ebay/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">&ldquo;Managing Complex Dependencies with Distributed Architecture at eBay&rdquo;</a> (InfoQ, April 2022).</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Amazon: internal services that became AWS</h3>
    <p><b>What happened.</b> In the early 2000s Amazon pursued service-oriented architecture as a way to scale its engineering, and was frustrated with the speed of its software engineering. An engineering leader, Matt Round, recommended giving engineering teams as much autonomy as possible, adopting REST, standardising infrastructure, removing gate-keeping decision-makers, and continuous deployment. In July 2002 Amazon launched its first public web services, opening the Amazon.com platform to developers; over a hundred applications were built on it by 2004, which convinced Amazon that developers were &ldquo;hungry for more&rdquo;. Andy Jassy then mapped out an &ldquo;Internet OS&rdquo; of basic infrastructure building blocks. Amazon S3 storage launched on 14 March 2006, the first generally available AWS infrastructure service, followed by EC2 compute in August 2006.</p>
    <p><i>Our view:</i> AWS grew out of work to make Amazon&rsquo;s own engineering faster, and outside developers&rsquo; demand for the 2002 web services confirmed there was a market for it. The lesson we draw is to build the platform your own teams need, and to treat outside demand as evidence.</p>
    <p><i>The other view:</i> few companies have Amazon&rsquo;s scale, so the same decomposition into many independent services can cost a smaller organisation more in coordination than it saves; the conditions that made it pay off at Amazon may not hold elsewhere.</p>
    <p><b>What you can take</b> (<i>analysis</i>): standard, automated building blocks with clear interfaces let teams move without waiting on each other. <b>Don&rsquo;t copy it if</b> you have a few teams: the coordination it removes may be smaller than the overhead it adds.</p>
    <p class="note">Their own account: <a href="https://www.allthingsdistributed.com/2019/08/modern-applications-at-aws.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Werner Vogels, &ldquo;Modern applications at AWS&rdquo;</a> (All Things Distributed, August 2019)<sup>&dagger;</sup>.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Facebook (Meta): kept the PHP, changed the language and runtime underneath</h3>
    <p><b>What happened.</b> Mark Zuckerberg launched &ldquo;TheFacebook&rdquo; on 4 February 2004, and facebook.com grew on a large PHP codebase. Rather than rewrite it in another language, Facebook changed what ran it. In February 2010 it announced <b>HipHop for PHP</b>, which translated PHP into C++ and compiled it; Wikipedia says its original motivation was to save resources on Facebook&rsquo;s servers. It then built the <b>HipHop Virtual Machine (HHVM)</b>, a just-in-time compiler, because HipHop had hit a plateau for performance and could not support all of PHP; HHVM matched HipHop&rsquo;s performance on facebook.com in the last quarter of 2012, and Facebook deprecated HipHop in early 2013. In March 2014 Facebook released <b>Hack</b>, a PHP dialect with gradual static typing: &ldquo;Over the last year, we have migrated nearly our entire PHP codebase to Hack, thanks to both organic adoption and a number of homegrown refactoring tools&rdquo;, it wrote. Type checking was incremental, so a file could be partly converted. The post gives the reason for the work: at Facebook scale, &ldquo;with thousands of engineers shipping new code twice a day&rdquo;, errors that a dynamic language only shows at run time slowed everyone down. From HHVM 4.0, in February 2019, HHVM supported Hack only. Along the way Facebook also built and open-sourced tools that spread far beyond it, among them the Cassandra database (open-sourced in July 2008) and React (May 2013).</p>
    <p><i>Our view:</i> Facebook treated its language as something it could change, a little at a time, instead of something to escape with a rewrite: first a faster way to run the same code, then types that could be added file by file while the site kept shipping. It is the same idea as GitHub&rsquo;s weekly upgrades, applied to the language itself.</p>
    <p><i>The other view:</i> building your own compiler, runtime and language needs a scale and a team that almost nobody has, and it leaves you maintaining a language few others use (HHVM dropped PHP support in 2019); in our view, for most teams, adding the type hints or type checker their own language already offers costs far less.</p>
    <p><b>What you can take</b> (<i>analysis</i>): when a codebase is too large to rewrite, look for changes that can be adopted gradually, with tools that do most of the work, while everything keeps running; types added file by file are one such change. <b>Don&rsquo;t copy it if</b> your language or a standard tool already gives you that path, as Python&rsquo;s type hints and TypeScript do for their languages.</p>
    <p class="note">Their own account: <a href="https://engineering.fb.com/2014/03/20/developer-tools/hack-a-new-programming-language-for-hhvm/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">&ldquo;Hack: a new programming language for HHVM&rdquo;</a> (Engineering at Meta, March 2014). The HipHop and HHVM history is from Wikipedia, which cites Facebook&rsquo;s own posts.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Netflix: to the cloud after an outage, then failure on purpose</h3>
    <p><b>What happened.</b> In August 2008 Netflix&rsquo;s database was corrupted and the company could not ship DVDs to customers for three days, which led it to move all its data to the Amazon Web Services cloud; Netflix published &ldquo;Completing the Netflix Cloud Migration&rdquo; in February 2016, and by August 2016 it had closed its last physical data centre. During the migration, in 2011, Netflix built <b>Chaos Monkey</b>, a tool that randomly chooses a server in production and disables it during its usual hours of activity, to move from assuming nothing breaks to treating breakdowns as inevitable. It released the code in 2012, and Chaos Monkey became part of a wider &ldquo;Simian Army&rdquo;, which includes Chaos Kong, simulating the loss of a whole AWS region. Netflix&rsquo;s Chaos Monkey repository describes the idea: &ldquo;Exposing engineers to failures more frequently incentivizes them to build resilient services.&rdquo;</p>
    <p>Its circuit-breaker library <b>Hystrix</b> is now in maintenance mode. Netflix&rsquo;s README says its focus moved to adaptive approaches that react to real-time performance, and that it uses resilience4j for new internal projects.</p>
    <p><i>Our view:</i> the outage showed that one database was a single point of failure; testing failure in production made resilience something checked every day rather than assumed. Netflix moving its own Hystrix to maintenance mode shows that a company&rsquo;s tools can change while the idea behind them stays.</p>
    <p><i>The other view:</i> deliberately breaking production needs mature monitoring, automation and on-call practice first; without those, chaos experiments add risk, so many teams start in a test environment, or with game days, and grow from there (see <a href="#teststrat">Test Strategy</a> and <a href="#sre">SRE</a>).</p>
    <p><b>What you can take</b> (<i>analysis</i>): find your single points of failure before an outage does, and test failure regularly, starting small. <b>Don&rsquo;t copy it if</b> you cannot yet see what your system is doing: monitoring and quick rollback come before deliberate failure.</p>
    <p class="note">Their own accounts: <a href="https://about.netflix.com/en/news/completing-the-netflix-cloud-migration" target="_blank" rel="noopener">&ldquo;Completing the Netflix Cloud Migration&rdquo;</a> (Netflix, February 2016)<sup>&dagger;</sup>; <a href="https://medium.com/netflix-techblog/the-netflix-simian-army-16e57fbab116" target="_blank" rel="noopener">&ldquo;The Netflix Simian Army&rdquo;</a> (Netflix Technology Blog, July 2011)<sup>&dagger;</sup>; <a href="https://github.com/Netflix/chaosmonkey" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Chaos Monkey</a> and <a href="https://github.com/Netflix/Hystrix" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Hystrix</a> on GitHub.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>GitHub: one Rails monolith, kept and scaled</h3>
    <p><b>What happened.</b> GitHub.com has been a Ruby on Rails monolith since the beginning. In 2023 GitHub wrote that it is nearly two million lines of code, that more than 1,000 engineers work on it daily, that it deploys as often as 20 times a day, and that nearly every week one of those deploys is a Rails upgrade. It was not always so: in 2018 the upgrade from Rails 3.2 to 5.2 took a year and a half, on a codebase then ten years old. Its data followed a similar path: GitHub.com was built around one main MySQL cluster, <code>mysql1</code>, and any incident there affected every feature stored on it. From 2019 GitHub grouped tables into &ldquo;schema domains&rdquo;, enforced the boundaries with SQL linters, and then moved them to separate clusters; by 2021 load on the hosts holding the former <code>mysql1</code> data had fallen by 50%, with fewer database incidents.</p>
    <p><i>Our view:</i> GitHub kept the single application and drew boundaries inside it instead, first in the data, enforced by tools, before moving anything physically. Upgrading a little every week turned a year-and-a-half project into routine work.</p>
    <p><i>The other view:</i> a monolith this large needs heavy investment in tooling, testing and deployment to stay workable, and teams still share one codebase and one release train; organisations that want teams to choose their own languages and release on their own may still prefer separate services.</p>
    <p><b>What you can take</b> (<i>analysis</i>): draw boundaries inside the application first, and enforce them with tools; upgrade little and often instead of rarely and painfully. <b>Don&rsquo;t copy it if</b> your teams truly need independent releases and different languages: then separate services may fit better.</p>
    <p class="note">Their own accounts: <a href="https://github.blog/2018-09-28-upgrading-github-from-rails-3-2-to-5-2/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">&ldquo;Upgrading GitHub from Rails 3.2 to 5.2&rdquo;</a> (Eileen M. Uchitelle, September 2018); <a href="https://github.blog/2021-09-27-partitioning-githubs-relational-databases-scale/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">&ldquo;Partitioning GitHub&rsquo;s relational databases to handle scale&rdquo;</a> (Thomas Maurer, September 2021); <a href="https://github.blog/2023-04-06-building-github-with-ruby-and-rails/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">&ldquo;Building GitHub with Ruby and Rails&rdquo;</a> (Adam Hess, April 2023).</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Shopify: built on Rails, scaled by splitting its data</h3>
    <p><b>What happened.</b> Shopify was founded in 2006. According to Wikipedia, Tobias L&uuml;tke had built the online snowboard shop Snowdevil with Ruby on Rails, and the platform grew from it. Shopify created Liquid, an open-source template language written in Ruby, so shops could change their pages safely. As it grew, Shopify introduced sharding in 2014 to spread its data across multiple databases, and later moved to fully isolated instances. Shopify also publishes <b>Packwerk</b>, a Ruby gem &ldquo;used to enforce boundaries and modularize Rails applications&rdquo;: it groups files into packages, controls which constants a package makes public, and is meant to help existing codebases become more modular &ldquo;without obstructing development&rdquo;. On mobile, Shopify wrote in 2025 that over five years it had moved all its apps to React Native, so the same code could serve every platform.</p>
    <p><i>Our view:</i> when one database became the limit, Shopify split the data, first into shards and later into isolated instances, which changes how much one problem can affect. Packwerk shows a second idea it has published for Rails code in general: make the boundaries between parts of an application explicit and checked, before deciding whether to split it. (The sources we could read do not say how Shopify applies Packwerk to its own main application.)</p>
    <p><i>The other view:</i> sharding and isolated instances add their own operational work, such as moving data between shards and answering questions that span them; some teams prefer one larger database for as long as it lasts, to keep that work away.</p>
    <p><b>What you can take</b> (<i>analysis</i>): split data by a natural key (for Shopify, the shop) when one database is the limit; make module boundaries explicit and checked by a tool, as Packwerk does for Rails, before deciding whether to split the code. <b>Don&rsquo;t copy it if</b> your data has no natural key that keeps most queries in one place: sharding then makes everyday queries harder.</p>
    <p class="note">Their own accounts: <a href="https://shopify.engineering/e-commerce-at-scale-inside-shopifys-tech-stack" target="_blank" rel="noopener">&ldquo;E-Commerce at Scale: Inside Shopify&rsquo;s Tech Stack&rdquo;</a> (Shopify Engineering)<sup>&dagger;</sup>; <a href="https://shopify.engineering/five-years-of-react-native-at-shopify" target="_blank" rel="noopener">&ldquo;Five years of React Native at Shopify&rdquo;</a> (Mustafa Ali, January 2025)<sup>&dagger;</sup>; <a href="https://github.com/Shopify/packwerk" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Packwerk</a> on GitHub.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Stack Overflow: its own data-access library, and a change in demand</h3>
    <p><b>What happened.</b> Jeff Atwood and Joel Spolsky created Stack Overflow in 2008. It is written in C# with ASP.NET MVC, uses Microsoft SQL Server, and accesses data through <b>Dapper</b>, &ldquo;a simple object mapper for .Net&rdquo; that was originally developed for and by Stack Overflow, and which its team advertises as fast; Wikipedia cites Sam Saffron&rsquo;s 2011 post &ldquo;How I learned to stop worrying and write my own ORM&rdquo; for it. In June 2021 Prosus agreed to buy the company for $1.8 billion. Then the questions fell: from over 100,000 a month in 2022 to a few thousand by early 2026, and by June 2026 more than 99% below the 2014 peak. Wikipedia attributes the fall to the arrival of AI coding tools and, according to some, a moderation culture seen as hostile. In the 2026 Stack Overflow Developer Survey, most respondents said they visit less often for simple questions, while a small group visit more to check answers from generative AI.</p>
    <p><i>Our view:</i> two lessons sit side by side. The engineering one is an inference: Stack Overflow built, and open-sourced, its own small data-access library instead of adopting a larger one, which we read as a choice to keep a stack its team could understand fully (Saffron&rsquo;s post, which we could not open, gives their own reasons). The bigger one: the largest change in the company&rsquo;s recent history came mainly from outside its architecture, from AI tools and, according to some, from a moderation culture, which is a community and product choice rather than an engineering one.</p>
    <p><i>The other view:</i> writing your own data-access layer is a cost few teams can justify, since well-supported general tools exist; and the decline may say less about engineering than about a product whose value, answering simple questions, moved to AI tools.</p>
    <p><b>What you can take</b> (<i>analysis</i>): watch how your users&rsquo; habits change, not only your system&rsquo;s metrics; a healthy system can still lose its reason to exist. A small stack you understand end to end is easier to change when that happens. <b>Don&rsquo;t copy it if</b> a well-supported general tool already meets your needs: writing and maintaining your own library then costs more than it saves.</p>
    <p class="note">Their own accounts: <a href="http://samsaffron.com/archive/2011/03/30/How+I+learned+to+stop+worrying+and+write+my+own+ORM" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sam Saffron, &ldquo;How I learned to stop worrying and write my own ORM&rdquo;</a> (March 2011)<sup>&dagger;</sup>; <a href="https://github.com/DapperLib/Dapper" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Dapper</a> on GitHub; <a href="https://survey.stackoverflow.co/2026/community/data/so-change" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Stack Overflow Developer Survey 2026</a>.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Side by side (<i>analysis</i>)</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th></th><th>What forced the change</th><th>What they changed</th><th>What they kept</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td><b>Yahoo</b></td><td>Search moving to Google, and users moving to Google and Facebook</td><td>Its own search (2004), Hadoop; then sold, in 2017</td><td>The brand and its portal services</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>eBay</b></td><td>Load that no single server or database could carry</td><td>Function split into pools and databases, data split by key; no distributed transactions</td><td>Consistency where an operation needs it, chosen per use case</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Amazon</b></td><td>Frustration with the speed of its software engineering</td><td>Teams and services with more autonomy; then infrastructure offered as a product</td><td>Amazon.com itself, which the services and infrastructure were first built for</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Facebook / Meta</b></td><td>A large PHP codebase that cost too much to run and was hard to check before it ran</td><td>The compiler, then the runtime, then the language (gradual types)</td><td>The PHP code itself, converted gradually rather than rewritten</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Netflix</b></td><td>A database corruption that stopped shipping for three days</td><td>All its data to AWS, its last data centre closed by 2016; failure tested in production</td><td>The ideas behind its tools, even as Hystrix moved to maintenance mode</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>GitHub</b></td><td>One database cluster whose incidents affected every feature, and a ten-year-old codebase on old framework versions</td><td>Data split by domain; upgrades made weekly</td><td>One Rails application</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Shopify</b></td><td>One application and its data serving ever more shops</td><td>Data split into shards, then isolated instances; one mobile codebase in React Native</td><td>Not stated in the sources we could read</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Stack Overflow</b></td><td>Far fewer questions: AI tools and, according to some, moderation culture</td><td>Not mainly its architecture: the change was in demand</td><td>C#, SQL Server, and its own small data-access library</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note"><i>Our view:</i> in six of the eight cases the change followed a concrete engineering problem, and each kept something stable while it changed the rest; Yahoo and Stack Overflow show the kind of change engineering cannot steer. <i>The other view:</i> these are well-documented successes; the companies that made the same moves and struggled write fewer blog posts, so lessons drawn only from success stories can mislead.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Choose by requirement</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>If this is true for you</th><th>A case to read (<i>analysis</i>)</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>One database can no longer carry the load, and some operations can wait</td><td>eBay: partition by function and key; consistency chosen per operation</td></tr>
      <tr><td>A large codebase in a dynamic language is hard to change safely, and too big to rewrite</td><td>Facebook: types added file by file, with tools doing the conversion</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Many teams are slowed by waiting on each other</td><td>Amazon: autonomy and service boundaries</td></tr>
      <tr><td>One component failing takes everything down</td><td>Netflix&rsquo;s outage, and GitHub&rsquo;s <code>mysql1</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>You want confidence that failures are survivable</td><td>Netflix: chaos engineering</td></tr>
      <tr><td>One large application works, but is slow to upgrade</td><td>GitHub: weekly upgrades and schema domains</td></tr>
      <tr><td>One database is the limit, and data has a natural key</td><td>Shopify: sharding by shop</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Your users&rsquo; habits are changing faster than your product</td><td>Stack Overflow: the fall in questions; Yahoo: users moving to Google and Facebook</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Your team already runs one well-understood application</td><td>GitHub: boundaries inside it before splitting it, since splitting has a cost (and Shopify&rsquo;s Packwerk for Rails)</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>About the sources</h3>
    <p>The facts on this page come from Wikipedia&rsquo;s articles on Yahoo, Apache Hadoop, eBay, Facebook, HipHop for PHP, HHVM, Hack, Apache Cassandra, React, Amazon Web Services, Netflix, chaos engineering, Shopify, Stack Overflow and Dapper; from Randy Shoup&rsquo;s 2008 InfoQ article and interview on eBay, InfoQ&rsquo;s 2022 report on eBay&rsquo;s releases, and Facebook&rsquo;s 2014 post introducing Hack, which we read in full; from the Netflix, Shopify (Packwerk) and Dapper repositories on GitHub; and from GitHub&rsquo;s own blog posts, which we read in full.</p>
    <p class="note"><sup>&dagger;</sup> Links marked with a dagger are the company&rsquo;s own posts as cited by Wikipedia; we could not open them from the environment this page was checked in, so the facts above do not rest on them alone.</p>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Retell one company's engineering change and what caused it</li><li>Say what a single point of failure is, with an example</li><li>Find a company's own account of a change</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Explain why Netflix tests failure in production</li><li>Explain how GitHub drew boundaries inside one application</li><li>Separate what a case study shows from what it suggests</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Judge whether a company's approach fits your organisation's size and needs</li><li>Plan a change that keeps one thing stable</li><li>Spot survivorship bias in success stories</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Use case studies to argue for or against an architecture change</li><li>Write your own organisation's case study honestly</li><li>Learn from failures as well as successes</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>Yahoo: a directory, then search, then Hadoop</li><li>eBay: partitioning by function and by key, no distributed transactions</li><li>Facebook: HipHop, HHVM and Hack</li><li>Amazon: service-oriented architecture to AWS</li><li>Netflix: the 2008 outage and the move to AWS</li><li>Chaos engineering and the Simian Army</li><li>GitHub: a Rails monolith at scale</li><li>Schema domains and database partitioning</li><li>Weekly framework upgrades</li><li>Shopify: sharding and Packwerk</li><li>Stack Overflow: Dapper, and a change in demand</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://github.blog/engineering/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub Engineering blog</a><span class="lnote"> GitHub's own engineering posts</span></li><li><a href="https://github.com/Netflix/chaosmonkey" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Netflix Chaos Monkey</a><span class="lnote"> Netflix's repository</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yahoo" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Yahoo</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apache_Hadoop" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Apache Hadoop</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/EBay" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: eBay</a>; <a href="https://www.infoq.com/articles/ebay-scalability-best-practices/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Randy Shoup, Scalability Best Practices: Lessons from eBay (InfoQ, 2008)</a>; <a href="https://www.infoq.com/news/2008/06/interview-shoup-ebay/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">InfoQ interview with Randy Shoup (2008)</a>; <a href="https://www.infoq.com/news/2022/04/distributed-arch-ebay/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">InfoQ: Managing Complex Dependencies with Distributed Architecture at eBay (2022)</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Facebook" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Facebook</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/HipHop_for_PHP" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: HipHop for PHP</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/HHVM" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: HHVM</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hack_(programming_language)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Hack</a>; <a href="https://engineering.fb.com/2014/03/20/developer-tools/hack-a-new-programming-language-for-hhvm/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Engineering at Meta: Hack, a new programming language for HHVM (2014)</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apache_Cassandra" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Apache Cassandra</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/React_(software)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: React</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amazon_Web_Services" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Amazon Web Services</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Netflix" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Netflix</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chaos_engineering" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Chaos engineering</a>; <a href="https://github.com/Netflix/chaosmonkey" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Netflix/chaosmonkey README</a>; <a href="https://github.com/Netflix/Hystrix" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Netflix/Hystrix README</a>; <a href="https://github.blog/2018-09-28-upgrading-github-from-rails-3-2-to-5-2/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub blog: Upgrading GitHub from Rails 3.2 to 5.2</a>; <a href="https://github.blog/2021-09-27-partitioning-githubs-relational-databases-scale/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub blog: Partitioning GitHub's relational databases to handle scale</a>; <a href="https://github.blog/2023-04-06-building-github-with-ruby-and-rails/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub blog: Building GitHub with Ruby and Rails</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shopify" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Shopify</a>; <a href="https://github.com/Shopify/packwerk" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Shopify/packwerk README</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stack_Overflow" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Stack Overflow</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dapper_ORM" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Dapper ORM</a>; <a href="https://github.com/DapperLib/Dapper" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Dapper README</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yahoo" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Yahoo</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_Yahoo!" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: History of Yahoo</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Altaba" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Altaba</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yahoo_data_breaches" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Yahoo data breaches</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GeoCities" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: GeoCities</a>.</p><p class="runcode">A question, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/enrichmeai.github.io/issues/new?title=%5BCase%20Studies%3A%20How%20Tech%20Companies%20Evolved%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>. Nothing here is tracked and there are no comments to store: questions live in the open, where the next learner can find the answer.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= EVENT SOURCING ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-eventsourcing" data-name="Event Sourcing">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Event sourcing and CQRS</h2><p>Storing every change as an immutable event and deriving current state from them. How the event store, aggregates and projections work, how to evolve events, and when the extra complexity is worth it.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>The idea</h3>
    <p>Most systems store <b>current state</b> and overwrite it: a subscription row says <code>plan = 'pro'</code>. Event sourcing stores the <b>sequence of facts</b> instead: <code>SubscriptionStarted(basic)</code>, <code>PlanUpgraded(pro)</code>, <code>PaymentFailed</code>, <code>PaymentSucceeded</code>. Current state is computed by replaying (folding) those events. The events are the source of truth; every other view is derived from them and can be rebuilt.</p>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Event</td><td>An immutable fact in the past tense, with data and metadata (who, when, causation)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Stream</td><td>The ordered events of one entity, e.g. <code>subscription-42</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Aggregate</td><td>The entity that enforces business rules; rebuilt from its stream; decides which new events a command produces</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Command</td><td>A request to change something (<code>UpgradePlan</code>); may be rejected</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Event store</td><td>Append-only storage of streams with per-stream versions</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Projection / read model</td><td>A view built from events for querying (a table, a search index, a cache)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Snapshot</td><td>Saved aggregate state at a version, to avoid replaying long streams</td></tr>
      <tr><td>CQRS</td><td>Separate models for writes (commands) and reads (queries); event sourcing usually comes with it</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>When it is worth it</h3>
    <p><b>Good fits</b></p>
    <ul>
      <li>History is the product: audit trails, finance, legal records, compliance.</li>
      <li>You need to answer "what was the state on date X, and why?"</li>
      <li>Several different read models from the same changes.</li>
      <li>Complex domains where business events are first-class (orders, claims, subscriptions).</li>
    </ul>
    <p><b>Poor fits</b></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Simple CRUD with no interest in history.</li>
      <li>Teams new to the pattern on a deadline.</li>
      <li>Data that must be truly deleted on request with no workaround.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">Apply it to the one part of the system that needs it, not the whole system.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>The flow</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>A <b>command</b> arrives: <code>UpgradePlan(subscription-42, pro)</code>.</li>
      <li>Load the aggregate's events (from the latest snapshot) and <b>fold</b> them into current state.</li>
      <li>The aggregate checks business rules and <b>decides</b> zero or more new events.</li>
      <li><b>Append</b> them with the expected version (optimistic concurrency). If someone else appended first, reload and retry.</li>
      <li><b>Projections</b> consume new events and update read models.</li>
      <li><b>Queries</b> read from the read models, which may lag slightly behind.</li>
    </ol>
      <figure class="fig"><svg viewBox="0 0 660 270" role="img" aria-labelledby="esflow-t esflow-d"><title id="esflow-t">The event sourcing flow</title><desc id="esflow-d">Six numbered steps. 1: a command arrives, UpgradePlan for subscription 42. 2: the aggregate's past events are loaded, from the latest snapshot, and folded into its current state. 3: the aggregate checks the business rules and decides new events. 4: the events are appended with the expected version; if someone else appended first, the write is refused and the command reloads and retries. 5: projections consume the new events and update read models. 6: queries read the read models, which may lag slightly behind.</desc><defs><marker id="esflow-a" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker><marker id="esflow-aa" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar acc" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker></defs><rect class="nd in" x="20" y="40" width="180" height="54" rx="6"/><text class="lb hd" x="110" y="63" text-anchor="middle">command</text><text class="lb mu" x="110" y="82" text-anchor="middle">UpgradePlan(42, pro)</text><circle class="nd in" cx="30" cy="40" r="9"/><text class="lb mu" x="30" y="44" text-anchor="middle">1</text><line class="ed" x1="200" y1="67" x2="236" y2="67" marker-end="url(#esflow-a)"/><rect class="nd" x="240" y="40" width="180" height="54" rx="6"/><text class="lb hd" x="330" y="63" text-anchor="middle">load + fold</text><text class="lb mu" x="330" y="82" text-anchor="middle">events → state</text><circle class="nd in" cx="250" cy="40" r="9"/><text class="lb mu" x="250" y="44" text-anchor="middle">2</text><line class="ed" x1="420" y1="67" x2="456" y2="67" marker-end="url(#esflow-a)"/><rect class="nd" x="460" y="40" width="180" height="54" rx="6"/><text class="lb hd" x="550" y="63" text-anchor="middle">decide</text><text class="lb mu" x="550" y="82" text-anchor="middle">rules → new events</text><circle class="nd in" cx="470" cy="40" r="9"/><text class="lb mu" x="470" y="44" text-anchor="middle">3</text><rect class="nd" x="460" y="170" width="180" height="54" rx="6"/><text class="lb hd" x="550" y="193" text-anchor="middle">append</text><text class="lb mu" x="550" y="212" text-anchor="middle">if version still N</text><circle class="nd in" cx="470" cy="170" r="9"/><text class="lb mu" x="470" y="174" text-anchor="middle">4</text><line class="ed" x1="460" y1="197" x2="424" y2="197" marker-end="url(#esflow-a)"/><rect class="nd" x="240" y="170" width="180" height="54" rx="6"/><text class="lb hd" x="330" y="193" text-anchor="middle">projections</text><text class="lb mu" x="330" y="212" text-anchor="middle">update read models</text><circle class="nd in" cx="250" cy="170" r="9"/><text class="lb mu" x="250" y="174" text-anchor="middle">5</text><line class="ed" x1="240" y1="197" x2="204" y2="197" marker-end="url(#esflow-a)"/><rect class="nd out" x="20" y="170" width="180" height="54" rx="6"/><text class="lb hd" x="110" y="193" text-anchor="middle">queries</text><text class="lb mu" x="110" y="212" text-anchor="middle">read models</text><circle class="nd in" cx="30" cy="170" r="9"/><text class="lb mu" x="30" y="174" text-anchor="middle">6</text><path class="ed" d="M550,94 L550,166" marker-end="url(#esflow-a)"/><path class="ed dash acc" d="M640,197 C665,150 665,110 330,104" marker-end="url(#esflow-aa)"/><text class="lb acc" x="470" y="130" text-anchor="middle">conflict: reload and retry</text><text class="lb mu" x="110" y="252" text-anchor="middle">may lag slightly behind the events</text></svg><figcaption>The six steps above, drawn: along the top, then back along the bottom. The dashed arrow is optimistic concurrency: an append that finds a newer version is refused, and the command starts again from fresh events.</figcaption></figure>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>An aggregate in Java</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>sealed interface SubEvent permits Started, PlanUpgraded, Cancelled {}
record Started(String id, String plan, Instant at)      implements SubEvent {}
record PlanUpgraded(String id, String plan, Instant at) implements SubEvent {}
record Cancelled(String id, String reason, Instant at)  implements SubEvent {}

enum Status { ACTIVE, CANCELLED }

record Subscription(String id, String plan, Status status, int version) {

  static Subscription replay(List&lt;SubEvent&gt; events) {
    Subscription s = null;
    for (SubEvent e : events) s = apply(s, e);
    return s;
  }

  static Subscription apply(Subscription s, SubEvent e) {     // pure: no side effects
    return switch (e) {
      case Started st     -&gt; new Subscription(st.id(), st.plan(), Status.ACTIVE, 1);
      case PlanUpgraded u -&gt; new Subscription(s.id(), u.plan(), s.status(), s.version() + 1);
      case Cancelled c    -&gt; new Subscription(s.id(), s.plan(), Status.CANCELLED, s.version() + 1);
    };
  }

  List&lt;SubEvent&gt; upgrade(String newPlan, Instant now) {        // decide
    if (status != Status.ACTIVE) throw new IllegalStateException("subscription not active");
    if (plan.equals(newPlan)) return List.of();                 // idempotent: nothing to do
    return List.of(new PlanUpgraded(id, newPlan, now));
  }
}</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">Separate <i>decide</i> (rules, may reject) from <i>apply</i> (pure state change). Apply must never fail for a stored event: it already happened.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>An event store in Postgres</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>CREATE TABLE events (
  global_position bigint GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,
  stream_id   text        NOT NULL,
  version     int         NOT NULL,
  type        text        NOT NULL,
  data        jsonb       NOT NULL,
  metadata    jsonb       NOT NULL DEFAULT '{}',   -- user, causation_id, correlation_id
  recorded_at timestamptz NOT NULL DEFAULT now(),
  UNIQUE (stream_id, version)                      -- optimistic concurrency
);

-- load a stream
SELECT type, data, version FROM events
WHERE stream_id = 'subscription-42' ORDER BY version;

-- append, expecting the stream to be at version 3
INSERT INTO events (stream_id, version, type, data)
VALUES ('subscription-42', 4, 'PlanUpgraded', '{"plan":"pro"}');
-- unique violation → another writer got version 4 first → reload, re-decide, retry</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">Caution: identity values are assigned at insert, not commit, so with concurrent writers a projection reading by <code>global_position</code> can miss a row committed late. Dedicated event stores (KurrentDB, formerly EventStoreDB) or a transactional outbox with careful checkpointing handle this.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Projections and read models</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>// a projection keeps a checkpoint and is idempotent
void handle(StoredEvent e) {
  tx.run(() -&gt; {
    switch (e.payload()) {
      case Started s      -&gt; db.upsert("subscription_view", s.id(), Map.of("plan", s.plan(), "status", "ACTIVE"));
      case PlanUpgraded u -&gt; db.update("subscription_view", u.id(), Map.of("plan", u.plan()));
      case Cancelled c    -&gt; db.update("subscription_view", c.id(), Map.of("status", "CANCELLED"));
    }
    db.saveCheckpoint("subscription_view", e.globalPosition());  // same transaction
  });
}</code></pre></div>
    <ul>
      <li>Store the checkpoint in the same transaction as the update, so a crash never applies an event twice or skips one.</li>
      <li>To change a read model, build a new one by replaying all events, then switch reads to it.</li>
      <li>Read models are eventually consistent. Show users "updating…" or read their own writes from the aggregate when it matters.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Evolving events</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Events are immutable and permanent.</b> You cannot fix old ones in place; plan their shape carefully.</li>
      <li><b>Additive changes</b> (new optional fields with defaults) are safe.</li>
      <li><b>Version the event type</b> (<code>PlanUpgraded.v2</code>) when the meaning changes.</li>
      <li><b>Upcasting:</b> convert old event versions to the current shape when reading, so the code handles one version.</li>
      <li><b>Never rename or reuse</b> an event type for a different meaning.</li>
      <li><b>Name events in business language</b> (<code>PaymentFailed</code>), not technical (<code>RowUpdated</code>).</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Snapshots, size and deletion</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Snapshots</b> every N events (or when replay gets slow) store state plus version; load the snapshot, then only later events.</li>
      <li><b>Keep streams short-lived</b> where the domain allows (one stream per billing period, not per customer forever).</li>
      <li><b>Personal data and deletion:</b> keep personal data out of events where possible (reference an ID), or encrypt it per person and destroy the key to "forget" them (crypto-shredding).</li>
      <li><b>Archiving:</b> move closed streams to cold storage after their retention period.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Testing: given, when, then</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>@Test
void upgradeEmitsEventWhenActive() {
  var given = List.&lt;SubEvent&gt;of(new Started("s1", "basic", T0));
  var events = Subscription.replay(given).upgrade("pro", T1);          // when
  assertThat(events).containsExactly(new PlanUpgraded("s1", "pro", T1));   // then
}

@Test
void cannotUpgradeCancelled() {
  var given = List.&lt;SubEvent&gt;of(new Started("s1", "basic", T0), new Cancelled("s1", "user", T1));
  assertThatThrownBy(() -&gt; Subscription.replay(given).upgrade("pro", T2))
      .hasMessageContaining("not active");
}</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">Aggregates tested this way need no database or mocks: given past events, when a command, then expected events or a rejection.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>The lightweight version: an append-only ledger</h3>
    <p>Many systems get most of the benefit without full event sourcing: an append-only table with one row per state change, and current state from a "latest event" view. Never UPDATE, never DELETE.</p>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>-- BigQuery: current state of each pipeline stage from its event log
CREATE OR REPLACE VIEW `p.ops.v_job_run_latest` AS
SELECT * FROM `p.ops.job_run_events`
WHERE TRUE
QUALIFY ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY run_date, stage ORDER BY event_ts DESC) = 1;</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">You get history, auditability and "what happened on date X" with ordinary SQL. Add aggregates, projections and replay only if the domain needs them.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Tools</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>KurrentDB (formerly EventStoreDB):</b> a database built for event streams, subscriptions and projections.</li>
      <li><b>Axon Framework / Axon Server</b> (Java): aggregates, sagas, projections and an event store.</li>
      <li><b>Postgres</b> with an events table: simple and often enough, with the caveats above.</li>
      <li><b>Kafka</b> is a good event <i>log</i> for distribution, and its docs offer it as a backend for event sourcing, but it lacks two things an event store usually gives you: efficient per-entity stream reads and per-stream optimistic concurrency. Many systems store events in a database and publish them to Kafka through an outbox.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Common mistakes</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Event sourcing the whole system instead of the part that needs history.</li>
      <li>Events that mirror table changes (<code>CustomerUpdated</code> with every field) instead of business facts.</li>
      <li>Side effects (emails, payments) inside <code>apply</code>, so a replay sends them again.</li>
      <li>Querying the event store for lists and searches instead of building read models.</li>
      <li>No plan for event versioning, personal data or replay time.</li>
      <li>Treating eventual consistency of read models as a bug rather than designing for it.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Explain events, streams, aggregates, projections and CQRS</li><li>Read an event stream and follow how state was built</li><li>Tell an append-only ledger from full event sourcing</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Implement an aggregate with decide/apply and given-when-then tests</li><li>Build idempotent projections with checkpoints</li><li>Append with optimistic concurrency</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Decide where event sourcing fits in a system and where it does not</li><li>Design event schemas, versioning and upcasting</li><li>Plan snapshots, replay, rebuilds and personal-data handling</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Lead event-sourced architectures across domains and teams</li><li>Choose and operate event stores at scale</li><li>Set standards for event modelling and governance</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>Event storming workshops</li><li>Upcasting and event versioning</li><li>Process managers and sagas over events</li><li>Crypto-shredding for personal data</li><li>Projection rebuild strategies</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://martinfowler.com/eaaDev/EventSourcing.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Event Sourcing (Martin Fowler)</a><span class="lnote"> The classic explanation</span></li><li><a href="https://martinfowler.com/bliki/CQRS.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CQRS (Martin Fowler)</a><span class="lnote"> When separate read and write models help</span></li><li><a href="https://microservices.io/patterns/data/event-sourcing.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Event sourcing pattern (microservices.io)</a><span class="lnote"> In a microservices context</span></li><li><a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/patterns/event-sourcing" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Event Sourcing pattern (Azure Architecture Center)</a><span class="lnote"> Issues and considerations</span></li><li><a href="https://leanpub.com/esversioning" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Versioning in an Event Sourced System (Greg Young)</a><span class="lnote"> Free book on evolving events</span></li><li><a href="https://docs.kurrent.io/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">KurrentDB documentation</a><span class="lnote"> Event store (formerly EventStoreDB)</span></li><li><a href="https://docs.axoniq.io/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Axon documentation</a><span class="lnote"> Java framework for event sourcing and CQRS</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://martinfowler.com/eaaDev/EventSourcing.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Fowler: Event Sourcing</a>; <a href="https://martinfowler.com/bliki/CQRS.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Fowler: CQRS</a>; <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/sql-createtable.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PostgreSQL: CREATE TABLE (identity columns)</a>; <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/functions-sequence.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PostgreSQL: sequence functions (nextval not rolled back)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/kurrent-io/KurrentDB/master/README.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">KurrentDB README (EventStoreDB renamed)</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/reference/standard-sql/query-syntax" target="_blank" rel="noopener">BigQuery query syntax (QUALIFY)</a>; <a href="https://kafka.apache.org/uses/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Apache Kafka: uses (event sourcing)</a>. A few claims could not be confirmed at their official source from here and are being re-checked.</p><p class="runcode">A question, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/enrichmeai.github.io/issues/new?title=%5BEvent%20Sourcing%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>. Nothing here is tracked and there are no comments to store: questions live in the open, where the next learner can find the answer.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= SYSTEM DESIGN ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-sysdesign" data-name="System Design">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>System design</h2><p>How to design a system from a blank page: a repeatable method, the numbers to estimate with, the building blocks, how each scales, and worked examples. The <a href="#architecture">Architecture</a> and <a href="#database">Database Design</a> tabs go deeper on patterns and storage.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>The method, in order</h3>
    <ol>
      <li><b>Clarify requirements.</b> Functional: what users can do. Non-functional: scale, latency, availability, consistency, durability, cost, security, compliance. Write down what is out of scope.</li>
      <li><b>Estimate.</b> Users, requests per second (average and peak), read/write ratio, data size per item, storage per year, bandwidth. Numbers decide the design.</li>
      <li><b>Define the API.</b> The few endpoints or events that deliver the functional requirements, with their inputs and outputs.</li>
      <li><b>Model the data.</b> Entities, keys, access patterns ("fetch by X, list by Y sorted by Z"), and which store fits each pattern.</li>
      <li><b>Draw the high-level design.</b> Clients, load balancer, services, stores, caches, queues. One request path end to end.</li>
      <li><b>Deep-dive the hard parts.</b> The component that is hottest, biggest or most consistency-sensitive. Show how it scales and fails.</li>
      <li><b>Find bottlenecks and failure modes.</b> What breaks first at 10×? What happens when each dependency is down?</li>
      <li><b>State trade-offs.</b> What you chose, what you gave up, and what would make you change it.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="note">The same steps make a good design document. Start simple, and add a component only when a requirement or a number demands it.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>System design concepts: a map</h3>
    <p>The concepts that come up in almost every system design discussion, each in one line, with where this guide covers it in depth. Most are not separate techniques but answers to the same few questions: how do we handle more load, how do we survive failure, and how do copies of data stay correct?</p>
    <div class="tbl"><table><thead><tr><th>Concept</th><th>In one line</th><th>Read it here</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td><b>Load balancing</b></td><td>Spread requests across many servers, and stop sending to the ones that fail</td><td><a href="#c-load-balancing-sharing-the-traffic">Load balancing: sharing the traffic</a>, <a href="/well-rounded-engineer/networking/#c-nat-proxies-load-balancers">NAT, proxies, load balancers</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>CDN</b></td><td>Copies of content in servers close to users</td><td><a href="/well-rounded-engineer/caching/#c-http-and-cdn-caching">HTTP and CDN caching</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Caching</b></td><td>Keep a copy of slow or expensive data where it is quick to read</td><td><a href="#caching">Caching</a>, <a href="/well-rounded-engineer/sysdesign/#c-caching-strategies">Caching strategies</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Cache invalidation</b></td><td>Deciding when a cached copy is wrong, and removing it</td><td><a href="/well-rounded-engineer/caching/#c-invalidation">Invalidation</a>, <a href="/well-rounded-engineer/caching/#c-the-race-drawn">The race, drawn</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Rate limiting</b></td><td>Cap how many requests a caller may make in a time window</td><td><a href="/well-rounded-engineer/sysdesign/#c-rate-limiting">Rate limiting</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>API gateway</b></td><td>One front door for APIs: authentication, limits, routing</td><td><a href="/well-rounded-engineer/apis/#c-gateway-load-balancer-proxy-mesh-who-does-what">Gateway, load balancer, proxy, mesh: who does what</a>, <a href="#apigee">Apigee</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>CAP theorem</b></td><td>During a network partition, choose consistency or availability</td><td><a href="/well-rounded-engineer/distsys/#c-cap-and-pacelc">CAP and PACELC</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Sharding</b></td><td>Split data across many databases by a key</td><td><a href="/well-rounded-engineer/database/#c-sharding-many-databases">Sharding (many databases)</a>, <a href="/well-rounded-engineer/casestudies/#c-shopify-built-on-rails-scaled-by-splitting-its-d">Shopify</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Replication</b></td><td>Keep copies of the same data on several machines</td><td><a href="/well-rounded-engineer/database/#c-replication-scaling-reads">Replication & scaling reads</a>, <a href="/well-rounded-engineer/database/#c-replica-lag-drawn">Replica lag, drawn</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Partitioning</b></td><td>Split one table or topic into parts that can be handled separately</td><td><a href="/well-rounded-engineer/database/#c-partitioning-one-database">Partitioning (one database)</a>, <a href="/well-rounded-engineer/kafka/#c-keys-partitions-and-groups-drawn">Keys, partitions and groups, drawn</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Queues</b></td><td>Hand work to another component to do later, at its own pace</td><td><a href="/well-rounded-engineer/sysdesign/#c-building-blocks">Building blocks</a>, <a href="/well-rounded-engineer/architecture/#c-event-driven-systems">Event-driven systems</a>, <a href="#kafka">Kafka</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Microservices</b></td><td>An application split into small services that deploy on their own</td><td><a href="#microservices">Microservices</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Microservices vs monoliths</b></td><td>When one application is better, and when splitting pays</td><td><a href="/well-rounded-engineer/microservices/#c-monolith-modular-monolith-microservices">Monolith, modular monolith, microservices</a>, <a href="/well-rounded-engineer/microservices/#c-what-microservices-buy-and-cost">What microservices buy, and cost</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Fault tolerance</b></td><td>Keep working when parts fail: timeouts, retries, circuit breakers</td><td><a href="/well-rounded-engineer/sysdesign/#c-designing-for-failure">Designing for failure</a>, <a href="/well-rounded-engineer/microservices/#c-how-a-circuit-breaker-works">How a circuit breaker works</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Database scaling</b></td><td>Cheapest first: indexes, caching, read replicas, then sharding</td><td><a href="/well-rounded-engineer/sysdesign/#c-scaling-playbook-cheapest-first">Scaling playbook, cheapest first</a>, <a href="/well-rounded-engineer/database/#c-replication-scaling-reads">Replication & scaling reads</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Service discovery</b></td><td>How a service finds the current address of another</td><td><a href="#c-service-discovery-how-services-find-each-other">Service discovery: how services find each other</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Consistency models</b></td><td>What a reader is promised to see after a write</td><td><a href="/well-rounded-engineer/distsys/#c-consistency-models">Consistency models</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Eventual consistency</b></td><td>Copies agree in the end, not immediately</td><td><a href="/well-rounded-engineer/distsys/#c-consistency-models">Consistency models</a>, <a href="/well-rounded-engineer/casestudies/#c-ebay-split-by-function-and-by-data-and-no-distri">eBay</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Distributed transactions</b></td><td>One change across several databases or services</td><td><a href="/well-rounded-engineer/distsys/#c-transactions-across-machines-two-phase-commit">Transactions across machines: two-phase commit</a>, <a href="/well-rounded-engineer/architecture/#c-saga-transactions-across-services">Saga: transactions across services</a>, <a href="/well-rounded-engineer/architecture/#c-transactional-outbox">Transactional outbox</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Leader election</b></td><td>Machines agree which one is in charge, and replace it when it fails</td><td><a href="/well-rounded-engineer/distsys/#c-agreeing-consensus-and-raft">Agreeing: consensus and Raft</a>, <a href="/well-rounded-engineer/distsys/#c-quorums-and-split-brain">Quorums and split brain</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Horizontal vs vertical scaling</b></td><td>More machines, or a bigger machine</td><td><a href="#c-scale-up-or-scale-out">Scale up or scale out</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Back-of-the-envelope estimation</b></td><td>Rough numbers first, to see which design can work at all</td><td><a href="/well-rounded-engineer/sysdesign/#c-back-of-the-envelope-numbers">Back-of-the-envelope numbers</a>, <a href="/well-rounded-engineer/sysdesign/#c-availability-in-downtime">Availability in downtime</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Idempotency</b></td><td>Doing the same request twice has the same effect as once</td><td><a href="/well-rounded-engineer/architecture/#c-idempotent-consumers">Idempotent consumers</a>, <a href="#payments">Payment Gateways</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Latency</b></td><td>How long one request takes; know the orders of magnitude</td><td><a href="/well-rounded-engineer/sysdesign/#c-latency-order-of-magnitude">Latency, order of magnitude</a>, <a href="#performance">Performance</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Session guarantees</b></td><td>Read your writes, monotonic reads: promises to one user that make stale data bearable</td><td><a href="/well-rounded-engineer/distsys/#c-living-with-eventual-consistency-session-guarant">Living with eventual consistency</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>CRDTs</b></td><td>Data types whose copies merge correctly without coordinating</td><td><a href="/well-rounded-engineer/distsys/#c-crdts-replicas-that-merge-without-asking-each-ot">CRDTs</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Fencing</b></td><td>Stop an old leader that wakes up and keeps writing</td><td><a href="/well-rounded-engineer/distsys/#c-fencing-stopping-the-old-leader">Fencing</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Cache write policies</b></td><td>Write-through, write-back and write-around</td><td><a href="/well-rounded-engineer/caching/#c-write-policies-by-name-through-back-around">Write policies by name</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Data locality</b></td><td>Move the work to the data, not the data to the work</td><td><a href="#c-data-locality-move-the-work-to-the-data">Data locality</a></td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note">Practise them with the <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/well-rounded-engineer-labs/tree/main/distributed-systems" target="_blank" rel="noopener">distributed systems drills</a> (the double charge, the retry storm, the stale read) and the worked examples below.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Back-of-the-envelope numbers</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Quantity</th><th>Rule of thumb</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Seconds per day</td><td>86,400 ≈ 10<sup>5</sup></td></tr>
      <tr><td>1 M requests/day</td><td>≈ 12 per second on average</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Peak</td><td>2–10× average, depending on the traffic shape</td></tr>
      <tr><td>1 KB × 1 M items</td><td>1 GB</td></tr>
      <tr><td>1 KB × 1 B items</td><td>1 TB</td></tr>
      <tr><td>One Postgres instance</td><td>thousands of simple queries/s; much less for heavy ones</td></tr>
      <tr><td>One app instance</td><td>hundreds to a few thousand requests/s, workload-dependent</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>Example: order search for 50k shop staff
  50k users × 20 searches/day = 1 M/day ≈ 12/s avg, ~60/s peak
  20M orders × 2 KB metadata ≈ 40 GB  (fits one Postgres with indexes)
  line items and notes: 20M × ~7 KB avg ≈ 140 GB   (store separately; index for search)
→ one primary + read replica + cache is plenty; no sharding needed</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Latency, order of magnitude</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Operation</th><th>Roughly</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Main memory read</td><td>100 ns</td></tr>
      <tr><td>SSD random read</td><td>~100 µs</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Round trip within a datacenter / zone</td><td>~0.5 ms</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Indexed database lookup</td><td>~1 ms</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Cache (Redis) get over the network</td><td>~1 ms</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Round trip between regions on one continent</td><td>10–50 ms</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Round trip across continents</td><td>~150 ms</td></tr>
      <tr><td>LLM response</td><td>hundreds of ms to tens of seconds</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note">Network calls dominate. Ten sequential service calls cost far more than ten database rows. Parallelise independent calls and keep hot paths short.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Availability in downtime</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Availability</th><th>Downtime / year</th><th>/ month</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>99%</td><td>3.65 days</td><td>7.3 h</td></tr>
      <tr><td>99.9%</td><td>8.8 h</td><td>43.8 min</td></tr>
      <tr><td>99.95%</td><td>4.4 h</td><td>21.9 min</td></tr>
      <tr><td>99.99%</td><td>52.6 min</td><td>4.4 min</td></tr>
      <tr><td>99.999%</td><td>5.3 min</td><td>26 s</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note">Dependencies in series multiply: three services at 99.9% each give about 99.7%. Redundancy in parallel raises it. Each extra nine costs a lot more; pick the target the users actually need.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Building blocks</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Block</th><th>Does</th><th>On GCP</th><th>Watch out for</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>DNS</td><td>Name to address; can route by region or weight</td><td>Cloud DNS</td><td>TTLs slow down changes</td></tr>
      <tr><td>CDN</td><td>Caches static and cacheable responses near users</td><td>Cloud CDN</td><td>Invalidation; never cache personalised responses by mistake</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Load balancer</td><td>Spreads traffic, health checks, TLS termination</td><td>Cloud Load Balancing</td><td>Sticky sessions hide stateful services</td></tr>
      <tr><td>API gateway</td><td>Auth, rate limits, routing for many backends</td><td>API Gateway, Apigee</td><td>Becoming a place for business logic</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Stateless service</td><td>Business logic, scales horizontally</td><td>Cloud Run, GKE</td><td>Hidden state in memory or local disk</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Relational DB</td><td>Transactions, constraints, flexible queries</td><td>Cloud SQL, AlloyDB, Spanner</td><td>Write scaling, connection limits</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Key-value / document store</td><td>Massive scale lookups by key</td><td>Firestore, Bigtable, Memorystore</td><td>Queries outside the key design are expensive</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Cache</td><td>Serves hot reads from memory</td><td>Memorystore (Redis)</td><td>Staleness, invalidation, stampedes</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Object storage</td><td>Files and blobs, cheap and durable</td><td>Cloud Storage</td><td>Listing huge prefixes is slow</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Queue / log</td><td>Decouples producers and consumers, absorbs spikes</td><td>Pub/Sub, Kafka, Cloud Tasks</td><td>Duplicates, ordering, poison messages</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Search index</td><td>Full-text and faceted search, ranking</td><td>Postgres FTS, Elasticsearch/OpenSearch, Vertex AI Search</td><td>Keeping it in sync with the source of truth</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Warehouse</td><td>Analytics over everything</td><td>BigQuery</td><td>Not for low-latency per-user reads</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Batch / stream processing</td><td>Heavy transforms off the request path</td><td>Dataflow, Cloud Run jobs, Managed Service for Apache Spark (formerly Dataproc)</td><td>Late data, reprocessing, cost</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Scheduler / workflow</td><td>Recurring and multi-step work</td><td>Cloud Scheduler, Workflows, Composer</td><td>A laptop or a person standing in for it</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Scaling playbook, cheapest first</h3>
    <ol>
      <li><b>Measure.</b> Find the actual bottleneck: CPU, memory, DB queries, a slow dependency.</li>
      <li><b>Fix the query.</b> Indexes, fewer round trips, no N+1. Often 10–100× for free.</li>
      <li><b>Scale up</b> the instance. Boring and effective.</li>
      <li><b>Make services stateless</b> and scale out behind a load balancer.</li>
      <li><b>Cache</b> hot reads; add a CDN for public content.</li>
      <li><b>Read replicas</b> for read-heavy databases.</li>
      <li><b>Move work off the request path</b> to queues and workers.</li>
      <li><b>Partition</b> big tables; archive cold data.</li>
      <li><b>Shard</b> or move to a distributed database only when one primary cannot take the writes.</li>
    </ol>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Load balancing: sharing the traffic</h3>
    <p>A load balancer sits in front of several servers and decides which one handles each request. It also watches them: a server that fails its health checks stops receiving traffic, which is why a failing check shows up to clients as a 502 or 503 (see <a href="#networking">Networking</a> for layer 4 and layer 7 balancers).</p>
    <div class="tbl"><table><thead><tr><th>How it chooses</th><th>What it does</th><th>Fits when</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td><b>Round robin</b></td><td>The first request to the first server, the next to the second, and round again; can be weighted so larger servers get more</td><td>Requests are similar in cost and servers are alike</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Least connections</b></td><td>Each new request to the server with the fewest active connections; can also be weighted</td><td>Requests vary in length, such as long downloads or streams</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Hash</b></td><td>The same key (a user, a session, an IP address) always goes to the same server</td><td>A server holds something per key, such as a local cache or session</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Consistent hashing</b></td><td>Keys and servers on a ring, so adding or removing a server moves only a small share of the keys (see Distributing data above)</td><td>The set of servers changes often; CDNs and distributed caches use it</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Random</b></td><td>Each request to a randomly chosen server; with many clients the load evens out</td><td>The simplest option, often good enough at scale</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Static or dynamic.</b> Round robin and random ignore how busy each server is; least connections and response-time-based methods use live load information. Static methods are simpler and work well for fairly regular work such as web requests.</li>
      <li><b>The balancer must not be the single point of failure.</b> Load balancers are usually run in high-availability pairs, or as a managed cloud service built that way.</li>
      <li><b>Sticky sessions</b> (sending a user back to the same server) make servers stateful again; prefer stateless servers with session data in a shared store, so any server can take any request.</li>
      <li><b>DNS can balance too</b>: round-robin DNS gives clients different addresses in turn, with no balancer at all, but it cannot react quickly to a failed server because answers are cached.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Service discovery: how services find each other</h3>
    <p>In a system of many services, instances start, stop and move all the time, so their addresses change. Service discovery is how one service finds the current address of another without anyone writing it down. The rule: never hard-code an instance&rsquo;s IP address.</p>
    <div class="tbl"><table><thead><tr><th>Approach</th><th>How it works</th><th>Where you meet it</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td><b>DNS names</b></td><td>Call a stable name; the platform keeps the name pointing at healthy instances</td><td>Kubernetes Services give each service a DNS name (see <a href="#kubernetes">Kubernetes</a>)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>A platform URL or load balancer</b></td><td>Call one stable address; the platform routes to whatever instances exist</td><td>Cloud Run URLs, a cloud load balancer in front of a group of machines (see <a href="#cloudrun">Cloud Run</a>)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>A service registry</b></td><td>Instances register themselves; callers ask the registry, which drops instances that fail health checks</td><td>Consul, often where services run on virtual machines as well as Kubernetes (see <a href="#servicemesh">Service Mesh &amp; Istio</a>)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>A service mesh</b></td><td>A proxy beside each service handles discovery, retries and encryption for it</td><td>Istio and Linkerd (see <a href="#servicemesh">Service Mesh &amp; Istio</a>)</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
    <p><i>Our view:</i> use what your platform already gives you (DNS names on Kubernetes, URLs on a managed platform) and add a registry or a mesh only when a requirement needs it, such as services spread across Kubernetes and virtual machines. <i>The other view:</i> some teams adopt a mesh early so that discovery, retries and encryption are consistent from the start, accepting the extra moving parts.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Scale up or scale out</h3>
    <p><b>Scaling up</b> (vertical) adds CPU, memory or storage to one machine. <b>Scaling out</b> (horizontal) adds more machines, for example going from one web server to three behind a load balancer.</p>
    <div class="tbl"><table><thead><tr><th>If this is true for you</th><th>It leans towards</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td>The bottleneck is one database or stateful service, and a bigger machine is available</td><td>Scale up first: no change to the code, no coordination between nodes</td></tr>
      <tr><td>The service is stateless and traffic rises and falls</td><td>Scale out, and scale back in when traffic drops</td></tr>
      <tr><td>You need to survive the loss of a machine</td><td>Scale out: one machine, however large, is a single point of failure</td></tr>
      <tr><td>The work cannot be split (Wikipedia notes that some applications do not scale horizontally)</td><td>Scale up, or redesign the work so it can be split</td></tr>
      <tr><td>You are near the largest machine you can buy or rent</td><td>Scale out, which usually means sharding or partitioning the data</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
    <p>Wikipedia lists the benefits of scaling up as avoiding the extra management, programming effort and coordination problems (throughput, latency, synchronisation) that come with more nodes. That is why the scaling playbook above starts with the cheapest step and keeps scaling out of the data for last.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Data locality: move the work to the data</h3>
    <p>Moving data across a network costs time and money; moving the code is usually far cheaper. Apache Hadoop was built on this idea: it splits files into blocks across the machines of a cluster, then sends packaged code to the machines that hold each block, so the processing happens where the data already is.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Keep services near their data</b>: an application in one region reading a database in another pays the round trip on every query (see the latency numbers above).</li>
      <li><b>Bring data near users</b>: CDNs and caches are data locality for reads (see <a href="#caching">Caching</a>).</li>
      <li><b>Push filtering down</b>: ask the database for the ten rows you need, not ten thousand to filter in the application (see <a href="#sql">SQL Skills</a>).</li>
      <li><b>Mind the bill</b>: cloud providers charge for data leaving a region or zone (see <a href="#cloudcost">Cloud Cost</a>).</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Caching strategies</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Cache-aside:</b> app reads cache, on a miss reads the DB and fills the cache. Most common.</li>
      <li><b>Read-through:</b> the cache library loads from the DB itself on a miss.</li>
      <li><b>Write-through:</b> write to cache and DB together; reads always warm, writes slower.</li>
      <li><b>Write-behind:</b> write to cache, flush to DB later; fast, but data can be lost.</li>
      <li><b>TTL</b> bounds staleness; <b>explicit invalidation</b> on write keeps it fresher but is easy to miss.</li>
      <li><b>Stampede:</b> a popular key expires and thousands of requests hit the DB. Use request coalescing, a lock, or early random refresh.</li>
      <li><b>What to key on:</b> include everything that changes the answer (user, locale, version). A missing part of the key serves one user's data to another.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Rate limiting</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Token bucket:</b> tokens refill at a steady rate up to a burst size; each request takes one. Allows short bursts. The usual choice.</li>
      <li><b>Leaky bucket:</b> requests drain at a fixed rate; smooths traffic, no bursts.</li>
      <li><b>Fixed window:</b> N per minute; simple, but allows 2N across a window boundary.</li>
      <li><b>Sliding window:</b> counts over the last 60 s; fairer, slightly more state.</li>
      <li><b>Where:</b> at the gateway per API key or user; inside services to protect a fragile dependency.</li>
      <li><b>Response:</b> HTTP 429 with <code>Retry-After</code>, so well-behaved clients back off.</li>
      <li><b>Distributed:</b> keep counters in Redis with atomic increments, or accept per-instance limits.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Distributing data</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Replication</b> copies data for availability and read scale. Leader-follower is the common model; multi-leader and leaderless trade consistency for write availability.</li>
      <li><b>Partitioning / sharding</b> splits data for write scale and size. Choose a key that spreads load and matches the main access pattern.</li>
      <li><b>Consistent hashing</b> places keys and nodes on a ring, so adding or removing a node moves only about 1/N of the keys. Used by caches and distributed stores.</li>
      <li><b>Hot keys</b> (one celebrity, one big tenant, today's date) defeat even spreading. Split them, cache them, or add a random suffix.</li>
      <li><b>Quorums:</b> with N replicas, reading R and writing W where R + W &gt; N means every read set overlaps every write set, so a read sees the latest acknowledged write.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Designing for failure</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Timeouts on every network call</b>, shorter than the caller's own timeout.</li>
      <li><b>Retries</b> only for idempotent operations, with backoff and jitter, and a cap.</li>
      <li><b>Circuit breakers and bulkheads</b> so one sick dependency cannot take down the rest.</li>
      <li><b>Graceful degradation:</b> serve cached or partial results, and say so. Never present "could not check" as "nothing found".</li>
      <li><b>Redundancy:</b> multiple instances across zones; regional failover only if the requirement justifies the cost.</li>
      <li><b>Backpressure:</b> queues with limits; shed load early (429/503) instead of collapsing.</li>
      <li><b>Backups you have restored,</b> with a known recovery point (RPO: how much data you can lose) and recovery time (RTO: how long you can be down).</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Worked example: a URL shortener</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Requirements:</b> create a short link; redirect fast; links never change; 100 M new links/year; reads 100× writes; 99.9% available.</li>
      <li><b>Estimates:</b> writes ≈ 3/s average; reads ≈ 300/s, peak ~1,500/s. 100 M × 500 B ≈ 50 GB/year.</li>
      <li><b>API:</b> <code>POST /links {url}</code> → <code>{code}</code>; <code>GET /{code}</code> → 301/302 redirect.</li>
      <li><b>Data:</b> <code>link(code PK, url, created_at, owner)</code>. Access pattern is lookup by code only: any key-value store or Postgres works.</li>
      <li><b>Code generation:</b> base62 of a unique ID (7 chars ≈ 3.5 trillion codes). Use a sequence or ID service to avoid collisions; random codes need a uniqueness check.</li>
      <li><b>High level:</b> load balancer → stateless service → cache (hot codes) → database. CDN can cache redirects for popular links.</li>
      <li><b>Deep dive:</b> reads are the load: cache-aside with long TTL works because links are immutable. Writes are tiny.</li>
      <li><b>Trade-offs:</b> 301 is cached by browsers (fewer hits, no click counting); 302 keeps analytics. Click analytics go to a queue and a warehouse, not the redirect path.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>The design, drawn</h3>
    <figure class="fig"><svg viewBox="0 0 660 270" role="img" aria-labelledby="urlshort-t urlshort-d"><title id="urlshort-t">A URL shortener, high level</title><desc id="urlshort-d">Two paths. The read path, which carries the load: a browser asks for a short code; a CDN may answer popular redirects; otherwise a load balancer passes it to a stateless service, which checks a cache of hot codes and only then the database, and returns a 301 or 302 redirect. The write path is small: POST a URL, get a code made from a unique ID in base62, stored in the database. Click analytics go to a queue and a warehouse, off the redirect path.</desc><defs><marker id="urlshort-a" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker><marker id="urlshort-aa" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar acc" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker></defs><text class="lb hd" x="10" y="22" text-anchor="start">read path (~300/s, peak ~1,500/s)</text><rect class="nd in" x="10" y="36" width="100" height="40" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="60" y="61" text-anchor="middle">browser</text><rect class="nd" x="120" y="36" width="100" height="40" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="170" y="61" text-anchor="middle">CDN</text><rect class="nd" x="230" y="36" width="100" height="40" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="280" y="61" text-anchor="middle">load balancer</text><rect class="nd out" x="360" y="36" width="100" height="40" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="410" y="61" text-anchor="middle">service</text><rect class="nd" x="470" y="36" width="130" height="40" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="535" y="61" text-anchor="middle">cache: hot codes</text><line class="ed" x1="110" y1="56" x2="116" y2="56" marker-end="url(#urlshort-a)"/><line class="ed" x1="220" y1="56" x2="226" y2="56" marker-end="url(#urlshort-a)"/><line class="ed" x1="330" y1="56" x2="356" y2="56" marker-end="url(#urlshort-a)"/><line class="ed" x1="460" y1="56" x2="466" y2="56" marker-end="url(#urlshort-a)"/><rect class="nd" x="470" y="120" width="130" height="40" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="535" y="145" text-anchor="middle">database</text><line class="ed dash" x1="535" y1="76" x2="535" y2="116" marker-end="url(#urlshort-a)"/><text class="lb mu" x="545" y="100" text-anchor="start">on a miss</text><text class="lb mu" x="10" y="110" text-anchor="start">redirect: 301 is cached by browsers; 302 keeps click counts</text><text class="lb hd" x="10" y="196" text-anchor="start">write path (~3/s)</text><rect class="nd in" x="10" y="210" width="210" height="40" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="115" y="235" text-anchor="middle">POST /links {url}</text><rect class="nd out" x="250" y="210" width="200" height="40" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="350" y="235" text-anchor="middle">code = base62(unique ID)</text><line class="ed" x1="220" y1="230" x2="246" y2="230" marker-end="url(#urlshort-a)"/><line class="ed" x1="450" y1="230" x2="535" y2="164" marker-end="url(#urlshort-a)"/><rect class="nd warn" x="480" y="210" width="170" height="40" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="565" y="235" text-anchor="middle">click analytics: queue</text></svg><figcaption>The worked example as a picture. Reads are a hundred times the writes, so the read path gets the cache and the CDN; the links never change, so a long TTL is safe.</figcaption></figure>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Worked example: search over 20 M orders</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Requirements:</b> keyword and filter search (store, date, product), open an order, look up its refunds. Results within 500 ms at p95. Data refreshed daily. A customer's erasure request must apply everywhere immediately.</li>
      <li><b>Estimates:</b> ~60 searches/s at peak; 20 M records, ~40 GB metadata, ~140 GB line items and notes.</li>
      <li><b>Data flow:</b> order-system exports → landing bucket → warehouse layers (raw → foundation → consumable) → serving Postgres. The warehouse is the record; Postgres is a read-optimised copy. See <a href="#data">Data Engineering</a>.</li>
      <li><b>Serving:</b> stateless API on Cloud Run; Postgres with full-text (GIN) and B-tree indexes on filter columns; read replica for search; cache for hot queries and order pages.</li>
      <li><b>Stable identity:</b> IDs never renumber, because URLs, invoices and refunds refer to them. Loads upsert on a stable key.</li>
      <li><b>Erasure requests:</b> checked on every read path (search, page, API, exports) from the application database, not baked into the copy, so a new request takes effect without a reload.</li>
      <li><b>Freshness and honesty:</b> each result carries its source and date; when a source is stale or unreachable, the API says so rather than returning a confident empty result.</li>
      <li><b>Scaling later:</b> move search to a dedicated index (or add semantic search with embeddings) when relevance or load outgrows Postgres; keep Postgres for lookups.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>More practice problems</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Rate limiter for a public API</li>
      <li>Notification service (email, push, SMS) with retries and user preferences</li>
      <li>News feed or timeline</li>
      <li>Chat with presence and message history</li>
      <li>File upload and processing pipeline</li>
      <li>Job scheduler that runs millions of tasks reliably</li>
      <li>Metrics and logging pipeline</li>
      <li>RAG question-answering over company documents</li>
      <li>Payment flow with a saga and idempotency keys</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">For each: run the method above, and write the trade-offs down. The written reasoning is the skill.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Where these ideas came from</h3>
    <p>Most of the concepts in the map above were named by particular people, in particular papers, often decades before they became interview questions. Reading the original is the best way to learn what an idea actually claims, and what it does not (CAP&rsquo;s &ldquo;consistency&rdquo; and &ldquo;availability&rdquo;, for example, mean narrower things than the everyday words; see the two traps on <a href="#distsys">Distributed Systems</a>).</p>
    <div class="tbl"><table><thead><tr><th>Idea</th><th>Who, and when</th><th>Read</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td><b>CAP theorem</b></td><td>Eric Brewer (conjecture, 2000); proved by Seth Gilbert and Nancy Lynch (2002)</td><td><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CAP_theorem" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: CAP theorem</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>PACELC</b></td><td>Daniel Abadi (2010, paper 2012)</td><td><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PACELC_design_principle" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: PACELC</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Linearizability</b></td><td>Maurice Herlihy and Jeannette Wing (1987)</td><td><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Linearizability" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Linearizability</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Logical clocks and event ordering</b></td><td>Leslie Lamport (1978), &ldquo;Time, Clocks, and the Ordering of Events in a Distributed System&rdquo;</td><td><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lamport_timestamp" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Lamport timestamp</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Paxos consensus</b></td><td>Leslie Lamport (first submitted 1989)</td><td><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paxos_(computer_science)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Paxos</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Raft consensus</b></td><td>Diego Ongaro and John Ousterhout (2014)</td><td><a href="https://raft.github.io/raft.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">the Raft paper</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Leader election (the bully algorithm)</b></td><td>Hector Garcia-Molina (1982)</td><td><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bully_algorithm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Bully algorithm</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Sagas</b></td><td>Hector Garcia-Molina and Kenneth Salem (1987)</td><td><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Long-running_transaction" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Long-running transaction</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Session guarantees</b></td><td>Douglas Terry and colleagues (1994)</td><td><a href="https://www.cs.cornell.edu/courses/cs734/2000FA/cached%20papers/SessionGuaranteesPDIS_1.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">the 1994 paper</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Consistent hashing</b></td><td>David Karger and colleagues at MIT (1997)</td><td><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Consistent_hashing" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Consistent hashing</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Dynamo: eventual consistency at scale</b></td><td>Giuseppe DeCandia and colleagues at Amazon (2007)</td><td><a href="https://www.allthingsdistributed.com/files/amazon-dynamo-sosp2007.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">the Dynamo paper</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>CRDTs</b></td><td>Marc Shapiro, Nuno Pregui&ccedil;a, Carlos Baquero and Marek Zawirski (2011)</td><td><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conflict-free_replicated_data_type" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: CRDT</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Circuit breaker</b></td><td>Popularised by Michael Nygard in his book <i>Release It!</i></td><td><a href="https://martinfowler.com/bliki/CircuitBreaker.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Martin Fowler: CircuitBreaker</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Microservices (the definition most cited)</b></td><td>James Lewis and Martin Fowler (25 March 2014)</td><td><a href="https://martinfowler.com/articles/microservices.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Microservices</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Monolith first</b></td><td>Martin Fowler (3 June 2015)</td><td><a href="https://martinfowler.com/bliki/MonolithFirst.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MonolithFirst</a></td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Practice questions, by domain</h3>
    <p>37 questions to practise with, each set in a situation with numbers or a failure, because that is how these problems arrive at work. Try to answer out loud first, in a few sentences: what you would check, what you would choose, and what it would cost. Then read the linked section. The levels match the rest of the guide: Foundation, Practitioner, Senior.</p>
    <h4>Scale and load</h4>
    <div class="tbl"><table><thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Question</th><th>Where to find the answer</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td>Foundation</td><td>Your API handles 50 requests a second today and marketing expects 20 times that next month. What do you estimate first, and what is likely to break first?</td><td><a href="#c-back-of-the-envelope-numbers">Back-of-the-envelope numbers</a>, <a href="#c-scaling-playbook-cheapest-first">Scaling playbook, cheapest first</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Practitioner</td><td>Two servers sit behind a load balancer and one starts answering in 5 seconds instead of 50 ms, but it still passes its health check. What happens to your users, and how do you stop it?</td><td><a href="#c-load-balancing-sharing-the-traffic">Load balancing: sharing the traffic</a>, <a href="/well-rounded-engineer/networking/#c-nat-proxies-load-balancers">NAT, proxies, load balancers</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Practitioner</td><td>When would you buy a bigger database server instead of adding more servers, and what tells you it is time to stop?</td><td><a href="#c-scale-up-or-scale-out">Scale up or scale out</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Senior</td><td>One customer sends a third of all your traffic. How do you protect everyone else without losing that customer?</td><td><a href="#c-rate-limiting">Rate limiting</a></td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
    <h4>Data, storage and indexing</h4>
    <div class="tbl"><table><thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Question</th><th>Where to find the answer</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td>Foundation</td><td>A query that took 20 ms now takes 4 seconds, and the table has grown from 1 million to 50 million rows. Where do you look first?</td><td><a href="/well-rounded-engineer/database/#c-reading-a-query-plan">Reading a query plan</a>, <a href="/well-rounded-engineer/database/#c-index-rules-of-thumb">Index rules of thumb</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Practitioner</td><td>You need to add a NOT NULL column to a 200-million-row table without downtime. Walk through the steps.</td><td><a href="/well-rounded-engineer/database/#c-safe-schema-changes-on-live-data">Safe schema changes on live data</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Practitioner</td><td>Partitioning or sharding: what is the difference, and which would you try first for a table that keeps growing?</td><td><a href="/well-rounded-engineer/database/#c-partitioning-one-database">Partitioning (one database)</a>, <a href="/well-rounded-engineer/database/#c-sharding-many-databases">Sharding (many databases)</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Senior</td><td>You chose a shard key two years ago and one shard is now ten times busier than the rest. What are your options, and what does each cost?</td><td><a href="/well-rounded-engineer/database/#c-sharding-many-databases">Sharding (many databases)</a>, <a href="#c-distributing-data">Distributing data</a></td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
    <h4>Consistency and transactions</h4>
    <div class="tbl"><table><thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Question</th><th>Where to find the answer</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td>Foundation</td><td>A user changes their address, refreshes the page, and sees the old one. Name the likely cause and two fixes.</td><td><a href="/well-rounded-engineer/distsys/#c-living-with-eventual-consistency-session-guarant">Living with eventual consistency: session guarantees</a>, <a href="/well-rounded-engineer/database/#c-replica-lag-drawn">Replica lag, drawn</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Practitioner</td><td>An order needs a payment, a stock reservation and an email, each in a different service. How do you keep them consistent without one big transaction?</td><td><a href="/well-rounded-engineer/architecture/#c-saga-transactions-across-services">Saga: transactions across services</a>, <a href="/well-rounded-engineer/architecture/#c-transactional-outbox">Transactional outbox</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Practitioner</td><td>During a network partition, should your shopping basket stay available or stay consistent? And your bank balance?</td><td><a href="/well-rounded-engineer/distsys/#c-cap-and-pacelc">CAP and PACELC</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Senior</td><td>Two-phase commit or a saga: list the requirements that would make you choose each.</td><td><a href="/well-rounded-engineer/distsys/#c-transactions-across-machines-two-phase-commit">Transactions across machines: two-phase commit</a>, <a href="/well-rounded-engineer/architecture/#c-saga-transactions-across-services">Saga: transactions across services</a></td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
    <h4>Reliability, failure and recovery</h4>
    <div class="tbl"><table><thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Question</th><th>Where to find the answer</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td>Foundation</td><td>A downstream service starts timing out. Without a timeout on your side, what happens to your service over the next minute?</td><td><a href="#c-designing-for-failure">Designing for failure</a>, <a href="/well-rounded-engineer/microservices/#c-how-a-circuit-breaker-works">How a circuit breaker works</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Practitioner</td><td>Your service retries failed calls three times. The dependency recovers, then falls over again under the retries. Why, and how do you change the retries?</td><td><a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/well-rounded-engineer-labs/tree/main/distributed-systems/02-retry-storm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">the retry storm drill</a>, <a href="#c-designing-for-failure">Designing for failure</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Practitioner</td><td>A leader node pauses for 30 seconds, a new leader is elected, then the old one wakes up and writes. What stops the damage?</td><td><a href="/well-rounded-engineer/distsys/#c-fencing-stopping-the-old-leader">Fencing: stopping the old leader</a>, <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/well-rounded-engineer-labs/tree/main/distributed-systems/03-lock-outlived-owner" target="_blank" rel="noopener">the lock drill</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Senior</td><td>You need to survive the loss of a whole region. What recovery time and data loss can you promise, and what would each option cost?</td><td><a href="/well-rounded-engineer/k8sdr/#c-four-dr-strategies-from-cheap-to-fast">Four DR strategies, from cheap to fast</a>, <a href="#c-availability-in-downtime">Availability in downtime</a></td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
    <h4>Asynchronous work, events and jobs</h4>
    <div class="tbl"><table><thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Question</th><th>Where to find the answer</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td>Foundation</td><td>Why put a queue between the checkout and the email service? What do you gain, and what new problem do you take on?</td><td><a href="/well-rounded-engineer/architecture/#c-event-driven-systems">Event-driven systems</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Practitioner</td><td>A payment message is delivered twice. How do you make sure the customer is charged once?</td><td><a href="/well-rounded-engineer/architecture/#c-idempotent-consumers">Idempotent consumers</a>, <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/well-rounded-engineer-labs/tree/main/distributed-systems/01-double-charge" target="_blank" rel="noopener">the double charge drill</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Practitioner</td><td>Events for one customer arrive out of order and the oldest update wins. How do you design the topic and the consumer?</td><td><a href="/well-rounded-engineer/kafka/#c-keys-partitions-and-groups-drawn">Keys, partitions and groups, drawn</a>, <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/well-rounded-engineer-labs/tree/main/distributed-systems/05-back-in-time" target="_blank" rel="noopener">the back-in-time drill</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Senior</td><td>What does exactly-once mean in practice, and where does at-least-once delivery plus idempotency get you the same result?</td><td><a href="/well-rounded-engineer/kafka/#c-durability-and-delivery-settings">Durability and delivery settings</a>, <a href="/well-rounded-engineer/architecture/#c-idempotent-consumers">Idempotent consumers</a></td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
    <h4>Caching and performance</h4>
    <div class="tbl"><table><thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Question</th><th>Where to find the answer</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td>Foundation</td><td>Where could you cache the product page: browser, CDN, application, database? What would you put in each?</td><td><a href="/well-rounded-engineer/caching/#c-where-caches-live">Where caches live</a>, <a href="/well-rounded-engineer/caching/#c-http-and-cdn-caching">HTTP and CDN caching</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Practitioner</td><td>A popular cache entry expires and 10,000 requests hit the database at once. What is this called and how do you prevent it?</td><td><a href="/well-rounded-engineer/caching/#c-stampedes-thundering-herd">Stampedes (thundering herd)</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Practitioner</td><td>A price changes but customers still see the old one for five minutes. What are your invalidation options and their trade-offs?</td><td><a href="/well-rounded-engineer/caching/#c-invalidation">Invalidation</a>, <a href="/well-rounded-engineer/caching/#c-write-policies-by-name-through-back-around">Write policies by name: through, back, around</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Senior</td><td>Your p50 latency is 80 ms but p99 is 3 seconds. How do you find out why, and why does p99 matter more than the average?</td><td><a href="/well-rounded-engineer/performance/#c-percentiles-not-averages">Percentiles, not averages</a>, <a href="/well-rounded-engineer/observability/#c-distributed-tracing">Distributed tracing</a></td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
    <h4>APIs, boundaries and contracts</h4>
    <div class="tbl"><table><thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Question</th><th>Where to find the answer</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td>Foundation</td><td>A client retries a POST that creates an order after a timeout. How do you stop it creating two orders?</td><td><a href="/well-rounded-engineer/apis/#c-reliable-integration">Reliable integration</a>, <a href="/well-rounded-engineer/architecture/#c-idempotent-consumers">Idempotent consumers</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Practitioner</td><td>You must change a field's meaning in a public API used by 40 partners. How do you roll it out without breaking them?</td><td><a href="/well-rounded-engineer/apis/#c-api-versioning-and-evolution">API versioning and evolution</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Practitioner</td><td>What belongs in an API gateway, and what belongs in the service behind it?</td><td><a href="/well-rounded-engineer/apis/#c-gateway-load-balancer-proxy-mesh-who-does-what">Gateway, load balancer, proxy, mesh: who does what</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Senior</td><td>Two teams keep breaking each other's integration in production. How do you catch it before deploy without a full end-to-end environment?</td><td><a href="/well-rounded-engineer/teststrat/#c-contracts-instead-of-a-full-stack">Contracts instead of a full stack</a></td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
    <h4>Security</h4>
    <div class="tbl"><table><thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Question</th><th>Where to find the answer</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td>Foundation</td><td>A URL contains /orders/1043. What must the server check before returning it?</td><td><a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/well-rounded-engineer-labs/tree/main/security/02-order-that-was-not-yours" target="_blank" rel="noopener">the IDOR drill</a>, <a href="/well-rounded-engineer/security/#c-authentication-authorisation">Authentication & authorisation</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Practitioner</td><td>Where should secrets live, and how do you rotate one without downtime?</td><td><a href="/well-rounded-engineer/security/#c-secrets">Secrets</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Senior</td><td>Threat-model a new file-upload feature in 30 minutes. What do you draw, and which threats do you look for first?</td><td><a href="/well-rounded-engineer/security/#c-threat-modelling-in-30-minutes">Threat modelling in 30 minutes</a></td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
    <h4>Observability and incidents</h4>
    <div class="tbl"><table><thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Question</th><th>Where to find the answer</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td>Foundation</td><td>A user says checkout is slow. What three signals would you look at, and in what order?</td><td><a href="/well-rounded-engineer/observability/#c-signals-and-what-each-answers">Signals and what each answers</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Practitioner</td><td>Your alert fires at 3 a.m. for a CPU spike that harms nobody. How would you change what you alert on?</td><td><a href="/well-rounded-engineer/sre/#c-alerting-on-slos-burn-rates">Alerting on SLOs: burn rates</a>, <a href="/well-rounded-engineer/observability/#c-alerting">Alerting</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Senior</td><td>You have just recovered from an outage. How do you run the postmortem so that it changes something?</td><td><a href="/well-rounded-engineer/sre/#c-blameless-postmortem-template">Blameless postmortem template</a></td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
    <h4>Trade-offs, cost and people</h4>
    <div class="tbl"><table><thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Question</th><th>Where to find the answer</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td>Practitioner</td><td>A team of six wants to split its monolith into twelve microservices. What questions do you ask before agreeing?</td><td><a href="/well-rounded-engineer/microservices/#c-monolith-modular-monolith-microservices">Monolith, modular monolith, microservices</a>, <a href="/well-rounded-engineer/casestudies/#c-github-one-rails-monolith-kept-and-scaled">GitHub&rsquo;s case</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Senior</td><td>How would you migrate a legacy system to a new design while it keeps serving customers?</td><td><a href="/well-rounded-engineer/microservices/#c-migrating-from-a-monolith">Migrating from a monolith</a>, <a href="#atob">Solution Architecture: A to B</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Senior</td><td>Your cloud bill doubled in a month with the same traffic. Where do you look?</td><td><a href="#cloudcost">Cloud Cost</a>, <a href="#c-data-locality-move-the-work-to-the-data">Data locality: move the work to the data</a></td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note">A good answer names a trade-off, gives a rough number, and says what you would measure to know you were right. Practise a few each week rather than all at once.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Design doc template</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># Title
## Context        why now, who asked, links
## Goals          measurable; and non-goals
## Requirements   functional / non-functional (with numbers)
## Estimates      traffic, data, cost
## Design         diagram, API, data model, request flow
## Alternatives   what else was considered and why not
## Failure modes  each dependency down / slow / wrong
## Rollout        migration, flags, rollback plan
## Observability  metrics, alerts, SLOs, run records
## Security       data, access, threats
## Open questions owner and due date for each</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Follow the method: requirements, estimates, API, data, diagram</li><li>Name the building blocks and what each is for</li><li>Do back-of-the-envelope estimates</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Design a small service end to end with caching and a queue</li><li>Identify bottlenecks and failure modes</li><li>Write a design document others can build from</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Design for scale, availability targets and cost together</li><li>Choose storage and consistency models with clear trade-offs</li><li>Lead design reviews and challenge assumptions with numbers</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Design multi-region, highly available systems</li><li>Set reliability and capacity strategy for the organisation</li><li>Mentor others in design and trade-off thinking</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>Consistent hashing and partitioning</li><li>Consensus (Raft) and leader election</li><li>Multi-region active-active design</li><li>Capacity planning and load testing</li><li>SLOs and error budgets</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://github.com/donnemartin/system-design-primer" target="_blank" rel="noopener">System Design Primer</a><span class="lnote"> Free, broad introduction with examples</span></li><li><a href="https://dataintensive.net/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Designing Data-Intensive Applications</a><span class="lnote"> The deep reference behind most designs</span></li><li><a href="https://sre.google/books/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google SRE books (free)</a><span class="lnote"> Reliability, SLOs, operations at scale</span></li><li><a href="https://cloud.google.com/architecture/framework" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google Cloud Well-Architected Framework</a><span class="lnote"> Reliability, cost, security, performance pillars</span></li><li><a href="https://highscalability.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">High Scalability</a><span class="lnote"> Real-world architecture write-ups</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CAP_theorem?action=raw" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: CAP theorem</a>; <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9111.txt" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RFC 9111 HTTP Caching</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fallacies_of_distributed_computing?action=raw" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Fallacies of distributed computing</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/redis/docs/main/content/commands/set.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Redis SET (NX/EX)</a>; <a href="https://gist.github.com/jboner/2841832" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Latency numbers every programmer should know (Dean, collected by Bonér)</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Load_balancing_(computing)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Load balancing (computing)</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Service_discovery" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Service discovery</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scalability" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Scalability (horizontal and vertical scaling)</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Consistent_hashing" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Consistent hashing</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apache_Hadoop" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Apache Hadoop (data locality)</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CAP_theorem" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Origins: CAP theorem</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PACELC_design_principle" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Origins: PACELC</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Linearizability" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Origins: Linearizability</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lamport_timestamp" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Origins: Logical clocks and event ordering</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paxos_(computer_science)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Origins: Paxos consensus</a>; <a href="https://raft.github.io/raft.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Origins: Raft consensus</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bully_algorithm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Origins: Leader election (the bully algorithm)</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Long-running_transaction" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Origins: Sagas</a>; <a href="https://www.cs.cornell.edu/courses/cs734/2000FA/cached%20papers/SessionGuaranteesPDIS_1.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Origins: Session guarantees</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Consistent_hashing" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Origins: Consistent hashing</a>; <a href="https://www.allthingsdistributed.com/files/amazon-dynamo-sosp2007.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Origins: Dynamo: eventual consistency at scale</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conflict-free_replicated_data_type" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Origins: CRDTs</a>; <a href="https://martinfowler.com/bliki/CircuitBreaker.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Origins: Circuit breaker</a>; <a href="https://martinfowler.com/articles/microservices.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Origins: Microservices (the definition most cited)</a>; <a href="https://martinfowler.com/bliki/MonolithFirst.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Origins: Monolith first</a>. A few claims could not be confirmed at their official source from here and are being re-checked.</p><p class="runcode">A question, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/enrichmeai.github.io/issues/new?title=%5BSystem%20Design%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>. Nothing here is tracked and there are no comments to store: questions live in the open, where the next learner can find the answer.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= SPECIFICATIONS ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-specs" data-name="Specifications">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Specifications: the documents everything is built on</h2><p>Every protocol, file format and language in this guide is defined by a specification somewhere. Libraries and tutorials are other people&rsquo;s readings of it. When two systems disagree, or a library behaves oddly, the specification is where the argument ends. This page gathers the ones engineers meet most often, says who publishes each, and shows how to read them.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Who writes specifications, and how they become final</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Body</th><th>What it publishes</th><th>How a document becomes final</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>IETF and the RFC Editor</td><td>RFCs: the Internet&rsquo;s protocols and formats (HTTP, TLS, JSON, OAuth)</td><td>Not every RFC is a standard: the IETF adopts some as Internet Standards, while many are informational or experimental. The header of each RFC says which (&ldquo;Category: Standards Track&rdquo;, &ldquo;Informational&rdquo;)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>W3C</td><td>Web standards such as WCAG</td><td>Drafts move through the W3C Process to a W3C Recommendation, the finished standard</td></tr>
      <tr><td>WHATWG</td><td>The HTML Living Standard and other web platform standards</td><td>A living standard: updated continuously rather than released as numbered versions</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Ecma TC39</td><td>ECMA-262, the ECMAScript (JavaScript) language</td><td>Proposals move through numbered stages; at Stage 4 a proposal is finished and incorporated into the specification, which is published as a yearly edition</td></tr>
      <tr><td>ISO and IEC, IEEE</td><td>SQL (ISO/IEC 9075), dates and times (ISO 8601), floating point (IEEE 754), POSIX (IEEE 1003)</td><td>Developed by the standards body&rsquo;s committees and published by the body</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Open-source foundations</td><td>OpenAPI (OpenAPI Initiative, a Linux Foundation project), CloudEvents (CNCF), OpenTelemetry, OCI image format</td><td>Public repositories, working groups and tagged releases</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Language communities</td><td>Java (the JLS and JVMS, with JDK Enhancement Proposals, JEPs, for changes), Python (PEPs)</td><td>A proposal document is discussed, accepted and implemented; a PEP is a design document with a concise technical specification and a rationale</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>How to read a specification</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Learn the key words.</b> RFC 2119 defines them: <b>MUST</b> (or REQUIRED, SHALL) is an absolute requirement; <b>SHOULD</b> (or RECOMMENDED) means there may be valid reasons to do otherwise, but the full implications must be understood and weighed first; <b>MAY</b> (or OPTIONAL) is truly optional.</li>
      <li><b>Capitals matter.</b> RFC 8174 clarifies that the words carry that meaning only when they appear in capitals. A lower-case &ldquo;should&rdquo; is ordinary English.</li>
      <li><b>Normative and informative.</b> Requirements are normative; examples, notes and appendices marked informative explain but do not bind.</li>
      <li><b>Check the status and what replaced it.</b> An RFC&rsquo;s header lists what it obsoletes and updates: RFC 9110 (HTTP Semantics) obsoletes RFCs 7231, 7232, 7233, 7235 and several others, and portions of RFC 7230. Quote the current one.</li>
      <li><b>Read the table of contents first,</b> then only the sections your question needs. Few people read a spec front to back the first time.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Why it is worth the effort</h3>
    <p><i>Our view:</i> reading the specification for the two or three formats you use every day pays back many times. You stop arguing from blog posts, you can tell a library bug from your own, and you can write a precise bug report: quoting the section of RFC 9110 on 404 Not Found settles a review in one line.</p>
    <p><i>The other view:</i> specifications are long, formal and written for implementers. For most daily work a good library and its documentation are enough, and time spent on the spec is time not spent shipping. Go to the spec when the library and your expectation disagree.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>The catalogue: web, HTTP and security</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Specification</th><th>Publisher</th><th>What it defines</th><th>See</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td><a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RFC 9110: HTTP Semantics</a></td><td>IETF</td><td>The architecture, terminology and parts of HTTP shared by all its versions: methods, status codes, headers, and the http and https URI schemes</td><td><a href="#web">Web Fundamentals</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9457" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RFC 9457: Problem Details for HTTP APIs</a></td><td>IETF</td><td>A machine-readable error body for HTTP APIs, so each API need not invent its own (it obsoletes RFC 7807)</td><td><a href="#apis">APIs</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">HTML Living Standard</a></td><td>WHATWG</td><td>HTML and the browser&rsquo;s handling of it, updated continuously</td><td><a href="#web">Web Fundamentals</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">WCAG</a></td><td>W3C</td><td>Web Content Accessibility Guidelines</td><td><a href="#accessibility">Accessibility</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8446" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RFC 8446: TLS</a></td><td>IETF</td><td>A version of Transport Layer Security, the protocol designed to prevent eavesdropping, tampering and message forgery</td><td><a href="#security">Security</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6749" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RFC 6749: OAuth 2.0</a> and <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7636" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RFC 7636: PKCE</a></td><td>IETF</td><td>How an application obtains limited access to an HTTP service on a user&rsquo;s behalf; PKCE defends public clients against stolen authorisation codes</td><td><a href="#oauth">OAuth &amp; OIDC</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>OpenID Connect</td><td>OpenID Foundation</td><td>An authentication layer on top of OAuth 2.0, so clients can verify who the user is</td><td><a href="#oauth">OAuth &amp; OIDC</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7519" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RFC 7519: JSON Web Token</a></td><td>IETF</td><td>A compact, URL-safe way to carry claims between two parties</td><td><a href="#oauth">OAuth &amp; OIDC</a></td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>The catalogue: data formats, text and time</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Specification</th><th>Publisher</th><th>What it defines</th><th>See</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td><a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8259" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RFC 8259: JSON</a></td><td>IETF</td><td>A lightweight, text-based, language-independent data interchange format, derived from ECMAScript</td><td><a href="#javascript">JavaScript</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="https://json-schema.org/specification" target="_blank" rel="noopener">JSON Schema</a></td><td>JSON Schema project</td><td>A vocabulary to validate and annotate JSON documents</td><td><a href="#apis">APIs</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc4180" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RFC 4180: CSV</a></td><td>IETF (informational)</td><td>A common format for comma-separated values and the text/csv media type. It is informational, not a standard. <i>Our view:</i> that is one reason CSV files differ in practice</td><td><a href="#data">Data</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>YAML and <a href="https://toml.io/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">TOML</a></td><td>Their own projects</td><td>Configuration formats; TOML aims to be minimal, easy to read and to map unambiguously to a hash table</td><td><a href="#devops">DevOps</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="https://spec.commonmark.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CommonMark</a></td><td>CommonMark project</td><td>A rationalised Markdown, with over 500 examples that serve as conformance tests</td><td><a href="#web">Web Fundamentals</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="https://github.com/apache/parquet-format" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Apache Parquet format</a> and Apache Iceberg table spec</td><td>Apache Software Foundation</td><td>A column-oriented data file format; and a table format for large analytic tables that many engines can share</td><td><a href="#data">Data</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Unicode and <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc3629" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RFC 3629: UTF-8</a></td><td>Unicode Consortium; IETF</td><td>One character set for most of the world&rsquo;s writing systems, and UTF-8, the encoding that has been the most common on the web since 2008</td><td><a href="#web">Web Fundamentals</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>ISO 8601 and <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc3339" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RFC 3339</a></td><td>ISO; IETF</td><td>Dates and times in the Gregorian calendar with an optional UTC offset; RFC 3339 is a profile of ISO 8601 for Internet protocols</td><td><a href="#sql">SQL</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>IEEE 754</td><td>IEEE</td><td>Floating-point arithmetic, the formats behind float and double. <i>Our view:</i> keep money in integer minor units or a decimal type instead</td><td><a href="#payments">Payments</a></td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>The catalogue: APIs, events and platforms</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Specification</th><th>Publisher</th><th>What it defines</th><th>See</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td><a href="https://github.com/OAI/OpenAPI-Specification" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OpenAPI</a></td><td>OpenAPI Initiative (Linux Foundation)</td><td>A language-agnostic interface description for HTTP APIs, readable by people and tools without the source code</td><td><a href="#apis">APIs</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="https://github.com/asyncapi/spec" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AsyncAPI</a></td><td>AsyncAPI project</td><td>A machine-readable description of message-driven APIs over any protocol, such as AMQP, MQTT, WebSockets or Kafka</td><td><a href="#kafka">Kafka</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="https://github.com/graphql/graphql-spec" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GraphQL</a></td><td>GraphQL project (GraphQL Foundation)</td><td>A query language for APIs, first created at Facebook</td><td><a href="#graphql">GraphQL</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Protocol Buffers and gRPC</td><td>Google (open source)</td><td>Protobuf: a language- and platform-neutral way to serialise structured data, with generated code. gRPC: a remote procedure call framework built on it</td><td><a href="#apis">APIs</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="https://github.com/cloudevents/spec" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CloudEvents</a></td><td>CNCF</td><td>A common way to describe event data, so producers and consumers across services and platforms can interoperate</td><td><a href="#architecture">Architecture</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="https://github.com/open-telemetry/opentelemetry-specification" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OpenTelemetry</a></td><td>OpenTelemetry project</td><td>The cross-language requirements and expectations for every OpenTelemetry implementation</td><td><a href="#observability">Observability</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="https://github.com/opencontainers/image-spec" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OCI Image Format</a></td><td>Open Container Initiative</td><td>The format of container images</td><td><a href="#docker">Docker</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/modelcontextprotocol" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Model Context Protocol</a></td><td>MCP project</td><td>An open protocol for giving AI applications access to tools and data; its schema is written in TypeScript and also published as JSON Schema</td><td><a href="#mcp">MCP</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>POSIX (IEEE 1003)</td><td>Austin Group (IEEE, The Open Group, ISO/IEC)</td><td>A portable operating system interface, including a threading library API, that many Unix and Unix-like systems follow</td><td><a href="#linux">Linux</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>SQL (ISO/IEC 9075)</td><td>ISO/IEC</td><td>The SQL language. Every database extends and departs from it, so check your engine&rsquo;s own documentation too</td><td><a href="#sql">SQL</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="https://semver.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Semantic Versioning</a></td><td>semver.org</td><td>MAJOR for incompatible API changes, MINOR for backward-compatible features, PATCH for backward-compatible fixes</td><td><a href="#buildtools">Build Tools</a></td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>The catalogue: languages</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b><a href="https://tc39.es/ecma262/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ECMA-262</a></b>, the ECMAScript Language Specification, edited in public by TC39; proposals are tracked in its proposals repository (see <a href="#javascript">JavaScript</a>).</li>
      <li><b><a href="https://docs.oracle.com/javase/specs/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The Java Language and Virtual Machine Specifications</a></b>, one edition per Java SE release; changes to the JDK are proposed as <a href="https://openjdk.org/jeps/1" target="_blank" rel="noopener">JEPs</a> (see <a href="#java">Java</a>).</li>
      <li><b><a href="https://peps.python.org/pep-0001/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PEPs</a></b>, Python Enhancement Proposals, and the Python Language Reference (see <a href="#python">Python</a>).</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Describing an interface: choose by requirement</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>If this is true for you</th><th>It leans towards</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Clients call you with HTTP requests and wait for a response</td><td>OpenAPI</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Services publish and consume messages on a broker</td><td>AsyncAPI, with CloudEvents for the envelope</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Clients need to choose the fields they get, across related data</td><td>GraphQL&rsquo;s schema</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Typed calls between your own services, with streaming and generated clients</td><td>Protocol Buffers with gRPC</td></tr>
      <tr><td>You only need to validate a JSON document, such as a config file or a message body</td><td>JSON Schema</td></tr>
      <tr><td>An AI agent must discover and call your tools</td><td>MCP</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note"><i>Our view:</i> write the description first and generate code from it, so the document is the contract. <i>The other view:</i> generating the description from code keeps it from drifting from what runs, and suits teams that change the API often.</p>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Read MUST, SHOULD and MAY as RFC 2119 and RFC 8174 define them</li><li>Find the specification behind a format or protocol you use every day</li><li>Tell an RFC that is a standard from one that is informational</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Settle a disagreement by quoting the section of a spec</li><li>Describe an API in OpenAPI, AsyncAPI, GraphQL or Protobuf</li><li>Check which RFCs a document obsoletes before you cite it</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Choose an interface description by requirement</li><li>Write a specification other teams can build from</li><li>Track proposals that will change your stack (JEPs, PEPs, TC39 stages)</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Represent your organisation in a standards body or working group</li><li>Set the interface and versioning standards across an organisation</li><li>Contribute to a specification or its conformance tests</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>RFC 2119 key words</li><li>IETF, W3C, WHATWG, TC39 and ISO</li><li>HTTP, TLS, OAuth and JWT RFCs</li><li>JSON, CSV, Unicode and dates</li><li>OpenAPI, AsyncAPI, GraphQL, Protobuf and CloudEvents</li><li>Language specifications and enhancement proposals</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc2119" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RFC 2119: key words for requirement levels</a><span class="lnote"> MUST, SHOULD, MAY</span></li><li><a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RFC 9110: HTTP Semantics</a><span class="lnote"> The HTTP every version shares</span></li><li><a href="https://github.com/OAI/OpenAPI-Specification" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The OpenAPI Specification</a><span class="lnote"> Describing HTTP APIs</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc2119" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RFC 2119</a>; <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8174" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RFC 8174</a>; <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RFC 9110</a>; <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9457" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RFC 9457</a>; <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8259" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RFC 8259</a>; <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc4180" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RFC 4180</a>; <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc3629" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RFC 3629</a>; <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc3339" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RFC 3339</a>; <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8446" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RFC 8446</a>; <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6749" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RFC 6749</a>; <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7636" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RFC 7636</a>; <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7519" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RFC 7519</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Request_for_Comments" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Request for Comments</a>; <a href="https://www.w3.org/standards/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">W3C standards</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/World_Wide_Web_Consortium" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: World Wide Web Consortium</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/WHATWG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: WHATWG</a>; <a href="https://github.com/tc39/process-document" target="_blank" rel="noopener">TC39 process document</a>; <a href="https://github.com/tc39/ecma262" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ECMA-262 repository</a>; <a href="https://openjdk.org/jeps/1" target="_blank" rel="noopener">JEP 1</a>; <a href="https://peps.python.org/pep-0001/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PEP 1</a>; <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/javase/specs/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Java SE Specifications</a>; <a href="https://github.com/OAI/OpenAPI-Specification" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OpenAPI Specification README</a>; <a href="https://github.com/asyncapi/spec" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AsyncAPI specification</a>; <a href="https://github.com/graphql/graphql-spec" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GraphQL specification</a>; <a href="https://github.com/protocolbuffers/protocolbuffers.github.io" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Protocol Buffers overview</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GRPC" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: gRPC</a>; <a href="https://github.com/cloudevents/spec" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CloudEvents</a>; <a href="https://github.com/open-telemetry/opentelemetry-specification" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OpenTelemetry specification</a>; <a href="https://github.com/opencontainers/image-spec" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OCI Image Format</a>; <a href="https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/modelcontextprotocol" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Model Context Protocol repository</a>; <a href="https://json-schema.org/specification" target="_blank" rel="noopener">JSON Schema specification</a>; <a href="https://github.com/commonmark/commonmark-spec" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CommonMark</a>; <a href="https://github.com/toml-lang/toml" target="_blank" rel="noopener">TOML</a>; <a href="https://github.com/apache/parquet-format" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Apache Parquet format</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apache_Iceberg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Apache Iceberg</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unicode" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Unicode (UTF-8 on the web)</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ISO_8601" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: ISO 8601</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IEEE_754" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: IEEE 754</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/POSIX" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: POSIX</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SQL" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: SQL (ISO/IEC 9075)</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/OpenID" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: OpenID (OpenID Connect)</a>; <a href="https://semver.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Semantic Versioning</a>.</p><p class="runcode">A question, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/enrichmeai.github.io/issues/new?title=%5BSpecifications%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>. Nothing here is tracked and there are no comments to store: questions live in the open, where the next learner can find the answer.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= CACHING ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-caching" data-name="Caching">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Caching</h2><p>Keeping copies of expensive results closer to where they are needed. Where caches live, the patterns for reading and writing them, invalidation and consistency, stampedes, key design, and working code for Spring, Python, Redis, HTTP and BigQuery.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Where caches live</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>Example</th><th>Good for</th><th>Watch out for</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Browser</td><td>HTTP cache</td><td>Static assets, per-user responses</td><td>Wrong <code>Cache-Control</code> serving stale pages</td></tr>
      <tr><td>CDN</td><td>Cloud CDN</td><td>Public content near users</td><td>Caching personalised responses by mistake</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Gateway</td><td>Apigee ResponseCache</td><td>Identical API responses</td><td>Keys that ignore the caller's identity</td></tr>
      <tr><td>In-process</td><td>Caffeine, <code>functools.lru_cache</code></td><td>Hot reference data, nanosecond reads</td><td>Each instance has its own copy; memory per instance</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Distributed</td><td>Redis / Memorystore</td><td>Shared across instances, sessions, rate limits</td><td>Network hop; one more system to run</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Database</td><td>Buffer cache, materialised views</td><td>Repeated queries and aggregates</td><td>Refresh schedules</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Warehouse</td><td>BigQuery cached results, BI Engine</td><td>Repeated dashboard queries</td><td>Cache is skipped for some queries (non-deterministic functions, changed tables)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Model API</td><td>Prompt caching</td><td>Long, repeated prompt prefixes</td><td>Any change in the prefix invalidates it</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note">A cache is a copy, so every cache is a consistency decision. Add one when measurements show the need, and decide up front how stale its data may be.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Read and write patterns</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Cache-aside (lazy loading):</b> read the cache; on a miss, read the source and fill the cache. Simple and the most common.</li>
      <li><b>Read-through:</b> the cache library loads from the source itself on a miss (Caffeine <code>LoadingCache</code>).</li>
      <li><b>Write-through:</b> writes go to the cache and the source together; reads are always warm; writes are slower.</li>
      <li><b>Write-behind:</b> write to the cache, persist later. Fast, but a crash loses writes. Rarely worth it.</li>
      <li><b>Refresh-ahead:</b> refresh popular entries before they expire so users never wait for a miss.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Write policies by name: through, back, around</h3>
    <p>The same three names appear in CPU caches and in application caches, and they answer one question: when data is written, what happens to the cache?</p>
    <div class="tbl"><table><thead><tr><th>Policy</th><th>On a write</th><th>Fits when</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td><b>Write-through</b></td><td>Write to the cache and the backing store together</td><td>Data is read soon after it is written, and writes can afford to be slower</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Write-back</b> (write-behind)</td><td>Write to the cache now, to the store later</td><td>Writes must be fast and losing the most recent ones in a crash is acceptable (rarely, in business data)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Write-around</b> (no-write allocate)</td><td>Write straight to the store; do not put the data in the cache until it is read</td><td>Much of what is written is rarely read back, such as logs or bulk imports, so it would only push useful entries out of the cache</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Invalidation</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>TTL:</b> entries expire after a set time. Bounds staleness even when an invalidation is missed. Every cache entry should have one.</li>
      <li><b>Delete on write:</b> after updating the source, delete the cache key (not update it), so the next read reloads fresh data.</li>
      <li><b>Versioned keys:</b> include a version in the key (<code>order:v3:123</code>, or a data version number); bump it to invalidate everything at once.</li>
      <li><b>Event-driven:</b> consume change events (CDC, domain events) and evict affected keys.</li>
      <li><b>Immutable data</b> (an issued invoice, a fingerprinted file) can be cached forever.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">The race to know: reader A misses and reads the old value; writer B updates the source and deletes the key; A then writes the old value into the cache. A TTL bounds the damage; versioned keys avoid it.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>The race, drawn</h3>
    <figure class="fig"><svg viewBox="0 0 640 290" role="img" aria-labelledby="race-t race-d"><title id="race-t">The cache invalidation race</title><desc id="race-d">A sequence diagram with four lifelines: reader A, the cache, the source database, and writer B. 1: A asks the cache for a key and misses. 2: A reads the old value from the source. 3: B updates the source. 4: B deletes the cache key. 5: A, still holding the old value, writes it into the cache. The cache now serves the old value until its TTL expires.</desc><defs><marker id="race-a" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker><marker id="race-aa" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar acc" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker></defs><rect class="nd in" x="18.0" y="14" width="139" height="36" rx="4"/><text class="lb hd" x="87.5" y="37" text-anchor="middle">Reader A</text><line class="ln" x1="87.5" y1="50" x2="87.5" y2="282"/><rect class="nd" x="173.0" y="14" width="139" height="36" rx="4"/><text class="lb hd" x="242.5" y="37" text-anchor="middle">Cache</text><line class="ln" x1="242.5" y1="50" x2="242.5" y2="282"/><rect class="nd out" x="328.0" y="14" width="139" height="36" rx="4"/><text class="lb hd" x="397.5" y="37" text-anchor="middle">Source</text><line class="ln" x1="397.5" y1="50" x2="397.5" y2="282"/><rect class="nd in" x="483.0" y="14" width="139" height="36" rx="4"/><text class="lb hd" x="552.5" y="37" text-anchor="middle">Writer B</text><line class="ln" x1="552.5" y1="50" x2="552.5" y2="282"/><line class="ed" x1="87.5" y1="80" x2="238.5" y2="80" marker-end="url(#race-a)"/><circle class="nd in" cx="100.5" cy="68" r="9"/><text class="lb mu" x="100.5" y="72" text-anchor="middle">1</text><text class="lb" x="115.5" y="73" text-anchor="start">get key: miss</text><line class="ed" x1="87.5" y1="114" x2="393.5" y2="114" marker-end="url(#race-a)"/><circle class="nd in" cx="100.5" cy="102" r="9"/><text class="lb mu" x="100.5" y="106" text-anchor="middle">2</text><text class="lb" x="115.5" y="107" text-anchor="start">read: old value</text><line class="ed" x1="552.5" y1="148" x2="401.5" y2="148" marker-end="url(#race-a)"/><circle class="nd in" cx="410.5" cy="136" r="9"/><text class="lb mu" x="410.5" y="140" text-anchor="middle">3</text><text class="lb" x="425.5" y="141" text-anchor="start">update to new value</text><line class="ed" x1="552.5" y1="182" x2="246.5" y2="182" marker-end="url(#race-a)"/><circle class="nd in" cx="255.5" cy="170" r="9"/><text class="lb mu" x="255.5" y="174" text-anchor="middle">4</text><text class="lb" x="270.5" y="175" text-anchor="start">delete key</text><line class="ed acc" x1="87.5" y1="216" x2="238.5" y2="216" marker-end="url(#race-aa)"/><circle class="nd in" cx="100.5" cy="204" r="9"/><text class="lb mu" x="100.5" y="208" text-anchor="middle">5</text><text class="lb acc" x="115.5" y="209" text-anchor="start">set key = old value</text><text class="lb acc" x="250.5" y="252" text-anchor="start">stale until the TTL expires</text></svg><figcaption>The race in the last bullet above, step by step. A&rsquo;s write in step 5 lands after B&rsquo;s delete, so the cache keeps an old value. A TTL bounds how long; versioned keys avoid it.</figcaption></figure>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Stampedes (thundering herd)</h3>
    <p>A popular key expires and thousands of requests miss at once and hit the database together.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Single flight / request coalescing:</b> only one caller loads; the others wait for its result (Caffeine does this per key in-process).</li>
      <li><b>Distributed lock:</b> <code>SET lock:key 1 NX EX 10</code> in Redis; the winner loads, others briefly serve stale or wait.</li>
      <li><b>Stale-while-revalidate:</b> serve the expired value while one background refresh runs.</li>
      <li><b>Jittered TTLs:</b> add randomness so many keys do not expire at the same moment.</li>
      <li><b>Probabilistic early refresh:</b> refresh slightly before expiry with rising probability.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Key design</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Include every input that changes the answer:</b> IDs, filters, page, locale, user or tenant, and the API or data version.</li>
      <li><b>Normalise</b> inputs (sort filters, lower-case where meaning allows) so equal requests share a key.</li>
      <li><b>Namespace</b> keys: <code>service:entity:version:id</code>.</li>
      <li><b>Never</b> share a cache entry across users when the response depends on permissions; include the permission scope in the key, or cache only the permission-independent part.</li>
      <li><b>Keep values small</b>; large values make the cache slow and evict everything else.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>What not to cache, or not that way</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Errors and outages as "not found":</b> caching an empty result because the source was down turns "cannot see" into a confident "nothing there" for the whole TTL.</li>
      <li><b>Negative results</b> (genuinely not found) only briefly, if at all.</li>
      <li><b>Secrets and tokens</b> in shared caches.</li>
      <li><b>Personalised data</b> in public caches (CDN, gateway) without per-user keys.</li>
      <li><b>Data with legal effect</b> (an erasure request, a deletion) behind a long TTL: it must take effect immediately, so check it outside the cache or evict on change.</li>
      <li><b>Cheap queries:</b> if the source answers in 1 ms, a cache adds complexity for little gain.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Spring Cache with Caffeine or Redis</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>@Configuration
@EnableCaching
class CacheConfig {
  @Bean
  CacheManager cacheManager() {
    var m = new CaffeineCacheManager("orders", "stores");
    m.setCaffeine(Caffeine.newBuilder()
        .maximumSize(10_000)
        .expireAfterWrite(Duration.ofMinutes(10))
        .recordStats());                     // hit ratio for metrics
    return m;
  }
}

@Service
class OrderService {
  @Cacheable(cacheNames = "orders", key = "#id", unless = "#result == null")
  public Order get(String id) { return repo.find(id).orElse(null); }

  @CacheEvict(cacheNames = "orders", key = "#o.id()")
  public void update(Order o) { repo.save(o); }
}</code></pre></div>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># switch to Redis shared across instances
spring:
  cache:
    type: redis
    redis:
      time-to-live: 10m
      key-prefix: "shop:v3:"
  data:
    redis:
      host: ${REDIS_HOST}
      port: 6379</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note"><code>@Cacheable</code> works through a proxy, like <code>@Transactional</code>: calling the method from inside the same class skips the cache.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Python: in-process and Redis</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>from functools import lru_cache
from cachetools import TTLCache, cached

@lru_cache(maxsize=1024)                     # immutable inputs, never expires
def store_name(code: str) -&gt; str: ...

@cached(TTLCache(maxsize=10_000, ttl=600))   # expires after 10 minutes
def order_summary(order_id: str) -&gt; dict: ...</code></pre></div>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>import json, random, redis
r = redis.Redis(host=REDIS_HOST, port=6379, decode_responses=True)

def get_order(order_id: str) -&gt; dict | None:
    key = f"shop:order:v3:{order_id}"
    if (hit := r.get(key)) is not None:
        return json.loads(hit)
    o = db.load_order(order_id)           # raises if the DB is down: do not cache that
    if o is not None:
        r.set(key, json.dumps(o), ex=600 + random.randint(0, 60))   # jittered TTL
    return o

def update_order(o: dict) -&gt; None:
    db.save(o)
    r.delete(f"shop:order:v3:{o['id']}")  # delete after write</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Redis essentials</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>SET order:v3:123 '{"order_ref":"…"}' EX 600     # with TTL
SET lock:order:123 1 NX EX 10             # only if absent: a simple lock
GET order:v3:123
DEL order:v3:123
TTL order:v3:123
INCR ratelimit:app42:202611121015            # counters, rate limits
EXPIRE ratelimit:app42:202611121015 60
INFO stats                                   # keyspace_hits, keyspace_misses, evicted_keys</code></pre></div>
    <ul>
      <li>Set <code>maxmemory</code> and an eviction policy (<code>allkeys-lru</code> or <code>allkeys-lfu</code> for a pure cache).</li>
      <li>Avoid <code>KEYS *</code> in production; use <code>SCAN</code>.</li>
      <li>Design so the system still works (slower) if Redis is down; a cache should not be a single point of failure.</li>
      <li>On GCP: Memorystore for Redis or Valkey, private IP, AUTH and in-transit encryption on.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>HTTP and CDN caching</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># fingerprinted static assets
Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# public API response, shared caches allowed, short freshness
Cache-Control: public, max-age=60, stale-while-revalidate=30
ETag: "v3-123-a1b2"

# per-user data
Cache-Control: private, max-age=30

# sensitive
Cache-Control: no-store</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">More on headers on <a href="#web">Web Fundamentals</a>. Configure the CDN to vary on, or bypass for, the <code>Authorization</code> header and cookies.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Caching in the data stack</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>BigQuery cached results:</b> identical queries reuse results for free for about 24 hours, unless tables changed or the query is non-deterministic.</li>
      <li><b>Materialised views:</b> BigQuery keeps aggregates up to date incrementally and can rewrite queries to use them.</li>
      <li><b>BI Engine:</b> in-memory acceleration for dashboards.</li>
      <li><b>Serving tables:</b> a consumable table in Postgres is itself a cache of the warehouse; it needs a refresh rule and freshness monitoring like any other cache.</li>
      <li><b>Pipeline-level:</b> skip work whose inputs have not changed (compare ETags or content hashes) instead of recomputing.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Measuring a cache</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Hit ratio</b> (hits ÷ requests): low ratios mean wrong keys, short TTLs or data that is not reused.</li>
      <li><b>Latency</b> on hit and on miss.</li>
      <li><b>Evictions and memory:</b> constant evictions mean the cache is too small for the working set.</li>
      <li><b>Staleness:</b> how old served data is, against the agreed maximum.</li>
      <li><b>Source load</b> with and without the cache, to prove it earns its keep.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Explain cache-aside, TTL and hit ratio</li><li>Use an in-process cache for reference data</li><li>Read HTTP caching headers</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Add Spring or Python caching with keys, TTLs and eviction on write</li><li>Use Redis safely with TTLs, eviction policy and a fallback</li><li>Measure hit ratio and staleness</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Design multi-layer caching for a system with explicit staleness rules</li><li>Prevent stampedes and invalidation races</li><li>Decide what must never be cached (outages, permissions, legal effect)</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Set caching standards and shared cache platforms</li><li>Design caching for global, multi-region systems</li><li>Lead consistency and cost trade-offs across products</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>Caffeine's W-TinyLFU eviction</li><li>Redis data structures and persistence</li><li>CDN cache keys and purging</li><li>Materialised views and incremental maintenance</li><li>Semantic caching for LLM calls (and its risks)</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://redis.io/docs/latest/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Redis documentation</a><span class="lnote"> Commands, data types, eviction</span></li><li><a href="https://github.com/ben-manes/caffeine" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Caffeine</a><span class="lnote"> High-performance Java cache</span></li><li><a href="https://docs.spring.io/spring-boot/reference/io/caching.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Spring Boot caching</a><span class="lnote"> Cache abstraction and providers</span></li><li><a href="https://cachetools.readthedocs.io/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">cachetools</a><span class="lnote"> Python caches with TTL and LRU</span></li><li><a href="https://cloud.google.com/memorystore/docs" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Memorystore documentation</a><span class="lnote"> Managed Redis and Valkey on GCP</span></li><li><a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Guides/Caching" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MDN: HTTP caching</a><span class="lnote"> Browser and shared caches</span></li><li><a href="https://cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/cached-results" target="_blank" rel="noopener">BigQuery cached results</a><span class="lnote"> When BigQuery reuses query results</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9111.txt" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RFC 9111 HTTP Caching (s3.5 Authorization)</a>; <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Reference/Headers/Cache-Control" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MDN Cache-Control</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/cached-results" target="_blank" rel="noopener">BigQuery cached results (~24 h, not charged, non-deterministic skip)</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/materialized-views-intro" target="_blank" rel="noopener">BigQuery materialized views (incremental, smart tuning)</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/apigee/docs/api-platform/reference/policies/response-cache-policy" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Apigee ResponseCache policy</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/memorystore/docs/valkey" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Memorystore for Valkey</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/redis/docs/main/content/commands/info.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Redis INFO (keyspace_hits/misses, evicted_keys)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/redis/docs/main/content/develop/reference/eviction/index.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Redis eviction policies</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/redis/docs/main/content/commands/set.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Redis SET</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/main/documentation/spring-boot-docs/src/docs/antora/modules/reference/pages/io/caching.adoc" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Spring Boot caching reference (source)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/main/module/spring-boot-cache/src/main/java/org/springframework/boot/cache/autoconfigure/CacheProperties.java" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Spring Boot CacheProperties (spring.cache.redis.time-to-live / key-prefix)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/main/module/spring-boot-data-redis/src/main/java/org/springframework/boot/data/redis/autoconfigure/DataRedisProperties.java" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Spring Boot DataRedisProperties (spring.data.redis.host/port)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ben-manes/caffeine/master/caffeine/src/main/java/com/github/benmanes/caffeine/cache/Cache.java" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Caffeine Cache javadoc (compute at most once per key)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/tkem/cachetools/master/src/cachetools/__init__.py" target="_blank" rel="noopener">cachetools cached() signature</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/redis/redis-py/master/redis/commands/core.py" target="_blank" rel="noopener">redis-py set(ex=...)</a>; <a href="https://docs.spring.io/spring-framework/reference/integration/cache/annotations.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Spring Framework: cache annotations (proxy mode, self-invocation)</a>; <a href="https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/prompt-caching" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Claude docs: prompt caching</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cache_(computing)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Cache (computing), write policies</a>.</p><p class="runcode">A question, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/enrichmeai.github.io/issues/new?title=%5BCaching%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>. Nothing here is tracked and there are no comments to store: questions live in the open, where the next learner can find the answer.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= DEVOPS ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-devops" data-name="DevOps">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>DevOps</h2><p>Getting a change from a laptop to production safely and keeping it running: Git, CI/CD, releases, observability, incidents and supply-chain security.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card"><h3>Git you will use daily</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>git switch -c feat/123-short-name origin/main
git add -p                         # stage hunk by hunk
git commit -m "fix: reject unparseable order dates"
git fetch origin &amp;&amp; git rebase origin/main
git push -u origin feat/123-short-name
git push --force-with-lease        # after a rebase; never plain --force

git log --oneline --graph -20
git log -S "parse_date" --oneline  # commits that added/removed a string
git blame -L 40,60 file.py
git diff origin/main...HEAD        # what my branch changes
git show &lt;sha&gt;:path/file           # a file at a revision
git stash push -m wip; git stash pop
git restore file                   # discard local edits
git revert &lt;sha&gt;                   # undo a merged commit safely
git bisect start BAD GOOD          # binary-search a regression
git worktree add ../wt2 -b other origin/main   # second checkout
git reflog                         # find "lost" commits</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Branching &amp; PR habits</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Trunk-based:</b> short-lived branches off <code>main</code>, merged within a day, behind feature flags if unfinished.</li>
      <li><b>One concern per PR.</b> If the title needs "and", split it.</li>
      <li><b>Protect <code>main</code>:</b> PR required, checks required, no force pushes.</li>
      <li><b>Commit messages</b> say why, not only what. Conventional prefixes (<code>feat:</code>, <code>fix:</code>, <code>chore:</code>) help changelogs.</li>
      <li><b>Review for behaviour:</b> tests that would fail without the change, error paths, what happens when a dependency is down.</li>
      <li><b>Promote artefacts, not branches:</b> build once, tag the image with the commit SHA, deploy that same image to each environment.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>GitHub Actions → GCP (no keys)</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>name: deploy
on:
  push: { branches: [main] }
permissions:
  contents: read
  id-token: write              # needed for Workload Identity Federation
concurrency: { group: "deploy-${{ github.ref }}", cancel-in-progress: false }
jobs:
  test:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v7
      - uses: actions/setup-java@v6
        with: { distribution: temurin, java-version: 21, cache: gradle }
      - run: ./gradlew test
  deploy:
    needs: test
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    environment: prod          # environment secrets, protection rules
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v7
      - uses: google-github-actions/auth@v3
        with:
          workload_identity_provider: ${{ vars.WIF_PROVIDER }}
          service_account: ${{ vars.DEPLOY_SA }}
      - uses: google-github-actions/setup-gcloud@v3
      - run: |
          IMG=asia-south1-docker.pkg.dev/${{ vars.GCP_PROJECT }}/apps/api:${GITHUB_SHA::12}
          gcloud auth configure-docker asia-south1-docker.pkg.dev --quiet
          docker build -t "$IMG" . &amp;&amp; docker push "$IMG"
          echo "IMG=$IMG" &gt;&gt; "$GITHUB_ENV"
      - uses: google-github-actions/deploy-cloudrun@v3
        with: { service: api, region: asia-south1, image: "${{ env.IMG }}" }</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">The major versions shown were current in November 2026; check each action's latest release. For security, pin third-party actions to a full commit SHA rather than a tag. A workflow-level <code>paths:</code> filter on a required check can leave a PR waiting forever; skip inside the job instead.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Release strategies</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Strategy</th><th>How</th><th>When</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Rolling</td><td>Replace instances a few at a time</td><td>Default for stateless services</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Blue/green</td><td>Run old and new side by side, switch traffic at once</td><td>Fast rollback, needs double capacity</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Canary</td><td>Send a small % to the new version, watch metrics, increase</td><td>Risky changes with good metrics</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Feature flag</td><td>Ship code dark, turn it on per user or percentage</td><td>Separating deploy from release</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Expand / contract</td><td>Add the new schema, migrate, remove the old in a later release</td><td>Database changes with zero downtime</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note">Every release needs a rollback you have practised. A database migration that cannot be rolled back makes the whole release one-way.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Blue-green, in plain words</h3>
    <p>Think of a theatre with two stages. The audience watches the blue stage while the crew builds the next show on the green one. When green is ready and rehearsed, you swing the audience over in one move. If something goes wrong, you swing them straight back: blue is still standing.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Before the switch:</b> green gets the same checks real users would give it, through a private URL or a test route.</li>
      <li><b>The switch is all at once,</b> so a fault reaches every user, but only until you switch back, which takes seconds.</li>
      <li><b>Keep blue running</b> until you trust green. Pulling it down early leaves you with no way back.</li>
    </ul>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># Kubernetes: a Deployment per colour,
# labelled version: blue / version: green.
# The Service's selector picks the live one.
kubectl patch service shop-api \
  -p '{"spec":{"selector":{"version":"green"}}}'
# Roll back: patch it back to "blue".

# Cloud Run: deploy green with no traffic
gcloud run deploy api --image=…:4b1c2d0 \
  --no-traffic --tag=green
# test https://green---api-xxxxx.a.run.app
gcloud run services update-traffic api \
  --to-tags=green=100</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">The price: for a while you run two full copies, so you pay for twice the capacity. Read Martin Fowler's <a href="https://martinfowler.com/bliki/BlueGreenDeployment.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">BlueGreenDeployment</a>.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Canary, in plain words</h3>
    <p>Miners once took a canary underground: if the air turned bad, the bird showed it first. A canary release works the same way. A small share of real users gets the new version, you watch closely, and only when it looks healthy do you send more.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Go in steps:</b> for example 1%, then 5%, 25%, 50%, 100%, pausing at each step long enough to learn something.</li>
      <li><b>Compare the canary with the old version running at the same time,</b> not with yesterday: error rate, p95 and p99 latency, saturation, and one number the business cares about.</li>
      <li><b>Decide the stop rule before you start.</b> "Roll back if errors rise above 1% for five minutes" is a rule; "keep an eye on it" is not.</li>
    </ul>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># Kubernetes Gateway API:
# weights on an HTTPRoute
apiVersion: gateway.networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: HTTPRoute
metadata:
  name: shop-api
spec:
  parentRefs:
    - name: public-gw
  rules:
    - backendRefs:
        - name: shop-api-stable
          port: 8080
          weight: 95
        - name: shop-api-canary
          port: 8080
          weight: 5

# Cloud Run: move traffic by revision
gcloud run services update-traffic api \
  --to-revisions=api-00042-xyz=5,api-00041-abc=95</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">With Istio, the same weights live on a VirtualService (see <a href="#servicemesh">Service Mesh</a>). Read the Gateway API guide to <a href="https://gateway-api.sigs.k8s.io/guides/user-guides/traffic-splitting/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">HTTP traffic splitting</a> and Fowler's <a href="https://martinfowler.com/bliki/CanaryRelease.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CanaryRelease</a>.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Choosing between them, and the hard parts</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Pick blue-green</b> when you need an instant, whole switch and an instant way back, and you can afford two copies for a while. It suits releases you can test fully before users arrive.</li>
      <li><b>Pick canary</b> when you have enough traffic and good metrics to learn from a small slice. At 1% of 100 requests an hour you learn almost nothing, so a quiet service is better served by blue-green and a thorough test.</li>
      <li><b>The database is the hard part of both.</b> Old and new versions run at the same time against the same data, so every schema change has to work for both: add first, move the data, remove the old shape in a later release (expand and contract).</li>
      <li><b>Sessions and caches:</b> a user who bounces between versions may see two behaviours, so keep a user on one version where that matters, and make sure caches and queues can be read by both.</li>
      <li><b>Feature flags are a separate tool.</b> A deploy puts code on servers; a flag decides who sees the feature. Used together, a canary checks the code is healthy and a flag controls the release to users.</li>
      <li><b>Automate it once you understand it.</b> <a href="https://argoproj.github.io/argo-rollouts/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Argo Rollouts</a> and Flagger run blue-green and canary steps on Kubernetes with automatic analysis, and <a href="#harness">Harness</a> has both built in. Do it by hand a few times first, so you know what the tool is deciding for you.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">Whichever you pick, rehearse the rollback before you need it. The way back is the part of a release that matters most when things go wrong, and it is usually the part nobody practised.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Observability</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Logs</b> say what happened (structured JSON, with a request or run ID). <b>Metrics</b> say how much and how often. <b>Traces</b> say where the time went across services.</li>
      <li><b>Golden signals:</b> latency, traffic, errors, saturation. RED for services (rate, errors, duration); USE for resources (utilisation, saturation, errors).</li>
      <li><b>SLI:</b> a measured ratio, e.g. requests under 500 ms ÷ all requests. <b>SLO:</b> the target, e.g. 99.5% over 30 days. <b>Error budget:</b> the 0.5% you may spend on releases and incidents.</li>
      <li><b>Alert on symptoms users feel</b> (SLO burn rate), not on every CPU spike. Every alert needs an owner and a runbook.</li>
      <li><b>Know "nothing there" from "cannot see":</b> a check whose data source is down must report unknown, not zero or green.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Incident response</h3>
    <ol>
      <li><b>Declare</b> it early and name an incident lead. One channel, one timeline.</li>
      <li><b>Mitigate before you diagnose:</b> roll back, fail over, scale up, turn the flag off.</li>
      <li><b>Communicate</b> status on a schedule, even "no change".</li>
      <li><b>Write down</b> what you saw and did, with timestamps, as you go.</li>
      <li><b>Review blamelessly</b> afterwards: what happened, why the system allowed it, which guard would have caught it. Track the follow-ups to done.</li>
    </ol>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Supply-chain &amp; secrets</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>No long-lived keys:</b> Workload Identity Federation for CI, service accounts for workloads.</li>
      <li><b>Secrets in a secret manager,</b> injected at runtime. Never in Git, images, logs or CI output.</li>
      <li><b>Least privilege:</b> one service account per workload, with only the roles it uses.</li>
      <li><b>Dependency updates</b> automated (Dependabot or Renovate), with tests gating them.</li>
      <li><b>Scan</b> code (SAST), dependencies (SCA), images (Trivy) and committed secrets (gitleaks, GitHub secret scanning).</li>
      <li><b>Know what you ship:</b> SBOMs and signed images (cosign) make "are we affected by CVE-X?" a query, not a hunt.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Measuring delivery</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>DORA metric</th><th>Question</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Deployment frequency</td><td>How often do we ship to production?</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Lead time for changes</td><td>Commit to production: how long?</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Change failure rate</td><td>What share of deploys cause a failure?</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Failed deployment recovery time (formerly time to restore)</td><td>How fast do we recover from a failed deploy?</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Deployment rework rate</td><td>What share of deploys are unplanned fixes for a production incident?</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note">Speed and stability rise together when changes are small, tested and easy to roll back.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Twelve-factor, short version</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>One codebase, many deploys; config in the environment, not the code.</li>
      <li>Dependencies declared and pinned; backing services (DB, queue) attached by URL.</li>
      <li>Build, release and run are separate steps; the same build goes to every environment.</li>
      <li>Processes are stateless and disposable: fast start, graceful shutdown on <code>SIGTERM</code>.</li>
      <li>Logs are an event stream to stdout; the platform collects them.</li>
      <li>Admin tasks (migrations, backfills) run as one-off processes from the same release.</li>
      <li>Dev and prod as similar as possible: same images, same database engine.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Use Git daily: branch, commit, rebase, PR</li><li>Read a CI run and find why it failed</li><li>Roll back a deploy</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Write CI/CD workflows with caching and keyless cloud auth</li><li>Add structured logs, metrics and alerts</li><li>Take part in incident response</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Design release strategies (canary, blue/green, flags)</li><li>Define SLOs and alerting on symptoms</li><li>Improve DORA metrics; lead post-incident reviews</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Build an internal developer platform and golden paths</li><li>Set delivery and reliability standards for the organisation</li><li>Lead culture change toward small, safe, frequent releases</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>Trunk-based development</li><li>OpenTelemetry for traces, metrics and logs</li><li>GitOps (Argo CD, Flux)</li><li>Platform engineering and golden paths</li><li>Chaos engineering</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://git-scm.com/book/en/v2" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Pro Git (free book)</a><span class="lnote"> Git from basics to internals</span></li><li><a href="https://docs.github.com/en/actions" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub Actions documentation</a><span class="lnote"> Workflows, environments, OIDC</span></li><li><a href="https://dora.dev/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">DORA</a><span class="lnote"> Research on delivery performance and its capabilities</span></li><li><a href="https://sre.google/books/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google SRE books (free)</a><span class="lnote"> SLOs, alerting, incident response</span></li><li><a href="https://12factor.net/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The Twelve-Factor App</a><span class="lnote"> Principles for cloud-native services</span></li><li><a href="https://trunkbaseddevelopment.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Trunk-based development</a><span class="lnote"> Branching that supports continuous delivery</span></li><li><a href="https://opentelemetry.io/docs/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OpenTelemetry</a><span class="lnote"> Vendor-neutral observability</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/actions/checkout/main/README.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">actions/checkout README (v7 current)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/actions/setup-java/main/action.yml" target="_blank" rel="noopener">actions/setup-java action.yml / README (v6)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/google-github-actions/auth/main/README.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">google-github-actions/auth action.yml + README (v3, id-token: write)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/google-github-actions/deploy-cloudrun/main/action.yml" target="_blank" rel="noopener">google-github-actions/deploy-cloudrun action.yml (v3, service/region/image)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/google-github-actions/setup-gcloud/main/README.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">google-github-actions/setup-gcloud README (v3)</a>; <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/actions/writing-workflows/workflow-syntax-for-github-actions" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub Actions workflow syntax (path filter leaves required checks Pending; concurrency)</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/run/docs/rollouts-rollbacks-traffic-migration" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cloud Run rollouts, tags and traffic migration</a>; <a href="https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/git-log.1.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">git-log man page (-S)</a>; <a href="https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man1/git-bisect.1.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">git-bisect man page</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/kubernetes-sigs/gateway-api/main/examples/standard/traffic-splitting/traffic-split-2.yaml" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Gateway API traffic-split example</a>; <a href="https://martinfowler.com/bliki/BlueGreenDeployment.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Fowler: BlueGreenDeployment</a>; <a href="https://martinfowler.com/bliki/CanaryRelease.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Fowler: CanaryRelease</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/heroku/12factor/master/content/en/disposability.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Twelve-Factor: disposability (SIGTERM)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/heroku/12factor/master/content/en/logs.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Twelve-Factor: logs (stdout)</a>; <a href="https://dora.dev/guides/dora-metrics-four-keys/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">DORA metrics guide</a>; <a href="https://sre.google/sre-book/monitoring-distributed-systems/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">SRE book: Monitoring distributed systems (golden signals)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/argoproj/argo-rollouts/master/README.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Argo Rollouts README</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/fluxcd/flagger/main/README.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Flagger README</a>; <a href="https://developer.harness.io/docs/continuous-delivery/manage-deployments/deployment-concepts/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Harness deployment strategies</a>. A few claims could not be confirmed at their official source from here and are being re-checked.</p><p class="runcode">A question, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/enrichmeai.github.io/issues/new?title=%5BDevOps%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>. Nothing here is tracked and there are no comments to store: questions live in the open, where the next learner can find the answer.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= KUBERNETES ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-kubernetes" data-name="Kubernetes">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Kubernetes</h2><p>Core objects, the kubectl you will use daily, manifests that hold up in production, and GKE specifics.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Core objects</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Kind</th><th>Use it for</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Pod</td><td>One or more containers sharing network and volumes. Rarely created directly.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Deployment</td><td>Stateless apps: keeps N replicas, rolling updates, rollback.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>StatefulSet</td><td>Stable names and per-pod disks: databases, Kafka, ZooKeeper.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>DaemonSet</td><td>One pod per node: log shippers, node agents.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Job / CronJob</td><td>Run to completion, once or on a schedule: batch loads, migrations.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Service</td><td>Stable virtual IP and DNS name in front of matching pods.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Ingress / Gateway</td><td>HTTP routing from outside into Services (Gateway API is the newer model).</td></tr>
      <tr><td>ConfigMap / Secret</td><td>Configuration and credentials, as env vars or files.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>PersistentVolumeClaim</td><td>A request for durable storage.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>ServiceAccount</td><td>Identity for pods (and, on GKE, the link to a Google service account).</td></tr>
      <tr><td>HorizontalPodAutoscaler</td><td>Scale replicas on CPU, memory or custom metrics.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>NetworkPolicy</td><td>Pod-level firewall rules.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Namespace</td><td>A scope for names, quotas and RBAC.</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>kubectl daily</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>kubectl config get-contexts
kubectl config use-context gke_my-proj_asia-south1_main
kubectl config set-context --current --namespace=shop

kubectl get pods -o wide
kubectl get deploy,svc,ing
kubectl get all -A                         # every namespace
kubectl describe pod api-7d9f-abcde        # events at the bottom
kubectl logs deploy/api -f --since=10m
kubectl logs api-7d9f-abcde -c sidecar --previous   # crashed container
kubectl exec -it deploy/api -- sh
kubectl port-forward svc/api 8080:80
kubectl top pods                           # needs metrics-server

kubectl apply -f k8s/ --dry-run=server     # validate against the cluster
kubectl diff -f k8s/
kubectl apply -f k8s/
kubectl rollout status deploy/api
kubectl rollout undo deploy/api
kubectl rollout restart deploy/api         # new pods re-read env from Secrets/ConfigMaps (re-pull only with imagePullPolicy: Always)
kubectl scale deploy/api --replicas=3
kubectl get events --sort-by=.lastTimestamp
kubectl explain deployment.spec.strategy   # built-in field docs</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Deployment + Service</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata: { name: api, labels: { app: api } }
spec:
  replicas: 2
  selector: { matchLabels: { app: api } }
  strategy: { rollingUpdate: { maxUnavailable: 0, maxSurge: 1 } }
  template:
    metadata: { labels: { app: api } }
    spec:
      serviceAccountName: api
      containers:
        - name: api
          image: asia-south1-docker.pkg.dev/my-proj/apps/api:3f2a9c1
          ports: [{ containerPort: 8080 }]
          envFrom: [{ configMapRef: { name: api-config } }]
          env:
            - name: DB_PASSWORD
              valueFrom: { secretKeyRef: { name: db, key: password } }
          resources:
            requests: { cpu: 250m, memory: 512Mi }
            limits:   { memory: 512Mi }
          readinessProbe:
            httpGet: { path: /actuator/health/readiness, port: 8080 }
            periodSeconds: 5
          livenessProbe:
            httpGet: { path: /actuator/health/liveness, port: 8080 }
            initialDelaySeconds: 20
          startupProbe:
            httpGet: { path: /actuator/health/liveness, port: 8080 }
            failureThreshold: 30
            periodSeconds: 2
---
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata: { name: api }
spec:
  selector: { app: api }
  ports: [{ port: 80, targetPort: 8080 }]</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Job &amp; CronJob</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>apiVersion: batch/v1
kind: CronJob
metadata: { name: orders-load }
spec:
  schedule: "15 6 * * *"
  timeZone: "Etc/UTC"
  concurrencyPolicy: Forbid          # never two runs at once
  startingDeadlineSeconds: 600
  successfulJobsHistoryLimit: 3
  failedJobsHistoryLimit: 5
  jobTemplate:
    spec:
      backoffLimit: 0                # no automatic retries
      activeDeadlineSeconds: 3600
      ttlSecondsAfterFinished: 86400
      template:
        spec:
          restartPolicy: Never
          serviceAccountName: loader
          containers:
            - name: loader
              image: asia-south1-docker.pkg.dev/my-proj/apps/loader:3f2a9c1
              args: ["--year=2024"]
              resources: { requests: { cpu: "1", memory: 2Gi }, limits: { memory: 2Gi } }</code></pre></div>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>kubectl create job --from=cronjob/orders-load manual-20250131   # run now
kubectl get jobs; kubectl logs job/manual-20250131</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Services &amp; traffic</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>ClusterIP</b> (default): reachable inside the cluster only.</li>
      <li><b>NodePort</b>: a port on every node (30000–32767). Mostly a building block.</li>
      <li><b>LoadBalancer</b>: provisions a cloud load balancer with an external (or internal, by annotation) IP.</li>
      <li><b>Headless</b> (<code>clusterIP: None</code>): DNS returns pod IPs directly; used with StatefulSets.</li>
      <li><b>DNS name:</b> <code>&lt;svc&gt;.&lt;namespace&gt;.svc.cluster.local</code>; within the same namespace just <code>&lt;svc&gt;</code>.</li>
      <li>A Service with no endpoints means its <code>selector</code> matches no ready pod: check labels and readiness.</li>
    </ul>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: Ingress
metadata: { name: api }
spec:
  ingressClassName: nginx          # GKE's own controller uses the annotation kubernetes.io/ingress.class: gce instead
                                   # Ingress NGINX was retired in March 2026: prefer Gateway API or a maintained controller
  rules:
    - host: api.example.com
      http:
        paths:
          - path: /
            pathType: Prefix
            backend: { service: { name: api, port: { number: 80 } } }

kubectl get endpointslices -l kubernetes.io/service-name=api</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>A request, drawn</h3>
    <figure class="fig"><svg viewBox="0 0 660 240" role="img" aria-labelledby="k8s-t k8s-d"><title id="k8s-t">How a request reaches a pod</title><desc id="k8s-d">A request from outside enters through an Ingress or Gateway, which routes by host and path to a Service. The Service has a stable name and IP and selects pods by label. A Deployment keeps three replicas. Two pods are ready and receive traffic. The third is failing its readiness probe, so the Service sends it nothing, though it keeps running.</desc><defs><marker id="k8s-a" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker><marker id="k8s-aa" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar acc" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker></defs><rect class="nd in" x="10" y="95" width="100" height="46" rx="6"/><text class="lb hd" x="60" y="123" text-anchor="middle">client</text><rect class="nd" x="140" y="95" width="140" height="46" rx="6"/><text class="lb hd" x="210" y="116" text-anchor="middle">Ingress /</text><text class="lb hd" x="210" y="133" text-anchor="middle">Gateway</text><rect class="nd" x="310" y="95" width="140" height="46" rx="6"/><text class="lb hd" x="380" y="116" text-anchor="middle">Service</text><text class="lb mu" x="380" y="133" text-anchor="middle">app=shop</text><line class="ed" x1="110" y1="118" x2="136" y2="118" marker-end="url(#k8s-a)"/><line class="ed" x1="280" y1="118" x2="306" y2="118" marker-end="url(#k8s-a)"/><rect class="bd" x="480" y="20" width="170" height="200" rx="8"/><text class="lb mu" x="565" y="14" text-anchor="middle">Deployment, 3 replicas</text><rect class="nd out" x="495" y="35" width="140" height="44" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="565" y="62" text-anchor="middle">pod 1: ready</text><line class="ed acc" x1="450" y1="118" x2="491" y2="57" marker-end="url(#k8s-aa)"/><rect class="nd out" x="495" y="97" width="140" height="44" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="565" y="124" text-anchor="middle">pod 2: ready</text><line class="ed acc" x1="450" y1="118" x2="491" y2="119" marker-end="url(#k8s-aa)"/><rect class="nd warn" x="495" y="159" width="140" height="44" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="565" y="186" text-anchor="middle">pod 3: not ready</text><text class="lb mu" x="380" y="200" text-anchor="middle">readiness failing:</text><text class="lb mu" x="380" y="217" text-anchor="middle">no traffic, not restarted</text></svg><figcaption>A Service sends traffic only to pods that are ready. Readiness failing takes a pod out of rotation; liveness failing restarts it. A Service with no ready pods has no endpoints.</figcaption></figure>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Requests, limits, probes</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Requests</b> decide scheduling: the node must have that much free. <b>Limits</b> cap use.</li>
      <li>Over the <b>memory</b> limit → the container is killed (<code>OOMKilled</code>, exit 137). Over the <b>CPU</b> limit → throttled, not killed.</li>
      <li>Set memory request = limit for steady apps. Many teams leave the CPU limit off to avoid throttling.</li>
      <li>JVM: size heap from the container with <code>-XX:MaxRAMPercentage=75</code>.</li>
      <li><b>Readiness</b> failing removes the pod from Service traffic. <b>Liveness</b> failing restarts it. <b>Startup</b> holds the other two off until the app has booted.</li>
      <li>Never make liveness depend on a database or another service: an outage then restarts every pod.</li>
      <li>QoS: Guaranteed (requests = limits for all), Burstable, BestEffort (none set; evicted first).</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Debug by status</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Status</th><th>Usually means</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Pending</td><td>No node fits the requests, a PVC is unbound, or a node selector/taint excludes all nodes. <code>describe pod</code> → Events.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>ImagePullBackOff</td><td>Wrong image name or tag, or the node's identity cannot read the registry.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>CrashLoopBackOff</td><td>The app starts and exits. <code>logs --previous</code> shows why: bad config, missing secret, failed probe.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>OOMKilled</td><td>Memory limit too low, or a leak. Check <code>describe</code> → Last State.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>CreateContainerConfigError</td><td>A referenced ConfigMap or Secret key does not exist.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Running, not Ready</td><td>Readiness probe failing: wrong path or port, or a dependency is down.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Evicted</td><td>Node ran short of memory or disk.</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>kubectl debug -it api-7d9f-abcde --image=busybox:1.36 --target=api   # ephemeral debug container
kubectl run tmp --rm -it --image=nicolaka/netshoot -- bash          # network toolbox pod</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Config, secrets, scaling, safety</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>kubectl create configmap api-config --from-literal=DATASET=analytics --from-file=app.yml
kubectl create secret generic db --from-literal=password='...'   # base64, NOT encrypted
kubectl get secret db -o jsonpath='{.data.password}' | base64 -d

# HPA
kubectl autoscale deploy api --cpu=70% --min=2 --max=10   # older kubectl: --cpu-percent=70

# PodDisruptionBudget: keep capacity during node upgrades
apiVersion: policy/v1
kind: PodDisruptionBudget
metadata: { name: api }
spec:
  minAvailable: 1
  selector: { matchLabels: { app: api } }

# NetworkPolicy: default deny ingress in the namespace
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata: { name: default-deny-ingress }
spec:
  podSelector: {}
  policyTypes: [Ingress]</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">For real secrets use Secret Manager via the CSI driver or External Secrets, not values committed in YAML.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>RBAC &amp; GKE Workload Identity</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># Pod's Kubernetes SA → Google service account (no JSON keys)
apiVersion: v1
kind: ServiceAccount
metadata:
  name: loader
  namespace: shop
  annotations:
    iam.gke.io/gcp-service-account: shop-loader@my-proj.iam.gserviceaccount.com

gcloud iam service-accounts add-iam-policy-binding \
  shop-loader@my-proj.iam.gserviceaccount.com \
  --role=roles/iam.workloadIdentityUser \
  --member="serviceAccount:my-proj.svc.id.goog[shop/loader]"

# RBAC: read-only on pods in one namespace
kubectl create role pod-reader --verb=get,list,watch --resource=pods -n shop
kubectl create rolebinding jo-reads --role=pod-reader --user=jo@example.com -n shop
kubectl auth can-i delete pods -n shop --as=jo@example.com</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">GKE can also grant IAM roles straight to the Kubernetes principal (<code>principal://iam.googleapis.com/projects/&lt;number&gt;/locations/global/workloadIdentityPools/&lt;proj&gt;.svc.id.goog/subject/ns/&lt;ns&gt;/sa/&lt;ksa&gt;</code>) without a Google service account.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>GKE, Helm, Kustomize</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>gcloud container clusters create-auto main --region=asia-south1   # Autopilot
gcloud container clusters get-credentials main --region=asia-south1
gcloud container clusters list

# Helm: packaged apps with values
helm install redis oci://registry-1.docker.io/bitnamicharts/redis \
  -n cache --create-namespace -f values.yaml     # check the chart's README for where its images come from
helm upgrade --install api ./chart --set image.tag=3f2a9c1
helm template ./chart | kubectl diff -f -
helm rollback api 1

# Kustomize: one base, overlays per env (built into kubectl)
k8s/base/kustomization.yaml       resources: [deploy.yaml, svc.yaml]
k8s/overlays/prod/kustomization.yaml
  resources: [../../base]
  images: [{ name: asia-south1-docker.pkg.dev/my-proj/apps/api, newTag: 3f2a9c1 }]
  patches: [{ path: replicas.yaml }]
kubectl apply -k k8s/overlays/prod</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">Autopilot: Google manages nodes and you pay per pod request. Standard: you manage node pools. For one-shot batch work, Cloud Run jobs are often simpler than a cluster.</p>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Use kubectl to get, describe, log and exec</li><li>Read a Deployment and Service manifest</li><li>Explain why a pod is Pending or crashing</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Write manifests with probes, resources and config</li><li>Use Helm or Kustomize per environment</li><li>Set up Workload Identity and RBAC</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Design namespaces, network policies and autoscaling</li><li>Run production clusters: upgrades, PDBs, capacity</li><li>Debug networking, DNS and scheduling problems</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Design multi-cluster platforms and tenancy models</li><li>Build operators and platform abstractions</li><li>Set Kubernetes strategy and security policy</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>Gateway API</li><li>Operators and custom resources</li><li>Pod security standards</li><li>Service meshes</li><li>GKE Autopilot vs Standard</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://kubernetes.io/docs/home/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kubernetes documentation</a><span class="lnote"> Official docs</span></li><li><a href="https://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/kubectl/quick-reference/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">kubectl quick reference</a><span class="lnote"> Common commands</span></li><li><a href="https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/debug/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Troubleshooting</a><span class="lnote"> Debug pods, services and clusters</span></li><li><a href="https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GKE documentation</a><span class="lnote"> Google Kubernetes Engine</span></li><li><a href="https://helm.sh/docs/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Helm</a><span class="lnote"> Package manager for Kubernetes</span></li><li><a href="https://gateway-api.sigs.k8s.io/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Gateway API</a><span class="lnote"> The successor to Ingress</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/kubectl/generated/kubectl_autoscale/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">kubectl autoscale reference</a>; <a href="https://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/kubectl/generated/kubectl_debug/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">kubectl debug reference</a>; <a href="https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/service/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Service (NodePort range)</a>; <a href="https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/controllers/cron-jobs/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CronJob (timeZone)</a>; <a href="https://kubernetes.io/blog/2025/11/11/ingress-nginx-retirement/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ingress NGINX retirement (Kubernetes blog)</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/concepts/ingress" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GKE Ingress: ingress.class annotation</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/docs/how-to/workload-identity" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GKE Workload Identity</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/kubernetes-sigs/cli-experimental/master/site/content/en/references/kustomize/kustomization/images/_index.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kustomize images field</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/kubernetes-sigs/kustomize/master/api/internal/image/image.go" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kustomize image matching source</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/bitnami/charts/main/bitnami/redis/README.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bitnami Redis chart README</a>.</p><p class="runcode">A question, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/enrichmeai.github.io/issues/new?title=%5BKubernetes%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>. Nothing here is tracked and there are no comments to store: questions live in the open, where the next learner can find the answer.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= HELM ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-k8sdr" data-name="Kubernetes DR &amp; Best Practices">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Kubernetes: disaster recovery and best practices</h2><p>How to keep Kubernetes workloads running through ordinary failures, and how to get them back after a bad one. Read <a href="#kubernetes">Kubernetes</a> first; this page is about what happens when things break.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Know what you are protecting</h3>
    <p>Two numbers decide everything else. <b>RPO</b> (recovery point objective): how much data you can afford to lose, measured in time. <b>RTO</b> (recovery time objective): how long you can afford to be down. Agree them with the business before choosing a design, because a shorter RTO or RPO always costs more.</p>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <tr><th>State</th><th>Where it lives</th><th>How it comes back</th></tr>
      <tr><td>Cluster objects (Deployments, Services, config)</td><td>The cluster's backing store, usually etcd</td><td>Re-apply from Git (GitOps), or restore an etcd snapshot or a Velero backup</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Application manifests and Helm values</td><td>Your Git repository</td><td>Re-apply. If Git is the source of truth, the cluster is replaceable. See <a href="#helm">Helm Charts</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Persistent data</td><td>Volumes, and databases inside or outside the cluster</td><td>Volume snapshots, database backups with point-in-time recovery</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Secrets</td><td>Kubernetes Secrets, or an external secret store</td><td>From the external store; never only inside the cluster you are trying to recover</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Everything around it</td><td>DNS, certificates, IAM, container images, load balancers</td><td>Infrastructure as code. See <a href="#terraform">Terraform</a></td></tr>
    </table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Best practices that prevent most outages</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Never run naked Pods:</b> use a Deployment or StatefulSet, so a failed Pod is replaced.</li>
      <li><b>Run more than one replica</b> of anything that matters.</li>
      <li><b>Spread across zones:</b> topology spread constraints (with a <code>topologyKey</code> such as the zone label) keep replicas apart, so one zone failing does not take them all.</li>
      <li><b>PodDisruptionBudgets:</b> say how many replicas must stay up during voluntary disruptions such as node drains and upgrades. Tools should evict through the Eviction API, which respects them.</li>
      <li><b>Probes:</b> readiness keeps traffic away until a Pod is ready; liveness restarts a stuck container; a startup probe holds the other two off until a slow starter is up.</li>
      <li><b>Requests and limits</b> on every container, so the scheduler can place work and one noisy Pod cannot starve the rest.</li>
      <li><b>Version-control all configuration</b> and keep it simple, with consistent labels.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Back up: three layers</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>etcd (self-managed clusters):</b> take regular snapshots with <code>etcdctl snapshot save</code>, store them off the cluster, and encrypt them: they hold every Secret.</li>
      <li><b>Workloads and volumes:</b> Velero backs up cluster objects and persistent volumes to object storage, on a schedule, with a retention period (<code>--ttl</code>). The VolumeSnapshot API takes snapshots of volumes through the storage system's driver.</li>
      <li><b>Managed clusters:</b> the provider runs the control plane and etcd; you protect your workloads and data. Each has a backup service: Backup for GKE, AKS backup, and AWS Backup for EKS.</li>
      <li><b>Databases:</b> prefer a managed database, or a database operator with its own backups and point-in-time recovery, over raw volume snapshots of a running database.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Restore: the commands</h3>
    <p>From the Kubernetes and Velero documentation. Names in angle brackets are yours.</p>
    <pre><code class="language-bash"># etcd (self-managed): snapshot, and restore into a new data directory
ETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl --endpoints $ENDPOINT snapshot save snapshot.db
etcdutl --data-dir &lt;data-dir-location&gt; snapshot restore snapshot.db

# Velero: back up on a schedule
velero schedule create &lt;schedule-name&gt; --schedule "0 7 * * *" --ttl 720h0m0s

# Velero disaster recovery: make the backup location read-only, restore, then set it back
kubectl patch backupstoragelocation &lt;location&gt; --namespace velero \
  --type merge --patch '{"spec":{"accessMode":"ReadOnly"}}'
velero restore create --from-backup &lt;schedule-name&gt;-&lt;timestamp&gt;
kubectl patch backupstoragelocation &lt;location&gt; --namespace velero \
  --type merge --patch '{"spec":{"accessMode":"ReadWrite"}}'</code></pre>
    <p class="note">Making the backup location read-only first stops a scheduled backup from overwriting good backups while you restore.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Four DR strategies, from cheap to fast</h3>
    <ol>
      <li><b>Backup and restore:</b> rebuild the cluster and restore data when disaster strikes. Cheapest; slowest to recover.</li>
      <li><b>Pilot light:</b> a minimal copy of the core (data replicated, little else running) in another region, scaled up on the day.</li>
      <li><b>Warm standby:</b> a smaller but working copy runs all the time, ready to take traffic and scale.</li>
      <li><b>Multi-site active/active:</b> two or more regions serve traffic at once. Fastest recovery; most expensive and hardest to keep consistent.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="note">These four are the categories in AWS's disaster recovery whitepaper; the same ideas apply on any cloud.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>A backup you have not restored is a hope</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Restore regularly</b> into a scratch cluster, and time it. That time is your real RTO.</li>
      <li><b>Write the runbook</b> a person can follow without the automation, and keep it outside the cluster. See <a href="#sre">SRE</a>.</li>
      <li><b>Run game days:</b> drain a zone, delete a namespace in a test cluster, lose the etcd leader, and watch what happens.</li>
      <li><b>Watch the backups:</b> alert when a scheduled backup fails or is late. See <a href="#observability">Observability</a>.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Run workloads as Deployments with several replicas</li><li>Add readiness and liveness probes</li><li>Explain RPO and RTO</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Add PodDisruptionBudgets and zone spreading</li><li>Schedule Velero or managed backups and restore one</li><li>Keep all manifests in Git so a cluster is replaceable</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Choose a DR strategy against agreed RPO and RTO</li><li>Design multi-zone and multi-region clusters</li><li>Run restore drills and game days</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Own platform resilience across clusters and regions</li><li>Set backup, restore and DR standards</li><li>Lead recovery during a major incident</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>etcd snapshots</li><li>Velero backup and restore</li><li>Volume snapshots</li><li>Topology spread constraints</li><li>Disaster recovery strategies</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/administer-cluster/configure-upgrade-etcd/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Operating etcd clusters for Kubernetes</a><span class="lnote"> Snapshots and restore, from the Kubernetes docs</span></li><li><a href="https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/pods/disruptions/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Disruptions and PodDisruptionBudgets</a><span class="lnote"> Voluntary and involuntary disruptions</span></li><li><a href="https://velero.io/docs/main/disaster-case/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Velero: disaster recovery</a><span class="lnote"> The DR flow with schedules and restores</span></li><li><a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/disaster-recovery-workloads-on-aws/disaster-recovery-options-in-the-cloud.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Disaster recovery options in the cloud</a><span class="lnote"> The four strategies, from AWS's whitepaper</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/administer-cluster/configure-upgrade-etcd/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Operating etcd clusters for Kubernetes</a>; <a href="https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/pods/disruptions/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Disruptions and PodDisruptionBudgets</a>; <a href="https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/scheduling-eviction/topology-spread-constraints/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Pod topology spread constraints</a>; <a href="https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/configuration/overview/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Configuration best practices</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/vmware-tanzu/velero/main/site/content/docs/main/disaster-case.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Velero: disaster recovery</a>; <a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/whitepapers/latest/disaster-recovery-workloads-on-aws/disaster-recovery-options-in-the-cloud.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AWS whitepaper: DR options in the cloud</a>.</p><p class="runcode">A question, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/enrichmeai.github.io/issues/new?title=%5BKubernetes%20DR%20%26%20Best%20Practices%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>. Nothing here is tracked and there are no comments to store: questions live in the open, where the next learner can find the answer.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<section class="panel" id="p-helm" data-name="Helm Charts">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Helm charts</h2><p>Helm packages Kubernetes manifests as <b>charts</b>: templates plus default values, versioned and installed as <b>releases</b> you can upgrade and roll back. Chart structure, templating, values per environment, hooks, testing, publishing to a registry, and how Helm fits with GitOps and Kustomize. Basics are on <a href="#kubernetes">Kubernetes</a>.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Concepts</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Chart</td><td>A package: templates, default values and metadata</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Values</td><td>Inputs that fill the templates; defaults in <code>values.yaml</code>, overridden per environment</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Release</td><td>One installed instance of a chart in a namespace, with a name</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Revision</td><td>Each install, upgrade or rollback of a release; the history Helm can roll back to</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Repository / registry</td><td>Where charts are published: an HTTP chart repo or an OCI registry (e.g. Artifact Registry)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Chart version vs app version</td><td><code>version</code> changes when the chart changes; <code>appVersion</code> names the application it deploys</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>The terms, drawn</h3>
    <figure class="fig"><svg viewBox="0 0 660 250" role="img" aria-labelledby="helmrel-t helmrel-d"><title id="helmrel-t">A chart, its values, and a release's revisions</title><desc id="helmrel-d">A chart holds templates and default values. Values for one environment override the defaults. Helm renders both into Kubernetes manifests and installs them as a release with a name in a namespace. Each install, upgrade and rollback adds a revision: revision 1 installed, revision 2 an upgrade, revision 3 a rollback that restores revision 1's manifests as a new revision.</desc><defs><marker id="helmrel-a" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker><marker id="helmrel-aa" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar acc" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker></defs><rect class="nd" x="10" y="30" width="180" height="56" rx="6"/><text class="lb hd" x="100" y="53" text-anchor="middle">chart</text><text class="lb mu" x="100" y="72" text-anchor="middle">templates + values.yaml</text><rect class="nd in" x="10" y="110" width="180" height="56" rx="6"/><text class="lb hd" x="100" y="133" text-anchor="middle">values-prod.yaml</text><text class="lb mu" x="100" y="152" text-anchor="middle">overrides the defaults</text><rect class="nd" x="240" y="70" width="150" height="56" rx="6"/><text class="lb hd" x="315" y="93" text-anchor="middle">helm upgrade</text><text class="lb mu" x="315" y="112" text-anchor="middle">render + apply</text><line class="ed" x1="190" y1="58" x2="236" y2="90" marker-end="url(#helmrel-a)"/><line class="ed" x1="190" y1="138" x2="236" y2="106" marker-end="url(#helmrel-a)"/><rect class="bd" x="430" y="20" width="220" height="200" rx="8"/><text class="lb hd" x="540" y="40" text-anchor="middle">release shop in prod</text><rect class="nd" x="450" y="56" width="180" height="40" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="540" y="81" text-anchor="middle">revision 1: install</text><rect class="nd" x="450" y="110" width="180" height="40" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="540" y="135" text-anchor="middle">revision 2: upgrade</text><rect class="nd out" x="450" y="164" width="180" height="40" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="540" y="189" text-anchor="middle">revision 3: rollback to 1</text><line class="ed" x1="390" y1="98" x2="426" y2="98" marker-end="url(#helmrel-a)"/><path class="ed dash" d="M630,166 C660,140 660,90 632,76" marker-end="url(#helmrel-a)"/><text class="lb mu" x="540" y="236" text-anchor="middle">a rollback is a new revision</text></svg><figcaption>The table above as one picture. The history of revisions is what lets <code>helm rollback</code> take you back, and the rollback itself becomes the next revision.</figcaption></figure>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Chart structure</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>shop-api/
  Chart.yaml            # name, versions, dependencies
  values.yaml           # defaults
  values.schema.json    # optional: validate values
  templates/
    _helpers.tpl        # named templates (labels, names)
    deployment.yaml
    service.yaml
    ingress.yaml
    configmap.yaml
    hpa.yaml
    NOTES.txt           # printed after install
    tests/
      test-connection.yaml
  charts/               # dependency charts (vendored)</code></pre></div>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># Chart.yaml
apiVersion: v2
name: shop-api
description: Shop API
type: application          # or "library" for shared templates only
version: 0.3.0             # chart version (SemVer)
appVersion: "3f2a9c1"      # image tag it deploys by default
dependencies:
  - name: common
    version: "1.x.x"
    repository: oci://asia-south1-docker.pkg.dev/my-proj/charts</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Commands</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>helm create shop-api                         # scaffold
helm lint ./shop-api
helm template shop ./shop-api -f values-prod.yaml    # render locally, no cluster
helm upgrade --install shop ./shop-api \
  -n shop --create-namespace \
  -f values-prod.yaml --set image.tag=3f2a9c1 \
  --atomic --timeout 5m                       # roll back automatically on failure
# Helm 4 renamed --atomic to --rollback-on-failure; check `helm version`
helm list -n shop
helm history shop -n shop
helm rollback shop 4 -n shop
helm get values shop -n shop                # what was applied
helm get manifest shop -n shop
helm uninstall shop -n shop

helm dependency update ./shop-api
helm package ./shop-api                      # → shop-api-0.3.0.tgz
helm push shop-api-0.3.0.tgz oci://asia-south1-docker.pkg.dev/my-proj/charts
helm install shop oci://asia-south1-docker.pkg.dev/my-proj/charts/shop-api --version 0.3.0</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Templates</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># templates/_helpers.tpl
{{- define "shop-api.fullname" -}}
{{- printf "%s-%s" .Release.Name .Chart.Name | trunc 63 | trimSuffix "-" -}}
{{- end -}}

{{- define "shop-api.selectorLabels" -}}
app.kubernetes.io/name: {{ .Chart.Name }}
app.kubernetes.io/instance: {{ .Release.Name }}
{{- end -}}</code></pre></div>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># templates/deployment.yaml
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: {{ include "shop-api.fullname" . }}
  labels:
    {{- include "shop-api.selectorLabels" . | nindent 4 }}
    app.kubernetes.io/version: {{ .Values.image.tag | default .Chart.AppVersion | quote }}
spec:
  replicas: {{ .Values.replicaCount }}
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      {{- include "shop-api.selectorLabels" . | nindent 6 }}
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        {{- include "shop-api.selectorLabels" . | nindent 8 }}
      annotations:
        checksum/config: {{ include (print $.Template.BasePath "/configmap.yaml") . | sha256sum }}
    spec:
      containers:
        - name: app
          image: "{{ .Values.image.repository }}:{{ .Values.image.tag | default .Chart.AppVersion }}"
          resources:
            {{- toYaml .Values.resources | nindent 12 }}
          {{- with .Values.env }}
          env:
            {{- toYaml . | nindent 12 }}
          {{- end }}
          envFrom:
            - secretRef:
                name: {{ required "secretName is required" .Values.secretName }}</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">The <code>checksum/config</code> annotation restarts pods when the ConfigMap changes. <code>required</code> fails the render with a clear message instead of deploying something broken.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Values per environment</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># values.yaml (defaults: safe, small)
replicaCount: 1
image:
  repository: asia-south1-docker.pkg.dev/my-proj/apps/shop-api
  tag: ""                       # defaults to appVersion
resources:
  requests: { cpu: 250m, memory: 512Mi }
  limits:   { memory: 512Mi }
env: []
secretName: ""

# values-prod.yaml (only what differs)
replicaCount: 3
resources:
  requests: { cpu: "1", memory: 1Gi }
  limits:   { memory: 1Gi }
secretName: shop-api-secrets</code></pre></div>
    <ul>
      <li>Later <code>-f</code> files override earlier ones; <code>--set</code> overrides files. Prefer files in Git over <code>--set</code>.</li>
      <li>Add <code>values.schema.json</code> so a typo in a value fails at install, not at runtime.</li>
      <li>Never put secret values in values files; reference Secrets created by External Secrets or the Secret Manager CSI driver.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Hooks and tests</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># templates/migrate-job.yaml: run DB migrations before an upgrade
apiVersion: batch/v1
kind: Job
metadata:
  name: {{ include "shop-api.fullname" . }}-migrate
  annotations:
    "helm.sh/hook": pre-install,pre-upgrade
    "helm.sh/hook-weight": "0"
    "helm.sh/hook-delete-policy": before-hook-creation,hook-succeeded
spec:
  backoffLimit: 0
  template:
    spec:
      restartPolicy: Never
      containers:
        - name: migrate
          image: "{{ .Values.image.repository }}:{{ .Values.image.tag | default .Chart.AppVersion }}"
          args: ["migrate"]</code></pre></div>
    <ul>
      <li>Hook events: <code>pre-install</code>, <code>post-install</code>, <code>pre-upgrade</code>, <code>post-upgrade</code>, <code>pre-rollback</code>, <code>pre-delete</code> and more.</li>
      <li>Migrations in a hook must be backward compatible: a rollback does not undo them.</li>
      <li><code>helm test shop</code> runs pods annotated <code>"helm.sh/hook": test</code> to smoke-test a release.</li>
      <li>Unit-test templates with the helm-unittest plugin; lint and install-test charts in CI with chart-testing (<code>ct</code>).</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Helm, Kustomize and GitOps</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th></th><th>Helm</th><th>Kustomize</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Approach</td><td>Templates + values</td><td>Plain YAML + patches per overlay</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Packaging and versions</td><td>Yes: charts, releases, rollback</td><td>No: just manifests</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Best for</td><td>Distributing reusable apps; many similar services</td><td>Your own manifests with small per-environment differences</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Risk</td><td>Template logic becomes hard to read</td><td>Many overlays become hard to follow</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <ul>
      <li><b>GitOps</b> (Argo CD, Flux): the desired chart version and values live in Git; a controller in the cluster applies them and reports drift. No one runs <code>helm upgrade</code> by hand in production.</li>
      <li>Run <code>helm diff upgrade</code> (plugin) or the GitOps diff before every production change.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Chart best practices</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Selector labels never change</b> after the first release: Kubernetes forbids changing a Deployment's selector.</li>
      <li><b>Standard labels</b> (<code>app.kubernetes.io/name</code>, <code>instance</code>, <code>version</code>, <code>managed-by</code>).</li>
      <li><b>Sensible defaults:</b> resources, probes, security context (non-root, read-only root filesystem) on by default.</li>
      <li><b>Keep templates boring:</b> little logic; move complexity into values, not nested conditionals.</li>
      <li><b>A library chart</b> of shared helpers is your blueprint: every service chart depends on it, so a fix reaches all of them.</li>
      <li><b>Pin dependency versions</b>; publish charts to an OCI registry with immutable versions.</li>
      <li><b>Document values</b> in <code>values.yaml</code> comments and the schema.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Install, upgrade, roll back and inspect a release</li><li>Read a chart's values and override them per environment</li><li>Render a chart locally with helm template</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Write charts with helpers, schema-validated values and sane defaults</li><li>Use hooks for migrations and helm test for smoke tests</li><li>Publish charts to an OCI registry</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Design library charts as a shared blueprint for many services</li><li>Deliver charts with GitOps (Argo CD or Flux) and diffs</li><li>Choose between Helm and Kustomize per case</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Own the organisation's deployment packaging standards</li><li>Run chart supply chain: signing, provenance, registries</li><li>Lead migrations between delivery models</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>Library charts</li><li>values.schema.json</li><li>Helm hooks and their limits</li><li>Argo CD ApplicationSets</li><li>Chart signing and provenance</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://helm.sh/docs/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Helm documentation</a><span class="lnote"> Concepts, commands, charts</span></li><li><a href="https://helm.sh/docs/chart_template_guide/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Chart template guide</a><span class="lnote"> Templates, functions, helpers</span></li><li><a href="https://helm.sh/docs/chart_best_practices/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Chart best practices</a><span class="lnote"> Conventions for maintainable charts</span></li><li><a href="https://artifacthub.io/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Artifact Hub</a><span class="lnote"> Find public charts and operators</span></li><li><a href="https://github.com/helm-unittest/helm-unittest" target="_blank" rel="noopener">helm-unittest</a><span class="lnote"> Unit tests for templates</span></li><li><a href="https://github.com/helm/chart-testing" target="_blank" rel="noopener">chart-testing (ct)</a><span class="lnote"> Lint and install-test charts in CI</span></li><li><a href="https://argo-cd.readthedocs.io/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Argo CD</a><span class="lnote"> GitOps for Kubernetes</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/helm/helm-www/main/docs/overview.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Helm 4 overview (flag renames)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/helm/helm-www/main/docs/helm/helm_upgrade.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">helm upgrade reference</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/helm/helm-www/main/docs/topics/charts_hooks.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Chart hooks</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/helm/helm-www/main/docs/topics/chart_tests.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Chart tests</a>.</p><p class="runcode">A question, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/enrichmeai.github.io/issues/new?title=%5BHelm%20Charts%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>. Nothing here is tracked and there are no comments to store: questions live in the open, where the next learner can find the answer.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= OPERATORS ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-operators" data-name="Kubernetes Operators">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Kubernetes operators</h2><p>An operator extends Kubernetes with your own resource types and a controller that manages them, encoding the knowledge a human operator would apply: how to install, upgrade, back up, fail over and heal a system. The reconcile pattern, CRDs, building operators in Go, Java and Python, and the well-known operators worth using rather than writing.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>The pattern</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>A <b>Custom Resource Definition (CRD)</b> adds a new resource type to the Kubernetes API, such as <code>PostgresCluster</code> or <code>PipelineRun</code>.</li>
      <li>Users create <b>custom resources</b> stating the <b>desired state</b> (<code>spec</code>): "three Postgres replicas, version 16, nightly backups".</li>
      <li>A <b>controller</b> watches those resources and the objects it owns, and <b>reconciles</b>: observe the actual state, compare it with the spec, act to close the gap, and record what it saw in <code>status</code>.</li>
      <li>Reconcile runs again on every change and periodically, so the system heals itself when reality drifts.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="note">Controllers are <b>level-triggered</b>: they act on the current state, not on the event that woke them. That makes them robust to missed events and restarts, as long as every reconcile is idempotent.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>The loop, drawn</h3>
    <figure class="fig"><svg viewBox="0 0 660 260" role="img" aria-labelledby="reconcile-t reconcile-d"><title id="reconcile-t">The reconcile loop</title><desc id="reconcile-d">A loop of four steps around a custom resource. 1 observe the actual state of the cluster. 2 compare it with the spec, the desired state, such as three Postgres replicas. 3 act to close the gap, for example create the missing replica. 4 record what was seen in status. The loop runs on every change and periodically, and acts on the current state rather than the event that woke it.</desc><defs><marker id="reconcile-a" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker><marker id="reconcile-aa" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar acc" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker></defs><rect class="nd in" x="240" y="100" width="180" height="60" rx="6"/><text class="lb hd" x="330" y="124" text-anchor="middle">custom resource</text><text class="lb mu" x="330" y="144" text-anchor="middle">spec: 3 replicas</text><text class="lb hd" x="330" y="25" text-anchor="middle">1 observe actual state</text><text class="lb hd" x="590" y="135" text-anchor="middle">2 compare with spec</text><text class="lb hd" x="330" y="240" text-anchor="middle">3 act to close the gap</text><text class="lb hd" x="70" y="135" text-anchor="middle">4 record in status</text><path class="ed acc" d="M420,28 C540,30 590,70 590,112" marker-end="url(#reconcile-aa)"/><path class="ed acc" d="M590,150 C590,200 520,232 440,236" marker-end="url(#reconcile-aa)"/><path class="ed acc" d="M220,236 C140,232 70,200 70,150" marker-end="url(#reconcile-aa)"/><path class="ed acc" d="M70,112 C70,70 120,30 240,26" marker-end="url(#reconcile-aa)"/><text class="lb mu" x="330" y="196" text-anchor="middle">again on every change, and periodically</text></svg><figcaption>The controller&rsquo;s loop. Because it looks at the current state each time, a missed event or a restart does no harm, as long as each pass is idempotent.</figcaption></figure>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Operator or Helm chart?</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th></th><th>Helm chart</th><th>Operator</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Does</td><td>Installs and upgrades a set of manifests</td><td>Continuously manages a system through its whole life</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Day-2 operations</td><td>None after apply</td><td>Backups, failover, scaling, upgrades, repair</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Fits</td><td>Stateless services, most applications</td><td>Stateful systems (databases, Kafka), complex platforms</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Cost</td><td>Templates</td><td>A program to build, test, secure and maintain</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note"><i>Our view:</i> use well-tested operators (often installed with Helm) rather than writing your own. Write one only when you have real operational knowledge to automate and no existing operator does it. <i>The other view:</i> a team with real operational knowledge to automate may get more from its own operator than from a general one.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Operators worth knowing</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>cert-manager:</b> issues and renews TLS certificates.</li>
      <li><b>Prometheus Operator:</b> Prometheus, Alertmanager and scrape targets as resources (<code>ServiceMonitor</code>, <code>PrometheusRule</code>).</li>
      <li><b>CloudNativePG:</b> PostgreSQL clusters with replication, failover and backups.</li>
      <li><b>Strimzi:</b> Kafka clusters, topics and users.</li>
      <li><b>Spark operator:</b> Spark applications as resources.</li>
      <li><b>Argo CD / Flux:</b> GitOps controllers.</li>
      <li><b>External Secrets Operator:</b> syncs secrets from Secret Manager into Kubernetes.</li>
      <li><b>Config Connector / Crossplane:</b> manage cloud resources (GCS buckets, Cloud SQL) as Kubernetes objects.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>A Custom Resource Definition</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>apiVersion: apiextensions.k8s.io/v1
kind: CustomResourceDefinition
metadata:
  name: pipelineruns.data.example.com
spec:
  group: data.example.com
  scope: Namespaced
  names:
    plural: pipelineruns
    singular: pipelinerun
    kind: PipelineRun
    shortNames: [plr]
  versions:
    - name: v1alpha1
      served: true
      storage: true
      subresources:
        status: {}
      additionalPrinterColumns:
        - { name: Date,  type: string, jsonPath: .spec.runDate }
        - { name: Phase, type: string, jsonPath: .status.phase }
      schema:
        openAPIV3Schema:
          type: object
          properties:
            spec:
              type: object
              required: [runDate, stages]
              properties:
                runDate: { type: string, format: date }
                stages:
                  type: array
                  items: { type: string, enum: [LAND, RAW_LOAD, BUILD] }
            status:
              type: object
              x-kubernetes-preserve-unknown-fields: true</code></pre></div>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>apiVersion: data.example.com/v1alpha1
kind: PipelineRun
metadata: { name: run-2026-11-12, namespace: data }
spec:
  runDate: "2026-11-12"
  stages: [LAND, RAW_LOAD, BUILD]

# kubectl get plr -n data</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Go: kubebuilder and controller-runtime</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>kubebuilder init --domain example.com --repo github.com/example/pipeline-operator
kubebuilder create api --group data --version v1alpha1 --kind PipelineRun
make manifests      # generate CRDs and RBAC from code markers
make install run    # install CRDs, run the controller locally</code></pre></div>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>func (r *PipelineRunReconciler) Reconcile(ctx context.Context, req ctrl.Request) (ctrl.Result, error) {
    var run datav1alpha1.PipelineRun
    if err := r.Get(ctx, req.NamespacedName, &amp;run); err != nil {
        return ctrl.Result{}, client.IgnoreNotFound(err)   // deleted: nothing to do
    }

    // 1. observe: the Jobs this run owns
    // 2. compare: which stage should run next, given spec and finished Jobs?
    // 3. act: create that stage's Job with a deterministic name
    //    (creating it twice returns AlreadyExists: idempotent)
    //    and set an owner reference so it is garbage-collected with the run
    // 4. record: update status.phase and conditions via the status subresource

    return ctrl.Result{RequeueAfter: time.Minute}, nil
}</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Java and Python</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>// Java Operator SDK
@ControllerConfiguration
public class PipelineRunReconciler implements Reconciler&lt;PipelineRun&gt; {
  @Override
  public UpdateControl&lt;PipelineRun&gt; reconcile(PipelineRun run, Context&lt;PipelineRun&gt; ctx) {
    // observe owned Jobs, create the next stage's Job if missing
    run.setStatus(computeStatus(run, ctx));
    return UpdateControl.patchStatus(run);
  }
}
// Quarkus has an extension for the Java Operator SDK</code></pre></div>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># Python: Kopf
import kopf

@kopf.on.create("data.example.com", "v1alpha1", "pipelineruns")
@kopf.on.update("data.example.com", "v1alpha1", "pipelineruns")
def reconcile(spec, name, namespace, status, logger, **_):
    next_stage = choose_next_stage(spec["stages"], status)
    if next_stage:
        ensure_job(name, namespace, next_stage)    # idempotent create
    return {"phase": "Running" if next_stage else "Succeeded"}   # Kopf stores it under status.reconcile</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Rules for a good controller</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Idempotent reconcile:</b> running it ten times gives the same result as once.</li>
      <li><b>No in-memory state:</b> everything needed is in the cluster (spec, status, owned objects), so restarts are safe.</li>
      <li><b>Status and conditions:</b> report <code>Ready</code>, <code>Progressing</code>, <code>Degraded</code> with reasons and the observed generation.</li>
      <li><b>Owner references</b> so owned objects are cleaned up automatically; <b>finalizers</b> to clean up things outside the cluster (cloud resources) before deletion.</li>
      <li><b>Requeue with backoff</b> on errors; never tight-loop.</li>
      <li><b>Leader election</b> so only one replica acts at a time.</li>
      <li><b>Least-privilege RBAC</b>, generated from the code.</li>
      <li><b>Validate early:</b> OpenAPI schema and CEL rules (<code>x-kubernetes-validations</code>) reject bad specs at the API.</li>
      <li><b>Distinguish unknown from failed:</b> if the controller cannot read a dependency, report that, rather than marking the resource failed or healthy.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Testing and operating operators</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Unit-test</b> the decision logic (spec + observed state → actions) without a cluster.</li>
      <li><b>envtest</b> (Go) runs a real API server for integration tests; kind or k3d clusters for end-to-end tests.</li>
      <li><b>CRD versioning:</b> start at <code>v1alpha1</code>; add conversion webhooks when you introduce new versions; never break stored objects.</li>
      <li><b>Metrics:</b> reconcile counts, errors, duration and queue depth (controller-runtime exposes them).</li>
      <li><b>Capability levels</b> (Operator Framework): basic install → seamless upgrades → full lifecycle → deep insights → autopilot. Aim for the level your users need.</li>
      <li><b>Distribution:</b> package the operator with a Helm chart or OLM bundle.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Explain CRDs, custom resources, controllers and reconcile</li><li>Use existing operators (cert-manager, CloudNativePG, Strimzi)</li><li>Read a resource's status and conditions</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Write a CRD with schema validation</li><li>Build a simple controller with kubebuilder, Java Operator SDK or Kopf</li><li>Use owner references, finalizers and status correctly</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Design operators for stateful systems and day-2 operations</li><li>Test with envtest and real clusters; version CRDs with conversion</li><li>Decide operator vs Helm vs managed service</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Build platform APIs on Kubernetes for the organisation</li><li>Maintain operators at high capability levels</li><li>Contribute to the operator ecosystem</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>controller-runtime internals</li><li>CEL validation rules</li><li>Conversion webhooks</li><li>Operator Lifecycle Manager</li><li>Crossplane and Config Connector</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/extend-kubernetes/operator/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Operator pattern</a><span class="lnote"> Kubernetes documentation</span></li><li><a href="https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/extend-kubernetes/custom-resources/custom-resource-definitions/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Custom Resource Definitions</a><span class="lnote"> Defining and versioning CRDs</span></li><li><a href="https://book.kubebuilder.io/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The Kubebuilder Book</a><span class="lnote"> Building controllers in Go</span></li><li><a href="https://sdk.operatorframework.io/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Operator SDK</a><span class="lnote"> Operator Framework tooling</span></li><li><a href="https://javaoperatorsdk.io/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Java Operator SDK</a><span class="lnote"> Operators in Java</span></li><li><a href="https://kopf.readthedocs.io/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kopf</a><span class="lnote"> Operators in Python</span></li><li><a href="https://operatorhub.io/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OperatorHub</a><span class="lnote"> Catalogue of operators</span></li><li><a href="https://cloud.google.com/config-connector/docs/overview" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Config Connector</a><span class="lnote"> GCP resources as Kubernetes objects</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/operator-framework/operator-sdk/master/website/content/en/docs/overview/operator-capabilities.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Operator SDK capability levels</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/kubernetes-sigs/kubebuilder/master/docs/book/src/quick-start.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kubebuilder quick start</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/operator-framework/java-operator-sdk/main/operator-framework-core/src/main/java/io/javaoperatorsdk/operator/api/reconciler/UpdateControl.java" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Java Operator SDK UpdateControl</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/nolar/kopf/main/docs/results.rst" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kopf results stored in status</a>.</p><p class="runcode">A question, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/enrichmeai.github.io/issues/new?title=%5BOperators%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>. Nothing here is tracked and there are no comments to store: questions live in the open, where the next learner can find the answer.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= SERVICE MESH ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-servicemesh" data-name="Service Mesh &amp; Istio">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Service mesh and Istio</h2><p>A service mesh moves service-to-service concerns (encryption, identity, retries, traffic splitting, telemetry, access policy) out of application code and into the platform. What a mesh does, when it is worth it, how Istio works in sidecar and ambient modes, the resources you will write, and how to debug it. Background on <a href="#microservices">Microservices</a> and <a href="#kubernetes">Kubernetes</a>.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>What a mesh gives you</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Capability</th><th>What it means</th><th>Without a mesh</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Mutual TLS</td><td>Every call between services encrypted and authenticated with workload certificates, rotated automatically</td><td>TLS and certificates managed in each service</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Workload identity and authorisation</td><td>Policies such as "only the frontend's service account may call GET /api/*"</td><td>Network policies (IP-based) and code checks</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Traffic management</td><td>Canary weights, header-based routing, mirroring, timeouts, retries</td><td>Load balancer rules or code</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Resilience</td><td>Circuit breaking, outlier detection, connection limits</td><td>Libraries such as Resilience4j in each service</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Observability</td><td>Uniform request metrics, access logs and trace spans for every hop</td><td>Instrumentation in every service</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Egress control</td><td>Only allow-listed external hosts reachable</td><td>Firewall and proxy rules</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note">North-south traffic (clients into the platform) is the gateway's job; east-west traffic (services to each other) is the mesh's. Business logic belongs in neither.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>When it is worth it</h3>
    <p><b>Good reasons</b></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Many services on Kubernetes, often in several languages.</li>
      <li>A requirement for encrypted, authenticated traffic between all services (zero trust, compliance).</li>
      <li>Frequent canary or progressive releases.</li>
      <li>Uniform telemetry without changing every service.</li>
    </ul>
    <p><b>Reasons to wait</b></p>
    <ul>
      <li>A handful of services, or a modular monolith.</li>
      <li>Serverless platforms such as Cloud Run, which already give service identity (IAM) and TLS.</li>
      <li>No one to own and upgrade the mesh: it is a critical system in every request path.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Istio architecture</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Control plane, <code>istiod</code>:</b> turns your configuration into proxy configuration, issues workload certificates, and pushes both to the data plane.</li>
      <li><b>Data plane, sidecar mode:</b> an Envoy proxy injected into every pod intercepts its inbound and outbound traffic. Full L7 features per pod, at the cost of a proxy per pod.</li>
      <li><b>Data plane, ambient mode:</b> a per-node <b>ztunnel</b> provides L4 mTLS and identity for all pods without sidecars; optional <b>waypoint</b> proxies add L7 features (routing, L7 authorisation) only where needed. Lower overhead and no pod restarts to join.</li>
      <li><b>Gateways:</b> Envoy deployments at the edge for ingress and egress, configured with the Kubernetes Gateway API or Istio's own Gateway resource.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">On Google Cloud, Cloud Service Mesh offers a managed, Istio-based mesh for GKE and other environments.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>The two modes, drawn</h3>
    <figure class="fig"><svg viewBox="0 0 660 280" role="img" aria-labelledby="mesh-t mesh-d"><title id="mesh-t">Istio's control plane and its two data plane modes</title><desc id="mesh-d">At the top, istiod, the control plane, which turns your configuration into proxy configuration and issues workload certificates. Below it two node pictures. Sidecar mode: every pod has its own Envoy proxy beside the app, with full L7 features per pod. Ambient mode: one ztunnel per node gives every pod L4 mTLS and identity without sidecars, and an optional waypoint proxy adds L7 features only where needed.</desc><defs><marker id="mesh-a" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker><marker id="mesh-aa" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar acc" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker></defs><rect class="nd in" x="230" y="10" width="200" height="46" rx="6"/><text class="lb hd" x="330" y="31" text-anchor="middle">istiod</text><text class="lb mu" x="330" y="48" text-anchor="middle">config + certificates</text><line class="ed dash" x1="300" y1="56" x2="170" y2="92" marker-end="url(#mesh-a)"/><line class="ed dash" x1="360" y1="56" x2="490" y2="92" marker-end="url(#mesh-a)"/><rect class="bd" x="20" y="96" width="300" height="170" rx="8"/><text class="lb hd" x="170" y="116" text-anchor="middle">sidecar mode: a node</text><rect class="nd" x="40" y="130" width="120" height="110" rx="6"/><text class="lb mu" x="100" y="148" text-anchor="middle">pod</text><rect class="nd out" x="50" y="156" width="100" height="30" rx="4"/><text class="lb" x="100" y="176" text-anchor="middle">app</text><rect class="nd in" x="50" y="196" width="100" height="30" rx="4"/><text class="lb" x="100" y="216" text-anchor="middle">Envoy</text><rect class="nd" x="180" y="130" width="120" height="110" rx="6"/><text class="lb mu" x="240" y="148" text-anchor="middle">pod</text><rect class="nd out" x="190" y="156" width="100" height="30" rx="4"/><text class="lb" x="240" y="176" text-anchor="middle">app</text><rect class="nd in" x="190" y="196" width="100" height="30" rx="4"/><text class="lb" x="240" y="216" text-anchor="middle">Envoy</text><rect class="bd" x="340" y="96" width="300" height="170" rx="8"/><text class="lb hd" x="490" y="116" text-anchor="middle">ambient mode: a node</text><rect class="nd" x="360" y="130" width="120" height="50" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="420" y="160" text-anchor="middle">pod: app only</text><rect class="nd" x="500" y="130" width="120" height="50" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="560" y="160" text-anchor="middle">pod: app only</text><rect class="nd in" x="360" y="196" width="260" height="30" rx="4"/><text class="lb" x="490" y="216" text-anchor="middle">ztunnel: L4 mTLS for every pod</text><text class="lb mu" x="490" y="254" text-anchor="middle">+ a waypoint proxy only where L7 is needed</text><text class="lb mu" x="170" y="254" text-anchor="middle">a proxy in every pod: full L7, more overhead</text></svg><figcaption>The same control plane drives both. Sidecar mode puts a proxy in every pod; ambient mode shares one ztunnel per node and adds L7 proxies only where you need them.</figcaption></figure>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Install and enrol workloads</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># sidecar mode
istioctl install --set profile=default -y
kubectl label namespace shop istio-injection=enabled
kubectl rollout restart deploy -n shop        # pods get sidecars on restart

# ambient mode
istioctl install --set profile=ambient -y
kubectl label namespace shop istio.io/dataplane-mode=ambient   # no restart needed
istioctl waypoint apply -n shop --enroll-namespace             # add L7 where needed

istioctl analyze -n shop                      # check configuration for problems</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">In production, install and upgrade Istio through Helm charts or your platform's managed offering, from Git, with canary upgrades of the control plane (revisions).</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>The resources you will write</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Resource</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Gateway + HTTPRoute (Kubernetes Gateway API)</td><td>Ingress and routing; the recommended API for new setups</td></tr>
      <tr><td>VirtualService</td><td>Istio routing rules: matches, weights, retries, timeouts, fault injection</td></tr>
      <tr><td>DestinationRule</td><td>Policies for a destination: subsets (versions), load balancing, connection pools, outlier detection, TLS</td></tr>
      <tr><td>PeerAuthentication</td><td>mTLS mode for workloads: STRICT or PERMISSIVE</td></tr>
      <tr><td>AuthorizationPolicy</td><td>Who may call what: by service account, namespace, path, method, JWT claims</td></tr>
      <tr><td>RequestAuthentication</td><td>Validate end-user JWTs at the proxy</td></tr>
      <tr><td>ServiceEntry</td><td>Register external services so they can be reached and controlled</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Sidecar</td><td>Limit which services a sidecar knows about, to reduce memory and config size</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Telemetry</td><td>Tune metrics, access logs and tracing</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Security: mTLS and authorisation</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># mesh-wide strict mTLS (after a PERMISSIVE migration period)
apiVersion: security.istio.io/v1
kind: PeerAuthentication
metadata: { name: default, namespace: istio-system }
spec:
  mtls: { mode: STRICT }
---
# only the frontend may call the API, and only GET on /api/*
apiVersion: security.istio.io/v1
kind: AuthorizationPolicy
metadata: { name: shop-api-allow, namespace: shop }
spec:
  selector: { matchLabels: { app: shop-api } }
  action: ALLOW
  rules:
    - from:
        - source: { principals: ["cluster.local/ns/web/sa/frontend"] }
      to:
        - operation: { methods: ["GET"], paths: ["/api/*"] }
---
# validate end-user JWTs at the proxy
apiVersion: security.istio.io/v1
kind: RequestAuthentication
metadata: { name: jwt, namespace: shop }
spec:
  selector: { matchLabels: { app: shop-api } }
  jwtRules:
    - issuer: "https://auth.example.com/"
      jwksUri: "https://auth.example.com/.well-known/jwks.json"</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">Once any ALLOW policy selects a workload, everything not allowed is denied. Roll out in a test namespace first. The service still checks object-level permissions: the mesh knows which service is calling, not whether this user owns this case.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Traffic: canary, retries, timeouts</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>apiVersion: networking.istio.io/v1
kind: DestinationRule
metadata: { name: shop-api, namespace: shop }
spec:
  host: shop-api
  subsets:
    - { name: v1, labels: { version: v1 } }
    - { name: v2, labels: { version: v2 } }
  trafficPolicy:
    connectionPool: { http: { http1MaxPendingRequests: 100 } }
    outlierDetection:                     # eject failing pods for a while
      consecutive5xxErrors: 5
      interval: 10s
      baseEjectionTime: 30s
---
apiVersion: networking.istio.io/v1
kind: VirtualService
metadata: { name: shop-api, namespace: shop }
spec:
  hosts: [shop-api]
  http:
    - timeout: 3s
      retries: { attempts: 2, perTryTimeout: 1s, retryOn: "5xx,connect-failure" }
      route:
        - { destination: { host: shop-api, subset: v1 }, weight: 90 }
        - { destination: { host: shop-api, subset: v2 }, weight: 10 }</code></pre></div>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># the same split with the Gateway API
apiVersion: gateway.networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: HTTPRoute
metadata: { name: shop-api, namespace: shop }
spec:
  parentRefs: [{ name: shop-gateway }]
  hostnames: ["api.example.com"]
  rules:
    - backendRefs:
        - { name: shop-api-v1, port: 8080, weight: 90 }
        - { name: shop-api-v2, port: 8080, weight: 10 }</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">Put retries in one place, mesh or application, not both: retries multiplied at every layer turn a slowdown into a retry storm. Retry only idempotent requests.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Egress control</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># with outbound traffic policy REGISTRY_ONLY, only registered hosts are reachable
apiVersion: networking.istio.io/v1
kind: ServiceEntry
metadata: { name: payment-gateway, namespace: shop }
spec:
  hosts: ["api.example-payments.com"]
  ports: [{ number: 443, name: https, protocol: TLS }]
  resolution: DNS
  location: MESH_EXTERNAL</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">Route egress through an egress gateway when you need a fixed exit point, central logging, or to combine with Cloud NAT and firewall rules.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Observability with a mesh</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Metrics</b> for every request, such as <code>istio_requests_total</code> and request duration histograms, labelled by source, destination and response code.</li>
      <li><b>Kiali</b> draws the live service graph with health and traffic.</li>
      <li><b>Tracing:</b> proxies create spans, but your application must <b>forward the trace headers</b> (<code>traceparent</code>) from incoming to outgoing requests, or traces break at every service.</li>
      <li><b>Access logs</b> per proxy, tunable with the Telemetry resource.</li>
    </ul>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># 5xx rate per destination service
sum by (destination_service) (rate(istio_requests_total{response_code=~"5.."}[5m]))
  / sum by (destination_service) (rate(istio_requests_total[5m]))</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Debugging</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>istioctl analyze -A                                # config problems across namespaces
istioctl proxy-status                              # is every proxy in sync with istiod?
istioctl proxy-config routes   deploy/shop-api -n shop
istioctl proxy-config clusters deploy/shop-api -n shop
istioctl x describe pod &lt;pod&gt; -n shop             # which policies apply to this pod
kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; -c istio-proxy -n shop         # sidecar access log</code></pre></div>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Usually</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>503 with <code>UF</code> / <code>URX</code> flags</td><td>Upstream failing or refusing; check the app and outlier ejection</td></tr>
      <tr><td>404 <code>NR</code></td><td>No route: VirtualService or HTTPRoute mismatch</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Connection reset after enabling STRICT</td><td>A client outside the mesh still sends plain text</td></tr>
      <tr><td>403 <code>RBAC: access denied</code></td><td>An AuthorizationPolicy denies it (or no ALLOW rule matches)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>App fails at startup calling a dependency</td><td>Sidecar not ready yet; hold the app until the proxy starts</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Jobs never complete</td><td>Sidecar keeps the pod alive; use native sidecars or ambient mode</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Pod has no sidecar</td><td>Namespace not labelled, or pod created before the label</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Meshes compared</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Mesh</th><th>Notes</th><th>It leans this way if</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Istio</td><td>Most features; sidecar or ambient; large community; more to learn and operate</td><td>You need the widest feature set and can staff its operation</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Cloud Service Mesh</td><td>Google-managed, Istio-based, integrated with GKE and Cloud Monitoring</td><td>You run on GKE and want Google to manage it</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Linkerd</td><td>Simpler and lightweight, with its own Rust proxy; fewer features</td><td>Its features cover what you need, and you want less to run</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Cilium service mesh</td><td>eBPF-based networking with mesh features, often without sidecars</td><td>You already use Cilium for networking, or want mesh features without sidecars</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Consul</td><td>Spans Kubernetes and VMs, with service discovery</td><td>Your services run on VMs as well as Kubernetes</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note"><i>Analysis:</i> the last column is read from the Notes column, not from any ranking of the meshes. List the features you actually need before you compare: a team that needs few of them has more choices than one that needs the most.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Adopting a mesh safely</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Install in a non-production cluster; enrol one namespace.</li>
      <li>Start with PERMISSIVE mTLS and observe traffic in Kiali and metrics.</li>
      <li>Move namespaces to STRICT once every client is in the mesh.</li>
      <li>Add AuthorizationPolicies service by service, tested in CI against a cluster.</li>
      <li>Move retries, timeouts and canaries into the mesh deliberately, removing duplicates from code.</li>
      <li>Keep all mesh config in Git, delivered by GitOps, with <code>istioctl analyze</code> in CI.</li>
      <li>Plan control-plane upgrades with revisions and canary them like any critical release.</li>
    </ol>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Explain mTLS, workload identity and east-west vs north-south traffic</li><li>Read VirtualService, DestinationRule and AuthorizationPolicy resources</li><li>Find a pod's sidecar logs and proxy status</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Enrol namespaces and roll out STRICT mTLS safely</li><li>Write authorisation policies and canary routes</li><li>Debug 503s and RBAC denials with istioctl</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Decide whether a mesh is worth it, and sidecar vs ambient</li><li>Design zero-trust service-to-service security</li><li>Run mesh upgrades with revisions; keep config in GitOps</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Own the mesh platform across clusters and environments</li><li>Design multi-cluster and multi-network meshes</li><li>Set network security strategy combining mesh, gateway and cloud controls</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>Istio ambient mode (ztunnel and waypoints)</li><li>Kubernetes Gateway API</li><li>Envoy proxy internals</li><li>Multi-cluster Istio</li><li>SPIFFE workload identity</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://istio.io/latest/docs/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Istio documentation</a><span class="lnote"> Concepts, tasks, reference</span></li><li><a href="https://istio.io/latest/docs/concepts/traffic-management/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Istio traffic management</a><span class="lnote"> Routing, resilience, gateways</span></li><li><a href="https://istio.io/latest/docs/concepts/security/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Istio security</a><span class="lnote"> mTLS, identity, authorisation</span></li><li><a href="https://istio.io/latest/docs/ambient/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Istio ambient mode</a><span class="lnote"> Sidecar-less data plane</span></li><li><a href="https://gateway-api.sigs.k8s.io/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kubernetes Gateway API</a><span class="lnote"> Standard routing API</span></li><li><a href="https://kiali.io/docs/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kiali</a><span class="lnote"> Service graph and mesh console</span></li><li><a href="https://cloud.google.com/service-mesh/docs" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cloud Service Mesh</a><span class="lnote"> Google's managed mesh</span></li><li><a href="https://linkerd.io/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Linkerd</a><span class="lnote"> A lighter-weight alternative</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/istio/istio.io/master/content/en/docs/ambient/usage/waypoint/index.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Istio waypoint (--enroll-namespace)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/istio/istio.io/master/content/en/docs/ambient/usage/add-workloads/index.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Istio ambient: add workloads (no restart)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/istio/istio.io/master/content/en/docs/ambient/install/istioctl/index.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Istio ambient install with istioctl</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/istio/istio.io/master/content/en/docs/reference/config/security/authorization-policy/index.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Istio AuthorizationPolicy reference</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/istio/istio.io/master/content/en/docs/ops/common-problems/network-issues/index.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Istio network problems (response flags)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/istio/istio.io/master/content/en/docs/reference/commands/istioctl/index.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">istioctl reference</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/envoyproxy/envoy/main/docs/root/configuration/advanced/substitution_formatter.rst" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Envoy response flags</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/linkerd/linkerd2-proxy/main/README.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">linkerd2-proxy README (Rust)</a>.</p><p class="runcode">A question, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/enrichmeai.github.io/issues/new?title=%5BService%20Mesh%20%26%20Istio%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>. Nothing here is tracked and there are no comments to store: questions live in the open, where the next learner can find the answer.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= TERRAFORM ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-terraform" data-name="Terraform">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Terraform on GCP</h2><p>HCL with the <code>google</code> provider: the CLI, state, the resources a data platform needs, and how to change things safely. Version tags show when a feature arrived in Terraform.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card"><h3>CLI workflow</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>terraform init                       # providers + backend
terraform init -upgrade              # newer providers within constraints
terraform fmt -recursive
terraform validate
terraform plan -var-file=env/dev.tfvars -out=plan.tfplan
terraform show plan.tfplan           # read it before applying
terraform apply plan.tfplan          # applies exactly what you read
terraform plan -destroy

terraform plan -target=module.landing          # emergency only
terraform apply -replace=google_cloud_run_v2_job.loader
terraform output -json
terraform console                    # try expressions
terraform providers lock -platform=linux_amd64 -platform=darwin_arm64</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note"><code>validate</code> checks syntax and types only. A missing optional module argument or a wrong IAM wiring shows up in a plan, not before.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>The safe path, drawn</h3>
    <figure class="fig"><svg viewBox="0 0 660 210" role="img" aria-labelledby="tfflow-t tfflow-d"><title id="tfflow-t">The Terraform workflow</title><desc id="tfflow-d">Five steps left to right: init downloads providers and sets up the backend; fmt and validate check syntax and types; plan with -out writes a plan file; show prints that plan so a person reads it; apply of the plan file applies exactly what was read. A note says validate cannot see a missing optional module argument or wrong IAM wiring; only a plan shows those.</desc><defs><marker id="tfflow-a" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker><marker id="tfflow-aa" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar acc" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker></defs><rect class="nd" x="10" y="50" width="116" height="56" rx="6"/><text class="lb hd" x="68" y="74" text-anchor="middle">init</text><text class="lb mu" x="68" y="94" text-anchor="middle">providers, backend</text><circle class="nd in" cx="20" cy="50" r="9"/><text class="lb mu" x="20" y="54" text-anchor="middle">1</text><line class="ed" x1="126" y1="78" x2="136" y2="78" marker-end="url(#tfflow-a)"/><rect class="nd" x="140" y="50" width="116" height="56" rx="6"/><text class="lb hd" x="198" y="74" text-anchor="middle">fmt, validate</text><text class="lb mu" x="198" y="94" text-anchor="middle">syntax and types</text><circle class="nd in" cx="150" cy="50" r="9"/><text class="lb mu" x="150" y="54" text-anchor="middle">2</text><line class="ed" x1="256" y1="78" x2="266" y2="78" marker-end="url(#tfflow-a)"/><rect class="nd" x="270" y="50" width="116" height="56" rx="6"/><text class="lb hd" x="328" y="74" text-anchor="middle">plan -out</text><text class="lb mu" x="328" y="94" text-anchor="middle">plan.tfplan</text><circle class="nd in" cx="280" cy="50" r="9"/><text class="lb mu" x="280" y="54" text-anchor="middle">3</text><line class="ed" x1="386" y1="78" x2="396" y2="78" marker-end="url(#tfflow-a)"/><rect class="nd in" x="400" y="50" width="116" height="56" rx="6"/><text class="lb hd" x="458" y="74" text-anchor="middle">show</text><text class="lb mu" x="458" y="94" text-anchor="middle">a person reads it</text><circle class="nd in" cx="410" cy="50" r="9"/><text class="lb mu" x="410" y="54" text-anchor="middle">4</text><line class="ed" x1="516" y1="78" x2="526" y2="78" marker-end="url(#tfflow-a)"/><rect class="nd out" x="530" y="50" width="116" height="56" rx="6"/><text class="lb hd" x="588" y="74" text-anchor="middle">apply the file</text><text class="lb mu" x="588" y="94" text-anchor="middle">what you read</text><circle class="nd in" cx="540" cy="50" r="9"/><text class="lb mu" x="540" y="54" text-anchor="middle">5</text><rect class="bd" x="140" y="140" width="380" height="50" rx="6"/><text class="lb acc" x="330" y="161" text-anchor="middle">validate cannot see a missing module argument</text><text class="lb acc" x="330" y="179" text-anchor="middle">or wrong IAM wiring: only a plan shows those</text></svg><figcaption>The commands above in the order that keeps you safe. Saving the plan to a file and applying that file means what runs is exactly what you read.</figcaption></figure>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Providers &amp; backend</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>terraform {
  required_version = "&gt;= 1.7"
  required_providers {
    google = { source = "hashicorp/google", version = "~&gt; 8.0" }   # pin the major version you have tested
  }
  backend "gcs" {
    bucket = "my-proj-tfstate"      # versioning on!
    prefix = "dataplatform"
  }
}

provider "google" {
  project = var.project_id
  region  = var.region
  default_labels = { managed_by = "terraform", env = var.env }
}

# backend values cannot use variables; pass them at init:
# terraform init -backend-config="bucket=my-proj-dev-tfstate"</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Variables, locals, outputs</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>variable "env" {
  type = string
  validation {
    condition     = contains(["dev", "prod"], var.env)
    error_message = "env must be dev or prod."
  }
}
variable "stages" {
  type = map(object({
    enabled = bool
    args    = optional(list(string), [])
  }))
}
variable "db_password" {
  type      = string
  sensitive = true
}

locals {
  name_prefix = "shop-${var.env}"
  enabled     = { for k, v in var.stages : k =&gt; v if v.enabled }
}

output "bucket_url" { value = google_storage_bucket.landing.url }

# env/dev.tfvars
env    = "dev"
stages = { land = { enabled = true, args = ["--max-objects=50"] } }</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>count, for_each, dynamic</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># for_each over a set or map: stable addresses by key (prefer over count)
resource "google_bigquery_dataset" "layer" {
  for_each   = toset(["raw", "analytics", "mart"])
  dataset_id = each.key
  location   = var.region
}
# reference: google_bigquery_dataset.layer["analytics"].dataset_id

# count for on/off
resource "google_cloud_scheduler_job" "tick" {
  count = var.clock_enabled ? 1 : 0
  ...
}
# reference: one(google_cloud_scheduler_job.tick[*].id)

# dynamic nested blocks
dynamic "env" {
  for_each = var.env_vars
  content {
    name  = env.key
    value = env.value
  }
}

# list of objects → map for for_each
for_each = { for t in var.tables : t.name =&gt; t }</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">With <code>count</code>, removing an item in the middle renumbers everything after it and Terraform destroys and recreates those resources.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>BigQuery dataset &amp; table</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>resource "google_bigquery_dataset" "analytics" {
  dataset_id                 = "analytics"
  location                   = "asia-south1"
  delete_contents_on_destroy = false
  labels = { layer = "analytics" }
}

resource "google_bigquery_table" "orders" {
  dataset_id          = google_bigquery_dataset.analytics.dataset_id
  table_id            = "orders"
  deletion_protection = true
  require_partition_filter = true

  time_partitioning {
    type  = "MONTH"
    field = "order_date"
  }
  clustering = ["store_id", "order_ref"]

  schema = jsonencode([
    { name = "order_id",   type = "STRING", mode = "REQUIRED" },
    { name = "store_id",   type = "STRING" },
    { name = "order_ref",  type = "STRING" },
    { name = "order_date", type = "DATE" },
  ])
}

resource "google_bigquery_dataset_iam_member" "api_reader" {
  dataset_id = google_bigquery_dataset.analytics.dataset_id
  role       = "roles/bigquery.dataViewer"
  member     = "serviceAccount:${google_service_account.api.email}"
}</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">Tables that dbt builds should not also be declared here; two owners fight over the schema. Declare datasets and IAM, let dbt own its tables.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>GCS bucket</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>resource "google_storage_bucket" "landing" {
  name                        = "${var.project_id}-landing"
  location                    = "ASIA-SOUTH1"
  storage_class               = "STANDARD"
  uniform_bucket_level_access = true
  public_access_prevention    = "enforced"
  force_destroy               = false

  versioning { enabled = true }

  lifecycle_rule {
    condition {
      age            = 90
      matches_prefix = ["tmp/"]
    }
    action { type = "Delete" }
  }
  lifecycle_rule {
    condition { age = 365 }
    action {
      type          = "SetStorageClass"
      storage_class = "COLDLINE"
    }
  }
}</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Cloud Run job + Cloud Scheduler</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>resource "google_cloud_run_v2_job" "loader" {
  name     = "${local.name_prefix}-loader"
  location = var.region
  deletion_protection = false

  template {
    task_count = 1
    template {
      service_account = google_service_account.loader.email
      timeout         = "3600s"
      max_retries     = 0
      containers {
        image = var.loader_image          # pass a tag or digest, never :latest
        args  = ["--year=2024"]
        resources { limits = { cpu = "1", memory = "2Gi" } }
        env {
          name = "DB_PASSWORD"
          value_source {
            secret_key_ref {
              secret  = google_secret_manager_secret.db.secret_id
              version = "latest"
            }
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}

resource "google_cloud_scheduler_job" "daily" {
  name      = "${local.name_prefix}-daily"
  region    = var.region
  schedule  = "15 6 * * *"
  time_zone = "Etc/UTC"
  paused    = var.clock_paused
  http_target {
    http_method = "POST"
    uri = "https://run.googleapis.com/v2/projects/${var.project_id}/locations/${var.region}/jobs/${google_cloud_run_v2_job.loader.name}:run"
    oauth_token {
      service_account_email = google_service_account.scheduler.email
      scope                 = "https://www.googleapis.com/auth/cloud-platform"
    }
  }
}</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">The scheduler's service account needs <code>roles/run.invoker</code> (or <code>run.developer</code> for overrides) on the job.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>IAM: member, binding, policy</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># _member: adds ONE principal to ONE role. Safe, composable. Use this.
resource "google_project_iam_member" "loader_bq" {
  project = var.project_id
  role    = "roles/bigquery.jobUser"
  member  = "serviceAccount:${google_service_account.loader.email}"
}

# _binding: OWNS the member list for one role (removes anyone else)
# _policy:  OWNS the whole policy (removes everything else, can lock you out)

resource "google_service_account" "loader" {
  account_id   = "shop-loader"
  display_name = "Orders loader job"
}

# Workload Identity Federation for GitHub Actions: no JSON keys
resource "google_service_account_iam_member" "gh_deploy" {
  service_account_id = google_service_account.deploy.name
  role               = "roles/iam.workloadIdentityUser"
  member = "principalSet://iam.googleapis.com/${google_iam_workload_identity_pool.gh.name}/attribute.repository/org/repo"
}</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">Never mix <code>_policy</code> or <code>_binding</code> with <code>_member</code> for the same role: they overwrite each other on every apply.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Modules</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>modules/pipeline-job/
  main.tf  variables.tf  outputs.tf  versions.tf

module "orchestrator" {
  source        = "./modules/pipeline-job"
  name          = "orchestrator"
  image         = var.orchestrator_image
  service_account_email = google_service_account.orch.email
  args          = ["--tick"]
}

module "jobs" {                         # for_each on modules (0.13+)
  source   = "./modules/pipeline-job"
  for_each = local.enabled
  name     = each.key
  args     = each.value.args
}

output "job_names" { value = { for k, m in module.jobs : k =&gt; m.name } }

# Registry / git sources: pin a version
source  = "terraform-google-modules/cloud-storage/google"
version = "~&gt; 12.0"
source  = "git::https://github.com/org/tf-modules.git//bq?ref=v1.4.0"</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Refactor without destroying</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># Bring an existing resource under management (1.5)
import {
  to = google_storage_bucket.landing
  id = "my-proj-landing"
}
# terraform plan -generate-config-out=generated.tf

# Rename / move into a module (1.1)
moved {
  from = google_cloud_run_v2_job.loader
  to   = module.jobs["land"].google_cloud_run_v2_job.this
}

# Stop managing, keep the real resource (1.7)
removed {
  from = google_bigquery_table.legacy
  lifecycle { destroy = false }
}

# CLI equivalents (no review step: prefer the blocks above)
terraform state list
terraform state show google_storage_bucket.landing
terraform state mv  'a.b' 'module.x.a.b'
terraform state rm  google_bigquery_table.legacy</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>lifecycle, checks, preconditions</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>resource "google_sql_database_instance" "shop" {
  ...
  lifecycle {
    prevent_destroy       = true           # plan fails instead of destroying
    ignore_changes        = [settings[0].disk_size]   # autoresize owns it
    create_before_destroy = false
    precondition {
      condition     = var.env != "prod" || var.tier != "db-f1-micro"
      error_message = "prod needs a real tier."
    }
  }
}

# replace when something else changes (1.2)
lifecycle { replace_triggered_by = [terraform_data.image_rev] }

# check block: warns on every plan, never blocks (1.5)
check "landing_bucket_reachable" {
  data "google_storage_bucket" "l" { name = google_storage_bucket.landing.name }
  assert {
    condition     = data.google_storage_bucket.l.versioning[0].enabled
    error_message = "Landing bucket versioning is off."
  }
}</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Functions &amp; expressions</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>"${var.env}-landing"                  format("shop-%s-%03d", var.env, 7)
join(",", var.stores)                 split(",", "a,b,c")
merge(local.base_labels, var.labels)  concat(list_a, list_b)
lookup(var.tiers, var.env, "small")   try(var.cfg.timeout, "600s")
coalesce(var.override, "default")     one(resource.x[*].id)
flatten([for d in var.datasets : [for t in d.tables : "${d.name}.${t}"]])
toset(list)  keys(map)  values(map)   zipmap(keys, vals)
setproduct(var.envs, var.regions)     contains(list, "x")
can(regex("^[a-z]+$", var.name))
jsonencode(obj)  yamldecode(file("${path.module}/rows.yaml"))
templatefile("${path.module}/startup.sh.tftpl", { bucket = local.bucket })
var.enabled ? "a" : "b"
[for s in var.stages : upper(s) if s != "skip"]
{ for k, v in var.stages : k =&gt; v.args }
timestamp()   # changes every plan: avoid in resource arguments</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>terraform test (1.6)</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># tests/stages.tftest.hcl
variables {
  env    = "dev"
  stages = { land = { enabled = true } }
}

run "bld_window_is_small" {
  command = plan
  assert {
    condition     = length(local.enabled) &lt;= 4
    error_message = "dev enables too many stages."
  }
}

run "rejects_unknown_env" {
  command   = plan
  variables { env = "staging" }
  expect_failures = [var.env]
}

# terraform test        (mock_provider "google" {} keeps it offline, 1.7+)</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Safety checklist</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Read every plan line</b> that says <code>destroy</code> or <code>-/+</code> (replace). A replaced database or bucket loses its data.</li>
      <li><b>State bucket:</b> versioning on, uniform access, only the deploy identity can write. State holds secrets in plain text.</li>
      <li><b>One state per environment</b> (separate prefix or project). Workspaces suit identical copies, not dev vs prod with different shapes.</li>
      <li><b>Pin providers</b> with <code>~&gt;</code> and commit <code>.terraform.lock.hcl</code>.</li>
      <li><b>Images by tag or digest</b> from a variable, never <code>:latest</code>, so a plan shows when the image moves.</li>
      <li><b>No secrets in tfvars.</b> Reference Secret Manager; mark variables <code>sensitive</code>.</li>
      <li><b>Apply from CI</b> with Workload Identity Federation, from a saved plan file.</li>
      <li><b>Config is not state:</b> after an apply, read the live resource to confirm it.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Run init, plan and apply; read a plan</li><li>Change a resource's arguments safely</li><li>Use variables and outputs</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Write reusable modules with validation</li><li>Manage state in a remote backend; use moved and import blocks</li><li>Run Terraform from CI with keyless auth</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Design module and state layout for many environments</li><li>Refactor live infrastructure without destroying it</li><li>Write terraform tests and policy checks</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Own infrastructure-as-code standards for the organisation</li><li>Design org-wide landing zones and guardrails</li><li>Lead provider upgrades and large migrations</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>State locking and drift detection</li><li>Policy as code (OPA, Sentinel)</li><li>Terraform test and mock providers</li><li>Ephemeral values and write-only arguments</li><li>Landing zones on GCP</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/docs" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Terraform documentation</a><span class="lnote"> Official docs</span></li><li><a href="https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/language" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Terraform language</a><span class="lnote"> HCL, modules, expressions, state</span></li><li><a href="https://developer.hashicorp.com/terraform/tutorials" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Terraform tutorials</a><span class="lnote"> Step-by-step learning</span></li><li><a href="https://registry.terraform.io/providers/hashicorp/google/latest/docs" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google provider docs</a><span class="lnote"> Every GCP resource and argument</span></li><li><a href="https://cloud.google.com/docs/terraform" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Terraform on Google Cloud</a><span class="lnote"> Google's guides and best practices</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/hashicorp/terraform/v1.1/CHANGELOG.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Terraform 1.1 changelog (moved)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/hashicorp/terraform/v1.2/CHANGELOG.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Terraform 1.2 changelog (replace_triggered_by)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/hashicorp/terraform/v1.5/CHANGELOG.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Terraform 1.5 changelog (import, check)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/hashicorp/terraform/v1.6/CHANGELOG.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Terraform 1.6 changelog (terraform test)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/hashicorp/terraform/v1.7/CHANGELOG.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Terraform 1.7 changelog (removed, mock_provider)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/hashicorp/web-unified-docs/main/content/terraform/v1.14.x/docs/language/tests/index.mdx" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Terraform tests (assertion references)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/hashicorp/terraform-provider-google/main/CHANGELOG.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google provider changelog (8.x)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/hashicorp/terraform-provider-google/main/website/docs/r/bigquery_table.html.markdown" target="_blank" rel="noopener">google_bigquery_table</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/hashicorp/terraform-provider-google/main/website/docs/r/storage_bucket.html.markdown" target="_blank" rel="noopener">google_storage_bucket</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/hashicorp/terraform-provider-google/main/website/docs/r/cloud_run_v2_job.html.markdown" target="_blank" rel="noopener">google_cloud_run_v2_job</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/hashicorp/terraform-provider-google/main/website/docs/r/cloud_scheduler_job.html.markdown" target="_blank" rel="noopener">google_cloud_scheduler_job</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/run/docs/execute/jobs-on-schedule" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cloud Run: execute jobs on a schedule</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/run/docs/execute/jobs" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cloud Run: execute jobs (overrides need run.developer)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/terraform-google-modules/terraform-google-cloud-storage/main/CHANGELOG.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">cloud-storage module changelog (12.x)</a>.</p><p class="runcode">A question, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/enrichmeai.github.io/issues/new?title=%5BTerraform%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>. Nothing here is tracked and there are no comments to store: questions live in the open, where the next learner can find the answer.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= OBSERVABILITY ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-observability" data-name="Observability">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Observability</h2><p>Being able to answer new questions about a running system from the outside: what happened, where, why, and to whom. Logs, metrics, traces and profiles; OpenTelemetry; Google Cloud's tools; dashboards and alerts; and observability for data pipelines and AI features.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Signals and what each answers</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Signal</th><th>Is</th><th>Answers</th><th>Cost driver</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Logs</td><td>Timestamped records of events, ideally structured JSON</td><td>What exactly happened in this request or run?</td><td>Volume and retention</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Metrics</td><td>Numbers aggregated over time (counts, gauges, histograms)</td><td>How much, how often, how fast, trending where?</td><td>Number of time series (label cardinality)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Traces</td><td>A request's path across services as a tree of timed spans</td><td>Where did the time go? Which dependency failed?</td><td>Sampling rate</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Profiles</td><td>Where CPU and memory go inside the code</td><td>Which function is slow or allocating?</td><td>Usually low (continuous sampling)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Events / run records</td><td>Business or pipeline facts (deploys, run outcomes)</td><td>What changed, and did the job do its work?</td><td>Low</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note">Monitoring tells you <i>that</i> something is wrong from questions you planned. Observability lets you find out <i>why</i>, including for failures nobody predicted. You need both.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Structured logging</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>{"timestamp":"2026-11-12T06:15:04.120Z",
 "severity":"ERROR",
 "message":"load failed",
 "service":"orders-load", "version":"3f2a9c1", "env":"prod",
 "run_id":"r-2026-11-12", "stage":"RAW_LOAD",
 "object":"gs://landing/data/year=2024/part-3.parquet",
 "error":"400 Bad Request", "error_body":"InvalidArgument: …",
 "logging.googleapis.com/trace":"projects/p/traces/4bf92f…"}</code></pre></div>
    <ul>
      <li>One JSON object per line; a <code>severity</code> field so levels work.</li>
      <li>Always include service, version, environment, and a request or run ID.</li>
      <li>Log the evidence: the upstream error body, the input that failed, counts. A log that says only "failed" helps no one.</li>
      <li>Never log secrets, tokens or unnecessary personal data.</li>
      <li>Levels: ERROR needs attention; WARN is unusual but handled; INFO is a business event; DEBUG is off in production.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Metrics that matter</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Counter:</b> only goes up (requests, errors, rows loaded). Graph its rate.</li>
      <li><b>Gauge:</b> current value (queue depth, connections, lag).</li>
      <li><b>Histogram:</b> distribution of values (latency); gives percentiles.</li>
      <li><b>RED for services:</b> rate, errors, duration.</li>
      <li><b>USE for resources:</b> utilisation, saturation, errors.</li>
      <li><b>Golden signals:</b> latency, traffic, errors, saturation.</li>
      <li><b>Cardinality:</b> each unique label combination is a separate series. Never use user IDs, request IDs or URLs with IDs as labels.</li>
      <li><b>Percentiles, not averages:</b> p50 for typical, p95/p99 for the users having a bad time.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Distributed tracing</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>A <b>trace</b> is one request end to end; a <b>span</b> is one operation in it (an HTTP call, a query), with start, duration, attributes and status.</li>
      <li><b>Context propagation:</b> the trace ID travels in the W3C <code>traceparent</code> header and in message attributes across queues.</li>
      <li><b>Sampling:</b> keep a percentage of traces (head sampling), or keep all errors and slow ones (tail sampling in a collector).</li>
      <li>Link logs to traces by writing the trace ID into every log line.</li>
      <li>Batch jobs: one trace per run, a span per stage or partition.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>OpenTelemetry</h3>
    <p>The vendor-neutral standard for producing logs, metrics and traces: one <b>API</b> in your code, an <b>SDK</b> that processes and exports, the <b>OTLP</b> protocol, and an optional <b>Collector</b> that receives, filters, samples and forwards to any backend.</p>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># Java: zero-code instrumentation with the agent (HTTP, JDBC, Kafka, gRPC …)
java -javaagent:/otel/opentelemetry-javaagent.jar -jar app.jar
#   OTEL_SERVICE_NAME=shop-api
#   OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=http://otel-collector:4318
#   OTEL_TRACES_SAMPLER=parentbased_traceidratio  OTEL_TRACES_SAMPLER_ARG=0.1

# Python: auto-instrumentation
pip install opentelemetry-distro opentelemetry-exporter-otlp
opentelemetry-bootstrap -a install
OTEL_SERVICE_NAME=orders-load opentelemetry-instrument python -m loader</code></pre></div>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># manual span around meaningful work (Python)
from opentelemetry import trace
tracer = trace.get_tracer("orders-load")

with tracer.start_as_current_span("load_object") as span:
    span.set_attribute("object.uri", uri)
    rows = load(uri)
    span.set_attribute("rows.loaded", rows)</code></pre></div>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># Collector: receive OTLP, batch, send to Google Cloud
receivers:
  otlp:
    protocols:
      grpc:
      http:
processors:
  batch:
exporters:
  googlecloud:
  googlemanagedprometheus:
service:
  pipelines:
    traces:  { receivers: [otlp], processors: [batch], exporters: [googlecloud] }
    logs:    { receivers: [otlp], processors: [batch], exporters: [googlecloud] }
    metrics: { receivers: [otlp], processors: [batch], exporters: [googlemanagedprometheus] }</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Spring Boot and Micrometer</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># application.yml
management:
  endpoints.web.exposure.include: health,prometheus
  tracing.sampling.probability: 0.1
  otlp.tracing.endpoint: http://otel-collector:4318/v1/traces   # Boot 4: opentelemetry.tracing.export.otlp.endpoint
  observations.annotations.enabled: true   # makes @Observed work (needs AspectJ on the classpath)
logging.structured.format.console: ecs     # 3.4+</code></pre></div>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>@Component
class LoadMetrics {
  private final Counter loaded, failed;
  private final Timer duration;
  LoadMetrics(MeterRegistry r) {
    loaded   = Counter.builder("orders.objects").tag("outcome", "loaded").register(r);
    failed   = Counter.builder("orders.objects").tag("outcome", "failed").register(r);
    duration = Timer.builder("orders.load.duration").publishPercentileHistogram().register(r);
  }
}

@Observed(name = "order.search")   // timer + span in one annotation
public List&lt;Order&gt; search(String q) { … }</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Google Cloud Observability</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>Use it for</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Cloud Logging</td><td>All logs from Cloud Run, GKE, BigQuery jobs and audit logs; Logs Explorer; log sinks to BigQuery or GCS; log-based metrics</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Cloud Monitoring</td><td>Metrics, dashboards, uptime checks, alerting policies, SLOs; queries in PromQL</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Managed Service for Prometheus</td><td>Prometheus metrics at scale without running Prometheus</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Cloud Trace</td><td>Distributed traces and latency analysis</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Cloud Profiler</td><td>Continuous CPU and heap profiling in production</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Error Reporting</td><td>Groups exceptions from logs, shows new and recurring errors</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Audit logs</td><td>Who did what: Admin Activity always on; enable Data Access logs for sensitive services</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># Logs Explorer query
resource.type="cloud_run_revision"
resource.labels.service_name="shop-api"
severity&gt;=ERROR
jsonPayload.run_id="r-2026-11-12"
timestamp&gt;="2026-11-12T00:00:00Z"

# log-based metric from a marker in the text
gcloud logging metrics create pipeline_incidents \
  --description="Pipeline incidents" \
  --log-filter='textPayload:"pipeline_incident"'

# PromQL in Cloud Monitoring: 5xx ratio and p95 latency
sum(rate(http_server_requests_seconds_count{status=~"5.."}[5m]))
  / sum(rate(http_server_requests_seconds_count[5m]))
histogram_quantile(0.95,
  sum by (le) (rate(http_server_requests_seconds_bucket[5m])))</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Dashboards that get used</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>One overview per service:</b> traffic, errors, latency percentiles, saturation, last deploy marker.</li>
      <li><b>Top-down:</b> user-facing SLOs first, then dependencies, then resources.</li>
      <li><b>Same layout for every service</b> (a dashboard template is a blueprint).</li>
      <li><b>Show the denominator:</b> "12 errors" means nothing without "of 1.2 million requests".</li>
      <li><b>Annotate deploys and incidents</b> so changes line up with effects.</li>
      <li>Delete dashboards nobody opens.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Alerting</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Page on symptoms users feel</b> (SLO burn rate, failed runs, stale data), not on causes (CPU at 80%).</li>
      <li><b>Every page is actionable</b> and links to a runbook. If nobody needs to act, it is a ticket or a dashboard, not a page.</li>
      <li><b>Alert when the signal is missing:</b> a job that did not run, a metric that stopped reporting. Silence is not health.</li>
      <li><b>Match on fields you control</b> (a marker in the message, a structured field), not on a severity that may not be set.</li>
      <li><b>Prove each alert fires</b> once in a test environment before trusting it.</li>
      <li><b>Review noisy alerts</b> monthly: tune, fix the cause, or delete.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Observing data pipelines</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Dimension</th><th>Check</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Run outcome</td><td>Every scheduled run recorded SUCCEEDED, FAILED or UNKNOWN; missing runs alert</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Freshness</td><td>Newest data per source within its expected age</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Volume</td><td>Row and object counts within expected ranges; reconciliation in = out + rejected</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Schema</td><td>Unexpected new, missing or retyped columns detected</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Quality</td><td>Test results (nulls, keys, parse failures) tracked over time</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Cost and duration</td><td>Bytes, slot time and runtime per run, trended</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note">The most important rule: a check whose source is unreachable reports UNKNOWN, never zero and never green.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Observing AI features</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Log model ID, input and output tokens, latency, stop reason and cost per call.</li>
      <li>Trace each tool call and retrieval step as a span.</li>
      <li>Track refusals, empty retrievals, citation failures and "not found" rates.</li>
      <li>Sample real conversations for quality review and feed them into evals (with privacy controls).</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Keeping observability affordable</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Exclude noisy, low-value logs at the router (exclusion filters) or lower their level.</li>
      <li>Sample traces; keep 100% of errors and slow requests with tail sampling.</li>
      <li>Watch metric cardinality; drop unused labels.</li>
      <li>Route long-term logs to BigQuery or GCS with retention rules instead of keeping everything hot.</li>
      <li>Label resources so observability cost can be attributed to teams.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Following a journey across services</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Give every request an ID,</b> and pass it on to every service the request touches. Log it with every line.</li>
      <li><b>Give every user journey an ID too,</b> when several requests belong together, such as a multi-step form or a checkout. Then you can follow the whole journey, not just one request.</li>
      <li><b>Use the standard for tracing:</b> the W3C Trace Context <code>traceparent</code> header carries a trace ID and the caller's span ID between services, so tools from different vendors can join the pieces up. OpenTelemetry propagates it for you.</li>
      <li><b>An audit trail makes rollback safe.</b> If a journey fails halfway, you can only undo the steps you know were taken. Record each step and why, then compensate in reverse order (see <a href="#architecture">Architecture</a> on sagas).</li>
    </ul>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># a W3C Trace Context header: version, trace ID, parent span ID, flags
traceparent: 00-0af7651916cd43dd8448eb211c80319c-b7ad6b7169203331-01</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>One trace, drawn</h3>
    <figure class="fig"><svg viewBox="0 0 660 280" role="img" aria-labelledby="trace-t trace-d"><title id="trace-t">One trace across three services</title><desc id="trace-d">A waterfall of spans over time for one request. The checkout request at the gateway is the root span and lasts the longest. Inside it, a call to the orders service, which itself makes a database query. After that, a call to the payments service. Every span shares one trace ID, carried between services in the traceparent header, and the gateway span is the parent of the others.</desc><defs><marker id="trace-a" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker><marker id="trace-aa" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar acc" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker></defs><text class="lb mu" x="10" y="24" text-anchor="start">trace 0af7651916cd43dd…</text><text class="lb" x="10" y="62" text-anchor="start">gateway: POST /checkout</text><rect class="nd in" x="200" y="44" width="426.4" height="26" rx="3"/><text class="lb" x="22" y="108" text-anchor="start">orders: create</text><rect class="nd" x="230" y="90" width="245.99999999999997" height="26" rx="3"/><text class="lb" x="34" y="154" text-anchor="start">orders: INSERT order</text><rect class="nd" x="260" y="136" width="114.8" height="26" rx="3"/><text class="lb" x="22" y="200" text-anchor="start">payments: authorise</text><rect class="nd out" x="420" y="182" width="196.79999999999998" height="26" rx="3"/><line class="ed" x1="200" y1="222" x2="640" y2="222" marker-end="url(#trace-a)"/><text class="lb mu" x="200" y="238" text-anchor="start">start</text><text class="lb mu" x="640" y="238" text-anchor="end">time →</text><text class="lb acc" x="330" y="268" text-anchor="middle">the traceparent header carries the trace ID and parent span ID between services</text></svg><figcaption>One request as a trace: each bar is a span, indented under the span that called it. The longest bar is the user&rsquo;s wait; the gaps show where it went.</figcaption></figure>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Find a request's or run's logs and errors in Cloud Logging</li><li>Read a service dashboard: traffic, errors, latency</li><li>Write structured logs with IDs and evidence</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Instrument a service with OpenTelemetry and custom metrics</li><li>Write log queries, log-based metrics and PromQL</li><li>Build dashboards and actionable alerts with runbooks</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Design observability for a system: signals, sampling, cardinality, cost</li><li>Trace problems across services, queues and pipelines</li><li>Set dashboard and alert templates for teams</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Own the observability platform and its standards</li><li>Lead cost and data-governance strategy for telemetry</li><li>Drive observability-led reliability across the organisation</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>OpenTelemetry Collector pipelines and tail sampling</li><li>High-cardinality observability and wide events</li><li>Continuous profiling</li><li>Exemplars linking metrics to traces</li><li>eBPF-based instrumentation</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://opentelemetry.io/docs/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OpenTelemetry documentation</a><span class="lnote"> APIs, SDKs, collector, semantic conventions</span></li><li><a href="https://cloud.google.com/logging/docs" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cloud Logging documentation</a><span class="lnote"> Logs, sinks, log-based metrics</span></li><li><a href="https://cloud.google.com/logging/docs/view/logging-query-language" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Logging query language</a><span class="lnote"> Filter syntax for Logs Explorer and alerts</span></li><li><a href="https://cloud.google.com/monitoring/docs" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cloud Monitoring documentation</a><span class="lnote"> Metrics, dashboards, alerting, PromQL</span></li><li><a href="https://cloud.google.com/trace/docs" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cloud Trace documentation</a><span class="lnote"> Distributed tracing on GCP</span></li><li><a href="https://prometheus.io/docs/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Prometheus documentation</a><span class="lnote"> Metric types and PromQL</span></li><li><a href="https://docs.micrometer.io/micrometer/reference/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Micrometer documentation</a><span class="lnote"> Metrics and observations for Java</span></li><li><a href="https://www.oreilly.com/library/view/observability-engineering/9781492076438/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Observability Engineering (book)</a><span class="lnote"> Majors, Fong-Jones and Miranda</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/open-telemetry/opentelemetry-specification/main/specification/protocol/exporter.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OTLP exporter config (ports, protocol)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/open-telemetry/opentelemetry-specification/main/specification/configuration/sdk-environment-variables.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OTel SDK env vars (samplers)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/w3c/trace-context/main/spec/20-http_request_header_format.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">W3C Trace Context header</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/open-telemetry/opentelemetry-java-instrumentation/main/README.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OTel Java agent README</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/open-telemetry/opentelemetry-python-contrib/main/opentelemetry-instrumentation/README.rst" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OTel Python instrumentation README</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/main/documentation/spring-boot-docs/src/docs/antora/modules/reference/pages/actuator/tracing.adoc" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Spring Boot tracing (Boot 4 OTLP property)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/main/documentation/spring-boot-docs/src/docs/antora/modules/reference/pages/actuator/observability.adoc" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Spring Boot observability (annotations)</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/logging/docs/audit" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cloud Audit Logs</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/logging/docs/structured-logging" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cloud Logging structured logging</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/open-telemetry/opentelemetry-collector-contrib/main/exporter/googlecloudexporter/README.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OTel Collector googlecloud exporter</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/open-telemetry/opentelemetry-collector-contrib/main/exporter/googlemanagedprometheusexporter/README.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OTel Collector googlemanagedprometheus exporter</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/sdk/gcloud/reference/logging/metrics/create" target="_blank" rel="noopener">gcloud logging metrics create</a>.</p><p class="runcode">A question, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/enrichmeai.github.io/issues/new?title=%5BObservability%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>. Nothing here is tracked and there are no comments to store: questions live in the open, where the next learner can find the answer.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= SRE ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-sre" data-name="SRE">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Site reliability engineering</h2><p>Running production as an engineering problem: reliability targets agreed with the business, error budgets that balance speed and stability, alerting on what users feel, incident response, blameless learning, and removing toil. Signals and tools are on <a href="#observability">Observability</a>.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Principles</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>100% is the wrong target.</b> Users cannot tell 99.99% from 100%, and chasing it stops all change. Pick the reliability users actually need.</li>
      <li><b>Service level objectives</b> make that target explicit and measurable.</li>
      <li><b>Error budgets</b> turn it into a decision rule: budget left, ship faster; budget spent, slow down and fix reliability.</li>
      <li><b>Toil is reduced by engineering:</b> automate repetitive manual work rather than staffing it.</li>
      <li><b>Blameless postmortems:</b> incidents are system failures to learn from, not people to blame.</li>
      <li><b>Simplicity:</b> every component you do not have cannot fail.</li>
      <li><b>No person or laptop is the mechanism:</b> if something only works while a human watches it, it is not built yet.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>SLI, SLO, SLA, error budget</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Is</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>SLI</td><td>A measured ratio of good events to valid events</td><td>Search requests answered successfully in under 500 ms ÷ all search requests</td></tr>
      <tr><td>SLO</td><td>The target for an SLI over a window</td><td>99.5% over 28 days</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Error budget</td><td>1 − SLO: the failure you may spend</td><td>0.5% of requests ≈ 3.4 hours of full outage in 28 days</td></tr>
      <tr><td>SLA</td><td>A contract with consequences, looser than the SLO</td><td>99% monthly, or credits</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note">Measure SLIs as close to the user as possible (load balancer or client), not from inside the server, which cannot see its own outages.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>The budget, drawn</h3>
    <figure class="fig"><svg viewBox="0 0 660 230" role="img" aria-labelledby="budget-t budget-d"><title id="budget-t">An SLO and its error budget</title><desc id="budget-d">A bar for 28 days of requests, 100 percent wide. 99.5 percent is the SLO target: requests that must be good. The remaining 0.5 percent is the error budget, about 3.4 hours of full outage. Below, the SLA at 99 percent sits looser than the SLO, so you are warned before the contract is broken. A burn rate of 1 spends the whole budget in exactly the window; 14.4 spends 2 percent of it in one hour.</desc><defs><marker id="budget-a" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker><marker id="budget-aa" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar acc" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker></defs><rect class="nd out" x="20" y="40" width="560" height="44" rx="4"/><text class="lb hd" x="300" y="67" text-anchor="middle">SLO: 99.5% of requests good over 28 days</text><rect class="nd warn" x="580" y="40" width="60" height="44" rx="4"/><text class="lb acc" x="610" y="30" text-anchor="middle">error budget</text><text class="lb hd" x="610" y="67" text-anchor="middle">0.5%</text><text class="lb mu" x="610" y="102" text-anchor="middle">≈ 3.4 h outage</text><text class="lb mu" x="20" y="130" text-anchor="start">SLA in the contract: looser than the SLO, e.g. 99% monthly, so the SLO warns you first</text><text class="lb" x="20" y="172" text-anchor="start">burn rate 1: the budget lasts exactly the window</text><text class="lb acc" x="20" y="196" text-anchor="start">burn rate 14.4: 2% of a 30-day budget gone in one hour → page</text></svg><figcaption>The table above as one bar. The budget is the failure you are allowed to spend; the burn rate says how fast you are spending it, and the alerts below fire on that rate.</figcaption></figure>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Choosing SLIs</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>System</th><th>Typical SLIs</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Request/response API</td><td>Availability (non-5xx), latency (fraction under a threshold)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Data pipeline</td><td>Freshness (data newer than X), completeness (rows reconciled), correctness (tests passing)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Batch job</td><td>Runs completed on time; runs succeeded</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Storage</td><td>Durability, read/write availability and latency</td></tr>
      <tr><td>AI feature</td><td>Successful responses, latency, grounded-answer rate from evals</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>Pipeline freshness SLO example
  SLI: minutes in which the newest order_date served is &lt; 26 h old
       ÷ minutes in the window
  SLO: 99% over 28 days
  Budget: ≈ 6.7 hours of staleness per 28 days</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Error budget policy</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Budget healthy:</b> ship at normal speed; take calculated risks.</li>
      <li><b>Budget at risk (e.g. 50% spent early):</b> extra review on risky changes; reliability work moves up the backlog.</li>
      <li><b>Budget exhausted:</b> freeze non-urgent feature releases for the service until reliability fixes land and the budget recovers.</li>
      <li><b>One incident ate over 20% of the budget:</b> a postmortem with tracked actions is mandatory.</li>
      <li>Agree the policy with product owners <i>before</i> it is needed.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Alerting on SLOs: burn rates</h3>
    <p><b>Burn rate</b> = how fast you are spending the budget, relative to spending exactly all of it over the window. Burn rate 1 uses the budget in exactly 28–30 days; burn rate 14.4 uses 2% of a 30-day budget in one hour.</p>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Alert</th><th>Burn rate</th><th>Long window</th><th>Short window</th><th>Budget consumed</th><th>Action</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Fast burn</td><td>14.4</td><td>1 hour</td><td>5 minutes</td><td>2%</td><td>Page</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Medium burn</td><td>6</td><td>6 hours</td><td>30 minutes</td><td>5%</td><td>Page</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Slow burn</td><td>1</td><td>3 days</td><td>6 hours</td><td>10%</td><td>Ticket</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note">Both windows must exceed the threshold: the long one shows it matters, the short one shows it is still happening, so the alert stops soon after recovery. These are the starting values from Google's SRE Workbook; tune to your traffic.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>On-call</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Sustainable load:</b> few enough pages that each one gets real attention; track pages per shift.</li>
      <li><b>Every page has a runbook:</b> what it means, how to check, how to mitigate, who to escalate to.</li>
      <li><b>Primary and secondary</b> on rotation, with handover notes.</li>
      <li><b>Access ready in advance:</b> break-glass roles, dashboards, tooling; never "I need permissions" at 3 a.m.</li>
      <li><b>Reduce pages continuously:</b> every page should lead to a fix, a tuned alert, or an automated response.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Incident response</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Role</th><th>Does</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Incident commander</td><td>Owns the incident, coordinates, decides; does not debug</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Operations lead</td><td>Investigates and applies mitigations</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Communications lead</td><td>Updates stakeholders and users on a schedule</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Planning lead</td><td>Files bugs, arranges handoffs, tracks what was changed so it can be undone; often keeps the timeline too</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <ol>
      <li>Declare early; set severity; open one channel.</li>
      <li><b>Mitigate first:</b> roll back, fail over, disable the feature, shed load.</li>
      <li>Communicate status regularly, including "no change".</li>
      <li>Resolve, confirm with the SLIs, then hand over to the postmortem.</li>
    </ol>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Severity levels</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Sev</th><th>Example</th><th>Response</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>1</td><td>Product down or data loss or leak for many users</td><td>Page now, all hands, exec updates</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2</td><td>Major feature broken or badly degraded</td><td>Page now, incident roles</td></tr>
      <tr><td>3</td><td>Minor feature or few users affected; workaround exists</td><td>Business hours</td></tr>
      <tr><td>4</td><td>Cosmetic or internal only</td><td>Ticket</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Blameless postmortem template</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># Incident: &lt;title&gt;            Sev: 2     Date: 2026-11-12
## Summary          what happened, impact, duration (2–3 sentences)
## Impact           users affected, SLO budget spent, data affected
## Timeline (UTC)   detection, escalation, mitigation, resolution
## Root cause(s)    the conditions that allowed it, not a person
## Detection        how we found out; how we should have
## What went well
## What went badly
## Where we got lucky
## Action items     owner · due date · ticket — prevent, detect, mitigate
## Lessons          what we now know about the system</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">Ask "what made this possible?" five times, not "who did it?". Turn each lesson into a guard: a test, an alert, a policy, an automation.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Toil</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Toil</b> is manual, repetitive, automatable, reactive work that grows with the service and leaves no lasting value: restarting jobs by hand, re-running loads, copying credentials.</li>
      <li><b>Cap it</b> (Google aims for under 50% of an SRE's time) and spend the rest on engineering that removes it.</li>
      <li><b>Measure it:</b> log toil tasks and their time; automate the biggest first.</li>
      <li><b>Automation must be durable:</b> a scheduler and a job in the platform, not a script on someone's laptop or a session polling in a loop.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Production readiness review</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>SLOs defined, measured and on a dashboard.</li>
      <li>Alerts on SLO burn and on missing runs, each with a runbook; proven to fire.</li>
      <li>Logs structured, traces flowing, dashboards from the template.</li>
      <li>Capacity tested at expected peak plus headroom.</li>
      <li>Deploys automated, with canary or rollback tested.</li>
      <li>Backups exist <i>and a restore has been tested</i>; RPO and RTO written down.</li>
      <li>Dependencies listed with their failure behaviour.</li>
      <li>Security review done; secrets in a manager; least-privilege IAM.</li>
      <li>On-call rotation and escalation path in place.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Reliability practices</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Progressive delivery:</b> canaries, feature flags and automatic rollback.</li>
      <li><b>Capacity planning:</b> forecast from growth, load-test to find limits, keep headroom.</li>
      <li><b>Graceful degradation:</b> serve partial or cached results and say so.</li>
      <li><b>Disaster recovery:</b> multi-zone by default; multi-region when the SLO needs it; rehearsed restores.</li>
      <li><b>Game days and chaos tests:</b> break things on purpose in a controlled way to test detection and response.</li>
      <li><b>Dependency SLOs:</b> your SLO cannot exceed what your critical dependencies provide unless you add redundancy.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>SLOs on Google Cloud</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Cloud Monitoring <b>service monitoring</b> defines services and SLOs (request-based or window-based) and charts budget.</li>
      <li>Alert policies can use the SLO <b>burn rate</b> directly.</li>
      <li>Declare SLOs and alerts in Terraform (<code>google_monitoring_slo</code>, <code>google_monitoring_alert_policy</code>) so they are reviewed and versioned.</li>
      <li>For pipelines, compute freshness and completeness SLIs from run records in BigQuery and export them as custom or log-based metrics.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Explain SLI, SLO, error budget and SLA</li><li>Follow a runbook and escalate during an incident</li><li>Contribute to a blameless postmortem</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Define SLIs and SLOs for a service or pipeline</li><li>Set up burn-rate alerts and runbooks</li><li>Take on-call shifts and lead mitigation</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Act as incident commander; run postmortems and track actions</li><li>Write and enforce an error budget policy with product owners</li><li>Run production readiness reviews and capacity plans</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Set reliability strategy and SRE practice for the organisation</li><li>Design multi-region resilience and DR programmes</li><li>Build a culture of blameless learning and toil reduction</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>Multi-window, multi-burn-rate alerting</li><li>Incident command system</li><li>Chaos engineering and game days</li><li>Capacity planning and load testing</li><li>Disaster recovery: RPO, RTO and restore drills</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://sre.google/books/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google SRE books (free)</a><span class="lnote"> Site Reliability Engineering, The SRE Workbook, Building Secure and Reliable Systems</span></li><li><a href="https://sre.google/workbook/implementing-slos/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Implementing SLOs (SRE Workbook)</a><span class="lnote"> Choosing SLIs and targets</span></li><li><a href="https://sre.google/workbook/alerting-on-slos/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Alerting on SLOs (SRE Workbook)</a><span class="lnote"> Burn-rate alerting in detail</span></li><li><a href="https://sre.google/sre-book/postmortem-culture/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Postmortem culture (SRE book)</a><span class="lnote"> Blameless learning from failure</span></li><li><a href="https://cloud.google.com/stackdriver/docs/solutions/slo-monitoring" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cloud Monitoring SLOs</a><span class="lnote"> Service monitoring and SLO alerts</span></li><li><a href="https://response.pagerduty.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PagerDuty incident response guide</a><span class="lnote"> Open incident response process</span></li><li><a href="https://www.oreilly.com/library/view/implementing-service-level/9781492076803/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Implementing Service Level Objectives (book)</a><span class="lnote"> Alex Hidalgo</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/stackdriver/docs/solutions/slo-monitoring/alerting-on-budget-burn-rate" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cloud Monitoring: alerting on burn rate</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/stackdriver/docs/solutions/slo-monitoring" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cloud Monitoring: SLO concepts (request- and windows-based)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/slok/sloth/main/internal/alert/windows/google-28d.yaml" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sloth: SRE Workbook window values (28 d)</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Site_reliability_engineering&action=raw" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Site reliability engineering (Wikipedia)</a>; <a href="https://sre.google/workbook/alerting-on-slos/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">SRE Workbook: Alerting on SLOs</a>; <a href="https://sre.google/sre-book/eliminating-toil/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">SRE book: Eliminating toil</a>; <a href="https://sre.google/workbook/error-budget-policy/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">SRE Workbook: Example error budget policy</a>; <a href="https://sre.google/sre-book/managing-incidents/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">SRE book: Managing incidents</a>.</p><p class="runcode">A question, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/enrichmeai.github.io/issues/new?title=%5BSRE%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>. Nothing here is tracked and there are no comments to store: questions live in the open, where the next learner can find the answer.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= HOW THE CLOUD IS BUILT ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-cloudinside" data-name="How the cloud is built">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>How the cloud is built</h2><p>"The cloud" is not magic, and it is not a genie that grants servers. It is buildings full of ordinary computers, joined by networks, run by software that lets millions of customers share them safely. Once you can see the layers, cost, failure, speed and lock-in all start to make sense.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>The layers, from the ground up</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>What it really is</th><th>Why it matters to you</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Buildings</td><td>Data centres: power, cooling, physical security, fibre. A <b>zone</b> is one or more of them that fail together; a <b>region</b> is a group of zones close enough to talk fast</td><td>Spread work across zones to survive a building failing; pick a region for latency and data residency</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Servers</td><td>Racks of ordinary machines: CPUs, memory, local disks, network cards. They fail every day at this scale</td><td>Design as if any one machine can disappear, because it will</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Virtualisation</td><td>A hypervisor splits one physical server into many virtual machines, each believing it has its own hardware</td><td>Your "server" shares a machine with strangers; isolation is the provider's hardest job</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Network</td><td>Software-defined networking: your private network, firewall rules and load balancers are configuration on shared switches and fibre</td><td>Moving data costs money and time, especially between regions and out to the internet</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Storage</td><td>Block disks attached over the network, object stores that spread copies of your files across machines and zones, managed file systems</td><td>Durability comes from copies in different places; every copy and every read has a price</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Control plane</td><td>The APIs, identity and permissions, scheduling, quotas and billing that turn all of the above into "click to create"</td><td>Every console click and every Terraform line is an API call; IAM decides who can make it</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Managed services</td><td>Databases, queues, serverless and AI services: software the provider runs for you, built from the same layers</td><td>You trade control for convenience; the parts underneath still fail and still cost money</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Virtual machines, containers and sandboxes</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>A virtual machine</b> has its own operating system kernel on virtual hardware. Strong isolation, slower to start.</li>
      <li><b>A container</b> shares the host's kernel and isolates processes with kernel features. Fast and light, but sharing a kernel means weaker isolation.</li>
      <li><b>Providers close the gap</b> with sandboxes. Firecracker, developed at Amazon Web Services for services such as AWS Lambda and now open source, uses Linux KVM to run lightweight "microVMs" that start fast but keep virtual-machine isolation. gVisor, open-sourced by Google, is an "application kernel" that sits between a container and the host, because, in its own words, containers are not a sandbox.</li>
      <li><b>Serverless</b> is this underneath: someone else's servers, packed densely, started on demand, and billed by the request or the second.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Scheduling: who runs where</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>A scheduler</b> decides which machine runs each piece of work, packing jobs tightly to use the hardware well and moving them when machines fail.</li>
      <li><b>Kubernetes</b> brings this idea to everyone. Its own README says it builds on Google's experience running production workloads at scale with an internal system called Borg.</li>
      <li><b>Why you care:</b> requests and limits, bin packing and preemption decide both your bill and whether your work gets evicted. See <a href="#kubernetes">Kubernetes</a>.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>What "managed" really means</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>A managed database</b> is still a database server on virtual machines with disks, replicas and backups. The provider patches and fails it over for you.</li>
      <li><b>Object storage</b> is many machines holding copies or encoded fragments of your files, with an API in front. That is why it is durable, cheap and slower than a local disk.</li>
      <li><b>A serverless function</b> runs in a sandbox on a shared machine; a "cold start" is that sandbox being created for you.</li>
      <li><b>Knowing the parts helps you ask the right questions:</b> where are the copies, what happens when a zone fails, what is billed, and what can I take elsewhere.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Shared responsibility</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>The provider secures the cloud:</b> buildings, hardware, hypervisors, their network and their services.</li>
      <li><b>You secure what you put in it:</b> identities and permissions, your data, your network rules, your code and its dependencies, and the settings you choose.</li>
      <li><b>The more managed the service,</b> the more the provider takes on, but your identities, data and settings are always yours. Many cloud breaches start with a setting someone left open or a leaked credential, not a broken data centre. See <a href="#security">Security</a>.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Follow one request through the layers</h3>
    <p>You open a web page served from the cloud. Your request reaches the provider's edge and a load balancer (software on shared hardware), which picks a healthy instance in one zone. That instance is a container or virtual machine, placed by a scheduler on a physical server in a data centre. It reads from a managed database (more virtual machines, with a replica in another zone) and from object storage (copies spread across machines), writes logs and metrics (yet more storage), and every step is checked against permissions and metered for the bill. When one machine, or one building, fails, the layers above route around it, if you designed for it.</p>
    <p class="note">Try it yourself: run a small app on <a href="#cloudrun">Cloud Run</a>, then find each layer in the console: the region and zone, the revision and its instances, the logs, the IAM roles, and the bill.</p>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Explain zones, regions and why work is spread across them</li><li>Tell a virtual machine from a container and a serverless function</li><li>Name what the provider secures and what you secure</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Choose zones and regions for a service's availability, latency and data residency</li><li>Read a bill and trace each line to a layer: compute, storage, network</li><li>Explain what a managed service still leaves to you</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Design for a machine, a zone or a region failing, and test it</li><li>Choose isolation (VM, container, sandbox) for untrusted or multi-tenant work</li><li>Estimate cost from first principles, including egress and copies</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Set organisation standards for failure domains, tenancy and shared responsibility</li><li>Lead decisions on regions, providers and lock-in with the layers in view</li><li>Teach how the cloud works underneath, not only which button to press</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>Failure domains: machine, zone, region</li><li>Hypervisors and microVMs</li><li>Container isolation and sandboxes</li><li>Cluster scheduling and bin packing</li><li>The shared responsibility model</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://github.com/firecracker-microvm/firecracker" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Firecracker</a><span class="lnote"> MicroVMs built at AWS for services such as Lambda, open source</span></li><li><a href="https://github.com/google/gvisor" target="_blank" rel="noopener">gVisor</a><span class="lnote"> An application kernel that sandboxes containers</span></li><li><a href="https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kubernetes</a><span class="lnote"> The open-source scheduler and orchestrator for containers</span></li><li><a href="https://research.google.com/pubs/pub43438.html?authuser=1" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The Borg paper</a><span class="lnote"> Google's paper on Borg, as linked from the Kubernetes README</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/firecracker-microvm/firecracker/main/README.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Firecracker README</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/google/gvisor/master/README.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">gVisor README</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/master/README.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kubernetes README (Borg, paper link)</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/compute/docs/regions-zones" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GCP regions and zones</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/run/docs/container-contract" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cloud Run container contract (sandboxes, in-memory FS)</a>. A few claims could not be confirmed at their official source from here and are being re-checked.</p><p class="runcode">A question, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/enrichmeai.github.io/issues/new?title=%5BHow%20the%20cloud%20is%20built%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>. Nothing here is tracked and there are no comments to store: questions live in the open, where the next learner can find the answer.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= CLOUD COMPARISON ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-cloudcompare" data-name="GCP vs AWS vs Azure">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>GCP vs AWS vs Azure</h2><p>The same building blocks under three names: a service-by-service map, how the providers differ in structure, identity, networking and pricing, each one's strengths, and how to stay portable. Service names change often; check each provider's own comparison page before deciding.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Account structure and identity</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Concept</th><th>Google Cloud</th><th>AWS</th><th>Azure</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Hierarchy</td><td>Organization → folders → projects</td><td>Organization → OUs → accounts</td><td>Entra tenant → management groups → subscriptions → resource groups</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Unit of isolation and billing</td><td>Project</td><td>Account</td><td>Subscription (resource groups inside)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Human identity</td><td>Cloud Identity / Google Workspace</td><td>IAM Identity Center</td><td>Microsoft Entra ID</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Access model</td><td>IAM roles bound to principals on resources</td><td>IAM policies (JSON) on users, roles and resources</td><td>Azure RBAC role assignments at a scope</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Workload identity</td><td>Service accounts</td><td>IAM roles (instance profiles, task roles)</td><td>Managed identities</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Keyless access from CI / other clouds</td><td>Workload Identity Federation</td><td>OIDC identity providers + <code>AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity</code></td><td>Workload identity federation in Entra</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Guardrails</td><td>Organization policies</td><td>Service control policies</td><td>Azure Policy</td></tr>
      <tr><td>CLI</td><td><code>gcloud</code>, <code>bq</code>, <code>gsutil</code>/<code>gcloud storage</code></td><td><code>aws</code></td><td><code>az</code></td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Compute and containers</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Need</th><th>Google Cloud</th><th>AWS</th><th>Azure</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Virtual machines</td><td>Compute Engine</td><td>EC2</td><td>Virtual Machines</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Serverless containers</td><td>Cloud Run (services)</td><td>ECS on Fargate (App Runner stopped taking new customers on 30 April 2026)</td><td>Azure Container Apps</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Run-to-completion containers</td><td>Cloud Run jobs; Batch</td><td>AWS Batch; ECS tasks</td><td>Container Apps jobs; Azure Batch</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Functions</td><td>Cloud Run functions</td><td>Lambda</td><td>Azure Functions</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Managed Kubernetes</td><td>GKE (Standard, Autopilot)</td><td>EKS (with Auto Mode)</td><td>AKS</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Platform as a service</td><td>App Engine</td><td>Elastic Beanstalk</td><td>App Service</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Container registry</td><td>Artifact Registry</td><td>ECR</td><td>Azure Container Registry</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Storage and databases</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Need</th><th>Google Cloud</th><th>AWS</th><th>Azure</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Object storage</td><td>Cloud Storage</td><td>S3</td><td>Blob Storage</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Block storage</td><td>Persistent Disk / Hyperdisk</td><td>EBS</td><td>Managed Disks</td></tr>
      <tr><td>File storage</td><td>Filestore</td><td>EFS, FSx</td><td>Azure Files, NetApp Files</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Managed Postgres / MySQL</td><td>Cloud SQL</td><td>RDS</td><td>Azure Database for PostgreSQL / MySQL</td></tr>
      <tr><td>High-performance Postgres-compatible</td><td>AlloyDB</td><td>Aurora</td><td>Azure Database for PostgreSQL (Flexible Server, elastic clusters)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Globally consistent relational</td><td>Spanner</td><td>Aurora DSQL</td><td>Cosmos DB (for some workloads)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>SQL Server</td><td>Cloud SQL for SQL Server</td><td>RDS for SQL Server</td><td>Azure SQL Database / Managed Instance</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Document / key-value</td><td>Firestore</td><td>DynamoDB, DocumentDB</td><td>Cosmos DB</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Wide-column</td><td>Bigtable</td><td>Keyspaces; DynamoDB</td><td>Cosmos DB (Cassandra API), Managed Instance for Apache Cassandra</td></tr>
      <tr><td>In-memory cache</td><td>Memorystore (Redis, Valkey)</td><td>ElastiCache, MemoryDB</td><td>Azure Managed Redis (Azure Cache for Redis is being retired)</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Data and analytics</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Need</th><th>Google Cloud</th><th>AWS</th><th>Azure</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Data warehouse</td><td>BigQuery</td><td>Redshift (plus Athena for SQL on S3)</td><td>Microsoft Fabric warehouse; Azure Synapse Analytics</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Lakehouse / open tables</td><td>Lakehouse, formerly BigLake (Iceberg)</td><td>S3 Tables (Iceberg), Lake Formation</td><td>OneLake (Delta) in Fabric; Azure Databricks</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Stream and batch processing</td><td>Dataflow (Apache Beam)</td><td>Managed Service for Apache Flink; Glue</td><td>Stream Analytics; Fabric; Databricks</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Spark / Hadoop</td><td>Managed Service for Apache Spark (formerly Dataproc and Serverless for Apache Spark)</td><td>EMR, EMR Serverless</td><td>HDInsight; Azure Databricks; Fabric Spark</td></tr>
      <tr><td>ETL / pipeline service</td><td>Dataflow, Data Fusion, Dataform</td><td>Glue</td><td>Data Factory (and in Fabric)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Managed Airflow</td><td>Managed Service for Apache Airflow (formerly Cloud Composer)</td><td>MWAA</td><td>Apache Airflow jobs in Fabric</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Messaging</td><td>Pub/Sub</td><td>SNS + SQS; Kinesis Data Streams</td><td>Service Bus; Event Hubs</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Managed Kafka</td><td>Managed Service for Apache Kafka</td><td>Amazon MSK</td><td>Event Hubs (Kafka protocol)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Event routing</td><td>Eventarc</td><td>EventBridge</td><td>Event Grid</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Change data capture</td><td>Datastream</td><td>Database Migration Service (CDC)</td><td>Fabric mirroring; Data Factory CDC</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Catalog and governance</td><td>Knowledge Catalog (formerly Dataplex)</td><td>Glue Data Catalog, Lake Formation, SageMaker Catalog</td><td>Microsoft Purview</td></tr>
      <tr><td>BI</td><td>Looker, Looker Studio</td><td>Quick Sight (part of AWS Quick, formerly QuickSight)</td><td>Power BI</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>One pipeline, three clouds</h3>
    <p>The same job on each provider, step by step. Each data page goes deeper: <a href="#gcpdata">Data on GCP</a>, <a href="#awsdata">Data on AWS</a>, <a href="#azuredata">Data on Azure</a>.</p>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>Google Cloud</th><th>AWS</th><th>Azure</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>1. Land files, unchanged</td><td>Cloud Storage</td><td>S3</td><td>Data Lake Storage; OneLake in Fabric</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2. Take in events</td><td>Pub/Sub; Managed Service for Apache Kafka</td><td>Kinesis Data Streams; Amazon MSK</td><td>Event Hubs (Kafka protocol)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>3. Capture database changes</td><td>Datastream</td><td>Database Migration Service (CDC)</td><td>Data Factory CDC; Fabric mirroring</td></tr>
      <tr><td>4. Transform</td><td>Dataflow; Managed Service for Apache Spark; Dataform or dbt</td><td>Glue; EMR</td><td>Data Factory; Azure Databricks; Fabric Spark</td></tr>
      <tr><td>5. Store as open tables</td><td>Lakehouse (Iceberg)</td><td>S3 Tables (Iceberg)</td><td>OneLake (Delta or Iceberg)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>6. Query and serve</td><td>BigQuery</td><td>Athena; Redshift</td><td>Fabric warehouse; Synapse serverless SQL</td></tr>
      <tr><td>7. Schedule</td><td>Managed Service for Apache Airflow</td><td>MWAA; Step Functions</td><td>Data Factory; Airflow jobs in Fabric</td></tr>
      <tr><td>8. Catalog and grant access</td><td>Knowledge Catalog; IAM</td><td>Glue Data Catalog and Lake Formation; IAM</td><td>Purview; Azure roles and ACLs</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note"><i>Analysis:</i> the steps line up, so the design and the skills move between clouds. <i>The other view:</i> equivalents are not identical. Access control is a clear example: Lake Formation grants tables and columns, while Data Lake Storage ACLs grant directories and files. Check the detail that matters to you on each provider before you treat two services as the same.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>AI, APIs, networking, security, operations</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Need</th><th>Google Cloud</th><th>AWS</th><th>Azure</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Generative AI platform</td><td>Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform, formerly Vertex AI (Gemini, Claude and open models in Model Garden)</td><td>Bedrock (Claude and others); SageMaker for custom ML</td><td>Microsoft Foundry, formerly Azure AI Foundry (OpenAI models, Claude and others)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Vector search</td><td>Vector Search on Agent Platform (formerly Vertex AI Vector Search); BigQuery; AlloyDB</td><td>OpenSearch; Aurora pgvector; S3 Vectors</td><td>Azure AI Search; Cosmos DB; PostgreSQL pgvector</td></tr>
      <tr><td>API management</td><td>Apigee; API Gateway</td><td>API Gateway</td><td>API Management</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Virtual network</td><td>VPC (global, subnets regional)</td><td>VPC (regional)</td><td>Virtual Network (regional)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Load balancing</td><td>Cloud Load Balancing (global L7 with one anycast IP)</td><td>ALB, NLB; Global Accelerator</td><td>Load Balancer, Application Gateway, Front Door</td></tr>
      <tr><td>CDN</td><td>Cloud CDN</td><td>CloudFront</td><td>Azure Front Door</td></tr>
      <tr><td>DNS</td><td>Cloud DNS</td><td>Route 53</td><td>Azure DNS</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Private link to services</td><td>Private Service Connect</td><td>PrivateLink</td><td>Private Link</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Dedicated connectivity</td><td>Cloud Interconnect</td><td>Direct Connect</td><td>ExpressRoute</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Secrets</td><td>Secret Manager</td><td>Secrets Manager</td><td>Key Vault</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Keys</td><td>Cloud KMS</td><td>KMS</td><td>Key Vault</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Security posture</td><td>Security Command Center</td><td>Security Hub, GuardDuty</td><td>Microsoft Defender for Cloud</td></tr>
      <tr><td>WAF / DDoS</td><td>Cloud Armor</td><td>AWS WAF, Shield</td><td>Azure WAF, DDoS Protection</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Logs and metrics</td><td>Cloud Logging, Cloud Monitoring</td><td>CloudWatch</td><td>Azure Monitor (Log Analytics)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Tracing / APM</td><td>Cloud Trace</td><td>X-Ray / CloudWatch Application Signals</td><td>Application Insights</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Infrastructure as code</td><td>Terraform; Infrastructure Manager</td><td>CloudFormation, CDK; Terraform</td><td>Bicep, ARM; Terraform</td></tr>
      <tr><td>CI/CD</td><td>Cloud Build, Cloud Deploy</td><td>CodeBuild, CodePipeline</td><td>Azure DevOps Pipelines; GitHub Actions</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Where each tends to stand out</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Google Cloud:</b> data and analytics (BigQuery is serverless with nothing to size), global networking with a global VPC and single-IP global load balancing, Kubernetes (GKE), developer-friendly serverless (Cloud Run), and AI with Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform (formerly Vertex AI). Projects make isolation simple.</li>
      <li><b>AWS:</b> the broadest and deepest service catalogue, the largest ecosystem and talent pool, many regions, and mature options for almost every niche. More configuration choices, and more to learn.</li>
      <li><b>Azure:</b> enterprises standardised on Microsoft (Entra ID, Microsoft 365, Windows, SQL Server, .NET), hybrid setups (Azure Arc), and the Microsoft data and AI stack (Fabric, Power BI, Azure OpenAI).</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">All three are capable for almost any workload. The deciding factors are usually existing skills and contracts, specific services you depend on, data location, and price after discounts.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Differences that bite</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>IAM thinking:</b> GCP binds roles to principals on resources; AWS writes JSON policies with explicit allow/deny and resource ARNs; Azure assigns roles at a scope in a strict hierarchy.</li>
      <li><b>Networking:</b> a GCP VPC spans regions; AWS and Azure networks are regional and are joined with peering or transit gateways/hubs.</li>
      <li><b>Isolation unit:</b> GCP teams often use many projects; AWS uses many accounts; Azure uses subscriptions plus resource groups.</li>
      <li><b>Warehouses:</b> BigQuery bills by bytes scanned or by slots with no cluster to run; Redshift has provisioned clusters or serverless capacity units; Fabric uses capacity units shared across workloads.</li>
      <li><b>Discounts:</b> GCP committed and sustained use discounts; AWS Savings Plans and Reserved Instances; Azure reservations and savings plans. Spot or preemptible capacity everywhere for interruptible work.</li>
      <li><b>Egress:</b> data leaving a cloud costs money on all three; cross-region and cross-cloud traffic adds up. Design data placement before choosing multi-cloud.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Regions in India (data residency)</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Provider</th><th>Regions</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Google Cloud</td><td>Mumbai (<code>asia-south1</code>), Delhi (<code>asia-south2</code>)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>AWS</td><td>Mumbai (<code>ap-south-1</code>), Hyderabad (<code>ap-south-2</code>)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Azure</td><td>Central India (Pune), South India (Chennai), West India (Mumbai), plus newer regions</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note">Not every service or model is available in every region. Check availability per service, and for AI models check which regions actually process the data.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>CLI equivalents</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># who am I / which project-account-subscription
gcloud config list            aws sts get-caller-identity     az account show

# list VMs
gcloud compute instances list aws ec2 describe-instances      az vm list -o table

# copy a file to object storage
gcloud storage cp f gs://b/   aws s3 cp f s3://b/             az storage blob upload -f f -c b -n f --account-name acct

# Kubernetes credentials
gcloud container clusters get-credentials c --region r
aws eks update-kubeconfig --name c --region r
az aks get-credentials -g rg -n c

# read a secret
gcloud secrets versions access latest --secret=s
aws secretsmanager get-secret-value --secret-id s
az keyvault secret show --vault-name v -n s</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Staying portable (where it matters)</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Containers and Kubernetes</b> run the same on GKE, EKS and AKS; Cloud Run-style serverless is less portable but simpler.</li>
      <li><b>Terraform</b> for all infrastructure, with provider-specific modules behind your own interfaces.</li>
      <li><b>Open data formats</b> (Parquet, Iceberg, Delta) in object storage keep data readable by any engine.</li>
      <li><b>Open protocols:</b> Postgres, Kafka, OpenTelemetry, OIDC, S3-compatible APIs.</li>
      <li><b>dbt and SQL</b> transfer between warehouses with dialect adjustments.</li>
      <li><b>Accept lock-in deliberately</b> where a managed service saves real effort (BigQuery, Spanner, DynamoDB); write down the exit cost.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">Multi-cloud for its own sake doubles skills, tooling and egress. Use it for a concrete reason: a customer requirement, a unique service, resilience against a provider-wide failure, or negotiation.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Choosing for a new workload</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Where is the data now, and where must it legally live?</li>
      <li>Which managed services would save the most work (warehouse, Kubernetes, AI models)?</li>
      <li>What does the team already know, and what do hiring markets offer?</li>
      <li>What identity and productivity stack does the organisation use?</li>
      <li>Price a realistic workload on each calculator, including egress and support.</li>
      <li>Prototype the riskiest piece on the leading candidate before committing.</li>
    </ol>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Certifications, side by side</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Google Cloud</th><th>AWS</th><th>Azure</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Foundational</td><td>Cloud Digital Leader</td><td>Cloud Practitioner</td><td>AZ-900 Fundamentals</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Associate</td><td>Associate Cloud Engineer</td><td>Solutions Architect – Associate</td><td>AZ-104 Administrator</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Data</td><td>Professional Data Engineer</td><td>Data Engineer – Associate</td><td>Fabric Data Engineer (DP-700)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Architect</td><td>Professional Cloud Architect</td><td>Solutions Architect – Professional</td><td>AZ-305 Solutions Architect Expert</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note">Certification names and codes change; check each provider's site.</p>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Map common services across GCP, AWS and Azure</li><li>Explain projects vs accounts vs subscriptions</li><li>Use each provider's CLI for basic tasks</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Translate a design from one cloud to another, including IAM and networking</li><li>Compare prices for a workload, including egress</li><li>Use Terraform across providers</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Choose a provider for a workload with written trade-offs</li><li>Design portable architectures where it matters, and accept lock-in deliberately</li><li>Plan data residency and region choices</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Set the organisation's cloud strategy, contracts and multi-cloud policy</li><li>Lead migrations between clouds or from on-premises</li><li>Govern cost, security and compliance across providers</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>Well-Architected frameworks of each provider</li><li>Cloud IAM models compared</li><li>Cross-cloud networking and egress costs</li><li>Open table formats for portability</li><li>FinOps practices</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://cloud.google.com/docs/get-started/aws-azure-gcp-service-comparison" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google Cloud: compare AWS and Azure services</a><span class="lnote"> Google's official service mapping</span></li><li><a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/aws-professional/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Azure for AWS professionals</a><span class="lnote"> Microsoft's AWS-to-Azure mapping</span></li><li><a href="https://cloud.google.com/architecture/framework" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google Cloud Well-Architected Framework</a><span class="lnote"> Google's design pillars</span></li><li><a href="https://aws.amazon.com/architecture/well-architected/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AWS Well-Architected</a><span class="lnote"> AWS design pillars</span></li><li><a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/well-architected/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Azure Well-Architected Framework</a><span class="lnote"> Azure design pillars</span></li><li><a href="https://www.finops.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">FinOps Foundation</a><span class="lnote"> Managing cloud cost across providers</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://aws.amazon.com/apprunner/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AWS App Runner (closed to new customers)</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/dataplex/docs/introduction" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Knowledge Catalog, formerly Dataplex</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/dataproc-serverless/docs/overview" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Managed Service for Apache Spark rename</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/biglake/docs/introduction" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Lakehouse, formerly BigLake</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/composer/docs" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Managed Service for Apache Airflow, formerly Cloud Composer</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/vertex-ai-name-changes" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Vertex AI name changes</a>; <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/ai-foundry/what-is-azure-ai-foundry" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Microsoft Foundry</a>; <a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/quicksight/latest/user/welcome.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AWS Quick (Quick Sight)</a>; <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-cache-for-redis/cache-overview" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Azure Cache for Redis retirement</a>; <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-factory/concepts-workflow-orchestration-manager" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ADF Workflow Orchestration Manager</a>; <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/reliability/regions-list" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Azure regions list</a>; <a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/global-infrastructure/latest/regions/aws-regions.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AWS regions</a>; <a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/aurora-dsql/latest/userguide/what-is-aurora-dsql.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Aurora DSQL</a>; <a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/eks/latest/userguide/automode.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">EKS Auto Mode</a>; <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/credentials/certifications/fabric-data-engineer-associate/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Fabric Data Engineer (DP-700)</a>; <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/frontdoor/front-door-overview" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Azure Front Door overview</a>; <a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cli/latest/reference/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AWS CLI reference</a>; <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/cli/azure/reference-index?view=azure-cli-latest" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Azure CLI reference</a>. A few claims could not be confirmed at their official source from here and are being re-checked.</p><p class="runcode">A question, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/enrichmeai.github.io/issues/new?title=%5BGCP%20vs%20AWS%20vs%20Azure%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>. Nothing here is tracked and there are no comments to store: questions live in the open, where the next learner can find the answer.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= CLOUD COST ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-cloudcost" data-name="Cloud Cost">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Cloud cost</h2><p>In the cloud, an engineer&rsquo;s design choices become the monthly bill: the machine size, the region, where data is copied, how long it is kept. This page covers how cloud pricing works, the two kinds of lever for lowering a bill, how to see who spends what, and how to decide on commitments. Provider-by-provider discount names are on <a href="#cloudcompare">GCP vs AWS vs Azure</a>; when multi-cloud pays off is on <a href="#retailcloud">Retail &amp; Hybrid Cloud</a>.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>The short answer</h3>
    <p><i>Our view:</i> make cost visible to the people who make the design choices, and treat it as one requirement among several, next to reliability, security and speed. <i>The other view</i> is in the card &ldquo;Our view, and the other view&rdquo;.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>FinOps, and what the providers say</h3>
    <p>Google describes cloud <b>FinOps</b> (financial operations) as an operational framework and cultural practice that brings engineering, finance and business teams together to manage cloud spending and the business value it buys; rather than simply cutting costs, it sets up shared accountability for spending that varies with use.</p>
    <p>All three providers publish cost guidance in their Well-Architected Frameworks. Among Google&rsquo;s principles: align spending with business value, make people aware of the cost of their decisions, use only what you need, and optimise continuously. AWS&rsquo;s cost pillar includes adopting a consumption model and measuring the business output of a workload against what it costs to deliver.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>How cloud pricing works</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>On demand:</b> pay for what you use, with no commitment.</li>
      <li><b>Commitments:</b> a lower price for agreeing to use, or spend, a minimum amount for one or three years: Google&rsquo;s committed use discounts, AWS Savings Plans and Azure reservations. AWS and Azure each say up to 72%; Google publishes its rates product by product.</li>
      <li><b>Spot capacity:</b> spare capacity at a much lower price, which the provider can take back. Google quotes discounts of up to 91% for Spot VMs; AWS gives a two-minute warning before stopping a Spot Instance, and Azure 30 seconds before evicting a Spot VM.</li>
      <li><b>Storage classes:</b> colder classes cost less to keep and more to read. In Cloud Storage, Nearline, Coldline and Archive have minimum storage durations of 30, 90 and 365 days.</li>
      <li><b>Data transfer:</b> Google charges nothing for inbound data, but moving data between zones in one region is charged per GiB, and leaving the cloud costs more; check each provider&rsquo;s price list.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Two kinds of lever</h3>
    <figure class="fig"><svg viewBox="0 0 620 372" role="img" aria-labelledby="cb-t cb-d"><title id="cb-t">Two ways to lower a cloud bill</title><desc id="cb-d">A tree. The cloud bill is usage multiplied by rate. Usage, how much you run, is lowered by switching off what is idle, right-sizing, autoscaling, deleting old data, and keeping data near the compute that reads it. Rate, what you pay for each unit, is lowered by commitments for one or three years, spot capacity for work that can be interrupted, and a cheaper storage class for data read rarely.</desc><rect class="nd" x="210" y="12" width="200" height="48" rx="6"/><text class="lb hd" x="310" y="33" text-anchor="middle">Cloud bill</text><text class="lb mu" x="310" y="51" text-anchor="middle">usage &#215; rate</text><line class="ed" x1="310" y1="60" x2="155" y2="92"/><rect class="nd in" x="50" y="92" width="210" height="46" rx="6"/><text class="lb hd" x="155" y="112" text-anchor="middle">Usage</text><text class="lb mu" x="155" y="129" text-anchor="middle">how much you run</text><line class="ed" x1="310" y1="60" x2="465" y2="92"/><rect class="nd in" x="360" y="92" width="210" height="46" rx="6"/><text class="lb hd" x="465" y="112" text-anchor="middle">Rate</text><text class="lb mu" x="465" y="129" text-anchor="middle">what each unit costs</text><line class="ed" x1="70" y1="138" x2="70" y2="341"/><line class="ed" x1="70" y1="181" x2="85" y2="181"/><rect class="nd out" x="85" y="164" width="175" height="34" rx="5"/><text class="lb" x="172" y="186" text-anchor="middle">Switch off what is idle</text><line class="ed" x1="70" y1="221" x2="85" y2="221"/><rect class="nd out" x="85" y="204" width="175" height="34" rx="5"/><text class="lb" x="172" y="226" text-anchor="middle">Right-size machines</text><line class="ed" x1="70" y1="261" x2="85" y2="261"/><rect class="nd out" x="85" y="244" width="175" height="34" rx="5"/><text class="lb" x="172" y="266" text-anchor="middle">Autoscale with demand</text><line class="ed" x1="70" y1="301" x2="85" y2="301"/><rect class="nd out" x="85" y="284" width="175" height="34" rx="5"/><text class="lb" x="172" y="306" text-anchor="middle">Delete old data</text><line class="ed" x1="70" y1="341" x2="85" y2="341"/><rect class="nd out" x="85" y="324" width="175" height="34" rx="5"/><text class="lb" x="172" y="346" text-anchor="middle">Keep data near compute</text><line class="ed" x1="380" y1="138" x2="380" y2="261"/><line class="ed" x1="380" y1="181" x2="395" y2="181"/><rect class="nd out" x="395" y="164" width="175" height="34" rx="5"/><text class="lb" x="482" y="186" text-anchor="middle">Commit for 1 or 3 years</text><line class="ed" x1="380" y1="221" x2="395" y2="221"/><rect class="nd out" x="395" y="204" width="175" height="34" rx="5"/><text class="lb" x="482" y="226" text-anchor="middle">Spot for interruptible work</text><line class="ed" x1="380" y1="261" x2="395" y2="261"/><rect class="nd out" x="395" y="244" width="175" height="34" rx="5"/><text class="lb" x="482" y="266" text-anchor="middle">Cheaper storage class</text></svg><figcaption>The bill is how much you use multiplied by what each unit costs, so there are two sets of levers. Azure&rsquo;s Well-Architected Framework names them <i>usage optimization</i> and <i>rate optimization</i>; the levers listed come from the three providers&rsquo; documentation, cited on this page.</figcaption></figure>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Worked example: when does a commitment pay off?</h3>
    <p>A commitment is paid whether or not you use it. With a discount of <i>d</i>, it is cheaper than paying on demand only if you use more than 1 &minus; <i>d</i> of what you committed to. Switching off a machine that is needed only in office hours is a usage lever with no commitment at all: AWS&rsquo;s cost pillar uses the example of development and test environments used eight hours a day in the working week, 40 hours of 168, and puts the potential saving at 75%.</p>
<pre><code># Is a commitment cheaper than paying on demand?
# You pay for the commitment whether you use it or not.

def cheaper_to_commit(discount, used):
    on_demand = used            # pay only for what you use (price 1.0)
    committed = 1 - discount    # pay the discounted price for all of it
    return committed &lt; on_demand

for discount in (0.20, 0.40, 0.60):
    break_even = 1 - discount
    print(f"discount {discount:.0%}: commit if you use more than {break_even:.0%};",
          "at 70% use:", "commit" if cheaper_to_commit(discount, 0.70) else "on demand")

hours_on, hours_week = 40, 24 * 7
print(f"office-hours dev box: runs {hours_on} of {hours_week} hours,",
      f"so switching it off saves {1 - hours_on / hours_week:.0%}")
</code></pre>
<pre><code>discount 20%: commit if you use more than 80%; at 70% use: on demand
discount 40%: commit if you use more than 60%; at 70% use: commit
discount 60%: commit if you use more than 40%; at 70% use: commit
office-hours dev box: runs 40 of 168 hours, so switching it off saves 76%</code></pre>
    <p class="note">Run as shown with Python 3; the second block is its real output. The discounts are round numbers for the arithmetic, not any provider&rsquo;s price. AWS gives 75%.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Seeing who spends what</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Tags and labels:</b> AWS cost allocation tags, once activated, group costs on the cost allocation report by each tag&rsquo;s key and value; Google Cloud passes resource labels, such as a team or cost centre, to billing, so charges can be grouped by label.</li>
      <li><b>Budgets and alerts:</b> Google warns that an alerts-only budget does not cap usage or spending; it only tells you how costs are trending. Stopping spend needs an action, which can be automated, for example by disabling billing on a project, or a spend-cap budget where the service supports one.</li>
      <li><b>One format for cost data:</b> FOCUS, the FinOps Open Cost and Usage Specification, is an open schema for cost and usage data across clouds, SaaS and data centres, so bills from different providers can be compared.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Cost as an engineering choice</h3>
    <p class="note"><i>Analysis:</i> some of the largest costs are fixed by design rather than by tuning: a chatty service in another zone or region pays for every byte it moves; a copy of data kept &ldquo;just in case&rdquo; is paid for every month; logs kept at full detail for ever grow without limit. These are cheapest to fix while the design is still on paper.</p>
    <p class="note">Azure&rsquo;s cost guidance also warns against choosing a cheaper option that weakens security, scalability, resilience or operability, because that can cost the business more later.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Choose by requirement</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>If this is true for you</th><th>It leans towards (<i>analysis</i>)</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Steady load that will run for a year or more</td><td>A commitment, sized to the base you are confident of, with on demand above it</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Batch or stateless work that can be stopped and retried</td><td>Spot capacity, with the work built to handle the notice period</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Load that rises and falls with users</td><td>Autoscaling, or serverless that scales to zero, before any commitment</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Environments needed only in working hours</td><td>A schedule that switches them off</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Data read rarely but kept for years</td><td>A colder storage class, with lifecycle rules; check the minimum storage duration and retrieval cost</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Services that call each other heavily</td><td>Place them, and their data, in the same region, and the same zone where resilience allows</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Nobody can say which team owns a cost</td><td>Tags or labels, applied by policy, before any optimisation</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Your team already has commitments or tooling with one provider</td><td>Use them first: moving has its own cost</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Our view, and the other view</h3>
    <p><i>Our view:</i> engineers should see the cost of what they build, per service and per unit of business output, as AWS&rsquo;s pillar suggests, and design with it from the start.</p>
    <p><i>The other view:</i> early in a product, speed of learning matters more than the bill, and time spent tuning cost can be time not spent finding out whether anyone wants the product; on this view, cost work waits until the spend is large enough to justify it.</p>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Read a cloud bill and name its biggest lines</li><li>Tag or label what you create</li><li>Switch off what you are not using</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Right-size and autoscale a service</li><li>Set budgets and alerts, knowing alerts-only budgets do not cap spend</li><li>Pick a storage class by how often data is read</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Decide on commitments from measured base load</li><li>Use spot capacity for interruptible work</li><li>Design data placement to limit transfer costs</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Build a FinOps practice across engineering and finance</li><li>Measure cost per unit of business output</li><li>Weigh cost against reliability and security</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>FinOps</li><li>On demand, commitments and spot capacity</li><li>Storage classes and minimum durations</li><li>Data transfer charges</li><li>Tags, labels and budgets</li><li>FOCUS cost data</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://cloud.google.com/architecture/framework/cost-optimization" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google Cloud Well-Architected: cost optimization</a><span class="lnote"> Google's principles</span></li><li><a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/well-architected/cost-optimization/principles" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Azure Well-Architected: cost optimization principles</a><span class="lnote"> Usage and rate optimization</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://cloud.google.com/learn/what-is-finops" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google Cloud: What is FinOps?</a>; <a href="https://cloud.google.com/architecture/framework/cost-optimization" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google Cloud Well-Architected Framework: cost optimization</a>; <a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/cost-optimization-pillar/design-principles.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AWS Well-Architected cost optimization pillar: design principles</a>; <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/well-architected/cost-optimization/principles" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Azure Well-Architected: cost optimization design principles</a>; <a href="https://cloud.google.com/docs/cuds" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google Cloud: committed use discounts</a>; <a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/savingsplans/latest/userguide/what-is-savings-plans.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AWS: What are Savings Plans?</a>; <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/cost-management-billing/reservations/save-compute-costs-reservations" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Azure: save costs with reservations</a>; <a href="https://cloud.google.com/compute/docs/instances/spot" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google Cloud: Spot VMs</a>; <a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSEC2/latest/UserGuide/spot-instance-termination-notices.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AWS: Spot Instance interruption notices</a>; <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/virtual-machines/spot-vms" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Azure: Spot Virtual Machines</a>; <a href="https://cloud.google.com/storage/docs/storage-classes" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google Cloud Storage: storage classes</a>; <a href="https://cloud.google.com/vpc/network-pricing" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google Cloud: network pricing</a>; <a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/awsaccountbilling/latest/aboutv2/cost-alloc-tags.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AWS: cost allocation tags</a>; <a href="https://cloud.google.com/resource-manager/docs/labels-overview" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google Cloud: labels overview</a>; <a href="https://cloud.google.com/billing/docs/how-to/budgets" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google Cloud: create budgets</a>; <a href="https://github.com/FinOps-Open-Cost-and-Usage-Spec/FOCUS_Spec" target="_blank" rel="noopener">FOCUS specification</a>.</p><p class="runcode"><b>Run the code:</b> <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/well-rounded-engineer-fundamentals/tree/main/cloudcost" target="_blank" rel="noopener">well-rounded-engineer-fundamentals/cloudcost</a>, with a README that says how to run it and what it shows. Stuck, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/well-rounded-engineer-fundamentals/issues/new?title=%5BCloud%20Cost%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= RETAIL AND HYBRID CLOUD ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-retailcloud" data-name="Retail &amp; Hybrid Cloud">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Retail integration, hybrid and multi-cloud</h2><p>A retailer joins tills in hundreds of stores, a website, warehouses, suppliers and a finance system, and each of them has to agree about stock and money. This page shows how those pieces are usually connected, the same building blocks on AWS, Azure and Google Cloud, and when keeping some work on premises or using more than one cloud pays for itself. The general comparison of the three providers is on <a href="#cloudcompare">GCP vs AWS vs Azure</a>.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>What a retailer has to connect</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Stores and tills:</b> the point of sale, where a retail transaction is completed. A till that stops when the network does stops the shop, so stores need to keep selling on their own (see below).</li>
      <li><b>Online:</b> the website, the apps, search and recommendations.</li>
      <li><b>Orders and stock:</b> order management, and one view of stock across stores, warehouses and goods on the way.</li>
      <li><b>Warehouses and delivery:</b> picking, packing, carriers and returns.</li>
      <li><b>Payments:</b> cards and wallets in store and online (see <a href="#payments">Payment Gateways</a>).</li>
      <li><b>Finance and planning:</b> the ERP, the general ledger, buying and replenishment.</li>
      <li><b>Suppliers:</b> often through EDI, the computer-to-computer exchange of structured business documents such as purchase orders, invoices and shipping notices, in standards such as UN/EDIFACT, ANSI ASC X12, GS1 EDI and TRADACOMS.</li>
      <li><b>Customers:</b> loyalty, marketing and service, which all want the same customer record.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note"><i>Analysis:</i> the hardest part is rarely one system. It is keeping stock and orders consistent between them, for example when a customer buys online and collects in a store that is also selling the same item at its tills.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Integration patterns that hold up in retail</h3>
    <p><i>Analysis, built on the pages linked.</i></p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Events for things that changed:</b> &ldquo;order placed&rdquo;, &ldquo;stock moved&rdquo;, &ldquo;item sold&rdquo;, published once and read by every system that cares (see <a href="#kafka">Kafka</a> and <a href="#eventsourcing">Event Sourcing</a>).</li>
      <li><b>APIs for questions that need an answer now:</b> the price of this item, or reserving stock for this order (see <a href="#apis">APIs &amp; Integration</a>).</li>
      <li><b>Store and forward at the till:</b> record each sale locally with its own unique id, and send it when the network returns. The receiving side must accept the same sale twice without counting it twice.</li>
      <li><b>The outbox:</b> write the change and the event in one local transaction, then publish, so the two cannot disagree.</li>
      <li><b>Batch where batch is fine:</b> supplier EDI files and nightly finance loads do not need to be real time.</li>
      <li><b>Reconcile:</b> compare till totals, orders, stock and payments every day, and treat a gap as an incident.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>A worked example: click and collect</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>The customer orders online. The order service, in the cloud, reserves stock at the chosen store through an API.</li>
      <li>An &ldquo;order placed&rdquo; event reaches the store system, which adds a pick task and takes the item out of sellable stock.</li>
      <li>The store picks the item and sends &ldquo;ready&rdquo;; the customer gets a message.</li>
      <li>At collection the till records the hand-over. If the store is offline, it records it locally and sends it later.</li>
      <li>The stock change flows to replenishment, which may send a purchase order to the supplier by EDI.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="note"><i>Analysis:</i> every arrow above can fail or arrive twice. Give each message a unique id, make each receiver idempotent, and decide in advance what the store does when it cannot reach the cloud.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>The same building blocks on AWS, Azure and Google Cloud</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>You need</th><th>AWS</th><th>Azure</th><th>Google Cloud</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Messaging and events between systems</td><td>Amazon EventBridge: a serverless service that uses events to connect application components</td><td>Azure Service Bus: a managed message broker with queues and publish-subscribe topics</td><td>Pub/Sub: an asynchronous messaging service that decouples services producing messages from services processing them</td></tr>
      <tr><td>A private network link from your data centre</td><td>Direct Connect: your network to a Direct Connect location over a fibre-optic cable</td><td>ExpressRoute: a private connection to Microsoft cloud services through a connectivity provider</td><td>Cloud Interconnect: low-latency, high-availability connections between your networks</td></tr>
      <tr><td>The provider&rsquo;s cloud on your own premises</td><td>AWS Outposts: AWS infrastructure and APIs at your site, operated by AWS as part of a Region</td><td>Azure Local: Azure capabilities on customer-owned machines, connected or disconnected, managed through Azure Arc</td><td>Google Distributed Cloud connected: for a very stable network connection, the lowest attainable latency, data too large to transfer for performance or cost reasons, or laws that require data to stay on premises</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p>Some useful pieces do not line up one to one. Each of these is described in its provider&rsquo;s own documentation:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Azure Arc</b> lets you manage servers, Kubernetes clusters and databases that run outside Azure, on premises or on other clouds, as if they ran in Azure.</li>
      <li><b>GKE Multi-Cloud</b> creates Google Kubernetes Engine clusters in AWS and Azure.</li>
      <li><b>Cross-Cloud Interconnect</b> is a dedicated physical connection between Google&rsquo;s network and another cloud provider&rsquo;s.</li>
      <li><b>A gateway you run yourself:</b> Azure API Management&rsquo;s self-hosted gateway is a container version of its managed gateway (Developer and Premium tiers); Apigee hybrid keeps the management plane in Google&rsquo;s cloud and runs the runtime plane on your own Kubernetes.</li>
      <li><b>On devices, for example in a store:</b> AWS IoT Greengrass is an open-source edge runtime that lets devices act on their own data locally and send it to AWS.</li>
      <li><b>For retail search and recommendations:</b> Amazon Personalize generates item recommendations from your data; Google&rsquo;s AI Commerce Search serves search and recommendations from catalogue and user-event data.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">This list is what we checked in each provider&rsquo;s documentation. It does not prove a provider has no equivalent; check the current documentation before you decide.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Hybrid: what stays on premises, and why. Choose by requirement</h3>
    <p>Hybrid cloud is a public cloud and a private environment, such as a private cloud or on-premises machines, that stay separate but are bound together.</p>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>If this is true for you</th><th>It leans towards</th><th>What it costs you</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Stores must keep selling when the network drops</td><td>Compute in every store, holding the sales and prices it needs</td><td>A fleet of small sites to update, secure and monitor</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Law or contract says data must stay in a place you control</td><td>On premises, or the provider&rsquo;s on-premises offering</td><td>Hardware and capacity you plan for yourself; Google lists limited processing capacity as a limit of its connected deployments</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Devices in the store make large data, such as camera video</td><td>Process it locally and send only the results</td><td>Local capacity, and models to update at every site</td></tr>
      <tr><td>The work cannot tolerate network delay or jitter</td><td>Run it next to the devices</td><td>Same as above</td></tr>
      <tr><td>A mainframe or ERP in your data centre runs the business and works</td><td>Keep it, and connect it with a private link, events and APIs</td><td>Two ways of operating, and the skills for both</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Seasonal peaks go beyond what you own</td><td>Cloud bursting: use public cloud capacity for the temporary peak</td><td>Data and networks designed so work can move</td></tr>
      <tr><td>None of the above</td><td>Public cloud only</td><td>The least to run</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Multi-cloud: when it pays, and when it costs. Choose by requirement</h3>
    <p>ISO/IEC 22123-1 defines multi-cloud as a deployment model in which a customer uses public cloud services from two or more providers.</p>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>If this is true for you</th><th>It leans towards</th><th>What it costs you</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>You need a service only one provider offers, and the rest runs elsewhere</td><td>Use that service on its own cloud for that job</td><td>Data transfer between clouds, a second identity and security setup</td></tr>
      <tr><td>A customer, regulator or contract requires a named provider, or a tested exit plan</td><td>Multi-cloud, or one cloud with a rehearsed exit</td><td>Keeping the exit working, not just written down</td></tr>
      <tr><td>You bought a company that runs on another cloud</td><td>Connect the two with APIs and events first; decide on moving later</td><td>Two platforms until you consolidate, if ever</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Your contract renewal depends on having a credible alternative</td><td>The ability to move a workload, shown in practice</td><td>Only credible if you have actually kept it portable</td></tr>
      <tr><td>You must survive a whole provider failing</td><td>The same system running on two clouds</td><td>The highest cost here: two of everything, and the data kept in step</td></tr>
      <tr><td>A small team builds one product</td><td>One provider</td><td>Some lock-in, accepted on purpose (see <a href="#cloudcompare">Staying portable</a>)</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note">Wikipedia&rsquo;s summary of the sources: multi-cloud can improve resilience, reduce dependence on one vendor and help in negotiating price, and it brings more operational complexity, security risk, cost and integration work.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>The costs that usually decide it</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Data leaving a cloud:</b> on Google Cloud, inbound data transfer has no charge (though services that process it, such as load balancers and Cloud NAT, may), while data leaving Google Cloud is priced by network tier and destination. Check each provider&rsquo;s network pricing for your traffic before you split a system across clouds.</li>
      <li><b>Switching in the EU:</b> under the EU Data Act, from 11 January 2024 to 12 January 2027 providers may charge only the costs directly linked to switching; from 12 January 2027 they may charge nothing for switching, data egress included.</li>
      <li><b>Skills and tools:</b> two clouds means two sets of identity, networking, monitoring and on-call knowledge.</li>
      <li><b>The lowest common denominator:</b> code written to run anywhere cannot use what makes one provider better at a job.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Our view, and the other view</h3>
    <p><i>Our view:</i> start on one cloud. Make every store able to work alone, connect the data centre you already have rather than rushing to empty it, and add a second provider only for a reason you can name from the tables above.</p>
    <p><i>The other view:</i> some teams start multi-cloud on purpose, so that portability is built in from day one rather than retrofitted at great cost, and so that no single provider&rsquo;s prices or outages can hold them hostage. Lower switching charges under the EU Data Act cut the cost of moving later, which weakens the first argument for some and strengthens the case for keeping a real exit open for others.</p>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Name the systems a retailer connects and what each one owns</li><li>Explain why a till must keep selling offline</li><li>Explain the difference between an event and an API call</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Build store-and-forward with idempotent receivers</li><li>Map a retail flow onto AWS, Azure or Google Cloud services</li><li>Connect a data centre to a cloud over a private link</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Decide what runs in the store, the data centre and the cloud, by requirement</li><li>Design one view of stock across channels</li><li>Price data transfer before splitting a system across clouds</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Set a hybrid and multi-cloud strategy with named reasons</li><li>Lead the integration of an acquired company on another cloud</li><li>Keep an exit plan from a provider tested, not just written</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>Point of sale and store systems</li><li>EDI with suppliers</li><li>Events, APIs and the outbox</li><li>Hybrid cloud and on-premises offerings</li><li>Multi-cloud and its costs</li><li>Switching charges under the EU Data Act</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/outposts/latest/userguide/what-is-outposts.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">What is AWS Outposts?</a><span class="lnote"> AWS infrastructure on your premises</span></li><li><a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-arc/overview" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Azure Arc overview</a><span class="lnote"> Manage resources outside Azure</span></li><li><a href="https://cloud.google.com/distributed-cloud/edge/latest/docs/overview" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google Distributed Cloud connected overview</a><span class="lnote"> When to use it, and its limits</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Point_of_sale" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Point of sale</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Electronic_data_interchange" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Electronic data interchange</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cloud_computing" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Cloud computing (hybrid cloud, cloud bursting)</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multicloud" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Multicloud</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Data_Act_(European_Union)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Data Act (European Union)</a>; <a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/eventbridge/latest/userguide/eb-what-is.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Amazon EventBridge: what is it</a>; <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/service-bus-messaging/service-bus-messaging-overview" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Azure Service Bus overview</a>; <a href="https://cloud.google.com/pubsub/docs/overview" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Pub/Sub overview</a>; <a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/directconnect/latest/UserGuide/Welcome.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AWS Direct Connect</a>; <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/expressroute/expressroute-introduction" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Azure ExpressRoute</a>; <a href="https://cloud.google.com/network-connectivity/docs/interconnect/concepts/overview" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cloud Interconnect overview</a>; <a href="https://cloud.google.com/network-connectivity/docs/interconnect/concepts/cci-overview" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cross-Cloud Interconnect overview</a>; <a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/outposts/latest/userguide/what-is-outposts.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">What is AWS Outposts?</a>; <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-local/overview" target="_blank" rel="noopener">What is Azure Local?</a>; <a href="https://cloud.google.com/distributed-cloud/edge/latest/docs/overview" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google Distributed Cloud connected overview</a>; <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-arc/overview" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Azure Arc overview</a>; <a href="https://cloud.google.com/kubernetes-engine/multi-cloud/docs" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GKE Multi-Cloud</a>; <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/api-management/self-hosted-gateway-overview" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Azure API Management self-hosted gateway</a>; <a href="https://cloud.google.com/apigee/docs/hybrid/latest/what-is-hybrid" target="_blank" rel="noopener">What is Apigee hybrid?</a>; <a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/greengrass/v2/developerguide/what-is-iot-greengrass.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">What is AWS IoT Greengrass?</a>; <a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/personalize/latest/dg/what-is-personalize.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">What is Amazon Personalize?</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-cx/ai-commerce-search/overview" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google AI Commerce Search overview</a>; <a href="https://cloud.google.com/vpc/network-pricing" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google Cloud network pricing</a>.</p><p class="runcode">A question, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/enrichmeai.github.io/issues/new?title=%5BRetail%20%26%20Hybrid%20Cloud%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>. Nothing here is tracked and there are no comments to store: questions live in the open, where the next learner can find the answer.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= GCP DATA ENGINEERING ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-gcpdata" data-name="Data Engineering on GCP">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Data engineering on Google Cloud</h2><p>Which Google Cloud service does each job in the data lifecycle, how they fit together in common architectures, and how to choose between similar ones. Concepts are on <a href="#data">Data Engineering</a>; the warehouse itself is on <a href="#bigquery">BigQuery</a>.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Service map by lifecycle stage</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>Service</th><th>Use it for</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Ingest: files</td><td>Storage Transfer Service</td><td>Bulk or scheduled copies from S3, HTTP lists, other buckets or on-premises into GCS</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Ingest: SaaS and Google sources</td><td>BigQuery Data Transfer Service</td><td>Scheduled loads from Google Ads, YouTube, other clouds' warehouses, S3 and more</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Ingest: events</td><td>Pub/Sub</td><td>Serverless messaging; push, pull, or straight into BigQuery or GCS with subscriptions</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Ingest: events (Kafka)</td><td>Managed Service for Apache Kafka</td><td>Kafka-compatible clusters run by Google, when your ecosystem is Kafka (<a href="#kafka">Kafka</a> tab)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Ingest: database changes</td><td>Datastream</td><td>Serverless CDC from MySQL, PostgreSQL, Oracle and SQL Server into BigQuery or GCS</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Ingest: custom</td><td>Cloud Run jobs / functions</td><td>APIs, scraping public mirrors, anything with its own logic (<a href="#cloudrun">Cloud Run</a> tab)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Store: files</td><td>Cloud Storage</td><td>Raw landing zone, archives, Parquet/Avro, lifecycle to cheaper classes</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Store: warehouse</td><td>BigQuery</td><td>Analytics tables, the layered model, BI and ML</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Store: lakehouse</td><td>Lakehouse (formerly BigLake)</td><td>Governed tables over files in GCS (including Iceberg), readable by BigQuery and Spark</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Store: serving</td><td>Cloud SQL, AlloyDB, Spanner, Bigtable</td><td>Low-latency reads for applications</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Transform: SQL</td><td>BigQuery + dbt or Dataform</td><td>ELT inside the warehouse, with tests and lineage</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Transform: code at scale</td><td>Dataflow (Apache Beam)</td><td>Batch and streaming pipelines, autoscaling, exactly-once into BigQuery (Storage Write API mode)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Transform: Spark/Hadoop</td><td>Managed Service for Apache Spark (formerly Dataproc and Serverless for Apache Spark)</td><td>Existing Spark jobs, Spark ML, lift-and-shift Hadoop</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Orchestrate</td><td>Managed Service for Apache Airflow (formerly Cloud Composer)</td><td>Many dependent pipelines, sensors, backfills (<a href="#dags">DAGs</a> tab)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Orchestrate (light)</td><td>Workflows + Cloud Scheduler</td><td>A few steps calling APIs and jobs, no Airflow to run</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Govern</td><td>Knowledge Catalog (formerly Dataplex)</td><td>Catalog, data quality scans, lineage, profiling, policy across lakes and warehouses</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Protect</td><td>IAM, policy tags, Sensitive Data Protection</td><td>Column-level access, masking, finding personal data</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Share</td><td>BigQuery sharing (formerly Analytics Hub)</td><td>Publish datasets to other projects or organisations without copying</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Serve: BI</td><td>Looker, Looker Studio</td><td>Governed metrics and dashboards</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Serve: ML/AI</td><td>BigQuery ML, Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform (formerly Vertex AI)</td><td>Models and embeddings close to the data (<a href="#ai">AI</a> tab)</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Reference architectures</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Pattern</th><th>Flow</th><th>Fits</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Batch ELT</td><td>Source → Storage Transfer / Cloud Run job → GCS (raw) → BigQuery load → dbt layers → marts → Looker / API</td><td>Daily or hourly data, files and exports, most analytics</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Streaming</td><td>Apps → Pub/Sub → Dataflow (parse, enrich, window) → BigQuery (+ GCS archive)</td><td>Seconds-level freshness, clickstreams, IoT, fraud signals</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Simple streaming</td><td>Apps → Pub/Sub → BigQuery subscription</td><td>Events that need no transformation before landing</td></tr>
      <tr><td>CDC replication</td><td>Postgres/MySQL → Datastream → BigQuery (merged or append) → dbt</td><td>Analytics on an operational database without load on it</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Lakehouse</td><td>GCS (Parquet/Iceberg) ← Dataproc Spark / Dataflow; Lakehouse (BigLake) tables → BigQuery SQL</td><td>Open formats shared by several engines</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Reverse ETL / serving</td><td>BigQuery mart → export or loader → Cloud SQL / AlloyDB → API</td><td>Serving curated data to an application with low latency</td></tr>
      <tr><td>RAG over documents</td><td>GCS docs → parse (Dataflow / Cloud Run) → chunks + embeddings (BigQuery ML / Agent Platform) → vector search → model</td><td>AI question answering over your corpus</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Choosing between similar services</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>SQL in BigQuery vs Dataflow:</b> if it can be expressed in SQL over data already in BigQuery, use SQL (dbt). Use Dataflow for streaming, non-SQL parsing, or work before data reaches BigQuery.</li>
      <li><b>Dataflow vs Dataproc:</b> Dataflow for new pipelines (serverless, Beam, streaming). Managed Service for Apache Spark (formerly Dataproc) for existing Spark code or Spark libraries.</li>
      <li><b>Cloud Run job vs Dataflow:</b> a job for simple, bounded work (copy files, call an API). Dataflow when data volume needs many workers and autoscaling.</li>
      <li><b>Managed Airflow (formerly Composer) vs Workflows:</b> managed Airflow for many pipelines with dependencies, backfills and sensors. Workflows plus Scheduler for a handful of steps; much cheaper to run.</li>
      <li><b>dbt vs Dataform:</b> both do SQL ELT with tests. Dataform is built into BigQuery; dbt has the larger ecosystem and runs on any warehouse.</li>
      <li><b>Pub/Sub vs Kafka:</b> Pub/Sub is serverless and global with nothing to size. Kafka gives log semantics, replay by offset, compaction and the Kafka ecosystem.</li>
      <li><b>Cloud SQL vs AlloyDB vs Spanner:</b> Cloud SQL for most Postgres needs; AlloyDB for heavier Postgres workloads and analytics; Spanner for global scale with strong consistency.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Handy commands</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># Pub/Sub straight into BigQuery
gcloud pubsub topics create order-events
gcloud pubsub subscriptions create order-to-bq \
  --topic=order-events \
  --bigquery-table=my-proj:raw.order_events \
  --use-table-schema

# Storage Transfer: one-off copy between buckets
gcloud transfer jobs create gs://source-bucket gs://my-landing

# Managed Service for Apache Spark, serverless (the CLI is still gcloud dataproc): run a PySpark job
gcloud dataproc batches submit pyspark job.py \
  --region=asia-south1 --deps-bucket=gs://my-deps

# Dataflow: list and inspect jobs
gcloud dataflow jobs list --region=asia-south1 --status=active
gcloud dataflow jobs describe JOB_ID --region=asia-south1

# Datastream streams
gcloud datastream streams list --location=asia-south1</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>IAM for data, in practice</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>One service account per pipeline component</b> (landing, load, transform, serve), each with only its roles.</li>
      <li><b>Dataset-level grants</b> rather than project-wide: <code>bigquery.dataEditor</code> on the datasets it writes, <code>dataViewer</code> on those it reads.</li>
      <li><b><code>bigquery.jobUser</code></b> on the project that pays for queries; this is separate from data access.</li>
      <li><b>Bucket-level roles</b> with uniform bucket-level access; <code>objectViewer</code> for readers, <code>objectAdmin</code> only for the writer.</li>
      <li><b>Policy tags</b> for sensitive columns; authorised views to share a subset.</li>
      <li><b>Serving databases</b> get a read-only user for the API.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Cost controls</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Partition and cluster; require partition filters on large tables.</li>
      <li>Set per-user and per-project custom query quotas, and <code>maximum_bytes_billed</code> on jobs.</li>
      <li>Export billing to BigQuery and build a cost dashboard by label.</li>
      <li>Label every resource with team, component and environment.</li>
      <li>GCS lifecycle rules to move old raw data to Nearline/Coldline/Archive.</li>
      <li>Stop or scale down non-production Cloud SQL, Dataproc and Composer when unused.</li>
      <li>Dataflow: right-size workers, use streaming engine, and set max workers.</li>
      <li>Keep development windows small: a few partitions, not the full history.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Data quality and governance on GCP</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>dbt tests</b> in the transformation layer for keys, relationships and accepted values.</li>
      <li><b>Knowledge Catalog (formerly Dataplex) data quality scans</b> for rules on tables, scheduled, with results in BigQuery.</li>
      <li><b>Knowledge Catalog data profiling</b> to see distributions, nulls and distinct counts.</li>
      <li><b>Lineage</b> from BigQuery jobs and Dataflow is recorded automatically in Knowledge Catalog once the Data Lineage API is enabled.</li>
      <li><b>Run records</b> of your own: every pipeline run writes what it read, wrote and rejected.</li>
      <li><b>Sensitive Data Protection</b> scans to find personal data you did not know you had.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Certification path</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Cloud Digital Leader:</b> broad, non-technical overview.</li>
      <li><b>Associate Cloud Engineer:</b> hands-on GCP basics: IAM, compute, networking, gcloud.</li>
      <li><b>Professional Data Engineer:</b> design, build and operate data systems on GCP. The exam tests choosing services for a scenario, which is exactly this tab.</li>
      <li><b>Professional Cloud Architect</b> next, if you design whole platforms.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">Certifications test breadth and judgement. Pair each with a real project built end to end.</p>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Name the GCP service for each lifecycle stage</li><li>Load files from GCS into BigQuery and query them</li><li>Navigate IAM roles for data access</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Build a batch ELT pipeline: landing, BigQuery layers, dbt, scheduling</li><li>Set up Pub/Sub to BigQuery, or Datastream CDC</li><li>Apply dataset-level IAM and cost controls</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Choose between Dataflow, Dataproc, SQL and Cloud Run for a workload</li><li>Design a platform with governance (Dataplex), lineage and quality</li><li>Plan cost, regions and data residency</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Own the organisation's GCP data architecture</li><li>Lead migrations from other clouds or on-premises</li><li>Hold the Professional Data Engineer or Architect level of judgement, and teach it</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>Dataflow streaming: windows, triggers, late data</li><li>BigLake and Iceberg on GCS</li><li>Dataplex data quality and lineage</li><li>BigQuery sharing (formerly Analytics Hub)</li><li>Professional Data Engineer exam guide</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://cloud.google.com/products/data-analytics" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google Cloud data analytics products</a><span class="lnote"> Overview of every data service</span></li><li><a href="https://cloud.google.com/dataflow/docs" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Dataflow documentation</a><span class="lnote"> Beam pipelines on GCP</span></li><li><a href="https://cloud.google.com/pubsub/docs" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Pub/Sub documentation</a><span class="lnote"> Messaging, including BigQuery subscriptions</span></li><li><a href="https://cloud.google.com/datastream/docs" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Datastream documentation</a><span class="lnote"> Change data capture</span></li><li><a href="https://cloud.google.com/dataproc/docs" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Dataproc documentation</a><span class="lnote"> Spark and Hadoop</span></li><li><a href="https://cloud.google.com/dataplex/docs" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Dataplex documentation</a><span class="lnote"> Governance, quality and lineage</span></li><li><a href="https://cloud.google.com/learn/certification/data-engineer" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Professional Data Engineer certification</a><span class="lnote"> Exam guide and learning path</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/sdk/gcloud/reference/pubsub/subscriptions/create" target="_blank" rel="noopener">gcloud pubsub subscriptions create --help (local)</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/dataplex/docs/introduction" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Knowledge Catalog, formerly Dataplex</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/dataplex/docs/about-data-lineage" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Data lineage</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/dataproc-serverless/docs/overview" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Managed Service for Apache Spark</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/biglake/docs/introduction" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Lakehouse, formerly BigLake</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/composer/docs" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Managed Service for Apache Airflow</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/vertex-ai-name-changes" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Vertex AI name changes</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/analytics-hub-introduction" target="_blank" rel="noopener">BigQuery sharing (formerly Analytics Hub)</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/datastream/docs/overview" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Datastream overview</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/dts-introduction" target="_blank" rel="noopener">BigQuery Data Transfer Service</a>; <a href="https://cloud.google.com/learn/certification/data-engineer" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Professional Data Engineer</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/dataflow/docs/guides/write-to-bigquery" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Dataflow: write to BigQuery</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/knowledge-catalog/docs/auto-data-quality-overview" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Knowledge Catalog: auto data quality</a>.</p><p class="runcode">A question, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/enrichmeai.github.io/issues/new?title=%5BData%20on%20GCP%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>. Nothing here is tracked and there are no comments to store: questions live in the open, where the next learner can find the answer.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= BIGQUERY ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-bigquery" data-name="BigQuery">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>BigQuery SQL</h2><p>GoogleSQL patterns, cost controls and the <code>bq</code> CLI. On-demand pricing bills bytes scanned, so most tips are about scanning less.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card"><h3>Partitioned, clustered table</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS `proj.analytics.orders` (
  order_id    STRING NOT NULL,
  order_ref   STRING,
  store_id    STRING,
  order_date  DATE,
  lines       ARRAY&lt;STRUCT&lt;sku STRING, qty INT64&gt;&gt;,
  _landed_at  TIMESTAMP
)
PARTITION BY DATE_TRUNC(order_date, MONTH)
CLUSTER BY store_id, order_ref
OPTIONS (
  require_partition_filter = TRUE,
  description = 'Orders, one row per order_id',
  labels = [('layer', 'analytics')]
);

-- integer range partition
PARTITION BY RANGE_BUCKET(order_year, GENERATE_ARRAY(1995, 2031, 1))
-- ingestion-time partition
PARTITION BY _PARTITIONDATE</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">Limit: 10,000 partitions per table. Daily partitions over 30 years (about 11,000 days) overflow it; use MONTH or YEAR.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>The table, drawn</h3>
    <figure class="fig"><svg viewBox="0 0 660 260" role="img" aria-labelledby="bqpart-t bqpart-d"><title id="bqpart-t">A partitioned, clustered table</title><desc id="bqpart-d">The orders table from the example, partitioned by month of order_date: January, February, March and so on. Inside each partition, rows are clustered, kept in order, by store_id then order_ref. A query that filters on order_date in March reads only the March partition; the others are not scanned. Because the table requires a partition filter, a query without one is refused.</desc><defs><marker id="bqpart-a" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker><marker id="bqpart-aa" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar acc" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker></defs><text class="lb hd" x="330" y="20" text-anchor="middle">proj.analytics.orders  PARTITION BY month  CLUSTER BY store_id, order_ref</text><rect class="nd" x="20" y="40" width="146" height="150" rx="6"/><text class="lb hd" x="93" y="60" text-anchor="middle">2026-01</text><rect class="nd" x="32" y="72" width="122" height="28" rx="3"/><text class="lb mu" x="93" y="91" text-anchor="middle">store S1 …</text><rect class="nd" x="32" y="108" width="122" height="28" rx="3"/><text class="lb mu" x="93" y="127" text-anchor="middle">store S2 …</text><rect class="nd" x="32" y="144" width="122" height="28" rx="3"/><text class="lb mu" x="93" y="163" text-anchor="middle">store S3 …</text><rect class="nd" x="178" y="40" width="146" height="150" rx="6"/><text class="lb hd" x="251" y="60" text-anchor="middle">2026-02</text><rect class="nd" x="190" y="72" width="122" height="28" rx="3"/><text class="lb mu" x="251" y="91" text-anchor="middle">store S1 …</text><rect class="nd" x="190" y="108" width="122" height="28" rx="3"/><text class="lb mu" x="251" y="127" text-anchor="middle">store S2 …</text><rect class="nd" x="190" y="144" width="122" height="28" rx="3"/><text class="lb mu" x="251" y="163" text-anchor="middle">store S3 …</text><rect class="nd in" x="336" y="40" width="146" height="150" rx="6"/><text class="lb hd" x="409" y="60" text-anchor="middle">2026-03</text><rect class="nd" x="348" y="72" width="122" height="28" rx="3"/><text class="lb mu" x="409" y="91" text-anchor="middle">store S1 …</text><rect class="nd" x="348" y="108" width="122" height="28" rx="3"/><text class="lb mu" x="409" y="127" text-anchor="middle">store S2 …</text><rect class="nd" x="348" y="144" width="122" height="28" rx="3"/><text class="lb mu" x="409" y="163" text-anchor="middle">store S3 …</text><rect class="nd" x="494" y="40" width="146" height="150" rx="6"/><text class="lb hd" x="567" y="60" text-anchor="middle">2026-04</text><rect class="nd" x="506" y="72" width="122" height="28" rx="3"/><text class="lb mu" x="567" y="91" text-anchor="middle">store S1 …</text><rect class="nd" x="506" y="108" width="122" height="28" rx="3"/><text class="lb mu" x="567" y="127" text-anchor="middle">store S2 …</text><rect class="nd" x="506" y="144" width="122" height="28" rx="3"/><text class="lb mu" x="567" y="163" text-anchor="middle">store S3 …</text><text class="lb acc" x="330" y="216" text-anchor="middle">WHERE order_date BETWEEN '2026-03-01' AND '2026-03-31': only March is scanned</text><text class="lb mu" x="330" y="240" text-anchor="middle">require_partition_filter = TRUE: a query with no date filter is refused</text></svg><figcaption>The example table above. On-demand pricing bills bytes scanned, and the partition filter is what keeps the scan to one month.</figcaption></figure>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Deduplicate with QUALIFY</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>-- latest version of each key
SELECT *
FROM `proj.raw.orders`
WHERE order_year = 2024
QUALIFY ROW_NUMBER() OVER (
  PARTITION BY store_id, order_ref
  ORDER BY _landed_at DESC
) = 1;

-- latest event per entity (append-only ledger → current state)
SELECT * EXCEPT(rn) FROM (
  SELECT *, ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY run_id, stage
                               ORDER BY event_ts DESC) rn
  FROM `proj.ops.job_run_events`)
WHERE rn = 1;

-- whole row of the max, no window
SELECT ARRAY_AGG(t ORDER BY order_date DESC LIMIT 1)[OFFSET(0)].*
FROM `proj.analytics.orders` t GROUP BY store_id;</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>MERGE (upsert)</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>MERGE `proj.mart.serving` T
USING (
  SELECT * FROM `proj.analytics.orders`
  WHERE order_date &gt;= DATE_SUB(CURRENT_DATE(), INTERVAL 7 DAY)
) S
ON T.order_id = S.order_id
   AND T.order_date &gt;= DATE_SUB(CURRENT_DATE(), INTERVAL 7 DAY)  -- prune T
WHEN MATCHED AND T.row_hash != S.row_hash THEN
  UPDATE SET order_ref = S.order_ref, order_date = S.order_date, row_hash = S.row_hash
WHEN NOT MATCHED BY TARGET THEN
  INSERT ROW
WHEN NOT MATCHED BY SOURCE AND T.order_date &gt;= DATE_SUB(CURRENT_DATE(), INTERVAL 7 DAY) THEN
  DELETE;

-- change detection hash
TO_HEX(SHA256(TO_JSON_STRING(STRUCT(order_ref, store_id, order_date)))) AS row_hash
FARM_FINGERPRINT(CONCAT(store_id, '|', order_ref))   -- INT64 key</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Dates &amp; safe parsing</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>PARSE_DATE('%d-%m-%Y', '05-05-1959')          -- errors on bad input
SAFE.PARSE_DATE('%d-%m-%Y', raw)               -- NULL on bad input
SAFE_CAST(order_seq AS INT64)

-- never let a parse failure hide as a NULL: count it
SELECT COUNTIF(raw IS NOT NULL AND SAFE.PARSE_DATE('%d-%m-%Y', raw) IS NULL) AS unparsed
FROM t;

DATE_TRUNC(d, MONTH)   DATE_ADD(d, INTERVAL 1 DAY)   DATE_DIFF(a, b, DAY)
LAST_DAY(d, MONTH)     EXTRACT(YEAR FROM d)          FORMAT_DATE('%Y%m%d', d)
TIMESTAMP_TRUNC(ts, HOUR)  DATETIME(ts, 'Asia/Kolkata')
CURRENT_DATE('Asia/Kolkata')
UNIX_MILLIS(ts)  TIMESTAMP_MICROS(n)

-- calendar spine for gap finding
SELECT d FROM UNNEST(GENERATE_DATE_ARRAY('2025-01-01', CURRENT_DATE())) d
LEFT JOIN facts f ON f.dt = d WHERE f.dt IS NULL;</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>ARRAY, STRUCT, UNNEST, JSON</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>-- explode
SELECT o.order_ref, i.sku, i.qty
FROM `proj.analytics.orders` o, UNNEST(o.lines) i;      -- comma = CROSS JOIN
-- keep rows with empty arrays
LEFT JOIN UNNEST(o.lines) i

-- build
SELECT order_ref, ARRAY_AGG(STRUCT(sku, qty) ORDER BY sku) lines
FROM order_lines GROUP BY order_ref;

ARRAY_LENGTH(a)  a[SAFE_OFFSET(0)]  ARRAY_TO_STRING(a, ',')
'x' IN UNNEST(a)  EXISTS(SELECT 1 FROM UNNEST(a) v WHERE v &gt; 3)

-- JSON
JSON_VALUE(payload, '$.store.code')            -- scalar as STRING
JSON_QUERY(payload, '$.lines')                  -- JSON fragment
JSON_QUERY_ARRAY(payload, '$.lines')            -- ARRAY&lt;JSON&gt; on a JSON column, ARRAY&lt;STRING&gt; on a STRING
LAX_INT64(payload.count)                       -- on a JSON column
TO_JSON_STRING(STRUCT(a, b))
PARSE_JSON(s)</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Window functions</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>SELECT
  store, order_date, n,
  SUM(n) OVER (PARTITION BY store ORDER BY order_date)          AS running,
  AVG(n) OVER (PARTITION BY store ORDER BY order_date
               ROWS BETWEEN 6 PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW)           AS avg_7d,
  LAG(n) OVER (PARTITION BY store ORDER BY order_date)          AS prev,
  n / SUM(n) OVER (PARTITION BY store)                             AS share,
  RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY store ORDER BY n DESC)                 AS rnk,
  NTILE(4) OVER (ORDER BY n)                                       AS quartile
FROM daily;

-- named window
SELECT SUM(n) OVER w, MAX(n) OVER w FROM daily
WINDOW w AS (PARTITION BY store ORDER BY order_date);

APPROX_COUNT_DISTINCT(order_ref)   APPROX_QUANTILES(n, 100)[OFFSET(50)]</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Load, export, external</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>LOAD DATA INTO `proj.raw.orders`
FROM FILES (format = 'PARQUET',
            uris = ['gs://landing/data/year=2024/*.parquet']);

EXPORT DATA OPTIONS (
  uri = 'gs://exports/serving/2025-01-31/*.parquet',   -- one * required
  format = 'PARQUET', overwrite = TRUE, compression = 'SNAPPY'
) AS SELECT * FROM `proj.mart.serving` WHERE _run_date = '2025-01-31';

CREATE OR REPLACE EXTERNAL TABLE `proj.raw.orders_ext`
WITH PARTITION COLUMNS
OPTIONS (format = 'PARQUET',
         uris = ['gs://landing/data/*'],
         hive_partition_uri_prefix = 'gs://landing/data');</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Scripting &amp; procedures</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>DECLARE run_date DATE DEFAULT CURRENT_DATE();
DECLARE n INT64;

SET n = (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM `proj.analytics.orders` WHERE _run_date = run_date);
IF n = 0 THEN
  RAISE USING MESSAGE = FORMAT('no rows for %t', run_date);
END IF;

BEGIN TRANSACTION;
  DELETE FROM `proj.mart.serving` WHERE _run_date = run_date;
  INSERT INTO `proj.mart.serving` SELECT * FROM staging;
COMMIT TRANSACTION;

EXECUTE IMMEDIATE FORMAT('SELECT COUNT(*) FROM `proj.%s.t`', ds);

CREATE OR REPLACE PROCEDURE `proj.ops.rebuild`(d DATE)
BEGIN ... END;
CALL `proj.ops.rebuild`(DATE '2025-01-31');</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Pipe syntax</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>FROM `proj.analytics.orders`
|&gt; WHERE order_date &gt;= '2024-01-01'
|&gt; AGGREGATE COUNT(*) AS n GROUP BY store_id
|&gt; ORDER BY n DESC
|&gt; LIMIT 10;

-- other operators: SELECT, EXTEND (add columns), SET, DROP, RENAME,
-- JOIN, AS (alias), PIVOT, UNPIVOT, TABLESAMPLE, CALL</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">Reads top to bottom in execution order. Mixes freely with standard SQL in the same project.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Metadata &amp; cost queries</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>-- most expensive queries in the last day
SELECT user_email, job_id,
       ROUND(total_bytes_billed / POW(1024, 4), 3) AS tib_billed,
       total_slot_ms, LEFT(query, 120) AS q
FROM `region-asia-south1`.INFORMATION_SCHEMA.JOBS
WHERE creation_time &gt; TIMESTAMP_SUB(CURRENT_TIMESTAMP(), INTERVAL 1 DAY)
  AND job_type = 'QUERY'
ORDER BY total_bytes_billed DESC LIMIT 20;

-- partitions and their row counts
SELECT partition_id, total_rows, total_logical_bytes
FROM `proj.analytics.INFORMATION_SCHEMA.PARTITIONS`
WHERE table_name = 'orders' ORDER BY partition_id;

-- columns of a table
SELECT column_name, data_type, is_nullable
FROM `proj.analytics.INFORMATION_SCHEMA.COLUMNS` WHERE table_name = 'orders';

-- table sizes
SELECT table_id, row_count, size_bytes / 1e9 AS gb
FROM `proj.analytics.__TABLES__` ORDER BY size_bytes DESC;</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Time travel, snapshots, wildcards</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>-- read the table as it was (up to 7 days back by default)
SELECT * FROM `proj.analytics.orders`
FOR SYSTEM_TIME AS OF TIMESTAMP_SUB(CURRENT_TIMESTAMP(), INTERVAL 1 HOUR);

-- undo a bad write
CREATE OR REPLACE TABLE `proj.analytics.orders_restore` AS
SELECT * FROM `proj.analytics.orders` FOR SYSTEM_TIME AS OF '2025-01-31 06:00:00+00';

-- zero-copy snapshot / clone
CREATE SNAPSHOT TABLE `proj.backup.orders_20250131` CLONE `proj.analytics.orders`;
CREATE TABLE `proj.dev.orders` CLONE `proj.analytics.orders`;

-- sharded tables
SELECT * FROM `proj.logs.events_*` WHERE _TABLE_SUFFIX BETWEEN '20250101' AND '20250131';</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Cost &amp; performance rules</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Name columns.</b> Storage is columnar; <code>SELECT *</code> bills every column. <code>SELECT * EXCEPT(raw_html)</code> drops wide ones.</li>
      <li><b>Filter on the partition column</b> with a constant or parameter. A filter through a function of another table's subquery may not prune.</li>
      <li><b><code>LIMIT</code> does not reduce bytes</b> on a regular table. Use table preview or <code>TABLESAMPLE SYSTEM (1 PERCENT)</code>.</li>
      <li><b>Dry-run first</b> and set <code>maximum_bytes_billed</code> on jobs that could run away.</li>
      <li><b>Cluster</b> on high-cardinality filter/join columns; order matters, first column filters best.</li>
      <li><b>Join big to small</b> after filtering; pre-aggregate before joining.</li>
      <li><b>Avoid row-by-row DML.</b> Batch into one MERGE: each statement is a query you pay for, and only two UPDATE, DELETE or MERGE statements per table run at once (20 more can queue; beyond that they fail).</li>
      <li><b>Materialised views</b> for repeated aggregates; BI Engine for dashboards.</li>
      <li><b>Execution graph</b> in the console shows skew, spills and which stage costs the slots.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>bq CLI</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>bq query --use_legacy_sql=false --dry_run 'SELECT ...'
bq query --use_legacy_sql=false --format=prettyjson --max_rows=20 \
  --parameter=from_d:DATE:2024-01-01 \
  'SELECT COUNT(*) FROM `proj.analytics.orders` WHERE order_date &gt;= @from_d'
bq query --nouse_legacy_sql --maximum_bytes_billed=10000000000 &lt; q.sql

bq ls proj:analytics
bq show --schema --format=prettyjson proj:analytics.orders
bq head -n 10 proj:analytics.orders

bq load --source_format=PARQUET --replace \
  'proj:raw.events$20250131' 'gs://landing/events/dt=2025-01-31/*.parquet'   # one partition
bq extract --destination_format=PARQUET proj:mart.serving 'gs://exp/serving-*.parquet'

bq cp proj:analytics.orders proj:backup.orders_20250131
bq mk --table --time_partitioning_field=order_date --clustering_fields=store_id \
  proj:analytics.t schema.json
bq ls -j -a -n 20          # recent jobs
bq show -j &lt;job_id&gt;</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Query with named columns and partition filters</li><li>Dry-run and read bytes processed</li><li>Load data from GCS</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Design partitioned and clustered tables</li><li>Write MERGE, scripting and parameterised queries</li><li>Use INFORMATION_SCHEMA to find cost and usage</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Optimise slot time and cost across a project</li><li>Choose pricing models and reservations</li><li>Design security: datasets, authorised views, row and column policies</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Own the warehouse platform and its cost governance</li><li>Integrate BigQuery with streaming, ML and the wider lakehouse</li><li>Advise on architecture across many teams</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>Storage Write API and streaming</li><li>Editions, reservations and autoscaling</li><li>Row-level security and policy tags</li><li>BigLake and external tables</li><li>BigQuery ML and vector search</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs" target="_blank" rel="noopener">BigQuery documentation</a><span class="lnote"> Official docs</span></li><li><a href="https://cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/best-practices-performance-overview" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Query performance best practices</a><span class="lnote"> Reduce bytes and slot time</span></li><li><a href="https://cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/reference/standard-sql/functions-and-operators" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Functions and operators</a><span class="lnote"> Every GoogleSQL function</span></li><li><a href="https://cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/partitioned-tables" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Partitioned tables</a><span class="lnote"> Types, limits and pruning</span></li><li><a href="https://cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/information-schema-intro" target="_blank" rel="noopener">INFORMATION_SCHEMA</a><span class="lnote"> Metadata and job history views</span></li><li><a href="https://cloud.google.com/bigquery/pricing" target="_blank" rel="noopener">BigQuery pricing</a><span class="lnote"> On-demand and capacity pricing</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/bigquery/quotas" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Quotas and limits (10,000 partitions; DML)</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/time-travel" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Time travel (7 days default)</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/reference/standard-sql/other-statements" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Other statements (EXPORT DATA)</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/reference/standard-sql/pipe-syntax" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Pipe syntax</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/reference/standard-sql/json_functions" target="_blank" rel="noopener">JSON functions</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/reference/standard-sql/data-definition-language" target="_blank" rel="noopener">DDL (partitioning, snapshots, external tables)</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/biglake/docs/introduction" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Lakehouse, formerly BigLake</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/reference/bq-cli-reference" target="_blank" rel="noopener">bq help (local CLI)</a>; <a href="https://cloud.google.com/bigquery/pricing" target="_blank" rel="noopener">BigQuery pricing</a>. A few claims could not be confirmed at their official source from here and are being re-checked.</p><p class="runcode">A question, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/enrichmeai.github.io/issues/new?title=%5BBigQuery%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>. Nothing here is tracked and there are no comments to store: questions live in the open, where the next learner can find the answer.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= CLOUD RUN ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-cloudrun" data-name="Cloud Run">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Cloud Run</h2><p>Serverless containers on GCP: services that answer requests, and jobs that run to completion. Deploy, scale, secure, connect and debug.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Services, jobs, worker pools</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Kind</th><th>Runs when</th><th>Use it for</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Service</td><td>A request arrives (HTTP, gRPC, WebSocket, Pub/Sub push, Eventarc)</td><td>APIs, web apps, webhooks. Scales on traffic, down to zero.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Job</td><td>Someone executes it (CLI, API, Cloud Scheduler, Workflows)</td><td>Batch loads, migrations, nightly exports. Runs N tasks in parallel, then exits.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Worker pool</td><td>Always, with no HTTP endpoint</td><td>Pull-based consumers (Kafka, Pub/Sub pull). Generally available since April 2026; some options are still in Preview.</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Container contract</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Listen on <code>$PORT</code></b> (default 8080) on <code>0.0.0.0</code>, not <code>127.0.0.1</code>. Jobs do not listen at all.</li>
      <li><b>Stateless.</b> Any instance can serve any request; instances come and go.</li>
      <li><b>The filesystem is in memory</b> and counts against the memory limit. Write big files to GCS or a mounted volume.</li>
      <li><b>Shutdown:</b> the container gets <code>SIGTERM</code>, then about 10 seconds before <code>SIGKILL</code>. Flush and close there.</li>
      <li><b>Logs:</b> anything written to stdout/stderr goes to Cloud Logging.</li>
      <li><b>Jobs:</b> exit code 0 means the task succeeded; anything else fails it (and retries it if <code>max-retries</code> &gt; 0).</li>
      <li><b>Platform:</b> build images for <code>linux/amd64</code>. An arm64 image from a Mac will not start.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Deploy a service</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>gcloud run deploy api \
  --image=asia-south1-docker.pkg.dev/my-proj/apps/api:3f2a9c1 \
  --region=asia-south1 \
  --service-account=api@my-proj.iam.gserviceaccount.com \
  --no-allow-unauthenticated \
  --cpu=1 --memory=1Gi \
  --concurrency=80 --min-instances=0 --max-instances=10 \
  --timeout=300 \
  --cpu-boost \
  --set-env-vars=APP_PROFILE=prod,DATASET=analytics \
  --set-secrets=DB_PASSWORD=db-password:3 \
  --labels=env=prod,team=data

# build from source with Cloud Build + buildpacks / Dockerfile
gcloud run deploy api --source . --region=asia-south1

gcloud run services describe api --region=asia-south1 --format='value(status.url)'
gcloud run services list</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note"><code>--set-env-vars</code> replaces all env vars; <code>--update-env-vars</code> adds or changes only the ones named.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Revisions &amp; traffic</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># deploy without sending traffic; reach it at a tagged URL
gcloud run deploy api --image=…:4b1c2d0 --no-traffic --tag=canary
# → https://canary---api-xxxxx.a.run.app

# shift 10% to the new revision
gcloud run services update-traffic api \
  --to-revisions=api-00042-xyz=10,api-00041-abc=90

gcloud run services update-traffic api --to-latest     # 100% to newest
gcloud run services update-traffic api --to-tags=canary=100

# roll back = send traffic to the previous revision
gcloud run revisions list --service=api --region=asia-south1
gcloud run services update-traffic api --to-revisions=api-00041-abc=100</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">Every deploy or config change creates a new immutable revision. Settings live on the revision, so rolling back traffic also rolls back env vars and secrets.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Traffic, drawn</h3>
    <figure class="fig"><svg viewBox="0 0 660 250" role="img" aria-labelledby="crtraffic-t crtraffic-d"><title id="crtraffic-t">Cloud Run revisions and traffic splitting</title><desc id="crtraffic-d">Three steps. Deploy with no traffic: revision 42 exists and is reachable only at its canary tag URL, while revision 41 takes all traffic. Shift 10 percent: revision 42 takes 10 percent and revision 41 90 percent. Roll back: send 100 percent to revision 41 again. Because settings live on the revision, the rollback also restores its environment variables and secrets.</desc><defs><marker id="crtraffic-a" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker><marker id="crtraffic-aa" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar acc" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker></defs><rect class="bd" x="10" y="20" width="204" height="200" rx="8"/><text class="lb hd" x="112" y="42" text-anchor="middle">1 deploy --no-traffic</text><rect class="nd out" x="24" y="62" width="176" height="44" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="34" y="89" text-anchor="start">api-00041</text><text class="lb acc" x="190" y="89" text-anchor="end">100%</text><rect class="nd" x="24" y="122" width="176" height="44" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="34" y="149" text-anchor="start">api-00042</text><text class="lb mu" x="190" y="149" text-anchor="end">0%</text><text class="lb mu" x="112" y="200" text-anchor="middle">canary tag URL only</text><rect class="bd" x="228" y="20" width="204" height="200" rx="8"/><text class="lb hd" x="330" y="42" text-anchor="middle">2 update-traffic 90 / 10</text><rect class="nd out" x="242" y="62" width="176" height="44" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="252" y="89" text-anchor="start">api-00041</text><text class="lb acc" x="408" y="89" text-anchor="end">90%</text><rect class="nd in" x="242" y="122" width="176" height="44" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="252" y="149" text-anchor="start">api-00042</text><text class="lb acc" x="408" y="149" text-anchor="end">10%</text><text class="lb mu" x="330" y="200" text-anchor="middle">watch errors and latency</text><rect class="bd" x="446" y="20" width="204" height="200" rx="8"/><text class="lb hd" x="548" y="42" text-anchor="middle">3 roll back: 41 = 100</text><rect class="nd out" x="460" y="62" width="176" height="44" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="470" y="89" text-anchor="start">api-00041</text><text class="lb acc" x="626" y="89" text-anchor="end">100%</text><rect class="nd" x="460" y="122" width="176" height="44" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="470" y="149" text-anchor="start">api-00042</text><text class="lb mu" x="626" y="149" text-anchor="end">0%</text><text class="lb mu" x="548" y="200" text-anchor="middle">env vars and secrets too</text></svg><figcaption>The commands above as three moments. A revision never changes, so moving traffic between revisions is how you release and how you roll back.</figcaption></figure>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Jobs</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>gcloud run jobs deploy loader \
  --image=asia-south1-docker.pkg.dev/my-proj/apps/loader:3f2a9c1 \
  --region=asia-south1 \
  --service-account=loader@my-proj.iam.gserviceaccount.com \
  --tasks=10 --parallelism=5 \
  --task-timeout=3600s --max-retries=0 \
  --cpu=2 --memory=4Gi \
  --args=--year=2024

gcloud run jobs execute loader --region=asia-south1 --wait
gcloud run jobs execute loader --wait \
  --args=--year=2023 --update-env-vars=DRY_RUN=true   # overrides for this run only

gcloud run jobs executions list --job=loader --region=asia-south1
gcloud run jobs executions describe loader-abc12 --region=asia-south1
gcloud run jobs executions cancel loader-abc12 --region=asia-south1</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">An execution's status is Cloud Run's word on whether the container exited 0. Whether it did the right work is your job to record, e.g. a summary row or log line per run.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Shard work across job tasks</h3>
    <p>Every task gets these environment variables:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><code>CLOUD_RUN_TASK_INDEX</code>: 0 … tasks − 1</li>
      <li><code>CLOUD_RUN_TASK_COUNT</code>: the <code>--tasks</code> value</li>
      <li><code>CLOUD_RUN_TASK_ATTEMPT</code>: 0 on the first try</li>
      <li><code>CLOUD_RUN_EXECUTION</code>, <code>CLOUD_RUN_JOB</code>: names for logs and run records</li>
    </ul>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># Python
import os
i = int(os.environ.get("CLOUD_RUN_TASK_INDEX", 0))
n = int(os.environ.get("CLOUD_RUN_TASK_COUNT", 1))
mine = [u for k, u in enumerate(sorted(all_uris)) if k % n == i]

// Java
int i = Integer.parseInt(System.getenv().getOrDefault("CLOUD_RUN_TASK_INDEX", "0"));
int n = Integer.parseInt(System.getenv().getOrDefault("CLOUD_RUN_TASK_COUNT", "1"));
List&lt;String&gt; mine = IntStream.range(0, uris.size())
    .filter(k -&gt; k % n == i).mapToObj(uris::get).toList();</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">Sort the input first so every task sees the same order. Make each task idempotent: a retry repeats the whole shard.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Scaling &amp; cost</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Concurrency</b> (default 80 per vCPU when deployed with gcloud or Terraform, 80 from the console; max 1000): requests one instance handles at once. Set 1 only for code that is not thread-safe.</li>
      <li><b>Min instances</b> keep warm instances to avoid cold starts; they bill while idle.</li>
      <li><b>Max instances</b> cap spend and protect downstream databases (connections = instances × pool size).</li>
      <li><b>CPU allocation:</b> by default CPU is only allocated during requests, so background threads stall after the response. <code>--no-cpu-throttling</code> keeps CPU on (instance-based billing).</li>
      <li><b>Startup CPU boost</b> (<code>--cpu-boost</code>) gives extra CPU while the container starts: helps JVM cold starts.</li>
      <li><b>Cold start cuts:</b> smaller images, lazy initialisation, CDS/AOT for Java, Quarkus or Spring native images.</li>
      <li><b>Jobs</b> bill for the time each task runs, at the CPU and memory you asked for.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Calling private services</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># grant the caller the invoker role
gcloud run services add-iam-policy-binding api --region=asia-south1 \
  --member=serviceAccount:caller@my-proj.iam.gserviceaccount.com \
  --role=roles/run.invoker

# from your terminal
curl -H "Authorization: Bearer $(gcloud auth print-identity-token)" https://api-xxxxx.a.run.app/health
gcloud run services proxy api --region=asia-south1 --port=8080   # local tunnel</code></pre></div>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># Python: ID token whose audience is the service URL
import google.auth.transport.requests, google.oauth2.id_token, requests
aud = "https://api-xxxxx.a.run.app"
tok = google.oauth2.id_token.fetch_id_token(google.auth.transport.requests.Request(), aud)
requests.get(f"{aud}/health", headers={"Authorization": f"Bearer {tok}"})

// Java (google-auth-library)
IdTokenCredentials creds = IdTokenCredentials.newBuilder()
    .setIdTokenProvider((IdTokenProvider) GoogleCredentials.getApplicationDefault())
    .setTargetAudience(aud).build();
creds.refresh();
String token = creds.getIdToken().getTokenValue();</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">Services need an ID token (audience = URL). Google APIs, including the Cloud Run Admin API that executes a job, need an access token.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Networking</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># who may reach the service
--ingress=all                                  # internet (still IAM-checked)
--ingress=internal                             # VPC and some Google sources only
--ingress=internal-and-cloud-load-balancing    # put a load balancer / Cloud Armor in front

# reach private IPs (Memorystore, internal services) with Direct VPC egress
--network=main --subnet=run-egress --vpc-egress=private-ranges-only
--vpc-egress=all-traffic        # everything via VPC → Cloud NAT gives a static egress IP

# Cloud SQL via the built-in connector: unix socket in the container
--add-cloudsql-instances=my-proj:asia-south1:shop
# JDBC: jdbc:postgresql:///shop?cloudSqlInstance=my-proj:asia-south1:shop&amp;socketFactory=com.google.cloud.sql.postgres.SocketFactory
# psycopg: host=/cloudsql/my-proj:asia-south1:shop</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">A third party that allowlists IPs needs <code>all-traffic</code> egress through Cloud NAT with a reserved address. Without it, egress comes from shared Google ranges.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Secrets, config, volumes</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># secret as env var: read once, when the instance starts
--set-secrets=DB_PASSWORD=db-password:3

# secret as a file: read when your code opens it
--set-secrets=/secrets/sa.json=sa-key:latest

# the runtime service account needs to read it
gcloud secrets add-iam-policy-binding db-password \
  --member=serviceAccount:api@my-proj.iam.gserviceaccount.com \
  --role=roles/secretmanager.secretAccessor

# GCS bucket or in-memory volume
--add-volume=name=data,type=cloud-storage,bucket=my-landing \
--add-volume-mount=volume=data,mount-path=/data
--add-volume=name=scratch,type=in-memory,size-limit=1Gi</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">Pin secret versions (<code>:3</code>) in prod so a rotation is a deliberate deploy. With <code>:latest</code> in an env var, running instances keep the old value until they restart.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Triggers</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Cloud Scheduler → job:</b> POST to <code>https://run.googleapis.com/v2/projects/P/locations/R/jobs/J:run</code> with an OAuth token (see the Terraform tab).</li>
      <li><b>Cloud Scheduler → service:</b> HTTP target with an OIDC token whose audience is the service URL.</li>
      <li><b>Pub/Sub push:</b> subscription pushes to the service URL with an OIDC token; return 2xx to ack, anything else retries.</li>
      <li><b>Eventarc:</b> route events (GCS object finalised, BigQuery job done, audit log entries) to a service.</li>
      <li><b>Cloud Tasks:</b> queued HTTP calls with rate limits and retries.</li>
      <li><b>Workflows:</b> run jobs in sequence, wait for each, branch on results.</li>
    </ul>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>gcloud eventarc triggers create on-landing --location=asia-south1 \
  --destination-run-service=ingest --destination-run-region=asia-south1 \
  --event-filters=type=google.cloud.storage.object.v1.finalized \
  --event-filters=bucket=my-landing \
  --service-account=eventarc@my-proj.iam.gserviceaccount.com</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Dockerfiles that start fast</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># Java (Spring Boot jar)
FROM eclipse-temurin:21-jdk AS build
WORKDIR /src
COPY . .
RUN ./gradlew bootJar -x test

FROM eclipse-temurin:21-jre
RUN useradd -u 1001 app
USER 1001
COPY --from=build /src/build/libs/*.jar /app.jar
ENV JAVA_TOOL_OPTIONS="-XX:MaxRAMPercentage=75 -XX:+UseSerialGC"
ENTRYPOINT ["java", "-jar", "/app.jar"]</code></pre></div>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># Python
FROM python:3.12-slim
ENV PYTHONUNBUFFERED=1 PIP_NO_CACHE_DIR=1
WORKDIR /app
COPY requirements.txt .
RUN pip install -r requirements.txt
COPY . .
USER 1001
CMD exec gunicorn --bind :$PORT --workers 1 --threads 8 --timeout 0 app:app</code></pre></div>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>docker buildx build --platform linux/amd64 -t asia-south1-docker.pkg.dev/my-proj/apps/api:$(git rev-parse --short=12 HEAD) --push .</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">Use the exec form (<code>["java", …]</code> or <code>exec</code>) so your process is PID 1 and receives <code>SIGTERM</code>.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Logging &amp; health</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># one JSON object per line on stdout → structured log entry
{"severity":"ERROR","message":"load failed","uri":"gs://…","run_id":"r-42",
 "logging.googleapis.com/trace":"projects/my-proj/traces/abc123"}

gcloud logging read 'resource.type="cloud_run_revision"
  AND resource.labels.service_name="api" AND severity&gt;=ERROR' --freshness=1h --limit=50
gcloud logging read 'resource.type="cloud_run_job"
  AND resource.labels.job_name="loader" AND severity&gt;=ERROR' --freshness=1d --limit=20</code></pre></div>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># service.yaml fragment: probes
spec:
  template:
    spec:
      containers:
        - image: …
          startupProbe:
            httpGet: { path: /health/started, port: 8080 }
            failureThreshold: 30
            periodSeconds: 2
          livenessProbe:
            httpGet: { path: /health/live, port: 8080 }</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">Without <code>severity</code>, every line is logged at DEFAULT, and severity-based alerts never fire.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Declarative YAML</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>gcloud run services describe api --region=asia-south1 --format=export &gt; service.yaml
# edit, then:
gcloud run services replace service.yaml --region=asia-south1

gcloud run jobs describe loader --region=asia-south1 --format=export &gt; job.yaml
gcloud run jobs replace job.yaml --region=asia-south1</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">Service YAML follows the Knative Serving schema; job YAML uses Cloud Run's own <code>run.googleapis.com/v1</code> Job kind. In a team, keep the definition in Terraform (<code>google_cloud_run_v2_service</code> / <code>_job</code>) and use the CLI for reading and one-off executions.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Errors and what they usually mean</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Usually means</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>"Container failed to start and listen on the port defined by PORT"</td><td>App listens on the wrong port or on 127.0.0.1, crashes on boot (missing env var or secret), or the image is arm64. Read the revision's logs.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>403 calling a service</td><td>Caller lacks <code>roles/run.invoker</code>, sent an access token instead of an ID token, or the token's audience is not the service URL.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>404 calling a service</td><td>Wrong URL or region, or ingress is <code>internal</code> and you called from outside the VPC.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>429 / "no available instance"</td><td><code>max-instances</code> reached with all instances at full concurrency.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>503 with "Memory limit exceeded" in logs</td><td>OOM: raise <code>--memory</code>, or stop writing large files to the in-memory filesystem.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>504 / request cancelled</td><td>Request ran past <code>--timeout</code>.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Permission denied on secret</td><td>Runtime service account lacks <code>secretmanager.secretAccessor</code> on that secret.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Image pull denied</td><td>The Cloud Run service agent cannot read the Artifact Registry repo (common across projects).</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Job execution FAILED, logs empty</td><td>Task timed out, was OOM-killed, or crashed before logging. <code>executions describe</code> shows the reason per task.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Background work never finishes</td><td>CPU is throttled after the response. Finish before responding, use <code>--no-cpu-throttling</code>, or hand the work to a job or Cloud Tasks.</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Deploy a service and read its logs</li><li>Execute a job and check its result</li><li>Roll traffic back to a previous revision</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Set scaling, CPU, memory, concurrency and timeouts deliberately</li><li>Secure services with IAM, secrets and service accounts</li><li>Connect to private resources with VPC egress</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Design job sharding, retries and run records</li><li>Tune cold starts and cost</li><li>Design service-to-service auth and traffic management</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Set the serverless platform blueprint for the organisation</li><li>Decide Cloud Run vs GKE vs other compute</li><li>Design multi-region serverless architectures</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>Container runtime contract</li><li>Direct VPC egress and Cloud NAT</li><li>Startup CPU boost and minimum instances</li><li>Cloud Run jobs with Workflows</li><li>Eventarc triggers</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://cloud.google.com/run/docs" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cloud Run documentation</a><span class="lnote"> Official docs</span></li><li><a href="https://cloud.google.com/run/docs/container-contract" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Container runtime contract</a><span class="lnote"> What your container must do</span></li><li><a href="https://cloud.google.com/run/docs/create-jobs" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cloud Run jobs</a><span class="lnote"> Create and execute jobs</span></li><li><a href="https://cloud.google.com/run/docs/tips/general" target="_blank" rel="noopener">General development tips</a><span class="lnote"> Performance and cost tips</span></li><li><a href="https://cloud.google.com/run/quotas" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Quotas and limits</a><span class="lnote"> Memory, CPU, timeouts, request size</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/run/docs/container-contract" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Container runtime contract</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/run/docs/about-concurrency" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Maximum concurrent requests</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/run/docs/troubleshooting" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Troubleshooting (404 ingress, 429)</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/run/docs/execute/jobs" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Execute jobs (per-execution overrides)</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/run/docs/execute/jobs-on-schedule" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Execute jobs on a schedule (v2 :run URI)</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/run/docs/configuring/billing-settings" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Billing settings</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/run/docs/reference/yaml/v1" target="_blank" rel="noopener">YAML reference</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/logging/docs/reference/v2/rest/v2/LogEntry" target="_blank" rel="noopener">LogEntry severity DEFAULT</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/sdk/gcloud/reference/run/deploy" target="_blank" rel="noopener">gcloud run deploy --help (local)</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/run/docs/release-notes" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cloud Run release notes</a>; <a href="https://docs.spring.io/spring-boot/reference/packaging/container-images/dockerfiles.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Spring Boot: Dockerfiles</a>. A few claims could not be confirmed at their official source from here and are being re-checked.</p><p class="runcode">A question, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/enrichmeai.github.io/issues/new?title=%5BCloud%20Run%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>. Nothing here is tracked and there are no comments to store: questions live in the open, where the next learner can find the answer.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= APIGEE ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-apigee" data-name="Apigee">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Apigee</h2><p>Google Cloud's API management platform: a gateway plus API products, developer apps, quotas, security policies, analytics and a developer portal. Background on gateways is on <a href="#apis">APIs &amp; Integration</a>.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Core concepts</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Concept</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Organisation</td><td>Top-level Apigee container, tied to a GCP project</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Environment</td><td>A runtime context where proxies are deployed (dev, test, prod)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Environment group</td><td>Hostnames that route to one or more environments</td></tr>
      <tr><td>API proxy</td><td>The façade clients call; it applies policies and forwards to the backend</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Proxy endpoint</td><td>Client-facing side: base path, request and response flows</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Target endpoint</td><td>Backend-facing side: the service URL, its auth and timeouts</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Flow</td><td>Ordered steps: PreFlow, conditional flows, PostFlow, on request and response</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Policy</td><td>A reusable step: verify a key, check a token, rate-limit, transform, cache</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Shared flow</td><td>A reusable sequence of policies used by many proxies (security, logging)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>API product</td><td>A bundle of proxies and operations with quotas and scopes, offered to consumers</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Developer / app</td><td>A consumer and their registered app, which gets keys for products</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note">Apigee X is the Google Cloud-hosted version; Apigee hybrid runs the runtime in your own Kubernetes clusters. Apigee Edge is the older generation.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Proxy endpoint</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>&lt;!-- apiproxy/proxies/default.xml --&gt;
&lt;ProxyEndpoint name="default"&gt;
  &lt;PreFlow name="PreFlow"&gt;
    &lt;Request&gt;
      &lt;Step&gt;&lt;Name&gt;VK-VerifyKey&lt;/Name&gt;&lt;/Step&gt;
      &lt;Step&gt;&lt;Name&gt;SA-SpikeArrest&lt;/Name&gt;&lt;/Step&gt;
      &lt;Step&gt;&lt;Name&gt;Q-PerApp&lt;/Name&gt;&lt;/Step&gt;
    &lt;/Request&gt;
  &lt;/PreFlow&gt;
  &lt;Flows&gt;
    &lt;Flow name="search"&gt;
      &lt;Condition&gt;proxy.pathsuffix MatchesPath "/search" and request.verb = "GET"&lt;/Condition&gt;
    &lt;/Flow&gt;
  &lt;/Flows&gt;
  &lt;HTTPProxyConnection&gt;
    &lt;BasePath&gt;/shop/v1&lt;/BasePath&gt;
  &lt;/HTTPProxyConnection&gt;
  &lt;RouteRule name="default"&gt;
    &lt;TargetEndpoint&gt;default&lt;/TargetEndpoint&gt;
  &lt;/RouteRule&gt;
&lt;/ProxyEndpoint&gt;</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Target endpoint to Cloud Run</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>&lt;!-- apiproxy/targets/default.xml --&gt;
&lt;TargetEndpoint name="default"&gt;
  &lt;HTTPTargetConnection&gt;
    &lt;Authentication&gt;
      &lt;GoogleIDToken&gt;
        &lt;Audience&gt;https://shop-api-xxxxx.a.run.app&lt;/Audience&gt;
      &lt;/GoogleIDToken&gt;
    &lt;/Authentication&gt;
    &lt;URL&gt;https://shop-api-xxxxx.a.run.app&lt;/URL&gt;
  &lt;/HTTPTargetConnection&gt;
&lt;/TargetEndpoint&gt;</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">Apigee calls the private Cloud Run service with a Google ID token from the proxy's service account, which needs <code>roles/run.invoker</code>. Clients never reach Cloud Run directly; set its ingress accordingly.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Common policies</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>&lt;VerifyAPIKey name="VK-VerifyKey"&gt;
  &lt;APIKey ref="request.header.x-api-key"/&gt;
&lt;/VerifyAPIKey&gt;

&lt;OAuthV2 name="OA-VerifyToken"&gt;
  &lt;Operation&gt;VerifyAccessToken&lt;/Operation&gt;
&lt;/OAuthV2&gt;

&lt;SpikeArrest name="SA-SpikeArrest"&gt;
  &lt;Rate&gt;30ps&lt;/Rate&gt;               &lt;!-- smooths bursts --&gt;
&lt;/SpikeArrest&gt;

&lt;Quota name="Q-PerApp"&gt;
  &lt;Interval&gt;1&lt;/Interval&gt;
  &lt;TimeUnit&gt;day&lt;/TimeUnit&gt;
  &lt;Allow count="10000"/&gt;
  &lt;Identifier ref="client_id"/&gt;
&lt;/Quota&gt;

&lt;AssignMessage name="AM-RemoveKey"&gt;
  &lt;Remove&gt;&lt;Headers&gt;&lt;Header name="x-api-key"/&gt;&lt;/Headers&gt;&lt;/Remove&gt;
&lt;/AssignMessage&gt;</code></pre></div>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>Policies</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Security</td><td>VerifyAPIKey, OAuthV2, VerifyJWT, BasicAuthentication, AccessControl, CORS</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Traffic</td><td>SpikeArrest, Quota, ResponseCache</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Mediation</td><td>AssignMessage, ExtractVariables, JSONtoXML, XMLtoJSON, RaiseFault</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Extension</td><td>JavaScript, ServiceCallout, MessageLogging, FlowCallout (shared flows)</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note">SpikeArrest protects the backend from bursts; Quota enforces each consumer's allowance. Use both.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Products, apps and keys</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Build a proxy and deploy it to an environment.</li>
      <li>Create an <b>API product</b> listing the proxy (and operations), environments, quota and scopes, e.g. "Shop API – Standard: 10,000 calls/day".</li>
      <li>A <b>developer</b> registers an <b>app</b> and subscribes it to the product.</li>
      <li>The app gets a <b>consumer key</b> (and secret for OAuth). The proxy's VerifyAPIKey or OAuthV2 policy checks it and loads the product's quota.</li>
      <li>Analytics then report traffic, latency and errors per product, app and developer.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="note">Products are how you package the same API differently: free and paid tiers, internal and partner access.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Apigee vs API Gateway</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th></th><th>Apigee</th><th>API Gateway</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Scope</td><td>Full API management</td><td>Lightweight managed gateway</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Features</td><td>Products, developer portal, monetisation, rich policies, analytics, hybrid</td><td>OpenAPI-defined routes, API keys, JWT auth, quotas</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Backends</td><td>Anything reachable</td><td>Cloud Run, Cloud Functions, App Engine and others</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Cost</td><td>Subscription, or pay-as-you-go: per call plus each deployed environment by the hour</td><td>Per call only; the first 2 million calls a month are free</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Choose when</td><td>Many APIs, external consumers, governance, analytics</td><td>A few APIs, mostly internal or simple</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note">Cloud Endpoints is the older Google option; new designs generally pick API Gateway or Apigee.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Delivering proxies like code</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Keep the <code>apiproxy/</code> bundle and shared flows in Git; review policy changes like code.</li>
      <li>Deploy from CI with the Apigee API, <code>apigeecli</code> or the Apigee Maven plugin, to dev → test → prod environments.</li>
      <li>Manage organisations, environments and environment groups with Terraform (<code>google_apigee_*</code> resources).</li>
      <li>Lint bundles with <b>apigeelint</b> before deploying.</li>
      <li>Use <b>shared flows</b> as your blueprint: one security and logging flow every proxy calls, instead of copied policies.</li>
      <li>Test with the trace/debug tool, and with automated API tests against each environment.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Security and operations</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Validate OAuth tokens or JWTs at the edge; the backend still checks object-level permissions.</li>
      <li>Strip credentials before forwarding; add a trusted identity header only from Apigee.</li>
      <li>Use <b>Advanced API Security</b> to detect abuse and misconfigured proxies.</li>
      <li>Put Cloud Armor in front of the external load balancer for WAF and DDoS protection.</li>
      <li>Log to Cloud Logging with MessageLogging; never log keys or tokens.</li>
      <li>Watch latency split: total vs target response time tells you if the gateway or the backend is slow.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Explain proxies, policies, products and apps</li><li>Call an API through Apigee with a key</li><li>Read Apigee analytics for an API</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Build a proxy with key/token verification, spike arrest and quota</li><li>Use shared flows; deploy through environments from CI</li><li>Connect securely to Cloud Run backends</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Design API products, tiers and the developer portal</li><li>Run Apigee across environments with Terraform and CI</li><li>Secure APIs at the edge with OAuth and Advanced API Security</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Own the organisation's API management platform and governance</li><li>Design hybrid or multi-region Apigee deployments</li><li>Lead API monetisation and partner programmes</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>Shared flows and flow hooks</li><li>Apigee hybrid on GKE</li><li>Advanced API Security</li><li>Developer portals (integrated and Drupal)</li><li>apigeelint and proxy testing</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://cloud.google.com/apigee/docs" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Apigee documentation</a><span class="lnote"> Official docs for Apigee X and hybrid</span></li><li><a href="https://cloud.google.com/apigee/docs/api-platform/reference/policies/reference-overview-policy" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Apigee policy reference</a><span class="lnote"> Every policy and its options</span></li><li><a href="https://github.com/GoogleCloudPlatform/apigee-samples" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Apigee samples</a><span class="lnote"> Working proxy examples</span></li><li><a href="https://github.com/apigee/apigeelint" target="_blank" rel="noopener">apigeelint</a><span class="lnote"> Static checks for proxy bundles</span></li><li><a href="https://cloud.google.com/api-gateway/docs" target="_blank" rel="noopener">API Gateway documentation</a><span class="lnote"> The lighter-weight alternative</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/apigee/docs/api-platform/get-started/what-apigee" target="_blank" rel="noopener">What is Apigee</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/apigee/docs/api-platform/reference/policies/reference-overview-policy" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Policy reference overview</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/apigee/docs/api-platform/security/google-auth/overview" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google authentication (GoogleIDToken)</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/apigee/docs/api-platform/reference/policies/verify-api-key-policy" target="_blank" rel="noopener">VerifyAPIKey policy</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/apigee/docs/api-platform/reference/policies/spike-arrest-policy" target="_blank" rel="noopener">SpikeArrest policy</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/apigee/docs/api-platform/reference/policies/quota-policy" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Quota policy</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/api-gateway/docs/about-api-gateway" target="_blank" rel="noopener">About API Gateway</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/apigee/docs/api-platform/fundamentals/organization-structure" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Apigee organisation structure</a>; <a href="https://cloud.google.com/apigee/pricing" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Apigee pricing</a>; <a href="https://cloud.google.com/api-gateway/pricing" target="_blank" rel="noopener">API Gateway pricing</a>; <a href="https://github.com/apigee/apigeecli" target="_blank" rel="noopener">apigeecli</a>; <a href="https://github.com/apigee/apigee-deploy-maven-plugin" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Apigee deploy Maven plugin</a>. A few claims could not be confirmed at their official source from here and are being re-checked.</p><p class="runcode">A question, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/enrichmeai.github.io/issues/new?title=%5BApigee%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>. Nothing here is tracked and there are no comments to store: questions live in the open, where the next learner can find the answer.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<section class="panel" id="p-awsdata" data-name="Data on AWS">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Data engineering on AWS</h2><p>The same lifecycle as <a href="#gcpdata">Data on GCP</a> and <a href="#azuredata">Data on Azure</a>, on Amazon Web Services: land, process, store, serve and govern. The service names differ; the ideas in <a href="#data">Data Engineering</a> do not. <a href="#cloudcompare">GCP vs AWS vs Azure</a> maps the services side by side.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Service map by lifecycle stage</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <tr><th>Stage</th><th>AWS service</th><th>What it is for</th></tr>
      <tr><td>Land and store</td><td>S3</td><td>Object storage: the data lake's files, raw and curated</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Stream in</td><td>Kinesis Data Streams; Amazon MSK (managed Kafka)</td><td>Events and change streams</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Change data capture</td><td>Database Migration Service (DMS)</td><td>Copy and replicate from operational databases</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Process (ETL)</td><td>Glue (Spark jobs, crawlers); EMR and EMR Serverless; Managed Service for Apache Flink</td><td>Batch and streaming transformation</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Open tables</td><td>S3 Tables (Apache Iceberg)</td><td>Tables on S3 with schema, snapshots and time travel</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Query</td><td>Athena (SQL on S3); Redshift (warehouse, provisioned or serverless)</td><td>Analysis and serving</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Orchestrate</td><td>MWAA (managed Airflow); Step Functions</td><td>Schedules and dependencies. See <a href="#dags">Airflow DAGs</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Catalog and govern</td><td>Glue Data Catalog; Lake Formation</td><td>Table metadata, and fine-grained access to it</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Visualise</td><td>QuickSight</td><td>Dashboards</td></tr>
    </table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>A reference architecture</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Files and events land in an S3 <b>raw</b> prefix, never edited.</li>
      <li>A Glue crawler or job registers the schema in the <b>Glue Data Catalog</b>.</li>
      <li>Glue or EMR jobs write cleaned, partitioned Parquet or Iceberg tables to a <b>curated</b> prefix.</li>
      <li>Athena queries the curated tables directly; Redshift serves heavy, repeated BI workloads.</li>
      <li>dbt models the curated layer into products (dbt has Athena and Redshift adapters). See <a href="#dbt">dbt</a>.</li>
      <li>Lake Formation grants each team only the tables and columns it needs.</li>
    </ol>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Choosing between similar services</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Athena or Redshift?</b> Athena for ad-hoc SQL on files, paid by data scanned. Redshift for a warehouse with steady, heavy queries and many users.</li>
      <li><b>Glue or EMR?</b> Glue for serverless Spark jobs without managing clusters. EMR when you need control of the cluster, other engines, or long-running jobs.</li>
      <li><b>Kinesis or MSK?</b> Kinesis for AWS-native streams with little to run. MSK when you want Kafka's API, tools and portability. See <a href="#kafka">Kafka</a>.</li>
      <li><b>MWAA or Step Functions?</b> Airflow for data pipelines with many dependencies; Step Functions for event-driven workflows across AWS services.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Handy commands</h3>
    <p>The AWS CLI calls the same APIs the console does. Names in angle brackets are yours to fill in.</p>
    <pre><code class="language-bash"># copy a file into the lake's raw prefix
aws s3 cp orders.csv s3://&lt;bucket&gt;/raw/orders/dt=2026-11-14/

# register its schema, then run a Glue job
aws glue start-crawler --name &lt;crawler&gt;
aws glue start-job-run --job-name &lt;job&gt; --arguments '{"--run_date":"2026-11-14"}'

# query it with Athena, then fetch the results
aws athena start-query-execution --work-group primary \
  --result-configuration OutputLocation=s3://&lt;bucket&gt;/athena-results/ \
  --query-string "SELECT status, count(*) FROM shop.orders GROUP BY status"
aws athena get-query-results --query-execution-id &lt;id-from-above&gt;

# run SQL on Redshift Serverless without a JDBC driver
aws redshift-data execute-statement --workgroup-name &lt;wg&gt; --database dev \
  --sql "SELECT count(*) FROM orders"

# put one event on a Kinesis stream
aws kinesis put-record --stream-name orders --partition-key a1 --data &lt;data&gt;</code></pre>
    <p class="note">Each operation and parameter was checked against the AWS SDK's service definitions (botocore).</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>IAM for data, in practice</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Roles, not keys:</b> jobs run as IAM roles; people sign in through single sign-on. No long-lived access keys in code.</li>
      <li><b>Two layers:</b> IAM decides who may call a service; Lake Formation decides which databases, tables, columns and rows they may read.</li>
      <li><b>Separate raw from curated</b> with different prefixes or buckets and different grants.</li>
      <li><b>Block public access</b> on every data bucket, and encrypt with KMS keys you control.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Cost controls</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Athena bills by data scanned:</b> Parquet or Iceberg, partitions and selecting only the columns you need cut it sharply. Workgroups can cap a query's scan.</li>
      <li><b>S3 lifecycle rules</b> move old raw data to cheaper storage classes.</li>
      <li><b>Pause or size</b> Redshift and EMR to the workload; serverless options bill only while they run.</li>
      <li><b>Budgets and tags</b> per team and pipeline, so a cost has an owner.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Name the AWS service for each data stage</li><li>Land files in S3 and query them with Athena</li><li>Explain IAM roles for data jobs</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Build a pipeline: S3 raw, Glue, curated Parquet or Iceberg, Athena</li><li>Use Lake Formation to grant table access</li><li>Model with dbt on Athena or Redshift</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Choose between Athena and Redshift, Glue and EMR, Kinesis and MSK</li><li>Design a governed data lake with a catalog</li><li>Plan cost, regions and data residency</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Own the organisation's AWS data architecture</li><li>Lead migrations between clouds or from on-premises</li><li>Set data platform standards across teams</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>S3 Tables and Apache Iceberg</li><li>Glue jobs and crawlers</li><li>Lake Formation permissions</li><li>Redshift Serverless</li><li>Kinesis and MSK streaming</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/glue/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AWS Glue documentation</a><span class="lnote"> ETL and the Data Catalog</span></li><li><a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Amazon Athena documentation</a><span class="lnote"> SQL on S3</span></li><li><a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Amazon Redshift documentation</a><span class="lnote"> The data warehouse</span></li><li><a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AWS Lake Formation documentation</a><span class="lnote"> Fine-grained data access</span></li><li><a href="https://github.com/aws/aws-sdk-pandas" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AWS SDK for pandas</a><span class="lnote"> Open-source pandas integration for S3, Athena, Glue and Redshift</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonS3/latest/userguide/s3-tables.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Amazon S3 Tables</a>; <a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lake-formation/latest/dg/what-is-lake-formation.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">What is Lake Formation</a>; <a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/athena/latest/ug/workgroups-setting-control-limits-cloudwatch.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Athena workgroup data usage controls</a>; <a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/redshift-data/latest/APIReference/API_ExecuteStatement.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Redshift Data API ExecuteStatement</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/boto/botocore/develop/botocore/data/athena/2017-05-18/service-2.json" target="_blank" rel="noopener">botocore Athena service definition</a>.</p><p class="runcode">A question, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/enrichmeai.github.io/issues/new?title=%5BData%20on%20AWS%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>. Nothing here is tracked and there are no comments to store: questions live in the open, where the next learner can find the answer.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= DATA ON AZURE ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-azuredata" data-name="Data on Azure">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Data engineering on Azure</h2><p>The same lifecycle as <a href="#gcpdata">Data on GCP</a> and <a href="#awsdata">Data on AWS</a>, on Microsoft Azure: land, process, store, serve and govern. The service names differ; the ideas in <a href="#data">Data Engineering</a> do not. <a href="#cloudcompare">GCP vs AWS vs Azure</a> maps the services side by side and follows one pipeline through all three.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Service map by lifecycle stage</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <tr><th>Stage</th><th>Azure service</th><th>What it is for</th></tr>
      <tr><td>Land and store</td><td>Azure Data Lake Storage (Blob Storage with the hierarchical namespace turned on); OneLake in Microsoft Fabric, built on it</td><td>The data lake&rsquo;s files, raw and curated, with directories and file-level permissions</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Stream in</td><td>Event Hubs, with a built-in Apache Kafka endpoint</td><td>Events at high volume; existing Kafka clients can often connect by changing only configuration</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Change data capture</td><td>Data Factory&rsquo;s change data capture resource; mirroring in Fabric</td><td>Read only what changed in an operational database, or replicate it continuously into OneLake</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Process (ETL)</td><td>Data Factory; Azure Databricks; Spark in Fabric; Stream Analytics for streams</td><td>Batch and streaming transformation</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Open tables</td><td>OneLake tables, stored as Delta Parquet or Iceberg</td><td>Tables any engine can read, without a proprietary format</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Query</td><td>Fabric Data Warehouse; serverless SQL pool in Azure Synapse (T-SQL on files in the lake); Azure Data Explorer for logs and time series</td><td>Analysis and serving</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Orchestrate</td><td>Data Factory pipelines; Apache Airflow jobs in Fabric</td><td>Schedules and dependencies. See <a href="#dags">Airflow DAGs</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Catalog and govern</td><td>Microsoft Purview (Data Map, Unified Catalog)</td><td>Find, classify and govern data wherever it lives</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Visualise</td><td>Power BI</td><td>Reports and dashboards</td></tr>
    </table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>A reference architecture</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Files and events land in a <b>raw</b> directory of a Data Lake Storage account, never edited.</li>
      <li>Data Factory copies from sources on a schedule; its change data capture reads only the rows that changed.</li>
      <li>Spark in Azure Databricks or Fabric writes cleaned, partitioned Delta or Iceberg tables to a <b>curated</b> area.</li>
      <li>The Fabric warehouse, or serverless SQL on the lake, serves SQL; Power BI reports read from it.</li>
      <li>Purview catalogs the tables; Azure roles and ACLs grant each team only what it needs.</li>
    </ol>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Choosing between similar services</h3>
    <p><i>Analysis, built on each service&rsquo;s own description.</i></p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Fabric or separate Azure services?</b> Fabric is one analytics platform covering ingestion, transformation, real-time processing, analytics and reporting, with OneLake underneath. Separate services (Data Lake Storage, Data Factory, Databricks, Synapse) suit teams that already run them or want to choose each piece.</li>
      <li><b>Fabric warehouse or serverless SQL?</b> The warehouse for star schemas and curated, governed data. Serverless SQL for ad-hoc T-SQL on files in the lake, billed by the data each query processes.</li>
      <li><b>Databricks or Spark in Fabric?</b> Databricks for a full data and AI platform in your cloud account; Fabric&rsquo;s Spark when the rest of your work is already in Fabric and OneLake.</li>
      <li><b>Data Factory or Airflow jobs?</b> Data Factory for copying between many data stores and building transformations visually; Airflow when pipelines are written as Python DAGs. See <a href="#dags">Airflow DAGs</a>.</li>
      <li><b>Event Hubs or a Kafka cluster you run?</b> Event Hubs runs many existing Kafka workloads with configuration changes only and no cluster to manage. Run Kafka yourself only for features or control it does not give you. See <a href="#kafka">Kafka</a>.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Handy commands</h3>
    <p>The Azure CLI calls the same APIs the portal does. Names in angle brackets are yours to fill in. <code>--auth-mode login</code> uses your Microsoft Entra sign-in instead of an account key.</p>
    <pre><code class="language-bash"># sign in
az login

# create the raw directory and upload a file into the lake (Data Lake Storage)
az storage fs directory create -n raw/orders -f &lt;filesystem&gt; --account-name &lt;account&gt; --auth-mode login
az storage fs file upload --source orders.csv -p raw/orders/dt=2026-11-14/orders.csv \
  -f &lt;filesystem&gt; --account-name &lt;account&gt; --auth-mode login

# run a Data Factory pipeline, then check the run (the datafactory extension)
az datafactory pipeline create-run --factory-name &lt;factory&gt; --resource-group &lt;rg&gt; \
  --name &lt;pipeline&gt; --parameters '{"run_date":"2026-11-14"}'
az datafactory pipeline-run show --factory-name &lt;factory&gt; --resource-group &lt;rg&gt; --run-id &lt;run-id-from-above&gt;

# create an event hub for order events
az eventhubs eventhub create --resource-group &lt;rg&gt; --namespace-name &lt;namespace&gt; --name orders --partition-count 4

# let a pipeline's managed identity read the lake's data
az role assignment create --assignee &lt;principal-id&gt; --role "Storage Blob Data Reader" --scope &lt;storage-account-id&gt;

# move raw data to cooler tiers as it ages
az storage account management-policy create --account-name &lt;account&gt; --resource-group &lt;rg&gt; --policy @policy.json</code></pre>
    <p class="note">Each command and option was checked against the Azure CLI&rsquo;s own help definitions (the azure-cli and azure-cli-extensions repositories).</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Identity and access for data, in practice</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Managed identities, not keys:</b> jobs run as managed identities, which remove the need to manage credentials; people sign in through Microsoft Entra ID.</li>
      <li><b>Two layers:</b> Azure roles grant coarse access, such as read on a whole storage account or container (<i>Storage Blob Data Reader</i>, <i>Contributor</i>, <i>Owner</i>); ACLs grant fine access to directories and files.</li>
      <li><b>Roles are checked first.</b> If a role already grants access, ACLs are ignored, so an ACL cannot narrow what a role allows. Give broad roles sparingly.</li>
      <li><b>Put groups in ACLs, not people:</b> Microsoft recommends security groups, so people can join and leave without rewriting ACLs across a directory tree.</li>
      <li><b>Avoid account keys:</b> Shared Key access is effectively super-user, and ACLs do not apply to it.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Cost controls</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Access tiers:</b> hot has the highest storage cost and lowest access cost; cool and cold cost less to store and more to read (cool data should stay at least 30 days); archive is offline.</li>
      <li><b>Lifecycle policies</b> move blobs to a cooler tier by age: time since creation, since last change, or since last access (last access needs access time tracking turned on).</li>
      <li><b>Serverless SQL bills by data processed:</b> you pay only for the data your queries process, and a query can read a chosen subset of columns.</li>
      <li><b>Pause a Fabric capacity</b> you are not using; pausing can make content on it unavailable, so check what depends on it first.</li>
      <li><b>Cost Management</b> shows where the money goes; tag resources per team and pipeline so a cost has an owner.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Name the Azure service for each data stage</li><li>Land files in Data Lake Storage and query them with SQL</li><li>Explain managed identities for data jobs</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Build a pipeline: Data Lake Storage raw, Data Factory or Spark, curated Delta or Iceberg, SQL</li><li>Grant access with Azure roles and ACLs together</li><li>Run and monitor Data Factory pipelines from the CLI</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Choose between Fabric and separate Azure services</li><li>Design a governed lake with Purview</li><li>Plan cost with access tiers, lifecycle policies and capacity</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Own the organisation's Azure data architecture</li><li>Lead migrations between clouds or from on-premises</li><li>Set data platform standards across teams</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>Data Lake Storage and the hierarchical namespace</li><li>Microsoft Fabric and OneLake</li><li>Data Factory and change data capture</li><li>Event Hubs and its Kafka endpoint</li><li>Azure roles, ACLs and Purview</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/blobs/data-lake-storage-introduction" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Azure Data Lake Storage introduction</a><span class="lnote"> The lake's storage</span></li><li><a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/fundamentals/microsoft-fabric-overview" target="_blank" rel="noopener">What is Microsoft Fabric?</a><span class="lnote"> The analytics platform</span></li><li><a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-factory/introduction" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Azure Data Factory introduction</a><span class="lnote"> Pipelines and copy</span></li><li><a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/blobs/data-lake-storage-access-control-model" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Access control model in Data Lake Storage</a><span class="lnote"> Roles, conditions and ACLs together</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/blobs/data-lake-storage-introduction" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Data Lake Storage introduction</a>; <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/event-hubs/event-hubs-about" target="_blank" rel="noopener">What is Event Hubs?</a>; <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/event-hubs/azure-event-hubs-apache-kafka-overview" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Event Hubs for Apache Kafka</a>; <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-factory/introduction" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Data Factory introduction</a>; <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-factory/concepts-change-data-capture" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Change data capture in Data Factory</a>; <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/fundamentals/microsoft-fabric-overview" target="_blank" rel="noopener">What is Microsoft Fabric?</a>; <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/onelake/onelake-overview" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OneLake overview</a>; <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-warehouse/data-warehousing" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Fabric Data Warehouse</a>; <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/synapse-analytics/overview-what-is" target="_blank" rel="noopener">What is Azure Synapse?</a>; <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/synapse-analytics/sql/on-demand-workspace-overview" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Synapse serverless SQL pool</a>; <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/databricks/introduction/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">What is Azure Databricks?</a>; <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/stream-analytics/stream-analytics-introduction" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Azure Stream Analytics</a>; <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/data-factory/apache-airflow-jobs-concepts" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Apache Airflow jobs in Fabric</a>; <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/data-explorer/data-explorer-overview" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Azure Data Explorer</a>; <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/purview/purview" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Microsoft Purview</a>; <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/power-bi/fundamentals/power-bi-overview" target="_blank" rel="noopener">What is Power BI?</a>; <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity/managed-identities-azure-resources/overview" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Managed identities</a>; <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/blobs/data-lake-storage-access-control-model" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Access control model in Data Lake Storage</a>; <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/blobs/access-tiers-overview" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Blob access tiers</a>; <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/storage/blobs/lifecycle-management-overview" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Blob lifecycle management</a>; <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/fabric/enterprise/pause-resume" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Pause and resume a Fabric capacity</a>; <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/cost-management-billing/costs/cost-mgt-best-practices" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cost Management best practices</a>; <a href="https://github.com/Azure/azure-cli/blob/dev/src/azure-cli/azure/cli/command_modules/storage/_help.py" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Azure CLI help: storage</a>; <a href="https://github.com/Azure/azure-cli/blob/dev/src/azure-cli/azure/cli/command_modules/eventhubs/_help.py" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Azure CLI help: eventhubs</a>; <a href="https://github.com/Azure/azure-cli/blob/dev/src/azure-cli/azure/cli/command_modules/role/_help.py" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Azure CLI help: role</a>; <a href="https://github.com/Azure/azure-cli-extensions/blob/main/src/datafactory/azext_datafactory/generated/_help.py" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Azure CLI extension help: datafactory</a>.</p><p class="runcode">A question, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/enrichmeai.github.io/issues/new?title=%5BData%20on%20Azure%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>. Nothing here is tracked and there are no comments to store: questions live in the open, where the next learner can find the answer.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= NEURAL NETWORKS ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-neural" data-name="Neural Networks">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Neural networks</h2><p>Most modern models for image recognition, speech and language are built from neural networks: many simple units, each doing a little arithmetic, connected in layers and tuned on data. This page explains one unit, how units form layers, how a network learns, the main kinds of network, and how the field got here. How large language models work in particular is on <a href="#llm">LLMs</a>; why GPUs suit this work is on <a href="#hardware">How Computers Are Built</a>.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>The short answer</h3>
    <p>A neural network is layers of simple units. Each unit multiplies its inputs by weights, adds them up and applies a small non-linear function. Training shows the network many examples, measures how wrong its answers are, and nudges every weight a little in the direction that makes the error smaller, again and again.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>One neuron</h3>
    <p>An artificial neuron takes several inputs, multiplies each by a <b>weight</b>, adds them up with a <b>bias</b>, and passes the total through an <b>activation function</b>. The weights are loosely analogous to the strengths of synapses between biological neurons. Warren McCulloch and Walter Pitts described a simple, non-learning model of this kind in 1943.</p>
    <figure class="fig"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 240" role="img" aria-labelledby="nn-one-t nn-one-d"><title id="nn-one-t">One artificial neuron</title><desc id="nn-one-d">Inputs x1, x2 and x3 are multiplied by weights w1, w2 and w3 and summed with a bias b in a node marked sigma. The sum z passes through an activation function f to give the output y.</desc><defs><marker id="nn-ar2" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker></defs><circle class="nd in" cx="60" cy="60" r="18"/><text class="lb" x="60" y="65" text-anchor="middle">x<tspan baseline-shift="sub" font-size="10">1</tspan></text><line class="ed" x1="78" y1="60" x2="270" y2="112.5" marker-end="url(#nn-ar2)"/><text class="lb mu" x="170" y="78.25" text-anchor="middle">w<tspan baseline-shift="sub" font-size="10">1</tspan></text><circle class="nd in" cx="60" cy="130" r="18"/><text class="lb" x="60" y="135" text-anchor="middle">x<tspan baseline-shift="sub" font-size="10">2</tspan></text><line class="ed" x1="78" y1="130" x2="270" y2="130.0" marker-end="url(#nn-ar2)"/><text class="lb mu" x="170" y="122.0" text-anchor="middle">w<tspan baseline-shift="sub" font-size="10">2</tspan></text><circle class="nd in" cx="60" cy="200" r="18"/><text class="lb" x="60" y="205" text-anchor="middle">x<tspan baseline-shift="sub" font-size="10">3</tspan></text><line class="ed" x1="78" y1="200" x2="270" y2="147.5" marker-end="url(#nn-ar2)"/><text class="lb mu" x="170" y="165.75" text-anchor="middle">w<tspan baseline-shift="sub" font-size="10">3</tspan></text><circle class="nd" cx="300" cy="130" r="28"/><text class="lb big" x="300" y="138" text-anchor="middle">Σ</text><text class="lb mu" x="300" y="44" text-anchor="middle">bias b</text><line class="ed" x1="300" y1="52" x2="300" y2="100" marker-end="url(#nn-ar2)"/><line class="ed" x1="328" y1="130" x2="380" y2="130" marker-end="url(#nn-ar2)"/><text class="lb mu" x="354" y="120" text-anchor="middle">z</text><rect class="nd" x="382" y="106" width="56" height="48" rx="6"/><text class="lb big" x="410" y="137" text-anchor="middle">f</text><line class="ed" x1="438" y1="130" x2="500" y2="130" marker-end="url(#nn-ar2)"/><circle class="nd out" cx="520" cy="130" r="18"/><text class="lb" x="520" y="135" text-anchor="middle">y</text><text class="lb mu" x="410" y="176" text-anchor="middle">activation</text></svg><figcaption>One neuron: <i>z</i> = <i>w</i><sub>1</sub><i>x</i><sub>1</sub> + <i>w</i><sub>2</sub><i>x</i><sub>2</sub> + <i>w</i><sub>3</sub><i>x</i><sub>3</sub> + <i>b</i>, then <i>y</i> = <i>f</i>(<i>z</i>).</figcaption></figure>
<pre><code>import math

x = [0.5, 0.8, 0.2]      # inputs
w = [0.4, 0.6, -0.9]     # weights
b = 0.1                  # bias

z = sum(xi * wi for xi, wi in zip(x, w)) + b   # weighted sum
y = 1 / (1 + math.exp(-z))                     # sigmoid activation
print(f"z = {z:.2f}, y = {y:.3f}")
# z = 0.60, y = 0.646</code></pre>
    <p class="note">Run as shown with Python 3; the last line is its real output.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Layers</h3>
    <figure class="fig"><svg viewBox="0 0 600 290" role="img" aria-labelledby="nn-net-t nn-net-d"><title id="nn-net-t">A feedforward neural network</title><desc id="nn-net-d">Three input nodes x1 to x3, each connected to all four hidden nodes h1 to h4, each of which is connected to both output nodes y-hat 1 and y-hat 2. Arrows run left to right.</desc><defs><marker id="nn-ar" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker></defs><line class="ed" x1="128" y1="80" x2="280" y2="55" marker-end="url(#nn-ar)"/><line class="ed" x1="128" y1="80" x2="280" y2="115" marker-end="url(#nn-ar)"/><line class="ed" x1="128" y1="80" x2="280" y2="175" marker-end="url(#nn-ar)"/><line class="ed" x1="128" y1="80" x2="280" y2="235" marker-end="url(#nn-ar)"/><line class="ed" x1="128" y1="150" x2="280" y2="55" marker-end="url(#nn-ar)"/><line class="ed" x1="128" y1="150" x2="280" y2="115" marker-end="url(#nn-ar)"/><line class="ed" x1="128" y1="150" x2="280" y2="175" marker-end="url(#nn-ar)"/><line class="ed" x1="128" y1="150" x2="280" y2="235" marker-end="url(#nn-ar)"/><line class="ed" x1="128" y1="220" x2="280" y2="55" marker-end="url(#nn-ar)"/><line class="ed" x1="128" y1="220" x2="280" y2="115" marker-end="url(#nn-ar)"/><line class="ed" x1="128" y1="220" x2="280" y2="175" marker-end="url(#nn-ar)"/><line class="ed" x1="128" y1="220" x2="280" y2="235" marker-end="url(#nn-ar)"/><line class="ed" x1="318" y1="55" x2="470" y2="115" marker-end="url(#nn-ar)"/><line class="ed" x1="318" y1="55" x2="470" y2="175" marker-end="url(#nn-ar)"/><line class="ed" x1="318" y1="115" x2="470" y2="115" marker-end="url(#nn-ar)"/><line class="ed" x1="318" y1="115" x2="470" y2="175" marker-end="url(#nn-ar)"/><line class="ed" x1="318" y1="175" x2="470" y2="115" marker-end="url(#nn-ar)"/><line class="ed" x1="318" y1="175" x2="470" y2="175" marker-end="url(#nn-ar)"/><line class="ed" x1="318" y1="235" x2="470" y2="115" marker-end="url(#nn-ar)"/><line class="ed" x1="318" y1="235" x2="470" y2="175" marker-end="url(#nn-ar)"/><circle class="nd in" cx="110" cy="80" r="18"/><text class="lb" x="110" y="85" text-anchor="middle">x<tspan baseline-shift="sub" font-size="10">1</tspan></text><circle class="nd in" cx="110" cy="150" r="18"/><text class="lb" x="110" y="155" text-anchor="middle">x<tspan baseline-shift="sub" font-size="10">2</tspan></text><circle class="nd in" cx="110" cy="220" r="18"/><text class="lb" x="110" y="225" text-anchor="middle">x<tspan baseline-shift="sub" font-size="10">3</tspan></text><circle class="nd" cx="300" cy="55" r="18"/><text class="lb" x="300" y="60" text-anchor="middle">h<tspan baseline-shift="sub" font-size="10">1</tspan></text><circle class="nd" cx="300" cy="115" r="18"/><text class="lb" x="300" y="120" text-anchor="middle">h<tspan baseline-shift="sub" font-size="10">2</tspan></text><circle class="nd" cx="300" cy="175" r="18"/><text class="lb" x="300" y="180" text-anchor="middle">h<tspan baseline-shift="sub" font-size="10">3</tspan></text><circle class="nd" cx="300" cy="235" r="18"/><text class="lb" x="300" y="240" text-anchor="middle">h<tspan baseline-shift="sub" font-size="10">4</tspan></text><circle class="nd out" cx="490" cy="115" r="18"/><text class="lb" x="490" y="120" text-anchor="middle">ŷ<tspan baseline-shift="sub" font-size="10">1</tspan></text><circle class="nd out" cx="490" cy="175" r="18"/><text class="lb" x="490" y="180" text-anchor="middle">ŷ<tspan baseline-shift="sub" font-size="10">2</tspan></text><text class="lb hd" x="110" y="22" text-anchor="middle">Input layer</text><text class="lb hd" x="300" y="22" text-anchor="middle">Hidden layer</text><text class="lb hd" x="490" y="22" text-anchor="middle">Output layer</text><text class="lb mu" x="205" y="282" text-anchor="middle">weights w</text><text class="lb mu" x="395" y="282" text-anchor="middle">weights w</text></svg><figcaption>A feedforward network with one hidden layer. Every arrow carries a weight; every node in the hidden and output layers is a neuron like the one above. A network is typically called deep if it has at least two hidden layers. Biases are left out of the drawing.</figcaption></figure>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Input layer:</b> the numbers you feed in, such as pixel values or the features of a transaction.</li>
      <li><b>Hidden layers:</b> neurons whose outputs feed the next layer. This shape, fully connected layer to layer, is called a multilayer perceptron.</li>
      <li><b>Output layer:</b> the answer, such as a score per class.</li>
      <li><b>Non-linear activations matter:</b> with a non-linear activation function, even a few neurons can solve non-trivial problems; the universal approximation theorems show that suitable networks can, in principle, approximate any continuous function.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>How a network learns</h3>
    <ol>
      <li><b>Predict:</b> run training examples forward through the network.</li>
      <li><b>Measure the error</b> with a <b>loss function</b>, a single number that training tries to make as small as possible.</li>
      <li><b>Find which way to move each weight:</b> <b>backpropagation</b> applies the chain rule of calculus efficiently to get the gradient of the loss with respect to every weight. Rumelhart, Hinton and Williams&rsquo; 1986 paper in <i>Nature</i>, &ldquo;Learning representations by back-propagating errors&rdquo;, describes the method.</li>
      <li><b>Take a small step:</b> <b>gradient descent</b> moves each weight a little in the direction opposite to the gradient, the direction of steepest descent. The size of the step is the <b>learning rate</b>.</li>
      <li><b>Repeat</b> over many batches. <b>Stochastic gradient descent</b> estimates the gradient from a randomly chosen subset of the data each time, instead of the whole data set.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="note">The risk is <b>overfitting</b>: a model that matches its training data too closely and then fails on new data. That is why models are checked on data held back from training.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Activation functions</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Function</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Sigmoid</td><td>Squashes any number into the range 0 to 1 (used in the code above)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>ReLU</td><td>Passes positive values, turns negatives into 0; used in the 2012 AlexNet image model</td></tr>
      <tr><td>GELU</td><td>A smooth version of ReLU, used in the 2018 BERT language model</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Softmax</td><td>Turns a list of scores into a probability distribution over the possible outcomes, often on the output layer</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Kinds of network</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Feedforward (multilayer perceptron):</b> data flows one way, layer to layer, as in the diagram.</li>
      <li><b>Convolutional (CNN):</b> learns small filters, or kernels, that slide across the input; widely used for images.</li>
      <li><b>Recurrent (RNN):</b> feeds its output back in, for sequences. Plain RNNs suffer from the vanishing gradient problem, which limits long-range memory; the <b>LSTM</b>, by Hochreiter and Schmidhuber (1997), was designed to ease it.</li>
      <li><b>Transformer:</b> introduced in the 2017 paper &ldquo;Attention Is All You Need&rdquo; by eight researchers at Google, built on the attention mechanism. Many modern large language models are built on it (see <a href="#llm">LLMs</a>).</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>A short history</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Year</th><th>What happened</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>1943</td><td>McCulloch and Pitts describe a non-learning computational model of neurons.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>1958</td><td>Frank Rosenblatt describes the perceptron, one of the first implemented neural networks.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>1969</td><td>Minsky and Papert&rsquo;s book <i>Perceptrons</i> sets out limits of simple perceptrons, such as computing XOR (they already knew multi-layer perceptrons could). It is claimed that its pessimism helped turn research towards symbolic systems.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>1986</td><td>Rumelhart, Hinton and Williams publish their backpropagation paper in <i>Nature</i>.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2012</td><td>AlexNet, a convolutional network trained on GPUs, wins the ImageNet image-recognition challenge with a top-5 error rate of 15.3%.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2017</td><td>The transformer is introduced in &ldquo;Attention Is All You Need&rdquo;.</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Choose by requirement</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>If this is true for you</th><th>It leans towards (<i>analysis</i>)</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Tabular data with a modest number of rows and a need to explain decisions</td><td>Simpler models first (linear models, decision trees); a neural network only if they fall short</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Images or other grid-shaped data</td><td>A convolutional network, or a pre-trained model to fine-tune</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Text, code or other sequences</td><td>A transformer, usually a pre-trained one used through an API or fine-tuned (see <a href="#llm">LLMs</a>)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Little labelled data</td><td>A pre-trained model adapted to your task, rather than training from scratch</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Your team already runs one ML framework</td><td>Stay with it unless a requirement forces a change: switching has a cost</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note">These leanings are <i>analysis</i>, not sourced facts; the other way is to try a neural network first and measure it against a simpler model.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Our view, and the other view</h3>
    <p><i>Our view:</i> every engineer who works with AI benefits from understanding one neuron, a loss and a gradient step well enough to explain them. It makes the behaviour of large models, and the limits of what they learned, less mysterious.</p>
    <p><i>The other view:</i> most engineers use models through an API and never train one. For them, time may be better spent on evaluation, prompting, cost and safety than on the mathematics of training.</p>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Explain a neuron: weights, bias, activation</li><li>Draw a network's input, hidden and output layers</li><li>Say what non-linear activations make possible</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Compute a neuron's output by hand and in code</li><li>Explain loss, gradient descent and the learning rate</li><li>Recognise overfitting and hold back data to check for it</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Explain backpropagation as the chain rule</li><li>Choose between feedforward, convolutional, recurrent and transformer networks</li><li>Decide between training, fine-tuning and using a pre-trained model</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Set an organisation's approach to building versus buying models</li><li>Judge claims about model capability against how training works</li><li>Plan evaluation for models in production</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>Artificial neurons</li><li>Layers and activation functions</li><li>Loss and gradient descent</li><li>Backpropagation</li><li>CNNs, RNNs and transformers</li><li>History of neural networks</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neural_network_(machine_learning)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Neural network (Wikipedia)</a><span class="lnote"> Overview with a history section</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neural_network_(machine_learning)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Neural network (machine learning)</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_neuron" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Artificial neuron</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Perceptron" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Perceptron</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Perceptrons_(book)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Perceptrons (book)</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Multilayer_perceptron" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Multilayer perceptron</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Universal_approximation_theorem" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Universal approximation theorem</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Activation_function" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Activation function</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Softmax_function" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Softmax function</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Loss_function" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Loss function</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Backpropagation" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Backpropagation</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gradient_descent" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Gradient descent</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stochastic_gradient_descent" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Stochastic gradient descent</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Overfitting" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Overfitting</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Convolutional_neural_network" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Convolutional neural network</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Recurrent_neural_network" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Recurrent neural network</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Long_short-term_memory" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Long short-term memory</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Attention_Is_All_You_Need" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Attention Is All You Need</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AlexNet" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: AlexNet</a>.</p><p class="runcode">A question, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/enrichmeai.github.io/issues/new?title=%5BNeural%20Networks%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>. Nothing here is tracked and there are no comments to store: questions live in the open, where the next learner can find the answer.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= LLMS ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-llm" data-name="LLMs">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>LLMs and their runtimes</h2><p>How large language models are built and why they behave as they do, the runtimes that serve them (hosted APIs, Google Cloud's Agent Platform (formerly Vertex AI), self-hosted servers), and how to build an application on a runtime: from one call, to a tool loop, to a full agent harness. Engineering practice is on <a href="#ai">AI for Engineers</a>.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>How an LLM works, briefly</h3>
    <ol>
      <li><b>Tokenise:</b> text is split into tokens (word pieces). Everything (price, limits, speed) is counted in tokens.</li>
      <li><b>Embed:</b> each token becomes a vector of numbers.</li>
      <li><b>Transformer layers:</b> attention lets every token look at every earlier token and mix in what is relevant; dozens of layers build up meaning.</li>
      <li><b>Predict:</b> the model outputs a probability for every possible next token; one is chosen, appended, and the process repeats. Generation is one token at a time.</li>
    </ol>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>What happens</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Pre-training</td><td>Predict the next token over a huge text and code corpus</td><td>Broad knowledge and language ability, up to a training cutoff</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Post-training</td><td>Instruction tuning, reinforcement learning from human and AI feedback, safety training</td><td>Follows instructions, uses tools, refuses harmful requests</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Inference</td><td>Your prompt in, tokens out, on GPUs or TPUs</td><td>The responses you build on</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note">Consequences you design around: no memory between calls; knowledge stops at a cutoff; outputs vary run to run; fluent text can be wrong; instructions inside data can be followed.</p>
      <figure class="fig"><svg viewBox="0 0 660 230" role="img" aria-labelledby="tokloop-t tokloop-d"><title id="tokloop-t">How an LLM generates text</title><desc id="tokloop-d">A loop. The text so far is split into tokens. Each token becomes a vector. Transformer layers let each token attend to the earlier ones. The model outputs a probability for every possible next token. One is chosen and appended to the text, and the loop runs again, so the answer is produced one token at a time.</desc><defs><marker id="tokloop-a" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker><marker id="tokloop-aa" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar acc" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker></defs><rect class="nd" x="10" y="50" width="118" height="56" rx="6"/><text class="lb hd" x="69" y="74" text-anchor="middle">tokenise</text><text class="lb mu" x="69" y="94" text-anchor="middle">text → tokens</text><circle class="nd in" cx="20" cy="50" r="9"/><text class="lb mu" x="20" y="54" text-anchor="middle">1</text><line class="ed" x1="128" y1="78" x2="136" y2="78" marker-end="url(#tokloop-a)"/><rect class="nd" x="140" y="50" width="118" height="56" rx="6"/><text class="lb hd" x="199" y="74" text-anchor="middle">embed</text><text class="lb mu" x="199" y="94" text-anchor="middle">tokens → vectors</text><circle class="nd in" cx="150" cy="50" r="9"/><text class="lb mu" x="150" y="54" text-anchor="middle">2</text><line class="ed" x1="258" y1="78" x2="266" y2="78" marker-end="url(#tokloop-a)"/><rect class="nd" x="270" y="50" width="118" height="56" rx="6"/><text class="lb hd" x="329" y="74" text-anchor="middle">transformer</text><text class="lb mu" x="329" y="94" text-anchor="middle">attention layers</text><circle class="nd in" cx="280" cy="50" r="9"/><text class="lb mu" x="280" y="54" text-anchor="middle">3</text><line class="ed" x1="388" y1="78" x2="396" y2="78" marker-end="url(#tokloop-a)"/><rect class="nd" x="400" y="50" width="118" height="56" rx="6"/><text class="lb hd" x="459" y="74" text-anchor="middle">predict</text><text class="lb mu" x="459" y="94" text-anchor="middle">P(next token)</text><circle class="nd in" cx="410" cy="50" r="9"/><text class="lb mu" x="410" y="54" text-anchor="middle">4</text><line class="ed" x1="518" y1="78" x2="526" y2="78" marker-end="url(#tokloop-a)"/><rect class="nd in" x="530" y="50" width="118" height="56" rx="6"/><text class="lb hd" x="589" y="74" text-anchor="middle">choose + append</text><text class="lb mu" x="589" y="94" text-anchor="middle">one token added</text><circle class="nd in" cx="540" cy="50" r="9"/><text class="lb mu" x="540" y="54" text-anchor="middle">5</text><path class="ed acc" d="M590,106 C590,190 70,190 70,110" marker-end="url(#tokloop-aa)"/><text class="lb acc" x="330" y="196" text-anchor="middle">repeat with the longer text: one token at a time</text></svg><figcaption>The four steps above as a loop. Each pass adds one token, which is why output is counted, priced and streamed in tokens.</figcaption></figure>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Prompt, RAG, tools or fine-tune?</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Need</th><th>Reach for</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Behaviour, format, tone</td><td>Clear instructions and examples in the prompt; structured outputs</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Knowledge the model lacks (your documents, current data)</td><td><a href="#rag">RAG</a>: retrieve and include it</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Live data or actions</td><td>Tools (function calling) or <a href="#mcp">MCP</a> servers</td></tr>
      <tr><td>A narrow task at high volume, cheaper or faster</td><td>A smaller model, or fine-tuning one, after measuring</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Consistent style the prompt cannot achieve</td><td>Fine-tuning (LoRA or full)</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note"><i>Our view:</i> try them in that order, the cheapest change first. <i>The other view:</i> for a narrow task at high volume, some teams fine-tune a smaller model early, because each call then costs less. Either way, fine-tuning does not add reliable facts; it changes behaviour. Facts belong in retrieval.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Runtime options</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Runtime</th><th>What it is</th><th>Choose when</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Hosted model API</td><td>Provider runs the model (Anthropic API, others)</td><td>Best models, no GPUs to run, pay per token</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Cloud platform</td><td>Models through your cloud account (Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform, formerly Vertex AI; Bedrock; Foundry)</td><td>Billing, IAM, region and data controls in your cloud</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Managed open-model endpoints</td><td>Deploy open-weight models to a managed endpoint (Model Garden on Google Cloud's Agent Platform, formerly Vertex AI)</td><td>Open models without running servers yourself</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Self-hosted server</td><td>vLLM or llama.cpp server (Hugging Face TGI is now in maintenance mode) on your GPUs (GKE, Cloud Run with GPUs, VMs)</td><td>Data must stay on your infrastructure; steady high volume; custom models</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Local</td><td>Ollama or llama.cpp on a laptop</td><td>Experiments, offline development, small models</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Self-hosting basics</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># vLLM: OpenAI-compatible server for an open-weight model
vllm serve meta-llama/Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct --max-model-len 8192
# → http://localhost:8000/v1/chat/completions

# Ollama: local models with one command
ollama run llama3.1
# → API on http://localhost:11434

# llama.cpp: quantised GGUF models on CPU or GPU
llama-server -m model-Q4_K_M.gguf --port 8080</code></pre></div>
    <ul>
      <li><b>GPU memory</b> ≈ parameters × bytes per parameter, plus the KV cache. An 8B model in 16-bit needs ~16 GB before the cache; 4-bit quantisation cuts that to ~5 GB at some quality cost.</li>
      <li><b>Throughput</b> comes from continuous batching (vLLM) and caching the prompt prefix.</li>
      <li><b>Measure</b> time to first token, tokens per second, and cost per thousand requests against a hosted API before committing.</li>
      <li><b>Licences</b> of open-weight models differ; read them before commercial use.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Building on a runtime: four levels of harness</h3>
    <p>A <b>harness</b> is the code around the model: the loop that sends messages, runs tools, feeds results back, manages context and stops. Pick the least you need.</p>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>You write</th><th>Who runs the loop and tools</th><th>Fits</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>1. Single call</td><td>Prompt and parsing</td><td>No loop</td><td>Classify, extract, summarise</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2. Manual tool loop</td><td>The <code>while stop_reason == "tool_use"</code> loop and the tools</td><td>You, on your infrastructure</td><td>Full control over each step</td></tr>
      <tr><td>3. SDK tool runner</td><td>Just the tool functions</td><td>The SDK runs the loop; you host it</td><td>Most custom agents</td></tr>
      <tr><td>4a. Agent SDK</td><td>A prompt and options</td><td>Claude Code's harness as a library, with built-in file, shell and search tools; you host it</td><td>Coding and file-system agents on your own infrastructure</td></tr>
      <tr><td>4b. Managed agents</td><td>Agent configuration and your tool results</td><td>The provider runs the loop and a sandbox per session</td><td>Long-running hosted agents without running infrastructure</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note">Start at level 1 and move up only when the task needs model-driven, multi-step work. Each level adds cost, latency and things to test.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Level 2: a manual tool loop</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>import anthropic, json
client = anthropic.Anthropic()

TOOLS = [{
  "name": "pipeline_status",
  "description": "Latest state of each pipeline stage for a date (YYYY-MM-DD).",
  "input_schema": {"type": "object",
                   "properties": {"run_date": {"type": "string"}},
                   "required": ["run_date"]},
}]

def run_tool(name, args):
    if name == "pipeline_status":
        return json.dumps(read_ledger(args["run_date"]))   # read-only
    return json.dumps({"error": f"unknown tool {name}"})

messages = [{"role": "user", "content": "Did yesterday's load finish?"}]
for _ in range(10):                                   # always bound the loop
    resp = client.messages.create(model="claude-opus-5-5", max_tokens=16000,
                                  tools=TOOLS, messages=messages)
    messages.append({"role": "assistant", "content": resp.content})
    if resp.stop_reason != "tool_use":
        break
    results = [{"type": "tool_result", "tool_use_id": b.id,
                "content": run_tool(b.name, b.input)}
               for b in resp.content if b.type == "tool_use"]
    messages.append({"role": "user", "content": results})   # all results in one message

print("".join(b.text for b in resp.content if b.type == "text"))</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">Append the full <code>resp.content</code>, not just its text. Return failed tools as results with <code>"is_error": true</code> rather than dropping them. Level 3 (the SDK's tool runner) does this loop for you.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Running LLM features in production</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Timeouts, retries and fallbacks</b> per call; handle refusals and rate limits explicitly.</li>
      <li><b>Stream</b> long outputs; use the batch API for offline bulk work.</li>
      <li><b>Prompt caching:</b> keep stable instructions and tool definitions first so repeated calls reuse them.</li>
      <li><b>Log</b> model, version, tokens, latency and outcome for every call; trace tool calls.</li>
      <li><b>Pin model IDs</b> and re-run your evals before switching models.</li>
      <li><b>Budget:</b> cap tokens per request and spend per day; alert on spikes.</li>
      <li><b>Data controls:</b> know the provider's retention terms and the region where inference runs.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Glossary</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Context window:</b> maximum tokens of input plus output in one call.</li>
      <li><b>Embedding:</b> a vector representing meaning, used for search.</li>
      <li><b>KV cache:</b> stored attention state that makes generating each next token fast.</li>
      <li><b>Quantisation:</b> storing weights with fewer bits to save memory.</li>
      <li><b>LoRA:</b> fine-tuning small adapter weights instead of the whole model.</li>
      <li><b>Temperature / sampling:</b> how random token choice is (some newer APIs fix this and expose an effort setting instead).</li>
      <li><b>Hallucination:</b> confident output not supported by facts.</li>
      <li><b>Grounding:</b> tying answers to retrieved sources.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

















  <article class="card"><h3>One we build: Penstock</h3>
    <p>To run agents on your own servers, on a hosted model or a local Ollama model, see <a href="/penstock/">Penstock</a>, our open-source (Apache 2.0) agent harness.</p>
    <p class="note">It is ours, so we are not neutral: compare it with the runtimes on this page.</p>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Explain tokens, context windows, next-token prediction and training cutoffs</li><li>Make a single model call with structured output</li><li>Know when to prompt, retrieve, use tools or fine-tune</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Build a tool loop or use an SDK tool runner</li><li>Run a local or self-hosted model and compare it with a hosted API</li><li>Log, budget and handle errors for LLM calls</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Choose runtimes and models by quality, cost, latency and data controls</li><li>Design agent harnesses with bounded loops, context management and approvals</li><li>Plan self-hosting capacity (GPU memory, batching, quantisation)</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Set the organisation's model and runtime strategy</li><li>Lead fine-tuning or custom model programmes where justified</li><li>Design platform-level AI infrastructure and governance</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>Transformer architecture and attention</li><li>Inference optimisation: KV cache, batching, speculative decoding</li><li>Fine-tuning with LoRA</li><li>Agent design and context management</li><li>Model evaluation and benchmarking</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://platform.claude.com/docs" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Claude developer docs</a><span class="lnote"> Messages API, tools, streaming, caching</span></li><li><a href="https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/building-effective-agents" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Building effective agents (Anthropic)</a><span class="lnote"> Workflows vs agents</span></li><li><a href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/agent-sdk" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Claude Agent SDK</a><span class="lnote"> Claude Code's harness as a library</span></li><li><a href="https://cloud.google.com/vertex-ai/generative-ai/docs/model-garden/explore-models" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Vertex AI Model Garden</a><span class="lnote"> Hosted and open models on GCP</span></li><li><a href="https://docs.vllm.ai/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">vLLM documentation</a><span class="lnote"> High-throughput self-hosted inference</span></li><li><a href="https://ollama.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ollama</a><span class="lnote"> Run models locally</span></li><li><a href="https://huggingface.co/learn" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Hugging Face course</a><span class="lnote"> Free courses on transformers and LLMs</span></li><li><a href="https://jalammar.github.io/illustrated-transformer/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The Illustrated Transformer (Jay Alammar)</a><span class="lnote"> Visual explanation of attention</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/models/overview.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Claude models overview (model IDs)</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/release-notes" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Agent Platform release notes (Vertex AI renamed, 2026-04-22)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/huggingface/text-generation-inference/main/README.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">TGI README (maintenance mode)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/vllm-project/vllm/main/docs/getting_started/quickstart.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">vLLM OpenAI-compatible server (port 8000, vllm serve)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/vllm-project/vllm/main/vllm/engine/arg_utils.py" target="_blank" rel="noopener">vLLM engine args (--max-model-len)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ollama/ollama/main/docs/api.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ollama API (port 11434)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp/master/tools/server/README.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">llama.cpp server README (-m, --port)</a>; <a href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/overview.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Claude Agent SDK / tool runner (via code.claude.com)</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Large_language_model&action=raw" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Large language model</a>; <a href="https://ollama.com/library/llama3.1" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ollama library: llama3.1</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/run/docs/configuring/services/gpu" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cloud Run GPU support</a>. A few claims could not be confirmed at their official source from here and are being re-checked.</p><p class="runcode">A question, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/enrichmeai.github.io/issues/new?title=%5BLLMs%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>. Nothing here is tracked and there are no comments to store: questions live in the open, where the next learner can find the answer.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= AI ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-ai" data-name="AI">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>AI for engineers</h2><p>How language models behave, how to build features on them that you can test and operate, and how to work with AI assistants without lowering the bar.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Use AI like a bulldozer, not a shovel</h3>
    <p>A shovel moves one scoop at a time while you hold the handle: ask for a line, paste it, ask again. A bulldozer moves the whole job while you choose where it goes, set the boundaries and check the result. The second gets far more done, but only if you prepare the site. And only if you know enough to manage the site: see <a href="#start">Fundamentals first</a> on Start here.</p>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Shovel habit</th><th>Bulldozer habit</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>"Write a function that…" with no context</td><td>Hand over a whole task: the issue, the files, the conventions and what done means</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Copy and paste answers from a chat window</td><td>Let an agent work in the repo: edit files, run the build and tests, read the errors, fix them</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Re-explain the project in every session</td><td>Write the context down once (a <code>CLAUDE.md</code> or <code>AGENTS.md</code>, skills, runbooks) so every session starts informed</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Check its work by reading every line as it types</td><td>Define checks up front (tests, lint, type checks, CI) and review the finished change against them</td></tr>
      <tr><td>One task at a time, waiting on each</td><td>Run independent tasks in parallel: separate branches or worktrees, subagents for research and review</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Fix the same mistake by hand again next week</td><td>Turn each correction into a rule, test or hook in the repo so it cannot recur</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Trust it because it sounds sure</td><td>Trust it because the tests passed and the docs agree</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Preparing the site</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Context file in the repo:</b> what the system is, how to build and test it, the rules that are not negotiable, the traps that cost time before.</li>
      <li><b>Fast, exact gates:</b> the commands that prove a change is done, written down so the agent runs them itself.</li>
      <li><b>Guardrails:</b> permission settings and hooks that stop destructive commands (force pushes, prod deploys, deletes) unless a human approves.</li>
      <li><b>Small, clear tasks:</b> one concern per task, with acceptance criteria. A vague brief gets a confident guess.</li>
      <li><b>Pinned docs:</b> links to the official docs for the versions you use, so it checks instead of recalling.</li>
      <li><b>A reviewer that is not the author:</b> a second agent or a person reviews the diff against the spec and the docs.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>A brief that moves earth</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>(An illustrative brief: the problem and the paths are examples.)
Goal:     Reject dates that do not parse, instead of storing NULL.
Why:      One source sends dd-mm-yyyy; the parser turns those into NULL
          without saying so, and the rows look like "no date".
Where:    models/intermediate/int_orders_normalised.sql and its unit tests.
Rules:    Follow CLAUDE.md. Do not change the other sources. No new dependencies.
Done when:
  - a unit test with "05-05-1959" and "bad" fails before the fix, passes after
  - dbt build -s int_orders_normalised+ is green
  - the reject table counts unparseable rows
Out of scope: backfilling old rows (separate issue).
Report:   what changed, the test output, anything you could not verify.</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">Goal, reason, location, rules, a checkable definition of done, scope, and what to report. The same brief works for a colleague.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Where you still drive by hand</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Irreversible actions:</b> production deploys, data deletion, schema changes on live data, anything sent outside the company.</li>
      <li><b>Unclear requirements:</b> decide what to build first; an agent will fill gaps with plausible guesses.</li>
      <li><b>Security and money:</b> auth, permissions, payments, secrets. Review line by line.</li>
      <li><b>Claims about state:</b> "prod has X" needs someone to look at prod, not a model's inference from config.</li>
      <li><b>Judgement calls</b> that belong to a person: priorities, trade-offs, wording that speaks for the company.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">The bulldozer is only as safe as its operator. Speed comes from preparation and checks, not from skipping them.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Why everyone's experience of AI is different</h3>
    <p>Ask ten people what AI is like at writing code and you will hear ten different answers, often strongly held, including from people who have never written code. Most of them are honest. They are describing different experiences, not the same one.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>What you bring decides what you get.</b> If you cannot read the code, you cannot tell good output from output that only looks good. An experienced engineer spots the bug, writes a precise brief, and knows when to stop and do it by hand. Same tool, very different results.</li>
      <li><b>The task is different.</b> A small demo built from nothing is not a ten-year-old system with real users, real data and real security needs. AI can shine at the first and still struggle with the second.</li>
      <li><b>When and how much you used it.</b> An afternoon is not years of daily use, and the tools change month to month. A view formed once, early or late, ages quickly.</li>
      <li><b>Where you stand.</b> A manager sees speed. A reviewer sees more code to check. The engineer on call sees the incident. A newcomer sees a patient teacher. Each is real.</li>
      <li><b>Talking about AI and code without ever having coded</b> is a little like describing a marathon without ever having walked: you can see the photos at the finish line, but not the miles. People who do not code still see things engineers miss, as users, designers and leaders, and their views on those things count. Judging how well AI writes and maintains code takes the experience of writing and maintaining code.</li>
      <li><b>Trust works the same way, in both directions.</b> Adaptive cruise control is a real help to someone who has driven for years, in manual and automatic cars: they know when to lean on it, and when to take the wheel back. For someone who has never driven at all, trusting it blindly and refusing it outright are both guesses. Trust in AI-written code grows from the same place: solid foundations, so you know what good looks like and when to take over.</li>
      <li><b>And even good drivers need guard rails.</b> Roads have speed limits, crash barriers and lane markings because skilled people still make mistakes. AI-written code needs its own: tests and type checks that run on every change (<a href="#testing">Testing</a>), CI gates that block a red build, code review by someone who did not write it, hooks that check each edit as it happens and least-privilege permissions so an agent cannot touch production on its own (<a href="#claudetools">Claude Code &amp; Cowork</a>, <a href="#agentharness">Agent Harness</a>), secrets kept out of prompts (<a href="#security">Security</a>), and a person who approves the merge.</li>
    </ul>
    <p><b>So everyone is right about what they experienced, and none of it is the whole truth for everyone else.</b> When you hear a strong view, ask: what did you try it on, for how long, and how did you check the result? Share specifics, not verdicts. And before forming your own view, try it yourself on real work, then check the result properly (see <a href="#behaviours">Behaviours</a>: learn by doing, not by opinion). We still need solid foundations and guard rails: learn to drive first (see <a href="#start">Start here</a>: fundamentals first), then drive on a road with barriers.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Blueprints and frameworks, not generated boilerplate</h3>
    <p>AI makes boilerplate cost almost nothing to <i>write</i>. It does not make it cheap to <i>own</i>. Every generated copy has to be reviewed, secured, tested, upgraded and debugged, and fifty copies that are each slightly different are fifty places to fix the same bug. A blueprint (a framework, shared library, template, Terraform module or dbt package) makes each decision once, tests it once, and fixes it once for everyone.</p>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th></th><th>Generated boilerplate</th><th>Blueprint or framework</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Consistency</td><td>Each copy drifts: retries, logging, auth and error handling differ per service</td><td>One behaviour everywhere, so people and tools know what to expect</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Fixing a bug or CVE</td><td>Find and patch every copy, and miss some</td><td>Patch once, bump the version, done</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Review</td><td>Hundreds of generated lines to read in every PR</td><td>Review only the part that is new; the rest is already proven</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Security</td><td>Each copy can reintroduce a hole the model learned from public code</td><td>Secure defaults built in and tested</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Upgrades</td><td>Every service upgrades on its own, or never</td><td>Upgrade the blueprint; services follow</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Knowledge</td><td>Decisions live in whichever prompt produced the code</td><td>Decisions are written down, versioned and reviewable</td></tr>
      <tr><td>AI quality</td><td>The model guesses your conventions from its training data</td><td>The model follows your blueprint, so its output is smaller and fits</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Rule of thumb:</b> if you would ask AI to generate the same thing twice, it belongs in a blueprint.</li>
      <li><b>Use AI to build and improve the blueprint,</b> then to fill in only the part that is specific: the business logic, the one new query, the tests for it.</li>
      <li><b>Point the AI at the blueprint</b> in your context file ("new jobs use <code>modules/pipeline-job</code>; new services start from the service template"), so it extends instead of reinventing.</li>
      <li><b>Generation is fine</b> for one-off scripts, prototypes you will throw away, and filling in a blueprint's slots.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">Examples in this guide: Spring Boot starters and Quarkus extensions; a Terraform module for every Cloud Run job; dbt macros, generic tests and packages; a shared library such as Culvert for storage and warehouse access; a service template in Backstage or Cookiecutter; and a written build-loop blueprint that is ported between repos instead of re-prompted in each. More on the <a href="#blueprints">Frameworks vs AI Boilerplate</a> tab.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>How models behave</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Tokens:</b> models read and write tokens (pieces of words). Cost, limits and latency are all counted in tokens.</li>
      <li><b>Context window:</b> everything the model can see in one call: instructions, history, retrieved documents, tool results. It remembers nothing between calls unless you send it again.</li>
      <li><b>Non-deterministic:</b> the same input can give different outputs. Test with sets of examples and scores, not one run.</li>
      <li><b>Confidently wrong:</b> a model can produce fluent, specific, false text (hallucination). Ground answers in retrieved sources and ask for citations you can check.</li>
      <li><b>Knowledge cutoff:</b> the model knows nothing after its training data. Current facts must come from your data or tools.</li>
      <li><b>Instructions in data:</b> the model cannot reliably tell your instructions from instructions hidden in a document it reads.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Calling a model (Anthropic Python SDK)</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># pip install anthropic   — credentials from ANTHROPIC_API_KEY
import anthropic
client = anthropic.Anthropic()

resp = client.messages.create(
    model="claude-opus-5-5",
    max_tokens=16000,
    system="You summarise customer reviews for the shop's buyers. Quote the review IDs you rely on.",
    messages=[{"role": "user", "content": reviews_text}],
)
if resp.stop_reason == "refusal":
    handle_refusal(resp)                   # check before reading content
for block in resp.content:
    if block.type == "text":
        print(block.text)
print(resp.usage.input_tokens, resp.usage.output_tokens)   # log every call's cost</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">Other current IDs: <code>claude-sonnet-5-5</code> (faster, cheaper) and <code>claude-haiku-5-5</code> (cheapest, for bulk work). Long outputs should stream. On Google Cloud's Agent Platform (formerly Vertex AI) use <code>AnthropicVertex(project_id=…, region=…)</code> with the same calls.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Structured output, not string parsing</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>from pydantic import BaseModel

class ProductMention(BaseModel):
    name: str
    sku: str | None
    store: str | None
    quantity: int | None

class Extraction(BaseModel):
    products: list[ProductMention]

resp = client.messages.parse(
    model="claude-opus-5-5",
    max_tokens=16000,
    messages=[{"role": "user", "content": f"Extract every product mentioned:\n\n{text}"}],
    output_format=Extraction,
)
result: Extraction = resp.parsed_output     # validated against the schema</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">The schema guarantees shape, not truth. Still check values against your data: does that order exist in your data?</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Tools: let the model call your code</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>from anthropic import beta_tool

@beta_tool
def search_orders(query: str, store: str | None = None) -&gt; str:
    """Search the shop's orders. Returns up to 10 results as JSON.

    Args:
        query: Full-text search terms.
        store: Optional store code, e.g. "1_05".
    """
    return json.dumps(index.search(query, store=store, limit=10))

runner = client.beta.messages.tool_runner(
    model="claude-opus-5-5",
    max_tokens=16000,
    tools=[search_orders],
    messages=[{"role": "user", "content": "Find recent orders for umbrellas in store 1_05."}],
)
for message in runner:          # the SDK runs the call → tool → call loop
    print(message)</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">Give tools the least privilege that works: read-only by default, writes behind human approval. MCP is the open protocol for exposing tools and data to any compatible client.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>RAG: retrieval-augmented generation</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>What you build</th><th>Where it goes wrong</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>1 Ingest</td><td>Pipeline that loads documents with IDs, dates and source</td><td>Stale or duplicate documents; no way to tell which version was used</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2 Chunk</td><td>Split into passages (by section or ~500–1,000 tokens with overlap)</td><td>Chunks cut through the sentence that answers the question</td></tr>
      <tr><td>3 Embed</td><td>Vector per chunk; store with metadata</td><td>Query and documents embedded with different models</td></tr>
      <tr><td>4 Retrieve</td><td>Vector search plus keyword search (hybrid), filtered by metadata</td><td>Exact names and citations missed by vectors alone</td></tr>
      <tr><td>5 Rerank</td><td>Score the top 50 and keep the best 5–10</td><td>Irrelevant passages crowd out the right one</td></tr>
      <tr><td>6 Generate</td><td>Prompt with the passages, ask for citations to their IDs</td><td>Model answers from memory when retrieval found nothing; say "not found" instead</td></tr>
      <tr><td>7 Evaluate</td><td>Measure retrieval (did the right chunk come back?) and answers separately</td><td>Only judging the final answer hides which step failed</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Vector search: Postgres &amp; BigQuery</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>-- pgvector
CREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS vector;
CREATE TABLE chunks (id bigserial PRIMARY KEY, doc_id text, body text,
                     embedding vector(768));
CREATE INDEX ON chunks USING hnsw (embedding vector_cosine_ops);
SELECT id, doc_id, 1 - (embedding &lt;=&gt; $1) AS similarity
FROM chunks WHERE doc_id LIKE 'HP%'          -- HNSW filters after the index scan: can return fewer rows (pgvector 0.8+ iterative scans help)
ORDER BY embedding &lt;=&gt; $1 LIMIT 10;        -- &lt;=&gt; cosine distance</code></pre></div>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>-- BigQuery: embed with a remote model, then search
CREATE OR REPLACE TABLE `p.ai.chunk_emb` AS
SELECT * FROM ML.GENERATE_EMBEDDING(
  MODEL `p.ai.embedding_model`,
  (SELECT id, body AS content FROM `p.ai.chunks`));

SELECT base.id, distance
FROM VECTOR_SEARCH(
  TABLE `p.ai.chunk_emb`, 'ml_generate_embedding_result',
  (SELECT ml_generate_embedding_result FROM ML.GENERATE_EMBEDDING(
     MODEL `p.ai.embedding_model`, (SELECT 'land acquisition compensation' AS content))),
  top_k =&gt; 10, distance_type =&gt; 'COSINE');</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Evals: how you know it works</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Build a test set</b> of 50–200 real inputs with expected outputs or grading notes. Include the hard and adversarial cases.</li>
      <li><b>Grade with code first:</b> exact match, schema valid, cited ID exists, number within tolerance.</li>
      <li><b>Model-as-judge</b> for open-ended answers, with a written rubric, checked against human grades on a sample.</li>
      <li><b>Run it in CI</b> on every prompt, model or retrieval change, and track the score over time.</li>
      <li><b>Hold out a test split</b> you never tune against, so improvements are real.</li>
      <li><b>Measure cost and latency</b> beside quality; a 2% gain at 3× the cost may not be worth it.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Security</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Prompt injection:</b> a web page, PDF or email the model reads can say "ignore your instructions". Treat everything retrieved as untrusted data.</li>
      <li><b>Limit the blast radius:</b> tools get least privilege; destructive actions need human confirmation; the model never holds credentials directly.</li>
      <li><b>Output is untrusted input:</b> escape it before rendering, validate it before running it as SQL or shell.</li>
      <li><b>Data leakage:</b> do not send secrets or personal data you are not allowed to process. Check the provider's retention terms.</li>
      <li><b>Log and audit</b> prompts, tool calls and outputs (minus sensitive fields) so you can investigate.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Cost &amp; latency</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Estimate first:</b> (input + output tokens) × price × calls per day. Log <code>usage</code> on every call.</li>
      <li><b>Prompt caching:</b> put the stable part (instructions, tool definitions, long reference text) first so repeated calls reuse it at a fraction of the price.</li>
      <li><b>Batch API</b> for work that can wait (bulk extraction overnight), at a lower price.</li>
      <li><b>Right-size the model:</b> measure whether a smaller model or lower effort holds quality on your eval before paying for the largest.</li>
      <li><b>Trim context:</b> retrieve 5 good passages, not 50 mediocre ones.</li>
      <li><b>Stream</b> long responses so users see progress, and set timeouts and retries with backoff.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Working with AI coding assistants</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Give context:</b> the repo's conventions, the failing test, the exact error. Vague asks get vague code.</li>
      <li><b>Tests first:</b> a test that fails before the change is both the spec and the check.</li>
      <li><b>Verify against docs:</b> an assistant can invent a flag, method or version. Check the official docs for anything external.</li>
      <li><b>Review every line</b> as you would a colleague's PR: error paths, edge cases, security.</li>
      <li><b>Keep secrets out</b> of prompts and pasted logs.</li>
      <li><b>Small steps:</b> one concern per change, so a wrong turn is cheap to undo.</li>
      <li><b>You own the result.</b> "The AI wrote it" is not a review comment.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Agents: when and when not</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Start with one call.</b> Classification, extraction and summaries rarely need more.</li>
      <li><b>Then a workflow:</b> fixed steps in your code, a model call at each. Predictable and testable.</li>
      <li><b>An agent</b> (the model chooses tools in a loop) only when the steps cannot be known in advance, the value justifies the cost, and mistakes can be caught and undone.</li>
      <li><b>Bound it:</b> step and token budgets, timeouts, an approval gate on writes, and a full log of each tool call.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>AI on Google Cloud</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform (formerly Vertex AI):</b> hosted models (Gemini, Claude and others through Model Garden), endpoints, evaluation and pipelines in your GCP project and region.</li>
      <li><b>BigQuery ML:</b> call remote models from SQL (<code>AI.GENERATE_TEXT</code>, <code>AI.GENERATE_EMBEDDING</code>; the older <code>ML.</code> functions still work) and search with <code>VECTOR_SEARCH</code>, without moving data out.</li>
      <li><b>Data residency:</b> pick the region deliberately; some models run only in global or specific regions.</li>
      <li><b>IAM as usual:</b> service accounts with the Agent Platform User role (<code>roles/aiplatform.user</code>), no API keys in code.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Call a model API and get structured output</li><li>Explain tokens, context, hallucination and prompt injection</li><li>Use an AI assistant with tests and review</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Build a RAG feature with retrieval, citations and an eval set</li><li>Use tools and handle refusals, errors and cost</li><li>Brief an AI agent with context, rules and a definition of done</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Design AI features with evals, guardrails, observability and fallbacks</li><li>Tune cost and latency (caching, batch, model choice)</li><li>Set up repos so AI agents work safely (context files, hooks, gates)</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Set AI strategy, governance and security across the organisation</li><li>Design agentic systems and their safety boundaries</li><li>Build shared AI platforms, blueprints and evaluation infrastructure</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>Evaluation design and model-graded evals</li><li>Retrieval: hybrid search, reranking, chunking</li><li>Agents, tool design and MCP</li><li>Prompt injection defences</li><li>Cost and latency engineering</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://platform.claude.com/docs" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Claude developer docs</a><span class="lnote"> Anthropic API, tools, structured outputs, prompt caching</span></li><li><a href="https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/building-effective-agents" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Building effective agents (Anthropic)</a><span class="lnote"> When to use workflows vs agents</span></li><li><a href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/overview" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Claude Code docs</a><span class="lnote"> Agentic coding, hooks, subagents</span></li><li><a href="https://modelcontextprotocol.io/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Model Context Protocol</a><span class="lnote"> Open protocol for tools and data</span></li><li><a href="https://cloud.google.com/vertex-ai/generative-ai/docs" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Vertex AI generative AI</a><span class="lnote"> Models and tooling on GCP</span></li><li><a href="https://cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/vector-search-intro" target="_blank" rel="noopener">BigQuery vector search</a><span class="lnote"> Embeddings and VECTOR_SEARCH</span></li><li><a href="https://github.com/pgvector/pgvector" target="_blank" rel="noopener">pgvector</a><span class="lnote"> Vector search in Postgres</span></li><li><a href="https://genai.owasp.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OWASP GenAI security</a><span class="lnote"> LLM application risks</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/models/overview.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Claude models overview (claude-opus-5-5, claude-sonnet-5-5, claude-haiku-5-5)</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/release-notes" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Agent Platform release notes (Vertex AI rename)</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/reference/standard-sql/bigqueryml-syntax-generate-text" target="_blank" rel="noopener">BigQuery ML.GENERATE_TEXT (AI.GENERATE_TEXT recommended for new queries)</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/reference/standard-sql/bigqueryml-syntax-generate-embedding" target="_blank" rel="noopener">BigQuery ML.GENERATE_EMBEDDING (output column, AI.GENERATE_EMBEDDING note)</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/reference/standard-sql/search_functions" target="_blank" rel="noopener">BigQuery VECTOR_SEARCH syntax</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/pgvector/pgvector/master/README.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">pgvector README (HNSW filtering, iterative scans in recent releases)</a>; <a href="https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/agents-and-tools/mcp-connector.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MCP connector / Claude API docs</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Retrieval-augmented_generation&action=raw" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Retrieval-augmented generation</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/iam/docs/roles-permissions/aiplatform" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Agent Platform IAM roles (aiplatform.user)</a>; <a href="https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/claude-models-on-google-cloud/google-managed.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Claude models on Google Cloud: endpoints</a>. A few claims could not be confirmed at their official source from here and are being re-checked.</p><p class="runcode">A question, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/enrichmeai.github.io/issues/new?title=%5BAI%20for%20Engineers%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>. Nothing here is tracked and there are no comments to store: questions live in the open, where the next learner can find the answer.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= RAG ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-rag" data-name="RAG">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Building RAG systems</h2><p>Retrieval-augmented generation: find the right passages from your own data and give them to the model, so answers are current, specific and cited. The whole pipeline, each design choice, code for Postgres and BigQuery, and how to evaluate it. RAG is a data pipeline first: see <a href="#data">Data Engineering</a>.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Architecture</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Phase</th><th>Steps</th><th>Runs</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Ingestion (offline)</td><td>Load documents → parse (PDF, HTML, OCR) → clean → chunk → add metadata → embed → index (vector + keyword)</td><td>As a pipeline: batch or on change</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Retrieval (online)</td><td>Query → rewrite (optional) → hybrid search with permission and metadata filters → rerank → top passages</td><td>Per question, in milliseconds</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Generation (online)</td><td>Prompt with passages and IDs → model answers with citations → validate citations → respond, or say "not found"</td><td>Per question, in seconds</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Evaluation (continuous)</td><td>Golden questions → retrieval metrics + answer metrics → dashboard and CI gate</td><td>On every change, and sampled in production</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>The phases, drawn</h3>
    <figure class="fig"><svg viewBox="0 0 660 300" role="img" aria-labelledby="rag-t rag-d"><title id="rag-t">The four phases of a RAG system</title><desc id="rag-d">Three rows and a loop. Ingestion, offline: load documents, parse, chunk, embed, then index into a vector and a keyword index. Retrieval, per question: the query is optionally rewritten, searched in both indexes with permission filters, reranked, and the top passages kept. Generation: the model answers from those passages with citations, which are checked, or it says not found. Evaluation runs golden questions against retrieval and answers on every change.</desc><defs><marker id="rag-a" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker><marker id="rag-aa" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar acc" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker></defs><text class="lb hd" x="10" y="47" text-anchor="start">Ingestion</text><rect class="nd" x="120" y="26" width="92" height="34" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="166.0" y="48" text-anchor="middle">load</text><line class="ed" x1="212" y1="43" x2="224" y2="43" marker-end="url(#rag-a)"/><rect class="nd" x="226" y="26" width="92" height="34" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="272.0" y="48" text-anchor="middle">parse</text><line class="ed" x1="318" y1="43" x2="330" y2="43" marker-end="url(#rag-a)"/><rect class="nd" x="332" y="26" width="92" height="34" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="378.0" y="48" text-anchor="middle">chunk</text><line class="ed" x1="424" y1="43" x2="436" y2="43" marker-end="url(#rag-a)"/><rect class="nd" x="438" y="26" width="92" height="34" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="484.0" y="48" text-anchor="middle">embed</text><line class="ed" x1="530" y1="43" x2="542" y2="43" marker-end="url(#rag-a)"/><rect class="nd" x="544" y="26" width="92" height="34" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="590.0" y="48" text-anchor="middle">index</text><text class="lb hd" x="10" y="117" text-anchor="start">Retrieval</text><rect class="nd in" x="120" y="96" width="92" height="34" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="166.0" y="118" text-anchor="middle">query</text><line class="ed" x1="212" y1="113" x2="224" y2="113" marker-end="url(#rag-a)"/><rect class="nd in" x="226" y="96" width="92" height="34" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="272.0" y="118" text-anchor="middle">rewrite?</text><line class="ed" x1="318" y1="113" x2="330" y2="113" marker-end="url(#rag-a)"/><rect class="nd in" x="332" y="96" width="92" height="34" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="378.0" y="118" text-anchor="middle">hybrid search</text><line class="ed" x1="424" y1="113" x2="436" y2="113" marker-end="url(#rag-a)"/><rect class="nd in" x="438" y="96" width="92" height="34" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="484.0" y="118" text-anchor="middle">rerank</text><line class="ed" x1="530" y1="113" x2="542" y2="113" marker-end="url(#rag-a)"/><rect class="nd in" x="544" y="96" width="92" height="34" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="590.0" y="118" text-anchor="middle">top passages</text><text class="lb hd" x="10" y="187" text-anchor="start">Generation</text><rect class="nd out" x="120" y="166" width="92" height="34" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="166.0" y="188" text-anchor="middle">prompt + IDs</text><line class="ed" x1="212" y1="183" x2="224" y2="183" marker-end="url(#rag-a)"/><rect class="nd out" x="226" y="166" width="92" height="34" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="272.0" y="188" text-anchor="middle">answer</text><line class="ed" x1="318" y1="183" x2="330" y2="183" marker-end="url(#rag-a)"/><rect class="nd out" x="332" y="166" width="92" height="34" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="378.0" y="188" text-anchor="middle">check cites</text><line class="ed" x1="424" y1="183" x2="436" y2="183" marker-end="url(#rag-a)"/><rect class="nd out" x="438" y="166" width="92" height="34" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="484.0" y="188" text-anchor="middle">respond</text><line class="ed" x1="530" y1="183" x2="542" y2="183" marker-end="url(#rag-a)"/><rect class="nd out" x="544" y="166" width="92" height="34" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="590.0" y="188" text-anchor="middle">or: not found</text><line class="ed acc" x1="574" y1="60" x2="374" y2="92" marker-end="url(#rag-aa)"/><text class="lb acc" x="500" y="86" text-anchor="middle">searched</text><line class="ed acc" x1="574" y1="130" x2="166" y2="162" marker-end="url(#rag-aa)"/><text class="lb acc" x="655" y="150" text-anchor="end">into the prompt</text><rect class="bd" x="120" y="235" width="512" height="40" rx="8"/><text class="lb acc" x="376" y="260" text-anchor="middle">Evaluation, on every change: golden questions → retrieval + answer metrics</text></svg><figcaption>The table above as a picture. Ingestion fills the indexes ahead of time; retrieval and generation run for every question; evaluation checks the whole chain on every change.</figcaption></figure>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Ingestion and parsing</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Keep a stable document ID, source, version and date</b> on everything, so each answer can name exactly what it used.</li>
      <li><b>Parse structure, not just text:</b> headings, paragraphs and numbered sections make natural chunk boundaries. Tables need special handling.</li>
      <li><b>Scanned PDFs need OCR</b> (Document AI, Tesseract); measure its error rate on your documents.</li>
      <li><b>Clean:</b> strip headers, footers, page numbers and boilerplate that pollute retrieval.</li>
      <li><b>Run ingestion as an idempotent, incremental pipeline</b> with run records: documents in = chunks indexed + rejected.</li>
      <li><b>Handle updates and deletions:</b> re-embed changed documents, remove withdrawn ones, and never serve a stale version silently.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Chunking</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Strategy</th><th>How</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Fixed size</td><td>N tokens with overlap</td><td>Simple baseline; cuts through sentences</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Recursive</td><td>Split by section, then paragraph, then sentence until small enough</td><td>Good default for prose</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Structural</td><td>One chunk per section or numbered paragraph</td><td>Best for legal and technical documents with structure</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Parent-child</td><td>Retrieve small chunks, give the model their larger parent section</td><td>Precise search, enough context to answer</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Contextual</td><td>Prepend a short summary of where the chunk sits in its document before embedding</td><td>Reduces retrieval failures for chunks that make no sense alone</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note">Typical sizes are 200–1,000 tokens. Measure: the right size is the one that maximises retrieval recall on your golden questions.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Embeddings and indexes</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Embedding model:</b> choose by quality on your domain and language, dimension (storage and speed), and cost. Options include Agent Platform (formerly Vertex AI) text embeddings and other providers' embedding APIs, or open models.</li>
      <li><b>Same model for documents and queries,</b> and re-embed everything when you change it. Store the model name and version with each vector.</li>
      <li><b>Distance:</b> cosine is the usual choice; normalise vectors if the model expects it.</li>
      <li><b>Approximate indexes</b> (HNSW, IVF) trade a little recall for speed. Tune and measure recall against exact search on a sample.</li>
      <li><b>Where to store:</b> pgvector (Postgres, AlloyDB), BigQuery <code>VECTOR_SEARCH</code>, Vector Search on Agent Platform (formerly Vertex AI Vector Search), Elasticsearch/OpenSearch. Keep vectors next to the metadata you filter on.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Hybrid search with rank fusion (Postgres)</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>-- chunks(id, doc_id, body, tsv tsvector, embedding vector(768), store text)
WITH vec AS (
  SELECT id, ROW_NUMBER() OVER (ORDER BY embedding &lt;=&gt; $1) AS r
  FROM chunks WHERE store = ANY($3)            -- filter first: permissions, metadata
  ORDER BY embedding &lt;=&gt; $1 LIMIT 50
),
kw AS (
  SELECT id, ROW_NUMBER() OVER (ORDER BY ts_rank_cd(tsv, q) DESC) AS r
  FROM chunks, websearch_to_tsquery('english', $2) AS q
  WHERE tsv @@ q AND store = ANY($3)
  ORDER BY ts_rank_cd(tsv, q) DESC LIMIT 50
)
SELECT id, SUM(1.0 / (60 + r)) AS score       -- reciprocal rank fusion, k = 60
FROM (SELECT * FROM vec UNION ALL SELECT * FROM kw) AS both_lists
GROUP BY id
ORDER BY score DESC
LIMIT 20;                                      -- then rerank these 20</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">Vectors find meaning ("money back for a parcel that never came"); keywords find exact names, citations and section numbers that vectors miss. Fusion combines both without tuning score scales.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Reranking and query handling</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Rerank</b> the top 20–50 with a cross-encoder or reranking API (for example Google Cloud's ranking API in Agent Search, formerly Vertex AI Search, or a provider's rerank model); keep the best 5–10.</li>
      <li><b>Query rewriting:</b> turn a conversational follow-up into a standalone query ("and last year?" → full question).</li>
      <li><b>Multi-query:</b> generate a few phrasings and fuse their results.</li>
      <li><b>Metadata extraction:</b> pull filters out of the question (store, year) and apply them in SQL.</li>
      <li><b>Agentic retrieval:</b> let the model call a search tool several times when one search is not enough, with a step budget.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Generation with citations</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>import anthropic
client = anthropic.Anthropic()

passages = retrieve(question, stores=user.allowed_stores)    # hybrid + rerank, top 8

content = [
    {"type": "document",
     "source": {"type": "text", "media_type": "text/plain", "data": p.body},
     "title": f"{p.title} ¶{p.paragraph} ({p.doc_id})",
     "citations": {"enabled": True}}
    for p in passages
]
content.append({"type": "text", "text":
    "Answer only from the documents above. If they do not contain the answer, "
    "say you could not find it. Question: " + question})

resp = client.messages.create(model="claude-opus-5-5", max_tokens=16000,
                              messages=[{"role": "user", "content": content}])
# text blocks carry a citations list pointing at the exact cited text in each document</code></pre></div>
    <ul>
      <li>Put instructions in the system prompt; treat retrieved text as data, never as instructions.</li>
      <li>Check that every cited document was actually retrieved; drop or flag answers with no citations.</li>
      <li>When retrieval returns nothing relevant, answer "not found". When the index is unavailable, say so: that is "cannot see", not "nothing exists".</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>RAG on BigQuery</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>-- 1. embed chunks with a remote embedding model
CREATE OR REPLACE TABLE `p.rag.chunk_emb` AS
SELECT * FROM ML.GENERATE_EMBEDDING(
  MODEL `p.rag.embedding_model`,
  (SELECT chunk_id, doc_id, store, body AS content FROM `p.rag.chunks`));

-- 2. optional vector index for large tables
CREATE VECTOR INDEX chunk_idx ON `p.rag.chunk_emb`(ml_generate_embedding_result)
OPTIONS (index_type = 'IVF', distance_type = 'COSINE');

-- 3. search
SELECT base.chunk_id, base.doc_id, base.content, distance
FROM VECTOR_SEARCH(
  (SELECT * FROM `p.rag.chunk_emb` WHERE store IN ('1_05', '4_12')),
  'ml_generate_embedding_result',
  (SELECT ml_generate_embedding_result FROM ML.GENERATE_EMBEDDING(
     MODEL `p.rag.embedding_model`,
     (SELECT 'compensation for land acquisition' AS content))),
  top_k =&gt; 20, distance_type =&gt; 'COSINE');</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">Good for batch RAG and analytics over large corpora. For interactive latency, serve from Postgres/AlloyDB or Vector Search on Agent Platform.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Evaluating RAG</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Measure</th><th>Question it answers</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Recall@k</td><td>Is the right passage in the top k results?</td></tr>
      <tr><td>MRR</td><td>How high is the first right passage?</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Faithfulness</td><td>Is every claim supported by the retrieved passages?</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Answer correctness</td><td>Does it match the expected answer?</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Citation accuracy</td><td>Do citations point to passages that support the sentence?</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Abstention</td><td>Does it say "not found" when the corpus has no answer?</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <ul>
      <li>Build 100–300 real questions with the passages that answer them, including unanswerable ones.</li>
      <li>Evaluate retrieval and generation separately, so you know which step to fix.</li>
      <li>Run in CI on every change to chunking, embeddings, prompts or models.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Security and access control</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Filter by permission at retrieval time.</b> Never retrieve documents the user may not see and hope the model ignores them.</li>
      <li><b>Prompt injection:</b> documents can contain instructions. Keep tools read-only, constrain outputs, and do not let retrieved text trigger actions.</li>
      <li><b>Legal holds and erasure requests</b> apply to the index too: remove or filter those documents immediately.</li>
      <li><b>Personal data:</b> decide what may be embedded and sent to a model; embeddings can leak content.</li>
      <li><b>Audit:</b> log the question, retrieved IDs and answer (with sensitive fields protected).</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Failure modes and fixes</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Likely cause</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Right document exists, never retrieved</td><td>Chunking splits the answer; missing keyword search; embedding model weak for the domain</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Retrieved, but answer wrong</td><td>Too many passages, noisy context, prompt does not demand grounding</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Exact citation or section number missed</td><td>Vector-only search; add keyword or exact-match lookup</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Old version quoted</td><td>Re-ingestion not incremental; no version filter</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Slow</td><td>No ANN index, reranking too many, large context</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Confident answer with no source</td><td>No abstention instruction or citation validation</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Explain the ingestion, retrieval and generation phases</li><li>Run a vector search over embedded chunks</li><li>Prompt a model to answer from passages with citations</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Build an incremental ingestion pipeline with chunking, metadata and embeddings</li><li>Add hybrid search, reranking and permission filters</li><li>Build a golden question set and measure recall and faithfulness</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Design RAG for a domain: chunking strategy, index choice, latency and cost</li><li>Diagnose retrieval vs generation failures from evals</li><li>Secure RAG: access control, prompt injection, suppressions</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Own retrieval and knowledge platforms across products</li><li>Lead advanced retrieval (agentic, graph-based) where it measurably helps</li><li>Set evaluation standards for AI answers in the organisation</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>Contextual retrieval</li><li>Hybrid search and reciprocal rank fusion</li><li>Rerankers and cross-encoders</li><li>Evaluation frameworks for RAG</li><li>GraphRAG and agentic retrieval</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://www.anthropic.com/news/contextual-retrieval" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Contextual Retrieval (Anthropic)</a><span class="lnote"> Reducing retrieval failures with chunk context</span></li><li><a href="https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/citations" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Claude citations</a><span class="lnote"> Cited answers from documents</span></li><li><a href="https://github.com/pgvector/pgvector" target="_blank" rel="noopener">pgvector</a><span class="lnote"> Vector search in Postgres</span></li><li><a href="https://cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/vector-search-intro" target="_blank" rel="noopener">BigQuery vector search</a><span class="lnote"> Embeddings and VECTOR_SEARCH</span></li><li><a href="https://cloud.google.com/vertex-ai/docs/vector-search/overview" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Vertex AI Vector Search</a><span class="lnote"> Managed approximate nearest neighbour search</span></li><li><a href="https://cloud.google.com/vertex-ai/generative-ai/docs/rag-engine/rag-overview" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Vertex AI RAG Engine</a><span class="lnote"> Managed RAG on GCP</span></li><li><a href="https://genai.owasp.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OWASP GenAI security</a><span class="lnote"> Prompt injection and LLM risks</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/release-notes" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Agent Platform release notes (Vertex AI Vector Search renamed Vector Search)</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/reference/standard-sql/search_functions" target="_blank" rel="noopener">BigQuery VECTOR_SEARCH (base table may be a subquery)</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/reference/standard-sql/data-definition-language" target="_blank" rel="noopener">BigQuery DDL: CREATE VECTOR INDEX (IVF, TREE_AH)</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/bigquery/docs/reference/standard-sql/bigqueryml-syntax-generate-embedding" target="_blank" rel="noopener">BigQuery ML.GENERATE_EMBEDDING</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/pgvector/pgvector/master/README.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">pgvector README</a>; <a href="https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/models/overview.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Claude models overview</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Retrieval-augmented_generation&action=raw" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Retrieval-augmented generation</a>; <a href="https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/citations.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Claude citations</a>; <a href="https://github.com/pgvector/pgvector-python/blob/master/examples/hybrid_search/rrf.py" target="_blank" rel="noopener">pgvector hybrid search RRF example (k = 60)</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/generative-ai-app-builder/docs/ranking" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google Cloud ranking API (Agent Search)</a>; <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/textsearch-controls.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PostgreSQL text search controls</a>. A few claims could not be confirmed at their official source from here and are being re-checked.</p><p class="runcode">A question, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/enrichmeai.github.io/issues/new?title=%5BRAG%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>. Nothing here is tracked and there are no comments to store: questions live in the open, where the next learner can find the answer.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= MCP ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-mcp" data-name="MCP">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>MCP: Model Context Protocol</h2><p>An open protocol for giving AI applications access to tools and data through a standard interface. What it is, how to build and add an MCP server, how to call one from the API, and how to use MCP safely around a data pipeline.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>What MCP is</h3>
    <p>Without MCP, every AI application needs custom integration code for every tool. With MCP, a tool or data source is wrapped once as an <b>MCP server</b>, and any MCP-capable <b>client</b> (Claude Code, Claude apps, IDEs, your own agent) can use it. Think of it as a standard port between models and systems.</p>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Concept</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Host / client</td><td>The AI application and its connection to a server</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Server</td><td>A program exposing capabilities over MCP</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Tools</td><td>Functions the model can call (query a table, check a pipeline run)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Resources</td><td>Read-only data the application can load into context (a file, a run record)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Prompts</td><td>Reusable prompt templates the server offers</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Transport: stdio</td><td>Server runs as a local process; messages over stdin/stdout</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Transport: Streamable HTTP</td><td>Server runs remotely over HTTP; optional authorisation with OAuth</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>MCP, drawn</h3>
    <figure class="fig"><svg viewBox="0 0 660 270" role="img" aria-labelledby="mcpmap-t mcpmap-d"><title id="mcpmap-t">MCP: one host, two servers</title><desc id="mcpmap-d">On the left, a host application such as Claude Code or an IDE, holding two MCP clients. One client talks to a local server over stdio: the server runs as a process on the same machine. The other talks to a remote server over Streamable HTTP, optionally authorised with OAuth. Each server exposes tools the model can call, resources the application can load, and prompts. Each server wraps one system: a file system, a database.</desc><defs><marker id="mcpmap-a" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker><marker id="mcpmap-aa" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto"><path class="ar acc" d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 z"/></marker></defs><rect class="bd" x="10" y="40" width="200" height="190" rx="8"/><text class="lb mu" x="110" y="30" text-anchor="middle">host: Claude Code, an IDE, your agent</text><rect class="nd in" x="30" y="70" width="160" height="46" rx="6"/><text class="lb hd" x="110" y="98" text-anchor="middle">MCP client</text><rect class="nd in" x="30" y="160" width="160" height="46" rx="6"/><text class="lb hd" x="110" y="188" text-anchor="middle">MCP client</text><rect class="nd" x="300" y="55" width="170" height="76" rx="6"/><text class="lb hd" x="385" y="80" text-anchor="middle">local server</text><text class="lb mu" x="385" y="99" text-anchor="middle">tools, resources,</text><text class="lb mu" x="385" y="116" text-anchor="middle">prompts</text><rect class="nd" x="300" y="145" width="170" height="76" rx="6"/><text class="lb hd" x="385" y="170" text-anchor="middle">remote server</text><text class="lb mu" x="385" y="189" text-anchor="middle">tools, resources,</text><text class="lb mu" x="385" y="206" text-anchor="middle">prompts</text><line class="ed" x1="190" y1="93" x2="296" y2="93" marker-end="url(#mcpmap-a)"/><text class="lb mu" x="245" y="85" text-anchor="middle">stdio</text><line class="ed acc" x1="190" y1="183" x2="296" y2="183" marker-end="url(#mcpmap-aa)"/><text class="lb acc" x="245" y="175" text-anchor="middle">HTTP + OAuth</text><rect class="nd out" x="520" y="70" width="130" height="46" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="585" y="98" text-anchor="middle">files, a repo</text><rect class="nd out" x="520" y="160" width="130" height="46" rx="6"/><text class="lb" x="585" y="188" text-anchor="middle">a database</text><line class="ed" x1="470" y1="93" x2="516" y2="93" marker-end="url(#mcpmap-a)"/><line class="ed" x1="470" y1="183" x2="516" y2="183" marker-end="url(#mcpmap-a)"/><text class="lb acc" x="330" y="255" text-anchor="middle">wrap a system once as a server; every MCP-capable host can use it</text></svg><figcaption>The table above as a picture. A server wraps one system once; any host that speaks MCP can then use it, locally over stdio or remotely over HTTP.</figcaption></figure>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>A server in Python (MCPServer)</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># pip install "mcp[cli]" google-cloud-bigquery      (mcp 2.x)
from mcp.server import MCPServer
from google.cloud import bigquery

mcp = MCPServer("pipeline-status")
bq = bigquery.Client()

@mcp.tool()
def latest_runs(run_date: str) -&gt; list[dict]:
    """Latest state of every pipeline stage for a run date (YYYY-MM-DD)."""
    sql = """SELECT stage, state, objects_examined, objects_failed, event_ts
             FROM `my-proj.ops.v_job_run_latest`
             WHERE run_date = @d ORDER BY stage"""
    cfg = bigquery.QueryJobConfig(query_parameters=[
        bigquery.ScalarQueryParameter("d", "DATE", run_date)])
    return [dict(r) for r in bq.query(sql, job_config=cfg).result()]

@mcp.resource("ledger://{run_date}")
def ledger(run_date: str) -&gt; str:
    """The full event history for a run date, as text."""
    return format_events(read_events(run_date))

if __name__ == "__main__":
    mcp.run()                              # stdio by default
    # mcp.run(transport="streamable-http") # to serve over HTTP</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">The function name, type hints and docstring become the tool's name, schema and description. Write the docstring for the model: what it does, inputs, and what it returns. A TypeScript SDK exists with the same concepts. In mcp 1.x the same class was <code>FastMCP</code>, imported from <code>mcp.server.fastmcp</code>; that import fails on 2.x.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Add it to Claude Code and test it</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># local stdio server, shared with the team via .mcp.json in the repo
claude mcp add --scope project pipeline-status -- python mcp/pipeline_status.py

# remote server over HTTP
claude mcp add --transport http pipeline-status https://mcp.example.com/mcp

claude mcp list
# inside Claude Code: /mcp shows servers, status and tools

# interactive testing without a model
npx @modelcontextprotocol/inspector python mcp/pipeline_status.py</code></pre></div>
    <ul>
      <li>Project scope writes <code>.mcp.json</code>, so everyone on the repo gets the same server.</li>
      <li>Keep credentials out of <code>.mcp.json</code>; servers read Application Default Credentials or environment variables.</li>
      <li>Approve which tools may run without asking in Claude Code's permission settings.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Call MCP servers from the API</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>resp = client.beta.messages.create(
    model="claude-opus-5-5",
    max_tokens=16000,
    betas=["mcp-client-2025-11-20"],
    mcp_servers=[{"type": "url", "name": "pipeline",
                  "url": "https://mcp.example.com/mcp"}],
    tools=[{"type": "mcp_toolset", "mcp_server_name": "pipeline"}],
    messages=[{"role": "user", "content": "Summarise yesterday's pipeline run."}],
)</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">The MCP connector needs both halves: the server in <code>mcp_servers</code> and a matching <code>mcp_toolset</code> in <code>tools</code>. It reaches remote (HTTP) servers only; check the docs for authentication options and current beta name.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>MCP and a data pipeline: four patterns</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Pattern</th><th>What the MCP server exposes</th><th>Who uses it</th><th>Rule</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>1. Pipeline observability</td><td>Run records, stage states, freshness per source, failures with reasons</td><td>Engineers in Claude Code: "why did RAW_LOAD fail on 19-09?"</td><td>Read-only. Reports UNKNOWN when it cannot read the ledger.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2. Governed warehouse access</td><td>Table catalog, schemas, a guarded query tool</td><td>Analysts and engineers asking questions in natural language</td><td>Dry-run first, byte cap, allow-listed datasets, read-only service account</td></tr>
      <tr><td>3. Data catalog and lineage</td><td>Table descriptions, owners, upstream and downstream models (dbt manifest, Dataplex)</td><td>Anyone changing a model: "what breaks if I rename this column?"</td><td>Generated from the source of truth, never hand-maintained</td></tr>
      <tr><td>4. LLM step inside the pipeline</td><td>Reference data or lookups the model needs while extracting or classifying</td><td>A pipeline job calling a model (e.g. extracting product names from support tickets)</td><td>Prefer plain tool calls or batch jobs; validate outputs; never let the model write to production tables directly</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note">MCP sits beside the pipeline, not inside its critical path. The pipeline must run, record and alert without any AI involved; MCP makes it easier to ask questions about it.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>A guarded BigQuery query tool</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>ALLOWED = {"analytics", "mart", "ops"}
MAX_BYTES = 5 * 1024**3                       # 5 GiB per query

@mcp.tool()
def run_query(sql: str) -&gt; dict:
    """Run a read-only GoogleSQL SELECT over the analytics, mart and ops datasets.
    Returns at most 200 rows. Fails if the query would scan more than 5 GiB."""
    if not sql.lstrip().lower().startswith(("select", "with")):
        return {"error": "only SELECT queries are allowed"}
    dry = bq.query(sql, job_config=bigquery.QueryJobConfig(
        dry_run=True, use_query_cache=False))
    datasets = {t.dataset_id for t in dry.referenced_tables}
    if not datasets &lt;= ALLOWED:
        return {"error": f"datasets not allowed: {sorted(datasets - ALLOWED)}"}
    if dry.total_bytes_processed &gt; MAX_BYTES:
        return {"error": f"would scan {dry.total_bytes_processed/1e9:.1f} GB; add filters"}
    job = bq.query(sql, job_config=bigquery.QueryJobConfig(maximum_bytes_billed=MAX_BYTES))
    rows = [dict(r) for r in job.result(max_results=200)]
    return {"rows": rows, "bytes_processed": job.total_bytes_processed}</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">The real protection is the service account: give it only <code>dataViewer</code> on those datasets and <code>jobUser</code>. The string checks are a convenience, not a security boundary.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Deploying a remote MCP server</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Serve with Streamable HTTP and run it on <a href="#cloudrun">Cloud Run</a> with its own service account.</li>
      <li>Require authentication: OAuth for user-facing clients (the MCP authorisation spec builds on OAuth 2.1), or Cloud Run IAM for internal callers.</li>
      <li>Rate-limit and log every tool call with the caller's identity.</li>
      <li>Version tools like an API: add new ones rather than changing behaviour under the same name.</li>
      <li>Ready-made servers exist for many systems, for example Google's MCP Toolbox for Databases (BigQuery, Postgres and others). Review what they expose before connecting them.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Designing good MCP tools</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Few, task-shaped tools</b> ("pipeline status for a date"): <i>our view</i> is that they serve a model better than many thin wrappers over every API call. <i>The other view:</i> thin wrappers are quicker to build and let the model combine calls itself.</li>
      <li><b>Clear names and descriptions</b>: the model chooses tools from them.</li>
      <li><b>Typed, validated inputs</b> with enums and formats where possible.</li>
      <li><b>Compact outputs:</b> summarise and paginate; huge results waste context.</li>
      <li><b>Honest errors:</b> return what failed and how to fix it; distinguish "no data" from "could not read".</li>
      <li><b>Read-only by default;</b> any write needs explicit human approval in the client.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Security</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>An MCP server runs with its own credentials: give it least privilege, as you would a service.</li>
      <li>Only install servers you trust and have reviewed; a malicious server can return instructions that steer the model (tool poisoning).</li>
      <li>Treat tool outputs as untrusted data in the model's context.</li>
      <li>Never put secrets in tool descriptions, outputs or <code>.mcp.json</code>.</li>
      <li>Audit tool calls; alert on unusual volume or failed authorisation.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

















  <article class="card"><h3>Ones we build</h3>
    <p><a href="/cistern/">Cistern</a> puts an MCP server in front of a data pod you own, and <a href="/penstock/">Penstock</a> works with Claude Code over MCP. Both are open source (Apache 2.0).</p>
    <p class="note">They are ours, so we are not neutral.</p>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Explain servers, clients, tools, resources and transports</li><li>Add an existing MCP server to Claude Code and use it</li><li>Know the security risks of untrusted servers</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Build an MCP server with the Python SDK and test it with the Inspector</li><li>Expose read-only pipeline or warehouse tools with guardrails</li><li>Share servers with a team via project configuration</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Design task-shaped tool sets with honest errors and compact outputs</li><li>Deploy remote servers on Cloud Run with OAuth or IAM</li><li>Integrate MCP with data pipelines without putting it on the critical path</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Set MCP standards, review and approval process for the organisation</li><li>Build shared MCP platforms over core data and systems</li><li>Contribute to the protocol ecosystem</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>MCP specification and transports</li><li>MCP authorisation (OAuth)</li><li>Tool design for agents</li><li>MCP Toolbox for Databases</li><li>Tool poisoning and prompt injection defences</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://modelcontextprotocol.io/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Model Context Protocol</a><span class="lnote"> Specification, concepts and SDKs</span></li><li><a href="https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/python-sdk" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MCP Python SDK</a><span class="lnote"> MCPServer (formerly FastMCP) and server examples</span></li><li><a href="https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/typescript-sdk" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MCP TypeScript SDK</a><span class="lnote"> Servers and clients in TypeScript</span></li><li><a href="https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/inspector" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MCP Inspector</a><span class="lnote"> Test servers interactively</span></li><li><a href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/mcp" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Claude Code: MCP</a><span class="lnote"> Adding servers, scopes and permissions</span></li><li><a href="https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/agents-and-tools/mcp-connector" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MCP connector (Claude API)</a><span class="lnote"> Calling remote MCP servers from the API</span></li><li><a href="https://github.com/googleapis/genai-toolbox" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MCP Toolbox for Databases</a><span class="lnote"> Google's MCP server for BigQuery, Postgres and more</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/modelcontextprotocol/modelcontextprotocol/main/docs/specification/2025-11-25/basic/transports.mdx" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MCP spec 2025-11-25: transports (stdio, Streamable HTTP)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/modelcontextprotocol/modelcontextprotocol/main/docs/specification/2025-11-25/basic/authorization.mdx" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MCP spec 2025-11-25: authorization (OAuth 2.1, optional)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/modelcontextprotocol/python-sdk/main/README.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MCP Python SDK README (MCPServer)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/modelcontextprotocol/python-sdk/main/docs/migration.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MCP Python SDK migration guide (FastMCP renamed, run(transport=...))</a>; <a href="https://pypi.org/pypi/mcp/json" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PyPI mcp (current 2.x)</a>; <a href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/mcp.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Claude Code MCP docs</a>; <a href="https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/agents-and-tools/mcp-connector.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MCP connector (Claude API)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/modelcontextprotocol/inspector/main/docs/v1-to-v2-migration.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MCP Inspector README and v1-to-v2 guide</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/googleapis/genai-toolbox/main/README.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MCP Toolbox README (repo badge names googleapis/mcp-toolbox)</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/python/docs/reference/bigquery/latest/google.cloud.bigquery.job.QueryJob" target="_blank" rel="noopener">BigQuery Python QueryJob reference</a>. A few claims could not be confirmed at their official source from here and are being re-checked.</p><p class="runcode">A question, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/enrichmeai.github.io/issues/new?title=%5BMCP%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>. Nothing here is tracked and there are no comments to store: questions live in the open, where the next learner can find the answer.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= AGENT HARNESS ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-agentharness" data-name="Agent Harness">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Agent harness</h2><p>A model on its own only produces text. The <b>harness</b> is everything around it that turns it into an agent: the loop, the tools, context management, permissions, checks, memory, and the record of what happened. How harnesses are built, what makes one reliable, and the options from writing your own to using a managed one. Model basics are on <a href="#llm">LLMs</a>.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Parts of a harness</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Does</th><th>Gets it wrong when</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Loop</td><td>Send messages, run requested tools, return results, repeat until done</td><td>No step or token budget: runaway cost</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Tools</td><td>Functions the model can call (files, shell, search, APIs, MCP)</td><td>Too many, vague descriptions, huge outputs, no error detail</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Context management</td><td>Decide what the model sees: instructions, history, retrieved files; compact or clear old content</td><td>Context fills with noise and the model loses the thread</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Memory</td><td>Persist knowledge across sessions (project files, notes, memory tools)</td><td>Stale memory states things that are no longer true</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Permissions and sandbox</td><td>Limit what tools may touch; ask a human for risky actions</td><td>Broad credentials; destructive commands unguarded</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Gates and hooks</td><td>Deterministic checks that run regardless of the model (lint, tests, policy)</td><td>The agent decides on its own when it is "done"</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Sub-agents</td><td>Delegate research or review to fresh contexts</td><td>Hand-offs lose detail; reviewers anchored on the builder's view</td></tr>
      <tr><td>State and resume</td><td>Save progress so work survives restarts</td><td>A crash loses hours of work</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Observability</td><td>Log every model call, tool call, cost and decision</td><td>Nobody can say why the agent did something</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Evaluation</td><td>Measure task success, cost and interventions on a test set</td><td>Changes judged by impression</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Build or use?</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>You own</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Manual loop on the model API</td><td>Everything (code on the <a href="#llm">LLMs</a> tab)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>SDK tool runner</td><td>Tools and hosting; the SDK runs the loop</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Claude Agent SDK</td><td>Prompt, options, hosting; Claude Code's harness and built-in tools come with it</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Managed agents (hosted)</td><td>Agent configuration and your own tools; the provider runs loop and sandbox</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Agent frameworks (e.g. LangGraph)</td><td>Graph of steps and state; framework handles orchestration</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Ready-made agents (Claude Code, Cowork, Copilot)</td><td>Configuration: context files, skills, hooks, connectors</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note"><i>Our view:</i> configure a ready-made harness before building one. Build your own only when the task, tools or deployment need something they cannot give. <i>The other view:</i> building the loop yourself avoids depending on one vendor&rsquo;s harness; when the agent is the product, owning the loop can be the point.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Principles for a reliable harness</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Done is decided by checks, not by the model:</b> tests, linters, schema validation, a reviewer.</li>
      <li><b>Bound everything:</b> steps, tokens, time, money, attempts at the same failure (three, then stop and report).</li>
      <li><b>Least privilege tools;</b> human approval for anything irreversible or external.</li>
      <li><b>Append-only history:</b> do not rewrite past turns; summarise forward when context is full.</li>
      <li><b>Fresh eyes for review:</b> a separate agent or person who sees the code, not the builder's conclusions.</li>
      <li><b>Make lessons permanent:</b> each failure becomes a rule, test or hook in the repo.</li>
      <li><b>Never let the agent weaken a gate</b> (skip a test, disable a check) to get to green.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Context engineering</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Put stable things first</b> (system prompt, tools) so they are cached and consistent.</li>
      <li><b>Load just in time:</b> give the agent tools to find files instead of pasting everything up front.</li>
      <li><b>Keep tool outputs compact;</b> return summaries with a way to fetch details.</li>
      <li><b>Compact or clear</b> old tool results in long sessions.</li>
      <li><b>Use sub-agents</b> to read widely and return only conclusions.</li>
      <li><b>Write state to files</b> (plans, progress notes) so a new context can pick up the work.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Designing tools for agents</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Few tools, each matching a task a person would do ("search orders", "run tests"), not every API endpoint.</li>
      <li>Names and descriptions written for the model: what it does, when to use it, what it returns.</li>
      <li>Typed inputs with enums and examples; strict schemas.</li>
      <li>Errors that say what went wrong and how to fix it.</li>
      <li>Distinguish "no results" from "could not search".</li>
      <li>Read-only by default; writes need confirmation.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Failure modes to design against</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Runaway loops</b> repeating the same failing action.</li>
      <li><b>Context rot:</b> long sessions where early instructions are drowned out.</li>
      <li><b>False completion:</b> claiming success without running the check.</li>
      <li><b>Gaming the check:</b> editing tests or disabling guards to pass.</li>
      <li><b>Prompt injection</b> from files, web pages or tool outputs.</li>
      <li><b>Scope creep:</b> changing more than the task asked.</li>
      <li><b>Credential misuse:</b> acting with more access than the task needs.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Measuring a harness</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Task success rate</b> on a fixed set of real tasks, judged by checks.</li>
      <li><b>Cost and time per completed task</b>, not per request.</li>
      <li><b>Intervention rate:</b> how often a human had to step in.</li>
      <li><b>Regression rate:</b> changes that later had to be reverted.</li>
      <li><b>Gate failures caught</b> before merge vs after.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>










  <article class="card"><h3>One we build: Penstock</h3>
    <p><a href="/penstock/">Penstock</a> is a headless, self-hosted agent harness: it runs agents from CI, Slack or webhooks on Copilot, Anthropic, OpenAI or a local Ollama model, and with database storage it logs every model call and tool call. It is open source (Apache 2.0). Run together with Cistern, the two are <a href="/headworks/">Headworks</a>.</p>
    <p class="note">It is ours, so we are not neutral: compare it with the other harnesses on this page.</p>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Explain loop, tools, context, permissions and gates</li><li>Configure a ready-made harness (context file, permissions)</li><li>Recognise runaway loops and false completion</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Build a bounded tool loop with honest errors and logging</li><li>Write agent-friendly tools</li><li>Add deterministic gates and a separate reviewer</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Design harnesses with context engineering, memory and resume</li><li>Evaluate harnesses on task success, cost and interventions</li><li>Defend against prompt injection and gate gaming</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Build agent platforms and shared harness components</li><li>Set safety and governance policy for autonomous agents</li><li>Research and adopt new agent patterns with evidence</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>Context engineering</li><li>Tool design for agents</li><li>Multi-agent orchestration</li><li>Agent evaluation</li><li>Sandboxing and permission design</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/building-effective-agents" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Building effective agents</a><span class="lnote"> Workflows vs agents, patterns</span></li><li><a href="https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Effective context engineering for AI agents</a><span class="lnote"> Managing what the model sees</span></li><li><a href="https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/writing-tools-for-agents" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Writing effective tools for agents</a><span class="lnote"> Tool design</span></li><li><a href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/agent-sdk" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Claude Agent SDK</a><span class="lnote"> Claude Code's harness as a library</span></li><li><a href="https://langchain-ai.github.io/langgraph/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">LangGraph</a><span class="lnote"> Graph-based agent orchestration</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/overview.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Claude Agent SDK overview</a>; <a href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/hooks.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Claude Code hooks (deterministic gates)</a>; <a href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/sub-agents.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Claude Code subagents</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/enrichmeai/penstock/main/README.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Penstock README (main)</a>; <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/penstock/blob/v0.6.0/README.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Penstock README at</a>.</p><p class="runcode">A question, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/enrichmeai.github.io/issues/new?title=%5BAgent%20Harness%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>. Nothing here is tracked and there are no comments to store: questions live in the open, where the next learner can find the answer.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= HUMANS AND AGENTS ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-humanagents" data-name="Humans &amp; Agents">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Humans and agents: working together, and personas</h2><p>An AI agent plans and uses tools on its own, but someone still sets the goal, owns the outcome and decides what is safe to do without asking. This page is about that relationship: how to brief an agent, how an agent should report back, how much freedom to give it, and how to build a named agent (a persona) with its own role, skills and model. Facts are linked at the end; where a card is our view, it says so and gives the other view.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Who does what</h3>
    <p><i>Analysis, and our view.</i> A useful starting split. Your team may draw the lines differently; the point is to draw them on purpose.</p>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>The person</th><th>The agent</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Sets the goal and says why it matters</td><td>Turns the goal into steps and asks when the goal is unclear</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Decides what &ldquo;done&rdquo; means and what must not change</td><td>Does the searching, drafting, running and checking</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Approves anything irreversible, costly, or visible to others</td><td>Stops and asks at those points, instead of guessing</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Owns the result, whoever typed it</td><td>Reports what it did, with evidence, and what it could not do</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Corrects it, and writes the lesson down where the next session will read it</td><td>Follows the written rules, and says when a rule seems to conflict with the task</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note"><i>The other view:</i> some teams give agents whole tasks end to end and review only the result. That can work for well-tested, reversible work; the table below helps you decide where.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>How to brief an agent</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>The goal and the why:</b> &ldquo;Add a retry to the payment call, because the gateway returns occasional 503s.&rdquo;</li>
      <li><b>Context it cannot guess:</b> the files, the error, the decision already made, what was tried.</li>
      <li><b>Constraints:</b> what must not change, which tools or commands are off limits, the budget.</li>
      <li><b>Done means:</b> the test that must pass, the output you expect, how you will check it.</li>
      <li><b>When to stop and ask:</b> before deleting, deploying, spending, or messaging anyone.</li>
      <li><b>Give it a role.</b> Anthropic&rsquo;s documentation says that setting a role in the system prompt focuses the model&rsquo;s behaviour and tone, and that even a single sentence makes a difference.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>How an agent should report back</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Evidence, not confidence:</b> the test output, the command and its result, the link.</li>
      <li><b>What it did not do,</b> and why: a check that could not run is &ldquo;not run&rdquo;, never &ldquo;passed&rdquo;.</li>
      <li><b>Questions at checkpoints.</b> Anthropic&rsquo;s guide to building agents: agents can pause for human feedback at checkpoints or when they meet blockers, and it is common to include stopping conditions such as a maximum number of iterations.</li>
      <li><b>Asking the person properly.</b> The Model Context Protocol has <i>elicitation</i>: a server can ask the user for more information through the client, and the user can accept, decline or cancel. The specification says servers must not use its form mode to ask for secrets or credentials.</li>
      <li><b>Short and plain:</b> the person reads the summary, not the log.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>How much freedom to give: choose by requirement</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>If this is true of the task</th><th>It leans towards</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Easy to undo (a branch, a draft, a local file)</td><td>Let it run, with stopping conditions; review the result</td></tr>
      <tr><td>A test or check can prove it right</td><td>Let it run until the check passes; read the evidence</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Hard or impossible to undo (deleting data, a deploy, a payment, an email)</td><td>The agent proposes, a person approves each time</td></tr>
      <tr><td>The cost of an error is high, or falls on someone else</td><td>A person approves at each checkpoint</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Regulated, or decides things about people</td><td>Documented human oversight, required by law in some places (see below)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Nobody can say what &ldquo;done&rdquo; looks like yet</td><td>Work with it step by step; it is not ready to hand over</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Automation bias</b> is the tendency to favour suggestions from automated systems and ignore contradicting information, even when that information is correct. Wikipedia lists contributing factors including lack of confidence in your own abilities, a lack of other information to hand, and the wish to save time and effort on complex tasks or heavy workloads. A review that only clicks &ldquo;approve&rdquo; is not oversight.</li>
      <li><b>The law is arriving.</b> The EU&rsquo;s Artificial Intelligence Act entered into force on 1 August 2024. High-risk uses, such as recruitment, education, critical infrastructure and justice, carry obligations that include human oversight; limited-risk uses carry transparency obligations.</li>
      <li><b>Agents cost more and errors compound.</b> Anthropic recommends testing agents extensively in sandboxed environments, with guardrails.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Building a persona: a named agent</h3>
    <p>A <b>persona</b> is a named agent with a defined job: who it is, what it is for, what it may touch, which skills it loads and which model it runs on. A name makes it easier for people to say who did what, and to give each one a narrow, testable remit.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>In Claude Code, a subagent</b> is a file with a <code>name</code>, a <code>description</code>, a <code>tools</code> list, a <code>model</code> and a system prompt. Each runs in its own context window with its own permissions. Limiting its tools enforces what it may do; the <code>model</code> field picks the model for that agent alone (it inherits the main one if you leave it out).</li>
      <li><b>Skills</b> are folders with a <code>SKILL.md</code> of instructions. A skill&rsquo;s body loads only when it is used, so reference material costs almost nothing until needed. A subagent can preload chosen skills with its <code>skills</code> field. Claude Code&rsquo;s skills follow the Agent Skills open standard, which works across several AI tools.</li>
    </ul>
    <h4>What you can do today, and what is only possible</h4>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Idea</th><th>Status in Claude Code (from its documentation)</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>A named agent, say &ldquo;Vikram, the backend engineer&rdquo;, with its own instructions and tools</td><td><b>Available.</b> A subagent file; Claude delegates to it when a task matches its <code>description</code>, or you call it by name with <code>@agent-&lt;name&gt;</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>One named agent carrying one skill or several</td><td><b>Available.</b> The <code>skills</code> field preloads the full content of each listed skill; it can still use other skills when needed</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Each named agent on its own model</td><td><b>Available, for Claude models:</b> <code>model</code> takes <code>sonnet</code>, <code>opus</code>, <code>haiku</code>, <code>fable</code>, a full model ID, or <code>inherit</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>A script that runs many agents and cross-checks them</td><td><b>Available.</b> Dynamic workflows run many subagents from a script, and the script can name a model for each stage</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Agents that talk to each other under a lead</td><td><b>Experimental:</b> agent teams, disabled by default</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Named agents on models from different vendors (one on Copilot, one on a local Ollama)</td><td><b>Not a Claude Code subagent feature.</b> Penstock&rsquo;s README describes choosing one vendor in its configuration. Per-agent vendors are a possibility, not a product</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># .claude/agents/reviewer.md
---
name: reviewer
description: Independent reviewer of a finished change. Use before a pull request is opened.
tools: Read, Grep, Glob, Bash
model: sonnet
skills: [house-style, security-checklist]
---
You review a finished change. You do not edit files.
Check every claim against its source and report what you could not verify.
Report findings as: severity, file:line, what is wrong, the evidence.</code></pre></div>
    <p><b>User personas are different, and both matter.</b> In user-centred design, a persona is a semi-fictional profile of a typical user, built from interviews and surveys; Alan Cooper introduced the idea in software design. Agent personas serve user personas: design the user persona first (see <a href="#designux">Design Thinking &amp; UX</a>), then decide which agents help that person, and what each one must never do on their behalf.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Where Headworks fits</h3>
    <p><a href="/headworks/">Headworks</a> is EnrichMeAI&rsquo;s own take on an agent you can hand real work to: Penstock and Cistern running together on your machine. What is released today, from each project&rsquo;s README at its latest release:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>You choose the model.</b> Penstock&rsquo;s model layer is pluggable: GitHub Copilot by default, or Anthropic, OpenAI or a local Ollama, switched in configuration.</li>
      <li><b>Memory you review.</b> Penstock&rsquo;s memory server, used from Claude Code or Claude Desktop over MCP, can write a <i>draft</i> episode of the work for you to review; its recall does not read drafts until you move one into the record and commit it. Recall inside Penstock&rsquo;s own agent is off by default.</li>
      <li><b>Access you grant.</b> Cistern gives the agent its own credential; you grant it read access to one folder, it is refused everywhere else, and you can revoke the grant from a terminal.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">A named <a href="/persona/">persona</a> built on that record is <b>planned</b>, not released.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Inspiration: named agents to try</h3>
    <p><i>Ideas, not recommendations. Each is a small, narrow agent you can build with today&rsquo;s subagents and skills; try one, measure it, keep it if it earns its place.</i></p>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Agent</th><th>Its job</th><th>What it must never do</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>A reviewer</td><td>Checks a finished change against its sources and the house rules; reports findings with evidence</td><td>Edit files (give it read-only tools)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>&ldquo;Vikram&rdquo;, backend; &ldquo;Asha&rdquo;, frontend</td><td>Each owns one part of the codebase, with that part&rsquo;s skills (API conventions; accessibility and design tokens)</td><td>Change the other&rsquo;s folders</td></tr>
      <tr><td>A test writer</td><td>Writes the failing test first, then hands over</td><td>Weaken or skip an existing test</td></tr>
      <tr><td>A docs keeper</td><td>After each merge, finds the docs the change made wrong and proposes fixes</td><td>Merge its own proposals</td></tr>
      <tr><td>An on-call helper</td><td>Reads alerts and logs, suggests the likely cause and the runbook step</td><td>Run anything against production</td></tr>
      <tr><td>A release-notes writer</td><td>Drafts notes from merged pull requests and commit messages</td><td>Publish them</td></tr>
      <tr><td>A tutor</td><td>Explains, quizzes, and asks you to try first, for something you are learning</td><td>Hand over the answer before you have attempted it</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note">One habit that makes any of these better (<i>our view</i>): when a person corrects an agent, write the lesson into its instructions or a skill, so the next run starts from it.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>A possibility for the future</h3>
    <p><i>Our view, not a description of anything released.</i> Teams of named agents, each with a narrow role, its own skills and a model chosen for its job, possibly from different vendors, working alongside people who set goals, approve the irreversible and own the result. A shared, reviewed record of decisions is what would let such a team learn without repeating mistakes.</p>
    <p class="note"><i>The other view:</i> more agents mean more coordination, more cost and more places for errors to compound. Anthropic&rsquo;s advice is to find the simplest solution possible and add complexity only when it is needed. Start with one well-briefed agent, and add a second only when you can say what it does that the first cannot.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/building-effective-agents" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Anthropic: Building effective agents</a>; <a href="https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/prompt-engineering/system-prompts" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Anthropic docs: Give Claude a role</a>; <a href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/sub-agents" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Claude Code: Subagents</a>; <a href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/skills" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Claude Code: Skills</a>; <a href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/agents" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Claude Code: Run agents in parallel</a>; <a href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/workflows" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Claude Code: Dynamic workflows</a>; <a href="https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/modelcontextprotocol/blob/main/docs/specification/draft/client/elicitation.mdx" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MCP specification (draft): Elicitation</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Automation_bias" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Automation bias</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_Intelligence_Act" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Artificial Intelligence Act</a> (the official EU text could not be reached from here); <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Persona_(user_experience)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Persona (user experience)</a>; <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/penstock" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Penstock README</a> and <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/cistern" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cistern README</a> at their latest release tags.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= BLUEPRINTS ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-blueprints" data-name="Frameworks vs AI Boilerplate">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Frameworks and blueprints vs AI-generated boilerplate</h2><p>AI can generate a new service, pipeline or Terraform stack in minutes. That makes it tempting to generate every time. This tab explains why a well-built framework usually wins, when generation is the right call, and how to use AI to build and maintain the framework instead.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>The core argument</h3>
    <p>Writing code was never the main cost; <b>owning</b> it is. Every copy of generated boilerplate must be reviewed, secured, tested, upgraded, debugged and understood by the next person. Generation makes the cheap part cheaper and multiplies the expensive part. A framework makes each decision once, tests it once, fixes it once, and upgrades everyone together.</p>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th></th><th>Generate per project</th><th>Framework / blueprint</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Time to first version</td><td>Minutes</td><td>Minutes, once the framework exists</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Consistency</td><td>Each copy differs subtly (retries, logging, auth, errors)</td><td>Same behaviour everywhere</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Fixing a bug or CVE</td><td>Find and patch N copies; some get missed</td><td>Patch once, release, bump versions</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Review burden</td><td>Hundreds of generated lines per PR</td><td>Only the business-specific part</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Security</td><td>Each copy may reintroduce a known hole</td><td>Secure defaults, tested once</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Upgrades (Java, Spring, providers)</td><td>N separate migrations</td><td>One migration in the framework, then a version bump</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Onboarding</td><td>Every repo is a new dialect</td><td>Learn the framework once</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Knowledge</td><td>Decisions live in prompts nobody kept</td><td>Decisions in versioned, reviewed code and docs</td></tr>
      <tr><td>AI output quality</td><td>Model guesses your conventions from public code</td><td>Model extends your framework; smaller, consistent output</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Total cost over 2 years</td><td>Grows with every copy</td><td>Mostly fixed, shared</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>A cost model in one line</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>generated:  N copies × (review + security fix + upgrade + drift) per year
framework:  build once + maintain once + N × (small version bump)

With N = 15 services, 3 framework-level fixes a year, ~1 day each:
  generated:  15 × 3 = 45 fix-days, plus drift
  framework:   1 × 3 =  3 fix-days + 15 version bumps (automatable)</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">The numbers are illustrative; the shape is the point. The gap grows with every new copy and every year.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>When generation is the right call</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>One-off scripts</b> and investigations you will delete.</li>
      <li><b>Prototypes and spikes</b> to learn whether an idea works.</li>
      <li><b>Filling a framework's slots:</b> the business logic, the new query, its tests.</li>
      <li><b>Codemods:</b> AI-written scripts that apply the same change across many repos (an upgrade, a rename), reviewed once.</li>
      <li><b>Tests and documentation</b> for code that follows the framework.</li>
      <li><b>Before the third repetition:</b> the first two times, write or generate it directly; you do not yet know what to abstract.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>When not to build a framework</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Rule of three:</b> abstract after three real uses, not one imagined future.</li>
      <li><b>Inner-platform effect:</b> a framework so configurable it becomes a worse version of the language or tool it wraps.</li>
      <li><b>Business logic in shared libraries:</b> forces lock-step releases between teams. Share infrastructure concerns, not domain rules.</li>
      <li><b>No owner:</b> a framework without a maintaining team decays faster than copies.</li>
      <li><b>Escape hatches missing:</b> teams need a supported way to do the unusual thing without forking.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>What a blueprint looks like in this stack</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Area</th><th>Blueprint</th><th>What it decides once</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Services</td><td>Service template + internal Spring Boot starter or Quarkus extension</td><td>Logging format, tracing, health checks, error responses, security config, Dockerfile</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Jobs</td><td>A job library + Terraform module for a Cloud Run job</td><td>Run records, exit codes, counters, retries, IAM, scheduling</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Data</td><td>dbt macros, generic tests, packages, project template</td><td>Layer conventions, key computation, date parsing that rejects, test baseline</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Infrastructure</td><td>Terraform modules (bucket, dataset, job, service)</td><td>Encryption, labels, IAM, deletion protection, naming</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Delivery</td><td>Harness or GitHub Actions templates; reusable workflows</td><td>Build once, scan, deploy by artefact, approvals, rollback</td></tr>
      <tr><td>APIs</td><td>Apigee shared flows; OpenAPI style guide and linting</td><td>Auth, rate limits, error format, versioning</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Observability</td><td>Dashboard and alert templates; OpenTelemetry config</td><td>Golden signals, SLO alerts, runbook links</td></tr>
      <tr><td>AI</td><td>Repo context files (CLAUDE.md), skills, hooks, an MCP server template, eval harness</td><td>How agents work in the repo, which checks run, what "done" means</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Using AI to build the framework</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Collect the 3+ existing implementations; ask AI to list what they share and where they differ.</li>
      <li>Decide the framework's API and its escape hatches yourself; that is the design work.</li>
      <li>Let AI draft the framework code, tests and documentation against that design.</li>
      <li>Review it like any critical library; add contract tests.</li>
      <li>Let AI write codemods that migrate each existing project onto it, one PR per repo.</li>
      <li>Point every repo's AI context file at the framework, so future work extends it instead of reinventing it.</li>
    </ol>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Keeping a framework healthy</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>An owning team</b> with a roadmap, changelog and support channel.</li>
      <li><b>Semantic versioning</b>; breaking changes rare, announced, with migration guides and codemods.</li>
      <li><b>Automated upgrades</b> (Renovate or Dependabot) open version bumps in every consumer.</li>
      <li><b>Golden path, not a cage:</b> the easy way is the right way, and the unusual way is possible.</li>
      <li><b>Scorecards</b> (e.g. a scorecard plugin in a Backstage catalogue) show which services are on current versions.</li>
      <li><b>Measure adoption and time to first deploy</b> for new services.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Decision guide</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Will this exist in more than one place for more than a few months? → <b>blueprint</b>.</li>
      <li>Does it carry security, compliance or reliability rules? → <b>blueprint</b>, even for two uses.</li>
      <li>Is it unique business logic? → <b>write it</b> (with AI help) inside the blueprint's slots.</li>
      <li>Is it throwaway or exploratory? → <b>generate</b>, and delete it afterwards.</li>
      <li>Is it the same change across many repos? → <b>AI-written codemod</b>, reviewed once.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">AI is the bulldozer; the framework is the road. Build the road once and drive on it, rather than bulldozing a new track for every trip.</p>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Explain why owning generated code costs more than writing it</li><li>Use the team's templates and libraries instead of generating new boilerplate</li><li>Fill a blueprint's slots with AI help</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Spot repeated code and propose a blueprint after the third use</li><li>Write codemods with AI to apply a change across repos</li><li>Point AI context files at the blueprints</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Design framework APIs with escape hatches</li><li>Own a blueprint: versioning, docs, migration guides</li><li>Decide build vs generate with a cost model</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Run a platform team with golden paths and scorecards</li><li>Set the organisation's policy on AI generation vs shared frameworks</li><li>Measure developer productivity and adoption</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>Platform engineering and golden paths</li><li>Backstage software templates and scorecards</li><li>Codemods (OpenRewrite for Java, LibCST for Python)</li><li>Semantic versioning and deprecation</li><li>Inner-platform effect</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://tag-app-delivery.cncf.io/whitepapers/platforms/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Platform engineering (CNCF white paper)</a><span class="lnote"> What internal platforms are for</span></li><li><a href="https://backstage.io/docs/features/software-templates/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Backstage software templates</a><span class="lnote"> Scaffolding from organisation templates</span></li><li><a href="https://docs.openrewrite.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OpenRewrite</a><span class="lnote"> Automated refactoring and upgrades for Java</span></li><li><a href="https://docs.renovatebot.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Renovate</a><span class="lnote"> Automated dependency and version updates</span></li><li><a href="https://semver.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Semantic Versioning</a><span class="lnote"> Version numbers that communicate compatibility</span></li><li><a href="https://teamtopologies.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Team Topologies</a><span class="lnote"> Platform teams and their consumers</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Inner-platform_effect&action=raw" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Inner-platform effect</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Rule_of_three_(computer_programming)&action=raw" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Rule of three (computer programming)</a>; <a href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/skills.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Claude Code skills (repo context files, skills)</a>; <a href="https://backstage.io/plugins/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Backstage plugin directory (scorecard plugins)</a>. A few claims could not be confirmed at their official source from here and are being re-checked.</p><p class="runcode">A question, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/enrichmeai.github.io/issues/new?title=%5BFrameworks%20vs%20AI%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>. Nothing here is tracked and there are no comments to store: questions live in the open, where the next learner can find the answer.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= CLAUDE CODE & COWORK ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-claudetools" data-name="Claude Code &amp; Cowork">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Claude Code and Claude Cowork</h2><p>Claude Code is Anthropic's agentic coding tool: it reads your repository, edits files, runs commands and tests, and works through tasks with you or on its own. Claude Cowork brings the same agentic way of working to non-coding knowledge work, on desktop, web and mobile. How each is set up, extended and kept safe.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Where Claude Code runs</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Surface</th><th>Use it for</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Terminal CLI (<code>claude</code>)</td><td>Interactive work in a local repository</td></tr>
      <tr><td>IDE extensions (VS Code, JetBrains)</td><td>The same agent beside your editor, with diffs inline</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Desktop app</td><td>Local and cloud sessions with a graphical interface</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Claude Code on the web</td><td>Cloud sessions in a managed container: work continues without your laptop</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Headless (<code>claude -p</code>)</td><td>Scripts and CI: one prompt in, result out (text or JSON)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>GitHub Actions (<code>claude-code-action</code>)</td><td>Respond to issues and PRs, e.g. a label that asks Claude to build a task</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Agent SDK</td><td>Claude Code's harness as a library inside your own application</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Everyday commands</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>claude                              # start in the current repo
claude "fix the failing date parser test"
claude -c                           # continue the last conversation
claude -r                           # pick an earlier session to resume
claude -p "summarise this diff" --output-format json &lt; diff.txt   # headless

# inside a session
/init        create a CLAUDE.md for this repo
/memory      edit memory files
/agents      how to create subagents (edit .claude/agents/)
/mcp         MCP servers and their status
/permissions view rules
/compact     summarise the conversation to free context
/clear       start fresh
Shift+Tab    cycle permission modes (incl. plan mode)
Esc          interrupt</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>The files that shape it</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>File</th><th>Holds</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>CLAUDE.md</code> (repo root, subfolders)</td><td>Project memory: what the system is, rules, commands, traps. Loaded every session.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>~/.claude/CLAUDE.md</code></td><td>Your personal preferences across projects</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>.claude/settings.json</code></td><td>Shared permissions, hooks, environment, model settings (committed)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>.claude/settings.local.json</code></td><td>Your local overrides (not committed)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>.claude/agents/*.md</code></td><td>Subagents: specialised helpers with their own prompt, tools and model</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>.claude/skills/&lt;name&gt;/SKILL.md</code></td><td>Skills: packaged procedures invoked by name or when relevant</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>.claude/commands/*.md</code></td><td>Custom slash commands (the older format: now merged into skills, still works)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>.mcp.json</code></td><td>MCP servers shared with the team</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note">These files are the blueprint for how agents work in your repo. Keep them short, specific and current; a stale rule misleads every session.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Permissions and hooks</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>// .claude/settings.json
{
  "permissions": {
    "allow": ["Bash(./gradlew test:*)", "Bash(git status)", "Bash(git diff:*)"],
    "deny":  ["Read(./.env)", "Bash(rm -rf:*)"]
  },
  "hooks": {
    "PreToolUse": [
      { "matcher": "Bash",
        "hooks": [{ "type": "command", "command": ".claude/hooks/guard-destructive.sh" }] }
    ],
    "PostToolUse": [
      { "matcher": "Edit|Write",
        "hooks": [{ "type": "command", "command": ".claude/hooks/check-edited-file.sh" }] }
    ],
    "Stop": [
      { "hooks": [{ "type": "command", "command": ".claude/hooks/stop-gate.sh" }] }
    ]
  }
}</code></pre></div>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Permission modes:</b> default (ask), accept edits, plan (read and propose only), auto (runs everything, with background safety checks), dontAsk (only pre-approved tools), and bypassPermissions (isolated containers and VMs only).</li>
      <li><b>Hooks</b> run your scripts at events (before/after a tool, on prompt submit, at stop, at session start). A hook that exits with code 2 blocks the action where the event allows it (PreToolUse blocks the tool call, Stop prevents stopping) and its stderr is fed back to Claude. A PostToolUse hook runs after the tool, so exit code 2 only feeds stderr back.</li>
      <li><b>Use hooks for rules that must always hold</b> (lint after every edit, block pushes to <code>main</code>); use CLAUDE.md for guidance.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Subagents and skills</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>&lt;!-- simplified example of .claude/agents/reviewer.md --&gt;
---
name: reviewer
description: Independent reviewer of a finished diff before a PR is opened.
  Use at the end of every task.
tools: Read, Grep, Glob, Bash
model: sonnet
---
Review the diff against the task, CLAUDE.md and the official docs of every
library it touches. Report bugs, missing tests and unverified API use.
Do not edit files.</code></pre></div>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>&lt;!-- simplified example of .claude/skills/&lt;name&gt;/SKILL.md --&gt;
---
name: release-sync
description: Bring a project page up to date with that project's latest
  release. Use after a release or when a sync issue is open.
---
1. Read the release tag and changelog.
2. Update the version markers and the "what's new" block.
3. Run the release checker until it reports 0 findings.</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">Subagents keep their own context, so a reviewer is not anchored on the builder's reasoning. Skills turn a procedure that cost a debugging cycle into something every session follows.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>A worked example: an autonomous build loop</h3>
    <ol>
      <li><code>/new-issue</code> turns an idea into a ready issue.</li>
      <li><code>/build-task</code> builds it: verify docs, write a failing test, implement.</li>
      <li><b>Hooks</b> check every edit; a stop hook runs the full check before the turn can end.</li>
      <li>A <b>reviewer</b> subagent checks the finished diff independently.</li>
      <li>Fix what it finds, <b>at most three attempts</b>, then write a blocker summary instead of looping.</li>
      <li><code>/compound</code> turns what went wrong into a permanent guard (a test, hook or rule).</li>
      <li>Open a PR; a human (or a gated merge skill) merges.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="note">Each step is a skill, agent or hook committed to the repository, so every session follows the same loop.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Claude Cowork</h3>
    <p>Cowork applies Claude's agentic loop to everyday knowledge work rather than code: it works through multi-step tasks on your files, documents and connected apps, on desktop, web or mobile (sessions can run in the cloud), and checks in with you as it goes.</p>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th></th><th>Claude Code</th><th>Claude Cowork</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Main users</td><td>Engineers</td><td>Anyone doing knowledge work: analysts, operations, legal, sales, founders</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Works on</td><td>Repositories: code, tests, builds, Git</td><td>Folders, documents, spreadsheets, presentations, connected apps</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Extended by</td><td>CLAUDE.md, skills, subagents, hooks, MCP, plugins</td><td>Skills, plugins and connectors (MCP), often packaged per role</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Typical tasks</td><td>Implement a feature, fix a bug, review a PR, run a migration</td><td>Prepare a report from files, reconcile spreadsheets, draft documents, run a recurring brief</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Plugins</b> bundle skills, commands, agents and connectors for a role or team, so the organisation's way of working is installed rather than re-explained.</li>
      <li><b>Same rules as any agent:</b> give it a clear brief and a definition of done, limit what it can access, and review what it produces before it is sent anywhere.</li>
      <li>Features and availability change frequently; check Anthropic's current product documentation.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Working well with Claude Code</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Plan first</b> for anything non-trivial: plan mode, read the plan, then let it build.</li>
      <li><b>Give it a way to check itself:</b> exact test and lint commands in CLAUDE.md.</li>
      <li><b>One task per session;</b> <code>/clear</code> between unrelated tasks to keep context clean.</li>
      <li><b>Worktrees</b> for parallel sessions, so they never edit the same checkout.</li>
      <li><b>Verify claims:</b> ask what it ran and what the output was; "done" means a green check you can see.</li>
      <li><b>Turn every correction into a rule</b> in CLAUDE.md, a skill or a hook.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>










  <article class="card"><h3>One we build: Persona</h3>
    <p><a href="/persona/">Persona</a> is memory for AI coding agents: Penstock keeps what was asked, decided, refused and learned as plain files you own and, when memory is on, hands them to the model before it writes. It works with Claude Code over MCP. The persona that speaks from it is planned.</p>
    <p class="note">It is ours, so we are not neutral.</p>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Run Claude Code in a repo; review and accept its changes</li><li>Write a useful CLAUDE.md with commands and rules</li><li>Use plan mode and permission prompts sensibly</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Add hooks, skills, subagents and MCP servers for the team</li><li>Run headless and GitHub Action workflows</li><li>Use Cowork with plugins and connectors for knowledge work</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Design an agent workflow with gates, reviewers and attempt limits</li><li>Govern permissions, secrets and sandboxing</li><li>Turn recurring corrections into skills and hooks</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Set the organisation's agentic engineering practice</li><li>Build shared plugins and blueprints across teams</li><li>Measure productivity and quality impact</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>Hooks reference and exit codes</li><li>Subagent design</li><li>Skills and plugins</li><li>Claude Code in CI</li><li>Agent SDK</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/overview" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Claude Code overview</a><span class="lnote"> Install, surfaces, getting started</span></li><li><a href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/settings" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Settings and permissions</a><span class="lnote"> settings.json, rules, modes</span></li><li><a href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/hooks" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Hooks</a><span class="lnote"> Events, matchers, exit codes</span></li><li><a href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/sub-agents" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Subagents</a><span class="lnote"> Defining and using subagents</span></li><li><a href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/skills" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Skills</a><span class="lnote"> Packaging procedures</span></li><li><a href="https://github.com/anthropics/claude-code-action" target="_blank" rel="noopener">claude-code-action</a><span class="lnote"> Claude Code in GitHub Actions</span></li><li><a href="https://support.claude.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Claude Help Center</a><span class="lnote"> Product guides, including Cowork</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/cli-reference.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Claude Code CLI reference (-c, -r, -p, --output-format)</a>; <a href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/commands.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Claude Code commands (/init, /memory, /agents, /mcp, /permissions, /compact, /clear)</a>; <a href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/interactive-mode.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Claude Code interactive mode (Shift+Tab cycles modes)</a>; <a href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/permission-modes.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Claude Code permission modes</a>; <a href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/permissions.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Claude Code permissions (Bash(x:*) equals Bash(x *))</a>; <a href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/hooks.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Claude Code hooks (exit code 2 per event)</a>; <a href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/skills.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Claude Code skills (custom commands merged into skills)</a>; <a href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/sub-agents.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Claude Code subagents</a>; <a href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/memory.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Claude Code memory (CLAUDE.md locations)</a>; <a href="https://code.claude.com/docs/en/overview.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Claude Code overview (surfaces)</a>; <a href="https://claude.com/docs/cowork/overview.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cowork overview</a>; <a href="https://support.claude.com/en/articles/13345190-get-started-with-claude-cowork" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Get started with Claude Cowork</a>; <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/penstock/blob/v0.6.0/README.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Penstock README at</a>. A few claims could not be confirmed at their official source from here and are being re-checked.</p><p class="runcode">A question, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/enrichmeai.github.io/issues/new?title=%5BClaude%20Code%20%26%20Cowork%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>. Nothing here is tracked and there are no comments to store: questions live in the open, where the next learner can find the answer.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= COPILOT ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-copilot" data-name="GitHub Copilot">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>GitHub Copilot</h2><p>GitHub's AI assistant across the editor, the command line and github.com: completions, chat, agent mode, a coding agent that works on issues and opens pull requests, and code review. How to configure it for a team and how it compares with Claude Code.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Features</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Where</th><th>Does</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Code completions</td><td>IDE</td><td>Suggests the next lines as you type</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Copilot Chat</td><td>IDE, github.com, mobile</td><td>Answers questions about code, explains, drafts changes</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Agent mode</td><td>IDE (e.g. VS Code)</td><td>Plans and makes multi-file edits, runs commands and tests, iterates</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Copilot cloud agent (formerly coding agent)</td><td>github.com</td><td>Assign it an issue, or start it from chat or an automation; it works in a cloud environment and opens a pull request for review</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Copilot code review</td><td>Pull requests</td><td>Requested as a reviewer; leaves comments and suggestions</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Copilot CLI</td><td>Terminal</td><td>Agentic help in the shell</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Model choice</td><td>Chat and agents</td><td>Several model families, including Claude models, depending on plan and admin settings</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note">GitHub Copilot (for developers) is a different product from Microsoft 365 Copilot (for Office documents and email). Plans and feature names change often; check GitHub's docs.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Team configuration</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>File</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>.github/copilot-instructions.md</code></td><td>Repository-wide instructions: stack, conventions, commands</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>.github/instructions/*.instructions.md</code></td><td>Instructions that apply to matching paths</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>.github/prompts/*.prompt.md</code></td><td>Reusable prompt files</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>AGENTS.md</code></td><td>Agent instructions read by several tools</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>&lt;!-- .github/copilot-instructions.md --&gt;
This repo is a Spring Boot 3 WebFlux service on Java 21.
- Tests: ./gradlew :api:test (unit), CI runs integration tests.
- Never call .block() on the request path.
- Errors go through GlobalExceptionHandler; no onErrorResume in controllers.
- Use JdbcClient; no new JPA entities.</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">Keep one source of truth: if Claude Code reads <code>CLAUDE.md</code> and Copilot reads its own file, generate one from the other or point both at a shared document, so the rules cannot drift apart. Copilot also reads <code>AGENTS.md</code>, <code>CLAUDE.md</code> or <code>GEMINI.md</code> as agent instructions, so one shared file can serve both.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Admin and governance</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Policies</b> at enterprise and organisation level: which features and models are allowed.</li>
      <li><b>Content exclusion:</b> paths or repositories Copilot must not use as context.</li>
      <li><b>Public code filter:</b> block suggestions matching public code, where licence risk matters.</li>
      <li><b>MCP servers</b> for agent mode and the cloud agent, configured per repository or organisation.</li>
      <li><b>Cloud agent environment:</b> a setup workflow, a firewall allow-list, and branch protections; its PRs go through normal review and CI.</li>
      <li><b>Usage metrics</b> for adoption and acceptance rates.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Copilot and Claude Code compared</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th></th><th>GitHub Copilot</th><th>Claude Code</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Home</td><td>The IDE and github.com</td><td>The terminal and repository (plus IDE, desktop, web)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Strengths</td><td>Inline completions, tight GitHub integration (issues, PRs, review)</td><td>Long, multi-step agentic tasks; deep customisation with hooks, skills and subagents</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Configuration</td><td>Instruction and prompt files, policies</td><td>CLAUDE.md, settings, hooks, skills, agents, plugins</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Asynchronous work</td><td>Cloud agent on issues, chat and automations</td><td>Cloud sessions, GitHub Action, headless mode</td></tr>
      <tr><td>It leans this way if</td><td>Your work lives in the IDE and on github.com, and you want completions as you type</td><td>You hand over whole multi-step tasks and want to shape the agent with hooks, skills and subagents</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note">Many teams use both: completions while typing, an agent for whole tasks. The disciplines are the same: clear briefs, tests as the definition of done, human review, and rules written into the repo.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Using it responsibly</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Review every suggestion; accepted code is your code.</li>
      <li>Watch for invented APIs and outdated patterns; check official docs.</li>
      <li>Keep secrets out of files Copilot can read; use content exclusion for sensitive paths.</li>
      <li>Cloud-agent PRs get the same CI, review and security checks as human PRs.</li>
      <li>Prefer extending your frameworks to generating new boilerplate (<a href="#blueprints">Frameworks vs AI</a>).</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Use completions and chat; review every suggestion</li><li>Know the difference between GitHub Copilot and Microsoft 365 Copilot</li><li>Find and follow the repo's Copilot instructions</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Write repository and path instructions and prompt files</li><li>Use agent mode and the coding agent on scoped issues</li><li>Request and act on Copilot code review</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Configure policies, content exclusion and MCP for a team</li><li>Set up the coding agent environment and protections</li><li>Keep Copilot and Claude Code instructions consistent</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Own AI-assisted development policy across the organisation</li><li>Evaluate tools and models with real task metrics</li><li>Lead adoption and training</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>Copilot coding agent</li><li>Custom instructions and prompt files</li><li>MCP in Copilot</li><li>Copilot policies for enterprises</li><li>Measuring AI-assisted development</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://docs.github.com/en/copilot" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub Copilot documentation</a><span class="lnote"> Features, setup, administration</span></li><li><a href="https://github.com/features/copilot" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub Copilot features</a><span class="lnote"> Overview and plans</span></li><li><a href="https://agents.md/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AGENTS.md</a><span class="lnote"> Shared format for agent instructions</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://docs.github.com/api/article/body?pathname=/en/copilot/get-started/features" target="_blank" rel="noopener">About GitHub Copilot</a>; <a href="https://docs.github.com/api/article/body?pathname=/en/copilot/concepts/agents/coding-agent/about-coding-agent" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Copilot on GitHub.com (cloud agent, Copilot in the cloud)</a>; <a href="https://docs.github.com/api/article/body?pathname=/en/copilot/how-tos/use-copilot-agents/cloud-agent" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Copilot cloud agent how-tos</a>; <a href="https://docs.github.com/api/article/body?pathname=/en/copilot/how-tos/use-copilot-agents/cloud-agent/start-copilot-sessions" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Starting Copilot sessions (some entry points open a PR)</a>; <a href="https://docs.github.com/api/article/body?pathname=/en/copilot/how-tos/use-copilot-agents/cloud-agent/use-cloud-agent-on-github" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Using cloud agent on GitHub (assign an issue)</a>; <a href="https://docs.github.com/api/article/body?pathname=/en/copilot/concepts/prompting/response-customization" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Custom instructions (copilot-instructions.md, .instructions.md, AGENTS.md/CLAUDE.md)</a>; <a href="https://docs.github.com/api/article/body?pathname=/en/copilot/tutorials/customization-library/prompt-files/your-first-prompt-file" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Your first prompt file (.github/prompts)</a>; <a href="https://docs.github.com/api/article/body?pathname=/en/copilot/concepts/agents/copilot-cli/about-copilot-cli" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Copilot CLI</a>; <a href="https://docs.github.com/api/article/body?pathname=/en/copilot/concepts/agents/code-review" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Copilot code review</a>; <a href="https://docs.github.com/api/article/body?pathname=/en/copilot/concepts/content-exclusion" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Content exclusion</a>; <a href="https://docs.github.com/api/article/body?pathname=/en/copilot/reference/ai-models/supported-models" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Supported AI models (includes Claude)</a>; <a href="https://docs.github.com/api/article/body?pathname=/en/copilot/concepts/billing-and-usage/copilot-usage-metrics/copilot-metrics" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Copilot usage metrics</a>; <a href="https://docs.github.com/api/article/body?pathname=/en/copilot/concepts/completions/code-referencing" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Copilot code referencing (public code policy)</a>; <a href="https://docs.github.com/api/article/body?pathname=/en/copilot/how-tos/chat-with-copilot/chat-in-mobile" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Copilot Chat in GitHub Mobile</a>.</p><p class="runcode">A question, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/enrichmeai.github.io/issues/new?title=%5BGitHub%20Copilot%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>. Nothing here is tracked and there are no comments to store: questions live in the open, where the next learner can find the answer.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<section class="panel" id="p-agentsgcp" data-name="Agents on GCP">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Running agents on Google Cloud</h2><p>Many teams run coding agents on GitHub: an issue starts the agent, Actions runs it, a pull request comes back. That is convenient, but the work, the secrets and the logs then live on someone else's platform. This page shows the other way: a cloud workspace in your own Google Cloud project, in the style of browser-based tools such as Replit, where people and agents share a running environment, and where triggers, secrets, models, approvals and audit all stay under your project's control.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>The shape of it</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Need</th><th>On GitHub</th><th>In your Google Cloud project</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>A place to work</td><td>Codespaces</td><td>Cloud Workstations: managed development environments from a container image you define, in the browser or your own IDE</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Run an agent task</td><td>An Actions job</td><td>A Cloud Run job: a container that runs to completion, with its own service account, timeout and retries</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Start it</td><td>Issue, comment, schedule</td><td>Cloud Scheduler, Pub/Sub messages, Eventarc events, or a Workflow</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Several steps and a human approval</td><td>Workflow jobs and environments</td><td>Workflows, which can run steps in order, wait, and call your services</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Secrets</td><td>Repository secrets</td><td>Secret Manager, with IAM on each secret and audit logs of every access once Data Access audit logs are turned on</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Build and test</td><td>Actions</td><td>Cloud Build</td></tr>
      <tr><td>A live preview</td><td>A preview deployment</td><td>A Cloud Run revision with no traffic and its own tagged URL</td></tr>
      <tr><td>The model</td><td>Copilot, or a key in a secret</td><td>Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform, formerly Vertex AI (Google's models and partner models in Model Garden), or any provider with its key in Secret Manager</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note">Git stays: the history of the code still lives in a Git repository, wherever you host it. What moves is where the agent runs, what it can reach, and who can see what it did.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Why run it yourself</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Your data stays in your project and region.</b> Useful when code or data may not leave a country or a company.</li>
      <li><b>One identity model.</b> The agent is a service account with exactly the IAM roles it needs, like any other workload.</li>
      <li><b>One audit trail.</b> Cloud Audit Logs record what the agent touched, next to everything else in the project.</li>
      <li><b>Your choice of model,</b> including models you are allowed to use under your own agreements.</li>
      <li><b>The trade-off:</b> you build and run the plumbing that a hosted service gives you for free. Start small.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>A shared workspace, Replit style</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># a workspace image you control, in your project
gcloud workstations configs create agent-ws \
  --cluster=dev --region=europe-west2 \
  --machine-type=e2-standard-4 \
  --container-custom-image=europe-west2-docker.pkg.dev/my-proj/tools/agent-ws:1 \
  --service-account=agent-ws@my-proj.iam.gserviceaccount.com \
  --idle-timeout=3600 \
  --disable-public-ip-addresses

gcloud workstations create alice \
  --config=agent-ws --cluster=dev --region=europe-west2
gcloud workstations start alice \
  --config=agent-ws --cluster=dev --region=europe-west2</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">Bake the agent's tools (CLI, language runtimes, test tools) into the image, so every workspace and every agent run starts the same. <code>--idle-timeout</code> is in seconds and stops idle workspaces, which saves money.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>An agent task as a Cloud Run job</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>gcloud run jobs create agent-task \
  --image=europe-west2-docker.pkg.dev/my-proj/agents/runner:1 \
  --region=europe-west2 \
  --service-account=agent-runner@my-proj.iam.gserviceaccount.com \
  --set-secrets=MODEL_API_KEY=model-api-key:latest \
  --task-timeout=30m --max-retries=0

# run one task and wait for the result
gcloud run jobs execute agent-task --region=europe-west2 \
  --update-env-vars=TASK="add tests for the date parser" --wait

# or on a schedule, calling the Cloud Run API
gcloud scheduler jobs create http nightly-triage \
  --location=europe-west2 --schedule="0 6 * * 1-5" \
  --uri="https://run.googleapis.com/v2/projects/my-proj/locations/europe-west2/jobs/agent-task:run" \
  --http-method=POST \
  --oauth-service-account-email=scheduler@my-proj.iam.gserviceaccount.com</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">One container, one task, a hard timeout, and no automatic retries for work that changes things: an agent that failed halfway should be looked at, not rerun blindly.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Preview what the agent built</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># deploy the agent's branch with no traffic, at its own URL
gcloud run deploy web --image=…/web:agent-1234 \
  --region=europe-west2 --no-traffic --tag=agent-1234
# open https://agent-1234---web-xxxxx.a.run.app, then
# move traffic only after a person has looked</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">This is the "see it running" moment that makes Replit-style tools feel good, with the same blue-green and canary steps as any release (see <a href="#devops">DevOps</a>).</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Guard rails, before the first run</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>A service account per agent,</b> with only the roles its task needs. No owner or editor roles, ever.</li>
      <li><b>No direct path to production.</b> The agent proposes (a branch, a preview, a plan); a person approves the merge or the deploy.</li>
      <li><b>Secrets from Secret Manager at run time,</b> never in the image, the prompt or the repository.</li>
      <li><b>Limit the network:</b> private workspaces (<code>--disable-public-ip-addresses</code>), egress only to what the agent needs, and VPC Service Controls around sensitive data.</li>
      <li><b>Budgets and alerts</b> on the project, and timeouts on every job, so a runaway loop costs minutes, not a month.</li>
      <li><b>Log every step</b> the agent takes with a run ID, so a person can follow the journey and undo it if needed (see <a href="#observability">Observability</a>).</li>
      <li><b>Evaluate the agent</b> on real tasks before trusting it with more (see <a href="#agentharness">Agent Harness</a>).</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">One open-source option for the agent runtime itself is <a href="/penstock/">Penstock</a>, our headless, self-hosted harness, which runs agents from CI, Slack or webhooks and can run as a container like the job above. It is ours, so we are not neutral.</p>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Explain where an agent runs, what it can reach and who sees its logs</li><li>Run a containerised task as a Cloud Run job</li><li>Keep secrets in Secret Manager</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Give each agent its own least-privilege service account</li><li>Trigger agent jobs from Scheduler, Pub/Sub or a Workflow</li><li>Preview agent changes on a tagged, no-traffic revision</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Design an agent platform with workspaces, approvals and audit</li><li>Set budgets, timeouts and network limits for agents</li><li>Evaluate agents on real tasks before widening their access</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Own the organisation's policy for where agents run and what they touch</li><li>Balance hosted agent services against self-run ones</li><li>Make agent work auditable end to end</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>Cloud Workstations</li><li>Cloud Run jobs</li><li>Workflows and Eventarc</li><li>Secret Manager and IAM</li><li>VPC Service Controls</li><li>Vertex AI Model Garden</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://cloud.google.com/workstations/docs" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cloud Workstations</a><span class="lnote"> Managed development environments</span></li><li><a href="https://cloud.google.com/run/docs/create-jobs" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cloud Run jobs</a><span class="lnote"> Containers that run to completion</span></li><li><a href="https://cloud.google.com/workflows/docs" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Workflows</a><span class="lnote"> Orchestrate steps and services</span></li><li><a href="https://cloud.google.com/secret-manager/docs" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Secret Manager</a><span class="lnote"> Store and audit secrets</span></li><li><a href="https://cloud.google.com/vertex-ai/generative-ai/docs/model-garden/explore-models" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Vertex AI Model Garden</a><span class="lnote"> Google and partner models</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/release-notes" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Agent Platform release notes (Vertex AI renamed)</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/run/docs/execute/jobs-on-schedule" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cloud Run: execute jobs on a schedule (run.googleapis.com/v2 ...:run)</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/run/docs/rollouts-rollbacks-traffic-migration" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cloud Run: rollouts, tags, tagged URL format</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/run/docs/create-jobs" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cloud Run jobs</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/sdk/gcloud/reference/workstations/configs/create" target="_blank" rel="noopener">gcloud workstations configs create --help (local gcloud; --idle-timeout in seconds)</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/sdk/gcloud/reference/run/jobs/execute" target="_blank" rel="noopener">gcloud run jobs create / execute --help (local gcloud)</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/sdk/gcloud/reference/run/jobs/create" target="_blank" rel="noopener">gcloud run jobs create</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/secret-manager/docs/audit-logging" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Secret Manager audit logging</a>; <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/penstock/blob/v0.6.0/README.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Penstock README at</a>.</p><p class="runcode">A question, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/enrichmeai.github.io/issues/new?title=%5BAgents%20on%20GCP%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>. Nothing here is tracked and there are no comments to store: questions live in the open, where the next learner can find the answer.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= HARNESS ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-harness" data-name="Harness">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Harness CI/CD</h2><p>Harness is a software delivery platform: CI, continuous delivery with verification and rollback, GitOps, feature flags, and more, organised as pipelines. This tab walks through Harness CI and Harness CD in depth, an end-to-end pipeline to Google Cloud, troubleshooting, and how to use Harness as a blueprint for every team.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Modules</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Module</th><th>Does</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Continuous Integration</td><td>Builds and tests, with caching and test intelligence (runs only tests affected by a change)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Continuous Delivery &amp; GitOps</td><td>Deploys services to Kubernetes, serverless, VMs and more, with strategies, approvals, verification and rollback; Argo CD-based GitOps</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Feature Management &amp; Experimentation (formerly Feature Flags)</td><td>Turn features on per user or percentage, separating deploy from release; experiments</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Security Testing Orchestration</td><td>Runs scanners in pipelines and gathers their results</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Cloud &amp; AI Cost Management</td><td>Cost visibility, budgets and recommendations, for cloud and AI spend</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Chaos Engineering</td><td>Inject failures to test resilience</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Infrastructure as Code Management</td><td>Run and govern Terraform/OpenTofu workspaces</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Internal Developer Portal</td><td>Service catalogue, self-service workflows and scorecards; can add Backstage plugins</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note">Modules and their names change over time; check the current Harness docs for what your account includes.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Building blocks</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Account → organisation → project:</b> the hierarchy for access control and shared resources.</li>
      <li><b>Pipeline:</b> stages run in order or parallel; each stage has steps.</li>
      <li><b>Stage types:</b> Build (CI), Deploy (CD), Approval, Custom.</li>
      <li><b>Service:</b> what you deploy (manifests, artefact).</li>
      <li><b>Environment and infrastructure definition:</b> where you deploy (dev, prod; a cluster or project).</li>
      <li><b>Connector:</b> a stored, permissioned link to GitHub, GCP, a registry or a cluster.</li>
      <li><b>Delegate:</b> an agent running in your infrastructure that executes tasks, so Harness never needs inbound access.</li>
      <li><b>Secrets:</b> stored in Harness or referenced from Google Secret Manager or Vault.</li>
      <li><b>Templates:</b> versioned step, stage and pipeline templates teams reuse.</li>
      <li><b>Triggers:</b> start pipelines on Git events, artefact pushes or schedules.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Pipeline as code (shape)</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>pipeline:
  name: shop-api
  identifier: shop_api
  orgIdentifier: default
  projectIdentifier: shop
  stages:
    - stage:
        name: Build
        identifier: build
        type: CI
        spec:
          cloneCodebase: true
          execution:
            steps:
              - step:
                  type: Run
                  name: Test
                  identifier: test
                  spec:
                    shell: Sh
                    command: ./gradlew test
    - stage:
        name: Approve prod
        identifier: approve
        type: Approval
        spec:
          execution:
            steps:
              - step:
                  type: HarnessApproval
                  name: Founder approval
                  identifier: founder
                  timeout: 1d
                  spec:
                    approvers:
                      userGroups: [prod_approvers]
                      minimumCount: 1
    - stage:
        name: Deploy prod
        identifier: deploy_prod
        type: Deployment
        spec:
          # service, environment, infrastructure and strategy
          # (rolling / canary / blue-green) go here
          ...</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">This shows the structure only. Build pipelines in the Harness editor first and copy its YAML into Git, since exact step specs vary by module and version.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Expressions</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>&lt;+pipeline.sequenceId&gt;           run number
&lt;+pipeline.executionId&gt;          unique run ID
&lt;+codebase.commitSha&gt;            the commit being built
&lt;+codebase.branch&gt;
&lt;+artifacts.primary.tag&gt;         deployed image tag
&lt;+env.name&gt;                      target environment
&lt;+service.name&gt;
&lt;+secrets.getValue("db_password")&gt;   resolved at runtime, masked in logs
&lt;+pipeline.variables.region&gt;</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Deploying safely</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Strategies:</b> rolling, canary and blue-green built in, with automatic rollback steps.</li>
      <li><b>Continuous verification:</b> compares metrics and logs (from Prometheus, Datadog, Cloud Monitoring and others) of the new version against the old, and fails the stage when they degrade.</li>
      <li><b>Approvals:</b> Harness user groups, or Jira/ServiceNow tickets.</li>
      <li><b>Freeze windows:</b> block deployments during set periods.</li>
      <li><b>Policy as code:</b> OPA policies on pipelines (e.g. "prod deploys must have an approval stage and a verification step").</li>
      <li><b>Audit trail:</b> who ran what, with which inputs, when.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Harness vs GitHub Actions</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th></th><th>GitHub Actions</th><th>Harness</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Strength</td><td>CI next to the code; huge action marketplace</td><td>CD: strategies, verification, approvals, governance at scale</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Deployment model</td><td>You script it in workflow steps</td><td>First-class services, environments and strategies</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Governance</td><td>Environments, rulesets, required reviewers</td><td>OPA policies, templates, RBAC, freeze windows, audit</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Fits</td><td>Small and medium teams; code-centric workflows</td><td>Many teams and services needing a standard, governed path to production</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note">A common split: GitHub Actions builds and tests on every PR; Harness promotes the built artefact through environments.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide" id="jenkins"><h3>Jenkins vs Harness: choose by requirement</h3>
    <p><b>Jenkins</b> is the open-source automation server that began as Hudson and was renamed in 2011 after a dispute with Oracle; it is MIT-licensed, extended by plugins, and since Jenkins 2 (April 2016) builds are written as pipelines in a Groovy-based language (a <code>Jenkinsfile</code>). <b>Harness</b> is a commercial delivery platform with CI, CD and governance in one product, run as a service or as its Self-Managed Enterprise Edition on your own infrastructure.</p>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>If this is true for you</th><th>It leans towards</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>No licence budget, and people who can run servers</td><td>Jenkins</td><td>Free and open source; you pay in operations time instead</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Years of Jenkinsfiles and plugins that work</td><td>Jenkins, for now</td><td>Migration has a real cost; move when a requirement forces it</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Fully offline or air-gapped</td><td>Either</td><td>Jenkins runs anywhere you install it; Harness Self-Managed documents an air-gapped install</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Canary and blue-green releases without writing them yourself</td><td>Harness</td><td>Blue-green, canary and rolling deployments are built in; in Jenkins they are plugins and scripts you maintain</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Central approvals, policies and audit across many teams</td><td>Harness</td><td>Governance is part of the product rather than assembled from plugins</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Little appetite for patching servers and plugins</td><td>Harness (as a service), or GitHub Actions</td><td>Someone else runs the platform</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Code already on GitHub, simple build and deploy</td><td>GitHub Actions</td><td>See &ldquo;Harness vs GitHub Actions&rdquo; above</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Is Jenkins fading? What the numbers say</h3>
    <p>Jenkins publishes a monthly count of installations that send it anonymous usage statistics:</p>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>January of</th><th>Installations reporting</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>2019</td><td>232,746</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2020</td><td>267,107</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2021</td><td>279,894</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2022</td><td>294,451</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2023</td><td>293,726</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2024</td><td>289,935</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2025</td><td>267,475</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2026</td><td>261,263</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <ul>
      <li><b>The peak</b> in the series was March 2023, with 320,774 installations reporting; the latest published month, February 2026, had 248,133, about 23% lower.</li>
      <li><b>What this count is not:</b> it includes only installations with usage statistics switched on (administrators can turn reporting off), and it counts servers, not how much each one builds. A company that merges ten Jenkins servers into one shows up as a fall.</li>
      <li><b>Meanwhile, hosted CI is growing:</b> GitHub reports 11.5 billion GitHub Actions minutes in public projects in 2025, up 35% on 2024. That counts public projects only, so it is a signal of direction, not a market share.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note"><i>Our view, from the numbers above:</i> Jenkins is declining from its 2023 peak, not disappearing: around a quarter of a million installations still report every month. New projects increasingly start on hosted CI; existing Jenkins estates are large and move slowly. <i>The other view:</i> the fall may partly be consolidation and opt-outs rather than teams leaving, and for self-hosted, air-gapped or cost-sensitive work Jenkins remains a reasonable choice.</p>
    <p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://stats.jenkins.io/jenkins-stats/svg/total-jenkins.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jenkins usage statistics: total installations (monthly)</a>; <a href="https://github.com/jenkinsci/jenkins/blob/master/core/src/main/java/hudson/model/UsageStatistics.java" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jenkins source: UsageStatistics (reporting can be switched off)</a>; <a href="https://github.blog/news-insights/octoverse/octoverse-a-new-developer-joins-github-every-second-as-ai-leads-typescript-to-1/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub Octoverse 2025</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jenkins_(software)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Jenkins</a>; <a href="https://developer.harness.io/docs/self-managed-enterprise-edition" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Harness Self-Managed Enterprise Edition</a>; <a href="https://developer.harness.io/docs/continuous-delivery/manage-deployments/deployment-concepts" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Harness deployment concepts and strategies</a>.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>End to end: commit to production on Google Cloud</h3>
    <ol>
      <li><b>Trigger:</b> a push or merged pull request on GitHub starts the pipeline (webhook trigger on <code>main</code>; PR trigger runs CI only).</li>
      <li><b>CI stage:</b> clone, restore cache, compile and run tests (with test intelligence), run scanners, then build the image once and push it to Artifact Registry tagged with the commit SHA.</li>
      <li><b>Deploy to dev:</b> a CD stage deploys that exact image to the dev environment (GKE or Cloud Run) with a rolling strategy.</li>
      <li><b>Verify dev:</b> smoke tests and continuous verification against dev metrics and logs.</li>
      <li><b>Approval:</b> a named group approves prod (or a policy auto-approves outside freeze windows).</li>
      <li><b>Deploy to prod:</b> canary or blue-green with the <i>same</i> artefact; verification watches error rate and latency; automatic rollback on degradation.</li>
      <li><b>Notify and record:</b> Slack/email notification, deployment recorded with who, what, which commit, and the verification result.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="note">Build once, promote the same artefact. Rebuilding per environment means prod runs something that was never tested.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Harness CI in depth</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Build infrastructure:</b> Harness Cloud (hosted machines), your Kubernetes cluster (pods per build), your own VMs, or a local runner. Choose by network access, cost and isolation.</li>
      <li><b>Codebase:</b> the repo connector and clone settings for the pipeline; each CI stage can clone automatically.</li>
      <li><b>Run step:</b> any shell command in a container image you choose (<code>eclipse-temurin:21</code>, <code>python:3.12</code>).</li>
      <li><b>Test step with test intelligence:</b> selects only tests affected by the change, and reports results in the UI (JUnit XML).</li>
      <li><b>Background step:</b> a service the build needs, such as Postgres or a Kafka broker, running beside the steps.</li>
      <li><b>Build and push steps:</b> build an image and push it to Artifact Registry, Docker Hub or another registry.</li>
      <li><b>Caching:</b> Cache Intelligence for common build tools, or explicit save/restore cache steps to GCS or S3.</li>
      <li><b>Plugins:</b> Drone plugins and many GitHub Actions can run as steps.</li>
      <li><b>Outputs:</b> steps export variables for later steps and stages, e.g. the image tag.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>A CI stage (shape)</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>- stage:
    name: Build
    identifier: build
    type: CI
    spec:
      cloneCodebase: true
      platform: { os: Linux, arch: Amd64 }
      runtime: { type: Cloud, spec: {} }       # Harness Cloud machines
      execution:
        steps:
          - step:
              type: Run
              name: Unit tests
              identifier: unit_tests
              spec:
                image: eclipse-temurin:21
                shell: Sh
                command: ./gradlew test
                reports:
                  type: JUnit
                  spec:
                    paths: ["**/build/test-results/test/*.xml"]
          - step:
              type: BuildAndPushGAR
              name: Image
              identifier: image
              spec:
                connectorRef: gcp_bld
                host: asia-south1-docker.pkg.dev
                projectID: my-proj
                imageName: apps/shop-api
                tags: [&lt;+codebase.shortCommitSha&gt;]</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">Field names follow Harness's YAML at the time of writing; generate the stage in the editor and compare before committing.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Harness CD in depth</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Service:</b> what you deploy: the artefact source (Artifact Registry image), manifests (Kubernetes YAML, Helm chart, Kustomize) and variables.</li>
      <li><b>Environment:</b> dev or prod, marked pre-production or production, with environment-level overrides (replicas, URLs, secrets).</li>
      <li><b>Infrastructure definition:</b> the exact target: a GKE cluster and namespace, or a GCP project and region for serverless.</li>
      <li><b>Deployment types:</b> Kubernetes, Native Helm, serverless targets, VMs over SSH/WinRM, Tanzu, ECS, and custom deployments for anything else. Google Cloud Run and Cloud Functions have their own deployment types.</li>
      <li><b>Execution:</b> generated from the chosen strategy, editable step by step; failure strategies say what to do on each kind of error (retry, rollback, manual intervention).</li>
      <li><b>Rollback:</b> a rollback section per stage, run automatically on failure or on a failed verification.</li>
      <li><b>Input sets and overlays:</b> saved sets of runtime inputs (which service, which tag) so one pipeline serves many services.</li>
      <li><b>Multi-service and multi-environment</b> deployments from a single stage.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Deployment strategies in Harness</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Strategy</th><th>How Harness runs it</th><th>Use when</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Rolling</td><td>Replaces pods or instances gradually; rollback restores the previous release</td><td>Default for stateless services</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Canary</td><td>Deploys a small canary set, verifies, then rolls out fully and deletes the canary</td><td>Risky changes with good metrics</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Blue-green</td><td>Deploys the new version as a stage set, tests it, then swaps traffic</td><td>Instant switch and rollback; capacity for two copies</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Basic / custom</td><td>Your own steps (shell, HTTP, Terraform, approvals)</td><td>Targets without a built-in type</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Connecting Harness to Google Cloud</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>GCP connector</b> with OIDC (workload identity federation) where your plan supports it, so no service account key is stored. Otherwise inherit credentials from a delegate running in GKE with Workload Identity.</li>
      <li><b>One service account per environment</b> with only the roles deploys need (Artifact Registry reader/writer, GKE developer or Cloud Run developer, and service account user on the runtime account).</li>
      <li><b>Delegates</b> run inside your network (for example a GKE namespace), so private clusters and services are reachable without opening inbound access. Run at least two for availability.</li>
      <li><b>Secrets</b> referenced from Google Secret Manager through a secret manager connector.</li>
      <li><b>GitHub connector</b> with a GitHub App for cloning, PR status checks and webhooks.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>GitHub Actions → Harness mapping</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>GitHub Actions</th><th>Harness</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Workflow</td><td>Pipeline</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Job</td><td>Stage</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Step / action</td><td>Step / plugin</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>on:</code> push, pull_request</td><td>Triggers</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Runner</td><td>Build infrastructure / delegate</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Secrets and variables</td><td>Secrets, variables, connectors</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Environments + required reviewers</td><td>Environments + approval stages + OPA policies</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Reusable workflows</td><td>Templates</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>services:</code> containers</td><td>Background steps</td></tr>
      <tr><td>matrix</td><td>Looping strategies (matrix, repeat, parallelism)</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Troubleshooting</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Check</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Step queued, never starts</td><td>No healthy delegate or build infrastructure matching the selector</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Connector test fails</td><td>Delegate network egress, credentials, or the account's IAM roles</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Image pull error in deploy</td><td>Cluster or runtime identity lacks Artifact Registry read; wrong tag expression</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Deployment times out</td><td>Readiness probes failing; check pod events and logs in the step output</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Verification fails unexpectedly</td><td>Baseline too short, wrong metric query, or too few data points; read the analysis detail</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Expression resolves to null</td><td>Wrong scope or step identifier; use the expression picker in the editor</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Pipeline in Git differs from UI</td><td>Remote pipeline branch selection; check which branch the run used</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Good practice</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Keep pipelines, templates and input sets in Git, reviewed like code.</li>
      <li>One pipeline template per deployment shape; services pass only inputs.</li>
      <li>Run unit and integration tests in CI on every PR; deploy only from <code>main</code>.</li>
      <li>Promote by artefact tag or digest; never rebuild per environment.</li>
      <li>Every prod stage has verification and an automatic rollback.</li>
      <li>Enforce with OPA: prod needs approval, verification, and an artefact from the trusted registry.</li>
      <li>Watch delegate health and versions; alert when none are connected.</li>
      <li>Track DORA metrics from Harness deployment data.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Harness as a blueprint</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Publish <b>account-level templates</b> for "build a JVM service", "deploy to Cloud Run", "deploy to GKE".</li>
      <li>Teams reference the template version; a fix to the template reaches every pipeline that uses it.</li>
      <li>Enforce the template with OPA policies rather than reviews.</li>
      <li>Keep pipelines in Git (remote pipelines) so changes are reviewed like code.</li>
      <li>This is the same idea as the AI tab's blueprints card: decide once, reuse everywhere.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Run a pipeline and read its logs</li><li>Understand stages, steps, services and environments</li><li>Approve or roll back a deployment</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Build CI and CD pipelines with connectors, secrets and triggers</li><li>Use canary or blue-green with verification</li><li>Keep pipelines in Git and use templates</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Design org-wide templates and OPA policies</li><li>Set up delegates, RBAC and environments securely</li><li>Integrate verification with monitoring and feature flags</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Own the delivery platform strategy and its governance</li><li>Lead migrations from other CI/CD tools</li><li>Measure and improve delivery (DORA) across teams</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>Pipeline, stage and step templates</li><li>Continuous verification</li><li>OPA policies in Harness</li><li>GitOps with Harness</li><li>Feature flags for progressive delivery</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://developer.harness.io/docs" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Harness developer hub</a><span class="lnote"> Official documentation and tutorials</span></li><li><a href="https://university.harness.io/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Harness University</a><span class="lnote"> Courses and certifications</span></li><li><a href="https://www.openpolicyagent.org/docs/latest/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open Policy Agent</a><span class="lnote"> Policy as code used by Harness</span></li><li><a href="https://dora.dev/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">DORA</a><span class="lnote"> Measuring delivery performance</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://docs.github.com/api/article/body?pathname=/en/actions/writing-workflows/workflow-syntax-for-github-actions" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub Actions workflow syntax (jobs, services, matrix, on:)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/harness/harness/main/README.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Harness open source repo (reachable; not the SaaS docs)</a>; <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/run/docs/rollouts-rollbacks-traffic-migration" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cloud Run tags (for the GCP side)</a>; <a href="https://developer.harness.io/harness-platform/use-harness-platform/variables-and-expressions/harness-expressions-reference.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Harness expressions reference (artifacts.primary.tag)</a>; <a href="https://developer.harness.io/continuous-integration/use-harness-ci/use-harness-ci/codebase-configuration/built-in-cie-codebase-variables-reference.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Harness CI codebase variables (commitSha, shortCommitSha)</a>; <a href="https://developer.harness.io/harness-platform/use-harness-platform/approvals/adding-harness-approval-stages.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Harness approval stages (HarnessApproval YAML)</a>; <a href="https://developer.harness.io/continuous-integration/use-harness-ci/use-harness-ci/build-and-upload-artifacts/build-and-push/build-and-push-to-gar.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Harness Build and Push to GAR</a>; <a href="https://developer.harness.io/continuous-integration/use-harness-ci/use-harness-ci/set-up-build-infrastructure/use-harness-cloud-build-infrastructure.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Harness Cloud build infrastructure (runtime type Cloud)</a>; <a href="https://developer.harness.io/harness-platform/use-harness-platform/connectors/cloud-providers/connect-to-google-cloud-platform-gcp/gcs-connector-settings-reference.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Harness GCP connector (OIDC)</a>; <a href="https://developer.harness.io/feature-flags/new-to-feature-flags/migrate-to-fme.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Harness Feature Flags to FME migration</a>. A few claims could not be confirmed at their official source from here and are being re-checked.</p><p class="runcode">A question, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/enrichmeai.github.io/issues/new?title=%5BHarness%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>. Nothing here is tracked and there are no comments to store: questions live in the open, where the next learner can find the answer.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= OPS TOOLING ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-opstools" data-name="Ops Tooling">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Ops tooling: Prometheus, Grafana, Fluentd, Dynatrace, ServiceNow</h2><p>The tools you will meet in operations teams, what each does, how they fit together, and working configuration. Concepts are on <a href="#observability">Observability</a> and <a href="#sre">SRE</a>.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>How they fit together</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>Category</th><th>Does</th><th>GCP-native alternative</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Prometheus</td><td>Metrics</td><td>Scrapes and stores time-series metrics; PromQL; alert rules</td><td>Managed Service for Prometheus, Cloud Monitoring</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Alertmanager</td><td>Alert routing</td><td>Groups, silences and routes Prometheus alerts to people and tools</td><td>Cloud Monitoring notification channels</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Grafana</td><td>Visualisation</td><td>Dashboards and alerting over many data sources; Loki (logs), Tempo (traces), Mimir (metrics) in the same family</td><td>Cloud Monitoring dashboards</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Fluentd / Fluent Bit</td><td>Log shipping</td><td>Collect, parse, enrich and forward logs to any backend</td><td>The built-in logging agents on GKE and Cloud Run</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Dynatrace</td><td>Full-stack APM / observability platform</td><td>Auto-instrumentation, topology, traces, logs, metrics, AI root-cause analysis</td><td>Cloud Trace, Profiler, Logging, Monitoring together</td></tr>
      <tr><td>ServiceNow</td><td>IT service management</td><td>Incidents, problems, changes, requests, CMDB, approvals and workflow</td><td>None: ITSM sits above monitoring</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note">Typical flow: apps emit telemetry → Fluent Bit ships logs, Prometheus scrapes metrics (or Dynatrace's agent collects all) → Grafana or Dynatrace shows it → alerts route through Alertmanager → ServiceNow opens an incident, pages on-call, and tracks the change that fixes it.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Prometheus: model</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Pull model:</b> Prometheus scrapes <code>/metrics</code> endpoints on an interval (Spring: <code>/actuator/prometheus</code>).</li>
      <li><b>Exporters</b> expose metrics for systems that do not: node_exporter (hosts), postgres_exporter, blackbox_exporter (probes).</li>
      <li><b>Time series</b> = metric name + labels. Keep label values bounded.</li>
      <li><b>Service discovery</b> finds targets in Kubernetes, GCE and others.</li>
      <li><b>Short-term storage</b> locally; long-term in Thanos, Mimir or Google's managed service.</li>
      <li><b>Recording rules</b> precompute expensive queries; <b>alerting rules</b> fire when an expression holds for a duration.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Prometheus: configuration</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># prometheus.yml
global:
  scrape_interval: 30s
scrape_configs:
  - job_name: shop-api
    metrics_path: /actuator/prometheus
    static_configs:
      - targets: ["shop-api:8080"]
rule_files: ["rules/*.yml"]
alerting:
  alertmanagers:
    - static_configs:
        - targets: ["alertmanager:9093"]</code></pre></div>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># rules/shop-api.yml
groups:
  - name: shop-api
    rules:
      - record: job:http_5xx_ratio:rate5m
        expr: |
          sum by (job) (rate(http_server_requests_seconds_count{status=~"5.."}[5m]))
          / sum by (job) (rate(http_server_requests_seconds_count[5m]))
      - alert: ShopApiHighErrorRate
        expr: job:http_5xx_ratio:rate5m{job="shop-api"} &gt; 0.02
        for: 10m
        labels: { severity: page }
        annotations:
          summary: "shop-api 5xx above 2% for 10 minutes"
          runbook_url: "https://runbooks.example.com/shop-api/high-errors"
      - alert: OrdersLoadMissing
        expr: time() - orders_load_last_success_timestamp_seconds &gt; 26 * 3600
        labels: { severity: page }
        annotations: { summary: "No successful orders load for 26 hours" }</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>PromQL you will use</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>rate(http_server_requests_seconds_count[5m])                 # per-second rate of a counter
sum by (status) (rate(http_server_requests_seconds_count[5m])) # grouped
histogram_quantile(0.95,
  sum by (le) (rate(http_server_requests_seconds_bucket[5m])))  # p95 latency
increase(orders_objects_total{outcome="failed"}[1h])             # count over a window
up == 0                                                       # targets not scraped
absent(orders_load_last_success_timestamp_seconds)                 # metric missing entirely
topk(5, sum by (pod) (container_memory_working_set_bytes))    # top memory users</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">Always <code>rate()</code> a counter before summing; never sum raw counters. Alert on <code>absent()</code> or <code>up == 0</code> so a dead exporter is not mistaken for health.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Alertmanager routing</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>route:
  receiver: team-ticket
  group_by: [alertname, job]
  group_wait: 30s
  repeat_interval: 4h
  routes:
    - matchers: [ 'severity="page"' ]
      receiver: oncall-page
receivers:
  - name: oncall-page
    pagerduty_configs:
      - routing_key_file: /etc/alertmanager/secrets/pagerduty_key
  - name: team-ticket
    webhook_configs:
      - url: https://integration.example.com/servicenow/alerts   # creates a ServiceNow incident</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">Group related alerts, silence during planned work, and inhibit child alerts when the parent (a whole cluster down) is firing.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Grafana</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Data sources:</b> Prometheus, Loki, Tempo, Cloud Monitoring, BigQuery, Postgres and many more, in one dashboard.</li>
      <li><b>Dashboards as code:</b> provision from files or the Grafana Terraform provider; keep JSON in Git and review changes.</li>
      <li><b>Variables</b> (<code>$service</code>, <code>$env</code>) make one dashboard serve every service: a dashboard template is a blueprint.</li>
      <li><b>Grafana Alerting</b> can evaluate rules across data sources; pick one alerting system per signal to avoid duplicates.</li>
      <li><b>Explore</b> view for ad-hoc queries, and jumping from a metric spike to logs and traces of the same time window.</li>
    </ul>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># provisioning/datasources/prometheus.yaml
apiVersion: 1
datasources:
  - name: Prometheus
    type: prometheus
    access: proxy
    url: http://prometheus:9090
    isDefault: true</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Fluentd and Fluent Bit</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Fluent Bit:</b> lightweight (C), the usual choice as a node agent or sidecar in Kubernetes.</li>
      <li><b>Fluentd:</b> heavier (Ruby), huge plugin ecosystem, often used as a central aggregator.</li>
      <li><b>Pipeline:</b> input → parser → filter (enrich, drop, mask) → buffer → output.</li>
      <li><b>On GKE and Cloud Run</b> logs are collected for you; add Fluent Bit when you need other destinations or self-managed clusters.</li>
      <li><b>Mask secrets and personal data</b> in filters before logs leave the node.</li>
    </ul>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># Fluent Bit (Kubernetes node agent)
[INPUT]
    Name    tail
    Path    /var/log/containers/*.log
    multiline.parser  cri
    Tag     kube.*
[FILTER]
    Name    kubernetes
    Match   kube.*
[FILTER]
    Name    grep
    Match   kube.*
    Exclude log healthcheck
[OUTPUT]
    Name    stackdriver
    Match   *</code></pre></div>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># Fluentd
&lt;source&gt;
  @type tail
  path /var/log/app/*.log
  pos_file /var/log/fluentd/app.pos
  tag app
  &lt;parse&gt;
    @type json
  &lt;/parse&gt;
&lt;/source&gt;
&lt;filter app&gt;
  @type record_transformer
  &lt;record&gt;
    env prod
  &lt;/record&gt;
&lt;/filter&gt;
&lt;match app&gt;
  @type stdout
&lt;/match&gt;</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Dynatrace</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>OneAgent:</b> installed on hosts or via the Kubernetes Operator; instruments JVM, Python, Node, databases and more without code changes.</li>
      <li><b>Smartscape:</b> a live topology of hosts, processes, services and their dependencies.</li>
      <li><b>PurePath:</b> end-to-end distributed traces, down to code-level timing.</li>
      <li><b>Dynatrace Intelligence (formerly Davis AI):</b> detects anomalies and groups related events into one "problem" with a probable root cause.</li>
      <li><b>Grail and DQL:</b> stores logs, metrics, traces and events together and queries them with Dynatrace Query Language.</li>
      <li><b>SLOs, synthetic monitoring, real-user monitoring</b> and OpenTelemetry ingestion.</li>
    </ul>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>// DQL example: error logs per namespace in the last hour
fetch logs, from: -1h
| filter loglevel == "ERROR"
| summarize errors = count(), by:{k8s.namespace.name}
| sort errors desc</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">Commercial platforms trade cost for breadth and automation. Instrument with OpenTelemetry where you can, so changing vendor later does not mean re-instrumenting.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>ServiceNow: the ITSM side</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Record</th><th>Is</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Incident</td><td>Something is broken now; restore service</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Problem</td><td>The underlying cause of one or more incidents; fix it for good</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Change request</td><td>A planned change to production: standard (pre-approved), normal (assessed and approved), emergency</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Request</td><td>A user asking for something (access, a new environment)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>CMDB CI</td><td>A configuration item: a service, app, database or host and its relationships</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Event management</b> turns monitoring alerts into correlated alerts and incidents, linked to the affected CI.</li>
      <li><b>Change automation:</b> CI/CD pipelines (Harness, GitHub Actions, Jenkins) open and update change requests automatically, so low-risk deploys are approved by policy rather than a weekly board.</li>
      <li><b>Keep the CMDB generated</b> from real sources (cloud APIs, Terraform state, service catalogues); hand-maintained CMDBs drift.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>ServiceNow Table API</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># open P1 incidents
curl -s -u "$SN_USER:$SN_PASS" -H "Accept: application/json" \
  "https://$INSTANCE.service-now.com/api/now/table/incident?sysparm_query=active=true^priority=1&amp;sysparm_limit=10&amp;sysparm_fields=number,short_description,state"

# create an incident from an automated check
curl -s -u "$SN_USER:$SN_PASS" -X POST \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" -H "Accept: application/json" \
  "https://$INSTANCE.service-now.com/api/now/table/incident" \
  -d '{"short_description":"Orders load missing for 26h",
       "urgency":"2","impact":"2",
       "assignment_group":"&lt;sys_id of the Data Platform group&gt;",
       "description":"Ledger shows no SUCCEEDED LAND since 2026-11-11 06:15 UTC. Runbook: …"}'</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">Use an integration user with only the roles it needs, OAuth rather than basic auth where available, and dedupe on a correlation ID so a flapping alert does not open fifty incidents.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Choosing and combining</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>All on GCP, small team:</b> Cloud Logging, Monitoring and Trace with OpenTelemetry; little to run.</li>
      <li><b>Kubernetes-heavy, open source:</b> Prometheus + Grafana + Loki + Tempo + Fluent Bit; you operate them (or use managed versions).</li>
      <li><b>Large enterprise, many stacks:</b> Dynatrace (or similar) for automatic coverage and root cause, ServiceNow for process.</li>
      <li><b>Whatever you choose:</b> one alerting path per signal, runbooks on every alert, telemetry defined as code, and OpenTelemetry at the source.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Read a Grafana or Dynatrace dashboard and find a service's errors</li><li>Look up and update a ServiceNow incident</li><li>Know what Prometheus, Fluent Bit and Alertmanager each do</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Write PromQL queries, recording and alerting rules</li><li>Configure log shipping and Grafana dashboards as code</li><li>Connect alerts to ServiceNow incidents and CI/CD to change requests</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Design the monitoring stack and alert routing for a platform</li><li>Integrate Dynatrace or open-source tooling with OpenTelemetry</li><li>Automate change management while keeping control</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Own observability and ITSM tooling strategy and cost</li><li>Lead tool consolidation or migration across the organisation</li><li>Set standards for telemetry, alerting and incident process</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>Prometheus long-term storage (Thanos, Mimir)</li><li>Grafana LGTM stack (Loki, Grafana, Tempo, Mimir)</li><li>Fluent Bit processors and multiline parsing</li><li>Dynatrace DQL and Grail</li><li>ServiceNow DevOps change automation</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://prometheus.io/docs/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Prometheus documentation</a><span class="lnote"> Concepts, PromQL, alerting</span></li><li><a href="https://prometheus.io/docs/alerting/latest/alertmanager/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Alertmanager</a><span class="lnote"> Routing, grouping, silences</span></li><li><a href="https://grafana.com/docs/grafana/latest/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Grafana documentation</a><span class="lnote"> Dashboards, data sources, alerting, provisioning</span></li><li><a href="https://docs.fluentbit.io/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Fluent Bit documentation</a><span class="lnote"> Inputs, filters, outputs</span></li><li><a href="https://docs.fluentd.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Fluentd documentation</a><span class="lnote"> Configuration and plugins</span></li><li><a href="https://docs.dynatrace.com/docs" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Dynatrace documentation</a><span class="lnote"> OneAgent, Davis, DQL</span></li><li><a href="https://developer.servicenow.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ServiceNow developer site</a><span class="lnote"> APIs, including the Table API</span></li><li><a href="https://cloud.google.com/stackdriver/docs/managed-prometheus" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google Cloud Managed Service for Prometheus</a><span class="lnote"> Prometheus without running it</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/prometheus/prometheus/main/docs/configuration/configuration.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Prometheus configuration (metrics_path, rule_files, alertmanagers)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/prometheus/prometheus/main/docs/configuration/alerting_rules.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Prometheus alerting rules (for:)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/prometheus/alertmanager/main/docs/configuration.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Alertmanager configuration (matchers, group_wait, pagerduty routing_key_file)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/fluent/fluent-bit-docs/master/pipeline/inputs/tail.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Fluent Bit tail input (multiline.parser)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/fluent/fluent-bit-docs/master/pipeline/parsers/multiline-parsing.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Fluent Bit multiline parsers (cri)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/fluent/fluent-bit-docs/master/pipeline/filters/grep.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Fluent Bit grep filter (Exclude KEY REGEX)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/fluent/fluent-bit-docs/master/pipeline/outputs/stackdriver.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Fluent Bit stackdriver output</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/fluent/fluentd-docs-gitbook/1.0/input/tail.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Fluentd in_tail (pos_file)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/fluent/fluentd-docs-gitbook/1.0/filter/record_transformer.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Fluentd record_transformer</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/grafana/grafana/main/docs/sources/administration/provisioning/index.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Grafana provisioning (datasource YAML)</a>; <a href="https://docs.dynatrace.com/docs/platform/grail/dynatrace-query-language/commands/data-source-commands" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Dynatrace DQL data source commands (fetch)</a>; <a href="https://docs.dynatrace.com/docs/dynatrace-intelligence" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Dynatrace Intelligence (formerly Davis)</a>; <a href="https://docs.dynatrace.com/docs/platform/grail" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Dynatrace Grail</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/prometheus/docs/main/docs/practices/rules.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Prometheus recording rule naming</a>. A few claims could not be confirmed at their official source from here and are being re-checked.</p><p class="runcode">A question, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/enrichmeai.github.io/issues/new?title=%5BOps%20Tooling%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>. Nothing here is tracked and there are no comments to store: questions live in the open, where the next learner can find the answer.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= BEHAVIOURS ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-behaviours" data-name="Behaviours">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Behaviours of a well-rounded engineer</h2><p>Skills are what you can do; behaviours are what you actually do every day. In the AI era the gap between them matters more: a model can produce code, but it cannot own the outcome, check the evidence, or learn from what broke. These are the behaviours that make the difference, with what they look like, and what they do not.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>The headline: engaged, not prompting and resting</h3>
    <p>AI gives every engineer a <b>bulldozer instead of a shovel</b>. That raises the bar, not lowers it. A well-rounded engineer does not write a prompt, accept whatever comes back, and take the rest of the day off. They decide where the bulldozer goes, prepare the ground, watch it work, check the result against evidence, and fix the road for the next person. The time AI saves is spent on what it cannot do: understanding the problem, testing assumptions, reviewing, and learning.</p>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Prompting and resting</th><th>Engaged engineering</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Accepts the first answer that looks plausible</td><td>Runs it, tests it, reads it, and checks it against the docs</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Says "the AI wrote it" when it breaks</td><td>Owns every line that ships, whoever typed it</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Uses saved time to do less</td><td>Uses saved time to understand more and improve the system</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Re-explains the same context every day</td><td>Writes it into the repo once so every session and colleague benefits</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Generates a new copy of the same boilerplate</td><td>Builds or extends the shared framework</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Stops learning because the tool "knows"</td><td>Learns faster, because the tool removes the typing, not the thinking</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Learn by doing, not by opinion</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Be slow to opinion, quick to experiment.</b> Before arguing for a tool, pattern or fix, build the smallest thing that tests it: a spike, a benchmark, a query, a prototype.</li>
      <li><b>Let the result decide.</b> "I ran it on 10,000 real rows and p95 went from 900 ms to 120 ms" ends a debate that opinions cannot.</li>
      <li><b>Say what you have and have not tried.</b> "I have read about it" and "I have run it in production" are different levels of confidence; name which one you have.</li>
      <li><b>Change your mind when the evidence does,</b> and say so plainly.</li>
      <li><b>Hands on the keyboard:</b> read the code, run the command, open the log. Second-hand summaries, including an AI's, are a starting point, not a conclusion.</li>
      <li><b>Write down what you learned</b> so the next person starts from your result, not from zero.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Evidence and honesty</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Claims match evidence.</b> "Tests pass" means you saw them pass, with the output. A check that could not run is reported as not run.</li>
      <li><b>Say what you checked</b> in the sentence: "no schedule in the file (searched the whole file)", not "there is no schedule".</li>
      <li><b>"Cannot see" is not "nothing there".</b> When a source is down, report unknown, never a confident zero.</li>
      <li><b>Verify state, not proxies:</b> config saying something is enabled is not proof that it is.</li>
      <li><b>Surface bad news early:</b> a risk raised on day one is a plan; on launch day it is an incident.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Ownership</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>You build it, you run it:</b> care what happens after merge: deploys, alerts, users, cost.</li>
      <li><b>Finish things:</b> small, complete changes over large half-done ones. Done means tested, reviewed, deployed and observed.</li>
      <li><b>Leave the system better:</b> fix the stale doc, the flaky test, the missing alert you tripped over.</li>
      <li><b>Turn every mistake into a guard:</b> a test, a hook, a rule, so it cannot happen twice.</li>
      <li><b>Know when to stop:</b> after a few failed attempts at the same problem, step back, write down what you know, and ask, rather than thrashing.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Quality habits</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>A failing test before the fix; a passing one after.</li>
      <li>Read the official docs for the version you use before relying on an API.</li>
      <li>Never weaken a check to get to green.</li>
      <li>Review your own diff before asking anyone else to.</li>
      <li>Prefer the simplest design that meets today's need, and write down the trade-off.</li>
      <li>Treat security, privacy and cost as part of "working", not extras.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Communication</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Write clearly:</b> PR descriptions that say what changed, why, and how it was verified; design docs others can build from.</li>
      <li><b>Lead with the answer,</b> then the evidence, then the detail.</li>
      <li><b>Plain words and real numbers</b> beat adjectives: "40% fewer bytes scanned", not "much faster".</li>
      <li><b>Ask early and specifically:</b> what you tried, what you saw, what you need.</li>
      <li><b>Claim before you build:</b> say what you are about to change so others do not duplicate it.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Collaboration</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Review generously and honestly:</b> catch real problems, explain why, suggest a fix; skip personal taste.</li>
      <li><b>Assume good intent;</b> discuss the system, not the person, especially after incidents.</li>
      <li><b>Teach what you know:</b> pair, document, record the trap you hit.</li>
      <li><b>Respect decisions once made;</b> reopen them with new evidence, not repeated opinion.</li>
      <li><b>Make others faster:</b> the best contribution is often a tool, template or fix that removes work for the whole team.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Breadth and curiosity</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Learn the layer below and the layer above your own work: the database under your service, the user above your API.</li>
      <li>Follow a request or a row end to end at least once: client, network, service, database, pipeline, warehouse.</li>
      <li>Spend time outside your area of depth: data people learn deploys and on-call; platform people learn SQL and modelling.</li>
      <li>Read incidents and postmortems from your own systems and others'.</li>
      <li>Keep a list of things you do not understand yet, and work through it.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Attitude, knowledge, adaptability, persistence</h3>
    <p><i>My view</i> of what each quality looks like at work:</p>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Quality</th><th>Looks like</th><th>Does not look like</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Attitude</td><td>Curious, humble about what you do not know, positive about hard problems, generous with credit</td><td>Defensive about mistakes; "not my job"; cynicism about every change</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Knowledge</td><td>Fundamentals understood deeply enough to reason from them; breadth across the stack; knows where to look and checks the source</td><td>Collecting tool names; answers from memory without checking; depth in one tool only</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Adaptability</td><td>Picks up a new language, cloud or tool when the problem needs it; drops a favourite approach when evidence says so; adjusts to new team practices, including AI</td><td>"We have always done it this way"; refusing new tools, or chasing every new one</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Persistence (never give up)</td><td>Stays with a hard problem until it is solved or clearly handed over; changes approach after each failed attempt; asks for help with evidence; finishes the last 10%</td><td>Repeating the same failing attempt; quietly abandoning work; declaring victory before the check is green</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note">Never giving up is not the same as never stopping. After a few failed attempts at the same thing, step back: write down what you tried and what you saw, change the approach, or bring someone in. The goal is never abandoned; the method is changed.</p>
    <p class="note"><i>The other view:</i> sometimes the right call is to abandon the goal itself, because the problem turned out not to be worth solving, and persistence then becomes a cost.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Chore or play</h3>
    <p><i>Our view:</i> the same task can feel like a chore to one person and like play to another, and that difference may shape how much people enjoy it and, we think, how good they become at it (the next cards include evidence that cuts the other way). This card gathers what others have said about that idea; it is a way of looking at work, not a rule.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Mark Twain wrote a scene about it.</b> In <i>The Adventures of Tom Sawyer</i> (1876), Tom is made to whitewash a fence as a punishment, and persuades the neighbourhood children that it is a privilege, until they trade him their treasures for a turn.</li>
      <li><b>Play</b> is described as a range of intrinsically motivated activities: done for their own sake. The player can still be intently focused, especially when the play has goals, as in a game. Johan Huizinga&rsquo;s <i>Homo Ludens</i> (1938) treated play as a separate sphere of human activity.</li>
      <li><b>Self-determination theory</b> (Edward Deci and Richard Ryan) names three basic needs behind self-motivated behaviour: <b>autonomy</b>, <b>competence</b> and <b>relatedness</b>.</li>
      <li><b>Flow</b>, described by Mihaly Csikszentmihalyi, is absorption in a task whose challenge matches your skill, with clear goals, quick feedback and the activity felt as rewarding in itself.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>When rewards change the feeling</h3>
    <p>The <b>overjustification effect</b>: offering an expected external reward, such as money or prizes, for something people already enjoy can reduce their own motivation to do it, sometimes called &ldquo;crowding out&rdquo;. Early demonstrations include Edward Deci (1971) and Lepper, Greene and Nisbett (1973). The effect seems stronger in children than in college students, but findings across studies are not entirely consistent.</p>
    <p class="note"><i>Analysis:</i> one way to read this is that a task can move from play towards chore when it is done mainly for the reward. The research summarised here does not say whether it moves back.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Where the idea meets its limits</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Deliberate practice</b>, studied by K. Anders Ericsson, is described as highly structured, cognitively effortful and without immediate rewards. Ericsson wrote that it &ldquo;is not inherently motivating&rdquo;. Our reading: getting very good often includes parts that do not feel like play.</li>
      <li><b>Turning work into a game</b> has critics: researcher Sebastian Deterding described early gamification as not fun and creating an artificial sense of achievement, and poorly designed workplace gamification has been compared to micromanagement.</li>
      <li><b>Job crafting</b> (Amy Wrzesniewski and Jane Dutton, 2001) describes people reshaping their own tasks to find more meaning at work; it is said to have the potential to improve quality of life at work and performance.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Two ways of seeing it</h3>
    <p><i>One view:</i> how we frame a task, as something we chose and are curious about rather than something imposed, may change how it feels to do. The ideas above (autonomy in self-determination theory, flow, job crafting) point that way.</p>
    <p><i>The other view:</i> some work is simply hard or dull, and calling it play does not change that. On this view, finishing what needs doing, and then resting, matters more than how it feels along the way.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>"I'll learn it", not just "I don't know"</h3>
    <p>When you meet something new, answer with what you will do, honestly.</p>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Instead of</th><th>Say</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>"I don't know Kafka."</td><td>"I haven't used Kafka in production yet. I learn quickly: I'll run it locally this week and come back with what I find."</td></tr>
      <tr><td>"Not my area."</td><td>"I don't know that part of the system yet. Who owns it? I'll read the code and pair with them."</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Bluffing an answer</td><td>"I'm not sure. I'll check the docs and confirm by end of day."</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <ul>
      <li>Be exact about your current level: honesty about what you have not done is what makes "I learn quickly" believable.</li>
      <li>Follow through: a promise to learn that is kept builds more trust than pretending to know.</li>
      <li>Show the evidence of learning: a lab, a spike, a written summary.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Behaviours with AI</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Behaviour</th><th>Looks like</th><th>Does not look like</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Direct the work</td><td>A clear brief: goal, context, rules, definition of done, what to report</td><td>"Make this better"</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Prepare the ground</td><td>Context files, exact test commands, guardrails, pinned docs in the repo</td><td>Re-explaining the project every session</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Verify</td><td>Runs the tests, reads the diff, checks claims against docs and data</td><td>Trusting confident output</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Own</td><td>Answers for every line that ships</td><td>"The AI did it"</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Protect</td><td>No secrets or personal data in prompts; least-privilege tools</td><td>Pasting production credentials into a chat</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Build roads</td><td>Extends frameworks and blueprints; writes codemods</td><td>Generating the same boilerplate again</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Keep learning</td><td>Asks the tool to explain, then confirms by doing</td><td>Shipping code you could not explain</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Stay engaged</td><td>Watches the run, steps in when it drifts, reviews the result</td><td>Prompt, walk away, merge whatever comes back</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Behaviours by level</h3>
    <div class="levels">
      <div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Asks for help with specifics: what was tried and seen</li><li>Tests own changes; reports results honestly</li><li>Reviews AI output line by line before committing</li><li>Writes down what they learn</li></ul></div>
      <div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Owns features from design to production and after</li><li>Proves ideas with spikes and measurements before arguing</li><li>Turns repeated mistakes into tests and rules</li><li>Gives useful, kind reviews</li></ul></div>
      <div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Makes the evidence standard for the team ("what did you check?")</li><li>Builds the blueprints and guardrails others use, including for AI agents</li><li>Raises risks early and stops work that is going wrong</li><li>Mentors through pairing and written examples</li></ul></div>
      <div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Shapes culture: blameless learning, honest status, small safe changes</li><li>Changes direction when the evidence says so, publicly</li><li>Multiplies the organisation through platforms, standards and teaching</li><li>Holds the line on quality when deadlines push against it</li></ul></div>
    </div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Weekly self-check</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Did every claim I made this week have evidence behind it?</li>
      <li>Did I try something myself before forming an opinion on it?</li>
      <li>Did I review and understand all the AI-written code I shipped?</li>
      <li>Did I leave one thing better than I found it?</li>
      <li>Did I turn a mistake into a guard?</li>
      <li>Did I learn something outside my usual area?</li>
      <li>Did I help someone else get faster?</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Questions to ask in reviews and interviews</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>"How do you know it works?" (looking for evidence, not confidence)</li>
      <li>"What did you try before deciding?" (looking for experiments)</li>
      <li>"What would make you change your mind?"</li>
      <li>"Walk me through what the AI produced and why you kept or changed it."</li>
      <li>"Tell me about something you broke and what you changed afterwards."</li>
      <li>"What happens if this dependency is down?"</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Falsifiability?action=raw" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Falsifiability</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Psychological_safety?action=raw" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Psychological safety</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Goodhart%27s_law?action=raw" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Goodhart&#x27;s law</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Play_(activity)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Play (activity)</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Homo_Ludens" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Homo Ludens</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Self-determination_theory" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Self-determination theory</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flow_(psychology)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Flow (psychology)</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Overjustification_effect" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Overjustification effect</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Practice_(learning_method)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Practice (learning method)</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gamification" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Gamification</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Job_crafting" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Job crafting</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Adventures_of_Tom_Sawyer" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: The Adventures of Tom Sawyer</a>.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= WAYS OF WORKING ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-ways" data-name="Ways of Working">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Ways of working: commits, tickets, Agile and XP</h2><p>The habits that decide whether a team is fast or merely busy: a commit history people can read, tickets people can build from, updates that show the real state, and a pace that lasts. Where a claim comes from a spec or a source it is linked at the end; where it is opinion from years of working in teams, it says so.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>feat, fix and chore: Conventional Commits</h3>
    <p>The Conventional Commits spec gives every commit message a type in front: <code>type(optional scope): description</code>. The point is a history a person can scan and a tool can read, for example to work out the next version number or write release notes.</p>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Use it when</th><th>Effect on the version (Semantic Versioning)</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>feat:</code></td><td>Something a user of the code can now do that they could not before</td><td>MINOR</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>fix:</code></td><td>A bug is patched: the code now does what it already claimed to do</td><td>PATCH</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>feat!:</code> or a <code>BREAKING CHANGE:</code> footer</td><td>Existing users must change something (any type can carry the <code>!</code>)</td><td>MAJOR</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>chore:</code></td><td>Housekeeping that changes nothing a user sees: dependency bumps, tidying config</td><td>none</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>docs:</code> <code>refactor:</code> <code>test:</code> <code>perf:</code> <code>build:</code> <code>ci:</code> <code>style:</code></td><td>What the name says. These come from the widely used commitlint convention (based on Angular's), not from the spec itself</td><td>none, unless marked breaking</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>feat(search): filter results by court
fix(login): stop the session expiring after 5 minutes
chore: bump test dependencies
refactor(api)!: rename /cases to /judgments

BREAKING CHANGE: clients must call /judgments</code></pre></div>
    <p><b>How to choose:</b> ask "what would a user of this code notice?" A new capability is <code>feat</code>. Wrong behaviour made right is <code>fix</code>. Nothing noticeable is <code>chore</code>, <code>refactor</code>, <code>docs</code> or <code>test</code>. If a commit needs two types, it is usually two commits.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Writing a ticket someone can build from</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Start with the why.</b> The common user story shape is "As a &lt;role&gt; I can &lt;capability&gt;, so that &lt;benefit&gt;". The "so that" is the part people skip and the part that matters most.</li>
      <li><b>Acceptance criteria:</b> a short list of things that are true when it is done, each one checkable. "Given a signed-in user, when they search for X, then they see Y."</li>
      <li><b>Context:</b> links to the error, the log line, the screenshot, the conversation. The reader should not have to find you to start.</li>
      <li><b>Out of scope:</b> say what this ticket will not do, so it does not quietly grow.</li>
      <li><b>INVEST</b> (Bill Wake): Independent, Negotiable, Valuable, Estimable, Small, Testable. If it fails "Small", split it.</li>
      <li><b>A bug ticket</b> has steps to reproduce, what you expected, what happened, and where (version, environment, browser).</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>What Agile and XP actually said</h3>
    <p><b>The Agile Manifesto</b> (17 practitioners, 2001) values:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>individuals and interactions over processes and tools</li>
      <li>working software over comprehensive documentation</li>
      <li>customer collaboration over contract negotiation</li>
      <li>responding to change over following a plan</li>
    </ul>
    <p><b>Extreme Programming</b> (Kent Beck, from the Chrysler C3 payroll project) named four values in 1999: communication, simplicity, feedback and courage. Respect was added in the second edition. Its practices include test-first, pair programming, continuous integration, small releases, and a <b>sustainable pace</b>: one of its rules is simply "no overtime".</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Honest updates: don't give an update and hide</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>The daily scrum</b> is meant to be under 15 minutes, to show progress toward the goal <b>and the issues in the way</b>. Its job is to surface problems, not to perform being busy.</li>
      <li><b>A burndown chart</b> is described as a publicly displayed chart of remaining work. Public is the point: the real state, visible to everyone, every day.</li>
      <li><b>Say "blocked" the moment you are,</b> and on what. A blocker raised at 10am costs an hour; raised at the sprint review it costs the sprint.</li>
      <li><b>"Nearly done" is not a status.</b> Say what is done, what is left, and what you are unsure about.</li>
      <li><b>Bad news early is a plan; bad news late is an incident.</b> Teams that punish the messenger get green dashboards and red launches.</li>
      <li><b>Show, don't tell:</b> a link to the PR, the test run, the demo, beats a sentence about progress.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Continuous proactiveness: not every day is the same</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Not every day is 9 to 5.</b> An incident at night, a launch, a customer stuck on a deadline: some days need more, and a good engineer shows up for them.</li>
      <li><b>Don&rsquo;t be complacent.</b> Keep perspective: a hard day at a keyboard is not as hard as a shift in a coal mine. Most of this work is safe, warm and well paid. That is a reason to give it full effort, not to coast.</li>
      <li><b>Proactive means you see it before you are told:</b> the alert that is noisy, the doc that is wrong, the dependency about to expire, the colleague who is stuck.</li>
      <li><b>But the long-run pace is sustainable.</b> XP's "no overtime" rule is about the normal week: a team that runs every week on heroics stops noticing its own mistakes. A hard day is followed by recovery, not by another hard day.</li>
      <li><b>Make the hard days rarer:</b> after every late night, ask what would have prevented it, and fix that (a test, an alert, a runbook).</li>
      <li><b>Flexible both ways:</b> giving extra on the day it is needed earns the quiet afternoon when it is not.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Why building a product still takes grinding, even with AI</h3>
    <p><i>Our view, with the evidence it rests on and the other view at the end.</i> AI tools and skills make the typing faster. A product is more than the typing: it is the problem understood, the edge cases found, the claims checked, the users listened to, and the same thing fixed and improved again and again. That repeated, unglamorous work is the grind, and it has not gone away.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>The hard part is the problem, not the code.</b> Fred Brooks argued in &ldquo;No Silver Bullet&rdquo; (1986) that no single technology or technique promises even a tenfold improvement within a decade, because much of software&rsquo;s difficulty is <i>essential complexity</i>: if users need a program to do 30 different things, it must do those 30 things. Tools remove <i>accidental</i> complexity. AI is a very strong tool for that, and the essential part still needs people to work through it.</li>
      <li><b>The last 10% takes the other 90%.</b> The ninety-ninety rule, attributed to Tom Cargill of Bell Labs: &ldquo;The first 90 percent of the code accounts for the first 90 percent of the development time. The remaining 10 percent of the code accounts for the other 90 percent.&rdquo; A demo is the first 90%. A product people rely on is the rest.</li>
      <li><b>Feeling fast is not being fast.</b> In a randomised controlled trial published by METR in July 2025, experienced open-source developers took 19% <i>longer</i> on their tasks when using AI tools, while believing beforehand that AI would make them 24% faster and still believing afterwards that it had made them 20% faster. One study, of one group, at one point in time; but it shows why you measure instead of trusting the feeling.</li>
      <li><b>What the grind looked like, building this guide</b> (first-hand): checking a claim about npm against npm&rsquo;s own changelog showed that dependency install scripts are now blocked by default, which memory would have got wrong; an encyclopaedia put async/await in the wrong edition of JavaScript until TC39&rsquo;s own list corrected it; and an independent review of each page found real mistakes before it went live. None of that was typing. All of it was grinding.</li>
    </ul>
    <h4>What the grind actually is</h4>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Grind</th><th>What AI helps with</th><th>What still needs you</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Understanding the problem</td><td>Summarising research, drafting questions</td><td>Talking to users, deciding what matters</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Edge cases and failures</td><td>Suggesting cases, writing tests</td><td>Knowing which failures your users would actually hit</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Checking claims</td><td>Finding sources fast</td><td>Opening the source, and refusing to publish what you could not check</td></tr>
      <tr><td>The last 10%</td><td>Drafting the fixes</td><td>Caring enough to finish: install, upgrade, docs, support</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Doing it again</td><td>Remembering the steps, if you wrote them down</td><td>Showing up the next day, and the day after</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note"><i>The other view:</i> &ldquo;work smarter, not harder&rdquo;, and some teams do ship far faster with AI. Both can be true. Grinding without direction is just tiredness, and the earlier cards on this page still hold: a sustainable pace, and a hard day followed by recovery. The point is not more hours; it is that the hours spent understanding, checking and finishing cannot be skipped.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>When Agile slows a company down</h3>
    <p><i>This card is opinion and observation from working in many teams, not a measured study.</i> Agile was meant to make teams faster. In many companies it has done the opposite, and the pattern is usually the same: the ceremonies were kept and the values were dropped.</p>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>What the manifesto said</th><th>What often happens instead</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Individuals and interactions over processes and tools</td><td>The process and the ticket tool become the work: estimation meetings, refinement of refinement, fields nobody reads</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Working software over comprehensive documentation</td><td>Status reports and slide decks about the sprint, while nothing ships for weeks</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Customer collaboration</td><td>The team never meets a user; requirements arrive through three layers of proxies</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Responding to change</td><td>A fixed quarterly plan, renamed a "roadmap", with sprints as deadlines</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Small, frequent releases</td><td>Two-week sprints that end in a release train once a quarter</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p><b>The way back</b> is small: ship something real more often, put the people who build next to the people who use it, cut any meeting whose output nobody uses, and measure delivery (how often you release, how long a change takes to reach users) rather than story points.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://www.conventionalcommits.org/en/v1.0.0/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Conventional Commits specification</a>; <a href="https://semver.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Semantic Versioning</a>; <a href="https://github.com/conventional-changelog/commitlint/tree/master/%40commitlint/config-conventional" target="_blank" rel="noopener">commitlint config-conventional</a>; <a href="https://agilemanifesto.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Manifesto for Agile Software Development</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Extreme_programming" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Extreme programming</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scrum_(software_development)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Scrum</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/User_story" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: User story</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/INVEST_(mnemonic)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: INVEST</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/No_Silver_Bullet" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: No Silver Bullet</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ninety%E2%80%93ninety_rule" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Ninety-ninety rule</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/METR" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: METR</a> and <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.09089" target="_blank" rel="noopener">the study on arXiv</a> (the arXiv page could not be reached from here; the figures are as Wikipedia reports them). The last card is the author's opinion.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= MATHEMATICS ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-mathematics" data-name="Mathematics">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Mathematics, for life and for engineering</h2><p>It starts with the everyday maths every person should know, for money, risk and reading the news, then the mathematics that keeps showing up in real engineering work: logic and discrete maths, how algorithms scale, probability and statistics, the linear algebra behind embeddings, how computers store numbers, and quick estimation. Not to pass an exam, but to reason precisely and catch what looks right and is not.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Everyday mathematics, for everyone</h3>
    <p>You do not need advanced maths to live well, but a handful of ideas protect your money, your health and your judgement every day. None of this is about being "good at maths"; it is about not being fooled, including by yourself.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Percentages and money</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Up and down are not equal.</b> A price that rises 50% and then falls 50% ends at 75% of where it started (1.5 × 0.5 = 0.75). Down 20% then up 20% leaves you at 96%.</li>
      <li><b>Percent is not percentage points.</b> An interest rate going from 2% to 3% is up one percentage point, but up 50% in percent terms. Watch which one you are being told.</li>
      <li><b>Quick mental maths:</b> 10% is the number with the decimal point moved one place; 5% is half of that; 15% is the two added; 1% is the point moved two places.</li>
      <li><b>Compare unit prices,</b> per kilo or per litre, not pack prices. The bigger pack is not always cheaper.</li>
      <li><b>Small amounts add up:</b> £4 a day is £1,460 a year, and £14,600 over ten years, before any interest.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Compound growth, for you and against you</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Compounding means growth on growth.</b> £1,000 at 5% a year becomes about £1,629 after ten years if the interest is reinvested, against £1,500 if it is not.</li>
      <li><b>The rule of 72:</b> divide 72 by the yearly rate to estimate how many years it takes to double. At 6% about 12 years (exactly 11.9); at 3% about 24 (exactly 23.4).</li>
      <li><b>Debt compounds too.</b> A balance at 24% a year, left unpaid, doubles in about 3 years (72 ÷ 24). Pay off the most expensive debt first.</li>
      <li><b>Inflation is compounding in reverse:</b> at 3% a year, money left in a drawer loses half its buying power in about 24 years.</li>
      <li><b>Start early.</b> Time does more of the work than the amount: small, regular savings started young often end up ahead of larger ones started late.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Risk and chance</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Relative risk can mislead.</b> "Doubles your risk" may mean going from 1 in 10,000 to 2 in 10,000. Always ask: out of how many?</li>
      <li><b>Base rates matter.</b> Suppose a test is 99% accurate both ways and the condition affects 1 person in 1,000. Of everyone who tests positive, only about 9% actually have it, because the false positives from the 999 healthy people outnumber the true ones.</li>
      <li><b>Expected value:</b> multiply each outcome by its chance and add them up. Lotteries and most casino games pay back less than you put in, on average, by design.</li>
      <li><b>Streaks happen by chance.</b> A coin that lands heads five times is not "due" tails; each toss is independent.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Reading numbers in the news</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Average or median?</b> A few very high incomes pull the average up; the median (the middle value) is often a fairer picture of a typical person.</li>
      <li><b>Correlation is not causation.</b> Two things rising together may share a third cause, or be coincidence.</li>
      <li><b>Check the chart's axis.</b> A bar chart whose axis does not start at zero can make a small difference look huge.</li>
      <li><b>Ask for the size of the sample</b> and who was asked. A survey of 20 people, or only of fans, says little.</li>
      <li><b>Feel the size of big numbers:</b> a million seconds is about 11.6 days; a billion seconds is about 31.7 years.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">The engineering cards below build on the same ideas: medians and percentiles, probability and estimation.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>What to know, and where it shows up</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Area</th><th>Core ideas</th><th>Where you meet it</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Logic</td><td>AND, OR, NOT, implication, De Morgan's laws, truth tables</td><td>Every <code>if</code>, SQL <code>WHERE</code> clauses, NULL's three-valued logic, access rules</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Sets and relations</td><td>Union, intersection, difference, functions, equivalence, ordering</td><td>SQL joins and set operators, deduplication, keys, permissions</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Proof and induction</td><td>Invariants, induction, proof by contradiction</td><td>Reasoning about loops, recursion, idempotency, correctness of migrations</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Graphs</td><td>Nodes, edges, paths, cycles, trees, DAGs, topological order</td><td>Dependency graphs, Airflow and dbt DAGs, networks, citations</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Combinatorics</td><td>Counting, permutations, combinations, the pigeonhole principle</td><td>Key spaces, test cases, collisions, capacity</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Complexity</td><td>Big-O, best/average/worst case, amortised cost</td><td>Choosing data structures, why a query or loop is slow</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Probability</td><td>Independence, conditional probability, Bayes' rule, expected value</td><td>Retries, failure rates, availability, A/B tests, ML</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Statistics</td><td>Distributions, mean vs median, percentiles, variance, sampling, confidence intervals</td><td>Latency, SLOs, data quality, experiments</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Linear algebra</td><td>Vectors, matrices, dot product, norms, cosine similarity</td><td>Embeddings, vector search, ML models</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Calculus and optimisation</td><td>Rates of change, gradients, minimising a function</td><td>How models are trained (gradient descent), growth rates</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Number representation</td><td>Binary, two's complement, floating point, overflow</td><td>Money, timestamps, IDs, numeric bugs</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Modular arithmetic</td><td>Remainders, hashing into buckets</td><td>Sharding, partitioning, hash tables, checksums</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Complexity you should feel, not just know</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Growth</th><th>Example</th><th>n = 1 million</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>O(1)</td><td>Hash table lookup</td><td>1 step</td></tr>
      <tr><td>O(log n)</td><td>Binary search, B-tree index lookup</td><td>about 20 steps</td></tr>
      <tr><td>O(n)</td><td>Scanning a list or a table</td><td>1 million steps</td></tr>
      <tr><td>O(n log n)</td><td>Good sorting</td><td>about 20 million steps</td></tr>
      <tr><td>O(n²)</td><td>Nested loops, a self-join without an index</td><td>1 trillion steps</td></tr>
      <tr><td>O(2ⁿ)</td><td>Trying every subset</td><td>never finishes</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note">At a million items, the jump from O(n log n) to O(n²) is a factor of about 50,000: a fraction of a second becomes many minutes, and at larger sizes, days. An N+1 query pattern and a nested loop over two big lists are the usual ways it sneaks in.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Numbers computers get "wrong"</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; 0.1 + 0.2
0.30000000000000004              # binary floating point cannot store 0.1 exactly
&gt;&gt;&gt; 0.1 + 0.2 == 0.3
False
&gt;&gt;&gt; from decimal import Decimal
&gt;&gt;&gt; Decimal("0.1") + Decimal("0.2") == Decimal("0.3")
True                             # decimal arithmetic for money

// Java: int overflow wraps around silently
int max = Integer.MAX_VALUE;     // 2,147,483,647
max + 1                          // -2,147,483,648
Math.addExact(max, 1)            // throws ArithmeticException instead</code></pre></div>
    <ul>
      <li>Use decimal types or integer minor units (paise, cents) for money.</li>
      <li>Never compare floats with <code>==</code>; compare within a tolerance.</li>
      <li>Know your integer sizes: a signed 32-bit counter of milliseconds overflows in under 25 days (unsigned, about 50).</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Statistics that keep you honest</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Median and percentiles beat the mean</b> for skewed data such as latency: one slow request moves the mean, not the median.</li>
      <li><b>Variance matters:</b> two systems with the same average can have very different worst cases.</li>
      <li><b>Sample size:</b> a difference seen in 20 requests may be noise; in 20,000 it is more likely real.</li>
      <li><b>Base rates (Bayes):</b> a 99% accurate alert for a problem that happens 1 time in 10,000 fires falsely about 100 times for each real case.</li>
      <li><b>Correlation is not causation:</b> two metrics rising together may share a cause.</li>
      <li><b>Survivorship:</b> you only see the systems, users or rows that made it through.</li>
      <li><b>Multiple comparisons:</b> test twenty things at 95% confidence and you should expect about one "significant" result by chance.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Probability in operations</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># availability of dependencies in series multiplies
0.999 * 0.999 * 0.999           # ≈ 0.997  (three 99.9% services)

# chance at least one of n calls is slow, if each is slow with p = 0.01
1 - (1 - 0.01) ** 100           # ≈ 0.63

# birthday paradox: random 32-bit IDs collide sooner than you think
import math
math.sqrt(2 ** 32) * 1.1774     # ≈ 77,000 IDs for a 50% chance of a collision</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">The birthday bound is why IDs are 128-bit UUIDs or longer, and why hash collisions are planned for, not assumed away.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Linear algebra behind embeddings</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>An <b>embedding</b> is a vector: a list of numbers that places a text in a space where similar meanings are close.</li>
      <li><b>Dot product:</b> multiply matching entries and add them up.</li>
      <li><b>Cosine similarity</b> = dot product ÷ (length of a × length of b): 1 means same direction, 0 unrelated.</li>
      <li>For vectors already normalised to length 1, cosine similarity and dot product are the same, which is why many vector databases normalise first.</li>
    </ul>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>import numpy as np
a, b = np.array([0.2, 0.8, 0.1]), np.array([0.25, 0.7, 0.0])
cosine = a @ b / (np.linalg.norm(a) * np.linalg.norm(b))   # ≈ 0.99: very similar</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Estimation: powers of two and orders of magnitude</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Power</th><th>Approx.</th><th>Name</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>2¹⁰</td><td>1 thousand</td><td>KiB</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2²⁰</td><td>1 million</td><td>MiB</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2³⁰</td><td>1 billion</td><td>GiB</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2⁴⁰</td><td>1 trillion</td><td>TiB</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <ul>
      <li>Round to one significant figure and multiply orders of magnitude: being within 2× is good enough to choose a design.</li>
      <li>Check units at every step; most estimation errors are unit errors.</li>
      <li>Sanity-check against something known: a day is about 10⁵ seconds; a year about 3 × 10⁷.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>How to learn it as an adult engineer</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Learn each topic <b>through a problem you have:</b> statistics through your latency data, graphs through your DAG, linear algebra through your embeddings.</li>
      <li>Work examples <b>by hand on paper</b>, then check them in Python.</li>
      <li>Use AI as a patient tutor: ask for an explanation, then for exercises, then check your answers yourself.</li>
      <li>Fifteen minutes a day beats a weekend once a year.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

    <article class="card wide"><h3>From counting to algebra, the Feynman way</h3>
    <p>In Chapter 22 of <i>The Feynman Lectures on Physics</i> (Volume I, "Algebra"), Richard Feynman builds mathematics up from counting, so that each new idea grows out of the one before. The path, in short:</p>
    <ol>
      <li><b>Counting</b> gives the whole numbers; <b>addition</b> is counting on.</li>
      <li><b>Multiplication</b> is repeated addition; <b>raising to a power</b> is repeated multiplication.</li>
      <li><b>Undo each operation</b> and new kinds of number appear: subtraction needs negative numbers, division needs fractions, and roots need irrational and then imaginary numbers.</li>
      <li><b>The inverse of a power</b> is a logarithm.</li>
      <li><b>Put it all together</b> and you reach Euler's formula, e<sup>iθ</sup> = cos θ + i sin θ, which Feynman called "our jewel" and "the most remarkable formula in mathematics".</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Nothing has to be taken on faith: every step is a question ("what undoes this?") and its answer. <i>Our view:</i> that is a good way to learn anything, mathematics or software; <i>the other view:</i> some people learn better from worked examples first. Run the steps yourself:</p>
    <pre><code class="language-python">&quot;&quot;&quot;From counting to Euler&#x27;s formula, one step at a time.&quot;&quot;&quot;
import cmath, math

# 1. Counting gives the whole numbers. Addition is counting on.
print(&quot;3 + 4 =&quot;, 3 + 4)
# 2. Multiplication is repeated addition; powers are repeated multiplication.
print(&quot;3 * 4 =&quot;, 4 + 4 + 4, &quot;| 2 ** 10 =&quot;, 2 ** 10)
# 3. Undo each operation and new kinds of number appear:
print(&quot;3 - 5 =&quot;, 3 - 5, &quot;  (subtraction needs negative numbers)&quot;)
print(&quot;3 / 4 =&quot;, 3 / 4, &quot; (division needs fractions)&quot;)
print(&quot;sqrt(2) =&quot;, math.sqrt(2), &quot; (roots need irrational numbers)&quot;)
print(&quot;sqrt(-1) =&quot;, cmath.sqrt(-1), &quot;  (roots of negatives need imaginary numbers)&quot;)
# 4. The inverse of a power is a logarithm.
print(&quot;log2(1024) =&quot;, math.log2(1024))
# 5. Put it together: e to an imaginary power goes round a circle.
theta = math.pi / 3
print(&quot;e^(i*pi/3) =&quot;, cmath.exp(1j * theta), &quot;= cos + i*sin:&quot;, complex(math.cos(theta), math.sin(theta)))
print(&quot;e^(i*pi) + 1 =&quot;, cmath.exp(1j * math.pi) + 1, &quot; (zero, to rounding)&quot;)</code></pre>
    <p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Feynman_Lectures_on_Physics" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The Feynman Lectures on Physics (Wikipedia; Volume I, chapter 22 is "Algebra")</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Euler%27s_formula" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Euler's formula (Wikipedia, citing Volume I p. 22-10)</a>. The lectures are free to read at <a href="https://www.feynmanlectures.caltech.edu/I_22.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Caltech's Feynman Lectures site</a>.</p>
  </article>

<!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Work out percentages, compound growth and risk in daily life</li><li>Read Big-O and spot a nested loop over large data</li><li>Use median and percentiles rather than averages</li><li>Know why floats are wrong for money</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Reason about complexity when choosing data structures and queries</li><li>Apply basic probability to retries, availability and alerts</li><li>Estimate capacity and cost with orders of magnitude</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Design experiments and read their statistics critically</li><li>Use linear algebra and statistics confidently in data and ML work</li><li>Prove or argue correctness of algorithms and migrations</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Bring mathematical rigour to architecture and research decisions</li><li>Teach the maths behind the team's systems</li><li>Spot subtle statistical and numerical errors in others' work</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>Personal finance: compounding and inflation</li><li>Discrete mathematics for computer science</li><li>Probability and Bayes' rule</li><li>Statistics for experiments (A/B testing)</li><li>Linear algebra for embeddings and ML</li><li>Floating-point arithmetic</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-042j-mathematics-for-computer-science-fall-2010/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Mathematics for Computer Science (MIT OpenCourseWare)</a><span class="lnote"> Free course: logic, proofs, graphs, counting, probability</span></li><li><a href="https://www.3blue1brown.com/topics/linear-algebra" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Essence of Linear Algebra (3Blue1Brown)</a><span class="lnote"> Visual introduction to vectors and matrices</span></li><li><a href="https://www.khanacademy.org/math/statistics-probability" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Khan Academy: Statistics and probability</a><span class="lnote"> Free self-paced statistics</span></li><li><a href="https://seeing-theory.brown.edu/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Seeing Theory</a><span class="lnote"> Interactive probability and statistics</span></li><li><a href="https://docs.oracle.com/cd/E19957-01/806-3568/ncg_goldberg.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">What Every Computer Scientist Should Know About Floating-Point Arithmetic</a><span class="lnote"> David Goldberg's classic paper</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="runcode">A question, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/enrichmeai.github.io/issues/new?title=%5BMathematics%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>. Nothing here is tracked and there are no comments to store: questions live in the open, where the next learner can find the answer.</p></article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Work out percentages, compound growth and risk in daily life</li><li>Read Big-O and spot a nested loop over large data</li><li>Use median and percentiles rather than averages</li><li>Know why floats are wrong for money</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Reason about complexity when choosing data structures and queries</li><li>Apply basic probability to retries, availability and alerts</li><li>Estimate capacity and cost with orders of magnitude</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Design experiments and read their statistics critically</li><li>Use linear algebra and statistics confidently in data and ML work</li><li>Prove or argue correctness of algorithms and migrations</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Bring mathematical rigour to architecture and research decisions</li><li>Teach the maths behind the team's systems</li><li>Spot subtle statistical and numerical errors in others' work</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>Personal finance: compounding and inflation</li><li>Discrete mathematics for computer science</li><li>Probability and Bayes' rule</li><li>Statistics for experiments (A/B testing)</li><li>Linear algebra for embeddings and ML</li><li>Floating-point arithmetic</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-042j-mathematics-for-computer-science-fall-2010/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Mathematics for Computer Science (MIT OpenCourseWare)</a><span class="lnote"> Free course: logic, proofs, graphs, counting, probability</span></li><li><a href="https://www.3blue1brown.com/topics/linear-algebra" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Essence of Linear Algebra (3Blue1Brown)</a><span class="lnote"> Visual introduction to vectors and matrices</span></li><li><a href="https://www.khanacademy.org/math/statistics-probability" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Khan Academy: Statistics and probability</a><span class="lnote"> Free self-paced statistics</span></li><li><a href="https://seeing-theory.brown.edu/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Seeing Theory</a><span class="lnote"> Interactive probability and statistics</span></li><li><a href="https://docs.oracle.com/cd/E19957-01/806-3568/ncg_goldberg.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">What Every Computer Scientist Should Know About Floating-Point Arithmetic</a><span class="lnote"> David Goldberg's classic paper</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="runcode">A question, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/enrichmeai.github.io/issues/new?title=%5BMathematics%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>. Nothing here is tracked and there are no comments to store: questions live in the open, where the next learner can find the answer.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= SCIENCE ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-science" data-name="Science &amp; Method">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Science and the scientific method</h2><p>Engineering runs on the same method as science: observe, form a hypothesis, predict, test, and let the result decide. Debugging, performance work, data quality and AI evaluation are all experiments. This tab is about thinking like a scientist at work, and knowing enough about how computers physically work to reason about them.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>The method, applied to engineering</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>In science</th><th>In engineering</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Observe</td><td>Notice something unexplained</td><td>p95 latency doubled after Tuesday's deploy</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Hypothesise</td><td>A testable explanation</td><td>The new query skips the index on <code>store_id</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Predict</td><td>What must be true if the hypothesis holds</td><td><code>EXPLAIN</code> will show a sequential scan; reverting the query will restore latency</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Test</td><td>An experiment that could prove it wrong</td><td>Run <code>EXPLAIN ANALYZE</code>; try the fix on a copy with production-sized data</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Conclude</td><td>Accept, reject or refine</td><td>Sequential scan confirmed; index added; p95 back to normal, measured</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Share</td><td>Publish so others can check</td><td>PR description with before/after numbers; a regression test</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note">The key word is <b>falsifiable</b>: a good hypothesis says what result would prove it wrong. "Something in the network is flaky" cannot be tested; "the timeouts happen only on calls through the NAT gateway" can.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>What science can answer, and what it cannot settle alone</h3>
    <p><i>My view:</i> science is the best method we have for questions about what is true of the world and how it works: questions an observation or an experiment could answer. Keep it for those, and keep a balance. Trying to find every answer in science is the same mistake as trying to find every answer in a religion, or in any single tradition: one tool treated as the whole toolbox.</p>
    <p class="note"><i>The other view:</i> some argue that evidence will in time inform, or even settle, more of the questions below about what we ought to do and what matters; others argue that science reaches less far than this card says.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>What is, and how it works.</b> Does this index make the query faster? Does this drug lower blood pressure? Observation, measurement and experiment answer these: this is science's home ground.</li>
      <li><b>What we ought to do.</b> Should we collect this data at all? Is this model fair to the people it scores? Ethics, law and judgement answer these, <b>informed</b> by evidence about what the consequences will be.</li>
      <li><b>What matters, and why.</b> What makes work worth doing? What gives a life meaning? People find these answers in philosophy, faith, tradition, art, relationships and their own experience.</li>
    </ul>
    <ul>
      <li><b>An "is" does not give an "ought" on its own.</b> David Hume noticed that arguments slide from what is the case to what should be done without saying how. Data tells you what users do, not what you should build for them. Metrics guide; values decide.</li>
      <li><b>Science rests on assumptions it does not prove by its own method:</b> that nature behaves regularly, that instruments and senses are roughly reliable, that results are reported honestly. They are good assumptions, and they are still assumptions.</li>
      <li><b>Treating science as the answer to every question</b> has a name, <i>scientism</i>. It is a philosophical position, not a scientific finding.</li>
      <li><b>People disagree about how far the domains overlap.</b> Stephen Jay Gould argued that science and religion answer different questions ("non-overlapping magisteria"); others argue they do overlap. You do not need to settle it to work well with people of every view.</li>
      <li><b>Respect people whose answers differ from yours.</b> Many careful scientists hold a religious faith and many hold none. A team works best when what is measurable is measured, and what is not is discussed with humility.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">Use each kind of knowledge for the questions it can answer, and be honest about where every one of them runs out, including your own favourite.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Debugging as an experiment</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Reproduce first.</b> A bug you cannot reproduce is a bug you cannot prove you fixed.</li>
      <li><b>Change one variable at a time,</b> or you will not know which change mattered.</li>
      <li><b>Bisect:</b> halve the search space each step (<code>git bisect</code>, removing half the input, disabling half the config).</li>
      <li><b>Write down each hypothesis and its result,</b> including the ones that failed. They narrow the search.</li>
      <li><b>Prefer evidence that could surprise you:</b> look at the raw log, the packet, the row, not a summary that agrees with your guess.</li>
      <li><b>Stop when the evidence explains everything,</b> not when you have a plausible story.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Measurement</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Accuracy vs precision:</b> a clock that is consistently 5 minutes fast is precise but not accurate.</li>
      <li><b>Know your instrument:</b> what it measures, its resolution, what it misses, and what it reports when it cannot measure.</li>
      <li><b>Units on every number.</b> "Latency 200" means nothing.</li>
      <li><b>Error bars:</b> run a benchmark several times and report the spread, not one lucky run.</li>
      <li><b>Baseline first:</b> measure before the change, in the same conditions, or the comparison is meaningless.</li>
      <li><b>The observer effect:</b> profilers, debug logging and tracing change what they measure. Account for it.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Experiments with data and users</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Control group:</b> compare against something that did not get the change, at the same time.</li>
      <li><b>Randomise</b> who gets which variant, so other differences average out.</li>
      <li><b>Decide the metric and sample size before you look,</b> or you will find what you hoped for (p-hacking).</li>
      <li><b>Confounders:</b> a feature launched in the same week as a holiday cannot be judged on that week alone.</li>
      <li><b>Reproducibility:</b> pin data versions, code versions and random seeds so someone else gets the same answer.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Biases that fool engineers</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Bias</th><th>Looks like</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Confirmation bias</td><td>Only looking for logs that support your theory</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Anchoring</td><td>Sticking to the first estimate or cause you heard</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Availability</td><td>Blaming the cause of the last incident because it is fresh</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Survivorship</td><td>Studying only the services that did not fail</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Sunk cost</td><td>Continuing a failing approach because of time already spent</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Automation bias</td><td>Trusting a tool's output, including an AI's, over contrary evidence</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Overconfidence</td><td>"It works on my machine" as proof</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>How computers physically work</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>CPU:</b> executes instructions in cycles; modern cores run billions per second and work on several instructions at once.</li>
      <li><b>Memory hierarchy:</b> registers, then CPU caches (L1, L2, L3), then RAM, then SSD, then network. Each step is slower than the one before: a few times slower between the CPU caches, and thousands of times slower from RAM to an SSD. Code that reads memory in order is far faster than code that jumps around.</li>
      <li><b>Storage:</b> SSDs read randomly far faster than spinning disks, but sequential reads are still fastest.</li>
      <li><b>Networks:</b> limited by the speed of light; a round trip across a continent cannot be faster than tens of milliseconds.</li>
      <li><b>Energy and heat:</b> compute has a physical cost, which is also a cost in money and carbon.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">These physical limits explain most performance rules on the <a href="#performance">Performance</a> and <a href="#sysdesign">System Design</a> tabs.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Reading research and technical papers</h3>
    <ol>
      <li><b>First pass (minutes):</b> title, abstract, introduction, headings, conclusions. Decide if it is worth more time.</li>
      <li><b>Second pass (an hour):</b> figures, tables, the main argument; note what you do not understand.</li>
      <li><b>Third pass (hours):</b> recreate the work in your head or in code; challenge every assumption.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="note">Ask of any claim: what was measured, on what data, compared with what, and could the result be explained another way?</p>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Reproduce a bug before fixing it</li><li>Change one thing at a time and measure before and after</li><li>Write down hypotheses and results while debugging</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Debug systematically with falsifiable hypotheses and bisection</li><li>Run fair benchmarks with baselines and error bars</li><li>Spot common biases in your own reasoning</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Design controlled experiments and A/B tests correctly</li><li>Read papers critically and apply their results with care</li><li>Teach the method through how you run investigations</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Build a culture of evidence and experiment across the organisation</li><li>Lead research-style work with rigour and reproducibility</li><li>Judge competing claims across fields</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>Falsifiability (Karl Popper)</li><li>Experimental design and controls</li><li>Cognitive biases</li><li>Memory hierarchy and computer architecture</li><li>How to read a paper</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://www.cs.virginia.edu/~robins/YouAndYourResearch.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">You and Your Research (Richard Hamming)</a><span class="lnote"> A classic talk on doing important work</span></li><li><a href="https://press.stripe.com/the-art-of-doing-science-and-engineering" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The Art of Doing Science and Engineering (Hamming)</a><span class="lnote"> Book on thinking as a scientist-engineer</span></li><li><a href="https://calteches.library.caltech.edu/51/2/CargoCult.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cargo Cult Science (Richard Feynman)</a><span class="lnote"> On scientific integrity</span></li><li><a href="http://ccr.sigcomm.org/online/files/p83-keshavA.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">How to Read a Paper (S. Keshav)</a><span class="lnote"> The three-pass method</span></li><li><a href="https://csapp.cs.cmu.edu/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Computer Systems: A Programmer's Perspective</a><span class="lnote"> How computers really work, for programmers</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Is%E2%80%93ought_problem?action=raw" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Is-ought problem (Hume)</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Non-overlapping_magisteria?action=raw" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Non-overlapping magisteria (Gould)</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scientism?action=raw" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Scientism</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Falsifiability?action=raw" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Falsifiability (Popper)</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Accuracy_and_precision?action=raw" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Accuracy and precision</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Memory_hierarchy?action=raw" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Memory hierarchy (latency table)</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Optical_fiber?action=raw" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Optical fiber (signal speed)</a>. A few claims could not be confirmed at their official source from here and are being re-checked.</p><p class="runcode">A question, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/enrichmeai.github.io/issues/new?title=%5BScience%20%26%20Method%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>. Nothing here is tracked and there are no comments to store: questions live in the open, where the next learner can find the answer.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<section class="panel" id="p-everydayscience" data-name="Everyday Science">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Everyday science: things we should all know</h2><p>Basic science for everyone, not only engineers: the reasons behind things we see every day. A grounded person knows why the sky is blue as well as how to use a phone. See <a href="#science">Science &amp; Method</a> for how we know.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card"><h3>Sky, light and weather</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Why the sky is blue:</b> air scatters blue light much more than red or green, so blue reaches our eyes from every direction of the sky.</li>
      <li><b>Rainbows:</b> sunlight is bent (refracted), reflected inside water droplets and split into its colours (dispersion).</li>
      <li><b>Lightning and thunder:</b> light arrives almost at once; sound travels at about 343 metres a second in air at 20 °C. Count the seconds between flash and thunder and divide by three for kilometres (by five for miles).</li>
      <li><b>The speed of light</b> is exactly 299,792,458 metres a second, the fastest anything can travel.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Earth, Moon and seasons</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Seasons</b> come from the tilt of Earth's axis, not its distance from the Sun: as Earth orbits, each hemisphere leans towards the Sun for part of the year.</li>
      <li><b>Tides</b> are the rise and fall of the sea caused mainly by the gravity of the Moon and the Sun.</li>
      <li><b>Moon phases:</b> we see different parts of the Moon's sunlit half as it orbits us, from crescent to full and back.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Water and heat</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Ice floats</b> because water expands by about 9% when it freezes, so ice is less dense than liquid water. Lakes freeze from the top, which lets life survive underneath.</li>
      <li><b>Boiling point drops with pressure:</b> under lower pressure, such as high in the mountains, water boils at a lower temperature, so food takes longer to cook.</li>
      <li><b>The greenhouse effect:</b> heat-trapping gases in the atmosphere stop Earth losing heat to space, raising its surface temperature. Some keeps the planet habitable; adding more warms it.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Life</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Photosynthesis:</b> plants, algae and some bacteria turn the energy in sunlight into chemical energy, the base of almost every food chain.</li>
      <li><b>Your body changes with use:</b> muscles, bones and the brain all respond to how you use them. See <a href="#body">Body, Mind &amp; Nature</a>.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">This page will grow into deeper physics, chemistry, biology and mathematics, as planned on <a href="#why">Why this guide exists</a>.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><p class="sources"><b>Checked against</b> (Wikipedia): <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diffuse_sky_radiation" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Diffuse sky radiation</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rainbow" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Rainbow</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thunder" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Thunder</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Speed_of_sound" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Speed of sound</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Speed_of_light" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Speed of light</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Season" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Season</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tide" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Tide</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lunar_phase" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Lunar phase</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ice" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ice</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boiling_point" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Boiling point</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greenhouse_effect" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Greenhouse effect</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Photosynthesis" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Photosynthesis</a>.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= PHILOSOPHY ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-philosophy" data-name="Philosophy">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Philosophy for engineers</h2><p>Philosophy is the practice of thinking clearly about hard questions: how we know things, how to reason, what matters and what we owe each other. Engineers use it every day without the names. Knowing the names makes it easier to do well.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card"><h3>Epistemology: how do you know?</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Knowledge needs justification:</b> a belief that turns out true by luck is not knowledge. "The tests passed" is justified only if you saw them run.</li>
      <li><b>Sources differ in reliability</b> (<i>our rule of thumb</i>): the running system beats the config, the config beats the docs, the docs beat memory, memory beats rumour. <i>The other view:</i> the order can flip, as when a stale config file is less reliable than a current doc, so check which source is fresher.</li>
      <li><b>Absence of evidence</b> is evidence of absence only if you looked properly. Say how thoroughly you looked.</li>
      <li><b>Calibration:</b> match your confidence to your evidence, and say how sure you are.</li>
      <li><b>Testimony,</b> including an AI's answer or a colleague's summary, is a reason to check, not a replacement for checking.</li>
      <li><b>Different questions need different methods:</b> experiments settle what is true of the world; ethics, judgement and tradition speak to what we should do and what matters. See <a href="#science">what science can answer, and what it cannot settle alone</a>.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Reasoning well</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Kind</th><th>Shape</th><th>Engineering example</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Deduction</td><td>If the premises are true, the conclusion must be</td><td>"Every write goes through this function; it validates input; so every write is validated."</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Induction</td><td>Generalising from observations</td><td>"It failed on every run with a null date; nulls probably cause it."</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Abduction</td><td>The best explanation for what you see</td><td>"Latency rose right after the deploy; the deploy is the most likely cause."</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note">Only deduction is certain, and only if the premises are. Induction and abduction give hypotheses to test, which is where the <a href="#science">scientific method</a> takes over.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Fallacies in technical debates</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Appeal to authority:</b> "Big Company uses it, so it is right for us."</li>
      <li><b>Bandwagon:</b> "Everyone is moving to X."</li>
      <li><b>False dilemma:</b> "Either we rewrite everything or we live with it."</li>
      <li><b>Straw man:</b> arguing against a weaker version of a colleague's proposal.</li>
      <li><b>Ad hominem:</b> judging the idea by who proposed it.</li>
      <li><b>Post hoc:</b> "It broke after my change, so my change broke it." Maybe, but test it.</li>
      <li><b>Appeal to novelty or tradition:</b> new is not better, old is not proven.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Useful razors and principles</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Occam's razor:</b> prefer the explanation or design with the fewest assumptions.</li>
      <li><b>Hanlon's razor:</b> do not assume malice where a mistake explains it. Most broken things are accidents.</li>
      <li><b>Chesterton's fence:</b> do not remove something until you know why it was put there.</li>
      <li><b>First principles:</b> reason from what is known to be true rather than from analogy and habit.</li>
      <li><b>Inversion:</b> ask how this could fail, then prevent that.</li>
      <li><b>Goodhart's law:</b> when a measure becomes a target, it stops being a good measure. Tests written to raise coverage, not to catch bugs, are an example.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Systems thinking</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Feedback loops:</b> reinforcing loops amplify (retries cause load, load causes timeouts, timeouts cause retries); balancing loops stabilise (autoscaling).</li>
      <li><b>Second-order effects:</b> ask "and then what?" A cache speeds reads, and then serves stale data, and then someone acts on it.</li>
      <li><b>Delays</b> in a system cause overshoot: scaling decisions based on old metrics oscillate.</li>
      <li><b>The structure produces the behaviour:</b> repeated incidents point to a system to change, not a person to blame.</li>
      <li><b>Leverage points:</b> small changes in rules, incentives and information flows often matter more than large changes in parameters.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Why big organisations move slowly</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Their processes are scar tissue.</b> Most approvals, reviews and controls exist because something went wrong once. Each one made sense; together they slow everything down.</li>
      <li><b>More people, many more conversations.</b> Ten people have 45 possible pairs to keep in step; a hundred have 4,950. Agreement gets expensive.</li>
      <li><b>Blame is lopsided.</b> A change that fails is noticed and blamed; a chance that was missed is rarely noticed at all. So people learn that "no" is safer than "yes".</li>
      <li><b>Today's customers pull hard.</b> Clayton Christensen's <i>The Innovator's Dilemma</i> (1997) showed how successful companies, by doing what their best customers ask, can miss the new thing that replaces them.</li>
      <li><b>The system mirrors the organisation.</b> Conway's law (Melvin Conway, 1968): a system's design copies the communication structure of the organisation that built it, so changing one often means changing the other.</li>
      <li><b>Some slowness is care.</b> A bank or a hospital that changes something wrongly hurts millions of people. Slow is sometimes the right speed.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">What helps: small teams that own their area, decisions made close to the work, and telling reversible decisions (move fast) from one-way doors (take care). Amazon's 2015 shareholder letter calls these "two-way" and "one-way" doors.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Why people find it hard to change</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Habits save effort.</b> The brain turns what we repeat into habit, so it needs little thought. Changing means thinking hard again about things that used to be easy.</li>
      <li><b>Skill becomes identity.</b> After years in one technology or one way of working, "I am the person who knows X" is part of who you are. Change can feel like losing that, and becoming a beginner again.</li>
      <li><b>Losses feel bigger than gains.</b> Kahneman and Tversky's research showed people weigh a loss more heavily than an equal gain, so what we might lose looms larger than what we might win.</li>
      <li><b>We prefer what we have:</b> status quo bias. And the more we have invested in a path, the harder it is to leave it, even when we should (the sunk-cost trap).</li>
      <li><b>Old scars:</b> people who lived through a change that went badly remember it, and they are not wrong to.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">What helps: explain why, start with a small experiment, make it safe to be a beginner, let people shape the change, and value the experience they bring to it. The brain keeps learning at any age (see <a href="#body">Body, Mind &amp; Nature</a>); change is hard, not impossible. Be patient with others, and honest with yourself about your own habits.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Why every decision has people for it and against it</h3>
    <p>For every option we choose, some people will find it right and some will find it wrong. Usually that is not because one side is foolish. It is because they stand in different places.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>They see different things:</b> the person on call sees the risk, the product owner sees the customer waiting, finance sees the cost, the new joiner sees what nobody questions any more.</li>
      <li><b>They pay different prices.</b> Every option has a cost, and it falls on someone. A choice that is cheap for one team is extra work, or risk, for another.</li>
      <li><b>They value different things:</b> speed or safety, simplicity or flexibility, today or next year. These are trade-offs, not right and wrong answers.</li>
      <li><b>So two honest, sensible people can disagree.</b> Assume good faith first.</li>
    </ul>
    <p><b>What to do about it:</b> say the trade-offs out loud, name who pays the cost, and listen hardest to the people who disagree, because they often see the risk you missed. Then decide, write down why (an architecture decision record is one way), and agree when you will look again. Once a decision is made, commit to making it work, even if you argued against it: Amazon calls this "disagree and commit". Aim for decisions people understand, not decisions everyone likes.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Ethics: what we owe users and society</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Lens</th><th>Asks</th><th>In an engineering decision</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Consequences</td><td>What outcomes does this cause, for whom?</td><td>Who is harmed if this model is wrong, and how badly?</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Duties and rights</td><td>What rules and rights apply, regardless of outcome?</td><td>Do we have consent to use this data? Can users delete it?</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Virtue</td><td>What would a person of good character do?</td><td>Would I be comfortable explaining this design to the people it affects?</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Fairness</td><td>Are burdens and benefits shared justly?</td><td>Does this work as well for users with disabilities, in other languages, on cheaper phones?</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <ul>
      <li>Professional codes (ACM, IEEE) put the public good, honesty, and avoiding harm first.</li>
      <li>AI raises the stakes: automated decisions at scale, opaque reasoning, and content that can mislead. Build in human review where harm is possible.</li>
      <li>You may be asked to build something wrong. Raise it, explain the harm, propose an alternative, and know where your line is.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Stoic habits for hard days</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Separate what you control from what you do not:</b> you control your response to an incident, not that it happened.</li>
      <li><b>Expect setbacks:</b> plan for failure, so it is a known path, not a shock.</li>
      <li><b>Judge actions, not outcomes alone:</b> a good decision can turn out badly; learn from it without self-blame.</li>
      <li><b>Daily review:</b> what went well, what to do differently, what you learned.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Nothing is permanent</h3>
    <p><i>Our reading:</i> several traditions notice that things change, though they draw different conclusions from it.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Buddhism</b> teaches impermanence (<i>anicca</i> in Pali) as one of the three marks of existence: all conditioned things are transient. The other two marks are <i>dukkha</i>, often translated as suffering but also as unease or &ldquo;an unreliable place to stand&rdquo;, and <i>anattā</i>, the lack of a lasting self.</li>
      <li><b>Heraclitus</b>, in ancient Greece, is reported by later writers as comparing being to a river: you cannot step into the same river, because fresh waters keep flowing in. The short form &ldquo;everything flows&rdquo; (<i>panta rhei</i>) is a later summary of his thought, not his own words.</li>
      <li><b>Ecclesiastes</b> calls all things <i>hevel</i>, Hebrew for vapour or breath, and gives them their seasons: &ldquo;a time to weep, and a time to laugh; a time to mourn, and a time to dance&rdquo;.</li>
      <li><b>Japan&rsquo;s</b> <i>mono no aware</i>, &ldquo;the pathos of things&rdquo;, is a Japanese idiom for sensitivity to the transience of things, said to heighten the appreciation of their beauty and to bring a gentle sadness at their passing.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Joy and sorrow come together</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>The Bhagavad Gita</b> describes the person who has risen above the qualities of nature as calm, keeping equanimity, and steadfast in joy and sorrow alike.</li>
      <li><b>Buddhist teaching</b> links <i>dukkha</i> to craving and grasping after things that do not last, and expecting lasting pleasure from them.</li>
      <li><b>Psychology&rsquo;s hedonic adaptation</b> proposes something similar: after big good or bad events, people tend to drift back towards a fairly stable level of happiness. Later studies found this is not inevitable: events such as a severe long-term disability can change it for good.</li>
      <li>Kahlil Gibran&rsquo;s <i>The Prophet</i> (1923) gives joy and sorrow a single chapter between them.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note"><i>Analysis:</i> the shared idea is that the things that bring joy can also bring sorrow, because they change or end. Holding them more lightly is meant to soften the sorrow, not to stop the joy.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Being at peace with what happens</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Equanimity</b> is steadiness of mind that is not overturned by emotion, pain or circumstance. Many religions and philosophies value it; in Buddhism it is <i>upekṣā</i>, one of the four &ldquo;sublime&rdquo; states cultivated on the path.</li>
      <li><b>The Stoic</b> Epictetus opens his handbook, the <i>Enchiridion</i>, with what is up to us and what is not (see the Stoic habits card above).</li>
      <li><b>The Serenity Prayer</b>, first composed by the Protestant theologian Reinhold Niebuhr in the early 1930s, asks for the serenity to accept what cannot be changed, the courage to change what can be, and the wisdom to know the difference.</li>
      <li><b>Acceptance is not giving up.</b> Acceptance and commitment therapy, for example, pairs acceptance with clarifying your values and acting on them.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note"><i>The other view:</i> Nietzsche&rsquo;s <i>amor fati</i>, &ldquo;love of fate&rdquo;, asks for more than peace: to see everything that happens, suffering and loss included, as necessary, and to want it exactly as it was.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Is happiness a choice?</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>What the research and the thinkers say</th><th>What it suggests</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>People seem to have happiness &ldquo;set points&rdquo; that are partly inherited, but they differ between people and can vary within a range (Diener, Lucas and Scollon, 2006).</td><td>Not all of it is chosen, and not all of it is fixed.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>How much is inherited is disputed: one study put the heritability of positive feelings at 40%, while a study of 11,500 unrelated people put heritability at 12 to 18%.</td><td>Any single percentage should be held loosely.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Sonja Lyubomirsky estimated roughly 50% genetic, 10% circumstances and 40% under our own control.</td><td>A popular case for &ldquo;choice&rdquo;, given as her estimate, not a settled fact.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Viktor Frankl named adopting an attitude as one of three ways to find meaning, alongside making a difference and having experiences.</td><td>Even when circumstances cannot be chosen, the stance towards them sometimes can. Frankl&rsquo;s emphasis on attitude has also been criticised for implying that those who died in the camps had given up.</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note"><i>The other view:</i> &ldquo;just choose to be happy&rdquo; can become toxic positivity, dismissing real sadness or anger, which can make hard things harder to deal with. Low mood that lasts is not a failure of choice, and doctors, counsellors and crisis lines are there for it.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Our view, and the other view</h3>
    <p><i>Our view:</i> none of us can choose what happens, or all of how we feel, and nobody has to carry that alone. Knowing that nothing is permanent, the good days and the hard ones alike, can make it easier to be at peace with what is happening. And much of what makes people feel better is shared: being listened to, being kind, being patient with someone on a hard day, and letting others do the same for us. Feeling better may be less a choice each of us makes alone than something we make possible for each other.</p>
    <p><i>The other view:</i> some people find peace in engaging fully with joy and grief rather than in letting go, and see equanimity as a risk of numbing both. Others find meaning in refusing to accept circumstances, such as injustice or illness, and in changing them.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Socratic questions for reviews and design</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>What exactly do you mean by…?</li>
      <li>How do we know that is true?</li>
      <li>What are we assuming?</li>
      <li>What would change your mind?</li>
      <li>What is the strongest argument against this?</li>
      <li>What follows if we are wrong?</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">Asked with curiosity, these improve the work. Asked to win, they damage trust.</p>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Ask "how do we know?" before accepting a claim</li><li>Recognise common fallacies in debates</li><li>Know the professional codes of ethics exist and what they ask</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Reason clearly with deduction, induction and abduction</li><li>Use razors and principles (Occam, Chesterton, Goodhart) in decisions</li><li>Raise ethical concerns about a feature constructively</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Apply systems thinking to recurring problems and incentives</li><li>Weigh ethical trade-offs in designs with several lenses</li><li>Lead discussions with Socratic questions that improve the work</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Shape organisational values, incentives and ethical standards</li><li>Advise on AI ethics and societal impact</li><li>Model wise judgement under uncertainty</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>Epistemology and calibration</li><li>Logic and argument</li><li>Systems thinking</li><li>Ethics of technology and AI</li><li>Stoicism</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://plato.stanford.edu/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</a><span class="lnote"> Free, authoritative articles on every topic</span></li><li><a href="https://www.acm.org/code-of-ethics" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ACM Code of Ethics and Professional Conduct</a><span class="lnote"> Computing professionals' code</span></li><li><a href="https://www.ieee.org/about/corporate/governance/p7-8.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">IEEE Code of Ethics</a><span class="lnote"> Engineers' code</span></li><li><a href="https://donellameadows.org/systems-thinking-book-sale/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Thinking in Systems (Donella Meadows)</a><span class="lnote"> Introduction to systems thinking</span></li><li><a href="https://iep.utm.edu/fallacy/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Internet Encyclopedia of Philosophy: fallacies</a><span class="lnote"> A catalogue of reasoning errors</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conway%27s_law?action=raw" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Conway's law (Datamation, April 1968)</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Innovator%27s_Dilemma?action=raw" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: The Innovator's Dilemma (1997)</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Loss_aversion?action=raw" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Loss aversion (Kahneman and Tversky)</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Goodhart%27s_law?action=raw" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Goodhart's law</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hanlon%27s_razor?action=raw" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Hanlon's razor</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abductive_reasoning?action=raw" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Abductive reasoning</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stoicism?action=raw" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Stoicism</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Programming_ethics?action=raw" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Programming ethics (ACM code principles)</a>; <a href="https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1018724/000119312516530910/d168744dex991.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Amazon 2015 letter to shareowners (SEC exhibit)</a>; <a href="https://www.aboutamazon.com/about-us/leadership-principles" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Amazon leadership principles</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/G._K._Chesterton?action=raw" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: G. K. Chesterton (Chesterton's fence)</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Impermanence" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Impermanence</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Impermanence_(Buddhism)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Impermanence (Buddhism)</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Du%E1%B8%A5kha" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Duḥkha</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Panta_rhei_(doctrine)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Panta rhei (doctrine)</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ecclesiastes" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Ecclesiastes</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mono_no_aware" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Mono no aware</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bhagavad_Gita" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Bhagavad Gita</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hedonic_treadmill" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Hedonic treadmill</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Prophet_(book)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: The Prophet (book)</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Equanimity" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Equanimity</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Upek%E1%B9%A3%C4%81" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Upekṣā</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Enchiridion_of_Epictetus" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Enchiridion of Epictetus</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Serenity_Prayer" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Serenity Prayer</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Acceptance_and_commitment_therapy" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Acceptance and commitment therapy</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amor_fati" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Amor fati</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Subjective_well-being" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Subjective well-being</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Happiness" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Happiness</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sonja_Lyubomirsky" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Sonja Lyubomirsky</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Viktor_Frankl" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Viktor Frankl</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Toxic_positivity" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Toxic positivity</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Man%27s_Search_for_Meaning" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Man's Search for Meaning</a>.</p><p class="runcode">A question, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/enrichmeai.github.io/issues/new?title=%5BPhilosophy%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>. Nothing here is tracked and there are no comments to store: questions live in the open, where the next learner can find the answer.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= GOOD HUMAN ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-goodhuman" data-name="Being a Good Human">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Being a good human</h2><p>Technical skill decides what you can build; character decides whether people want to build with you, and whether what you build is good for the people who use it. This tab is about the person behind the engineer: honesty, kindness, humility, care for others and for yourself.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Core qualities, in practice</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Quality</th><th>Looks like at work</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Honesty</td><td>Saying "I broke it", "I don't know yet", "this estimate was wrong" early and plainly</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Integrity</td><td>Doing the right thing when nobody would notice: the test you could skip, the shortcut with user data</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Kindness</td><td>Reviewing code without contempt; helping a struggling colleague; patience with beginners</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Humility</td><td>Assuming you might be wrong; learning from juniors and from mistakes; sharing credit</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Respect</td><td>Listening fully, valuing different backgrounds and roles, respecting people's time</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Gratitude</td><td>Thanking people specifically for what they did</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Responsibility</td><td>Owning outcomes, keeping promises, finishing what you start</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Generosity</td><td>Teaching, mentoring, documenting, contributing back to open source and the community</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Psychological safety</h3>
    <p>Teams do their best work when people can ask questions, admit mistakes and disagree without fear. Google's study of its own teams (Project Aristotle) found it was the most important factor in team effectiveness.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Admit your own mistakes first; it makes it safe for others.</li>
      <li>Thank people for raising problems, even when the timing is bad.</li>
      <li>Ask quieter people for their view.</li>
      <li>Criticise the work and the system, never the person.</li>
      <li>Keep blameless postmortems genuinely blameless.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Feedback, given and received</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Situation, behaviour, impact:</b> "In yesterday's review (situation) you rewrote Asha's query without explaining (behaviour); she didn't learn why and felt sidelined (impact)."</li>
      <li><b>Be specific and timely;</b> praise in public, correct in private.</li>
      <li><b>Receive feedback with "thank you"</b> first, questions second, defence never.</li>
      <li><b>Ask for feedback regularly,</b> not just at review time.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Conflict and disagreement</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Assume good intent; most conflict is about unclear goals or information.</li>
      <li>Restate the other view until they agree you understood it.</li>
      <li>Find the shared goal, then disagree about the route.</li>
      <li>Use data and small experiments to settle technical disagreements.</li>
      <li>Disagree and commit: once decided, support the decision fully, or reopen it openly with new evidence.</li>
      <li>Apologise well: name what you did, its effect, and what you will do differently, with no "but".</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Care for users and society</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Build for everyone: accessibility, other languages, slow networks, older devices.</li>
      <li>Treat user data as if it were your family's.</li>
      <li>Be honest in what the product claims; never ship dark patterns that trick people.</li>
      <li>Think about who could be harmed, not only who benefits.</li>
      <li>Consider the environmental cost of compute you do not need.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Care for yourself</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Sustainable pace:</b> long hours produce bugs, poor decisions and burnout. The WHO recognises burnout as an occupational phenomenon resulting from chronic workplace stress.</li>
      <li><b>Sleep, movement, breaks:</b> they are part of doing good work, not a reward for finishing it.</li>
      <li><b>Boundaries:</b> be reachable when on call; be off when you are off.</li>
      <li><b>Ask for help early,</b> technical or personal.</li>
      <li><b>Life outside work:</b> family, friends, interests and rest make you a better engineer and a happier person.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Run your day, or the day runs you</h3>
    <p>If you do not decide how your day starts, the inbox, the chat and whatever is on fire will decide for you. A small routine you keep every day is how you take the day back.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Move your body, every day.</b> It does not matter what: a walk, a run, yoga, cycling, a swim, push-ups in the kitchen, dancing, playing with your children. The best exercise is the one you will actually do tomorrow.</li>
      <li><b>Start small enough to keep.</b> Twenty minutes you look forward to becomes a habit; an hour you dread is easy to skip. Build the habit first, then make it harder.</li>
      <li><b>Anchor it to something you already do:</b> after the first coffee, before you open the laptop, at lunch. A habit tied to a moment survives busy days.</li>
      <li><b>Get up from the desk</b> during the day too. Stand, stretch, walk while you talk through a problem: a bug that will not give way at the desk often does on a walk.</li>
      <li><b>Protect the basics around it:</b> a regular time to sleep and wake, food that is not only snacks, and a few minutes with no screen.</li>
      <li><b>Miss a day, not two.</b> Life will break the routine; just start again the next day, without guilt and without making up for it.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">Discipline in small things carries into the work: the engineer who keeps a daily habit is the one who keeps the tests green and the runbook current. If you have a health condition, check with a doctor what kind of movement suits you.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Mentoring and lifting others</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Explain the why, not just the what.</li>
      <li>Let people struggle a little before rescuing; then help them reflect.</li>
      <li>Give stretch work with support.</li>
      <li>Credit people by name for their work, especially when they are not in the room.</li>
      <li>Remember who helped you, and pass it on.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>A short daily check</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Was I honest today, including about my mistakes?</li>
      <li>Did I help someone, and thank someone?</li>
      <li>Did I listen more than I spoke in at least one conversation?</li>
      <li>Did I do work I would be proud to have the users see?</li>
      <li>Did I move my body, and look after my own health?</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Be honest about mistakes and what you do not know</li><li>Thank people specifically; review kindly</li><li>Look after your health and ask for help early</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Give and receive feedback well</li><li>Handle disagreement with respect and evidence</li><li>Build for all users, including accessibility</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Create psychological safety in your team</li><li>Mentor others and share credit generously</li><li>Raise ethical concerns and stand by them</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Shape a humane, inclusive culture across the organisation</li><li>Lead with integrity under pressure</li><li>Give back to the wider community</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>Psychological safety</li><li>Feedback models</li><li>Conflict resolution</li><li>Inclusive and accessible design</li><li>Sustainable pace and burnout</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://rework.withgoogle.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google re:Work</a><span class="lnote"> Research on team effectiveness, including psychological safety</span></li><li><a href="https://www.who.int/news/item/28-05-2019-burn-out-an-occupational-phenomenon-international-classification-of-diseases" target="_blank" rel="noopener">WHO: burn-out an occupational phenomenon</a><span class="lnote"> How burnout is defined</span></li><li><a href="https://www.acm.org/code-of-ethics" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ACM Code of Ethics</a><span class="lnote"> Honesty, avoiding harm, the public good</span></li><li><a href="https://www.w3.org/WAI/standards-guidelines/wcag/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Web Content Accessibility Guidelines</a><span class="lnote"> Building for everyone</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Occupational_burnout?action=raw" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Occupational burnout (WHO 'occupational phenomenon')</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Psychological_safety?action=raw" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Psychological safety</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Programming_ethics?action=raw" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Programming ethics (ACM code: avoid harm, be honest)</a>; <a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">W3C WCAG 2.2</a>; <a href="https://rework.withgoogle.com/intl/en/guides/understand-team-effectiveness" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google re:Work: Understand team effectiveness (Project Aristotle)</a>.</p><p class="runcode">A question, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/enrichmeai.github.io/issues/new?title=%5BBeing%20a%20Good%20Human%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>. Nothing here is tracked and there are no comments to store: questions live in the open, where the next learner can find the answer.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<section class="panel" id="p-body" data-name="Body, Mind &amp; Nature">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Body, mind and nature</h2><p>We get one life, and the body we live it in. Work is a large part of that life, whatever the job: an engineer, a nurse, a teacher, a driver, a shopkeeper. This tab is about the person doing the work: how your nervous system and body respond to the day, how to look after them, and how we belong to the natural world rather than stand outside it. Look after these, and the journey gets more beautiful, and the work gets better too.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>One life: make the journey beautiful</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Your job is part of your life, not all of it.</b> The habits on this page help whatever work you do, and they help most in the parts of life that are not work at all.</li>
      <li><b>Notice the good things,</b> however small: a conversation, a problem solved, a walk, a meal shared. Noticing is a habit, and it can be practised.</li>
      <li><b>Spend time on what you will be glad of later:</b> the people you love, your health, learning, making something, helping someone.</li>
      <li><b>Be kind to yourself as well as others.</b> You will have bad days. A bad day is a day, not a verdict on you.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Your nervous system, in plain words</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>The central nervous system</b> is the brain and spinal cord. <b>The peripheral nervous system</b> is the network of nerves that carries signals between them and the rest of the body.</li>
      <li><b>The autonomic nervous system</b> runs what you do not think about: heartbeat, breathing, digestion. It has two main branches that balance each other.</li>
      <li><b>Sympathetic: "fight or flight".</b> Heart faster, breathing quicker, muscles ready, digestion paused. Useful in a real emergency.</li>
      <li><b>Parasympathetic: "rest and digest".</b> Heart slower, breathing deeper, digestion and repair. This is where recovery happens.</li>
      <li>Both are normal. The aim is not to switch one off, but to move between them, and not to stay stuck in alarm.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Stress: an alarm that should switch off</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Your body cannot tell a deadline from a danger.</b> A page at 2am, a tense meeting, an angry message: each can start the same stress response, with adrenaline and cortisol.</li>
      <li><b>Short bursts are fine,</b> and can sharpen you. The harm comes when the alarm never switches off: sleep, mood, focus and health all suffer.</li>
      <li><b>Notice your signals:</b> a tight jaw or shoulders, shallow breathing, short temper, waking at night thinking about work.</li>
      <li><b>Slow your breathing,</b> with the out-breath longer than the in-breath. Breathing is one of the few parts of the autonomic system you can change on purpose, and many people find a few slow breaths calming.</li>
      <li><b>Move, sleep, and talk to someone.</b> If stress or low mood lasts for weeks, speak to a doctor or a professional. Asking for help is a strength.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Sleep is part of the work</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Sleep is when the brain consolidates what you learned</b> during the day. Cutting sleep to learn more works against you.</li>
      <li><b>Most adults need at least seven hours,</b> as the major health bodies advise. Needs vary; how you feel during the day is a good guide.</li>
      <li><b>Keep regular times</b> for sleeping and waking, even at weekends where you can. Your body clock likes routine.</li>
      <li><b>Get daylight in the morning</b> and dim the lights and screens in the evening. Light is the main signal that sets your body clock.</li>
      <li><b>Watch late caffeine.</b> Its effects can last for hours after the last cup.</li>
      <li><b>On-call nights are real costs.</b> Plan recovery after them, as you would after any hard physical shift.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Your brain keeps changing</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>The brain changes with what you do.</b> Learning and practice strengthen connections between neurons throughout life. This is called neuroplasticity, and it is why "I will learn it" is true at any age.</li>
      <li><b>Spread practice out.</b> Studying in several shorter sessions over days is remembered better than one long cram. This is the spacing effect.</li>
      <li><b>Test yourself</b> rather than rereading. Recalling something from memory strengthens it more than looking at it again.</li>
      <li><b>Learn by doing.</b> Building the thing, breaking it and fixing it engages far more of you than watching someone else do it.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Attention is a limited resource</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Focus tires, like a muscle.</b> Plan your hardest thinking for the time of day you are sharpest.</li>
      <li><b>Every interruption has a cost:</b> getting back into a hard problem takes time. Turn off notifications you do not need, and batch messages.</li>
      <li><b>Work in blocks,</b> then take a real break: stand up, look away from the screen, step outside.</li>
      <li><b>One thing at a time.</b> Switching between tasks feels productive and usually is not.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Why a day of meetings tires you more than a day of real work</h3>
    <p>Many people finish a day of back-to-back meetings more drained than after eight hours of hard, focused work, and feel they did nothing. There are several published explanations, and they point to the same few causes.</p>
    <div class="tbl"><table><thead><tr><th>Cause</th><th>What the evidence says</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td><b>Stress builds up without breaks</b></td><td>In a 2021 study, Microsoft&rsquo;s Human Factors Lab put EEG caps on 14 volunteers. On one day they had four half-hour video meetings back to back; on another, the same four with 10-minute breaks between them. Without breaks, brain activity associated with stress (beta waves) kept rising over the two hours and spiked at each changeover, and the brain patterns suggested people were less engaged. With breaks, it stayed level.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Every meeting is a switch</b></td><td>Psychologists have studied task switching since 1927: right after switching from one task to another, people are slower and make more mistakes than when they repeat the same task. A day of meetings on different topics is one switch after another, each with nothing to show for it.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Video adds its own load</b></td><td>&ldquo;Zoom fatigue&rdquo; has been attributed to an overload of nonverbal cues not present in face-to-face conversation, and to larger groups in video calls.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Someone else sets the pace</b></td><td>Focused work at its best can become flow, and one of the conditions described for flow is a sense of personal control over the activity. In a meeting the agenda, the speed and the moment you must respond belong to others, and you are on show the whole time (<i>analysis</i>).</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
    <p><b>What helps</b> (<i>our view</i>, drawn from the causes above):</p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Leave gaps.</b> End meetings five or ten minutes early; Microsoft added settings to Outlook that shorten meetings by default for exactly this, after the study.</li>
      <li><b>Fewer switches.</b> Group meetings together, and keep long blocks for focused work.</li>
      <li><b>Write it down first.</b> An agenda, a decision to make, or a written update can replace a meeting altogether (see <a href="#review">Writing &amp; Code Review</a> and <a href="#ways">Ways of Working</a>).</li>
      <li><b>Not every call needs a camera</b>, and not every meeting needs everyone.</li>
      <li><b>Recover between them</b>: stand up, look away from the screen, breathe (see stress and attention above).</li>
    </ul>
    <p><b>How to weigh it.</b> The Microsoft study is small (14 people), was published by a company that also sells meeting software, and its breaks were meditation sessions; treat it as an illustration, not proof. A related popular idea, that willpower is a battery drained by every decision (&ldquo;ego depletion&rdquo;), has been seriously questioned: a 23-lab pre-registered replication found no effect, though researchers still disagree about what that means.</p>
    <p><i>Our view:</i> meetings are real work, but a different kind, and they cost more than the clock shows; protecting time to think is part of doing the job well. <i>The other view:</i> for some people and some work, conversation is where the thinking happens, and a good meeting gives energy rather than taking it. The tiredness may come less from meetings themselves than from too many, too long and too close together.</p>
    <p class="note">Sources: <a href="https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/worklab/work-trend-index/brain-research" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Microsoft WorkLab, &ldquo;Research Proves Your Brain Needs Breaks&rdquo;</a> (20 April 2021); Wikipedia&rsquo;s articles on <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Task_switching_(psychology)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">task switching</a>, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zoom_fatigue" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Zoom fatigue</a>, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flow_(psychology)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">flow</a> and <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ego_depletion" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ego depletion</a>.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Building good habits</h3>
    <p>Most of what lasts, in health, learning and work, comes from small things done every day rather than big efforts now and then. Several books on the <a href="#reading">Reading list</a> are about exactly this.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Start small:</b> <i>Atomic Habits</i> (James Clear) builds a habit by making it small and easy, and aims to get about 1% better each day.</li>
      <li><b>Stack habits:</b> attach a new habit to one you already have ("after I pour my coffee, I read one page").</li>
      <li><b>Pair the hard with the pleasant:</b> "temptation bundling", giving yourself a small reward for doing the habit.</li>
      <li><b>Protect the start of the day:</b> <i>The 5 AM Club</i> (Robin Sharma) is about owning the morning before the world asks for it.</li>
      <li><b>Build on the basics:</b> <i>The 4 Pillar Plan</i> (Dr Rangan Chatterjee) makes the case for small, consistent changes for everyday health.</li>
      <li><b>Mind the story you tell yourself:</b> <i>Psycho-Cybernetics</i> and <i>As a Man Thinketh</i> are about how self-image and thought shape what we do. See "Believe in yourself" on <a href="#aiworry">Worried about AI?</a>.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atomic_Habits" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Atomic Habits (Wikipedia)</a>.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Look after the body you work in</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Rest your eyes.</b> Eye-care bodies suggest the 20-20-20 rule: every 20 minutes, look at something about 20 feet (6 metres) away for 20 seconds. Remember to blink.</li>
      <li><b>Change position often.</b> No single posture is perfect for hours; the best posture is the next one. Set the screen at eye level and keep your wrists relaxed.</li>
      <li><b>Move every day</b> (see <a href="#goodhuman">Being a Good Human</a>): it helps your heart, your mood, your sleep and your thinking.</li>
      <li><b>Drink water, eat real food,</b> and do not let the day run on snacks and caffeine.</li>
      <li><b>Protect your hearing:</b> keep headphones at a moderate volume.</li>
      <li><b>Listen to pain.</b> Wrist, neck or back pain that keeps coming back is a signal to change something, and to see a professional.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Mother nature</h3>
    <p>We are part of nature, not visitors to it. The air, water, food and climate that every job depends on come from it, and we are the only ones who can look after it.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Go outside.</b> Time in green and blue spaces (parks, woods, rivers, the sea) is linked in research with better wellbeing. A walk outside is one of the cheapest good things you can do for yourself.</li>
      <li><b>Nature is the original systems engineer.</b> Ecosystems run on feedback loops, recycle everything, spread risk through diversity and redundancy, and grow within limits. Those are good lessons for any system you build.</li>
      <li><b>Learn from its designs.</b> Engineers call this biomimicry: Velcro came from burdock burrs catching on a dog's fur, and the nose of Japan's 500 Series Shinkansen was shaped after a kingfisher's beak to reduce noise in tunnels.</li>
      <li><b>Notice the seasons.</b> Energy, light and mood change through the year. Planning with them, rather than against them, is kinder to people.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Care for the planet in your work</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Computing uses real energy and water.</b> Every server, model and stored copy has a footprint, even when nobody looks at it.</li>
      <li><b>Turn off what you do not use:</b> idle machines, forgotten environments, old snapshots, data nobody reads. It saves money and energy together.</li>
      <li><b>Right-size:</b> pick the smallest machine and the simplest design that does the job, and scale to zero where you can.</li>
      <li><b>Choose cleaner regions</b> where you have the choice. Cloud providers publish how much of each region's electricity comes from carbon-free sources.</li>
      <li><b>Use AI where it helps,</b> not by reflex. A large model is a heavy tool; a query, a script or a small model is often enough.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">None of this is about guilt. It is about being a good guest on a planet we share, in the same spirit as being a good colleague.</p>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Explain fight-or-flight and rest-and-digest in plain words</li><li>Keep regular sleep, daily movement and real breaks</li><li>Notice your own stress signals</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Plan hard thinking for your best hours; protect focus</li><li>Recover after on-call and crunch periods</li><li>Switch off idle resources you own</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Set a sustainable pace and humane on-call for a team</li><li>Spot burnout signals in others and act early</li><li>Design systems that are lean in energy and cost</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Shape a culture where health and rest are normal</li><li>Make sustainability part of architecture decisions</li><li>Model a full life, not only a full calendar</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>The autonomic nervous system</li><li>Sleep and the body clock</li><li>How learning works: spacing and retrieval</li><li>Ergonomics and eye care</li><li>Biomimicry</li><li>Sustainable software</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://www.nhs.uk/live-well/sleep-and-tiredness/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NHS: sleep and tiredness</a><span class="lnote"> Practical advice on sleep</span></li><li><a href="https://www.nhs.uk/live-well/exercise/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NHS: exercise</a><span class="lnote"> Activity guidance for adults</span></li><li><a href="https://www.ninds.nih.gov/health-information/public-education/brain-basics/brain-basics-know-your-brain" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NINDS: Brain Basics</a><span class="lnote"> How the brain and nervous system are organised</span></li><li><a href="https://asknature.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AskNature</a><span class="lnote"> The Biomimicry Institute's library of nature's designs</span></li><li><a href="https://greensoftware.foundation/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Green Software Foundation</a><span class="lnote"> Principles and practice for greener software</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/worklab/work-trend-index/brain-research" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Microsoft WorkLab: Research Proves Your Brain Needs Breaks (2021)</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Task_switching_(psychology)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Task switching</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zoom_fatigue" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Zoom fatigue</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flow_(psychology)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Flow (psychology)</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ego_depletion" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Ego depletion</a>.</p><p class="runcode">A question, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/enrichmeai.github.io/issues/new?title=%5BBody%2C%20Mind%20%26%20Nature%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>. Nothing here is tracked and there are no comments to store: questions live in the open, where the next learner can find the answer.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<section class="panel" id="p-quantum" data-name="Quantum Computing">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Quantum computing, in plain words</h2><p>Quantum computers are not faster versions of ordinary computers. They are a different kind of machine that is very good at a few specific problems and no help at most others. For engineers today, the most practical part is not building quantum programs: it is preparing cryptography for the day large quantum computers exist.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card"><h3>The core ideas</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>A qubit</b> is the quantum version of a bit. Before it is measured it can be in a combination (a superposition) of 0 and 1; measuring it gives 0 or 1 with certain probabilities.</li>
      <li><b>Entanglement</b> links qubits so that their measurement results are correlated in ways ordinary bits cannot be.</li>
      <li><b>Interference</b> is how quantum algorithms work: they arrange for wrong answers to cancel out and right answers to reinforce, so that measuring gives a useful result.</li>
      <li><b>Noise is the hard part.</b> Qubits are fragile; today's machines make errors, and correcting them needs many physical qubits for each reliable one.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>What it is good for, and what it is not</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Promising:</b> simulating molecules and materials, some optimisation and sampling problems, and certain mathematical problems such as factoring large numbers. In finance, a technique called quantum amplitude estimation could in principle speed up Monte Carlo risk calculations; today that is research and pilots, not production.</li>
      <li><b>Not useful for:</b> running websites, databases, everyday apps or most business software. Ordinary computers stay better at almost everything.</li>
      <li><b>Be wary of hype:</b> claims of quantum advantage are usually about narrow, carefully chosen problems.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>From Planck to quantum computers</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>When</th><th>Who</th><th>What changed</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>1900</td><td>Max Planck</td><td>Energy comes in small, fixed packets, "quanta". Quantum physics begins.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>1905</td><td>Albert Einstein</td><td>Light itself comes in packets (photons).</td></tr>
      <tr><td>1925–26</td><td>Heisenberg, Schrödinger, Dirac</td><td>Full quantum mechanics, including superposition.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>1935</td><td>Einstein, Podolsky and Rosen; Schrödinger</td><td>Entanglement is identified.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>1980–82</td><td>Paul Benioff, Richard Feynman</td><td>Computers could run on quantum mechanics, and only quantum computers could simulate nature efficiently.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>1985</td><td>David Deutsch</td><td>The universal quantum computer, and the first algorithm that beats a classical one.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>1994</td><td>Peter Shor</td><td>A quantum algorithm to factor large numbers: the threat to today's public-key cryptography.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>1996</td><td>Lov Grover</td><td>A quadratic speed-up for searching.</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note">The ideas behind a qubit go back to Planck; using them to compute comes from Deutsch. See also <a href="#history">A brief history</a>.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Shor's algorithm in simple terms</h3>
    <p>Multiplying two primes is easy: 61 × 53 = 3,233. Going back, finding which primes make 3,233, is hard, and for the very large numbers used in RSA it is impractical for ordinary computers. Shor turned factoring into finding a repeating pattern. Here it is for 15:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Pick a number, say 7, and take its powers, keeping only the remainder after dividing by 15: 7, 4, 13, 1, 7, 4, 13, 1…</li>
      <li>The pattern repeats every 4 steps, so the period is 4.</li>
      <li>Take half the period: 7² − 1 = 48 and 7² + 1 = 50.</li>
      <li>What each shares with 15: 48 and 15 share 3; 50 and 15 share 5. So 15 = 3 × 5.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Every step is easy for an ordinary computer except finding the period when the number has hundreds of digits. That is the one step the quantum computer does: it puts many powers into superposition, computes them together, and uses interference (the quantum Fourier transform) so that the period stands out when measured. The rest runs on an ordinary computer, so it is a hybrid of the two.</p>
    <p class="note">The numbers factored this way on real quantum hardware are still tiny. Breaking RSA needs a large, error-corrected machine that does not exist yet, which is why the move to post-quantum cryptography is happening now, before it does.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Why it matters for security: post-quantum cryptography</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Shor's algorithm</b>, on a large, error-corrected quantum computer, could break the public-key cryptography the internet relies on today (RSA and elliptic-curve keys and signatures).</li>
      <li><b>"Harvest now, decrypt later":</b> someone can record encrypted traffic today and decrypt it once such a machine exists, so data that must stay secret for many years is already at risk.</li>
      <li><b>New standards exist.</b> NIST has standardised post-quantum algorithms: <b>ML-KEM</b> (FIPS 203) for key exchange, based on Kyber, and <b>ML-DSA</b> (FIPS 204) and <b>SLH-DSA</b> (FIPS 205) for digital signatures.</li>
      <li><b>Symmetric cryptography holds up better:</b> Grover's algorithm only gives a quadratic speed-up, so longer keys (such as AES-256) remain a sound choice.</li>
      <li><b>What to do now:</b> know where your systems use public-key cryptography (TLS, signing, key exchange); keep crypto libraries current, since many are adding post-quantum and hybrid options; design so algorithms can be swapped (crypto agility); and plan the move for long-lived secrets first.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Try it yourself</h3>
    <p><a href="https://github.com/Qiskit/qiskit" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Qiskit</a> is an open-source SDK for building quantum circuits in Python and running them on simulators on your laptop. A good first exercise: build a circuit that entangles two qubits (a Bell state), run it many times on the simulator, and see that the two results always agree.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Keep your feet on the ground</h3>
    <p>You do not need quantum physics to be ready. You need to know where your cryptography lives, keep it up to date, and be able to change it. That is good engineering whether quantum computers arrive soon or late.</p>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Explain qubits, superposition and entanglement in plain words</li><li>Say what quantum computers are and are not good for</li><li>Explain why Shor's algorithm threatens RSA and elliptic curves</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Inventory where your systems use public-key cryptography</li><li>Keep crypto libraries current and follow their post-quantum options</li><li>Build and run a simple circuit on a simulator</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Plan a post-quantum migration, long-lived secrets first</li><li>Design for crypto agility</li><li>Judge vendor quantum claims critically</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Own the organisation's post-quantum roadmap</li><li>Advise on where quantum computing could genuinely help</li><li>Track standards and research and translate them for teams</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>Qubits and measurement</li><li>Shor's and Grover's algorithms</li><li>Post-quantum cryptography (ML-KEM, ML-DSA, SLH-DSA)</li><li>Quantum error correction</li><li>Hybrid quantum-classical algorithms</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://github.com/open-quantum-safe/liboqs" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open Quantum Safe (liboqs)</a><span class="lnote"> Open-source post-quantum algorithms, including the NIST standards</span></li><li><a href="https://github.com/Qiskit/qiskit" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Qiskit</a><span class="lnote"> Open-source SDK for quantum circuits</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shor%27s_algorithm?action=raw" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Shor's algorithm</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Post-quantum_cryptography?action=raw" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Post-quantum cryptography (FIPS 203/204/205)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Qiskit/qiskit/main/README.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Qiskit README</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/open-quantum-safe/liboqs/main/README.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open Quantum Safe liboqs README</a>.</p><p class="runcode">A question, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/enrichmeai.github.io/issues/new?title=%5BQuantum%20Computing%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>. Nothing here is tracked and there are no comments to store: questions live in the open, where the next learner can find the answer.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<section class="panel" id="p-robotics" data-name="Robotics">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Robotics: software that moves things</h2><p>A robot senses the world, decides what to do and acts on it, over and over, many times a second. Robotics joins electronics, mechanics, control theory and software, and mistakes move real objects near real people, so safety comes first.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card"><h3>Sense, think, act</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Sense:</b> cameras, lidar, encoders on the wheels and joints, inertial sensors, force and touch sensors.</li>
      <li><b>Think:</b> work out where the robot is, what is around it, and what to do next.</li>
      <li><b>Act:</b> motors and actuators turn decisions into movement.</li>
      <li><b>The loop never stops:</b> every sensor is noisy and every motor imperfect, so the robot measures again and corrects, all the time.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>The core ideas</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Control:</b> a PID controller turns the error between where you are and where you want to be into a command. The example below shows each term at work.</li>
      <li><b>Kinematics:</b> forward kinematics asks where the hand is, given the joint angles; inverse kinematics asks which joint angles put the hand there. The second is the hard one.</li>
      <li><b>Localisation and mapping (SLAM):</b> building a map of an unknown place while keeping track of where you are in it, the problem self-driving cars and warehouse robots solve.</li>
      <li><b>Planning:</b> finding a path that avoids obstacles and respects what the robot can physically do.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>A PID controller you can run</h3>
    <p>Plain Python. A toy motor with friction is driven to a target speed of 100. Proportional control alone settles short, because it needs an error to produce any power; the integral term removes that gap.</p>
    <pre><code class="language-python">&quot;&quot;&quot;A PID controller holding a simple motor at a target speed.&quot;&quot;&quot;
def simulate(kp, ki, kd, target=100.0, steps=200, dt=0.05):
    speed, integral, prev_error = 0.0, 0.0, target
    for _ in range(steps):
        error = target - speed                    # how far off are we?
        integral += error * dt                    # I: past error that has not gone away
        derivative = (error - prev_error) / dt    # D: how fast the error is changing
        prev_error = error
        power = kp * error + ki * integral + kd * derivative
        power = max(-100.0, min(100.0, power))    # real motors have limits
        speed += (power - 0.5 * speed) * dt       # toy physics: power pushes, friction resists
    return round(speed, 1)

print(&quot;P only:  &quot;, simulate(kp=2.0, ki=0.0, kd=0.0))   # settles short of the target
print(&quot;PI:      &quot;, simulate(kp=2.0, ki=1.0, kd=0.0))   # the integral removes the gap
print(&quot;PID:     &quot;, simulate(kp=2.0, ki=1.0, kd=0.1))</code></pre>
    <pre><code>P only:   80.0
PI:       100.1
PID:      100.1</code></pre>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>The software stack: ROS 2</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>ROS (Robot Operating System)</b> is open-source robotics middleware, not an operating system. ROS 2 is the current generation.</li>
      <li><b>Nodes and topics:</b> each part of the robot (a camera driver, a planner, a motor controller) is a node; nodes exchange messages over named topics. It is publish/subscribe, as in <a href="#kafka">Kafka</a>, on a robot.</li>
      <li><b>Simulate first:</b> test in a simulator before the real hardware, where a bug costs a crash in software rather than a broken arm.</li>
      <li><b>Languages:</b> C++ where timing matters, Python for tools and glue; <a href="#rust">Rust</a> is growing.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Safety comes first</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Standards exist</b> for a reason: ISO 10218 is the international standard for industrial robot safety.</li>
      <li><b>Emergency stop:</b> every robot has one a person can reach, and it works even when the software does not. The manual override from <a href="#aiworry">Worried about AI?</a>, made of hardware.</li>
      <li><b>Fail safe:</b> on lost signal, low battery or a sensor fault, stop or move to a safe state.</li>
      <li><b>Test the bad cases</b> in simulation and on the bench: a person stepping in, a sensor going dark, a motor stalling.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Where to start</h3>
    <p>Begin small and physical: a microcontroller, two motors, a distance sensor and a line to follow. Then add a PID loop, then a camera, then ROS 2 in a simulator. Robotics rewards the foundations: <a href="#mathematics">Mathematics</a> (geometry, probability), <a href="#linux">Linux</a>, <a href="#networking">Networking</a>, and patient <a href="#science">experiment</a>. AI models are increasingly part of the "think" step, but the loop, the limits and the emergency stop stay the engineer's job.</p>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Explain the sense-think-act loop</li><li>Tune a PID controller in simulation</li><li>Know why every robot has an emergency stop</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Build a small robot or simulated one with ROS 2 nodes and topics</li><li>Read sensor data and filter its noise</li><li>Apply forward and inverse kinematics to an arm</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Design localisation, mapping and path planning</li><li>Design for functional safety and failure modes</li><li>Move from simulation to hardware safely</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Lead a robotics platform or product</li><li>Set safety and testing standards</li><li>Combine learned models with classical control responsibly</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>PID control</li><li>Kinematics</li><li>SLAM</li><li>ROS 2 nodes, topics and services</li><li>Robot safety standards (ISO 10218)</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robot_Operating_System" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Robot Operating System (Wikipedia)</a><span class="lnote"> What ROS is and how it is organised</span></li><li><a href="https://github.com/ros2/ros2" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ROS 2 source</a><span class="lnote"> The ROS 2 repositories and installation</span></li><li><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Proportional%E2%80%93integral%E2%80%93derivative_controller" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PID controller (Wikipedia)</a><span class="lnote"> The maths behind the example</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robot_Operating_System?action=raw" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Robot Operating System</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PID_controller?action=raw" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: PID controller</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ros2/ros2/rolling/README.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ROS 2 README</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ISO_10218?action=raw" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: ISO 10218</a>. A few claims could not be confirmed at their official source from here and are being re-checked.</p><p class="runcode">A question, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/enrichmeai.github.io/issues/new?title=%5BRobotics%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>. Nothing here is tracked and there are no comments to store: questions live in the open, where the next learner can find the answer.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= WEB3 ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-bitcoin" data-name="Bitcoin &amp; Blockchain">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Bitcoin and blockchain, from first principles</h2><p>How a blockchain works underneath, using Bitcoin as the example: hashes, linked blocks, proof of work and keys. Understand the mechanism before forming a view on the money. Smart contracts and the wider Web3 ideas are on <a href="#web3">Web 3.0</a>.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card"><h3>The story in brief</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>31 October 2008:</b> a white paper by Satoshi Nakamoto, <i>Bitcoin: A Peer-to-Peer Electronic Cash System</i>, was posted to a cryptography mailing list.</li>
      <li><b>3 January 2009:</b> the network started when the first block, the genesis block, was mined.</li>
      <li><b>The problem it solved:</b> how strangers can agree on who owns what, with no bank or central server in the middle, and without anyone spending the same coin twice.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>How a blockchain works</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Hashes:</b> a hash is a short fingerprint of data. Change one character and the fingerprint changes completely. See <a href="#mathematics">Mathematics</a>.</li>
      <li><b>Linked blocks:</b> each block holds transactions and the hash of the block before it, so changing an old block breaks every link after it.</li>
      <li><b>Proof of work:</b> to add a block, miners search for a number (a nonce) that makes the block's double SHA-256 hash fall below a target. Finding it costs a lot of computing; checking it costs one hash.</li>
      <li><b>Consensus:</b> thousands of independent nodes check every block and follow the chain with the most accumulated work. Rewriting history means redoing that work faster than everyone else combined.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Build a toy blockchain</h3>
    <p>Plain Python, standard library only. It mines three blocks, checks the chain, then tampers with a past transaction and shows the chain fail. The nonce it prints changes from run to run, because the timestamp does.</p>
    <pre><code class="language-python">&quot;&quot;&quot;A toy blockchain: blocks linked by hashes, sealed by proof of work.&quot;&quot;&quot;
import hashlib, json, time

def block_hash(block):
    data = json.dumps(block, sort_keys=True).encode()
    return hashlib.sha256(hashlib.sha256(data).digest()).hexdigest()  # double SHA-256, as Bitcoin does

def mine(prev_hash, transactions, difficulty=4):
    &quot;&quot;&quot;Try nonces until the hash starts with `difficulty` zeros: costly to find, cheap to check.&quot;&quot;&quot;
    block = {&quot;prev&quot;: prev_hash, &quot;tx&quot;: transactions, &quot;time&quot;: int(time.time()), &quot;nonce&quot;: 0}
    while not block_hash(block).startswith(&quot;0&quot; * difficulty):
        block[&quot;nonce&quot;] += 1
    return block

def valid(chain, difficulty=4):
    for prev, block in zip(chain, chain[1:]):
        if block[&quot;prev&quot;] != block_hash(prev):
            return False                          # a link is broken
        if not block_hash(block).startswith(&quot;0&quot; * difficulty):
            return False                          # no proof of work
    return True

genesis = mine(&quot;0&quot; * 64, [&quot;genesis&quot;])
chain = [genesis]
chain.append(mine(block_hash(chain[-1]), [&quot;alice pays bob 5&quot;]))
chain.append(mine(block_hash(chain[-1]), [&quot;bob pays carol 2&quot;]))
print(&quot;valid:&quot;, valid(chain), &quot;| nonce of block 2:&quot;, chain[2][&quot;nonce&quot;])

chain[1][&quot;tx&quot;] = [&quot;alice pays bob 500&quot;]          # tamper with history
print(&quot;after tampering:&quot;, valid(chain))          # every later link now breaks</code></pre>
    <pre><code>valid: True | nonce of block 2: 116188
after tampering: False</code></pre>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Bitcoin's rules, in numbers</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>About 10 minutes</b> per block on average; the difficulty adjusts so it stays there as miners come and go.</li>
      <li><b>A fixed supply</b> of 21 million bitcoin at most.</li>
      <li><b>The mining reward halves every 210,000 blocks</b>, roughly every four years.</li>
      <li><b>Keys:</b> an address is computed from a randomly generated private key. Computing the address is instant; finding the key from the address is practically impossible. Whoever holds the key controls the coins.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Trade-offs and risks</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Energy:</b> proof of work uses a great deal of electricity, and has been criticised for its environmental impact. Ethereum moved from proof of work to proof of stake in September 2022 ("The Merge"), cutting its energy use by over 99%.</li>
      <li><b>No undo:</b> a payment to the wrong address, or a lost key, is usually gone for good.</li>
      <li><b>Scams thrive</b> where payments are irreversible: fake investments, "recovery" services, impersonation. Nobody legitimate asks for your private key or seed phrase.</li>
      <li><b>Volatility:</b> prices can swing widely. This page explains the technology; it is not investment advice.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>What engineers can take from it</h3>
    <p>Even if you never touch a cryptocurrency, the ideas travel: content-addressed data (Git commits are linked by hashes too, see <a href="#devops">DevOps</a>), append-only logs (see <a href="#eventsourcing">Event Sourcing</a>), public-key cryptography (see <a href="#security">Security</a>), and agreement between machines that do not trust each other. Ask the question on <a href="#web3">Web 3.0</a> first: do you actually need a blockchain, or a database with good audit logs?</p>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Explain hashes and how blocks link</li><li>Explain proof of work and why rewriting history is hard</li><li>Keep keys safe and recognise common scams</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Build a toy chain and validate it</li><li>Explain Bitcoin's supply, halving and difficulty rules</li><li>Compare proof of work and proof of stake</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Judge whether a problem needs a blockchain at all</li><li>Design key custody and recovery</li><li>Assess the security model of a chain or protocol</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Advise an organisation on distributed-ledger strategy</li><li>Review consensus and cryptographic designs</li><li>Explain the technology to non-specialists without hype</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>SHA-256 and Merkle trees</li><li>Proof of work and difficulty</li><li>Public-key cryptography and wallets</li><li>Proof of stake</li><li>Layer 2 payment channels</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://bitcoin.org/bitcoin.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bitcoin: A Peer-to-Peer Electronic Cash System</a><span class="lnote"> Satoshi Nakamoto's white paper, nine pages</span></li><li><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bitcoin_protocol" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bitcoin protocol (Wikipedia)</a><span class="lnote"> How mining, blocks and transactions work</span></li><li><a href="https://github.com/bitcoin/bitcoin" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bitcoin Core source</a><span class="lnote"> The reference implementation</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bitcoin?action=raw" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Bitcoin (31 Oct 2008, 3 Jan 2009, halving every 210,000 blocks)</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bitcoin_protocol?action=raw" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Bitcoin protocol</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ethereum?action=raw" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Ethereum (The Merge, 15 Sept 2022, >99% energy cut)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/bitcoin/bitcoin/master/README.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bitcoin Core README</a>.</p><p class="runcode">A question, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/enrichmeai.github.io/issues/new?title=%5BBitcoin%20%26%20Blockchain%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>. Nothing here is tracked and there are no comments to store: questions live in the open, where the next learner can find the answer.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<section class="panel" id="p-web3" data-name="Web 3.0">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Web 3.0</h2><p>"Web 3.0" means two different things. <b>Web3</b> is the blockchain-based vision: decentralised ledgers, smart contracts, tokens and wallets. The <b>Semantic Web</b> (Tim Berners-Lee's earlier "Web 3.0") is about machine-readable, linked data. Both matter to engineers, for different reasons. The <a href="#solid">Solid</a> tab covers Berners-Lee's current project for personal data.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Web 1.0, 2.0, 3.0</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th></th><th>Web 1.0</th><th>Web 2.0</th><th>Web3 (blockchain)</th><th>Semantic Web</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Era</td><td>1990s</td><td>2000s onward</td><td>2014 onward</td><td>2000s onward</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Shape</td><td>Read-only pages</td><td>Platforms with user content</td><td>Decentralised apps on blockchains</td><td>Linked, machine-readable data</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Who holds data</td><td>Site owners</td><td>Platforms</td><td>Public ledgers and users' wallets</td><td>Publishers, linked by URIs</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Key tech</td><td>HTML, HTTP</td><td>APIs, cloud, mobile</td><td>Blockchains, smart contracts, tokens</td><td>RDF, SPARQL, JSON-LD, ontologies</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Web3 building blocks</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Blockchain:</b> a replicated, append-only ledger agreed by consensus (proof of stake on Ethereum), without a central operator.</li>
      <li><b>Wallet and keys:</b> a private key controls an account; whoever holds the key controls the assets. Lost key, lost assets.</li>
      <li><b>Smart contract:</b> a program deployed on the chain (Solidity on Ethereum), public, and immutable unless designed to be upgradeable.</li>
      <li><b>Tokens:</b> fungible (ERC-20) and non-fungible (ERC-721 NFTs) assets defined by contracts.</li>
      <li><b>Gas:</b> fees paid for computation and storage on chain.</li>
      <li><b>Layer 2 rollups:</b> process transactions off the main chain and post their data to it (zero-knowledge rollups also post a validity proof; optimistic rollups only produce a fraud proof if someone challenges), for lower fees.</li>
      <li><b>Oracles:</b> bring outside data on chain, a trust point in themselves.</li>
      <li><b>Decentralised storage:</b> IPFS and similar address content by its hash.</li>
      <li><b>DeFi and DAOs:</b> financial services and organisations governed by contracts and token votes.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>A minimal smart contract</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
pragma solidity ^0.8.24;

/// Records a document hash and who registered it, once.
contract DocumentRegistry {
    mapping(bytes32 =&gt; address) public registeredBy;
    event Registered(bytes32 indexed docHash, address indexed by);

    function register(bytes32 docHash) external {
        require(registeredBy[docHash] == address(0), "already registered");
        registeredBy[docHash] = msg.sender;
        emit Registered(docHash, msg.sender);
    }
}</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">Tooling: Foundry or Hardhat to build and test, ethers.js or viem to call contracts, a testnet before mainnet, and an external audit before holding real value. Deployed bugs are public and often permanent.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Do you need a blockchain?</h3>
    <ol>
      <li>Do several parties need to write to shared data?</li>
      <li>Do they not trust each other, or any single operator?</li>
      <li>Is there no acceptable trusted intermediary (a database run by a neutral party, a regulator)?</li>
      <li>Is public, permanent, tamper-evident history worth the cost, latency and loss of privacy?</li>
    </ol>
    <p>If any answer is no, a conventional database, an append-only ledger table, signed records or a timestamping service is simpler, cheaper and easier to correct.</p>
    <p class="note">Also weigh regulation: rules for crypto assets differ by country and change often (India, for example, taxes crypto transfers and has evolving rules). Personal data on an immutable public chain conflicts with deletion rights.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>The Semantic Web and linked data</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>RDF:</b> data as triples (subject, predicate, object), each named by a URI, so data from different sources joins up.</li>
      <li><b>Vocabularies and ontologies:</b> shared meanings (schema.org, Dublin Core, FOAF; OWL for formal ontologies).</li>
      <li><b>Formats:</b> Turtle, JSON-LD (linked data in ordinary JSON), RDF/XML.</li>
      <li><b>SPARQL:</b> the query language for RDF data and knowledge graphs.</li>
      <li><b>Knowledge graphs:</b> entities and relationships, used in search, AI grounding and data integration.</li>
      <li><b>For product data:</b> schema.org's <code>Product</code> type, with identifiers such as <code>sku</code> and GTIN codes (<code>gtin13</code>), gives items stable, linkable identities across shops.</li>
    </ul>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>&lt;!-- JSON-LD with schema.org, embedded in a web page --&gt;
&lt;script type="application/ld+json"&gt;
{ "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "Product",
  "name": "Folding umbrella",
  "sku": "UMB-001",
  "offers": { "@type": "Offer", "price": "12.99", "priceCurrency": "GBP" } }
&lt;/script&gt;</code></pre></div>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># SPARQL: products in a given category, newest first
PREFIX ex: &lt;https://example.org/shop#&gt;
SELECT ?p ?date WHERE {
  ?p ex:inCategory &lt;https://example.org/category/umbrellas&gt; ;
     ex:listedOn ?date .
} ORDER BY DESC(?date)</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Tell Web3 (blockchain) from the Semantic Web</li><li>Explain blockchains, wallets, smart contracts and tokens</li><li>Ask whether a problem really needs a blockchain</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Write and test a simple smart contract on a testnet</li><li>Publish structured data with JSON-LD and schema.org</li><li>Query linked data with SPARQL</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Assess security, cost, privacy and regulation of a Web3 design</li><li>Design knowledge graphs and ontologies for a domain</li><li>Model product data with shared vocabularies (schema.org Product, GTIN codes)</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Set strategy on decentralised and linked-data technologies</li><li>Lead audits and governance for on-chain systems</li><li>Shape open data standards in a sector</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>Ethereum and proof of stake</li><li>Smart contract security</li><li>Layer 2 rollups</li><li>RDF, OWL and SHACL</li><li>Knowledge graphs for AI grounding</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://ethereum.org/en/developers/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ethereum.org developers</a><span class="lnote"> Concepts and tutorials</span></li><li><a href="https://docs.soliditylang.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Solidity documentation</a><span class="lnote"> Language reference</span></li><li><a href="https://www.w3.org/2001/sw/wiki/Main_Page" target="_blank" rel="noopener">W3C Semantic Web</a><span class="lnote"> RDF, SPARQL, OWL standards</span></li><li><a href="https://json-ld.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">JSON-LD</a><span class="lnote"> Linked data in JSON</span></li><li><a href="https://schema.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">schema.org</a><span class="lnote"> Shared vocabulary for structured data</span></li><li><a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/sparql11-query/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">SPARQL 1.1 Query Language</a><span class="lnote"> The specification</span></li><li><a href="https://github.com/schemaorg/schemaorg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">schema.org vocabulary source</a><span class="lnote"> The Product, Offer and other type definitions</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Web3?action=raw" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Web3 (term coined by Gavin Wood, 2014)</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Semantic_Web?action=raw" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Semantic Web ('sometimes known as Web 3.0')</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ethereum?action=raw" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Ethereum (The Merge, proof of stake)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ethereum/ethereum-org-website/dev/public/content/developers/docs/scaling/index.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ethereum.org: scaling and rollups</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ethereum/ethereum-org-website/dev/public/content/developers/docs/standards/tokens/erc-20/index.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ethereum.org: ERC-20</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ethereum/ethereum-org-website/dev/public/content/developers/docs/standards/tokens/erc-721/index.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ethereum.org: ERC-721</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ethereum/solidity/develop/docs/contracts/events.rst" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Solidity docs: events</a>; <a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/rdf11-concepts/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">W3C RDF 1.1 Concepts</a>; <a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/sparql11-query/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">W3C SPARQL 1.1 Query</a>; <a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/json-ld11/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">W3C JSON-LD 1.1</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/schemaorg/schemaorg/main/data/schema.ttl" target="_blank" rel="noopener">schema.org vocabulary (sku, gtin13)</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/WazirX?action=raw" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: WazirX (India's 2022 crypto tax)</a>. A few claims could not be confirmed at their official source from here and are being re-checked.</p><p class="runcode">A question, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/enrichmeai.github.io/issues/new?title=%5BWeb%203.0%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>. Nothing here is tracked and there are no comments to store: questions live in the open, where the next learner can find the answer.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= SOLID ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-solid" data-name="Solid">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Solid</h2><p>Solid is a set of open specifications, led by Tim Berners-Lee, for putting people in control of their data. Each person keeps data in a <b>Pod</b> (a personal online data store) and grants apps access to it, instead of every app keeping its own copy. It uses ordinary web standards (HTTP, linked data, OpenID Connect), not a blockchain.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>How Solid works</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Piece</th><th>Is</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Pod</td><td>A personal data store on a Solid server, self-hosted or from a provider; organisations can run Pods for their users</td></tr>
      <tr><td>WebID</td><td>A URI that identifies a person or agent, pointing to a profile document</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Solid-OIDC</td><td>Authentication built on OpenID Connect: the user logs in with their identity provider; apps act on their behalf</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Access control</td><td>Web Access Control (WAC) or Access Control Policies (ACP): who may read, write, append or control each resource</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Resources</td><td>Files and containers in the Pod, read and written over HTTP (GET, PUT, POST, PATCH, DELETE)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Linked data</td><td>Structured data stored as RDF (often Turtle) with shared vocabularies, so different apps understand the same data</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Notifications</td><td>Apps can subscribe to changes in resources</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note">The key shift: apps and data are separated. A user can switch apps without losing data, and grant or revoke access per app or person.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Data in a Pod</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># a public profile document (Turtle)
@prefix foaf: &lt;http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/&gt; .
@prefix solid: &lt;http://www.w3.org/ns/solid/terms#&gt; .

&lt;#me&gt; a foaf:Person ;
  foaf:name "Asha Rao" ;
  solid:oidcIssuer &lt;https://login.example-provider.com&gt; .</code></pre></div>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># read it over plain HTTP
curl -H "Accept: text/turtle" https://asha.pod.example/profile/card</code></pre></div>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># Web Access Control: owner has full control of a folder
@prefix acl: &lt;http://www.w3.org/ns/auth/acl#&gt; .
&lt;#owner&gt; a acl:Authorization ;
  acl:agent &lt;https://asha.pod.example/profile/card#me&gt; ;
  acl:accessTo &lt;./&gt; ; acl:default &lt;./&gt; ;
  acl:mode acl:Read, acl:Write, acl:Control .</code></pre></div>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>An app reading from a Pod (JavaScript)</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>import { login, handleIncomingRedirect, getDefaultSession, fetch }
  from "@inrupt/solid-client-authn-browser";
import { getSolidDataset, getThing, getStringNoLocale }
  from "@inrupt/solid-client";

await handleIncomingRedirect();
if (!getDefaultSession().info.isLoggedIn) {
  await login({ oidcIssuer: "https://login.example-provider.com",
                redirectUrl: window.location.href, clientName: "Order Notes" });
}

const profileUrl = "https://asha.pod.example/profile/card";
const dataset = await getSolidDataset(profileUrl, { fetch });   // authenticated fetch
const me = getThing(dataset, profileUrl + "#me");
const name = getStringNoLocale(me, "http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/name");</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">Libraries: Inrupt's JavaScript client libraries, and others for Java and Python. Servers: the open-source Community Solid Server for development and research; commercial servers for production.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Where it fits</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Personal data that many services need:</b> health records, education credentials, identity documents, preferences.</li>
      <li><b>Public-sector data sharing</b> with citizen consent; some governments are piloting or running Solid-based services.</li>
      <li><b>Portability:</b> users switch apps without exporting and importing.</li>
      <li><b>Consent you can audit:</b> access is granted per resource and can be revoked.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">Challenges: app ecosystem still small, linked-data skills are less common, querying across many Pods is slower than a central database, and Pod providers must be trusted to run storage well.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Solid vs Web3 vs a normal platform</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th></th><th>Platform</th><th>Web3</th><th>Solid</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Data held by</td><td>The platform</td><td>A public ledger</td><td>The user's chosen Pod</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Identity</td><td>Platform account</td><td>Wallet key</td><td>WebID with OpenID Connect</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Privacy</td><td>Platform policy</td><td>Public by default</td><td>Private by default, shared by grant</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Deletion</td><td>Platform decides</td><td>Effectively impossible on chain</td><td>User deletes from their Pod</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Built on</td><td>Proprietary APIs</td><td>Blockchains, tokens</td><td>HTTP, RDF, OIDC</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
  </article>










  <article class="card"><h3>One we build: Cistern</h3>
    <p><a href="/cistern/">Cistern</a> is a self-hostable data pod with an MCP server: every request from an AI agent is checked against your rules when it is made, never from a cache, and gets a receipt naming the rule that decided it. Its README describes it as an open Solid pod server. It is open source (Apache 2.0).</p>
    <p class="note">It is ours, so we are not neutral: compare it with the other Solid servers on this page.</p>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Explain Pods, WebIDs and why apps and data are separated</li><li>Read a public resource from a Pod</li><li>Compare Solid with Web3 and platforms</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Build an app that logs in with Solid-OIDC and reads and writes Pod data</li><li>Set access control with WAC or ACP</li><li>Model data with shared RDF vocabularies</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Design consent-based data sharing on Solid</li><li>Choose and operate Solid servers</li><li>Plan performance and querying across many Pods</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Lead Solid adoption in an organisation or sector</li><li>Contribute to the specifications</li><li>Set data-sovereignty strategy</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>Solid Protocol specification</li><li>Solid-OIDC</li><li>WAC vs ACP access control</li><li>Linked data vocabularies</li><li>Community Solid Server</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="https://solidproject.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Solid Project</a><span class="lnote"> Overview, apps, community</span></li><li><a href="https://solidproject.org/TR/protocol" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Solid Protocol</a><span class="lnote"> The specification</span></li><li><a href="https://solidproject.org/TR/wac" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Web Access Control</a><span class="lnote"> Access control specification</span></li><li><a href="https://docs.inrupt.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Inrupt documentation</a><span class="lnote"> Client libraries and server</span></li><li><a href="https://communitysolidserver.github.io/CommunitySolidServer/latest/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Community Solid Server</a><span class="lnote"> Open-source server</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/solid/specification/main/protocol.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Solid Protocol (spec source)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/solid/web-access-control-spec/main/index.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Web Access Control (spec source)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/solid/solid-oidc/main/index.bs" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Solid-OIDC (spec source, solid:oidcIssuer)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/solid/notifications/main/protocol.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Solid Notifications Protocol</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Solid_project?action=raw" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Solid project (led by Tim Berners-Lee)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/inrupt/solid-client-authn-js/main/packages/browser/src/defaultSession.ts" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Inrupt authn-browser: exported fetch/login/handleIncomingRedirect</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/inrupt/solid-client-authn-js/main/packages/core/src/ILoginInputOptions.ts" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Inrupt login options (oidcIssuer, redirectUrl, clientName)</a>; <a href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/CommunitySolidServer/CommunitySolidServer/main/README.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Community Solid Server README</a>; <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/cistern/blob/v0.3.1/README.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cistern README (open Solid pod server, Apache 2.0), read at its latest release tag in a local clone</a>; <a href="https://github.com/solid/specification/blob/main/meetings/2023-06-14.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Solid CG minutes: Flanders use case</a>; <a href="https://github.com/solid/specification/blob/main/meetings/2022-10-19.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Solid CG minutes: UK GDS pilot</a>.</p><p class="runcode">A question, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/enrichmeai.github.io/issues/new?title=%5BSolid%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>. Nothing here is tracked and there are no comments to store: questions live in the open, where the next learner can find the answer.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<section class="panel" id="p-why" data-name="Why this guide exists">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Why this guide exists</h2><p>A short story, and a promise: a click is cheaper than a prompt.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Where I started</h3>
    <p>I started as an electronics engineer, moved into computer hardware, and then into software. I first used computers around 1995, when most machines around me ran DOS and Windows was not yet common. The first languages I learned were BASIC and Fortran; I still remember typing <code>GWBASIC</code> at the DOS prompt to start BASIC. We got hold of a Windows 95 computer later, certainly by 1998, and we booted machines from floppy disks.</p>
    <p>By the time I started working, around 2000, most desks ran Windows 95 or 98. As I remember it, Windows NT was light and good for simply getting work done, but people found it plain next to 95 and 98; Windows 2000 looked much better but needed more memory than NT. Memory was scarce: we worked with 128 MB of RAM, and 256 MB was a luxury. In my first software job, at one of the smallest startups you can imagine, our internet came over a dial-up line.</p>
    <p>Java, as I remember it, was at version 1.1. We could not download the documentation, so we used <code>javap</code> to read a class's methods and worked out the rest. We wrote code in Notepad. Then we found EditPlus, wired it to <code>javac</code> and <code>java</code> so we could compile and run from the editor, and the coloured code alone made us feel we had arrived.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Learning from the platform up</h3>
    <p>We learned core Java, then servlets and applets, and within two years we were building web applications. Struts arrived around then: an Apache project, founded in May 2000 by Craig McClanahan (started, as I remember it, as a holiday project). Hans Bergsten's writing on JSP was how many of us learned, and I remember puzzling over the difference between the servlet context and the page context.</p>
    <p>Browsers were not standard. What we now call Ajax worked through Microsoft's XMLHTTP object in Internet Explorer, so we wrote separate code for Internet Explorer and Netscape. There was no Chrome.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>The EJB years</h3>
    <p>At one of the biggest retail software companies of the time, we built with Enterprise JavaBeans: stateless session beans to read data, entity beans for the database, and, in booking systems of that era, stateful session beans holding a conversation while the database held the lock on a ticket. XSLT generated our screens from data, and UML and model-driven architecture promised that drawing the model would write the code.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>We generated code long before AI</h3>
    <p>Code generation is not new. I used database metadata to generate reusable boilerplate code, much as people use AI today, and XSLT templates to turn data into dynamic screens. We generated the JavaScript for tree structures, pagination and web-based tutorials.</p>
    <p>The lesson has stayed with me: generating code is easy; understanding what you generate is the job.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>The framework years</h3>
    <p>Enterprise Java was hard. We wrestled with CORBA and its interface definitions, with two-phase commits for transactions that spanned systems, and later with enterprise service buses. Then came Spring, which its own documentation says came into being in 2003 as a response to the complexity of the early J2EE specifications. Rod Johnson had argued for simpler designs in his book <i>Expert One-on-One J2EE Design and Development</i> (Wrox), which is still on my shelf; as I remember it, his ideas about plain Java objects were at Spring's heart, and after VMware took the company over, Spring became the standard choice. For many of us, it started with a single core jar.</p>
    <p>Something changed in those years. Many engineers learned the frameworks without ever looking at a servlet. The frameworks were good, but the foundations underneath them became invisible.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>My bookshelf</h3>
    <p>The first technical book I read cover to cover was Hunter and Crawford's <i>Java Servlet Programming</i> (O'Reilly). My first UML tool was MagicDraw.</p>
    <p>At my first startup, from 2000 to 2002, I could not afford <i>Enterprise Java with UML</i> (Arrington and Rayhan, Wiley). When I could, in 2003, I bought it for myself, out of love for it: it explained every layer, why DTOs exist, and when a plain hash map is enough. Seeing why each layer is there is what today's polished frameworks and generated answers rarely show.</p>
    <p>Beside them still: <i>Java Message Service</i> (Monson-Haefel and Chappell), <i>Java Enterprise Best Practices</i>, <i>JavaServer Pages</i> and Rod Johnson's <i>Expert One-on-One J2EE Design and Development</i>, packed away for a house move and going back on a shelf soon.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>What worries me now</h3>
    <p>I do not want to generalise, but I see a newer version of the same pattern. Some new engineers go straight to AI without learning the fundamentals, or even the frameworks. They can get an answer, but they cannot always tell whether it is right, or fix it when it breaks.</p>
    <p>At the same time, much of the simple knowledge people once found on a website, and followed through themselves, is now asked of an AI, one question at a time. Every one of those questions costs tokens, energy and data-centre capacity, often for something a page could answer with a click.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Where this guide is going</h3>
    <p>What this site can give people is a base knowledge of everything: enough to understand how the pieces fit, so they can then go deeper wherever they choose. If many people share that base, more of them can move into fields the world needs and is short of: building semiconductors, doing good science, and being knowledgeable and grounded in general. That makes for a better world.</p>
    <p>I work in IT, so the guide starts with technology, alongside the personal side: health, money and good books. It is meant to grow. <b>Planned, not yet written:</b> deeper sections on physics, chemistry, biology and mathematics, and on understanding your own body: why it responds as it does, simple exercise anyone can do, and ageing. There are many experts in each of these; the aim here is to state the established facts plainly and link to their research.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Why this guide is free</h3>
    <p>I would rather spend my own tokens once, building something useful, than have thousands of people spend theirs asking for the same basics. So this guide is free, and so are the frameworks we build: <a href="/culvert/">Culvert</a>, <a href="/penstock/">Penstock</a>, <a href="/cistern/">Cistern</a> and the others on the <a href="/">home page</a>, all open source.</p>
    <p><b>A click is cheaper than a prompt.</b> For what is already known, read the tab. Use AI where it earns its keep: complex business logic, building frameworks, investigating a hard problem, or when you genuinely need to move fast. AI is here to stay; use it in the right context.</p>
    <p>And keep the foundations. They are what let you tell a good answer from one that only looks good.</p>
    <p class="note">Joseph Aruja · start with <a href="#start">Start here</a></p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> this is a personal story, and where it says "as I remember it", it is memory. Dated facts it mentions are checked on the pages that cover them, such as <a href="#history">A brief history</a> (Struts, Spring, EJB) and <a href="#hardware">How Computers Are Built</a>.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<section class="panel" id="p-history" data-name="A brief history">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>A brief history of software systems</h2><p>How we got here, in plain words: each era solved the last one's pain and rediscovered the same hard problems. Knowing the story makes today's tools easier to understand, and easier to judge.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>The thread through it all</h3>
    <p>Every era wrestled with the same four questions: <b>how do two programs agree on a contract, where does state live, what happens when something fails, and how do we see what went wrong?</b> The answers moved from heavy and agreed in advance, to light and flexible, and back again whenever the light way hurt. The questions never went away.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Why learning how it evolved matters</h3>
    <p><i>Our view, with the ideas it rests on, and the other view at the end.</i> Tools change every few years; the problems they solve change far more slowly. Knowing how we got here is how you tell a new idea from an old one with a new name, and how you choose and fix things without guessing.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>You recognise the pattern when it comes back.</b> The same four questions run through every era on this page (contracts, state, failure, seeing what went wrong), and the answers swing between &ldquo;agreed in advance&rdquo; and &ldquo;light and flexible&rdquo;. When a new tool arrives, you can ask which swing it is, and what the last one learned.</li>
      <li><b>You know why the fence is there.</b> Chesterton&rsquo;s fence (see <a href="#philosophy">Philosophy</a>): do not remove something until you know why it was put up. Most &ldquo;legacy&rdquo; code is a decision whose reason was forgotten; history is how you get the reason back.</li>
      <li><b>You avoid known traps.</b> Fred Brooks named the <i>second-system effect</i> in <i>The Mythical Man-Month</i> (1975), from IBM&rsquo;s move to the far more ambitious OS/360: after a small, clean first system, the second tends to become over-engineered, because the designer adds every idea deferred the first time. Rewrites still fall into it. As George Santayana put it: &ldquo;Those who cannot remember the past are condemned to repeat it.&rdquo;</li>
      <li><b>You judge what will last.</b> The Lindy effect is a heuristic, not a law: for things that do not wear out, such as ideas and technologies, the longer something has already lasted, the longer it is likely to keep lasting. It is a reason to learn the long-lived foundations on this guide&rsquo;s Foundations pages first, and to treat this year&rsquo;s framework as unproven until it has survived a few years.</li>
      <li><b>You choose by requirement, not fashion.</b> Knowing what problem a tool was built for (see <a href="#apps">Apps</a>, <a href="#buildtools">Build Tools</a>, <a href="#uihistory">UI History</a>) tells you whether that problem is yours.</li>
      <li><b>You can work with AI on old systems.</b> Models produce code in the style of every era they learned from, and many systems people rely on are decades old. Knowing the history helps you recognise an outdated pattern in a suggestion, and maintain the old system with care rather than fear.</li>
    </ul>
    <p><b>New engineers are lucky</b> (<i>our view</i>). What experienced engineers learned over 25 to 30 years, one release, outage and rewrite at a time, is now written down, explained and searchable, and AI can walk you through it. If you are ready to put in the work, you can learn in two or three years what took them decades to live through. The history on this page is that shortcut.</p>
    <p class="note"><i>The other view:</i> history can turn into nostalgia (&ldquo;we did this in 2005&rdquo;) and into a reason to dismiss things that really are better. Tools do improve. Learn the <i>why</i> behind each change, not the trivia, and never use the past only to say no. And reading is not the same as living through it: judgement from your own incidents and mistakes still takes time, so build things and run them while you read.</p>
    <p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Second-system_effect" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Second-system effect</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/George_Santayana" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: George Santayana</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lindy_effect" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Lindy effect</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/G._K._Chesterton" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: G. K. Chesterton (Chesterton&rsquo;s fence)</a>.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide" id="cycles"><h3>A look back: technology goes in cycles</h3>
    <p>The same ideas keep coming back, each time on cheaper hardware, faster networks and better tools. Knowing the earlier turn of the wheel tells you what the new one will get wrong.</p>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>The swing</th><th>Earlier</th><th>Then</th><th>Now</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Where the computer is</td><td>1960s time-sharing: many users on one shared mainframe</td><td>Personal computers and client-server: a computer on every desk</td><td>Cloud computing: shared computers rented by the minute. Its history is usually traced back to time-sharing</td></tr>
      <tr><td>How thin the client is</td><td>Terminals on a mainframe: the screen did almost nothing</td><td>Desktop applications, then web pages made on the server</td><td>Single-page apps in the browser, alongside server rendering, chosen per page</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Contracts between programs</td><td>CORBA (first version October 1991): an interface definition language agreed in advance</td><td>JSON over HTTP: light, flexible, often with no schema at all</td><td>gRPC: Protocol Buffers as the interface definition language. Agreed in advance again</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Data</td><td>Relational databases: SQL and ACID transactions</td><td>NoSQL: scale out, give up some guarantees</td><td>NewSQL: NoSQL&rsquo;s scale with ACID guarantees back</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Work across several systems</td><td>Distributed transactions with two-phase commit (CORBA defined a transaction service among its standard services)</td><td>The enterprise service bus orchestrating flows between systems</td><td>Sagas: local transactions, with a compensating step to undo each one. The idea comes from a 1987 paper, older than CORBA itself</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Integration</td><td>The enterprise service bus: a smart centre</td><td>Microservices: &ldquo;smart endpoints and dumb pipes&rdquo;</td><td>Service meshes and gateways: some of the centre returns, as infrastructure</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note"><i>Analysis, and the other view:</i> it is less a circle than a spiral. Each return happens because a constraint changed (hardware got cheap, networks got fast, operations got automated), and it comes back with lessons from the last time. The skill is to ask, of any &ldquo;new&rdquo; idea: what changed that makes it work now, when it struggled before?</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Microservices and CORBA: not the same, but alike in places</h3>
    <p><i>Our view, built on the facts below, with the other view at the end.</i> Microservices are not the new CORBA, but they share some of its problems, and a badly split system can repeat its mistakes. How alike they are depends on how the services were split.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>The similarities.</b> Both spread one system across a network, so both pay the same tax. Peter Deutsch&rsquo;s list of the fallacies of distributed computing (Sun, 1994), which begins &ldquo;the network is reliable&rdquo; and &ldquo;latency is zero&rdquo;, applies to both. Fine-grained synchronous calls between services that share a database and release together recreate distributed objects: a <i>distributed monolith</i>.</li>
      <li><b>The differences.</b> CORBA&rsquo;s <i>location transparency</i>, treating a remote object like a local one, is the part most criticised. The 2014 article by James Lewis and Martin Fowler that named microservices does the opposite. It defines them as independently deployable services organised around business capabilities, and says remote calls are more expensive, so remote APIs need to be coarser-grained. Done that way, it is an organisational choice, not a remote-object technology.</li>
      <li><b>Was CORBA bad technology?</b> Not as simply as its reputation says. Wikipedia&rsquo;s account of it notes that much of the criticism of CORBA stems from poor implementations of the standard rather than the standard itself. It also records real problems: early versions defined the interface language but not the format on the wire, so different vendors&rsquo; products could not talk to each other until CORBA 2; design by committee; and location transparency. <i>Our view:</i> many of its ideas were right and came back under other names, such as interface definitions in gRPC.</li>
      <li><b>Why sagas came back.</b> Two-phase commit is a blocking protocol: if the coordinator fails permanently, participants that have voted can wait for ever. Across independently deployed services that is too fragile, so the work becomes a saga of local steps, each with a compensating step to undo it (see &ldquo;REST, long journeys, and sagas&rdquo; further down this page).</li>
      <li><b>Are we only surviving because of modern tools?</b> Partly. Container platforms, service meshes, retries, circuit breakers and distributed tracing absorb pain that earlier teams carried by hand. But the same tools make failure visible and recovery automatic, which is an improvement, not just a mask.</li>
      <li><b>A one-question test</b> (<i>our view</i>): can one team deploy one service on a Tuesday afternoon without coordinating with anyone else? If not, the system has the costs of distribution without the benefit, and the tools are keeping it alive.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note"><i>The other view:</i> many teams would do better to start with a modular monolith and split only when team size or scaling demands it (see <a href="#microservices">Microservices</a>). Others point out that at large scale, with many teams, independent deployment is the only thing that keeps them moving at all. Both are true for different requirements.</p>
    <p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Common_Object_Request_Broker_Architecture" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Common Object Request Broker Architecture</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fallacies_of_distributed_computing" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Fallacies of distributed computing</a>; <a href="https://martinfowler.com/articles/microservices.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">James Lewis and Martin Fowler: Microservices (2014)</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cloud_computing" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Cloud computing (history)</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thin_client" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Thin client</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GRPC" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: gRPC</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NewSQL" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: NewSQL</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Two-phase_commit_protocol" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Two-phase commit protocol</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Compensating_transaction" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Compensating transaction (sagas)</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Enterprise_service_bus" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia: Enterprise service bus</a>.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Distributed objects: everything agreed in advance</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>CORBA</b>, from the Object Management Group, let programs in different languages call each other's objects across a network. You wrote the contract in an interface definition language and generated client stubs and server skeletons before anything could talk.</li>
      <li><b>Java RMI</b> did the same between Java programs, more simply, and Java could load the classes it needed over the network.</li>
      <li><b>Application servers</b> such as WebLogic wrapped this with transactions, security and pooling. Powerful, and heavy.</li>
      <li><b>What it taught us:</b> a remote call is not a local call. It can be slow, fail halfway, or never answer.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Enterprise JavaBeans: the beans we lived with</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Session beans</b> held business logic. <b>Stateless</b> ones served any caller and were pooled, so they suited reads and simple operations. <b>Stateful</b> ones kept a conversation with one client across calls.</li>
      <li><b>Stateful session beans and database locks</b> were how some booking and ticketing systems held a seat while a customer finished: the bean kept the conversation, the database kept the lock.</li>
      <li><b>Entity beans</b> represented rows in the database, with the container managing how they were loaded and saved. They were heavy and slow to work with, and plain Java objects mapped by tools such as Hibernate, and later JPA, replaced them.</li>
      <li><b>Message-driven beans</b> consumed messages from a JMS queue or topic, bringing event-driven work inside the application server.</li>
      <li><b>What it taught us:</b> the ideas (stateless services, conversations, transactions, messaging) were right; the weight was not. Spring, and later EJB itself, kept the ideas and dropped the ceremony.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Models, UML and generated screens</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>UML</b>, which appeared in 1997, gave teams one language for drawing systems: class diagrams, sequence diagrams, use cases. Grady Booch, James Rumbaugh and Ivar Jacobson brought their methods together into it.</li>
      <li><b>Model-driven architecture</b>, from the Object Management Group, went further: draw the model, then generate the code from it. It worked well for some layers and poorly for the rest.</li>
      <li><b>XSLT</b> transformed XML into HTML, so screens could be generated from data and templates rather than written by hand.</li>
      <li><b>What it taught us:</b> generating code from a model is powerful when the model is the truth, and painful when people edit the generated code. The same lesson applies to AI-generated code today.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>The early web: stateless by design</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>HTTP forgets you between requests.</b> To remember who you were, pages carried state in <b>hidden form fields</b>, in the URL (URL rewriting) and, increasingly, in cookies.</li>
      <li><b>HTML was standardised</b> in the mid-1990s, but browsers still differed: code checked whether it ran in Internet Explorer or Netscape, and what we now call Ajax worked first through Microsoft's XMLHTTP object.</li>
      <li><b>Caching mattered</b> because bandwidth was precious on dial-up: browsers and proxies kept copies, and pages said how long a copy stayed fresh.</li>
      <li><b>What it taught us:</b> statelessness scales, and every bit of state has to live somewhere on purpose.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Pages made on the server</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>CGI scripts</b> started a new process for every request.</li>
      <li><b>PHP</b> began in 1994 as Rasmus Lerdorf's "Personal Home Page" tools and grew into a language for building web pages on the server.</li>
      <li><b>Java servlets and JSP</b> ran inside a long-lived server, handling many requests without starting a process each time.</li>
      <li><b>Struts</b>, an Apache project founded in May 2000, brought the model-view-controller pattern to Java web applications.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Runtimes get fast, and get company</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Java began by interpreting bytecode,</b> which was slow. Just-in-time (JIT) compilers, which compile the busiest code to machine code while the program runs, made it fast, and the JVM's HotSpot became the standard approach.</li>
      <li><b>Microsoft's C# and .NET</b> arrived in the early 2000s with many of the same ideas: a managed runtime, garbage collection and code compiled just in time.</li>
      <li><b>What it taught us:</b> a good runtime lets you write simpler code and still go fast, and ideas spread quickly once they work.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Integration: buses, services and lighter ways</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>SOAP web services</b> brought back contracts agreed in advance, written in WSDL, with generated clients, much as CORBA had done.</li>
      <li><b>Enterprise service buses</b> put routing, transformation and orchestration into one large central product.</li>
      <li><b>Lighter tools followed.</b> Apache Camel implemented the patterns from Hohpe and Woolf's <i>Enterprise Integration Patterns</i> as a library. Spring came into being in 2003, by its own account, as a response to the complexity of the early J2EE specifications.</li>
      <li><b>What it taught us:</b> one big central hub becomes the bottleneck and the single point of failure.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>REST, long journeys, and sagas</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>REST</b>, which Roy Fielding described in his doctoral dissertation, used the web's own design (resources, URLs, standard methods, caching) instead of a custom remote-call layer.</li>
      <li><b>Without distributed transactions,</b> a long piece of work becomes a journey of steps. If it fails halfway, you undo the completed steps in reverse order, which only works if you recorded every step you took.</li>
      <li><b>This idea is old.</b> It was described as the "saga" in a 1987 database paper by Hector Garcia-Molina and Kenneth Salem, long before microservices made the word popular again. See <a href="#architecture">Architecture</a>.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>One runnable JAR: from app servers to "java -jar"</h3>
    <p>For years a Java web application was a WAR file deployed into a separate application server. Then frameworks started shipping the server inside the application: one file you start with <code>java -jar</code>.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Dropwizard</b> (first published to Maven Central in December 2011) bundled an embedded Jetty server with Jersey, Jackson, Logback and Metrics, and its getting-started guide builds the whole application as a "fat JAR". As I remember it, that was the first time many of us ran a service this way, before Spring Boot (1.0 in April 2014) made the idea mainstream.</li>
      <li><b>How the fat JAR is built:</b> the Maven Shade Plugin unpacks every dependency into one archive. Its <code>ServicesResourceTransformer</code> merges the <code>META-INF/services</code> files so libraries such as Jersey and Jackson do not overwrite each other's, and its <code>ManifestResourceTransformer</code> sets the <code>Main-Class</code>, so <code>java -jar app.jar server config.yml</code> just works.</li>
      <li><b>Why it mattered:</b> one file to build, copy and run made services simple to deploy, and fitted containers perfectly a few years later. See <a href="#docker">Docker</a> and <a href="#spring">Spring Boot</a>.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://repo1.maven.org/maven2/com/yammer/dropwizard/dropwizard-core/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Maven Central: dropwizard-core, first release dated 22 December 2011</a>; <a href="https://repo1.maven.org/maven2/org/springframework/boot/spring-boot/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Maven Central: spring-boot, first release dated 1 April 2014</a>; <a href="https://github.com/dropwizard/dropwizard" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Dropwizard README and getting-started guide</a>; the transformer classes inside the maven-shade-plugin artefact.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Why Spring lasted, and how it helped Java</h3>
    <p>Rod Johnson released the first version of Spring with his book <i>Expert One-on-One J2EE Design and Development</i> in October 2002, as a lighter way to build enterprise Java than Enterprise JavaBeans; it was released under the Apache licence in June 2003. Many of the tools in the table below faded. Spring did not, and the reasons are worth learning:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>It solved the real pain.</b> Plain Java objects, dependency injection, and transactions set up by configuration rather than by the EJB container. Ordinary code became easy to write and to test.</li>
      <li><b>It kept changing with the platform.</b> Annotations instead of XML, Spring Boot's runnable JAR and sensible defaults (1.0 in April 2014), then a sixth major version of the framework (November 2022) that moved to a Java 17 baseline and the <code>jakarta</code> namespace. It adopted new Java features rather than fighting them. See <a href="#spring">Spring Boot</a>.</li>
      <li><b>It integrated rather than replaced.</b> It worked with the libraries people already used (Hibernate, messaging, web frameworks) instead of asking them to throw everything away.</li>
      <li><b>It stayed open and backward-compatible enough</b> that a team could upgrade step by step over the years, without rewrites.</li>
    </ul>
    <p><b>How that helped Java:</b> when enterprise Java was heavy, Spring made it productive again, and when cloud and containers arrived, Spring Boot made Java a natural fit for small runnable services. Keeping millions of developers productive on the JVM gave Java time to modernise itself: faster releases, records, virtual threads. (The reasons above are this guide's analysis; the dates are sourced.)</p>
    <p class="sources"><b>Checked against</b> (Wikipedia): <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spring_Framework" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Spring Framework</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spring_Boot" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Spring Boot</a>.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Replaced by modern tools</h3>
    <p>Many tools from those years did not fail; the problems they solved moved somewhere else. A few we used every day:</p>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <tr><th>Then</th><th>What it did</th><th>Now, mostly</th><th>Why it moved on</th></tr>
      <tr><td><b>OSGi</b> (OSGi Alliance founded 1999)</td><td>Dynamic modules inside one JVM: bundles with declared dependencies, loaded and replaced while running, plus a service registry. Eclipse is built on it.</td><td>Separate services in containers; the Java Platform Module System (Java 9, 2017) for module boundaries</td><td>Isolation moved between processes instead of inside one; bundle manifests and class loading were hard to live with. The OSGi work moved to the Eclipse Foundation at the end of 2020.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Google Guice</b></td><td>Lightweight dependency injection</td><td>Injection built into the main frameworks, such as Spring Boot</td><td>Frameworks now do injection plus configuration, servers and data access. Guice is stable rather than gone: its last two releases came out together in May 2023, the second moving it to the jakarta namespace.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>EAR and RAR files</b></td><td>An EAR bundled modules for an application server; a RAR held a resource adapter connecting the server to enterprise systems (Jakarta Connectors)</td><td>One runnable JAR or container image per service; client libraries, REST and messaging for integration</td><td>No application server, nowhere to deploy them. They remain where WebSphere-style servers remain. WAR files are still used with servlet containers.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>PuTTY and its connection managers</b> (PuTTY from 1999)</td><td>SSH from Windows, with saved sessions; add-ons gave it tabs</td><td>OpenSSH built into Windows 10 since version 1803; Windows Terminal (announced 2019), which has tabs</td><td>Windows ships <code>ssh</code> itself. PuTTY is still maintained by Simon Tatham; it is simply no longer the only way.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><b>Logs on a shared network drive</b></td><td>Every server wrote files to a mapped drive; you searched them by hand</td><td>Logs, metrics and traces sent to a central platform: a cloud logging service or an open-source stack, often through OpenTelemetry</td><td>Hundreds of short-lived containers cannot share a drive, and you need search, alerts and one view across services. Syslog started the idea of sending logs elsewhere in the 1980s. See <a href="#observability">Observability</a>.</td></tr>
    </table></div>
    <p class="note">The lesson: learn the problem a tool solves, not just the tool. The problem stays when the tool is replaced.</p>
    <p class="sources"><b>Checked against</b> (Wikipedia unless stated): <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/OSGi" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OSGi</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Java_Platform_Module_System" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Java Platform Module System</a>; <a href="https://repo1.maven.org/maven2/com/google/inject/guice/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Maven Central: Guice releases</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/EAR_(file_format)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">EAR</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Java_EE_Connector_Architecture" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jakarta Connectors</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PuTTY" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PuTTY</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/OpenSSH" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OpenSSH</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Windows_Terminal" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Windows Terminal</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Syslog" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Syslog</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cloud_Native_Computing_Foundation" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CNCF (OpenTelemetry)</a>.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Microservices, cloud, and seeing what happens</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Microservices</b> split systems into small services that each own their data. They brought back every distributed-systems problem at once, now spread across many teams.</li>
      <li><b>For years, failures were hard to follow</b> from one service to the next. Observability caught up: logs, metrics and traces, and a shared trace context passed with every call (the W3C <code>traceparent</code> header). See <a href="#observability">Observability</a>.</li>
      <li><b>The cloud</b> turned servers into something you rent by the minute and declare in code.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>The people behind the ideas</h3>
    <p>We remember the famous product launches and forget many of the people whose ideas we use every day. A few worth knowing, and reading:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>The "Gang of Four"</b>, Erich Gamma, Richard Helm, Ralph Johnson and John Vlissides, wrote <i>Design Patterns: Elements of Reusable Object-Oriented Software</i>, which gave us a shared vocabulary: factory, observer, strategy, adapter.</li>
      <li><b>Martin Fowler</b> wrote <i>Refactoring</i> (its second edition with Kent Beck) and <i>Patterns of Enterprise Application Architecture</i>, and has explained ideas such as continuous integration and microservices to a generation of engineers. He calls the Gang of Four's book "the best book ever written on object-oriented design".</li>
      <li><b>Kent Beck</b> developed Extreme Programming, and test-driven development within it, in the late 1990s.</li>
      <li><b>Grady Booch, James Rumbaugh and Ivar Jacobson</b> created UML. <b>Gregor Hohpe and Bobby Woolf</b> wrote <i>Enterprise Integration Patterns</i>. <b>Roy Fielding</b> described REST. <b>Hector Garcia-Molina and Kenneth Salem</b> described sagas.</li>
      <li><b>And thousands of open-source maintainers</b> whose names most of us never learn, whose work runs in almost everything we build.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">Before you ask an AI to explain a pattern, read the person who named it. The original is usually clearer, and it tells you why. Start with <a href="https://martinfowler.com/bliki/GangOfFour.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Fowler on the Gang of Four</a>, <a href="https://martinfowler.com/bliki/ExtremeProgramming.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Extreme Programming</a> and <a href="https://martinfowler.com/bliki/TestDrivenDevelopment.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">test-driven development</a>.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Names to remember</h3>
    <p>A name sticks when it is tied to something you use. Each row links a person to an idea you meet in your daily work, and to where it appears in this guide.</p>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Person</th><th>The idea you use</th><th>When</th><th>In this guide</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Grace Hopper</td><td>FLOW-MATIC, a language she developed that influenced COBOL; she argued for programming in English words, translated into machine code by a compiler</td><td></td><td><a href="#banking">Banking</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Edsger W. Dijkstra</td><td>The shortest-path algorithm in every route planner, and structured programming</td><td>Algorithm conceived 1956</td><td><a href="#mathematics">Mathematics</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Tony Hoare</td><td>Quicksort; and the null reference, which he later called his &ldquo;billion-dollar mistake&rdquo;</td><td>1959; 1965</td><td><a href="#kotlin">Kotlin</a> (null safety)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Melvin Conway</td><td>Conway&rsquo;s law: organisations design systems that mirror their own communication structure</td><td>1967</td><td><a href="#microservices">Microservices</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Donald Knuth</td><td><i>The Art of Computer Programming</i>, and the TeX typesetting system</td><td>First volume 1968</td><td><a href="#mathematics">Mathematics</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Edgar F. Codd</td><td>The relational model behind every SQL database</td><td>1970 paper</td><td><a href="#database">Database Design</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Ken Thompson and Dennis Ritchie</td><td>Unix; and the C language (Ritchie)</td><td></td><td><a href="#linux">Linux</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Fred Brooks</td><td><i>The Mythical Man-Month</i> and Brooks&rsquo;s law: adding people to a late software project makes it later</td><td>1975</td><td><a href="#ways">Ways of Working</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Vint Cerf and Bob Kahn</td><td>TCP/IP; Cerf is described as one of the &ldquo;fathers of the Internet&rdquo;, a title he shares with Kahn</td><td></td><td><a href="#networking">Networking</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Leslie Lamport</td><td>Logical clocks for ordering events across machines; the Paxos consensus protocol; LaTeX</td><td>1978; Paxos submitted 1989</td><td><a href="#architecture">Architecture</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Barbara Liskov</td><td>The Liskov substitution principle, the &ldquo;L&rdquo; in SOLID</td><td>1987</td><td><a href="#patterns">SOLID &amp; Design Patterns</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Hector Garcia-Molina and Kenneth Salem</td><td>Sagas: long transactions as steps with compensations</td><td>1987</td><td><a href="#architecture">Architecture</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Tim Berners-Lee</td><td>The World Wide Web and HTTP</td><td>Proposed 1989</td><td><a href="#web">Web Fundamentals</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Linus Torvalds</td><td>The Linux kernel; and Git</td><td>Linux 1991</td><td><a href="#linux">Linux</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>The Gang of Four</td><td><i>Design Patterns</i>: factory, observer, strategy, adapter</td><td>1994</td><td><a href="#patterns">SOLID &amp; Design Patterns</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Jim Gray</td><td>Work on database and transaction processing systems</td><td>Turing Award 1998</td><td><a href="#database">Database Design</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Kent Beck</td><td>Extreme Programming, and test-driven development</td><td>Late 1990s</td><td><a href="#testing">Testing</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Roy Fielding</td><td>REST, defined in his doctoral dissertation</td><td>2000</td><td><a href="#apis">APIs</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Robert C. Martin</td><td>The SOLID principles of object-oriented design (the acronym was coined around 2004 by Michael Feathers)</td><td>2000 paper</td><td><a href="#patterns">SOLID &amp; Design Patterns</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Eric Evans</td><td>Domain-driven design, from his book of that name</td><td>2003</td><td><a href="#microservices">Microservices</a></td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note">Years are from each person&rsquo;s or idea&rsquo;s Wikipedia article; a blank means the article gives no single year for it.</p>
    <p class="sources"><b>Checked against Wikipedia:</b> <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grace_Hopper" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Grace Hopper</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edsger_W._Dijkstra" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Edsger W. Dijkstra</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dijkstra%27s_algorithm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Dijkstra&rsquo;s algorithm</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tony_Hoare" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Tony Hoare</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quicksort" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Quicksort</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Null_pointer" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Null pointer</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conway%27s_law" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Conway&rsquo;s law</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donald_Knuth" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Donald Knuth</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Art_of_Computer_Programming" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The Art of Computer Programming</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edgar_F._Codd" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Edgar F. Codd</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Relational_model" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Relational model</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ken_Thompson" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ken Thompson</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dennis_Ritchie" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Dennis Ritchie</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fred_Brooks" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Fred Brooks</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brooks%27s_law" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Brooks&rsquo;s law</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vint_Cerf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Vint Cerf</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bob_Kahn" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bob Kahn</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leslie_Lamport" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Leslie Lamport</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lamport_timestamp" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Lamport timestamp</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paxos_(computer_science)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Paxos (computer science)</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barbara_Liskov" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Barbara Liskov</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liskov_substitution_principle" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Liskov substitution principle</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saga_pattern" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Saga pattern</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tim_Berners-Lee" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Tim Berners-Lee</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Linus_Torvalds" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Linus Torvalds</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Design_Patterns" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Design Patterns</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jim_Gray_(computer_scientist)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jim Gray (computer scientist)</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Extreme_programming" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Extreme programming</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Representational_state_transfer" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Representational state transfer</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SOLID" target="_blank" rel="noopener">SOLID</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Domain-driven_design" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Domain-driven design</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eric_Evans_(technologist)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Eric Evans</a>.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Who named it? Test yourself</h3>
    <p>Recalling a name fixes it better than reading it again. Answer each one before you open it.</p>
    <details><summary>Who called the null reference a &ldquo;billion-dollar mistake&rdquo;?</summary><p>Tony Hoare, who introduced it in ALGOL W in 1965. Kotlin&rsquo;s null safety is one answer to it.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Why does adding people to a late project make it later, and who said so?</summary><p>Fred Brooks, in <i>The Mythical Man-Month</i>: new people need time to learn the project, and every extra person adds lines of communication.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Your services end up shaped like your teams. Whose law is that?</summary><p>Melvin Conway&rsquo;s, from 1967.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Whose dissertation defined REST?</summary><p>Roy Fielding&rsquo;s, in 2000.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Who described sagas, and when?</summary><p>Hector Garcia-Molina and Kenneth Salem, in 1987, long before microservice designs reused the idea.</p></details>
    <details><summary>What does the &ldquo;L&rdquo; in SOLID stand for, and who is it named after?</summary><p>The Liskov substitution principle, after Barbara Liskov. SOLID itself comes from Robert C. Martin&rsquo;s 2000 paper on design principles; the acronym was coined around 2004 by Michael Feathers.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Who created both Linux and Git?</summary><p>Linus Torvalds.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Whose paper gave us the relational model?</summary><p>Edgar F. Codd&rsquo;s, in 1970.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Who gave us logical clocks and Paxos?</summary><p>Leslie Lamport.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Who are the Gang of Four?</summary><p>Erich Gamma, Richard Helm, Ralph Johnson and John Vlissides, authors of <i>Design Patterns</i> (1994).</p></details>
    <p class="note">Come back in a week and try again: recall spread over days lasts longer than one long session (see <a href="#body">Body, Mind &amp; Nature</a>).</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>And now, AI</h3>
    <p>AI can generate the code that each of these eras made us write by hand: the stubs, the boilerplate, the glue. But the four questions have not changed: contracts, state, failure, and seeing what went wrong. People who know this history recognise an old problem in a new form, and do not mistake generated code for understanding. See <a href="#ai">AI for Engineers</a>.</p>
    <p class="note">Dates here are given where we could check them against the project's own record; elsewhere the page speaks in eras rather than years.</p>
  </article>

  </div>
</section>

<section class="panel" id="p-reading" data-name="Reading List">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Reading list</h2><p>Books that shaped how this guide thinks about health, character, money and ideas. Not technical manuals: the engineering bookshelf is on <a href="#why">Why this guide exists</a>. Read slowly, take what helps, and leave the rest.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card"><h3>Health and habits</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b><i>The 4 Pillar Plan</i></b>, Dr Rangan Chatterjee (2017). A doctor's plan for health built on four everyday pillars, small changes made consistently.</li>
      <li><b><i>The 5 AM Club</i></b>, Robin Sharma (2018). Owning the start of your day before the world asks for it.</li>
      <li><b><i>The Everyday Hero Manifesto</i></b>, Robin Sharma (2021). Doing ordinary work, every day, to a high standard.</li>
      <li><b><i>Atomic Habits</i></b>, James Clear (2018). Change by small steps: build a habit by making it small and easy, and aim to get about 1% better each day.</li>
      <li><b><i>Psycho-Cybernetics</i></b>, Maxwell Maltz (1960). How your self-image sets the limits of what you attempt, and how to change it.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">See <a href="#body">Body, Mind &amp; Nature</a>.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Mind and character</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b><i>As a Man Thinketh</i></b>, James Allen (1903). A short book on the power of thought: by working patiently on our thoughts we can remake our lives and change our circumstances. Old enough to be free to read: it is on <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/As_a_Man_Thinketh" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>.</li>
      <li><b><i>Meditations</i></b>, Marcus Aurelius. Private notes on Stoic philosophy, written by a Roman emperor to himself, much of it while on military campaign. Nearly two thousand years old and still practical: control what you can, accept what you cannot, do your duty well.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">See <a href="#philosophy">Philosophy</a> and <a href="#goodhuman">Being a Good Human</a>.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Money, work and building</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b><i>The Almanack of Naval Ravikant</i></b>, compiled by Eric Jorgenson (2020). Naval Ravikant's thinking on wealth, judgement and happiness, gathered from his talks and writing.</li>
      <li><b><i>Zero to One</i></b>, Peter Thiel with Blake Masters (2014). Building something new rather than copying what exists; it grew out of Thiel's startup class at Stanford.</li>
      <li><b><i>I Will Teach You to Be Rich</i></b>, Ramit Sethi (2009, updated 2019). A beginner's guide to managing money, written for people starting out.</li>
      <li><b><i>The Psychology of Money</i></b>, Morgan Housel (2020). Why behaviour matters more than intelligence when it comes to money.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Thinking and science</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b><i>The Beginning of Infinity: Explanations that Transform the World</i></b>, David Deutsch (2011). Progress comes from good explanations, and there is no limit to how far it can go.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">See <a href="#science">Science &amp; Method</a>.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Why a reading list on an engineering site</h3>
    <p>Technology changes every year; these ideas have lasted far longer. Looking after your health, mastering your own thinking, being careful with money and valuing good explanations make a better engineer and a better person. If AI saves you an hour, spend some of it on a chapter of one of these. Suggest a book through the <a href="#start">ask-on-GitHub link</a> at the end of any page.</p>
    <p class="sources"><b>Checked against</b> (Wikipedia): <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rangan_Chatterjee" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Rangan Chatterjee</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robin_Sharma" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Robin Sharma</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Atomic_Habits" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Atomic Habits</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Psycho-Cybernetics" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Psycho-Cybernetics</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/As_a_Man_Thinketh" target="_blank" rel="noopener">As a Man Thinketh</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Meditations" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Meditations</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Naval_Ravikant" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Naval Ravikant</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zero_to_One" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Zero to One</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/I_Will_Teach_You_to_Be_Rich" target="_blank" rel="noopener">I Will Teach You to Be Rich</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Psychology_of_Money" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The Psychology of Money</a>; <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Beginning_of_Infinity" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The Beginning of Infinity</a>.</p>
  </article>

  </div>
</section>

<section class="panel" id="p-opensource" data-name="Our open source">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Our open source</h2><p>What EnrichMeAI builds in the open, kept apart from the learning pages. The guide stays neutral; these are the real projects, each with its own page and source.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card"><h3>Cistern</h3>
    <p>A self-hostable data server built on the Solid specifications. Apps and AI assistants ask permission instead of taking copies: the owner writes the rule, Cistern enforces it on every request, and every allow and deny gets a receipt. Java, Apache 2.0.</p>
    <p><a href="/cistern/">The Cistern page</a> · <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/cistern" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source and issues</a></p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Penstock</h3>
    <p>A self-hosted agent harness. With database storage, every model call and tool call is written to an audit log the owner reads back, and its pod tool reads from a Cistern pod only what was granted. Java, Spring Boot.</p>
    <p><a href="/penstock/">The Penstock page</a> · <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/penstock" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source and issues</a></p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Culvert</h3>
    <p>A cloud-agnostic data-pipeline framework: pipelines are defined once against a language-neutral contract set and run on any cloud by swapping adapters. Java and Python, Apache 2.0. The guide's <a href="#culvert">Culvert page</a> teaches the ideas.</p>
    <p><a href="/culvert/">The Culvert page</a> · <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/culvert" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source and issues</a></p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>enrich-test-api</h3>
    <p>Java integration tests for cloud code, on local emulators: a test asks for a bucket or a queue and gets one, backed by a container the library started, so no cloud account is needed. An alpha, and its page says so. Java 17, Apache 2.0.</p>
    <p><a href="/enrich-test-api/">The enrich-test-api page</a> · <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/enrich-test-api" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source and issues</a></p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><h3>Learning from them</h3>
    <p>The learning pages use neutral examples on purpose, so they teach the idea rather than a product. When you want to see an idea in a real codebase, these projects are where to look: <a href="#agentharness">Agent Harness</a> and Penstock, <a href="#solid">Solid</a> and Cistern, <a href="#data">Data Engineering</a> and Culvert, <a href="#testing">Testing</a> and enrich-test-api. <a href="/headworks/">Headworks</a> shows Penstock and Cistern running together. Questions about a project go to that project's issues.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card wide"><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> each project's own page on this site and its repository at its latest release tag: <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/cistern" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Cistern</a>; <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/penstock" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Penstock</a>; <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/culvert" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Culvert</a>; <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/enrich-test-api" target="_blank" rel="noopener">enrich-test-api</a>. Versions are kept in step by the site's release checker.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= CULVERT ================= -->
<section class="panel" id="p-culvert" data-name="Culvert">
  <div class="panel-head"><h2>Culvert</h2><p>enrichme.ai's open-source, cloud-agnostic data pipeline framework for Java and Python. Pipeline code depends on a set of contracts, and adapters implement them for each cloud. Every fact on this tab is taken from the <a href="/culvert/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Culvert page on enrichmeai.com</a>, which is checked daily against Maven Central and PyPI; read it there for the current release.</p></div>
  <div class="grid">

  <article class="card wide"><h3>The idea</h3>
    <p>Pipelines are defined once against a <b>language-neutral contract set</b> (sixteen contracts plus a metrics record: <code>BlobStore</code>, <code>Warehouse</code>, <code>Source</code>, <code>Sink</code>, <code>JobControlRepository</code> and eleven more), realised as Java interfaces and Python Protocols. <b>Adapters</b> implement the contracts for each cloud, and every adapter must pass the same <b>conformance suite</b>, or it is not an adapter. Swap the cloud, keep the logic.</p>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>Is</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Your business logic</td><td>Written once; depends only on the contracts, never on a cloud SDK</td></tr>
      <tr><td>The contracts</td><td>Java interfaces and Python Protocols from one spec</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Cloud adapters</td><td>Swappable implementations that pass the shared conformance suite</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note">This is the "framework, not generated boilerplate" argument from the <a href="#blueprints">Frameworks vs AI</a> tab made concrete: one contract and one test suite instead of a hand-written cloud integration in every pipeline.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Design choices</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Java owns Beam:</b> the Dataflow/Beam execution layer is Java; legacy Python Beam is not carried forward. The two languages deliberately do different jobs.</li>
      <li><b>dbt, reused:</b> transformation is SQL and macros, packaged once; there is deliberately no Java transform module.</li>
      <li><b>Local-first:</b> the whole stack runs against emulators, so you can develop and test without a cloud account.</li>
      <li><b>No silent defaults:</b> <code>Warehouse.loadFromUri</code> requires a <code>LoadOptions</code>, because a defaulted write disposition let a re-run silently double its data.</li>
      <li><b>Honest failure:</b> reconciliation runs before the target is written, so a failed load cannot be reported as succeeded.</li>
      <li><b>Not a replacement</b> for Beam, Airflow or dbt: they are engines, and Culvert is a framework around them.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Install (Python)</h3>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># core contracts only, no cloud SDKs
pip install culvert
# + BigQuery, GCS, Pub/Sub, Secret Manager, observability
pip install 'culvert[gcp]'
# + Airflow-side DAG factory / operators / sensors, or dbt
pip install 'culvert[orchestration]'
pip install 'culvert[transform]'
# + StageClaim on PostgreSQL
pip install 'culvert[postgres]'
# test fixtures, and the conformance suite for your own adapters
pip install 'culvert[tester]'
pip install 'culvert[contract-tests]'
# gcp + orchestration + transform
pip install 'culvert[all]'</code></pre></div>
<div class="snip"><pre><code># adapters are discovered through entry points
from data_pipeline_core import autoconfig
blob_store = autoconfig.discover().first("blob_store")
# -&gt; GcsBlobStore, when culvert[gcp] is installed</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">Python 3.10 or newer.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Java artifacts (Maven Central)</h3>
    <p>Separate artifacts under <code>com.enrichmeai.culvert</code>, all at the same version:</p>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Area</th><th>Artifacts</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>Core</td><td><code>data-pipeline-core</code>, <code>-contract-tests</code>, <code>-tester</code>, <code>-it-support</code>, <code>-orchestration</code>, <code>-console</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>PostgreSQL</td><td><code>data-pipeline-postgres</code> (job control, StageClaim, InputReadiness)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>GCP</td><td><code>-gcp-gcs</code>, <code>-gcp-bigquery</code>, <code>-gcp-pubsub</code>, <code>-gcp-secrets</code>, <code>-gcp-observability</code>, <code>-gcp-dataflow</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>AWS</td><td><code>-aws-s3</code>, <code>-aws-athena</code>, <code>-aws-dynamodb</code>, <code>-aws-sqs</code>, <code>-aws-secrets</code>, <code>-aws-cloudwatch</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Azure</td><td><code>data-pipeline-azure-blob</code> (a skeleton: its methods throw until built out)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Documents</td><td><code>data-pipeline-pdf-text</code> (PDF to text with PDFBox; returns null rather than throwing for a scanned or unreadable PDF)</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
<div class="snip"><pre><code>// Gradle
implementation("com.enrichmeai.culvert:data-pipeline-core:&lt;version&gt;")
implementation("com.enrichmeai.culvert:data-pipeline-gcp-bigquery:&lt;version&gt;")</code></pre></div>
    <p class="note">The current version of every artifact is on the <a href="/culvert/">Culvert page</a>.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>What runs where</h3>
    <div class="tbl"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Contract</th><th>GCP</th><th>AWS</th><th>Azure</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      <tr><td>BlobStore</td><td>GCS</td><td>S3</td><td>skeleton</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Warehouse</td><td>BigQuery</td><td>Athena</td><td>roadmap</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Source · Sink</td><td>Pub/Sub</td><td>SQS</td><td>roadmap</td></tr>
      <tr><td>JobControlRepository</td><td>BigQuery</td><td>DynamoDB · Athena</td><td>roadmap</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Observability</td><td>Cloud Monitoring</td><td>CloudWatch</td><td>roadmap</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Secrets</td><td>Secret Manager</td><td>Secrets Manager</td><td>roadmap</td></tr>
      </tbody></table></div>
    <p class="note">GCP is the full implementation; AWS has the Java adapter family; Azure has a Blob Storage skeleton and is otherwise roadmap. <code>JobControlRepository</code> also has a PostgreSQL implementation.</p>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Features worth knowing</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Job control:</b> run state through <code>JobControlRepository</code> on BigQuery, DynamoDB, Athena or PostgreSQL; a read-only <code>culvert</code> command line (<code>runs</code>, <code>run</code>, <code>entities</code>, <code>failures</code>, <code>adapters</code>).</li>
      <li><b>StageClaim</b> (PostgreSQL): one stage of one unit for one period cannot start twice.</li>
      <li><b>InputReadiness</b> (PostgreSQL): names each expected input that is missing, failed or still running, rather than counting rows.</li>
      <li><b>Orchestration:</b> a cloud-neutral DAG model rendered to Airflow/Composer or Cloud Run jobs, with stage gates and a concurrency ceiling.</li>
      <li><b>Schema files:</b> an entity's columns, types, required flags, classification, masking, ranges and allowed values in YAML or JSON, loaded with the same rules in Java and Python.</li>
      <li><b><code>BlobStore.head(uri)</code>:</b> size, version token and metadata without reading the object, for loaders that must be idempotent by object version.</li>
      <li><b><code>BigQueryWarehouse.merge()</code>:</b> upserts with one MERGE and refuses a key that is not a target column.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card"><h3>Status, stated plainly</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Released in both languages on Maven Central and PyPI, under Apache 2.0 (the earliest releases were MIT).</li>
      <li>Ran end to end on a real GCP project (Cloud Run to BigQuery, event-driven) before release; that run caught eight production-only bugs every local test had passed.</li>
      <li>Culvert is pre-1.0: read the changelog's upgrade notes before moving between versions.</li>
      <li>A book on its design is in progress.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">Pin a version in your build, and verify any API against that version's sources, not against <code>main</code>.</p>
  </article>

  <!-- levels -->
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Levels: what to expect</h3><div class="levels"><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">1</span> Foundation</h4><ul><li>Explain contracts, adapters and the conformance suite</li><li>Install culvert with the right extras or add the Maven artifacts</li><li>Use an adapter through auto-discovery instead of a cloud SDK</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">2</span> Practitioner</h4><ul><li>Write pipeline code against BlobStore, Warehouse and job control</li><li>Use LoadOptions, head() and merge() for idempotent loads</li><li>Run and test against emulators locally</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">3</span> Senior</h4><ul><li>Design pipelines whose logic is cloud-neutral</li><li>Add an adapter that passes the conformance suite</li><li>Use StageClaim, InputReadiness and orchestration gates</li></ul></div><div class="lvl"><h4><span class="lvtag">4</span> Expert</h4><ul><li>Shape the contract spec and its evolution</li><li>Lead adoption across teams and clouds</li><li>Contribute adapters for new clouds (Azure)</li></ul></div></div><p class="note">Each level includes the ones before it. See <a href="#start">Start here</a> for what the levels mean.</p></article>
  <article class="card wide"><h3>Explore further</h3><div class="explore"><div><h4>Study next</h4><ul><li>The contract specification (CONTRACT.md)</li><li>Conformance suites as a design tool</li><li>Job control and run records</li><li>Orchestration renderers</li><li>Upgrading across 0.x releases</li></ul></div><div><h4>Official docs and good reading</h4><ul class="links"><li><a href="/culvert/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Culvert on enrichmeai.com</a><span class="lnote"> Overview, libraries, releases, status</span></li><li><a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/culvert" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Culvert on GitHub</a><span class="lnote"> Source, issues</span></li><li><a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/culvert/blob/main/docs/CONTRACT.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Contract specification</a><span class="lnote"> The authoritative list of contracts</span></li><li><a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/culvert/blob/main/CHANGELOG.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Changelog</a><span class="lnote"> Every release, including fixes</span></li><li><a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/culvert/blob/main/MIGRATION.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Migration guide</a><span class="lnote"> Upgrading between versions</span></li><li><a href="https://pypi.org/project/culvert/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">culvert on PyPI</a><span class="lnote"> Python package</span></li></ul></div></div><p class="sources"><b>Checked against:</b> <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/culvert" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Culvert repository</a>; <a href="https://pypi.org/project/culvert/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Culvert on PyPI</a>; <a href="https://enrichmeai.com/culvert/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The Culvert page on this site, kept in step with Maven Central and PyPI by the release checker</a>.</p><p class="runcode">A question, or found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/enrichmeai/enrichmeai.github.io/issues/new?title=%5BCulvert%5D%20" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open an issue on GitHub</a>. Nothing here is tracked and there are no comments to store: questions live in the open, where the next learner can find the answer.</p></article>

  </div>
</section>

<p class="empty" id="empty" hidden>Nothing matches that filter.</p>
</main>

<script>
(function(){
  const tabs = ["start","aiworry","idea2prod","electronics","hardware","linux","networking","internet","web","docker","dsa","concurrency","sql","database","graphdb","datastore","homelab","java","javathreads","ionio","kotlin","python","javascript","functional","fetoolchain","go","rust","buildtools","apis","apihistory","graphql","realtime","reactive","kafka","payments","banking","spring","springhistory","quarkus","faded","designux","accessibility","apps","uihistory","testing","teststrat","review","performance","security","pki","privacy","cyber","agentsec","oauth","data","datalibs","dbt","dags","patterns","architecture","quality","atob","distsys","microservices","casestudies","eventsourcing","sysdesign","specs","caching","devops","kubernetes","k8sdr","helm","operators","servicemesh","terraform","observability","sre","cloudinside","cloudcompare","cloudcost","retailcloud","gcpdata","bigquery","cloudrun","apigee","awsdata","azuredata","neural","llm","ai","rag","mcp","agentharness","humanagents","blueprints","claudetools","copilot","agentsgcp","harness","opstools","behaviours","ways","mathematics","science","everydayscience","philosophy","goodhuman","body","quantum","robotics","bitcoin","web3","solid","why","history","reading","opensource","culvert"];
  const tabs = ["start","aiworry","idea2prod","electronics","hardware","linux","networking","internet","web","docker","dsa","concurrency","sql","database","graphdb","datastore","homelab","java","javathreads","ionio","kotlin","python","javascript","functional","fetoolchain","go","rust","buildtools","apis","apihistory","graphql","realtime","reactive","kafka","payments","banking","spring","springhistory","quarkus","faded","designux","accessibility","apps","uihistory","testing","teststrat","review","performance","security","pki","privacy","cyber","agentsec","oauth","data","datalibs","dbt","dags","patterns","architecture","quality","atob","distsys","microservices","casestudies","eventsourcing","sysdesign","specs","caching","devops","kubernetes","k8sdr","helm","operators","servicemesh","terraform","observability","sre","cloudinside","cloudcompare","cloudcost","retailcloud","gcpdata","bigquery","cloudrun","apigee","awsdata","azuredata","neural","llm","ai","rag","mcp","agentharness","humanagents","blueprints","claudetools","copilot","agentsgcp","harness","opstools","behaviours","ways","mathematics","science","everydayscience","philosophy","goodhuman","body","quantum","robotics","bitcoin","web3","solid","why","history","reading","opensource","culvert"];
  const panels = Object.fromEntries(tabs.map(t => [t, document.getElementById("p-"+t)]));
  const links = document.querySelectorAll("nav a");
  const q = document.getElementById("q");
  const empty = document.getElementById("empty");

  // copy buttons
  document.querySelectorAll(".snip").forEach(s => {
    const b = document.createElement("button");
    b.className = "copy"; b.type = "button"; b.textContent = "Copy";
    b.addEventListener("click", () => {
      const txt = s.querySelector("pre").innerText;
      const done = () => { b.textContent = "Copied"; setTimeout(() => b.textContent = "Copy", 1200); };
      if (navigator.clipboard && navigator.clipboard.writeText) {
        navigator.clipboard.writeText(txt).then(done, () => selectIt(s));
      } else selectIt(s);
    });
    s.appendChild(b);
  });
  function selectIt(s){
    const r = document.createRange(); r.selectNodeContents(s.querySelector("pre"));
    const sel = getSelection(); sel.removeAllRanges(); sel.addRange(r);
  }

  function current(){
    const h = location.hash.replace("#","");
    return tabs.includes(h) ? h : "start";
  }

  function render(){
    const term = q.value.trim().toLowerCase();
    const active = current();
    links.forEach(a => {
      if (a.dataset.tab === active && !term) { a.setAttribute("aria-current","page"); try { a.scrollIntoView({block:"nearest",inline:"nearest"}); } catch(e){} }
      else a.removeAttribute("aria-current");
    });
    let any = false;
    tabs.forEach(t => {
      const p = panels[t];
      const cards = p.querySelectorAll(".card");
      if (!term){
        p.hidden = t !== active;
        cards.forEach(c => c.hidden = false);
        if (t === active) any = true;
      } else {
        let hits = 0;
        cards.forEach(c => {
          const m = c.textContent.toLowerCase().includes(term);
          c.hidden = !m; if (m) hits++;
        });
        p.hidden = hits === 0;
        if (hits) any = true;
      }
    });
    empty.hidden = any;
  }

  window.addEventListener("hashchange", () => { q.value = ""; render(); window.scrollTo(0,0); });
  q.addEventListener("input", render);
  render();
})();
</script>
